/* The heavy half of the ru cards — GENERATED, never hand-edited.
 *
 * abstract / sources / why / quote / wiki / image / places, for the cards whose ids begin `ru-`. None of it is
 * read until a reader REVEALS a card in this collection, so it is fetched then (bundle
 * `cardExtra:ru`) rather than downloaded by every visitor before they can flip one.
 *
 * IT STAGES ONTO A QUEUE rather than assigning, for the reason glossary-extra.js does: app.js
 * snapshots PRISTINE_CARDS at boot, which is BEFORE this file lands, so a plain assignment would
 * leave the editor's revert baseline empty and Revert would DELETE a shipped abstract rather than
 * restore it. cardExtraIngest drains the queue and re-seeds that baseline.
 *
 * An id data.js does not carry is not resurrected — the light half decides what a card is. */
(function () {
  var CARD_EXTRA = {
    "ru-001": {"questions":["Low and seasonal rainfall keeps trees off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so forest lies along its northern edge and desert and mountain shut it in on the south.","Iron Age herders left closely similar weapons, harness and animal ornament across more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why it is read as one world rather than many."],"abstract":"The <b>Eurasian Steppe</b> is the belt of temperate grassland that crosses the middle of the continent, reaching from the plains of the lower Danube and Hungary in the west to Manchuria in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is open country because the rain is scarce and seasonal, too little for forest and enough for grass, so trees keep to the river valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Woodland and then taiga close it in on the north, while deserts and the mountain walls of the Caucasus, the Tian Shan and the Altai shut it off on the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its western third, the Pontic-Caspian steppe, runs from the southern Urals to the lands north of the Black Sea, and it is the part on which Russian history opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lower Volga and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Don_(river)\">Don</span>, inside that western third, is where the ancestors of every modern domestic horse were bred, and their descendants spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE with the spoke-wheeled chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the grass made possible was a life built on herds that moved and people who moved with them, and by the 1st millennium BCE it ran the whole length of the belt. Communities from the Altai to the Carpathians buried their dead under mounds with horses and gold, in weapons and ornament so alike across more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) that they are handled as one culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek writers who met the westernmost of them thought the arrangement unbeatable: a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, carrying their houses on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The belt carried far more than herds, and confederations formed along it moved west again and again, though whether the Huns of Europe descended from the Xiongnu of Mongolia is still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of the steppe peoples of these centuries wrote anything down, so nearly everything said about them comes either from their neighbours' books or out of the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Jürgen Dengler, Idoia Biurrun, Steffen Boch, Iwona Dembicz and Péter Török, “Grasslands of the Palaearctic Biogeographic Realm: Introduction and Synthesis,” in <i>Encyclopedia of the World's Biomes</i>, vol. 3 (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 2020), 617–37, http://www.grassland-restoration.eu/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Dengler-et-al-2020.pdf. [Open access]","Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.46, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","Alexander Savelyev and Choongwon Jeong, “Early Nomads of the Eastern Steppe and Their Tentative Connections in the West,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e20, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Astana-steppe-7748.jpg/960px-Astana-steppe-7748.jpg","title":"The steppe of central Kazakhstan","desc":"Open grassland in Akmola province, north of Astana, photographed from a train. This is the middle section of the belt, between the Pontic-Caspian steppe in the west and the Mongolian steppe in the east.","credit":"Photograph by Vmenkov, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astana-steppe-7748.jpg","alt":"Flat grassland running to a low horizon under a pale sky, with no trees, buildings or hills in sight."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this belt open country rather than forest?","a":"Because the rain is scarce and seasonal, too little for trees and enough for grass, so woodland keeps to the river valleys."},{"q":"Why can weapons and ornament from 3,500 kilometres apart be handled as one culture?","a":"Because communities from the Altai to the Carpathians buried their dead under mounds with horses and gold in forms so alike across that distance."},{"q":"Why does nearly everything known about these peoples come from outside?","a":"Because none of them wrote anything down, so the record is their neighbours' books or what comes out of the ground."}],"wiki":"Eurasian Steppe","places":["pl:q77120"]},
    "ru-002": {"questions":["The Volga, the Don and the Dnieper all run south across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose grass carried the Srubnaya herders, then the Cimmerians, then the Scythians and finally the Sarmatians.","Ancient genomes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that its Bronze and Iron Age peoples were linked but that none of them was the direct ancestor of the group that followed."],"abstract":"The <b>Pontic-Caspian steppe</b> is the western third of the Eurasian grassland belt, reaching from the southern Urals to the lower Danube and lying north of the Black Sea and the Caspian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, writing in the 5th century BCE from what travellers told him, called the country flat and grassy and well watered, with rivers running through it almost as many as the canals of Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those rivers run south across the grass, and the greatest of them, the Dnieper, he thought the most productive water anywhere outside the Nile, with the finest pasture and the best fishing on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Along the northern edge the grass thins into forest-steppe, and it was there that Iron Age communities grew millet and lived in large fortified places rather than roaming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lower Volga and Don, at the eastern end, is where the ancestors of all modern domestic horses were bred, from which they spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The steppe was held in turn by a sequence of peoples: the Srubnaya and Alakulskaya cultures from about 1800 BCE, the Cimmerians from about 1000, the Scythians from the 7th century, and the Sarmatians after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last succession ended around 400 CE, under the attack of the Goths and then the invasion of the Huns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sequence is not a family tree, and this is the most useful thing genetics has said about it: the four groups are linked, but none is a direct ancestor of the next, and each was mixed in its own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Movement out of these grasslands mattered as much as movement across them, and the Bronze Age migration of about 3000 BCE carried steppe ancestry deep into Europe, where it is still measurable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rus', the Golden Horde and Muscovy all formed at this end of the belt, so this is the ground on which the rest of the collection opens.","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.47, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.53, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg/1920px-Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg","title":"Feather grass in the Askania-Nova reserve","desc":"One of the last unploughed pieces of the Pontic steppe, in Kherson province in southern Ukraine. Almost all of the grassland described here is now under crops.","credit":"Photograph by NativePlanting, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg","alt":"Dry pale grass, waist high and going to seed, filling the lower half of the frame under an empty blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the northern edge of this grassland behave differently?","a":"Because there the grass thins into forest-steppe, and Iron Age communities grew millet and lived in large fortified places rather than roaming."},{"q":"Why is the sequence of peoples here not a family tree?","a":"Because genetics shows the four groups are linked but none is a direct ancestor of the next, and each was mixed in its own way."},{"q":"Why does movement out of this ground matter as much as movement across it?","a":"Because the Bronze Age migration of about 3000 BCE carried steppe ancestry deep into Europe, where it is still measurable."}],"wiki":"Pontic–Caspian steppe","places":["reg:1730073981"],"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"4","text":"<p>They have made this discovery in a land that suits their purpose and has rivers that are their allies; for their country is flat and grassy and well-watered, and rivers run through it not very many fewer in number than the canals of Egypt.</p>","cite":"Book 4.47"}},
    "ru-003": {"questions":["Because a people practising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no cities and no forts and carried their dwellings on wagons, Herodotus thought them impossible either to catch or to escape from.","Isotopes and ancient proteins from Iron Age Ukraine have complicated the old picture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there, showing millet farming, large settlements and people who mostly stayed put."],"abstract":"<b>Pastoral nomadism</b> is a way of living off herds of grazing animals by moving with them, so that a household follows the grass and the water instead of waiting on a harvest in one place. Herodotus thought the Scythian version of it unbeatable: a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, living off their cattle and carrying their dwellings on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo describes the same arrangement four centuries later, with woollen tents fixed on the wagons, the herds spread round them, and the people passing their lives inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The horse is what made the steppe version of it work, and horses bred on the lower Volga and Don spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE, replacing almost every other local population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Proteins preserved in the dental calculus of Scythian-era people in Ukraine show that they drank the milk of ruminants and of horses alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The picture of an entire population permanently on the move is, for the western steppe at least, wrong. Isotopes from Iron Age cemeteries in Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in and around large settlements, and eating millet grown close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A handful of individuals in those same cemeteries had come a long way, which suits a society with wide trading connections better than one that moved as a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herders and farmers were not separate worlds either, and Strabo says the steppe peoples let others work the land in return for a fixed and moderate tribute, going to war only when it was withheld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That mixture of herds, some farming, tribute from settled neighbours and the ability to move faster than anyone else is what the Greek cities on the Black Sea met, and what later states on the same frontier met after them.","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.46, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]","Jaruschka Pecnik et al., “Paleo-proteomic Analysis of Iron Age Dental Calculus Provides Direct Evidence of Scythian Reliance on Ruminant Dairy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): e0339464, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0339464. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Mongolia%2C_horses_and_cowboys.jpg/1920px-Mongolia%2C_horses_and_cowboys.jpg","title":"Herders and their animals on the Mongolian steppe","desc":"A herd strung out along the grass at the eastern end of the same grassland belt. The practice is illustrated here because the western steppe it also fed has been under the plough for a century and more.","credit":"Photograph by Jeanne Menj, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongolia,_horses_and_cowboys.jpg","alt":"A long line of grazing animals and a mounted herder strung out across bare rolling grassland under a wide cloudy sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Herodotus think the arrangement unbeatable?","a":"Because a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, living off their cattle and carrying their dwellings on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from."},{"q":"Why is the picture of a whole population on the move wrong for the western steppe?","a":"Because isotopes from Iron Age cemeteries in Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in and around large settlements, and eating millet grown close by."},{"q":"Why were herders and farmers not separate worlds?","a":"Because Strabo says the steppe peoples let others work the land in return for a fixed and moderate tribute, going to war only when it was withheld."}],"wiki":"Nomadic pastoralism","quote":{"book":"strabo-geography","n":"7","text":"<p>The woollen tents of the nomades are fixed upon their chariots, in which they pass their lives. Their herds are scattered round their tents, and they live on the milk, the cheese, and the meat which they supply. They shift their quarters ever in search of pasture, changing the places they have exhausted for others full of grass. In the winter they encamp in the marshes near the Palus Maeotis, and in the summer on the plains.</p>","cite":"Book 7.3.17"}},
    "ru-004": {"questions":["Pressed out of their grasslands by the Scythians, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went south into Asia Minor, and Sardis fell to them under Ardys, all but its citadel.","Because no single set of graves or metalwork can be matched to them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are far easier to find in other people's writing than in the ground."],"abstract":"The <b>Cimmerians</b> are the earliest people of the steppe north of the Black Sea whose name survives, appearing in the written record from about 1000 BCE and gone from it within four centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They left no uniform material culture behind them, so the name is far easier to find in other people's texts than in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Assyrian royal inscriptions know them as a danger from beyond the northern mountains, and Esarhaddon boasts of cutting down Teushpa the Cimmerian, 'a barbarian whose home was afar off', with all his troops in the land of Hubushna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A generation later Gyges of Lydia sent Ashurbanipal two captured Cimmerian chieftains in irons, and when Gyges then backed Egypt against Assyria the Assyrian record says the Cimmerians he had beaten overran his whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus tells it from the other end: pressed by the Scythians, the common people wanted to leave and the princes to fight, so the princes killed one another, were buried by the Dniester, and the rest went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He adds that Cimmerian walls, a Cimmerian ferry, a district called Cimmeria and a strait of the same name were all still to be seen in Scythia in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their move south was no retreat into obscurity, for while Ardys ruled at Sardis they took the city, all but its acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Strabo has them and a branch called the Treres raiding Paphlagonia and Phrygia again and again, with a leader named Lygdamis taking Sardis and dying in Cilicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Genomes from burials of the period on the western steppe show a varied population with connections running east, rather than one incoming people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of the rest is history is hard to weigh, because everything written about them was written by their enemies, or by Greeks a long way off.","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), §530, https://archive.org/details/ancient_records_assyria2. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), §§784–85, https://archive.org/details/ancient_records_assyria2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.11, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.12, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.15, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 1.3.21, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D21. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Cimmerian_Stelae_Odessa.jpg/960px-Cimmerian_Stelae_Odessa.jpg","title":"Cimmerian stelae in the Odesa Archaeological Museum","desc":"Carved stone posts of about 900 to 800 BCE from the north Black Sea steppe, of the kind conventionally assigned to the Cimmerians. The attribution rests on date and region rather than on any inscription.","credit":"Photograph by Venzz, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimmerian_Stelae_Odessa.jpg","alt":"A tall weathered stone slab, roughly rectangular and taller than a person, standing on a plinth in a museum gallery."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this people easier to find in texts than in the ground?","a":"Because they left no uniform material culture behind them, so the name survives in other people's writing rather than in an assemblage."},{"q":"Why is what is told of them hard to weigh?","a":"Because everything written about them was written by their enemies, or by Greeks a long way off."},{"q":"Why does the genetic evidence complicate the story of an incoming people?","a":"Because genomes from burials of the period on the western steppe show a varied population with connections running east rather than one arriving group."}],"wiki":"Cimmerians","quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"4","text":"<p>Having made up their minds, the princes separated into two equal bands and fought with each other until they were all killed by each other's hands; then the Cimmerian people buried them by the Tyras river, where their tombs are still to be seen, and having buried them left the land; and the Scythians came and took possession of the country left empty.</p>","cite":"Book 4.11"}},
    "ru-005": {"questions":["Herodotus reports that the people the Greeks called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named themselves Skoloti, and that their own origin story had gold implements fall burning from the sky.","Ancient genomes show that the eastern and western <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arose separately from one another, which is why their graves look so alike while the people in them do not."],"abstract":"The <b>Scythians</b> are the Iron Age people who held the steppe from the Altai to the Carpathians between about 700 and 300 BCE, and who gave the Greek world its idea of the nomad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a Greek one: Herodotus says they called themselves Skoloti after a king, and that their own account of their origin had a plough, a yoke, a sword and a flask, all of gold, fall burning from the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient genomes have taken the single people apart, showing eastern and western groups of independent origin, each a mixture of Bronze Age steppe herders and an eastern Asian component, with movement between them afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is why the weapons, harness and animal ornament look so alike over such distances while the populations beneath them do not, and why the eastern half of the steppe looks like the source of the western groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not all on the move either, since isotopes from cemeteries in Iron Age Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in large settlements, on millet grown close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their kings were another matter, and were buried out on the grass in a deep pit under a raised mound, with strangled servants, horses, golden cups and first-fruits of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A year later, Herodotus says, fifty of the best young men and fifty horses were killed, set upright on stakes and stood in a ring about the tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also says that a warrior flayed the enemies he killed and covered his quiver with the skin taken from their right hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That was long read as travellers' slander, until protein analysis of 45 leather objects from graves in southern Ukraine turned up two samples of human skin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their hold on the western steppe broke around 300 BCE, under pressure from the Macedonians on one side and the arriving Sarmatians on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.5–6, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71–72, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.64, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Luise Ørsted Brandt, Meaghan Mackie, Marina Daragan, Matthew J. Collins and Margarita Gleba, “Human and Animal Skin Identified by Palaeoproteomics in Scythian Leather Objects from Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 12 (2023): e0294129, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0294129. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG/1280px-Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG","title":"The gold vessel from the Kul-Oba barrow","desc":"Made in a Greek workshop on the Black Sea in the later 4th century BCE and buried in a Scythian tomb near Kerch. Its figures are the closest thing there is to a contemporary portrait of Scythian dress, hair and weapons.","credit":"Photograph by Joanbanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG","alt":"A round gold vessel with a flaring rim, its body worked in relief with bearded men in belted tunics and soft caps, one stringing a bow."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the objects look uniform when the people do not?","a":"Because ancient genomes show eastern and western groups of independent origin beneath one set of weapons, harness and animal ornament that travelled across the whole belt."},{"q":"Why was one of Herodotus's grimmest claims believed only recently?","a":"Because the flaying of enemies was long read as travellers' slander, until protein analysis of 45 leather objects from graves in southern Ukraine turned up two samples of human skin."},{"q":"Why do the royal burials sit oddly with the settlements?","a":"Because isotopes show most people living and dying near one place on millet grown close by, while kings were buried out on the grass under raised mounds with horses and gold."}],"wiki":"Scythians","places":["pol:scythia"],"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"4","text":"<p>The Scythians say that their nation is the youngest in the world, and that it came into being in this way. A man whose name was Targitaüs appeared in this country, which was then desolate. They say that his parents were Zeus and a daughter of the Borysthenes river (I do not believe the story, but it is told).</p>","cite":"Book 4.5"}},
    "ru-006": {"questions":["On the earliest securely dated pieces, from a Tuvan royal mound of the late 9th century BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears on horse gear and on almost nothing else.","Because the finest pieces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were buried with the dead, what survives of it comes almost entirely out of tombs rather than out of settlements."],"abstract":"The Scytho-Siberian <b>animal style</b> is the ornament that ties the steppe's Iron Age material culture together: stylised deer, felines, birds of prey and snakes, sometimes combined into one creature, worked in gold, bronze, wood, bone and felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not confined to treasure, and the same animals turn up on bridle fittings, weapons, clothing appliqués, vessels and rock art from the seventh to the 2nd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The images are not taken to be decoration alone, and are usually read as carrying meaning about the ordering of the world, about rank and about who the wearer was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest securely dated group of them comes from Tunnug 1, a royal mound raised in the Tuvan valley of the Uyuk in the late 9th century BCE, half a continent east of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There the style sits on horse equipment and on almost nothing else, and only four kinds of animal appear at all: sheep and goats, cats, birds of prey and serpents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That narrow beginning is worth knowing, because the tradition a reader meets later is a broad one, spread across nearly everything a wealthy household owned. Its uniformity is the striking part: weapons, harness and ornament in this manner are found over more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) of grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the west the type piece is the golden stag from the Kostromskaya barrow in the Kuban, its legs folded under it and its antlers laid back along its spine, found fixed to the centre of a round iron shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Outside the burial chamber there lay 22 horses arranged in pairs, and the whole timber structure had been daubed with clay and then burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost all of this work survives because somebody buried it, and Herodotus says as much from the other side, reporting that a Scythian king went into the ground with golden cups and that his people used neither silver nor bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Timur Sadykov, Jegor Blochin, Sergey Khavrin and Gino Caspari, “Expanding the Corpus of the Earliest Scythian Animal-Style Artefacts,” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 408 (2025): 1569–85, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2025.10214. [Open access]","Timur Sadykov et al., “A Spectral Cavalcade: Early Iron Age Horse Sacrifice at a Royal Tomb in Southern Siberia,” <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1538–57, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.145. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 224–26, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG/1920px-Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG","title":"The golden stag from Kostromskaya","desc":"A gold plaque of the 7th century BCE from a barrow in the Kuban, found at the centre of an iron shield. The folded legs, the long muzzle and the antlers running back over the body are the standard vocabulary of the style.","credit":"Photograph by Joanbanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG","alt":"A gold plaque of a stag lying with its legs tucked beneath it, muzzle stretched forward and a row of scrolled antler tines sweeping back along its spine."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the earliest form of the style worth knowing?","a":"Because at Tunnug 1 it sits on horse equipment and almost nothing else and uses only four kinds of animal, where the later tradition covers nearly everything a wealthy household owned."},{"q":"Why is this ornament not read as decoration alone?","a":"Because the images are usually taken to carry meaning about the ordering of the world, about rank and about who the wearer was."},{"q":"Why does almost all of it survive?","a":"Because somebody buried it, and Herodotus reports from the other side that a Scythian king went into the ground with golden cups."}],"wiki":"Animal style"},
    "ru-007": {"questions":["Herodotus says the mourners at a Scythian royal funeral competed to pile the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as high as they possibly could over the pit they had dug.","Because the herders who raised them left few settlements and little else standing, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very nearly the whole visible archaeology of the grasslands."],"abstract":"A <b>kurgan</b> is a mound of earth heaped over a grave, the word being simply the Russian for a barrow, and it is the characteristic monument of the whole Eurasian grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burials of this kind run right across it, from the upper waters of the Ob to the Elbe and south as far as Thessaly and Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes how one was built for a Scythian king: a great four-cornered pit was dug, the body laid on a couch inside it, spears planted at either side, and planks and braided osiers laid across them for a roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Into the space left in the tomb went a strangled concubine, cupbearer, cook, groom, squire and messenger, together with horses, golden cups and first-fruits of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A year afterwards, he says, fifty of the king's servants and fifty horses were killed, stuffed, mounted on stakes and stood upright in a ring around the mound before the mourners rode away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That ring of dead riders was for a long time the least believable thing in his account. Then a royal mound at Tunnug in Tuva, raised in the late 9th century BCE, gave up the remains of at least eighteen horses and one person laid around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Something similar had already been found in the Kuban at Kostromskaya, where 22 horses lay in pairs outside a timber chamber that had been daubed with clay and set on fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rite was not a Scythian invention and did not end with them, having been practised on these grasslands from the Bronze Age and still in use among steppe peoples of the 13th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scale is what turns a grave into a monument, and Herodotus puts his finger on the reason when he says the mourners vied with one another to make the barrow as great as they could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 145, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.72, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Timur Sadykov et al., “A Spectral Cavalcade: Early Iron Age Horse Sacrifice at a Royal Tomb in Southern Siberia,” <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1538–57, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.145. [Open access]","Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 224–26, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The Solokha barrow above the Dnieper","desc":"A Scythian royal mound of the 5th century BCE in southern Ukraine, standing well above ploughed fields. A mound of this size is visible from a long way off, which is a good part of what it was for.","credit":"Photograph by Валерий Дед, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A broad grass-covered mound rising out of an otherwise flat ploughed field, with a track curving past its foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the least believable part of Herodotus's account gain support?","a":"Because a royal mound at Tunnug gave up at least eighteen horses and one person laid around it, and at Kostromskaya 22 horses lay in pairs outside a burnt timber chamber."},{"q":"Why does scale turn a grave into a monument?","a":"Because the mourners vied with one another to make the barrow as great as they could, which is Herodotus's own explanation of the size."},{"q":"Why is the rite not a Scythian invention?","a":"Because it had been practised on these grasslands since the Bronze Age and was still in use among steppe peoples of the 13th century CE."}],"wiki":"Kurgan","quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"4","text":"<p>Then, having laid the body on a couch in the tomb, they plant spears on each side of the body and lay wooden planks across them, which they then roof over with braided osiers; in the open space which is left in the tomb they bury one of the king's concubines, his cupbearer, his cook, his groom, his squire, and his messenger, after strangling them, besides horses, and first-fruits of everything else, and golden cups; for the Scythians do not use silver or bronze. Having done this, they all build a great barrow of earth, vying eagerly with one another to make this as great as possible.</p>","cite":"Book 4.71"}},
    "ru-008": {"questions":["Two of the many groups gathered under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani, who grazed the plains between the Don and the Dnieper.","In 175 CE Marcus Aurelius drafted defeated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cavalry into his legions and sent five and a half thousand of them to Britain, a Roman historian records."],"abstract":"The <b>Sarmatians</b> are the Iranian-speaking horse peoples who took the steppe north of the Black Sea over from the Scythians and held it from about the 3rd century BCE until roughly 400 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were never one nation but a set of groups of similar background, and Strabo names the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani grazing the plains between the Don and the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He describes them living in woollen tents fixed on their wagons with the herds spread about, wearing helmets and breastplates of untanned ox-hide, and carrying wicker shields, lances, bows and swords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also records what that equipment was worth against a phalanx: fifty thousand of them, fighting as allies of a Crimean king, were almost all cut down by six thousand men under Diophantus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus, writing of an earlier people he calls the Sauromatae, says their women rode out hunting with the men or without them, went to war, and dressed as the men dressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He adds that no girl among them married until she had killed an enemy, and that some grew old unmarried because they never managed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome came to know them well, and by the 2nd century CE they were the most politically weighty force along the empire's eastern edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far they were a genuinely new population is still open, though the genomes read so far resemble Bronze Age groups of the southern Urals more than the Scythians they displaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Individuals could travel extraordinary distances inside the empire: a man buried at a farmstead in what is now Cambridgeshire between 126 and 228 CE carried Sarmatian-related ancestry and had grown up abroad, which fits the 5,500 Sarmatian cavalry that Marcus Aurelius sent to Britain in 175.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their own dominance ended around 400 CE, broken by the Goths and then by the Huns arriving from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.116, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D116. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.117, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D117. [Open access]","Marina Silva et al., “An Individual with Sarmatian-Related Ancestry in Roman Britain,” <i>Current Biology</i> 34, no. 1 (2024): 204–12.e6, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2023.11.049. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/028_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XXVIII_%28Ausschnitt_01%29.jpg/960px-028_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XXVIII_%28Ausschnitt_01%29.jpg","title":"Armoured cavalry on Trajan's Column","desc":"A detail from the 1896 plates of the column in Rome, showing scale-armoured horsemen breaking before Roman riders. Horsemen of this kind are conventionally identified as Sarmatian Roxolani, whose horses as well as their men were armoured.","credit":"Plate by Conrad Cichorius, 1896, after the column attributed to Apollodorus of Damascus, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:028_Conrad_Cichorius,_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule,_Tafel_XXVIII_%28Ausschnitt_01%29.jpg","alt":"A carved relief of horsemen whose riders and mounts are both covered in overlapping scales, tumbling away to the right as Roman cavalry ride them down."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this name not that of a nation?","a":"Because it covers a set of groups of similar background, Strabo naming the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani between the Don and the Dnieper."},{"q":"Why does one British burial bear on their history?","a":"Because a man buried in Cambridgeshire between 126 and 228 CE carried Sarmatian-related ancestry and had grown up abroad, which fits the 5,500 cavalry Marcus Aurelius sent to Britain in 175."},{"q":"Why is it open whether they were a new population at all?","a":"Because the genomes read so far resemble Bronze Age groups of the southern Urals more than the Scythians they displaced."}],"wiki":"Sarmatians","quote":{"book":"strabo-geography","n":"7","text":"<p>However, the Roxolani fought against the generals of Mithridates Eupator. Their leader was Tasius. They came as allies of Palacus, the son of Scilurus, and were considered good soldiers, but against the serried and wellarmed phalanx every barbarous and light-armed tribe is ineffective. Thus they, although numbering fifty thousand men, could not withstand the six thousand arrayed by Diophantus, the general of Mithridates, but were almost all cut to pieces.</p>","cite":"Book 7.3.17"}},
    "ru-009": {"questions":["Herodotus knew the biggest Greek city of the northern Black Sea as the trading post of the Borysthenites, though its own people called it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and said they were Milesians.","The Scythian king Scyles kept a fine house inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, changing out of his Scythian clothes at the gate, and lost his throne when his people found out."],"abstract":"<b>Olbia</b> stood at the joined estuary of the Bug and the Dnieper in what is now southern Ukraine, and Strabo, writing long after its founding, still describes it as a great trading centre and a foundation of the Milesians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus knew it as the trading post of the Borysthenites, who told him that they were Milesians themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was one of a ring of such places: on the island of Berezan close by lay a fishing village whose pottery belongs to the seventh and 6th centuries BCE and which may be older than the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eastward along the coast came Chersonesus, then Theodosia, Nymphaeum and Panticapaeum, with more settlements across the strait, so that Greek ports ran from the Danube to the Sea of Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ships reached them either by working along the western shore or by crossing open water from Sinope, Amisos and Heracleia to the Crimean coast, which is visible from Asia in clear weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the cities were there for was trade, and the Greeks had been taking grain out of this country, and salted fish out of the Sea of Azov, since early times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That put a few thousand townsmen among peoples far more numerous, and the arrangement lasted because it paid both sides. How awkward it could be shows in the story of Scyles, a Scythian king with a Greek mother who kept a costly house in the city and, on each visit, left his army in the suburb, shut the gates and lived, dressed and worshipped as a Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His own leading men were taken up a tower to watch him celebrating the rites of Dionysus, and the kingship was lost to him over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Greek written in these ports is the reason so much of what is known about the early steppe was recorded at all, and the reason all of it was recorded by outsiders.","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.78, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D78. [Open access]","M. Rostovtzeff, <i>Iranians and Greeks in South Russia</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), 63–64, https://archive.org/details/iraniansgreeksin00rostuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.79, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D79. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/AncientTownOlvia.jpg/500px-AncientTownOlvia.jpg","title":"The site of Olbia above the Bug estuary","desc":"Excavated house walls and streets on the terrace above the water, in Mykolaiv province, Ukraine. The estuary in the background is the reason the city stood here.","credit":"Photograph by Ivengo, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AncientTownOlvia.jpg","alt":"Low stone foundations of streets and small rooms laid bare on a grassy headland, with a wide estuary and a far shore behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a few thousand Greeks manage to live among far more numerous peoples?","a":"Because the arrangement paid both sides: the cities existed for trade, taking grain out of the country and salted fish out of the Sea of Azov, and it lasted as long as that exchange held."},{"q":"Why does the story of Scyles show how awkward the arrangement was?","a":"Because a Scythian king had to leave his army in the suburb and shut the gates to live as a Greek inside the city, and when his leading men were taken up a tower to watch him at the rites of Dionysus he lost the kingship over it."},{"q":"Why is what we know of the early steppe knowledge of a particular kind?","a":"Because the Greek written in these ports is the reason any of it was recorded at all, which means all of it was recorded by outsiders looking in."}],"wiki":"Olbia","places":["pl:q1143233"]},
    "ru-010": {"questions":["Demosthenes told an Athenian jury that the corn reaching the city from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to about four hundred thousand measures a year, free of the usual export toll.","After a dynasty of 42 years, the throne of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed in 438 BCE to Spartocus, whose successors bore Thracian and Greek names alike."],"abstract":"The <b>Bosporan Kingdom</b> was the state that grew up on both shores of the Cimmerian Bosporus, the strait now called Kerch, with its capital at Panticapaeum, a Milesian foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus records that a line called the Archaeanactidae ruled there for 42 years before the kingship passed to Spartocus, who reigned seven, which puts the beginning of the kingdom at about 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The year was 438 BCE, and the Spartocids who followed bore Thracian and Greek names side by side, Pairisades and Spartocos beside Leucon and Satyros, styling themselves archons rather than kings over the Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, who calls them tyrants, allows that most of them governed moderately and justly from the time of Pairisades and Leucon onward, and says one of the Pairisades was reckoned a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the kingdom matter far beyond its own coast, though, was neither its rulers nor its harbours but the corn grown on the plains behind them and shipped out through Theodosia and Panticapaeum. <br><br> Demosthenes told an Athenian jury that the grain reaching the city from the Black Sea equalled everything imported from every other source put together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gave the yearly figure as about four hundred thousand measures out of the Bosporus, checkable against the books of the grain commissioners, and said Leucon had waived his thirtieth on cargoes bound for Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strabo reports a still larger consignment, two million one hundred thousand measures sent to the Athenians out of Theodosia alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The land could bear it, since he says these plains yielded thirtyfold under the most ordinary ploughing, and that the tribute later paid to Mithridates ran to 180,000 measures of corn and 200 talents of silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The last of the Pairisades, unable to meet the heavy tribute the steppe peoples were demanding of him, handed the kingdom to Mithridates about 107 BCE, and after him it passed under Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.4, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D4. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.31.1, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D12%3Achapter%3D31%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","M. Rostovtzeff, <i>Iranians and Greeks in South Russia</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), 67–68, https://archive.org/details/iraniansgreeksin00rostuoft. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 31, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0072%3Aspeech%3D20%3Asection%3D31. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 32, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0072%3Aspeech%3D20%3Asection%3D32. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]","Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 571, 582–83, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Panticapaeum_01.jpg/1920px-Panticapaeum_01.jpg","title":"The ruins of Panticapaeum above Kerch","desc":"Foundations on the slope of Mount Mithridat, the acropolis hill of the Bosporan capital, with the modern city of Kerch below. The strait the kingdom straddled lies just beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Dmytro Petishkin, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panticapaeum_01.jpg","alt":"Grassy terraces scattered with cut stone blocks and wall footings on a hillside, with roofs and trees of a modern town spreading across the valley below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this kingdom matter far beyond its own coast?","a":"Because of neither its rulers nor its harbours but the corn grown on the plains behind them, shipped out through Theodosia and Panticapaeum in quantities Athens depended on."},{"q":"Why could Demosthenes expect a jury to accept his grain figure?","a":"Because he gave about four hundred thousand measures a year out of the Bosporus as a figure checkable against the books of the grain commissioners, and added that Leucon had waived his thirtieth on cargoes bound for Athens."},{"q":"Why is such a yield credible from these plains at all?","a":"Because Strabo says they returned thirtyfold under the most ordinary ploughing, which is also why the later tribute to Mithridates could be set at 180,000 measures of corn and 200 talents of silver."}],"wiki":"Bosporan Kingdom","places":["pol:bosporus"],"quote":{"book":"strabo-geography","n":"7","text":"<p>Both this place and the neighbouring settlements on each side of the mouth of the Palus Maeotis were for a long period under the monarchical dynasty of Leucon, and Satyrus, and Pairisades, till the latter surrendered the sovereignty to Mithridates. They had the name of tyrants, although most of them were moderate and just in their government, from the time of Pairisades and Leucon. Pairisades was accounted even a god.</p>","cite":"Book 7.4.4"}},
    "ru-011": {"questions":["A Roman officer records that the Huns overran the Alans of the Don and then burst into the rich cantons of a dreaded king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who chose death instead.","An imperial ambassador at Constantinople in the 1560s took down some eighty words from two Crimean men whose speech still carried the tongue of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who had settled there."],"abstract":"The <b>Goths</b> are the Germanic-speaking people who dominated the steppe north-west of the Black Sea from the middle of the 3rd century CE until the Huns broke their power in the 370s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their own tradition, set down two centuries later by a Gothic historian, says part of the people crossed a river under a king called Filimer into a country their language named Oium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There they met a race he calls the Spali, joined battle and won, then hurried on to the farthest part of Scythia, near the sea of Pontus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He concedes the story is told that way in their early songs rather than in any document, which is about as much weight as it will carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation shows what the songs do not: the harbour town of Tyras on the Dniester was alive again after that century's raids, still taking imports from Asia Minor, the Bosporus, Greece and the Danube lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The same historian gives them a great king, Hermanaric, said to have subdued many warlike northern peoples and made them obey his laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Roman officer writing within living memory tells a grimmer story, in which the Huns overran the Alans of the Don, bound the survivors by treaty and with them burst into the rich cantons of a monarch he calls Ermenrichus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That king held out a while and then, as rumour magnified the coming horror, put an end to his fear by a voluntary death; his successor hired Huns of his own, lost battle after battle and fell in one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Gothic tradition instead has Hermanaric stabbed by two brothers avenging a sister he had ordered torn apart between horses, dying at a hundred and ten as Hunnic raids began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those who did not cross the Danube in 376 stayed in the Crimean mountains, where an imperial ambassador at Constantinople in the 1560s could still take down some eighty words of their language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 27–28, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 57–58, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 116, 129–30, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 82, 86–87, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Oleh Saveliev, “Tyras in Late Antiquity,” <i>Archaeologia Polona</i> 62 (2024): 159–83, https://doi.org/10.23858/APa62.2024.3553. [Open access]","Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Roman History</i> 31.3.1–3, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/31*.html. [Open access]","Ogier Ghiselin de Busbecq, <i>The Life and Letters of Ogier Ghiselin de Busbecq</i>, vol. 1, trans. Charles Thornton Forster and F. H. Blackburne Daniell (London: C. Kegan Paul, 1881), 355–56, https://archive.org/details/lifelettbusbecq01forsuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Chernyakhov_culture._Coral%2C_vessel%2C_glass%2C_Roman_amphora._Ukrainian_Academy_of_Sciences_Archaeological_Museum.jpeg/1920px-Chernyakhov_culture._Coral%2C_vessel%2C_glass%2C_Roman_amphora._Ukrainian_Academy_of_Sciences_Archaeological_Museum.jpeg","title":"Chernyakhov-culture goods from Ukraine","desc":"Coral beads, buckets, vases, a glass goblet and a Roman vinegar amphora of about 300 to 350 CE, from sites between the Dniester and the Dnieper. The assemblage belongs to the material culture of the region the Goths dominated, which mixed local, steppe and Roman goods.","credit":"Photograph by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chernyakhov_culture._Coral%2C_vessel%2C_glass%2C_Roman_amphora._Ukrainian_Academy_of_Sciences_Archaeological_Museum.jpeg","alt":"A museum case holding rows of dark clay jugs and beakers, a string of pale beads and a tall two-handled amphora."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Oium story treated carefully rather than as a record?","a":"Because the historian who tells it wrote two centuries afterwards and says himself that it was told that way in the Goths' own early songs rather than in any document."},{"q":"Why do two sources disagree about how Hermanaric died?","a":"Because the Roman officer has him take his own life as the Hunnic storm broke, while the Gothic tradition has him stabbed by two brothers avenging their sister and dying at a hundred and ten."},{"q":"Why does a sixteenth-century word list matter to this people's history?","a":"Because it shows that Goths who did not cross the Danube in 376 were still in the Crimean mountains speaking a Germanic tongue nearly twelve hundred years later."}],"wiki":"Goths","places":["pol:gothia"]},
    "ru-012": {"questions":["Ancient DNA shows that the European realm of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was genetically highly heterogeneous, with only a small minority carrying the eastern ancestry that links it to the Xiongnu.","Gothic tradition made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the offspring of witches driven into the Maeotic marshes, which is one measure of the terror the newcomers caused on the steppe."],"abstract":"The <b>Huns</b> are the nomadic people who came out of the east onto the steppe north of the Black Sea in the 370s and destroyed the order the Alans and the Goths had kept there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Roman_Empire\">Roman</span> officer writing within living memory says they were little known from ancient records, dwelt beyond the Maeotic Sea near the ice-bound ocean, and exceeded every degree of savagery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reports that they overran the lands of the Alans of the Tanaïs, killed and plundered many of them, joined the survivors to themselves in a treaty of alliance, and with them struck at the Gothic king Ermenrichus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gothic tradition makes them the offspring of witch-women driven into the wilderness and of unclean spirits, and has a doe lead their hunters across the Maeotic swamp into a Scythia they had not known was there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither account was written by anyone who had lived among them, and both were written by peoples the Huns had lately beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient DNA has settled part of the argument and complicated the rest, in a study of 370 individuals buried between the 2nd century BCE and the 6th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some late Xiongnu from Mongolia, two of them in imperial elite burials, turn out to be the direct ancestors of Hun-period people in Europe or to be separated from them by a few generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> But only a small minority carried that eastern ancestry, no sign was found of a larger community of eastern or steppe descent, and the population of the Hun realm in Europe was, in the study’s own words, genetically highly heterogeneous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotope work on Hun-period cemeteries in Pannonia points the same way, finding people who moved between herding and farming rather than a herding population replacing a settled one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A separate study of Hun, Avar and Hungarian-conquest graves traced the immigrant core of both the Huns and the Avars to what is now Mongolia, and behind that to the Xiongnu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Roman History</i> 31.2.1–2, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/31*.html. [Open access]","Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Roman History</i> 31.3.1–3, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/31*.html. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 121–24, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Guido Alberto Gnecchi-Ruscone et al., “Ancient Genomes Reveal Trans-Eurasian Connections between the European Huns and the Xiongnu Empire,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 9 (2025): e2418485122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11892651/. [Open access]","Susanne E. Hakenbeck et al., “Practising Pastoralism in an Agricultural Environment: An Isotopic Analysis of the Impact of the Hunnic Incursions on Pannonian Populations,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): e0173079, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0173079. [Open access]","Zoltán Maróti et al., “The Genetic Origin of Huns, Avars, and Conquering Hungarians,” <i>Current Biology</i> 32, no. 13 (2022): 2858–70, https://real.mtak.hu/159253/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Hun_period_hungary_5th_cAD_distorted_skull_IMG_1164.JPG","title":"A reshaped skull of the Hun period","desc":"A skull from a fifth-century grave in Hungary, drawn up into a long dome by binding in infancy. Graves of the Hun period across the steppe and central Europe hold many such skulls, from several peoples rather than one.","credit":"Photograph by Bjoertvedt, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hun_period_hungary_5th_cAD_distorted_skull_IMG_1164.JPG","alt":"A brown human skull in a museum case, its braincase drawn up and back into a long tapering dome instead of a rounded one."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the written accounts of this people treated with caution?","a":"Because neither was written by anyone who had lived among them: one is by a Roman officer setting out to show them as barely human, the other by a Goth whose people they had lately beaten."},{"q":"Why does the genetic evidence argue against a mass migration?","a":"Because among 370 individuals only a small minority carried eastern ancestry, and the study found no sign of a larger community of eastern or steppe descent in the region."},{"q":"Why is the link to the Xiongnu still a real finding?","a":"Because some late Xiongnu from Mongolia, two of them in imperial elite burials, are either the direct ancestors of Hun-period people in Europe or separated from them by only a few generations."}],"wiki":"Huns","places":["pol:huns"]},
    "ru-013": {"questions":["Driven west when the first Turkic khaganate rose in Mongolia in the 550s, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the steppe and held the middle Danube for better than two centuries.","Genomes from the richest early graves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry north-east Asian ancestry matching Mongolian steppe populations, a Rouran-period genome fitting many of them as a single source."],"abstract":"The <b>Avars</b> are the steppe people who crossed the Pontic-Caspian grasslands in the 560s and built a khaganate on the middle Danube that stood for better than two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their move west was set off by the rise of the first Turkic khaganate in the 550s, centred in what is now Mongolia, which destroyed the empire its Chinese neighbours called the Rouran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from the richest early Avar graves carry north-east Asian ancestry matching preceding Mongolian steppe populations, and a Rouran-period genome fits many of them as a single source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second study, of Hun, Avar and Hungarian-conquest burials together, traced the immigrant core of the Avars to present-day Mongolia as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They settled the Carpathian Basin in 567 or 568, and a Lombard historian says his own people handed Pannonia to them on leaving for Italy after Easter in 568, on condition that they could take their fields back if they ever had to return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the Rus' chronicle remembers of them is not a state but a cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Obri, as it calls them, marched against the emperor Heraclius and nearly took him prisoner, then made war on the Slavs and beat the Dulebi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Obre who wanted to travel, it says, had three, four or five Dulebi women harnessed to his wagon in place of a horse or an ox.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It adds that they were tall of body and proud of spirit, that God destroyed them so that not one survived, and that a proverb was current in Rus' to this day: they perished like the Obri, for they left neither descendants nor heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archaeology does not bear out the rule over eastern Europe that memory implies, since early Avar belt fittings are absent north, east and south of the Carpathians beyond a couple of pieces, and the elites who copied Avar dress there did so late and were copying the Khazars instead by about 850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i> 8, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 8–9, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Guido Alberto Gnecchi-Ruscone et al., “Ancient Genomes Reveal Origin and Rapid Trans-Eurasian Migration of 7th Century Avar Elites,” <i>Cell</i> 185, no. 8 (2022): 1402–13, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9042794/. [Open access]","Paul the Deacon, <i>History of the Langobards</i> 2.7, trans. William Dudley Foulke (Philadelphia: Department of History, University of Pennsylvania, 1907), 62, with the editor’s note at 33 quoting the Annals of Einhard for 796, https://archive.org/details/historyoflangoba00pauluoft. [Open access]","Florin Curta, “Have Avars Gone? A View from Eastern Europe,” <i>Plural: History, Culture, Society</i> 11, no. 2 (2023): 23–40, https://doi.org/10.37710/plural.v11i2_2. [Open access]","Zoltán Maróti et al., “The Genetic Origin of Huns, Avars, and Conquering Hungarians,” <i>Current Biology</i> 32, no. 13 (2022): 2858–70, https://real.mtak.hu/159253/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Grave_goods_from_the_Avar_cemetery_of_Gyenesdi%C3%A1s%2C_Hungary_-_saber_and_sheath.jpg/1920px-Grave_goods_from_the_Avar_cemetery_of_Gyenesdi%C3%A1s%2C_Hungary_-_saber_and_sheath.jpg","title":"Sabre and sheath from an Avar grave","desc":"Weapons from grave 64 of the Avar cemetery at Gyenesdiás in western Hungary, dated to the middle third of the 7th century. The man was buried in a wooden coffin with a reflex bow and this sabre, the characteristic arms of a middle Avar warrior.","credit":"Photograph by James Steakley, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_goods_from_the_Avar_cemetery_of_Gyenesdi%C3%A1s%2C_Hungary_-_saber_and_sheath.jpg","alt":"A long slightly curved iron blade laid beside the decayed wooden sheath it was buried in, with small metal mounts along its length."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this people leave the eastern steppe at all?","a":"Because the first Turkic khaganate rose in what is now Mongolia in the 550s and destroyed the Rouran empire, and the Byzantine sources agree that the westward move followed from it."},{"q":"Why is the chronicle's memory of them a poor guide to their rule?","a":"Because early Avar belt fittings are absent from the lands north, east and south of the Carpathians beyond a couple of pieces, so the archaeology shows no such dominion east of the mountains."},{"q":"Why does a Lombard historian know how they came by Pannonia?","a":"Because his own people handed it over on leaving for Italy after Easter in 568, keeping the right to take their fields back if they ever had to return."}],"places":["pol:avars"]},
    "ru-014": {"questions":["The short chronicle of the patriarch Nikephoros gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a chief called Kuvrat, nephew of Organa, who expelled everyone of Avar stock from his own country.","Tribes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the coastlands from the Dniester almost to the Don, and that long steppe frontage was not cut in two until the 9th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Bulgars</b> are the Turkic-speaking steppe people whose splitting in the 7th century produced a state on the lower Danube, another on the middle Volga and a third group left under Khazar rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They belong to the Ogur group of the western steppe, whom the Khazars, Suvars and Barsils are thought to have driven out of the lands north of the Caucasus about the middle of the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The short chronicle of the patriarch Nikephoros gives the next century a ruler called Kuvrat, nephew of Organa and chief of the Onogundurs, who expelled everyone of Avar stock from his country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle remembers only the western branch, and from the Slavs' side: out of Scythia, which it explains as Khazaria, came the people called Bulgars, who settled on the Danube and oppressed the Slavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That western branch held a long frontage on the steppe as well as a kingdom in the Balkans, and in the 7th and 8th centuries its lands took in what later became Wallachia and Bessarabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Bulgar tribes occupied the coastlands from the Dniester almost to the Don, and that continuity was not cut in two until the 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The easterly part were known as the Inner Bulgars and probably reckoned to the Khazar empire, though nobody can now say whether Khazar authority stopped at the Dniester or the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A third group had gone north instead, to the middle Volga, where a 10th-century Arab geographer found a king who had lately become a Muslim and whose son had made the pilgrimage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their caravans ran to Khwarezm and back through wandering Turkish hordes who made the road unsafe, so the northern branch was tied to the Muslim world rather than to Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The split is why the word Bulgar reaches the modern map twice, in the Balkans and on the middle Volga, and why a chronicler in Kyiv treated the two as separate neighbours rather than one people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i> 8, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 8, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Florin Curta, “Have Avars Gone? A View from Eastern Europe,” <i>Plural: History, Culture, Society</i> 11, no. 2 (2023): 23–40, https://doi.org/10.37710/plural.v11i2_2. [Open access]","Osman Karatay, “The Appearance of the Khazars in the Caucasus,” <i>Gazi Akademik Bakış</i> 18, no. 36 (2025): 1–22, https://doi.org/10.19060/gav.1717306. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>A History of the Eastern Roman Empire from the Fall of Irene to the Accession of Basil I</i> (London: Macmillan, 1912), 337–38, https://archive.org/details/historyofeastern00buryuoft. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 413, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/The_rings_of_Kubrat.png","title":"Rings naming Kubrat the patrician","desc":"Three gold rings from a rich burial at Malaya Pereshchepina on the Vorskla, in the Poltava region of Ukraine. Each is engraved with a Greek monogram read as “of Kubrat the patrician”, the Byzantine title granted to a Bulgar ruler of the 7th century.","credit":"Photograph by ChernorizetsHrabar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_rings_of_Kubrat.png","alt":"Three plain gold finger rings photographed against a dark ground, each with a flat oval bezel cut with interlaced Greek letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why does one steppe people end up with three histories?","a":"Because the group split in the 7th century: one part founded a state on the lower Danube, one went north to the middle Volga, and the easterly part stayed and was reckoned to the Khazar empire."},{"q":"Why can the eastern edge of Khazar authority not be fixed?","a":"Because Bulgar tribes held the coastlands from the Dniester almost to the Don without a recorded frontier, so whether Khazar power stopped at the Dniester or the Dnieper cannot now be decided."},{"q":"Why did the northern branch face the Muslim world rather than Byzantium?","a":"Because its trade ran by caravan to Khwarezm and back, and by the 10th century its king had become a Muslim and his son had made the pilgrimage."}],"wiki":"Bulgars","places":["pol:old_great_bulgaria"]},
    "ru-015": {"questions":["Vladimir went by boat against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Torks riding overland, and was then told its prisoners all wore boots and would never pay anybody tribute.","Burial grounds excavated round the middle Volga put the Muslimisation of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 9th century, with white-stone mosque ruins at Bilyar tying the faith to town growth."],"abstract":"<b>Volga Bulgaria</b> is the state the northern branch of the Bulgars built on the middle Volga and its tributary the Kama, in the country round what is now Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Khazar dependency called the Burdas lay across the central Volga and served the khaganate as a barrier against these outer Bulgars to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was busy river traffic between the two all the same, and the Burtas country sent out the black and red fox furs that Arab kings wore, a black one costing a hundred dinars or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 10th-century Arab geographer says the Bulgar king had become a Muslim after the year 310 of the Islamic era, in consequence of a vision, and that his son had made the pilgrimage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That puts the conversion at about 922 in the Christian era, and he adds that caravans ran from the Bulgar country to Khwarezm and back through wandering Turkish hordes who made the road unsafe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Excavation of the town and village burial grounds puts the Muslimisation of the population from the 9th century, general well before the Mongol invasion, and the white-stone mosque ruins at Bilyar tie it to the growth of towns rather than to the court alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From Kyiv the state looked like a neighbour to be raided and then bargained with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the chronicle's year 6493 Vladimir went against the Bulgars by boat with his uncle Dobrynya and Torks riding overland, and beat them; Dobrynya then told him the prisoners all wore boots and would never pay tribute, so they should look for enemies shod in bast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peace was sworn by both sides, and the Bulgars said it would hold until the stones swam and the hops sank to the bottom of the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The year after, Bulgars of the Muslim faith came to Vladimir and pressed their religion on him, and in 1236 the Tartars took the whole Bolgar land and its great city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 413, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Мелтем Акынджы and Фаяз Шарипович Хузин, “Ранний ислам в Волжской Булгарии в исследованиях Е.А. Халиковой и А.Х. Халикова,” <i>Археология Евразийских степей</i>, no. 5 (2022): 8–18, https://doi.org/10.24852/2587-6112.2022.5.8.18. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>A History of the Eastern Roman Empire from the Fall of Irene to the Accession of Basil I</i> (London: Macmillan, 1912), 410–11, https://archive.org/details/historyofeastern00buryuoft. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 412, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 81, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bolgar_ruin.jpg/1920px-Bolgar_ruin.jpg","title":"Ruins of the Cathedral Mosque at Bolgar","desc":"What stands of the congregational mosque in the old town of Bolgar on the left bank of the Volga, in the Spassky district of Tatarstan. The site was the chief town of the Bulgars on the middle Volga.","credit":"Photograph by Aleksandra Glukhikh, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolgar_ruin.jpg","alt":"A low ring of pale stone walls on mown grass, with a round stone tower at one corner and a flat green plain behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this state face the Muslim world rather than Byzantium?","a":"Because its trade ran by caravan to Khwarezm and back rather than down to the Black Sea, and its king had adopted Islam by about 922, a generation before Rus' turned to Constantinople."},{"q":"Why is the ninth century the archaeologists' date and not the tenth?","a":"Because excavation of town and village burial grounds shows Muslim burial practice beginning among the population in the 9th century, well before the king's own conversion is recorded."},{"q":"Why did Vladimir make peace instead of imposing tribute?","a":"Because his uncle Dobrynya pointed out that the prisoners all wore boots, meaning they were too prosperous to pay tribute, and advised looking for enemies shod in bast instead."}],"wiki":"Volga Bulgaria","places":["pol:volga_bulgaria"]},
    "ru-016": {"questions":["Nine supreme judges sat in the country of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, two for the Muslims, two for the Christians, two ruling by the Pentateuch and one for the pagans.","In the chronicle's year 6473, conventionally 965, Sviatoslav marched against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, beat them and their khagan, and took their town of Belaya Vezha."],"abstract":"The <b>Khazars</b> are the Turkic people whose khaganate held the lower Volga, the north Caucasus and the steppe north of the Black Sea from the 7th century to the 10th, and it is the power the Slavs round Kyiv first paid tribute to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their arrival in the Caucasus can be put at the beginning of the 6th century or a little earlier, in company with the Suvars and the Barsils, after the three were driven west out of the South Siberian belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 10th-century Arab geographer says their metropolis was Semender until an Arab commander took it, after which the king moved his residence seven days away to Itil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He describes a court that let every faith judge its own: nine supreme judges, two for the Muslims, two for the Khazars following the Pentateuch, two for the Christians, and one for Slavs, Rus’ and other pagans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standing army was seven thousand armoured horsemen from Khwarezm, who served on condition that they could profess Islam openly, that the vizier be of their own nation, and that they need never fight fellow Muslims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Revenue came largely from what passed through, and excavation in the forest-steppe of the western khaganate has recovered grain, field tools and settlement traces, so the population farmed as well as herded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rus' owed the khaganate more than tribute, and one recent account has its khagan acknowledged as ruler of the Rus' and reads the first Rus' attack on Constantinople in 860 as Khazar-instigated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle tells the relationship as an omen instead, in which the Polyanians of the wooded hills above the Dnieper paid a sword for each hearth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Khazar elders read the double edge against their own single-edged sabres and told their prince that one day they and other lands would pay tribute to this people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicler says it came true: in his year 6473, conventionally 965, Sviatoslav marched on the Khazars, beat them and their khagan and took their town of Belaya Vezha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 406–9, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 12–13, 51, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 381–418, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Osman Karatay, “The Appearance of the Khazars in the Caucasus,” <i>Gazi Akademik Bakış</i> 18, no. 36 (2025): 1–22, https://doi.org/10.19060/gav.1717306. [Open access]","V. V. Koloda and S. A. Gorbanenko, <i>Agriculture of the Khazar Khaganate in a Forest-Steppe Zone</i> (Kyiv: Akademperiodyka, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15407/akademperiodyka.347.170. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>A History of the Eastern Roman Empire from the Fall of Irene to the Accession of Basil I</i> (London: Macmillan, 1912), 410–11, https://archive.org/details/historyofeastern00buryuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D0%98%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D1%83.jpg/1920px-%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D0%98%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D1%83.jpg","title":"The Vyatichi bring tribute to Sviatoslav","desc":"A miniature of the late 15th century from the Radziwiłł chronicle, showing the Vyatichi paying tribute to Sviatoslav after the campaigns that ended the khaganate. The chronicle says they had until then given the Khazars a silver coin for each plough.","credit":"Unknown illuminator, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D0%98%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D1%83.jpg","alt":"A pen and wash drawing in two halves: robed men carrying bundles towards a seated prince on the left, and riders with spears on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the khaganate keep judges of four different faiths?","a":"Because its revenue came from the trade passing through, and the merchants, the paid army and the subject peoples were Muslims, Jews, Christians and pagans, each judged by their own law."},{"q":"Why is the sword tribute told as an omen rather than as a tax?","a":"Because the chronicler wanted the story to prefigure what followed: Khazar elders reading the Slavs' double-edged blades against their own sabres and predicting that the tribute would one day run the other way."},{"q":"Why does farming evidence matter for a steppe khaganate?","a":"Because grain, field tools and settlement traces from the forest-steppe of the western khaganate show a population that cultivated as well as herded, rather than nomads living only off their animals."}],"wiki":"Khazars","places":["pol:khazaria"]},
    "ru-017": {"questions":["Letters between a minister of the caliph of Cordova and a king on the Volga, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the chief evidence that a steppe ruling house adopted Judaism.","A second and stranger version of the conversion turned up in the Cairo Genizah, differing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by crediting a general's wife rather than an angel."],"abstract":"The <b>Khazar Correspondence</b> is the exchange of letters between Hasdai ibn Shaprut, a minister of the caliph of Cordova, and Joseph, king of the Khazars, and it is the chief evidence that the khaganate adopted Judaism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some authorities place that conversion about the middle of the 8th century and others in the first half of the 7th, and it left very few traces in medieval Jewish writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without the 12th-century Spanish dialogue known as the <i>Kuzari</i>, the very name Khazar would have dropped out of Jewish annals altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book opens on the story as remembered: an angel told a Khazar king in a dream that his thinking pleased God but his acting did not, and he questioned a philosopher, a Christian and a Muslim before converting with many of his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was only in the 16th century, when a collector travelling between Constantinople and Egypt recovered the letters, that the kingdom passed from the region of fable to that of fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A different account came to light in the Cairo Genizah, where the decisive push is not an angel but a Jewish woman named Serah, who with her father turned the heart of her husband, one of the Khazars’ best generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A disputation with Christians and Muslims followed, and the general became the first real Jewish king of Khazaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Arab geographer of the 940s puts the conversion in the reign of the caliph al-Rashid, and says Jews flocked to the Khazar king from every Muslim district and from Byzantium, whose emperor was forcing his own Jews to turn Christian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle records the far end of that story, when Khazar Jews came to Vladimir at Kyiv in its year 6494 and told him their own country was Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conversion has since been made to carry arguments it cannot bear, among them a claim advanced in early 20th-century Hungary that Hungarians and Jews shared a Khazar descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. Schechter, “An Unknown Khazar Document,” <i>The Jewish Quarterly Review</i>, n.s., 3, no. 2 (1912): 181–219, https://archive.org/details/jewishquarterlyr03drop. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 407, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]","Judah Halevi, <i>Kitab al Khazari</i> 1.1, trans. Hartwig Hirschfeld (London: George Routledge and Sons, 1905), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/judahhalleviskit00judauoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 70, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Mari Réthelyi, “Hungarian Jewish Stories of Origin: Samuel Kohn, the Khazar Connection and the Conquest of Hungary,” <i>Hungarian Cultural Studies</i> 14 (2021): article 427, https://doi.org/10.5195/ahea.2021.427. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Kiev_Letter_T-S_12.122.jpg","title":"The Kyiv letter from the Cairo Genizah","desc":"A 10th-century Hebrew letter of recommendation written by the Jewish community of Kyiv, now in Cambridge University Library. It comes from the same Cairo Genizah that produced the second account of the Khazar conversion.","credit":"Manuscript T-S 12.122, Cambridge University Library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kiev_Letter_T-S_12.122.jpg","alt":"A worn sheet of parchment covered in closely written square Hebrew letters, darkened and frayed along its lower edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why would the name Khazar have vanished from Jewish writing?","a":"Because the conversion left very few traces in medieval Jewish literature, the references being rare, casual and short, so only the 12th-century dialogue called the Kuzari kept the name in view."},{"q":"Why do the two surviving accounts of the conversion disagree?","a":"Because one credits an angel appearing to the king in a dream, while the Genizah document credits the zeal of a Jewish woman named Serah, who with her father turned her husband, a Khazar general."},{"q":"Why is this correspondence still handled with care by historians?","a":"Because the conversion it records has been pressed into modern arguments about descent, including the early 20th-century Hungarian claim that Hungarians and Jews shared a Khazar ancestry."}],"wiki":"Khazar Correspondence"},
    "ru-018": {"questions":["A palace joined to the town by a bridge of boats stood on an island in the river at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Muslims, Christians, Jews and pagans all lived.","A settlement of three parts in the Volga delta, dug for twenty years, matches the descriptions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though its excavator says the identification needs further study."],"abstract":"<b>Itil</b> is the name the Arab geographers give to the last capital of the Khazar khaganate, on the lower Volga, and nobody has yet shown where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A 10th-century geographer says the khaganate's metropolis had been Semender until an Arab commander took it, after which the king moved his residence seven days away to Itil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He describes a town in three parts, split by a great river rising in the country of the Turks, with an island in midstream where the king lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palace stood at the end of that island and was joined to one side of the town by a bridge of boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Muslims, Christians, Jews and pagans all lived there, and the Rus' and Slavs had one of the two banks, where they burned their dead with their cattle, arms and ornaments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The leading candidate for the site is the Samosdelka settlement in the Volga delta, dug for twenty years and known from the medieval sources as the trading town of Saksin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its lower layers hold a settlement of three parts, two banks and an island, with brick walls and two gate towers, which does coincide with the descriptions of Itil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The excavator is careful all the same: the burnt layer once taken for the Khazar destruction is now dated to the 11th or early 12th century, the fortifications may be no older than about 900, and he writes that the question needs further study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His explanation for a city being there is trade rather than ceremony, goods having to be shifted from sea-going to river craft, and he puts the first settlement higher up the delta near Semibugry, moved west about the 10th century as the Caspian rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whatever stood there did not outlast the khaganate: the Rus' chronicle has Sviatoslav take the Khazar town of Belaya Vezha in its year 6473, and one recent account dates the collapse of Khazar power to that revolt of 965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 406–8, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]","Дмитрий Викторович Васильев, “Результаты 20 лет исследований на Самосдельском городище и новые перспективы,” <i>Археология Евразийских степей</i>, no. 5 (2022): 42–50, https://doi.org/10.24852/2587-6112.2022.5.42.50. [Open access]","Дмитрий Викторович Васильев, “О причинах возникновения хазарского города в дельте Волги,” <i>Археология Евразийских степей</i>, no. 2 (2024): 64–69, https://doi.org/10.24852/2587-6112.2024.2.64.69. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 51, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 381–418, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5.jpg/1920px-%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5.jpg","title":"The Samosdelka settlement site","desc":"The mound of the Samosdelka settlement, two kilometres north-east of the village of that name in the Kamyzyak district of Astrakhan province. It is the site most often proposed for the Khazar capital, and its lower layers hold a settlement of two banks and an island.","credit":"Photograph by Ted.ns, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5.jpg","alt":"A low grassy rise above flat reed-covered delta country under a wide pale sky, with scattered bushes and no buildings."},"why":[{"q":"Why has the site of this capital never been settled?","a":"Because the one candidate whose lower layers match the three-part description has a burnt layer dated to the 11th or early 12th century and fortifications perhaps no older than about 900, so its excavator leaves the question open."},{"q":"Why would a khaganate put its capital in a river delta?","a":"Because goods on the Caspian and Volga route had to be moved from sea-going ships to river craft, so the trade itself required a town where the river met the sea."},{"q":"Why did so many faiths live in one Khazar town?","a":"Because the khaganate lived on the trade passing through it, so Muslim soldiers and merchants, Christians, Jews and pagan Rus' and Slavs all had quarters and their own judges there."}],"places":["pl:q4406882"]},
    "ru-019": {"questions":["A Khazar ruler proposed a single king over the seven tribes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Árpád was chosen and raised on a shield after the Khazar custom.","The Rus' chronicle says the emperor Leo hired the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the Bulgars, whose country was ravaged until Symeon could barely escape to Dorostolon."],"abstract":"The <b>Magyars</b> are the people who held the steppe north of the Black Sea in the 9th century as clients of the Khazars before crossing the Carpathians about 895 to found Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle knows two sets of them: white Ugrians who appeared under the emperor Heraclius and took Slav land after driving out the Vlakhs, and black Ugrians who passed beyond Kyiv later, in Oleg's time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before their seven tribes had any single chief, the Khazar ruler proposed making the foremost of them, a man the Greek source calls Lebedias, king over the whole people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lebedias refused for himself and named another chief or that chief’s son Árpád, and the Magyars chose Árpád, who was raised on a shield after the Khazar custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About the same time a Khazar tribe called the Kabars rose against their khagan, and those who survived the revolt fled across the Dnieper and were taken in as an eighth Magyar tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They sat inside a Khazar orbit that took in the whole region, since across eastern Europe after about 850 it was the Khazars rather than the Avars whom local elites copied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The move that mattered was forced on them, when the Pechenegs, beaten by the Khazars and pushed west from the Volga, drove the Magyars over the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They took the country between the Dnieper and the lower Pruth and Seret, which had belonged to the khans of Bulgaria, and from there struck in 862 into the lands of Louis the German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Byzantium used them as a weapon, and the Rus' chronicle says the emperor Leo hired the Ugrians against the Bulgars, whose country was ravaged until Symeon barely escaped to Dorostolon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whole-genome work traces the conquerors’ immigrant core to an earlier mixture of Mansi, early Sarmatians and late Xiongnu, and the Y chromosome of Béla III belongs to a lineage whose closest living paternal kin are the Bashkirs of the Volga and the Urals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 8–9, 22, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>A History of the Eastern Roman Empire from the Fall of Irene to the Accession of Basil I</i> (London: Macmillan, 1912), 423–27, 489–90, https://archive.org/details/historyofeastern00buryuoft. [Open access]","Zoltán Maróti et al., “The Genetic Origin of Huns, Avars, and Conquering Hungarians,” <i>Current Biology</i> 32, no. 13 (2022): 2858–70, https://real.mtak.hu/159253/. [Open access]","Chuan-Chao Wang et al., “Genome-Wide Autosomal, mtDNA, and Y Chromosome Analysis of King Béla III of the Hungarian Árpád Dynasty,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 19210, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8478946/. [Open access]","Florin Curta, “Have Avars Gone? A View from Eastern Europe,” <i>Plural: History, Culture, Society</i> 11, no. 2 (2023): 23–40, https://doi.org/10.37710/plural.v11i2_2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Magyar_honfoglal%C3%A1skori_tarsolylemezek.jpg/1920px-Magyar_honfoglal%C3%A1skori_tarsolylemezek.jpg","title":"Sabretache plates of the conquest period","desc":"Metal plates that faced the leather pouches hung from a warrior’s belt, from 10th-century graves in Hungary and Slovakia. The grave at Szolnok also held a dirham of the Samanid emir Nasr ibn Ahmad struck in 920 or 921, which dates the burial.","credit":"Photograph by OrionNimrod, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magyar_honfoglal%C3%A1skori_tarsolylemezek.jpg","alt":"Four ornate metal plaques laid in a row, each covered in dense interlacing plant scrollwork worked in low relief."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this people leave the steppe north of the Black Sea?","a":"Because the Pechenegs, beaten by the Khazars and pushed west from the Volga, drove them over the Dnieper, and about 895 they moved again into the Carpathian Basin."},{"q":"Why did the Khazars want a single Magyar king?","a":"Because the seven tribes had no common chief, and the khagan proposed raising the foremost of them over the whole people, which made the office a Khazar creation rather than a Magyar one."},{"q":"Why do the Bashkirs bear on the history of a Hungarian dynasty?","a":"Because the Y chromosome of Béla III belongs to a lineage whose closest living paternal kin are the Bashkirs of the Volga and the Urals, separated about two thousand years ago."}],"wiki":"Hungarians","places":["pol:magyars"]},
    "ru-020": {"questions":["Beaten by the Khazars and forced out of their own country between the Volga and the Ural, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the land of the Magyars instead.","Eight tribes of five divisions each and no king at all, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed a chieftainship to a first cousin rather than to a brother or a son."],"abstract":"The <b>Pechenegs</b> are the Turkic nomads who held the steppe between the Volga and the lower Danube and were Rus'’ most dangerous neighbours for two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their earlier country was a wide dominion between the Volga and the Ural, with the Burdas to the north-west, the Kipchaks north, the Uzes east and the Khazars south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They made war on the Khazars, were beaten, and so left it to take the land of the Magyars, whom they drove beyond the Dnieper; later the Uzes and the Khazars pushed them west of that river in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those Uzes are the Ghozz whom an Arab geographer has wintering on the lower Don and crossing the ice on horseback where the Khazar garrison could not hold them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They had no king: eight tribes under their own chiefs, each in five divisions, and a chief was succeeded by a first cousin or a cousin's son rather than a brother or a son, so the office should not settle in one branch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Rus' chronicle first brings them in at its year 6423, when they made peace with Igor and went on to the Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 968 they ringed Kyiv in numbers nobody could count, Olga and her grandsons shut inside and the people worn out by hunger, until a young man who knew their language walked through the camp asking whether anyone had seen his horse, and swam the Dnieper for help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four years later a prince of theirs called Kuria killed Sviatoslav at the cataracts, cut off his head and made a drinking cup of the skull, mounted in metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The steppe they held was the corridor Rus' needed, and the Dnipro-Buh estuary was the strategic point on the waterway from Kyiv towards the Caspian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One recent account has the Khazar khagan acknowledged as ruler of the Rus', so the collapse of Khazar power at the revolt of 965 left Kyiv without the overlord that had also been a buffer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 30, 52, 59–60, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>A History of the Eastern Roman Empire from the Fall of Irene to the Accession of Basil I</i> (London: Macmillan, 1912), 411, 424, https://archive.org/details/historyofeastern00buryuoft. [Open access]","Volodymyr Kuzovkov, “Dnipro-Buh Estuary Coast in Context of Eastern Campaigns of Prince Sviatoslav (964–972),” <i>Eminak</i>, no. 3 (2021): 46–56, https://doi.org/10.33782/eminak2021.3%2835%29.541. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 381–418, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 416–17, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/The_Pechenegs_defeating_the_Rus%2C_from_the_Skyllitzes_Matritensis%2C_fol._173r%2C_%28Public_Domain%29_via_Creative_Commons.jpg","title":"Pechenegs defeating the Rus","desc":"A miniature from the Madrid manuscript of the chronicle of John Skylitzes, folio 173r, made in the 12th century. Mounted Pechenegs ride down a body of Rus’ troops, the encounter the Byzantine chronicler records.","credit":"Unknown illuminator, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pechenegs_defeating_the_Rus%2C_from_the_Skyllitzes_Matritensis%2C_fol._173r%2C_%28Public_Domain%29_via_Creative_Commons.jpg","alt":"A coloured manuscript drawing of horsemen with round shields riding over fallen soldiers, against a plain gold ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this people move onto the western steppe at all?","a":"Because they made war on the Khazars and lost, which forced them out of their country between the Volga and the Ural and into the land of the Magyars, whom they drove beyond the Dnieper."},{"q":"Why could Kyiv not simply buy them off for good?","a":"Because they had no king to treat with: eight tribes under their own chiefs, each in five divisions, with a chieftainship passing sideways to a cousin rather than down to a son."},{"q":"Why was the Dnieper corridor worth fighting over?","a":"Because the waterway from Kyiv towards the Caspian ran through it, and the Dnipro-Buh estuary was the strategic point on that route during Sviatoslav's eastern campaigns."}],"wiki":"Pechenegs"},
    "ru-021": {"questions":["Disputed between the Vistula and the middle Dnieper, the homeland of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued from river names, borrowed words and the late date at which its dialects part.","Ancient DNA from central Europe shows a large movement out of eastern Europe in the 6th to 8th centuries, which may be how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the Elbe."],"abstract":"<b>Proto-Slavic</b> is the reconstructed language from which every Slavic tongue descends, recovered by comparing its daughters rather than read off any text, because nothing survives written in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its speakers left no inscriptions, so it is dated from the words it borrowed and lent and from the striking uniformity of those daughters at the moment they were first written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest question in the subject is where it was spoken, and the candidate homelands run from the Vistula and the Oder in the west to the middle Dnieper in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contact with Germanic speakers is not in doubt, since a large body of Germanic words entered the language, although for about nine of the clearest cases the direction of borrowing is argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archaeology divides over whether the Slavs moved into central Europe from outside or were formed where they are found, and the Polish discussion calls those positions allochthonic and autochthonic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two peoples of the lower Danube, the Sclaveni and the Antes, are described in the 6th century as speaking one tongue and as having borne a single name in the remote past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Gothic history of the same century treats Venethi, Antes and Sclaveni as three names for offshoots of one stock, spread from the source of the Vistula as far as the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the earliest direct testimony to a single Slavic speech community, and all of it comes from outside, in Greek and in Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genome-wide data from 555 ancient individuals show a large movement of people out of eastern Europe between the 6th and 8th centuries, replacing more than 80% of the local gene pool in eastern Germany, Poland and Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same work calls it plausible that the changes in language and material culture belong with those movements, while warning that the eastern source population itself has not yet been sampled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Michał Kara, “Archaeology, Mainly Polish, in the Current Discussion on the Ethnogenesis of the Slavs,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i> 63 (2022): 65–128, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2022.63.3. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “Ancient DNA Connects Large-Scale Migration with the Spread of Slavs,” <i>Nature</i> 646, no. 8084 (2025): 384–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09437-6. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 34–35, 119, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 59–60, 84–85, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 7.14.22–30, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), 269–73, https://archive.org/details/procopiuswitheng04procuoft. [Open access]","Marta Noińska and Mikołaj Rychło, “From Proto-Slavic into Germanic or from Germanic into Proto-Slavic? A Review of Controversial Loanwords,” <i>Studia Rossica Gedanensia</i>, no. 4 (2017): 39–52, https://doi.org/10.26881/srg.2017.4.02. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the parent language dated so late rather than to deep antiquity?","a":"Because the Slavic languages are still strikingly alike at the moment they are first written down, which leaves little time for divergence and puts the break-up of the parent tongue about the middle of the 1st millennium."},{"q":"Why do borrowed words help locate where it was spoken?","a":"Because a large body of Germanic vocabulary entered the language, which means its speakers lived within reach of Germanic speakers; the direction of borrowing is argued for about nine of the clearest cases."},{"q":"Why does ancient DNA not settle the question of the homeland?","a":"Because the eastern source population has not yet been sampled: the movement out of eastern Europe is visible in central European graves, and the closest surrogate for its origin is a Polish site of 600 to 900."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Prague_type_vessel%2C_Earliest_Slavic_pottery%2C_550-700_AD%2C_City_of_Prague_Museum%2C_175467.jpg/1920px-Prague_type_vessel%2C_Earliest_Slavic_pottery%2C_550-700_AD%2C_City_of_Prague_Museum%2C_175467.jpg","title":"Prague-type vessel, 550–700 CE","desc":"A hand-built cooking pot of the Prague type, found at Praha-Dejvice and held in the City of Prague Museum. Vessels of this plain shape are the earliest pottery widely identified as Slavic, and they belong to the centuries in which the parent language was breaking up.","credit":"Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prague_type_vessel%2C_Earliest_Slavic_pottery%2C_550-700_AD%2C_City_of_Prague_Museum%2C_175467.jpg","alt":"A plain dark clay pot with a wide mouth, a rounded body and no handles, standing on a museum shelf."},"wiki":"Proto-Slavic language"},
    "ru-022": {"questions":["Ruled by no single man and living in scattered hovels, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Antes are said to have spoken one tongue and borne one name in the remote past.","The earliest boundary anyone gives for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from a city on the Danube and a lake to the Dniester, and northward as far as the Vistula."],"abstract":"The <b>Sclaveni</b> are the people of the lower Danube whom Greek and Latin writers of the 6th century name as one of the two great divisions of the early Slavs, the other being the Antes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Gothic history written about 551 places their abode between a Danube city called Noviodunum, a lake called Mursianus, the Dniester and the Vistula, and says they have swamps and forests for their cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same work treats Venethi, Antes and Sclaveni as three names for offshoots of a single stock, a people despised in war but strong in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Byzantine account of the same generation says the two peoples speak one tongue, are ruled by no single man but refer every decision to the assembly, and had once shared the name Spori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lived in wretched huts set far apart and shifted their dwelling place often, and they came into battle on foot with small shields and javelins and no body armour at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their raids across the Danube are the reason they are recorded at all, and the crossings became routine once the general Chilbudius, who had held the river for three years, was killed on the far bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A great throng of them had already crossed the Ister, plundered the country beyond and enslaved a very great number of Romans before a body of Eruli caught up with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two peoples also fought one another, and in one such battle the Antes were beaten and a young man of theirs was carried off into a Sclavene household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle remembers the Slavs as having sat on the Danube before they scattered, driven from there by the Vlakhs, which is the same movement seen from the far end of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes show a large movement of people out of eastern Europe across the 6th to 8th centuries, and the Polish discussion sets this group in the middle of the 6th-century map, on both flanks of the Carpathian arc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 34–35, 119, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 59–60, 84–85, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 7.14.22–30, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), 269–73, https://archive.org/details/procopiuswitheng04procuoft. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 7.13.21–24, 7.14.1–12, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), 261, 263–67, https://archive.org/details/procopiuswitheng04procuoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 7–8, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “Ancient DNA Connects Large-Scale Migration with the Spread of Slavs,” <i>Nature</i> 646, no. 8084 (2025): 384–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09437-6. [Open access]","Michał Kara, “Archaeology, Mainly Polish, in the Current Discussion on the Ethnogenesis of the Slavs,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i> 63 (2022): 65–128, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2022.63.3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are they recorded by outsiders rather than in their own words?","a":"Because they left no writing of their own, and the only 6th-century accounts of them are Greek and Latin: a Gothic history of about 551 and a Byzantine narrative of the same generation, both written from the empire they raided."},{"q":"Why did the Danube stop holding them back?","a":"Because the general Chilbudius, who had crossed the river every year for three years and killed and enslaved the barbarians beyond it, went over with a small force and was killed; thereafter the river was free to cross whenever they wished."},{"q":"Why is their political shape worth noticing?","a":"Because they had no single ruler to treat with: everything touching their welfare was referred to the people, so the empire could not buy peace from one man, and the two peoples could even go to war with each other."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/01940_Prague_type_cinerary_urns%2C_Bubenec.png","title":"Prague-type urns drawn in 1940","desc":"Cinerary urns of the Prague type from Bubeneč, published in a 1940 study of early Slavic pottery in central Europe. Cremation in plain pots of this kind is the burial rite of the 6th- and 7th-century Slavs, and it is why so few of their graves yield bone.","credit":"Ivan Borkovský, from <i>Staroslovanská keramika ve střední Evropě</i>, 1940, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01940_Prague_type_cinerary_urns%2C_Bubenec.png","alt":"A printed plate of line drawings showing five plain handleless pots in profile, with a scale bar beneath."},"wiki":"Sclaveni"},
    "ru-023": {"questions":["Substrate river names of Baltic type across the upper Dnieper and the middle Oka show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread over country that already had a language of its own.","Ancient genomes trace a large movement out of eastern Europe between the 6th and 8th centuries, though no grave yet sampled lies in the homeland the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from."],"abstract":"The <b>East Slavs</b> are the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Slavic_languages\">Slavic</span>-speaking peoples of the East European Plain whose tribal names the Rus' chronicle lists and out of whose lands Kievan Rus' was afterwards built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That list gives the Polyane, the Drevlyane, the Dregovichi, the Polochane on the Polota, the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Ilmen_Slavs\">Slovene</span> about Novgorod, the Severyane, the Krivichi at the headwaters of the Volga, the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Daugava\">Dvina</span> and the Dnieper, and the Buzhane on the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Western_Bug\">Bug</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicler is careful to set them apart from the neighbours who paid tribute to Rus' and spoke other tongues, among them the Chud’, the Merya, the Ves’, the Muroma, the Cheremis and the Mordva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also has the Slavs sitting on the Danube before they scattered, which is a memory of movement rather than a record of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Setting that list beside a 4th-century catalogue of northern peoples shows how far the ethnic and linguistic map of eastern Europe had shifted in the intervening six centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The country they spread into was not empty of language, for substrate river names of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Baltic_languages\">Baltic</span> type run across the upper Dnieper and the middle <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Oka_(river)\">Oka</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those names correlate with the distribution of Early Iron Age cultures rather than with anything Slavic, and whether a distinct West Baltic layer stands among them is argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archaeology still divides over whether the Slavs of central Europe came from outside or were formed where they are found, and the same argument bears on the eastern branch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genome-wide data from 555 ancient individuals show a large movement out of eastern Europe between the 6th and 8th centuries, with a Polish site of 600 to 900 as the closest surrogate for its source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That work fixes no eastern homeland, because the source population itself has not been sampled, so the east is inferred from the direction of travel rather than observed in a grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 7–8, 14, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “Ancient DNA Connects Large-Scale Migration with the Spread of Slavs,” <i>Nature</i> 646, no. 8084 (2025): 384–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09437-6. [Open access]","Michał Kara, “Archaeology, Mainly Polish, in the Current Discussion on the Ethnogenesis of the Slavs,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i> 63 (2022): 65–128, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2022.63.3. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Napolskikh, “Ethno-Linguistic Situation in the Forest Zone of Eastern Europe in the First Centuries AD and the Data of Jordanes’ Getica,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 15, no. 1 (2018): 7–29, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2018.15.1.001. [Open access]","Pavel A. Gusenkov, “Revisiting the “West-Baltic” Type Hydronymy in Central Russia,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 18, no. 2 (2021): 67–87, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2021.18.2.019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicler bother to separate them from their neighbours?","a":"Because the distinction he is drawing is linguistic: he names the peoples who speak Slavic in Rus' and then lists the tribute-paying peoples who have each their own language, among them the Chud', the Merya, the Ves' and the Mordva."},{"q":"Why do river names matter for working out what happened?","a":"Because water names survive changes of population: a Baltic stratum of them across the upper Dnieper and the middle Oka shows that the ground was occupied by speakers of another language before the Slavs reached it."},{"q":"Why can ancient DNA not yet name their homeland?","a":"Because the source population has not been sampled. The movement out of eastern Europe is visible in the graves of the lands people moved into, and the nearest stand-in for its origin is a Polish site of 600 to 900."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D1%87%D1%83%D0%B4%D1%8C%D1%8E_%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%B3%D0%B0%D0%BC%2C_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B8_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D1%85%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC.jpg","title":"Tribute taken from seven peoples","desc":"A miniature of the late 15th century illustrating the chronicle entry for 859, in which the Varangians take tribute from the Chud’, the Slovene, the Merya and the Krivichi, and the Khazars from the Polyane, the Severyane and the Vyatichi. The manuscript is in the Library of the Academy of Sciences in St Petersburg.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D1%87%D1%83%D0%B4%D1%8C%D1%8E_%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%B3%D0%B0%D0%BC%2C_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8%2C_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D0%B8_%D0%B2%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D1%85%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC.jpg","alt":"A pen-and-ink manuscript drawing of two groups of standing figures handing bundles to armed men, above a block of Cyrillic script."},"wiki":"East Slavs"},
    "ru-024": {"questions":["Cremating their dead and setting the bones on a post by the road, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were still doing so when the chronicler wrote, more than a century after the conversion.","Beaten in 966 and twice again in 981 and 982, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held an Oka basin that princely houses were still fighting over in the 12th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Vyatichi</b> were the East Slavic people of the upper and middle Oka, and the chronicle derives them from a man called Vyatko who settled on that river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It makes him the brother of a Radim who settled on the Sozh and gave his name to the Radimichi, and calls both brothers Lyakhs, which is to say Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their burial rite is described as cremation, the bones gathered into a small vessel and the vessel set on a post beside the road, and the chronicler adds that they still do it in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radial and lobed temple rings are the jewellery most particular to them, shared with the Radimichi and the Severians and traced to the earlier Romny culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their country had carried other tongues first, for substrate river names of Baltic type run across the middle Oka,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the peoples of the forest zone had already changed once between the 4th century and the chronicler’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 859 they were paying tribute to the Khazars, and a century later they told a Rus' prince who asked them that they gave the Khazars a silver coin for every plough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That prince beat them in 966 and laid his own tribute on them, and his son had to do it twice again, in 981 and in 982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The upper Oka stayed a frontier rather than a province, and its districts were still being fought over by rival princely houses into the 12th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reason was the basin itself, a resource untouched by princely and boyar colonisation, which made it worth a long contest rather than a single conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A prince writing his own memoir says he went to Rostov through the land of the Vyatichi, which is how the region reads in the sources even then: country to be crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 9–10, 14, 51, 66, 251, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","D. L. Tsybakov, “The Military-Political Struggle for the Land of the Vyatichi in the Late X–First Half of the XII Centuries,” <i>History: Facts and Symbols</i>, no. 2 (2024): 32–42, https://doi.org/10.24888/2410-4205-2024-39-2-32-42. [Open access]","Evgenii Shinakov, Sergei Kuznetsov, and Vladislav Piskunov, “Temporal Rings of Severians, Radimichi, Vyatichi in the North-West of Chernigov Land: Correlation in the Light of Chemical Analysis,” <i>Stratum plus</i>, no. 5 (2025): 187–97, https://doi.org/10.55086/sp255187197. [Open access]","Pavel A. Gusenkov, “Revisiting the “West-Baltic” Type Hydronymy in Central Russia,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 18, no. 2 (2021): 67–87, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2021.18.2.019. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Napolskikh, “Ethno-Linguistic Situation in the Forest Zone of Eastern Europe in the First Centuries AD and the Data of Jordanes’ Getica,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 15, no. 1 (2018): 7–29, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2018.15.1.001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicler mention their funeral rite in the present tense?","a":"Because it was still going on as he wrote: he describes the cremation, the bones put in a small vessel and the vessel set on a post by the roadside, and then says that the Vyatichi do it even now, which dates their paganism to his own century."},{"q":"Why did it take three campaigns to bring them in?","a":"Because the upper Oka was a frontier rather than a province. One prince imposed tribute in 966 and his son had to beat them again in 981 and 982, and the basin was still being disputed between princely houses in the 12th century."},{"q":"Why was their basin worth so long a fight?","a":"Because it was a resource base that princely and boyar colonisation had left largely alone until the 12th century, which turned the upper Oka districts into the object of a long rivalry rather than a single conquest."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Jewellery_-_Vyatichi_-_Tula_regional_museum_of_local_lore.jpg/1920px-Jewellery_-_Vyatichi_-_Tula_regional_museum_of_local_lore.jpg","title":"Women’s jewellery of the Vyatichi","desc":"Bronze ornaments attributed to the Vyatichi, in the Tula regional museum of local lore. The lobed temple rings worn at the side of the head are the ornament most particular to this people and to its neighbours on the Sozh and the Desna.","credit":"Лапоть, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jewellery_-_Vyatichi_-_Tula_regional_museum_of_local_lore.jpg","alt":"A museum case of corroded bronze ornaments: several rings with broad flat lobes, a twisted neck ring and rows of small beads."},"wiki":"Vyatichi"},
    "ru-025": {"questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left no writing of its own, it is reconstructed from Greek observers, from chroniclers writing after the conversion and from words the languages kept.","Oaths sworn on swords by Perun and by Volos the god of herds are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> enters the treaties Rus' made with Byzantium in the 10th century."],"abstract":"<b>Slavic paganism</b> is the pre-Christian religion of the Slavs, and almost everything known about it comes from outside it, from Greek observers, from Christian chroniclers writing after the conversion, and from the words the languages kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest description says the Slavs held one god, the maker of the lightning, to be sole lord of all things, and sacrificed cattle and every other kind of victim to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They denied that fate had any power over men, but promised a sacrifice when sickness or war brought death near, paid it if they escaped, and reckoned their safety bought with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They revered rivers and nymphs and other spirits besides, sacrificed to all of them, and took their divinations at those same sacrifices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The names of the gods are far better recorded than anything they were thought to do, and the standard accounts of even the most familiar of them are full of contradictions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Rus' treaties show the religion in use, for Oleg’s men swore by Perun their god and by Volos the god of herds, and a later treaty curses an oath-breaker by God and by Perun together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 980 a Kyivan prince set idols on the hill above the town, a wooden Perun with a head of silver and a moustache of gold beside Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog, Simargl and Mokosh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sacrifices were offered there, the chronicler says the people gave their sons and daughters as victims, and the same prince’s uncle raised a Perun on the Volkhov at Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A four-faced stone pillar found in the Zbruch is often reproduced as an image of that pantheon, but since 2011 its antiquity has itself been argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gods did not leave the language at the conversion: they lost their standing as a source of religious meaning, fell into folklore, were addressed by the Church as maleficent idols, and the thunder god’s name survives in curses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 7.14.22–25, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), 269–71, https://archive.org/details/procopiuswitheng04procuoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 24, 41, 64, 96–97, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Oleg Vladislavovich Kutarev, “Dazhbog: The Ancient Slavic Pagan Deity of the Shining Sky,” <i>Studia Mythologica Slavica</i> 24 (2021), https://doi.org/10.3986/SMS20212411. [Open access]","Yu. Pysarenko, “Against Bias in the Study of the Zbruch Idol (Regarding the Psychology of Pseudoscientific Populism),” <i>Archaeology and Early History of Ukraine</i> 52, no. 3 (2024): 215–33, https://doi.org/10.37445/adiu.2024.03.14. [Open access]","Giuliano Gajetti, “The Slavic Thunder God in Eastern Slavic and Polish Phraseological Units,” in <i>Junge Slavistik im Dialog</i> (Kiel: Kiel University Publishing, 2024), https://doi.org/10.38072/2750-9605/p2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the religion so hard to describe from the inside?","a":"Because its adherents wrote nothing. The earliest account is a Byzantine one of about 550, the fuller accounts are by Christian chroniclers writing after the conversion, and the rest is inference from vocabulary and folklore."},{"q":"Why are the treaty oaths better evidence than the chroniclers' descriptions?","a":"Because they record the religion being used rather than described: Oleg's men swore on their swords by Perun and by Volos, and a later treaty curses an oath-breaker by God and by Perun, which is a pagan formula preserved inside a Christian document."},{"q":"Why did the gods' names outlive the religion?","a":"Because they moved into the language rather than disappearing from it. Stripped of religious standing after the conversion, they were taken up by folklore and denounced by the Church as maleficent idols, and the thunder god's name is still used in curses."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Svetovid_%E2%80%93_Zbruch_Idol_%28MAK-63%29.jpg/1920px-Svetovid_%E2%80%93_Zbruch_Idol_%28MAK-63%29.jpg","title":"Four-faced pillar from the Zbruch","desc":"The limestone pillar pulled from the Zbruch river in 1848, carved on four sides in three tiers and long read as a Slavic cult image of the 10th century. Since 2011 a case has been argued that it is a 19th-century work instead, and that case has been argued against in turn.","credit":"Unknown maker, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons; photograph from the Małopolska museums portal. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetovid_%E2%80%93_Zbruch_Idol_(MAK-63).jpg","alt":"A tall weathered stone pillar with a rounded cap, carved on each face with a standing human figure above two smaller registers of figures."},"wiki":"Slavic paganism"},
    "ru-026": {"questions":["Oleg’s men swore on their swords by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by Volos the god of herds in 907, and Igor’s treaty curses an oath-breaker by God and by the same god.","In Polabian the name for Thursday meant the day of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the place Latin gave Jupiter and English gave Thor in the week."],"abstract":"<b>Perun</b> is the thunder god of the Slavs, and the earliest written evidence of the name is the Old Rus' form Perunŭ in the Rus' chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traditional etymology derives it from a verb meaning to strike, making the god the one who strikes; another account starts from an old word for rock or mountain instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In several Slavic languages the word for a thunderstone, a belemnite or a stray flint taken for a fallen thunderbolt, is the same word as the god’s name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Polabian name for Thursday meant Perun’s day, which puts him where Latin put Jupiter and English put Thor in the week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Byzantine writer of the 540s had already reported that the Slavs held one god, the maker of the lightning, to be sole lord of all things and sacrificed cattle to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He is the god Rus' swore by: Oleg’s men in 907 swore on their swords by Perun and by Volos the god of herds, and Igor’s treaty curses the oath-breaker by God and by Perun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 980 a wooden Perun with a silver head and a gold moustache stood on the hill above Kyiv among five other idols, and the prince’s uncle raised another on the Volkhov at Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the conversion the Kyiv idol was tied to a horse’s tail, dragged down to the stream and beaten by twelve men with sticks, not because the wood could feel it but to shame the demon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was then thrown into the Dnieper with orders to push it off any shore it touched until it had passed the cataracts, and the sandbank where the wind left it kept his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the evidence is a hostile chronicle and a set of much later folk sayings, reconstructions of what he was thought to do vary a great deal, and the same caution applies to the whole pantheon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 24, 41, 64, 96–97, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Giuliano Gajetti, “The Slavic Thunder God in Eastern Slavic and Polish Phraseological Units,” in <i>Junge Slavistik im Dialog</i> (Kiel: Kiel University Publishing, 2024), https://doi.org/10.38072/2750-9605/p2. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 7.14.22–25, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), 269–71, https://archive.org/details/procopiuswitheng04procuoft. [Open access]","N. Fatyushyna, “The Image of the Thunder God Perun in the Pagan Outlook of Ancient Russia,” <i>Ukrainian Religious Studies</i>, no. 10 (1999): 64–67, https://doi.org/10.32420/1999.10.842. [Open access]","Oleg Vladislavovich Kutarev, “Dazhbog: The Ancient Slavic Pagan Deity of the Shining Sky,” <i>Studia Mythologica Slavica</i> 24 (2021), https://doi.org/10.3986/SMS20212411. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the thunderstone worth mentioning at all?","a":"Because in several Slavic languages the word for it is the god's own name: a belemnite or a worked flint picked up after a storm was taken for the thunderbolt itself, which shows the name doing duty for the weapon as well as the wielder."},{"q":"Why was the idol beaten rather than simply burned?","a":"Because the point was public humiliation. The chronicler is explicit that nobody supposed the wood could feel anything, and says the beating was meant to shame the demon that had used that shape to deceive people, and to punish its deceits."},{"q":"Why do modern reconstructions of him differ so much?","a":"Because the sources are hostile or late: a Christian chronicle recording his destruction, treaty formulas, and folk sayings collected many centuries afterwards, so what he was thought to do has to be inferred rather than read."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Perun_%28Radzivill_Chronicle%29.jpg","title":"Offerings at the idol, drawn c. 1490","desc":"A miniature of the late 15th century illustrating the chronicle entry for 980, in which the prince of Kyiv orders offerings at the idol of Perun. The drawing is five centuries later than the event and shows the idol as its Christian illustrator imagined it.","credit":"Unknown illuminator, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perun_(Radzivill_Chronicle).jpg","alt":"A pen drawing of a crowned figure and attendants standing before a tall post topped by a human head, with Cyrillic script above."},"wiki":"Perun","places":["pl:q4359567"]},
    "ru-027": {"questions":["Extinct and recoverable only from substrate place names, the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been shown by those names to be related to Mari.","Russian names that translate older Finnic ones work like a bilingual inscription, and more than seventy such pairs now underpin the etymologies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tongue."],"abstract":"The <b>Merya</b> were a Finnic-speaking people of the upper Volga whom the Rus' chronicle places on Lake Rostov and Lake Kleshchino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lists them among the peoples that paid tribute to Rus' and had each their own language, apart from the Slavic-speaking tribes, and names them as the first inhabitants at Rostov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They appear far earlier under the name Merens, in a 4th-century list of northern peoples subject to a Gothic king, where they are the northernmost groups along the Volga bend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their language is extinct and is recovered from substrate place names in central Russia, which is why it is spoken of as a group of dialects rather than one tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those names have been shown to be related to Mari, and the ethnonyms Mari, Merya and Muroma go back to one proto-form that may be a reflex of an Aryan word for a young warrior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The key to reading the substrate names is calquing, where a Russian name translates an older Finnic one and the pair works like a bilingual inscription.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than seventy name stems are involved, which makes the matches too numerous to be coincidence and yields a first set of reliable etymologies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mapped out, the calques correlate closely with the archaeological sites attributed to the Merya in the late 1st and early 2nd millennia, which dates the layer as well as placing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the calques translate between two extinct Finnic dialects rather than into Russian, evidence that more than one such language was spoken in the region before Slavic settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chronicle has them still paying tribute to Varangians from beyond the sea in 859, and after that they disappear from the record into the population of the Rostov country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 7–8, 14, 16, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Napolskikh and Alexander V. Savelyev, “Mari, Merya, Muroma — History of the Ethnonyms and Reconstruction of the Substrate Toponymy Languages,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 20, no. 3 (2023): 9–30, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2023.20.3.029. [Open access]","Oleg V. Smirnov, “Volga-Finnic Dialects in the Historical Merya Lands According to Toponymic Data. Linguistic Calques. I,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 22, no. 1 (2025): 55–81, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2025.22.1.002. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Napolskikh, “Ethno-Linguistic Situation in the Forest Zone of Eastern Europe in the First Centuries AD and the Data of Jordanes’ Getica,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 15, no. 1 (2018): 7–29, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2018.15.1.001. [Open access]","Oleg V. Smirnov, “Volga-Finnic Dialects in the Historical Merya Lands According to Toponymic Data. Linguistic Calques. II,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 22, no. 3 (2025): 101–48, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2025.22.3.031. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a dead language be recovered from place names at all?","a":"Because water and settlement names survive a change of population. Where a Russian name translates the older Finnic one, the pair behaves like a bilingual inscription, and more than seventy such stems give a first set of reliable etymologies."},{"q":"Why do scholars speak of Merya dialects rather than one language?","a":"Because the substrate toponymy of central Russia separates into several groups, reconstructed as the Merya of Rostov, of Kostroma and of Murom, and some names translate between two extinct Finnic dialects rather than into Russian."},{"q":"Why does the mapping of those names matter as much as their etymology?","a":"Because the calques cluster where the archaeological sites attributed to the Merya lie, in the late 1st and early 2nd millennia, so the linguistic layer can be dated and placed rather than merely reconstructed."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Buckle_-_Merya%2C_10-11c_-_Rostov_kremlin_museum-reserve.jpg/1920px-Buckle_-_Merya%2C_10-11c_-_Rostov_kremlin_museum-reserve.jpg","title":"Bronze buckle, 10th to 11th century","desc":"A cast bronze buckle attributed to the Merya, of the second half of the 10th or the early 11th century, in the Rostov kremlin museum-reserve. Metalwork of this kind is most of what survives of a people whose language is known only from place names.","credit":"Лапоть, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buckle_-_Merya%2C_10-11c_-_Rostov_kremlin_museum-reserve.jpg","alt":"A small corroded bronze buckle with an openwork frame and a central tongue, photographed against a pale background."},"places":["pl:q193342"]},
    "ru-028": {"questions":["Substrate river names across the upper Dnieper and the middle Oka show that the languages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once reached far east of the sea they are named for.","One of the languages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Old Prussian, is first attested in a German-Prussian glossary of 802 words copied at the end of the 14th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Balts</b> are the peoples of the south-eastern Baltic and its hinterland, and their languages are Lithuanian, Latvian and the extinct Old Prussian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their earliest description calls them the Aestii, on the eastern shore of the Suebian Sea, with the rites and dress of the Suebi but a language nearer to the British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That account has them worshipping the mother of the gods, wearing a boar as a religious device, using clubs oftener than iron, and gathering amber, which they called glesum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They brought it in shapeless lumps and marvelled at the price paid for it, since to them the stuff was useless and had lain among the sea’s other refuse until Roman luxury named it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The amber reached the empire through Pannonia, and a Roman knight sent to the coast by the manager of Nero’s games came back with enough of it to stud the nets that shielded the arena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A Gothic history has the Aesti holding the shore of Ocean beyond the mouths of the Vistula, a subject race that the Gothic king Ermanaric brought under his rule in the 4th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle names Lithuania, Semigallia, Kors, the Letts and the Prussians among the northern peoples of Japheth’s portion, each with a language of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Old Prussian died out, and its written record begins only with the Teutonic Order’s campaigns against these peoples in the 13th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest monument of it is a German-Prussian glossary of 802 words, a copy of the late 14th century made from an original perhaps a hundred years older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their languages once reached far inland: substrate river names of Baltic type run across the upper Dnieper and the middle Oka, correlating with the Early Iron Age cultures there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Tacitus, <i>Germania</i> 45, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0083%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 37.11, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D37%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i> 36, 120, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 60, 85, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 2, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Letas Palmaitis, “Written Monuments in Old Prussian,” <i>Knygotyra</i> 32 (1997): 12–19, https://doi.org/10.15388/Knygotyra.1997.30. [Open access]","Pavel A. Gusenkov, “Revisiting the “West-Baltic” Type Hydronymy in Central Russia,” <i>Вопросы ономастики</i> 18, no. 2 (2021): 67–87, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2021.18.2.019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did amber bring them into Roman writing when nothing else did?","a":"Because they were the only people who gathered it, and Roman luxury wanted it. The trade ran through Pannonia, and a knight was sent to their coast for Nero's games and came back with enough amber to stud the nets round the arena."},{"q":"Why is one of their languages attested so late and so thinly?","a":"Because its written record begins only with the Teutonic Order's campaigns in the 13th century. The oldest monument of Old Prussian is a German-Prussian glossary of 802 words, and it survives as a copy made at the end of the fourteenth."},{"q":"Why do river names place them so much further east than the sea they are named for?","a":"Because water names outlast the people who gave them: a Baltic stratum of them runs across the upper Dnieper and the middle Oka, correlating with the Early Iron Age cultures of that region rather than with anything later."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Baltic_amber_necklace_-_MUSE.jpg/1920px-Baltic_amber_necklace_-_MUSE.jpg","title":"Beads of Baltic amber","desc":"A necklace strung from Baltic amber, in the science museum at Trento. Amber gathered on this coast is the one product that brought its people into Roman writing at all, and Roman authors describe it being traded inland in unworked lumps.","credit":"Matteo De Stefano / MUSE – Science Museum of Trento, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_amber_necklace_-_MUSE.jpg","alt":"A loop of graded honey-coloured translucent beads, the largest in the middle, laid flat on a dark surface."},"wiki":"Balts","places":["pl:q19658174"]},
    "ru-029": {"questions":["Because the Rus' chronicle calls the Baltic their sea and puts them along it from Asia to the land of the English, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> frame the whole northern geography of its opening pages.","Asked for in 988 against the revolt of Bardas Phokas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a corps of their own and the best paid of all the imperial guards."],"abstract":"<b>Varangians</b> is the name the eastern Slavs gave to the Scandinavians who came among them, and the Rus' chronicle uses it for raiders, for merchants and for hired soldiers alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It calls the Baltic the Varangian Sea and says they live along it, eastward towards Asia and westward as far as the land of the English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 859 Varangians from beyond that sea were taking tribute from the Chud’, the Slovene, the Merya and the Krivichi, and three years later they were driven back over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantinople knew them too, and a 10th-century Italian bishop lists Hungarians, Pechenegs, Khazars and Rus' among the warlike peoples on its northern side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scandinavians had entered imperial service by the middle of the 9th century, but no separate unit was formed out of them until 988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In that year the emperor asked the prince of Kyiv for a body of his warriors against the revolt of Bardas Phokas, and a courtier at the palace describes a picked band trained as a corps of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guard that grew out of it became the best paid of all the imperial guards, which is a fair measure of what its loyalty was thought to be worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were hired on the same terms in the north and were as hard to hold: a prince keeping many of them at Novgorod in 1016 found them assaulting the townsmen’s wives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The townsmen gathered by night and killed those Varangians in Poromon’s Court, the prince had the killers cut down by a ruse, and a thousand Varangians then marched south in his army of four thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What carried them east was a traffic in silver, slaves and service that ran through Khazar territory as much as through Byzantium, and how far the khagan’s authority over the Rus' reached is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 2, 14, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Alexandra Airinei, “The Varangian Guard and Its Contribution to the Manifestation of the Imperial Power in Byzantium,” <i>The Romanian Journal for Baltic and Nordic Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2015): 7–26, https://doi.org/10.53604/rjbns.v7i2_2. [Open access]","Liudprand of Cremona, <i>Antapodosis</i> 1.11, 5.15, in <i>The Works of Liudprand of Cremona</i>, trans. F. A. Wright (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1930), 8, 185, https://archive.org/details/worksofliudprand00liud. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 381–418, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Byzantium want them for the emperor's own guard?","a":"Because they were outsiders with no stake in palace factions, and they were hired at the moment one was most dangerous: the emperor asked Kyiv for them in 988 against the revolt of Bardas Phokas, and the corps that grew from it was the best paid of the guards."},{"q":"Why does the chronicle use one word for raiders, traders and soldiers?","a":"Because from the Slav side these were the same men in different relations: the Varangians of 859 took tribute, were driven out three years later, and were afterwards hired by the same towns and princes they had preyed on."},{"q":"Why was hiring them a risk as well as a resource?","a":"Because they were only loyal while employed and paid. The Novgorod entry for 1016 has a prince's Varangians assaulting the townsmen's wives, the townsmen killing them by night, and the prince destroying the killers in turn before marching south."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/A_Thracesian_woman_kills_a_Varangian.jpg","title":"A Varangian killed, drawn in the 12th century","desc":"A miniature from the Madrid manuscript of the chronicle of John Skylitzes, showing a woman of the Thracesian theme killing a Varangian who had attacked her, and his companions handing over his belongings to her. It is one of the few pictures of the guard made by anyone who had seen it.","credit":"Unknown illuminator, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Thracesian_woman_kills_a_Varangian.jpg","alt":"A coloured manuscript strip showing a woman with a raised sword over a fallen armoured man, watched by a group of soldiers with spears."},"wiki":"Varangians"},
    "ru-030": {"questions":["Called Rusii by the Greeks from the colour of their skins, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called Nordmanni in the west from the quarter their country lay in.","Counted among the peoples of Japheth beside Varangians, Swedes, Normans and Goths, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are grouped by the chronicle with the northerners rather than the Slavs."],"abstract":"The <b>Rus' people</b> are the group whose name the eastern Slavs took over and gave to a state, and the earliest notice of that name is western rather than eastern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 839 a Byzantine embassy reached the Frankish emperor at Ingelheim bringing men who said that they, and their people, were called Rhos, sent by a king styled chacanus for friendship’s sake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They asked leave to go home by another road, because the route by which they had come to Constantinople had lain among barbarous peoples of extreme savagery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Looking into why they had come, the emperor found them to be of the people of the Sueones, judged them spies rather than envoys, and held them until he could learn whether they were honest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where their king’s seat lay is unsettled, and one recent argument places them outside mainland Sweden altogether, on the Baltic islands and the eastern Baltic shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A 10th-century Italian bishop says the Greeks call them Rusii from the colour of their skins, while westerners call them Nordmanni from the quarter their country lies in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sets them beside the Hungarians, the Pechenegs and the Khazars as the warlike peoples north of Constantinople, and names their king Igor and his fleet of a thousand ships and more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Rus' chronicle grades them the same way, counting Varangians, Swedes, Normans, Goths, Rus' and English together among the peoples of Japheth rather than with the Slavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also lays out the sea it calls the Varangians’ own, running east towards Asia and west as far as the land of the English, with these peoples settled along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their dealings ran through the Khazar khaganate as much as through Byzantium, and how far the khagan’s authority over them reached is argued rather than settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Les annales de Saint-Bertin et de Saint-Vaast</i>, anno 839, ed. C. Dehaisnes (Paris: Jules Renouard, 1871), 34–35, https://archive.org/details/lesannalesdesai00hincgoog. [Open access]","Liudprand of Cremona, <i>Antapodosis</i> 1.11, 5.15, in <i>The Works of Liudprand of Cremona</i>, trans. F. A. Wright (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1930), 8, 185, https://archive.org/details/worksofliudprand00liud. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 2, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Alexey E. Vinogradov, “On the Localization of the People of Rhos of the Bertine Annals,” <i>Locus: People, Society, Culture, Meanings</i> 11, no. 3 (2020): 28–39, https://doi.org/10.31862/2500-2988-2020-11-3-28-39. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 381–418, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the first record of the name a Frankish one?","a":"Because the men who carried it turned up at Ingelheim in 839 in the train of a Byzantine embassy, asking for a safe route home; the Frankish annalist wrote down what they called themselves, and eastern sources for the name are all later."},{"q":"Why did the Frankish emperor detain them?","a":"Because their story did not hold together. They said they had come for friendship, but he investigated their arrival, concluded they were of the Sueones and were scouting his realm and the empire, and kept them until he could find out whether they had come in good faith."},{"q":"Why do the sources file them with northerners rather than Slavs?","a":"Because that is how their contemporaries saw them: the Frankish annalist makes them Swedes, an Italian bishop equates Rusii with Nordmanni, and the Rus' chronicle itself counts them among the peoples of Japheth beside Varangians, Swedes, Normans and Goths."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Ingelheim_Kaiserpfalz_img01.jpg/1920px-Ingelheim_Kaiserpfalz_img01.jpg","title":"Remains of the palace at Ingelheim","desc":"What is left of the Carolingian palace at Nieder-Ingelheim on the Rhine. This is where the Frankish emperor received the Byzantine embassy of 839 and, with it, the men who said their people were called Rhos.","credit":"Andreas Praefcke, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingelheim_Kaiserpfalz_img01.jpg","alt":"Low stone walls and the stub of a rounded apse standing in mown grass, with modern houses and trees behind."},"wiki":"Rus' people"},
    "ru-031": {"questions":["Imperial patronage, a coined insult and modern genome sequencing have all been drawn into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began as a quarrel between academicians writing in German.","Because the earliest names in the Rus' elite are Norse and its own chronicle says the rulers came from over the sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns on what that means."],"abstract":"The <b>Normanist controversy</b> is the argument, almost two centuries old when an Arabist surveyed it in 2000, over how far Scandinavians were involved in making the first state among the eastern Slavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its documentary core is short, for the chronicle compiled at Kyiv says only that the northern peoples sent over the Varangian Sea for rulers, and that those Varangians were called Rus', as others are called Swedes, Normans, Angles or Goths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An independent notice is older still: a Frankish annal of 839 records men calling themselves Rhos who proved, on questioning, to be Swedes, sent on by a ruler styled khagan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Specialists read that title as the Khazar ruler's, since traders establishing themselves in the valley of the Volkhov could not plausibly have claimed a style carrying such universal pretensions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From those few lines grew a dispute whose terms were set outside scholarship, a Swedish writer having already claimed in 1614 and 1615 that Rurik was a Swede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The theory's fullest early form was a five-volume commentary on the chronicle printed at Göttingen between 1802 and 1809, which derived the name Rus' from Roslagen on the Swedish coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A professor at Dorpat answered it in 1808 and again in 1814 and 1826, showing that Roslagen was a late name and calling the sagas' complete failure to mention Rurik a 'convincing silence'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His opponent replied with the charge that has shadowed the argument ever since, that what had risen against the Norman theory was Russian national pride rather than evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two centuries on, the scholar who translated the fullest Arabic description of the Rus' declined to take either side, on the ground that the Arabic sources simply do not afford the clarity the question needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genetics has entered the same ground without closing it, for whole-genome work on a 13th-century prince found a male line whose nearest ancestral components include early medieval eastern Scandinavia, while the earliest Rurikids remain without direct evidence of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James E. Montgomery, “Ibn Faḍlān and the Rūsiyyah,” <i>Journal of Arabic and Islamic Studies</i> 3 (2000): 1, 3, https://doi.org/10.5617/jais.4553. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 386–389, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","V. V. Fomin and L. V. Isakova, “Gustav Ewers’ Contribution to the Study of the Initial Period of Ancient Rus’ History,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i> 17, no. 1 (2018): 153–156, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8674-2018-17-1-151-174. [Open access]","K. V. Zhur et al., “The Rurikids: The First Experience of Reconstructing the Genetic Portrait of the Ruling Family of Medieval Rus’ Based on Paleogenomic Data,” <i>Acta Naturae</i> 15, no. 3 (2023): 50–51, 60–62, https://doi.org/10.32607/actanaturae.23425. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can so short a set of sources sustain so long an argument?","a":"The chronicle gives a bare narrative of invitation and a list of names, and the Frankish annal one interrogation, so almost every question a modern reader wants answered has to be argued from inference rather than read off a text."},{"q":"Why does the silence of the sagas count as evidence at all?","a":"Scandinavian writers preserved material about rulers far further away, so an advocate of the theory has to explain why the tradition kept the distant Rollo and dropped the nearer Rurik, whose career should have drawn attention at home."},{"q":"Why has genome sequencing not settled the question?","a":"The remains tested belong to a prince of the 13th century, four hundred years after the events, and the line's ancestral components are mixed; no material from the earliest generation has been sequenced at all."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/August_Ludwig_von_Schl%C3%B6zer_AGE_V30_1809.jpg","title":"Schlözer engraved in 1809","desc":"A stipple engraving of August Ludwig von Schlözer, whose five-volume commentary on the Slavonic chronicle set out the Norman theory in its fullest early form.","credit":"Engraved by Carl Ermer; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:August_Ludwig_von_Schl%C3%B6zer_AGE_V30_1809.jpg","alt":"A stipple-engraved oval portrait bust of an elderly man with powdered hair in a high-collared coat, turned slightly to one side, the engraver's signature below."},"wiki":"Anti-Normanism"},
    "ru-032": {"questions":["Excavated timber halls of the 880s and the 920s, with balances, weights and eastern silver inside them, make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the gateway where Baltic shipping met the rivers running south.","Of every place Scandinavians reached in eastern Europe, only <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a name built to their own pattern, a local word with the element borg fastened to it."],"abstract":"<b>Staraya Ladoga</b> stands on the lower Volkhov a little above Lake Ladoga, where shipping out of the Baltic had to change to the rivers of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was in touch with the Islamic world from the 8th and 9th centuries, as were the hillfort that preceded Novgorod and the fort at Supruty far to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley of the Volkhov between the two lakes was in fact the main tract in which Scandinavian traders and adventurers established themselves in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Norse name was Aldeigja, and in the fuller form Aldeigjuborg it is the only settlement in eastern Europe whose name follows the ordinary Scandinavian pattern of a local word plus the element borg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That name is first written down in a court poem composed about 1010, which is very much later than the archaeology it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Excavation has recovered a series of large halls built in a post-and-plank technique, the first raised after 880 and standing until about 910 or 920, and a second with floors dated by tree rings to the 920s and the 930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What lay inside them, balances and weights and objects fetched from the east, points to the houses of men living by long-distance trade rather than to ordinary dwellings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A still larger building of the 940s held wooden figures of human shape as well as trading gear, and the same combination turns up at Wolin on the Baltic coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Old Rus' historical memory does record the place for the 10th century, along with Novgorod on the Volkhov, Pskov, Chernigov, Polotsk and Lyubech, though what relation any of them stood in to the princes at Kyiv is unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle makes the town the first seat of the invited Varangian prince, who moved to Lake Ilmen and built Novgorod only after his brothers died, while other recensions of the same work seat him at Novgorod from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aleksandr Musin and Błażej Stanisławski, “Ośrodki węzłowe i kontakty transbałtyckie pomiędzy społecznościami północno-zachodniej Rusi i ziem polskich w X stuleciu,” <i>Przegląd Archeologiczny</i> 70 (2022): 176, 207–208, https://doi.org/10.23858/PA70.2022.2708. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 389, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 47, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 45, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a town grow at this particular bend of the river?","a":"It sits where sea-going ships from the Baltic could go no further and cargo had to be moved into river craft, so the site collects the traffic of a whole trading system rather than merely a local district."},{"q":"Why does the Norse form of the name matter to historians?","a":"It is built on the ordinary Scandinavian pattern of a local word plus borg, and no other place in eastern Europe carries such a name, which places the town inside the zone where Scandinavians were naming settlements themselves."},{"q":"Why are the large halls read as merchants' houses rather than homes?","a":"Their contents set them apart: balances and weights for measuring silver, goods brought from the east, and in the latest of them wooden figures, a combination matched at Wolin across the Baltic."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Volkhov_River_at_Staraya_Ladoga._The_Russian_Province_%284019543361%29.jpg/1920px-Volkhov_River_at_Staraya_Ladoga._The_Russian_Province_%284019543361%29.jpg","title":"The Volkhov at Staraya Ladoga","desc":"The lower Volkhov at Staraya Ladoga, the reach where cargo out of the Baltic had to be moved into river craft.","credit":"Andrey Korchagin; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volkhov_River_at_Staraya_Ladoga._The_Russian_Province_%284019543361%29.jpg","alt":"A broad grey river curving between low wooded banks under a cloudy sky, with a small white church on the far shore."},"wiki":"Staraya Ladoga","places":["pl:q216794"]},
    "ru-033": {"questions":["With some four and a half thousand barrows around it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was excavated for more than 130 years and is named in no written source of its own age.","Glass beads matched at Kyiv, imported pottery and Byzantine and Arab coin hoards show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived on the river traffic rather than on the land around it."],"abstract":"<b>Gnyozdovo</b> lies on the right bank of the Dnieper about 12 kilometres (7 miles) downstream of Smolensk, and is the largest archaeological complex the 10th century left in the lands of the Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemetery holds something like four and a half thousand barrows, raised between the beginning of the 10th century and the beginning of the 11th, and the site has been dug for more than 130 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the 9th century it was almost nothing, an ephemeral settlement barely readable in the ground and perhaps no more than one or two farmsteads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cremation predominates in the graves, and the burnt deposits routinely contain animal bone, horses among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beads from the inhumations can be matched against sets from the barrows at Kyiv, and wheel-thrown pottery of a distinctive profile dominates production here from the middle of the 10th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The place has no name of its own in any surviving source, which is the difficulty every account of it has to begin with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One proposal identifies it with a Norse name reconstructed as Sýrnesgarðr, whose Slavonic counterpart would come from the Svinets, a brook running into the Dnieper nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another would make it the town a Byzantine treatise of the 950s calls Miliniska, usually read as Smolensk, though a small fortified site on the Irsha answers the Greek form at least as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The warning worth heeding is that the towns that treatise lists need not be the great emporia at all, since several may have been temporary bases that vanished and were replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What is certain is that the complex stood on the river road south, and that its wealth came from that traffic rather than from the forest and field around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 48, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","I. V. Kirillova and N. N. Spasskaya, “Horse Remains from the Gnezdovo Archaeological Complex, Smolensk Region, Russia,” <i>Russian Journal of Theriology</i> 14, no. 1 (2015): 85–86, https://doi.org/10.15298/rusjtheriol.14.1.04. [Open access]","Olga Dobrova, “Beads from Inhumation Rite Burials of Gnezdovo Burial Mound,” <i>Povolzhskaya Arkheologiya (The Volga River Region Archaeology)</i> 4, no. 22 (2017): 236, 251, https://doi.org/10.24852/pa2017.4.22.236.257. [Open access]","Aleksandr Musin and Błażej Stanisławski, “Ośrodki węzłowe i kontakty transbałtyckie pomiędzy społecznościami północno-zachodniej Rusi i ziem polskich w X stuleciu,” <i>Przegląd Archeologiczny</i> 70 (2022): 207, https://doi.org/10.23858/PA70.2022.2708. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 40, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it awkward that no source names this place?","a":"It is the largest 10th-century complex known in the lands of the Rus', so the written record and the archaeological record cannot be fitted together at exactly the point where the material evidence is richest."},{"q":"Why does the ninth-century evidence matter for reading the site?","a":"Almost nothing of that date survives here, so the complex is not an old centre that grew gradually but something that appeared quickly once the river traffic did, which dates the traffic as much as the place."},{"q":"Why are the beads worth comparing with those from Kyiv?","a":"Matching sets at two points hundreds of miles apart on the same river road show goods and fashions moving along it, which is better evidence of a working route than any single rich grave could be."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Sword_and_spear_Gnezdovo_GIM_Zlatnik.jpg/1920px-Sword_and_spear_Gnezdovo_GIM_Zlatnik.jpg","title":"Sword and spear from Gnyozdovo","desc":"A sword and a spearhead recovered from the Gnyozdovo complex near Smolensk, assigned to the second quarter of the 10th century.","credit":"Лапоть, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sword_and_spear_Gnezdovo_GIM_Zlatnik.jpg","alt":"A long corroded iron sword with a lobed pommel laid above a slender socketed spearhead, both mounted on deep blue cloth."},"wiki":"Gnyozdovo","places":["pl:q13424755"]},
    "ru-034": {"questions":["A Byzantine emperor's handbook describes dugout boats gathering below Kyiv each spring and running a series of named rapids, which is the southern half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the other end.","One recent reading makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a description of land crossings set down by a traveller watching the watersheds, rather than a sailor's account of an unbroken waterway."],"abstract":"The <b>Route from the Varangians to the Greeks</b> is the chronicle's own name for the road of rivers and portages that joined the Baltic to the Black Sea across the East European Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opening pages trace the way up the Dnieper, over a portage to the Lovat, into Lake Ilmen, down the Volkhov into Lake Nevo and out at last to the Varangian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same passage insists the road could be taken in either direction, from the Greeks to the Varangians as well, and notes that the Volga carried travellers east instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Byzantine handbook of the 950s describes the southern half from the imperial end: dugout boats come down from the towns of the interior, gather below Kyiv and then run a series of named rapids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That handbook survives in one manuscript alone, copied between 1059 and 1073 by the household scribe of a caesar who taught two future emperors, so the fullest account of the route reaches us by a single thread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What moved along it can be measured, for the traffic was paid for in silver struck in the caliphate, and the southern end supplied glass beads and cloth in return for northern goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The boats were bought rather than built by the men who sailed them, the handbook reporting that Slavs cut hulls through the winter months and sold them on in the spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not every scholar reads the chronicle passage as a boatman's itinerary: one recent study argues that it is written from the land, since in medieval usage the word for a portage named the whole watershed a traveller crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On that reading its author had gone the road himself, and the study identifies him with a Greek-born churchman in the circle of a prince ruling at Kyiv late in the 11th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Either way the description does a second job, fitting the enormous territory of the Rus' into a Christian map of the world that had never contained it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 9, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 74–80, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","Aleksei S. Shchavelev, “Treatise “De Administrando Imperio” by Emperor Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus: Date of the Paris. gr. 2009 Copy, Years of Compiling of the Original Codex, and a Hypothesis about the Number of Authors,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 9 (2019): 682–683, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.09.33. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 383–384, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Sergei A. Kozlov, “According to Chronicles, Did the “Route from the Varangians to the Greeks” Go by Sea or Overland?,” <i>Izvestia of the Ural Federal University. Series 2. Humanities and Arts</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 26, https://doi.org/10.15826/izv2.2023.25.2.021. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicle describe the road in both directions?","a":"The passage is orienting a reader rather than guiding a traveller: naming the two ends against each other places the whole of the Rus' lands between the Baltic and Byzantium, which is the frame the work needs before its narrative begins."},{"q":"Why is the Byzantine account so much more detailed than the Slavonic one?","a":"It was written for the empire's own use, by a court that needed to know how the boats came and where they could be stopped, so it records the rapids, the gathering point and the season."},{"q":"Why would it matter whether the passage was written from land or water?","a":"A boatman's itinerary would be evidence that the whole corridor was navigated as one journey, while a description of watersheds suggests a road made of separate river stretches joined by crossings on foot."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%86%D1%8C_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%96%D0%B4.jpg","title":"The Nenasytets rapid","desc":"The rapid called Nenasytets on the Dnieper, photographed before the river was dammed and the cataracts drowned.","credit":"Unknown author; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%86%D1%8C_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%96%D0%B4.jpg","alt":"An old photograph of a wide river broken by long ridges of bare rock, with white water pouring between them and flat banks beyond."},"wiki":"Route from the Varangians to the Greeks","places":["pl:q4163154"]},
    "ru-035": {"questions":["An envoy who reached the Bulgar king in 921 left the fullest description of the traders working the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, down to the silver bands their wives wore at the neck.","Because furs were what the caliphate wanted and coin was what it paid with, buried hoards of dirhams map the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> better than any written itinerary can."],"abstract":"The <b>Volga trade route</b> ran from the Baltic and the northern forests down the Volga to the market of the Volga Bulgars and on to the Caspian and the caliphate beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chronicle compiled at Kyiv knew it as the alternative to the road south, saying that one could go by the Volga to the Bulgars and the Khvalisians and eastward as far as the lands of Shem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Silver coins struck in the caliphate, and above all in Khurasan and Transoxiana, are what document how far the traffic reached in the 9th and 10th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the Rus' who sailed down to Bulgar and traded slaves and furs there for dirhams and for goods out of Arab lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around 800 they opened direct connections, by the Don as well as the Volga, between the developing northern markets and the richest economy of western Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The thing that galvanised the exchange was fur, for southern taste had despised the wearing of pelts in antiquity, and the change of mind in the 8th century created a demand only a dispersed system of forest hunters could meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hoards of dirhams therefore map the trade, reaching much of the Baltic and Poland as well as the Russian rivers, with Gotland holding by far the largest concentration and so acting as its financial centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern end was in touch with the Islamic world from the 8th and 9th centuries, at Staraya Ladoga, at the hillfort that preceded Novgorod, and at the fort at Supruty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An embassy sent from Baghdad to the Bulgar king in 921 left the fullest description of the traders, its author watching them disembark on the Atil and raise great wooden houses along its bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He reports that a man whose wealth reached ten thousand dirhams had a band made for his wife's neck and another for every ten thousand more, and that their prayer was to meet a merchant with many coins who would buy whatever they offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 383–384, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Aleksandr Musin and Błażej Stanisławski, “Ośrodki węzłowe i kontakty transbałtyckie pomiędzy społecznościami północno-zachodniej Rusi i ziem polskich w X stuleciu,” <i>Przegląd Archeologiczny</i> 70 (2022): 176, https://doi.org/10.23858/PA70.2022.2708. [Open access]","James E. Montgomery, “Ibn Faḍlān and the Rūsiyyah,” <i>Journal of Arabic and Islamic Studies</i> 3 (2000): 5, 7, 10, https://doi.org/10.5617/jais.4553. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 406–8, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does silver rather than fur survive as the evidence for this trade?","a":"Coin was struck, dated and buried in hoards that archaeologists recover, while the pelts it paid for were worn out and thrown away, so the commodity that drove the route is nearly invisible and the payment for it is not."},{"q":"Why did demand for fur appear in the south when it did?","a":"Antiquity had treated the wearing of animal pelts as a mark of barbarism, and that prejudice was set aside in the 8th century, which turned a northern necessity into a luxury a rich urban world would pay for."},{"q":"Why was Gotland, rather than a river town, the financial centre?","a":"It holds by far the densest concentration of dirham hoards, which is the measure available for where the silver came to rest, and an island in the middle of the Baltic sat where the northern end of the traffic converged."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Coins_Spilling_Silver_Hoard_2_closeup.jpg/1920px-Coins_Spilling_Silver_Hoard_2_closeup.jpg","title":"Coins from the Spillings hoard","desc":"Coins from the second of the Spillings hoards on Gotland, the island where finds of this kind lie thickest anywhere in the Baltic.","credit":"W.carter, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_Spilling_Silver_Hoard_2_closeup.jpg","alt":"A dense heap of thin, worn coins and coin fragments tarnished to a dull brown, several showing Arabic inscriptions."},"wiki":"Volga trade route"},
    "ru-036": {"questions":["Only four towns can be confidently assigned to the 10th-century polity of the Rurikids, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a name a good deal tidier than the thing it describes.","Byzantine, Arabic and Norse writers name ten towns its own chronicle never mentions, so the map of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the chronicle draws is a later one."],"abstract":"<b>Kievan Rus'</b> is the modern name for the polity that grew along the river roads of eastern Europe under princes of the house of Rurik ruling from Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nobody who lived in it used that phrase: its chroniclers set the Rus' among the lists of the peoples of the earth and otherwise wrote simply of the Rus' land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A study of the polity between 911 and 987 sets the modern term aside altogether, arguing that Kievan Rus', Ancient Rus' and the Old Russian state all import assumptions that do not fit a pagan century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rests less on the chronicle, whose chronology draws increasing criticism, than on a Byzantine emperor's works on tactics and on governing the empire, and on the letters of a Cordoban caliph's minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The writers of the chronicle's own lost predecessor, composed at Kyiv in the 1090s, had already fitted the Rus' into world history as a Christian people and a powerful state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The written record of its towns is thinner than any map suggests: of 25 town names in the chronicle, only Kyiv, Vyshgorod, Vruchiy and Rodnya can be confidently placed in the 10th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ladoga, Novgorod, Pskov, Chernigov, Polotsk and Lyubech are remembered for that century too, with Smolensk and Turov doubtful, but what relation any of them stood in to the princes at Kyiv is unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At least ten town names known from Arabic, Greek and Scandinavian writers appear nowhere in the early chronicle at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What held the polity together was the fur trade, whose scale and reach help explain the growth of institutions above the level of the tribe among the Slavs of the Volga, Oka, Don and Dnieper basins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Rus' were too few and too unfamiliar with the forest to take furs by force, so they dealt with the Slavs largely as equals, which is why the leadership they offered came to be accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["А.Г. Еманов, “Рецензия на книгу: Щавелев А.С. 2020. Хронотоп державы Рюриковичей (911—987). Москва: Аквилон,” <i>Materials in Archaeology and History of Ancient and Medieval Black Sea Region</i>, no. 14 (2022): 435–437, https://doi.org/10.53737/2713-2021.2022.88.33.023. [Open access]","T. L. Vilkul and S. L. Nikolaev, “Rus’ within and without the Lists of Peoples in the Primary Rus’ Chronicle,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (27) (2020): 138–140, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2020.107. [Open access]","Petr S. Stefanovich, “On the Problem of the Name rus’ in the Earliest Rus’ian Chronicles,” <i>Slovene</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 356–357, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.14. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 33, 45, 49, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 385, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do specialists object to the name itself?","a":"It fastens a capital and a nationality onto a tenth-century polity that was pagan, loosely held and not obviously centred anywhere, so the term quietly answers questions about its shape that the sources leave open."},{"q":"Why is the chronicle's list of towns misleading for the 10th century?","a":"It was drawn up around 1100, by which time the map of Rus' towns had settled, and projecting it backwards gives the tenth century places and a coherence it cannot be shown to have had."},{"q":"Why does the fur trade help explain the polity's growth?","a":"Collecting pelts over vast forests and moving them south needed arrangements larger than any single tribe, and the profits gave both the Rus' and the Slav communities a reason to sustain them."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Morgilevsky_-_Isometric_reconstruction_of_St_Sophia_Cathedral_%281926%29.jpg","title":"St Sophia at Kyiv as first built","desc":"An isometric reconstruction drawn in 1926 of the cathedral of St Sophia at Kyiv in its 11th-century form, before the rebuilding that gave it its present outline.","credit":"Ipolit Vladyslavovich Morgilevskyi; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morgilevsky_-_Isometric_reconstruction_of_St_Sophia_Cathedral_%281926%29.jpg","alt":"A line drawing of a large church seen from above and to one side, crowded with small domes and ringed by open arcaded galleries."},"wiki":"Kievan Rus'","places":["pol:rus"]},
    "ru-037": {"questions":["No Scandinavian saga mentions <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, a silence an early critic of the Norman theory called convincing and that nobody has since explained away.","Whole-genome work on a prince who died in 1294 has tied the later house of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to one male lineage, but no remains of his own generation have been recovered."],"abstract":"<b>Rurik</b> is the Varangian prince whom the chronicle compiled at Kyiv makes the founder of the dynasty that ruled the Rus' and the states that grew out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its account has the northern peoples drive their Varangian tribute-takers back over the sea, fall out among themselves, and then send for a prince to rule and judge them according to law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three brothers came with their households and all the Rus' with them: he as the eldest, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Sineus_and_Truvor\">Sineus</span> by the White Lake and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Sineus_and_Truvor\">Truvor</span> at Izborsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One recension seats him first at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Staraya_Ladoga\">Ladoga</span> and has him move to Lake Ilmen only after the other two died, fortifying a small town he called Novgorod; another puts him at Novgorod from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is then said to have shared out lands and towns among his companions, giving Polotsk to one, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Rostov_(Yaroslavl_Oblast)\">Rostov</span> to another and Beloozero to a third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing outside the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Old_East_Slavic\">Slavonic</span> chronicle tradition mentions him, and the absence from Scandinavian writing was pressed against the Norman theory early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A critic of that theory called the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Saga_(Norse_literature)\">sagas</span>' failure a 'convincing silence', pointing out that the same tradition had remembered the far more distant Rollo and forgotten the nearer prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicle was itself compiled more than two centuries after the year it gives, and its author had a dynasty to legitimise as well as traditions to preserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A recent reconstruction accordingly puts the crystallising of the Rurikid core around Kyiv in the first half of the 10th century, a lifetime after the date the chronicle supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whole-genome sequencing of a prince who died in 1294 has established a single male line for the later house, and no material of the earliest generation has been recovered at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. V. Fomin and L. V. Isakova, “Gustav Ewers’ Contribution to the Study of the Initial Period of Ancient Rus’ History,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i> 17, no. 1 (2018): 156, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8674-2018-17-1-151-174. [Open access]","Марта Фонт, “Ранние славяне в Повести временных лет: мифы летописцев и историографии,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 61 (2020): 53, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2020.61.5. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 33, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","K. V. Zhur et al., “The Rurikids: The First Experience of Reconstructing the Genetic Portrait of the Ruling Family of Medieval Rus’ Based on Paleogenomic Data,” <i>Acta Naturae</i> 15, no. 3 (2023): 50–51, https://doi.org/10.32607/actanaturae.23425. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a founder so central to the tradition so poorly attested?","a":"Everything known of him comes from one chronicle tradition written two centuries later, and the peoples he is said to have ruled left no writing of their own, so there is nothing independent to test the story against."},{"q":"Why do the chronicle's two recensions disagree about where he sat?","a":"The surviving manuscripts descend from earlier texts that had already been reworked, and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Staraya_Ladoga\">Ladoga</span> and Novgorod each had reason to be remembered as the first seat, so the variant is evidence about the compilers rather than about him."},{"q":"Why does a study of the dynasty's polity begin in 911 rather than 862?","a":"911 is where dated documentary evidence for the family starts, so a reconstruction that means to rest on records rather than on narrative has to open there and leave the earlier generation aside."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Rurik_%281805%29_2.jpg","title":"Rurik imagined in 1805","desc":"An engraving of 1805 showing Rurik above a plinth inscribed with his name and title; no contemporary description of him survives, so every portrait of him is an invention.","credit":"Anonymous Russian painter; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rurik_%281805%29_2.jpg","alt":"An engraved oval medallion portrait of a bearded man in scale armour and a plumed helmet holding a staff, set above a stone plinth and framed by shields, drums and standards."},"wiki":"Rurik"},
    "ru-038": {"questions":["Behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a lost composition of the 1090s has been reconstructed, and behind that earlier annals again, none of which survives as a manuscript anywhere.","Proving a dynasty of Scandinavian origin legitimate and preserving the traditions of two populations were both purposes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why its early entries need reading twice."],"abstract":"The <b>Primary Chronicle</b>, called in Slavonic the Tale of Bygone Years, is the narrative compiled at Kyiv early in the 12th century on which nearly every account of the early Rus' rests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens outside dated time, dividing the earth among the sons of Noah and describing the road from the Varangians to the Greeks, and only afterwards begins to set down entries year by year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That opening had work to do beyond geography, fitting the enormous territory of the Rus' into a Christian ordering of the world which had never contained it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within the same frame the chronicler places the Rus' in lists of the peoples of the earth, and elsewhere treats them as something standing outside those lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its author had two purposes at once, to prove a dynasty of Scandinavian origin legitimate and to preserve the traditions of the Scandinavian and Slavic populations he wrote among.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Before a critical method for reading medieval narrative was developed at the turn of the 20th century, historians took everything in such texts to be authentic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scholar who laid the foundations of that method in Russia did it on this chronicle, and founded a school of historical philology with textual analysis at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reconstruction set behind the surviving work a lost composition written at Kyiv in the 1090s, and behind that annalistic texts of the middle of the 11th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recovered layers do not say the same thing, for the writers of the 1090s understood the Rus' as a Christian people and a powerful state and tried to extend that identity over local communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earlier texts instead define the Rus' against neighbouring groups, stressing military victories and ignoring religious boundaries, and a late 20th-century hypothesis that set aside both the textual analysis and the archaeology has been judged a blind alley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Petr S. Stefanovich, “On the Problem of the Name rus’ in the Earliest Rus’ian Chronicles,” <i>Slovene</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 356–357, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.14. [Open access]","T. L. Vilkul and S. L. Nikolaev, “Rus’ within and without the Lists of Peoples in the Primary Rus’ Chronicle,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (27) (2020): 138–140, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2020.107. [Open access]","Марта Фонт, “Ранние славяне в Повести временных лет: мифы летописцев и историографии,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 61 (2020): 53, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2020.61.5. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 2, 4–5, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Sergei A. Kozlov, “According to Chronicles, Did the “Route from the Varangians to the Greeks” Go by Sea or Overland?,” <i>Izvestia of the Ural Federal University. Series 2. Humanities and Arts</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 26, https://doi.org/10.15826/izv2.2023.25.2.021. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must the chronicle's early entries be read twice over?","a":"The work preserves genuine tradition and argues a case for a ruling house at the same time, so a reader has to ask of each entry both what it reports and what it is doing for the dynasty."},{"q":"Why are the layers behind it called reconstructions rather than sources?","a":"No manuscript of them exists. They are inferences drawn from the disagreements between this chronicle and the Novgorod tradition, so using them means accepting an argument about how the texts are related."},{"q":"Why does the undated opening matter as much as the dated annals?","a":"It sets the terms the rest of the work uses, placing the Rus' among the peoples of the earth and inside a Christian geography, which decides how every later entry is to be understood."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Laurentian_Codex_27.jpg","title":"The Laurentian Codex, folio 27r","desc":"A page of the manuscript copied in 1377, the oldest surviving witness to the chronicle's text.","credit":"National Library of Russia; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurentian_Codex_27.jpg","alt":"A parchment page closely written in Cyrillic script, with several lines opened by large red initials and a red heading part way down."},"wiki":"Primary Chronicle","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-039": {"questions":["'Our country is great and rich, but there is no order in it': with those words the chronicle opens the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and explains why a foreign prince was wanted.","Three brothers, a division of towns between them and the death of two within two years are all part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no outside source confirms."],"abstract":"The <b>Calling of the Varangians</b> is the chronicle's account, set under the year 862, of how the peoples of the north sent overseas for a prince to rule over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three years earlier, it says, Varangians from beyond the sea had been taking tribute from the Chud, the Slavs, the Merya, the Ves and the Krivichi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those peoples then drove the Varangians back across the sea and stopped paying, but on setting about governing themselves found no justice among them, family quarrelling with family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they resolved to look for a prince who would rule and judge them according to law, and crossed the Varangian Sea to the Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their message, as the chronicle gives it, was that their country was great and rich but had no order in it, and that the Rus' should come and govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three brothers answered, bringing their households and all the Rus' with them, and took seats at Ladoga, by the White Lake and at Izborsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two died within two years, leaving the eldest to take the whole country, and the chronicle adds that the Novgorodians were called Rus' after these Varangians, having been Slavs before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The passage serves its writer's two aims at once, establishing a Scandinavian dynasty's legitimacy while preserving a tradition, and it is exactly the kind of entry the critical method was developed to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is also where the chronicler fixes the place of the Rus' among the peoples of the earth, a question the work returns to both inside and outside its lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dynasty the episode founds went on ruling for seven centuries, and the whole-genome work done on it records that direct evidence for the earliest generation is still lacking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Марта Фонт, “Ранние славяне в Повести временных лет: мифы летописцев и историографии,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 61 (2020): 53, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2020.61.5. [Open access]","K. V. Zhur et al., “The Rurikids: The First Experience of Reconstructing the Genetic Portrait of the Ruling Family of Medieval Rus’ Based on Paleogenomic Data,” <i>Acta Naturae</i> 15, no. 3 (2023): 50–51, https://doi.org/10.32607/actanaturae.23425. [Open access]","T. L. Vilkul and S. L. Nikolaev, “Rus’ within and without the Lists of Peoples in the Primary Rus’ Chronicle,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (27) (2020): 138–140, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2020.107. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a chronicler tell a story of rulers being invited in?","a":"An invitation makes the dynasty's authority a grant from the governed rather than a conquest, which legitimises it; a compiler writing for that house had every reason to shape the tradition that way."},{"q":"Why does the episode begin with an expulsion rather than an arrival?","a":"The sequence matters to the argument: the peoples first prove they can throw the Varangians out, then discover they cannot govern themselves, so the prince is presented as their own remedy for disorder."},{"q":"Why is the detail of the three brothers treated with suspicion?","a":"Two of them die almost at once and leave nothing behind but place names, which is the shape of a story explaining why three seats were remembered rather than of a record of three careers."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Calling_of_varangians_by_A.Krivshenko_%281889%29_color.jpg","title":"The calling imagined in 1889","desc":"A painting of 1889 showing the Varangians received on the shore, made a thousand years after the meeting it imagines.","credit":"Aleksey Kivshenko; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calling_of_varangians_by_A.Krivshenko_%281889%29_color.jpg","alt":"A painting of armed men in cloaks and mail standing on a grassy shore facing a waiting group, with a sailed boat and a wooden town behind."},"wiki":"Calling of the Varangians","places":["pl:q1135534"]},
    "ru-040": {"questions":["The chronicle has the invited prince fortify a small town on the Volkhov and name it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which one recension makes his first seat and another his second.","Imported pottery and tree-ring dates put the earliest layers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> well after the years its own chronicle assigns to the coming of the Rus'."],"abstract":"<b>Novgorod</b> stands where the Volkhov leaves Lake Ilmen, and writers in Old Norse of every kind called it Hólmgarðr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a rendering of a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Proto-Slavic\">Slavonic</span> compound meaning the town on the holm, the low flat bank of a river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The likeliest account of it is that both the Slavonic form and its Norse equivalent belonged first to the large political centre of the 9th and 10th centuries now known as Rurik's Hillfort, and were carried over to the city that grew up beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fortified core of that predecessor, at the mouth of the Volkhov on Lake Ilmen, measured no more than six or seven hectares (15 to 17 acres) in the middle of the 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is a close parallel for such a transfer in the name of the ruined emporium of Hedeby passing to the neighbouring centre at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Schleswig,_Schleswig-Holstein\">Schleswig</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The chronicle has the invited Varangian prince move to Lake Ilmen after his brothers died and fortify a small town there which he called Novgorod, then share out Polotsk, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Rostov_(Yaroslavl_Oblast)\">Rostov</span> and Beloozero among his men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It adds that the Novgorodians came to be called Rus' after those Varangians, having been Slavs before, and that the first inhabitants of the town were Slavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another recension of the same work seats the prince at Novgorod from the start rather than at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Staraya_Ladoga\">Ladoga</span>, so the two texts disagree about the place's first role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The material record is later than either: pottery imported from the west Slavic lands appears here from the 930s, on a chronology built from tree-ring dates at Ladoga together with the finds from the city and the hillfort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Old Rus' memory does place the town in the 10th century, along with Ladoga, Pskov, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Principality_of_Chernigov\">Chernigov</span>, Polotsk and Lyubech, but what relation it then stood in to the princes at Kyiv is not clear from any source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 45, 47–48, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 389, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Aleksandr Musin and Błażej Stanisławski, “Ośrodki węzłowe i kontakty transbałtyckie pomiędzy społecznościami północno-zachodniej Rusi i ziem polskich w X stuleciu,” <i>Przegląd Archeologiczny</i> 70 (2022): 207, https://doi.org/10.23858/PA70.2022.2708. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the city's name older than the city?","a":"A large centre stood a short way upstream through the 9th and 10th centuries, and when the settlement beside it grew into a town the older name came with it, which is why Norse writers apply it to the whole place."},{"q":"Why does the chronicle's date sit awkwardly with the archaeology?","a":"It puts the Varangian prince on the Volkhov in 862, while the datable material from the city itself begins appreciably later, so the entry describes the region's history rather than the city's foundation."},{"q":"Why is the parallel with Hedeby and Schleswig useful here?","a":"It shows the same thing happening elsewhere in the same period: a trading centre fails, a neighbouring place takes over its role, and the old name survives attached to the new town."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/%D0%A0%D1%8E%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_1943.jpg/1920px-%D0%A0%D1%8E%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_1943.jpg","title":"Rurik's Hillfort from the air","desc":"A German aerial photograph of 1943 covering the hillfort upstream of Novgorod and the church of the Saviour at Nereditsa.","credit":"U.S. National Archives and Records Administration; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A0%D1%8E%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_1943.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white vertical aerial photograph of a river winding past open fields, with pale patches of settlement beside it and a marginal data strip below."},"wiki":"Veliky Novgorod","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-041": {"questions":["The chronicle gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the surname of the Seer after he came home from Constantinople with gold and silk, and has him die of a snakebite.","Fifteen envoys sailed to Leo and Alexander in the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, great prince of Rus', and sealed the trade terms that opened Constantinople to northern merchants."],"abstract":"<b>Oleg the Wise</b> is the Varangian ruler whom the Primary Chronicle makes Rurik's kinsman and regent for the boy Igor, and the prince who shifted the centre of Rus' from the Volkhov to the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chronicle sets his arrival at Kyiv in 882 and his death 33 years into his rule, and gives him the epithet the Seer, which Louis Leger's French version renders as the Magician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost nothing about him can be checked outside a compilation put together more than two centuries after the years it dates. One outside trace may exist: a Hebrew letter recovered from the Cairo Geniza names a HLGW, read as Helgo, as king of Rus', whom the Khazars pushed into attacking Byzantium and who later sailed for Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On that reading he was still active in the 940s, campaigning beside Igor rather than a generation before him, and was perhaps eventually ousted by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The chronicle's Oleg takes Smolensk and Lyubech on his way south, then draws Askold and Dir out of Kyiv by sending word that he is a merchant of their own people, and has them cut down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He makes the Polianians and their neighbours pay tribute to him rather than to the Khazars, a silver coin a head, and the chronicle has him declare Kyiv the mother of Rus' towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 907, by the chronicle's reckoning, he brings a fleet to Constantinople and extracts a payment; four years later fifteen named envoys conclude a treaty on his behalf, on terms the chronicle's own later document would not match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What that treaty gave was six months' lodging in the quarter of St Mamas with free board and baths, gear for the return voyage, and exemption from the trade taxes every other merchant paid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ending is folklore rather than record: a seer foretells death from his horse, Oleg keeps clear of the animal for years, laughs over its bare skull, and a viper strikes him from inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 17–18, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 22–25, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 30, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 401, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 407–408, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Arkadiusz Siwko, “A Commonwealth of Interest in the Rus’ian-Byzantine Treaty (ca. 944),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 11 (2021): 407, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.11.20. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Oleg's move to Kyiv treated as the founding act of the Kievan state rather than Rurik's arrival at Novgorod?","a":"Because Kyiv sat where the Dnieper trade to Constantinople could be taxed at one point. The chronicle has Oleg take the tribute the Polianians and their neighbours had paid the Khazars, and four years after his fleet appeared his envoys carried away six months' lodging, board and tax exemption — terms a northern trading town could not have won."},{"q":"Why does so little of Oleg's reign survive outside the chronicle?","a":"Because the compilation that records him was assembled more than two centuries later, and the Greek and Arabic writers who noticed the Rus' cared about fleets and markets rather than who ruled them. The one apparent outside trace, the Geniza letter's HLGW, is a single damaged Hebrew document, and identifying its Helgo with Oleg is itself an argument."},{"q":"Why would a Geniza letter that puts Helgo in the 940s matter for the shape of the chronicle's account?","a":"Because the chronicle arranges Oleg and Igor as predecessor and successor, with Oleg dead about 912. If the same man was still leading Rus' forces in the 940s, the chronicle has stretched two overlapping careers into a tidy sequence of reigns, and its regnal years become a frame imposed later rather than a record kept at the time."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B2%D1%96%D0%BB%D0%BB%D1%96%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BB%D1%96%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B7%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%8F_%D1%96_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3_%28912_%D1%80%D1%96%D0%BA%29.jpg/1280px-%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B2%D1%96%D0%BB%D0%BB%D1%96%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BB%D1%96%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B7%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%8F_%D1%96_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3_%28912_%D1%80%D1%96%D0%BA%29.jpg","title":"Oleg and the horse's skull","desc":"A miniature from the Radziwiłł Chronicle, a late 15th-century copy, illustrating the entry for 912: Oleg's men stand before the horse he was warned of, and he lies stricken at the right.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B2%D1%96%D0%BB%D0%BB%D1%96%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BB%D1%96%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B7%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%8F_%D1%96_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3_%28912_%D1%80%D1%96%D0%BA%29.jpg","alt":"A pen-and-colour drawing of a saddled horse on green ground, a group of robed men beside it, and a figure lying at the far right."},"wiki":"Oleg the Wise","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-042": {"questions":["The chronicle makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> boyars of Rurik who left him for Constantinople, stopped at a stronghold paying tribute to the Khazars, and stayed to rule it.","A fleet of two hundred ships that reached Constantinople in 860 is credited by the chronicle to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the pair it says held Kyiv before Oleg came."],"abstract":"<b>Askold and Dir</b> are the two men the Primary Chronicle names as rulers of Kyiv in the years before Oleg came down the river and killed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It presents them as followers of Rurik who were not of his family, who left Novgorod for Constantinople, and who broke their journey at a fortified hill above the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people there told them the place had been built by three brothers, Kii, Shchek and Khoriv, and that their descendants now paid tribute to the Khazars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine VII, writing in the 950s, calls the town Kioava and adds a second name, Sambatas, that has never been convincingly explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> James Howard-Johnston has suggested that the site began as one of the Khazar forts guarding the river and land routes above the Dnieper rapids, which would fit the tribute the chronicle reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The chronicle then has the pair gather Varangians about them and rule the Polianians while Rurik ruled at Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under a cluster of years in the 860s it credits them with the first Rus' attack on Constantinople, sailing against the Greeks while the emperor Michael was away fighting the Arabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Byzantine writers date that attack to 18 June 860 and put two hundred ships outside the walls, but they name no leader at all, and the chronicle's own year is four to six years out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oleg's approach is a trick: he hides his men in the boats, sends word that merchants of their own race wish to be greeted, and produces the infant Igor as Rurik's son before having the two struck down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle points to their graves, Askold's on a hill by the Dnieper and Dir's behind the church of St Irene, which is the sort of detail a Kyivan monk of about 1113 could check for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 16–17, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 16, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 9, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 388, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Oleksandr Fylypchuk, “The Attack of the Rus’ on Constantinople in the Light of the Chronicon Bruxellense,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 12 (2022): 417, 425–426, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.12.26. [Open access]","Arkadiusz Siwko, “A Commonwealth of Interest in the Rus’ian-Byzantine Treaty (ca. 944),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 11 (2021): 406, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.11.20. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a chronicler writing about 1113 insist on pointing out where Askold and Dir were buried?","a":"Because a grave is the one part of such a story a reader in Kyiv could go and look at. Naming the hill and the church beside each tomb turns a tale about men long dead into a claim about the reader's own city, and lets the chronicle vouch for a line of rulers nothing else recorded."},{"q":"Why do historians doubt that Askold and Dir led the 860 attack on Constantinople?","a":"Because the Byzantine sources that record the attack, and date it to a single day in June 860, name no leader at all, while the chronicle that names the pair dates the raid four to six years late and was compiled two centuries afterwards. The attribution looks like a later fit of a remembered event to known rulers."},{"q":"Why does the tribute the chronicle mentions matter more than the story of the three brothers?","a":"Because it puts Kyiv inside somebody else's empire. If the people on the hill were paying the Khazars, the town was a tributary on the Khazar frontier rather than an independent Slavic settlement, which is why some historians read the site as beginning life as a Khazar fort controlling the river route."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/A04_Smert_Askolda_%26_Dira.jpg/1280px-A04_Smert_Askolda_%26_Dira.jpg","title":"The death of Askold and Dir","desc":"Fyodor Bruni's engraving of the killing of Askold and Dir, from a 19th-century album of scenes from Russian history.","credit":"Fyodor Bruni, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A04_Smert_Askolda_%26_Dira.jpg","alt":"An engraving of armed men striking down two figures on a riverbank, with boats drawn up behind them."},"wiki":"Askold and Dir","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-043": {"questions":["Under the year 6420 the chronicle copies out a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose own dating clause reads September of the fifteenth indiction, which is 911 rather than 912.","The oath closing a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sworn twice over, by the Christians among the envoys on the cross and by the pagans on their weapons and on Perun."],"abstract":"A <b>Rus'–Byzantine Treaty</b> is one of the agreements struck between Kyiv and Constantinople in the 10th century, three of which the Primary Chronicle copies out at length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They reach us only through that chronicle, as Slavonic translations of Greek originals, and how the documents travelled to Kyiv and into a monk's hands is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One sign that the texts really are Byzantine is their dating: they use the September year that the imperial chancery used, where the rest of the chronicle counts from March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, concluded in the name of Oleg, is signed by fifteen envoys with Scandinavian names and addressed to the emperors Leo, Alexander and Constantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own clause dates the peace to September in the fifteenth indiction, which falls in 911, though the chronicle files the whole entry under a year answering to 912.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What the treaties bought was access to a market: six months in the merchants' quarter outside the walls, board, baths, ships' gear for the voyage home, and freedom from trade taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The renewal negotiated by Igor after his fleet was destroyed was harder: a cap of fifty gold pieces on silk bought by any one Rus', no raiding of the Byzantine districts in Crimea, and princely licences in place of the old seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both documents let individual Rus' take service with the emperor, and both oblige the prince to send troops when Constantinople asked, which is how Varangians came to garrison the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The preamble to Igor's treaty names dozens of envoys and merchants, several of them bearing Slavonic rather than Norse names, so the delegation reads as a whole governing class rather than one man's household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oath at the end is sworn twice over, on the cross by the Christians present and on their weapons and on Perun by the rest, a divided settlement written into a single document.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Arkadiusz Siwko, “A Commonwealth of Interest in the Rus’ian-Byzantine Treaty (ca. 944),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 11 (2021): 405–409, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.11.20. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 25, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 30, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 401, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 409, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 129–130, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a treaty copied into a chronicle two centuries later still treated as a genuine document?","a":"Chiefly because of how it counts time. The treaty texts date themselves by the September year used in the imperial chancery, while the chronicle around them counts from March, so the monk who copied them was working from something he had not composed himself. The Scandinavian envoy names and the detailed legal clauses point the same way."},{"q":"Why would Byzantium grant free board and tax exemption to raiders who had just attacked the city?","a":"Because the terms bought something Constantinople wanted more than customs revenue: a northern power obliged to send troops on request, and one that had reason to keep the peace. The privileges were worth a great deal to Rus' merchants at a moment when their trade with the Caliphate was faltering, which made the obligation to supply soldiers cheap to accept."},{"q":"Why does the double oath at the end of a treaty tell us something the rest of the text does not?","a":"Because it shows the Rus' elite already divided in religion decades before any prince was baptised. One embassy held men who would swear on the cross and men who would swear on their weapons and on Perun, and the Byzantine chancery drafted around that rather than insisting on one form. That is evidence about Kyiv rather than about diplomacy."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%8F%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%B8_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BC%3B_%D1%83%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%8E_%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D1%81_%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8.jpg/1280px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"The oath on the treaty","desc":"A pen-and-ink miniature made at the end of the 15th century for the Radziwiłł Chronicle: envoys holding the written terms on the left, and on the right an oath sworn before a red idol of Perun.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%8F%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%B8_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BC%3B_%D1%83%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%8E_%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%9E%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B0_%D1%81_%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8.jpg","alt":"A drawing in two halves — robed men holding out tablets and a scroll, and beside them a figure at a block before a red standing idol."},"places":["pl:q16869"]},
    "ru-044": {"questions":["Romanos had fifteen laid-up galleys fitted with projectors of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at bow, stern and both sides, and sent them against a Rus' fleet many times their number.","Men who swam away from the burning boats told their families that the Greeks possessed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that this was why the Rus' could not beat them."],"abstract":"<b>Greek fire</b> was the Byzantine navy's incendiary weapon, pumped through bronze tubes at enemy hulls and almost impossible to put out once it had taken hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its effect on the Rus' is recorded from both ends of the Black Sea, by a Latin bishop who was in Constantinople and by a monk writing at Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rus' fleets came down on Constantinople three times in the 10th century and the century before it, and the weapon decided the last and largest of those attempts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first attack, on 18 June 860, ended in a storm rather than in flame, and the Byzantine writers who describe it name no Rus' leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second, which the chronicle dates to 907, is doubted altogether: no Byzantine, Latin or Arabic source mentions it, and the account has ships carried overland on wheels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In June 941 the fleet reached the Bosphorus while the imperial navy was away against the Arabs and the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Liudprand of Cremona heard the story from his stepfather, who was in the city on an embassy from Italy at the time, and reports that Romanos had fifteen old galleys dragged out of the yards and fitted with fire projectors at bow, stern and both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Rus' commander ordered his men to take the ships alive rather than sink them; the sea then fell calm, which is what the throwers needed, and the fire went out in every direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Men jumped overboard in armour and drowned, others burned as they swam, and the prisoners taken were beheaded in front of the Italian envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle keeps the Rus' side of it, which is the same scene from the water: a terrible marvel, men swimming for their lives, and survivors telling their households that the Greeks had a fire out of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 129–130, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]","Liudprand of Cremona, <i>Antapodosis</i> 5.15, in <i>The Works of Liudprand of Cremona</i>, trans. F. A. Wright (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1930), 184–186, https://archive.org/details/worksofliudprand00liud. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 32, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 407–408, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Oleksandr Fylypchuk, “The Attack of the Rus’ on Constantinople in the Light of the Chronicon Bruxellense,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 12 (2022): 417, 431–432, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.12.26. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 402, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a weapon that had existed for centuries prove decisive against the Rus' in 941 in particular?","a":"Because the Rus' fought in small shallow boats in close order, which is the one target the projectors could hardly miss, and because the sea happened to be calm, without which the fire could not be thrown accurately. The imperial fleet was away, so the ships sent out were fifteen reconditioned hulks; their advantage was entirely the weapon."},{"q":"Why does Liudprand's account carry more weight than most Latin writing about the Rus'?","a":"Because it reaches us at only one remove from a witness. Liudprand's stepfather was in Constantinople on an embassy when the fleet arrived and saw the captured Rus' executed, and the details he passes on — the number of reconditioned galleys, the placing of the projectors — are what a diplomat there would learn and an outsider would not invent."},{"q":"Why is the raid the chronicle dates to 907 treated as something that never happened?","a":"Because an assault on the capital could not have gone unmentioned by every Byzantine, Latin and Arabic writer, and it does. The chronicle also splits one narrative across entries four years apart and includes ships propelled overland on wheels, so what survives looks like a single tradition about the 911 treaty dressed up as a campaign."}],"wiki":"Greek fire","places":["pl:q16869"]},
    "ru-045": {"questions":["The treaty of 944 was concluded in the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, great prince of Rus', by a delegation of 25 envoys and as many merchants.","Constantine VII, writing in the 950s, calls the ruler at Novgorod the son of Ingor, which is the one contemporary trace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside the chronicle."],"abstract":"<b>Igor of Kiev</b> is the prince the Primary Chronicle places between Oleg and Olga, and the first Rus' ruler whose existence a contemporary outside source confirms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine VII, compiling his handbook of empire in the 950s, writes that the boats coming down to Constantinople set out from Novgorod, where Sviatoslav son of Ingor had his seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle has him succeed on Oleg's death and immediately fight the Drevlians, on whom he imposed a heavier tribute than Oleg had taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One modern reading puts him alongside Oleg for years rather than after him, and has him eventually push his co-ruler aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What the sources agree on is the shape of his reign: two campaigns against Byzantium, a treaty, and a violent death among his own tributaries in the forest country west of Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The fleet he took south in 941 was destroyed by Greek fire, and the chronicle has him send beyond the sea for Varangians to try again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 944 he came with Varangians, Rus', Polianians, Slavs, Krivichians and Tivertsians, took Pecheneg hostages, and reached the Danube with boats and horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There imperial envoys offered the tribute Oleg had taken and more; his commanders advised taking gold and silk without a fight, asking who it was that had the sea as an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The treaty that followed named him great prince of Rus' and made him answerable for its terms, and his embassy balanced 25 merchants against 25 political envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 945 his retinue complained that another commander's men were better clothed than they were; he went twice to the Drevlians for tribute, and at Iskorosten they killed him and the small troop with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 34, 42, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 9, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 74, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 32–33, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Arkadiusz Siwko, “A Commonwealth of Interest in the Rus’ian-Byzantine Treaty (ca. 944),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 11 (2021): 407–409, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.11.20. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 409, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 408, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Igor the first prince of Kyiv whose existence is not in doubt?","a":"Because a Byzantine emperor mentions his son by name and calls him Ingor's son, in a handbook compiled within a few years of the events. Everything known about Oleg and about Rurik comes from a chronicle written two centuries later, whereas Igor is named in a document produced by a court that was dealing with him."},{"q":"Why did Igor accept a payment in 944 instead of attacking Constantinople again?","a":"Because his advisers thought the odds had changed. The chronicle records their reasoning plainly: they could take gold, silver and silk without fighting, and nobody could say who would win a battle fought on the sea, where drowning threatened both sides. Igor had already lost one fleet to Greek fire, which is precisely the argument they were making."},{"q":"Why does the manner of Igor's death say something about how Rus' was governed?","a":"Because it shows tribute was collected in person, repeatedly, and by force. His retinue pressed him to go because another commander's men were better equipped, and he returned a second time with a small escort for more. A state that raised its revenue that way depended on the prince's presence and could be resisted by a single tributary people."}],"image":{"title":"Prince Mal at Korosten","desc":"A modern bronze monument at Korosten — the Iskorosten of the chronicle — to Mal, the Drevlian prince the chronicle says decided that Igor had to be killed. It is a 20th-century statue, not an early object.","credit":"IgorT at Ukrainian Wikipedia, Attribution licence, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragment_of_monument_to_knyaz_Mal_in_Korosten_town%2C_Ukraine.jpg","alt":"A bronze statue of a helmeted warrior in a cloak, seen from below against the sky with trees behind.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Fragment_of_monument_to_knyaz_Mal_in_Korosten_town%2C_Ukraine.jpg/1280px-Fragment_of_monument_to_knyaz_Mal_in_Korosten_town%2C_Ukraine.jpg"},"wiki":"Igor of Kiev","places":["pl:q158904"]},
    "ru-046": {"questions":["The chronicle has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ask the besieged for three pigeons and three sparrows from each house, then tie burning tinder to them and let them fly home.","Two thirds of the tribute imposed after the siege went to Kyiv and a third to Vyshhorod, which the chronicle says was the town of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> herself."],"abstract":"<b>Olga of Kiev</b> governed Rus' for some fifteen years after her husband Igor was killed by the Drevlians in 945, while their son Sviatoslav was a child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle names the men around her, the boy's tutor Asmud and the commander Sveneld, but leaves no doubt who was deciding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her reputation rests on two things that sit oddly together: a revenge story of great cruelty, and the first serious attempt to turn tribute-taking into administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Constantine VII, writing within a few years, confirms that her son held Novgorod while the Kyivan boats came down to Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She was also the first ruler of Rus' to be baptised, which the chronicle places at Constantinople and later tradition made the founding act of Russian Christianity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The revenge occupies three episodes, ending at the Drevlian capital Iskorosten, which she besieged and could not take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She then asked for a token tribute of three pigeons and three sparrows from every household, tied sulphurous tinder to each bird in a scrap of cloth, and released them at dusk to fly back to their nests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town burned from its own dovecotes and eaves; she killed some of the elders, enslaved others, and laid a heavy tribute on the rest, two thirds for Kyiv and a third for her own town of Vyshhorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What she did next is the part that mattered: she went through the Drevlian land with her son and her household establishing dues and laws, and the following year set up trading stations and levies along the Msta on the way to Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her embassy to Constantinople was large enough to speak for the whole of Rus', with 22 princes and 44 merchants represented, and on her return she told the patriarch that her people and her son were still pagan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 42–43, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 46–47, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 47–48, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 409–410, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 9, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 74, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","Boris N. Florya, “Princess Olga and the Byzantine Emperor: Evolution of the Storyline in Russian Historical Tradition of the Sixteenth Century,” <i>Slovene</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 487–488, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.19. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Olga's journey through the Drevlian land treated as more important than the burning of Iskorosten?","a":"Because it changed how Rus' was run. Igor had died collecting tribute in person, twice in one year, from people who could refuse. Fixing dues and setting up stations and levies on the route north replaced that with something a ruler need not be present for, and it is the earliest thing in the chronicle resembling administration."},{"q":"Why should the story of the pigeons and sparrows be read with caution?","a":"Because the same device appears in saga and in other medieval literature, and the chronicle is telling it two centuries later about a woman it venerates as a saint. Its function in the text is to complete a sequence of three revenges and to show cunning triumphing over numbers, which is a literary shape rather than a siege report."},{"q":"Why did Olga's own baptism not make Rus' a Christian country?","a":"Because the decision was hers and reached no further. She told the patriarch on her return that her people and her son were pagan, and Sviatoslav's whole career was fought as a pagan prince. Conversion in Rus' followed from a ruler imposing it a generation later, not from a personal one made at Constantinople."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/A09_Mshenie_Olgi.jpg/1280px-A09_Mshenie_Olgi.jpg","title":"Olga's vengeance","desc":"Fyodor Bruni's engraving of Olga's vengeance on the Drevlian envoys, tipped out of the boat they had arrived in, from a 19th-century album of scenes from Russian history.","credit":"Fyodor Bruni, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A09_Mshenie_Olgi.jpg","alt":"An engraving of a large boat being upended inside a timber hall, with men falling out of it and onlookers on either side."},"wiki":"Olga of Kiev","places":["pl:q1004257"]},
    "ru-047": {"questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lists gifts down to five silver coins for the men of Sviatoslav, it shows what her embassy contained without saying which year it came.","A reception held on 9 September and another on 18 October are set out in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the court handbook compiled under Constantine VII."],"abstract":"<b>De Ceremoniis</b> is the handbook of Byzantine court ceremonial assembled under Constantine VII, and its second book contains the one contemporary account of Olga's visit to Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two receptions of the archontissa of Rus' are described: she entered with her kinswomen and her senior attendants, stood where envoys were questioned, and her merchants and envoys waited below the curtains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gift list is exact to the coin, thirty silver pieces for her uncle, twelve for each of her twenty envoys, eight for the priest Gregory, and five each for the men of Sviatoslav.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the chapter does not contain is any mention of her being baptised, or any year at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does give the weekdays, the September reception falling on a Wednesday and the October one on a Sunday, and that pair of coincidences is most of the evidence for which year she came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The chronicle, by contrast, makes the baptism the point of the journey and dates it to a year answering to 955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its telling the emperor admires her, says she is fit to reign with him, and she answers that if he wishes to baptise her he must do it himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was given the name Helena after the mother of Constantine the Great; when the emperor then proposed marriage she replied that he had made himself her godfather, and he said she had outwitted him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicle branches cannot even agree which emperor this was, and Leger's own edition notes a manuscript that puts John Tzimiskes there, a man who did not reach the throne until 969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Modern accounts divide between 946 and 957 for the visit, and read the marriage exchange as folklore attached later to a real embassy whose business was the alliance rather than the font.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae</i> 2.15, in <i>Constantini Porphyrogeniti Imperatoris De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae libri duo</i>, ed. Johann Jacob Reiske (Bonn: Weber, 1829), 594–598, https://archive.org/details/decerimoniisaul01reisgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 409–410, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 47–48, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Boris N. Florya, “Princess Olga and the Byzantine Emperor: Evolution of the Storyline in Russian Historical Tradition of the Sixteenth Century,” <i>Slovene</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 488–489, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.19. [Open access]","Boris N. Florya, “Princess Olga and the Byzantine Emperor: Evolution of the Storyline in Russian Historical Tradition of the Sixteenth Century,” <i>Slovene</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 490–492, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.19. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 130, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the silence of De Ceremoniis about a baptism a problem rather than an oversight?","a":"Because the chapter is exhaustive about everything else. It records where the princess stood, which curtains were raised, and payments down to five silver coins for individual servants. A ceremony as significant as the baptism of a foreign ruler, with the emperor himself as godfather, is not what a document of that grain omits by accident."},{"q":"Why do two calendar dates without a year still narrow down when Olga came?","a":"Because the chapter gives the days of the week as well as the dates, and 9 September and 18 October fall on those weekdays only in particular years. That is the whole of the evidence for the date, which is why the argument has settled on two candidates rather than one and cannot be closed from this source alone."},{"q":"Why can the chronicle's account of the baptism not simply be added to what the handbook says?","a":"Because the two are different kinds of text. The handbook is a protocol drafted for reuse; the chronicle's version is a story told to a purpose, complete with a proposal of marriage and a witty refusal, and its own branches disagree about which emperor was present — one of them naming a man who became emperor twelve years later."}],"image":{"title":"Christ crowning Constantine VII","desc":"A carved ivory panel in the Pushkin Museum showing Christ crowning Constantine VII, the emperor in whose reign the court handbook was compiled.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porphyrogenetus.jpg","alt":"A tall carved ivory panel under an arch: a haloed figure laying a hand on the crown of an emperor in a patterned robe, with Greek lettering between them.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Porphyrogenetus.jpg"},"wiki":"De Ceremoniis","places":["pl:q16869"]},
    "ru-048": {"questions":["Leo the Deacon saw <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come to terms sitting on the thwart of a boat, in a plain white shirt with a single gold earring and a lock of hair on one side.","Pechenegs ambushed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on his way home from the Danube, killed him, and made a drinking cup out of his skull mounted in metal."],"abstract":"<b>Sviatoslav I</b> ruled Rus' from his mother's regency until 972 and spent almost the whole of it on campaign, from the middle Volga to the lower Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle's portrait of him is the most vivid in the whole compilation: moving like a leopard at the head of his army, carrying neither wagon nor cooking pot, eating thin slices of horse or game grilled on the coals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He slept under a cloak with a saddle for a pillow and his men did the same, and he sent word ahead to those he meant to attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine VII, writing while he was still a boy, records him at Novgorod as the son of Ingor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His men appear again in the Byzantine court record of his mother's embassy, receiving five silver coins each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 965 he went against the Khazars, beat their army and took the fortress the chronicle calls Belaya Vezha, and the khaganate did not recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two years later he crossed the Danube, took eighty towns from the Bulgarians and settled at Pereyaslavets, telling his mother that Kyiv did not please him because the centre of his lands lay on the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Leo the Deacon, who wrote in Constantinople within living memory, describes him at the peace of 971: shaven but for a long moustache, his head bare except for one lock, a gold earring with two pearls, and a white shirt no cleaner than his men's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He spoke a few words about terms from the thwart of a small boat and rowed away, which is the only description of a ruler of Rus' by anyone who could have seen him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Going home in 972 he ignored advice to ride round the Dnieper rapids, wintered short of food where a horse's head cost half a grivna, and was killed there by the Pecheneg prince Kurya, who had his skull mounted as a cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 49–50, 59–60, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 9, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 74, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae</i> 2.15, in <i>Constantini Porphyrogeniti Imperatoris De cerimoniis aulae Byzantinae libri duo</i>, ed. Johann Jacob Reiske (Bonn: Weber, 1829), 597, https://archive.org/details/decerimoniisaul01reisgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 50, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 53–54, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Leo the Deacon, <i>Historiae libri decem</i> 9.11, in <i>Leonis Diaconi Caloensis Historiae libri decem</i>, ed. Carl Benedict Hase (Bonn: Weber, 1828), 157, https://archive.org/details/leonisdiaconica00liudgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did breaking Khazar power leave Rus' worse off in the long run?","a":"Because the khaganate had held the steppe corridor between the Rus' forests and the Black Sea. With it gone, nothing stood between Kyiv and the Pechenegs, and later the Cumans, who could cut the Dnieper route at the rapids. Sviatoslav himself was killed there within seven years of the campaign, which is the point made in miniature."},{"q":"Why is Leo the Deacon's description of Sviatoslav unusual among sources for early Rus'?","a":"Because it is the only physical account of a Rus' ruler by someone placed to have seen him or spoken to men who had. Leo wrote at Constantinople within living memory of 971, and what he records — the shaven head, the single earring, the plain shirt — no chronicle written in Kyiv two centuries later could supply."},{"q":"Why would a prince of Kyiv say that the centre of his lands lay on the Danube?","a":"Because that is where the trade he lived on converged. He named what came to Pereyaslavets: silver, cloth, fruit and wine from Byzantium, silver and horses from Bohemia and Hungary, and from Rus' itself furs, wax, honey and slaves. Kyiv was the northern end of one route; a Danube town commanded several at once."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Lebedev_Svyatoslavs_meeting_with_Emperor_John.jpg","title":"Sviatoslav meets the emperor","desc":"Klavdiy Lebedev's painting of Sviatoslav's meeting with John Tzimiskes on the Danube in 971, composed from Leo the Deacon's account of it.","credit":"Klavdy Lebedev, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lebedev_Svyatoslavs_meeting_with_Emperor_John.jpg","alt":"A painting of a bare-headed man in a plain white shirt seated in a small boat with an oar, facing a crowned emperor in gilded armour with a mounted retinue on the bank."},"wiki":"Sviatoslav I","places":["pl:q4163154"]},
    "ru-049": {"questions":["Because the lower Don had no stone fit for building, the officer sent to raise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made kilns, fired brick in them, and burned lime from fine river gravel.","From the Danube to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reckoned a journey of sixty days, which is how the handbook of empire measured the western edge of Khazar power."],"abstract":"<b>Sarkel</b> was a brick fortress on the lower Don, raised for the Khazars by a Byzantine military mission and destroyed by the Rus' a century and a half later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine VII tells the story of its building: Khazar envoys asked the emperor Theophilos for the fort, and the officer Petronas Kamateros was sent with imperial warships to make it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He put in at Cherson, ferried his workmen up to a place on the Don, and finding no stone fit for the job built kilns, fired brick in them and burned lime from the finest river gravel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His handbook also measures the distance: from the Danube to Sarkel is sixty days' travelling, with the Dniester and the Dnieper the greatest of the rivers between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is usually taken to mean white house or white tower, which the Slavonic Belaya Vezha of the chronicle translates exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The forts of that programme were meant to impress as much as to defend, advertising Khazar power to everyone who passed and holding down the peoples guarding the northern approaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The request for engineers came to Constantinople some time around 832, and Byzantine influence shows at several sites of the same period in brick sizes and in the units used for cutting stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 965, by the chronicle's reckoning, Sviatoslav marched against the Khazars, met their army under its khagan, beat it and took Belaya Vezha, going on to defeat the Yasians and the Kasogians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That campaign is treated as the moment Khazar power broke; the fall of the capital on the Volga seems to have brought the whole structure down, perhaps helped by an attack from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On his way south Sviatoslav had asked the Vyatichi of the Oka whom they paid, and was told the Khazars took a silver coin from every plough — the kind of revenue that the fall of the fort and of the capital together brought to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 42, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 178–179, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 50, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 411, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 388, 395, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 49–50, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would the Khazars ask a foreign power to build their frontier fortress?","a":"Because what they wanted was not a wall but expertise, and the psychological effect of visible foreign backing. Byzantium had the engineers; the Khazars had a vulnerable salient on the Don and northern neighbours they wished to deter. A brick fort raised by imperial officers advertised an alliance as much as it closed a river crossing."},{"q":"Why does the Slavonic name Belaya Vezha make the identification with Sarkel reasonably secure?","a":"Because it is a translation rather than a similar-sounding word. Sarkel is generally read as white house or white tower, and Belaya Vezha means exactly that in Slavonic, so the chronicle appears to be rendering a name its writers understood. A fortress of fired white brick on the lower Don fits both the name and the emperor's account."},{"q":"Why was taking one fortress enough to break a state as large as the khaganate?","a":"It was not, on its own. Sarkel was a frontier work; what ended Khazar power was losing the capital on the Volga in the same campaign, and with it the tributary peoples held in place by fear of it. The eastern shield had already weakened, and the Rus' rising may have been concerted with an attack from that side."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_1943.jpg/1280px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_1943.jpg","title":"The Left-Bank Tsimlyansk site","desc":"The Left-Bank Tsimlyansk fortified site on the lower Don and the country round it, photographed from the air in 1943; this is the site usually identified with Sarkel.","credit":"U.S. National Archives and Records Administration, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_1943.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white vertical aerial photograph of flat field patterns beside a river, with the film's edge markings down one side."},"wiki":"Sarkel","places":["pl:q1068190"]},
    "ru-050": {"questions":["Silver and horses from Bohemia and Hungary, wine and cloth from Byzantium, and furs, wax, honey and slaves from Rus' all converged on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by his own account of it.","Nikephoros Phokas paid the Rus' to attack Bulgaria in 968; a year later they came back on their own and made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the seat of a Danube dominion."],"abstract":"<b>Pereyaslavets</b> is the Danube town that Sviatoslav made his seat between 967 and 971, and it is the nearest thing Rus' had to a second capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle says he crossed the Danube against the Bulgarians, beat them, took eighty towns and settled there as prince, taking tribute from the Greeks as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked by his dying mother why he would not stay in Kyiv, he answered that he did not like it and that the centre of his lands was on the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then listed what came there: silver, cloth, fruit and wine from Byzantium, silver and horses from Bohemia and Hungary, and from Rus' itself furs, wax, honey and slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its site is not settled, and the two traditions do not even share a name: the chronicle writes Pereyaslavets where the Byzantine account of the war calls the city Preslav.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The first campaign was Byzantine business: Nikephoros Phokas paid the Rus' to attack Bulgaria in 968, and the following year they returned on their own account and stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Danube base gave them an artery of trade and a lever against Constantinople, which is why the empire's answer, when it came, was the emperor in person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> John Tzimiskes stormed the Bulgarian capital in 971 and then shut the Rus' up in Dorostolon on the river, where the fighting ended in a negotiated surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its terms were that the Rus' give up the town, return their prisoners, keep out of Bulgaria and go home, in exchange for safe passage, grain and the old trading rights at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Leo the Deacon reports two measures of grain handed to each man leaving, and says the 22,000 who took it were what remained of 60,000; Tzimiskes renamed the town Theodoropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 50, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 53–54, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 411, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","Leo the Deacon, <i>Historiae libri decem</i> 9.10, in <i>Leonis Diaconi Caloensis Historiae libri decem</i>, ed. Carl Benedict Hase (Bonn: Weber, 1828), 155–156, https://archive.org/details/leonisdiaconica00liudgoog. [Open access]","Leo the Deacon, <i>Historiae libri decem</i> 9.11–9.12, in <i>Leonis Diaconi Caloensis Historiae libri decem</i>, ed. Carl Benedict Hase (Bonn: Weber, 1828), 156–158, https://archive.org/details/leonisdiaconica00liudgoog. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 130, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]","Leo the Deacon, <i>Historiae libri decem</i> 8.8–9, in <i>Leonis Diaconi Caloensis Historiae libri decem</i>, ed. Carl Benedict Hase (Bonn: Weber, 1828), 139, https://archive.org/details/leonisdiaconica00liudgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a prince of Kyiv prefer a small Danube town to the city he had inherited?","a":"Because of where the goods met. Kyiv sat at the northern end of one route to Constantinople; a town on the lower Danube stood where the Byzantine trade, the central European trade in silver and horses, and the Rus' trade in furs and slaves converged. He called it the centre of his lands, which is commerce rather than sentiment."},{"q":"Why did Byzantium destroy a Rus' position on the Danube it had paid to create?","a":"Because the point of hiring the Rus' in 968 was to punish Bulgaria, not to replace it with a stronger neighbour. When they came back a year later on their own account and stayed, the empire faced a power on the Danube able to press it directly and reach into central Europe. Hence the emperor taking personal command."},{"q":"Why is it significant that nobody can say for certain where Pereyaslavets stood?","a":"Because it shows how much of this period rests on a single narrative. A town described as the centre of a prince's dominion, held four years and fought over by an emperor, has no securely identified site — the chronicle names it, the Byzantine accounts describe the war, and the two do not even agree on its name."}],"image":{"title":"The fortress at Durostorum","desc":"The excavated stone foundations of the Danube fortress at Durostorum, the Dorostolon where the war of 971 ended, with the river behind them.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortress_on_the_Danube_at_Durostorum_%28Moesia_Inferior%29%2C_Silistra%2C_Bulgaria%29%2C_%2841160582835%29.jpg","alt":"Low stone wall foundations among grass on a riverbank, with the broad Danube and a wooded far shore behind.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Fortress_on_the_Danube_at_Durostorum_%28Moesia_Inferior%29%2C_Silistra%2C_Bulgaria%29%2C_%2841160582835%29.jpg/1280px-Fortress_on_the_Danube_at_Durostorum_%28Moesia_Inferior%29%2C_Silistra%2C_Bulgaria%29%2C_%2841160582835%29.jpg"},"wiki":"Pereyaslavets","places":["pl:q182428"]},
    "ru-051": {"questions":["The chronicle gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> twelve sons and settles each in a town, then complains that too few fortified places stood between Kyiv and the Pecheneg steppe.","A German bishop writing within a decade of his death called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an immense and cruel fornicator who took the faith at his Greek wife's urging and adorned it with no just works."],"abstract":"<b>Vladimir the Great</b> was prince of Kyiv from 980 to 1015, and the ruler under whom Rus' became a Christian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was a younger son of Sviatoslav, held Novgorod while his brothers held Kyiv and the Drevlian land, fled to Scandinavia when the eldest killed the second, and came back with Varangian troops to take Kyiv and kill Yaropolk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle marks his sole rule by the idols he raised behind the palace yard: a wooden Perun with a silver head and a golden beard, and beside him Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog, Simargl and Mokosh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also counts his household with disapproval, giving him twelve sons by several wives and hundreds of concubines at Vyshgorod, Belgorod and Berestovo, and then calls him wise all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thietmar of Merseburg, a bishop writing while Vladimir's sons were still fighting over the inheritance, put it less gently and named him an immense and cruel fornicator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What he did with those sons mattered more than their number, because he planted them as governors rather than leaving them at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vysheslav went to Novgorod, Izyaslav to Polotsk, Svyatopolk to Turov and Yaroslav to Rostov, and when the eldest died the whole arrangement shifted, Yaroslav moving to Novgorod and Boris to Rostov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He built fortified towns along the Desna, the Oster, the Trubezh, the Sula and the Stugna and peopled them from the Slavs, the Krivichi, the Chud and the Vyatichi, because the Pecheneg war ran the whole length of his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conversion of 988 or 989 brought Greek clergy, a stone cathedral, a tithe on his own revenues and a marriage into the imperial house, and Thietmar says the faith came at his wife's urging rather than his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died at Berestovo on 15 July 1015 and his household hid the death, which bought Svyatopolk the days he needed to seize Kyiv and begin killing his brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 64–65, 100, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Oleg V. Kutarev, “Dazhbog: The Ancient Slavic Pagan Deity of the Shining Sky,” <i>Studia mythologica Slavica</i> 24 (2021): 196, https://doi.org/10.3986/SMS20212411. [Open access]","Thietmar of Merseburg, <i>Chronicon</i> 8.72–73, in <i>Thietmari Merseburgensis episcopi Chronicon</i>, ed. Friedrich Kurze, Scriptores rerum Germanicarum in usum scholarum (Hanover: Hahn, 1889), 236–237, https://archive.org/details/thietmarimersebu00thieuoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 108, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Jan Máchal, “Slavic Mythology,” in <i>The Mythology of All Races</i>, vol. 3, <i>Celtic, Slavic</i>, ed. Louis Herbert Gray (Boston: Marshall Jones, 1918), 293–294, https://archive.org/details/mythologyofallra31gray. [Open access]","Paul Babie, “All Roads Lead to New Rome: The Canonical Origins and Status of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic Churches of Ukraine,” <i>Ecclesiastical Law Journal</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 225, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956618X23000066. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicle record Vladimir's concubines and then still call him wise?","a":"Because the entry was written by monks more than a century later, who wanted both the scandal of the pagan prince and the glory of the Christian one. The excess belongs to the man before his baptism and makes the change afterwards look larger."},{"q":"Why did Vladimir plant his sons in towns rather than keep them at Kyiv?","a":"Because Rus' had no machinery for governing at a distance. A son in Novgorod or Polotsk collected tribute, held the river route and answered to Kyiv, which is why the chronicle records each posting by name. The same arrangement made his death a succession war."},{"q":"Why would a German bishop write so harshly about a prince he never met?","a":"Because Thietmar's information came through Poland, where Vladimir was an enemy, and because a convert who kept his old household was exactly the figure a reforming churchman would use as a warning. The hostility is evidence of how Rus' looked from the Latin west."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/%D0%A1%D1%80%D1%96%D0%B1%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%88%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BF%D1%83.jpg","title":"A srebrennik of Vladimir","desc":"Both faces of a silver coin of the first type struck for Vladimir, with Christ enthroned on one side and the prince enthroned with a trident and a cross on the other.","credit":"Anaharsiss, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A1%D1%80%D1%96%D0%B1%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%88%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BF%D1%83.jpg","alt":"Two small irregular silver coins photographed side by side, each stamped with a seated figure inside a ring of beads."},"wiki":"Vladimir the Great","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-052": {"questions":["The Igor Tale calls the people of Rus' the grandchildren of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the strongest evidence that the deity was thought of as an ancestor rather than only a power.","One reading derives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a Slavic word for day and makes the name a cognate of the Indo-European sky father rather than a plea to a giving god."],"abstract":"<b>Dazhbog</b> is one of the six deities the Primary Chronicle says Vladimir set up on the hill behind his palace yard at Kyiv in 980, and the best attested of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The list names a wooden Perun with a silver head and a golden beard, then Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog, Simargl and Mokosh, and says sacrifices were made to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the only description of a pre-Christian sanctuary in Rus' that survives, and it is very short: the churchman who wrote it saw no reason to explain gods his readers still knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The set is not one people's, either, since the most accepted etymology of Khors is North Iranian, a Sarmatian and Alanian form meaning sun king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eight years after the list the same chronicle has Vladimir order the idols thrown down, some chopped and some burnt, and after the dragging of Perun it never mentions five of the six again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Dazhbog is the exception, and he returns by a strange route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the year 1114 the Hypatian manuscript breaks off from rains of glass beads to retell a translated Greek chronograph in which a smith called Pheosta, whom the Egyptians name Svarog, sees tongs fall from heaven, and is succeeded by the Sun king, Svarog's son, or Dazhbog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scribe had folded a Slavic name into a Byzantine story about Hephaestus, which is why the one deity with a family in the record owes it to a copyist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is usually read as a plea, Dazhd-bog or God grant, but that spelling with two letter d's appears in a small minority of manuscripts and in none of the Laurentian editions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The alternative takes the stem from a Slavic word for day, which makes Dazhbog a shining sky father rather than a giver, leaves Khors as the solar deity and Svarog as the fiery one, and fits the Igor Tale calling the Rus' his grandchildren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Oleg V. Kutarev, “Dazhbog: The Ancient Slavic Pagan Deity of the Shining Sky,” <i>Studia mythologica Slavica</i> 24 (2021): 196–197, 205, https://doi.org/10.3986/SMS20212411. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 64, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Oleg V. Kutarev, “Dazhbog: The Ancient Slavic Pagan Deity of the Shining Sky,” <i>Studia mythologica Slavica</i> 24 (2021): 210–212, https://doi.org/10.3986/SMS20212411. [Open access]","Jan Máchal, “Slavic Mythology,” in <i>The Mythology of All Races</i>, vol. 3, <i>Celtic, Slavic</i>, ed. Louis Herbert Gray (Boston: Marshall Jones, 1918), 293–294, https://archive.org/details/mythologyofallra31gray. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 290, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the chronicle's list of gods so short?","a":"Because it was written by a churchman for readers who still knew the names, and who needed no instruction in a religion the entry exists to condemn. He recorded what the idols were made of and left out what they meant."},{"q":"Why does the 1114 entry matter more than its subject suggests?","a":"Because it is the only place any of the six gods is given a relationship. Everything else is a bare name in a list, so the whole idea of a Slavic pantheon with fathers and sons rests on one scribe's borrowing from a Greek chronograph."},{"q":"Why does the spelling of the name decide what the name means?","a":"Because a d in the middle makes the first half an imperative of the verb to give, and its absence allows a stem meaning day instead. One reading gives a god who grants and the other a sky father, and the manuscripts mostly lack the d."}],"wiki":"Dazhbog","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-053": {"questions":["Tradition did not treat the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a single moment at all, and later compilations counted as many as six separate stages spread across two centuries.","Long after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the old worship survived: one account has the cult of the thunder god disappearing only at the end of the eleventh century."],"abstract":"The <b>Christianisation of Kievan Rus'</b> is the adoption of Byzantine Christianity as the religion of the Kyivan state under Vladimir, conventionally dated to 988 or 989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle stages it as a decision: Vladimir returns from Kherson with his Greek wife, Anastasius and priests, and at once orders the idols thrown down, some to be chopped and some burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Perun was treated differently, tied to a horse's tail, dragged down to the stream and beaten by twelve men with rods, not because wood feels pain but so that the demon should be disgraced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pagans wept as it went, and Vladimir had it pushed off the banks until it passed the rapids, where it grounded on sands afterwards called Perun's Sands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the site where the idol had stood he built a church of St Basil, and the people of Kyiv were baptised in the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Novgorod was handled the same way a year later, its idols destroyed and its own Perun thrown into the Volkhov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difficulty is that a decree is not a conversion, and the same account admits that the worship of Perun did not finally disappear from the land until the end of the eleventh century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later tradition was even less tidy, and did not count one baptism at all: compilers assembled a series of separate conversions of Rus' spread over two centuries, at one point as many as six.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the conversion did create quickly was an institution, since Constantinople treated the eastern Slavic lands as a single missionary province under a metropolitan of its own appointing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within two generations that church had produced its own writers, and Ilarion's sermon compared Vladimir to Constantine the Great and named him the teacher of a land known to the ends of the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Paul Babie, “All Roads Lead to New Rome: The Canonical Origins and Status of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic Churches of Ukraine,” <i>Ecclesiastical Law Journal</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 225, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956618X23000066. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 95–96, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Jan Máchal, “Slavic Mythology,” in <i>The Mythology of All Races</i>, vol. 3, <i>Celtic, Slavic</i>, ed. Louis Herbert Gray (Boston: Marshall Jones, 1918), 294–295, https://archive.org/details/mythologyofallra31gray. [Open access]","Dmitrii M. Bulanin, “Paralipomenon by Joannes Zonaras and the First Baptism of Rus,” <i>Slovene</i> 10, no. 1 (2021): 72–73, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2021.10.1.5. [Open access]","Paul Babie, “All Roads Lead to New Rome: The Canonical Origins and Status of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic Churches of Ukraine,” <i>Ecclesiastical Law Journal</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 225–226, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956618X23000066. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 64–66, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vladimir have the Perun idol beaten rather than simply burnt?","a":"Because the point was public humiliation of a power people still believed in. Burning would have destroyed the image quietly; dragging and beating it through Kyiv showed a crowd that the god could not defend itself, which is the argument the chronicle says was intended."},{"q":"Why does the tradition count several baptisms of Rus' instead of one?","a":"Because separate stories had accumulated — a mission under Photius, a miracle of the Gospel, Olga's baptism, Vladimir's — and medieval compilers saw no reason to choose. A repeated mercy looked to them like providence favouring a chosen people rather than a weak sacrament."},{"q":"Why is the late eleventh century, not 988, the honest end of Rus' paganism?","a":"Because the decree changed what the prince and his church did, not what villages did. The same sources that record the baptism also record the old worship continuing for roughly a century, which is what a conversion from above looks like from below."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/40/Fedor_Bronnikov_003.jpg","title":"Vladimir's baptism imagined","desc":"A nineteenth-century painting of Vladimir's baptism, set in a Byzantine church with the imperial party looking on from the left.","credit":"Fyodor Bronnikov, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fedor_Bronnikov_003.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a bearded man in a white robe holding a candle before a bishop in a gold cope, in a crowded domed church."},"wiki":"Christianization of Kievan Rus'","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-054": {"questions":["An imperial handbook written a generation earlier forbade marriage into the northern nations, which is what made the match between <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a prince of Kyiv extraordinary.","The Greek record has Basil II buying an army with the hand of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the chronicle explains the conversion as a reasoned comparison of four religions."],"abstract":"<b>Anna Porphyrogenita</b> was a purple-born sister of the emperors Basil II and Constantine VIII, the princess whose marriage sealed the conversion of Rus', and she died in Kyiv between 1008 and 1011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The chronicle tells it as an enquiry, with Muslim Bulgars, Germans sent from Rome, Jews and Greeks each setting out their faith before Vladimir, who sent ten men to watch them worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His envoys came back saying that among the Bulgars there was no gladness and among the Germans nothing beautiful, and that in the Greek church they had not known whether they stood in heaven or earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His boyars settled it from family rather than doctrine: if the Greek religion were bad, his grandmother Olga, the wisest of all, would not have taken it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next chapter is less serene, because Vladimir besieged Kherson, cut its water supply on the advice of an arrow shot from the wall, and then told the emperors he would treat their capital as he had their famous city unless they sent their virgin sister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They answered that Christians may not marry pagans, so he must be baptised first, and only then persuaded Anna, who said she was going into slavery and would sooner die at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greek record is blunter, and has Basil II, facing rebellion by the Phokades and Skleroi, buy six thousand elite troops from Vladimir with the hand of a purple-born princess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those troops won at Abydos in April 989 and became the emperor's guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What made it remarkable is that her own grandfather had written the rule against it, warning his son to reject any such request from the infidel and dishonoured northern nations, on the authority of an ordinance said to stand on the altar of St Sophia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It excepted only the Franks and called anyone who broke the rule an alien from the catalogue of Christians, explaining away its one admitted breach by saying the emperor responsible had been unlearned and illiterate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 69, 89–90, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 91, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 128, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 135, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 13, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 86–87, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, <i>De administrando imperio</i> 13, in <i>De thematibus et de administrando imperio</i>, ed. Immanuel Bekker (Bonn: Weber, 1840), 88–89, https://archive.org/details/dethematibusetd01bekkgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 108, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the emperors part with a purple-born princess at all?","a":"Because Basil II was facing a rebellion by two of the greatest families of Asia Minor and needed troops more than he needed the rule. The six thousand Rus' who arrived won at Abydos and stayed as his guard, which was worth breaking a precedent for."},{"q":"Why does the chronicle tell a story about comparing religions instead of the bargain?","a":"Because it was written in Kyiv, for readers whose church rested on Vladimir's choice. A prince who weighed four faiths and picked the beautiful one is a better founder than one who took a wife as payment for an army."},{"q":"Why was marrying a Byzantine princess worth a siege and a baptism?","a":"Because the empire refused such marriages on principle, so obtaining one placed a Kyivan prince inside the imperial family and above every other ruler on his frontiers. The prohibition is what gave the prize its value."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B9_II_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD_VIII_%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%90%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%83_%D0%B2%D1%8B%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"The emperors persuade Anna","desc":"A miniature from the Radziwiłł Chronicle in which two crowned emperors sit on either side of their crowned sister and gesture as they argue her into the marriage.","credit":"Radziwiłł Chronicle, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B9_II_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD_VIII_%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%90%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%83_%D0%B2%D1%8B%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"A manuscript illumination of three crowned figures seated under arcades, the two outer ones reaching towards the woman in the middle."},"wiki":"Anna Porphyrogenita","places":["pl:q638445"]},
    "ru-055": {"questions":["Bronze horses looted from Kherson still stood before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a century later, and the chronicle notes that the ignorant took them for marble.","The gift that named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written as an oath with a curse attached, and Vladimir laid the document inside the building."],"abstract":"The <b>Church of the Tithes</b> was Vladimir's cathedral church at Kyiv, and it takes its name from the endowment he settled on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle says he resolved to build a stone church to the Virgin, sent to Greece for architects, decorated it with paintings, and put it in the charge of Anastasius of Kherson with priests from that city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything carried off from Kherson went into it, the images, the vessels and the crosses, and the bronze horses stood before the church long enough for the chronicler to note that ignorant people thought them marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seeing the building complete, he prayed, and then said that he gave to this church of the holy Mother of God the tenth part of his goods and of his towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote a curse against anyone who broke the oath, laid it inside the church, handed the tithe to Anastasius, and feasted his boyars and the town elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A later reader made the arrangement legal rather than pious, because the vow is repeated in the document known as Vladimir's Church Ordinance, which binds his successors to keep its provisions and claims to follow the councils and the laws of the Greek emperors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That text said what the church was to govern as well as what it was to receive, giving it jurisdiction over family matters, sacrilege, heresy, morality and offences against women's honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its oldest copy is bound into the same late thirteenth-century Novgorodian collection that carries the oldest copy of the Russkaya Pravda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not the only foundation, and a second church, dedicated to St Basil, went up where the Perun idol had stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle keeps using the name, burying a prince in 1093 in the church of the Mother of God called Desyatinnaya, and Anastasius, the man entrusted with the tithe, ended as the Polish king's treasurer after Bolesław took Kyiv in 1018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 96, 101, 103, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 165, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 165–166, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Jan Máchal, “Slavic Mythology,” in <i>The Mythology of All Races</i>, vol. 3, <i>Celtic, Slavic</i>, ed. Louis Herbert Gray (Boston: Marshall Jones, 1918), 294, https://archive.org/details/mythologyofallra31gray. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 121, 189, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vladimir attach a curse to the endowment instead of just granting it?","a":"Because there was no institution that could hold a prince's successors to a grant. A written anathema deposited in the church put the obligation under a sanction the next ruler would recognise, which is the same reason his Church Ordinance binds his heirs by name."},{"q":"Why does it matter that the oldest copy of the Ordinance sits beside the oldest Pravda?","a":"Because it shows the two earliest Rus' codes were preserved by the same hands, in a church legal collection. The law of the land and the law of the church grew up in the same ground and were copied by the same clergy."},{"q":"Why were the Kherson spoils put in the church rather than the treasury?","a":"Because they were the trophies of the campaign that brought the baptism and the marriage, so displaying them at the cathedral made the church the monument of the conquest. The chronicler records the bronze horses as still standing there, which is the point of mentioning them."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Restoration_of_tracing_of_the_Desyatynna_Church_foundations.jpg/1920px-Restoration_of_tracing_of_the_Desyatynna_Church_foundations.jpg","title":"The traced foundations at Kyiv","desc":"The ground plan of the church laid out in stone on the Kyiv square where it stood, with the eighteenth-century church of St Andrew on the skyline behind.","credit":"V. Lapitski, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Restoration_of_tracing_of_the_Desyatynna_Church_foundations.jpg","alt":"An open paved square with the outline of a large church marked out in rough pink and grey stone across the grass."},"wiki":"Church of the Tithes","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-056": {"questions":["In 1072 the Greek metropolitan doubted whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were holy at all, and consecrated the church at Vyshgorod with other bones before moving their relics in.","A message from Yaroslav's sister at Kyiv, that his father was dead and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> slain, is what sent him south with a Novgorodian army."],"abstract":"<b>Boris and Gleb</b> were younger sons of Vladimir, killed in the fighting that followed his death in 1015, and the saints Rus' came to venerate as its own within a generation of their deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle names them among Vladimir's twelve sons, born of a Bulgarian wife, and posts Boris to Rostov and Gleb to Murom after the eldest brother died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Vladimir died at Berestovo his household hid it, and Svyatopolk took Kyiv and had both brothers killed before Yaroslav at Novgorod knew of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What reached Yaroslav was a message from his sister Peredslava, that his father was dead and his brothers slain, which sent him south with a Novgorodian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A contemporary bishop adds a Polish thread, recording that Vladimir married a son to Bolesław's daughter, that the Poles sent a bishop with her, and that all three were shut up when the son was said to be turning against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The cult grew from the manner of the deaths rather than from martyrdom for the faith, since the brothers were remembered as having refused to fight a brother, and their bones were moved four times between the 1020s and 1115.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The feasts followed no single pattern, one day or two, with or without a consecration, and the choice rested each time with the Greek metropolitan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That dependence shows most clearly in the third translation of 1072, when the metropolitan doubted whether the princes were holy, consecrated the church at Vyshgorod with other relics, and only then moved the brothers into a building already made sacred without them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Yaroslav and again in 1115 the consecration was performed beside the relics themselves, which is the treatment a recognised saint receives, so the first saints of Rus' were pressed by its own princes and admitted slowly by its Byzantine hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Svyatopolk ended as the villain of both traditions, driven out by Yaroslav and coming back with the Polish army his marriage had won him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 65, 100, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 108–110, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 1, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Andrey Yu. Vinogradov, “The Features of Boris and Gleb Festivals in the Light of Byzantine Tradition,” <i>Slovene</i> 1, no. 2 (2012): 118, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2012.1.2.7. [Open access]","Thietmar of Merseburg, <i>Chronicon</i> 8.72, in <i>Thietmari Merseburgensis episcopi Chronicon</i>, ed. Friedrich Kurze, Scriptores rerum Germanicarum in usum scholarum (Hanover: Hahn, 1889), 236, https://archive.org/details/thietmarimersebu00thieuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were two murdered princes made saints rather than simply mourned?","a":"Because Yaroslav, who won the war, needed his brothers' deaths to condemn Svyatopolk, and a new church needed saints of its own. Holiness built out of a refusal to fight a brother also preached against exactly the kind of succession war that had produced it."},{"q":"Why did a metropolitan consecrate the church with other relics in 1072?","a":"Because he was not satisfied that the princes were saints, and consecration with their bones would have settled the question. Using other relics let him hold the ceremony while withholding the judgement, and the brothers were moved in afterwards."},{"q":"Why does the shape of each feast tell us who was in charge?","a":"Because the liturgy was the metropolitan's to set, and he was appointed from Constantinople. A one-day feast without a consecration and a two-day feast beside the relics are two different verdicts on a cult the Kyivan princes were pressing."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/%D0%91%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B1_%D1%81_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-%D0%91%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B1_%D1%81_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Boris and Gleb with their life","desc":"A fourteenth-century icon from the church of Saints Boris and Gleb at Kolomna: the two princes stand with swords and cloaks, ringed by scenes of their killings and the moving of their relics.","credit":"Unknown icon painter, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B1_%D1%81_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A tall painted icon of two young crowned men holding swords, surrounded by twenty small panels of narrative scenes."},"wiki":"Boris and Gleb","places":["pl:q1004257"]},
    "ru-057": {"questions":["The reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is remembered for books, churches and a law code, but it opened with a massacre of his own Varangian troops by the townsmen of Novgorod.","A German bishop records that a Polish army took Kyiv from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in August 1018 after the Pechenegs had battered the city at Bolesław's urging."],"abstract":"<b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> was prince of Kyiv from 1016, sole ruler of Rus' from 1036, and the ruler under whom Kyiv came closest to matching Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was holding Novgorod when Vladimir died, and his campaign began badly, with the townsmen killing his Varangian troops at Poromon's Court and Yaroslav answering by killing the killers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then had to ask the same town for help, and got it: a thousand Varangians and three thousand Novgorodians marched with him against Svyatopolk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The armies faced each other across the Dnieper near Lyubech for three months while Svyatopolk's general jeered that the Novgorodians were carpenters who would be set to building houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yaroslav crossed at night, told his men to wind their heads in kerchiefs as a mark, broke Svyatopolk before dawn and paid the Novgorodians ten grivnas a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Winning Kyiv did not end the war, and a bishop in Saxony recorded a Polish army under Bolesław beating Yaroslav at a river crossing on 22 July 1018 and entering Kyiv on 14 August after the Pechenegs had battered the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only from 1036 did he rule without a rival, and the reign after that date is the one the chronicle praises, for gathering scribes to copy and translate books and for founding churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He founded the metropolitan church of St Sophia at Kyiv in 1037, whose decoration marks the point at which the classical Byzantine system of church decoration reached maturity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1051 he had Ilarion, a learned priest from Berestovo, made metropolitan of Rus' at St Sophia, the first native to hold the office, and when he died on 20 February 1054 he was laid in a marble coffin in that same church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His name is also attached to the earliest written law of Rus', and a later church ordinance issued in his name records an agreement with Ilarion dividing the courts between prince and bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 62, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Thietmar of Merseburg, <i>Chronicon</i> 9.31–32, in <i>Thietmari Merseburgensis episcopi Chronicon</i>, ed. Friedrich Kurze, Scriptores rerum Germanicarum in usum scholarum (Hanover: Hahn, 1889), 257–258, https://archive.org/details/thietmarimersebu00thieuoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 128, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Anna Zakharova, “Images of saints in the wall paintings of Saint Sophia in Kiev,” <i>Δελτίον της Χριστιανικής Αρχαιολογικής Εταιρείας</i> 39 (2018): 297, https://doi.org/10.12681/dchae.18564. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 132, 137, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 171, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yaroslav have to ask Novgorod for help after punishing it?","a":"Because he had no other army. His Varangians were dead and his own following was small, so the town that had just defied him held the only troops within reach. The chronicle makes him say so at the assembly, which is why the episode survives."},{"q":"Why did the Novgorodians tie kerchiefs round their heads?","a":"Because the attack was made in darkness against an army of the same people, speaking the same language and armed the same way. Without a visible mark a night battle turns into men killing their own, which is the practical reason the detail was remembered."},{"q":"Why is 1036, not 1016, the date his reign is usually praised from?","a":"Because he did not rule alone until then. Bolesław took Kyiv from him in 1018 and his brother Mstislav held the left bank until 1036, so the books, the churches and the law belong to the twenty years after the fighting stopped."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Sarcophagus_of_Yaroslav_the_Wise.jpg/1920px-Sarcophagus_of_Yaroslav_the_Wise.jpg","title":"Yaroslav's marble sarcophagus","desc":"The carved marble tomb standing in a gallery of Saint Sophia at Kyiv, cut with crosses and palm fronds.","credit":"Stas-Adolf, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophagus_of_Yaroslav_the_Wise.jpg","alt":"A gabled white marble chest carved with a cross between palm branches, standing on a patterned floor in a frescoed room."},"wiki":"Yaroslav the Wise","places":["pl:q76561"]},
    "ru-058": {"questions":["A slave could not be tried for theft in the prince's court under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the reasoning that a slave has no freedom of his own.","Almost every penalty in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a fine to the prince plus compensation to the injured party, which is the whole of its system of punishment."],"abstract":"The <b>Russkaya Pravda</b> is the earliest written law of Rus', traditionally attributed to Yaroslav the Wise and his sons and surviving in two main versions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The short version falls into two parts, the first of seventeen articles defining killing, assault and offences against property with the compensation due, the second setting the penalty for each case and the court costs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The full version develops those articles and folds in the ordinances made at the council of Yaroslav's sons, which the short version gives separately and in outline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One article shows which came first: where the short text says the owner of a slave who struck a freeman must pay or hand the slave over to be killed, the full text adds that although Yaroslav judged so, his sons allowed damages instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fine for killing was called vira and the payment to the family golovnichestvo, and both rose with rank, eighty grivnas for a boyar, forty for a freeman and twenty for a woman or for grave mutilation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pairing a fine to the prince with compensation to the injured party is the whole of the code's system of punishment, and it valued a hand above a finger because a one-handed man works less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A slave could not be tried for theft in the prince's court, having no freedom, and a man who lent more than three grivnas without witnesses could not recover it at law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grivnas were real money and not an accounting fiction, and Yaroslav paid his Novgorodian troops ten each after taking Kyiv in 1016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest copy comes down in a Novgorodian church legal collection of the late thirteenth century, bound beside the oldest copy of Vladimir's Church Ordinance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That setting is now the code's hardest problem, because one study argues the short version is not an eleventh-century document at all but a passage written for Novgorod chronicle-keeping in the early fifteenth century, and a review of it found the replies brief and never systematic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 145, 147–148, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 159, 165, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 2, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Anna Joukovskaia, “Oleksiy Tolochko’s Concept and the Origin of the Short Pravda Russkaia,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 1097–1098, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2021.3.629. [Open access]","Anna Joukovskaia, “Oleksiy Tolochko’s Concept and the Origin of the Short Pravda Russkaia,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 1099–1100, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2021.3.629. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the code pay two sums for one killing?","a":"Because two injuries were recognised. The vira went to the prince as the fine for breaking his peace, and the golovnichestvo to the dead man's family as compensation for their loss, so the state's interest and the kin's were satisfied separately."},{"q":"Why was a hand worth more than a finger in the tariff?","a":"Because the code measured injury by working capacity rather than by suffering. A man who lost a hand was a much poorer worker than a man who lost a finger, so the compensation followed the loss to the household and the community."},{"q":"Why does the fifteenth-century argument matter so much?","a":"Because almost everything said about eleventh-century Rus' law rests on the short version. If it was written for a Novgorod chronicle four hundred years later, it is evidence about the fifteenth century and not about Yaroslav, and the earliest law of Rus' becomes the full version."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/The_instance_Sinodal%60niy_of_Pravda_Ruskaya_page_1.jpg/1920px-The_instance_Sinodal%60niy_of_Pravda_Ruskaya_page_1.jpg","title":"The Synodal copy's first page","desc":"A facsimile of the opening page of the Russkaya Pravda in the Synodal manuscript, its heading and initials picked out in red.","credit":"B. D. Grekov, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_instance_Sinodal%60niy_of_Pravda_Ruskaya_page_1.jpg","alt":"A worn parchment page in two columns of Cyrillic script, with a large decorated initial and a red heading at the top left."},"wiki":"Russkaya Pravda","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-059": {"questions":["The mosaics and frescoes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive in greater part than those of any other eleventh-century church, and more than eight hundred figures have been catalogued.","The sermon On Law and Grace was probably preached at the consecration of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which its author treated as proof that Kyiv could equal Constantinople."],"abstract":"<b>Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv</b> is Yaroslav's great church, and the monument by which eleventh-century Rus' measured itself against Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It marks a point in the history of Byzantine art at which the classical system of church decoration reached maturity, and its influence on later art in Rus' was enormous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It keeps the greater part of its mosaics and at least half of its wall paintings, made in the 1030s and 1040s, and more than eight hundred images and fragments survive to be catalogued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Identifying the figures is hard because almost every accompanying inscription was washed off during renovations in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries and again in the first restoration of the mid-nineteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Medieval graffiti scratched on the walls are now the main tool for naming them, which has also produced a dispute about when the decoration was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One reading of those graffiti dates the mosaics and frescoes to 1011 to 1018 and reads the whole programme as glorifying Vladimir rather than Yaroslav, a view other specialists reject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The written record is no tidier, since the Kyiv chronicle puts the foundation in 1037 while the Novgorod chronicle states flatly that St Sophia was founded at Kyiv in 1017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A bishop in Saxony adds a third piece, recording that when Bolesław entered Kyiv in August 1018 the archbishop honoured him in the holy monastery of Sophia, which had burnt down the year before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whatever its date, the building became the place where the new church made its claims, and the sermon On Law and Grace was most likely preached at its consecration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That homily treats Kyiv as a city with an ambition to be a cultural centre the equal of Constantinople and independent of it, and it was at St Sophia in 1051 that the first native metropolitan of Rus' was enthroned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Anna Zakharova, “Images of saints in the wall paintings of Saint Sophia in Kiev,” <i>Δελτίον της Χριστιανικής Αρχαιολογικής Εταιρείας</i> 39 (2018): 297–298, https://doi.org/10.12681/dchae.18564. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 2, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Thietmar of Merseburg, <i>Chronicon</i> 9.32, in <i>Thietmari Merseburgensis episcopi Chronicon</i>, ed. Friedrich Kurze, Scriptores rerum Germanicarum in usum scholarum (Hanover: Hahn, 1889), 258, https://archive.org/details/thietmarimersebu00thieuoft. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 62, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 128, 132, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can three sources disagree about when the cathedral was founded?","a":"Because each was written elsewhere and later. The Kyiv chronicle was compiled around 1118, the Novgorod chronicle kept its own reckoning, and Thietmar wrote in Saxony from second-hand news, so none of them is an eyewitness to a building's first stone."},{"q":"Why does Thietmar's note about a fire complicate the 1037 date?","a":"Because a church of Sophia at Kyiv that burnt in 1017 must have existed before then, twenty years before the Kyiv chronicle has Yaroslav found one. Either there was an earlier Sophia or the chronicle is dating a rebuilding."},{"q":"Why are graffiti now the main evidence for who is painted on the walls?","a":"Because the painted labels are gone, scrubbed away by successive renovations, while visitors' scratched inscriptions survived underneath the later work. What was never meant to be read has outlasted what was."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Kyiv-Saint_Sophia_Cathedral-south_view.jpg/1920px-Kyiv-Saint_Sophia_Cathedral-south_view.jpg","title":"Saint Sophia from the south","desc":"The cathedral as it stands, its eleventh-century core inside the white walls and green roofs of a rebuilding that ran from the late seventeenth century to the late nineteenth.","credit":"Galvm, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyiv-Saint_Sophia_Cathedral-south_view.jpg","alt":"A long white church with green pitched roofs and several gilded onion domes, seen across a lawn through bare trees."},"wiki":"Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-060": {"questions":["Only two men of local birth are known to have led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the middle of the twelfth century, and the first of them was enthroned in 1051.","Yaroslav's church ordinance opens by recording an agreement that split the courts of Rus' between the prince and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, giving the bishops the cases named in the Greek Nomocanon."],"abstract":"The <b>Metropolis of Kyiv</b> was the church province Constantinople created for Rus' after its conversion, and the senior ecclesiastical authority in the eastern Slavic lands for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The patriarchate held canonical jurisdiction over newly converted territory, and treated the whole of the eastern Slavic lands as a single missionary diocese under one metropolitan of Kyiv and all Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement was deliberately lopsided: the metropolitans came from Byzantium and held canonical and administrative authority over the bishops and clergy, who were chosen locally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest glimpse of a hierarchy at Kyiv is indirect, a Saxon bishop noting that in August 1018 the archbishop of the city met Bolesław with relics and honoured him in the monastery of Sophia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first named head is Ilarion, a priest of Berestovo whom Yaroslav had installed as metropolitan of Rus' at St Sophia in 1051.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He was also a writer, and his sermon On Law and Grace is the earliest surviving statement of what the new church thought it was, praising Vladimir as the teacher of a land known to the ends of the earth and setting him beside Constantine the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How exceptional his appointment was becomes clear from the next one, because Kliment Smolatič, metropolitan between 1147 and 1154, was only the second man of local origin to hold the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ilarion's other appearance is legal rather than literary, since the preface to Yaroslav's church ordinance has the prince record an agreement with him granting the metropolitan and the bishops the courts named in the Greek Nomocanon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That agreement drew the line between the prince's jurisdiction and the church's, and Rus' law then grew from the practice of those courts rather than from a code laid down in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The pre-schism period from 988 to 1054 is therefore the one in which Rus' had a single church, under a hierarchy it staffed but did not appoint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Paul Babie, “All Roads Lead to New Rome: The Canonical Origins and Status of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic Churches of Ukraine,” <i>Ecclesiastical Law Journal</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 222, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956618X23000066. [Open access]","Paul Babie, “All Roads Lead to New Rome: The Canonical Origins and Status of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic Churches of Ukraine,” <i>Ecclesiastical Law Journal</i> 25, no. 2 (2023): 225–226, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956618X23000066. [Open access]","Thietmar of Merseburg, <i>Chronicon</i> 9.32, in <i>Thietmari Merseburgensis episcopi Chronicon</i>, ed. Friedrich Kurze, Scriptores rerum Germanicarum in usum scholarum (Hanover: Hahn, 1889), 258, https://archive.org/details/thietmarimersebu00thieuoft. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 132, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 64–66, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 67, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 171, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Constantinople keep appointing the metropolitan from outside?","a":"Because the office was the patriarchate's hold on a province it had created. A Greek metropolitan answered to Constantinople and could not become the instrument of a Kyivan prince, while the bishops under him could safely be local because he supervised them."},{"q":"Why is Ilarion's appointment in 1051 remembered as unusual?","a":"Because he was chosen by the prince rather than sent from Byzantium, and almost a century passed before a second native held the office. One exception in a hundred years shows how firmly the appointment stayed in Greek hands."},{"q":"Why did Yaroslav need a written agreement about the courts?","a":"Because both powers claimed the same cases. Marriage, morality and offences by clergy could be judged as sin or as crime, so the ordinance names which courts belong to the bishops by the Greek Nomocanon and leaves the rest to the prince."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%AF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BC_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5_%D0%98%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%A0%D1%83%D1%81%D0%B8.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Ilarion made metropolitan","desc":"A miniature from the Radziwiłł Chronicle of Yaroslav installing Ilarion in Saint Sophia, the prince and his men on the left and the clergy on the right.","credit":"Radziwiłł Chronicle, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%AF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BC_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5_%D0%98%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%A0%D1%83%D1%81%D0%B8.jpg","alt":"A manuscript illumination of a haloed bishop standing before an altar canopy, with a crowned prince and courtiers to one side and clergy to the other."},"wiki":"Metropolis of Kyiv","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-061": {"questions":["Theodosius copied the discipline of the Studion house in Constantinople for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the chronicler claims every later monastery in the land took its order from that.","Prince Iziaslav gave a community of burrowing hermits the wooded hill above their crypt, and on it they raised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that still bears the name of the caves below."],"abstract":"The <b>Kyiv Pechersk Lavra</b>, the monastery of the caves on the high right bank of the Dnieper below Kyiv, became the oldest and most imitated religious house in Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name comes from the Slavonic word for a cave, and the first cells there were genuinely dug into the soft hillside above the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle credits the original burrow to Ilarion, a learned priest of the village of Berestovo who walked out to pray in it before Yaroslav made him metropolitan in 1051.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A layman of Liubech named Antipa then found the hole empty, having travelled to Mount Athos, taken the habit there under the name Antony and been sent home with a blessing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twelve brothers gathered around him, cut a larger crypt with a church and cells inside it, and asked Prince Iziaslav for the wooded hill directly overhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Iziaslav granted the hill, and the church and palisaded cells raised on the slope turned a warren of tunnels into a monastery that took its name from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antony withdrew to a fresh cave and handed the community to Varlaam and then to Theodosius, under whom the brethren grew from twenty men to about a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Theodosius wanted a written discipline and copied the rule of the Studion house in Constantinople from a monk who had travelled to Rus' in the metropolitan George's company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The great stone church of the Dormition was begun in 1073 and finished two years later, though its decoration and consecration waited until 1089, and its bricks are the type specimens of Kyivan building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation has since read the monks' diet out of the soil beneath the walls,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the life of Theodosius has been read for the emotional vocabulary it shares with Byzantine models,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and medieval writers treated the tunnels themselves as rivers carrying souls towards eternity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 132–36, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 155, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Olena Olehivna Makhota, “Main Characteristics of 11th–15th Centuries Bricks from Monumental Buildings of the Pechersk Monastery in Kyiv,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 64 (2023): 306–7, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2023.64.11. [Open access]","Serhiy Taranenko et al., “Integrated Bioarchaeological Research during the Excavations in Kyiv-Pechersk Reserve in 2016,” <i>City: History, Culture, Society</i>, no. 2 (2017): 24–6, https://doi.org/10.15407/mics2017.02.023. [Open access]","Liudmyla Petrushko, “Emotional World in Lives of Saints Theodosius of Pechersk and Sava the Sanctified,” <i>Rukopysna ta Knyzhkova Spadshchyna Ukrainy</i>, no. 2 (2024): 53–5, https://doi.org/10.15407/rksu.33.052. [Open access]","Maryana Nikitenko, “Caves as a River: Medieval Spatial Imaginaries of the Underground Labyrinths of the Kyiv-Pechersk Monastery,” <i>NaUKMA Research Papers. History and Theory of Culture</i> 9 (2026): 69–71, https://doi.org/10.18523/2617-8907.2026.9.68-87. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a community of hermits need a hill from the prince?","a":"The caves could hold only a dozen or so men, and once the brethren multiplied they had to build above ground; the land on the slope was the prince's, so expansion needed his grant rather than more digging."},{"q":"Why send to Constantinople for a monastic rule?","a":"Rus' had been Christian for barely two generations and had no written discipline of its own, so the order of services, meals and reading had to be copied from a Greek house; the Studion rule arrived with a monk travelling in the metropolitan's company."},{"q":"Why was this house imitated rather than the richer ones?","a":"The chronicler contrasts monasteries founded with princely wealth, which he thought came to little, with one founded on fasting and prayer, and he credits its written rule with spreading to every later house in Rus'."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Kyiv_Pechersk_Lavra_View_from_the_Dnieper_River.jpg/1280px-Kyiv_Pechersk_Lavra_View_from_the_Dnieper_River.jpg","title":"The Caves monastery above the Dnieper","desc":"Seen from the river, the monastery's gilded domes and great bell tower stand on the high wooded bank into which its first cells were dug.","credit":"Ввласенко, CC BY 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyiv_Pechersk_Lavra_View_from_the_Dnieper_River.jpg","alt":"Gilded and green domes and a tall white bell tower rising above a densely wooded hill under a cloudy sky."},"wiki":"Kyiv Pechersk Lavra","places":["pl:q242711"]},
    "ru-062": {"questions":["Ilarion praised Vladimir as an equal of the apostles and of Constantine in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in doing so handed Yaroslav an argument for a church of his own.","Sarah and Hagar stand for grace and law in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an allegory taken from Byzantine preaching and turned into a history of the baptism of Rus'."],"abstract":"The <b>Sermon on Law and Grace</b> is a solemn homily by Ilarion, a priest of Berestovo who became the first metropolitan of Kyiv drawn from Rus' itself, and it is the earliest surviving work of literature written in the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into four parts: the law and the grace that replaced it, the spread of that grace as far as Rus', a long encomium of Vladimir, and a closing prayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first part turns on the Old Testament pair of Sarah and Hagar, an allegory Ilarion took from Byzantine preaching and above all from the homilies of John Chrysostom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its argument is that Rus' entered the family of Christian nations by baptism and stands equal before God with the rest, without any nation being a chosen one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vladimir is never called a saint outright, but he is likened to the apostles and to Constantine, which raises his heirs with him and makes Yaroslav the continuer of his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ilarion also introduced into the literature of Rus' the idea that princely power is established by God, a claim that let Yaroslav act towards his own church as an emperor did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the sermon was delivered is disputed: one reading places it about 1037 at the consecration of Saint Sophia, another on 26 March 1049 in the Church of the Tithes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The later dating carries a political argument with it, that the text was Yaroslav's programme for the standing of his church and that the prince kept his own part in it quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within two years Yaroslav gathered his bishops and had Ilarion installed at Saint Sophia without asking Constantinople, and the Novgorod chronicle records the appointment in a single line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ilarion was remembered afterwards as the first of the bookmen of Rus' and a writer worth imitating, and the chronicle also names him as the man who dug the first cave at Berestovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 63–6, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","Andrey Yu. Vinogradov, “The ‘Sermon on Law and Grace’ in the Context of the Russian-Byzantine Conflict in the Mid-11th Century,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4, Istoriya. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniya</i> 29, no. 6 (2024): 128–41, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2024.6.9. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 132, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 88, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 4, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why open a sermon about Rus' with Sarah and Hagar?","a":"The pair was the standard Byzantine figure for the old law giving way to grace, and it let Ilarion fit a newly baptised people into a scheme of salvation history his audience already knew, rather than arguing the case from nothing."},{"q":"Why compare Vladimir to Constantine rather than call him a saint?","a":"Declaring a saint was not Ilarion's to do, and the comparison did the same work more safely: it made the baptiser of Rus' an equal of the emperor who baptised Rome, and sanctified his descendants along with him."},{"q":"Why does the date of the sermon change how it is read?","a":"Placed at the consecration of Saint Sophia it is a dedication piece; placed in 1049, two years before Ilarion's elevation, it reads instead as an argument prepared in advance for a church less dependent on Constantinople."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Sermon_on_Law_and_Grace_GIM.jpg/1280px-Sermon_on_Law_and_Grace_GIM.jpg","title":"The sermon in a copy of the 1640s","desc":"The text survives only in much later manuscripts; here it opens in the middle of a compendium, under an ornamental heading in red cinnabar.","credit":"Лапоть, CC0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sermon_on_Law_and_Grace_GIM.jpg","alt":"An open manuscript in a display case, the right-hand page beginning a new text with a large ornamental heading in red ink and several red lines beneath it, the rest in black Cyrillic script."},"wiki":"Sermon on Law and Grace","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-063": {"questions":["The Novgorod birch bark letters preserve forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the books do not, among them a pronoun untouched by a sound change the rest of Slavonic underwent.","Four Cyrillic graffiti on a cathedral wall in Lucca were cut by pilgrims whose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dates them to the twelfth or early 13th century."],"abstract":"<b>Old East Slavic</b> is the speech of the eastern Slavs in the centuries when Rus' was one state, and it is known from books, from letters scratched on birch bark and from graffiti cut into church walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The books are the awkward witness, because writing arrived in Rus' with Christianity and the literary language that came with it was Church Slavonic rather than the local vernacular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its East Slavonic recension of the 11th to 13th centuries is a mixture, carrying older local forms alongside the South Slavonic ones the tradition was built on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The letters dug out of Novgorod are the other witness, and they show a northern dialect with features that the southern manuscripts simply do not have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest is a pronoun written vьxe, which lacks a palatalisation every other Slavic dialect carried out and which appears 23 times in the birch bark material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One reading of that form is that the Novgorod dialect broke away from common Slavonic early enough for the sound change never to have reached it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everyday usage also shows in how words were made, and the bark writers and the Novgorod chroniclers built possessive adjectives from the same suffixes in noticeably different proportions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Further west the same language was later called Ruthenian, and translations made in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania show it working as a language for people while Church Slavonic was kept for God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest names in the record are not Slavonic at all, for the envoys of the treaty of 944 carry north Germanic ones, spelled in Cyrillic by scribes copying them at second hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Short inscriptions carry the language furthest of all, and four Cyrillic graffiti on the cathedral of Lucca in Tuscany were cut by pilgrims out of Murom, Briansk and Polotsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Helmut Keipert, “Conceptions of Church Slavonic,” <i>Slověne</i> 6, no. 1 (2017): 9–12, https://slovene.ru/ojs/index.php/slovene/article/view/257. [Open access]","Е. А. Галинская, “The Progressive Palatalization and the Old Novgorodian Pronoun vьxe,” <i>Slavistica Vilnensis</i> 59 (2014): 7–9, https://doi.org/10.15388/SlavViln.2014.59.4895. [Open access]","S. V. Saltovskaia, “Word Building of Possessive Adjectives in Birch Bark Letters and the Novgorod First Chronicle,” <i>Russian Studies in Philology</i>, no. 1 (2025): 60–2, https://doi.org/10.18384/2949-5008-2025-1-59-67. [Open access]","Sergei Temchin, “The Ruthenian Version of the Early Rus’ Exegesis on the Easter Canon and the Functioning of Ruthenian,” <i>Slavistica Vilnensis</i> 65, no. 1 (2020): 131–3, https://doi.org/10.15388/SlavViln.2020.65%281%29.41. [Open access]","Sergey L. Nikolaev, “Etymology and Comparative Phonology of North Germanic Personal Names in the Primary Chronicle,” <i>Voprosy Onomastiki</i> 14, no. 2 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2017.14.2.009. [Open access]","Savva Mikhailovich Mikheev, “Travelers from Murom, Briansk and Polotsk in Lucca (On Previously Known and Newly Discovered Old East Slavic Inscriptions on the Walls of St. Martin Cathedral),” <i>Studi Slavistici</i> 20, no. 2 (2023): 5–8, https://doi.org/10.36253/Studi_Slavis-14816. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the birch bark letters matter more here than the books?","a":"The manuscripts were written in a learned language imported for the liturgy, so they smooth local speech away; the letters were written by ordinary people for ordinary purposes and keep dialect forms the books suppress."},{"q":"Why are the oldest names in the written record not Slavonic?","a":"The men who negotiated with Byzantium in the 10th century were the prince's retinue, which was still largely of Scandinavian origin, so the earliest long list of names in a Cyrillic text is a list of north Germanic ones."},{"q":"Why can graffiti in Italy be dated by their language alone?","a":"Letter shapes, spelling habits and grammatical forms all changed over time, so even a short text carries enough of those features to be placed within a century or so with nothing around it to date it by."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Graffiti_St._Sophia%27s_Cathedral_in_Kiev_%28XII_century%29._-_1.jpg/1280px-Graffiti_St._Sophia%27s_Cathedral_in_Kiev_%28XII_century%29._-_1.jpg","title":"A charter cut into the wall of Saint Sophia in Kyiv","desc":"Scratched into the cathedral plaster early in the 12th century, the inscription records the purchase of Boyan's land and counts the price in grivny.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graffiti_St._Sophia%27s_Cathedral_in_Kiev_%28XII_century%29._-_1.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a wall inscription: a dense block of Old Cyrillic letters cut into plaster, with a large incised cross at the right and a scale bar along the foot."},"wiki":"Old East Slavic"},
    "ru-064": {"questions":["Yaroslav gathered scribes and had books turned out of Greek into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the earliest library of Rus' is a translated one rather than a native one.","A language without native speakers that is nonetheless not dead, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is heard in worship though nobody has ever grown up speaking it at home."],"abstract":"<b>Church Slavonic</b> is the liturgical language of the Slavonic Orthodox churches, descended from the Old Church Slavonic into which Greek service books were first translated for the Slavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been called a language without native speakers that is not for all that a dead one, since it is still used in worship although nobody has ever learned it at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Slavs have given it a great many different names, and which name a philologist reaches for has tended to follow national and ideological purposes rather than anything in the language itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside it there are varieties by region, by period, by function, by individual writer and by group, so the classical manuscripts give only a thin idea of the whole tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The East Slavic recension of the 11th to 13th centuries is the form Rus' used, and it carries local forms beside the South Slavonic ones it inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rus' received the language with Christianity, and the chronicle says Yaroslav applied himself to books, gathered many scribes and had translations made from Greek into the Slavonic tongue and letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because most books arrived translated rather than composed, the earliest library of Rus' is a Byzantine one in Slavonic dress, and original writing in the land is the exception rather than the rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek did not stop at the service books either, and clauses of the tenth-century treaties with Byzantium carry phrases that render Roman legal formulas almost word for word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A commentary on the Easter canon of John of Damascus, put together in Rus' in the late twelfth or early 13th century, is one of those rare local compositions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its later translation into Ruthenian shows the division of labour plainly, since Ruthenian served as a language addressed to people while Church Slavonic stayed the one addressed to God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Helmut Keipert, “Conceptions of Church Slavonic,” <i>Slověne</i> 6, no. 1 (2017): 9–12, https://slovene.ru/ojs/index.php/slovene/article/view/257. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 128, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Sergei Temchin, “The Ruthenian Version of the Early Rus’ Exegesis on the Easter Canon and the Functioning of Ruthenian,” <i>Slavistica Vilnensis</i> 65, no. 1 (2020): 131–3, https://doi.org/10.15388/SlavViln.2020.65%281%29.41. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’ (as Exemplified by Ilarion of Kiev, Kliment Smolatič, and Kirill of Turov),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 61–2, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 84, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a language with no native speakers not simply dead?","a":"It is still used, and used aloud: the Orthodox liturgy is sung in it every week across eastern Europe, so it has living speech communities in worship even though nobody acquires it as a first language."},{"q":"Why did Rus' write in a South Slavonic language rather than its own?","a":"The service books arrived already translated from Greek into the Slavonic of the Balkan missions, and that translated corpus came with the church; a written standard was inherited rather than made locally."},{"q":"Why does the choice of name for the language matter to scholars?","a":"Calling it Old Bulgarian, Old Macedonian, Old Slavonic or Church Slavonic makes different claims about whose language it was, so the name carries national and ideological weight beyond anything linguistic."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Ostromir_Gospel_4.jpg/1280px-Ostromir_Gospel_4.jpg","title":"A leaf of the Ostromir Gospel","desc":"The large uncial hand is the one kept for service books, and the small red line above the decorated initial opens a reading from Matthew.","credit":"shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostromir_Gospel_4.jpg","alt":"A single parchment leaf with two columns of large Cyrillic uncial letters, a red and dark interlaced initial partway down the left column, and a small line of red script above it."},"wiki":"Church Slavonic"},
    "ru-065": {"questions":["Carrying debts, trade, law and family business rather than affairs of state, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the everyday writing of Rus', and over nine-tenths of the finds come from Novgorod.","Wills written on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follow the same formulas as those on parchment, which is how the private charters of north-western Rus' can be traced back before 1400."],"abstract":"A <b>birch bark letter</b> is a document scratched into the inner layer of birch bark, which was the writing material of everyday life in medieval northern Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole class of evidence was unknown until 1951, when excavation in post-war Novgorod turned up bark with writing on it and handed Slavonic philology a new kind of source altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 1,200 are now published, in print and online, and they reach back to the 11th century and forward to the 15th, which vastly deepened what could be said about the language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over nine-tenths come from Novgorod, with the rest from Staraya Russa and Pskov, so the material is northern and cannot be read as a record of all Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives does so because it was thrown away, very often torn up first, while the documents anyone meant to keep were written on parchment instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The contents are everyday life, trade, law and personal business, which is more or less exactly what the parchment record leaves out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parchment counterpart is the Novgorod chronicle, which keeps princes, fires, famine and war and almost never a private transaction between two named people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the historian of the language the bark is better evidence than the books, since it was written without the filter of a literary Slavonic imported for the liturgy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It preserves a northern pronoun written vьxe that escaped a palatalisation the rest of Slavonic underwent, attested 23 times in the bark and once besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wills on bark share the formulas of those on parchment, which is how the private charters of the north-west are traced back before 1400, and the bark gave way as paper spread; the same suffixes also let word formation be compared across the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Sebastian Kempgen, review of <i>Voices on Birchbark: Everyday Communication in Medieval Russia</i>, by Jos Schaeken, <i>Scando-Slavica</i> 67, no. 1 (2021): 1–2, https://doi.org/10.20378/irb-49984. [Open access]","Е. А. Галинская, “The Progressive Palatalization and the Old Novgorodian Pronoun vьxe,” <i>Slavistica Vilnensis</i> 59 (2014): 7–9, https://doi.org/10.15388/SlavViln.2014.59.4895. [Open access]","S. V. Saltovskaia, “Word Building of Possessive Adjectives in Birch Bark Letters and the Novgorod First Chronicle,” <i>Russian Studies in Philology</i>, no. 1 (2025): 60–2, https://doi.org/10.18384/2949-5008-2025-1-59-67. [Open access]","Alexei A. Vovin, “Birch Bark Documents and Parchment Charters: North-West Rus Charters Evolution in 13th–15th Centuries,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4, Istoriya. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniya</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 17–28, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2026.1.3. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xlii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did birch bark survive where parchment did not?","a":"Novgorod sits on waterlogged clay that keeps organic material from rotting, and bark thrown into the street was buried in layers that never dried out; parchment was valuable enough to be reused and rarely ended up in the ground."},{"q":"Why does it matter that these letters were rubbish?","a":"Documents preserved on purpose record what somebody wanted remembered, usually state affairs; documents thrown away survive at random, so they show what people actually wrote to each other about."},{"q":"Why can the bark not stand for the whole of medieval Rus'?","a":"Over nine-tenths of the finds come from Novgorod and the rest from two neighbouring towns, so the corpus describes writing habits in the north-west rather than in Kyiv or the south."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Birch-bark_letter_292_real.jpg/1280px-Birch-bark_letter_292_real.jpg","title":"Letter 292, from the early 13th century","desc":"The letters are Cyrillic but the language is not Slavonic at all: this is a Baltic-Finnic text, written on bark like everything else in daily use.","credit":"Unknown, photographed by Gratomy.ru, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birch-bark_letter_292_real.jpg","alt":"A dark brown strip of birch bark with three lines of angular letters scratched into its surface, the edges torn and uneven."},"wiki":"Birch bark manuscript","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-066": {"questions":["Told that wooden spoons were beneath them, Vladimir had silver ones cast for his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and said that with gold he would find no men, but with men he would find gold.","Not one of the 25 envoys who sealed Igor's treaty of 944 bore a Slavonic name, which shows how Scandinavian a prince's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still was in the 10th century."],"abstract":"The <b>druzhina</b> was the body of free men who served a prince of Rus' voluntarily, on terms they had bargained for and could leave for a better offer elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not quite a court, a staff or a bodyguard, and the word has no clean equivalent in English; it comes from the Slavonic for a friend or companion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Membership had nothing to do with birth in Rus', and the chronicle names Chudin the Finn, Torchin the Turk and Vladislav the Lekh among the prince's men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divided into an upper grade of boyars and a lower one of pages, called together grid or gridba, from a Scandinavian word for the servants of a household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its origin lay in the armed merchant class of the great trading towns, and in the 10th century it had not yet separated from that class either politically or economically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the middle of that century it was still largely Scandinavian, for of the 25 envoys who sealed Igor's treaty with the Greeks not one carries a Slavonic name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Cyrillic spellings of those names preserve sound changes matching none of the known Scandinavian languages, which has been read as a dialect of its own spoken in the prince's service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A prince ruled through the druzhina and consulted it, and the chronicle says Vladimir loved his and took counsel with it about the administration of the land, the wars and the statutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also fed it, holding feasts that brought in boyars, court officers, the commanders of hundreds and of tens, and the most distinguished men of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The habit had a cost, and one reading of Sviatopolk's defeat at Liubech in 1016 is that his army had spent the whole night before the battle at a feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 300–1, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 89–91, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sergey L. Nikolaev, “Etymology and Comparative Phonology of North Germanic Personal Names in the Primary Chronicle,” <i>Voprosy Onomastiki</i> 14, no. 2 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.15826/vopr_onom.2017.14.2.009. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 105, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Vitaliy Nagirnyy, “An Unexpected Ally: The Influence of Alcohol on the Course of Military Campaigns in Rus’ in the Eleventh–Twelfth Centuries,” <i>Historický časopis</i> 71, no. 4 (2023): 710–13, https://doi.org/10.31577/histcaso.2023.71.4.8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a prince rather give silver than keep it?","a":"His revenue came from tribute and campaigns, both of which needed armed men who could leave for another prince at will; spending the silver on them bought the force that produced more of it, which is what the chronicle's saying about gold and men means."},{"q":"Why did the retinue not become a landed nobility sooner?","a":"Its wealth came from trade and tribute rather than from estates, and no trace of landowning by the upper class appears in the record before the 11th century, so rank rested on service to the prince rather than on property."},{"q":"Why does a feast turn up in accounts of a lost battle?","a":"Feasting was how a prince held his men, so a campaign moved with its drink; one reading of Liubech is that Sviatopolk's army was still recovering from a night of it when his brother crossed the river at dawn."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Battle_of_Liubech_%281016%29._1.jpeg/500px-Battle_of_Liubech_%281016%29._1.jpeg","title":"Yaroslav's men meet Sviatopolk's at Liubech, 1016","desc":"A chronicle miniature of the 15th century: mailed riders in tall conical helmets ride at each other under their princes’ banners.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Liubech_%281016%29._1.jpeg","alt":"A medieval miniature of two bands of mounted warriors in tall conical helmets meeting head on, an archer drawing a bow at the centre and red banners above them."},"wiki":"Druzhina"},
    "ru-067": {"questions":["When fire took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Novgorod in 1097 the chronicler recorded it as the loss of the citadel itself rather than of one district among many.","Kyiv's defences were raised in stages from the 9th century, and banks of earth with timber boxes inside them enclosed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the princes lived and ruled in."],"abstract":"A <b>detinets</b> is the fortified inner citadel of a town of Rus', the walled core that the rest of the settlement grew around and sheltered behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word belongs to the chronicles, where the translators of the Novgorod annals render it simply as citadel and treat it as a district of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The towns it belonged to grew out of trade, for the places where hunters and beekeepers met foreign buyers to barter became markets, and the busiest of those markets became towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle names Kyiv, Pereiaslavl, Chernihiv, Smolensk, Liubech, Novgorod, Rostov and Polotsk without saying when any of them began, because all were already important when it opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of them lie in a chain along the river road from the Baltic to the Greek colonies, which is the clearest sign of what brought them into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The outside evidence is thinner than the chronicle suggests, and of the 25 town names it gives only a handful appear in the Greek, Arabic and Scandinavian texts of the 10th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At least ten places those foreign texts do name are unknown to the early chronicle altogether, so the map it draws belongs to its own time and cannot be pushed back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defences themselves were earth and timber, and at Kyiv the banks were built up in stages from the 9th century, with framed boxes of logs inside them and a parapet along the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A citadel of that construction burned, and the Novgorod chronicle has the whole detinets going up in 1097, again in the 14th century and once more in 1405.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Princes fortified frontiers as well as towns, and the chronicle has Vladimir throwing up earthworks on the Desna, the Trubizh, the Stuhna and the Sula against the Pechenegs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xlii, 7, 152, 175, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 52–3, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","A. S. Shchavelev, “Old Russian Cities of the East European Plain in the 10th Century According to Written Sources (Updating the Perspective for Research),” <i>Shagi / Steps</i> 9, no. 2 (2023): 33, 45, 49, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-2-33-56. [Open access]","Vadym Lukianchenko, “Gorodnya – Kyiv Defensive Walls in IX–XIII Centuries. Part Three. Zaborola,” <i>City: History, Culture, Society</i>, no. 3 (2017): 20–3, https://doi.org/10.15407/mics2019.03.019. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 86, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the towns of Rus' grow where they did?","a":"They stand along the rivers between the Baltic and the Black Sea because they began as places where trappers and beekeepers met foreign buyers; the busiest of those meeting places turned into storage depots and then into towns."},{"q":"Why can the chronicle's list of towns not be dated from itself?","a":"It names its towns as places already important when its account opens, so it records the map of its own day around 1100; the Greek, Arabic and Scandinavian texts of the 10th century name a different and partly unknown set."},{"q":"Why was a citadel of earth and timber worth building?","a":"Earth banks with log frames inside them could be raised quickly by local labour and stood up to the raiding that actually threatened a town, while the timber that made them cheap also made them burn."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Velikiy_Novgorod_Detinets_06.jpg/500px-Velikiy_Novgorod_Detinets_06.jpg","title":"The citadel at Novgorod as it stands now","desc":"The brick curtain and the covered timber walkway along its top are a much later rebuilding; the earlier defences on this line were earth and timber.","credit":"Lite, CC BY-SA 3.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velikiy_Novgorod_Detinets_06.jpg","alt":"A red-brick fortress wall curving away into the distance, a timber-roofed covered walkway running along its top and a brick tower with a tented roof beyond."},"wiki":"Detinets","places":["pl:q1277545"]},
    "ru-068": {"questions":["Ibn Fadlan watched merchants of Rus' at Bulgar on the Volga trading <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Islamic coins and beads brought by a caravan out of Khorezm.","Guards of the caliphs, eunuchs of their household offices and women of their harems were drawn from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whom the northern trade carried south in numbers."],"abstract":"<b>Saqaliba</b> is the word medieval Arabic writers used for the Slavonic peoples of central and eastern Europe, and perhaps for some of their neighbours as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because so many Slavs reached Islamic markets as captives between the early 9th century and the early 11th, the name of the people became a word for a slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the texts they appear mostly in palaces: men in the guards of the Abbasid, Fatimid and Umayyad caliphs, women and eunuchs in their households and offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomats visited the markets where they were bought, and in 921 Ibn Fadlan watched merchants of Rus' at Bulgar on the Volga exchange slave girls for coins and beads from a Khorezm caravan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other market was Prague, forty years later, which looked west to Muslim Spain rather than east to Central Asia and was paid in goods rather than in silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the eastern trade paid in is countable, and a conservative estimate puts the Abbasid and Samanid dirhams found in Scandinavia and the Slavic lands at four hundred thousand coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their distribution points to the belt of Slavonic country from the Elbe to the rivers of Russia as the place the captives came from, and their dates give the rhythm of the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 9th century the silver came north from Iraq through Khazaria; after about 900 the route swung east to the Samanid mines and the market at Bulgar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Rus' themselves are described by Arabic and Byzantine writers as a people living by war and trade, holding no land, and dealing in furs, honey, wax and slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their own accounts of the Volga Rus' include the funeral of a chief at which a slave girl was killed, which is the kind of detail the eastern trade is documented by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Marek Jankowiak, “What Does the Slave Trade in the Saqaliba Tell Us about Early Islamic Slavery?,” <i>International Journal of Middle East Studies</i> 49, no. 1 (2017): 169–72, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0020743816001240. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 90, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 383–4, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]","James E. Montgomery, “Ibn Faḍlān and the Rūsiyyah,” <i>Journal of Arabic and Islamic Studies</i> 3 (2000): 5, 7, 10, https://doi.org/10.5617/jais.4553. [Open access]","al-Mas‘udi, <i>El-Mas‘udi’s Historical Encyclopaedia, Entitled “Meadows of Gold and Mines of Gems”</i>, trans. Aloys Sprenger (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1841), 406–8, https://archive.org/details/elmasdshistoric00unkngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the name of a people become a word for a slave?","a":"For two centuries Slavonic captives were the main supply of unfree labour reaching Islamic courts, so in Arabic and then in the languages that borrowed from it the ethnonym and the condition became the same word."},{"q":"Why were Islamic palaces the main buyers rather than private owners?","a":"The demand was for guards, eunuchs, bureaucrats and concubines, all of which were household offices of rulers; private ownership shows in the sources only occasionally, in the inventories of very rich men."},{"q":"Why do dirham hoards in the north date the slave trade?","a":"The coins were what the captives were paid for, so where they pile up and when they stop marks where the trade drew its supply and how its routes shifted from Iraq to Central Asia after about 900."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Saint_Adalbert_of_Prague_pleads_with_Boleslaus_II%2C_Duke_of_Bohemia%2C_for_the_release_of_Christians_slaves_by_their_masters%2C_Jewish_merchants%2C_Gniezno_Door_ca._1170.png/1280px-thumbnail.png","title":"The Gniezno door, cast about 1170","desc":"Adalbert of Prague pleads before Boleslaus II of Bohemia for the release of the captives standing bound at the left of the panel.","credit":"Maciej Szczepańczyk, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Adalbert_of_Prague_pleads_with_Boleslaus_II%2C_Duke_of_Bohemia%2C_for_the_release_of_Christians_slaves_by_their_masters%2C_Jewish_merchants%2C_Gniezno_Door_ca._1170.png","alt":"A bronze relief: four men at the left, the two in front bound at the neck and hands, a bishop with a crozier gesturing between them, and a crowned ruler enthroned at the right."},"wiki":"Saqaliba","places":["city:1159151547"]},
    "ru-069": {"questions":["Vladimir struck the first silver coins of Rus' in small quantities, but trade ran on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and on clipped Arabic dirhams reckoned against it and against furs.","When silver grew dear in the 12th century the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> halved in weight, and the rezana was renamed so that fifty of them went to it instead of 25."],"abstract":"The <b>grivna</b> was the chief unit of money in Rus', a pound's weight of silver that took the form of an ingot, usually oblong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fuller name was grivna kun, from the ordinary word for money, and it stayed the largest medium of exchange in the market until the rouble appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divided into 20 nogaty, 25 kuny or 50 rezany, and the rezana subdivided further into vekshi, though into how many is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those names had been fur values before they were coin values, and kuny in particular stood for pelts passing from hand to hand as currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle of Novgorod glosses the word simply as a pound, and counts tributes and fines in them without stopping to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Metal money arrived early and from outside, and hoards of Arabic dirhams of the 8th to 10th centuries are found across European Russia, most of them small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The silver came north in exchange for the slaves and furs sold at Bulgar on the Volga, the capital of the eastern Bulgars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Coins were cut up for small change, and in the basin of the Seim the round cut-outs were taken by size rather than by weight, with larger payments weighed out in batches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Native coin came late and thinly, for the silver srebrennik was not struck until Vladimir's reign, and analyses of his coins and Sviatopolk's give purities from about three hundred parts of silver in a thousand up to 960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The grivna weighed about a third of a pound in the 10th century and only a quarter by the early 13th, which is how the rezana came to be called a kuna with fifty of them to the grivna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 137–40, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Enukov, “Chronology of the Final of the Romny Culture ‘Posemye’ Based on Numismatic Materials,” <i>Nizhnevolzhskiy Arkheologicheskiy Vestnik</i> 25, no. 1 (2026): 100–4, https://doi.org/10.15688/nav.jvolsu.2026.1.5. [Open access]","Georgij A. Kozubowskij, “About the Silver of the Old Rus Coins,” <i>Arheologia</i>, no. 1 (2019): 59–61, https://doi.org/10.15407/archaeologyua2019.01.059. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xlii, 2, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","James Howard-Johnston, “Rus and Khazars,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 383–4, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were coins cut into fragments rather than used whole?","a":"The dirham was worth too much for daily buying and Rus' struck almost nothing of its own, so the coins that came in were clipped into halves, quarters and smaller pieces to make change."},{"q":"Why did money in Rus' have two sets of names?","a":"The units began as fur values and were still quoted that way when metal arrived, so the same words came to name both a pelt and the coin it was worth, and a price could be given in either."},{"q":"Why did the grivna lose half its weight?","a":"Foreign trade contracted, less silver came in, and the metal grew dearer; since the unit was a weight of silver rather than a fixed price, the weight fell while what it bought stayed much the same."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Hoard%2C_silver_grivnas_%28Kiev%2C_12-13_c.%2C_GIM%29_01_by_shakko.jpg/1280px-Hoard%2C_silver_grivnas_%28Kiev%2C_12-13_c.%2C_GIM%29_01_by_shakko.jpg","title":"56 silver grivny from Kyiv","desc":"Buried in a clay pot in the 12th century or the first half of the 13th, the cast ingots are flat and lozenge-shaped, with a few drawn out into bars.","credit":"shakko, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoard%2C_silver_grivnas_%28Kiev%2C_12-13_c.%2C_GIM%29_01_by_shakko.jpg","alt":"A heap of dull silver ingots, most flat and lozenge-shaped and a few long and rod-like, spilled beside a dark grey clay pot lying on its side."},"wiki":"Grivna","places":["pl:q162677"]},
    "ru-070": {"questions":["Yaroslav dealt out Kyiv, Chernihiv, Pereiaslavl, Smolensk and Volhynia to his sons by age in 1054, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name later given to what followed.","When Viacheslav of Smolensk died in 1057 his brothers moved Igor up from Volhynia rather than let a son inherit, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> working as intended."],"abstract":"The <b>rota system</b> is the name given to the order of collateral succession the princes of Rus' followed after Yaroslav the Wise, by which a seat passed to a brother rather than to a son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The princes left no statement of the rule, so both the name and the scheme are later reconstructions from what they can be seen doing in the first generations after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before Yaroslav there is hardly a system to describe: a prince gave his grown sons provinces to hold as his deputies, and the ties between brothers snapped when the father died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle gives Yaroslav's dying speech, which leaves Kyiv to Iziaslav his eldest, Chernihiv to Sviatoslav, Pereiaslavl to Vsevolod, Volhynia to Igor and Smolensk to Viacheslav.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told the younger brothers to obey the eldest as they had obeyed him, since the eldest would stand in his place, and told the eldest to take the part of any brother wronged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The provinces were graded by wealth in the same order as the brothers' ages, so the ladder of seats matched the ladder of seniority exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Viacheslav died in 1057 the brothers moved Igor from Volhynia up to Smolensk and put their nephew Rostislav into Volhynia, which is the order working as it was meant to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Igor died three years later neither his sons nor Rostislav were given Smolensk, and Rostislav went off to Tmutarakan to raise men for a feud against his uncles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was the weakness in the scheme, for it shut out any prince whose father had died before reaching Kyiv and left him nothing to lose by fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicles of the day record only the bare successions, while the rites of enthronement were elaborated in the 12th century as the seats grew more nearly hereditary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 94–5, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 136–8, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 4, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Alexandra Vukovich, “Enthronement Rituals of the Princes of Rus’ (Twelfth–Thirteenth Centuries),” <i>FORUM: University of Edinburgh Postgraduate Journal of Culture and the Arts</i>, no. 17 (2013), https://doi.org/10.2218/forum.17.704. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 96–7, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rus' pass seats between brothers rather than to sons?","a":"The land was held by the family rather than by individual princes, and Yaroslav's arrangement graded the provinces by wealth against the brothers' ages, so seniority in the family decided which town a prince held."},{"q":"Why did the order break down within a generation?","a":"The family multiplied faster than the seats, and a prince whose father died before reaching Kyiv dropped out of the ladder altogether; having no prospect of a turn, he had no reason not to take a province by force."},{"q":"Why must the rule be inferred rather than read off a text?","a":"No prince set it down. Yaroslav's speech says only that the younger brothers should obey the eldest and that the eldest should protect the wronged, so the scheme has to be reconstructed from the transfers that actually happened."}],"wiki":"Rota system","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-071": {"questions":["Vasilko Rostislavich rode unarmed into Sviatopolk's hall because the princes had kissed the cross at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weeks earlier, and was seized the next morning and blinded.","Tired of feuds that left the Polovtsy free to plunder, the princes of Rus' agreed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a seat should pass from father to son rather than between brothers."],"sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 200–203, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","V. B. Sukhomlyn and A. A. Nastiuk, “A Separate Peace between Volodymyr Vsevolodovych and Davyd Sviatoslavych, as a Secret Side of the Council of Liubech, 1097,” <i>Juridical Scientific and Electronic Journal</i>, no. 4 (2026): 25–34, https://doi.org/10.32782/2524-0374/2026-4/4. [Open access]","A. V. Korenevskiy and N. D. Nikolaeva, “Polish Vector in Politics of Vladimir Monomakh and His Heirs: from the Lyubech Congress to the ‘Pereyaslavl Crisis,’” <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i> 1, no. 10 (2020): 337–352, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-10-337-352. [Open access]","Ihor Viktorovich Kondratiev, “Liubech Castle: In Search of Symbols and Meanings,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i> 29, no. 1 (2021): 114–129, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2021.107. [Open access]","S. V. Alekseev, “Seniority in the Rurikid Dynasty: Socio-Cultural Ideas and Legitimacy,” <i>Humanities and Social Sciences. Bulletin of the Financial University</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 59–65, https://doi.org/10.26794/2226-7867-2023-13-3-59-65. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>Council of Liubech</b> was a meeting of six princes of Rus' at Liubech, a fortified town on the Dnieper above Chernihiv, held in 1097 to stop the wars they had been fighting among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle names Sviatopolk Iziaslavich of Kyiv, Vladimir Monomakh, David Igorovich, Vasilko Rostislavich and the brothers David and Oleg Sviatoslavich, and gives them a speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why, they ask, do we ruin the land of Rus' by our own quarrels, when the Polovtsy plunder it as they please and are glad to watch us fight each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their answer was that each prince should keep the towns his own father had held, so that a seat would pass from father to son instead of sideways along a ladder of brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terms are set out town by town and sealed by kissing the cross, with an oath that all of them and the cross itself would stand against whoever broke them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the council settled was a quarrel forty years old over Chernihiv, where the sons of Sviatoslav refused to accept that their father's town had passed to his brother's descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On one reading Vladimir Monomakh won the fighting and lost the settlement, since the Severian land was divided and confirmed to those cousins rather than to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peace did not last the year: David Igorovich told Sviatopolk that Vladimir and Vasilko were plotting against them both, and Vasilko, coming to Kyiv on 4 November, was seized the next morning and blinded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle has him wave away a servant's warning on the road, reasoning that the princes had kissed the cross and so could not touch him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians still divide over how much the meeting changed, and the celebrated reconstruction of the castle it is pictured in draws on buildings five centuries younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicle put the Polovtsy into the princes' own mouths at Liubech?","a":"Because the raids were the price of the feuding. The speech makes ending the quarrel a defence of the land rather than a concession by any one prince, which is the only ground on which men who had been fighting for forty years could be asked to stop."},{"q":"Why did Vasilko think he was safe riding into Kyiv?","a":"Because the princes had kissed the cross at Liubech only weeks before and had sworn that all of them, and the cross itself, would stand against whoever broke the terms. The chronicle has him reason from that oath and ignore a servant's warning on the road."},{"q":"Why can Liubech be read as a defeat for Vladimir Monomakh when he had won the war?","a":"Because the terms confirmed the Severian land, Chernihiv with it, to the sons of Sviatoslav. The branch that had lost the fighting kept the town the fighting was about, so on that reading Monomakh took the battles and his cousins took the settlement."}],"wiki":"Council of Liubech","places":["pl:q76561"]},
    "ru-072": {"questions":["The prince of Pereiaslavl who took Kyiv in 1113 and held it until 1125 was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Russian historians still argue over his part in what followed.","In 1111 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led a great combined army deep into the steppe, and the Polovtsian town of Sharukan paid the Rus' off with gifts rather than fight."],"sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 240, 255, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","A. V. Korenevskiy and N. D. Nikolaeva, “Polish Vector in Politics of Vladimir Monomakh and His Heirs: from the Lyubech Congress to the ‘Pereyaslavl Crisis,’” <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i> 1, no. 10 (2020): 337–352, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-10-337-352. [Open access]","V. B. Sukhomlyn and A. A. Nastiuk, “A Separate Peace between Volodymyr Vsevolodovych and Davyd Sviatoslavych, as a Secret Side of the Council of Liubech, 1097,” <i>Juridical Scientific and Electronic Journal</i>, no. 4 (2026): 25–34, https://doi.org/10.32782/2524-0374/2026-4/4. [Open access]","D. A. Borovkov, “Vladimir Monomakh and His Role in the Crisis of Ancient Russian Statehood (Based on the Works of Russian Historians of the 19th–21st Centuries),” <i>Slavianovedenie</i>, no. 2 (2025): 5–18, https://doi.org/10.7868/S2949392725020011. [Open access]","Vitaliy Nagirnyy, “An Unexpected Ally: The Influence of Alcohol on the Course of Military Campaigns in Rus’ in the Eleventh–Twelfth Centuries,” <i>Historický časopis</i> 71, no. 4 (2023): 709–721, https://doi.org/10.31577/histcaso.2023.71.4.8. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> was prince of Pereiaslavl and then of Kyiv, where he ruled from 1113 until 1125, and the last years of a Rus' whose princes still generally deferred to one man are his and his son's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took his Greek second name from his mother's family, and the editor's notice of him makes his life a run of expeditions against rival princes and against the steppe peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was one of the six who met at Liubech in 1097, and the settlement there gave him Pereiaslavl while confirming Chernihiv, the town his father had held, to his cousins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own account of himself counts 83 journeys, not reckoning the small ones, and nineteen peace treaties made with the Polovtsy, some with his father and some without him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the same passage he records freeing the chief Polovtsian princes from their irons and, a line later, cutting down other captured chiefs and throwing them into the river Slavlia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was not the senior prince of his generation and did not take Kyiv when he first could: the chronicle's notice says he refused the seat twice and consented only in 1113.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That made him grand prince by invitation rather than by the ladder of seniority the princes had followed since Yaroslav, which is one reason his reign reads as an exception to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1111 he led a great combined campaign far into the steppe, and when the Rus' army reached the Polovtsian town of Sharukan its people bought them off with gifts instead of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His leaning towards the crusading movement and the Western empire is held to have put an alliance between Kyiv and Kraków out of reach in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is also thought to have arranged that Pereiaslavl and Kyiv should descend in the family of his eldest son, at the expense of his younger sons, and the wars of his children and grandchildren followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does being invited to Kyiv in 1113 make Monomakh's reign an exception?","a":"Because Kyiv had passed by seniority since Yaroslav, along a ladder of brothers and cousins, and Monomakh was not the senior prince of his generation. Taking the seat by invitation put him there on a different title from the one the system recognised."},{"q":"Why is the tally of 83 journeys and nineteen treaties worth more than a round number of victories?","a":"Because it is what the prince himself chose to count. Treaties beside campaigns show a man managing a frontier rather than conquering one, and the number of them says the Polovtsy were dealt with by agreement at least as often as by battle."},{"q":"Why might his own succession arrangement have made the crisis that followed worse?","a":"Because settling Pereiaslavl and Kyiv on his eldest son's family cut his younger sons out of the ladder they would otherwise have climbed. Princes with no prospect of rising had nothing to lose by fighting, and the wars of his sons and grandsons are traced to it."}],"wiki":"Vladimir II Monomakh","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-073": {"questions":["Repentance, tears and almsgiving are called three small works in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which tells its readers that neither the hermit's life nor fasting is required of them.","Honour a guest whoever he is, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says, because travellers will make a man's name good or bad in every country they reach."],"sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 239–256, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Anastasiia A. Volkova, “‘Teaching’ of Vladimir Monomakh as Synthesis of Ethical Traditions Byzantine Patristics and National Paganism,” <i>RUDN Journal of Philosophy</i> 27, no. 2 (2023): 372–389, https://doi.org/10.22363/2313-2302-2023-27-2-372-389. [Open access]","Timofey Guimon, “‘Love to the Miserable’ of Old Rus Princes: Saint Vladimir and Andrey Bogolyubsky,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 14, no. 5 (127) (2023), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840026796-4. [Open access]","G. S. Solodova, “Structure of the Supreme Authorities in the First Period of Russian Statehood,” <i>Bulletin of Kemerovo State University. Series: Political, Sociological and Economic Sciences</i> 8, no. 2 (2023): 131–138, https://doi.org/10.21603/2500-3372-2023-8-2-131-138. [Open access]","Alexandra Vukovich, “Enthronement Rituals of the Princes of Rus’ (Twelfth–Thirteenth Centuries),” <i>FORUM: University of Edinburgh Postgraduate Journal of Culture and the Arts</i>, no. 17 (2013), https://doi.org/10.2218/forum.17.704. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>Instruction of Vladimir Monomakh</b> is a short book of precepts a prince of Kyiv wrote for his sons, and nothing else quite like it survives from Rus' before the Mongols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches us by accident, a copyist having dropped it, with a letter to a cousin and a prayer, into the middle of the chronicle's narrative, and it stands in one manuscript tradition only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prince opens by saying that he sat in his sledge and thought over the life God had led him through, and asks his children not to laugh at the writing but to take it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central claim is that salvation is cheap: repentance, tears and almsgiving are three small works, and the hermit's life, the monk's and the fasting other just men impose on themselves are not required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That argument is a version of the Byzantine theology of repentance, traced to John Chrysostom, with the idea of holiness lived out in the world taken from Basil the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The practical half is a list of duties for a man who governs: fear God, give alms widely, honour the old as a father and love the young as brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tells his sons to look into their own households rather than trust a steward, to set their own sentries before lying down, and never to take their armour off in haste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A guest is to be honoured above everything, poor or noble or an ambassador alike, because travellers will make a man's name good or bad in every country they reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Care for the poor was already one of the marks a chronicler looked for in a good prince, and the obituaries of princes name it among their virtues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The day it describes has the prince rise before the sun, pray, then sit in council with his druzhina, judge the people or hunt, which is the advisory half of Rus' government seen from inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does the text call repentance, tears and almsgiving small works?","a":"Because it is arguing that the demanding forms of holiness are optional. Set against the hermit's life, the monk's and long fasting, three acts anyone can perform look light, and the point of calling them small is to leave a layman with no excuse."},{"q":"Why would a prince tell his sons that hospitality matters more than almost anything?","a":"Because reputation travelled by the only people who moved between courts. The text says outright that travellers will make a man's name good or bad in every country they reach, so feeding a stranger is a piece of statecraft rather than a private kindness."},{"q":"Why is the work read as syncretic rather than simply devotional?","a":"Because its Byzantine theology of repentance sits beside older attitudes to death, burial and good fortune, so the ethics do not fit one tradition. The reading holds that a Christian frame was laid over inherited habits of thought rather than replacing them."}]},
    "ru-074": {"questions":["Eleven marriages between princes of Rus' and the daughters of steppe rulers are recorded, so the grandsons of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat in Rus' as well as on the steppe.","When a Rus' army reached the town of Sharukan in 1111 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> living there paid the attackers off with wine and gifts rather than fight them."],"sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 137–138, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","M. S. Osypenko, “Чотири згадки давньоруських літописів про перші русько-половецькі контакти 50–70-ті рр. XI ст.,” <i>The World of the Orient</i>, no. 2 (2014): 44–50, https://doi.org/10.15407/orientw2014.02.044. [Open access]","Yury V. Seleznev, “The Rus Grandchildren of the Polovtsian Princes (The Question of Polovtsian Kinship in the Political Struggle in Rus from the 11th to the First Half of the 13th Century),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 225–241, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.225-241. [Open access]","Vitaliy Nagirnyy, “An Unexpected Ally: The Influence of Alcohol on the Course of Military Campaigns in Rus’ in the Eleventh–Twelfth Centuries,” <i>Historický časopis</i> 71, no. 4 (2023): 709–721, https://doi.org/10.31577/histcaso.2023.71.4.8. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 14, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>Cumans</b> were a Turkic people of the western steppe whom the sources of Rus' call the Polovtsy, and who displaced the Pechenegs and the Torks in the middle of the 11th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle first meets them in 1055, when a leader named Bolush came to terms with Vsevolod and went back the way he had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first fighting came six years later: on 2 February 1061 they beat Vsevolod, plundered the country and left, and the chronicler calls it the first defeat Rus' suffered at their hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He names their prince Sokal, which is as much as the early entries give, and the notices of the next decade are short and scattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read together those first entries suggest a people occupied with the steppe's own quarrels, raiding locally rather than threatening the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were not only raiders, for a Rus' army marching deep into the steppe in 1111 reached a town called Sharukan whose people chose to buy the attackers off with wine and other gifts rather than fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The relationship ran through marriage as much as through war, and eleven marriages between princes of Rus' and the daughters of steppe rulers can be traced in the chronicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The children and grandchildren of those matches held seats in Rus' and could call on relatives across the frontier, so a prince's steppe kin were an asset in a quarrel at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That cuts both ways in the record: the same men appear leading armies against the Polovtsy and bringing Polovtsian troops into Rus' to settle a dispute between cousins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the princes fell out in the 1130s the chronicler of Novgorod noted that the Polovtsy and everyone else began mustering fighting men, which is how a divided Rus' looked from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does the chronicler call 1061 the first defeat rather than the first meeting?","a":"Because the first meeting, six years earlier, ended in terms: Bolush came, agreed with Vsevolod and went home. The chronicler is counting the first time the steppe beat a Rus' army in the field, which is the event he thinks worth a date."},{"q":"Why were marriages with steppe rulers useful to a prince of Rus'?","a":"Because they turned a frontier into a network of relatives. A prince with Polovtsian kin could call on their troops in a quarrel with his cousins, which is why the same men appear both fighting the Polovtsy and bringing them into Rus'."},{"q":"Why does the town of Sharukan complicate the picture of the Cumans as nomads?","a":"Because a town has inhabitants who can be reached, negotiated with and paid off, which is what happened in 1111. It shows the steppe had fixed points the Rus' could march to, not only herds and camps that moved away from an army."}],"wiki":"Cumans"},
    "ru-075": {"questions":["Monomakh is thought to have settled Pereiaslavl and Kyiv on the family of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, his eldest son, which cut the younger brothers out of the ladder.","Three years after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died the chronicler of Novgorod wrote that the whole Russian land was in great disorder and every side was mustering men."],"sources":["D. A. Borovkov, “Vladimir Monomakh and His Role in the Crisis of Ancient Russian Statehood (Based on the Works of Russian Historians of the 19th–21st Centuries),” <i>Slavianovedenie</i>, no. 2 (2025): 5–18, https://doi.org/10.7868/S2949392725020011. [Open access]","A. V. Korenevskiy and N. D. Nikolaeva, “Polish Vector in Politics of Vladimir Monomakh and His Heirs: from the Lyubech Congress to the ‘Pereyaslavl Crisis,’” <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i> 1, no. 10 (2020): 337–352, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-10-337-352. [Open access]","S. V. Alekseev, “Seniority in the Rurikid Dynasty: Socio-Cultural Ideas and Legitimacy,” <i>Humanities and Social Sciences. Bulletin of the Financial University</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 59–65, https://doi.org/10.26794/2226-7867-2023-13-3-59-65. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 13–14, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 199, 251, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Mstislav I of Kiev</b>, the eldest son of Vladimir Monomakh, ruled Kyiv from 1125 until 1132, and his death is the usual date for the end of a single Rus' held together from one seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle has him fighting his father's war in the north-east in 1096, taking Murom, raising the men of Rostov and Suzdal, driving Oleg out of Ryazan and going home to Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Monomakh is thought to have arranged that Pereiaslavl and Kyiv should descend in Mstislav's family rather than pass to his younger sons Yuri and Andrey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such an arrangement rewrote the older order, in which a seat went to the next brother by age, and it left the excluded branches with a grievance and nothing to lose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period from the council at Liubech in 1097 to the end of Mstislav's reign is accordingly read as a stabilisation rather than a settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What followed was not a single collapse but a long contest, and Russian historians trace the weakening of the Kyivan princes to the wars of Monomakh's sons and grandsons in the second third of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicler of Novgorod, writing of 1135, says the whole Russian land was in great disorder, that Kyiv and Chernihiv fought and that the Polovtsy and everyone else were mustering men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lands did not stop being Rus', and what changed was that Kyiv became one prize among several while the north-east and the south-west grew in weight against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their rulers took different views of what a common Rus' meant, and by the end of the process seniority was a claim made in several capitals at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Assessments of Monomakh himself remain split between the apologetic and the critical, and how far his own arrangements caused the crisis is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is 1132 used as the date the unity of Rus' ends?","a":"Because Mstislav was the last prince of Kyiv whose authority the other lands broadly accepted, and the wars of his brothers and nephews start immediately after him. The chronicler of Novgorod is describing the whole land as disordered within three years."},{"q":"Why would settling Kyiv on one son's family break the succession rather than secure it?","a":"Because the older order gave each brother a place on a ladder he could expect to climb. Fixing the best seats in the eldest son's line left the younger branches permanently excluded, and a prince with nothing to gain by waiting has no reason not to fight."},{"q":"Why is it misleading to call what followed the collapse of Rus'?","a":"Because the lands went on being Rus' and went on sharing a dynasty, a church and a law. What ended was Kyiv's monopoly: it became one prize among several while the north-east and the south-west rose, and seniority came to be claimed in more than one capital."}],"wiki":"Mstislav I of Kiev","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-076": {"questions":["Archaeology reads the town on the Volkhov as a princely seat down to 1136, after which it became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the prince lived outside the walls.","The name Novgorod the Great appears first in a Kyivan text, not a northern one, which is awkward for the identity the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later built on it."],"sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 14, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Vladimir A. Burov, “On the Origin of Novgorod. 862–1136 — Princely Town of Rurikids,” <i>Rossiiskaia arkheologiia</i>, no. 3 (2023): 187–197, https://doi.org/10.31857/s086960632303008x. [Open access]","Andrey Vinogradov, “The Role of Archbishop Nifont in the Novgorod Events of 1136,” <i>Slověne</i> 11, no. 2 (2022): 53–67, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2021.11.2.3. [Open access]","Pavel V. Lukin, “‘Novgorod the Great,’” <i>Slověne</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 383–413, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.15. [Open access]","Alexei A. Vovin, “Veche and the Terms ‘All Pskov’ and ‘Pskov Men’: The Russian Medieval City Assembly as a Communal Structure,” <i>Mesto a dejiny</i> 9, no. 2 (2020): 6–28, https://doi.org/10.33542/cah2020-2-01. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>Novgorod Republic</b> is the name given to the state on the Volkhov which from 1136 hired and dismissed its princes instead of receiving whichever one Kyiv sent it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeology reads the town before that as a princely seat, held by the Rurikid family since the 9th century as its own land, with fortresses raised on it in the 860s, the 940s and 1044.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The turn came in 1136, when the men of Novgorod called in the men of Pskov and Ladoga, shut Prince Vsevolod in the bishop's court with his family on 28 May and guarded him day and night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They kept him two months, let him out of the town on 15 July, took his son instead and then sent for Svyatoslav Olgovich from Chernihiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle lists the charges: he neglected the serfs, he had wanted a seat at Pereiaslavl, and he had ridden away from the army ahead of everyone and could not make up his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The archbishop was the other power in the town, and Nifont's part in the change is argued from his embassy to Kyiv and Chernihiv the winter before, when the discontent was already plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also refused to marry Svyatoslav in church, so the new prince was wedded by his own priests, which shows how far a Novgorod prelate could set himself against a prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The title Novgorod the Great, which the town's own documents later make the heart of its identity, is first found not there but in a Kyivan chronicle entry under 1141.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It spread through the Suzdal land in the second half of the century, where it distinguished this Novgorod from the smaller ones, and reached Novgorod itself much later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Comparison with Pskov suggests why such a state could hold: the horizontal ties inside these towns outweighed the vertical ones, in a pattern close to the early communes of western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why does 1136 matter more than the expulsion of any earlier prince?","a":"Because Novgorod had thrown out princes before without changing who chose the next one. In 1136 the town took counsel with Pskov and Ladoga, stated charges, and then sent for a prince of its own choosing from Chernihiv, which made the appointment the town's to make."},{"q":"Why are the three charges against Vsevolod worth reading closely?","a":"Because they are the terms on which the town thought a prince could be judged: care for its people, attention to Novgorod rather than a better seat elsewhere, and courage in the field. They read as a job description with a dismissal attached."},{"q":"Why is it awkward that Novgorod the Great first appears in a Kyivan text?","a":"Because the name became the centre of the town's own sense of itself. Finding it first in the south, then in the Suzdal land where it distinguished this Novgorod from smaller ones, makes it a label others applied before the town adopted it."}],"wiki":"Novgorod Republic","places":["pol:novgorod"]},
    "ru-077": {"questions":["Prince, mayor or any respectable party could summon the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and rival factions sometimes called two at once and settled the matter by fighting on the bridge.","Alongside the prince and his council of boyars, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the third element of supreme power in the early Rus' state."],"sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), x, xiv–xv, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Alexei A. Vovin, “Veche and the Terms ‘All Pskov’ and ‘Pskov Men’: The Russian Medieval City Assembly as a Communal Structure,” <i>Mesto a dejiny</i> 9, no. 2 (2020): 6–28, https://doi.org/10.33542/cah2020-2-01. [Open access]","G. S. Solodova, “Structure of the Supreme Authorities in the First Period of Russian Statehood,” <i>Bulletin of Kemerovo State University. Series: Political, Sociological and Economic Sciences</i> 8, no. 2 (2023): 131–138, https://doi.org/10.21603/2500-3372-2023-8-2-131-138. [Open access]","Pavel V. Lukin, “‘Novgorod the Great,’” <i>Slověne</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 383–413, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.15. [Open access]","Alexandra Vukovich, “Enthronement Rituals of the Princes of Rus’ (Twelfth–Thirteenth Centuries),” <i>FORUM: University of Edinburgh Postgraduate Journal of Culture and the Arts</i>, no. 17 (2013), https://doi.org/10.2218/forum.17.704. [Open access]"],"abstract":"The <b>veche</b> was the popular assembly of a town of Rus', and in Novgorod it became the body that decided who ruled there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It invited a prince and could arraign, imprison or expel him; it elected and deposed the mayor and the lesser officers; and it chose the archbishop from the few names it had picked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It decided peace and war and punished criminals, and an unpopular man might be thrown from the great bridge over the Volkhov when an assembly broke up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alongside the prince and his council of boyars it made the third element of supreme power in the early Rus' state, which combined sole rule, collegiality and a measure of self-organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its business was not only political: it banned pagan practice, punished sorcery and deposed prelates it disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In form it respected the old Slavonic principle that a decision be unanimous, but there was no real veto on the Volkhov and a minority could be beaten, ducked or driven out of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A prince, the mayor or any respectable party among the nobles or the commons could call it, and it met either outside St Sophia or in the Court of Yaroslav on the trading side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rival factions sometimes summoned rival assemblies, which ended in a conference on the bridge between them or in a fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle shows what that meant in practice: in 1209 the town held an assembly over its mayor, and in 1167 and 1209 mayors were executed or proscribed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparison with Pskov, where the same institution is best documented, suggests reading it as the political form of a community of townsmen rather than a parliament of estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why did unanimity in form not protect a minority at the veche?","a":"Because nothing enforced it. The rule said a decision should be agreed by all, and in practice a losing party was beaten, ducked or expelled until the vote looked unanimous, so the principle described the outcome rather than restraining the majority."},{"q":"Why does it matter that the assembly chose the archbishop?","a":"Because it made the church in Novgorod answerable to the town and not only to the metropolitan at Kyiv. Narrowing the candidates to a short list the assembly approved put the most powerful permanent office in the town within its gift."},{"q":"Why is it better read as a community of townsmen than as a parliament?","a":"Because it had no estates, no fixed membership and no procedure for representation. What it had were the horizontal ties of a town whose people could all be summoned, which is the pattern the better-documented Pskov assembly shows."}],"wiki":"Veche","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-078": {"questions":["A Novgorod man's servant went from the Pechora people to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and came back with a story about men walled inside mountains who bartered furs for iron.","A treaty of 1264 lists <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Perm and the Pechora among the domains of Novgorod, and Moscow took the claim over with the town in 1471."],"sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xv–xvii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 33, 36–37, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 2, 196–197, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Pavel Petrukhin, “Where and When Did the Novgorodian Savva Gather the Tribute?,” <i>Slověne</i> 8, no. 1 (2019): 55–108, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2019.8.1.3. [Open access]","Pavel V. Lukin, “‘Novgorod the Great,’” <i>Slověne</i> 7, no. 2 (2018): 383–413, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.2.15. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Yugra</b> was the land of the Ob-Ugrian peoples on both flanks of the northern Urals, and the furthest place from which Novgorod drew tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle's list of the peoples of the north puts it with the Perm, the Pechora and the Yam beyond the portage, all of them paying to Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fullest early notice is a traveller's tale: a Novgorod man sent his servant to the Pechora people and on from them to Yugra, where he was told of men shut inside mountains who thrust an iron finger through a gap and bartered furs for knives and axes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicler took the story for the nations Alexander was said to have walled up, but the frame of it, that Pechora paid tribute and that Yugra lay beyond, is ordinary information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Novgorod men had reached Lapland, the White Sea and the Urals earlier still, and the disaster at the Iron Gates in 1032, from which few returned, is probably one of those passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The trade was in furs and silver and it was resisted: in 1187 the collectors in Pechora and Yugra were killed and about a hundred notable men fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A punitive force under the voevoda Yadrei went out in 1193, besieged a town for five weeks, was told the defenders were gathering silver and sables, and had its leaders cut down in parties of ten, thirty and fifty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Novgorod kept the claim all the same, and an agreement of 1264 names Yugra beside Perm and the Pechora among the domains of the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far this northern business shaped the town's own documents is disputed, and the reading of one birch-bark letter as a tax collector's report from the far north has been argued against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Moscow broke Novgorod in 1471 it took over the northern claims with the town, and the name of the town it had taken them from was by then Novgorod the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is a story about men walled inside mountains still useful evidence?","a":"Because the frame around the marvel is administrative. To reach the tale at all a Novgorod household had to send a servant through the Pechora people, who are said to pay tribute, and on to Yugra beyond them, which is a trade route described in passing."},{"q":"Why did Novgorod keep asserting a claim to Yugra after two disasters?","a":"Because the northern furs and silver were worth the risk, and a claim once made was an asset in itself. The treaty of 1264 lists Yugra with Perm and the Pechora among the republic's domains whether or not tribute was arriving that year."},{"q":"Why does it matter that Moscow inherited the claim rather than making its own?","a":"Because it makes the Russian advance into Siberia continuous with a northern trade Novgorod had run for centuries. Taking the town in 1471 brought the tribute rights with it, so the later conquest began from a position already held."}],"wiki":"Yugra","places":["adm1:rus:ru-khm"]},
    "ru-079": {"questions":["The inauguration rites of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were adapted from Kyiv's and given fresh Byzantine trappings, which is one measure of how seriously it took its own standing.","Monomakh founded a town on the Klyazma in the forests behind Suzdal, and it gave its name to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the north-east outgrew the south."],"sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 240, 251, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Alexandra Vukovich, “Enthronement Rituals of the Princes of Rus’ (Twelfth–Thirteenth Centuries),” <i>FORUM: University of Edinburgh Postgraduate Journal of Culture and the Arts</i>, no. 17 (2013), https://doi.org/10.2218/forum.17.704. [Open access]","S. V. Alekseev, “Seniority in the Rurikid Dynasty: Socio-Cultural Ideas and Legitimacy,” <i>Humanities and Social Sciences. Bulletin of the Financial University</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 59–65, https://doi.org/10.26794/2226-7867-2023-13-3-59-65. [Open access]","Hennadii Noha, “The ‘Sword of Punishment’ of Rus: Creating the Image of Andrey Yuryevich (Bogolyubsky) in the Kyivan Chronicle,” <i>The Journal of V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University, Series “Philology”</i>, no. 96 (2025): 109–114, https://doi.org/10.26565/2227-1864-2025-96-16. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 13–14, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Vladimir-Suzdal</b> is the name given to the principality that grew in the forests north-east of the Oka, around the older towns of Rostov and Suzdal and then around Vladimir on the Klyazma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was frontier country in the 11th century, reached from the south through the lands of the Vyatichi, and the chronicle has the young Monomakh sent that way on his first journey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town of Vladimir itself is credited to Monomakh, who is said to have founded it in the woods of the Suzdal country and given it his own name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its princes were his descendants, and the weight of the north-east rose steadily against Kyiv as the 12th century went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those princes took a different view of what a common Rus' meant from the rulers of the south-west, and by the end of the process seniority was being claimed in more than one capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rise shows in ceremony as well as in armies, for the rites by which a prince was set on his throne were carried north from Kyiv, reworked there and given new Byzantine trappings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows in building too, in a school of white limestone churches that had no equal in the other lands of Rus' at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And it shows in ambition: one of its princes is described as trying to make a new great capital in the forest country rather than take the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle of Novgorod records the cost of that from the other side, with men of Suzdal killed in numbers outside Novgorod in the fighting of 1134.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the 13th century its grand princes, not those of Kyiv, were the ones whose seniority the rest of Rus' had to reckon with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why did the north-east rise while Kyiv fell back?","a":"Because it was new country being opened rather than old country being fought over. Its princes were building towns, churches and a following of their own in the forests, while the south spent the same decades contesting a single seat among too many claimants."},{"q":"Why is the reworking of enthronement ritual evidence of political weight?","a":"Because ceremony is a claim about standing. Carrying Kyiv's rites north, reworking them and adding Byzantine trappings says the northern prince meant to be understood as a ruler of the same kind, not a deputy holding a frontier."},{"q":"Why does a school of white stone churches belong in a political account?","a":"Because quarried limestone, masons and a decade of work are what a prince can command rather than what he can buy. Building on that scale in the forest country advertised resources the older lands could not match at the time."}],"wiki":"Vladimir-Suzdal","places":["pol:vladimir_suzdal"]},
    "ru-080": {"questions":["Calling him great prince and his killers mere servants, the inscription about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest closely datable piece of writing from the north-east.","The chronicle's account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written by a man in his own service, so the noble and God-chosen ruler in it is a portrait made to order."],"sources":["Alexey A. Gippius and Savva M. Mikheev, “‘Assassins of the Great Prince Andrey’: An Inscription about the Murder of Andrey Bogolyubsky from Pereslavl-Zalessky,” <i>Slověne</i> 9, no. 2 (2020): 63–102, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2020.9.2.3. [Open access]","Hennadii Noha, “The ‘Sword of Punishment’ of Rus: Creating the Image of Andrey Yuryevich (Bogolyubsky) in the Kyivan Chronicle,” <i>The Journal of V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University, Series “Philology”</i>, no. 96 (2025): 109–114, https://doi.org/10.26565/2227-1864-2025-96-16. [Open access]","Timofey Guimon, “‘Love to the Miserable’ of Old Rus Princes: Saint Vladimir and Andrey Bogolyubsky,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 14, no. 5 (127) (2023), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840026796-4. [Open access]","D. A. Borovkov, “Vladimir Monomakh and His Role in the Crisis of Ancient Russian Statehood (Based on the Works of Russian Historians of the 19th–21st Centuries),” <i>Slavianovedenie</i>, no. 2 (2025): 5–18, https://doi.org/10.7868/S2949392725020011. [Open access]","Alexandra Vukovich, “Enthronement Rituals of the Princes of Rus’ (Twelfth–Thirteenth Centuries),” <i>FORUM: University of Edinburgh Postgraduate Journal of Culture and the Arts</i>, no. 17 (2013), https://doi.org/10.2218/forum.17.704. [Open access]"],"abstract":"<b>Andrey Bogolyubsky</b> was prince of the Vladimir and Suzdal land from 1157 until his murder at Bogolyubovo on 29 June 1174, and he ruled the north-east rather than moving south to Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2015 a long inscription was uncovered on the outer wall of the southern apse of the twelfth-century Transfiguration cathedral at Pereslavl-Zalessky, cursing his killers and asking eternal memory for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was probably cut in 1175 or 1176 and is the oldest piece of writing from the north-east that can be dated at all closely, which makes it a check on the chronicles rather than an illustration of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It calls Andrey great prince and lumps his killers together as servants, although some of the men it names were of high standing, and it lists between eleven and thirteen of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The list is the first case in Rus' of the church anathematising men for a political crime, and it confirms that the chronicle accounts of the murder are broadly reliable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The written account of him is not neutral, the entries about Andrey in the Kyivan chronicle being attributed to a man in his own service whose business was to make him noble and God-chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Read against that intention the same text shows a prince who was not much liked by those closest to him, who ignored church custom and other holders of power, and who made himself a stranger among his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His obituary supplies the one concrete instance of a prince's charity that the chronicles give, in food and drink carried through the streets for the poor and the imprisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was modelled on what the chronicle says of Vladimir who converted Rus', so the memory of the earlier reign was still being drawn on two centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His claim came through a branch his own grandfather had passed over, which is part of why a prince of his standing had to build a capital and a ceremony of his own instead of inheriting one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","why":[{"q":"Why is an inscription cut a year after the murder worth more than the chronicle's account of it?","a":"Not more, but differently. It was made within two years, names the killers openly and can be dated closely, so it gives an independent check on a chronicle account written by a man who was in the dead prince's pay."},{"q":"Why does the inscription call men of high standing mere servants?","a":"Because the word is doing work. Calling the conspirators servants denies them the status that might have justified what they did, and it goes with the curse laid on them, which is the first time the church in Rus' is seen anathematising a political crime."},{"q":"Why would a prince of the north-east copy the charity of Vladimir the Great?","a":"Because it was a recognised sign of a good ruler and it was remembered in detail: food and drink carried through the streets for the poor. Repeating a gesture two centuries old claimed the earlier prince's standing for a new capital that had none."}],"wiki":"Andrey Bogolyubsky","places":["pl:q762099"]},
    "ru-081": {"questions":["Andrey Bogolyubsky took the standing of senior prince and stayed in Vladimir after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with his brother Gleb in a capital he had no intention of living in himself.","The Kyivan chronicler laments churches stripped of their bells and ornaments in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Novgorod annalist gives the same march on the city a single flat sentence."],"abstract":"The <b>sack of Kyiv</b> was the storming and plunder of the city between 8 and 12 March 1169 by a coalition of eleven princes assembled by Andrey Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal. It fell in the middle of a long contest for the Kyivan seat, during which the compilers of the Kyivan chronicle had begun to argue about the legitimacy of a claimant rather than about seniority alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Andrey did not ride with the army himself; the Novgorod annalist lists his son among the princes who marched, with the men of Smolensk, Polotsk, Murom and Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city's prince, Mstislav Izyaslavich, chose not to give battle and withdrew. Andrey's brother Gleb was afterwards prince at Kyiv, where he died the next year, while Andrey himself kept his residence at Vladimir and took the standing that went with the city he would not live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Kyivan compilation treats the four days as sacrilege, and its portrait of Andrey as a punishing sword was made by scribes who had watched what his men did to their own churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chronicle notices of bells carried off Rus' churches cluster in 1066 or 1067, 1146 and 1169, and are written up in the same breath as the plundering of the Temple at Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle, whose own prince was the son of the man driven out, gives the march one sentence and says only that Mstislav “did not fight with them, he retired of his own accord from Kiev”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That same coalition came north the following winter and was beaten outside Novgorod on 25 February, which the Novgorod scribe reports at length and credits to the prayers of his own bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vsevolod Yurievich, Andrey's youngest brother, came back to Rus' from a childhood in Byzantium in that same year of 1169, and his own rise in the north-east is dated from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hennadii Noha, “Wars for the Capital in the Kyivan Chronicle,” <i>Слово і Час</i>, no. 5 (2024): 41–57, https://doi.org/10.33608/0236-1477.2024.05.41-57. [Open access]","Hennadii Noha, “The ‘Sword of Punishment’ of Rus: Creating the Image of Andrey Yuryevich (Bogolyubsky) in the Kyivan Chronicle,” <i>The Journal of V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University, Series “Philology”</i>, no. 96 (2025): 109–114, https://doi.org/10.26565/2227-1864-2025-96-16. [Open access]","Y. Kezha, “«И колоколы съима у святыя Софие»: The Removal of the Temple Bells in Ancient Rus,” <i>Herald of Polotsk State University. Series A, Humanity Sciences</i>, no. 2 (2023): 30–35, https://doi.org/10.52928/2070-1608-2023-67-2-30-35. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 26–7, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Andrey A. Kuznetsov, “Vsevolod Yurievich (Big Nest) in 1169—1183: From Dmitrok to the Vladimir Prince,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 15, no. 5 (139) (2024), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840031430-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Andrey Bogolyubsky not move to Kyiv after taking it?","a":"His power rested on the north-east, where he had built his own residence, churches and following. Kyiv was the seat that carried seniority, but holding it meant living among princes and townsmen who were not his. He took the standing, his brother Gleb was prince at Kyiv instead, and his own court stayed at Vladimir."},{"q":"Why do the Kyivan and Novgorod accounts of 1169 differ so much in weight?","a":"The Kyivan compilers were writing about their own churches being stripped, and their portrait of Andrey was built out of that injury. The Novgorod scribe was writing for a city with its own quarrel to record, and gave the march on Kyiv a single flat sentence before turning to the siege of his own walls."},{"q":"Why does the chronicle record the carrying off of bells so particularly?","a":"For an 11th- and 12th-century scribe, bells were church furniture of the same order as relics and ornaments, so their removal stood for the desecration of the building. Notices of it are set beside the plundering of the Temple at Jerusalem, which told a reader how to judge the act."}],"places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-083": {"questions":["Roman Mstyslavych's son Danylo had rebuilt his father's inheritance by 1238 and ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the west while the rest of Rus' was being overrun from the steppe.","Stone churches, imported ornament and a chronicle of its own mark <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the land where the culture of Rus' kept going after the southern capital had been sacked."],"abstract":"<b>Galicia–Volhynia</b> was the western state of Rus' founded in 1199 when Roman Mstyslavych, prince of Volhynia since 1170, brought Galicia under his rule as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two lands had been held by different branches of the family before that, and neither had shown much sign of joining the other. The settlement made at Liubech in 1097 gave Terebovlia to Vasilko and Peremyshl to his brother Volodar, and within the same year Vasilko was lured to Kyiv, seized and blinded by men acting for his rivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volhynia went to David Igorevich and descended in his line, so that the two western lands grew up as neighbours rather than as parts of one inheritance. By the middle of the 12th century the Galician princes could put armies into the field against Poland and Hungary, and the whole Russian Land once marched against Galich, devastated its countryside and took not one of its towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After Roman's death in 1205 the inheritance was fought over by Hungary, by Poland and by rival princes until his son Danylo had put it together again by 1238, and it lasted under Danylo's descendants until 1340.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own record is the Galician–Volhynian Chronicle, a 13th-century compilation surviving in the Hypatian manuscript, whose several stages of editing can still be traced through the text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That chronicle is the reason a western land of Rus' in this period can be followed year by year at all. Its churches were built in the forms that had reached Rus' from Byzantium, and the same plans and the same liturgical demands shaped building in Galicia as at Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its craftsmen worked in ornament and technique that travelled in both directions between Rus' and the Latin kingdoms next door, which is what makes the land's decorative work look unlike that of the Dnieper towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Galicia-Volhynia, Principality of,” in <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CG%5CA%5CGalicia6VolhyniaPrincipalityof.htm. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 201–3, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 18, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","O. P. Tolochko, ed., <i>Galician–Volhynian Chronicle: Textology</i> (Kyiv: Akademperiodyka, 2020), https://doi.org/10.15407/akademperiodyka.419.929. [Open access]","M. H. Dovhan, “Sacred Architecture in the Sociocultural Space of Kyivan Rus’ and the Principality of Galicia-Volhynia,” <i>Актуальні проблеми філософії та соціології</i>, no. 57 (2025): 35–40, https://doi.org/10.32782/apfs.v057.2025.6. [Open access]","Oleg Chuyko, “Decorative and Applied Arts of the Principality of Galicia-Volhynia in Terms of Mutual Cultural Exchange with European Countries,” <i>Humanities Science Current Issues</i> 2, no. 42 (2021): 67–75, https://doi.org/10.24919/2308-4863/42-2-11. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Galicia and Volhynia joined only at the end of the 12th century?","a":"They had been assigned to different lines at Liubech in 1097 and descended separately for a century, Galicia through Vasilko and Volodar and Volhynia through David Igorevich. The union came when Roman Mstyslavych, already prince of Volhynia, took Galicia in 1199 and held both together."},{"q":"Why does the state matter to the history of Rus' after 1240?","a":"Its centre lay west of the steppe frontier, so it was not broken as the Dnieper lands were. Danylo had rebuilt his father's inheritance by 1238 and his descendants ruled until 1340, which meant that the institutions, the building and the chronicle-writing of Rus' continued there for another century."},{"q":"Why does the land's decorative work look different from that of the Dnieper towns?","a":"Its churches followed the plans and liturgical needs Rus' had taken from Byzantium, so the religious architecture is recognisably of the same family. Its ornament, though, was made in constant exchange with the Latin kingdoms on its western border, and carries forms and techniques that travelled both ways."}],"wiki":"Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia","places":["pol:galicia_volhynia"]},
    "ru-084": {"questions":["Vseslav Bryachislavich ruled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for well over fifty years, seized Novgorod, spent a few months on the Kyivan throne and died in his own town in 1101.","Monomakh burned it, Mstislav deported its princes to Byzantium, and Novgorod and Pskov fired its countryside, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept a separate dynasty for two centuries."],"abstract":"The <b>Principality of Polotsk</b> was the land of the upper Western Dvina, and the one part of Rus' that descended in a single line rather than passing round the family. The chronicle tells how Vladimir asked for the daughter of Rogvolod of Polotsk, was refused as the son of a servant, and answered by taking the town, killing Rogvolod and two of his sons, and marrying Rogneda by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The son she bore him, Izyaslav, was seated at Polotsk when Vladimir distributed the towns among his children, and the principality stayed with Izyaslav's descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because that branch stood outside the rotation, its princes had no claim on Kyiv and no obligation to give up Polotsk. When the princes of Rus' met at Liubech in 1097 they acknowledged Polotsk as Vseslav Bryachislavich's, which was a recognition of fact rather than a grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Vseslav was the land's most notorious prince: he took Novgorod in 1067 and carried off the bells of its cathedral, an act the chronicles set beside the stripping of a temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The three sons of Yaroslav sacked Minsk and beat him in a snowstorm on the Nemiga on 3 March, then swore on the cross that he would come to no harm and imprisoned him anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A rising in Kyiv the next year freed him and put him briefly on the Kyivan throne itself, from which he slipped away home by night; he died at Polotsk on 14 April 1101.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vladimir Monomakh's own account of his campaigns includes going against Polotsk and burning it, and Mstislav I afterwards reoccupied the land and had its princes deported to Byzantium for customs that reportedly included selling their own subjects into slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Novgorod and Pskov burned the Polotsk countryside in 1168 and its prince rode with Andrey Bogolyubsky's coalition against Kyiv the next year<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>, while in later poetry Vseslav survived as a sorcerer and shape-changer out of older Slavic story rather than as the prince the chronicles judged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 61, 100, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Leonard A. Magnus, <i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), Introduction, xx, xxii–xxiii, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","Y. Kezha, “«И колоколы съима у святыя Софие»: The Removal of the Temple Bells in Ancient Rus,” <i>Herald of Polotsk State University. Series A, Humanity Sciences</i>, no. 2 (2023): 30–35, https://doi.org/10.52928/2070-1608-2023-67-2-30-35. [Open access]","<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 141–2, 147, 215, 251, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 26, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. V. Leonova, “Vseslav of Polotsk in «The Lay of Igor’s Campaign»: Authorial Intention and Mythological Context,” <i>RUDN Journal of Studies in Literature and Journalism</i> 31, no. 4 (2015): 7–16, https://journals.rudn.ru/literary-criticism/article/view/13346. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Polotsk stay with one family when the rest of Rus' rotated?","a":"Vladimir seated his son by Rogneda there, and the land descended to that son's heirs rather than re-entering the pool of towns the senior princes moved between. Having no claim on Kyiv, the Polotsk line also had no reason to leave, which made the principality a permanent separate interest inside Rus'."},{"q":"Why was Vseslav's seizure of Novgorod remembered as more than a raid?","a":"He carried off the bells of St Sophia, and for the chroniclers bells were church furniture of the same order as relics, so their removal stood for the desecration of the building itself. The notice sits with other such reports beside the plundering of the Temple at Jerusalem."},{"q":"Why did the senior princes keep having to deal with Polotsk by force?","a":"Its line could not be brought into the rotation, so the ordinary machinery of seniority and reassignment had no purchase on it. What was left was invasion: the sons of Yaroslav beat Vseslav on the Nemiga, Monomakh burned the town, and Mstislav finally deported its princes to Byzantium."}],"wiki":"Principality of Polotsk","places":["pol:polotsk"]},
    "ru-085": {"questions":["Steppe marriages and steppe alliances ran through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose prince was greeted in 1147 by Polovtsian envoys using a formula borrowed straight out of Turkic.","Fifty years of pressure from Vsevolod the Big Nest and his sons drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of northern Rus' and left it much weakened when the Mongols arrived."],"abstract":"The <b>Principality of Chernigov</b> was the land of the middle Desna and one of the three senior seats of Rus', ranking with Kyiv and Pereyaslavl. Yaroslav the Wise divided the towns among his sons on his deathbed in 1054 and gave Chernigov to Sviatoslav, and the following year Sviatoslav duly took his seat there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement made at Liubech in 1097 confirmed Chernigov, with Peremyshl, to Sviatoslav's sons Oleg, David and Yaroslav, which fixed the land on their line for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From Oleg the branch took the name by which the chronicles know it, the Olgovichi. Its system of princely power was never a settled one, and the instability of the arrangement is part of why the land quarrelled so much with its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Olgovichi were the Rus' princes closest to the steppe, and the closeness shows in small things: when Polovtsian envoys came to Sviatoslav Olgovich in 1147 they greeted him with a formula taken over from Turkic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land also threw off a branch of its own at Novgorod-Seversk, which held together as a separate princely house from the end of the 12th century into the 14th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That branch produced Igor Svyatoslavich, whose raid on the Cumans in 1185 became the subject of the best-known poem in Old East Slavic. The principality's long decline was the work of the north-east rather than the steppe: Vsevolod the Big Nest and his son Yaroslav fought the Olgovichi over Ryazan, southern Pereyaslavl and Novgorod across roughly fifty years from 1180.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the time the Mongols came the Chernigov princes had been pushed out of northern Rus' altogether, and they met the invasion with much less weight than the land's rank suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 137, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Leonard A. Magnus, <i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), Introduction, xx, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","N. V. Logacheva, “Peculiarities of Princely Power in Chernigov,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i> 2, no. 2 (2014): 76–84, https://journals.rudn.ru/russian-history/article/view/3876. [Open access]","Timofey Guimon and Vladimir Tishin, “A Turkic Greeting Formula in a Rus Chronicle: Polovtsian Envoys to Prince Svyatoslav Olgovich in 1147,” <i>Drevneishie gosudarstva Vostochnoi Evropy</i> 2019–2020 (2020): 267–296, https://doi.org/10.32608/1560-1382-2020-41-267-296. [Open access]","С. Н. Келембет, “Princes Novgorod-Seversky: The End of the 12th – The Beginning of the 14th Century,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 4 (78) (2019), https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2019.78.4.001. [Open access]","Andrey A. Kuznetsov, “Conflicts and Reconciliations of Vsevolod the Big Nest and His Sons with Chernigov Princes (1170s–1230s),” <i>Drevneishie gosudarstva Vostochnoi Evropy</i> 46 (2025): 140–158, https://doi.org/10.32608/1560-1382-2025-46-140-158. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chernigov end up with a line of its own rather than in the rotation?","a":"Yaroslav's division of 1054 gave it to Sviatoslav, and the Liubech settlement of 1097 confirmed it to Sviatoslav's three sons rather than returning it to the pool. That made the Olgovichi a standing interest with a base of their own, able to contest Kyiv without risking the loss of their home land."},{"q":"Why were the Chernigov princes so closely tied to the steppe?","a":"Their land lay nearest the Polovtsian pastures, so alliance and marriage there were worth more to them than to princes further north. The connection went deep enough to reach the language of diplomacy: Polovtsian envoys greeted Sviatoslav Olgovich in 1147 with a formula carried over from Turkic."},{"q":"Why did Chernigov lose its standing before the Mongols arrived?","a":"The damage was done by Vladimir-Suzdal, not the steppe. Vsevolod the Big Nest and his son Yaroslav pressed the Olgovichi hard over Ryazan, southern Pereyaslavl and Novgorod for about fifty years from 1180, and the result was that Chernigov had been shut out of northern Rus' before the invasion came."}],"wiki":"Principality of Chernigov","places":["pol:chernigov"]},
    "ru-087": {"questions":["Whether a church was raised in thin brick at Kyiv or in white limestone at Vladimir, its plan was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had come up the Black Sea from Byzantium.","Andrey Bogolyubsky swapped brick for white stone in the middle of the 12th century, but kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the liturgy that had asked for it in the first place."],"abstract":"A <b>cross-in-square</b> church puts a dome over the central bay of a roughly square building, carries it on four internal supports, and vaults the four arms around it so that a cross is inscribed in the plan. It was the standard form of the middle Byzantine period, and it spread along the southern, eastern and northern shores of the Black Sea with the craftsmen and the clergy who built in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rus' took its architecture from that world along with its Christianity, and the earliest stone churches at Kyiv were built by masons working in the Byzantine manner. What the plan was for was the liturgy performed inside it, which is why the same arrangement of bays, screens and galleries recurs in lands as far apart as Kyiv and Galicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The building material, by contrast, was local and changed from place to place. <br><br> In the south the walls were laid in thin brick, and Andrey Bogolyubsky's switch to white limestone in the middle of the 12th century was so abrupt that its sources are still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For him the choice was not practical but scriptural: the whiteness came out of the Old Testament, and it produced a north-eastern manner with no close parallel in the neighbouring countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The forms themselves also travelled west as well as south, and the Vladimir churches have been read against Lombard Romanesque work of the same decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Dormition Cathedral at Vladimir is the building where that combination of an inherited plan and a new material and ornament can best be followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What went inside was inherited too, down to the bells, which the chroniclers treated as furniture of the same order as relics and whose removal they wrote up as the stripping of a temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Andrey Vinogradov, “The Mid-Byzantine Traditions of Church Architecture on the Southern, Eastern, and Northern Black Sea Shores,” <i>Античная древность и средние века</i> 46 (2018): 73–89, https://doi.org/10.15826/adsv.2018.46.004. [Open access]","M. H. Dovhan, “Sacred Architecture in the Sociocultural Space of Kyivan Rus’ and the Principality of Galicia-Volhynia,” <i>Актуальні проблеми філософії та соціології</i>, no. 57 (2025): 35–40, https://doi.org/10.32782/apfs.v057.2025.6. [Open access]","Alexandr Ignatevich Skvortsov, “The Sacred Nature of White Stone in the Temple Construction of Andrey Bogolyubsky,” <i>Ethnic Culture</i> 3, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://doi.org/10.31483/r-98924. [Open access]","Вл. В. Седов, “Churches of the Intercession on the Nerl’ and of the Nativity of the Theotokos in Bogolyubovo in the Context of Lombardy’s Romanesque Architecture,” <i>Architectural Archeology</i> (2020): 101–126, https://doi.org/10.25681/iaras.2020.978-5-94375-327-5.101-126. [Open access]","Вл. В. Седов, “Успенский собор во Владимире и архитектура князя Андрея Боголюбского,” <i>Architectural Archeology</i>, no. 5 (2025): 7–28, https://doi.org/10.25681/iaras.2023.978-5-94375-411-1.7-28. [Open access]","Y. Kezha, “«И колоколы съима у святыя Софие»: The Removal of the Temple Bells in Ancient Rus,” <i>Herald of Polotsk State University. Series A, Humanity Sciences</i>, no. 2 (2023): 30–35, https://doi.org/10.52928/2070-1608-2023-67-2-30-35. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rus' build its churches to a Byzantine plan rather than inventing one?","a":"The plan existed to serve a liturgy, and Rus' had taken the liturgy from Byzantium along with the clergy who performed it. The form came with the craftsmen who knew how to build it, spreading around the Black Sea shores, so the same arrangement of domed bay, supports and vaulted arms turns up wherever that rite was celebrated."},{"q":"Why does the switch from brick to white stone matter if the plan stayed the same?","a":"It changed what a church looked like and what could be done to its surfaces, since limestone can be carved as brick cannot. The change came suddenly in the mid-12th century north-east, and the reasons argued for it are scriptural rather than practical, which is why the resulting manner has no close parallel nearby."},{"q":"Why are the Vladimir churches compared with Romanesque buildings in Italy?","a":"Their carved stone surfaces and some of their detail have no source in Byzantine brick building, so the comparison is a way of asking where the masons and the motifs came from. Reading the Nerl and Bogolyubovo churches against Lombard work of the same decades tests whether western craftsmen or western models were involved."}],"wiki":"Cross-in-square","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-088": {"questions":["Carved reliefs and blind arcading make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look unlike anything in brick-built Rus', and its stonework has been read against Romanesque work in Lombardy.","The chronicle notices are read more than one way, so the year work began on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still argued over even though the prince who ordered it is not in doubt."],"abstract":"The <b>Church of the Intercession on the Nerl</b> is a small single-domed church of white limestone standing in the water meadows where the Nerl runs into the Klyazma, below the princely residence at Bogolyubovo. It belongs to the building Andrey Bogolyubsky carried out in the north-east during his rule there between 1157 and 1177.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exactly when work on it began is not settled: the chronicle notices can be read in more than one way, and the question has been argued from the building as well as from the texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is built in the white limestone Andrey adopted in place of brick in the middle of the 12th century, a change so abrupt that its sources are still contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For him the whiteness carried an Old Testament sense, and what it produced was a north-eastern manner with no close parallel in the surrounding countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The plan underneath is the ordinary middle Byzantine one that had reached Rus' with its clergy, so what is unusual about the church is its surface rather than its shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That surface is the point: carved reliefs and a band of blind arcading run round walls that in a brick church would have been left plain. Those features have no source in Byzantine brick building, and the Nerl church and the neighbouring Nativity church at Bogolyubovo have both been read against Lombard Romanesque work of the same decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The comparison is a way of asking where the masons and the motifs came from rather than a settled answer about them. The same combination of an inherited plan with a new material can be followed at larger scale in the Dormition Cathedral at Vladimir, the other great building of Andrey's programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexandr Ignatevich Skvortsov, “The Sacred Nature of White Stone in the Temple Construction of Andrey Bogolyubsky,” <i>Ethnic Culture</i> 3, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://doi.org/10.31483/r-98924. [Open access]","Н. И. Солнцев, “К вопросу о датировке и символике Церкви Покрова Богородицы на Нерли,” <i>Via in Tempore. История. Политология</i> 50, no. 3 (2023): 662–672, https://doi.org/10.52575/2687-0967-2023-50-3-662-672. [Open access]","Andrey Vinogradov, “The Mid-Byzantine Traditions of Church Architecture on the Southern, Eastern, and Northern Black Sea Shores,” <i>Античная древность и средние века</i> 46 (2018): 73–89, https://doi.org/10.15826/adsv.2018.46.004. [Open access]","Вл. В. Седов, “Churches of the Intercession on the Nerl’ and of the Nativity of the Theotokos in Bogolyubovo in the Context of Lombardy’s Romanesque Architecture,” <i>Architectural Archeology</i> (2020): 101–126, https://doi.org/10.25681/iaras.2020.978-5-94375-327-5.101-126. [Open access]","Вл. В. Седов, “Успенский собор во Владимире и архитектура князя Андрея Боголюбского,” <i>Architectural Archeology</i>, no. 5 (2025): 7–28, https://doi.org/10.25681/iaras.2023.978-5-94375-411-1.7-28. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the year the church was begun still uncertain?","a":"The chronicle notices that bear on it can be read in more than one way, and no inscription fixes the date. Arguments have therefore been made from the building itself as well as from the texts, which is why the question is treated as open even though the prince who ordered it is not in doubt."},{"q":"Why does the church look so unlike a church at Kyiv?","a":"The plan is the same Byzantine arrangement, but the material is not. Andrey replaced brick with white limestone in the mid-12th century, and limestone can be carved where brick cannot, so the walls carry reliefs and blind arcading that a southern church of the same plan would have had no way of showing."},{"q":"Why do scholars compare it with buildings in northern Italy?","a":"Its carved stonework and some of its detail have no source in the Byzantine brick tradition Rus' inherited, so the resemblance to Lombard Romanesque work of the same decades is worth testing. The comparison asks whether western masons or western models reached Vladimir; it is a question rather than an answer."}],"wiki":"Church of the Intercession on the Nerl","places":["pl:q762099"]},
    "ru-089": {"questions":["Anna Comnena calls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the axe-bearing barbarians from Thule and says their loyalty to the emperors was a pledge handed down from father to son.","Advised on where to attack the walls in 1081, Alexios was told to leave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone, since its men would sooner die than betray the man on the throne."],"abstract":"The <b>Varangian Guard</b> was the body of northern and Rus' fighting men kept in the service of the Byzantine emperors. The arrangement began as a treaty obligation rather than an institution: the agreement of 945 provided both that the empire would send troops to the prince of Rus' when he asked, and that the prince would send the emperor as many men as he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rus', Varangian and Frankish mercenaries served in the imperial armies through the 9th, 10th and 11th centuries, in Asia Minor and in the Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How those units were organised, how many men came down to Constantinople, where they stood in the army and how dangerous they were to the throne are all argued from thin evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the late 11th century, however, the corps was plainly a standing part of the palace rather than a hire for a season. <br><br> Anna Comnena, writing about her father's reign, calls them the Varangians from Thule and the axe-bearing barbarians, and says they held their loyalty to the emperors to be a pledge and an ancestral tradition passed from father to son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That reputation had practical consequences: when Alexios was looking for a weak point in the walls in 1081 he was advised not to approach the Varangians or the native regiment that guarded beside them, because both would sooner lose their lives than turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They had officers of their own, and she names Nabites as prefect of the Varangians and one of six men the emperor later chose as his personal bodyguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The traffic between Rus' and the empire ran the other way as well, and Mstislav I dealt with the troublesome princes of Polotsk by having them deported to Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vsevolod Yurievich, who would become the strongest prince in Rus', spent his whole boyhood in exile there and came home only in 1169.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Chronique dite de Nestor</i>, trans. Louis Leger (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1884), 39–40, https://archive.org/details/chroniqueditede00nestgoog. [Open access]","Georgios Theotokis, “Rus, Varangian and Frankish Mercenaries in the Service of the Byzantine Emperors (9th–11th c.): Numbers, Organisation and Battle Tactics in the Operational Theatres of Asia Minor and the Balkans,” <i>Byzantina Symmeikta</i> 22 (2012): 125–156, https://doi.org/10.12681/byzsym.1039. [Open access]","Anna Comnena, <i>The Alexiad</i>, trans. Elizabeth A. S. Dawes (London: Routledge, 1928), bk. 2, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/AnnaComnena-Alexiad00.asp. [Open access]","Anna Comnena, <i>The Alexiad</i>, trans. Elizabeth A. S. Dawes (London: Routledge, 1928), bk. 4, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/AnnaComnena-Alexiad00.asp. [Open access]","Leonard A. Magnus, <i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), Introduction, xxii–xxiii, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","Andrey A. Kuznetsov, “Vsevolod Yurievich (Big Nest) in 1169—1183: From Dmitrok to the Vladimir Prince,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 15, no. 5 (139) (2024), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840031430-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rus'men end up guarding a Byzantine emperor at all?","a":"The relationship was written into the treaties. The agreement of 945 obliged the prince of Rus' to send the emperor as many soldiers as he asked for, and to receive imperial troops in return. What began as a reciprocal clause about campaign armies turned into a standing supply of northern fighting men at Constantinople."},{"q":"Why were the Varangians thought safer than other troops in a coup?","a":"Their loyalty was to the office rather than to any faction in the city, and Anna Comnena presents it as an inheritance handed from father to son rather than a matter of pay. That made them useless to a conspirator: Alexios was advised in 1081 to look for his weak point somewhere else on the walls."},{"q":"Why does the Byzantine connection matter for the history of Rus' itself?","a":"It was a route for people as well as soldiers and goods. Princes and their families crossed it in both directions: Mstislav I disposed of the Polotsk line by deporting them to the empire, and Vsevolod the Big Nest grew up there in exile before coming back in 1169 to build the strongest principality in Rus'."}],"wiki":"Varangian Guard","places":["pl:q16869"]},
    "ru-090": {"questions":["Dynasty, Orthodoxy, statehood and the memory of Kyiv were the frames through which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was assembled, over several phases rather than at any single moment.","The chronicles use “Rus'” of three southern principalities and sometimes of one, which is narrower than the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built on them centuries afterwards."],"abstract":"The <b>All-Russian nation</b> is the proposition that Russians, Ukrainians and Belarusians are one people descended from Kyivan Rus'. It is a claim made about the past rather than a finding drawn from it, and it developed through several historical phases rather than being formulated once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian self-description has been built out of dynastic power, Orthodoxy, the history of Kyivan Rus', statehood, nationalism and culture, and whether the two neighbouring peoples counted as parts of the nation or as nations of their own followed from whichever of those frames was in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicles are narrower than the claim built on them: historians of very different sympathies have accepted that “Rus'” and “the Rus' land” in the writing of the period mean the Kyiv, Pereyaslav and Chernihiv principalities, and sometimes the Kyiv land alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vocabulary has its own history too, and the terms “russkiy” and “drevnerusskiy” were brought into scholarly use by Russian philologists in the 19th and 20th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the written records of the intervening centuries actually carry is “Rusyn” for the people and “ruska mova” for the language, attested across a long series of documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survey work in the present day shows how far the older frame still shapes Russian views of Ukrainians and Belarusians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other side of the question is what Rus' itself belonged to, and its princes married into ruling houses across Latin Europe on a scale that places the state inside a European dynastic network rather than outside one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The political ideas its rulers worked with came from Byzantium, which is a further reason the state reads badly as the first chapter of any single modern nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That three countries descend in part from one state is not in dispute; what is disputed is what follows from it, and that argument belongs to the present rather than to the 12th century.","sources":["Ryszard Radzik, “Rosyjska wizja narodu ogólnoruskiego,” <i>Studia Białorutenistyczne</i> 10 (2016), https://doi.org/10.17951/sb.2016.10.55. [Open access]","Hennadii Noha, “Wars for the Capital in the Kyivan Chronicle,” <i>Слово і Час</i>, no. 5 (2024): 41–57, https://doi.org/10.33608/0236-1477.2024.05.41-57. [Open access]","Viktor M. Moysiyenko, “Who Were ‘Ruthenians’ in Kyivan Rus’ and post-Kyivan Rus’ Geopolitical Formations,” <i>Studia Linguistica</i>, no. 28 (2026): 9–34, https://doi.org/10.17721/studling2026.28.9-34. [Open access]","Oleksandr Fylypchuk, review of <i>Ties of Kinship: Genealogy and Dynastic Marriage in Kyivan Rus’</i>, by Christian Raffensperger, <i>East/West: Journal of Ukrainian Studies</i> 5, no. 2 (2018): 215–217, https://doi.org/10.21226/ewjus432. [Open access]","Francis Dvornik, “Byzantine Political Ideas in Kievan Russia,” <i>Dumbarton Oaks Papers</i> 9/10 (1956): 73–121, https://archive.org/details/DOP09-10_04_Dvornik. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the inheritance of Rus' disputed at all?","a":"Kyivan Rus' broke up long before Russia, Ukraine and Belarus existed, so all three can trace institutions, language and religion back to it. What is contested is not the descent but what the descent entitles anyone to say now, which makes it an argument about the present conducted in the language of the past."},{"q":"Why does the chronicles' own use of the word “Rus'” matter here?","a":"The modern claim treats Rus' as the whole of the East Slavic world, while in the writing of the 12th century the word usually means the Kyiv, Pereyaslav and Chernihiv principalities and sometimes only the Kyiv land. A term that was narrow at the time cannot carry the weight later argument puts on it without that being noticed."},{"q":"Why do the words used for the medieval language and people keep changing?","a":"The records of the intervening centuries call the people Rusyns and the language ruska mova. The forms “russkiy” and “drevnerusskiy” were introduced by Russian philologists in the 19th and 20th centuries, so the vocabulary now used to describe the medieval material was coined during the period when the national claims were being made."}],"wiki":"All-Russian nation"},
    "ru-082": {"questions":["Unyielding over Ryazan, southern Pereyaslavl and Novgorod, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his sons pushed the Chernigov princes out of northern Rus' across fifty years of quarrels and patched-up truces.","The poet of the Tale of Igor's Campaign begs <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to fly to the golden throne, telling him he could splash the Volga with oars and empty the Don with helmets."],"abstract":"<b>Vsevolod the Big Nest</b> was the youngest son of Yuri Dolgorukiy and the prince under whom Vladimir-Suzdal became the strongest power in Rus'. He lost his father as a small child and spent his boyhood in exile in Byzantium, returning to Rus' only in 1169, so that his political making belongs to the fourteen years after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In those years he went from a prince of the second rank to the ruler of the whole north-east, and he kept that place until his death in 1212.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His nickname records the size of his family rather than any victory of his own. The power he built rested less on Kyiv, which he never made his seat, than on a cluster of northern towns and on the ability to decide who ruled in places he did not live in. <br><br> The sharpest of those contests was with the princes of Chernigov, and it ran through Ryazan, through southern Pereyaslavl and through Novgorod over roughly fifty years from 1180.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vsevolod and his son Yaroslav won it by pressing their claims hard and without the usual courtesies of princely dealing, and the end of it was that Chernigov was shut out of northern Rus' and went into the Mongol invasion much weakened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two sides made peace when a common enemy appeared, most often the princes of Smolensk, and fell out again as soon as it had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Novgorod felt the same pressure from the other side, its townsmen sending to Vsevolod at Vladimir for a prince and taking the man he offered them<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, while Chernigov pushed back where it could and in one winter Svyatoslav Vsevolodovich marched out of the south against Suzdal in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tale of Igor's Campaign appeals to him directly as the prince whose armies could splash the Volga with oars, which is how a southern poet of the 1180s measured the weight of the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrey A. Kuznetsov, “Vsevolod Yurievich (Big Nest) in 1169—1183: From Dmitrok to the Vladimir Prince,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 15, no. 5 (139) (2024), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840031430-2. [Open access]","Andrey A. Kuznetsov, “Conflicts and Reconciliations of Vsevolod the Big Nest and His Sons with Chernigov Princes (1170s–1230s),” <i>Drevneishie gosudarstva Vostochnoi Evropy</i> 46 (2025): 140–158, https://doi.org/10.32608/1560-1382-2025-46-140-158. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 31, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 30, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i>, trans. Leonard A. Magnus (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), 14, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Vsevolod's childhood in Byzantium matter to his reign?","a":"It kept him out of Rus' politics until he was fifteen, so he began with no following of his own and everything to build. His rise from the second rank to ruler of the north-east happened in the fourteen years after his return, which is why his formation is dated from 1169."},{"q":"Why did Vsevolod's quarrels with Chernigov end in Chernigov's ruin?","a":"He and his son Yaroslav pressed their claims in Ryazan, southern Pereyaslavl and Novgorod without the regard for princely etiquette a rival expected. Over fifty years that drove the Chernigov princes out of northern Rus' altogether, so they met the Mongol invasion with much less weight than they had once carried."},{"q":"Why was Novgorod willing to ask Vsevolod for a prince?","a":"The city chose its princes and could expel them, but it needed one, and the strongest neighbour was the readiest source. Asking Vladimir for a man settled the question quickly; the cost was that the north-east then had a say in who sat in Novgorod at all."}],"wiki":"Vsevolod the Big Nest","places":["city:1159148121"]},
    "ru-086": {"questions":["Igor Svyatoslavich's raid on the Cumans in 1185 is told three times over, and only the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes his defeat into an eclipse and a crossing from light into darkness.","Pagan names, a sorcerer prince and a wife whose prayers bring her husband home run through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a century and a half after Rus' had accepted Christianity."],"abstract":"The <b>Tale of Igor's Campaign</b> is a short poem about the raid Igor Svyatoslavich of Novgorod-Seversk led against the Cumans in 1185, and the disaster it ended in. Its manuscript history is unusually thin for a work of its standing. Count Musin-Pushkin bought a bound volume of manuscripts from the archimandrite of the Spaso-Yaroslavski monastery in 1795, published the first edition in 1800 in twelve hundred copies, and lost the original together with most of those copies in the Moscow fire of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only six scholars of the day are known to have seen the manuscript, and they could not agree whether it was of the 14th, 15th or 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything later editors have done rests on that printed book and on a second modern copy found among Catherine II's papers. <br><br> Three works describe the campaign, and they read it in different ways: the Laurentian account blames Igor outright for a vainglorious raid, while the Kyivan chronicle and the poem treat the defeat as a punishment he comes to understand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poem does that in images rather than argument, opening on an eclipse and taking Igor across the Donets from light into darkness, and bringing him home on the prayers of his wife Yaroslavna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also speaks directly to princes who were alive when it was made, calling on Vsevolod of Suzdal to fly to the golden throne and reproaching Yaroslav of Galicia for shattering armies and staying at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its language is full of things the Church had not displaced, and a whole section of the old commentary is given to the survivals of heathendom in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among them is Vseslav of Polotsk, remembered as a sorcerer and a shape-changer drawn from older Slavic story rather than from any chronicle's judgement of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Leonard A. Magnus, <i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), Introduction, i–iii, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","Aleksander Uzhankov, “God Opposes the Proud: Crime and Punishment in Works about Prince Igor Svyatoslavich’s Campaign,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 7, no. 4 (2019): 1067–1085, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2019.4.425. [Open access]","<i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i>, trans. Leonard A. Magnus (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","Leonard A. Magnus, <i>The Tale of the Armament of Igor, A.D. 1185: A Russian Historical Epic</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1915), Introduction, xliv, https://archive.org/details/taleofarmamentof00magnuoft. [Open access]","V. V. Leonova, “Vseslav of Polotsk in «The Lay of Igor’s Campaign»: Authorial Intention and Mythological Context,” <i>RUDN Journal of Studies in Literature and Journalism</i> 31, no. 4 (2015): 7–16, https://journals.rudn.ru/literary-criticism/article/view/13346. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the poem's manuscript history so important to reading it?","a":"There is no medieval copy left. The volume Musin-Pushkin bought in 1795 burned in the Moscow fire of 1812 with most of the 1800 printing, so every later edition rests on that printed text and on a second modern copy. Only six scholars had seen the original, and they dated it three different ways."},{"q":"Why do the three accounts of 1185 read the same defeat so differently?","a":"Each was written to make a point about the prince. The Laurentian text blames Igor for a vainglorious campaign and sets a rival up as the hero. The Kyivan chronicle and the poem instead follow Igor through punishment, repentance and return, so the defeat becomes instructive rather than simply shameful."},{"q":"Why does a Christian poem carry so much pagan material?","a":"Christianity had been the religion of Rus' for under two centuries, and the older stories had not gone. The poem names old gods, calls on natural powers and remembers Vseslav of Polotsk as a shape-changer out of Slavic legend, which is why editors have always treated the survivals of heathendom in it as a subject of their own."}],"wiki":"The Tale of Igor's Campaign","places":["pl:q147758"]},
    "ru-091": {"questions":["An army counted in tens and ten thousands carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Korea to the Danube, and its western share fell to the line of the founder's eldest son.","The chroniclers of Rus' first met the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as an army that appeared from nowhere in 1223, destroyed their princes on the Kalka and rode away again."],"abstract":"The <b>Mongol Empire</b> was the state proclaimed on the eastern steppe in 1206, and within two generations its armies had ridden from Korea to the Danube, over more continuous ground than any power before or since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its soldiers were counted in tens, hundreds, thousands and ten thousands, and the men of conquered peoples were absorbed into those units and sent to fight a long way from home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A body of custom ascribed to its founder was invoked by every later Chinggisid state, though it is now read as a set of principles rather than a written code, and only outsiders from lettered legal cultures ever called it one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The empire was divided among the founder's sons, and its western share fell to the line of Jochi, the eldest of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian sources record the titles of that system, the dealings between the Jochid ulus and the imperial centre at Karakorum, and the steppe rituals a prince had to perform when he came to the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Rus' met the empire twice, and the first meeting left behind almost nothing but bewilderment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A column coming back round the Caspian destroyed the assembled princes on the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Kalchyk_(river)\">Kalka</span> in 1223, and the Novgorod chronicler closed his account by writing that nobody knew where the attackers had come from or where they had hidden themselves again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fourteen years later a full campaign returned under Batu, a grandson of the founder, and between 1237 and 1242 it took the towns of Rus' and rode on into Poland and Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What that campaign left behind is in the account of a papal envoy who crossed the steppe in 1246 and found human skulls and bones lying on the ground like cattle dung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For all that, the Russian material does not bear out the idea that the Mongols set out to conquer the whole world, which is a later gloss rather than a claim those sources make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["David Morgan, “The Mongol Empire in World History,” in <i>Beyond the Legacy of Genghis Khan</i> (Leiden: Brill, 2006), 425–37, https://doi.org/10.1163/9789047418573_029. [Paywalled]","R.Yu. Pochekaev, “Chinggis Khan’s Great Yasa in the Mongol Empire and Chinggisid States of the 13th–14th centuries: Legal Code or Ideal ‘Law and Order’?,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 4, no. 4 (2016): 724–733, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2016-4-4.724-733. [Open access]","Yuliy I. Drobyshev, “Reflection of the Mongol imperial ideas in medieval Russian sources,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 532–550, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.532-550. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 64–66, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Yevgen M. Luniak, “Batu Khan’s Invasion in the Imagination of French Medieval Authors,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 28–42, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.28-42. [Open access]","John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 12, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first Mongol contact with Rus' in 1223 leave so little trace in its chronicles?","a":"Because the column that beat the princes on the Kalka rode away again and did not come back for fourteen years. The Novgorod chronicler could only record that nobody knew where the attackers had come from or where they went afterwards."},{"q":"Why is the custom ascribed to the empire's founder now read as principles rather than a code?","a":"Because no authentic source describes it as a code of laws. That description comes from writers in lettered legal cultures, who expected a conquering state to have one, and the Mongols of the 13th and 14th centuries did not need codification to regulate their own law."},{"q":"Why do Russian sources say so little about Mongol religion and material culture?","a":"The chroniclers of Rus' took little interest in either, so what survives about Mongol ideas of power has to be reconstructed from scattered traces: the Eternal Heaven, the standing of the founder, and the rituals a prince performed at the Horde."}],"wiki":"Mongol Empire","places":["pol:mongol"]},
    "ru-092": {"questions":["The two commanders <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent round the Caspian met the princes of Rus' on the Kalka in 1223, in a march the sources describe as conquest rather than reconnaissance.","Rus' later dealt with khans who ruled because they descended from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose ascribed law the chronicles invoke and whose standing they treat almost as a title."],"abstract":"<b>Genghis Khan</b>, born <b>Temüjin</b>, was the ruler who united the tribes of the eastern steppe and took that title at an assembly of them in 1206.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reorganised his followers into units counted in tens, which cut across the old clan loyalties, and he died on campaign in 1227 with the empire still growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A body of custom called the yasa was ascribed to him and invoked by every later Chinggisid state, though no complete text of it survives and it is now read as a set of principles rather than a code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Descent from him became the qualification for the title of khan, so that only a man of his line could strike coin in his own name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He never went near Rus', and the Russian chronicles that had most to do with his descendants took remarkably little interest in the man himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What reached Rus' in his lifetime was the far end of the war he opened on Khwarezm in 1219, which carried two of his commanders round the Caspian and into the steppe beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those two, Jebe and Subutai, came up through the Caucasus, and in 1223 they destroyed the princes of Rus' and their Polovtsian allies on the Kalka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their march is usually called a reconnaissance in force, but the sources do not support that reading: what they record as its object is conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before the battle the commanders sent envoys to say that they had not come against Rus' at all but against the Polovtsians, whom they described as their own runaway serfs and horse-herds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The princes killed the envoys, and the chronicler who recorded it put the disaster that followed down to the sins of his own people rather than to anything about the man who had sent them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Morgan, “The Mongol Empire in World History,” in <i>Beyond the Legacy of Genghis Khan</i> (Leiden: Brill, 2006), 425–37, https://doi.org/10.1163/9789047418573_029. [Paywalled]","R.Yu. Pochekaev, “Chinggis Khan’s Great Yasa in the Mongol Empire and Chinggisid States of the 13th–14th centuries: Legal Code or Ideal ‘Law and Order’?,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 4, no. 4 (2016): 724–733, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2016-4-4.724-733. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 32–50, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]","Yuliy I. Drobyshev, “Reflection of the Mongol imperial ideas in medieval Russian sources,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 532–550, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.532-550. [Open access]","Stephen Pow, “The Last Campaign and Death of Jebe Noyan,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 698–725, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.698-725. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 64–65, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How did Genghis Khan come to power?","a":"By uniting the tribes of the eastern steppe, whose assembly proclaimed him khan in 1206; he then regrouped his followers into units counted in tens that cut across the old clan loyalties, so that they answered to him rather than to their kin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his forces invade Rus'?","a":"Rus' lay at the far end of his war on Khwarezm: two of his commanders rode round the Caspian and through the Caucasus after the Polovtsians, and when the princes of Rus' took the Polovtsians' side and killed the Mongol envoys, they were routed on the Kalka in 1223.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the Rus' know of the Mongols?","a":"Almost nothing. The Novgorod chronicler wrote that unknown tribes had come whose language, race and faith no one knew, called Tartars by some and Taurmen or Pechenegs by others, and knew only that they had conquered many lands and crushed the Polovtsians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Genghis Khan","places":["pol:mongol"]},
    "ru-093": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought on 31 May 1223, and the chronicle that describes it best files it a year late and admits that nobody knew who the victors were.","Where exactly the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took place is still argued, and the route of the allied army has been rebuilt from the chronicles' own place names and from archaeology."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Kalka River</b> was fought on 31 May 1223 between a Mongol column returning from the Caucasus and a coalition of the princes of Rus' with their Polovtsian allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The appeal came from the Polovtsian khan Kotyan, father-in-law of Mstislav of Galich, who brought gifts and told the princes that their land would be taken tomorrow if his was taken today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mongols sent envoys ahead of them, saying that they had come against the Polovtsians and not against Rus', and the princes killed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The allied army crossed the Dnieper, pursued the Mongols for nine days and passed the Kalka before the two forces met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which river that was is still argued, and the march has been rebuilt out of the chronicles' place names checked against archaeology, which puts the fighting near the Karatysh and the Kalchik.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Polovtsians broke first and fled back through the camp of the princes, who had not had time to form up against them, and the rout became a slaughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mstislav of Kiev held a stockade on a stony hill above the river for three days, gave it up on a promise sworn over the cross, and was suffocated under boards on which the victors sat down to eat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six more princes were killed in the pursuit back to the Dnieper and, by the chronicler's reckoning, one man in ten came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of the two Mongol commanders appears to have died about a week before the battle, and the column's whole march is better read as a campaign of conquest than as the scouting expedition it is usually called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It went home by way of a defeat at the hands of the Volga Bulgars that only one Arabic writer records, and then Rus' heard nothing of the Mongols for fourteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 64–65, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 65–66, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Andrey A. Astaykin, “The nine geographic notes about the campaign and battle on the Kalka River in 1223,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 292–306, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2024-12-2.292-306. [Open access]","Stephen Pow, “The Last Campaign and Death of Jebe Noyan,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 698–725, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.698-725. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Stavitsky, “The problem of locating the battle of 1223 between the Mongols and the Volga Bulgars,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 282–291, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2024-12-2.282-291. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the princes of Rus' kill the Mongol envoys before the battle?","a":"The message asked them to abandon the Polovtsians, whose khan had just bought their help with gifts and a warning. Breaking that alliance would have left the steppe to the newcomers, so the princes kept the alliance and killed the messengers."},{"q":"Why is the site of the battlefield still disputed?","a":"No source gives a position for it. The name Kalka is matched to more than one river, so the place has to be argued from the other names the chronicles give along the route and from what archaeology has found on the ground."},{"q":"Why does calling the Mongol column's march a reconnaissance sit badly with its sources?","a":"Because they record conquest as its aim rather than the gathering of intelligence. The label is a modern one, tidied backwards from the column's return to the east, which the defeat by the Volga Bulgars may explain better."}],"wiki":"Battle of the Kalka River"},
    "ru-094": {"questions":["A papal envoy who reached <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1246 was made to walk between two fires, and found him seated under linen tents that had belonged to the king of Hungary.","At the end of 1242 the three strongest princes of Rus' were summoned to the camp of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and only the one who went was given a seniority over the others."],"abstract":"<b>Batu Khan</b> was a grandson of Genghis Khan and the commander of the campaign that carried the Mongol Empire into eastern and central Europe between 1237 and 1242.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ground he overran became the western share of the empire, the ulus of his father Jochi, and he held it from a camp on the lower Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A papal envoy who was brought to him in 1246 reported that he was more powerful than any other Mongol prince save the emperor, whom he was held to obey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same envoy was made to walk between two fires, on the grounds that fire would remove any harm he might be carrying, and found a court with doorkeepers and officers like the emperor's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sat raised as though on a throne beside one of his wives, under large linen tents that had belonged to the king of Hungary, and was described as kind to his own people, greatly feared by them and crafty in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His hold on Rus' was settled after the fighting rather than during it, and its shape was not obvious at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the end of 1242 and the beginning of 1243 all three of the strongest princes were ordered to come to his camp, and direct Mongol administration of the conquered lands was still a live possibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two of them found ways of not going; Yaroslav Vsevolodovich went, and was given a seniority over the rest, which set the pattern of ruling Rus' through its own princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lesser princes made the journey too, and one of Ryazan seems to have been granted his seat after an audience at the imperial regent's headquarters rather than held for years as a hostage, as later writers supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Western authors who never saw him made him a figure of terror, and the trade in slaves from Rus' that reached the ports of southern France in the next two centuries is one measure of what his conquest had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 8, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 10–11, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “The First Challenges of the Russian Princes to the Mongol Rulers: Years 1242–1243,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 303–313, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-2.303-313. [Open access]","Yevgen M. Luniak, “Batu Khan’s Invasion in the Imagination of French Medieval Authors,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 28–42, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.28-42. [Open access]","Leonid V. Vorotyntsev, “The ‘Horde Captivity’ of the Ryazan Prince Oleg Ingvarevich Krasny (1238–1258): From Historiographical Myth to Historical Realities,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 4 (2021): 733–748, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-4.733-748. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the form of Mongol rule over Rus' not settled by the conquest itself?","a":"Because the decision to leave the princes in their own lands had not been taken when the fighting stopped. Direct administration was still possible at the end of 1242, and the summonses to his camp were where the alternative was chosen."},{"q":"Why did going to the camp help Yaroslav Vsevolodovich rather than ruin him?","a":"The two princes who stayed away gained nothing by it. He went, and came back with a recognised seniority over the other princes of Rus', which made obedience to the khan the route to standing at home."},{"q":"Why does the account of a papal envoy matter so much for what is known of him?","a":"Because it is an eyewitness description by somebody with no stake in Russian politics. It records his rank within the empire, the ceremony of his court and the captured Hungarian tents, none of which the chronicles of Rus' describe."}],"wiki":"Batu Khan"},
    "ru-095": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with a demand for a tenth of everything, of men and princes and horses, which the princes of Ryazan refused and paid for within the week.","How far the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> really set the country back is still argued, and one measure used is which bishoprics kept their rank and their property and which did not."],"abstract":"The <b>Mongol invasion of Rus'</b> was the campaign of 1237 to 1241 in which the armies of the western ulus took the towns of the north-east and then those of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with an embassy to the princes of Ryazan, a sorceress and two men with her, demanding a tenth of everything: of men, of princes and of horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The princes answered that when none of them were left it would all be theirs, and the grand prince at Vladimir neither came to help nor listened, preferring to make war on his own account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ryazan, Kolomna, Moscow, Vladimir, Pereyaslavl, Yurev, Dmitrov, Volok, Tver and Torzhok fell in that order, and the army then turned back short of Novgorod, a hundred versts or about 107 kilometres (66 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicler who set all this down was writing at Novgorod, and explained the whole of it as God's punishment for the sins of his own people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How far the invasion set Rus' back has been argued for a century, and the argument turns on what can be measured rather than on the laments of the chronicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One measure is the church: the sees of Vladimir and Pereyaslavl were sacked and plundered, while the bishops of Rostov, Chernigov and Galich kept both their rank and their means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another is the ground, where a fortified settlement on the upper Sura was destroyed and left, so that hoards of the early 13th century and a large collection of Mongol weapons lie where they fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A papal envoy who rode through in 1246 wrote that the greater part of the men of Rus' had been killed or carried into captivity, so that the country could offer no resistance even to raiders from Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What followed for the church was not simple ruin: the clergy were treated with a deliberate tolerance, the church was exempted from the Horde's tribute, and its weight in the politics of Rus' grew rather than shrank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 81–84, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [Open access]","Gennadiy N. Belorybkin, Tatiana V. Osipova, and Ariel S. Sobol, “Hoard of Artifacts of the Beginning of 13th Century from Zolotorevka Fortified Settlement and Mongol Invasion,” <i>Povolzhskaya Arkheologiya</i> 4, no. 30 (2019): 99–109, https://doi.org/10.24852/pa2019.4.30.99.109. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Stavitsky, “The problem of locating the battle of 1223 between the Mongols and the Volga Bulgars,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 282–291, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2024-12-2.282-291. [Open access]","John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 3–4, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","T.R. Galimov, “Hierarchy of the Russian Church and Relations between the Horde and the Russian Church during the Tatar-Mongol Invasion of 1237–1240 according to Contemporary Estimates,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 29–55, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-1.29-55. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the princes of Ryazan refuse the demand for a tenth?","a":"A tenth of men, princes and horses would have left them without the means to defend anything. Their answer, that when none of them remained it would all belong to the Mongols, treats the demand as surrender by instalments."},{"q":"Why do historians measure the damage by bishoprics rather than by the chronicles?","a":"Because the chronicles describe the invasion in the language of divine punishment and make no attempt at proportion. A list of which sees kept their rank, property and bishop is a record of institutions that can be compared before and after."},{"q":"Why did the church come out of the invasion stronger than the princes?","a":"Mongol practice was to leave local clergy alone, and the church was exempted from the tribute the princes had to collect. Its property was also taken out of princely hands, so it kept its means while their authority depended on grants from the khan."}],"wiki":"Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'","places":["pl:q196678"]},
    "ru-096": {"questions":["Asked for a tenth of everything before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, the princes replied that when none of them remained it would all belong to the Mongols anyway.","The best-known account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a literary tale written down long afterwards, and how much of it is history at all is now disputed."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Ryazan</b> was the first assault on a town in the Mongol invasion of Rus', opened on 16 December 1237 and finished on the 21st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army had halted on the river Nukhla and sent envoys ahead, a sorceress and two men with her, to demand a tenth of everything from the princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The princes of Ryazan, Murom and Pronsk went out to meet them at Voronezh without letting them into their towns, and answered that when none of them were left it would all be theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grand prince at Vladimir was asked for help and neither sent it nor came, wishing to make his own war separately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was fenced about with a stockade, the prince shut himself inside it with his people, and a relieving force under a commander from Vladimir was surrounded at Kolomna and destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the town fell the chronicler records the prince and princess killed with the townspeople, monks, nuns and priests, some by fire and some by the sword, and women violated in front of their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bishop escaped, he says, only because he had left at the moment the army invested the place, and the church's losses across the whole campaign were uneven in just that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The account most people know is not the chronicle but a tale of the destruction of Ryazan by Batu, written down a long time afterwards, and how far it is history is now argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reading that the Mongols meant to absorb the north-east peacefully and provoked Ryazan into war by killing one of its princes has been challenged as unsupported by the Russian and the foreign sources alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ryazan's later princes dealt with the conquerors as the others did, and one of them appears to have been granted his seat at the imperial regent's court rather than spending years as a hostage, as older accounts had it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 81–82, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Leonid V. Vorotyntsev, “Batu’s Ryazan Gambit: on new Interpretations of the Messages from ‘The Tale of the Destruction of Ryazan by Batu’ in Modern Research,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 284–302, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-2.284-302. [Open access]","Leonid V. Vorotyntsev, “The ‘Horde Captivity’ of the Ryazan Prince Oleg Ingvarevich Krasny (1238–1258): From Historiographical Myth to Historical Realities,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 4 (2021): 733–748, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-4.733-748. [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [Open access]","T.R. Galimov, “Hierarchy of the Russian Church and Relations between the Horde and the Russian Church during the Tatar-Mongol Invasion of 1237–1240 according to Contemporary Estimates,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 29–55, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-1.29-55. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the grand prince at Vladimir not help Ryazan?","a":"The chronicler says he wished to make war separately, on his own account. The principalities of Rus' were rivals as much as allies, and a joint defence would have meant accepting somebody else's command at the point where his own lands were still untouched."},{"q":"Why is the famous tale of the town's destruction treated with caution?","a":"It was written down long after the event and is a work of literature rather than a chronicle entry. Modern readings of it disagree about how much of its detail can stand, so it is used for what people later believed rather than for what happened."},{"q":"Why does the bishop's escape matter to historians of the invasion?","a":"It is one of the cases that show the church's losses following the fighting rather than any policy. Clergy who were away when a town was invested generally survived, and afterwards their position turned on their standing with the new power."}],"wiki":"Siege of Ryazan"},
    "ru-097": {"questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was over the prince's son, his mother and the bishop had themselves tonsured as monks and nuns inside the church in which they then died.","A month after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the grand prince was run down on the river Sit and killed, and his brother took the throne on the conquerors' terms."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Vladimir</b> was the taking of the capital of the north-east in February 1238, a few weeks after Ryazan had fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yuri Vsevolodovich left the city before the army arrived and went north to Yaroslavl, leaving his son Vsevolod, the women of his household and the bishop Mitrofan to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mongols fenced the town round, and on the morning it became plain that it would fall the prince's son and the bishop went into the Church of the Holy Mother of God and were tonsured into the monastic order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was taken and fired, and the family shut themselves in the sacristy, whose doors were broken down, after which wood was piled against the church and it was burnt with them inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yuri was overtaken on the river Sit not long afterwards and killed, and the chronicler adds only that God knows how he died, since people said a good deal about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The loss of the city was the loss of a see as well, and the chronicles name Vladimir and Pereyaslavl as the two that were sacked and plundered while other bishops kept their rank and their means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those who came through it owed their survival to having been away from the fighting, and once the campaign was over a churchman's position turned chiefly on his standing with the new power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mongol practice obliged a considerable tolerance towards local clergy, and the church of Rus' was in the end exempted from paying the Horde's tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yuri's brother Yaroslav took the vacant throne, and at the end of 1242 he was called to Batu's camp and given a seniority over the other princes of Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He died in 1246 coming back from the imperial court in Mongolia, in circumstances the sources leave open and which are still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 82–83, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [Open access]","T.R. Galimov, “Hierarchy of the Russian Church and Relations between the Horde and the Russian Church during the Tatar-Mongol Invasion of 1237–1240 according to Contemporary Estimates,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 29–55, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-1.29-55. [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “The First Challenges of the Russian Princes to the Mongol Rulers: Years 1242–1243,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 303–313, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-2.303-313. [Open access]","Leonid V. Vorotyntsev and Teymur R. Galimov, “The ‘necessary death’ of the Grand Duke: on the question of the causes and circumstances of the death of Yaroslav Vsevolodovich in the autumn of 1246,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 562–581, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.562-581. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the grand prince leave his capital before the Mongols reached it?","a":"He went north to raise an army in the open rather than be shut up behind a wall. The choice cost him the city and his family, and he was caught and killed on the river Sit before the force he was gathering could be brought to a battle."},{"q":"Why were the prince's family and the bishop tonsured while the town was falling?","a":"Taking monastic vows at the point of death was a recognised way of preparing for it. The chronicler records it as the last act available to them once it was clear the walls would not hold, and it made the church the place they died in."},{"q":"Why did the throne pass on the conquerors' terms rather than by the old rules?","a":"The grand prince was dead and the city was ash, so the seat had to be filled by somebody the new power would accept. His brother took it and then went to the khan's camp, and came back with a seniority the khan had granted."}],"wiki":"Siege of Vladimir","places":["city:1159148121"]},
    "ru-098": {"questions":["When a papal mission reached the city after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its head man gave them an escort, and merchants from Constantinople were trading there again.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a country so emptied that its own people could not resist raiders, and the archbishop of Rus' carried the news of it to a council at Lyon."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Kyiv</b> was the taking of the city in 1240 by the army of the western ulus, at the far end of the campaign that had begun at Ryazan three years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kyiv was the seat of the metropolitan of all Rus', so its fall was a matter for the church as much as for the princes, and the dealings between the hierarchy and the conquerors date from these years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the place was like afterwards is known chiefly from a papal mission that reached it in February 1246 on its way east to the Mongol court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members called Kyiv the metropolis of Rus', took counsel there with the head man of the town and the other nobles about their route, and were given pack-horses and an escort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The friar who dictated the shorter account of the journey put it plainly: Kyiv was a city of Rus' now under the dominion of the Tartars, and its headman gave them men for six days' riding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the road to it they had travelled in fear of Lithuanian raiders, because the greater part of the men of Rus' had been killed by the Mongols or carried into captivity and could offer no resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade had not stopped: the mission met merchants from Constantinople at Kyiv, Italians among them, who had come through by way of the Mongol lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> News of what had happened travelled west with the archbishop of Rus', who appeared at the council held at Lyon in 1245.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two centuries later slaves from Rus' were still reaching the ports of southern France, which is one measure of the kind of rule the conquest had established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recent work warns against the opposite error of making that rule sound like a partnership, since it rested on a destructive conquest and was often destructively exercised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 3–4, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","Benedict the Pole, “Narrative of Friar John of Pian de Carpine’s Mission,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [Open access]","T.R. Galimov, “Hierarchy of the Russian Church and Relations between the Horde and the Russian Church during the Tatar-Mongol Invasion of 1237–1240 according to Contemporary Estimates,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 29–55, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-1.29-55. [Open access]","Yevgen M. Luniak, “Batu Khan’s Invasion in the Imagination of French Medieval Authors,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 28–42, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.28-42. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 32–50, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a papal mission the main evidence for the state of the city after 1240?","a":"Because it was there, six years later, and wrote down what it saw on its way to the Mongol court. The chronicles of Rus' were kept in the north, where the fall of Kyiv was reported rather than witnessed."},{"q":"Why could the people of Rus' not resist Lithuanian raiders in 1246?","a":"Because most of the men had been killed or carried off. The envoys record travelling in fear along roads where raids went unpunished, which is a measure of the invasion's effect that no total of casualties could give."},{"q":"Why did merchants from Constantinople reach Kyiv so soon after the sack?","a":"Mongol rule made the routes across the steppe usable again for anyone who accepted it. The Italians the mission met at Kyiv had come through Mongol territory, so the trade the conquest interrupted was also the trade it reopened."}],"wiki":"Siege of Kyiv (1658)","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ru-099": {"questions":["Hungarian resistance to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was stronger than it used to be thought, and the Mongols afterwards remembered Mohi as their hardest battle against a European enemy.","Moravia's famous victory over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> never happened, and the hero credited with it was invented, in a story that ended up among 19th-century forgeries."],"abstract":"The <b>Mongol invasion of Europe</b> was the western end of the same campaign that had taken the towns of Rus': in 1241 its armies struck into Poland and over the mountains into Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hungary was invaded in the spring of that year and held until the middle of 1242.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The battle that decided it was fought at Mohi on 11 April and has long been taught as a textbook case of bad preparation and worse generalship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That reading has been revised: the accounts show the Hungarians trying to adapt their tactics, and what beat them was Mongol discipline, experience and a command system that went on working after heavy losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Hungarian knights began to ignore orders as their position worsened, while the Mongol commanders still had rested units in hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Resistance after the battle was stronger than used to be supposed, and the military orders did the Mongols enough damage that they remembered Mohi as the hardest fight they had had with a European enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was slow to cross the Danube because fresh Hungarian forces were gathering beyond it, and when it did cross in January 1242 it laid waste only the country near the main roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where its camps stood is now being worked out by modelling the ground itself, which is a way of testing the written accounts against the landscape they describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Christian west the invasion arrived in the middle of the quarrel between the emperor and the papacy, was underestimated until it was nearly too late, and then ended in a withdrawal nobody there could account for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some of what is still repeated about it is invention: the Moravian episode was the brief passage of troops, and the victory, the hero and the documents were all added afterwards, the last of them in 19th-century forgeries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Ferenc Sebők, “The Mongol Invasion of Hungary in 1241–1242. New Perspective,” <i>Faces of War</i>, no. 1 (2024): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.18778/3071-7779.2024.1.02. [Open access]","János B. Szabó, “The Hungarian View of the Battle of Muhi (April 11, 1241): A New Interpretation of Historiographic Traditions,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 2 (2020): 243–257, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-2.243-257. [Open access]","Tomáš Somer, “Forging the Past: Facts and Myths behind the Mongol Invasion of Moravia in 1241,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 238–251, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-2.238-251. [Open access]","Huub Kurstjens, “The Invasion of the Christian West by the Tatars (Mongols). A Clash of Civilizations between Frederick II, Gregory IX and the Tatars,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 258–275, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.258-275. [Open access]","András Dobai, András Gömöri, Tamás Deák, and Endre Dobos, “Digital spatial modelling for encampment localisation related to the Battle of Mohi (1241), Hungary,” <i>Archeologické rozhledy</i> 78, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.35686/ar.2026.320. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why has the Hungarian defeat at Mohi been re-read in recent scholarship?","a":"Because the sources show attempts to adapt tactics rather than blind adherence to western-style charges. The difference that decided the battle lay in discipline, experience and command: the Mongols held their formations after losses and the Hungarian knights stopped obeying orders."},{"q":"Why did a Moravian victory over the Mongols come to be believed in?","a":"Later chroniclers confused the passage of troops through Moravia with a Hungarian invasion of 1253, then a victory at Olomouc was invented, then a hero to win it, and finally documents were forged in the 19th century to support the whole story."},{"q":"Why did the withdrawal from Hungary look mysterious to western observers?","a":"They had no way of knowing what was happening inside the empire, and had expected either conquest or a battle they would have to fight themselves. The army left after a year of occupation, and the west was left with a fright it could not explain."}],"wiki":"Mongol invasion of Europe"},
    "ru-100": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Rus' through its own princes rather than directly, a choice made in the winter of 1242 when the strongest of them were called to Batu's camp.","Writing about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has swung between treating its rule as a partnership and as a catastrophe, and neither the destruction nor the arrangement that followed can be left out."],"abstract":"The <b>Golden Horde</b> was the western part of the Mongol Empire, the ulus of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Jochi\">Jochi</span>, and the power to which the princes of Rus' answered for the next two and a half centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its hold was fixed in the winter of 1242 and 1243, when Batu came back from the campaign in central Europe and ordered the three strongest princes of Rus' to his camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Direct Mongol administration was still possible at that point, and what was settled on instead was rule through the existing princes, one of whom was given a seniority over the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That made the journey to the khan the way to hold a seat, and the two princes who found reasons not to go gained nothing by staying away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It could also kill: the prince who first held that seniority died in 1246 coming back from the imperial court, in circumstances that are still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The church was handled differently from the princes, exempted from the Horde's tribute and left in possession of its property, so that its weight in the politics of Rus' grew rather than shrank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some recent work in English has come close to describing the relationship as a partnership, which understates a rule founded on destructive conquest and often destructively exercised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much Russian-language scholarship has made the opposite mistake, overstating how much Rus' mattered inside the ulus, where the Kipchaks of the steppe counted for a great deal more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology offers a way past both, though it has been slow, because for many years the nomad burials of the Horde's steppe could only be compared with Kipchak ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Enough material from the eastern steppe has now been excavated to pick out burials of Mongols proper among them and to map where in the ulus they lie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 32–50, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “The First Challenges of the Russian Princes to the Mongol Rulers: Years 1242–1243,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 303–313, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-2.303-313. [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [Open access]","Leonid V. Vorotyntsev and Teymur R. Galimov, “The ‘necessary death’ of the Grand Duke: on the question of the causes and circumstances of the death of Yaroslav Vsevolodovich in the autumn of 1246,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 562–581, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.562-581. [Open access]","Vladimir A. Ivanov, “Mongolian Nomads in the Ulus of Jochi (Golden Horde) According to Archaeological Materials,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 4 (2019): 636–651, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-4.636-651. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Horde rule Rus' through its princes instead of governing it directly?","a":"The choice was open when the fighting ended, and the summonses of 1242 settled it. Leaving the princes in place cost nothing in garrisons and made each of them a competitor for the khan's favour, which did the work that an administration would have had to do."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to describe the relationship as a partnership?","a":"Because it began in a conquest that destroyed towns and emptied the countryside, and the rule that followed was frequently destructive too. Describing it as cooperation takes the arrangement of later decades and reads it backwards over the years that made it possible."},{"q":"Why has it been hard to identify Mongols archaeologically in the Horde's own steppe?","a":"For a long time the only comparison available was with the many Kipchak burials of the same region, so anything unfamiliar was called Mongol by default. Enough has now been excavated further east to make a real comparison possible."}],"wiki":"Golden Horde"},
    "ru-101": {"questions":["Ibn Battuta rode from morning until after midday to cross <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a capital of thirteen cathedral mosques where each people of the Horde lived in a quarter of its own.","Excavation has shown the Selitrennoye ruins to be Uzbek's new <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the 1330s, but where Batu's original town of that name stood is still disputed."],"abstract":"<b>Sarai</b>, a word meaning 'palace', was the name of the capital that the khans of the Golden Horde built on the lower Volga, and in time it was given to more than one town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> William of Rubruck, returning west in the autumn of 1254, called it the new town that Batu was making on the Volga and placed it, with Batu's palace, on the eastern shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Batu began building in about 1250, choosing a site near the centre of his ulus and at a crossing of trade routes rather than taking over one of the conquered cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The earliest silver coins known to name the mint of Sarai were struck under Berke, and one of the series carries a year of the Islamic calendar that falls in 1264 or 1265.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 1330s Uzbek Khan built a new capital, Sarai al-Jadid, laid out as a Muslim city with mosques, madrasas, baths and caravanserais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ibn Battuta reached the capital in the same decade and called it one of the finest of towns, so large that a ride from one end to the other lasted from morning until after midday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He counted thirteen cathedral mosques and found Mongols, Ossetes, Kipchaks, Circassians, Russians and Greeks each living in a quarter of their own, with merchants from Iraq, Egypt and Syria behind a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The princes of Rus' were among the rulers of dependent lands summoned to the city, where the khans also received embassies and arranged dynastic marriages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Black Death struck the towns of the Horde in the 1340s, Sarai among them, although the sources say little about what it did there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Which ruin is which is still argued, for digging has shown the Selitrennoye site, with houses dated by Uzbek's coins, to be the new Sarai, while the site of Batu's town remains disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William of Rubruck, <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 256, 260, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","Ibn Battuta, <i>Travels in Asia and Africa, 1325–1354</i>, trans. H. A. R. Gibb (London: Routledge & Kegan Paul, 1963), 165–66, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.16353. [Open access]","Sergey A. Vasyutin, “Origins of Mongolian Urbanization and the Role of Towns in the Golden Horde,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 14, no. 1 (2026): 26–28, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2026-14-1.19-36. [in Russian] [Open access]","Pavel N. Petrov, Konstantin V. Kravtsov and Sergey V. Gumayunov, “The Coins of Saray in the First Half of the 660s AH / 1260s AD,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): 145–46, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-1.145-158. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 325–26, 334–35, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]","Ekaterina M. Boldyreva, “Исследования 2022–2023 гг. в ремесленном квартале средневекового города Сарай,” <i>Археология Евразийских степей</i>, no. 2 (2024): 56–63, https://doi.org/10.24852/2587-6112.2024.2.56.63. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the khans build a capital of their own instead of ruling from a conquered city?","a":"A town on the lower Volga sat near the middle of the ulus and on the trade routes that crossed there, and it kept the steppe close for a ruler who still moved with his herds between summer and winter grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also gave a safe place to keep the tribute, loot and goods that could not be carried on the move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many peoples live in separate quarters of the city?","a":"The capital drew in the subject peoples of the Horde as well as foreign merchants, and Ibn Battuta found each group living apart with its own bazaars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merchants from the Middle East kept to a walled quarter to protect their property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the ruins not simply be labelled with the name?","a":"The name meant 'palace' and was used for more than one place, and early coins struck at Sarai cannot be tied to a single site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Digging has identified the Selitrennoye ruins as the new capital of the 1330s, while the candidates for Batu's town are still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sarai (city)"},
    "ru-102": {"questions":["Copies of each khan's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issued to the metropolitans of Rus' freed the clergy from the Horde's taxes, but they survive only in Russian translations made in Rus' itself.","Tokhtamysh's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1393 to King Władysław Jagiełło, written in Uyghur script beside the khan's gold seal, demanded the tribute owed from lands subject to the king."],"abstract":"A <b>yarlyk</b> was a written order or grant of a Mongol khan, and the word covered decrees of appointment, letters to foreign rulers and charters of privilege alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Rus' it meant above all the charter by which a khan confirmed a prince in his principality, and each prince had to travel to the Horde to receive his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grant was followed by a khan's envoy, under whose eye the new holder was solemnly seated on his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grand princes of Vladimir went through that ceremony in the Dormition Cathedral of Vladimir with the khan's envoys present, and only Ivan III escaped it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By giving and withdrawing yarlyks the khans played the princes off against one another, taking the grand principality from Moscow in 1360 and handing it to Tver in 1371.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The church received yarlyks too, and a short collection of those issued to the metropolitans of Rus' freed the clergy from the taxes owed to the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> These survive only in Russian translations made in Rus', and the long lists of dues they forbid to be taken were partly added by the translators themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others survive in the original, such as the letter of 1393, written in Uyghur script beside the khan's gold seal and now kept in Warsaw, in which Tokhtamysh told King Władysław Jagiełło of his victory over rebels and demanded the tribute owed from the king's lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Catholic missionaries sought such grants as well, and Franciscans in the Horde obtained one from Uzbek Khan on 20 March 1314.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Grand princes still sought them in the 15th century, and a yarlyk from Ulugh Muhammad confirming Vasily II as grand prince was possibly obtained at the end of 1424 or early in 1425.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V.A. Kuchkin, “Завоевание Руси Батыем и ордынский «выход»,” in <i>Русь в XIII–XV веках: Новые открытия в области археологии и истории</i>, ed. L.A. Belyaev and E.L. Konyavskaya (Moscow: Indrik, 2021), 23–24, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2021.978-5-91674-655-6.22-35. [in Russian] [Open access]","Svetlana A. Maslova, “The Tax Terminology of the Short Collection of Khan’s Jarlyks Issued to Rus’ Metropolitans,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 636–38, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.636-651. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “The Yarliq of Tokhtamish Khan to King Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland: The Problem of the Old Russian Text’s Origins,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 314–15, 321, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-2.314-335. [in Russian] [Open access]","Yury V. Sochnev, “Notes and Additions to the Comments on the Yarlik Granted by Uzbek Khan to the Franciscans in 1314,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 4 (2018): 783–84, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-4.783-806. [in Russian] [Open access]","Irina G. Ponomareva, “About the Yarliq of Ulugh Muhammad Khan Approving the Rule of Grand Prince Vasily II,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 583–92, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-3.583-592. [Open access]","Lenar F. Abzalov, Marat S. Gatin, Ilias A. Mustakimov and Roman Yu. Pochekaev, “The Jarlyk on Appointment of Amir-e Tuman from the ‘Dastur al-katib’ and Status of Temnik in the Uluses of Hulagu, Jochi and Chagatay,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 592–610, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.592-610. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a prince of Rus' have to travel to the Horde to keep his own principality?","a":"Because the khan's charter was what made his rule lawful in the conquerors' eyes, and it was handed out at the khan's court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The journey could be humiliating or fatal, and a prince who did not make it had nothing in writing to show for his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the church want yarlyks of its own?","a":"Because a charter from the khan protected the clergy from the dues that the Horde took from everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Russian translations that survive list many more exempt payments than the originals probably did, which shows what the church hoped to be spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a yarlyk sent to a Polish king matter for Rus'?","a":"Because the tribute Tokhtamysh demanded was owed from lands subject to the king, so the Horde still claimed payment from territory it no longer ruled directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is also one of the few yarlyks that survives in its Turkic original rather than in translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jarlig"},
    "ru-103": {"questions":["In 1339 Novgorod sent Ivan Kalita its agreed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the Horde and then refused his demand for a second payment, saying no such thing had happened since the world began.","The annual payment that Rus' sent to the khans, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was shared out among heirs in units of a thousand roubles, so its real total cannot be read off the princes' wills."],"abstract":"The <b>vykhod</b> was the yearly tribute that the lands of Rus' paid to the Golden Horde, a word that rendered the Arabic tax term kharaj and that the sources also give simply as the Horde tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gifts to the khans and their courts were costly but irregular, whereas the vykhod fell due every year and weighed on every part of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It rested on the Mongol censuses, and when Novgorod numbered itself for tribute in the winter of 1259 the census-takers rode through the streets writing down the houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribute was collected by the princes in their own lands and passed up to the grand prince, who answered to the Horde for the vykhod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dmitry Donskoy's will of 1389 set what each son should pay into it and added that if God changed the Horde, his children would not pay it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Wills and treaties name sums of 5,000 and 7,000 roubles, but since the 19th century historians have read the thousand in them as a conditional unit for dividing the burden among heirs, so the real total cannot be read off directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Novgorod paid its share through the grand prince, and in 1339 it sent Ivan Kalita the agreed tribute and then refused his demand for a second payment that the khan had asked of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town's contribution was known as the bor, and later as the black bor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The payment was not always made, for in 1375 Moscow, Novgorod and Tver agreed to decide together whether to pay the vykhod at all, and Mamai received none after his defeat in 1380.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His heirs took it back after hard clashes with the princes, and in 1393 Tokhtamysh was still demanding tribute from lands subject to the Polish king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 96–97, 133, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V.A. Kuchkin, “Завоевание Руси Батыем и ордынский «выход»,” in <i>Русь в XIII–XV веках: Новые открытия в области археологии и истории</i>, ed. L.A. Belyaev and E.L. Konyavskaya (Moscow: Indrik, 2021), 24–26, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2021.978-5-91674-655-6.22-35. [in Russian] [Open access]","Svetlana A. Maslova, “The Tax Terminology of the Short Collection of Khan’s Jarlyks Issued to Rus’ Metropolitans,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 640, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.636-651. [in Russian] [Open access]","S.A. Maslova, “Монголы и Новгородская земля в XIII–XV вв.,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 2 (92) (2023): 33–34, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2023.92.2.005. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “The Yarliq of Tokhtamish Khan to King Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland: The Problem of the Old Russian Text’s Origins,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 315, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-2.314-335. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the princes rather than Mongol officials gather the tribute?","a":"Because by the 14th century the princes collected tribute in their own lands and handed it on to the grand prince, who was answerable to the Horde for the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Novgorod paid its share through him on agreed terms and resisted any demand beyond them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the figures in the wills not be taken as the real size of the tribute?","a":"Because, on the reading followed since the 19th century, the thousand roubles they name was a unit for sharing the burden among heirs, not a real sum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same 105 roubles from Uglich appears under a 7,000-rouble vykhod and a 5,000-rouble one, which only makes sense if each thousand was a share of whatever the Horde demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Donskoy write about the Horde changing?","a":"Because by 1389 the princes could imagine an end to the payment, and Moscow had already stopped paying Mamai in the years around Kulikovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The will kept the tribute that the sons collected locally while releasing them from the vykhod if the Horde fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-104": {"questions":["During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a papal envoy saw under way in Rus', every person down to a day-old infant owed a white bear, beaver, sable or black fox skin.","The Yuan annals record that early in 1253 the great khan Möngke sent the official Berke to carry out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which reached the north-east only in the winter of 1257."],"abstract":"The <b>Mongol census</b> of Rus' was the counting of the conquered population for tax and service, carried out land by land in the 1240s and 1250s, and in Novgorod it was called the chislo, 'the number'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first reached the Kyiv land while the papal envoy John of Plano Carpini was there, when an official sent by Güyük and Batu took one boy in three, together with the unmarried and the beggars, and counted the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Everyone counted, down to a day-old child, owed a white bear, beaver, sable, polecat or black fox skin, and anyone who did not pay was led off into slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soon after taking power the great khan Möngke ordered a census of all the tax-paying subjects of the empire, and early in 1253 he sent an official named Berke to count Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The aim was a regular tax system, with a poll tax added to the older dues, and the revenue was meant for recruiting soldiers, running the post roads and keeping envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the north-east the counting took place in the winter of 1257 and 1258, while Novgorod, which the invasion had spared, refused the first demand in 1257 and sent gifts to the khan instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two years later the town heard that an army stood ready in the Suzdal land, and the commons swore they would rather die honourably for St Sophia than give their numbers for tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander Nevsky had the sons of the boyars guard the Tatar officials Berkai and Kasachik by night, and the boyars agreed to the count, which the chronicler says they made light for themselves and heavy for the lesser men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The census-takers rode through the streets writing down the houses, so Novgorod's tribute was set by household, where in the Kyiv land every man had been counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tribute presumably began after the first census, but no source says how much Rus' paid until the second half of the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 95–97, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","John of Pian de Carpine, quoted in translation in William of Rubruck, <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 94 n. 1, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","A.V. Maiorov, “Александр Невский, Бату и хан Мунке: Нашествие Неврюя и контроль над русскими землями,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 4 (90) (2022): 23–25, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2022.4.90.003. [in Russian] [Open access]","S.A. Maslova, “Монголы и Новгородская земля в XIII–XV вв.,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 2 (92) (2023): 29–32, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2023.92.2.005. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey A. Vasyutin, “Origins of Mongolian Urbanization and the Role of Towns in the Golden Horde,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 14, no. 1 (2026): 28, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2026-14-1.19-36. [in Russian] [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 33, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Mongols count the population at all?","a":"Because a regular tax needed to know who could pay it, and the great khan wanted the revenue gathered to the centre rather than left with local rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting also showed who could be taken for service, which is why the envoy saw boys and the unmarried carried off as well as skins collected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Novgorod resist when the rest of Rus' had already been counted?","a":"Because the town had never been taken in the invasion, and submitting to the count meant accepting the conquerors' rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It gave way only under the threat of an army and with the grand prince standing beside the Tatar officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the commons and the boyars of Novgorod split over the census?","a":"Because the boyars expected the burden to fall lightly on themselves and heavily on the lesser men, and the chronicler says exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counting by household rather than by head was also gentler than what the Kyiv land had suffered, which may have made the bargain easier for the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-105": {"questions":["A papal envoy crossing Rus' in 1246 wrote that the Tatars set a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the lands of each prince they let go home, whom princes had to obey at a nod.","By the early 14th century the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had disappeared from the main towns of north-eastern Rus', though one still rode with the prince of Kyiv to waylay Novgorod's archbishop in 1331."],"abstract":"A <b>baskak</b> was an official whom the Mongol rulers placed in a conquered land, backed by armed men, to keep its people obedient, and the word was the Turkic name for the office the Mongols called darughachi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the baskak matched the darugha and the Persian shihne is still debated, though the shihne represented the ruler in a region and kept it loyal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> John of Plano Carpini, crossing Rus' in 1246, wrote that the conquerors set baskaks over the lands of princes they let return home, whom princes and others had to obey at a nod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If a town did not do what they wanted, he added, the baskaks accused it of disloyalty to the Tatars and a Tatar force fell on it without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Baskaks are recorded in Galich in 1255 and in Ustyug and Borovsk in the 1260s, and some sat permanently in Vladimir, Rostov, Kyiv and Tula, seeing that tribute was paid and watching the princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1269 Amragan, called the great baskak of Vladimir for the forces he commanded rather than his rank, was in Novgorod as an army gathered against the Germans, who at once offered to give up the whole Narova.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Near the end of the century the baskak Akhmat, serving Nogai near Kursk, fell into a long and bloody conflict with two princes of Rus' over his settlements, the slobody of the chronicle story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicles place the story under 1283 and 1284, but the events most probably happened in 1289 and 1290, and the account seems to have been written for a higher authority, most likely Metropolitan Maksim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Baskaks grew fewer once the Golden Horde went its own way in the 1260s, and by the early 14th century the office had vanished from the main towns of the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lingered in the south, and in 1331 a baskak with fifty men joined the prince of Kyiv in waylaying Novgorod's new archbishop near Chernigov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V.A. Kuchkin, “Завоевание Руси Батыем и ордынский «выход»,” in <i>Русь в XIII–XV веках: Новые открытия в области археологии и истории</i>, ed. L.A. Belyaev and E.L. Konyavskaya (Moscow: Indrik, 2021), 22, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2021.978-5-91674-655-6.22-35. [in Russian] [Open access]","John of Plano Carpini, <i>Historia Mongalorum</i>, in <i>The Texts and Versions of John de Plano Carpini and William de Rubruquis</i>, ed. C. Raymond Beazley (London: Hakluyt Society, 1903), 67, https://archive.org/details/textsversionsofj00hakluoft. [in Latin] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 103–4, 127, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Timofey Guimon, “Рассказ о слободах баскака Ахмата и его место в русском летописании рубежа XIII–XIV вв.,” <i>ISTORIYA</i> 13, no. 5 (115) (2022), https://doi.org/10.18254/S207987840021283-0. [in Russian] [Open access]","S.A. Maslova, “Монголы и Новгородская земля в XIII–XV вв.,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 2 (92) (2023): 32–33, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2023.92.2.005. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ilias A. Mustakimov, Lenar F. Abzalov, Marat S. Gatin and Roman Yu. Pochekaev, “Basqaq, Daruga, Shihne: The Problem of Correlation. Pt. 1: The Institution of Shihne and Its Evolution,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 3 (2025): 523–42, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-3.523-542. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the khans need officials of their own once the princes had submitted?","a":"Because a prince confirmed by the khan still had to be watched, and the baskak with his armed men saw that the people stayed obedient and the tribute came in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A papal envoy reported that a town which crossed him could be accused of disloyalty and destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans give way so quickly in 1269?","a":"Because the great baskak of Vladimir was in Novgorod while the princes gathered their army, and his presence meant the Horde's support stood behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They offered to withdraw from the whole Narova if no blood was shed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the baskaks disappear from the heart of the north-east?","a":"Because their numbers fell when the Golden Horde became a state of its own in the 1260s and again when it split in the last decades of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the early 14th century the princes themselves gathered the tribute, and baskaks survived only on the southern margins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Darughachi"},
    "ru-106": {"questions":["Ivan III's Greek wife reproached him under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for standing before the khan's seated envoys, telling him daily that she had married a slave.","Chroniclers read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a punishment for sins, and Catholic and Orthodox writers alike compared it to the Hebrews' bondage in Egypt and Babylon."],"abstract":"The <b>Tatar yoke</b> is the name long used in Russian for the period and the system of Mongol rule over Rus', which began when the princes were summoned to Batu in 1242 and lasted almost a quarter of a millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 'Tatar' was the name that writers in Rus', Western Europe, China and the Islamic world commonly gave the Mongols, although most of the Mongols' heirs rejected it as a name for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The rule meant tribute, a khan's charter for every principality, envoys at every installation and khans who redrew the map of Rus' when it suited them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 16th-century visitor to Moscow wrote that from 1237 nearly all the princes had been tributaries of the Tatars and every principality had been given at their will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chroniclers explained it as a punishment for sins, and in 1259 one wrote that God had brought wild beasts out of the desert to eat the flesh of the strong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The phrase itself is not found in Rus' sources of the 13th to 15th centuries, which speak instead of captivity, and no Rus' writer used it before the second half of the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Latin it is far older, appearing in a Polish chronicle of 1521 and an account of Muscovy of 1575, and two Latin texts of the 13th century already speak of a Tatar yoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any writer who knew the Bible could reach the image unaided, since Catholic and Orthodox authors both read the conquest through the Hebrews' bondage in Egypt and their captivity in Babylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same visitor heard that Ivan III still went out to meet the Tatar envoys and stood while they sat, until his Greek wife told him she had married a slave of the Tatars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later historians weighed the yoke differently, and one early 20th-century survey argued that because the khans imposed no administration of their own, their authority gave the quarrelling principalities a semblance of unity that Moscow used best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Charles J. Halperin, “A Note on Recent Research on the Term the ‘Tatar Yoke’ (Tatarskoe igo),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2025): 8–16, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-1.8-16. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “Book Review: Seleznev Yu.V. <i>Kartiny ordynskogo iga</i> [Scenes of the Horde’s Yoke],” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 393, 396, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.392-400. [Open access]","V.A. Kuchkin, “Завоевание Руси Батыем и ордынский «выход»,” in <i>Русь в XIII–XV веках: Новые открытия в области археологии и истории</i>, ed. L.A. Belyaev and E.L. Konyavskaya (Moscow: Indrik, 2021), 22–23, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2021.978-5-91674-655-6.22-35. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 84, 93, 97, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 18, 25, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 308–9, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Joo-Yup Lee, “The Historical Meanings of the Term Tatar: A Critical and Comprehensive Examination,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 2 (2025): 240–61, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-2.240-261. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the phrase called an anachronism?","a":"Because the Rus' sources written during Mongol rule never use it, speaking instead of captivity, and no Rus' author adopted it until the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Applying it to the 13th century puts a later name on the period, even though Latin writers of the time already used the image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did chroniclers explain the conquest as a punishment for sins?","a":"Because the Bible gave them a ready pattern in the bondage of the Hebrews, and the chronicles of Novgorod repeatedly treat invasion and famine as God's correction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same reading of history let writers of different churches arrive at the image of a yoke on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could a historian see any benefit in the yoke?","a":"Because the khans took tribute without imposing a government of their own, and their authority could settle feuds that the princes would otherwise have fought out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That view sits beside the evidence of humiliation and destruction, such as princes executed at the Horde and towns laid waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-107": {"questions":["When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died at Gorodets in November 1263, the metropolitan announced that the sun of the Suzdal land had set.","Orders named after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were founded by the Russian Empire in 1725, the Soviet Union in 1942 and the Russian Federation in 2010, honouring a prince who submitted to the Horde."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, Alexander Yaroslavich, was prince of Novgorod and from 1252 grand prince of Vladimir, a son of Yaroslav Vsevolodovich who died in 1263 and was recognised as a saint in the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His nickname recalls his victory over the Swedes on the Neva in 1240, but it is attested only from the 15th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In April 1242 he beat the Livonian knights on the ice of Lake Peipus, after which they gave up their claims to the land of Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Mongol order in Rus' was settled while he was still a junior prince, and he first went to Batu's camp in 1247, since 15th-century reports of an earlier journey are doubtful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Batu sent him and his brother Andrei on to the great khan, where Alexander received Kyiv and a nominal seniority and Andrei the throne of Vladimir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 1248 Pope Innocent IV wrote to him twice, praising him for refusing the yoke of the Tatars and pressing him to accept the authority of Rome, but on his return he said no.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1252, after Alexander went to the Horde, Batu sent an army under Nevryu against Andrei, and Alexander came back to take the throne of Vladimir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The story that he had denounced his brother to the khan first appears in an 18th-century historian, and the campaign fits the Horde's own moves against disloyal princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As grand prince he had the followers of his son Vasily mutilated for resisting the khan's envoys, and in 1259 he set guards over the census-takers in Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died on 14 November 1263 at Gorodets on his way back from the Horde, having taken monastic vows, and historians still argue whether his submission fastened the yoke on Rus' or saved it from the Catholic West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 86–87, 96–98, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>Житие Александра Невского</i>, ed. and trans. V. I. Okhotnikova, in <i>Библиотека литературы Древней Руси</i>, vol. 5, <i>XIII век</i>, ed. D. S. Likhachev et al. (St Petersburg: Nauka, 1997), http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=4962. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “Александр Невский как исторический деятель: спорные проблемы,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 35 (2021): 10–20, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nevskiy-kak-istoricheskiy-deyatel-spornye-problemy. [in Russian] [Open access]","A.V. Maiorov, “Александр Невский, римский папа и монгольский хан: к вопросу о «выборе» между Западом и Востоком,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 4 (86) (2021): 5–8, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2021.86.4.001. [in Russian] [Open access]","A.V. Maiorov, “Александр Невский, Бату и хан Мунке: Нашествие Неврюя и контроль над русскими землями,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 4 (90) (2022): 23–24, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2022.4.90.003. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikita Yu. Grebennikov, “Alexander Yaroslavich’s Journey to Batu: Textual Aspect of the Chronicle Reports of 1241–1242,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 577–86, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2026-14-3.577-586. [in Russian] [Open access]","S.A. Maslova, “Монголы и Новгородская земля в XIII–XV вв.,” <i>Древняя Русь. Вопросы медиевистики</i>, no. 2 (92) (2023): 28, https://doi.org/10.25986/IRI.2023.92.2.005. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the pope write to a prince of Novgorod?","a":"Because Rome hoped to bring the princes of Rus' into union with the Catholic Church and to win allies against a new Mongol attack, and it had already reached an agreement with the prince of Galicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alexander's refusal to go to the great khan was read in Rome as resistance to the Tatars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Alexander gain the throne of Vladimir in 1252?","a":"Because Batu, now able to install his own man as great khan, revisited the grants made by the hostile court at Karakorum in 1249.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander answered the summons to the Horde, Andrei did not, and an army was sent against Andrei while Alexander was given the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does his reputation divide historians?","a":"Because the same man beat the Swedes and the Livonian knights and also enforced the Mongol census on Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some read his submission as fastening the yoke on Rus', others as a choice that protected Orthodox Rus' from the Catholic West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander Nevsky"},
    "ru-108": {"questions":["The Novgorod chronicler counted about twenty dead of his own side at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 'or less, God knows', and named among them the son of a tanner.","Swedish sources say nothing of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the name of Birger as its leader appears only in Novgorod writings of the 15th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Neva</b> was fought on 15 July 1240 near the mouth of the Izhora, where a Swedish force that had sailed into the Neva was surprised in its camp by Alexander Yaroslavich, prince of Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle says the Swedes came in great strength in many ships, with Norwegians, Finns of the Sum and Yem, their prince and their bishops, meaning to take Ladoga and the whole Novgorod land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander rode first for Ladoga and then turned west, for the Swedes had halted at the Izhora and were digging ditches, perhaps for a fortress like those they had built in Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He attacked with his own retinue and the men of Novgorod and Ladoga without waiting for a full levy, and that night the Swedes buried their dead in a pit and sailed away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicler counted about twenty dead on his own side, or less, God knows, and named among them the son of a tanner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Life of Alexander, written by a younger contemporary probably in the 1280s, turned the fight into a holy war in which an Izhorian elder saw Boris and Gleb rowing to help their kinsman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also tells of six brave men, one riding up a gangplank onto a ship and another cutting down the gold-topped tent of the Swedish leader, stories its author said he had from Alexander himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its account of Alexander praying in St Sophia before he set out uses an old motif of enemies who cross the holy borders of the Rus' land and meet divine punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name of Birger as the Swedish leader appears only in Novgorod writings of the 15th century, and in 1240 he was not yet jarl, so who led the expedition is uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians have rated the battle as anything from a stand against a Catholic onslaught to a routine border clash, but the Swedes did not enter the Neva in force again until 1300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 84–85, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>Житие Александра Невского</i>, ed. and trans. V. I. Okhotnikova, in <i>Библиотека литературы Древней Руси</i>, vol. 5, <i>XIII век</i>, ed. D. S. Likhachev et al. (St Petersburg: Nauka, 1997), http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=4962. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir A. Kuchkin, “Александр Невский: победа на Неве, возвращение Пскова, сражение на Чудском озере,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 35 (2021): 30–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nevskiy-pobeda-na-neve-vozvraschenie-pskova-srazhenie-na-chudskom-ozere. [in Russian] [Open access]","M.A. Nesin and M.V. Fomichev, “О некоторых дискуссионных вопросах и мифах, относящихся к Невской битве 1240 года,” <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 1 (2020): 70–71, 102–3, https://doi.org/10.25797/NG.2020.5.1.003. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “Александр Невский как исторический деятель: спорные проблемы,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 35 (2021): 11–12, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nevskiy-kak-istoricheskiy-deyatel-spornye-problemy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Denis A. Lyapin, “О чем молился Александр Невский: эволюция житийного сюжета предыстории Невской битвы,” <i>Палеоросия</i>, no. 1 (12) (2020): 112–23, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-chem-molilsya-aleksandr-nevskiy-evolyutsiya-zhitiynogo-syuzheta-predystorii-nevskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Swedes stop at the mouth of the Izhora instead of sailing on to Ladoga?","a":"The sources do not say outright, but the Swedes stayed there for days and dug ditches, which suggests they meant to build a stronghold to hold the river mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Swedes did come back in 1300 they built exactly such a fortress on the Neva, and it was destroyed the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Alexander fight without the full army of Novgorod?","a":"Because he moved before the levy could gather, taking his own retinue and the men he had with him, and the Life says many Novgorodians never caught up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Speed and surprise against a camp were worth more than numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Swedish commander's name uncertain?","a":"Because the earliest sources call him only a prince or a king, and Birger is first named in Novgorod writings two centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Swedish chronicle of the 14th century that took a close interest in Birger knew nothing of his taking part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of the Neva"},
    "ru-109": {"questions":["The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle says twenty brothers were killed and six captured in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after Rus' archers opened the fight and the brothers were surrounded.","The Russian name for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dates only from the end of the 15th century, and the exact site of that fight of 1242 on Lake Peipus is still disputed."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle on the Ice</b> was fought on 5 April 1242 on the frozen narrows between Lake Peipus and Lake Pskov, where Alexander Yaroslavich of Novgorod defeated the Livonian knights of the Teutonic Order and the men of the bishop of Dorpat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ended a war begun in 1240, when the crusaders took Izborsk and Pskov and raided to within thirty versts of Novgorod, and in which Alexander had already won back Koporye and Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle places the battle on Lake Chud at the Uzmen by the Raven's Rock, and says the Germans and the Chud rode at the Rus' like a wedge through their army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were driven seven versts across the ice to the Subol shore, and the chronicler counted countless Chud and 400 Germans dead and fifty taken to Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the same year the Germans made peace, giving up the lands of the Vod, Luga, Pskov and Latgalia and exchanging prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle, written on the Order's side around 1290, says that Rus' archers opened the fight, that the brothers were surrounded, and that twenty brothers were killed and six captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The two counts need not clash, since the Order had only a few dozen brothers in the region and the chroniclers of Novgorod seem to have counted every western soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Life of Alexander added that the ice could not be seen for blood and that an eyewitness saw God's host in the air, while the Russian name for the battle dates only from the end of the 15th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its exact site is still disputed, and underwater surveys in 2021 began looking for traces of the fight around the Raven Stone in the narrows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its fame now overshadows later battles with the Order, such as Rakovor in 1268, which Pskov chroniclers of the 15th century still held up as the measure of a great fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 85–87, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>Livländische Reimchronik</i>, ed. Leo Meyer (Paderborn: Ferdinand Schöningh, 1876), lines 2203–2264, pp. 51–52, https://archive.org/details/livlndischereim00reimgoog. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Житие Александра Невского</i>, ed. and trans. V. I. Okhotnikova, in <i>Библиотека литературы Древней Руси</i>, vol. 5, <i>XIII век</i>, ed. D. S. Likhachev et al. (St Petersburg: Nauka, 1997), http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=4962. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir A. Kuchkin, “Александр Невский: победа на Неве, возвращение Пскова, сражение на Чудском озере,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 35 (2021): 38–41, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nevskiy-pobeda-na-neve-vozvraschenie-pskova-srazhenie-na-chudskom-ozere. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anton A. Gorsky, “Александр Невский как исторический деятель: спорные проблемы,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 35 (2021): 11–12, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nevskiy-kak-istoricheskiy-deyatel-spornye-problemy. [in Russian] [Open access]","A.V. Stepanov and S.G. Fokin, “Подводно-археологические исследования на Чудском озере в 2021 г.,” <i>Археология и история Пскова и Псковской земли</i> 37 (2022): 231–44, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2022.978-5-94375-421-0.231-244. [in Russian] [Open access]","V.I. Kleimenov, “«Сие бысть побоище силно, якоже бысть Ледовое побоище, оу Раковора»: забытая страница военной истории Северо-Запада Руси,” <i>Археология и история Пскова и Псковской земли</i> 37 (2022): 272–82, https://doi.org/10.25681/IARAS.2022.978-5-94375-421-0.272-282. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alan Murray, “The <i>Livonian Rhymed Chronicle</i> as a Transitional Text: Formulaic Language in Middle High German Verse History,” <i>Rilce</i> 36, no. 4 (2020): 1324–43, https://doi.org/10.15581/008.36.4.1324-43. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Rus' and Livonian accounts give such different numbers of dead?","a":"Because the Livonian chronicle counted only the knight brothers, of whom the Order had a few dozen in the region, while the Novgorod chronicler seems to have counted every German soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both agree that the Order's men were beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could a large battle be fought on a lake ice in April?","a":"Because the winter of 1241 and 1242 was unusually hard across Europe, so cold that the Danube froze to its mouth, and the narrows where the armies met froze to the bottom near the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle's pursuit of seven versts across the ice fits that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Order make peace so soon afterwards?","a":"Because the defeat ended its hold on Pskov and its raids towards Novgorod, and it gave up every claim it had made in 1240 and 1241.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livonian forces never again took so much Novgorod land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle on the Ice"},
    "ru-110": {"questions":["Recruited mostly from Westphalia and the Rhineland, the knights of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split in the 15th century into rival regional parties fighting over the order's highest offices.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the Knighthood of Christ founded at Riga around 1202, the first military order created to fight anywhere outside the Holy Land and Iberia."],"abstract":"The <b>Livonian Order</b> was the branch of the Teutonic Order that ruled much of what is now Latvia and Estonia after 1236, and it was the crusading power that Novgorod and Pskov faced across their western border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of the Knighthood of Christ of Livonia, founded at Riga around 1202 on the model of the Templars, the first military order created for service outside the Holy Land and Iberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pope gave those knights the Templar rule and a sword and a cross to wear, which is why they were called the Sword Brothers, and he put them under the obedience of the bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1236 they were routed by the Lithuanians at Saule, where the master and about half the knight brethren were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The survivors laid down their own sign and took the black cross of the Teutonic Order, which sent Hermann Balk with 54 brothers to be their new master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The order and the bishop of Dorpat soon pushed east, and in 1240 the Germans took Izborsk and held Pskov through a party inside the town until Alexander Yaroslavich drove them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The order's own verse chronicle admits that at the lake battle of 1242 twenty brothers were killed and six captured after the Rus' surrounded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peace that year cost it every conquest in the Novgorod land, and in 1269 the Germans gave up the whole Narova rather than face an army gathered with the great baskak of Vladimir present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In time the Livonian branch became an almost autonomous unit with masters appointed for life, recruiting its knights in Westphalia and the Rhineland and ruling about thirty districts from castles with convents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its last master, Gotthard Kettler, left the order in 1561, turned its lands into a duchy under the king of Poland and became duke himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Henry of Livonia, <i>The Chronicle of Henry of Livonia</i> 6.6, trans. James A. Brundage (Madison: University of Wisconsin Press, 1961), 40, https://archive.org/details/chronicle-of-henry-of-livonia-1961. [Open access]","Alan V. Murray, “The Sword Brothers at War: Observations on the Military Activity of the Knighthood of Christ in the Conquest of Livonia and Estonia (1203–1227),” <i>Ordines Militares</i> 18 (2013): 27–29, 31–32, https://doi.org/10.12775/OM.2013.002. [Open access]","<i>Livländische Reimchronik</i>, ed. Leo Meyer (Paderborn: Ferdinand Schöningh, 1876), lines 1906–2016, 2236–2264, pp. 44–47, 52, https://archive.org/details/livlndischereim00reimgoog. [in German] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 85–87, 103–4, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Johannes A. Mol, “The Knight Brothers from the Low Countries in the Conflict between the Westphalians and the Rhinelanders in the Livonian Branch of the Teutonic Order,” <i>Ordines Militares</i> 20 (2015): 124–26, https://apcz.umk.pl/OM/article/view/OM.2015.006. [Open access]","Juhan Kreem, “Die inneren Voraussetzungen der Säkularisation des Deutschen Ordens in Livland,” <i>Ordines Militares</i> 22 (2017): 93–94, https://doi.org/10.12775/OM.2017.004. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a military order founded so far from the Holy Land?","a":"Because the bishop of Livonia and his helpers needed a permanent fighting force to hold the new church among pagan neighbours, and pilgrims came and went each year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Templars offered a ready model, adapted with a sword on the brothers' insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Sword Brothers join the Teutonic Order?","a":"Because the defeat at Saule killed their master and about half their knights, and the Teutonic Order could send a new master and fresh brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The survivors took the black cross and became its Livonian branch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the order end as a secular duchy?","a":"Because its last master chose to leave the order and keep part of its land as a duchy under the Polish crown, much as the grand master in Prussia had done in 1525.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His supporters said this saved what was left of the country, and his critics called him a traitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Livonian Order"},
    "ru-111": {"questions":["Peace sworn on Orekhov island in 1323 interrupted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a generation, until a Swedish king proposed a debate between the churches and then besieged the island fort.","Karelians and Izhorians, peoples that both a Baltic kingdom and a Russian merchant republic wished to tax and baptise, lived on the ground contested in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for two centuries."],"abstract":"The <b>Swedish–Novgorodian Wars</b> were the campaigns fought on and off between the kingdom of Sweden and the republic of Novgorod from the 13th century into the 15th, over the Neva, the Gulf of Finland and Karelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was at stake was the water road from Lake Ladoga to the Baltic and the allegiance of the Karelians and the Izhorians, peoples whom both powers wished to tax and to baptise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1293 Sweden built a castle at Vyborg on the decision of the regent Torgils Knutsson, and a Novgorod force sent against it withdrew when a thaw flooded the ground round the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1300 a Swedish fleet founded a fort called the crown of the land where the Okhta joins the Neva, built with the help of a master the chronicler says came from the pope in Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grand prince Andrei and the men of Novgorod stormed it in May 1301, and excavations between 2006 and 2010 uncovered about 70 per cent of its site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1323 Novgorod built a fort on Orekhov island at the head of the Neva, and envoys of the Swedish king came there and concluded an everlasting peace on the old terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peace was renewed in 1338, and for a generation the frontier it drew through Karelia held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1348 King Magnus Eriksson sent to Novgorod proposing a debate between the two churches and threatening war if the city would not take the faith judged better, and Novgorod replied that the question belonged to the patriarch in Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Magnus took the fort that August and had Izhorians baptised into the Latin rite, but it was retaken in February 1349, and his campaign of 1350 ended with much of his fleet wrecked by a storm off Narva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The grand prince Simeon of Moscow turned back on the road and his brother Ivan left Novgorod once the fort fell, a failure to help that historians still try to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 112–115, 122–123, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 141–143, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxiii, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Ю. В. Селезнёв, «Из похода “придоша новгородци с князем Олександромь вси здорови”: Русско-шведские конфликты в XIII столетии», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-pohoda-pridosha-novgorodtsi-s-knyazem-oleksandrom-vsi-zdorovi-russko-shvedskie-konflikty-v-xiii-stoletii. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Е. Сорокин, В. Н. Матвеев, Б. С. Короткевич и К. А. Михайлов, «Штурм крепости Ландскрона по археологическим данным», <i>Stratum plus</i>, no. 6 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shturm-kreposti-landskrona-po-arheologicheskim-dannym. [in Russian] [Open access]","Jean-Marie Maillefer, “La croisade du roi de Suède Magnus Eriksson contre Novgorod (1348–1351),” in <i>L’expansion occidentale (XIe–XVe siècles): Formes et conséquences</i> (Paris: Éditions de la Sorbonne, 2003), 87–96, https://doi.org/10.4000/books.psorbonne.13682. [in French] [Open access]","Моше Гринберг, «Шведский крестовый поход против Новгорода в 1348 г. и московско-новгородские отношения», <i>Rossica Antiqua</i>, no. 1–2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedskiy-krestovyy-pohod-protiv-novgoroda-v-1348-g-i-moskovsko-novgorodskie-otnosheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sweden and Novgorod keep fighting over the Neva?","a":"Because the river was the water road from Lake Ladoga to the Baltic, and whoever held its mouth could tax that trade and cut Karelia off from the Russian lands. The Swedish fort of 1300 at the Okhta did exactly that until Novgorod destroyed it."},{"q":"Why did a Swedish king offer Novgorod a debate about faith?","a":"Because his campaign of 1348 was a crusade meant to bring the Orthodox and the pagan peoples of the region into the Latin church. Novgorod refused to argue doctrine and sent him to the patriarch in Constantinople, so the king attacked."},{"q":"Why did the peace of 1323 matter for so long?","a":"Because it ended thirty years of raids and forts with a settlement both sides swore to keep. It was renewed in 1338, and the frontier it drew through Karelia held for a generation, until the king of Sweden turned the quarrel into a crusade."}],"wiki":"Swedish–Novgorodian Wars"},
    "ru-112": {"questions":["A Franciscan envoy riding to the Mongols in 1245 found that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had already gone to Batu's camp, and on the way home was kept eight days at his court.","Asked by Pope Innocent IV to accept a royal crown, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first answered that he could not take one without help against the Tatar army that troubled his land."],"abstract":"<b>Daniel of Galicia</b>, Daniel Romanovich, was the prince who ruled Galicia and Volhynia in the south-west of Rus' after the Mongol invasion, and in 1253 he was crowned king by a legate of Pope Innocent IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His victory at Yaroslav in 1245 settled his hold on Galicia, and his favourite town of Kholm became the seat of his court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The chronicle written for his house praises him as second only to Solomon and dresses him in the symbols of Byzantine imperial power, an eagle monument at Kholm among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Franciscan envoy John of Pian de Carpine, riding east in 1245, learnt from Daniel's brother Vasilko that Daniel had been given a safe-conduct to go to Batu, and found him gone there when he passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1246 Daniel reached terms with the Mongols that he could live with, and on the envoy's return the two brothers kept him eight days and said they wished to have the pope as their particular lord and father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Innocent IV took Daniel's land under the protection of the Roman church and promised in 1247 to respect its Orthodox rite, but the prince at first refused a crown, asking how he could take one without help against the Tatar army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He accepted it at the end of 1253 at Dorohychyn from the legate Opizo of Mezzano, while Rome was also negotiating a union of the churches with the emperor at Nicaea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The border town was chosen because the road into the land of the Yatvingians, a Baltic people against whom Daniel and his Polish allies were planning a campaign, began there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1259 the Mongol commander Burundai made Daniel's brother Vasilko and his son Lev pull down the fortifications of their towns, probably to keep his communications safe while he invaded Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the end of the 1250s the relations of the Romanovich princes with the Horde had settled into the vassalage and tribute that their crown had been meant to escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John of Pian de Carpine, “The Journey of Friar John of Pian de Carpine to the Court of Kuyuk Khan,” in <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 2–3, 32, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","М. В. Киселев, «“Вторый по Соломонѣ”: “Царственный образ” Даниила Галицкого и политическое наследие Византии», <i>Русин</i>, no. 1 (47) (2017): 34–49, https://doi.org/10.17223/18572685/47/4. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Майоров, «Коронация Даниила Галицкого: Никея и Рим во внешней политике галицко-волынских князей», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/koronatsiya-daniila-galitskogo-nikeya-i-rim-vo-vneshney-politike-galitsko-volynskih-knyazey. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Майоров, «Даниил Галицкий и крестовый поход в Пруссию», <i>Русин</i>, no. 4 (26) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/daniil-galitskiy-i-krestovyy-pohod-v-prussiyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Головко, «Встреча цивилизаций в середине XIII века: государство князей Романовичей между католическим Западом и монгольским Востоком», <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vstrecha-tsivilizatsiy-v-seredine-hiii-veka-gosudarstvo-knyazey-romanovichey-mezhdu-katolicheskim-zapadom-i-mongolskim-vostokom. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Воротынцев, «“Идоша ко Батыеви”: “Ордынская” дипломатия Даниила и Василька Романовичей в 40–50-х гг. XIII в.», <i>Русин</i>, no. 70 (2022): 54–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/idosha-ko-batyevi-ordynskaya-diplomatiya-daniila-i-vasilka-romanovichey-v-40-50-h-gg-xiii-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Воротынцев, «“Розмечете ж городы свое все”: ультиматум Бурундая Васильку Романовичу и Льву Даниловичу в контексте военно-дипломатических практик монголов эпохи завоеваний», <i>Русин</i>, no. 84 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rozmechete-zh-gorody-svoe-vse-ultimatum-burundaya-vasilku-romanovichu-i-lvu-danilovichu-v-kontekste-voenno-diplomaticheskih-praktik. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Daniel go to Batu's camp in person?","a":"Because after the invasion every prince of Rus' held his land on the Mongols' terms, and a ruler who stayed away risked the attack that a visit might prevent. Daniel came back in 1246 with relations he could live with for a while."},{"q":"Why did the pope want to crown a prince of the Orthodox church?","a":"Because Rome hoped to bring the churches of Rus' into union and to make Daniel's land a shield for Poland and Hungary against a second Mongol invasion. Innocent IV even promised to leave the Orthodox rite untouched."},{"q":"Why did the crown change so little?","a":"Because it brought no army with it. When Burundai came in 1259 Daniel's family obeyed his order to slight their towns, and by then the house was living as a vassal and tributary of the Horde."}],"wiki":"Daniel of Galicia"},
    "ru-113": {"questions":["Under Gediminas the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mediated between Novgorod and the Germans in 1326 and tried a few years later to name the bishop of Pskov over Novgorod's head.","A chancery with a Latin section and a Ruthenian one served the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ruled wide stretches of the old lands of Rus', Kyiv among them, by the late 14th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</b> was the state that the pagan Lithuanians built in the 13th century, and by the end of the 14th it had taken in most of the western and south-western lands of Rus', Kyiv among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rise rested on a ruling family that kept the right to rule for itself and on a nobility that became hereditary as the territory grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mindaugas, the prince who first drew the Lithuanian lands together, accepted Latin baptism in 1251 and was crowned king soon afterwards, and papal favour gave his war against Volhynia a religious sanction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The push into Rus' grew out of raids along the trade rivers, the Dvina, the Dnieper, the Pripyat and the Bug, and became conquest once a single ruler could direct it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polotsk came over relatively peacefully, while Novogrudok and the towns of the upper Neman, a region known as Black Rus', were won gradually and by war against Galicia and Volhynia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the 14th century Gediminas made the grand duchy a serious power, mediating between Novgorod and the Germans in 1326 and trying in 1331 to name the bishop of Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mongol attack had weakened the principalities that might have resisted, and in the later 13th century the grand dukes came to terms with the Horde, which did not stand in their way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian lands entered the grand duchy on terms that acknowledged the Horde's sovereignty over them, and a yarlyk of Tokhtamysh in 1393 handing Rus' lands to Vytautas is better read as Vytautas borrowing the Horde's legitimacy than as the Horde giving up its rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From the 1440s its chancery had a Latin section and a Ruthenian one, which wrote in a Slavic language close to Old Belarusian and Old Ukrainian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> How far back the state's history should be dated is itself argued over, and some recent national histories have pushed its origins earlier for reasons of present-day politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxv–xxvi, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","А. Дубонис, «Две модели литовской экспансии на Руси (XIII – начало XIV века): овладение Полоцком и Новогрудком», <i>Исторический вестник</i>, no. 7 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dve-modeli-litovskoy-ekspansii-na-rusi-xiii-nachalo-xiv-veka-ovladenie-polotskom-i-novogrudkom. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Петраускас, «Правящий род и знать: к вопросу о предпосылках формирования Литовского государства», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravyaschiy-rod-i-znat-k-voprosu-o-predposylkah-formirovaniya-litovskogo-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Головко, «Встреча цивилизаций в середине XIII века: государство князей Романовичей между католическим Западом и монгольским Востоком», <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vstrecha-tsivilizatsiy-v-seredine-hiii-veka-gosudarstvo-knyazey-romanovichey-mezhdu-katolicheskim-zapadom-i-mongolskim-vostokom. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Кибинь, «Начальный этап литовской экспансии в “Черной Руси”», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 3 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalnyy-etap-litovskoy-ekspansii-v-chernoy-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Александров, «Великое княжество Литовское и Орда», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 20, no. 19 (114) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikoe-knyazhestvo-litovskoe-i-orda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Кром, «Канцелярии и документы великих княжеств Литовского и Московского в XV – первой половине XVI в.: опыт сравнительного анализа», <i>Вспомогательные исторические дисциплины</i> 31 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kantselyarii-i-dokumenty-velikih-knyazhestv-litovskogo-i-moskovskogo-v-xv-pervoy-polovine-xvi-v-opyt-sravnitelnogo-analiza. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Старкус и Э. Б. Боев, «Проблема определения хронологических рамок истории Великого княжества Литовского в современной западной историографии», <i>Власть</i>, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-opredeleniya-hronologicheskih-ramok-istorii-velikogo-knyazhestva-litovskogo-v-sovremennoy-zapadnoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a small pagan people take so much of Rus'?","a":"Because the Mongol invasion had broken the strength of the principalities around them, and the grand dukes came to terms with the Horde, which then did not stop them. Some towns, like Polotsk, also came over peacefully for the sake of their trade."},{"q":"Why did the Horde allow the Lithuanian advance?","a":"Because the lands that passed to the grand duchy still owed the Horde its sovereignty on paper, and later grand dukes even used khans' charters to back their own claims. Alliance alternated with war rather than replacing it."},{"q":"Why did the grand duchy write in a Slavic language?","a":"Because so much of its land and so many of its people came from the old Rus'. Its chancery kept a Ruthenian section beside the Latin one, writing in a language close to Old Belarusian and Old Ukrainian."}],"wiki":"Grand Duchy of Lithuania"},
    "ru-114": {"questions":["In the autumn of 1362, a Tver chronicle records, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ravaged the Blue Waters in Podolia, a raid that later tradition remembered as a victory over three Tatar princes.","In 1370 the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood before Moscow and burned the town outside its walls, years after his brother Jaunutis had fled there and been baptised."],"abstract":"<b>Algirdas</b>, called Olgerd in the Russian chronicles, was the grand duke of Lithuania from 1345 to 1377, and under him the grand duchy pressed deeper into the lands of Rus' than ever before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He took power with his brother Kestutis by driving out their brother Jaunutis, whom their father Gediminas had named heir, and Jaunutis fled to Moscow and was baptised there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1346 he invaded Novgorod's Shelon country, complaining that the city's posadnik had called him a hound, and the Novgorodians killed the posadnik at their assembly for the land they had lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1349 he sent his brother Koriat to Khan Janibek to ask for an army, but a Muscovite embassy reminded the khan of Lithuanian raids on Novgorod, which paid the Horde tribute, and the envoys were handed over to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moscow remained his chief rival, and in 1370 his army stood before the city and burned the town outside its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the south his decisive stroke came in the autumn of 1362, when, a Tver chronicle records, he ravaged the Blue Waters and Belobereg'e in Podolia, then a land of the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later tradition made this a victory over three Tatar princes that expelled the Horde's power from Podolia, and its consequences mattered greatly for the lands of present-day Ukraine and Belarus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sources are few, late and partisan, and some historians doubt that the battle was more than a raid, since it left no echo in the annals to match that of Kulikovo or the Vorskla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The contest reached the church as well: in 1358 the metropolitan Alexius, an ally of Moscow, was beaten in Kyiv on Algirdas's orders, an episode known only from a synodal act of 1381.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Algirdas died in 1377, and the quarrels that followed among his heirs brought a real relief to Novgorod and Moscow, pressed as they were between Tatars, Lithuanians and Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 140–141, 152, 156, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxvi, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","С. А. Петров, «Литва и Орда», <i>Вопросы национализма</i>, no. 1 (21) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/litva-i-orda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. М. Шабульдо, «К итогам изучения синеводской проблемы», <i>История и современность</i>, no. 1 (17) (2013): 69–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-itogam-izucheniya-sinevodskoy-problemy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Бабенко и О. В. Комаров, «Битва на Синих Водах – факт или историографический миф?», <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 2 (6) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bitva-na-sinih-vodah-fakt-ili-istoriograficheskiy-mif. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Д. Кадура, «Почему князья допускали физическую расправу над русскими митрополитами в XIV в.? Сходство событий 1358 и 1378 гг.», <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 2 (2024): 244–252, https://doi.org/10.47132/1814-5574_2024_2_244. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Algirdas ask the khan for help?","a":"Because in 1349 he was losing to the crusaders and the Poles, and Lithuania had been allied with the Horde before. Moscow wrecked the plan by reminding the khan that Lithuanians had raided Novgorod, a tributary of the Horde, and the khan handed the envoys over."},{"q":"Why is the battle at the Blue Waters disputed?","a":"Because the earliest record is a few lines in a Tver chronicle about a raid in Podolia, and the story of three defeated Tatar princes comes from later writing produced during wars over the region. Some historians think the event was much smaller than the legend."},{"q":"Why did a metropolitan end up beaten in Kyiv?","a":"Because the head of the Russian church sided with Moscow, Algirdas's enemy, and their conflict came to a head in 1358. The episode survives only in a church council's record of 1381."}],"wiki":"Algirdas"},
    "ru-115": {"questions":["A year after a treaty promised him a Muscovite bride, Jogaila chose a Polish queen instead, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sealed at Kreva castle turned Lithuania towards Latin Christianity.","Polish historians now often call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an act rather than a union, since its verb applicare has been read as anything from alliance to annexation."],"abstract":"The <b>Union of Krewo</b> was the agreement declared at Kreva castle on 14 August 1385 under which Jogaila, grand duke of Lithuania, was to marry the young Queen Jadwiga and take the throne of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jadwiga had been betrothed as a child to William of Austria, but after her father Louis of Hungary died leaving no son, the Polish lords looked instead to the pagan grand duke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The talks began in October 1384, when Polish envoys came secretly to Vilnius and a Lithuanian embassy went back to Kraków.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both states were under attack from the Teutonic Order, and the alliance the threat pushed them into would break the Order's army at Grunwald in 1410.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only a year earlier a treaty agreed with Dmitry Donskoy of Moscow had provided for Jogaila to marry Dmitry's daughter instead, and its terms match those of the Kreva act closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The choice of Kraków over Moscow brought the conversion of Lithuania to Latin Christianity and a grand duchy of several faiths, as Lithuanian and Polish historians now agree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The act's most argued word is applicare, the verb by which Jogaila attached his lands to the Polish crown, and historians have read it as anything from union to outright annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For that reason many Polish historians now prefer to call it the Act of Krewo, a record of the marriage bargain rather than a union in law, since the grand duchy kept its statehood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many Orthodox nobles of the grand duchy saw in it a threat to their country's independence and their faith, and Russian historians from Karamzin onwards dated the oppression of Orthodoxy in Lithuania from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jogaila was crowned king of Poland as Władysław in 1386, the year of the marriage, and the Jagiellonian dynasty he founded ruled Poland at its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["И. Ю. Уваров, «Кревская уния 1385 года – военно-политический договор ВКЛ и Польши», <i>Вестник Могилевского государственного университета имени А. А. Кулешова. Серия A</i>, no. 1 (45) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krevskaya-uniya-1385-goda-voenno-politicheskiy-dogovor-vkl-i-polshi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Яворский и Д. Шульц, «Польско-литовские отношения 1385–1569 гг. в польской историографии XIX–XXI вв.», <i>Исторический вестник</i>, no. 7 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polsko-litovskie-otnosheniya-1385-1569-gg-v-polskoy-istoriografii-xix-xxi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Столяров, «Кревская уния 1385 года в русской историографии XIX – начала XX века», <i>Филология и культура</i>, no. 19 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krevskaya-uniya-1385-goda-v-russkoy-istoriografii-xix-nachala-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Петкевич, «Литовцы, русские, поляки в польско-литовском государстве XIV–XVII вв.», <i>История и современность</i>, no. 1 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/litovtsy-russkie-polyaki-v-polsko-litovskom-gosudarstve-xiv-xvii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxvi, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 180, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Polish lords want a pagan grand duke as king?","a":"Because Louis of Hungary had left no son, the young Queen Jadwiga needed a husband, and Poland needed help against the Teutonic Order. A Lithuanian ruler who would convert brought an army and an end to a dangerous neighbour."},{"q":"Why did Jogaila choose Poland over Moscow?","a":"The reasons are not fully known. A treaty of 1384 with Dmitry Donskoy had already promised him a Muscovite bride on very similar terms, and historians can only guess why the Polish offer, with its crown, won."},{"q":"Why do historians argue over a single Latin verb?","a":"Because applicare decides what the agreement did. If it meant incorporation, Lithuania was annexed to Poland; if it meant something looser, the two states were joined only through their ruler, and the grand duchy's later separate history fits that reading."}],"wiki":"Union of Krewo"},
    "ru-116": {"questions":["Letters from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Mamluk sultan Baybars asked for help against Hulagu, presenting a war between two grandsons of Genghis Khan as a struggle to restore the faith.","The Arab historians say a sheikh of Bukhara converted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first of Genghis Khan's descendants to accept Islam, more than half a century before Uzbeg."],"abstract":"<b>Berke</b>, a grandson of Genghis Khan and a brother of Batu, ruled the Golden Horde from 1257 to the mid-1260s and was the first of Genghis Khan's descendants to accept Islam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Arab historians credit his conversion to the Bukharan sheikh al-Bakharzi, and Ibn Khaldun tells how the sheikh made the prince wait before admitting him to repeat his profession of faith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Franciscan William of Rubruck, crossing the steppe in 1253, learnt that Berke kept his pastures on the road by which Muslim merchants came from Persia, had made himself a Saracen and allowed no pork to be eaten in his camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Batu, he adds, had since ordered his brother to move beyond the Volga, not wanting Muslims to pass by where Berke was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arab writers praised him for building mosques and schools, yet his troops helped Hulagu take Baghdad in 1258, and he did not recall them when the caliph was put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The break came after 1262, when Berke demanded a share of the conquered lands under the law of Genghis Khan, and the quarrel became a war in which pagan Mongols and Muslims fought on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His letters to the Mamluk sultan Baybars asked for help against Hulagu and presented the war as a fight to restore the faith, and the alliance of the two rulers was aimed above all at the Ilkhanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some historians date the conversion of Berke, his family and his commanders to May 1263, because the letters show no sign of his Islam before that year, which sits awkwardly beside Rubruck's report of ten years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the Horde did not exchange one religion for another, since the belief in Tengri, the Eternal Heaven on which a khan's authority rested, was kept alongside Islam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Islam became the settled faith of the khans only with Uzbeg's accession in 1313, and whether Berke's own faith shaped his policy or simply served it is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["William of Rubruck, <i>The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55</i>, trans. William Woodville Rockhill (London: Hakluyt Society, 1900), 117, https://archive.org/details/journeyofwilliam00ruys. [Open access]","А. Г. Шереметьев, «Религиозный фактор во внешней политике Золотой Орды при хане Берке», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i> 13, no. 3 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/religioznyy-faktor-vo-vneshney-politike-zolotoy-ordy-pri-hane-berke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Гарустович, «Средневековые авторы о распространении ислама в улусе Джучи», <i>Вестник Академии наук Республики Башкортостан</i> 17, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/srednevekovye-avtory-o-rasprostranenii-islama-v-uluse-dzhuchi. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Сидоренко, «Принятие религии ислама Золотой Ордой и начальный этап дипломатических отношений между Берке и Бейбарсом по документальным сведениям Абд аз-Захира», <i>Материалы по археологии, истории и этнографии Таврии</i>, no. 25 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prinyatie-religii-islama-zolotoy-ordoy-i-nachalnyy-etap-diplomaticheskih-otnosheniy-mezhdu-berke-i-beybarsom-po-dokumentalnym. [in Russian] [Open access]","Elmira G. Sayfetdinova, “The Personality of Sultan Baybars and His Role in Developing Relations of the Mamluk Egypt with the Golden Horde according to Arab Sources,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 726–735, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.726-735. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 737–738, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a Muslim khan help to destroy the caliphate's capital?","a":"Because in 1258 the Mongol princes still acted together, and the Golden Horde had troops in Hulagu's army. Berke only turned on Hulagu after 1262, in a quarrel over conquered land, and the religious case was put forward afterwards."},{"q":"Why is the date of his conversion uncertain?","a":"Because the witnesses disagree. Rubruck heard in 1253 that Berke was already a Muslim, while a recent reading of Baybars's correspondence finds no sign of it before May 1263."},{"q":"Why did Islam not take hold among the khans straight away?","a":"Because the Mongols' own belief in Tengri, the Eternal Heaven, still underpinned a khan's authority, and Islam was added alongside it rather than replacing it. Only Uzbeg, more than forty years later, made it the khans' settled faith."}],"wiki":"Berke"},
    "ru-117": {"questions":["Mikhail of Tver was tried for two months and executed in 1318 at the camp of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose sister had died as a captive of Mikhail's men.","A Moroccan traveller called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the seven mighty kings of the world and marvelled at his camp, a whole town on the move with mosques and bazaars."],"abstract":"<b>Uzbeg Khan</b> ruled the Golden Horde from 1313 to 1341, and under him Islam took root in its towns, where Muslim quarters and religious buildings spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Muslim faction backed his bid for the throne, and a Persian chronicler says he told the amirs to exchange the Mongol law, the Yasa, for the sharia and executed over a hundred plotters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1314 his envoys told the Mamluk sultan that the last unbelievers in his realm had been offered Islam or war and destroyed, yet on 20 March that year he granted the Franciscans a charter to preach freely through his lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Latin letters from inside the Horde show much of the steppe keeping its old beliefs, and the likeliest reading is that Uzbeg tried forced conversion and gave it up within two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Coins show that his headquarters stayed in the Moksha country to the north-west until 1321, when, his power secure, he moved back to the old capital region on the lower Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For the princes of Rus' his reign meant the khan's judgement on the rivalry of Tver and Moscow, and he gave it to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1318 he summoned Mikhail of Tver, whose men had captured Uzbeg's sister, Yuri of Moscow's wife, who died in captivity, and after a two-month trial Mikhail was executed on 22 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Tver rose in 1327 and killed many of the Tatars who had come with the envoy Shevkal, a Tatar army took the town and wasted the land, and in 1339 Uzbeg had Alexander of Tver and his son killed at the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tale of Mikhail of Tver, written by a witness, says the new tsar had entered the Saracen faith and likens him to Nebuchadnezzar, but it names his religion in that one passage only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ibn Battuta, who visited his camp, called him one of the seven mighty kings of the world and described a whole town on the move, with mosques and bazaars carried on wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 121, 124, 133, 136, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxv, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 736–755, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","Yury V. Sochnev, “Notes and Additions to the Comments on the Yarlik Granted by Uzbek Khan to the Franciscans in 1314,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 4 (2018): 783–806, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-4.783-806. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. М. Пигарёв, «Формирование на Нижней Волге столичного центра Улуса Джучи в эпоху правления хана Узбека: анализ нумизматического материала», <i>Археология евразийских степей</i>, no. 3 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-na-nizhney-volge-stolichnogo-tsentra-ulusa-dzhuchi-v-epohu-pravleniya-hana-uzbeka-analiz-numizmaticheskogo-materiala. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ibn Battuta, <i>Travels in Asia and Africa, 1325–1354</i>, trans. H. A. R. Gibb (London: Routledge and Kegan Paul, 1929), 147–149, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.16353. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Uzbeg let Christian missionaries preach in a Muslim realm?","a":"Because the attempt to force the nomads into Islam met fierce resistance, and the old Mongol habit of tolerating every religion served a khan better. The Franciscans and the Russian metropolitan alike were asked only to pray for him and his family."},{"q":"Why did Uzbeg side with Moscow against Tver?","a":"Because the princes of Moscow had become his main allies in the north-east, and Tver had defied him. Mikhail of Tver's men had captured the khan's own sister, and Tver later rose against the khan's envoy."},{"q":"Why do the Russian chronicles say so little about his religion?","a":"Because the conversion changed little for his Orthodox subjects. Apart from one passage in the Tale of Mikhail of Tver, the chroniclers dwell on what the khan did to their princes, not on what he believed."}],"wiki":"Özbeg Khan"},
    "ru-118": {"questions":["In 1276 a bishop of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put questions to a council in Constantinople about mission and dealings with people of other faiths who came from the Horde.","As the khans' power waned in the 15th century the bishops of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved to the Krutitsy in Moscow, where the title survives today."],"abstract":"The <b>Diocese of Sarai</b> was the Orthodox bishopric founded in 1261, when Metropolitan Kirill II made Mitrofan bishop of Sarai, the Horde's capital on the lower Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual explanation points to the many Russian captives held in the Horde, the princes who came constantly to the khan's headquarters and some hope of bringing the Mongols to Orthodoxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded in the year Michael VIII Palaeologus recovered Constantinople, and one reading sees in it a step by the Russian church to guard the autonomy it had won from the patriarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second bishop, Theognost, appointed in 1269, was styled bishop of Pereyaslavl and Sarai, and in August 1276 he put a list of questions to a church council in Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some questions that survive only in the Old Russian version of the council's answers concern mission and dealings with people of other faiths from the Horde, though a later Russian editor added several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The see was one sign of a wider bargain, for Mongol law required tolerance of local clergy, and the church of Rus' was freed from paying the Horde's tribute and from the princes' control over its people and property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The khans' charters to the metropolitans, such as the one Mengu-Timur granted Kirill on 1 August 1267, exempted the clergy from taxes and duties and asked in return for prayers for the khan and his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They survive in a collection that the metropolitans in Moscow compiled to defend their property, and many of the taxes listed in the Russian texts were added during translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The metropolitans repaid the khans with loyalty to them and to Moscow, their chief ally, as when the metropolitan Theognostus helped to force the rebellious Alexander of Tver out of Pskov in 1329.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the khans' power waned in the 15th century the bishops of Sarai moved their see to the Krutitsy in Moscow, and the metropolitan of Krutitsy still serves as the patriarch's deputy there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["В. И. Петрушко, «К вопросу об основании митрополитом Кириллом II епископских кафедр в Сарае и Твери», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 4 (28) (2024): 73–85, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2024_4_73. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Мухин, «Сарайская православная епархия и Золотая Орда: современный взгляд, евразийское преломление», <i>Казанский вестник молодых ученых</i> 6, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sarayskaya-pravoslavnaya-eparhiya-i-zolotaya-orda-sovremennyy-vzglyad-evraziyskoe-prelomlenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Корогодина, «Несториане, яковиты и татары в правилах Константинопольского собора 1276 г.», <i>Вспомогательные исторические дисциплины</i> 39 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nestoriane-yakovity-i-tatary-v-pravilah-konstantinopolskogo-sobora-1276-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tat’yana Yu. Fomina, “The Diocesan Structure of Rus during the Mongol-Tatar Invasion in 1237–1240 (according to the chronicles),” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 254–265, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.254-265. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 741–743, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","Svetlana A. Maslova, “The Tax Terminology of the Short Collection of Khan’s Jarlyks Issued to Rus’ Metropolitans,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 636–651, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.636-651. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would the Orthodox church put a bishop in the khan's capital?","a":"Because Russians were there in numbers, captives and princes on their constant journeys to the khan, and they needed a pastor. The church may also have hoped to win Mongol converts and to strengthen its own standing at the court that ruled Rus'."},{"q":"Why did the khans protect the Russian clergy?","a":"Because Mongol law required tolerance of local priests, and the khans' charters freed the church from tribute and duties in return for prayers for the khan's family. A loyal church was useful to a distant ruler."},{"q":"Why can the khans' charters not be read at face value?","a":"Because they survive in a collection the Moscow metropolitans made to defend their property, and the Russian translators added long lists of taxes that the khans had not written."}]},
    "ru-119": {"questions":["Moscow's customs officers in the 16th century took only half the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from envoys who also traded, so Nogai merchants tried hard to join diplomatic missions.","Named after a clan's seal or brand, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a duty on commercial dealings that Ögedei first fixed at a thirtieth of the value of most goods."],"abstract":"The <b>tamga</b> was the duty on trade levied in the Mongol Empire and its successor states, named after the Turkic word for a seal or brand because a stamp on the goods showed that the tax was owed to the khan's treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tamga was first a clan's sign and mark of property, and the Uighur keeper of the seal whom Genghis Khan took over from the defeated Naimans became one of the first officers of his state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ögedei issued the first decree fixing a tax on commercial dealings in 1234 or 1235, at 10 per cent on alcoholic drink and a thirtieth of the value of other goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Golden Horde the rate was lower, 3 per cent, although Khan Janibek raised it to 5 per cent in 1347 after his war with the Italian trading colonies of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of the many dues the Horde drew from the Russian principalities, while the metropolitans and all the clergy were exempt from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The duty outlived the Horde in Muscovy, which founded its first customs houses at the end of the 15th century, and the Russian word tamozhnya, a customs house, preserves its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herberstein recorded in the early 16th century that the customs on all goods imported or exported went into the grand prince's treasury, at seven dengi on every ruble's worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow's customs officers took only half the duty, 2 per cent instead of 4, from envoys who also traded, so Nogai merchants tried to join diplomatic missions, and a customs statute fixed the system in the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Horde also left Russian the words kazna, the treasury, and yarlyk, a charter, though borrowed words do not by themselves prove that the institutions behind them were new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How deep the borrowings went is still argued: one recent survey accepts the Horde's political influence on the Rus' principalities while holding that their idea of the state owed much more to Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["R. Yu. Pochekaev, “Tamgha and the Struggle against It: On the History of Medieval Turkic-Mongol Taxation System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i>, no. 4 (6) (2014): 77–95, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tamgha-and-the-struggle-against-it-on-the-history-of-medieval-turkic-mongol-taxation-system. [Open access]","М. Тезджан, «“Тамга” – государственный символ и пошлина в Золотой Орде и наследных ханствах», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tamga-gosudarstvennyy-simvol-i-poshlina-v-zolotoy-orde-i-naslednyh-hanstvah. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Нефедов, «К вопросу о цивилизационном влиянии Золотой Орды и Османской империи на русские княжества», <i>Золотоордынское обозрение</i> 8, no. 4 (2020): 753–770, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-4.753-770. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 116, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 46, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]","Sinan Meriç and Derya Derin Paşaoğlu, “Postal Organisation (Yam) in the Golden Horde,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 1 (2023): 51, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-1.37-61. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a tax named after a seal?","a":"Because the tamga was first a clan's mark of property, and stamping goods with the khan's mark showed that the duty on them belonged to his treasury. The sign and the tax kept the same name."},{"q":"Why did Muscovy keep the Horde's trade duty?","a":"Because it worked and it paid. Muscovy founded customs houses at the end of the 15th century, collected duty on goods coming in and going out for the treasury, and in the 17th century fixed the system in a statute."},{"q":"Why do borrowed words not settle the question of Mongol influence?","a":"Because Rus' had money, market dues and officials before the conquest, so a new name could cover an old practice. That is why historians still argue how far the Horde changed Russian institutions."}],"wiki":"Tamga"},
    "ru-120": {"questions":["Under 1360 the Novgorod chronicler wrote of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a great tumult in the Horde, in which many tsars were killed with their wives and children.","Waves of plague may explain the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, two decades after 1359 in which dozens of claimants rose and fell because orderly succession by genealogy had broken down."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Troubles</b>, in Russian the great zamyatnya, were the two decades of civil war in the Golden Horde that followed the death of Khan Berdibek in 1359, when khan after khan was raised and overthrown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Until then the Horde had been strikingly stable, its khan chosen and sometimes removed by a council of the leaders of four ruling tribes, but after Berdibek the sources name dozens of rival claimants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Waves of plague, very active in the late 1350s and early 1360s, may explain the collapse, since the deaths of so many made orderly succession by genealogy impossible to manage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first blow fell in the winter of 1360, when a conspiracy of the nobility overthrew Khan Kulpa, killed him with two of his sons and put Navruz on the throne, as a Venetian notary at Tana recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the same year the Novgorod chronicler noted a great tumult in the Horde in which many tsars were killed with their wives and children and the ranks fought one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The princes of Rus' felt every change, since each new khan had to confirm their patents, and for that reason their chronicles record the break-up of the western Horde in close detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Khans of the line of Shiban held the lower Volga in the early 1360s, among them Murid, who tried to build himself a new centre at Gulistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> West of the Volga the commander Mamai ruled through khans of his own choosing, beginning with Abdallah, and in 1380 the Novgorod chronicler could still describe a weak tsar with Mamai controlling all his affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While the Horde was divided it could not prevent Algirdas of Lithuania from taking Podolia and the Kyiv region, and the khans made the best of it by taking tribute for the lost lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The troubles ended with a Mongol recovery under Tokhtamysh, who burned Moscow in 1382, two years after Dmitry Donskoy had beaten Mamai on the field of Kulikovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 148, 157, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxv, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sergey Karpov, “The Beginning of the Time of Troubles in the Golden Horde and the Coup of Navruz Khan,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 3 (2018): 528–536, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-3.528-536. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. И. Сорогин, «Начальный период “великой замятни” в русских летописях», <i>Вестник Южно-Уральского государственного университета. Серия: Социально-гуманитарные науки</i> 22, no. 2 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalnyy-period-velikoy-zamyatni-v-russkih-letopisyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Moshe Grinberg, “‘The First Wave’: The Shibanid Dominance in the Lower Volga Region in 1361–1367,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 355–378, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-2.355-378. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 327–329, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “On Recent Studies of Rus’ Relations with the Tatars of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 45, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.32-50. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a stable Horde collapse so suddenly after 1359?","a":"Because its succession rested on genealogy and on a council of tribal leaders, and the plague of the late 1350s killed so many that nobody could tell who had the right to rule. One claimant after another was raised and either died or was overthrown."},{"q":"Why do Russian chronicles describe the Horde's civil war so closely?","a":"Because every new khan had to confirm the princes' patents, so the princes and their chroniclers had to know who was khan at any moment. The turnover sent princes to the Horde again and again."},{"q":"Why could Mamai not stop Lithuania taking Kyiv and Podolia?","a":"Because during the troubles he did not have the means. He made the best of it by taking tribute for the lost lands, which Lithuania went on paying."}],"wiki":"Great Troubles"},
    "ru-121": {"sources":["Henry H. Howorth, <i>History of the Mongols from the 9th to the 19th Century</i>, part 2, <i>The So-Called Tartars of Russia and Central Asia</i>, division 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1880), 202, 208, 212–16, https://archive.org/details/historyofmongols21howo. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 157–58, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sergey Karpov, “The Beginning of the Time of Troubles in the Golden Horde and the Coup of Navruz Khan,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 3 (2018): 528–536, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-3.528-536. [in Russian] [Open access]","Victor N. Talakh, “Genoese-Tatar Treaties of 1380 and 1381 in Their Historical Context,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 108–148, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.108-148. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oleg I. Khoruzhenko, “The Tamga of Mamai in the Glinsky Princes Seals of the 16th and 17th Centuries,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2025): 166–182, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-1.166-182. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 325–343, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg (New York: Macmillan, 1925), 95–96, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Golden Horde split in two in the 1360s?","a":"A coup in 1359 and 1360 killed the reigning khan, his sons and his emirs, and plague had already weakened the realm. By 1361 one part obeyed a khan at Sarai and the other obeyed Mamai, and they raided each other across the Volga."},{"q":"Why did Mamai march against Moscow in 1380?","a":"Dmitry of Moscow had stopped deferring to him, and in 1378 an army Mamai sent was beaten on the Vozha. Tatar prestige had suffered, and he had either to give up his rule over the Russians or restore it by force."},{"q":"Why did Mamai die in the Crimea and not at Russian hands?","a":"After Kulikovo he retreated south, where Tokhtamysh, who had taken the eastern half of the Horde, beat him in a second battle. He fled to the Genoese at Caffa, who killed him, perhaps in return for the land their treaties gave them."}],"abstract":"<b>Mamai</b> was an emir of the Golden Horde who for about twenty years after 1360 ruled its western half in the name of khans he set up and removed, and the Novgorod chronicle calls him a prince, never a tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Horde had fallen into disorder in 1359 and 1360, when a coup killed Khan Kulpa with two of his sons and the emirs who supported him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Black Death had already emptied many of its towns, and the loss of people fed the political instability of the second half of the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By 1361 the Horde was virtually split in two, one part obeying a khan at Sarai and the other obeying Mamai and his protégé Abdullah, and the two sides raided each other across the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coins from the mints he controlled carry a bird-shaped tamga, or clan sign, and the Glinsky princes, who claimed him as their ancestor, put a version of it on their seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Moscow under Dmitry Ivanovich stopped deferring to him, and in 1378 an army he sent under the murza Begich was beaten on the Vozha in the Ryazan land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mamai saw that he must either give up his rule over the Russians or restore it by force, so he spent two years gathering a larger army and agreed with Jogaila of Lithuania that their forces would join in September 1380.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicler wrote that there was some weak man tsar among the Tatars while Mamai controlled all their affairs, savagely enraged against the grand prince and the whole Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dmitry crossed the Don and met him where the Nepryadva joins it on 8 September 1380, routing the Tatars before the Lithuanians arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mamai fled south, lost a second battle to the new khan Tokhtamysh and was killed by the Genoese at Caffa, perhaps between late November 1380 and January 1381, and land they gained by treaty in those months may have been their reward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","questions":["Beaten by Dmitry of Moscow at Kulikovo in 1380, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fled to the Crimea, lost a second battle to Tokhtamysh and was put to death by the Genoese at Caffa.","Although no khan, the emir <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governed the western half of the Golden Horde for about twenty years through khans that he set on the throne and removed."],"wiki":"Mamai"},
    "ru-122": {"sources":["Henry H. Howorth, <i>History of the Mongols from the 9th to the 19th Century</i>, part 2, <i>The So-Called Tartars of Russia and Central Asia</i>, division 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1880), 216, 222–27, 235, 246–55, 261–62, https://archive.org/details/historyofmongols21howo. [Open access]","Victor N. Talakh, “Genoese-Tatar Treaties of 1380 and 1381 in Their Historical Context,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 108–148, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.108-148. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr V. Dzhanov, “The Tatar-Genoese Treaties of 1380 and 1381,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 4 (2020): 675–713, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-4.675-713. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “The Yarliq of Tokhtamish Khan to King Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland: The Problem of the Old Russian Text’s Origins,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 314–335, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-2.314-335. [Open access]","Sergey P. Karpov, “Like a Phoenix from the Ashes: The Rebirth of Tana’s Trade after the 1395 Disaster,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 3 (2020): 504–514, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-3.504-514. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dinara Suleimenova and Aigerim Zhumabayeva, “Political and Dynastic Relations between Khan Tokhtamysh, His Descendants, and Rulers of Moghulistan,” <i>Kazakhstan Oriental Studies</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 39–48, https://doi.org/10.63051/kos.2025.4.39. [Open access]","Olga A. Tufanova, “The Tale of Khan Tokhtamysh’ Invasion of Moscow: Paradoxes of Regret,” <i>Studia Litterarum</i> 6, no. 3 (2021): 134–147, https://doi.org/10.22455/2500-4247-2021-6-3-134-147. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Timur back Tokhtamysh at first?","a":"Tokhtamysh came to him as a refugee from the White Horde, whose khan Urus was Timur's rival in the eastern steppe. Timur called him his son, gave him towns and men, and kept arming him until he had won the eastern throne."},{"q":"Why did the Genoese of Caffa make treaties in his name?","a":"He had overthrown Mamai and, perhaps already before March 1380, made his power felt in the Crimea. The treaties of 1380 and 1381 gave Caffa rural districts around Soldaia and in Gothia, perhaps as a reward for the killing of Mamai."},{"q":"Why did his descendants turn to Moghulistan?","a":"Moghulistan had been his ally against Timur, and after his fall its rulers sheltered and protected his line. That support kept his family in play in the steppe and helped carry the Golden Horde's traditions into later states."}],"abstract":"<b>Tokhtamysh</b> was a khan of the Golden Horde, a prince of the house of Jochi, who united its eastern and western halves after 1380 and ruled them until Timur broke his power in the 1390s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When his father died he fled to Timur at Samarkand, who received him as a son, gave him towns in the eastern steppe and armed him again after each defeat by Urus Khan of the White Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Timur's backing he was finally installed at Sighnak, beat the last of Urus Khan's sons and then crossed the Volga against Mamai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His power in the Crimea may already have been established before March 1380, earlier than was once thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Genoese of Caffa concluded treaties with the Horde's lords of Solkhat in November 1380 and February 1381, and both seem to have been made in his name and interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The princes of Rus' sent him homage, but he wanted tribute as well, and in 1382 his army took Moscow and burned it, a disaster one chronicler told as plunder and destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He also corresponded with Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland, and the Old Russian text of his letter of 1393 was written down in the king's chancery from what his envoys said.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The break with his patron began on the Persian side, when he sacked Tabriz in the winter of 1385, and Timur answered with campaigns that beat him on the Kondurcha in 1391 and on the Terek in 1395.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1395 he was a fugitive who fled from the Vorskla in 1399 beside Vytautas of Lithuania, and he was put to death about seven years later, at Tyumen according to the Russian chronicles or by Edigu according to other writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Venetian trade through Tana revived only slowly after his fall, while his descendants found refuge in Moghulistan, whose rulers helped carry the Golden Horde's political tradition into the steppe states that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","questions":["The Genoese of Caffa signed treaties with the lords of Solkhat in November 1380 and February 1381 in the name of the new khan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who had just overthrown Mamai.","Beaten by Timur and then again beside Vytautas on the Vorskla in 1399, the fugitive khan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed about seven years later, by Edigu's hand in some accounts."],"wiki":"Tokhtamysh"},
    "ru-123": {"sources":["Sharaf al-Din Ali Yazdi, <i>The History of Timur-Bec, Known by the Name of Tamerlain the Great</i>, trans. from the French of François Pétis de la Croix, vol. 1 (London: J. Darby, 1723), 491–98, https://archive.org/details/39020024846878-thehistoryoftim. [Open access]","Henry H. Howorth, <i>History of the Mongols from the 9th to the 19th Century</i>, part 2, <i>The So-Called Tartars of Russia and Central Asia</i>, division 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1880), 246–47, 251–57, https://archive.org/details/historyofmongols21howo. [Open access]","Sergey P. Karpov, “Like a Phoenix from the Ashes: The Rebirth of Tana’s Trade after the 1395 Disaster,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 3 (2020): 504–514, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-3.504-514. [in Russian] [Open access]","Denis A. Lyapin, “Image of Tamerlane in the Tale of Temir Aksak,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 1 (2022): 89–101, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-1.89-101. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dinara Suleimenova and Aigerim Zhumabayeva, “Political and Dynastic Relations between Khan Tokhtamysh, His Descendants, and Rulers of Moghulistan,” <i>Kazakhstan Oriental Studies</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 39–48, https://doi.org/10.63051/kos.2025.4.39. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Timur go to war with the khan he had put on the throne?","a":"Tokhtamysh had turned on his patron, sacking Tabriz in 1385 and fighting him again in 1391. By 1395 Timur meant to finish him, and his court historian describes the march north through Derbent to the Terek."},{"q":"Why did Moscow think it had been saved in 1395?","a":"Timur's army took Yelets on the Ryazan border and then turned away along the Don. Moscow had just brought the icon of the Mother of God from Vladimir, and its chroniclers told the retreat as the Virgin's intercession."},{"q":"Why did the battle matter for trade on the Black Sea?","a":"After the victory Timur sacked Tana at the mouth of the Don, where Italian merchants had their trading stations, and destroyed Sarai and Astrakhan. Venetian trade through Tana revived only slowly in the years that followed."}],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Terek River</b> was fought on 22 April 1395 in the northern Caucasus between Timur and Tokhtamysh, khan of the Golden Horde, and it ended in the khan's flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timur had come north through the pass of Derbent, and Tokhtamysh, first camped behind a rampart of waggons on the Terek, fell back across the river before turning to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court historian Sharaf al-Din Ali Yazdi tells that the two armies faced each other in entrenched camps where no fire might be lit, and that one of Timur's princes deserted to the enemy the night before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the battle Timur's own reserve was driven back, his arrows spent and his lance and sword broken, and he was saved only when Sheikh Nur al-Din and fifty men dismounted around him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Horde gave way when Tokhtamysh fled with the princes of his house, and Timur's men killed great numbers of the fugitives and hanged many of those they took alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Timur followed to the Volga, where he made Koirijak, a son of Urus Khan, khan of the Golden Horde, and his men pursued the fugitives up the river while Tokhtamysh hid in the forests of the Bulgar country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of his armies took Yelets on the borders of Ryazan, and Moscow brought the old icon of the Mother of God from Vladimir, but Timur turned away after marching for some distance along the Don.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later Moscow chronicles told the story of Temir Aksak, the Lame Timur, as a divine intercession for the Orthodox, and each version recast the conqueror to suit the politics of its time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On his way south Timur sacked Tana at the mouth of the Don, where Venetian, Genoese and other merchants traded, and that winter he destroyed Astrakhan and Sarai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Venetian trade through Tana was rebuilt only slowly, and in 1398 the new masters of the steppe, Edigu and Timur-Qutlug, sent a joint embassy to Timur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","questions":["After winning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1395 Timur crossed the Volga, set up a son of Urus Khan as khan and sacked Tana, Astrakhan and Sarai before turning home.","Fought in the northern Caucasus on 22 April 1395, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke Tokhtamysh, and Moscow believed it was saved from the victor by an icon brought from Vladimir."],"wiki":"Battle of the Terek River"},
    "ru-124": {"sources":["Henry H. Howorth, <i>History of the Mongols from the 9th to the 19th Century</i>, part 2, <i>The So-Called Tartars of Russia and Central Asia</i>, division 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1880), 261–63, 266–68, 272–75, https://archive.org/details/historyofmongols21howo. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 179, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sergey P. Karpov, “Timur-Sultan and Kerim-Birdi: Two Attacks on Venetian Tana in 1410 and in 1418,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 758–769, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.758-769. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dinara Suleimenova and Aigerim Zhumabayeva, “Political and Dynastic Relations between Khan Tokhtamysh, His Descendants, and Rulers of Moghulistan,” <i>Kazakhstan Oriental Studies</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 39–48, https://doi.org/10.63051/kos.2025.4.39. [Open access]","Vitaly V. Penskoy and Tatyana M. Penskaya, “The Post-Horde World and the Union of Ivan III and Mengli-Girey I: An Attempt to Create a “Bipolar” Political System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 868–898, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.868-898. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “Moscow, Lithuania, the Horde and the Last Testaments of Vasily I,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 4 (2023): 881–901, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-4.881-901. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 325–343, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Edigu attack Moscow in 1408?","a":"Vasily I had for years ignored his dependence on the Horde, withheld tribute, sent no princes or envoys to the khans and given shelter to the sons of Tokhtamysh. Edigu's letter afterwards listed these failures one by one."},{"q":"Why did he lift the siege of Moscow?","a":"News came that a rival threatened Khan Pulad at Sarai, and the Horde could no longer muster its old numbers after plague, Timur's attack and internal quarrels. He took a ransom of 3,000 roubles and withdrew."},{"q":"Why did the Venetians at Tana suffer in his time?","a":"Power in the Horde changed hands often during the war between Tokhtamysh's sons and Edigu's men, and the merchants quarrelled with the khans over rent for their land. Tana was raided in 1410 and 1418, and Venice then walled it in stone."}],"abstract":"<b>Edigu</b> was an emir of the Golden Horde, called the Nogai chief in some accounts, who dominated it as beklerbek for about twenty years after Timur broke the power of Tokhtamysh, while other men held the title of khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1398 he joined Timur-Qutlug and Khizr Khwaja of Moghulistan in an embassy to Timur, a turning point in the diplomacy of the steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He reached the camp of Khan Timur-Qutlug before the battle on the Vorskla in 1399, mocked the terms of Vytautas of Lithuania and helped rout his army, with Tokhtamysh among the first to flee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the khans Shadibeg and Pulad he was the real master of the Horde's policy, and some writers say that Tokhtamysh himself died at his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Venetian sources show Tana caught in a civil war between the sons of Tokhtamysh and Edigu's supporters, and its trading station was raided in 1410 and again in 1418.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 1408 he marched on Moscow, whose grand prince Vasily I had stopped paying tribute and sheltered the sons of Tokhtamysh, and his troops took Pereyaslavl, Rostov, Nizhny Novgorod and Serpukhov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He left only after taking a ransom of 3,000 roubles from the city, and on his way out of the Russian land he took Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a letter to Vasily he complained that Russia had once paid tribute regularly and honoured the khans, but that Vasily had not gone to the Horde under three successive khans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had lifted the siege because a rival threatened Pulad at Sarai, for plague, Timur's attack and internal quarrels had left the Horde unable to muster its old numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He was killed fighting Kadirberdi, a son of Tokhtamysh, or drowned in another account, and the turmoil outlived him until the wars of the 1430s and 1440s broke the Horde world into separate khanates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","questions":["Venetian merchants at Tana were caught in the civil war between the sons of Tokhtamysh and the supporters of the beklerbek <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who dominated the Golden Horde after 1395.","Arriving at the camp of Timur-Qutlug before the battle on the Vorskla in 1399, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mocked the terms of Vytautas and helped rout the Lithuanian army."],"wiki":"Edigu"},
    "ru-125": {"sources":["Henry H. Howorth, <i>History of the Mongols from the 9th to the 19th Century</i>, part 2, <i>The So-Called Tartars of Russia and Central Asia</i>, division 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1880), 363–68, 422–23, https://archive.org/details/historyofmongols21howo. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 58–59, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","Anvar Aksanov, “Ulugh Muhammad’s Sons on the Throne of Kazan,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 3 (2018): 551–568, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-3.551-568. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vadim Trepavlov, “The “Father’s Sons”: The Elite of the Kazan Khanate in the Lithuanian Metrica,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 3 (2017): 600–611, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-3.600-611. [in Russian] [Open access]","Umut Yolsever, “The Importance of the Kazan Khanate in the Foreign Policy of the Crimean Khanate,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 4 (2025): 806–822, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-4.806-822. [in Turkish] [Open access]","Damir M. Iskhakov and Zaituna A. Tychinskikh, “The Problem of the Eastern Borders of the Bulgar Vilayet of the Golden Horde and the Kazan Khanate at the End of the 14th–15th Centuries,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 4 (2025): 859–879, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-4.859-879. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander L. Novoselov and Alexander A. Litvin, “Chronology of the Moscow-Kazan Wars of 1467–1530 and the Church Calendar,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 4 (2025): 939–958, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-4.939-958. [in Russian] [Open access]","Maksim V. Moiseev, “On the Organization of the Diplomatic Service in the Post-Horde Chingisid States in the 16th Century (Crimean and Kazan Khanates): “Tets” and “Bakshis”,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 681–692, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-3.681-692. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do historians argue over who founded the khanate?","a":"Most writers credit Ulugh Muhammad, the khan driven from the Golden Horde in 1437 who later settled on the middle Volga. One older historian argued that his father had already ruled there, which would make him a restorer rather than a founder."},{"q":"Why did the Crimean khans care who ruled Kazan?","a":"Kazan mattered to the Crimea in its struggle to inherit the Golden Horde's legacy and to gain influence over Moscow. Mehmed Giray placed his brother Sahib Giray on its throne, but quarrels at home and pressure from Moscow made Kazan hard to hold."},{"q":"Why did Moscow fight Kazan so often?","a":"The rivers flowing from Moscow into the Volga tied the two states together, and Moscow wanted control of Kazan's throne. Between 1467 and 1530 the grand princes fought a long series of wars to put and keep their own candidates there."}],"abstract":"The <b>Khanate of Kazan</b> was a Tatar state on the middle Volga, one of the khanates that grew out of the breakup of the Golden Horde, which was ruled from the city of Kazan until Muscovy conquered it in 1552.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It held the lands of the old Volga Bulgars, where Chuvash, Mari, Udmurts and Mordvins lived beside Tatars who had come there from the Golden Horde after the Mongol conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the Crimean Khanate it was a prize in the contest to inherit the Golden Horde and to gain influence over Moscow, and Mehmed Giray put his brother Sahib Giray on its throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its eastern borders in the Urals shifted over time, with the Nogais and the Shibanid princes of the Volga and Ural country as its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herberstein, an envoy of the German court who reached Moscow in 1526, wrote that its king could raise 30,000 men, chiefly foot, that the Mari and Chuvash were its best archers, and that these Tatars farmed, lived in houses and traded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most writers have made Ulugh Muhammad its founder, a khan driven from the Golden Horde in 1437 who stayed for a time at Belev on the Oka before moving east, though one older historian held that his family had ruled on the Volga before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1445 his sons defeated Vasily II of Moscow near Suzdal and took him prisoner, and the passing of power from Ulugh Muhammad to his son Mahmud has been judged legitimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its warrior elite was large, and a 16th-century letter from Kazan to the Crimean khan counted 20,000 'father's sons' besides countless Mordvins and Mari.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kazan and Moscow fought a long series of wars between 1467 and 1530, and Vasily III brought its khans so far under his sway that Herberstein thought them entirely subject to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like the Crimean Khanate it ran an embassy service of ambassadors, deputies and secretaries, until Ivan IV's army stormed Kazan on 2 October 1552.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","questions":["An envoy of the German court who reached Moscow in 1526 wrote that the king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could raise 30,000 men, chiefly foot, among whom the Mari and Chuvash were the best archers.","Ivan III and Vasily III fought a long series of wars with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1467 and 1530, and Ivan IV's army stormed its capital on 2 October 1552."],"wiki":"Khanate of Kazan"},
    "ru-126": {"sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 273–78, 281–88, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolI. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg (New York: Macmillan, 1925), 89–92, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 187, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussi01ramb. [Open access]","O. V. Bolshakova, review of <i>Politika moskovskikh knyazey, konets XIII – pervaya polovina XIV v.</i> [The policy of the Moscow princes, late 13th to first half of the 14th century], by N. S. Borisov, <i>Sotsial'nye i gumanitarnye nauki. Otechestvennaya i zarubezhnaya literatura. Seriya 5: Istoriya</i>, no. 4 (2000), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2000-04-007-borisov-n-s-politika-moskovskih-knyazey-konets-xiii-pervaya-polovina-xiv-v-mgu-im-m-v-lomonosova-ist-fak-m-izd-vo-mosk-un-ta-1999-391-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “Moscow, Lithuania, the Horde and the Last Testaments of Vasily I,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 4 (2023): 881–901, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-4.881-901. [in Russian] [Open access]","Irina G. Ponomareva, “About the Yarliq of Ulugh Muhammad Khan Approving the Rule of Grand Prince Vasily II,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 583–592, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-3.583-592. [in Russian] [Open access]","Teymur R. Galimov and Il’nur M. Mirgaleev, “The Interpretation of the “Great Stand on the Ugra River” in 1480,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 4 (2019): 652–662, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-4.652-662. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a small appanage become the leading power of north-eastern Rus'?","a":"Its princes were rich and opportunist. They bought land, seized Kolomna and Mozhaisk, inherited Pereyaslavl and courted the khans, who gave them the grand princely title and the right to gather the Horde's tribute."},{"q":"Why do historians still argue over Moscow's rise?","a":"The classic account stressed geography: three roads, a busy river and a sheltered place among the settlers. Later historians gave less weight to that and more to the princes' policy and their contest with Tver for the Horde's favour."},{"q":"Why did the khan's yarlik still matter to Vasily I?","a":"A Moscow prince still needed the Horde's sanction to pass the throne to his son. Vasily I seems to have obtained a yarlik from Ulugh Muhammad for the boy, and his last will was drawn up in the light of it."}],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Principality of Moscow</b>, often called Muscovy, was the state that grew from a small appanage of the late 13th century into the ruler of north-eastern Rus' and the core of the Russian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first lasting prince was Daniel, the youngest son of Alexander Nevsky, and at the start of the 14th century it was among the smallest appanages in the north, with only Moscow and Zvenigorod as towns, hemmed in by Tver, Suzdal and Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its princes took Kolomna from Ryazan and Mozhaisk from Smolensk, which gave them the whole course of the Moskva, and inherited Pereyaslavl-Zalessky from a childless kinsman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1328 its princes held the title of grand prince of Vladimir, collected the Horde's tribute from the other princes and used that office to bind them to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its territory grew through purchase, seizure and the favour of the khans, and by the middle of the 15th century it gave Moscow an immense preponderance over the rest of Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The classic explanation pointed to Moscow's place where three great roads met and to its sheltered position among the Russian settlers, which drew colonists and boyars from every side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moskva also linked the upper Volga with the middle Oka, and the princes grew rich on the traffic, including Novgorod's trade in grain, wax and honey from the Ryazan country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later historians questioned the weight given to geography and stressed the policy of the first princes, who competed with the princes of Tver for the Horde's support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The khans' patents still mattered in the 15th century, and the last wills of Vasily I were shaped by yarliks of Ulugh Muhammad, one probably confirming his son's succession and another restoring Nizhny Novgorod to a rival prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The standoff on the Ugra in 1480 is often taken as the end of the yoke, but tribute went on being paid to the Tatar khanates afterwards and the post-Horde world remained a threat to the Moscow state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","questions":["Where three great roads met and the Moskva linked the Oka with the upper Volga, the princes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew rich on settlers, boyars and the river trade.","From 1328 the rulers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the title of grand prince of Vladimir and gathered the Horde's tribute from the other princes of Rus'."],"wiki":"Grand Principality of Moscow"},
    "ru-127": {"sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 272–74, 276–78, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolI. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg (New York: Macmillan, 1925), 89, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 187, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussi01ramb. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 82, 111, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 1–4, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","F. A. Seleznev, “Planirovka i nazvaniya gorodov Yuriya Dolgorukogo” [The planning and names of Yuri Dolgoruky's towns], <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/planirovka-i-nazvaniya-gorodov-yuriya-dolgorukogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai Artemov, “Necropolises of the Moscow Kremlin: History and Stages of Field Archaeological Research,” <i>Istoricheskii zhurnal: nauchnye issledovaniia</i>, no. 1 (2023): 1–17, https://doi.org/10.7256/2454-0609.2023.1.37350. [in Russian] [Open access]","Olga A. Tufanova, “The Tale of Khan Tokhtamysh’ Invasion of Moscow: Paradoxes of Regret,” <i>Studia Litterarum</i> 6, no. 3 (2021): 134–147, https://doi.org/10.22455/2500-4247-2021-6-3-134-147. [in Russian] [Open access]","Denis A. Lyapin, “Image of Tamerlane in the Tale of Temir Aksak,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 1 (2022): 89–101, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-1.89-101. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Moscow at first so unimportant?","a":"In 1147 it was only a prince's estate on the edge of the Suzdal land, a halting place on the road south to Kyiv. Even after Yuri walled it in 1156 it stayed a minor town, and for most of the 13th century it had no prince of its own."},{"q":"Why did the city, the province and the river share one name?","a":"Herberstein noted the single name in 1526 and thought it probably came from the river. Modern study of Yuri Dolgoruky's towns agrees that Moscow belongs to the group of names taken from geography, in its case the river Moskva."},{"q":"Why does the founding story include a murdered boyar?","a":"A later tradition said the land belonged to a boyar named Stepan Kuchka, whom Yuri put to death, and the chronicles do call the place Kuchkovo. The tale explains the older name, but it is a tradition, not a contemporary record."}],"abstract":"<b>Moscow</b> is the capital of Russia, a city on the Moskva river that grew from a minor frontier settlement of the Suzdal land into the seat of the grand princes who united north-eastern Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herberstein, an envoy of the German court who reached it in 1526, noted that the city, its province and its river shared one name, which he thought was probably taken from the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He described a large city built of wood, which looked larger than it was because every house had its garden and courtyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tatars took it in the winter of 1237 to 1238, capturing Prince Vladimir, a younger son of the grand prince, there, and in 1293 a Tatar army laid waste Vladimir, Moscow, Dmitrov and other towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1382 Tokhtamysh's army plundered and burned it, and in 1395 Moscow believed itself saved from Timur by divine intercession, a story its chroniclers retold in the 15th and 16th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> The chronicle first names the place in 1147, when Yuri Dolgoruky invited his neighbour Sviatoslav of Novgorod-Seversky there and gave a mighty dinner in his honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that date it was probably the prince's country estate on the border between the Suzdal land and Chernigov, a halting place on the road between the north-east and Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1156, according to the chronicle, Yuri laid out the town, which meant ringing it with timber walls, and the new wall closed the landward side of a promontory fortress between rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A later tradition said that the land had belonged to a boyar named Stepan Kuchka, whom Yuri put to death, and the chronicles also call the place Kuchkovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One excavator identified beneath the Dormition Cathedral the white-stone foundations of a church he dated to the end of the 13th century, older than the cathedral of 1326.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","questions":["An envoy of the German court who arrived in 1526 noted that the city, the province and the river of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> all shared one name, which was probably taken from the river.","A later tradition held that the land on which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built had belonged to the boyar Stepan Kuchka, whom the founding prince put to death."],"wiki":"Moscow"},
    "ru-128": {"sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 109, 111, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 273–74, 279–81, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolI. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 187–88, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussi01ramb. [Open access]","A. A. Medvedev, “Izmenenie sistemy otnosheniy knyazey Ryurikova roda v period ot ustanovleniya ordynskoy vlasti nad russkimi zemlyami do nachala XIV v.” [Changes in the relations of the Rurikid princes from the establishment of Horde rule over the Russian lands to the early 14th century], <i>Lokus: lyudi, obshchestvo, kul'tury, smysly</i> 16, no. 2 (2025): 13–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izmenenie-sistemy-otnosheniy-knyazey-ryurikova-roda-v-period-ot-ustanovleniya-ordynskoy-vlasti-nad-russkimi-zemlyami-do-nachala-xiv. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Dyudeneva rat' i politicheskoe razvitie Tverskoy zemli” [Dyuden's host and the political development of the Tver land], <i>Izvestiya Penzenskogo gosudarstvennogo pedagogicheskogo universiteta imeni V. G. Belinskogo</i>, no. 27 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dyudeneva-rat-i-politicheskoe-razvitie-tverskoy-zemli. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg (New York: Macmillan, 1925), 90, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the youngest son of Alexander Nevsky get only Moscow?","a":"Moscow was a minor frontier town, and princes had held it only briefly in the 13th century, always as younger sons. As the youngest son, Daniel received this junior appanage, and it became the seat of a permanent principality only in his line."},{"q":"Why did Daniel join coalitions against his own brothers?","a":"The grand princes Dmitry and Andrei each tried to enlarge their own holdings, which alarmed the junior princes. Daniel sided first against Dmitry and then led a coalition with Mikhail of Tver against Andrei."},{"q":"Why did Pereyaslavl pass to Moscow and not to a senior prince?","a":"Its prince Ivan Dmitrievich died childless and left it to his uncle Daniel as his own patrimony. The bequest followed the new idea of inheritance within one family rather than the old rule of seniority."}],"abstract":"<b>Daniel of Moscow</b>, Daniil Aleksandrovich, was the youngest son of Alexander Nevsky and the first prince to make Moscow the seat of a lasting line, the house from which the later grand princes of Moscow descended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kluchevsky dated his appointment to 1263, the year his father died, when he was still a child, and from then on Moscow was the capital of a permanent principality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His appanage was small, a strip along the middle Moskva and the upper Klyazma with only two towns, Moscow and Zvenigorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was drawn into the wars between his elder brothers Dmitry and Andrei for the grand principality, and in 1283 he marched with the men of Moscow beside Novgorod and Tver against Dmitry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1293 Andrei brought a Tatar army under Duden against Dmitry, and it laid waste Vladimir, Moscow, Dmitrov and other towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Once Andrei was grand prince, Daniel and Mikhail of Tver led a coalition against him, with Daniel as its senior partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With the protection of the khan Nogai, Daniel and Mikhail helped his nephew Ivan Dmitrievich keep Pereyaslavl, and Daniel also contested the Novgorod throne, where his son Ivan sat for a time at the Novgorodians' invitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Ivan Dmitrievich died childless he left Pereyaslavl-Zalessky to Daniel rather than to his senior kinsmen, disposing of it as his own patrimony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Daniel also attacked Konstantin of Ryazan, took him prisoner by a stratagem and seized Kolomna, the town at the mouth of the Moskva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He died in 1303 and was the first prince buried in the Church of St Michael the Archangel, which remained the burial church of Moscow's princes until Peter the Great, and his death weakened his sons' claim to the grand principality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","questions":["In 1283 the Novgorod chronicle has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marching with the men of Moscow beside Novgorod and Tver against his brother Dmitry, then the grand prince.","Ivan Dmitrievich of Pereyaslavl died childless and left his principality to his uncle <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, passing over his senior kinsmen in favour of the prince of a junior appanage."],"wiki":"Daniel of Moscow"},
    "ru-129": {"sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 115–16, 119–24, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 188–91, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussi01ramb. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 279, 285, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolI. [Open access]","Yuri V. Seleznev, “A Muscovite Prince in Khan Uzbek’s Campaign: Reconstructing Yuri Danilovich’s Sojourn in the Horde,” <i>History, Archeology and Ethnography of the Caucasus</i> 21, no. 4 (2025): 416–422, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-knyaz-v-pohode-hana-uzbeka-shtrihi-k-hronologii-prebyvaniya-v-orde-knyazya-yuriya-danilovicha-moskovskogo-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Oborona novgorodskikh rubezhey v kontekste moskovsko-tverskikh otnosheniy rubezha XIII–XIV vv.” [The defence of the Novgorod frontiers in the context of Moscow–Tver relations at the turn of the 13th and 14th centuries], <i>Izvestiya Samarskogo nauchnogo tsentra Rossiyskoy akademii nauk</i> 17, no. 3-2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oborona-novgorodskih-rubezhey-v-kontekste-moskovsko-tverskih-otnosheniy-rubezha-xiii-xiv-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai Artemov, “Crypts in the History of the Assumption Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin: Discovery, Origin, Dating,” <i>Istoricheskii zhurnal: nauchnye issledovaniia</i>, no. 2 (2023): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.7256/2454-0609.2023.2.39858. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yury's claim to the grand principality offend the chroniclers?","a":"By the old rule of seniority his uncle Mikhail of Tver had the better right, and the khan Tokhta had confirmed it. Yury pressed his claim anyway, and the chroniclers judged him for overturning custom."},{"q":"Why did marrying the khan's sister matter so much?","a":"It tied Yury to Uzbek's family and brought him the khan's grant of the title and a Tatar army to enforce it. When Konchaka died in Tver captivity, the charge of poison helped bring Mikhail to his death at the Horde."},{"q":"Why was Yury in Novgorod in his last years?","a":"After the khan gave the title to Dmitry of Tver in 1322, Novgorod remained Yury's base. He led its men against Swedish Viborg and founded the fort on Orekhov island, where the Swedes made peace in 1323."}],"abstract":"<b>Yury of Moscow</b>, Yury Danilovich, was the eldest son of Daniel of Moscow and the first prince of Moscow to hold the title of grand prince of Vladimir, which the khan Uzbek gave him in place of Mikhail of Tver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began his rule by seizing Mozhaisk from the prince of Smolensk and murdering Konstantin of Ryazan, his father's prisoner, which made the Moskva Muscovite to its mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Grand Prince Andrei died in 1304, Yury and his uncle Mikhail of Tver both journeyed to the Horde to claim the title, and the khan Tokhta gave it to Mikhail, the senior claimant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two princes fought for years over the title and over Novgorod, where most citizens preferred Yury to Mikhail and arrested Tver's lieutenants in his favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chroniclers condemn him for breaking the old rule of seniority, and Tver remembered him as the man who brought about the death of a prince it venerated as a saint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Uzbek's court he married the khan's sister Konchaka, baptised Agafya, and he came back with a Tatar army under Kavgadyi to make good the khan's grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mikhail defeated him forty versts from Tver and captured his wife and his brother Boris, and Konchaka died in Tver captivity, which Yury's party blamed on poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mikhail was put to death at the khan's camp, and the princes' retinues stayed at Uzbek's headquarters that winter, perhaps because they fought in his campaign beyond the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yury spent his last years as Novgorod's prince, besieged the Swedish town of Viborg in vain in 1322, and in 1323 he and the Novgorodians founded a town on Orekhov island at the mouth of the Neva, where Swedish envoys made an everlasting peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1325 Dmitry of Tver, Mikhail's son, killed him at the Horde without the khan's word and was executed for it, and Yury was later the only prince whose grave lay in the Kremlin's stone Cathedral of the Dormition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","questions":["Novgorod's men went with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1323 to found a town on Orekhov island at the mouth of the Neva, where envoys of the Swedish king made an everlasting peace.","Dmitry of Tver, whose father Mikhail had died at the Horde, struck down <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there in 1325 without the khan's word, and paid for it with his own life."],"wiki":"Yury of Moscow"},
    "ru-130": {"sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 280–88, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolI. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 191–93, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussi01ramb. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg (New York: Macmillan, 1925), 90–92, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 124–25, 134, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","O. V. Babenko, review of <i>Zagadka zaveshchaniya Ivana Kality</i> [The riddle of Ivan Kalita's testament], by K. A. Aver'yanov, <i>Sotsial'nye i gumanitarnye nauki. Otechestvennaya i zarubezhnaya literatura. Seriya 5: Istoriya</i>, no. S1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2018-05-008-averyanov-k-a-zagadka-zaveschaniya-ivana-kality-prisoedinenie-galicha-uglicha-i-beloozera-k-moskovskomu-knyazhestvu-v-xiv-v-m. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai Artemov, “Crypts in the History of the Assumption Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin: Discovery, Origin, Dating,” <i>Istoricheskii zhurnal: nauchnye issledovaniia</i>, no. 2 (2023): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.7256/2454-0609.2023.2.39858. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Ivan called 'the purse'?","a":"He was known for his wealth and for using it, buying lands and villages in other principalities and arriving at the Horde with gold and silver. As collector of the Horde's tribute he also handled the money of the other princes."},{"q":"Why did Tver's rising of 1327 help Moscow?","a":"Tver's people killed the khan's envoy and many Tatars, and Ivan went straight to the Horde. A Tatar army laid Tver waste, its prince fled, and the khan gave Ivan the grand principality as his reward."},{"q":"Why do historians argue about his purchases of Galich, Uglich and Beloozero?","a":"Later sources say he bought these towns, yet neither of his surviving wills mentions them. Historians have suggested that he bought only part of their rights, left the local princes in place, or that the purchases came after his wills were made."}],"abstract":"<b>Ivan Kalita</b>, Ivan I Danilovich, was prince of Moscow from 1325 and grand prince of Vladimir from 1328 until his death in 1340, and his nickname means 'the purse'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Tver rose against the khan's envoy Shevkal in 1327 and killed many Tatars, Ivan sent his lieutenants to Novgorod and went himself to the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That winter a great Tatar force, joined by the men of Moscow and Suzdal, took Tver, Kashin and the Torzhok district and, as the Novgorod chronicler put it, laid waste all the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The khan Uzbek then made Ivan grand prince, and no prince went more often to the Horde or took more care never to arrive empty-handed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He later had Alexander of Tver summoned to the Horde again, where he was beheaded with his son Fyodor, and Tver sent the great bell of its cathedral to Moscow as a token of submission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan was the first prince of Moscow to whom the khans entrusted the collection of the Horde's tribute from the other princes, an office that let a grand prince punish a junior with the rouble instead of the sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A chronicler writing a generation later said that after his return from the Horde in 1328 there was great quiet for forty years and the Tatar raids on the Russian land ceased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He bought lands and villages in other principalities, and later sources credit him with buying Galich, Uglich and Beloozero, though his own surviving wills say nothing of them, a puzzle historians still argue over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With Metropolitan Peter he founded the first stone Cathedral of the Dormition in the Kremlin in 1326, Peter was laid in it when he died that winter, and his successor Theognostus settled the metropolitan see in Moscow for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His first will, made around 1327, lists only Moscow, Kolomna, Mozhaisk, Zvenigorod, Serpukhov, Ruza and Radonezh with some fifty rural communes, which shows how small Moscow still was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","questions":["With Metropolitan Peter the Moscow prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the first stone Cathedral of the Dormition in the Kremlin in 1326, and Peter was buried in it that winter.","Later sources credit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with buying Galich, Uglich and Beloozero, though his own surviving wills say nothing of them, a puzzle historians still argue over."],"wiki":"Ivan I of Moscow"},
    "ru-131": {"questions":["In 1299 Metropolitan Maxim left Kyiv for the north-east, yet the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on bearing the name of the city he had abandoned.","Separate metropolitans for Galicia and Lithuania were created early in the 14th century and soon wound up again, leaving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one church province once more."],"abstract":"The <b>Metropolis of Kiev and all Rus'</b> was the single church province of the lands of Rus', headed by a metropolitan appointed in Constantinople whose title kept the name of Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under 1299 the chronicle reports that Metropolitan Maxim, not bearing Tatar violence, abandoned the see and fled from Kyiv, and went on to the Suzdalian land with all his household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recent work reads the move, completed in 1300, as a planned decision rather than a flight, driven by fear of a war for Kyiv between the Horde, Lithuania and Galicia and by poor relations with the southern bishops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The province did not stay whole, for a separate Galician metropolis was formed around 1302 or 1303 and a Lithuanian one around 1317, and both were soon wound up again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vladimir on the Klyazma was left without a bishop of its own, since its see had been merged in the office of the metropolitan from the time of Maxim's arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Maxim's successor Peter often halted in Moscow on his journeys, became a friend of Ivan Kalita, and with him founded the church of the Dormition there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He died in Moscow in the winter of 1326 and was laid in the church of the Holy Mother of God which he himself had begun to build in stone, and miracles were soon reported at his tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His successor Feognost, a Greek, kept to the same line of support for the Moscow princes, and the later view was that he settled beside the tomb, making Moscow the church capital long before it was the political one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move was in fact slower, since for four years after 1328 Feognost governed largely from Vladimir in Volhynia, where he consecrated the new archbishop of Novgorod in 1331.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An imperial envoy of the 16th century was told that the two metropolitans buried in the Kremlin's cathedral were made saints chiefly for bringing the seat of prince and metropolis there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Pavel I. Gaydenko, “Tatar Violence over Metropolitan Maxim and the Sack of Kiev in 1299: Causes, Circumstances, and Consequences of Events That ‘Did Not Happen’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 76–89, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.76-89. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 289–90, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 124, 126, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Vladislav Igorevich Petrushko, “Vladimir na Volyni kak tserkovnyy tsentr Russkoy mitropolii v nachalnyy period sluzheniya mitropolita Feognosta” [Vladimir in Volyn as the church centre of the Russian metropolis in the initial period of Metropolitan Theognost’s ministry], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 1 (21) (2023): 53–60, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2023_1_53. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 736–755, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","Roman Sokolov, “Politicheskaya deyatelnost sv. mitropolita Aleksiya: suzdalskiy vektor” [Political activity of the holy Metropolitan Alexey: the Suzdal vector], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 2 (18) (2022): 148–156, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2022_2_148. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. and ed. R. H. Major (London: Hakluyt Society, 1852), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the metropolitan leave Kyiv at all?","a":"The chronicle says Maxim could not bear Tatar violence and fled with his whole household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A closer reading suggests a planned move, made from fear that Kyiv would be fought over by the Horde, Lithuania and Galicia, and because the metropolitans' relations with the southern bishops were difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow rather than Vladimir end up with the see?","a":"Peter made long stays in Moscow, befriended Ivan Kalita and began a stone church there, and he died and was buried in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His successors treated the tomb as a sign, and the princes of Moscow gained the respect that came with having the head of the church beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the title still name Kyiv?","a":"The province was the old church of all Rus', and its head was still named metropolitan of Kiev and all Rus' while living in the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Keeping one title served the effort to stop the province splitting, which is why Feognost spent years in the western lands after 1328.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Metropolis of Kiev and all Rus'"},
    "ru-132": {"questions":["Buried in 1326 in the stone church he had begun in Moscow, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was soon credited with miracles at the tomb and later counted among the city's saints.","Preferred by the patriarch over the candidate of Mikhail of Tver, the abbot who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went to the khan Uzbek in 1313 for a patent protecting the church."],"abstract":"<b>Metropolitan Peter</b> was the head of the church of Rus' from the first decade of the 14th century until his death in Moscow in 1326, and he was later venerated as the first of Moscow's sainted metropolitans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He had been abbot of the Ratsky monastery, and the patriarch Athanasius preferred him to Geronty, the man whom the prince of Tver, Mikhail Yaroslavich, had put forward for the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once in Rus' he visited Kyiv in 1308 and Vladimir in 1309, where he consecrated an archbishop of Novgorod, and he went on to Bryansk to try to end a feud there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bishop of Tver, Andrei, accused him of taking fees for ordinations, a charge of simony heard at a council at Pereyaslavl while the prince of Tver was away at the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The council was less a trial than an inquiry for the patriarch, and its main result was a close alliance between Peter and the Moscow princes descended from Daniel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 1313 he went to the Horde when the Muslim Uzbek became khan, and came back with a patent confirming the church's freedom from a number of taxes, though the text that survives is a 16th-century forgery built on a real grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He often stayed in Moscow, became a friend of Ivan Kalita, and with him founded the church of the Dormition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle records that he died in Moscow in the winter of 1326 and was laid in the church he had begun to build in stone, and that miracles followed at his tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The early account of his life is short and plain, while a later one by the metropolitan Cyprian adds a deathbed prophecy that Moscow would be glorified above all Russian towns while his bones lay there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1389 he was already shown beside the metropolitan Alexius on an embroidered church cloth, and paired icons of the two were later painted for the Kremlin's Dormition cathedral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. Yu. Tarabrin, “Mitropolit Pyotr i Pereyaslavskiy sobor” [Metropolitan Peter and the Council of Pereyaslavl-Zalessky], <i>Izvestiya Saratovskogo universiteta. Novaya seriya. Seriya Istoriya. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniya</i> 17, no. 3 (2017): 291–295, https://doi.org/10.18500/1819-4907-2017-17-3-291-295. [in Russian] [Open access]","P. I. Gaidenko, “Sud nad mitropolitom Petrom v glazakh sovremennikov i knizhnikov XVI v.” [The trial of Metropolitan Peter in the eyes of contemporaries and scribes of the 16th century], <i>Yuridicheskiy vestnik Samarskogo universiteta</i> 6, no. 3 (2020): 19–27, https://doi.org/10.18287/2542-047X-2020-6-3-19-27. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 736–755, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 124, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 289–90, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","A. A. Medvedev, “Izobrazhenie posmertnykh chudes v svyatitelskikh zhitiyakh (na materialakh zhitiy mitropolitov Petra, Aleksiya, Iony)” [Posthumous miracles in the lives of the metropolitans Peter, Alexius and Jonah], <i>Vestnik slavyanskikh kultur</i>, no. 2 (32) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izobrazhenie-posmertnyh-chudes-v-svyatitelskih-zhitiyah-na-materialah-zhitiy-mitropolitov-petra-aleksiya-iony. [in Russian] [Open access]","G. A. Nazarova, “Zhitiynye tsikly mitropolita Kievskogo i vseya Rusi Aleksiya: problemy izucheniya” [The hagiographic cycles of Alexius, Metropolitan of Kiev and all Rus’: problems of study], <i>Prepodavatel XXI vek</i>, no. 4, pt. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zhitiynye-tsikly-mitropolita-kievskogo-i-vseya-rusi-aleksiya-problemy-izucheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the prince of Tver oppose him?","a":"Mikhail of Tver had wanted his own man, Geronty, at the head of the church, and the patriarch chose Peter instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charges of simony followed from Tver, and the council that heard them pushed Peter towards Mikhail's rivals in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a khan's patent worth a journey to the Horde?","a":"A patent from the khan confirmed the church's exemption from a number of taxes and its legal independence, in return for prayers for the khan and his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The surviving text is a later forgery, but it was built on a grant that really existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the accounts of his death differ?","a":"The first account of his life is brief and plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The later version by Cyprian adds Peter's charge to Ivan Kalita to finish the church and a prophecy of Moscow's glory, an episode taken from popular legend shaped by the events of the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-133": {"questions":["Prince Mikhail of Tver came to Moscow in 1368 trusting the safe conduct of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the talks ended with his arrest and later his excommunication.","The Chudov monastery in the Kremlin, with its stone church of the Archangel Michael, was founded by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and received the founder's body in 1378."],"abstract":"<b>Metropolitan Alexius</b> was the head of the church of Rus' from the 1350s until his death in 1378, and he used the resources of the church more actively than anyone before him to strengthen the principality of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came from a family of Moscow boyars and continued the policy of his predecessors Peter and Feognost, acting in the interests of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1353 Feognost made him bishop of Vladimir, a see without a bishop of its own since 1300, and with the grand prince Simeon asked the patriarch to name no other successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle records that he came to Rus' in 1355 having been appointed in Constantinople, and that in 1360 he consecrated an archbishop at Vladimir before the grand prince Dmitry Konstantinovich of Suzdal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stayed out of the contest between Moscow and Suzdal in 1362 to 1365, and the trust he won from the Suzdal prince helped to build the alliance that served Dmitry Ivanovich so well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His most disputed act came in 1368, when he and Dmitry Ivanovich invited Mikhail Alexandrovich of Tver to Moscow in apparent friendship and the talks ended with Mikhail's arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tver side saw it as a treacherous breach of guarantees of safety given above all by the metropolitan, and historians have judged his conduct very differently ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once released, Mikhail went to Olgerd of Lithuania, and Alexius excommunicated him and asked the patriarch Philotheus to confirm it, which he at first did before letters from Mikhail and Olgerd made him reconsider.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He founded the Chudov monastery in the Kremlin with a stone church of the Miracle of the Archangel Michael, built around 1365, and was buried there after he died in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Veneration began early, for he appears with Peter on an embroidered cloth of 1389, and a life by Pachomius the Serb followed about 1450, once the metropolitan Jonah had set a feast for him across the whole church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 146, 148, 156, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Roman Sokolov, “Politicheskaya deyatelnost sv. mitropolita Aleksiya: suzdalskiy vektor” [Political activity of the holy Metropolitan Alexey: the Suzdal vector], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 2 (18) (2022): 148–156, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2022_2_148. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. Yu. Tarabrin, “Tserkovnaya politika Mikhaila Aleksandrovicha Tverskogo” [Church policy of Prince Mikhail Alexandrovich of Tver], <i>Vestnik Samarskogo universiteta. Istoriya, pedagogika, filologiya</i> 24, no. 4 (2018): 13–19, https://doi.org/10.18287/2542-0445-2018-24-4-13-19. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Yaganov, “Novyy vzglyad na khronologiyu kamennogo stroitelstva v Chudove monastyre XIV–XV vekov” [A new look at the chronology of stone building in the Chudov monastery, 14th–15th centuries], <i>Vestnik sektora drevnerusskogo iskusstva</i>, no. 1 (2021): 58–73, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novyy-vzglyad-na-hronologiyu-kamennogo-stroitelstva-v-chudove-monastyre-xiv-xv-vekov. [in Russian] [Open access]","G. A. Nazarova, “Zhitiynye tsikly mitropolita Kievskogo i vseya Rusi Aleksiya: problemy izucheniya” [The hagiographic cycles of Alexius, Metropolitan of Kiev and all Rus’: problems of study], <i>Prepodavatel XXI vek</i>, no. 4, pt. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zhitiynye-tsikly-mitropolita-kievskogo-i-vseya-rusi-aleksiya-problemy-izucheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Medvedev, “Izobrazhenie posmertnykh chudes v svyatitelskikh zhitiyakh (na materialakh zhitiy mitropolitov Petra, Aleksiya, Iony)” [Posthumous miracles in the lives of the metropolitans Peter, Alexius and Jonah], <i>Vestnik slavyanskikh kultur</i>, no. 2 (32) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izobrazhenie-posmertnyh-chudes-v-svyatitelskih-zhitiyah-na-materialah-zhitiy-mitropolitov-petra-aleksiya-iony. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Feognost make him a bishop before dying?","a":"By putting Alexius in charge of Vladimir, an old see that had had no bishop of its own for half a century, Feognost marked him out as heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He and the grand prince Simeon then asked the patriarch to appoint no one else, so the succession was arranged before the old metropolitan died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the arrest of Mikhail of Tver so damaging?","a":"The guarantees of safety had been given above all by the metropolitan, so the arrest looked like a betrayal sanctioned by the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mikhail turned to Lithuania, and letters from him and from Olgerd persuaded the patriarch to reconsider the excommunication Alexius had asked him to confirm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he honoured as a saint so soon?","a":"He was shown beside Peter, Moscow's first sainted metropolitan, on an embroidered cloth made only eleven years after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A full life followed about 1450, written by Pachomius the Serb once a feast for him had been set across the whole church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexius, Metropolitan of Kiev"},
    "ru-134": {"questions":["Khan Uzbek's sister Konchaka, wife of Yury of Moscow, died as a captive of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after a battle near Tver in December 1317, a death later laid at his door.","When the patriarch passed over Geronty, the candidate of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and made Peter metropolitan instead, a charge of simony against Peter followed from Tver."],"abstract":"<b>Mikhail of Tver</b> was Mikhail Yaroslavich, prince of Tver and grand prince of Vladimir, whose long contest with Yury of Moscow for the grand princely title ended with his execution at the Horde on 22 November 1318.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A son of Yaroslav Yaroslavich, he ruled Tver when it became the largest political centre of north-eastern Rus', and after the grand prince Andrei died in 1304 a large group of grand princely boyars moved to his court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The princes of Tver had seniority and courage on their side, while those of Moscow could set against them money and the wit to make the most of their chances with the khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He wanted his own man at the head of the church, and when the patriarch chose Peter instead of his candidate Geronty, he backed charges of simony against the new metropolitan, which pushed Peter towards Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was hard on Novgorod too, taking hostages and 50,000 silver grivnas after a battle at Torzhok, and leading an army against the city in 1316 that came home starving through the lakes and swamps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Early in 1317 the khan Uzbek took the title from him and gave it to Yury, who came back from the Horde with Tatar troops under Kavgadyj and invaded the principality of Tver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 22 December Mikhail routed them, and Yury's Tatar wife Konchaka, the khan's sister, was taken prisoner and died in Tver in unclear circumstances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Summoned to the Horde for trial, he arrived in September 1318 and was charged with resisting the khan, with poisoning Konchaka and with withholding tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a trial of two months he was put to death in the presence of Kavgadyj and Yury, and his widow Anna governed in Tver for a long time afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An account written by an eyewitness, probably his confessor, presents him as a martyr who gave his life to spare his people a Tatar attack, and it became the basis of his canonisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 736–755, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 120–21, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Tverskaya zemlya pri knyaze Dmitrii Mikhayloviche: osobennosti politicheskogo razvitiya” [The land of Tver under Prince Dmitry Mikhailovich: features of its political development], <i>Trudy Istoricheskogo fakulteta Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tverskaya-zemlya-pri-knyaze-dmitrii-mihayloviche-osobennosti-politicheskogo-razvitiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. Yu. Tarabrin, “Mitropolit Pyotr i Pereyaslavskiy sobor” [Metropolitan Peter and the Council of Pereyaslavl-Zalessky], <i>Izvestiya Saratovskogo universiteta. Novaya seriya. Seriya Istoriya. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniya</i> 17, no. 3 (2017): 291–295, https://doi.org/10.18500/1819-4907-2017-17-3-291-295. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Tsentry Rossiyskoy gosudarstvennosti XIII–XV vv. v sovremennoy otechestvennoy istoriografii” [Centres of Russian statehood in the 13th–15th centuries in modern Russian historiography], <i>Trudy Istoricheskogo fakulteta Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta</i>, no. 15 (2013): 206–221, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsentry-rossiyskoy-gosudarstvennosti-xiii-xv-vv-v-sovremennoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 285, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","A. L. Korzinin, “Fenomen doveriya v dukhovnykh gramotakh moskovskikh velikikh knyazey v XIV veke” [The phenomenon of trust in the testaments of the Moscow grand princes in the 14th century], <i>Upravlencheskoe konsultirovanie</i>, no. 3 (2022): 154–171, https://doi.org/10.22394/1726-1139-2022-3-154-171. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Uzbek take the title away from him?","a":"The khan abandoned the Horde's traditional support for Tver in favour of the prince of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yury came back from the Horde with Tatar troops to enforce the change, and Mikhail had to fight for his own principality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Konchaka's death so dangerous for him?","a":"She was the khan's sister and Yury's wife, and she died in Tver after being taken prisoner in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the trial he was accused of poisoning her, and several Moscow chronicles later repeated the charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he go to the Horde knowing the danger?","a":"A Tatar envoy made it clear that the safety of his principality depended on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account of his death presents the journey as a self-sacrifice made to save his people from a Tatar attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mikhail of Tver","places":["city:1159149539"]},
    "ru-135": {"questions":["Ivan Kalita of Moscow rode with the Tatar army that crushed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the prince of Tver fled to Pskov rather than answer for it at the khan's court.","Later chronicles retold the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by giving the Horde envoy Cholkhan a plan to take the throne of Tver and force its Christians into Islam."],"abstract":"The <b>Tver uprising</b> was the rising of the townspeople of Tver on 15 August 1327 against a Horde envoy and his retinue, in which every Tatar in the town was killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The envoy, Cholkhan, called Shevkal or Shchelkan in the chronicles of Rus', arrived soon after Alexander Mikhailovich of Tver had returned from the Horde with the khan's patent as grand prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was probably sent to collect an extraordinary levy, and the harassment of the townspeople by his men set off a spontaneous revolt which the prince was compelled to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicle, the nearest source in time, says only that on the feast of the Assumption the prince killed a great many Tatars in Tver and in other towns, and some merchants as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That winter a very great Tatar force took Tver and Kashin and, in the chronicler's words, laid waste all the Russian land, while Alexander fled to Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The punitive army of five tumens was joined by the forces of Ivan Kalita of Moscow, and the main towns of the principality were looted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return for that service Ivan received the grand princely throne, which from then on never left the hands of the princes of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1329 Ivan led the princes against Pskov to make Alexander answer at the khan's court, and the new metropolitan Feognost came north while the campaign was under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later versions of the story changed it, giving the envoy plans to seize the throne of Tver and to force its Christians into Islam, details which most likely did not correspond to what happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nikon chronicle of the 16th century makes the envoy a nephew of the khan Uzbek and puts the blame on Alexander rather than the town assembly, and some recent writers doubt that the rising was any step towards liberation, since the Horde was then at the height of its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 124–26, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Roman Hautala, “Russian Chronicles on the Religious Policy of Uzbek Khan (1313–1341) and His Relations with the Principalities of the North-Eastern Rus’,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 4 (2017): 736–755, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-4.736-755. [Open access]","Timur G. Imamov, “Politika zolotoordynskogo khana Uzbeka po otnosheniyu k russkim zemlyam v angloyazychnoy istoriografii” [The policy of the Golden Horde khan Özbek towards the Russian lands in English-language historiography], <i>Izvestiya Saratovskogo universiteta. Novaya seriya. Seriya Istoriya. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniya</i> 25, no. 4 (2025): 477–481, https://doi.org/10.18500/1819-4907-2025-25-4-477-481. [in Russian] [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “Perepisyvaya istoriyu: Nikonovskaya letopis o vzaimootnosheniyakh Rusi s Ordoy” [Rewriting history: the Nikon chronicle on the relations of Rus’ with the Horde], <i>Rossica Antiqua</i>, no. 2 (2010): 148–170, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/perepisyvaya-istoriyu-nikonovskaya-letopis-o-vzaimootnosheniyah-rusi-s-ordoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Tverskaya zemlya pri knyaze Dmitrii Mikhayloviche: osobennosti politicheskogo razvitiya” [The land of Tver under Prince Dmitry Mikhailovich: features of its political development], <i>Trudy Istoricheskogo fakulteta Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tverskaya-zemlya-pri-knyaze-dmitrii-mihayloviche-osobennosti-politicheskogo-razvitiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 285–86, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","Vladislav Igorevich Petrushko, “Vladimir na Volyni kak tserkovnyy tsentr Russkoy mitropolii v nachalnyy period sluzheniya mitropolita Feognosta” [Vladimir in Volyn as the church centre of the Russian metropolis in the initial period of Metropolitan Theognost’s ministry], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 1 (21) (2023): 53–60, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2023_1_53. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a tax visit turn into a massacre?","a":"The envoy was probably collecting an extraordinary levy, a normal order of the khan, but his men harassed the townspeople so badly that a spontaneous revolt broke out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tver had seen its prince Mikhail executed at the Horde nine years before, which made the town quick to anger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the prince of Moscow help the Tatars?","a":"Uzbek ordered the punishment, and the Moscow princes never thought of resisting the Horde, preferring to win it with obedience and money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ivan was rewarded with the grand princely throne, which stayed with Moscow from then on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the chronicles tell the story so differently?","a":"The Novgorod chronicle is brief and neutral, while later versions blamed the prince or invented plans to convert Tver to Islam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those later details reflected the fears of the 15th and 16th centuries more than the events of 1327.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"places":["city:1159149539"]},
    "ru-136": {"questions":["Princes of Ryazan, Rostov and even Tver did everything according to the word of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a chronicle says, and a later age gave the eldest son of Ivan Kalita his nickname.","The will of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written in the plague spring of 1353, left his lands to his wife, a princess of Tver, and charged his brothers not to let their candle go out."],"abstract":"<b>Simeon the Proud</b> was Simeon Ivanovich, the eldest son of Ivan Kalita, who held the title of grand prince with Moscow from his father's death until his own in 1353.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the brothers went to the Horde after their father died, the khan Uzbek received them with honour and assured them that the grand princely throne would stay with Moscow, and Simeon was named to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A chronicle says that all the princes of Ryazan and Rostov, and even those of Tver, were so obedient to him that they did everything according to his word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He bound his younger brothers Ivan and Andrei to him by a written treaty, which historians date to about 1350 or 1351.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was married to Maria Alexandrovna, a princess of the house of Tver, and they had four sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He died in April 1353 in the epidemic now called the Black Death, a few weeks after the metropolitan Feognost and two of his young sons, and his brother Andrei followed in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His will left his lands to his wife rather than to his sons, perhaps because none was then alive, and it charged his brothers not to let the memory of their forebears cease nor their own candle wane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Muscovy did not collapse after his death, and the grand princely title passed, after a challenge from Konstantin of Suzdal, to his brother Ivan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The nickname is later than the man, for he is first called 'the Proud' in the Book of Degrees in the middle of the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A later chronicle compilation explained it by saying that he loved neither falsehood nor sedition and punished both, drank without drunkenness, and did not love war yet always kept his army ready.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 288, 315–16, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 145, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","A. L. Korzinin, “Fenomen doveriya v dukhovnykh gramotakh moskovskikh velikikh knyazey v XIV veke” [The phenomenon of trust in the testaments of the Moscow grand princes in the 14th century], <i>Upravlencheskoe konsultirovanie</i>, no. 3 (2022): 154–171, https://doi.org/10.22394/1726-1139-2022-3-154-171. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Sirenov, “O prozvishchakh drevnerusskikh knyazey” [On the nicknames of the princes of early Rus’], <i>Peterburgskiy istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (14) (2017): 184–193, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-prozvischah-drevnerusskih-knyazey. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. V. Penskoj, V. V. Lipich, N. N. Reutov and A. V. Artyuh, “‘Byst mor zol…’: ‘Chernaya smert’ XIV veka na Rusi” [“The pestilence was evil…”: the Black Death of the 14th century in Rus’], <i>Problemy sotsialnoy gigieny, zdravookhraneniya i istorii meditsiny</i> 29, no. 4 (2021): 1017–1024, https://doi.org/10.32687/0869-866X-2021-29-4-1017-1024. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Sokolov, “Politicheskaya deyatelnost sv. mitropolita Aleksiya: suzdalskiy vektor” [Political activity of the holy Metropolitan Alexey: the Suzdal vector], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 2 (18) (2022): 148–156, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2022_2_148. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 325–343, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the khan give the title to Simeon?","a":"The chronicle says Uzbek received Ivan Kalita's sons with honour out of respect for their father and promised that the throne would stay with Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As grand prince Simeon also had the other princes placed under his hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his will leave nothing to sons?","a":"He left his lands to his wife, and one explanation is that none of his sons was alive when the will was drawn up in April 1353.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two young sons had died of the plague the month before, and nothing is recorded about the deaths of the two elder ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"When did he become 'the Proud'?","a":"Not in his lifetime: the nickname first appears in the Book of Degrees in the middle of the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of the famous nicknames of the early princes were given by later writers rather than by people who knew them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Simeon of Moscow"},
    "ru-137": {"questions":["A man would spit blood and be dead in three days, wrote a Novgorod chronicler of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which killed the grand prince Simeon in Moscow the next spring.","Common graves, the deaths of a metropolitan and a grand prince, and returning waves until 1391 marked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had already swept the Golden Horde in the 1340s."],"abstract":"The <b>Black Death in Russia</b> was the arrival of the great plague pandemic of the 14th century in the lands of Rus', first recorded at Pskov in 1352 and followed by further waves for the rest of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pandemic had struck the cities of the Golden Horde in the 1340s and reached the Crimea in 1346, and the chronicles of Rus' report outbreaks in many of its towns with heavy loss of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet it did not reach Rus' from the steppe: it came from the west, most likely from the trading towns of Livonia, after crossing Europe and Scandinavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Disease was no stranger before it, for the chronicles record earlier epidemics in Ryazan in 1257, in Pskov in 1265 and in many lands of Rus' around 1280.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later waves came from the steppe in 1364 to 1366, beginning at Nizhny Novgorod, and in 1387 to 1391 to Smolensk, Pskov and Novgorod, where the chronicles note swellings in the glands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Pskov in 1352 the priests could not bury the dead one by one, so each family carried its own to the churchyard, and three or five bodies were laid in a single coffin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The archbishop of Novgorod, Vasily, went to Pskov at its people's request to bless them, and died of the sickness on the road home in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Novgorod the dying lasted until Easter, and the chronicler noted that a man would spit blood and be dead after three days, and believed that the death had passed over the face of all the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Moscow in the spring of 1353 the metropolitan Feognost died, then two young sons of the grand prince Simeon, then Simeon himself, of what was probably the pneumonic form of plague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Muscovy did not collapse, and one view holds that the depopulation which followed led to power being gathered into fewer hands, ending in the gathering of the Russian lands under Ivan III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 145, 163, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. V. Penskoj, V. V. Lipich, N. N. Reutov and A. V. Artyuh, “‘Byst mor zol…’: ‘Chernaya smert’ XIV veka na Rusi” [“The pestilence was evil…”: the Black Death of the 14th century in Rus’], <i>Problemy sotsialnoy gigieny, zdravookhraneniya i istorii meditsiny</i> 29, no. 4 (2021): 1017–1024, https://doi.org/10.32687/0869-866X-2021-29-4-1017-1024. [in Russian] [Open access]","Uli Schamiloglu, “The Impact of the Black Death on the Golden Horde: Politics, Economy, Society, Civilization,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 5, no. 2 (2017): 325–343, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2017-5-2.325-343. [Open access]","A. N. Sorokin, “Skudelnitsy drevnego Novgoroda (k voprosu ob osobennostyakh drevnerusskogo pogrebalnogo obryada v chrezvychaynykh situatsiyakh)” [The common graves of medieval Novgorod: burial rites in emergencies in early Rus’], <i>Istoricheskie issledovaniya. Zhurnal Istoricheskogo fakulteta MGU</i>, no. 3 (2015): 236–262, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/skudelnitsy-drevnego-novgoroda-k-voprosu-ob-osobennostyah-drevnerusskogo-pogrebalnogo-obryada-v-chrezvychaynyh-situatsiyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. L. Korzinin, “Fenomen doveriya v dukhovnykh gramotakh moskovskikh velikikh knyazey v XIV veke” [The phenomenon of trust in the testaments of the Moscow grand princes in the 14th century], <i>Upravlencheskoe konsultirovanie</i>, no. 3 (2022): 154–171, https://doi.org/10.22394/1726-1139-2022-3-154-171. [in Russian] [Open access]","Timur F. Khaydarov and Dmitry A. Dolbin, “Theoretical Aspects of Understanding the Second Pandemic of the ‘Black Death’ Plague in the Territory of the Jochid Ulus,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 264–282, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2018-6-2.264-282. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the plague reach Pskov and Novgorod first?","a":"It came from the west rather than from the Horde: after crossing Europe and Scandinavia it reached Livonia, whose trading towns had close ties with Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From there it moved on to Novgorod and then across the rest of Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the usual burial customs abandoned?","a":"So many died that priests could no longer take each body from its house, and families had to bring their own dead to the churchyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bodies were laid several to a coffin or a grave, and new graves had to be dug away from the churches because the old ground was full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the plague weaken Moscow?","a":"It killed the grand prince, two of his sons, his brother and the metropolitan within a few months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> But the principality held together, and the loss of people may even have pushed power into fewer hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Black Death in Russia","places":["city:1159149529"]},
    "ru-138": {"questions":["The surname of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taken from the river near which he beat Mamai's army in 1380, first appears in a chronicle compiled nearly a century and a half later.","After an army led by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> besieged Tver in 1375, its prince Mikhail Alexandrovich renounced for ever any claim to the grand principality of Vladimir."],"abstract":"<b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> was Dmitry Ivanovich, grand prince of Moscow and Vladimir, whose reign brought Moscow its first victories in the field over the Horde, above all on Kulikovo field in 1380.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Circumstances put him on a war-horse at the age of eleven, and his thirty years of rule were filled with a fourfold struggle against Tver, Lithuania, Ryazan and the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metropolitan Alexius stood behind him, and an alliance with Dmitry Konstantinovich of Suzdal, who accepted defeat in his own claim to the grand principality, strengthened him against Tver and the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1367 the timber walls of the Kremlin were replaced in stone, and Olgerd of Lithuania besieged Moscow without success in 1368 and again in 1370.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1375 he led an army of the Russian princes against Tver, and its prince Mikhail Alexandrovich made peace and renounced the grand principality of Vladimir for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Horde, now led by the commander Mamai, was the next enemy, and in 1378 a Tatar army sent against him was beaten on the river Vozha and fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two years later the whole of northern Rus' stood with Moscow on Kulikovo field and won its first victory over the Tatars, which made the prince of Moscow the leader of the north against its enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He died in Moscow on 9 May 1389, only 39 years old, and a contemporary biographer wrote that although he was not learned in books he carried the sacred books in his heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His surname came much later than he did, for the name Donskoy, from the river Don, first appears in the Nikon chronicle compiled in the 1520s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His dealings with rivals were not always clean, and in 1368 he and the metropolitan invited Mikhail of Tver to Moscow in apparent friendship and then held him prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 156, 163, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 288, 315–16, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","Roman Sokolov, “Politicheskaya deyatelnost sv. mitropolita Aleksiya: suzdalskiy vektor” [Political activity of the holy Metropolitan Alexey: the Suzdal vector], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 2 (18) (2022): 148–156, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2022_2_148. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Tsentry Rossiyskoy gosudarstvennosti XIII–XV vv. v sovremennoy otechestvennoy istoriografii” [Centres of Russian statehood in the 13th–15th centuries in modern Russian historiography], <i>Trudy Istoricheskogo fakulteta Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta</i>, no. 15 (2013): 206–221, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsentry-rossiyskoy-gosudarstvennosti-xiii-xv-vv-v-sovremennoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. Yu. Tarabrin, “Tserkovnaya politika Mikhaila Aleksandrovicha Tverskogo” [Church policy of Prince Mikhail Alexandrovich of Tver], <i>Vestnik Samarskogo universiteta. Istoriya, pedagogika, filologiya</i> 24, no. 4 (2018): 13–19, https://doi.org/10.18287/2542-0445-2018-24-4-13-19. [in Russian] [Open access]","O. A. Cheremnykh, “Istorik G. V. Vernadskiy o Dmitrii Donskom (1359–1379 gg.)” [The historian G. V. Vernadsky on Dmitry Donskoy, 1359–1379], <i>Vestnik Gosudarstvennogo universiteta prosveshcheniya. Seriya: Istoriya i politicheskie nauki</i>, no. 2 (2025): 144–151, https://doi.org/10.18384/2949-5164-2025-2-144-151. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Sirenov, “O prozvishchakh drevnerusskikh knyazey” [On the nicknames of the princes of early Rus’], <i>Peterburgskiy istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (14) (2017): 184–193, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-prozvischah-drevnerusskih-knyazey. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many princes follow him against Tver in 1375?","a":"Mikhail of Tver had turned for help to Lithuania and to Mamai, which destroyed the popularity Tver's princes had enjoyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nineteen princes joined Moscow's campaign, some as dependants but others, it seems, out of real conviction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the new stone walls matter?","a":"Expecting an attack from Lithuania, Moscow replaced the Kremlin's timber walls with stone in 1367.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Olgerd came in 1368 and again in 1370 he could not take the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is he called Donskoy?","a":"The name refers to his great victory on the Don in 1380.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not used in his lifetime: it first appears in the Nikon chronicle of the 1520s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dmitry Donskoy"},
    "ru-139": {"questions":["Oak under Ivan Kalita, white stone under Dmitry Ivanovich and brick under Italian builders from 1485, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was rebuilt three times in a century and a half.","Moscow's old epithet 'white-stone' is traced to the limestone of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised under Dmitry Ivanovich, long before Italian architects rebuilt it in brick."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Kremlin Wall</b> is the defensive circuit round the citadel of Moscow, built in turn of timber, of white limestone and finally of brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first enclosure, a ring of timber walls raised under Yuri Dolgoruky, took in only a third or at most a half of the present area of the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1339 Ivan Kalita surrounded it with earth banks and walls of oak, at a time when Moscow had come to rival Novgorod in importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An imperial envoy of the early 16th century was told that the fortress had at first been surrounded only by oaks and had been small and mean in appearance until the time of Ivan Kalita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The walls standing today were begun in 1485 under Ivan III by Italian architects, among them Pietro Antonio Solari, and their swallow-tailed battlements are the best-known Italian feature of Russian military building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After a fire, in the reign of Dmitry Ivanovich, walls of white limestone were built, and the old epithet of 'white-stone Moscow' is traced to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decision to replace the timber walls with stone in 1367 was taken in the expectation of an attack from Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new walls proved their worth when Olgerd of Lithuania besieged Moscow without success in 1368 and again in 1370.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind them the Moscow princes still preferred to leave the city when a great enemy came near, taking their troops with them and leaving their families and the metropolitan to defend it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what happened in 1382, when the grand prince withdrew to Kostroma with his wife and children and the khan Tokhtamysh took Moscow and burned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["O. A. Cheremnykh, “Istorik G. V. Vernadskiy o Dmitrii Donskom (1359–1379 gg.)” [The historian G. V. Vernadsky on Dmitry Donskoy, 1359–1379], <i>Vestnik Gosudarstvennogo universiteta prosveshcheniya. Seriya: Istoriya i politicheskie nauki</i>, no. 2 (2025): 144–151, https://doi.org/10.18384/2949-5164-2025-2-144-151. [in Russian] [Open access]","Elena Belyaeva and Aleksandr Belyaev, “Ob istorii gorodskogo blagoustroystva i inzhenernykh setey Moskvy. Chast I. Istoriya blagoustroystva drevney Moskvy. XIV–XVII veka” [On the history of the urban improvement and engineering networks of Moscow, part 1: old Moscow, 14th–17th centuries], <i>Academia. Arkhitektura i stroitelstvo</i>, no. 3 (2021): 115–124, https://doi.org/10.22337/2077-9038-2021-3-115-124. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. and ed. R. H. Major (London: Hakluyt Society, 1852), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1911), 271, 315, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01kliu. [Open access]","K. S. Nosov, “Italyantsy i ‘italyanizmy’ v russkom voennom zodchestve” [Italians and ‘Italianisms’ in Russian military architecture: foreign masters in the fortifications of the grand principality of Moscow, late 15th to mid-16th century], <i>Voenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (2020): 84–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/italyantsy-i-italyanizmy-v-russkom-voennom-zodchestve-vklad-inostrannyh-masterov-v-stroitelstvo-krepostey-i-drugih-oboronitelnyh. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 159, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the wall rebuilt in stone in 1367?","a":"Moscow expected an attack from Lithuania and replaced its timber walls with stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new walls held when Olgerd besieged the city in 1368 and in 1370.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a stone wall not save Moscow in 1382?","a":"The princes' habit was to leave the city with their troops when a great enemy approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1382 the grand prince withdrew to Kostroma, and Tokhtamysh took Moscow and burned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the present walls look Italian?","a":"Ivan III brought in Italian architects, who began the new walls in 1485.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A visitor of the early 16th century noted that the ramparts and battlements were built of brick in the Italian style by Italians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Moscow Kremlin Wall"},
    "ru-140": {"questions":["Two years before Kulikovo, Mamai's commander Begich was routed by Dmitry Ivanovich at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a battle fought on Ryazan land beyond the Oka.","Prince Daniil of Pronsk, named in the chronicles only for his part in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, led one wing of the Muscovite army against the Tatars that day."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Vozha River</b> was fought on 11 August 1378 in the land of Ryazan, where the army of Dmitry Ivanovich of Moscow defeated a Tatar force sent by Mamai under the commander Begich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mamai, the commander who then held real power in the Horde, had set himself to fight the growing strength of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Russians crossed the Oka and took up a position on the Vozha, a right-bank tributary of the Oka, where they waited a few days for the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tatars attacked as soon as they reached the river, and were struck from one side by Prince Daniil of Pronsk, from the other by the okolnichy Timofei, and head-on by the grand prince with his own troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They broke and fled across the river, leaving their tents and wagons in the field, and the Russians pursued them until dark and again the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Novgorod chronicle gives the battle a single entry under 1379, saying only that the forces met on the Ovozha, God aided the grand prince, and the Tatars turned and fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Daniil of Pronsk, the only prince from outside Moscow known to have fought, is named in the chronicles only in connection with this battle, and the princes of Suzdal took no part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fullest account survives in the Nikon and Simeon chronicles, and details found only there, such as the capture of a priest who had come from the Horde, suggest a lost chronicle of Ryazan behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mamai answered that same autumn with a raid on the land of Ryazan, so destructive that its people are said to have settled afterwards as if in an uninhabited country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the first victory over an enemy long thought invincible, yet it had no strategic consequences, and both sides now prepared for the decisive battle fought two years later on Kulikovo field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 156, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","I. V. Denisova, “Ryazanskie bitvy v letopisnoy traditsii Drevney Rusi” [The battles of Ryazan in the chronicle tradition of early Rus’], <i>Uchenye zapiski Kazanskogo universiteta. Seriya Gumanitarnye nauki</i> 156, no. 2 (2014): 65–76, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ryazanskie-bitvy-v-letopisnoy-traditsii-drevney-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Yu. G. Alekseev, “Dve stoletnie voyny” [Two hundred years’ wars], <i>Trudy Istoricheskogo fakulteta Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta</i>, no. 19 (2014): 14–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dve-stoletnie-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Lavrentiev, “‘Zadonshchina’, Ryazan i moskovskaya velikoknyazheskaya semya” [The Zadonshchina, Ryazan and the grand princely family of Moscow], <i>Slověne</i> 4, no. 2 (2015): 180–213, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zadonschina-ryazan-i-moskovskaya-velikoknyazheskaya-semya. [in Russian] [Open access]","O. A. Cheremnykh, “Istorik G. V. Vernadskiy o Dmitrii Donskom (1359–1379 gg.)” [The historian G. V. Vernadsky on Dmitry Donskoy, 1359–1379], <i>Vestnik Gosudarstvennogo universiteta prosveshcheniya. Seriya: Istoriya i politicheskie nauki</i>, no. 2 (2025): 144–151, https://doi.org/10.18384/2949-5164-2025-2-144-151. [in Russian] [Open access]","Roman Sokolov, “Politicheskaya deyatelnost sv. mitropolita Aleksiya: suzdalskiy vektor” [Political activity of the holy Metropolitan Alexey: the Suzdal vector], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 2 (18) (2022): 148–156, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2022_2_148. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mamai send an army against Moscow in 1378?","a":"Mamai, who then held real power in the Horde, had made it his aim to fight Moscow's growing strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One view is that he chose to break Moscow first and only then turn on his rival Tokhtamysh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians win so clearly?","a":"Dmitry knew the Tatars were coming, took up his position in advance and let them attack first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then hit them from three sides and pursued them hard instead of stopping to celebrate on the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ryazan suffer after a Muscovite victory?","a":"The battle was fought on Ryazan land, and Mamai answered the defeat that same autumn with a raid on Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most chronicle accounts, written from Moscow's side, praise Dmitry and pass over the ruin of Ryazan with indifference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of the Vozha River"},
    "ru-141": {"questions":["The Novgorod chronicler admitted that many inexperienced Muscovites ran at the sight of the Tatars at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet the Horde was routed and many of its men drowned in the river.","The fullest account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the <i>Tale of the Battle with Mamai</i>, was written more than a century afterwards and modelled the grand prince on Alexander the Great."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kulikovo</b> was fought on 8 September 1380 at the mouth of the river Nepryadva, beyond the Don, between Grand Prince Dmitry Ivanovich of Moscow and the forces of the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicler explains that the Horde then had a weak man for its khan while a commander named Mamai controlled all its affairs and was savagely enraged against the grand prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dmitry crossed the Don with Prince Vladimir Andreyevich, and the chronicler admits that many of the Muscovites were inexperienced and that some fled at the sight of so many Tatars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a long and fierce fight the Horde turned, and its men were cut down or drowned in the river, though Prince Fyodor of Beloozero and his son Ivan were among the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even Novgorod, which had long resisted Moscow’s ascendancy, recorded the victory exultantly, in the same year in which Dmitry confirmed all the city’s old rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under Dmitry the union of princes around Moscow had already widened, with nineteen princes joining him against Tver in 1375, and on the field all northern Rus fought under the Muscovite standard for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An older reading held that forty years of quiet under Moscow had raised two generations free of their grandfathers’ terror of the Tatars, and that these were the men who went out to Kulikovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet tribute rather than troops went on being sent to the Horde afterwards, and the envoy Herberstein later heard only of a slaughter that covered the ground with dead bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of the familiar detail is late, for the fullest account, the <i>Tale of the Battle with Mamai</i>, was composed in the early 16th century, and its author modelled Dmitry on Alexander the Great of the Serbian <i>Alexandria</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Attempts to reckon the armies from what they could have eaten put them far below the chronicle figures, since 14th-century Moscow had no settled habit of taking large supply trains on campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 157–158, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxi, xxv, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 267, 287–288, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 18, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Михаил Александрович Несин, “К вопросу о логистике Куликова поля,” <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i> 28, no. 1 (2018): 70–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-logistike-kulikova-polya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Антон Анатольевич Горский, “«Сказание о Мамаевом побоище», «Повесть о разорении Рязани Батыем», Никоновская летопись: о начале русской исторической беллетристики,” <i>Исторический вестник</i> 45 (2023): 156–177, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/skazanie-o-mamaevom-poboische-povest-o-razorenii-ryazani-batyem-nikonovskaya-letopis-o-nachale-russkoy-istoricheskoy-belletristiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dmitrii M. Bulanin, “In Search of the Model: Alexander the Great on the Kulikovo Field,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2020): 87–106, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-1.87-106. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dmitry fight a commander rather than the khan himself?","a":"The Horde was divided at the time. The Novgorod chronicler says a weak man sat as khan while Mamai, a commander, ran its affairs, so the army that met Moscow on the Don was the one Mamai had raised."},{"q":"Why did the victory not end Moscow’s dependence on the Horde?","a":"Winning one battle did not remove the Horde’s power to raise new armies. Tribute went on being paid after 1380, and two years later the khan Tokhtamysh burnt Moscow itself."},{"q":"Why are many famous details of the battle doubted?","a":"The fullest account was written in the early 16th century and modelled Dmitry on Alexander the Great. Its figures for the armies are also far larger than 14th-century Moscow could have fed on a campaign without big supply trains."}],"wiki":"Battle of Kulikovo"},
    "ru-142": {"questions":["Two years after Kulikovo the grand prince withdrew to Kostroma rather than face the khan, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the city and six other towns burnt.","A later envoy recorded that after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1382 the dead were redeemed for burial at eighty bodies to the rouble, three thousand roubles in all."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Moscow</b> of 1382 ended in the taking and burning of the city by Tokhtamysh, khan of the Horde, two years after the Muscovite victory at Kulikovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Seeing the size of the army, Grand Prince Dmitry did not stand against it but withdrew to Kostroma with his wife and children, while the metropolitan went to Tver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The khan’s men also took and burnt Pereyaslavl, Kolomna, Serpukhov, Dmitrov, Vladimir and Yuriev, so that the blow fell on much of the north-east and not on the capital alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leaving Moscow was not unusual, for grand princes and later a tsar abandoned it before the Horde’s armies in 1382, 1521, 1571 and 1572, chiefly because they lacked the force to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They thought of the state as a family estate, and on that view saving the ruler mattered more than saving the capital and its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Most chronicles that tell the story date the arrival of the Tatars to Monday 23 August and the fall of the city to Thursday 26 August, two dates that agree with each other though not with the year the tale is filed under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the walls the defence was led by Prince Ostei, a grandson of the Lithuanian ruler Algirdas, who with the townspeople and refugees from the countryside beat off three days of assaults.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unable to break the defence, the Tatars persuaded the citizens with flattering promises to open the gates, and then stripped the churches and icons and destroyed the books brought in for safety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Writing in the following century, Herberstein recorded that the dead were redeemed for burial at eighty bodies to the rouble, and that the total came to three thousand roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within a year Dmitry’s son Vasily had gone to the Horde at the khan’s summons, a sign that the victory of 1380 had not ended Moscow’s subjection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 159–160, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 18, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Александр Васильевич Журавель, “Когда Тохтамыш захватил Москву?,” <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i> 31, no. 4 (2021): 700–709, https://doi.org/10.35634/2412-9534-2021-31-4-700-709. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Туфанова, “«Повесть о нашествии Тохтамыша на Москву»: парадоксы жалости,” <i>Studia Litterarum</i> 6, no. 3 (2021): 134–147, https://doi.org/10.22455/2500-4247-2021-6-3-134-147. [in Russian] [Open access]","Людмила Владимировна Мининкова, “«…Великий князь, видевъ нашествие, …самъ же отъиде…»: бегство великого князя и процесс становления Московского государства,” <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i> 34, no. 3 (2024): 693–698, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-knyaz-videv-nashestvie-sam-zhe-otide-begstvo-velikogo-knyazya-i-protsess-stanovleniya-moskovskogo-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","C. Raymond Beazley, “General Introduction,” in <i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), xxv, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the grand prince leave Moscow instead of defending it?","a":"He did not have the strength to meet so large an army. Princes of Moscow also treated the state as their family estate, so saving the ruler came before saving the city."},{"q":"Why did a defended city open its gates?","a":"The defenders had held off three days of assaults, but the Tatars then offered flattering promises. Once the gates were open the promises were broken and the city was plundered and burnt."},{"q":"Why are the dates of the fall of Moscow argued over?","a":"Most chronicles give Monday 23 August and Thursday 26 August, which fit each other. They do not fit the year in which the tale is placed, so the calendar has to be reconstructed."}],"wiki":"Siege of Moscow (1382)","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ru-143": {"questions":["In 1418 the monk Epiphanius began the life of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, complaining that 26 years had passed since the abbot’s death without anyone writing it.","Missionaries from the forest monastery founded by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the mid-14th century became the advance guard of the settlers crossing the Volga into Kostroma and Vologda."],"abstract":"<b>Sergius of Radonezh</b> was the monk who founded the Trinity monastery in the forest near Moscow in the 1340s and became perhaps the most famous of all Russian saints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He went into the wilderness before he had even taken the cowl, and the newcomers who joined him were warned to expect poverty, hunger and every kind of temptation rather than ease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> According to his life, envoys of the patriarch of Constantinople, Philotheos, later brought him a blessing and gifts and urged on him the rule of the common life for his brethren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Moscow, Serpukhov and Kolomna sent for him to found houses, and in 1365 Dmitry Donskoy used him as an envoy to reconcile the quarrelling princes of Nizhny Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His pupils carried his model outwards, and missionaries from the Trinity monastery became the advance guard of the settlers who crossed the Volga into the forests of Kostroma and Vologda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Before his death the metropolitan Alexius summoned him and pressed a gold cross on him, which Sergius tried to refuse because he had worn no gold since his youth, and then named him the only worthy successor, an office Sergius refused outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He died in 1392, and no life of him existed until the autumn of 1418, when the monk Epiphanius complained that 26 years had passed without one and began to write it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Epiphanius died within months with the work about half done, and the rest of the abbot’s life is known from Pachomius, a Serb who had come from Mount Athos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The story of his blessing on Dmitry before the campaign against Mamai comes from that life, while his gift of the monks Peresvet and Oslyabya as warriors is first told in a tale of the early 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His grave drew the rulers of Muscovy, and in the following century the envoy Herberstein found the grand prince himself going to the monastery where Sergius was buried to eat at its expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 152–154, 184–185, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 284, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 85, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Константин Александрович Аверьянов, “Хронология редакций жития Сергия Радонежского,” <i>Славянский альманах</i> 2010 (2011): 36–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-redaktsiy-zhitiya-sergiya-radonezhskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Александр Сергеевич Терентьев, “Глава «О хотящем святого повинути на митрополию»: особенности ранних редакций Жития прп. Сергия Радонежского,” <i>Богослов</i>, no. 2 (2024): 139–162, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/glava-o-hotyaschem-svyatogo-povinuti-na-mitropoliyu-osobennosti-rannih-redaktsiy-zhitiya-prp-sergiya-radonezhskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Антон Анатольевич Горский, “«Сказание о Мамаевом побоище», «Повесть о разорении Рязани Батыем», Никоновская летопись: о начале русской исторической беллетристики,” <i>Исторический вестник</i> 45 (2023): 156–177, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/skazanie-o-mamaevom-poboische-povest-o-razorenii-ryazani-batyem-nikonovskaya-letopis-o-nachale-russkoy-istoricheskoy-belletristiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Наталья Александровна Ложкина, “Троице-Сергиев монастырь как русский центр исихазма,” <i>Труды Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i> 185 (2009): 76–81, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/troitse-sergiev-monastyr-kak-russkiy-tsentr-isihazma. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sergius refuse to become metropolitan?","a":"His life presents it as humility. He tried to refuse the gold cross Alexius pressed on him, saying he had worn no gold since his youth, and then declined the office as one he was unworthy of."},{"q":"Why is so little known for certain about his early years?","a":"Nobody wrote his life until 26 years after his death. Epiphanius began it in 1418 but died with the work half done, and Pachomius finished it later."},{"q":"Why did his monastery matter beyond its own walls?","a":"Monks trained there left to found new houses in the northern forests. Peasant settlers followed them across the Volga, so the monastery became a starting point for the settlement of the north."}],"wiki":"Sergius of Radonezh"},
    "ru-144": {"questions":["In 1446 rebel princes seized Vasily II inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, behind the founder’s tomb, and blinded him before setting up a rival grand prince.","The army of the second False Dmitry besieged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from September 1608 to January 1610 without taking the walled monastery Sergius had founded."],"abstract":"The <b>Trinity Lavra of St Sergius</b> is the monastery that Sergius of Radonezh founded in the forest near Moscow in the 1340s, and it became the most venerated and the richest house in Muscovite Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began as a desert monastery, and during the 14th century it gave birth to thirteen new desert houses and in the 15th century to two more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In time it grew into the largest and most skilful acquirer of land among all the monasteries of old Russia, much of it given in return for prayers for the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stone cathedral of the Trinity was raised over the grave of Sergius in 1422 and 1423, soon after his relics had been taken up from the ground on 5 July 1422.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its walls and iconostasis were painted by 1425–1427, and the famous <i>Trinity</i> icon was made for that iconostasis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its prestige made it a stage for politics, and in 1446 princes in revolt seized Grand Prince Vasily II there, behind the tomb of Sergius, and blinded him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The envoy Herberstein found the grand prince himself going there to dine at the monastery’s expense, while ordinary people came only on certain annual feasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Giles Fletcher, the English envoy of 1588, described it as built like a castle, walled round with great guns on the walls, with some seven hundred monks inside besides their servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He put its income from lands and fees at 100,000 roubles a year, and wrote that the tsaritsa went there on foot almost every year to pray to Sergius for a child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those walls were tested in the Time of Troubles, when the monastery held out against the army of the second False Dmitry from 23 September 1608 to 12 January 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 153–154, 175, 179, 187, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 115–116, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 85, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 205, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Людмила Михайловна Воронцова, “Местный ряд иконостаса Троицкого собора Троице-Сергиева монастыря. Реконструкция по Описи 1641 года,” <i>Вестник сектора древнерусского искусства</i>, no. 1 (2023): 46–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mestnyy-ryad-ikonostasa-troitskogo-sobora-troitse-sergieva-monastyrya-rekonstruktsiya-po-opisi-1641-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Игорь Олегович Тюменцев, “Дело об измене казначея Иосифа (Девочкина) в истории обороны Троице-Сергиева монастыря в 1608–1610 годах,” <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 1 (2022): 290–296, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/delo-ob-izmene-kaznacheya-iosifa-devochkina-v-istorii-oborony-troitse-sergieva-monastyrya-v-1608-1610-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Константин Александрович Аверьянов, “Хронология редакций жития Сергия Радонежского,” <i>Славянский альманах</i> 2010 (2011): 36–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-redaktsiy-zhitiya-sergiya-radonezhskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the monastery become so rich?","a":"Donors gave it land and money in return for prayers for their dead. Over the centuries those gifts made it the largest landowner among the monasteries of old Russia, with a vast yearly income."},{"q":"Why was a monastery built like a fortress?","a":"By the 16th century it was walled round with guns on its walls. That defence proved its worth when it held out for more than a year against the second False Dmitry’s army."},{"q":"Why was the cathedral built where it stands?","a":"It was raised over the grave of Sergius soon after his relics were taken up in 1422. The church made the founder’s tomb the centre of the monastery and of the pilgrimages to it."}],"wiki":"Trinity Lavra of St. Sergius"},
    "ru-145": {"questions":["A pilgrimage book of 1855 gave the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the northern forests where monks such as Cyril and Ferapont of Beloozero had settled in the wilderness.","Peasants clearing the forest followed the monks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the paths of its hermitages trace the unrecorded routes of settlement towards the White Sea."],"abstract":"The <b>Northern Thebaid</b> is the name given to the forest country around Beloozero, Vologda and Kostroma, which hermits from central Russia filled with monasteries in the 14th and 15th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name, which likens the region to the Thebaid of the desert fathers of Egypt, was put into circulation in 1855 by the writer Andrei Muravyov in a book about pilgrimage to the holy places of Vologda and Beloozero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the 14th century nearly every Russian monastery stood inside a town or close to one, and of the hundred-odd known by 1300 not more than ten were desert houses in the wilds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 14th century saw desert monasteries founded in the same number as urban ones, 42 of each, and in the 15th century they outnumbered them by more than two to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three-quarters of these desert houses were founded by monks who had left an older one, so that each foundation became the parent of new ones once it had grown rich and busy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Trinity monastery of Sergius of Radonezh led the movement, and its colonies founded 27 desert and eight urban monasteries in the 14th and 15th centuries, St Cyril’s on the White Lake among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pupils and companions of Sergius spread across Galich, the Obnora river and the Vologda country, among them Cyril and Ferapont, who settled near Beloozero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The movement reached the White Sea at Solovki, where a monk from St Cyril’s who had come by way of Valaam met a current of monks moving north from Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peasants followed the monks and cleared land round each house, so that the routes of the hermitages trace otherwise unknown routes of settlement, and missionaries from the Trinity monastery led settlers beyond the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the houses stayed small and remote, yet by the late 16th century Giles Fletcher could complain that wherever any part of the realm was better and sweeter than the rest, a monastery stood on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 151–155, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 284, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Дмитрий Витольдович Кашин, “Северная Фиваида — сакральная территория Руси. К формированию монашеской традиции Костромской земли,” <i>Ипатьевский вестник</i>, no. 3 (2015): 10–20, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/severnaya-fivaida-sakralnaya-territoriya-rusi-k-formirovaniyu-monasheskoy-traditsii-kostromskoy-zemli. [in Russian] [Open access]","Надежда Евгеньевна Каменская, “Духовное возвышение Соловецкого монастыря в России в XV–XVII вв.,” <i>Научно-технический вестник информационных технологий, механики и оптики</i>, no. 17 (2004): 123–128, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/duhovnoe-vozvyshenie-solovetskogo-monastyrya-v-rossii-v-xv-xvii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Дмитрий Анатольевич Пономарев, “Малые монастыри русского Севера: к постановке проблемы,” <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 2 (2018): 80–91, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/malye-monastyri-russkogo-severa-k-postanovke-problemy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 114, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did monks leave monasteries that were already in the wilderness?","a":"A desert monastery that prospered drew peasants, donors and bustle around it. Monks who wanted the old solitude left with their abbot’s blessing to find untouched forest, and the process repeated itself."},{"q":"Why are the monasteries a guide to where peasants settled?","a":"Peasants followed the monks into the northern forests, clearing land and forming parishes round the new houses. The hermitages are recorded where the peasant movement mostly is not, so their routes stand in for it."},{"q":"Why does the name compare Russia’s north with Egypt?","a":"The first Christian hermits lived in the desert of the Thebaid in Egypt. The 19th-century name casts the northern forests, full of hermitages, as Russia’s own desert of the monks."}],"wiki":"Northern Thebaid"},
    "ru-146": {"questions":["The grand prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked his father-in-law Vytautas of Lithuania to seal his last will, guarding his young son’s succession against a brother with a claim by seniority.","In 1408 Edigu camped before the Moscow of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ravaged towns from Pereyaslavl to Nizhny Novgorod and took a ransom of three thousand roubles from the city."],"abstract":"<b>Vasily I</b> was grand prince of Moscow and Vladimir from the death of his father Dmitry Donskoy in 1389 until his own death in 1425, and he took the throne without any dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As a boy he had been sent to the Horde at the khan’s summons in the year after Tokhtamysh burnt Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1392 he came back from the Horde, took Nizhny Novgorod and carried its princes off as hostages, having induced the khan and his nobles with gifts to sell him the patent for Murom, Tarusa and the whole principality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next year he quarrelled with Novgorod over its refusal to be summoned before the metropolitan in Moscow and seized Torzhok, Volok Lamsky and Vologda before peace was made on the old terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The steppe was not done with Moscow, for in 1408 Edigu, the khan’s father-in-law, camped before the city, ravaged towns from Pereyaslavl to Nizhny Novgorod and took a ransom of three thousand roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He married Sophia, daughter of Grand Duke Vytautas of Lithuania, who as his widow took up the Kremlin cathedral of the Ascension convent that his mother had founded in 1407.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In his last years he asked his father-in-law to confirm his will, for his brother Yury could claim the throne by seniority and was an ally of Vytautas’s rival Švitrigaila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His last will was made after a khan granted Nizhny Novgorod back to its old dynasty, and it was signed first by the metropolitan Photius and sealed at the very end by Vytautas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether he also secured a khan’s patent for his son is still argued over, and the latest suggestion places it at the very end of 1424 or the start of 1425.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herberstein later heard that he disowned the boy, suspecting his wife of adultery, and left the throne to his brother, a story that the surviving wills contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 160, 163, 165–166, 179, 191, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 282–283, 310, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 19, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “Moscow, Lithuania, the Horde and the last testaments of Vasily I,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 4 (2023): 881–901, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-4.881-901. [Open access]","Irina G. Ponomareva, “About the Yarliq of Ulugh Muhammad Khan Approving the Rule of Grand Prince Vasily II,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 583–592, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-3.583-592. [Open access]","Александр Владимирович Лаврентьев, “«Кремлевские вдовы»: жены великих князей московских и архитектурно-художественные работы в Кремле XIV–XV веков,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2024): 105–128, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kremlevskie-vdovy-zheny-velikih-knyazey-moskovskih-i-arhitekturno-hudozhestvennye-raboty-v-kremle-xiv-xv-vekov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ирина Гавриловна Пономарева, “Великий князь Московский Василий Васильевич Темный — опыт реконструкции исторического портрета,” <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 1 (2025): 30–48, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2025_1_30. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vasily I need gifts to gain Nizhny Novgorod?","a":"The khan still granted the patents by which Russian princes held their lands. Vasily bought the patent for the principality from the khan and his nobles, then used it to take the city and its princes."},{"q":"Why did he ask Vytautas of Lithuania to confirm his will?","a":"His son was young and his brother Yury could claim the throne by seniority. Vytautas was the boy’s grandfather and a powerful neighbour, so his seal was meant to protect the succession."},{"q":"Why was his reign still shadowed by the Horde?","a":"Buying patents did not make Moscow safe. In 1408 Edigu camped before the city, ravaged the towns around it and took a large ransom."}],"wiki":"Vasily I of Moscow"},
    "ru-147": {"questions":["Konstantin Vasilyevich of Suzdal founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1341, and one of his sons later fought Dmitry of Moscow for the khan’s patent to Vladimir and lost.","In 1444–1445 the khan Ulugh Muhammad restored the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Shuisky princes, half a century after Moscow had first absorbed it."],"abstract":"The <b>Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-Suzdal</b> was the grand principality ruled from Nizhny Novgorod by a branch of the princes of Suzdal from 1341 until Moscow took it in 1392.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first grand prince, Konstantin Vasilyevich of Suzdal, made Nizhny Novgorod its centre in 1341, and after his death the city passed to his son Andrei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1359–1363 Andrei’s brother Dmitry Konstantinovich contended with Dmitry of Moscow for the khan’s patent to the grand principality of Vladimir and lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city kept the marks of a capital, with its own chronicle-writing, ties to the Horde and Byzantium and a growing apparatus of rule, and only it, Tver and Moscow contested the primacy of the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the brothers quarrelled in 1365, Dmitry of Moscow sent Sergius of Radonezh to Nizhny Novgorod to reconcile them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The end came under Dmitry’s son Vasily I, who induced the khan and his nobles with gifts, for much gold and silver, to sell him a patent to Murom, Tarusa and the whole principality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1392 Vasily returned from the Horde, took Nizhny Novgorod and carried off its princes and their wives as hostages, and Prince Simeon fled to the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dynasty did not give up, and in 1410 Daniil Borisovich, whose old patrimony it was, seized and burnt Vladimir while Tatars with him stripped its gold-domed church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Late in the reign of Vasily I a khan granted Daniil a patent restoring the principality free of Moscow, a blow that forced Vasily to remake his will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1444–1445 the khan Ulugh Muhammad, who may have spent eight months in Nizhny Novgorod, restored it once more under the Shuisky princes, and for more than twenty years afterwards the region almost vanishes from the written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Николай Валерьевич Штыков, “Центры российской государственности XIII–XV вв. в современной отечественной историографии,” <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 15 (2013): 206–221, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsentry-rossiyskoy-gosudarstvennosti-xiii-xv-vv-v-sovremennoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 165, 180, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 282–284, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 154, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “Moscow, Lithuania, the Horde and the last testaments of Vasily I,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 4 (2023): 881–901, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-4.881-901. [Open access]","Булат Раимович Рахимзянов, “К вопросу о «буферных зонах» во взаимоотношениях поздней Золотой Орды и северо-восточных русских княжеств,” <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 2 (2010): 91–95, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-bufernyh-zonah-vo-vzaimootnosheniyah-pozdney-zolotoy-ordy-i-severo-vostochnyh-russkih-knyazhestv. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Moscow take the principality without a war?","a":"The khan granted the patents by which princes held their lands. Vasily I bought the patent with gold and silver, and the khan’s grant let him seize the city and remove its princes."},{"q":"Why did the principality keep coming back?","a":"Its old dynasty never accepted the loss, and khans were willing to back it against Moscow. A khan’s patent late in Vasily I’s reign and Ulugh Muhammad’s restoration of 1444–1445 both revived it."},{"q":"Why did Nizhny Novgorod count as a possible capital of all Rus?","a":"It had its own grand prince, chronicle-writing, links with the Horde and Byzantium and an apparatus of rule. Its princes even contended with Moscow for the patent to Vladimir."}],"wiki":"Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-Suzdal","places":["city:1159150695"]},
    "ru-148": {"questions":["A council of bishops declared in 1447 that the throne had never been Yury’s by God or ancient custom, deciding in principle the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in favour of descent from father to son.","Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was over both sides had blinded a rival, Vasily Yurievich in 1436 and Grand Prince Vasily II himself in 1446."],"abstract":"The <b>Muscovite War of Succession</b> was the struggle for the throne of Moscow that broke out after the death of Vasily I in 1425 and ended only with the death of Dmitry Shemyaka in 1453.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vasily I’s brother Yury of Galich would not accept his ten-year-old nephew Vasily II as his senior, citing his own seniority and the will of their father Dmitry Donskoy, and took the case to the khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1432 the khan gave the title of grand prince to Vasily, but two years later Yury seized Moscow and took his seat as grand prince, dying in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His sons carried on the claim, and in 1436 Vasily II had one of them, Vasily Yurievich, blinded in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Older historians treated it as the last feud of the appanage age and the only one within the house of Moscow, while 20th-century writers recast its leaders as feudal lords defending or dismantling the old order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The worst years were 1445 and 1446, when the Tatars captured Vasily near Suzdal and released him for a ransom, and Shemyaka and his allies then seized him at the Trinity monastery and blinded him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the clergy, the princes and the boyars were for Vasily, and the princes of Galich were greeted in Moscow as aliens and usurpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1447 a council of bishops condemned Shemyaka for breaking his treaty and declared that the throne had not been his father’s to take, since neither God nor the ancient custom of the land had given it to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tver’s guns helped batter Shemyaka’s fortresses, and after his defeat beneath Galich on 27 January 1450 he fled to Novgorod, where he died, almost certainly poisoned, in 1453.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The war settled that the throne passed from father to son rather than by seniority among brothers, and Vasily ended it holding nearly every appanage of the Moscow inheritance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 193–195, 203–205, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 309–313, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 19–20, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","И. Б. Михайлова, “Смута на Руси во второй четверти XV в.,” <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1–2 (2004): 4–18, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smuta-na-rusi-vo-vtoroy-chetverti-xv-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov, “Moscow, Lithuania, the Horde and the last testaments of Vasily I,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 11, no. 4 (2023): 881–901, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2023-11-4.881-901. [Open access]","Владимир Алексеевич Волков, “Роковой яд «в куряти». Смерть Дмитрия Шемяки и окончание московско-галичских войн второй четверти XV в.,” <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 15, no. 2 (2024): 27–35, https://doi.org/10.31862/2500-2988-2024-15-2-27-35. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yury believe the throne was his?","a":"The old custom passed seniority among brothers before sons. Yury was the eldest surviving son of Dmitry Donskoy and cited his father’s will, while Vasily II was only ten."},{"q":"Why did Vasily II win despite his defeats and blinding?","a":"The clergy, the princes and the boyars stood with him and treated the Galich princes as usurpers. Tver’s alliance and its guns then helped break Shemyaka’s hold on his towns."},{"q":"Why does the war matter beyond the men who fought it?","a":"It fixed the rule that the throne went from father to son. The bishops’ judgement of 1447 called that rule the ancient custom of the land, and the victorious line kept it afterwards."}],"wiki":"Muscovite War of Succession"},
    "ru-149": {"questions":["Hearing the returned metropolitan Isidore name the pope in the liturgy, the grand prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rejected it as unheard of in the Russian land and had him confined to a monastery.","Though blinded in 1446, the grand prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won back his throne and by his will of 1462 left the grand princely power itself to his eldest son."],"abstract":"<b>Vasily II</b> was grand prince of Moscow from 1425 to 1462, a reign that officially lasted 37 years, though he was born only in 1415 and began to rule in his own right in 1432.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He succeeded at the age of ten, and a khan’s patent confirming his right may have been obtained by his father only months before he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1432 the khan gave him the title of grand prince over the whole Russian land, after his uncle Yury had argued his own claim in person at the Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the metropolitan Isidore returned from the council of the churches and named the pope in the liturgy, Vasily refused to hear it and ordered him to live in a monastery under guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1445 the Tatars of Ulugh Muhammad defeated and captured him near Suzdal, and he was let go for a ransom that the Novgorod chronicler put at two hundred thousand roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> He is remembered as Vasily the Dark, and his blindness dates from 1446, when Dmitry Shemyaka seized Moscow and his allies took Vasily prisoner at the Trinity monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Blind, he still won the war, for the clergy, the princes and the boyars stood with him, and Tver’s alliance was sealed by betrothing his young son Ivan to a Tver princess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Some historians doubt that he really governed after his blinding, or think power passed to Ivan in his last years, but the sources show no sign of his removal and depict a warlike, decisive ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end he held almost the whole Moscow inheritance with Suzdal, Novgorod and Vyatka submissive to him, and in his will of 1462 he left the grand princely power itself to his eldest son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herberstein still wrote in the following century that from Vladimir Monomakh up to this Vasily, Russia had had no monarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 193, 197–198, 203–205, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 309, 311, 313, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 19–20, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Ирина Гавриловна Пономарева, “Великий князь Московский Василий Васильевич Темный — опыт реконструкции исторического портрета,” <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 1 (2025): 30–48, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2025_1_30. [in Russian] [Open access]","Irina G. Ponomareva, “About the Yarliq of Ulugh Muhammad Khan Approving the Rule of Grand Prince Vasily II,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 583–592, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-3.583-592. [Open access]","Булат Раимович Рахимзянов, “К вопросу о «буферных зонах» во взаимоотношениях поздней Золотой Орды и северо-восточных русских княжеств,” <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 2 (2010): 91–95, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-bufernyh-zonah-vo-vzaimootnosheniyah-pozdney-zolotoy-ordy-i-severo-vostochnyh-russkih-knyazhestv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Владимир Алексеевич Волков, “Роковой яд «в куряти». Смерть Дмитрия Шемяки и окончание московско-галичских войн второй четверти XV в.,” <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 15, no. 2 (2024): 27–35, https://doi.org/10.31862/2500-2988-2024-15-2-27-35. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vasily II reject the metropolitan Isidore?","a":"Isidore came back from Rome naming the pope rather than the patriarch of Constantinople in the liturgy. Vasily called it something never done in the Russian land and had him confined to a monastery."},{"q":"Why could a blinded prince recover his throne?","a":"The clergy, the princes and the boyars supported him against his cousins. Tver changed sides to join him, and the alliance was sealed by betrothing his son to a Tver princess."},{"q":"Why is it argued whether he ruled after 1446?","a":"Some historians think a blind man could not have governed and that power passed to his son Ivan. The sources show no sign of his removal and describe an active, warlike ruler."}],"wiki":"Vasily II of Moscow"},
    "ru-150": {"questions":["Beaten beneath Galich in 1450, the prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fled to Novgorod and died there in 1453, by most accounts of chicken poisoned by a cook bribed from Moscow.","In 1447 a council of bishops warned the prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the throne his father had claimed was given neither by God nor by the ancient custom of the land."],"abstract":"<b>Dmitry Shemyaka</b> was the prince of Galich, a son of Yury Dmitrievich, who seized Moscow in 1446 and had his cousin Grand Prince Vasily II blinded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He inherited his father’s claim to the throne on Yury’s death in 1434, and his elder brother Vasily Yurievich had already been blinded on Vasily II’s orders in 1436.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1446 he conspired with Prince Ivan of Mozhaisk, Prince Boris of Tver and two of the khan’s envoys, and the conspirators levied a contribution and took the money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three princes seized the grand prince in the Trinity monastery, behind the tomb of Sergius, and blinded him, and Shemyaka became grand prince in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herberstein’s version, which garbles the names, has armed men hidden in wagons passed off as merchandise, and the blinded prince sent with his wife to Uglich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His hold did not last, for most of the nobles went over to the blind Vasily, and Tver, alarmed by Shemyaka’s public promise to defend Novgorod’s independence, changed sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shemyaka broke the treaty he then made, and in 1447 the bishops warned him that the throne had been given to his father neither by God nor by the ancient custom of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Defeated beneath Galich on 27 January 1450, he took refuge in Novgorod, still calling himself grand prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He died there suddenly in 1453, and most historians hold that a cook bribed by Moscow and nicknamed Poganka served him poisoned chicken, in an operation run by the grand prince’s clerk Stepan Borodaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His years of power were part of a wider turmoil in which, on one modern reading, boyars, provincial princes and the grand prince of Moscow held power in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 194–195, 204–205, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 19–20, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 1 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 310–311, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79986. [Open access]","Владимир Алексеевич Волков, “Роковой яд «в куряти». Смерть Дмитрия Шемяки и окончание московско-галичских войн второй четверти XV в.,” <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 15, no. 2 (2024): 27–35, https://doi.org/10.31862/2500-2988-2024-15-2-27-35. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Б. Михайлова, “Смута на Руси во второй четверти XV в.,” <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1–2 (2004): 4–18, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smuta-na-rusi-vo-vtoroy-chetverti-xv-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ирина Гавриловна Пономарева, “Великий князь Московский Василий Васильевич Темный — опыт реконструкции исторического портрета,” <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 1 (2025): 30–48, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2025_1_30. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Shemyaka think he had a right to the throne?","a":"His father Yury had claimed it by seniority as the eldest surviving son of Dmitry Donskoy. After Yury died in 1434 the claim passed to his sons, and Shemyaka kept pressing it."},{"q":"Why did his seizure of Moscow fail?","a":"Most of the nobles went over to the blind Vasily, and Tver abandoned Shemyaka after he promised to defend Novgorod’s independence. The bishops then condemned his claim as against God and custom."},{"q":"Why do historians think his death was murder?","a":"He died suddenly in Novgorod while still calling himself grand prince. The accounts point to a cook bribed by Moscow who served him poisoned chicken, in an operation run by one of Vasily II’s clerks."}],"wiki":"Dmitry Shemyaka"},
    "ru-151": {"questions":["A Suzdal traveller on the road to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> noted silkworms, a hospital said to hold a thousand beds and cedars that looked like the Russian pine.","Venetian galleys carried about 700 Byzantines, the emperor among them, to Italy for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the hope of winning western help against the Ottoman Turks."],"abstract":"The <b>Council of Florence</b> was the church council of 1438 and 1439, opened at Ferrara and moved to Florence, at which the Byzantine emperor and his bishops accepted a union with the Church of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emperor John VIII Palaiologos needed western help against the Ottoman Turks, and Venetian galleys carried a delegation of about 700 Byzantines, who left Constantinople on 27 November 1437 and reached Venice on 8 February 1438.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disputes turned on the procession of the Holy Spirit, the bread of the Eucharist, purgatory and the supremacy of the pope, and the Greeks signed the decree of union on 5 July 1439.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mark of Ephesus alone refused, and the decree was read out in Latin and Greek in Florence cathedral on the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italian historians of the next century barely mentioned the council, while in Rus' its story was copied and retold, and the fullest account of the Russian mission survives in four manuscripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Rus' was represented by its metropolitan, Isidore, a Greek sent from Constantinople, who left Moscow on 8 September 1437 with Bishop Abraham of Suzdal and a train of about a hundred people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In less than a year in Rus' he had won the favour of Grand Prince Vasily II and drawn Tver and Novgorod into the venture as well as Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tver sent an envoy of its own, a layman named Thomas, and the priest Simeon of Suzdal left Italy before the rest and made his way back to Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An anonymous member of the suite kept a businesslike record of the road, noting silkworms, a hospital said to hold a thousand beds and cedars that looked like the Russian pine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicler called the gathering the Eighth Council, and wrote that Isidore came home naming the pope in his services, something never heard since the baptism of the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Lilia Campana, “Sailing into Union: The Byzantine Naval Convoy for the Council of Ferrara–Florence (1438–1439),” <i>Dumbarton Oaks Papers</i> 73 (2019): 103–25, https://archive.org/details/DOP73_06_Campana. [Open access]","Ivan N. Ostroumov, <i>The History of the Council of Florence</i>, trans. Basil Popoff, ed. J. M. Neale (London: Joseph Masters, 1861), 47, 59–60, 154–55, https://archive.org/details/historycouncilf00nealgoog. [Open access]","S. N. Kisterev, “Хождение на Ферраро-Флорентийский собор” [The Journey to the Council of Ferrara–Florence], <i>Вестник «Альянс-Архео»</i>, no. 47 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hozhdenie-na-ferraro-florentiyskiy-sobor. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladislav A. Tulyanov, “Митрополит Исидор Киевский на Руси в 1437–1438 гг.” [Metropolitan Isidore of Kiev in Rus' in 1437–1438], <i>Теологический вестник Смоленской православной духовной семинарии</i>, no. 3 (2024): 62–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-isidor-kievskiy-na-rusi-v-1437-1438-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandra V. Nikitina, “Тверское княжество и Ферраро-Флорентийский собор (1439 г.)” [The principality of Tver and the Council of Ferrara–Florence], <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 8 (2017): 310–17, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tverskoe-knyazhestvo-i-ferraro-florentiyskiy-sobor-1439-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. B. Rogachevskaya, “Западный мир в «Хождении на Флорентийский собор»” [The western world in the Journey to the Council of Florence], <i>Герменевтика древнерусской литературы</i>, no. 10 (2000), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zapadnyy-mir-v-hozhdenii-na-florentiyskiy-sobor. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 197–98, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Byzantine emperor go to Italy at all?","a":"Because the Ottoman Turks had left his empire little more than its capital, and help could only come from the Latin West. Union with Rome was the price the pope asked, and the emperor brought his patriarch and bishops to pay it."},{"q":"Why did Moscow send its metropolitan to a council in a Latin land?","a":"Because Isidore insisted, citing an oath to the patriarch, and the grand prince let him go only after he swore to bring back nothing contrary to the Orthodox faith."},{"q":"Why does a Russian travel account of the council say so little about theology?","a":"Because its author wrote like a clerk rather than a polemicist. He recorded distances, dates, prices and the novelties of western towns, and listed the sessions without reporting what was argued in them."}],"wiki":"Council of Florence","places":["city:1159149751"]},
    "ru-152": {"questions":["Born at Monemvasia in the Peloponnese, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended his days as a cardinal in Rome, having escaped from Constantinople when the Turks took the city in 1453.","The Novgorod chronicler complained that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, home from the Eighth Council, called himself legate of the apostolic see and named the pope of Rome in his services."],"abstract":"<b>Isidore of Kiev</b> was the Greek churchman who became metropolitan of Kiev and all Rus' in 1436, signed the union of the churches at Florence and was deposed in Moscow for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born between 1380 and 1390 at Monemvasia in the Peloponnese, took monastic vows there and was sent to the Council of Basel as one of the emperor's envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His speech at Basel is the earliest evidence of his support for union, and he was consecrated to the see of Rus' in the autumn of 1436, while Jonah, the bishop of Ryazan who had gone to Constantinople to be made metropolitan, travelled back in his train.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Florence he was among the first to speak for union, saying that it was better to unite with the Catholics in spirit and body than to go home with nothing, and he signed the decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within weeks the pope proclaimed him a papal legate, and a cardinal's hat followed for his services to the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He entered Moscow in the spring of 1441 behind a Latin cross, named Pope Eugenius in the liturgy in the Dormition Cathedral and had the decree of union read out at the end of the service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vasily II denounced him in the church itself as a false pastor and a heretic and confined him in the Chudov monastery, while a council of six bishops examined the decree and rejected it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Novgorod chronicler, who files the scene a year later, has the grand prince say that this had never happened under his forefathers and that he did not wish to hear it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 15 September 1441 Isidore escaped to Tver, where Prince Boris also had him confined, and he then made his way through Lithuania to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was inside Constantinople when it fell in 1453 and got away, and he died in Rome on 27 April 1463, the last metropolitan of Rus' to be appointed by the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sergey Yu. Akishin, “Митрополит Исидор Киевский и проблема церковной унии в поздней Византии” [Metropolitan Isidore of Kiev and the problem of church union in late Byzantium], <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-isidor-kievskiy-i-problema-tserkovnoy-unii-v-pozdney-vizantii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ivan N. Ostroumov, <i>The History of the Council of Florence</i>, trans. Basil Popoff, ed. J. M. Neale (London: Joseph Masters, 1861), 59–60, 181–84, https://archive.org/details/historycouncilf00nealgoog. [Open access]","V. M. Shevyrin, review of <i>Средневековая Русь и Константинополь: дипломатические отношения в конце XIV – середине XV в.</i>, by E. I. Maleto, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2019-02-005-maletoe-i-srednevekovaya-rus-i-konstantinopol-diplomaticheskie-otnosheniya-v-kontse-xiv-seredine-xv-v-m-tsentropoligraf-2018-333. [in Russian] [Open access]","Paul Pierling, <i>La Russie et le Saint-Siège: études diplomatiques</i>, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (Paris: Plon-Nourrit, 1906), 56–59, 74–78, 102, https://archive.org/details/larussieetlesain01pier. [in French] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 197–98, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sergey Yu. Akishin, “Митрополит Исидор Киевский в отечественной и зарубежной историографии” [Metropolitan Isidore of Kiev in Russian and foreign historiography], <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i>, no. 1 (17) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-isidor-kievskiy-v-otechestvennoy-i-zarubezhnoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow reject a union its own metropolitan had signed?","a":"Because the grand prince held that Isidore had handed the Russian Church over to Rome and accepted the pope's primacy. Naming the pope in the liturgy and reading the decree in the cathedral made the change public, and a council of bishops then rejected the decree as contrary to the faith."},{"q":"Why was Isidore able to escape from his monastery prison?","a":"Because nobody tried very hard to hold him. His guards were lax and no one pursued him, and the clergy were uneasy about a prince sitting in judgement on the head of their church, so letting him go spared Moscow a harder decision."},{"q":"Why was Isidore in Constantinople when it fell?","a":"Because the pope sent him back as legate to put the union into effect in the threatened capital. He proclaimed it in Hagia Sophia in December 1452 and was given a stretch of the walls to defend during the siege."}],"wiki":"Isidore of Kiev"},
    "ru-153": {"questions":["An Orthodox church enjoying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chooses its own head and judges its own bishops, yet it remains bound to the other churches in matters of doctrine.","How a church gains <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whether granted by its mother church or proclaimed by itself, is a question the Orthodox churches have never settled among themselves."],"abstract":"<b>Autocephaly</b>, a word meaning 'self-headed', is the standing of an Orthodox church that has its own supreme authority, chooses its own head and is subject to no other church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a church governs itself and may try even its own chief bishop in a local council, but it has no independence in doctrine, which it holds in common with the other Orthodox churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How it is gained has never been agreed among the Orthodox churches, and one line of argument holds that a church proclaiming its own independence is more fully sovereign than one granted it from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian church historians long divided their past at this point, with a period of dependence on Constantinople, autocephalous metropolitans from 1448 and a patriarchate from 1589.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That scheme is a backward look, since the appointment of 1448 does not seem to have been seen at the time as a bid for independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Russian case began with a disappointment, for Jonah, the bishop of Ryazan, had gone to Constantinople to be made metropolitan and came back in the train of Isidore, the Greek appointed instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After Isidore had signed the union of Florence and been driven out of Moscow, a council of Russian bishops called by Vasily II installed Jonah on 15 December 1448 without the patriarch's consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jonah justified his appointment by the turmoil over the union in Constantinople, and in his letters he taught that the canons had long forbidden the summoning of any eighth council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The metropolitanate then split, and in 1471 a Muscovite narrative still denounced Gregory, Isidore's pupil, who was called metropolitan in Kiev but not received in the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vasily II and then Jonah wrote to the patriarchs of Constantinople until the later 1450s, after which the letters stopped, and the matter was closed only when Moscow gained a patriarch of its own in 1589.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrey Shishkov, “Церковная автокефалия через призму теории суверенитета Карла Шмитта” [Church autocephaly through the prism of Carl Schmitt's theory of sovereignty], <i>Государство, религия, церковь в России и за рубежом</i>, no. 3 (32) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tserkovnaya-avtokefaliya-cherez-prizmu-teorii-suvereniteta-karla-shmitta. [in Russian] [Open access]","Makary Veretennikov, “О периодизации истории Русской Церкви” [On the periodisation of the history of the Russian Church], <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i> (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-periodizatsii-istorii-russkoy-tserkvi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ivan N. Ostroumov, <i>The History of the Council of Florence</i>, trans. Basil Popoff, ed. J. M. Neale (London: Joseph Masters, 1861), 59–60, https://archive.org/details/historycouncilf00nealgoog. [Open access]","V. M. Shevyrin, review of <i>Средневековая Русь и Константинополь: дипломатические отношения в конце XIV – середине XV в.</i>, by E. I. Maleto, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2019-02-005-maletoe-i-srednevekovaya-rus-i-konstantinopol-diplomaticheskie-otnosheniya-v-kontse-xiv-seredine-xv-v-m-tsentropoligraf-2018-333. [in Russian] [Open access]","Paul Pierling, <i>La Russie et le Saint-Siège: études diplomatiques</i>, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (Paris: Plon-Nourrit, 1906), 103–4, https://archive.org/details/larussieetlesain01pier. [in French] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 208, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Konstantin A. Kostromin, “Автокефалия 1448 года и отношения Русской Церкви с Поместными Церквями” [The autocephaly of 1448 and the Russian Church's relations with the local churches], <i>Церковный историк</i>, no. 4 (14) (2023): 15–29, https://doi.org/10.31802/CH.2023.14.4.001. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Russian bishops act without Constantinople in 1448?","a":"Because the patriarchate had accepted the union of Florence, which Moscow had rejected. Jonah himself said that his appointment had been made out of necessity, because of the turmoil over the union in Constantinople among its emperors and patriarchs."},{"q":"Why is it misleading to say Russia simply declared independence in 1448?","a":"Because nobody at the time seems to have put it that way. The idea of a gradual passage from dependence to independence is a later historians' scheme, and letters to the patriarchs went on for several years after Jonah was installed."},{"q":"Why does it matter whether autocephaly is granted or proclaimed?","a":"Because one view holds that only a church with supreme authority can create another, so a church given its independence remains limited by the giver. A church that proclaims its own is, on that argument, sovereign in the full sense."}],"wiki":"Autocephaly"},
    "ru-154": {"questions":["A Russian tale of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closes with a prophecy that a fair-haired race will one day defeat the Ishmaelites and reign in the city of seven hills.","Cardinal Isidore, once metropolitan of Rus', held a stretch of the walls by the Golden Horn during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and escaped when the Ottoman army broke in."],"abstract":"The <b>fall of Constantinople</b> was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman army of Sultan Mehmed II on 29 May 1453, after a siege of about two months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting on the walls, and the city became the capital of the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The union with Rome agreed at Florence had been proclaimed in Hagia Sophia in December 1452 as the danger grew, but the city stayed bitterly divided over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Isidore, the former metropolitan of Rus', now a cardinal and papal legate, was given the shore by the Golden Horn to defend and got away when the city fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Orthodox historians later argued that by letting the capital of Eastern Christianity perish the popes had only hardened Greek hostility to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Moscow the fall was read as a punishment for the sins of the Greeks, above all for their lapse into the Latin heresy at Florence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That reading confirmed Moscow in its rejection of the union and made it less willing than ever to take metropolitans from Constantinople, while Tver, by contrast, wanted to keep its ties with the patriarchate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Russian tale of the taking of the city, ascribed to a Nestor Iskander, closes with a prophecy that the 'fair-haired race' will defeat the Ishmaelites and reign in the city of seven hills, which was read as a promise about the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Ivan III married Sophia, niece of the last emperor, in 1472, Byzantine titles and symbols began to pass to Moscow, and the idea of Moscow as a third Rome was formulated in the next century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Russian writers kept returning to the event, and after the Time of Troubles the cellarer of the Trinity-Sergius monastery compared its siege with that of Constantinople, a city lost for its sins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Nestor Iskander, “Повесть о взятии Царьграда турками в 1453 году” [The tale of the capture of Constantinople by the Turks in 1453], ed. and trans. O. V. Tvorogov, in <i>Библиотека литературы Древней Руси</i>, vol. 7 (St Petersburg: Nauka, 1999), http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=5059. [in Russian] [Open access]","Paul Pierling, <i>La Russie et le Saint-Siège: études diplomatiques</i>, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (Paris: Plon-Nourrit, 1906), 74–78, 103–5, https://archive.org/details/larussieetlesain01pier. [in French] [Open access]","Ivan N. Ostroumov, <i>The History of the Council of Florence</i>, trans. Basil Popoff, ed. J. M. Neale (London: Joseph Masters, 1861), 181, https://archive.org/details/historycouncilf00nealgoog. [Open access]","V. M. Shevyrin, review of <i>Средневековая Русь и Константинополь: дипломатические отношения в конце XIV – середине XV в.</i>, by E. I. Maleto, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2019-02-005-maletoe-i-srednevekovaya-rus-i-konstantinopol-diplomaticheskie-otnosheniya-v-kontse-xiv-seredine-xv-v-m-tsentropoligraf-2018-333. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 17, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Pavel A. Vorobyov, “Византийский фактор в политико-символической и церковной практике восточных славян (середина XV — конец XVII в.)” [The Byzantine factor in the political-symbolic and church practice of the Eastern Slavs, mid-15th to late 17th century], <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 4 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vizantiyskiy-faktor-v-politiko-simvolicheskoy-i-tserkovnoy-praktike-vostochnyh-slavyan-seredina-xv-konets-xvii-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Konstantin A. Kostromin, “Автокефалия 1448 года и отношения Русской Церкви с Поместными Церквями” [The autocephaly of 1448 and the Russian Church's relations with the local churches], <i>Церковный историк</i>, no. 4 (14) (2023): 15–29, https://doi.org/10.31802/CH.2023.14.4.001. [in Russian] [Open access]","I. O. Tyumentsev, “Взятие Константинополя турками в 1453 году в русской публицистике Смутного времени” [The Turkish capture of Constantinople in 1453 in Russian writing of the Time of Troubles], <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 8: Литературоведение. Журналистика</i>, no. 5 (2006): 103–5, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzyatie-konstantinopolya-turkami-v-1453-godu-v-russkoy-publitsistike-smutnogo-vremeni. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow see the fall as a punishment rather than a disaster for all Christians?","a":"Because it had already rejected the union of Florence as apostasy, and the fall looked like proof. The Greeks had joined the Latins they had long called heretics, and the loss of their city seemed to show what that had cost them."},{"q":"Why did the fall make Moscow less dependent on Constantinople?","a":"Because Moscow no longer wished to take metropolitans sent from there, or to send its own candidates to be consecrated. It had installed Jonah without the patriarch in 1448, and after 1453 it had even less reason to look back."},{"q":"Why did a Russian tale promise that the city would be recovered?","a":"Because its author read the old prophecies about Constantinople as still unfinished. If the predictions of its fall had come true, he argued, so would the one about a fair-haired race reigning in the city of seven hills."}],"wiki":"Fall of Constantinople","places":["pl:q16869"]},
    "ru-155": {"questions":["The frescoes of 1378 in the Transfiguration church on Ilyina Street in Novgorod are the only surviving work firmly attributed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who had painted churches from Constantinople to Caffa.","In 1405 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the elder Prokhor of Gorodets and the monk Andrei Rublev painted the Church of the Annunciation at the grand prince's court in Moscow."],"abstract":"<b>Theophanes the Greek</b> was a Byzantine painter of the later 14th century and the early 15th who worked in Rus', admired by his contemporaries as a philosopher as well as a master of his art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A letter of Epiphanius the Wise says that he had painted more than forty stone churches with his own hand, in Constantinople, Chalcedon, Galata and Caffa as well as in Novgorod and Nizhny Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Epiphanius, who knew him in Moscow, marvels that he never looked at pattern books as he painted, but stood talking with visitors while his hand worked and his mind dwelt on distant and wise things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Epiphanius asked for a picture of Hagia Sophia in Constantinople for a Gospel book, Theophanes answered that it could not be drawn and that he would give only a hundredth part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His only firmly attested surviving work is the fresco scheme of 1378 in the Church of the Transfiguration on Ilyina Street in Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A later Novgorod chronicle says the church was painted at the order of the boyar Vasily Danilovich and the people of Ilyina Street, an entry apparently copied from the donor's inscription on the church wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its stylites and desert hermits, painted in a small chapel on the choir gallery, have struck viewers by a sharpness of individual character that seems almost unbelievable for their time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Moscow the Trinity Chronicle records him painting the Church of the Nativity of the Virgin in 1395 with Semyon Chorny, the church of the Archangel Michael in 1399 with his pupils, and the Annunciation in 1405.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the Annunciation, the church of the grand prince's court, his fellow masters were the elder Prokhor of Gorodets and the monk Andrei Rublev, and the work begun that spring was finished the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Annunciation iconostasis was made under his direction, and his example is often thought to lie behind Rublev's art, though how much the younger painter owed him has been disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maria Orlova, review of <i>Феофан Грек. Фрески в Великом Новгороде</i>, by Tatiana Tsarevskaya, <i>Искусствознание</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tatyana-tsarevskaya-feofan-g-rek-freski-v-velikom-novgorode-velikiy-novgorod-novgorodskiy-gosudarstvennyy-obedinennyy-muzey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Epiphanius the Wise, “Письмо Епифания Премудрого к Кириллу Тверскому” [Letter to Cyril of Tver], ed. and trans. O. A. Belobrova, in <i>Библиотека литературы Древней Руси</i>, vol. 6 (St Petersburg: Nauka, 1999), http://lib.pushkinskijdom.ru/Default.aspx?tabid=4992. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. E. Nosova, “Об изображении святого Макария в церкви Спаса на Ильине улице в Великом Новгороде” [On the image of St Macarius in the Church of the Saviour on Ilyina Street in Veliky Novgorod], <i>Вестник Новгородского государственного университета</i>, no. 72, vol. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ob-izobrazhenii-svyatogo-makariya-v-tserkvi-spasa-na-iline-ulitse-v-velikom-novgorode. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr V. Lavrentyev, “К истории появления фресок Андрея Рублева и Даниила Черного в Успенском соборе Владимира” [On the origin of the frescoes of Andrei Rublev and Daniil Chorny in the Dormition Cathedral of Vladimir], <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (28) (2020): 141–58, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2020.209. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikodim P. Kondakov, <i>The Russian Icon</i>, trans. Ellis H. Minns (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1927), 87–89, https://archive.org/details/bwb_P8-BIM-707. [Open access]","Vera A. Serkova, “Художники-греки в русском и испанском искусстве” [Greek painters in Russian and Spanish art], <i>Terra Linguistica</i>, no. 4 (136) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hudozhniki-greki-v-russkom-i-ispanskom-iskusstve. [in Russian] [Open access]","Maria A. Orlova, “Феофан Грек и античность. Некоторые замечания” [Theophanes the Greek and antiquity: some remarks], <i>Вестник сектора древнерусского искусства</i>, no. 2 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/feofan-grek-i-antichnost-nekotorye-zamechaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a Byzantine painter working in Rus' at all?","a":"Because Rus' was rebuilding in the second half of the 14th century and putting up stone churches that needed painters. Masters from the Byzantine world had been coming to work there since at least the second quarter of the century, though most of their names and works are lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does so little of his work survive?","a":"Because wall paintings were the most easily lost kind of medieval art. The Novgorod scheme began to be damaged within a few years of its making, and the Moscow churches he painted were rebuilt, so their frescoes are gone."},{"q":"Why did Epiphanius want a drawing of Hagia Sophia?","a":"Because he wished to place it at the head of his book. Looking at it, he wrote, he could remember the painter and imagine himself standing in Constantinople, a city he had heard compared in size with the Moscow Kremlin."}],"wiki":"Theophanes the Greek"},
    "ru-156": {"questions":["Only two works are certainly by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Trinity icon and the Vladimir frescoes of 1408, though far more has been given to him on grounds of style.","Canonised by the Russian Orthodox Church in 1988, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was named in the chronicle for 1405 as a monk painting the Annunciation church alongside Theophanes the Greek."],"abstract":"<b>Andrei Rublev</b> was a monk and icon painter of Moscow in the early 15th century, the most celebrated painter of medieval Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is recorded of his work comes from the first quarter of the century, and he is thought to have died about 1430.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Trinity Chronicle first names him in 1405, when the monk Andrei Rublev painted the grand prince's Church of the Annunciation with Theophanes the Greek and the elder Prokhor of Gorodets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 25 May 1408 he and Daniil the icon painter began to paint the Dormition Cathedral in Vladimir at the grand prince's order, and the Last Judgement on its west end is usually given to Rublev himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph of Volokolamsk heard from an elder that Daniil and his pupil Andrei, monks of the Andronikov monastery, would sit before the icons even on Easter Day, lifting their minds to the divine light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Judged strictly by documents and the oldest core of tradition, only two works are certainly his, the Trinity icon and the Vladimir frescoes of 1408.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much else, including the Deesis panels known as the Zvenigorod tier, is attributed to him or to painters around him on grounds of style, and specialists disagree about where his hand ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His art has often been traced back to the Novgorod frescoes of Theophanes, and an English translator writing in 1927 thought it hard to believe that he owed nothing to the older master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The book he translated praised his refined drawing and his colouring of deep purples and pale ivory flesh, which his contemporaries took for the severe Greek manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Russian Orthodox Church canonised him as a saint at its council of June 1988, held for the thousandth anniversary of the baptism of Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Engelina S. Smirnova, “Об изучении творчества Андрея Рублева: некоторые уроки выставки 2010–2011 гг. и недавних исследований” [On the study of Andrei Rublev's work: lessons of the 2010–2011 exhibition and recent research], <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2018): 77–83, https://doi.org/10.7256/2454-0609.2018.6.28239. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr V. Lavrentyev, “К истории появления фресок Андрея Рублева и Даниила Черного в Успенском соборе Владимира” [On the origin of the frescoes of Andrei Rublev and Daniil Chorny in the Dormition Cathedral of Vladimir], <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (28) (2020): 141–58, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2020.209. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vera A. Serkova, “Художники-греки в русском и испанском искусстве” [Greek painters in Russian and Spanish art], <i>Terra Linguistica</i>, no. 4 (136) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hudozhniki-greki-v-russkom-i-ispanskom-iskusstve. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail A. Maslin and Mikhail V. Shpakovsky, “Толкование Иосифом Волоцким «Троицы» Андрея Рублева — к вопросу о возможной преемственности” [Joseph of Volokolamsk's interpretation of Andrei Rublev's Trinity], <i>Вестник Русской христианской гуманитарной академии</i> 22, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tolkovanie-iosifom-volotskim-troitsy-andreya-rubleva-k-voprosu-o-vozmozhnoy-preemstvennosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Maria Orlova, review of <i>Феофан Грек. Фрески в Великом Новгороде</i>, by Tatiana Tsarevskaya, <i>Искусствознание</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tatyana-tsarevskaya-feofan-g-rek-freski-v-velikom-novgorode-velikiy-novgorod-novgorodskiy-gosudarstvennyy-obedinennyy-muzey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikodim P. Kondakov, <i>The Russian Icon</i>, trans. Ellis H. Minns (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1927), 86–91, https://archive.org/details/bwb_P8-BIM-707. [Open access]","R. A. Lopin and D. V. Kuznetsov, “«Троица» прп. Андрея Рублева: к художественно-исторической герменевтике и богословию иконы” [St Andrei Rublev's Trinity: towards the hermeneutics and theology of the icon], <i>Научный результат. Социальные и гуманитарные исследования</i> 9, no. 2 (2023), https://doi.org/10.18413/2408-932X-2023-9-2-0-10. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are so few works certainly by Rublev?","a":"Because icon painters did not sign their work and the written record names him only a handful of times. Beyond the Trinity and the Vladimir frescoes, every attribution rests on comparing styles, and the panels long given to him differ enough among themselves to divide specialists."},{"q":"Why did Moscow send its painters to Vladimir in 1408?","a":"The older answer is that Moscow was restoring the shrines of Vladimir, which were seen as the sacred works of its own dynasty's ancestors. More recent work asks whether the commission answered a particular turn in the politics of the church and the principality that year."},{"q":"Why was Rublev remembered as a holy man and not only as a craftsman?","a":"Because the monastic tradition passed on stories of his prayer as well as his painting. An elder told Joseph of Volokolamsk how he and Daniil contemplated icons as a way of raising the mind to God, and the church later canonised him."}],"wiki":"Andrei Rublev"},
    "ru-157": {"questions":["Ivan the Terrible and Boris Godunov gave costly covers for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose 15th-century paint lay hidden under later layers until it was cleaned in 1904.","Joseph of Volokolamsk interpreted Rublev's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his writings on the veneration of icons, perhaps drawing on a tradition handed down from the painter's own circle."],"abstract":"The <b>Trinity</b> is the icon painted by Andrei Rublev for the Trinity Cathedral of the Trinity-Sergius monastery, showing the three angels who visited Abraham as an image of the triune God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The story is the hospitality of Abraham in Genesis, and the three angels at his table had come by convention to stand for the Holy Trinity in the type of image called the Old Testament Trinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where Novgorod painters kept Abraham, Sarah and even the preparation of the calf, Rublev left out the household and painted only the three angels seated at a table round a single cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cup was first shaped to look like both a chalice and a paten, so that it would be read as the cup of the Eucharist, the visual centre of the whole composition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Judged by documents and the oldest tradition, it is one of only two works that are certainly Rublev's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its date is disputed between the years about 1408 to 1412 and about 1425, when the stone Trinity Cathedral was finished and its iconostasis painted, and recent study favours the later date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph of Volokolamsk gave an interpretation of the icon in his writings on the veneration of icons, and some think it goes back to an oral tradition from Rublev's own circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The icon was honoured as a relic, with costly covers given by Ivan the Terrible and Boris Godunov, and its paint was hidden under three or four later layers of overpainting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The icon painter Vasily Guryanov uncovered the 15th-century paint in the spring of 1904, though he is thought to have retouched the faces, and when the surface darkened again it was cleaned once more from 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A copy painted by the restorer N. A. Baranov in 1929 now stands in its place in the iconostasis, and theologians have read the angels' gestures and the cup between them in many different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Lyudmila M. Vorontsova, “Местный ряд иконостаса Троицкого собора Троице-Сергиева монастыря. Реконструкция по Описи 1641 года” [The local tier of the iconostasis of the Trinity Cathedral of the Trinity-Sergius Monastery], <i>Вестник сектора древнерусского искусства</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mestnyy-ryad-ikonostasa-troitskogo-sobora-troitse-sergieva-monastyrya-rekonstruktsiya-po-opisi-1641-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikodim P. Kondakov, <i>The Russian Icon</i>, trans. Ellis H. Minns (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1927), 90–91, https://archive.org/details/bwb_P8-BIM-707. [Open access]","Boris N. Dudochkin, “Андрей Рублев. Жизнь и творчество в свете современных представлений (часть 2)” [Andrei Rublev: life and work in the light of current views, part 2], <i>Культурологический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/andrey-rublev-zhizn-i-tvorchestvo-v-svete-sovremennyh-predstavleniy-chast-2. [in Russian] [Open access]","Engelina S. Smirnova, “Об изучении творчества Андрея Рублева: некоторые уроки выставки 2010–2011 гг. и недавних исследований” [On the study of Andrei Rublev's work: lessons of the 2010–2011 exhibition and recent research], <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2018): 77–83, https://doi.org/10.7256/2454-0609.2018.6.28239. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail A. Maslin and Mikhail V. Shpakovsky, “Толкование Иосифом Волоцким «Троицы» Андрея Рублева — к вопросу о возможной преемственности” [Joseph of Volokolamsk's interpretation of Andrei Rublev's Trinity], <i>Вестник Русской христианской гуманитарной академии</i> 22, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tolkovanie-iosifom-volotskim-troitsy-andreya-rubleva-k-voprosu-o-vozmozhnoy-preemstvennosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ivan A. Ivanyushkin, “«Троица» Андрея Рублева: загадка и разгадка” [Andrei Rublev's Trinity: riddle and solution], <i>Горизонты гуманитарного знания</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/troitsa-andreya-rubleva-zagadka-i-razgadka. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. A. Lopin and D. V. Kuznetsov, “«Троица» прп. Андрея Рублева: к художественно-исторической герменевтике и богословию иконы” [St Andrei Rublev's Trinity: towards the hermeneutics and theology of the icon], <i>Научный результат. Социальные и гуманитарные исследования</i> 9, no. 2 (2023), https://doi.org/10.18413/2408-932X-2023-9-2-0-10. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rublev leave Abraham and Sarah out of the scene?","a":"Because the icon was made for prayer rather than to tell a story. Dropping the household turned an episode from Genesis into an image of the three persons of God, with the cup on the table pointing to the Eucharist."},{"q":"Why is the date of the icon disputed?","a":"Because no document records when it was painted. One view places it early in Rublev's career, about 1408 to 1412, while the other ties it to the building of the stone Trinity Cathedral and the painting of its iconostasis about 1425."},{"q":"Why was the original paint hidden for so long?","a":"Because a revered icon was repeatedly renewed rather than preserved. Each generation repainted it and covered it with a precious metal cover, so the 15th-century surface was only seen again when it was cleaned in the 20th century."}],"wiki":"Trinity (Andrei Rublev)"},
    "ru-158": {"questions":["Okolnichie, gentry of the duma and duma secretaries sat below the boyars proper in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had grown from a prince's few advisers into a council of several score.","When Vasily Shuisky was deposed in 1610, Moscow swore allegiance to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a temporary government while rival claimants contended for the throne."],"abstract":"The <b>Boyar Duma</b> was the council of the rulers of Moscow, made up of the leading men of the court, which dealt with legislation and the business of government under the grand prince and later the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in the councils of the princes of Kievan Rus', who consulted the senior men of their retinues, and a classic 19th-century study traced the boyar class and its council from those beginnings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the appanage period a prince summoned only a few boyars for matters of importance, but in the Muscovite state the duma became a permanent body of several score members in ranked grades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below the boyars proper sat the okolnichie, from less exalted untitled families, then the gentry of the duma and the duma secretaries, men of humbler birth appointed for merit or service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New laws were issued under the formula that the tsar had commanded and the boyars had ordained, and the law code of 1550 gave that practice the backing of statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its membership under Ivan III is hard to reconstruct, because the list of duma ranks long relied on by historians, published in 1791, credits him with far more boyars than he actually had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sigismund von Herberstein, who visited the court of Vasily III, wrote that none of the prince's counsellors had the authority to oppose him or even to differ from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Vasily died in 1533, appointments to the duma passed within a narrow circle of boyar clans close to the throne, and powerful aristocrats held more than one high rank at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The English envoy Giles Fletcher named 31 privy councillors who met on Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays at seven in the morning, but wrote that Boris Godunov settled matters with five or six men of his choosing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Tsar Vasily Shuisky was deposed in 1610, Moscow swore allegiance to the Boyar Duma as a temporary government while the boyars weighed rival claimants to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 258–60, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Evgenia A. Teterina, “В. О. Ключевский о становлении боярского класса Киевской Руси” [Klyuchevsky on the formation of the boyar class of Kievan Rus'], <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (8) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-o-klyuchevskiy-o-stanovlenii-boyarskogo-klassa-kievskoy-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander L. Korzinin, “Состав думных и дворцовых чинов в правление великого князя Ивана III. Часть 1. Думные чины” [The duma and palace ranks under Grand Prince Ivan III, part 1: the duma ranks], <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 8 (2017): 318–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sostav-dumnyh-i-dvortsovyh-chinov-v-pravlenie-velikogo-knyazya-ivana-iii-chast-1-dumnye-chiny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. R. H. Major (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 32, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Alexander L. Korzinin, “О составе Боярской думы и дворцовой администрации в период боярского правления в 30–40-е гг. XVI века” [On the composition of the Boyar Duma and palace administration during the boyar rule of the 1530s and 1540s], <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История</i> 24, no. 5 (2019): 67–85, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.5.5. [in Russian] [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, “Of the Russe Common Wealth,” in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 46–48, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 3, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 42, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.91917. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the council grow from a handful of advisers into a large body?","a":"Because as Moscow absorbed other principalities, their princes and boyars entered its service and the official class split into layers of different origin and standing. Each layer found its own grade in the council, from the great boyar families down to secretaries of humble birth."},{"q":"Why did foreign visitors describe the council as having so little power?","a":"Because what they saw was a court where the ruler's will was final. One visitor wrote that no counsellor dared differ from the prince, and another that the title of counsellor was often given for honour while a favourite and a few others decided affairs."},{"q":"Why is it hard to say who sat in the council under Ivan III?","a":"Because the handy printed list of ranks turns out to be unreliable, often recording as the date of an appointment the first year a man appears with a title. Historians now rebuild the membership from land charters, service registers, embassy books and the earliest genealogies."}]},
    "ru-159": {"questions":["Herberstein wrote that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the rank of a noble or knight in his own country, the lowest of the Russian titles, beneath that of the princes.","In Novgorod each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belonged to the governing aristocracy, directing and financing trade rather than trading, and the chronicle names them beside the posadniks and merchants."],"abstract":"A <b>boyar</b> was a member of the highest rank of the old Russian aristocracy below the princes, from the days of Kievan Rus' until the old boyar families merged into the service gentry in the age of Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Kiev the name was first given to the senior members of the prince's retinue, largely of Varangian origin, who served as his standing military advisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Novgorod the boyars formed the governing aristocracy, men who directed and financed trade rather than trading themselves, and the chronicle names them beside the posadniks and merchants whenever the city acted together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the appanage period a boyar served a prince under a personal agreement and might move to another court, which kept the order of rank at any court unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The metropolitans of Moscow had boyars of their own, drawn from the same service families as the grand prince's court and for a century and a half from a single family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Muscovite rulers boyar became a rank conferred by the sovereign, so that to call a man a boyar meant not that he had been born one but that he had been promoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was the highest rank in the Boyar Duma, above the okolnichie, and the senior men of the great families were summoned to it as they reached the proper age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sigismund von Herberstein, the imperial envoy, wrote that the boyars held the rank of nobles or knights in his own country, the lowest of the Russian titles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Giles Fletcher placed them second in the Russian nobility, men honoured with the title of counsellor, whose lands brought about a thousand marks a year and whose war pensions were at most 700 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The abolition of the precedence system in 1682 hastened the absorption of the boyars into the service gentry, and the Table of Ranks of 1722 opened that class to men of every origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenia A. Teterina, “В. О. Ключевский о становлении боярского класса Киевской Руси” [Klyuchevsky on the formation of the boyar class of Kievan Rus'], <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (8) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-o-klyuchevskiy-o-stanovlenii-boyarskogo-klassa-kievskoy-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 3, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.91917. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 196–97, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 44–45, 53, 258–59, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","A. Yu. Savosichev, “Митрополичьи бояре из рода Бяконта” [The metropolitan's boyars of the Byakont family], <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolichi-boyare-iz-roda-byakonta. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, vol. 1, trans. R. H. Major (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 36, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, “Of the Russe Common Wealth,” in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 34–37, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the meaning of the word change under Moscow?","a":"Because the grand princes turned service at court into a ladder of ranks that they controlled. A man of a great family still expected the rank, but it was the sovereign who granted it, so boyar became the name of a grade rather than of a birth."},{"q":"Why could the order of rank at a prince's court be so unsettled before Muscovite rule?","a":"Because service rested on a personal agreement with the prince. A newcomer of high standing could make his own bargain and be placed above men who had served for years, upsetting an order they claimed from their fathers."},{"q":"Why did the boyars disappear as a separate class?","a":"Because their standing had rested on inherited precedence. Once that system was abolished in 1682 the old families merged into the wider service gentry, and the ranks Peter the Great created in 1722 could be reached by men of any background."}],"wiki":"Boyar"},
    "ru-160": {"questions":["A law of 1682 abolished <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the system by which Muscovite noblemen claimed their places in service and at court from the posts held by their forefathers.","From 1550 some commands were declared 'without places', so that serving in them could not be held against a family under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Muscovite reckoning of precedence by ancestry."],"abstract":"<b>Mestnichestvo</b>, from the word for 'place', was the Muscovite system of precedence by which a nobleman's rank in service and at court depended on the posts his ancestors had held and on his own place in his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each family stood in a fixed relation to every other, so that the princes of Odoev always ranked above the Buturlins, and no man would serve beneath someone whose family stood lower than his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reckoning combined the genealogical register of the great families with the service registers of appointments to high posts, which survive from the 1470s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside a family, rank was counted in places from the founder by rules modelled on the seating of a household at table, by which a father's eldest son stood level with his fourth brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A man who thought an appointment beneath him petitioned the tsar for a trial and a reckoning, and the court decided whether he had suffered dishonour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its beginnings were long dated to the reign of Ivan III, but copies survive of a complete precedence case from the middle of the 15th century, which points to an earlier start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Quarrels over the commands of an army's five regiments were constant, and from 1550 some posts were declared to be 'without places', so that serving in them could not be counted against a family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The system made honour collective, since each gain or loss by one man raised or lowered all his kinsmen, and families fought precedence quarrels as a single body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Newcomers had to find their level, and the princes Trubetskoy, who had held posts only now and then before the 1560s, began a dispute at Polotsk in 1564 to settle their standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of 1682 abolished mestnichestvo, which hastened the merging of the old boyar families into the service gentry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 44–55, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","K. V. Petrov, “Местнические правоотношения в русском праве XVI–XVII вв.” [Precedence relations in Russian law of the 16th and 17th centuries], <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mestnicheskie-pravootnosheniya-v-russkom-prave-xvi-xvii-vv-soderzhanie-pravovaya-priroda-osnovaniya-i-sposoby-zaschity. [in Russian] [Open access]","I. G. Ponomareva, “Древнейший комплекс местнической документации (обстоятельства создания и состав)” [The oldest body of precedence documents], <i>Вестник Московского государственного лингвистического университета. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (844) (2021): 140–55, https://doi.org/10.52070/2500-347X_2021_3_844_140. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. V. Petrov, “Проблема местнического старшинства полковых воевод в первой половине XVI века” [The problem of precedence among regimental commanders in the first half of the 16th century], <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i> 1, no. 1 (1993), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-mestnicheskogo-starshinstva-polkovyh-voevod-v-pervoy-polovine-xvi-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Belov, “Местнический конфликт князей А. Б. Трубецкого и П. М. Щенятева в Полоцке весной 1564 года” [The precedence dispute of Princes A. B. Trubetskoy and P. M. Shchenyatev at Polotsk in spring 1564], <i>Вестник Самарского университета. История, педагогика, филология</i> 27, no. 4 (2021): 8–18, https://doi.org/10.18287/2542-0445-2021-27-4-8-18. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 3, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 4, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.91917. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a nobleman refuse a command that seemed perfectly honourable?","a":"Because accepting a post below a man of lower family would lower not only him but every kinsman after him. The relation between families, once recorded, was meant never to change, so a single bad appointment could become a precedent against his descendants."},{"q":"Why did the tsars put up with a system that hampered appointments?","a":"Because it also tied the great families to service in Moscow, where every post was recorded and counted. When it became unworkable in war the rulers limited it rather than ending it, declaring some commands to be without places from 1550."},{"q":"Why does the date of its origin matter?","a":"Because it shows whether precedence grew up with the old Muscovite boyars or arrived with the princes who flooded into Moscow's service later. A surviving case from the middle of the 15th century suggests the system was taking shape before that influx."}],"wiki":"Mestnichestvo"},
    "ru-161": {"questions":["At the accession of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1462 almost every Russian community paid homage to the Horde or to Poland-Lithuania, and by 1505 Novgorod and Tver had both passed to Moscow.","Married in 1472 to a Byzantine princess living in Rome, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Italian builders to Moscow to give the Kremlin a new cathedral and a palace of stone."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan III</b> was grand prince of Moscow from 1462 to 1505, the son of Vasily II and the ruler under whom Moscow became the centre of a single state in north-eastern Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At his accession every Russian community except Vyatka paid homage either to the khan of the Horde or to Poland-Lithuania, and the lands of Rus' were divided among many principalities and free cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his reign Yaroslavl, Novgorod, Perm, Rostov and Tver passed to Moscow, and his son completed the work by taking Pskov, Smolensk and Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account of his war on Novgorod in 1471 that the chronicle preserves already calls him grand prince of all Russia and casts the campaign as the punishment of a city that had turned to the Latin king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bringing many lands under one ruler was not unusual in the Europe of his day, and historians compare his work with the making of the French, Spanish and English monarchies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His second marriage, to the Byzantine princess Sophia Palaiologina in 1472, brought Greek émigrés to Moscow, and Italian builders gave the Kremlin a new cathedral and a palace of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Contact with Italy was older than the marriage, and by the 1470s the Moscow court already knew enough of Italian affairs to welcome much of Italian craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sigismund von Herberstein, who visited Moscow under his son, was told that Ivan went to war in person only once, when Novgorod and Tver were taken, and yet nearly always won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stephen the Great of Moldavia is said to have joked at his banquets that Ivan enlarged his realm while sitting at home asleep, while he himself could barely hold his borders fighting every day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against the Horde Ivan likewise preferred alliance to battle, and his friendship with the Crimean khan Mengli Giray became the axis of a new order in the steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 1–19, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 216–231, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 20–25, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 205–219, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Vitaly V. Penskoy and Tatyana M. Penskaya, “The Post-Horde World and the Union of Ivan III and Mengli-Girey I: An Attempt to Create a ‘Bipolar’ Political System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 868–898, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.868-898. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tat’iana A. Matasova, “Skladyvanie ital’ianskogo napravleniia politiki moskovskikh kniazei v XV v.,” <i>Vestnik Moskovskogo universiteta. Seriia 8. Istoriia</i>, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/skladyvanie-italyanskogo-napravleniya-politiki-moskovskih-knyazey-v-xv-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai K. Baturin, “Istoriko-pravovye aspekty zaversheniia ob’edineniia Severo-Vostochnoi Rusi,” <i>Vestnik Rossiiskogo universiteta kooperatsii</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-pravovye-aspekty-zaversheniya-obedineniya-severo-vostochnoy-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Ivan III win so much while rarely taking the field?","a":"Because much of the work was done by others. Communities and nobles were already turning to Moscow of their own accord, his commanders fought the battles, and allies such as the Crimean khan kept his enemies busy, so the grand prince could gather lands by negotiation and delay."},{"q":"Why does the Novgorod chronicle call him grand prince of all Russia?","a":"Because the account of 1471 it preserves was written from Moscow's side. It treats Novgorod as the grand prince's patrimony and its turn towards the Polish king as a betrayal of Orthodoxy, which is exactly the claim the wider title made."},{"q":"Why did his marriage matter to the Kremlin's buildings?","a":"Because it strengthened ties with Italy that already existed. Greeks came with Sophia, and Italian architects and engineers followed, building a new cathedral and a stone palace in place of the old wooden quarters."}],"wiki":"Ivan III of Russia"},
    "ru-162": {"questions":["Usually dated from Ivan Kalita to Ivan the Terrible, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought principalities and free cities such as Yaroslavl, Rostov, Novgorod and Tver under the rule of Moscow.","Once local communities began turning to Moscow of their own accord in the mid-15th century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> quickened sharply, with Yaroslavl, Perm, Rostov and Tver all absorbed under Ivan III."],"abstract":"The <b>gathering of the Russian lands</b> is the name traditionally given to the process by which the princes of Moscow brought the principalities and free cities of north-eastern Rus' under their own rule between the 14th and the 16th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians use it alongside the phrase 'the unification of the Russian lands around Moscow', and it usually covers the span from Ivan Kalita to Ivan the Terrible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its early stages worked by seizure and by private bargains with neighbouring princes, in which the local communities took little part beyond an occasional show of sympathy for Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the middle of the 15th century the communities themselves began to turn to Moscow, so that the process quickened and took on the colour of a national and religious cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Similar unions of lands under one crown were under way across Europe at the time, though the case of north-eastern Rus' had features of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under Ivan III the pace was fastest: Yaroslavl swore fealty in 1463, Novgorod was conquered in the 1470s, Perm submitted in 1472, Rostov's princes sold the rest of their lands in 1474 and Tver was taken in 1485.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A western envoy was told that once Tver and Novgorod were his, the other princes submitted to Ivan out of awe at his success or out of fear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the conquest of Novgorod in 1471 his chancery insisted on a title claiming that the grand prince's power over every part of Rus' was a patrimony inherited from his forefathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> During the war with Lithuania of 1500 to 1503 his government told a papal and Hungarian envoy that the Jagiellons held their lands only from their fathers, whereas Moscow was heir to the Russian land from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern concept is often treated as a plain fact, yet the ideas behind it formed only gradually and mixed several unstable claims rather than one fixed programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 4–14, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 216–217, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Aleksandr B. Kamenskii, “‘Ottorzhennaia vozvratikh’: razdely Pol’shi i kontseptsiia sobiraniia russkikh zemel’,” <i>Trudy po rossievedeniiu</i>, no. 6 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ottorzhennaya-vozvratih-razdely-polshi-i-kontseptsiya-sobiraniya-russkih-zemel. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai K. Baturin, “Istoriko-pravovye aspekty zaversheniia ob’edineniia Severo-Vostochnoi Rusi,” <i>Vestnik Rossiiskogo universiteta kooperatsii</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-pravovye-aspekty-zaversheniya-obedineniya-severo-vostochnoy-rusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Magdolna Agoshton, “Titul pravitelia moskovskogo gosudarstva, 1474–1533 gg.,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i>, no. 9 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/titul-pravitelya-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-1474-1533-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the process speed up in the middle of the 15th century?","a":"Because the communities being absorbed began to take part in it. Townspeople in Novgorod looked to Moscow against their own upper class, and nobles elsewhere were drawn by the rewards of serving a strong and rich ruler, so annexation stopped being a matter of private deals between princes."},{"q":"Why did the claim to be heir to the Russian land matter for Lithuania?","a":"Because large parts of old Rus' lay inside the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. If every Russian land was the grand prince's inheritance, then Lithuania's Russian provinces were his by right, which gave Moscow a standing reason for war."},{"q":"Why do some historians treat the concept with caution?","a":"Because it is easy to read a single plan back into events. The term is often used as though it described a programme fixed from the start, when the ideas behind it took shape slowly and combined claims that did not always fit together."}],"wiki":"Gathering of the Russian lands"},
    "ru-163": {"questions":["A draft treaty naming the Polish king as Novgorod's sovereign was found in the baggage after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and sent to Ivan III as evidence of the city's treason.","Whether Tatar troops of the Kasimov prince Daniyar lay in ambush at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still divides historians, for the ambush appears in a Novgorodian chronicle but not in Moscow's official account."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Shelon</b> was fought on 14 July 1471 on the river Shelon, which flows into Lake Ilmen, between the army of Grand Prince Ivan III of Moscow and the levy of the city of Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan had marched on Novgorod because a party in the city wished to place it under Casimir IV, king of Poland, and the Moscow account calls the city's offence a lapse into Latinism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Muscovite commanders had already taken Rusa and beaten two Novgorodian forces sent by boat before they were ordered to the Shelon to join the men of Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two armies marched up opposite banks and sighted each other on the evening of 13 July, and the next morning the Muscovites forded the river and routed the Novgorodians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicles give Moscow four to five thousand men and Novgorod twenty to forty thousand, but those figures came from prisoners, and modern historians accept only that Novgorod had clearly more men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the baggage the victors found the draft of a treaty with the Polish king that named him the city's sovereign, and it was sent to Ivan as proof of treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several leading men were beheaded, among them Dmitri, the son of Marfa Boretskaya, a widow whose real part in these events the surviving sources do not allow historians to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Novgorod paid a ransom that one chronicle puts at 16,000 roubles, and it remained a republic only on the grand prince's sufferance until its final submission seven years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A Novgorodian chronicle tells a different story, in which the city's men first threw the Muscovites back and a Tatar force in ambush decided the day, while Moscow's official account gives a simpler picture of a swift rout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Moscow's chroniclers, for their part, wrote the victory into the heroic tradition of the Battle of Kulikovo and used it to present the grand prince as the defender of the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 205–219, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","M. A. Nesin, “Shelonskaia bitva 14 iiulia 1471 g.: k voprosu o taktike moskovskikh voisk i uchastii zasadnoi tatarskoi rati,” <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i> 4 (2014): 464–482, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shelonskaya-bitva-14-iyulya-1471-g-k-voprosu-o-taktike-moskovskih-voysk-i-uchastii-zasadnoy-tatarskoy-rati. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 24–25, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","E. A. Korogodina, “Letopisanie kak instrument politicheskoi bor’by v pravlenie Ivana III: retseptsiia obraza Dmitriia Donskogo i ritoriki ‘Zadonshchiny’ v kontekste Shelonskoi bitvy,” <i>Vlast’ istorii – Istoriia vlasti</i> 12, no. 5 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/letopisanie-kak-instrument-politicheskoy-borby-v-pravlenie-ivana-iii-retseptsiya-obraza-dmitriya-donskogo-i-ritoriki-zadonschiny-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail A. Nesin, “Uchastie kasimovskogo tsarevicha Daniiara v voennykh pokhodakh velikokniazheskikh voisk 1471–1472 gg.,” <i>Srednevekovye tiurko-tatarskie gosudarstva</i>, no. 8 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchastie-kasimovskogo-tsarevicha-daniyara-v-voennyh-pohodah-velikoknyazheskih-voysk-1471-1472-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 218–220, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Sergei S. Pashin and Viktoriia A. Iurshina, “Marfa Boretskaia i prisoedinenie Novgoroda k Moskve,” <i>Vestnik Udmurtskogo universiteta. Seriia Istoriia i filologiia</i> 31, no. 1 (2021): 14–21, https://doi.org/10.35634/2412-9534-2021-31-1-14-21. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a small Muscovite force beat a much larger Novgorodian one?","a":"Because the numbers were not what they seem. The large Novgorodian figures came from prisoners and are distrusted, and Novgorod had already lost men in two defeats by boat, while the Muscovites were professional servicemen who crossed the river quickly and broke the levy before it could recover."},{"q":"Why was the draft treaty found in the baggage so useful to Ivan III?","a":"Because it proved the charge he had made. The draft named the Polish king as Novgorod's sovereign and handed him the grand prince's lands and taxes, so Moscow could present the war as the punishment of treason and of a turn towards the Latin faith."},{"q":"Why do the chronicles disagree about how the battle was won?","a":"Because each side wrote its own version. The Novgorodian account lets the city's men fight well and lose only to a hidden Tatar force, while Moscow's chroniclers told of a swift rout and linked it to the glory of Kulikovo."}],"wiki":"Battle of Shelon"},
    "ru-164": {"questions":["Only a literary tale in the Sofia First Chronicle makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the leader of Novgorod's opposition to Ivan III, while Novgorod's own chronicles say nothing of any political role.","The eldest son of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was beheaded after the defeat on the Shelon in 1471, and seven years later, when the republic fell, the widow herself was taken to Moscow."],"abstract":"<b>Marfa Boretskaya</b> was a Novgorod noblewoman, the widow of the posadnik Isaak Boretsky, whom a few lines of the chronicles turned into the symbol of the old city's resistance to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moscow's official chroniclers said that she, her sons and other opponents of the grand prince stirred up the townspeople for the Polish king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only a literary piece preserved in the Sofia First Chronicle makes her the leader of the opposition, and the Novgorod chronicles say nothing of any political part she played in the 1470s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her eldest son Dmitri was among the men beheaded on Ivan III's orders after the Novgorodian defeat on the Shelon in 1471.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians have argued over her origins, her wealth and her real influence, and the image of her owes much to fiction and drama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After 1471 Novgorod remained a republic only on the grand prince's sufferance, and when the city rejected Ivan's claim to be its sovereign rather than its lord he marched on it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Blockaded and starving, Novgorod capitulated in 1478, its assembly and the office of posadnik were abolished for ever, and the elected post of tysyatsky disappeared with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A western envoy was told that Ivan entered the city with the help of Archbishop Theophilus and carried off more than three hundred waggons of gold, silver and goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The chronicles name Marfa again only after the fall, when the grand prince had her arrested with her grandson Vasily and sent to Moscow in February 1478.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 19th century Karamzin presented the absorption of Novgorod as a historical necessity, while poets close to the Decembrists idealised the city as a republic, and plays and poems on both sides fixed the posadnitsa in memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sergei S. Pashin and Viktoriia A. Iurshina, “Marfa Boretskaia i prisoedinenie Novgoroda k Moskve,” <i>Vestnik Udmurtskogo universiteta. Seriia Istoriia i filologiia</i> 31, no. 1 (2021): 14–21, https://doi.org/10.35634/2412-9534-2021-31-1-14-21. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai V. Khaliavin, “Marfa Boretskaia v russkoi istoriografii,” <i>Vestnik Udmurtskogo universiteta. Seriia Istoriia i filologiia</i> 34, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/marfa-boretskaya-v-russkoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 216, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 218–220, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Natal’ia A. Demicheva, “Svoeobrazie istoriosofskoi pozitsii N. M. Karamzina v literaturnoi traditsii osmysleniia prisoedineniia Novgoroda k Moskve v 1471–1478 gody,” <i>Istoriko-pravovye problemy: novyi rakurs</i>, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svoeobrazie-istoriosofskoy-pozitsii-n-m-karamzina-v-literaturnoy-traditsii-osmysleniya-prisoedineniya-novgoroda-k-moskve-v-1471. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail A. Nesin, “Novgorodskie tysiatskie v poslednie desiatiletiia novgorodskoi nezavisimosti: s nachala XV v. do 1478 g.,” <i>Istoricheskaia i sotsial’no-obrazovatel’naia mysl’</i> 7, no. 8 (2015): 41–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novgorodskie-tysyatskie-v-poslednie-desyatiletiya-novgorodskoy-nezavisimosti-s-nachala-xv-v-do-1478-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 24, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow's chroniclers make so much of a widow?","a":"Because blaming her served Moscow's case. A city led astray by a scheming woman towards the Latin king made the war look like the rescue of Novgorod from treason, which suited the grand prince's claim that the city was his patrimony."},{"q":"Why is her real role so hard to establish?","a":"Because the sources disagree and none is neutral. Novgorod's own chronicles are silent, Moscow's official accounts name her among the opponents, and only one literary tale makes her the leader, so historians cannot tell how much power she actually held."},{"q":"Why did she become famous long after her death?","a":"Because writers needed a face for the end of the republic. Karamzin and later dramatists cast the fall of Novgorod as a lesson about strong monarchy, while others idealised the free city, and in both versions the posadnitsa was the obvious figure to put at its centre."}],"wiki":"Marfa Boretskaya","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-165": {"questions":["Ivan III's first wife Maria Borisovna came from the ruling house of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the upper Volga state Moscow absorbed in 1485 after the grand prince there allied with Lithuania.","A chronicle written in Tver blamed treason by the boyars for the fall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1485, since nobles from Tver had been deserting to Moscow for years before Ivan III arrived."],"abstract":"The <b>Principality of Tver</b> was a grand principality on the upper Volga, ruled by its own line of princes, whose frontier began only about 85 kilometres (53 miles) north of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its princes had been Moscow's chief rivals for the grand princely throne in the 14th century, when Dmitri of Tver killed Yuri of Moscow at the khan's court in 1325 and was put to death for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the 15th century the grand prince of Tver still ruled with his own junior princes, boyars and governors, through an administration separate from Moscow's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ivan III's first wife, Maria Borisovna, was a sister of the grand prince of Tver, a marriage that for a time bound the two houses together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Long before the end, Tver boyars and servitors had begun to move into the service of the Muscovite prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Grand Prince Mikhail Borisovich allied himself with Lithuania, Ivan treated the treaty as a pretext and marched on Tver in person, with the Italian engineer Aristotele Fioravanti in charge of his artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More boyars and two appanage princes of Tver went over to Moscow while the campaign was being prepared, and others crossed to the Muscovite camp when Ivan reached the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1485 the city surrendered without a fight and its people swore allegiance to Ivan, while Mikhail fled to Lithuania with a small following and took refuge with King Casimir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tver chronicle blamed the fall on treason by the boyars, and the princes Kholmsky went on to stand at the head of the Tver nobility at the Moscow court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within a few years the title Ivan used in letters abroad had added 'of Tver' to the list of his lands, a change that followed the events of September 1485.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 3–9, 16, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 190–191, 222, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Andrei A. Bondarenko, “Tverskie boiare Ziuziny na sluzhbe v Litve i v Moskve,” <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 49 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tverskie-boyare-zyuziny-na-sluzhbe-v-litve-i-v-moskve. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Shtykov, “Apparat upravleniia tverskikh kniazei v XV v.,” <i>Izvestiia Samarskogo nauchnogo tsentra Rossiiskoi akademii nauk</i> 13, no. 3-2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/apparat-upravleniya-tverskih-knyazey-v-xv-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ivan V. Merkulov and Nikolai V. Shtykov, “Kniaz’ia Kholmskie v sisteme politicheskoi elity Russkogo gosudarstva v kontse XV–XVI v.,” <i>Izvestiia Samarskogo nauchnogo tsentra Rossiiskoi akademii nauk</i> 18, no. 6 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/knyazya-holmskie-v-sisteme-politicheskoy-elity-russkogo-gosudarstva-v-kontse-xv-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Magdolna Agoshton, “Titul pravitelia moskovskogo gosudarstva, 1474–1533 gg.,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i>, no. 9 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/titul-pravitelya-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-1474-1533-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Tver not resist Moscow in 1485?","a":"Because its own nobles had abandoned it. Boyars and even appanage princes of Tver had been moving into Muscovite service for years, more went over as the campaign was prepared, and when Ivan reached the city it surrendered without a battle."},{"q":"Why did the alliance with Lithuania give Ivan III his opening?","a":"Because it let him present the war as a defence against a foreign enemy. A grand prince of Tver allied with Lithuania could be treated as a traitor to Rus', which turned the conquest of an old rival into a punishment."},{"q":"Why did 'of Tver' appear in Ivan's title after 1485?","a":"Because the title listed the lands the grand prince held. Once Tver was his, it joined Vladimir, Moscow, Novgorod and the rest in the list, a public record of what had been gathered."}],"wiki":"Principality of Tver","places":["city:1159149539"]},
    "ru-166": {"questions":["Archbishop Vassian of Rostov wrote to Ivan III during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, urging the hesitating grand prince to stand against Khan Akhmat's army whatever the cost.","Long taught as the end of the Tatar yoke, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1480 is questioned as a turning point by historians who note that payments to the khanates succeeding the Golden Horde continued."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Stand on the Ugra River</b> was the confrontation of autumn 1480 in which the army of Akhmat, khan of the Great Horde, and the army of Ivan III faced each other across the Ugra, a tributary of the Oka, without fighting a pitched battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan had long treated the khan's envoys with deference, but he had ceased paying the Horde's tribute, and Akhmat took the field counting on help from Casimir IV of Poland-Lithuania that never came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tatars failed to force the crossing in several days of fighting, and the two armies then spent weeks exchanging arrows and insults across the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ivan's hesitation scandalised the churchmen around him, and Archbishop Vassian of Rostov wrote him a letter urging him to stand against the godless Tatars whatever the cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In October Ivan tried to negotiate and sent gifts to the khan, but Akhmat refused them and demanded that the grand prince come in person to his stirrup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In November the khan withdrew, and Muscovite service records date his flight to the night of 6 November, soon after Ivan's estranged brothers Andrei and Boris had made peace with him and joined him with their troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicles credit the outcome to divine power rather than to either army, and an older account even describes both sides falling back from the river in panic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian historians long treated the stand as the end of the Tatar yoke, which one of them dated from 1238 to 1480, shaken off without a blow being struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some hold that Rus' paid the Horde no more tribute after 1480, while others point to payments that went on to the Tatar khanates that succeeded the Golden Horde and doubt that dependence ended at a stroke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is clearer is that the Great Horde never recovered, and the steppe after 1480 was shaped by rivalry among the successor khanates and by Moscow's alliance with Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 224–226, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 19, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Teymur R. Galimov and Il’nur M. Mirgaleev, “The Interpretation of the ‘Great Stand on the Ugra River’ in 1480,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 4 (2019): 652–662, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-4.652-662. [in Russian] [Open access]","M. A. Nesin, “K voprosu o prichine otstupleniia tatarskogo voiska posle stoianiia na Ugre,” <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i>, special issue 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-prichine-otstupleniya-tatarskogo-voyska-posle-stoyaniya-na-ugre. [in Russian] [Open access]","M. V. Moiseev, “Stepnye voiny ot Ugry do nogaiskogo pogroma Kryma, 1480–1522 gg.,” <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i>, special issue 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stepnye-voyny-ot-ugry-do-nogayskogo-pogroma-kryma-1480-1522-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vitaly V. Penskoy and Tatyana M. Penskaya, “The Post-Horde World and the Union of Ivan III and Mengli-Girey I: An Attempt to Create a ‘Bipolar’ Political System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 868–898, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.868-898. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 25, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did neither army force a battle on the Ugra?","a":"Because both had reasons to wait. The Tatars could not get across the defended river, and Ivan, who had tried to negotiate, preferred to hold the line rather than risk everything in the open, so the armies traded arrows across the water for weeks."},{"q":"Why might the reconciliation of Ivan's brothers have mattered?","a":"Because it added troops at the moment the khan was weakest. Akhmat had counted on Lithuanian help that never arrived, and once Andrei and Boris had made peace with Ivan and joined him, a battle would have been even harder to win."},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about whether 1480 ended the Tatar yoke?","a":"Because the evidence points two ways. The Horde withdrew and never threatened Moscow so seriously again, yet payments continued to the khanates that succeeded the Golden Horde, so some see a turning point and others a slower end to dependence."}],"wiki":"Great Stand on the Ugra River"},
    "ru-167": {"questions":["Khan Akhmat led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Ivan III in 1480, but after the stand on the Ugra the khanate never recovered and was destroyed by Crimea 22 years later.","Lithuanian chroniclers sympathised with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the defeat of 1502 and blamed their own rulers, whereas Russian chronicles recorded the Crimean victory in a reserved, neutral tone."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Horde</b> was the khanate that held the central steppes of the former Golden Horde in the 15th century, from the Dnieper in the west to the Volga in the east, with its capital at Sarai on the lower Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rulers called it the Great Horde, Ulugh Orda or Ulu Ulus, names that announced a claim to be the lawful heirs of the Golden Horde against the newer khanates that had broken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian name translated one version of the official name of the Jochid state, and its stress fell on the first syllable, so that it meant 'great' rather than 'greater'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The other successor states, Kazan, Crimea and the Nogai Horde, pursued their own quarrels with it and with one another, and princes of Kazan, Crimea and Sarai itself sought refuge at Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow kept the khans of Sarai at a distance, avoiding tribute while sending presents, and allied itself with Mengli Giray of Crimea, a friendship that lasted the lives of both rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Khan Akhmat's campaign of 1480 ended in the stand on the Ugra, after which the Horde's power declined, though historians disagree over whether Russian payments to the Tatar khanates really ceased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Horde had counted on an alliance with Lithuania against Moscow and Crimea, but Vilnius would not commit itself, and the Horde was left to fight both alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the autumn of 1501 Mengli Giray had the steppe burnt near Belgorod so that Khan Sheikh-Ahmed's horde had nowhere to winter, and the khan spent the harsh winter near Kyiv on the left bank of the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1502 Mengli Giray routed the Horde on the river Sula, and leadership among the heirs of the Golden Horde passed to the Crimean khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian chronicles recorded the fall in a reserved, neutral tone, while Lithuanian chronicles sympathised with the Horde and condemned their own rulers for the outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["István Vásáry, “Krymskoe khanstvo i Bol’shaia Orda, 1440–1500-e gody: bor’ba za pervenstvo,” <i>Srednevekovye tiurko-tatarskie gosudarstva</i>, no. 5 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krymskoe-hanstvo-i-bolshaya-orda-1440-1500-e-gody-borba-za-pervenstvo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vadim V. Trepavlov, “Accentology of the Takht Eli: The Great Horde or the Greater Horde?,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 166–187, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.166-187. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 223–226, 230, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","M. V. Moiseev, “Stepnye voiny ot Ugry do nogaiskogo pogroma Kryma, 1480–1522 gg.,” <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i>, special issue 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stepnye-voyny-ot-ugry-do-nogayskogo-pogroma-kryma-1480-1522-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Valentin S. Bityukov, “The Fall of the Great Horde in the Assessments of Russian and Lithuanian Sources,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2025): 183–195, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2025-13-1.183-195. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vitaly V. Penskoy and Tatyana M. Penskaya, “The Post-Horde World and the Union of Ivan III and Mengli-Girey I: An Attempt to Create a ‘Bipolar’ Political System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 868–898, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.868-898. [in Russian] [Open access]","Teymur R. Galimov and Il’nur M. Mirgaleev, “The Interpretation of the ‘Great Stand on the Ugra River’ in 1480,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 4 (2019): 652–662, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-4.652-662. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Great Horde call itself 'great'?","a":"Because the name was a claim. It held the old centre of the Golden Horde, with Sarai as its capital, and by calling itself the Great Horde it presented itself as the lawful heir against the khanates that had broken away from it."},{"q":"Why did the Crimean alliance matter so much to Moscow?","a":"Because Crimea was the Horde's most dangerous neighbour. With Mengli Giray as an ally, Moscow could avoid tribute and resist the Horde without fighting it alone, and in the end it was Crimea that destroyed the Horde in 1502."},{"q":"Why did Lithuanian and Russian chroniclers react so differently to the Horde's fall?","a":"Because they stood on different sides. Lithuania and Poland had hoped to use the Horde against Moscow and Crimea, and the final battle was fought on Lithuanian territory, while for Moscow the Horde was no longer the main danger."}],"wiki":"Great Horde"},
    "ru-168": {"questions":["Proposed by the papacy as a bride for Ivan III, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled from Rome by way of Lübeck, Reval and Pskov before the wedding of 1472.","In 1498, 26 years after marrying Ivan III, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still signed embroidery for the Trinity monastery as an imperial princess of Byzantium rather than as grand princess of Moscow."],"abstract":"<b>Sophia Palaiologina</b> was a niece of the last Byzantine emperor and the daughter of Thomas Palaiologos, who had once held wide sway in the Peloponnese and died in exile in Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The papacy, hoping for a crusade against the Turks and a union of the churches, proposed her as a bride for Ivan III, and Cardinal Bessarion is said to have advised the match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan swallowed his scruples about a princess who had lived so close to the pope and married her in 1472, after a journey that took her from Rome by Lübeck and Reval to Pskov and Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Contacts between Moscow and Italy were older than the marriage, and by the 1470s the Muscovite elite already knew Italian affairs well enough to welcome Italian skills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek émigrés came to Moscow in her wake, and Ivan's court went on to engage Italian architects, engineers and gunfounders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Boyars of the 16th century blamed her for most of the unwelcome novelties at court, and a western envoy heard that she was a very artful woman with great influence over her husband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stories credited her with shaming Ivan out of his deference to the Tatars, telling him daily that she had married a slave of the khan and persuading the khan's wife to give up the Tatars' house in the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was also said to have turned Ivan against his grandson Dmitry in favour of her own son Vasily, a contest that the crisis with Pskov in 1498 and 1499 helped to decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1498 she still signed an embroidered cloth for the Trinity monastery as imperial princess of Byzantium rather than grand princess of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later tradition also credited her marriage with Moscow's double-headed eagle, a link some scholars still defend and others trace to Karamzin's reading of the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 16–19, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 21–25, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 230–232, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Tat’iana A. Matasova, “Skladyvanie ital’ianskogo napravleniia politiki moskovskikh kniazei v XV v.,” <i>Vestnik Moskovskogo universiteta. Seriia 8. Istoriia</i>, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/skladyvanie-italyanskogo-napravleniya-politiki-moskovskih-knyazey-v-xv-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei A. Salmin, “Novogorodskie pokhody i moskovsko-pskovskii konflikt 1489/99 g. v svete ‘vizantiiskogo braka’ Ivana III,” <i>Pskovskii voenno-istoricheskii vestnik</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novogorodskie-pohody-i-moskovsko-pskovskiy-konflikt-1489-99-g-v-svete-vizantiyskogo-braka-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Pavel P. Polkh, “N. M. Karamzin i ‘vizantiiskaia versiia’ proiskhozhdeniia rossiiskogo gerba,” <i>Slovo.ru: Baltiiskii aktsent</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/n-m-karamzin-i-vizantiyskaya-versiya-proishozhdeniya-rossiyskogo-gerba. [in Russian] [Open access]","Inna A. Gvozdeva and Vladimir O. Nikishin, “Otkuda i kogda ‘priletel’ v Moskvu dvuglavyi orel?,” <i>Pravo i gosudarstvo: teoriia i praktika</i>, no. 11 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otkuda-i-kogda-priletel-v-moskvu-dvuglavyy-orel. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the papacy want Sophia to marry the grand prince of Moscow?","a":"Because Rome hoped to gain an ally. The pope wanted a crusade against the Turks and a union of the Catholic and Orthodox churches, and a Byzantine princess raised under his protection seemed a way to draw Moscow towards both."},{"q":"Why should the stories about her influence be read with care?","a":"Because many come from boyars who resented the changes at court and from a foreign envoy repeating what he heard. They mix prejudice with observation, and it is hard to tell which of Ivan's decisions were really hers."},{"q":"Why did she sign herself an imperial princess of Byzantium?","a":"Because she valued her descent from the emperors. Even decades after her marriage she presented herself as a daughter of Byzantium, which fitted Moscow's growing claim to inherit the dignity of the fallen empire."}],"wiki":"Sophia Palaiologina"},
    "ru-169": {"questions":["Moscow's coins under Ivan III named the grand prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a style implying that every land of old Rus', Lithuanian-held ones included, was his inheritance.","An inscription of 1491 on the Spassky Gate of the Moscow Kremlin calls Ivan III autocrat and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the style he made Lithuania accept three years later."],"abstract":"<b>Sovereign of all Rus'</b>, in Russian gosudar vseya Rusi, was the style by which Ivan III and his heirs claimed rule over every land that had once belonged to Rus', not merely over the principality of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grand prince of all Rus' was an old and solemn form already known in the 14th century, and from about 1484 it became compulsory in Moscow's own letters, charters and chronicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word gosudar had appeared on coins as early as 1446, when Dmitry Shemyaka styled himself sovereign of all the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coins struck for Ivan towards 1480 read 'Grand Prince Ivan Vasilyevich, sovereign of all Rus'', and an inscription of 1491 on the Kremlin's Spassky Gate calls him sovereign and autocrat of all Rus'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title implied that the grand prince's power over every part of Rus' was a patrimony inherited from his forefathers, a claim that pointed straight at the Russian lands held by Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The conquest of Novgorod in 1471 was the turning point, and the official account of that war repeatedly names Ivan grand prince of all Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Novgorod Ivan later seized on the envoys' use of gosudar in place of the customary gospodin, 'lord', turning a slip of wording into a claim of sovereignty in law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1494 he made the government of Lithuania write the title into the peace treaty of that year, the first time it was displayed so openly to a foreign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In dealings with lesser Baltic powers he also called himself tsar, and in 1493 a Danish envoy was persuaded to put the word, as imperator, into the grand prince's full title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A western envoy who reached Moscow under his son summed up the process by saying that Ivan first called himself grand prince of Vladimir, Moscow and Novgorod and finally monarch of all Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Magdolna Agoshton, “Titul pravitelia moskovskogo gosudarstva, 1474–1533 gg.,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i>, no. 9 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/titul-pravitelya-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-1474-1533-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 19–21, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 219–220, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Carsten Pape, “Titul Ivana III po datskim istochnikam pozdnego Srednevekov’ia,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2016): 65–75, https://doi.org/10.21638/11701/spbu19.2016.205. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Chronicle of Novgorod, 1016–1471</i>, trans. Robert Michell and Nevill Forbes, Camden Third Series 25 (London: Offices of the Society, 1914), 205–219, https://archive.org/details/chronicleofnovgo00michrich. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Aleksandr B. Kamenskii, “‘Ottorzhennaia vozvratikh’: razdely Pol’shi i kontseptsiia sobiraniia russkikh zemel’,” <i>Trudy po rossievedeniiu</i>, no. 6 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ottorzhennaya-vozvratih-razdely-polshi-i-kontseptsiya-sobiraniya-russkih-zemel. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the words 'of all Rus'' worry Lithuania?","a":"Because much of old Rus' lay inside the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. A ruler who called himself sovereign of all Rus' was claiming those lands as his inheritance, so accepting the title in a treaty meant accepting the claim behind it."},{"q":"Why was the conquest of Novgorod a turning point for the title?","a":"Because it was the first great war Moscow fought in the name of the grand prince's patrimony. The official account of 1471 used the full style again and again, and the new outward-facing title grew from it."},{"q":"Why did Ivan call himself tsar only with some rulers?","a":"Because the word claimed equality with an emperor. He used it with lesser Baltic powers, which could hardly object, and persuaded a Danish envoy to accept it, while avoiding it with the greater sovereigns of Europe."}]},
    "ru-170": {"questions":["Popularly credited to Ivan III's Byzantine marriage, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Moscow's seals may instead answer the Holy Roman Empire's emblem after Ivan refused a royal crown from the emperor's envoy.","Traced back to Hittite art of the 13th century BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later served the Holy Roman Empire, appeared on Golden Horde coins and sealed the charters of Ivan III."],"abstract":"The <b>double-headed eagle</b> is an ancient image of an eagle with two heads facing opposite ways, traced back to Hittite monumental art of the 13th century BCE and later found in Egypt, Sumer and Assyria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Christian Europe it spread in the 12th and 13th centuries and became the emblem of the Holy Roman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Islamic world it appears in Córdoba in the 11th and 12th centuries, in the Seljuk state, on 14th-century coins of the khans of the Golden Horde and in the Crimean khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Russia its earliest known appearance is the red wax seal attached to a charter of Ivan III of 1497, which bears a horseman spearing a dragon on one side and a crowned double-headed eagle on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the close of the 15th century the eagle was impressed on the seals of the grand prince's state documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Since the 19th century the usual explanation has linked it to Ivan's marriage in 1472 to Sophia Palaiologina, niece of the last Byzantine emperor, a version Karamzin did much to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics answer that the Byzantine Empire had no coat of arms in the western sense and that the seal's matrix was cut no earlier than 1490, so that there was nothing to borrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Others date the new seal to 1490 and tie it to the imperial envoy Nicholas Poppel, who offered Ivan a royal crown that he refused, so that the eagle answered the Holy Roman Empire's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same writers reject a Golden Horde origin for the Russian eagle, although the image is attested on the khans' coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The eagle outlived the grand princes, appearing on Catherine II's medal for the partitions of Poland with maps of the annexed lands in its claws, and in 1993 a golden double-headed eagle became the arms of the Russian Federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Oleg I. Zakutnov, “Gosudarstvennye emblemy v russkoi srednevekovoi sfragistike na primere pechati 1497 g.,” <i>Kaspiiskii region: politika, ekonomika, kul’tura</i>, no. 4 (2019): 9–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvennye-emblemy-v-russkoy-srednevekovoy-sfragistike-na-primere-pechati-1497-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 21, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 231–232, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Pavel P. Polkh, “N. M. Karamzin i ‘vizantiiskaia versiia’ proiskhozhdeniia rossiiskogo gerba,” <i>Slovo.ru: Baltiiskii aktsent</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/n-m-karamzin-i-vizantiyskaya-versiya-proishozhdeniya-rossiyskogo-gerba. [in Russian] [Open access]","Inna A. Gvozdeva and Vladimir O. Nikishin, “Otkuda i kogda ‘priletel’ v Moskvu dvuglavyi orel?,” <i>Pravo i gosudarstvo: teoriia i praktika</i>, no. 11 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otkuda-i-kogda-priletel-v-moskvu-dvuglavyy-orel. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nailia N. Khalitova and Niiaz Kh. Khalitov, “Dvukhglavyi orel v islamskom i tiurko-tatarskom srednevekovom iskusstve,” <i>Srednevekovye tiurko-tatarskie gosudarstva</i>, no. 5 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvuhglavyy-orel-v-islamskom-i-tyurko-tatarskom-srednevekovom-iskusstve. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr B. Kamenskii, “‘Ottorzhennaia vozvratikh’: razdely Pol’shi i kontseptsiia sobiraniia russkikh zemel’,” <i>Trudy po rossievedeniiu</i>, no. 6 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ottorzhennaya-vozvratih-razdely-polshi-i-kontseptsiya-sobiraniya-russkih-zemel. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a seal of 1497 matter so much to the history of the emblem?","a":"Because it is the earliest known Russian example. It joins the horseman spearing a dragon and the double-headed eagle on one state seal, the pairing that later Russian arms kept."},{"q":"Why is the Byzantine explanation disputed?","a":"Because Byzantium had no coat of arms in the western sense, and the seal may have been cut around 1490, years after the marriage. Critics therefore doubt that there was a Byzantine emblem to inherit."},{"q":"Why might the eagle have been a reply to the Holy Roman Emperor?","a":"Because Ivan had refused a royal crown offered on the emperor's behalf. A royal crown would have made him a lesser ruler, while the double-headed eagle, the emblem of the empire itself, put him on the emperor's level."}],"wiki":"Double-headed eagle"},
    "ru-171": {"questions":["An imperial envoy printed the fees owed to judges, clerks and duel officials under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a code that the chroniclers of its own day barely mentioned.","Of 163 court records surviving from the half century before 1550, only 30 cite the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which survives in just one manuscript copied decades after it was approved."],"abstract":"The <b>Sudebnik of 1497</b> was the code of court procedure that Ivan III approved with his boyars in September 1497, and it has traditionally been called the first law code of the united Russian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its red heading announced rules on how the boyars and okolnichie, the two highest ranks at court, were to judge, and its first article ordered that a clerk sit beside every judge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the text fixes the fees owed to judges and clerks, and an imperial envoy later printed these rules, the fees for a judicial duel among them, in his book on Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Penalties were severe, for a thief caught a second time who could not satisfy his accuser was to die, as were church robbers, kidnappers and men who killed their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its article on suits over land deals with disputes between taxpaying peasants and men holding the grand prince's land for service, although the code never uses or defines the word pomestie itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> One article fixed when a peasant might leave his landlord, the week before and after the autumn feast of St George on 26 November, once the harvest was in and accounts could be settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The code asked only for that date and a payment on leaving, and its successor of 1550, which began as a revision of it, added new conditions to the rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far the code reached is debated, since chroniclers hardly mentioned it, it survives in one manuscript copied decades later, and of 163 surviving court records from before 1550 only 30 cite it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One reading holds that it was meant to organise justice only in the grand prince's own domain, the districts of 53 towns, whose servitors and taxpaying peasants it placed under the boyars and okolnichie alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another treats it as an ordinary charter of its time, unusual only in that many of the prince's officials helped to draft it, and doubts that it shaped the courts at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 102–4, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 74, 200, 235, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","V. A. Kuchkin, “Notes Regarding the Sudebnik of Ivan III of 1497,” <i>Drevnyaya Rus’. Voprosy medievistiki</i>, no. 2 (80) (2020): 166–70, https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2020.80.2.0012. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tatyana M. Penskaya and Alexander V. Penskoy, “The Judicial Book of Ivan III – a Monument of the Law of the Centralised Russian State?,” <i>NOMOTHETIKA: Philosophy. Sociology. Law</i> 47, no. 3 (2022): 525–27, https://doi.org/10.52575/2712-746X-2022-47-3-525-538. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. A. Shatalov, “Main Issues of the Doctrinal Study of the Judicial Code of 1497: Critical Analysis,” <i>Actual Problems of Russian Law</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): 22–29, https://doi.org/10.17803/1994-1471.2023.146.1.022-029. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. V. Zazolina, “‘Pomest’e,’ ‘Votchina,’ ‘Estate,’ ‘Dacha,’ ‘Land Plot’ in the Conceptual Apparatus of 16th–18th Century Legislative Acts: A Historical-Legal Analysis,” <i>Siberian Law Review</i> 22, no. 3 (2025): 375–87, https://doi.org/10.19073/2658-7602-2025-22-3-375-387. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the code fix a single week for peasants to leave?","a":"Because the land and the labour on it ran on the farming year. The rule set the week before and after the autumn feast of St George, when the harvest was in and landlord and peasant could settle their accounts, and it asked only for that date and a payment on leaving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about how important the code was?","a":"Because the evidence of its use is thin. It survives in one manuscript copied decades later, the chroniclers barely noticed it, and only 30 of 163 surviving court records from before 1550 cite it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One reading limits it to the grand prince's own domain, and another sees an ordinary charter that hardly changed how courts worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the code say so much about fees?","a":"Because a judgment was also a source of income for the men who gave it. The code set what the judge, the clerk and the officials of a judicial duel were each to receive, and an imperial envoy found the tariff worth printing for his readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study argues that the fees now went to the judges and their clerks rather than to the prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sudebnik of 1497"},
    "ru-172": {"questions":["Held for life and only while the holder served the grand prince in war, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> differed from the votchina, which passed by inheritance and was owned outright.","After 1489 the younger members of noble families received a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the confiscated lands of Novgorod, and these deti boyarskie became the core of the Muscovite cavalry."],"abstract":"A <b>pomestie</b> was an estate granted by the Muscovite ruler to a man in return for military service, held only while he served and usually only for his lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That conditional, personal tenure set it apart from the votchina, the hereditary estate held outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law code of 1497 neither uses nor defines the word pomestie, yet one of its articles already settles land suits brought by and against men who held the grand prince's land on these terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the 1580s a register office kept the record of every grant of land made for service, and a young man of a serving family presented himself on coming of age to be enrolled and allotted land, usually his father's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English envoy complained that the land set aside for the army was fixed, so that a soldier with many sons often had only one of them provided for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Grants on this pattern spread fast after the annexation of Novgorod, whose boyars were deported and whose confiscated lands were handed out to Ivan III's men in the 1480s and 1490s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A survey of 1500 found 106 pomestie holders in just two districts of the Vod fifth of Novgorod, with some 3,000 homesteads on their land and more than half the arable land of those districts in their hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grants of 1484 to 1489 went to the grand prince's closest princely and boyar circle, but after 1489 the land went to the younger members of noble houses, the deti boyarskie, who became the core of the pomestie cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The holdings were small, commonly 20 to 30 obzhi, a Novgorod unit of taxed land, against the 500 to 1,000 of a great boyar estate, and averaged about 22 peasant households.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians still argue over where the system came from, some pointing to the Ottoman timar, others to Byzantine law, and others to the conditional land of the household servants of the earlier princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 121–27, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 40, 70–71, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","I. L. Lunden, “To the History of Economic Transformations in Russia: The Agrarian Reform of Ivan III (1462–1505) and Its Value for Formation of the Russian Statehood,” <i>Vestnik Instituta ekonomiki Rossiiskoi akademii nauk</i>, no. 5 (2014): 176–85, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-ekonomicheskih-preobrazovaniy-v-rossii-agrarnaya-reforma-ivana-iii-1462-1505-i-ee-znachenie-dlya-stanovleniya-rossiyskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander L. Korzinin, “‘Tsar of All the Russias’ Ivan III and the Russian Nobility,” <i>Istoricheskii format</i>, no. 1 (5) (2016): 174–76, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudar-vseya-rusi-ivan-iii-i-russkaya-aristokratiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. V. Zazolina, “‘Pomest’e,’ ‘Votchina,’ ‘Estate,’ ‘Dacha,’ ‘Land Plot’ in the Conceptual Apparatus of 16th–18th Century Legislative Acts: A Historical-Legal Analysis,” <i>Siberian Law Review</i> 22, no. 3 (2025): 375–87, https://doi.org/10.19073/2658-7602-2025-22-3-375-387. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. A. Kuchkin, “Notes Regarding the Sudebnik of Ivan III of 1497,” <i>Drevnyaya Rus’. Voprosy medievistiki</i>, no. 2 (80) (2020): 167, https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2020.80.2.0012. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why give land instead of paying soldiers in money?","a":"Because land was what the grand prince had in plenty, above all after he confiscated the estates of Novgorod. A grant held only for life and only while its holder served tied the soldier to the ruler and supported him from the peasants on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the pomestie different from a votchina?","a":"Because it was conditional. A votchina was a hereditary estate owned outright, while a pomestie was held for service and usually only for life, so it could be taken back when the service stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some sons of soldiers end up with nothing?","a":"Because the land assigned to the army was fixed. An English envoy of the 1580s saw that when it was all allotted, a soldier with many sons might have only one of them enrolled and provided for, and the rest were left to fend for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-173": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Muscovy were divided into those who served by birth, the dvoriane and deti boyarskie, and those recruited for pay, such as musketeers and gunners.","From the middle of the 15th century a military servitor could no longer leave Moscow for another prince, and with household servants he merged into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that served the ruler for land."],"abstract":"The <b>service class people</b> of Muscovy were the free men obliged to serve the state in war or in its administration, the class on which the grand princes built their army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those who served by birth, the dvoriane and the deti boyarskie, were distinguished from those recruited for pay, such as musketeers and gunners, and in a narrower sense the term meant the first group alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The deti boyarskie are first named in a document of 1428 to 1432, and the law code of 1497 already treated them as a recognised group among the grand prince's subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Under the appanage princes a free military servitor could leave one prince for another without losing his estates, but from the middle of the 15th century that right disappeared and service to Moscow became compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Household servants and free men-at-arms merged into one body of military retainers, all holding land on condition of service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An imperial envoy at the court of Vasily III wrote that whoever was summoned to court, to war or on an embassy had to go at his own expense, apart from poorer young nobles who were paid a small fixed stipend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every second or third year the prince counted the sons of boyars in each province with their horses and serfs, and each year he posted some 20,000 men on the Don and Oka against Tatar raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ivan III's court drew princes and nobles from formerly independent principalities into one hierarchy, and an older view held that he set out to create a middling serviceman dependent on himself alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the 1580s an English envoy described every soldier as a gentleman by birth and every gentleman as a soldier, enrolled at the military office on coming of age and given land to support himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Records of this service are thin, and one of the fullest pictures of a field army comes from lists of 193 servicemen captured at Orsha in 1514, traced through genealogies and land registers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 30–31, 95–96, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 70–71, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 122–25, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Maria Igorevna Balykina and Ekaterina Sergeevna Grishakova, “Peculiarities of Russian-to-English Translation of Terms Related to the History of the Service Class of Russia in the XVI–XVII Centuries,” <i>Philology. Theory &amp; Practice</i> 15, no. 12 (2022): 3938–42, https://doi.org/10.30853/phil20220646. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander L. Korzinin, “Gosudarev dvor Ivana III,” <i>Paleorossiya. Drevnyaya Rus’ vo vremeni, v lichnostyakh, v ideyakh</i>, no. 7 (2017): 134, 146, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarev-dvor-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. A. Kuchkin, “Notes Regarding the Sudebnik of Ivan III of 1497,” <i>Drevnyaya Rus’. Voprosy medievistiki</i>, no. 2 (80) (2020): 167, https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2020.80.2.0012. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksei N. Lobin and Mikhail M. Bentsianov, “K voprosu o strukture russkoi armii v bitve pri Orshe,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (14) (2013): 155–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-strukture-russkoy-armii-v-bitve-pri-orshe. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did men of this class serve at their own expense?","a":"Because service was owed, not hired. An imperial envoy found that a man summoned to court, to war or on an embassy had to pay his own way, and only poorer young nobles drew a small fixed stipend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In return the class held land on condition of service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could a servitor no longer change masters?","a":"Because Moscow had swallowed the other principalities. Under the appanage princes a free servitor could move to another prince and keep his estates, but once Moscow ruled the north-east there was nowhere left to go, and service became compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are lists of prisoners useful evidence?","a":"Because the ordinary records of Muscovite service are thin. The names of 193 servicemen captured at Orsha in 1514, traced through genealogies and land registers, show where a field army came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Service class people"},
    "ru-174": {"questions":["The stone palace inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began in 1487 with the Palace of Facets, a hall for state receptions laid out by the Italian known in Moscow as Marco Fryazin.","Ivan Kalita moved his residence into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the urging of the metropolitan Peter, and a 16th-century envoy thought it large enough to pass for a city."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Kremlin</b> is the walled citadel above the meeting of the Moskva and Neglinnaya rivers, the seat of the grand princes and tsars of Muscovy and later of the Soviet and Russian heads of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A 16th-century visitor found that it held the prince's stone palace, the houses of the metropolitan, the prince's brothers and the nobles, and many churches, so that it might almost be taken for a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had once been small and mean, and it became the princely seat under Ivan Kalita, who moved his residence there at the urging of the metropolitan Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its present walls, 2,235 metres (about 1.4 miles) long and between 5 and 19 metres (16 and 62 feet) high, were built of brick under Italian masters from 1485 to about 1495.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An English envoy of the 1580s described the city as ringed by three walls, the innermost of which enclosed the emperor's castle, and recorded that a Tatar army had burnt the town in 1571.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rebuilding began in 1485 with the Taynitskaya gate tower on the river side, and the remaining gate towers, the Spasskaya among them, went up in 1490 to 1492 under Pietro Antonio Solari.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ivan III, newly married to Sophia Palaiologina, found his old quarters in the Kremlin mean and narrow, and sent for Italians to build a new cathedral, a stone palace and a hall for state receptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The palace was begun in 1487 with the Palace of Facets, laid out by the Italian called Marco Fryazin in Moscow, and its main block was built in 1499 to 1508 by Aloisio da Carcano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The new brick walls replaced the old white-stone ones section by section, so that the defences were never weakened while the work went on, and how much the Italians changed Russian fortification is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Italians were hired with large rewards, and their citadel gave the capital of a newly united state a setting its decaying white-stone fortress could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 16–17, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Konstantin S. Nosov, “The Defenses of the Moscow Kremlin in the Reign of Ivan III: A Historical and Architectural Essay,” <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 10 (2020): 62–74, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oboronitelnye-sooruzheniya-moskovskogo-kremlya-epohi-pravleniya-ivana-iii-istoriko-arhitekturnyy-ocherk. [in Russian] [Open access]","Konstantin S. Nosov, “Italians and ‘Italianisms’ in Russian Military Architecture: The Contribution of Foreign Masters to the Building of Fortresses and Other Defensive Installations of the Grand Duchy of Moscovy,” <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (2020): 84–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/italyantsy-i-italyanizmy-v-russkom-voennom-zodchestve-vklad-inostrannyh-masterov-v-stroitelstvo-krepostey-i-drugih-oboronitelnyh. [in Russian] [Open access]","P. A. Aleshin, “Fortifications of the Moscow Kremlin and Architectural Theory in 15th Century Italy,” <i>Bulletin of the Russian Medieval Art Department</i>, no. 1 (2025): 48–49, https://doi.org/10.51678/2658-543x-2025-1-48-63. [in Russian] [Open access]","Irina A. Maslennikova, “The ‘Secret Room’ of the Kremlin Palace of Facets: Historical Mythology and Realities,” <i>Vestnik Moskovskogo universiteta. Seriya 8: Istoriya</i>, no. 3 (2018): 3–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/taynik-granovitoy-palaty-bolshogo-kremlevskogo-dvortsa-istoricheskaya-mifologiya-i-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tatyana V. Chernikova, “Nachalo evropeizatsii Rossii vo vremena Ivana III,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i>, no. 5 (2011): 107–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalo-evropeizatsiya-rossii-vo-vremena-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oxana Mushtanova, “Aristotele Fioravanti and His Role in Formation of Moscow Kremlin Ensemble in the XV–XVI Centuries,” <i>Manuscript</i> 14, no. 2 (2021): 393–98, https://doi.org/10.30853/mns210028. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the old walls replaced bit by bit rather than all at once?","a":"Because Moscow could not afford to be left without a fortress. The brick walls went up in place of the white-stone ones section by section, so that the defences were never weakened while the work went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Building started in 1485 with a gate tower on the river side and went round the circuit over the next decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ivan III bring in Italian builders?","a":"Because the old Kremlin no longer suited the ruler he had become. Newly married to Sophia Palaiologina, he found his quarters mean and narrow and sent for Italians to build a new cathedral and a stone palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were offered large rewards to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a visitor think the Kremlin looked like a city?","a":"Because so much lay inside it. Besides the prince's stone palace it held the houses of the metropolitan, the prince's brothers and the nobles and many churches, all within one wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kremlin"},
    "ru-175": {"questions":["Muscovite rulers were crowned from 1547 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where an English envoy of the 1580s described the patriarch seating the new tsar beside him on a raised stage.","The metropolitans who brought the seat of the Russian church to Moscow lie buried in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which kept a synodikon naming the princely dead to be remembered in prayer."],"abstract":"The <b>Dormition Cathedral</b> in the Moscow Kremlin is the church of the Dormition of the Mother of God that Aristotele Fioravanti built in 1475 to 1479, for centuries the principal church of Muscovite Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first church on the site, of white stone, was founded in 1326, and the metropolitan Peter, who had resolved to move the seat of the head of the church to Moscow, took part in laying its foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 16th-century visitor noted that the churchmen whose coming had brought the metropolitan see to Moscow were buried in it and venerated there as saints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rulers were crowned in it from 1547, and an English envoy of the 1580s described a coronation on a stage raised inside the church, where the patriarch seated the new ruler beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cathedral also kept a synodikon, a list of the dead to be named in prayer, whose entries for princely families are now evidence for genealogy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan III first gave the rebuilding to the Pskov masters Krivtsov and Myshkin, but in 1474, three years into the work, their church fell down, perhaps in an earthquake or because their mortar was too thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fioravanti pulled down the ruins in under a week, drove oak piles into the foundation trenches, and built in white stone with brick for the vaults and columns, from a kiln that made a thinner, longer and harder brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was told to take the cathedral at Vladimir as his model, and he kept its five domes, white stone and arcaded band while making the inside a single bright hall with round columns and aisles of equal height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His proportions were laid out with compass and rule on a grid of equal squares, a geometry that has been traced to the architectural thought of Leon Battista Alberti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cathedral headed Ivan III's whole building programme, which included two more churches of the Dormition, at the Joseph-Volokolamsk monastery in 1484 and the Kirillo-Belozersky monastery in 1497.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 6, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 23–24, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Oxana Mushtanova, “Aristotele Fioravanti and His Role in Formation of Moscow Kremlin Ensemble in the XV–XVI Centuries,” <i>Manuscript</i> 14, no. 2 (2021): 393–98, https://doi.org/10.30853/mns210028. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oxana Mushtanova, “Aristotele Fioravanti: Renaissance Genius in the Context of Medieval Russia,” <i>Manuscript</i> 14, no. 3 (2021): 572–78, https://doi.org/10.30853/mns210099. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Zabelin and A. V. Radzyukevich, “The Fioravanti Cathedral of the Dormition as an Idealized Morphotype of the Vladimir-Suzdal and Moscow Architectural Schools,” <i>Vestnik Tomskogo gosudarstvennogo arkhitekturno-stroitel’nogo universiteta</i> 28, no. 5 (2026): 103–22, https://doi.org/10.31675/1607-1859-2026-28-5-103-122. [in Russian] [Open access]","Artemii V. Zabelin, “The Symbolic Features of Ivan III’s Building Program,” <i>Gumanitarnye nauki v Sibiri</i> 29, no. 3 (2022): 62–74, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/simvolicheskie-osobennosti-stroitelnoy-programmy-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oleg Khoruzhenko, “The Last Princes of Tarusa in the Synodikon of the Moscow Cathedral of the Dormition,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 9, no. 3 (2021): 877–85, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2021.3.615. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first rebuilding fail?","a":"Because the church put up by the Pskov masters Krivtsov and Myshkin fell down in 1474, three years into the work. One explanation is an earthquake, another that their mortar was too thin to hold the rubble-filled walls together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the cathedral look old-fashioned outside but feel new inside?","a":"Because Fioravanti was told to copy the cathedral at Vladimir and did so only on the surface. He kept its five domes, white stone and arcaded band, but inside he made one bright hall with round columns and aisles of equal height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was this church so important to Muscovite rulers?","a":"Because it was the church of the metropolitans and later of coronations. The churchmen who brought the see to Moscow were buried there, and rulers were crowned in it from 1547.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dormition Cathedral, Moscow"},
    "ru-176": {"questions":["A Venetian envoy at Ivan III's court noted the Bolognese builder <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raising the church on the square, a master who also cast the grand prince's cannon.","Disgraced in 1483 and lost from the record after 1485, the engineer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had earlier built a bridge of boats over the Volkhov for Ivan III's army."],"abstract":"<b>Aristotele Fioravanti</b> was a Bolognese engineer and architect who came to Moscow in 1475 and built the Dormition Cathedral in the Kremlin, the first Italian architect invited to Ivan III's court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Italy he was famous as the master who moved towers, having shifted the bell tower of Santa Maria del Tempio in Bologna, about 25 metres (82 feet) high, by some 13 metres (43 feet) in 1455.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also raised bells, dug navigable canals in Lombardy for Francesco Sforza, straightened a leaning tower at Mantua and built bridges over the Danube for Matthias Corvinus of Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet almost no building in Italy can be securely given to him, and his Italian career was that of an engineer far more than an architect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A charge of making false coin at Bologna, later dropped, may have been one reason why he took up the offer brought by the Muscovite envoy Semyon Tolbuzin, a salary of ten roubles a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Venetian envoy in Moscow named among the westerners at court a master Aristotle of Bologna, a builder who was raising the church on the square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Besides the cathedral he cast cannon and went with Ivan III's army in charge of the guns, building a bridge of boats over the Volkhov in the campaign against Novgorod in 1478.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A silver coin of Ivan III carries the Latin name Orristoteles, which suggests that he struck coin in Moscow as he had been allowed to do in Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1483 he fell into disgrace, his property was seized and he was put under guard, and after the campaign against Tver in 1485 nothing more is heard of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some historians credit him with the plan for rebuilding the Kremlin's walls, a view others doubt, and one study holds that he did not live to see that rebuilding begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Oxana Mushtanova, “Aristotele Fioravanti and His Role in Formation of Moscow Kremlin Ensemble in the XV–XVI Centuries,” <i>Manuscript</i> 14, no. 2 (2021): 393–98, https://doi.org/10.30853/mns210028. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oxana Mushtanova, “Aristotele Fioravanti: Renaissance Genius in the Context of Medieval Russia,” <i>Manuscript</i> 14, no. 3 (2021): 572–78, https://doi.org/10.30853/mns210099. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tatyana V. Chernikova, “Nachalo evropeizatsii Rossii vo vremena Ivana III,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i>, no. 5 (2011): 107–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalo-evropeizatsiya-rossii-vo-vremena-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Zabelin and A. V. Radzyukevich, “The Fioravanti Cathedral of the Dormition as an Idealized Morphotype of the Vladimir-Suzdal and Moscow Architectural Schools,” <i>Vestnik Tomskogo gosudarstvennogo arkhitekturno-stroitel’nogo universiteta</i> 28, no. 5 (2026): 103–22, https://doi.org/10.31675/1607-1859-2026-28-5-103-122. [in Russian] [Open access]","Konstantin S. Nosov, “The Defenses of the Moscow Kremlin in the Reign of Ivan III: A Historical and Architectural Essay,” <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 10 (2020): 62–74, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oboronitelnye-sooruzheniya-moskovskogo-kremlya-epohi-pravleniya-ivana-iii-istoriko-arhitekturnyy-ocherk. [in Russian] [Open access]","Artemii V. Zabelin, “The Symbolic Features of Ivan III’s Building Program,” <i>Gumanitarnye nauki v Sibiri</i> 29, no. 3 (2022): 62–74, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/simvolicheskie-osobennosti-stroitelnoy-programmy-ivana-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a famous Italian engineer go to Moscow at all?","a":"Because the offer was generous and his position at home was uneasy. The Muscovite envoy Semyon Tolbuzin promised ten roubles a month, and a charge of making false coin at Bologna, later dropped, may have helped him decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His famous feats had been engineering ones, moving towers and digging canals, more than buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he useful to Ivan III beyond the cathedral?","a":"Because an engineer could do what an architect could not. He cast cannon, went on campaign in charge of the guns and built a bridge of boats over the Volkhov for the march on Novgorod in 1478.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it uncertain whether he planned the Kremlin walls?","a":"Because the evidence is circumstantial. Some historians give him the plan, others doubt it, and one study holds that he did not live to see the rebuilding begin, since he vanishes from the record after 1485.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Aristotele Fioravanti"},
    "ru-177": {"questions":["Ivan III's declaration of war on Alexander of Lithuania, dated 24 June 1500, was issued months into a new round of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that defecting borderland princes had set off.","Claiming to be heir to the whole Russian land, Ivan III kept Bryansk, Starodub and Toropets at the truce of 1503, which paused the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for six years."],"abstract":"The <b>Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars</b> were a series of wars between the grand princes of Moscow and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania over the lands of the old Rus' that lay between them, fought most often from the 1480s to the 1530s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both of Ivan III's wars arose from the defection of princes of the Chernigov borderland to Moscow, and the first ended in 1494 with a peace that ceded Vyazma and Odoev and with the marriage of Ivan's daughter Helena to Alexander of Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow met Lithuanian protests by claiming that the rulers of Poland and Lithuania held their lands only from their fathers, whereas Ivan was heir to the whole Russian land from the beginning, a claim he repeated at the peace of 1503.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Vasily III the fighting resumed and Smolensk was taken in 1514, but in September of that year a Lithuanian army under Konstanty Ostrogski routed the Muscovites near Orsha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The names of 193 Muscovite servicemen taken prisoner at Orsha survive, and tracing them suggests that the scale of the battle has been exaggerated in Polish and Belarusian accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The war of 1500 to 1503 began with another wave of defecting princes, and Ivan III's declaration of war, dated 24 June 1500, was sent months after the fighting had started.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its grievances were those that Muscovite diplomats had pressed since the eternal peace of 1494, including Alexander's treatment of his wife Helena, Ivan's daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 14 July 1500 the Muscovites crushed a Lithuanian army on the little river Vedrosha, using a force held in ambush, and took its commander Ostrogski prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The truce of 1503, made for six years, left Moscow with Starodub, Novgorod-Seversky, Bryansk, Dorogobuzh, Toropets and Belaya, by one count nearly a third of Lithuania's Rus' lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The last war of the series broke out in 1534, after Vasily III's death, when Sigismund of Poland and Lithuania set out to win back the lands lost in the decades before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 22–29, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 13–15, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Sergey V. Polekhov and Catherine R. Squires, “Casus belli. Ivan the Third’s Declaration of War to Aleksander Jagiellończyk, June 24th, 1500,” <i>Slověne</i> 10, no. 1 (2021): 262–95, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2021.10.1.12. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nataliia Tokar, “Formation of Principles of Expansionist Policy of the Moscow Principality on Ukrainian Lands during the Moscow-Lithuanian Wars 1487–1494 and 1500–1503,” <i>Scientific Bulletin of the Izmail State University of Humanities. Historical Sciences</i>, no. 59 (2022): 172–81, https://doi.org/10.31909/26168774.2022-(59)-16. [in Ukrainian] [Open access]","Aleksei N. Lobin and Mikhail M. Bentsianov, “K voprosu o strukture russkoi armii v bitve pri Orshe,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (14) (2013): 155–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-strukture-russkoy-armii-v-bitve-pri-orshe. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vyacheslav V. Shaposhnik, “Bor’ba za vlast’ v Moskve posle smerti Vasiliya III i vneshnyaya politika Russkogo gosudarstva,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (15) (2014): 27–37, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-za-vlast-v-moskve-posle-smerti-vasiliya-iii-i-vneshnyaya-politika-russkogo-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did these wars keep breaking out?","a":"Because the border never settled. Princes of the Chernigov borderland kept going over to Moscow with their lands, and each wave of defections gave Ivan III a reason to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peace of 1494 left the frontier on the Oka undefined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ivan III call Lithuanian towns his own inheritance?","a":"Because Moscow had begun to claim the whole of the old Rus'. Its reply to Lithuanian protests was that the rulers of Poland and Lithuania held their lands only from their fathers, while Ivan was heir to the Russian land from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the declaration of war of 1500 sent so late?","a":"Because the fighting had already begun. The document, which survives in a Low German translation at Lübeck, gathers complaints Muscovite diplomats had made since 1494 and was issued months after the war started.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars"},
    "ru-178": {"questions":["Genoese envoys sent in 1481 to urge the Crimean khan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to rise against the Ottomans received a reply in Greek, the only Greek document known from a 15th-century Crimean chancery.","Crimean raids on Lithuania during the war of 1500 to 1503 showed the worth of Moscow's alliance with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose son was raiding Moscow's own borderlands within a decade."],"abstract":"<b>Mengli Giray</b> was khan of the Crimean Tatars in the late 15th and early 16th centuries, whose alliance with Ivan III became the axis of the order that replaced the Golden Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The khans of Crimea traced their descent from the rulers of the Horde on the Volga, and an imperial envoy at the court of Vasily III noted that the two houses had long been at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Ottomans took the Genoese ports of Crimea he ruled under Ottoman overlordship, and in December 1481 Genoese envoys came to urge him to rise against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reply to them is the only known document in Greek produced by the chancery of a 15th-century Crimean ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also wrote to the khans of Kazan, and a letter of his to Muhammad Amin, in Tatar, Ottoman Turkish, Arabic and Persian, survives in a late 16th-century Ottoman copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan III first sent an embassy to him in 1472, after Khan Akhmat of the Great Horde had marched against Moscow, proposing an alliance against Akhmat and against Casimir IV of Poland and Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty made in April 1480 bound the two rulers to stand together against Akhmat and against the king, each moving against whichever of them attacked the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How much Crimean help counted at the Ugra later that year is disputed, though Crimean raids on the king's southern lands may have kept him from aiding Akhmat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the Muscovite–Lithuanian war of 1500 to 1503 Crimean forces raided Lithuania again and again, while Moscow sent troops to help the khan against the Great Horde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Great Horde broke up in 1502 after its army was routed, but once the common enemy was gone Crimean forces, one of them led by his son, raided Moscow's borderlands, and relations worsened further after his death in 1515.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 77–80, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","Yu. V. Zelenskii, “Vzaimootnosheniya Moskovskogo gosudarstva i Krymskogo khanstva: ot sotrudnichestva k vrazhde,” <i>Srednevekovye tyurko-tatarskie gosudarstva</i>, no. 4 (2012): 198–200, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzaimootnosheniya-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-i-krymskogo-hanstva-ot-sotrudnichestva-k-vrazhde. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vitaly V. Penskoy and Tatyana M. Penskaya, “The Post-Horde World and the Union of Ivan III and Mengli-Girey I: An Attempt to Create a ‘Bipolar’ Political System,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 10, no. 4 (2022): 868–98, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2022-10-4.868-898. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladislav P. Gulevich and Aleksandr V. Dzhanov, “A Letter of the Crimean Khan Mengli Giray to the Genoese Emissaries Bartolomeo Campofregoso and Lodizio Fieschi of December 30, 1481,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 318–32, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-2.318-332. [in Russian] [Open access]","Serkan Acar, “On the Letter Sent by the Crimean Khan Mengli Giray to Kazan Khan Muhammad Amin,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 14, no. 2 (2026): 368–84, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2026-14-2.368-384. [Open access]","I. L. Lunden, “To the History of Economic Transformations in Russia: The Agrarian Reform of Ivan III (1462–1505) and Its Value for Formation of the Russian Statehood,” <i>Vestnik Instituta ekonomiki Rossiiskoi akademii nauk</i>, no. 5 (2014): 176, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-ekonomicheskih-preobrazovaniy-v-rossii-agrarnaya-reforma-ivana-iii-1462-1505-i-ee-znachenie-dlya-stanovleniya-rossiyskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow and Crimea become allies?","a":"Because they had the same enemies. Khan Akhmat of the Great Horde threatened both, and Casimir IV of Poland and Lithuania was Moscow's rival, so the treaty of 1480 bound the two rulers to act together against both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The khans of Crimea had long been at war with the Horde on the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the alliance fall apart?","a":"Because it had been held together by the Great Horde. Once the Horde broke up in 1502 Crimea demanded payments from Moscow, its forces raided Moscow's borderlands, and relations worsened further after the khan's death in 1515.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the khan's letter to the Genoese so valued?","a":"Because it is unique. Written in December 1481 to envoys sent to stir him against Ottoman rule, it is the only known document in Greek from a 15th-century Crimean chancery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Meñli I Giray"},
    "ru-179": {"questions":["Archbishop Gennady of Novgorod and Joseph of Volokolamsk wrote nearly everything known about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, accusing them of rejecting icons and denying the Trinity.","Half a century after the burnings of 1504, an elder remarked that nobody knew why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been burnt, though a council had condemned them as heretics."],"abstract":"The <b>Judaisers</b> were a circle of clergy and laymen in Novgorod and then Moscow at the end of the 15th century, so named by the churchmen who denounced and condemned them as heretics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything known about them comes from their enemies, above all the epistles of Archbishop Gennady of Novgorod and the writings of Joseph of Volokolamsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The accusers charged them with rejecting icons and denying the Trinity, but the heretics themselves did not regard their worship as heretical, and whether they were really antitrinitarian is still questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name came from the charge that a Jew, whom Joseph called Skhariya, had led them astray, and the Jewish element of the accusations changed sharply as early as the council of 1490.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Texts long linked with the circle, the <i>Logika</i> and the <i>Secret of Secrets</i>, were translated from Hebrew into Ruthenian, though neither survives in its first form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Gennady found the heresy among his flock in the mid-1480s and alerted the bishops, and a council in Moscow in the autumn of 1490 condemned the Novgorod heretics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the condemned were sent back to Novgorod, where Gennady punished them by having birch-bark caps burnt on their heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Joseph went further and accused the new metropolitan Zosima himself of heresy, and the circle has been thought to have had patrons at court until April 1502, when Ivan III disgraced his grandson Dmitry and Dmitry's mother Elena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A council of 1504 ended in harsh executions, and half a century later an elder could still say that men had been burnt and nobody knew what for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the accounts were written by the prosecutors, and the charges grew in the telling, how far the heretics really held the beliefs ascribed to them remains an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander A. Kazakov, “Joseph Volotsky and Gennady of Novgorod: The Borrowing of Ideas in the Context on Anti-‘Judaizers’ Polemics,” <i>Slověne</i> 7, no. 1 (2018): 71–92, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.1.5. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Kazakov, “Iosif Volotskii, udel’nyi knyaz’ Boris Vasil’evich i eretiki,” <i>Paleorossiya. Drevnyaya Rus’: vo vremeni, v lichnostyakh, v ideyakh</i>, no. 3 (27) (2024): 210–34, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2024_3_210. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Manokhin, “Evolyutsiya ‘zhidovskogo’ komponenta novgorodskoi eresi i problemy kodikologii,” <i>Paleorossiya. Drevnyaya Rus’: vo vremeni, v lichnostyakh, v ideyakh</i>, no. 3 (27) (2024): 235–49, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2024_3_235. [in Russian] [Open access]","D. M. Bulanin, “Nad stranitsami ‘Perevodov zhidovstvuyushchikh’,” <i>Drevnyaya Rus’. Voprosy medievistiki</i>, no. 2 (84) (2021): 215–23, https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2021.84.2.017. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oleg Zhigankov, “The Issue of Antitrinitarianism in the Fifteenth-Century Novgorod-Moscow Movement: Analysis and Evaluation” (PhD diss., Andrews University, 2000), https://doi.org/10.32597/dissertations/172/. [Open access]","Igor Ivanov, “Ternary Structures in The Life of Joseph Volotsky,” <i>Problemy istoricheskoi poetiki</i> 21, no. 4 (2023): 63–81, https://doi.org/10.15393/j9.art.2023.13082. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is so little certain about what the Judaisers believed?","a":"Because nearly every account was written by their enemies, Archbishop Gennady and Joseph of Volokolamsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The charges against them also shifted as the case went on, which makes the later polemics less reliable as evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were they called Judaisers?","a":"Because their accusers blamed a Jew, whom Joseph called Skhariya, for leading them astray.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Jewish part of the charge changed sharply as early as the council of 1490, so the name tells us more about the prosecution than about the accused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the affair reach the court?","a":"Because Joseph accused the metropolitan Zosima himself, and the circle has been thought to have had protectors close to Ivan III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their fall from favour in 1502 came shortly before the council of 1504 sent men to be burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Judaizers"},
    "ru-180": {"questions":["Endowed by Prince Boris of Volok, the monastery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became one of the richest in Rus', and its library kept the only copy of the 1497 code.","The most active foe of the Novgorod heretics, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> aimed his first letters against them at the metropolitan Zosima, whom he took for all but the chief heresiarch."],"abstract":"<b>Joseph of Volokolamsk</b>, also called Joseph Volotsky, was a Russian monk who lived from 1439 to 1515 and founded the monastery of the Mother of God near Volok Lamsky that bears his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was trained in the strict school of the monastery of Paphnutius at Borovsk, and the oration on his death by his nephew portrays a man of quick mind, fine voice and great capacity for work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His monastery was founded with the help of the appanage prince Boris Vasilyevich of Volok, who granted it land in 1479 to 1483 that became the base of a great estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Joseph wrote a rule for his monks, known in a longer and a shorter redaction, and a fragment of it copied in his own lifetime was identified only recently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His house became one of the richest monasteries in Rus', and its library later held the only surviving copy of Ivan III's law code of 1497, in a hand identified as that of his nephew Nifont Kormilitsyn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> He was the most active of the churchmen who campaigned against the heretics known as the Judaisers, and his writings and Archbishop Gennady's epistles are the main sources for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His first letters against the heretics date from the 1490s, after the council of 1490, and they were aimed above all at the metropolitan Zosima, whom he regarded as all but the chief heresiarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of what he knew came from Novgorod, for books copied at the archbishop's scriptorium reached his monastery, and they shaped his idea of what the heresy was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the church council of 1503 he defended the right of monasteries to own villages, arguing that without them there would be no monks of good birth to fill the sees, and the council sided with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His Life, written in the 1540s by Savva Cherny, bishop of Krutitsy, presents him as the champion of the Trinity against the heretics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 176, 190–94, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","A. A. Manokhin, “Proto-Rule of Joseph Volotsky,” <i>Lomonosov History Journal</i> 64, no. 3 (2023): 3–29, https://doi.org/10.55959/msu0130-0083-8-2023-64-3-3-29. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Kazakov, “Iosif Volotskii, udel’nyi knyaz’ Boris Vasil’evich i eretiki,” <i>Paleorossiya. Drevnyaya Rus’: vo vremeni, v lichnostyakh, v ideyakh</i>, no. 3 (27) (2024): 210–34, https://doi.org/10.47132/2618-9674_2024_3_210. [in Russian] [Open access]","Igor Ivanov, “Ternary Structures in The Life of Joseph Volotsky,” <i>Problemy istoricheskoi poetiki</i> 21, no. 4 (2023): 63–81, https://doi.org/10.15393/j9.art.2023.13082. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. A. Kuchkin, “Notes Regarding the Sudebnik of Ivan III of 1497,” <i>Drevnyaya Rus’. Voprosy medievistiki</i>, no. 2 (80) (2020): 166–67, https://doi.org/10.25986/iri.2020.80.2.0012. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander A. Kazakov, “Joseph Volotsky and Gennady of Novgorod: The Borrowing of Ideas in the Context on Anti-‘Judaizers’ Polemics,” <i>Slověne</i> 7, no. 1 (2018): 71–92, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2018.7.1.5. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he defend monastic landowning?","a":"Because he believed the church needed well-born monks. At the council of 1503 he argued that if monasteries owned no land, men of noble birth would not take vows, and there would be no one fit to become a bishop or metropolitan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he turn on the metropolitan?","a":"Because he regarded Zosima as all but the chief heresiarch. His first letters against the heretics, written in the 1490s after the council of 1490, were aimed above all at him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his monastery grow so rich?","a":"Because princes and nobles endowed it. Prince Boris of Volok granted it land from 1479 to 1483, and Joseph himself said that its growth dated from the day it began to admit wealthy men who paid to take vows there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Joseph Volotsky"},
    "ru-181": {"questions":["A Rule in eleven chapters, teaching that prayer said with the lips while the heart takes no part is prayer to the wind, was the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a northern hermitage.","At the church council of 1503, by the traditional account, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> urged that monasteries should own no villages, and the party of Joseph of Volokolamsk defeated him."],"abstract":"<b>Nil Sorsky</b> was a monk of the Kirillo-Belozersky monastery in the far north of Muscovy who spent a long time on Mount Athos and came home to found the first hermitage known in Rus, on the little river Sora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born Nil Maikov around the middle of the 15th century, in 1433 by the usual reckoning, he died in 1508.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the early 1480s he made a pilgrimage to the Christian East, and his own writings say that he saw Constantinople and Athos, while it has been argued that he may also have reached Palestine and Sinai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Back in Rus he copied and corrected more than twenty lives of early saints, the collection known as his <i>Sobornik</i>, which the monk Gury Tushin recopied in 1508–09, just after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A hermitage as he ran it held only two or three cells, with dress, food and routine the same for every monk, a middle way between the common life of a monastery and complete seclusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His teaching is set out above all in his <i>Ustav</i>, or Rule, in eleven chapters, the first of which traces how a passing thought grows by stages into a settled passion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its sources are the Greek masters of hesychasm, the Byzantine tradition of inner prayer, but in Nil's hands the weight falls more on the practical and moral discipline of the soul than on contemplative theology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Poverty lay at the centre of it, and Nil, like Maximus the Greek after him, is remembered for holding that non-possession should bind the Church as well as the monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At a church council in 1503 he is said to have asked that monks should own no villages and live by the work of their own hands, and the party of Joseph of Volokolamsk carried the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that clash happened at the council at all is now disputed, and the old picture of Nil and Joseph as sworn opponents has been criticised as too simple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 188–194, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Elena V. Romanenko, “Palomnichestvo Nila Sorskogo na Khristianskii Vostok” [Nil Sorsky's pilgrimage to the Christian East], <i>Istoricheskii vestnik</i> 48 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/palomnichestvo-nila-sorskogo-na-hristianskiy-vostok. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oleg S. Klimkov, “Retseptsiia vizantiisko-afonskogo isikhazma v uchenii Nila Sorskogo” [The reception of Byzantine and Athonite hesychasm in the teaching of Nil Sorsky], <i>Filosofiia i kul'tura</i>, no. 8 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/retseptsiya-vizantiysko-afonskogo-isihazma-v-uchenii-nila-sorskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Peshkov, “Obraz prp. Nila Sorskogo i prp. Iosifa Volotskogo v otechestvennykh issledovaniiakh” [The image of St Nil Sorsky and St Joseph of Volokolamsk in Russian scholarship], <i>Trudy Nizhegorodskoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 18 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obraz-prp-nila-sorskogo-i-prp-iosifa-volotskogo-v-otechestvennyh-issledovaniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Stanislav G. Savitskii, “Predystoriia spora ‘nestiazhatelei’ i ‘iosiflian’: po materialam drevnerusskikh pamiatnikov kanonicheskogo prava” [The prehistory of the dispute between the non-possessors and the Josephites, from Old Russian canon law], <i>Paleorossiia</i>, no. 8 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/predystoriya-spora-nestyazhateley-i-iosiflyan-po-materialam-drevnerusskih-pamyatnikov-kanonicheskogo-prava. [in Russian] [Open access]","Olga A. Balabeikina, Dmitrii A. Zvorygin and Irina V. Mikhel'son, “Polemika mezhdu iosiflianami i nestiazhateliami: bogoslovskii aspekt” [The polemic between the Josephites and the non-possessors: the theological aspect], <i>Teologicheskii vestnik Smolenskoi pravoslavnoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 2 (2025): 220–235, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polemika-mezhdu-iosiflyanami-i-nestyazhatelyami-bogoslovskiy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nil Sorsky found a hermitage rather than an ordinary monastery?","a":"Because what he had seen on Athos and in Byzantium was a middle way: two or three cells under one shared rule, without the estates and crowds of a great house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His teaching put the inner work of the soul first, which suited a small and quiet community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the card call his clash with Joseph of Volokolamsk disputed?","a":"Because the traditional account sets it at the council of 1503, but there are grounds for thinking the land dispute arose only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians have also been criticised for turning the two monks into simple opposites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the pilgrimage give his later work?","a":"He knew at first hand places he read about, and when he edited more than twenty lives of saints for his <i>Sobornik</i> he corrected many details from what he had seen in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nilus of Sora"},
    "ru-182": {"questions":["Followers of Nil Sorsky, the hermits beyond the Volga were called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for insisting that monks should live by their own labour and own no villages.","Writing against the great monastic estates with the Gospels and the canons, the prince-monk Vassian Patrikeev became the sharpest pen of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after Nil Sorsky's death."],"abstract":"The <b>non-possessors</b> were the party in the Muscovite church of the late 15th and 16th centuries who held that monasteries should own no land, against the Josephites, who defended monastic estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both sides agreed that the monasteries needed reform, but one wanted them stripped of property while the other wanted strict common life, in which the monk owned nothing and the house a great deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first party looked to Nil Sorsky and the hermits beyond the Volga, the second to Joseph of Volokolamsk, whose own monastery grew from nothing into one of the richest in Rus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quarrel had a long prehistory, for the standing of church property had been argued over and defended in canonical texts since the church charters of the princes Vladimir and Yaroslav.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Joseph's case, as he is said to have put it in 1503, was that without estates no man of noble birth would take vows, and without such monks the Church would lack bishops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The council sided with Joseph, and Ivan III, who had hoped to use monastic land for the state, gave way, a retreat one account links to a stroke that struck him during the sessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the land question was really raised at that council rather than later is itself disputed, and the old picture of two sworn enemies has been criticised as too simple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The argument then moved into writing, and the prince-monk Vassian Patrikeev attacked monastic estates with the Gospels, the examples of ancient saints and the canons of church councils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vasily III leaned now to one party and now to the other, and in 1511 he raised to the metropolitan's throne Varlaam, whom he seems to have seen as a man of the trans-Volga elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the end the government gave up its designs on monastic land and settled, by a decree of 1580, for stopping monasteries from acquiring more of it from servicemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 188–196, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Vladislav I. Petrushko, “Tserkovnaia politika velikogo kniazia Vasiliia III v kontekste protivostoianiia ‘iosiflian’ i ‘nestiazhatelei’” [The church policy of Grand Prince Vasily III and the confrontation of the Josephites and the non-possessors], <i>Paleorossiia</i>, no. 3 (2022): 12–22, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tserkovnaya-politika-velikogo-knyazya-vasiliya-iii-v-kontekste-protivostoyaniya-iosiflyan-i-nestyazhateley. [in Russian] [Open access]","Stanislav G. Savitskii, “Predystoriia spora ‘nestiazhatelei’ i ‘iosiflian’: po materialam drevnerusskikh pamiatnikov kanonicheskogo prava” [The prehistory of the dispute between the non-possessors and the Josephites, from Old Russian canon law], <i>Paleorossiia</i>, no. 8 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/predystoriya-spora-nestyazhateley-i-iosiflyan-po-materialam-drevnerusskih-pamyatnikov-kanonicheskogo-prava. [in Russian] [Open access]","Evgenii V. Mar'in and Evgenii V. Nikol'skii, “Moral'nye i pravovye aspekty monastyrskogo zemlevladeniia v osveshchenii kniazia-inoka Vassiana Patrikeeva” [Moral and legal aspects of monastic landownership in the writings of the prince-monk Vassian Patrikeev], <i>Vestnik Udmurtskogo universiteta. Seriia Istoriia i filologiia</i> 31, no. 6 (2021): 1247–1251, https://doi.org/10.35634/2412-9534-2021-31-6-1247-1251. [in Russian] [Open access]","Olga A. Balabeikina, Dmitrii A. Zvorygin and Irina V. Mikhel'son, “Polemika mezhdu iosiflianami i nestiazhateliami: bogoslovskii aspekt” [The polemic between the Josephites and the non-possessors: the theological aspect], <i>Teologicheskii vestnik Smolenskoi pravoslavnoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 2 (2025): 220–235, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polemika-mezhdu-iosiflyanami-i-nestyazhatelyami-bogoslovskiy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Peshkov, “Obraz prp. Nila Sorskogo i prp. Iosifa Volotskogo v otechestvennykh issledovaniiakh” [The image of St Nil Sorsky and St Joseph of Volokolamsk in Russian scholarship], <i>Trudy Nizhegorodskoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 18 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obraz-prp-nila-sorskogo-i-prp-iosifa-volotskogo-v-otechestvennyh-issledovaniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan III sympathise with the non-possessors?","a":"Because the state needed land for its servicemen, and the great monastic estates were the largest store of it that a ruler might hope to take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the council of 1503 refused, he gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Joseph of Volokolamsk think monasteries must own land?","a":"Because, he argued, without estates men of noble birth would not take vows, and without them the Church would have no one fit to be a bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the dispute not settled once and for all in 1503?","a":"Because it passed into writing, where Vassian Patrikeev and others kept attacking monastic wealth for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vasily III himself swung between the two parties as it suited him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Non-possessors"},
    "ru-183": {"questions":["A German's forecast of a world flood for 1524 drew from a Pskov monk the letter that announced Moscow as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a secretary of Vasily III.","Repudiated by the church council of 1666–67, the idea of Moscow as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was revived in the 19th century as a supposed key to Russian messianism."],"abstract":"The <b>Third Rome</b> was the idea that Moscow had taken the place of Rome and Constantinople as the last seat of a true Christian empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its classic formula comes from Filofei, an elder of the Eleazarov monastery near Pskov, who wrote that two Romes had fallen, the third stands, and a fourth there shall not be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The phrase sits in a letter against the astrologers and the Catholics sent to the grand prince's secretary Misyur Munekhin around 1523, in answer to a German, Nicolaus Bülow, who had forecast a new flood for 1524.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For Filofei, Rome's mission to the world rested on loyalty to the Orthodox faith and the church canons, not on any imperial power that could not be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He judged everything that came from the Western church to be the devil's work, a harder line than several leading Russian churchmen of his day, who were ready to learn from the Catholics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Filofei also wrote to Vasily III himself that all the Christian realms had come together in his person, so that he was now the one Orthodox emperor in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state at first took no notice, and the formula entered an official text only with the charter founding the Moscow patriarchate in 1589.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The church council of 1666–67 repudiated the notion, though it lived on among the Old Believers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the 19th and 20th centuries it was revived and read as proof of Russian messianism and imperialism, a weight of meaning for a concept that early sources mention only twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Specialists still disagree whether it was a passing by-product of a quarrel about astrology or a real current in 16th-century thought, and even how original Filofei was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 23, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Andrey V. Korenevskiy, “The Ideologeme of Deferred Demand, or How Many Times the Theory of ‘Moscow as the Third Rome’ Was ‘Invented’?” <i>Historical Courier</i>, no. 6 (2021): 9–28, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2021-6-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Paul Bushkovitch, “The First Rome Context of Filofei's Epistle: Orthodoxy and Catholicism in Russia in the 15th–16th Centuries,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2023): 14–28, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2023.202. [in Russian] [Open access]","Marcello Garzaniti, “Imperium and Sacerdotium in Sixteenth-Century Russia: The Reflections of the Starets Filofei of Pskov and the Athonite Monk Maximus the Greek,” <i>The New Past</i>, no. 3 (2023): 54–71, https://doi.org/10.18522/2500-3224-2023-3-54-71. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail V. Dmitriev, Natalia S. Gurianova, Dmitriy M. Bulanin, Aleksey V. Sirenov and Andrey V. Korenevskiy, “The Theory of Moscow as the Third Rome: Texts and Contexts,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2023): 67–98, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu19.2023.205. [in Russian] [Open access]","Maureen Perrie, “Moscow in 1666: New Jerusalem, Third Rome, Third Apostasy,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i>, no. 3 (2014): 75–85, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2014.3.062. [Open access]","Petr N. Bazanov, “Istorik N. I. Ul'ianov i teoriia ‘Moskva — Tretii Rim’” [The historian N. I. Ulyanov and the theory of Moscow as the Third Rome], <i>Russko-Vizantiiskii vestnik</i>, no. 4 (2024): 169–184, https://doi.org/10.47132/2588-0276_2024_4_169. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a letter about astrology carry a claim about Moscow's place in history?","a":"Because Filofei was answering a prediction of a world flood for 1524, and to refute it he set out how God's plan ran through the fall of two Romes to a third that would stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made Rome's mission last, in his view, was the true faith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians doubt that the idea was official policy under Vasily III?","a":"Because the formula appears in no programme text of the state until the patriarchate charter of 1589.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early sources mention the concept only twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Third Rome often linked with Russian imperialism?","a":"Because 19th- and 20th-century writers revived it and read it as evidence of messianism and empire-building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That reading is much argued over, and some deny Filofei any originality at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Succession of the Roman Empire"},
    "ru-184": {"questions":["Childless in a first marriage to Solomonia Saburova, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had her tonsured as a nun in 1525 and took Elena Glinskaya, the mother of Ivan the Terrible, as a second wife.","Pskov, Smolensk and Ryazan were all gathered into Muscovy under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the son of Ivan III and Sophia Palaiologina, who reigned from 1505 to 1533."],"abstract":"<b>Vasily III</b> was grand prince of Moscow from 1505 to 1533, the son of Ivan III and Sophia Palaiologina and the ruler under whom Pskov, Smolensk and Ryazan were added to Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His way to the throne was not smooth, since his father had for a time favoured a grandson, Dmitry, and Vasily kept that nephew in prison once he ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sigismund von Herberstein, an imperial envoy who knew his court, wrote that he added provinces less by war, in which he had little luck, than by persistence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pskov lost its self-government in 1510, Smolensk fell to a third siege in 1514, and Ryazan's turn came soon after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great gain at Smolensk was followed within weeks by a heavy defeat at Orsha, and in 1521 a Crimean army reached the outskirts of Moscow itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At home Herberstein judged his sway over his subjects greater than that of any monarch in the world, and heard Muscovites answer a hard question with the words that God and the great prince know.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first marriage, to Solomonia Saburova, produced no children, and in November 1525 he had her tonsured as a nun, officially because she was barren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1526 he married Elena Glinskaya, a princess from the Lithuanian lands, and their son, the future Ivan IV, was still a child when Vasily died in 1533.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His testament tried to split power, naming Elena head of the family while placing the boy's guardianship and the business of state in different hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In church affairs he swung between the Josephites and their opponents, and the ceremonial headgear of the dynasty, the cap of Monomakh among it, took on its meaning of unbroken succession in his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 25–33, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 8–9, 36, 63–64, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Vladimir A. Volkov, “Sotvorenie Rossii. Pobedy i porazheniia velikogo gosudaria Vasiliia III” [The making of Russia: the victories and defeats of the sovereign Vasily III], <i>Gumanitarnye issledovaniia Tsentral'noi Rossii</i>, no. 1 (2020): 7–23, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotvorenie-rossii-pobedy-i-porazheniya-velikogo-gosudarya-vasiliya-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Liudmila E. Morozova, “Brachnaia politika Vasiliia III” [The marriage policy of Vasily III], <i>Paleorossiia</i>, no. 7 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/brachnaya-politika-vasiliya-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Viacheslav V. Shaposhnik, “K voprosu o zaveshchanii Vasiliia III” [On the testament of Vasily III], <i>Vestnik Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta. Istoriia</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-zaveschanii-vasiliya-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei N. Bogatyrev, “Shapka Monomakha i shlem naslednika: reprezentatsiia vlasti i dinasticheskaia politika pri Vasilii III i Ivane Groznom” [The cap of Monomakh and the heir's helmet: the representation of power and dynastic policy under Vasily III and Ivan the Terrible], <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shapka-monomaha-i-shlem-naslednika-reprezentatsiya-vlasti-i-dinasticheskaya-politika-pri-vasilii-iii-i-ivane-groznom. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladislav I. Petrushko, “Tserkovnaia politika velikogo kniazia Vasiliia III v kontekste protivostoianiia ‘iosiflian’ i ‘nestiazhatelei’” [The church policy of Grand Prince Vasily III and the confrontation of the Josephites and the non-possessors], <i>Paleorossiia</i>, no. 3 (2022): 12–22, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tserkovnaya-politika-velikogo-knyazya-vasiliya-iii-v-kontekste-protivostoyaniya-iosiflyan-i-nestyazhateley. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vasily III divorce his first wife?","a":"Because his marriage to Solomonia Saburova had produced no heir, and the official chronicles gave her barrenness as the reason for her tonsure in November 1525.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his testament divide power among several people?","a":"Because his son was a small child, and Vasily seems to have wanted checks and balances so that no single person could seize power before the boy came of age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Herberstein think him so powerful?","a":"Because no counsellor dared to contradict him, his people called his will the will of God, and he held the lives and property of all his subjects in his hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vasili III of Russia"},
    "ru-185": {"questions":["A city with four walled quarters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made its own laws, set its own taxes and struck its own coins until Vasily III abolished its veche.","Townsmen quarrelling with their governor took their case to Vasily III at Novgorod in 1509, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not survive the judgement he gave on it."],"abstract":"The <b>Pskov Republic</b> was the self-governing city-state of Pskov in north-western Rus, which ran its own affairs through its assembly, the veche, until Vasily III abolished it in 1510.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its veche made and amended laws, set taxes, mobilised troops, declared war and made peace, and the city used its own seals and coins, all marks of a sovereign state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sigismund von Herberstein called Pskov the only city in the Muscovite's lands with a wall round it, divided into four parts each walled in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the later 15th century, though, Pskov depended heavily on Moscow, whose governors had a large share in running it and whose wars it had to join, as it did against Novgorod in 1477–78.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The veche and the elected officials survived all the same, and the grand prince would sometimes replace a governor the people of Pskov disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the autumn of 1509 the townsmen and their governor, Prince Ivan Repnya Obolensky, brought complaints against each other to Vasily III at Novgorod, and he found for the governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He arrested leading Pskovians, ordered the veche abolished, and on 24 January 1510 entered the city, where a small wooden church of the saint went up at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bell that had summoned the assembly was taken away, and some 300 families were sent to Moscow while men from Muscovy were settled in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herberstein, writing a generation later, mourned the plain and honest dealing of the old Pskovians, which he thought had given way to Muscovite manners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Old Russian writings on the annexation take Moscow's side, Pskov's side or neither, and one tale of the taking of Pskov carries both views at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1852), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","Mikhail A. Nesin, “K istorii prisoedineniia Pskova k Moskovskomu gosudarstvu” [On the history of the annexation of Pskov to the Muscovite state], <i>Vestnik Udmurtskogo universiteta. Seriia Istoriia i filologiia</i> 27, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-prisoedineniya-pskova-k-moskovskomu-gosudarstvu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Natal'ia A. Demicheva, “Povest' osobogo sostava ‘Vziatie Pskovskoe’: problematika, tekstologiia, stil'” [The tale ‘The Taking of Pskov’: themes, textual history, style], <i>Litera</i>, no. 4 (2018): 95–100, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/povest-osobogo-sostava-vzyatie-pskovskoe-problematika-tekstologiya-stil. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dmitrii A. Petrov, “The Church Building of the 1510s–1520s as the Reflection of the Accession of Pskov and Smolensk by Vasily III,” <i>St. Tikhon's University Review. Series V: Christian Art</i>, no. 4 (2016): 67–80, https://doi.org/10.15382/sturv201624.67-80. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Arakcheev, “Political Organization in the Pskov Republic: The Prince, the Veche and Sovereignty,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i>, no. 1 (2013): 101–116, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2013.1.011. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 8–9, 106–107, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Pskov be called a sovereign state before 1510?","a":"Because its veche made laws, set taxes, raised troops and decided on war and peace, and the city used its own seals and coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the veche bell taken away?","a":"Because it was the bell that called the assembly together, so removing it ended the republic in a way everyone could see and hear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The veche itself was abolished at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Pskov families moved to Moscow?","a":"Because removing the leading families and settling Muscovites in their place broke the old governing class, as had been done at Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pskov Republic"},
    "ru-186": {"questions":["In Herberstein's description the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, between the Oka and the Don, was the most fertile land in Russia, where wheat grew so thick that horses could hardly pass.","Ruled for years by grand princesses, a sister of Ivan III among them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost a third of its land to Moscow in 1503 and the rest by 1521."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Principality of Ryazan</b> was a Russian state between the Oka and the Don whose princes kept a court and government of their own until Moscow absorbed it in 1521.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herberstein thought it more fertile than any other province of Russia, rich in grain, honey, fish and game, and its people the most daring and warlike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chancery developed much as Moscow's did, with the clerks, the dyaks, taking a growing part in the making and witnessing of documents in the later 15th and early 16th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Long before the end Ryazan was not fully its own master, and in 1503 Moscow took the third of the land that had belonged to Prince Fyodor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For years women governed it, first Anna, a sister of Ivan III, for her son and then her grandson, and after her the grand princess Agrafena for her son Ivan Ivanovich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan Ivanovich was arrested in Moscow in the winter of 1520–21, and by Herberstein's account he had been seeking a Crimean bride while Moscow was at war with the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herberstein adds that Vasily III shut the prince's mother in a convent and scattered many of the people through other lands, so that the strength of the whole principality was broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The story of the mother has been doubted, since she seems by then to have taken vows already and to have lived in a hermitage she had founded 14.5 kilometres (9 miles) from Ryazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ivan escaped in the confusion when the Crimean Tatars came up to Moscow in 1521 and fled to Lithuania, where he stayed in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Few followed him, for most Ryazan boyars and gentry had long looked to service with Moscow and refused to back their own prince in 1521.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 2 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1852), 9–11, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab02herbuoft. [Open access]","Alexander L. Korzinin and Anatoly L. Gryaznov, “The Courts of the Grand Duchesses of Ryazan,” <i>Vestnik of Saint Petersburg University. History</i> 69, no. 3 (2024): 596–620, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu02.2024.304. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrei A. Bondarenko, “Vykhodtsy iz Velikogo kniazhestva Riazanskogo v Litve v kontse XV — seredine XVI veka” [Natives of the Grand Principality of Ryazan in Lithuania, late 15th to mid-16th century], <i>Vestnik Riazanskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta imeni S. A. Esenina</i>, no. 4 (2024): 7–22, https://doi.org/10.37724/RSU.2024.85.4.001. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anna A. Reshetova, “Travelog kak istochnik issledovaniia lokal'nogo teksta: na materiale zapisok inostrannykh puteshestvennikov XV–XVII vekov o Riazani” [The travelogue as a source for a local text: foreign travellers on Ryazan, 15th–17th centuries], <i>Vestnik Riazanskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta imeni S. A. Esenina</i>, no. 2 (2023): 101–114, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/travelog-kak-istochnik-issledovaniya-lokalnogo-teksta-na-materiale-zapisok-inostrannyh-puteshestvennikov-xv-xvii-vekov-o-ryazani. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ivan A. Kirpichnikov, “D'iacheskii apparat Velikogo kniazhestva Riazanskogo” [The clerks of the Grand Principality of Ryazan], <i>Vestnik Tambovskogo universiteta. Seriia: Gumanitarnye nauki</i>, no. 184 (2020): 114–120, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dyacheskiy-apparat-velikogo-knyazhestva-ryazanskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow arrest the last prince of Ryazan?","a":"Herberstein says he wanted to rule unchecked and was courting the daughter of the Crimean khan while Moscow was at war with the Crimea, so he was seized when he came to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few Ryazan nobles follow their prince into exile?","a":"Because for years before the end the local elite had been turning towards service with the grand princes of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Herberstein's story about the prince's mother doubted?","a":"Because other evidence suggests she had probably already become a nun and was living in a hermitage she had founded near Ryazan, so there was no need to force her into a convent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Principality of Ryazan","places":["city:1159149553"]},
    "ru-187": {"questions":["Herberstein blamed Prince Michael Glinsky and his bribes for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended more than a century of Lithuanian rule over the city on the Dnieper.","Within weeks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Muscovite army that marched deeper into Lithuania was routed at Orsha, yet the city itself stayed in Vasily III's hands."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Smolensk</b> of 1514 was the third attempt by Vasily III to take the city, held by Lithuania for more than a century, and it ended with its surrender to Moscow at the end of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two earlier Muscovite attacks had been beaten off by the commander Yuri Glebovich, whom King Sigismund nonetheless replaced with Yuri Sologub shortly before the decisive campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herberstein says Vasily made the treatment of his sister, the widow of King Alexander, and a claim that Sigismund had set the Tatars on him his grounds for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capture was the main event of the war of 1512–1522, and it had a religious side too, since Vasily and his advisers saw Russia as the centre of the Orthodox world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Foreign engineers served the Muscovite artillery, and the chronicles single out a gunner named Stefan, whose shot struck a defending cannon so that it burst and killed many people in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Talks about surrender went on for weeks, with the boyar Mikhail Pivov acting for the bishop Varsonofy and the commander, and a bombardment on 29 July seems to have been the final push.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 1 August Vasily entered the city in state, received the bishop's blessing and made Prince Vasily Shuisky its governor, while Sologub was let go to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herberstein laid the surrender to Prince Michael Glinsky, who he says won the city by bribery, and the Polish and Lithuanian chroniclers blamed Glinsky too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other evidence makes Glinsky a negotiator who helped persuade the garrison, and how far bribes or treachery played a part is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The victory soured quickly, for the army that marched on into Lithuania was routed at Orsha on 8 September 1514, though Smolensk itself stayed in Moscow's hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 25–29, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Vladimir A. Volkov, “Sotvorenie Rossii. Pobedy i porazheniia velikogo gosudaria Vasiliia III” [The making of Russia: the victories and defeats of the sovereign Vasily III], <i>Gumanitarnye issledovaniia Tsentral'noi Rossii</i>, no. 1 (2020): 7–23, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotvorenie-rossii-pobedy-i-porazheniya-velikogo-gosudarya-vasiliya-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksei N. Lobin, “K voprosu o roli Mikhaila Glinskogo v Smolenskoi kampanii 1512–1514 gg.” [On the role of Mikhail Glinsky in the Smolensk campaign of 1512–1514], <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i>, special issue 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-roli-mihaila-glinskogo-v-smolenskoy-kampanii-1512-1514-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. V. Penskoi and T. M. Penskaia, “Smolenskaia osada 1514 goda” [The siege of Smolensk in 1514], <i>Istoriia voennogo dela: issledovaniia i istochniki</i> 5 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smolenskaya-osada-1514-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Irina B. Mikhailova, “K voprosu o ‘Smolenskom vziatii’ 1514 g.” [On the taking of Smolensk in 1514], <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-smolenskom-vzyatii-1514-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nina V. Trofimova, “The Narrations about the Conquest of Smolensk in 1514 in the Chronicles of the 16th–17th Centuries,” <i>Hermeneutics of Old Russian Literature</i> 19 (2020): 474–484, https://doi.org/10.22455/978-5-9208-0610-9-474-484. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Smolensk fall at the third attempt when two earlier sieges had failed?","a":"Because the heavy guns arrived and talks with the townsmen had been going on for weeks, so a last bombardment on 29 July pushed the defenders to accept terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The experienced commander who had beaten off the first two sieges had also been replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Michael Glinsky's part in the surrender disputed?","a":"Because Herberstein and the Polish and Lithuanian chroniclers accused him of bribery, while other evidence shows him working as a negotiator with the garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the capture matter so much to Moscow?","a":"Because it was the main prize of the whole war of 1512–1522, and in Moscow it was also seen as a gain for Orthodoxy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Siege of Smolensk (1514)","places":["city:1159149533"]},
    "ru-188": {"questions":["Born Michael Trivolis in Arta and schooled among the humanists of Florence and Venice, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent his last 37 years in Russia, never allowed back to Athos.","The disgraced councillor Bersen Beklemishev poured out his grievances against Vasily III to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their talk was read out at Bersen's trial in 1525."],"abstract":"<b>Maximus the Greek</b> was a learned monk of the Vatopedi monastery on Mount Athos who came to Moscow in 1518 to translate Greek church books and spent the rest of his long life in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born Michael Trivolis in the town of Arta, he had a humanist education in northern Italy, above all in Florence and Venice, where he worked on some of the first printed editions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Florence he heard Savonarola preach, saw his execution in 1498, and spent about a year as a novice in the friary of San Marco without, it now seems, taking Catholic vows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a monk at Vatopedi from 1506 to 1516, until Vasily III and the metropolitan Varlaam invited him to put the Greek books of the grand prince's library into Church Slavonic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first years in Moscow went well, and he translated commentaries on the Acts and Epistles and on the Psalter while educated Muscovites crowded into his cell to argue about books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One visitor was the disgraced councillor Ivan Bersen Beklemishev, whose bitter talk with the old monk about the grand prince and the Greeks who had come with Sophia was read out at Bersen's trial in 1525.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That year Maximus himself was condemned by a church council, and a second council in 1531 tried him again, charging him with heresy and with spoiling the sacred texts he had corrected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicles say very little about either trial, which suggests that the leading churchmen did not want the details made public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was held for years in the Joseph-Volokolamsk, Tver Otroch and Trinity-Sergius monasteries, begged again and again to be sent back to Athos, and was kept in Muscovy against his will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He wrote against monastic estates and on the duty of rulers in the voice of a prophet, and the Russian church canonised him in 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Liudmila I. Zhurova, “Prepodobnyi Maksim Grek. 500 let v Rossii” [St Maximus the Greek: 500 years in Russia], <i>Vestnik Ekaterinburgskoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 4 (2018): 13–18, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prepodobnyy-maksim-grek-500-let-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Neža Zajc, “The Byzantine-Poetic Path of the Works of St. Maximus the Greek (Mikhail Trivolis, Arta, ca. 1470 – St. Maximus the Greek, Moscow, 1556),” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 8 (2018): 285–318, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.08.15. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 61–64, 194–195, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","I. V. Vaganov, “K istorii prebyvaniia Maksima Greka v Rossii: po materialam russkikh letopisei” [On the stay of Maximus the Greek in Russia, from the Russian chronicles], <i>Izvestiia Rossiiskogo gosudarstvennogo pedagogicheskogo universiteta im. A. I. Gertsena</i>, no. 60 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-prebyvaniya-maksima-greka-v-rossii-po-materialam-russkih-letopisey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dmitrii M. Bulanin, “Maxim the Greek: Was He a Greek Litterateur or an Expert in Muscovite Writings?” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2017), https://doi.org/10.21638/11701/spbu19.2017.207. [in Russian] [Open access]","Marcello Garzaniti, “Imperium and Sacerdotium in Sixteenth-Century Russia: The Reflections of the Starets Filofei of Pskov and the Athonite Monk Maximus the Greek,” <i>The New Past</i>, no. 3 (2023): 54–71, https://doi.org/10.18522/2500-3224-2023-3-54-71. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Maximus brought to Moscow?","a":"Because the grand prince's library held Greek books that no one could use, and Vasily III and the metropolitan wanted them translated into Church Slavonic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he put on trial?","a":"Among the charges were heresy and the corruption of sacred texts, since he had dared to correct books that Russian scribes had translated badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His friendship with discontented men such as Bersen Beklemishev did not help him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the chronicles say so little about his trials?","a":"Because the leading churchmen seem not to have wanted the details of the councils against him made public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Maximus the Greek"},
    "ru-189": {"questions":["Born from personal commissions given to boyars, each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a permanent office in Moscow with its own clerks, secretaries and records as the state grew.","The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is first found for a government office in 1512, and by the end of the century such offices ran Muscovy's diplomacy, army and treasury."],"abstract":"A <b>prikaz</b> was a central government office of Muscovy, one of the departments through which the grand princes and then the tsars ran the business of a fast-growing state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many began as personal commissions given to a boyar, which turned into permanent offices with their own clerks, secretaries and records as the work grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The core of the system was the Treasury, the Kazna, together with the Palace, and many early offices were temporary, lasting on average one to five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law code of 1497 shows the change under way, for it gave judges a staff of clerks called dyaks and sent cases beyond their competence up to the grand prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New needs made new offices, among them an Office of Ambassadors for foreign affairs, the Razriad for military service, a bureau for service estates, and central bureaus for felonies and for slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The word itself is first found for such an office in 1512, and it came into wide use for offices of this kind only in the second half of the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> There was no single plan behind them, and by the end of the 16th century some thirty offices had been added one beside another, the way the tsars' palaces grew by new wings and storeys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The English envoy Giles Fletcher, writing in 1591, described the country as split into four quarters, each attached to an office in Moscow which took complaints from its provinces and sent back orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The career of one dyak, Fyodor Mishurin, first recorded in 1516–17 taking money into the treasury, shows how much rested on such men, for of 114 charters issued in 1533–34 he endorsed 84.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Long studied mainly as a tangle of institutions, the offices are now more and more approached through the clerks who staffed them, as a social group in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 252–256, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 39–41, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alina V. Kostiukova and Denis S. Shtreis, “K istokam zarozhdeniia prikaznoi sistemy v Rossii v kontse XV – pervoi polovine XVI veka” [On the origins of the prikaz system in Russia, late 15th to mid-16th century], <i>Gosudarstvennaia sluzhba</i> 22, no. 6 (2020): 72–77, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istokam-zarozhdeniya-prikaznoy-sistemy-v-rossii-v-kontse-xv-pervoy-polovine-xvi-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrei Iu. Savosichev, “D'iak Fedor Mishurin: sud'ba prikaznogo biurokrata XVI v.” [The clerk Fyodor Mishurin: the fate of a 16th-century prikaz bureaucrat], <i>Vestnik Rossiiskogo universiteta druzhby narodov. Seriia: Istoriia Rossii</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dyak-fedor-mishurin-sudba-prikaznogo-byurokrata-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Natal'ia V. Rybalko, “Izuchenie prikaznogo apparata upravleniia Moskovskogo gosudarstva v XXI veke” [The study of the prikaz administration of the Muscovite state in the 21st century], <i>Istoriia: fakty i simvoly</i>, no. 2 (2022): 78–91, https://doi.org/10.24888/2410-4205-2022-31-2-78-91. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail M. Shumilov, “Formirovanie tsentral'nykh finansovykh organov Russkogo gosudarstva v XVI–XVII vv.” [The formation of the central financial bodies of the Russian state, 16th–17th centuries], <i>Upravlencheskoe konsul'tirovanie</i>, no. 3 (2005): 217–218, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-tsentralnyh-finansovyh-organov-russkogo-gosudarstva-v-xvi-xvii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Muscovy need permanent government offices?","a":"Because as the principality became a great state, the business once handled by a single boyar by word of mouth grew too large and too written to manage without a standing staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the system so untidy?","a":"Because the offices were never planned as a whole: some grew out of the old court departments, some met new tasks, and some took over work from local officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many early offices lasted only a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the dyaks matter so much to the story?","a":"Because the clerks did the daily work of the offices, and a single dyak might endorse most of the charters issued in a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fletcher thought a provincial governor's secretary was there to direct him rather than to assist him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Prikaz"},
    "ru-190": {"questions":["The saying that only God and the great prince know, and the picture of a ruler more absolute than any other, reached Europe through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of an imperial envoy.","Revised by its author through Latin editions of 1549, 1551 and 1556 and a German one of 1557, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Europe its earliest full account of Muscovy."],"abstract":"The <b>Rerum Moscoviticarum Commentarii</b>, or Notes on Muscovite Affairs, is the account of Muscovy written by the imperial diplomat Sigismund von Herberstein and first printed at Vienna in 1549.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herberstein was born in 1486 at Wippach, now Vipava, in Carniola, and as a boy learned both German and the Slavic speech of the district, as he recalled in his autobiography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He went twice as envoy to Vasily III, in 1517 for the Emperor Maximilian and in 1526 for the Archduke Ferdinand, beside a colleague who represented Charles V.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the second journey Ferdinand asked him to write down everything he observed along the way, as he had done on the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The book describes the country and its religion, sets out how ambassadors were received and treated, and adds two itineraries to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its portrait of Vasily III became the classic picture of Muscovite autocracy, a ruler whose sway over his subjects surpassed that of any monarch in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herberstein reported that Muscovites called the will of the prince the will of God and answered a doubtful question with the words that God and the great prince know.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He kept adding to the work, which appeared in Latin in 1549, 1551 and 1556 and in his own German version in 1557.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its pages on money, which set the silver coins of Moscow, Novgorod, Tver and Pskov side by side, are still read beside the coins themselves as evidence for Russian currency before reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Accounts by foreign travellers like this one were long underused as historical sources, though they show how outsiders pictured the lands of Lithuania and Rus and where one ended and the other began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["R. H. Major, introduction to <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, by Sigismund von Herberstein, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), lxxxviii–lxxxix, cxix–cxx, cxxxvii–cxxxviii, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851), 30–33, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 36, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","A. L. Khoroshkevich, “Baron Sigizmund Gerbershtein. Avtobiografiia v zerkale ‘Zapisok o Moskovii’” [Baron Sigismund von Herberstein: his autobiography in the mirror of the Notes on Muscovy], <i>Slovenica</i> 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/baron-sigizmund-gerbershteyn-avtobiografiya-v-zerkale-zapisok-o-moskovii. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. Yu. Dvornichenko, “‘Lithuania’ and ‘Russia’ in the Reflection of Travelogues: From Karpini to Herberstein,” <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 3 (2021): 380–396, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2021-3-380-396. [in Russian] [Open access]","Klim V. Bulavkin and Filipp V. Zhigarev, “Features of the Monetary System of the Russian State of the Early 16th Century, as Described by S. Herberstein,” <i>Post-Soviet Continent</i>, no. 3 (2025): 85–95, https://doi.org/10.48137/23116412_2025_3_85. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Herberstein able to write so fully about Muscovy?","a":"Because he spent months there on two embassies, in 1517 and 1526, and his master Ferdinand told him to record everything he observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had also grown up with a Slavic language as well as German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the book shape the European image of the Russian ruler?","a":"Because it described a prince whose power over his subjects outdid any other monarch's and whose people called his will the will of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the book appear in several different versions?","a":"Because Herberstein went on adding new details, through Latin editions of 1549, 1551 and 1556 and his own German version of 1557.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Notes on Muscovite Affairs"},
    "ru-191": {"questions":["An English merchant at court reported that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in March 1584 just after his bath, while he was setting out the pieces on a chessboard.","From the winter of 1564–65 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split Muscovy into two parts, and an English envoy wrote that men of the oprichnina could rob and kill the others without remedy."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan the Terrible</b>, Ivan IV Vasilyevich, was born in 1530, became grand prince of Moscow as a small child when his father Vasily III died in 1533, and ruled until 1584.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Orphaned by the age of eight, he later complained that great nobles had plundered the treasury in his name while he and his brother were fed and clothed like paupers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1547, months before that summer's great fire and rising, he was crowned tsar, and the next decade brought a new law code, a church council and the conquest of Kazan and Astrakhan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His court justified that power differently for different audiences, tracing his line to the Roman emperor Augustus in some letters and appealing to the legacy of Chinggis Khan in others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> In the 19th century Nikolai Karamzin made the death of his first wife in 1560 the turning point between a good reign and a cruel one, a scheme that shaped Russian novels and plays of the 1830s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tatars from the vassal khanate of Kasimov fought for him against Kazan, Astrakhan and the Crimea, and Nogai merchants sold horses to his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From the winter of 1564–65 he split the realm in two, and an English envoy later reported that the men of his own part, the oprichnina, could rob and kill those of the other with no remedy at law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Older historians explained such violence by his character, describing a suspicious and highly strung man who could not bear opposition and who sacked Novgorod on a mere suspicion of disloyalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent study instead sets him in the strained society Muscovy had become, and treats the mass terror as a social pathology with many accomplices rather than the product of one sick mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He struck his eldest son a fatal blow in 1581, and died in March 1584 after his bath while setting out a game of chess, by an English merchant's account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 74–75, 91–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 117–29, 296–97, 374–76, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 201–2, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Maria Salomon Arel, “Review of the Book: Ch.J. Halperin. Ivan the Terrible: Free to Reward and Free to Punish,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 8, no. 2 (2020): 386–390, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2020-8-2.386-390. [Open access]","Charles J. Halperin, “Ivan IV and the Tatars,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 9, no. 1 (2021): 188–200, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2021-9-1.188-200. [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Очередная биография первого русского царя», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 64, no. 2 (2019): 786–94, https://doi.org/10.21638/11701/spbu02.2019.219. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Жбанкова, «Иван Грозный в оценке Н. М. Карамзина и русских писателей 30-х гг. XIX века», <i>Ученые записки Орловского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (78) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ivan-groznyy-v-otsenke-n-m-karamzina-i-russkih-pisateley-30-h-gg-h1h-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey A. Chernyshov, “Ivan the Terrible – Chinggis Khan’s or Augustus’s Descendant: Legitimization of the Supreme Authority of the Moscow Tsardom in Communication Practices of the 15th–16th centuries,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 1 (2019): 159–174, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-1.159-174. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he grow up so distrustful of the great nobles?","a":"Because his father died when he was a small child and his mother soon after, and the boyar clans who then ruled in his name fought one another around him. He later wrote that they plundered the treasury while he and his brother were treated like paupers, and those memories coloured his politics as an adult."},{"q":"Why did Tatars fight on his side against other Tatars?","a":"Because Muscovite policy towards the successor states of the Golden Horde was practical rather than driven by one motive. The khanate of Kasimov was a Muscovite vassal, so its Tatars served in his campaigns against Kazan, Astrakhan and the Crimea, while Nogai merchants found a market for horses in his army."},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about how to explain the terror of his later reign?","a":"Because the evidence can be read as the story of one man or of a whole society. Older historians stressed his suspicious, unstable character, while a recent study sees the mass terror as a social pathology in which many Muscovites took part, which the tsar unleashed but did not wholly control."}],"wiki":"Ivan the Terrible"},
    "ru-192": {"questions":["An imperial envoy heard that Vasily III married <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1526 partly because her uncle Mikhail, then in prison, was a soldier of uncommon talent and courage.","The chronicles credit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a coinage reform of the 1530s that ordered forgers and clippers of coins to be hunted down and put to death."],"abstract":"<b>Elena Glinskaya</b> was the second wife of the grand prince Vasily III of Moscow and the mother of Ivan IV, and she governed in her small son's name from his father's death in December 1533 until her own in April 1538.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her family were refugees from Lithuania, and an imperial envoy at Moscow recorded that Vasily married her after forcing his childless first wife Solomonia into a convent, which he dated to 1526.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same envoy heard that the prince chose the match partly because her uncle Mikhail Glinsky, then in prison, was a soldier of uncommon talent who might protect the children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In power she had that uncle imprisoned, while the appanage princes Yuri of Dmitrov and Andrei of Staritsa, Vasily's brothers, were arrested and died in confinement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her favourite, Prince Ivan Ovchina Telepnev-Obolensky, rose quickly at court, and she died suddenly in 1538, amid rumours of poison that later writers repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Whether she was ever formally regent is disputed, because the account of Vasily's last illness has him settle only how the boyars were to report to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Moscow chronicles nonetheless present her as ruling by her husband's will, and one study argues that this version was devised by the Moscow elite in the 1530s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later official histories made her ever grander, and a chronicle of the 1550s says she held power with her son for four years and four months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They credit her with a coinage reform that ordered forgers and coin clippers to be sought out and executed, and with receiving the freed khan Shah Ali in person in January 1536.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan himself treated her rule as lawful and dated the boyars' lawlessness from her death, and the powers of such guardians were written into a dying ruler's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 50–52, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 118–20, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Правление Елены Глинской в оценке источников и исследователей», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 7 (2017): 225–37, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravlenie-eleny-glinskoy-v-otsenke-istochnikov-i-issledovateley. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «Политическая борьба в России в годы боярского правления (1533–1538 гг.)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 2</i>, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-v-rossii-v-gody-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1533-1538-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Е. Жуков, «Репрезентация политических институтов в памятниках русской исторической книжности XVI в.: правление Елены Глинской», <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 1 (13) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reprezentatsiya-politicheskih-institutov-v-pamyatnikah-russkoy-istoricheskoy-knizhnosti-xvi-v-pravlenie-eleny-glinskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Регентство и делегирование властных полномочий в Московской Руси», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 68, no. 1 (2023): 176–89, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu02.2023.110. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vasily III marry a woman from a Lithuanian refugee family?","a":"Because his first marriage had produced no children after twenty years, and he wanted an heir. An imperial envoy also heard that the prince valued her uncle, Mikhail Glinsky, as a capable soldier who might protect any children she bore."},{"q":"Why is it disputed whether she was regent at all?","a":"Because the account of Vasily III dying does not hand her the government, only arranging how the boyars should report to her. Later Moscow chronicles describe her ruling by her husband's wish, and one study treats that version as a story shaped by the court elite in the 1530s."},{"q":"Why did the official chronicles praise her more as time went on?","a":"Because they were written under her son, who regarded his mother's rule as lawful. A chronicle of the 1550s presents her almost as an equal of the young grand prince, while earlier texts give her a smaller part."}],"wiki":"Elena Glinskaya"},
    "ru-193": {"questions":["On 29 December 1543 the young Ivan IV had Andrei Shuisky handed to his kennelmen and killed, a turning point in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed his mother's death.","Ivan later recalled a Shuisky prince resting his feet on his dead father's bed, the best-known image of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1538 and his coronation in 1547."],"abstract":"<b>Boyar rule</b> is the name historians give to the years between the death of Elena Glinskaya in April 1538 and the coronation of her son Ivan IV in 1547, when rival clans of great nobles governed in the boy's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two leading clans were the Shuiskys, who descended from Alexander Nevsky like the ruling house, and the Belskys, who traced their line to the Lithuanian grand duke Gediminas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each coup changed the men around the throne and often the metropolitan too, so that in 1539 the Belskys installed Ioasaf and in 1542 his enemies drove him into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In January 1542 conspirators seized Ivan Belsky and sent him to Beloozero, bursting into the boy's bedchamber three hours before dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Macarius, made metropolitan in March 1542, kept largely out of the factions and spent his influence on preparing the coronation that ended the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan's own memories are the best-known evidence, and he later recalled a Shuisky prince lounging with his feet on his dead father's bed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 29 December 1543 the thirteen-year-old ruler had Andrei Shuisky, the leading figure of the boyar council, seized and handed to his kennelmen, who killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle says he could no longer bear the boyars' lawlessness and self-will, and in July 1546 Ivan Kubensky and two Vorontsovs were beheaded without trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his speeches to the church council of 1551 he blamed every misfortune of his youth on the nobles' self-will, though he named no one, in the spirit of the reconciliation of the late 1540s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A study of appointments shows that seats in the boyar council still passed by family custom within a narrow circle of clans, and that powerful courtiers now held two ranks at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 91–92, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 120–23, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «Политическая борьба в России в годы боярского правления (1541–1547)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 2</i>, no. 2 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-v-rossii-v-gody-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1541-1547. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «О составе Боярской думы и дворцовой администрации в период боярского правления в 30–40-е гг. XVI века», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 5 (2019): 67–85, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.5.5. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Митрополит Макарий в период боярского правления (1542–1547 гг.)», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 11 (2022): 174–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-makariy-v-period-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1542-1547-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «События 30–40-х гг. XVI в. в „царских речах“ на Стоглавом соборе», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 13 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sobytiya-30-40-h-gg-xvi-v-v-tsarskih-rechah-na-stoglavom-sobore. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the boyars govern at all in these years?","a":"Because the grand prince was a child whose father had died in 1533 and whose mother died in 1538. With no adult ruler, the leading clans competed for control of the court, the boyar council and the person of the boy himself."},{"q":"Why did the metropolitan keep changing?","a":"Because the head of the church was drawn into the feuds. When the Belskys were up they installed their own man, and when their enemies won in 1542 he was driven out, so each coup could reach the metropolitan see as well as the council."},{"q":"Why did the killing of Andrei Shuisky matter?","a":"Because it was the first time the young ruler struck down a leading noble on his own order. The chronicle presents it as his answer to the boyars' lawlessness, and the beheadings of 1546 showed that it was not a single outburst."}]},
    "ru-194": {"questions":["The earliest account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says that the cap of Vladimir Monomakh was used, tying the new tsar to a legend of gifts from a Byzantine emperor.","Metropolitan Macarius spent his influence during the years of boyar rule on preparing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which made a sixteen-year-old grand prince a crowned tsar."],"abstract":"The <b>coronation of Ivan IV</b> on 16 January 1547 made the sixteen-year-old grand prince of Moscow a crowned tsar, a title his grandfather had used only now and then in diplomatic letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In December 1546 he told his boyars that he meant to marry and to be crowned first, not as grand prince like his predecessors but as tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clergy prayed for the new tsar while gold coins were scattered over him, and the dish of coins was carried by his uncle Mikhail Glinsky, whose family then led the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metropolitan Macarius, who had kept out of the boyar feuds, made his main contribution of those years in preparing and conducting the ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rite was held in the Dormition Cathedral of the Kremlin, and on 3 February Ivan married Anastasia Romanovna Zakharyina-Yurieva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest description of the rite says that Ivan was crowned with the cap or crown of Vladimir Monomakh, the 12th-century prince of Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A court legend held that the Byzantine emperor Constantine Monomachus had sent this cap to Vladimir, and it was used to present the Muscovite tsars as heirs of Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legend took shape under his father Vasily III, building on the crowning of Ivan III's grandson Dmitry in 1498, whose earliest accounts call the headgear simply a cap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An English envoy who described the rite as used for Ivan's son Fyodor saw the cap and robe placed on the tsar while the clergy chanted many years to him as emperor of all Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Recognition abroad was slower, and of the 37 signatures on a confirming charter sent from Constantinople in 1561, 35 were later shown to be forgeries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 123–26, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 22–23, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","С. Н. Богатырев, «Шапка Мономаха и шлем наследника: репрезентация власти и династическая политика при Василии III и Иване Грозном», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shapka-monomaha-i-shlem-naslednika-reprezentatsiya-vlasti-i-dinasticheskaya-politika-pri-vasilii-iii-i-ivane-groznom. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «Политическая борьба в России в годы боярского правления (1541–1547)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 2</i>, no. 2 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-v-rossii-v-gody-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1541-1547. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Митрополит Макарий в период боярского правления (1542–1547 гг.)», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 11 (2022): 174–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-makariy-v-period-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1542-1547-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. Г. Давиденко, «Успенский собор Московского Кремля в пожар 1547 года», <i>Вестник сектора древнерусского искусства</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uspenskiy-sobor-moskovskogo-kremlya-v-pozhar-1547-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 23–25, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan want to be crowned as tsar rather than grand prince?","a":"Because the title tied the ruler of Moscow to the emperors of Byzantium and to biblical kingship. His grandfather had used it only occasionally in diplomacy, and a formal coronation made it the ruler's own title at home and in foreign relations."},{"q":"Why did the cap of Monomakh matter at the ceremony?","a":"Because a court legend claimed that a Byzantine emperor had sent it to Vladimir Monomakh of Kyiv. Crowning Ivan with it presented the Moscow dynasty as heirs of Kyiv and of Byzantium at once."},{"q":"Why was foreign recognition of the title slow?","a":"Because neighbouring rulers saw the title as a claim to imperial rank. Even the charter that Constantinople sent in 1561 to confirm it was later found to carry mostly forged signatures."}]},
    "ru-195": {"questions":["An imperial envoy explained that the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meant king in Russian, but that Poles and Bohemians heard it as emperor, so interpreters began translating it that way.","Before 1547 the Slavonic title <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given to biblical kings, Byzantine emperors and the khans of the Golden Horde, who had granted patents of rule to Russian princes."],"abstract":"<b>Tsar</b> is the Slavonic title that church books gave to kings and emperors alike, and Ivan IV made it the formal title of the rulers of Moscow at his coronation in January 1547.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before that it was used for the rulers of the Bible and of Byzantium, and also for the khans of the Golden Horde, the overlords who had issued patents of rule to the Russian princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ivan III had allowed his subjects to call him tsar, and his heirs' advisers drew on a legend that a Byzantine emperor had sent his crown to Vladimir Monomakh of Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An imperial envoy at the court of Vasily III explained that tsar meant king in Russian, but that Poles, Bohemians and other Slavs heard it as emperor, so interpreters began to translate it so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A word for 'possessor' that entered the style with Ivan IV stayed in it for every tsar down to Peter I and vanished only with the imperial title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition abroad was the problem, and an English envoy of the 1580s noted that Tatar and Polish ambassadors refused to call the Russian ruler tsar, that is emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the same envoy left out most of the long style at his audience, the chancellor shouted at him to say out the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new title changed less inside the country than outside, for charters issued after 1547 simply wrote tsar beside grand prince while the ruler's powers stayed as before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Muscovite writers tied the title to the conquest of Kazan in 1552, and in folklore Ivan takes the crown of the Tatar tsar and so becomes tsar himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The court traced the title to Augustus for some correspondents and to Chinggis Khan for others, while an unofficial name, the White Tsar, spread among ordinary book copyists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Sigismund von Herberstein, <i>Notes upon Russia</i>, trans. and ed. R. H. Major, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1851–52), 31–34, https://archive.org/details/notesuponrussiab01herbuoft. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 117–18, 123–26, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 22–23, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Sergey A. Chernyshov, “Ivan the Terrible – Chinggis Khan’s or Augustus’s Descendant: Legitimization of the Supreme Authority of the Moscow Tsardom in Communication Practices of the 15th–16th centuries,” <i>Golden Horde Review</i> 7, no. 1 (2019): 159–174, https://doi.org/10.22378/2313-6197.2019-7-1.159-174. [Open access]","А. И. Филюшкин, «Модель „царства“ в русской средневековой книжности XV–XVI вв.», <i>Герменевтика древнерусской литературы</i>, no. 10 (2000), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/model-tsapctba-v-russkoy-srednevekovoy-knizhnosti-xv-xvi-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. А. Успенский, «Загадочная форма в титуле русских царей», <i>Slověne</i> 9, no. 1 (2020): 163–84, https://doi.org/10.31168/2305-6754.2020.9.1.6. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Усачев, «„Белый царь“ в русских письменных источниках XVI в.», <i>Сибирские исторические исследования</i>, no. 4 (2020): 261–73, https://doi.org/10.17223/2312461X/30/13. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Ковин и В. В. Калмыков, «Использование титулатуры Ивана IV в юридических актах (по материалам Соловецкого монастыря)», <i>Вестник Южно-Уральского государственного университета. Серия: Социально-гуманитарные науки</i> 22, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ispolzovanie-titulatury-ivana-iv-v-yuridicheskih-aktah-po-materialam-solovetskogo-monastyrya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did foreigners translate tsar as emperor?","a":"Because Poles, Bohemians and other Slavs heard the word as the equivalent of emperor, as an imperial envoy explained in the 16th century. Russian interpreters then began to translate it the same way, although in Russian books it was used for kings as well."},{"q":"Why did Polish and Tatar ambassadors refuse to use the title?","a":"Because accepting it would have acknowledged a claim to imperial rank. An English envoy of the 1580s recorded that their refusal to say tsar, and to repeat the ruler's long style, caused quarrels at the Moscow court."},{"q":"Why did the title matter after the conquest of Kazan?","a":"Because the khans of the Horde and its successor states had themselves been called tsars. Taking a Tatar tsardom let Muscovite writers present Ivan as a tsar who had overcome the old overlords, and folklore told the story as Ivan taking the Tatar tsar's crown."}],"wiki":"Tsar"},
    "ru-196": {"questions":["Rumour said Anna Glinskaya had sprinkled Moscow with water in which human hearts were soaked, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed ended her family's dominance at court.","On the third day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> townspeople marched armed to the tsar's residence at Vorobyovo and demanded that Anna and Mikhail Glinsky be handed over."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow uprising of 1547</b> was a rising of the townspeople of the capital on 26–29 June 1547, set off by a great fire on 21 June that destroyed much of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the largest popular rising of the 16th century in Russia, and the only one, and it ended the dominance at court of the Glinskys, the young tsar's maternal kin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fires had already struck Moscow twice in April, and the great bell of the Kremlin had fallen, before the June blaze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The June fire reached the Kremlin, burning the tsar's palace, the treasury and the metropolitan's residence, and Macarius was badly hurt in his escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One chronicle gives 1,700 dead, a Novgorod chronicle 2,700 and another text 3,700, and the Novgorod writer counts 25,000 houses and 250 churches burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The tsar's confessor and some boyars spoke of witchcraft, and a crowd questioned before the burnt cathedral said that his grandmother Anna Glinskaya had sprinkled the streets with water in which human hearts had been soaked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her son Yuri Glinsky fled into the cathedral, where the crowd seized him during the liturgy and killed him, and his body was dragged out of the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three days later townspeople marched to the tsar at Vorobyovo, armed as if for battle according to a Novgorod chronicler, and demanded Anna and her son Mikhail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet historians saw a rising of the poor alone, but a comparison of two chronicles suggests that great nobles may well have taken part, as a later official history claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan told the church council of 1551 that during the fires fear entered his soul and trembling his bones, and that he then recognised his own sins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 127–29, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 291–92, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","В. В. Пенской, «Московский пожар 1547 г. и его политические последствия: опыт реинтерпретации», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 46, no. 3 (2019): 479–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-pozhar-1547-g-i-ego-politicheskie-posledstviya-opyt-reinterpretatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Е. Жуков, «К вопросу о социальном составе участников московского восстания июня 1547 г. (по летописным памятникам Москвы и Новгорода)», <i>Вестник Новгородского государственного университета им. Ярослава Мудрого</i>, no. 83, pt. 2 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-sotsialnom-sostave-uchastnikov-moskovskogo-vosstaniya-iyunya-1547-g-po-letopisnym-pamyatnikam-moskvy-i-novgoroda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Е. Жуков, «Летописные известия о московских пожарах 1547 г.», <i>Вспомогательные исторические дисциплины</i> 35 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/letopisnye-izvestiya-o-moskovskih-pozharah-1547-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «События 30–40-х гг. XVI в. в „царских речах“ на Стоглавом соборе», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 13 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sobytiya-30-40-h-gg-xvi-v-v-tsarskih-rechah-na-stoglavom-sobore. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the crowd blame the Glinskys for the fire?","a":"Because rumours of witchcraft were already spreading, and the Glinskys, the tsar's maternal relatives, were the most powerful family at court and widely resented. When the crowd was asked who had set Moscow alight, it named Anna Glinskaya and her children."},{"q":"Why do historians argue about who took part?","a":"Because the early chronicles describe a rising of ordinary townspeople, while a later official history says boyars encouraged it. Soviet historians dismissed that claim, but a comparison with a Novgorod chronicle suggests that members of the high nobility may well have been involved."},{"q":"Why did the young tsar remember the fires years later?","a":"Because he presented them as the moment that brought him to repentance. In his speech to the church council of 1551 he said that fear entered his soul and that he then recognised his sins and turned to the church."}]},
    "ru-197": {"questions":["Because no official document of the 1550s mentions the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some historians argue that Kurbsky invented it to serve his political quarrel with the tsar.","The disgrace of the priest Sylvester and of Alexei Adashev in 1560 is taken to mark the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had advised Ivan IV since about 1549."],"abstract":"The <b>Chosen Council</b> is the name given to the circle of advisers said to have guided the young Ivan IV and his reforms from about 1549 to 1560, led by the priest Sylvester and the courtier Alexei Adashev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from Prince Andrei Kurbsky, who called them a chosen body in the history of Ivan he wrote in 1564, soon after he had fled to Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No official source mentions such a council, so some historians deny it existed, while others argue that the tsar himself drove the reforms against the interests it represented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The years credited to it saw an assembly in 1549 or 1550, a revised law code, the church council of 1551, the conquest of Kazan in 1552 and the start of the Livonian War in 1558.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its beginning is unclear, and one view holds that power passed first to the Shuiskys after 1547, but its end is dated to 1560, when Sylvester and Adashev fell into disgrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Adashev, a man of modest origin who had served in the royal bedchamber since 1543, was made an okolnichy and put in charge of petitions on the day of the great assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ivan told him to look most carefully into the complaints of the humblest subjects and not to fear the anger of the great lords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 19th-century historian Karamzin wrote that Moscow had to burn for Ivan to be reformed, tying the fire of 1547 to the reforms the council is credited with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kurbsky described a priest who confronted Ivan during the riots of 1547 like an Old Testament prophet and named him as Sylvester, though Sylvester had served the Kremlin cathedral for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Years later the tsar asked Kurbsky in a letter why they had divided him from his wife, who had died in 1560, and blamed the advisers' quarrels with her relatives for the killings that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 123, 129–34, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","В. Ю. Захаров и В. А. Волков, «Избранная Рада и Негласный комитет: сравнительный анализ», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 13, no. 2 (2022): 22–43, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izbrannaya-rada-i-neglasnyy-komitet-sravnitelnyy-analiz. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Сахаров, «Царь Иван Васильевич Грозный и Избранная рада: дискуссия о судьбах русской государственности», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление (государство и общество)</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsar-ivan-vasilievich-groznyy-i-izbrannaya-rada-diskussiya-o-sudbah-russkoy-gosudarstvennosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Митрополит Макарий в период боярского правления (1542–1547 гг.)», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 11 (2022): 174–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-makariy-v-period-boyarskogo-pravleniya-1542-1547-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Пенской, «Московский пожар 1547 г. и его политические последствия: опыт реинтерпретации», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 46, no. 3 (2019): 479–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-pozhar-1547-g-i-ego-politicheskie-posledstviya-opyt-reinterpretatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do some historians doubt that the council existed?","a":"Because it is known from Prince Andrei Kurbsky, who wrote after fleeing to Lithuania and had a political case to make against the tsar. No official document of the time names such a body, so sceptics treat it as Kurbsky's invention."},{"q":"Why was Adashev given charge of petitions?","a":"Because the young tsar presented himself as a protector of ordinary subjects against the abuses of great nobles. At the assembly he raised Adashev in rank and told him to examine the complaints of the humblest people without fear of the powerful."},{"q":"Why did the tsar later blame his former advisers?","a":"Because he connected their fall with the death of his first wife in 1560. In his letter to Kurbsky he claimed that their quarrels with her relatives had driven them apart and led to the killings of boyars that followed."}],"wiki":"Chosen Council"},
    "ru-198": {"questions":["Article 98 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered that new cases be decided on the sovereign's report with the verdict of all the boyars, and then written into the code.","Ivan IV laid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the church council of 1551 for its approval, a revised version of his grandfather's code that contained 100 articles without headings."],"abstract":"The <b>Sudebnik of 1550</b> was the law code of Ivan IV, a revised and enlarged version of his grandfather Ivan III's Sudebnik of 1497, and above all a rule of court procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had 100 articles without headings, which mixed criminal law and criminal procedure with rules on how the courts of the realm were organised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An assembly of 1550 resolved to revise the old code, and in 1551 the tsar laid the new one before the church council to be approved, signed and kept in the treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It made the presence of elected elders and sworn men at the governors' courts obligatory everywhere, and confirmed criminal jurisdiction for elected local officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is often called the first codification of Russian positive law, because it gave written law priority over custom and set out the ruler's prerogatives in a series of articles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its most novel feature was a set of penalties for officials, and the code added ten articles punishing judges and clerks for abuses of office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A boyar, okolnichy, steward or secretary who took a bribe and judged unjustly had to pay the plaintiff's whole claim and treble costs, with further punishment at the sovereign's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It laid the foundations of imprisonment as a regular punishment, applied for example to bystanders who would not leave the ground of a judicial duel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It let a peasant who sold himself into full slavery leave his land at any time without the usual payment, and it raised the fee due for a peasant's dwelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article 98 said that new cases not covered by the code were to be decided on the sovereign's report with the verdict of all the boyars, a phrase still argued over as a limit on royal power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 142–47, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 74, 235, 294–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","К. К. Кораблин, «Эволюция наказания в виде тюремного заключения в Судебнике 1550 года», <i>Образование и право</i>, no. 11 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-nakazaniya-v-vide-tyuremnogo-zaklyucheniya-v-sudebnike-1550-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Р. Загидуллин, «Юридическая ответственность в судебном процессе России по Судебникам 1497 и 1550 гг.», <i>Ученые записки Казанского университета. Серия Гуманитарные науки</i> 161, no. 1 (2019): 32–40, https://doi.org/10.26907/2541-7738.2019.1.32-40. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Проблема хронологической трактовки статьи Судебника 1550 года о введении новых статей», <i>Историческое обозрение</i>, no. 14 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-hronologicheskoy-traktovki-stati-sudebnika-1550-goda-o-vvedenii-novyh-statey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Шатковская и М. Х. Феодорова, «Первая кодификация российского позитивного права: к юбилею Судебника 1550 года», <i>Северо-Кавказский юридический вестник</i>, no. 3 (2020): 22–30, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervaya-kodifikatsiya-rossiyskogo-pozitivnogo-prava-k-yubileyu-sudebnika-1550-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan IV revise his grandfather's code rather than write a new one?","a":"Because the Sudebnik of 1497 already set out a uniform court procedure, and the problem in the 1540s was that local justice had been abused. The revision kept that framework and added rules on elected assessors and penalties for corrupt officials."},{"q":"Why did the code punish judges so heavily?","a":"Because the abuses of governors and their agents during the years of boyar rule were a central complaint. Making a bribe-taking judge repay the whole claim and treble costs put the loss on the official rather than on the plaintiff."},{"q":"Why is article 98 argued over?","a":"Because its wording ties new laws to the sovereign's report and the verdict of all the boyars. Some historians read that as a check on the ruler's power, while others see it as a statement of ordinary procedure, and the phrase has supported very different theories."}],"wiki":"Sudebnik of 1550"},
    "ru-199": {"questions":["Rulings in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on crossing oneself with two fingers became banners of the Old Believers, and the Great Moscow Council of 1666–67 condemned them.","Gifts made to the church during the tsar's minority were annulled under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the book of decisions of the council Metropolitan Macarius led in 1551."],"abstract":"The <b>Stoglav</b>, or 'Hundred Chapters', is the book of decisions of the church council held in Moscow in 1551 under Ivan IV and Metropolitan Macarius, and the name passed from the book to the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar set the agenda in 69 questions, and the answers covered church courts, the conduct of services, the morals of clergy and monks, and customs condemned as pagan or immoral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Court dignitaries and the boyar council sat with the metropolitan and bishops, and the tsar also asked the fathers to approve his new law code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Monasteries were forbidden to acquire princes' patrimonial estates without the sovereign's leave, and gifts made to the church during the tsar's minority were annulled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its financial rulings settled quarrels between bishops and the monastic and parish clergy, in one recent reading, rather than a clash over monastic landholding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The book does not record the council as it happened but arranges its materials, and the date of 23 February 1551 with which it opens is not the date the meetings began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decisions were also sent to the dioceses in bishops' instructions, which survive for Vladimir, Kargopol, Vyazma and Khlepen and word the rulings independently of the book.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fathers ordered schools for training priests and the correction of corrupted service books, though a biographer of 1904 judged most of these reforms a dead letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several rulings on ritual, among them making the sign of the cross with two fingers and singing a double alleluia, later became banners of the Old Believers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Great Moscow Council of 1666–67 condemned those rulings, putting them down to the simplicity and ignorance of the fathers of 1551.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 150–55, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 294–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.169286. [Open access]","Е. А. Ляховицкий, «Царские речи в составе Стоглава и их место в композиции памятника», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 3 (2015): 176–81, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarskie-rechi-v-sostave-stoglava-i-ih-mesto-v-kompozitsii-pamyatnika. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. А. Ляховицкий, «Кодификация решений Стоглавого собора в Стоглаве полной редакции и архиерейских наказных грамотах», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 5 (2016): 410–26, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kodifikatsiya-resheniy-stoglavogo-sobora-v-stoglave-polnoy-redaktsii-i-arhiereyskih-nakaznyh-gramotah. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шапошник, «Финансовые вопросы на Стоглавом соборе», <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/finansovye-voprosy-na-stoglavom-sobore. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Дылевский, «Стоглавый собор: предпосылки, ход, значение», <i>Новосибирский временник</i>, no. 1 (5) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stoglavyy-sobor-predposylki-hod-znachenie. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the book called the Hundred Chapters?","a":"Because its account of the council's decisions is divided into a hundred chapters. The name was at first informal, and over time it passed from the book to the council of 1551 itself."},{"q":"Why did the tsar set the council's agenda?","a":"Because the council followed his reforms of the secular government, and he wanted the church to correct abuses in its courts, services and monasteries. His questions, and the council's answers to them, make up much of the book."},{"q":"Why did the Stoglav matter to the Old Believers?","a":"Because it had approved the sign of the cross with two fingers and the double alleluia. When the council of 1666–67 condemned those rulings, those who kept the old ritual could point to the Stoglav as an earlier authority on their side."}],"wiki":"Stoglav"},
    "ru-200": {"questions":["In the summer of 1550 the government chose 3,000 men to be the first Moscow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a standing infantry armed with arquebuses and divided into six units.","A revolt of four regiments of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1698 brought Peter I hurrying home from Europe, and a decree soon afterwards disbanded the regiments stationed at Azov."],"abstract":"The <b>streltsy</b>, or 'shooters', were the standing infantry of Muscovy, foot soldiers armed with the arquebus who served the tsars from the mid-16th century to the end of the 17th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the summer of 1550 the government chose 3,000 men for the Moscow streltsy and divided them into six units, later called prikazy, and their numbers grew from then on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They differed from the earlier arquebusiers, the pishchalniki, who were often levied for a single campaign, because the streltsy formed a permanent infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each man received a rouble to build a house, a rouble a year in pay, a uniform, powder and measures of flour, and in time land and the right to trade beside his service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English envoy of the 1580s counted 12,000 of them, 5,000 about Moscow and 2,000 'at the stirrup' guarding the tsar, at seven roubles a year with rye and oats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first of them were chosen men, the best shots, who served at first as the tsar's foot guard and were settled in a quarter of their own at Vorobyovo near Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They had a new chain of command, with men in charge of tens, fifties and hundreds under a head, and a separate office, later the Streltsy prikaz, to run them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Moscow garrison grew from 8 to 26 prikazy between 1631 and 1671, and the units were called regiments from 1682.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In May 1682, after the death of Tsar Fyodor, a revolt of the streltsy massacred boyars before the eyes of the young Peter and set up him and his half-brother Ivan as joint rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A new revolt of four regiments in 1698 brought Peter home from Europe, mass executions followed, and a decree of 1698–99 disbanded the regiments stationed at Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 72–73, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 159–60, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","В. Н. Глазьев, «Стрельцы и их начальники в XVI в.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 1, pt. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/streltsy-i-ih-nachalniki-v-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Лобин, «К истории образования стрелецкого войска (несколько замечаний о статье В. Н. Глазьева)», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 1, pt. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-obrazovaniya-streletskogo-voyska-neskolko-zamechaniy-o-statie-v-n-glazieva. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Курбатов, «Русское войско XV–XVII вв.: очерк истории организационных структур», <i>Исторический вестник</i> 44 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkoe-voysko-xv-xvii-vv-ocherk-istorii-organizatsionnyh-struktur. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ю. Романов, «„И на боях бились, не щедя голов своих…“: опыт реконструкции истории 18-го московского стрелецкого полка», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 20, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/i-na-boyah-bilis-ne-schedya-golov-svoih-opyt-rekonstruktsii-istorii-18-go-moskovskogo-streletskogo-polka. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 5, 23–24, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the streltsy different from earlier musketeers?","a":"Because they were a permanent force. The earlier arquebusiers were often called up for a single campaign, while the streltsy served continuously, lived in their own settlements and were paid and supplied by the state."},{"q":"Why were the first streltsy close to the tsar?","a":"Because they were picked as the best shots and served as his foot guard. The government wanted reliable armed men around the ruler as well as a standing infantry for war, and later 2,000 guarded his person at the stirrup."},{"q":"Why did Peter turn against the streltsy?","a":"Because they had taken part in the revolt of 1682 that he witnessed as a boy, and in 1698 four regiments rose again while he was abroad. He returned, punished the rebels harshly and disbanded regiments, ending their place in the army."}],"wiki":"Streltsy"},
    "ru-211": {"questions":["Muscovite envoys abroad were told to deny that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed at all, saying that only ignorant peasants talked of it and that the tsar simply lived where he pleased.","No decree survives that abolished the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but by November 1572 a Lithuanian frontier official reported that the grand prince had broken it up and made peace with his land."],"abstract":"The <b>oprichnina</b> was the separate realm that Ivan IV carved out of Muscovy in 1565, with its own lands, court, council and armed force under his personal rule, while the rest of the country stayed under its old government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word was an old one, for in the age of the appanage princes it had meant a portion set apart, above all the land granted to a prince's widow, and Ivan borrowed it rather than inventing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its territory was not one province but a scatter of towns, districts and quarters of Moscow, and it grew until it covered something like half of the tsar's dominions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Landholders whose estates fell inside it were moved out, often in the depth of winter, and given land elsewhere, while the tsar's chosen servants took their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Muscovite diplomats were ordered to deny that it existed at all, telling foreign courts that there was no oprichnina and that only ignorant peasants spoke of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ivan created it after leaving Moscow without warning at the end of 1564, and he returned in February 1565 only on condition that he could punish traitors as he chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Executions began the very next day, and the tsar later sent lists of his victims to monasteries for prayers, one of which counts 3,470 victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An English envoy of the late 1580s wrote that one side could rob and kill the other with no remedy at law, and that 300 men were slain in Moscow within a single week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians still disagree about its purpose, though its make-up gives clues: until 1570 its commanders came largely from old untitled Moscow boyar families rather than from the princely clans, and most of its members came from the tsar's existing court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No decree abolishing it has survived, for it was taken apart in stages after the disaster of 1571, and by November 1572 a Lithuanian official could report that it had been broken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 75–89, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 241–45, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 263–66, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Когда была отменена опричнина?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-byla-otmenena-oprichnina. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «О персональном составе Опричного двора Ивана Грозного», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 4 (8) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-personalnom-sostave-oprichnogo-dvora-ivana-groznogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Русская военная элита в опричнине и земщине», <i>Знание. Понимание. Умение</i>, no. 4 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-voennaya-elita-v-oprichnine-i-zemschine. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan give his new realm an old name?","a":"Because the word already meant a portion set apart, above all the land given to a prince's widow, and Ivan treated the oprichnina as his own appanage, separate from the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He even planned to leave the main state to his elder son and the oprichnina, as a lesser appanage, to the younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow deny abroad that the oprichnina existed?","a":"Because the division of the realm and the terror that came with it were embarrassing to explain to foreign courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Envoys sent to Poland were told in advance to say there was no oprichnina, that the tsar simply lived where he chose, and that only peasants talked of such a thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date of its end uncertain?","a":"Because no decree abolishing it survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its separate army stopped taking the field after the defeat of 1571, while a letter of November 1572 already reports it broken up, so it seems to have been dismantled step by step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Oprichnina"},
    "ru-212": {"questions":["At Alexandrov the tsar played abbot to three hundred trusted men, and each chosen <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wore a black cassock and joined in a mock monastic round of prayer and feasting.","The official chronicle says Ivan IV enrolled a thousand servitors in 1565, and each new <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swore to forswear friends and kin and to serve the tsar alone."],"abstract":"An <b>oprichnik</b> was a member of the oprichnina, the corps of picked servants that Ivan IV raised in 1565 to guard his person and hunt down treason, and the plural oprichniki became a byword for state terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official chronicle says that the tsar enrolled a thousand princes, nobles and lesser servitors at the start, and the corps later grew to five or six thousand men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each was bound by an oath, which an exiled prince hostile to the tsar said made him forswear friends, brothers and even parents and serve Ivan alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They dressed in black, rode black horses and carried a dog's head and a broom at the saddle, signs that they would bite the tsar's enemies and sweep treason from the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An English envoy of the late 1580s called them 'select men' and explained that the members of both halves of the realm were entered in a book, so that everyone knew who belonged to which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Alexandrov three hundred of them formed a mock monastic brotherhood, with the tsar as abbot, a prince as cellarer, black cassocks and services sung by Ivan himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a letter of 1574 the tsar told one of them that men of his sort had been raised from humble servants, and an English merchant thought so too, yet a recent study of the known names finds that most came from the existing court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Foreigners served in the corps too, among them a German soldier who held estates as an oprichnik and later wrote an account of how the institution ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terror turned on its own makers in the end, and the leading men of the corps, Prince Afanasy Vyazemsky and the Basmanovs, father and son, were put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a fighting force it last took the field on its own in 1570 and 1571, and after its failure against the Crimean khan in May 1571 several of its commanders were executed and it never served separately again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 77–83, 89, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 242–43, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «О персональном составе Опричного двора Ивана Грозного», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 4 (8) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-personalnom-sostave-oprichnogo-dvora-ivana-groznogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Когда была отменена опричнина?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-byla-otmenena-oprichnina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 264, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 163, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the oprichniki carry a dog's head and a broom?","a":"Because the emblems announced their task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dog's head meant that they would worry the tsar's enemies and the broom that they would sweep treason off the face of the land, and the black clothes and horses earned the corps the name 'the Blackness of Hell'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ivan dress his favourites as monks?","a":"Because at Alexandrov he ran the inner circle as a parody of a monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three hundred chosen men wore black cassocks, the tsar called himself abbot and rang the bell for services, and feasting in the refectory followed the prayers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Were the oprichniki really men of low birth?","a":"Not mostly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tsar himself wrote in 1574 that men like the one he was addressing had been raised from humble servants, but a study of the names that survive finds that the corps was drawn largely from the old court, so that noble families were set against one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-213": {"questions":["A list of victims sent by Ivan IV to a monastery for prayers names 1,505 Novgorodians killed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, far fewer than the 60,000 dead claimed by a Pskov chronicle.","A letter said to have been found behind an icon in St Sophia, promising the city to the Polish king, was the pretext for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried out by the oprichniki in 1570."],"abstract":"The <b>massacre of Novgorod</b> was the punitive expedition that Ivan IV led against the city of Novgorod in the winter of 1570, when his oprichniki killed, tortured and robbed its people for weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An informer claimed that the archbishop and leading citizens meant to hand the city to the king of Poland, and a letter said to prove it was found hidden behind an icon in St Sophia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether any real plot lay behind the charge is still unknown, since the papers of the inquiry into the alleged treason later disappeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army laid waste the whole country all along its road from Klin to Novgorod, and on 2 January its outposts surrounded the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Monks and priests were seized and beaten daily for ransom, and townspeople were brought before the tsar a hundred at a time, tortured and thrown into the Volkhov River to drown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The tsar's own list of victims, sent to a monastery so that prayers could be said for them, names 1,505 Novgorodians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>First Chronicle of Pskov</i> put the dead at 60,000, a figure that seems improbable, while an English merchant then in Russia wrote of 700,000 men, women and children slain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shops and warehouses were stripped and razed, the English account describing melted wax and tallow running down the gutters with the blood, and part of the city was later taken into the oprichnina itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city never recovered its old prosperity, and to the same English merchant the slaughter at Novgorod, with the sack of Narva, was the worst of all the tsar's deeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reprisals went on in Moscow, where executions that followed the campaign can be dated from other sources to 25 July 1570.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 250–54, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 161–63, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 265–66, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Я. Г. Солодкин, «Легендарно ли известие Пискаревского летописца о месте московских казней лета 1570 г.?», <i>Вестник «Альянс-Архео»</i> 27 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/legendarno-li-izvestie-piskarevskogo-letopistsa-o-meste-moskovskih-kazney-leta-1570-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Михеев, «Формирование образа Ивана Грозного в английских источниках второй половины XVI века», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 12 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-obraza-ivana-groznogo-v-angliyskih-istochnikah-vtoroy-poloviny-xvi-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 78, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan turn on Novgorod?","a":"Because an informer accused its archbishop and leading men of planning to go over to the Polish king, and a document said to prove it was found where he said it would be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Novgorod had once placed itself under Lithuanian protection, which gave the charge some colour, but the inquiry papers are lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the death tolls differ so much?","a":"Because they come from very different kinds of source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar's own list for prayers names 1,505 Novgorodians, a Pskov chronicle says 60,000, and an English merchant wrote of 700,000, a figure far beyond the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Novgorod never the same again?","a":"Because the expedition destroyed its trade as well as its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shops, warehouses and suburbs were stripped and razed, and people brought in afterwards to resettle the ruined city died of disease there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Massacre of Novgorod","places":["city:1159146503"]},
    "ru-214": {"questions":["A former abbot of Solovetsky, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> agreed to lead the Russian Church in 1566 only after promising in writing not to interfere in the tsar's household or in affairs of state.","Deposed and imprisoned in a monastery at Tver, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said in most accounts to have been strangled by Malyuta Skuratov while the tsar marched on Novgorod."],"abstract":"<b>Metropolitan Philip</b>, a monk from the noble Kolychev family, headed the Russian Church from 1566 to 1568 and openly condemned the oprichnina to Ivan IV's face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Driven from court by the disgrace of his kinsmen, he had become a monk and then abbot of the Solovetsky monastery on the White Sea, where he was known for his virtue and his gift for government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was chosen after Metropolitan Afanasy left the see in May 1566 and after Archbishop Herman of Kazan, first offered it, was driven out for his sharp words against the oprichnina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Philip at first refused unless the oprichnina was abolished, then gave way and promised in writing not to meddle in politics or in the tsar's private life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept, however, the old right of the head of the Church to intercede for the condemned, which the tsar acknowledged in principle and which Philip used on their behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 1568, in the Cathedral of the Dormition in the Kremlin, he refused three times to bless the tsar, who had come in with oprichniki dressed as monks, and then rebuked him before the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A council of bishops staged a trial, and oprichniki burst into the cathedral during his service, tore off his vestments and carried him away on a sledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was shut up in a monastery at Tver, where most accounts say Malyuta Skuratov strangled him in 1569 as the tsar marched on Novgorod, though another version has him burned alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Executions of the servants of the bishop of Kolomna appear in the lists of victims next to Philip's case, a sign that the purge may have reached the men around him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His <i>Life</i>, which survives in six redactions, shaped how later writers judged both the quarrel and the oprichnina, and in 1652 his relics were carried to the Dormition Cathedral in Moscow, the church where the quarrel had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 248–49, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 265, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","И. А. Лобакова, «Житие митрополита Филиппа и исторические сказания о святителе», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 1 (13) (2021): 74–81, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zhitie-mitropolita-filippa-i-istoricheskie-skazaniya-o-svyatitele. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Усачев, «Митрополит Филипп (Колычев) и коломенский епископ Иосиф», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 2 (34) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-filipp-kolychev-i-kolomenskiy-episkop-iosif. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Белов, «Антиопричное выступление Германа Казанского: исторические реалии и их интерпретации», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (30) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/antioprichnoe-vystuplenie-germana-kazanskogo-istoricheskie-realii-i-ih-interpretatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. И. Девина, review of <i>Святой Филипп, митрополит Московский</i>, by Г. П. Федотов (Москва: Стрижев-Центр, 1991), <i>Религия и культура</i> (2000), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/g-p-fedotov-svyatoy-filipp-mitropolit-moskovskiy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Philip agree to become metropolitan at all?","a":"Because the tsar pressed him and conceded something in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philip first demanded the end of the oprichnina, then accepted a promise to keep out of politics, while Ivan recognised his right to plead for the condemned and turn the tsar's anger aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Philip refuse to bless the tsar?","a":"Because he used the only weapon left to him in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the court in the cathedral he listed the tsar's crimes and debauchery, and when ordered to be silent he answered that his silence would lay a sin on the tsar's soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the manner of his death uncertain?","a":"Because the accounts disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most say Malyuta Skuratov strangled him in his prison at Tver in 1569, but other witnesses say he was taken to Alexandrov and burned, and his <i>Life</i> and later tales each tell the story in their own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Philip II, Metropolitan of Moscow","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Philip_Metropolitan_of_Moscow_(icon).jpg/960px-Philip_Metropolitan_of_Moscow_(icon).jpg","title":"Icon of Metropolitan Philip","desc":"Icon of Saint Philip, Metropolitan of Moscow, by Simon Ushakov, second half of the 17th century.","credit":"Icon by Simon Ushakov, 17th century; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_Metropolitan_of_Moscow_(icon).jpg","alt":"A standing bishop with a halo and a white mitre, in red and gold vestments marked with crosses, holding a book against a gold ground."}},
    "ru-215": {"questions":["With the Russian regiments waiting on the Serpukhov road, the Crimean khan reached the capital by a western route and set its suburbs alight, starting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that left most of it in ashes.","The great fortified court of the oprichnina perished in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a German who served in the corps later dated the end of the whole institution from that disaster."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow fire of 1571</b> destroyed most of the Russian capital on 24 May 1571, when the army of the Crimean khan Devlet I Giray reached the city and set its suburbs alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The khan had crossed the Ugra west of Kaluga and come at Moscow from that side, while the main Russian regiments waited for him on the direct road by Serpukhov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ivan IV, who had marched south with the oprichnina army of about 6,000 men, did not stand to fight but withdrew with his sons and his treasure towards the Trinity monastery and beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Contemporaries put the khan's force at about 40,000 horsemen, and the Russian troops under three separate commands may have been roughly as many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The khan's men fired the suburbs, and a strong wind carried the flames across a city built almost wholly of wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An English envoy wrote that most of the city burned within about four hours, and that crowds trying to escape by the gates farthest from the enemy were wedged together three ranks deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave, as hearsay, a death toll of 800,000, a number a later historian called evidently exaggerated, while an English merchant described a river so choked with bodies that it took a year to clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same historian says that everything but the Kremlin was reduced to ashes, and the khan watched the blaze from the Simonov monastery before riding home with tens of thousands of captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The great fortified court of the oprichnina burned with the rest, and a German who served in the corps dated the end of the institution from that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Afterwards the tsar had commanders tortured and executed, and the khan wrote demanding Kazan and Astrakhan and mocking him for refusing battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 164–66, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 268–69, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","В. В. Пенской and Т. М. Пенская, «„Яз деи деда своего и прадеда ныне зделал лутчи…“: поход Девлет-Гирея I и сожжение Москвы в мае 1571 г.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 4 (2013): 183–226, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yaz-dei-deda-svoego-i-pradeda-nyne-zdelal-lutchi-pohod-devlet-gireya-i-i-sozhzhenie-moskvy-v-mae-1571-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Когда была отменена опричнина?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-byla-otmenena-oprichnina. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Пенской, «Сражение при Молодях 28 июля — 3 августа 1572 г.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/srazhenie-pri-molodyah-28-iyulya-3-avgusta-1572-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fire spread so far so fast?","a":"Because Moscow was built almost entirely of wood and a strong wind was blowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once the suburbs were set alight, the flames swept through houses, churches and palaces, and most of the city was gone within a few hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russian army fail to stop the khan?","a":"Because he did not take the road where it was waiting for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The main regiments stood near Serpukhov, while the khan crossed the Ugra to the west and struck at Moscow from an unexpected side, and the forces under separate commands could not join in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the fire matter for the oprichnina?","a":"Because the disaster exposed the corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its fortified court in Moscow burned, its commanders were among those executed afterwards, and from then on it never took the field again as a separate army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fire of Moscow (1571)","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ru-216": {"questions":["A nephew of Sahib I Giray, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the Crimean throne in a palace coup set in motion by Istanbul and ruled until his death in 1577.","An English envoy heard that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent the Russian ruler a knife to stab himself with, taunting him for daring neither to fight nor to trust his own subjects."],"abstract":"<b>Devlet I Giray</b> was khan of the Crimean Tatars from about 1551 to 1577, the ruler who burned Moscow in 1571 and fought Ivan IV for a quarter of a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was a nephew of Khan Sahib I Giray and came to power in a palace coup that Istanbul set in motion, in which his uncle lost his throne and his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coup is usually dated to the autumn of 1551, though a recent study of Russian chronicles, service books and diplomatic letters argues for the autumn of 1550.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His khanate stood under the Ottoman sultan, and Ottoman archives keep many documents on relations between the two courts during his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1569 his horsemen joined an Ottoman army in a failed siege of Astrakhan, the Volga city that Muscovy had recently conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> His raid of 1571 outdid those of his uncles, for he beat the Russian army under the walls of the capital, burned it and withdrew unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then wrote that he had sought the tsar in the field, that Ivan had shown neither honour nor dignity in avoiding him, and that he wanted Kazan and Astrakhan rather than money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An English envoy reported that he sent the tsar a knife to kill himself with, and that the khans claimed Moscow itself and the land up to it as their ancient right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His second campaign, in 1572, ended in defeat at Molodi south of Moscow, a milestone that helped to secure Muscovy's hold on the newly conquered Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He died in 1577 after a reign of almost 25 years, and historians count him among the most vivid rulers of the Giray dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["В. В. Пенской, «К вопросу об обстоятельствах дворцового переворота и времени воцарения в Крыму Девлет-Гирея I», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i> 12, no. 1 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-ob-obstoyatelstvah-dvortsovogo-perevorota-i-vremeni-votsareniya-v-krymu-devlet-gireya-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Мустакимов, «Османские документальные источники по истории крымско-османских отношений периода правления хана Девлет-Гирея I (1551–1577)», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 7 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osmanskie-dokumentalnye-istochniki-po-istorii-krymsko-osmanskih-otnosheniy-perioda-pravleniya-hana-devlet-gireya-i-1551-1577. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Пенской and Т. М. Пенская, «„Яз деи деда своего и прадеда ныне зделал лутчи…“: поход Девлет-Гирея I и сожжение Москвы в мае 1571 г.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 4 (2013): 183–226, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yaz-dei-deda-svoego-i-pradeda-nyne-zdelal-lutchi-pohod-devlet-gireya-i-i-sozhzhenie-moskvy-v-mae-1571-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 268–69, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","А. В. Малов, «Молодинская битва в контексте военно-политической ситуации в мусульмано-христианской контактной зоне», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/molodinskaya-bitva-v-kontekste-voenno-politicheskoy-situatsii-v-musulmano-hristianskoy-kontaktnoy-zone. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Istanbul want a new khan in Crimea?","a":"Because Sahib I Giray had become too independent for the Ottoman court, at a time when Muscovy was growing stronger on the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A palace coup removed him and put his nephew Devlet on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the khan demand Kazan and Astrakhan?","a":"Because the Crimean khans saw themselves as heirs to the Golden Horde and to its Volga khanates, which Ivan IV had conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After burning Moscow, Devlet wrote that money was useless to him and that what he wanted was those two cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was 1572 a turning point for him?","a":"Because his second march on Moscow ended in defeat at Molodi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The failure became a milestone in securing Muscovy's hold on the newly conquered Volga lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Devlet I Giray"},
    "ru-217": {"questions":["The veteran Crimean commander Divey Murza was taken prisoner at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the defeat that ended the khan's hopes of repeating his burning of Moscow the year before.","Contemporary Russian sources on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> say nothing of a Tatar rout in 1572, explaining the khan's retreat instead by God's mercy and his fear of fresh troops."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Molodi</b> was fought over several days between about 28 July and 2 August 1572 south of Moscow, when Russian regiments stopped a second invasion by the Crimean khan Devlet I Giray.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After burning Moscow the year before, the khan had demanded Kazan and Astrakhan, and a new defeat might have cost Ivan IV both and left him paying tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian army under Prince Mikhail Vorotynsky guarded the line of the Oka, but the khan reached the river on 27 July and crossed it, and a later history places the decisive fighting on the banks of the Lopasnya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vorotynsky's regiments followed and fought him near the village of Molodi from a hill held with a gulyay-gorod, a mobile field fort of wooden walls whose name means 'walking town'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Crimean commander Divey Murza, a veteran raider of the Russian frontier, was taken prisoner during the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 2 August the Tatars stormed the fort, and by that evening the khan was withdrawing towards the Oka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many modern accounts describe a rout and a slaughter of the fleeing Tatars, but the contemporary Russian sources stop at the hand-to-hand fighting and explain the retreat by God's mercy and the fear of fresh Muscovite troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor do any documents support the common claim that Ottoman janissaries fought in the khan's army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The size of both armies is unknown, and one estimate puts the force that stormed the hill on 2 August at 10,000 to 15,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, the khan's failure helped to secure Muscovy's hold on the Volga, and many historians link this joint victory of oprichnina and zemshchina troops with the end of the oprichnina soon afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["В. В. Пенской, «Сражение при Молодях 28 июля — 3 августа 1572 г.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/srazhenie-pri-molodyah-28-iyulya-3-avgusta-1572-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А.-Х. А. Шейхумеров, «Мифологии и реалии битвы под Молодями», <i>Крымское историческое обозрение</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mifologii-i-realii-bitvy-pod-molodyami. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Г. Бахтин, «Жизнь и смерть мангытского карачи-бека Дивея», <i>Золотоордынское обозрение</i>, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zhizn-i-smert-mangytskogo-karachi-beka-diveya. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Комаров, «Источники о битве при Молоди», <i>Крымское историческое обозрение</i> 12, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istochniki-o-bitve-pri-molodi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 269, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","А. В. Малов, «Молодинская битва в контексте военно-политической ситуации в мусульмано-христианской контактной зоне», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/molodinskaya-bitva-v-kontekste-voenno-politicheskoy-situatsii-v-musulmano-hristianskoy-kontaktnoy-zone. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Когда была отменена опричнина?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-byla-otmenena-oprichnina. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was so much at stake in 1572?","a":"Because the khan had burned Moscow the year before and now meant to finish the job.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defeat could have cost Ivan IV Kazan and Astrakhan and bound him to pay the khan tribute, so the battle decided more than one campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians fight from a field fort?","a":"Because a fortified hill gave the army a strong point that the Tatar horsemen had to attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gulyay-gorod on a hill near Molodi, about 1.5 kilometres (1 mile) long, became the centre of the fighting, and the khan's great assault of 2 August was made against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians argue about how great the victory was?","a":"Because the sources are thinner than the legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Contemporary Russian accounts mention no panic or slaughter of fleeing Tatars, the size of both armies is unknown, and no document shows janissaries in the khan's army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Molodi"},
    "ru-218": {"questions":["From the winter of 1571–72 the service books began to list commanders of the oprichnina and of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the same regiments, a first sign that the division was ending.","An English envoy of the late 1580s glossed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as 'the commons', the half of the realm whose people the tsar's chosen men could rob and kill without redress."],"abstract":"The <b>zemshchina</b>, from the Russian word for 'land', was the larger part of Muscovy that Ivan IV left under its old government in 1565 when he set the oprichnina apart as his own domain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It kept the existing offices and courts and was run by a council of boyars, who referred only the gravest matters of state and of war to the tsar himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar charged it 100,000 roubles to cover the cost of his move, and it lost land as the oprichnina grew to cover something like half of the realm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English envoy of the late 1580s glossed it as 'the commons', the half of the people whom the tsar had put outside his protection, so that his own men could rob and kill them without redress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the two administrations also worked side by side, and in 1570 boyars of both met separately on a question of the Lithuanian frontier and reached an agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the winter of 1571–72 the service books began to place commanders of the zemshchina and the oprichnina in the same regiments without saying which each belonged to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many historians have linked the end of the division to the joint victory over the Crimean khan at Molodi in 1572, though a close reading of the sources suggests the process had begun a year earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A German who had served in the oprichnina wrote that its men had to give back the estates they had seized, so that every surviving landholder of the zemshchina recovered his plundered property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He added that anyone who so much as mentioned the oprichnina afterwards was stripped to the waist and flogged with the knout in the market place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The division returned in another form in the autumn of 1575, when the tsar again split the country into his own appanage and the zemshchina, briefly setting a baptised Tatar prince, Simeon Bekbulatovich, at its head with the title of tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 78–81, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 244–45, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Д. М. Володихин, «Когда была отменена опричнина?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-byla-otmenena-oprichnina. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Корзинин, «Царский удел и владения земщины в послеопричный период (1573–1584 гг.)», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 122–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarskiy-udel-i-vladeniya-zemschiny-v-posleoprichnyy-period-1573-1584-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 264, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan leave most of the realm to the boyars?","a":"Because he could neither govern without the old administration nor replace it quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept it running in the zemshchina under a boyar council and took for himself only the lands and men he wanted for the oprichnina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the division come to an end?","a":"Because it had failed in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Separate commands fared badly against the Crimean khan in 1571, commanders of both halves were then put into the same regiments, and by November 1572 the oprichnina was reported broken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the zemshchina disappear after 1572?","a":"No.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the autumn of 1575 the tsar divided the country again into his own appanage and the zemshchina, the general fund of state lands, and the arrangement lasted until his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Zemshchina"},
    "ru-219": {"questions":["Buried in the Cathedral of the Archangel in November 1581, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the dynasty with only a feeble younger brother and an infant to inherit, ending the hope of a capable successor.","An English merchant claimed that a dispute over five or six post horses led the tsar to strike <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the ear, and that the heir died of fever three days later."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan Ivanovich</b> was the eldest surviving son and heir of Ivan IV, and his death in November 1581, after a quarrel in which his father struck him, left the dynasty with no capable successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was brought up as the future tsar, and his father's will left the main body of the state to him and the oprichnina, as a lesser appanage, to his younger brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the Crimean khan reached Moscow in 1571, he fled from the city with his father, his brother, the treasure and the household guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He married three times, and his first two wives were shut up in convents for failing to bear children, the second at his father's insistence around 1579.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners taken by the Poles in 1581 spoke of quarrels between father and son, one claiming that the heir pressed for troops to fight the king's army before Pskov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He died on 19 November 1581 at the Alexandrovskaya Sloboda and was buried three days later in the Cathedral of the Archangel in the Moscow Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The papal envoy Antonio Possevino wrote that the tsar's anger fell first on his pregnant daughter-in-law for wearing one gown instead of three, that he struck his son with his staff when he defended her, and that the son died five days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English merchant gave another cause, a quarrel over post horses and the heir's sympathy for foreigners, and said the young man died of fever three days after a blow to the ear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An English envoy of the late 1580s wrote that the father's grief after this blow with his staff never left him until it brought him to his grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some writers have argued from tests on his bones that illness or its treatment killed him, but specialists in the period mostly accept that the blow did, while admitting that no document proves it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Л. Ю. Таймасова, «Смерть царевича Ивана Ивановича: закулисная дипломатия и „дело о ямских лошадях“», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 3 (23) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smert-tsarevicha-ivana-ivanovicha-zakulisnaya-diplomatiya-i-delo-o-yamskih-loshadyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Ищенко, «„Иван Грозный и сын его Иван 16 ноября 1581 года“: убийство или миф?», <i>Клио</i>, no. 2 (230) (2026): 226–29, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ivan-groznyy-i-syn-ego-ivan-16-noyabrya-1581-goda-ubiystvo-ili-mif. [in Russian] [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 165, 195, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 21, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 279, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 80, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did his death matter so much?","a":"Because he was the only son fit to rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His brother Fyodor was weak in body and mind and the youngest son was an infant, so the dynasty's future now depended on heirs who could not carry it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the stories of the quarrel differ?","a":"Because they come from foreigners who heard them second hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners spoke of a row about the war, Possevino of an attack on the heir's pregnant wife, and an English merchant of a dispute over post horses and sympathy for foreigners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is it certain that his father killed him?","a":"No.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most specialists accept the blow as the cause, and many sources mention it, but none is a document from the court, and some writers have argued from studies of his bones that illness or its treatment was to blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tsarevich Ivan Ivanovich of Russia","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_IV%2C_%D1%86%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B8_%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%A4%D1%91%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%80.png/960px-%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_IV%2C_%D1%86%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B8_%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%A4%D1%91%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%80.png","title":"Ivan IV with his sons","desc":"Ivan IV and the tsareviches Ivan and Feodor with the icon of Our Lady of Kazan, a miniature from a 17th-century illuminated manuscript.","credit":"Miniature from the illuminated Tale of the Kazan Icon, 17th century; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_IV%2C_%D1%86%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B8_%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%A4%D1%91%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%80.png","alt":"A manuscript miniature: a crowned tsar holds an icon above two young princes, while bearded men in long robes stand facing them."}},
    "ru-220": {"questions":["Commanding the Muscovite forces in Livonia in 1564, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> abandoned his wife and young son and crossed over into the service of the Polish king.","Ivan IV's first reply to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled about 62 of the hundred printed pages of their exchange, insisting that Russia's autocrats had always ruled their lands themselves."],"abstract":"<b>Andrey Kurbsky</b>, born around 1528 into a princely house that traced its line to Rurik, was a commander and writer who deserted Ivan IV in 1564 and then argued with him in a famous exchange of letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had fought against Kazan and in Livonia and had been a friend of the tsar and of his advisers Adashev and Silvester, whose fall left him exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1564, while commanding in Livonia, he abandoned his wife and young son and crossed into the service of the Polish king, and he later fought against his own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He chose Lithuania partly because it was near and partly because of its religious tolerance and the freedoms of its nobles, which its kings used to attract Muscovite servitors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In exile he wrote a hostile <i>History</i> of the reign, dividing it into a good period under wise counsellors and a criminal one after their fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The letters ran from 1564 to 1579, two from the tsar and three or four from the prince, and Ivan's first filled about 62 of the 100 pages of one printed edition, while the whole exchange survives in more than 80 manuscript copies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were probably open letters meant to be read by others rather than carried by courier, which makes the famous story of the servant whose foot the tsar pinned with his staff a legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kurbsky asked why the tsar had killed 'the strong ones of Israel', the commanders God had given him, and warned him of divine judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan replied that Russia's autocrats had always ruled their lands themselves, not through boyars, and that he was free to reward his servants and free to punish them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each wrote past the other, the prince defending rule with a council of boyars and the tsar unlimited personal power, and a recent study calls the exchange a watershed in Russian political thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, trans. C. J. Hogarth (London: J. M. Dent, 1912), 65–72, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 230–32, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 261–63, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","В. А. Исаков, «Переписка Ивана Грозного с Андреем Курбским в развитии политической коммуникации», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 1 (21) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/perepiska-ivana-groznogo-s-andreem-kurbskim-v-razvitii-politicheskoy-kommunikatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Черняев, «У водораздела русской политической мысли: переписка Андрея Курбского с Иваном Грозным», <i>Философский журнал</i> 9, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/u-vodorazdela-russkoy-politicheskoy-mysli-perepiska-andreya-kurbskogo-s-ivanom-groznym. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Н. Бекасова, «Лингвистические аспекты прений Андрея Курбского с Иваном IV», <i>Русин</i>, no. 75 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lingvisticheskie-aspekty-preniy-andreya-kurbskogo-s-ivanom-iv. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kurbsky flee in 1564?","a":"Because he feared the tsar's anger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had lost a battle in Livonia and was close to Adashev and Silvester, who had already fallen, so he left his family behind and went over to the Polish king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tsar answer a deserter at such length?","a":"Because the letters were public statements, not private notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ivan wrote to defend his whole idea of autocracy, and his first reply ran to more than half of the entire correspondence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What were the two men really arguing about?","a":"About who should share in power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kurbsky held that a good tsar rules with the advice of his boyars, while Ivan insisted that the autocrat alone governs and is free to reward or punish his servants as he wills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Andrey Kurbsky","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Facial_Chronicle_-_b.23%2C_p._006.jpg/960px-Facial_Chronicle_-_b.23%2C_p._006.jpg","title":"Kurbsky at Novgorodok","desc":"Andrey Kurbsky in the capture of Novgorodok, a miniature from the Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible, 16th century.","credit":"Miniature from the Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible, 16th century; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.23%2C_p._006.jpg","alt":"A chronicle page: soldiers and horsemen fight before a walled town, above lines of red and black Cyrillic script."}},
    "ru-241": {"questions":["Only one family of chronographs names the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so some historians suspect that the number in the council's name was a figure of speech rather than a count.","Prince Mstislavsky, the premier boyar of Muscovy, led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose authority collapsed once Moscow burned in March 1611 and the Poles shut themselves inside the Kremlin."],"abstract":"The <b>Seven Boyars</b> is the name given to the council of great nobles that held power in Moscow after Tsar Vasily Shuisky was deposed on 17 July 1610, in the middle of the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name rests on a chronograph written soon after the Troubles, which speaks of seven boyars who took power once the royal diadem had been stripped away, and later writers made it the label of the whole interregnum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Poles entered the Kremlin, the boyars' council effaced itself and was succeeded by a small band of boyars headed by Prince Mstislavsky, the premier boyar of Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether a fixed body of exactly seven ever existed is doubted, since the number appears in only one family of sources and some historians read it as a figure of speech, like the proverb about seven nannies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study concludes that the government probably did number seven for its first two months, from late July to September 1610, after which its membership changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Fearing the second False Dmitry, whom many townsmen favoured, the leading boyars, Mstislavsky first among them, backed the Polish prince Władysław, and in August 1610 Moscow swore allegiance to him on terms agreed with the hetman Stanisław Żółkiewski.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the night of 20 to 21 September 1610 they let Polish troops into the Kremlin, which gave the foreigners command of the capital, and Żółkiewski left his lieutenant Gonsiewski in charge of the garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The older view that the treaty was the selfish work of a narrow boyar clique has been called a patriotic legend, since townsmen and hundreds of gentry also pressed their own terms on the hetman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In March 1611 fighting broke out between the garrison and the people of Moscow, and the Poles set fire to the city and shut themselves inside the Kremlin and Kitay-gorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The boyars who stayed with them lost all authority in the country and remained besieged until the starving garrison surrendered in late October 1612.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Я. Г. Солодкин, «Существовала ли Семибоярщина в годы Московской смуты?», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/suschestvovala-li-semiboyarschina-v-gody-moskovskoy-smuty. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 157–61, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 44, 57, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 180–83, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Б. Н. Флоря, «Прокопий Ляпунов и Сигизмунд III», <i>Славянский альманах</i> (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prokopiy-lyapunov-i-sigizmund-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Э. Цветков, «Великий печальник за Отечество. Патриарх Гермоген» (continued), <i>Гуманитарные науки. Вестник Финансового университета</i> (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-pechalnik-za-otechestvo-patriarh-germogen-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the boyars prefer a Polish prince to the second False Dmitry?","a":"Because they feared the pretender's Cossack host, and the Moscow crowd that favoured him, more than a foreign king's son. The treaty they made bound Władysław to rule with the boyars' council and a national assembly, which protected their own position."},{"q":"Why is the name Seven Boyars doubted?","a":"Because the number seven appears in only one family of narrative sources, a chronograph and the texts that copy it. Some historians think it was a figure of speech, though one study argues that the council really did have seven members for its first two months."},{"q":"Why did the boyars lose their authority?","a":"Because they let a foreign garrison into the Kremlin and stayed with it after it burned the city in March 1611. To most Russians they now looked like traitors working for the Polish king, so no one obeyed their orders."}],"wiki":"Seven Boyars"},
    "ru-242": {"questions":["In 1617 the Polish prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marched on Moscow with a court of Russian boyars and clerks, styling himself tsar and grand prince of all Rus'.","Not until the treaty of Polyanovka in 1634 did <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by then king of Poland, give up all his rights to the throne of Muscovy."],"abstract":"<b>Władysław IV Vasa</b> was the son of King Sigismund III of Poland, a prince who was chosen tsar of Muscovy in 1610 during the Time of Troubles and who later reigned in Poland–Lithuania as king himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first offer came from Russians of the Tushino camp, who asked Sigismund for his son on condition that he govern with the boyars' council and a national assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their treaty, concluded outside Smolensk on 4 February 1610, also set out rights for the subject, among them the rule that no one should be punished without trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1610, with Vasily Shuisky deposed, a meeting in Moscow elected Władysław on condition that he embrace Orthodoxy and keep Muscovy independent of Poland, and the city kissed the cross to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sigismund wanted Muscovy for himself rather than for his son, so he refused the Russian terms, detained the grand embassy sent to him and pressed on with the siege of Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The older picture of the August treaty as the work of a narrow boyar clique has been challenged, for townsmen and gentry crowded to the hetman's negotiations to insist on Orthodoxy and on the country's borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even Prokopy Lyapunov, soon the leader of the first rising against the Poles, at first counted among the prince's supporters before he turned against the king late in 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1613 the electoral assembly in Moscow rejected every foreign candidate, yet in 1617 Władysław marched on Moscow to claim the throne, using the full title of tsar and taking a court of Russian boyars and clerks with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His night assault on Moscow in October 1618 was beaten off, and the Truce of Deulino that followed said nothing about his claim, which he therefore kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only in 1634, as king of Poland, did Władysław give up all his rights to the Muscovite throne under the treaty of Polyanovka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 38–43, 59, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 157–58, 170, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 180–82, 190–91, 197–98, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Б. Н. Флоря, «Прокопий Ляпунов и Сигизмунд III», <i>Славянский альманах</i> (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prokopiy-lyapunov-i-sigizmund-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. К. Яворская, «„Государев двор“ королевича Владислава Вазы в 1617–1618 гг.», <i>Славянский альманах</i> (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarev-dvor-korolevicha-vladislava-vazy-v-1617-1618-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Н. Жукович, «Деулинское перемирие Москвы с Польшей в связи с историческими обстоятельствами эпохи», <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 10 (1904): 493–509, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deulinskoe-peremirie-moskvy-s-polshey-v-svyazi-s-istoricheskimi-obstoyatelstvami-epohi. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sigismund refuse to let his son go to Moscow?","a":"Because he meant to keep Muscovy for himself rather than hand it to Władysław on the Russians' conditions. Those conditions, Orthodoxy for the prince and the return of conquered land, could also have cost Sigismund his own crown in Poland."},{"q":"Why did Russians accept a foreign prince at all in 1610?","a":"Because the alternative was the second False Dmitry and his Cossack host, whom the boyars feared more. Many townsmen and gentry also took part in shaping the terms, insisting on Orthodoxy and on keeping the country whole."},{"q":"Why did the claim outlive the Troubles?","a":"Because the Truce of Deulino in 1618 was only a truce, and it said nothing about Władysław giving up the crown. He kept the claim until the treaty of Polyanovka in 1634, when he renounced it as king of Poland."}],"wiki":"Władysław IV Vasa"},
    "ru-243": {"questions":["Before he led the Russian Church, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served as a priest in Kazan and later wrote a tale of the finding of the city's wonder-working icon of the Mother of God.","Shut in a cell of the Chudov Monastery, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in February 1612, starved to death according to some contemporaries, months before the militia freed Moscow."],"abstract":"<b>Patriarch Hermogenes</b>, who lived from about 1530 to 1612, was head of the Russian Church from 1606 and the churchman who most stubbornly resisted Polish rule in Moscow during the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He served as a priest in Kazan and took part in the finding of the icon of the Mother of God of Kazan, which tradition dates to 1579 and about which he later wrote a long tale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Widowed in 1587, he became a monk and in 1589 archbishop of Kazan and Astrakhan, and from 1602 he was the first bishop of that see to hold the rank of metropolitan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After he defied the first False Dmitry over the pretender's Catholic bride, a church council of 1606 under the new tsar Vasily Shuisky chose him as patriarch in place of the pretender's appointee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newly found pastoral letter shows another side of him, as a bishop scolding priests and parishioners for careless services and for hiring clergy cheaply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> When the boyars accepted the Polish prince Władysław in 1610, Hermogenes demanded that the prince first become Orthodox, and he refused to sign letters placing the country at the will of King Sigismund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Christmas Day 1610 he called on Russians to rise against Sigismund, and the commander of the Polish garrison placed him under arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even as a prisoner he sent appeals to the towns, warning them against the Cossacks as well as the Poles, and his last letter went to Nizhny Novgorod, the town that raised the second militia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Poles kept him in a cell of the Chudov Monastery in the Kremlin, where he died on 17 February 1612, and contemporaries disagreed whether he was starved, smothered by smoke or strangled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1613 he was remembered as the man who had named the young Michael Romanov as the proper successor to Vasily Shuisky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["С. Э. Цветков, «Великий печальник за Отечество. Патриарх Гермоген», <i>Гуманитарные науки. Вестник Финансового университета</i> (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-pechalnik-za-otechestvo-patriarh-germogen. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Э. Цветков, «Великий печальник за Отечество. Патриарх Гермоген» (continued), <i>Гуманитарные науки. Вестник Финансового университета</i> (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-pechalnik-za-otechestvo-patriarh-germogen-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 158–62, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 61, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 184, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Н. В. Савельева, «Вновь найденное послание патриарха Гермогена священникам и мирянам», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 69, no. 1 (2024): 209–32, https://doi.org/10.21638/spbu02.2024.115. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Макашова, «Традиции ноябрьских праздников в истории России: символическое содержание праздников», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 6</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/traditsii-noyabrskih-prazdnikov-v-istorii-rossii-simvolicheskoe-soderzhanie-prazdnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hermogenes oppose Władysław's election?","a":"Because he insisted that a tsar of Moscow must be Orthodox. He would accept the prince only if Władysław was baptised into the Orthodox faith and the Poles left Moscow, and he refused to sign letters putting the country at King Sigismund's will."},{"q":"Why did his letters matter so much?","a":"Because Russia had no tsar and its boyar government sat under a Polish garrison, so the patriarch was the one figure many Russians still trusted. His appeals helped rouse the towns, first behind Lyapunov's militia and later in Nizhny Novgorod."},{"q":"Why is the manner of his death uncertain?","a":"Because he died a prisoner of the Poles in the Kremlin, with no impartial witnesses. Contemporaries gave different accounts: some said he was starved to death, others that he was smothered by stove smoke or strangled."}],"wiki":"Patriarch Hermogenes of Moscow","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BD.png/960px-%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BD.png","title":"Patriarch Hermogenes","desc":"Patriarch Hermogenes as drawn in the Tsarsky titulyarnik, the royal book of titles of 1672–1673.","credit":"Tsarsky titulyarnik, 1672–1673; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BD.png","alt":"An old bearded patriarch in a white cowl, holding a staff, inside an oval frame of painted flowers."}},
    "ru-244": {"questions":["A Ryazan nobleman who had first backed the Polish prince, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned against King Sigismund late in 1610 and called on all the land to march on Moscow.","The treaty signed before Moscow on 30 June 1611 bound <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and two Cossack leaders, a ruling trio, to take no important step without a council of the whole camp."],"abstract":"<b>Prokopy Lyapunov</b> was a nobleman of Ryazan who led the provincial gentry of the southern borderlands in the Time of Troubles and in 1611 headed the first national militia raised to drive the Poles from Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His family was a branch of an old Ryazan line that had sunk into the ordinary service gentry, and in 1602 he held the third-largest land allotment among Ryazan's chosen nobles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1606 he joined the rising against Tsar Vasily Shuisky and helped lead the march on Moscow, but that November, as quarrels grew between Cossacks and gentry in the rebel camp, he went over to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An older account calls him resolute, overbearing and tempestuous, a man whose hand set to work before his head had thought it through, and his hailing of Prince Skopin-Shuisky as tsar damaged the prince at his uncle's court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like much of the elite he at first backed the Polish prince Władysław in 1610, but by the end of that year he had turned against King Sigismund and called on all the land to gather before Moscow and choose a sovereign in council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ryazan was among the first towns to rise, and by Easter 1611 the militia was besieging the Polish garrison in the Kremlin and Kitay-gorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its council chose a government of three, Lyapunov with the Cossack leaders Prince Dmitry Trubetskoy and Ivan Zarutsky, and on 30 June 1611 it signed a treaty of all the land that ordered runaway peasants returned to their old masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The triumvirate split over the Cossacks, for Lyapunov sided with the gentry against their plundering while his two colleagues defended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 July 1611 by the Julian calendar he was called into a Cossack circle and cut down, after letters forged in his name and ordering Cossacks to be killed had been spread among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With his death the gentry went home and the first militia broke up, leaving the Cossacks before Moscow, too few to take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 43–44, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 159–61, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","Б. Н. Флоря, «Прокопий Ляпунов и Сигизмунд III», <i>Славянский альманах</i> (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prokopiy-lyapunov-i-sigizmund-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Д. Назаров, «Феномен П. П. Ляпунова: провинциальное дворянство и политическая борьба в годы Смуты», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-p-p-lyapunova-provintsialnoe-dvoryanstvo-i-politicheskaya-borba-v-gody-smuty. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Туфанова, «Рассказы об убийстве Прокопия Ляпунова в сочинениях современников о Смуте», <i>Вестник славянских культур</i> (2021): 114–22, https://doi.org/10.37816/2073-9567-2021-60-114-122. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 182–83, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first militia fall apart?","a":"Because it joined gentry, townsmen and Cossacks whose interests clashed. The gentry wanted runaway peasants returned and Cossack plundering stopped, the Cossacks refused, and when they killed Lyapunov the gentry went home."},{"q":"Why did Lyapunov turn against the Poles?","a":"Because King Sigismund would not send his son on the agreed terms and treated Moscow as a conquered city. Lyapunov, who had at first supported Władysław, called instead for all the land to gather before Moscow and choose a sovereign in council."},{"q":"Why are there different stories of his death?","a":"Because writers of the time told it to suit their own purposes. Many agree that forged letters in his name, said to order the killing of Cossacks, were spread in the camp before he was cut down in the Cossack circle."}],"wiki":"Prokopy Petrovich Lyapunov"},
    "ru-245": {"questions":["Lacking any rank in the state's order of offices, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was styled 'the man chosen by all the land' by the clerks of the militia he financed and supplied.","On 12 July 1613 the young Tsar Michael raised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a meat trader of Nizhny Novgorod, to the Duma rank of dumny dvoryanin for his part in freeing Moscow."],"abstract":"<b>Kuzma Minin</b> was a townsman and meat trader of Nizhny Novgorod who, as the town's elected elder, organised and financed the second national militia that freed Moscow from the Poles in 1612.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost nothing certain is known of his life before 1611, for the documents cover only about five years from then on, and neither the date nor the place of his birth can be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The popular story that he came from Balakhna, like the claim that he was of Tatar descent, has not stood up to examination of the records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1611 he persuaded his fellow citizens to give a third of each householder's yearly income to raise an army, and the town and its neighbours then entrusted their funds to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The famous words of his appeal, offering to sell homes and pawn wives and children, come from accounts written years or decades later, such as a tale of the late 1620s that paints him as an ideal hero, and they differ among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Minin chose Prince Dmitry Pozharsky as the militia's commander and served as its treasurer, and the clerks invented a new title for him, the man chosen by all the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the spring of 1612 the militia made Yaroslavl its temporary capital, where its council of all the land gathered nobles, clergy, townsmen and peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In August 1612, when the hetman Chodkiewicz attacked the militia outside Moscow to relieve the Polish garrison, Minin asked Pozharsky for a few companies and led an attack that turned the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 12 July 1613 the new tsar made him a dumny dvoryanin, the third rank of the Duma, a striking rise for a butcher in a society ordered by birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sent to the Kazan district at the turn of 1616 to investigate unrest, he died on the way back, at some time between March and June of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["А. В. Морохин and А. А. Кузнецов, «Кузьма Минин: факты и легенды», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kuzma-minin-fakty-i-legendy. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Б. Перхавко, «Военно-организаторская деятельность Кузьмы Минина», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i> (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-organizatorskaya-deyatelnost-kuzmy-minina. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 57–58, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 162, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 183–85, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","А. В. Морохин and А. А. Кузнецов, «Спорные моменты биографии Кузьмы Минина», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/spornye-momenty-biografii-kuzmy-minina. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Туфанова, «Кузьма Минин в „Повести о победах Московского государства“: к вопросу о способах изображения героя», <i>Вестник славянских культур</i> (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kuzma-minin-v-povesti-o-pobedah-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-k-voprosu-o-sposobah-izobrazheniya-geroya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Minin given a new title?","a":"Because no rank in the Muscovite order fitted a townsman running a national army's money. The clerks coined 'the man chosen by all the land', which stood well above his old post as elder of Nizhny Novgorod."},{"q":"Why are the details of his famous appeal doubtful?","a":"Because every version of it was written decades after 1611, and the versions differ. The vivid phrases later readers knew best were gathered into one story by a historian long afterwards, adding details the early sources lack."},{"q":"Why did Minin's charge in August 1612 matter?","a":"Because the hetman Chodkiewicz was trying to break through to the starving Polish garrison in the Kremlin. Minin's attack with a few companies routed part of his force, heartened the militia and helped drive the relief army back."}],"wiki":"Kuzma Minin","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Kuzma_Minin_engraving.png/960px-Kuzma_Minin_engraving.png","title":"Kuzma Minin, an engraving","desc":"Kuzma Minin, engraved by G. A. Afanasyev in 1821 for a collection of portraits of Russians.","credit":"Engraving by G. A. Afanasyev, 1821; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuzma_Minin_engraving.png","alt":"An oval engraved portrait of a bearded man in a plain shirt, his arm stretched out, with a Cyrillic caption beneath."}},
    "ru-246": {"questions":["In October 1612 the starving Polish garrison of the Kremlin surrendered to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, four days after the Russians had stormed the neighbouring quarter of Kitay-gorod.","A prince of the Starodub line who held only the rank of stolnik before the Troubles, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made a boyar in 1613 yet lost precedence disputes to better-born rivals."],"abstract":"<b>Dmitry Pozharsky</b> was the prince who commanded the second national militia, the army raised in Nizhny Novgorod that drove the Polish garrison out of Moscow in 1612 and ended the foreign occupation of the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in 1577 or 1578, he came from the Starodub princes, a branch of the house of Rurik, but his family had sunk in the service ranks and none of its members had reached the boyars' council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Troubles he held only the court rank of stolnik, roughly equal to a colonel, and he had never served as a governor of note.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fought in the Moscow rising of 1611 and was recovering from his wounds at Suzdal when Kuzma Minin invited him to lead the new army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His circular to the towns refused to recognise Władysław, the son of Marina Mniszech or any new pretender, and so turned the militia against the Cossacks as well as the Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In July 1612, hearing that Sigismund was sending reinforcements, Pozharsky hurried from Yaroslavl towards Moscow, where the Cossacks of the first militia already stood under Prince Dmitry Trubetskoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 22 to 24 August he fought off the hetman Chodkiewicz, who was trying to bring supplies to the starving garrison, while Trubetskoy was slow to come to his aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Russians stormed Kitay-gorod on 22 October, and four days later the Polish garrison in the Kremlin, exhausted by hunger, surrendered to Pozharsky, who with Trubetskoy then headed the provisional government that summoned an assembly to elect a tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The feast of the icon of Our Lady of Kazan, which tradition says went with his army, came to mark the taking of Kitay-gorod, and the army built the Kazan Cathedral on Red Square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Made a boyar in 1613, he still suffered under the precedence system, and in one dispute he was forced to yield to the better-born Saltykov and walk to his rival's house in humiliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Д. М. Володихин, «Князь Д. М. Пожарский как представитель русской служилой аристократии», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/knyaz-d-m-pozharskiy-kak-predstavitel-russkoy-sluzhiloy-aristokratii. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 162–63, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 184–85, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 58, 71–72, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","В. Б. Перхавко, «Военно-организаторская деятельность Кузьмы Минина», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i> (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-organizatorskaya-deyatelnost-kuzmy-minina. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Макашова, «Традиции ноябрьских праздников в истории России: символическое содержание праздников», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 6</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/traditsii-noyabrskih-prazdnikov-v-istorii-rossii-simvolicheskoe-soderzhanie-prazdnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Павлов, «Правящие круги России на завершающем этапе Смутного времени», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 63, no. 3 (2018): 729–47, https://doi.org/10.21638/11701/spbu02.2018.304. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Pozharsky's army oppose the Cossacks too?","a":"Because the Cossacks of the first militia had killed Lyapunov and kept Marina Mniszech's son as their candidate for tsar. Pozharsky's circular refused every pretender, and Nizhny Novgorod made clear that its army was meant for the Cossacks as well as the Poles."},{"q":"Why did the Kremlin garrison surrender?","a":"Because Chodkiewicz's relief army had been beaten back in August and could not bring in supplies. Cut off and starving, the Poles held out until late October 1612, days after the Russians took Kitay-gorod."},{"q":"Why did nobles quarrel with him after 1613?","a":"Because the precedence system ranked men by their family's past service, not by their own deeds. The Pozharskys had held only minor posts, so better-born nobles refused to stand below him, and in one dispute he was made to yield to Saltykov."}],"wiki":"Dmitry Pozharsky","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Boyarin_Dmitry_Mikhailovich_Pozharsky.png/960px-Boyarin_Dmitry_Mikhailovich_Pozharsky.png","title":"Dmitry Pozharsky, an engraving","desc":"Pozharsky engraved by G. A. Afanasyev in 1821 after an old portrait, for a collection of portraits of Russians.","credit":"Engraving by G. A. Afanasyev, 1821, after an unknown painter; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boyarin_Dmitry_Mikhailovich_Pozharsky.png","alt":"An oval engraved portrait of a bearded man in a patterned robe, holding a mace."}},
    "ru-247": {"questions":["With members questioned rank by rank, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no law fixing when it met or who sat in it, yet the first Romanov tsar called it again and again.","In 1653 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advised the tsar to take Bohdan Khmelnytsky's Cossacks under Moscow's rule, among the last great acts of an assembly first called by Ivan IV."],"abstract":"A <b>Zemsky Sobor</b>, or 'assembly of the land', was a gathering of representatives of the clergy, the boyars, the service gentry and the townsmen that the rulers of Muscovy summoned in the 16th and 17th centuries to advise on the gravest affairs of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first is usually dated to 1549 under Ivan IV, who called it a council of all the land, and further assemblies met in 1566 and 1598.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members gave their opinions, often questioned rank by rank, but no law ever fixed when it should meet, who should sit in it or what it could decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Tsar Michael it met at the councils of 1618, 1619, 1621, 1632 and 1634, and for about ten years after 1613 it sat almost without a break, approving important measures on behalf of the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most assemblies were called when the treasury was empty, and that of 1653 advised the tsar to accept Bohdan Khmelnytsky's Cossacks into Moscow's allegiance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The most famous assembly was the one called after the liberation of Moscow in 1612, when the militia's government asked the towns to send elected deputies to choose a sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Representatives of about fifty towns gathered in January 1613, and their first decision was that no prince of another faith, Polish or Swedish, should be chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some recent work argues that the militia's leaders and many deputies had at first leaned towards the Swedish prince Charles Philip, so the outcome was less certain than the official account suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cossacks and the provincial deputies pressed for Michael Romanov, the assembly first voted for him on 7 February, and after sounding out the towns it proclaimed him tsar on 21 February 1613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians still argue whether this was a free choice by the whole land or one forced by the Cossacks, whose decisive part in the election is now widely accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Р. Р. Ласынов, «Земский Собор — орган сословного представительства Московского царства XVI–XVII вв.», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 2 (2023): 74–99, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zemskiy-sobor-organ-soslovnogo-predstavitelstva-moskovskogo-tsarstva-xvi-xvii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 59–61, 206–9, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 163–64, 168, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","Б. Н. Флоря, «Избрание царя Михаила», <i>Исторический вестник</i> (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izbranie-tsarya-mihaila. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Лисейцев, «Земский собор 1613 г.: выборы без выбора или выбор без выборов?», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.2.12. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Павлов, «Царь Михаил Романов: вхождение во власть», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.2.13. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 38, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the assembly of 1613 reject foreign candidates?","a":"Because the Troubles had taught the deputies to fear a ruler of another faith. Polish rule in Moscow had ended in fire and siege, so the assembly resolved that neither a Polish nor a Swedish prince, nor anyone non-Orthodox, should be chosen."},{"q":"Why did Tsar Michael keep calling the assembly?","a":"Because his new government needed help to pacify the country and to raise money. An assembly gave moral support and approved levies on behalf of the whole country, which made them easier to collect."},{"q":"Why did the assembly never become a parliament?","a":"Because no law fixed its meetings, its membership or its powers. Its share in lawmaking was a favour granted by the tsar rather than a right, and it won no lasting guarantees in return for the money it approved."}],"wiki":"Zemsky Sobor"},
    "ru-248": {"questions":["With stone walls begun in 1586 on gifts from the Godunov family, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Kostroma was hailed under Nicholas I as the cradle of the House of Romanov.","Tradition says a Tatar noble baptised as Zachariah founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1330, where the nun Martha and her son sheltered behind strong walls in 1613."],"abstract":"The <b>Ipatiev Monastery</b> is a fortified Orthodox monastery of the Holy Trinity at Kostroma on the Volga, where the young Michael Romanov was living when envoys from Moscow came in March 1613 to offer him the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tradition says it was founded in 1330 by a Tatar noble called Chet, baptised as Zachariah, from whom the Godunovs traced their descent, though its origins are disputed and one recent view credits Grand Prince Ivan Kalita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its stone walls and holy gates were begun in 1586 with gifts from Dmitry Ivanovich Godunov and Boris Godunov, as a surviving building inscription records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After their release from the Kremlin in 1612, Michael went to his estates near Kostroma and then took refuge in the strongly fortified monastery, where his mother, the nun Martha, was staying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An old tradition adds that a peasant, Ivan Susanin, saved Michael by leading a band of enemy soldiers astray into the forest, where he died with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After the assembly proclaimed Michael tsar on 21 February 1613, an embassy that included Archbishop Feodorit and the boyar Fyodor Sheremetev set out to find him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The envoys reached Kostroma on the evening of 13 March and next morning went in procession to the monastery, and one account says they first took the city's revered Feodorovskaya icon from its cathedral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Michael refused with tears and great wrath, and his mother protested that he was too young and that the people had betrayed earlier tsars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only after hours of pleading, when the envoys warned that he would answer to God for the ruin of the country, did he consent on 14 March 1613 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the 19th century Nicholas I had the so-called Romanov chambers restored as a memorial, and from his reign the monastery came to be called the cradle of the House of Romanov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ферапонт (Д. В. Кашин), «К вопросу о времени и обстоятельствах основания Ипатьевского монастыря», <i>Ипатьевский вестник</i>, no. 1 (2024): 48–70, https://doi.org/10.24412/2309-5164-2024-1-48-70. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Г. Авдеев, «Строительная надпись 1586 г. из Троицкого Ипатьевского монастыря и ансамбль Святых ворот конца XVI в.», <i>Ипатьевский вестник</i>, no. 3 (2022): 62–88, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroitelnaya-nadpis-1586-g-iz-troitskogo-ipatievskogo-monastyrya-i-ansambl-svyatyh-vorot-kontsa-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 164, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 38–39, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Ферапонт (Д. В. Кашин), «Шествие московского посольства 14 марта 1613 г. в Ипатьевский монастырь: версии события», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i> 28, no. 1 (2022): 21–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shestvie-moskovskogo-posolstva-14-marta-1613-g-v-ipatievskiy-monastyr-versii-sobytiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Никифорова, «Романовские кельи в Костромском Ипатьевском монастыре: музей в сценариях власти Российской монархии», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 6</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/romanovskie-keli-v-kostromskom-ipatievskom-monastyre-muzey-v-stsenariyah-vlasti-rossiyskoy-monarhii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Павлов, «Царь Михаил Романов: вхождение во власть», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.2.13. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Michael at the Ipatiev Monastery in 1613?","a":"Because after his release from the Kremlin in 1612 he went to his family's estates near Kostroma, where armed bands still roamed. The fortified monastery, where his mother was staying, gave the two of them shelter."},{"q":"Why did Michael and his mother at first refuse the crown?","a":"Because the throne looked deadly and the country was in ruins. His mother said he was too young and that the people had betrayed earlier tsars, and only hours of pleading from the envoys changed his mind."},{"q":"Why is it called the cradle of the House of Romanov?","a":"Because it was there that the first Romanov tsar accepted the throne. Under Nicholas I the rooms said to have been the family's were restored as a memorial, and the phrase became part of the dynasty's own story."}],"wiki":"Ipatiev Monastery"},
    "ru-249": {"questions":["The tsar's father, Metropolitan Philaret, came home from Polish captivity in 1619 under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which set an armistice of fourteen and a half years.","Because neither side would yield its claims after Władysław's failed march on Moscow, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was only an armistice and did not openly recognise Michael Romanov as tsar."],"abstract":"The <b>Truce of Deulino</b> was the armistice that Muscovy and the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth concluded on 1 December 1618 by the Julian calendar, 11 December by the Gregorian, ending the fighting that had begun in the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was agreed at the village of Deulino near the Trinity-Sergius Monastery, where the two sides' commissioners had met from 23 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The talks followed the failure of Prince Władysław's campaign of 1617 and 1618, launched to set him on the Russian throne, whose night assault on Moscow in October was beaten off with heavy loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With winter coming and his unpaid army close to mutiny, Władysław agreed to negotiate, and a truce rather than a peace was made, since neither side would give up its claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was to last fourteen and a half years, and it established peaceful relations between the two states after the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Muscovy paid heavily, ceding to Poland–Lithuania Smolensk, Chernigov, Starodub, Novgorod-Seversky and a string of other towns and districts on its western border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The treaty did not openly recognise Michael Romanov as tsar, nor did it record that Władysław renounced the Muscovite crown, which he went on claiming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did provide for an exchange of prisoners, and the most important of them was the tsar's father, Metropolitan Philaret, held in Poland since the failed embassy to Sigismund in 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The exchange took place in 1619 on two bridges over the little river Polyanovka, after months of delay and quarrelling over the handover of the border towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philaret came home to become patriarch and co-ruler with his son, and some border disputes the truce left open were settled only in 1634, by the treaty that ended the Smolensk War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Tomasz Bohun, “Exchange of Captives within the Framework of the Truce of Deulino,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4: Istoriya</i> 24, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.2.15. [in Polish] [Open access]","П. Н. Жукович, «Деулинское перемирие Москвы с Польшей в связи с историческими обстоятельствами эпохи», <i>Христианское чтение</i>, no. 10 (1904): 493–509, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deulinskoe-peremirie-moskvy-s-polshey-v-svyazi-s-istoricheskimi-obstoyatelstvami-epohi. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 190–91, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 169–70, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 92, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","М. К. Яворская, «„Государев двор“ королевича Владислава Вазы в 1617–1618 гг.», <i>Славянский альманах</i> (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarev-dvor-korolevicha-vladislava-vazy-v-1617-1618-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Deulino a truce and not a peace?","a":"Because neither side would give up its claims. Michael would not permanently surrender Smolensk and the other towns, and Sigismund and Władysław would not abandon their claim to the Muscovite throne, so the parties agreed only to stop fighting for fourteen and a half years."},{"q":"Why did Moscow accept such heavy losses?","a":"Because the country was exhausted after the Troubles and Władysław's army had just reached Moscow itself. Peace in the west also brought home the tsar's father, Philaret, and other prisoners held in Poland since 1610 and 1611."},{"q":"Why did the prisoner exchange take so long?","a":"Because the two sides quarrelled over carrying out the treaty, including the handover of the border towns and the new frontier. The Russian envoys waited for months before the two groups of captives crossed the bridges over the Polyanovka in 1619."}],"wiki":"Truce of Deulino"},
    "ru-250": {"questions":["When a holding passed 'from livingness to emptiness' and became a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its taxpayers were gone, and after the Troubles neighbours might plough its land as 'strange' tillage.","In a register of 1628 a landlord recorded that he had settled his household slaves 'among the peasantry' on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an empty holding left by the Time of Troubles."],"abstract":"A <b>pustosh</b>, from the Russian word for 'empty', was the term Muscovite land registers used for a deserted settlement or holding whose people had died or gone, though others might still plough or resettle its land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The registers listed such places beside inhabited villages, and a holding passed, in their phrase, 'from livingness to emptiness' when its taxpayers were gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Empty holdings were already multiplying in the central provinces in the late 16th century, as peasants moved to the outlying lands and tillage shrank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Time of Troubles turned this drift into a catastrophe, for according to a contemporary writer ploughing ceased almost everywhere and people lived on old stocks of grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners who travelled through Muscovy soon after Michael's accession described burned villages whose deserted huts were choked with corpses, so that even in winter they slept in the open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The surveys made after the Troubles show the cost: a census of 1624 of a church estate on the Lezha river in the Vologda region counted 59 pustoshi, and 67 of its 147 settlements stood empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its scribes recorded why, noting peasants killed by 'Lithuanian people', others who died or left without trace, and old pustoshi so overgrown with forest that no one could find them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the walls of Galich a survey of 1620 found 57 empty homesteads beside 123 inhabited ones, and it explained that many townsmen had been cut down in a Lithuanian raid or had wandered off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Census registers show one place where peasants had tilled more than thirty times as much land at the close of the 16th century as the taxable land left to them in 1616.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To fill the empty land, landlords settled household slaves 'among the peasantry' on pustoshi, and the government extended the period for reclaiming runaway peasants to ten and then fifteen years, steps on the road to serfdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 86–87, 175–77, 234–35, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","S. F. Platonov, <i>History of Russia</i>, trans. E. Aronsberg, ed. F. A. Golder (London: Macmillan, 1925), 171–73, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.13563. [Open access]","Н. В. Башнин, «Вотчина Вологодского архиерейского дома св. Софии на Лежском Волоке по сотной 1624 г.», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i> (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/votchina-vologodskogo-arhiereyskogo-doma-sv-sofii-na-lezhskom-voloke-po-sotnoy-1624-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Абеленцева, «Описания вотчины Успенского Тихвинского монастыря в Николаевском Шунгском погосте Обонежской пятины Новгородского уезда в первой четверти XVII в.», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i> (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/opisaniya-votchiny-uspenskogo-tihvinskogo-monastyrya-v-nikolaevskom-shungskom-pogoste-obonezhskoy-pyatiny-novgorodskogo-uezda-v-pervoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Кузьмичёв, «Социально-экономическое развитие города Галича в первой четверти XVII века», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i> (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialno-ekonomicheskoe-razvitie-goroda-galicha-v-pervoy-chetverti-xvii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 39–40, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many settlements become pustoshi?","a":"Because the Troubles brought war, raids and hunger at once. The Vologda census of 1624 records peasants killed by 'Lithuanian people', others who died, and many who left without trace, so whole villages stood empty."},{"q":"Why did empty land matter to the state?","a":"Because taxes fell on inhabited, 'living' land and were paid by communities of peasants. When holdings passed into emptiness the tax base shrank, so one of the new dynasty's first tasks was to register the people and restore the communes."},{"q":"Why did desolation push Russia towards serfdom?","a":"Because landlords short of labour fought to keep the peasants they had. The government lengthened the time allowed for recovering runaways, and landlords settled slaves on empty holdings, tying more people to the land."}],"wiki":"Pustosh"},
    "ru-221": {"questions":["Fishermen near the mouth of the Dvina fled from the great ship of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and then told his men that their country was called Russia, or Muscovy, ruled by Ivan Vasilyevich.","Wrecked in Pitsligo Bay in 1556, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drowned with many of his crew while bringing home Osip Nepeya, the first Russian envoy ever sent to the English court."],"abstract":"<b>Richard Chancellor</b> was the English pilot who in 1553 sailed round the north of Norway into the White Sea and travelled overland to Moscow, opening the first direct sea route between England and Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He sailed as pilot major of a three-ship expedition under Sir Hugh Willoughby, sent out by London merchants who hoped to reach Cathay, that is China, by a north-eastern passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A storm off the Norwegian coast scattered the fleet, and Chancellor, after waiting seven days at the agreed meeting place of Vardø, went on alone in the <i>Edward Bonaventure</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Willoughby's two ships were later found in an Arctic bay with every man aboard dead of cold and hunger, so the voyage meant for China ended in the ice for its commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Chancellor reached Moscow, was received by Ivan IV, and carried home a letter in which the tsar allowed English subjects to visit his lands and trade freely there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The account of the voyage, written in Latin by the schoolmaster Clement Adams from Chancellor's own telling, has him sail on until he found no night at all but continual daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fishermen in a great bay, amazed by the size of his ship, fled and then prostrated themselves before him, and from them the English learned that the country was called Russia, or Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Kholmogory on the Northern Dvina he was received by the local governor, and the English were afterwards closely linked with that town and with the nearby monastery of Saint Nicholas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In his own description of the country, Chancellor judged Moscow greater than London with its suburbs, though it was built of timber, very rude and dangerously exposed to fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Returning from a second voyage in 1556 with Osip Nepeya, the first Russian envoy to England, he was wrecked off the Scottish coast in Pitsligo Bay and drowned, while the envoy survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Clement Adams, “The Newe Navigation and Discoverie of the Kingdome of Moscovia, by the Northeast, in the Yeere 1553,” in Richard Hakluyt, <i>The Principal Navigations, Voyages, Traffiques and Discoveries of the English Nation</i>, vol. 2 (Glasgow: James MacLehose and Sons, 1903), 245–51, https://archive.org/details/principalnavigat02hakl. [Open access]","Richard Chancellor, “The Booke of the Great and Mighty Emperor of Russia, and Duke of Moscovia,” in Richard Hakluyt, <i>The Principal Navigations, Voyages, Traffiques and Discoveries of the English Nation</i>, vol. 2 (Glasgow: James MacLehose and Sons, 1903), 224–26, https://archive.org/details/principalnavigat02hakl. [Open access]","E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, eds., <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia by Anthony Jenkinson and Other Englishmen</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886; repr., New York: Burt Franklin), i–iv, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 272–74, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Г. Н. Питулько, «Англичане в России XVI века: опыт изучения первоначального этапа русско-английских связей», <i>Proslogion: Проблемы социальной истории и культуры Средних веков и раннего Нового времени</i> 3, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anglichane-v-rossii-xvi-veka-opyt-izucheniya-pervonachalnogo-etapa-russko-angliyskih-svyazey. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Шегулова и О. И. Марискин, «Из истории становления русско-английских торгово-дипломатических отношений: Московская компания», <i>Огарёв-Online</i> 12, no. 8 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-stanovleniya-russko-angliyskih-torgovo-diplomaticheskih-otnosheniy-moskovskaya-kompaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were English ships sailing north-east at all?","a":"Because London merchants found English goods in small demand among their neighbours and saw how Spain and Portugal had grown rich from new trades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They resolved on a new navigation, and the expedition was planned as a search for a north-eastern route to Cathay and India rather than as a voyage to Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the local people not trade with him at once?","a":"Because they were bound by custom not to buy foreign goods without the knowledge and consent of their king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governors fed the English but held them back, day after day, until a messenger sent to the tsar returned with orders to bring them to court and to provide post horses free of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does his voyage matter so much?","a":"Because it laid the foundation of the commerce between England and Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chancellor's second voyage brought agents who negotiated a trading settlement, and the regular sailings that followed gave Russia a direct route to western Europe through the White Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Richard Chancellor","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Facial_Chronicle_-_b.22%2C_p._243_-_English_ambassadors.gif/960px-Facial_Chronicle_-_b.22%2C_p._243_-_English_ambassadors.gif","title":"The English envoys in Moscow","desc":"The reception of the English envoys, Chancellor's embassy, as shown in the Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible, 16th century.","credit":"Miniature from the Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible, 16th century; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.22%2C_p._243_-_English_ambassadors.gif","alt":"A chronicle page of towers, domes and robed figures receiving visitors, above lines of red and black Cyrillic script."}},
    "ru-222": {"questions":["A decree of June 1649, issued after news of the execution of Charles I reached Moscow, expelled the merchants of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Russia and confined them to Arkhangelsk.","Seeking duty-free passage through Astrakhan, agents of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the Caspian Sea towards Persia from the 1560s and carried raw silk home to England."],"abstract":"The <b>Muscovy Company</b>, also called the Russia Company, was the London corporation of merchants that held a royal charter for the trade with Russia opened by Richard Chancellor's voyage of 1553.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its charter of 1555, granted by Philip and Mary, made the merchant adventurers for the discovery of unknown lands one perpetual fellowship and named Sebastian Cabot its first governor for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A list of that year names 207 noblemen, knights, aldermen and others as members, and the risk of each venture was divided into shares, so that each man took part according to what he put in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been called one of the first joint-stock companies in English business, since no single merchant could raise the sums that the long and dangerous northern voyages required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The English won the right to trade in the White Sea and to set up houses of business at Kholmogory, Vologda and Moscow, where an English house soon opened on Varvarka Street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The company's privileges rested both on its English charter and on grants from the tsar, which Ivan IV extended and confirmed when Anthony Jenkinson came to Moscow on its behalf in 1566.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its agents pushed down the Volga and across the Caspian Sea towards Persia from the 1560s, seeking duty-free passage through Astrakhan, and raw silk became the chief good they carried home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The arrangement was never equal in practice, for Russian merchants were promised the same protection in England but had no ships fit for long sea voyages and so could not use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dishonest agents, Dutch rivals and English traders outside the company all threatened the monopoly, and in 1587 one of its own men proposed throwing the trade open to every merchant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After news of the execution of Charles I reached Moscow, a decree of 1 June 1649 expelled the English merchants from Russia and allowed them to come only to Arkhangelsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Philip and Mary, “The Charter of the Merchants of the Moscovie Company,” in Richard Hakluyt, <i>The Principal Navigations, Voyages, Traffiques and Discoveries of the English Nation</i>, vol. 2 (Glasgow: James MacLehose and Sons, 1903), 304–5, https://archive.org/details/principalnavigat02hakl. [Open access]","E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, eds., <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia by Anthony Jenkinson and Other Englishmen</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886; repr., New York: Burt Franklin), iii, xlix–l, lv–lviii, lxvii, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 273–74, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","М. А. Шегулова и О. И. Марискин, «Из истории становления русско-английских торгово-дипломатических отношений: Московская компания», <i>Огарёв-Online</i> 12, no. 8 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-stanovleniya-russko-angliyskih-torgovo-diplomaticheskih-otnosheniy-moskovskaya-kompaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Питулько, «Англичане в России XVI века: опыт изучения первоначального этапа русско-английских связей», <i>Proslogion: Проблемы социальной истории и культуры Средних веков и раннего Нового времени</i> 3, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anglichane-v-rossii-xvi-veka-opyt-izucheniya-pervonachalnogo-etapa-russko-angliyskih-svyazey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ш. А. Магарамов, «Англичане на Каспийском море в XVI в.», <i>Вестник Кемеровского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2017): 62–66, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anglichane-na-kaspiyskom-more-v-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Козулин, «Революционная Англия и царская Россия в середине XVII в.: их отношения и восприятие Англии и англичан в этот период русскими дипломатами и правящей элитой», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i> 2, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/revolyutsionnaya-angliya-i-tsarskaya-rossiya-v-seredine-xvii-v-ih-otnosheniya-i-vospriyatie-anglii-i-anglichan-v-etot-period-russkimi. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the merchants want a royal charter?","a":"Because the charter made them one perpetual body with a governor, consuls and assistants, with power to make rules and enforce fines on those who broke them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In consideration of their opening of intercourse with Russia they were granted the exclusive right of trading with that country, and with any other they might discover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russian merchants not trade in England on equal terms?","a":"Because, although the privileges were meant to be mutual, Russians owned no ships fit to cross the seas on long voyages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trade therefore ran in English ships through the White Sea, and the English houses at Kholmogory, Vologda and Moscow handled it inside Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the company lose its place in Russia in 1649?","a":"Because the execution of Charles I seemed an unthinkable crime to the tsar's government, and a decree of 1 June 1649 sent the English merchants out of the country and limited them to Arkhangelsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The monopoly had long had enemies already, among them Dutch rivals and English interlopers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Muscovy Company"},
    "ru-223": {"questions":["Leading a band that one chronicle numbers at 540 men, the Cossack <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> defeated Khan Kuchum's forces near the Chuvash cape and held his capital at Kashlyk.","Local tradition marks the place near the mouth of the Vagai where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed on the Irtysh during a night attack by Kuchum's men in August 1585."],"abstract":"<b>Yermak</b> Timofeyevich was the Cossack ataman whose band crossed the Urals in the early 1580s, broke the army of Khan Kuchum and occupied his capital, beginning the Russian conquest of Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Almost nothing certain is known of him before the campaign, and he survives mainly in 17th-century chronicles that make him a figure of mixed and half-legendary reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He first appears in an official document in the tsar's angry letter of 16 November 1582, which ordered the Stroganovs, on pain of disgrace, to send 'the Volga Cossacks, ataman Yermak Timofeyev' out of their forts to Perm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Stroganovs, merchants who held royal grants of land along the Kama and from 1574 beyond the Urals, helped to organise the campaign and shaped what Moscow heard of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was killed on the Irtysh in a night attack by Kuchum's men, by the tradition still marked near the mouth of the Vagai on the night of 5 to 6 August 1585, though some accounts place his death in 1584.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The sources disagree about how many men he led, for one Siberian chronicle gives 540 Orthodox warriors while the Stroganov chronicle adds three hundred Lithuanians, Germans and Tatars hired by the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their firearms terrified an enemy who had not met them before, and after defeating the khan's forces near the Chuvash cape the Cossacks held his capital, Kashlyk, also called Isker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The quick victory was followed by nearly thirty years of Tatar resistance, and soon after the Cossacks abandoned the capital in 1585 one of Kuchum's sons took it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the following decades the church turned him into an Orthodox warrior for the faith in a commemoration list of 1622, and a chronicle finished in 1636 filled his story with miracles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The view of Yermak as the conqueror of Siberia came from Cossack tradition almost at once, and most of the text of the Siberian chronicles of the early 17th century is about his campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 277–78, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Я. Г. Солодкин, «О двух спорных вопросах „Ермакова взятия“ Сибири», <i>Вестник «Альянс-Архео»</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-dvuh-spornyh-voprosah-ermakova-vzyatiya-sibiri. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Чернышов, «Конструирование образа Ермака в конце XVI–XVII веках: официальная, церковная и народная практики», <i>Омский научный вестник. Серия «Общество. История. Современность»</i> 6, no. 4 (2021): 40–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konstruirovanie-obraza-ermaka-v-kontse-xvi-xvii-vekah-ofitsialnaya-tserkovnaya-i-narodnaya-praktiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Чернышов, «Строгановское „Присоединение Сибири“ в военно-хозяйственных и коммуникативных практиках», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i> 23, no. 3 (2023): 36–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroganovskoe-prisoedinenie-sibiri-v-voenno-hozyaystvennyh-i-kommunikativnyh-praktikah. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Майничева, «Ермак и место его гибели в народной памяти», <i>Вестник Новосибирского государственного университета. Серия: История, филология</i> 12, no. 7 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ermak-i-mesto-ego-gibeli-v-narodnoy-pamyati. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Менщиков и Д. Н. Маслюженко, «Этнополитические условия падения Сибирского ханства Кучума и начало освоения Сибири русскими», <i>Вестник Курганского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/etnopoliticheskie-usloviya-padeniya-sibirskogo-hanstva-kuchuma-i-nachalo-osvoeniya-sibiri-russkimi. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Алексеев, «История забытого царя», review of <i>Царь Федор Иванович</i>, by Д. М. Володихин, <i>Знание. Понимание. Умение</i>, no. 4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-zabytogo-tsarya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tsar threaten the Stroganovs over Yermak?","a":"Because his letter of 16 November 1582 wanted the Volga Cossacks of ataman Yermak Timofeyev sent out of the Stroganov forts to Perm, and it threatened the family with the tsar's disgrace if they were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The family's grants and their accounts of danger from Siberia gave them great influence over how Moscow saw that frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the size of his band uncertain?","a":"Because the early chronicles give different figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One gives 540 Orthodox warriors, a number most scholars accept, while the chronicle written for the Stroganovs adds three hundred Lithuanians, Germans and Tatars supplied by the family, perhaps to enlarge its own part in the conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the conquest not end with his victory?","a":"Because Kuchum's followers went on fighting for almost thirty years after their first defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yermak himself was killed on the Irtysh, and soon after the Cossacks left the capital in 1585 one of Kuchum's sons occupied it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Yermak Timofeyevich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Yermak_Timofeyevich.jpg/960px-Yermak_Timofeyevich.jpg","title":"Yermak, a parsuna","desc":"A 17th-century portrait of Yermak in the parsuna style, between the icon and the Western portrait.","credit":"Unknown painter, 17th century; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yermak_Timofeyevich.jpg","alt":"A dark portrait of a bearded man in armour and a plumed cap, holding a spear."}},
    "ru-224": {"questions":["Kuchum, a Shibanid backed by Bukhara, took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the Taybugid princes by 1563 and settled Nogai and Kazakh clans along its southern borders.","In 1555 the Taybugid prince Yadigar accepted Moscow's overlordship, binding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a tribute treaty and later asking the tsar in vain for troops."],"abstract":"The <b>Khanate of Sibir</b> was the Tatar state of western Siberia on the Irtysh and the Tobol, whose capital, Isker or Kashlyk, fell to Yermak's Cossacks in the early 1580s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before Kuchum the land was held by the Taybugid princes, whom most neighbours did not count as full sovereigns because they were not descended from Genghis Khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their prince Yadigar accepted Moscow's overlordship and a tribute treaty in 1555, and in 1557 his envoys came to Moscow to acknowledge full vassalage and to ask, in vain, for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kuchum, a prince of the Shibanid branch of Genghis Khan's line, conquered the Taybugid principality between 1555 and 1563 and, with help from Bukhara, restored his dynasty's rule there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kuchum's forces were driven out of the capital by Yermak, and in the reign of Boris Godunov the khan himself was defeated and driven far beyond the Irtysh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Kuchum invited clan leaders of the Nogai and Kazakh hordes to settle on his southern borders, and the newcomers soon swamped the hostile Taybugid nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also promoted Islam as a common faith for his many peoples, a policy that won him control of the land but turned part of the population against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The khanate was divided into large districts called uluses and smaller units called yurts, and small forts on the Irtysh's tributaries, built in the 1570s, guarded its borders and gathered fur tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1571 Moscow sent Kuchum a sworn treaty of allegiance to sign, but within a few years his forces were raiding the Stroganov lands near Perm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many local people saw Kuchum as a conqueror rather than a lawful khan, which helps explain why his army broke so quickly before Yermak even though his followers fought on for almost thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["А. В. Матвеев и С. Ф. Татауров, «Сибирское ханство Кучума царя: некоторые вопросы государственного устройства», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sibirskoe-hanstvo-kuchuma-tsarya-nekotorye-voprosy-gosudarstvennogo-ustroystva. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Менщиков и Д. Н. Маслюженко, «Этнополитические условия падения Сибирского ханства Кучума и начало освоения Сибири русскими», <i>Вестник Курганского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/etnopoliticheskie-usloviya-padeniya-sibirskogo-hanstva-kuchuma-i-nachalo-osvoeniya-sibiri-russkimi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. Н. Маслюженко и Е. А. Рябинина, «Москва и Искер в 1569–1582 гг. в контексте международной политики», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskva-i-isker-v-1569-1582-gg-v-kontekste-mezhdunarodnoy-politiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 277–78, 319–20, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Я. Г. Солодкин, «О двух спорных вопросах „Ермакова взятия“ Сибири», <i>Вестник «Альянс-Архео»</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-dvuh-spornyh-voprosah-ermakova-vzyatiya-sibiri. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Ф. Татауров, «Последняя крепость хана Кучума на Иртыше», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 8 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poslednyaya-krepost-hana-kuchuma-na-irtyshe. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kuchum need newcomers to settle his lands?","a":"Because the Taybugid nobility he had defeated still controlled the local people and resources, and he needed loyal warriors against enemies inside and outside the khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He brought in clan leaders of the Nogai and Kazakh hordes, who settled on the southern borders and outnumbered the opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow claim the khanate before Yermak?","a":"Because the Taybugid prince Yadigar had accepted a treaty of 1555 that bound his land to Moscow, and his envoys acknowledged full vassalage in 1557.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1571 Moscow sent Kuchum a sworn treaty of allegiance to sign as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did resistance last so long after the capital fell?","a":"Because the first battles broke the khan's army but not his people's will to fight on, and his followers resisted for almost thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kuchum himself was defeated and driven far beyond the Irtysh only in the reign of Boris Godunov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Khanate of Sibir"},
    "ru-225": {"questions":["Corrections in the Tsar Book, the last volume of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, reworked the official story of boyar treason and of the succession crisis that shook the court in 1553.","Now divided between Moscow and Saint Petersburg in ten surviving volumes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called a 'historical encyclopedia of the 16th century' by one early student."],"abstract":"The <b>Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible</b>, in Russian the <i>Litsevoy letopisny svod</i>, is a vast illuminated history made for the court of Ivan IV that runs from the Creation of the world to Russian events of 1568.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It fills almost ten thousand large leaves with some sixteen thousand coloured miniatures and has been described as having no equal in medieval history-writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its ten volumes are scattered among the State Historical Museum in Moscow and two great libraries in Saint Petersburg, and an early student of it called it a 'historical encyclopedia of the 16th century'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most specialists now date its making to about 1568–1576, although one view holds that work on its first volume began as early as the 1540s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By December 1576 the part on world history was finished, while at least 40 to 45 per cent of the planned work on the chronicle part still lay ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its last part, on Ivan's own reign and preserved in the volume known as the Tsar Book, is covered with corrections and additions that rework the official account of his early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its pages denounce 'treason' and 'ill will' towards the grand princes and towards Ivan himself, and the figure of his cousin Prince Vladimir of Staritsa was reworked in that spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In September 1581, answering a letter of King Stephen Báthory, Ivan used words that closely echo the chronicle, a sign that it was being read and used at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The corrections may record the tsar's displeasure at its picture of boyar plots and of the succession crisis of 1553, but they have been dated to the 1560s by some historians and to later decades by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why the chronicle was made is also disputed, with readings that range from a claim to great-power status to a picture book for the tsar's children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["О. В. Большакова, review of <i>Лицевой летописный свод Ивана Грозного: комплексное кодикологическое исследование</i>, by А. А. Амосов, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 3 (1999), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/99-03-024-amosov-a-a-litsevoy-letopisnyy-svod-ivana-groznogo-kompleksnoe-kodikologicheskoe-issledovanie-ran-arheogr-komis-m-editorial-urss. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. И. Журова, «Лицевой летописный свод — памятник эпохи Ивана Грозного (Международная научная конференция „Лицевой летописный свод царя Ивана Грозного“, Мюнхен, 7–9 декабря 2011 г.)», <i>Сибирский филологический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/litsevoy-letopisnyy-svod-pamyatnik-epohi-ivana-groznogo-mezhdunarodnaya-nauchnaya-konferentsiya-litsevoy-letopisnyy-svod-tsarya-ivana. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. И. Журова, «Историческая энциклопедия XVI в. (Международная научная конференция „Лицевой летописный свод царя Ивана Грозного“, Мюнхен, 7–9 декабря 2011 г.)», <i>Гуманитарные науки в Сибири</i>, no. 1 (2012): 104, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskaya-entsiklopediya-xvi-v-mezhdunarodnaya-nauchnaya-konferentsiya-litsevoy-letopisnyy-svod-tsarya-ivana-groznogo-myunhen-7-9. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Морозов, «К истории официального летописания и Лицевого летописного свода второй половины XVI века», <i>Культурная жизнь Юга России</i>, no. 4 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-ofitsialnogo-letopisaniya-i-litsevogo-letopisnogo-svoda-vtoroy-poloviny-xvi-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Ю. Ерусалимский, «Лицевой летописный свод в дипломатии Ивана Грозного», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 6 (2017): 24–33, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/litsevoy-letopisnyy-svod-v-diplomatii-ivana-groznogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Митрофанов, «С. Ф. Платонов и А. Е. Пресняков: о первоначальном этапе исследования Царственной книги», <i>Вестник СГУПС: гуманитарные исследования</i>, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/s-f-platonov-i-a-e-presnyakov-o-pervonachalnom-etape-issledovaniya-tsarstvennoy-knigi. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the chronicle so valuable to historians?","a":"Because it tells world and Russian history from the Creation to 1568 on almost ten thousand leaves with some sixteen thousand miniatures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its pictures and text together make it one of the largest monuments of written culture in Russia, which an early student called a historical encyclopedia of the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Tsar Book matter for Ivan's reign?","a":"Because its pages on Ivan's own years were corrected and added to, so the official story of his early reign was rewritten while the work was still in hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The additions stress treason against the grand princes and against Ivan, and the picture of his cousin Prince Vladimir of Staritsa was reworked in that spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date of the corrections disputed?","a":"Because the additions carry no date of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some historians tie them to the tsar's quarrel with Prince Andrey Kurbsky in the 1560s, while others place them in the 1570s or link the reworking to the court's use of the chronicle in diplomacy around 1581.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible"},
    "ru-226": {"questions":["Official documents still used the word 'smuta' until the autumn of 1618, so some historians end the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the Truce of Deulino rather than in 1613.","Contemporaries called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 'the stateless time' and 'the Moscow ruin', and explained its pretenders, risings and invasions as punishment for the sins of all Christendom."],"abstract":"The <b>Time of Troubles</b>, in Russian <i>Smuta</i> or <i>Smutnoe vremya</i>, was the crisis of the Muscovite state between the end of the old ruling dynasty in 1598 and the election of Michael Romanov in 1613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contemporaries already called it 'smuta', with variants such as 'the stateless time' and 'the Moscow ruin', and saw it as punishment for the sins of all Christendom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began with a dynastic break, for the death of the childless Feodor I ended the old line, and a pretender claiming to be Ivan IV's dead son Dmitry soon challenged the elected tsar Boris Godunov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pretenders, peasant and Cossack risings and Polish–Lithuanian and Swedish intervention followed, and every class of society, from boyars to peasants and slaves, took part on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Romanovs came to the throne in 1613, their accession coincided, in one older account, with a powerful awakening of patriotism and a universal longing for order and peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its limits were never fixed, though one contemporary, a monk of the Trinity monastery who described its siege by the Poles, also counted it as fourteen years long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its end is disputed, since the freeing of the Kremlin in 1612, the election of 1613 and the Truce of Deulino of 1 December 1618 have all been proposed, and officials spoke of 'smuta' until the autumn of 1618.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet historians described the events as a peasant war and a foreign intervention, but later work has largely dropped the first label as too narrow and often speaks of a civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> As early as 1591 an English envoy wrote that the hatreds sown by Ivan IV's oprichnina would not be quenched until they burned again into 'a civill flame'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The memory of the Troubles still carries political weight, and the 4 November state holiday, tied to the liberation of Moscow in 1612, has met sharp criticism from many historians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1913), 13–14, 24–26, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 327–45, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Д. В. Лисейцев, «Дискуссионные вопросы в истории Смуты начала XVII в.», <i>Гуманитарный вестник</i>, no. 9 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussionnye-voprosy-v-istorii-smuty-nachala-xvii-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Селин, «Смутное время в историографии последних лет», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 10 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smutnoe-vremya-v-istoriografii-poslednih-let. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Столярова и П. В. Белоусов, «Смерть царевича Дмитрия в Угличе 15 мая 1591 г.: новая версия», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smert-tsarevicha-dmitriya-v-ugliche-15-maya-1591-g-novaya-versiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 34–35, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","А. Ю. Дворниченко, «Смута как фактор российской истории», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 63, no. 3 (2018): 677–701, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smuta-kak-faktor-rossiyskoy-istorii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the end of a dynasty cause such chaos?","a":"Because the death of Feodor I left no heir of the old line, and the man elected in his place had no hereditary claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pretender's claim to be the dead Dmitry then gave every discontented group a cause, and pretenders became a chronic malady of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the end of the Troubles dated differently?","a":"Because the crisis did not stop at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moscow was freed in 1612 and a tsar elected in 1613, but war with the Commonwealth went on until the Truce of Deulino in December 1618, and officials and eyewitnesses went on writing of 'smuta' until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did historians stop calling it a peasant war?","a":"Because the conflict did not divide society horizontally, nobles against peasants, but vertically, with men of every rank on each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word smuta itself, in its familiar meaning, was born in these years, and historians have used it since for later Russian crises too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Time of Troubles"},
    "ru-227": {"questions":["An English envoy described the short, sallow tsar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as unsteady on his feet, inclined to dropsy, 'commonly smiling almost to a laughter' and fond of weekly pilgrimages.","The Peace of Teusina of 1595, which won back Ivangorod, Yam, Koporye and Korela from Sweden, was made in the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Ivan IV's middle son."],"abstract":"<b>Feodor I</b>, Fyodor Ivanovich, was the son of Ivan IV and Anastasia Romanovna who reigned as tsar from 1584 to 1598, the last ruler of the old Moscow line of princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ivan, knowing his weakness, left a council of leading nobles to guide him, and within a few years his brother-in-law Boris Godunov had pushed the others aside and was governing in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reign was nonetheless remembered as a time of rest after the oprichnina, and a Dutch merchant who lived in Moscow wrote that the whole state began to recover and grow rich again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia won back Ivangorod, Yam, Koporye and Korela from Sweden in the Peace of Teusina of 1595, and a Crimean raid that reached the outskirts of Moscow in 1591 was turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His death in January 1598 left no heir, for his younger brother Dmitry had died at Uglich in 1591, and with him the old dynasty came to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Foreign visitors described him as a simple man, and an English envoy wrote of a short, sallow tsar inclined to dropsy, unsteady on his feet and 'commonly smiling almost to a laughter'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same envoy found him gentle, merciful and very devout, a ruler who went on pilgrimage to some monastery or other almost every week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many historians accepted that picture and credited the calm of his reign to Godunov's skill, while a 19th-century playwright imagined Feodor as weak in mind but deep in moral insight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More recent work has challenged the portrait, arguing that Feodor was neither feeble-minded nor mentally ill, but a meek and pious man who consciously served as the guarantee of lawful power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Documents of the time credit the founding of the Moscow patriarchate in 1589 to Feodor and his wife Irina, though historians differ on whether he or Godunov truly drove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 144–45, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1913), 15–18, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 311–17, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Д. С. Точеный и Н. Г. Точеная, «Царствование Федора Ивановича (1584–1598 гг.)», <i>Вестник Волжского университета им. В. Н. Татищева</i>, no. 74 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarstvovanie-fedora-ivanovicha-1584-1598-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Алексеев, «История забытого царя», review of <i>Царь Федор Иванович</i>, by Д. М. Володихин, <i>Знание. Понимание. Умение</i>, no. 4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-zabytogo-tsarya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Дискуссия об учреждении Московской патриархии», <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussiya-ob-uchrezhdenii-moskovskoy-patriarhii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Godunov rule in Feodor's name?","a":"Because Ivan IV, aware of his son's weakness, had left a council of nobles to guide him, and Boris, the tsar's brother-in-law, gradually ousted his fellow regents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Feodor, by one later account, occupied himself only in prayer and humbleness, while Boris received ambassadors almost like a sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his reign remembered as a good time?","a":"Because it came after the terror of the oprichnina and the long Livonian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Contemporaries describe fourteen years of rest, and a Dutch merchant wrote that the ruined state began to recover and grow rich again, while a war with Sweden ended with lost towns regained in 1595.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about his mind?","a":"Because the main descriptions come from foreign envoys who found him simple and slow-witted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some later historians argue that these accounts were unfair and that Feodor, though meek and no active politician, understood his role and stepped in himself when he judged it necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Feodor I of Russia"},
    "ru-228": {"questions":["Rumour blamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the death of the young Dmitry at Uglich in 1591, though no direct evidence has ever linked the regent to the boy's death.","An English envoy of 1588 called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the tsar's 'protectour' and counted his yearly income from lands, offices and pensions at more than 93,700 roubles."],"abstract":"<b>Boris Godunov</b> was the boyar who governed Russia for his brother-in-law Feodor I and then reigned as tsar from 1598 to 1605, the first Russian tsar who was not of princely birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He rose in the service of Ivan IV through the oprichnina, married a daughter of its chief Malyuta Skuratov, and saw his sister Irina married to the tsar's son Feodor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An English envoy of 1588 counted his yearly income from lands and pensions at more than 93,700 roubles and called him the tsar's 'protectour' for command and authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Feodor died childless in January 1598, an assembly of the land headed by Patriarch Job elected Boris tsar, after he had made a show of refusing the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chroniclers praised his wisdom, his generosity to the poor and his care for good order, yet he could not inspire trust and became the favourite target of every sort of rumour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The darkest rumour made him the murderer of Ivan IV's youngest son, Dmitry, who died at Uglich in 1591, though no direct evidence of his guilt has ever been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Englishman at court in those years wrote that a plot had been uncovered to poison the young prince, then kept under close guard with his mother's family in a remote place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1604 the rumour spread that the boy had escaped his killers and that a man calling himself Dmitry was about to come from Lithuania to claim his father's throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boris died in the spring of 1605 as the pretender advanced, and his death opened the way to the worst years of the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians have judged him very differently, some explaining his rise by service to the reigning tsars rather than by ability, while 19th-century Slavophile thinkers rated him highly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1913), 18–26, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 312–20, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 37–38, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, <i>Travels</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 226–27, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Л. Е. Морозова, «Годуновы у царского трона», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 8 (2017): 384–401, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/godunovy-u-tsarskogo-trona. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Мартынова, «Личность и царствование Бориса Годунова в трудах ранних славянофилов», <i>Философская мысль</i>, no. 2 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lichnost-i-tsarstvovanie-borisa-godunova-v-trudah-rannih-slavyanofilov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. С. Точеный и Н. Г. Точеная, «Царствование Федора Ивановича (1584–1598 гг.)», <i>Вестник Волжского университета им. В. Н. Татищева</i>, no. 74 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarstvovanie-fedora-ivanovicha-1584-1598-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Дискуссия об учреждении Московской патриархии», <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussiya-ob-uchrezhdenii-moskovskoy-patriarhii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a boyar become tsar?","a":"Because Feodor I died without an heir and the old dynasty ended with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An assembly of the land headed by Patriarch Job elected Boris, whose long experience of government under Feodor and close family tie to the late tsar made him the obvious candidate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he suspected over Dmitry's death?","a":"Because the boy stood between Boris and the throne, and the Nagoy family would have shown the Godunovs no mercy had Dmitry become tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chroniclers repeated the charge, but only on the strength of hearsay and conjecture, and no direct evidence of his part was ever found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he matter to the Russian Church?","a":"Because the Moscow patriarchate was set up while he governed for Feodor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One historian argues that the plan was his from beginning to end, though the documents of the time give the idea to Feodor and his wife Irina, Boris's sister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Boris Godunov"},
    "ru-229": {"questions":["Refusing to swear allegiance to the first False Dmitry in 1605, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dragged from the Dormition cathedral of the Kremlin and exiled to a monastery at Staritsa.","Heading the assembly that elected Boris Godunov tsar in 1598, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote its resolution claiming that Ivan IV and Feodor had each entrusted the realm to Boris."],"abstract":"<b>Patriarch Job</b> was the first patriarch of Moscow and all Rus', who headed the Russian Church as metropolitan from 1586 and as patriarch from 1589 until he was deposed in 1605.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had been archimandrite of the Dormition monastery at Staritsa under Ivan IV, and he rose to the see of Moscow when Metropolitan Dionisy was deposed for siding with the Shuisky princes against Godunov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His elevation came during the visit of Jeremias II, patriarch of Constantinople, who reached Moscow in 1588 to collect alms and was persuaded to consecrate a Russian patriarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That act finally confirmed the long-pending separation of the Russian Church from the patriarchate of Constantinople, and a council there in 1593 confirmed Moscow in fifth place among the patriarchates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Job headed the assembly that elected Boris Godunov tsar in 1598, and he composed its resolution claiming that Ivan IV and Feodor had both entrusted the realm to Boris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An English envoy then in Moscow recorded that the Greek patriarch processed through the city, laid down his patriarchal staff in the Kremlin's church of Our Lady and saw it taken up by the metropolitan of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same witness thought the Greek had made 'good advantage' of Russian devotion and gone off to Poland 'with a rich bootie', doubting whether the new office would be recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Older histories called Job a tool of Godunov, who wanted a great church authority that would owe everything to him when the tsar died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians still debate whose plan the patriarchate was, for documents of the time give the idea to Feodor and his wife Irina, while others credit Godunov or Metropolitan Dionisy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the first False Dmitry won Moscow in 1605, Job refused to swear allegiance, was dragged from the Dormition cathedral and exiled to Staritsa, where he died in 1607 after losing his sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 104–7, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 316–17, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1913), 19, 26, 303, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Макарий (Веретенников), «Первый патриарх Иов и его время», <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervyy-patriarh-iov-i-ego-vremya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. И. Батюк, «К 420-летию учреждения патриаршества в Русской православной церкви», <i>Вестник Российского экономического университета им. Г. В. Плеханова</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-420-letiyu-uchrezhdeniya-patriarshestva-v-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkvi. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Дискуссия об учреждении Московской патриархии», <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussiya-ob-uchrezhdenii-moskovskoy-patriarhii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia want its own patriarch?","a":"Because Russian churchmen resented obeying patriarchs who lived under Turkish rule, and Moscow saw itself as the heir of Orthodox Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greek patriarch himself argued that his see lay under an enemy of the faith and that the Russian Church was the only free daughter of the Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Job thought to depend on Godunov?","a":"Because he rose to the see of Moscow when his predecessor was deposed for opposing Godunov, and in 1589 his patriarchal rank came with Godunov's help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1598 he led the assembly that elected Boris and wrote its resolution in Boris's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Job deposed in 1605?","a":"Because he had anathematised the first False Dmitry and would not swear allegiance to him once Moscow fell to the pretender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Supporters of the pretender seized him in the Dormition cathedral and sent him to Staritsa, the monastery where his monastic life had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Patriarch Job of Moscow","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%98%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB%D1%8F%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA.png/960px-%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%98%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB%D1%8F%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA.png","title":"Patriarch Job","desc":"Patriarch Job as drawn in the Tsarsky titulyarnik, the royal book of titles of the 1670s.","credit":"Tsarsky titulyarnik, 1670s; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%98%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB%D1%8F%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA.png","alt":"A bearded patriarch in a white cowl and red mantle, holding a staff, framed by painted flowers."}},
    "ru-230": {"questions":["Canonised under Tsar Vasily Shuisky as an innocently murdered child, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had already lent his name to pretenders who claimed to be Ivan IV's miraculously rescued son.","The alarm bell of Uglich rang on 15 May 1591 for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the crowd killed the secretary Bityagovsky and his son as the boy's supposed murderers."],"abstract":"<b>Dmitry of Uglich</b>, Tsarevich Dmitry Ivanovich, was the youngest son of Ivan IV, whose death at Uglich on 15 May 1591, at the age of eight, became one of the key events behind the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was the son of Maria Nagaya, Ivan's last wife, and early in Feodor I's reign he was sent with his mother and her Nagoy relatives away from Moscow to his small appanage of Uglich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English envoy heard that the boy was not safe from attempts by those who aspired to the throne if Feodor died without issue, and that a nurse who tasted his food had died at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The alarm bell of Uglich announced his death in the courtyard of his palace, and the crowd at once killed the secretary Mikhail Bityagovsky, his son and several others as the murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A commission led by Prince Vasily Shuisky concluded that the boy had stabbed himself with a knife during a game, in a fit of the 'falling sickness', the period's name for epilepsy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Nagoy were sent to prison, Maria Nagaya was forced to take the veil, and townspeople who had joined the rising were tortured, executed or exiled to Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rumour at once blamed Boris Godunov, but the chroniclers who repeated the charge relied on hearsay and conjecture, and no direct evidence of his guilt has survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Englishman Jerome Horsey, then at Yaroslavl, wrote that the boy's uncle came to his gate at midnight crying that Dmitry's throat had been cut and his mother poisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pretenders who claimed his name from 1604 led the government of Vasily Shuisky to canonise the 'innocently murdered child', overturning the findings of the very commission Shuisky had led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians still argue between murder and accident, and one study by a historian and a psychiatrist suggests that a prolonged epileptic seizure, not the knife wound, caused his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1913), 18–24, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, vol. 1, trans. L. B. Lang (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1879), 312, 317–18, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia01ramb. [Open access]","Edward A. Bond, introduction to <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i> (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), cix–cx, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","Л. В. Столярова и П. В. Белоусов, «Смерть царевича Дмитрия в Угличе 15 мая 1591 г.: новая версия», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smert-tsarevicha-dmitriya-v-ugliche-15-maya-1591-g-novaya-versiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Столярова и П. В. Белоусов, «Материалы Углического следственного дела о гибели царевича Дмитрия в 1591 г.: новый опыт исторической реконструкции», <i>Люди и тексты. Исторический альманах</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/materialy-uglicheskogo-sledstvennogo-dela-o-gibeli-tsarevicha-dmitriya-v-1591-g-novyy-opyt-istoricheskoy-rekonstruktsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a small boy a danger to anyone?","a":"Because Feodor I had no son, so Dmitry was the likely heir, and his Nagoy kinsmen would have taken power and revenge if he came to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English envoy heard that the boy was not safe from those who hoped to succeed if Feodor died childless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the commission's verdict satisfy few people?","a":"Because it asked minute questions about trifles but left contradictions in the evidence unresolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its finding of a self-inflicted wound served the government well, since it allowed the Nagoy to be punished and removed from any claim to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he made a saint in 1606?","a":"Because pretenders kept claiming to be Dmitry rescued from his killers, and the first had reigned in Moscow for a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By proclaiming the boy an innocently murdered martyr, Vasily Shuisky's government showed that the real Dmitry was dead, though it meant overturning the verdict his own commission had given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dmitry of Uglich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/1899._Tzarevich_Dmitry_by_M._Nesterov.jpg/960px-1899._Tzarevich_Dmitry_by_M._Nesterov.jpg","title":"Tsarevich Dmitry","desc":"Tsarevich Dmitry by Mikhail Nesterov, 1899: the murdered boy pictured as a saint among birches.","credit":"Painting by Mikhail Nesterov, 1899; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1899._Tzarevich_Dmitry_by_M._Nesterov.jpg","alt":"A boy in a white robe with a halo stands among thin birch trees in a green landscape, a church far behind."}},
    "ru-231": {"questions":["Declaring 'forbidden years' from 1581, the Muscovite government suspended even the one lawful departure that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had allowed a peasant once the harvest was in.","In 1601 and 1602 Boris Godunov let small landowners take peasants from one another around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> again, a temporary concession made during the years of famine."],"abstract":"<b>St George's Day</b>, called Yuri's Day in Russian, was the autumn feast of St George on 26 November, and in Muscovy it marked the one season of the year when a peasant might lawfully leave his landlord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The autumn feast was one of the few church holidays of Russian origin, instituted in the 11th century by Yaroslav the Wise, baptised George, to mark the consecration of his church of St George in Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The law code of Ivan III, the Sudebnik of 1497, allowed peasants to move from village to village only in the week before and the week after the autumn feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A departing peasant paid a fee called the pozhiloe, a rouble for a homestead in open country and half a rouble in the forest, owing a quarter of it for each year he had lived there and the whole after four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date fell after the harvest, when the fieldwork of the year was done, and in practice rich landowners bought other men's peasants away by paying off their debts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From 1581 the government began to proclaim 'forbidden years', in which even this one lawful departure was suspended on the private estates of the north-west, the west and the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The forbidden years are last mentioned in 1592, and a decree of 1597 set a limit of five years within which a landlord could sue to recover a runaway peasant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians have long argued over whether a lost decree of about 1592 abolished St George's Day everywhere, and a close study of the surviving documents finds that idea unproven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The older view held that a decree of Tsar Fyodor bound the free peasant to the soil, and that St George's Day remained for centuries a day of bitter regret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Boris Godunov's decrees of 1601 and 1602 let small landowners take over one another's peasants around St George's Day once more, but only as temporary measures during the famine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 200, 221, 234–37, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","В. А. Аракчеев, «Крестьянские переходы по Судебникам и происхождение пожилого», <i>Вестник Новгородского государственного университета им. Ярослава Мудрого</i>, no. 51 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyanskie-perehody-po-sudebnikam-i-proishozhdenie-pozhilogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Аракчеев, «Закрепостительные мероприятия в русском государстве в начале 1590-х гг.», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 81 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zakrepostitelnye-meropriyatiya-v-russkom-gosudarstve-v-nachale-1590-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 315–16, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 167, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","И. А. Новицкий, «От Любеча к Юрьеву дню», <i>Вестник славянских культур</i>, no. 72 (2024): 138–41, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-lyubecha-k-yurievu-dnyu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the moving day set in late November?","a":"Because the harvest was in by then. The fieldwork of the year was done, so a peasant could leave without abandoning a crop, and he and his landlord could settle what each owed the other before he went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government suspend the right to leave?","a":"Because land without peasants paid no tax and kept no soldier. Great landowners lured peasants away from small servicemen, who formed the cavalry, and the forbidden years from 1581 let landlords recover their peasants through the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about when the right ended?","a":"Because the decisive decree, if there ever was one, does not survive. The forbidden years vanish from the record after 1592 and a five-year limit on suits appears in 1597, and a close study of the documents finds no proof of a ban for the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Yuri's Day"},
    "ru-232": {"questions":["A French captain in Moscow blamed the daily alms of Boris Godunov for swelling the death toll of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because the hungry flocked to the capital and died there.","Turned out by masters who could no longer feed them, slaves and runaway peasants roamed the south as brigands during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and an army had to be sent against them."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian famine of 1601–1603</b> was a run of failed harvests across Muscovy in the reign of Boris Godunov that lasted three years and killed people in enormous numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For three years running the crops failed, and plague and pestilence followed the hunger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>New Chronicle</i> blamed the weather, describing rain all summer that left the grain green and unripe until a great frost on the feast of the Dormition killed it all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other Russian writers explained the famine as God's punishment for the people's sins, while a recent study links the bad seasons to a colder climate and the eruption of the volcano Huaynaputina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The price of grain soared, and the French captain Jacques Margeret, who served in Moscow, wrote that a measure of corn that had sold for fifteen sous now cost three roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Boris met the crisis with charity on a vast scale, selling grain at half price in Moscow, feeding crowds every day and setting the hungry to work building stone houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Margeret thought the charity itself raised the death toll, because people flocked to Moscow for the daily alms, could not live on them, and died in the city or on the roads home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He put the dead buried at the tsar's expense in three public cemeteries outside Moscow at more than 120,000, while the German mercenary Conrad Bussow gave more than 500,000, and the figures disagree widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masters who could not feed their slaves turned them out, and hungry peasants left their landlords in large numbers, many of them becoming bondsmen of others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bands of the desperate joined the servants of disgraced nobles and roamed the southern provinces as brigands, so that a regular army had to be sent against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Jacques Margeret, <i>Estat de l'empire de Russie et grand duché de Moscovie</i>, ed. Henri Chevreul (Paris: L. Potier, 1860), 71–73, https://archive.org/details/estatdelempired00chevgoog. [in French] [Open access]","О. А. Туфанова, «Изображение голода 1601–1603 гг. в русских и иностранных источниках о Смутном времени», <i>Вестник славянских культур</i> 24, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izobrazhenie-goloda-1601-1603-gg-v-russkih-i-inostrannyh-istochnikah-o-smutnom-vremeni. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. О. Козловский, «„Великий голод и смутное время“: к вопросу о влиянии природно-климатического фактора на социально-политический кризис России второй половины XVI – первой половины XVII в.», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 10 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-golod-i-smutnoe-vremya-k-voprosu-o-vliyanii-prirodno-klimaticheskogo-faktora-na-sotsialno-politicheskiy-krizis-rossii-vtoroy. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 168, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 55–56, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent, 1911), 237, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia02kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 321–22, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many die in Moscow itself?","a":"Because the tsar's alms drew the hungry to the capital. Margeret wrote that Boris gave every poor person a small coin each day, that people came even when they could still feed themselves, and that they could not live on it and died in the city or on the way home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the death tolls disagree so widely?","a":"Because no one counted the dead systematically. Foreign writers gave figures of their own, Margeret more than 120,000 buried in Moscow and Bussow more than 500,000, while Russian chroniclers said only that the dead were beyond number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the famine feed the Time of Troubles?","a":"It loosened the bonds of society. Slaves turned out by their masters and peasants who fled their land became rootless men, bands of them roamed the south as brigands, and a recent study counts the famine among the decisive causes of the Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian famine of 1601–1603"},
    "ru-233": {"questions":["Boris Godunov declared that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was really the runaway monk Grigory Otrepev, though whether the man who crossed the border in 1604 was Otrepev has never been settled.","A French captain commanded the hundred archers of the foreign guard raised by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a tsar who also told his boyars that they ought to travel abroad to learn."],"abstract":"<b>False Dmitry I</b> was the pretender who claimed to be Dmitry, the youngest son of Ivan the Terrible, miraculously saved at Uglich, and who ruled Muscovy as tsar from June 1605 until his murder in May 1606.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appeared in 1603 at the court of the Lithuanian magnate Adam Wiśniowiecki and was then taken up by Jerzy Mniszech, Palatine of Sandomierz, to whose daughter Marina he was betrothed after accepting the Catholic faith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boris Godunov declared him to be Grigory Otrepev, a runaway monk who had once served the Romanovs and the patriarch, and that identification became the official Russian account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether he really was Otrepev has never been established, and even historians who studied him closely disagreed, some accepting the identification and others thinking him someone else altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1604 he crossed into Muscovy with about 1,600 mercenaries and 2,000 Don Cossacks, and after Boris died suddenly in April 1605 the tsar's army went over to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He entered Moscow in June 1605, was accepted by Ivan's widow Martha as her son, and was crowned at the end of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As tsar he sat in the council every day, released Filaret Romanov, raised a guard of foreign soldiers never before seen in Russia, and told the boyars that they ought to go abroad to learn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moscow was offended by the Poles who crowded into the city, by his disregard for ceremony and by Western music, dress and food, and a mock 'hell' he had built by the river was read as heresy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clerk Afanasy Vlasyev, promoted to treasurer and 'great secretary', was sent to Poland for Marina, and her arrival for the wedding in May 1606 brought still more Poles to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vasily Shuisky, who said he had accepted the pretender only to be rid of Godunov, raised the crowd on 17 May with the cry that the Poles were killing the tsar, and the conspirators murdered him in the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 169–76, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 28–32, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Jacques Margeret, <i>Estat de l'empire de Russie et grand duché de Moscovie</i>, ed. Henri Chevreul (Paris: L. Potier, 1860), 87, https://archive.org/details/estatdelempired00chevgoog. [in French] [Open access]","Д. И. Антонов, «„Демонология самозванства“: потешный ад Лжедмитрия I», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 10 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/demonologiya-samozvanstva-poteshnyy-ad-lzhedmitriya-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Рыбалко, «Система московских приказов и их служащие в царствование Лжедмитрия I», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sistema-moskovskih-prikazov-i-ih-sluzhaschie-v-tsarstvovanie-lzhedmitriya-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sonia E. Howe, ed., <i>The False Dmitri: A Russian Romance and Tragedy Described by British Eye-Witnesses, 1604–1612</i> (New York: Frederick A. Stokes, 1916), v–ix, https://archive.org/details/falsedmitrirussi00howeuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 322–23, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tsar's army go over to the pretender in 1605?","a":"Because Boris Godunov died suddenly in April 1605 and his young son could not hold the generals. The commanders judged the cause of the Godunovs hopeless, persuaded the army to change sides, and the chief boyars followed them to the pretender's camp at Orel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow turn against him so quickly?","a":"Because he broke with Muscovite custom at every turn. He ignored ceremony, favoured foreigners and Catholics, filled the city with Poles and enjoyed Western music, dress and food, all of which his enemies could present as heresy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did boyars support a man many of them thought an impostor?","a":"Because he was useful against Godunov. Shuisky told his fellow conspirators that he had accepted the pretender only to be delivered from Boris, and the boyars who did homage to him at Orel are said to have recognised him as Otrepev at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"False Dmitry I"},
    "ru-234": {"questions":["Concluded on 1 December 1618, the Truce of Deulino ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for fourteen years and left Smolensk and Novgorod-Seversky in the hands of the Commonwealth.","Filaret Romanov came home to Moscow in the exchange of prisoners that closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after nine years as a captive of the Polish king."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish–Muscovite War</b> was the conflict of the Time of Troubles in which the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth first backed pretenders to the Muscovite throne and then invaded openly, until the Truce of Deulino in 1618.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In its first phase Polish and Lithuanian nobles fought as private adventurers for the two false Dmitrys, while King Sigismund III acknowledged the first pretender privately but refused to back him in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many Poles joined the second pretender, drawn by the easy success of the first, by revenge for kinsmen killed in Moscow in 1606 and by hopes of power and riches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sigismund, who had returned to plans for war with Russia by the summer of 1608, treated Vasily Shuisky's alliance of 1609 with Sweden, his enemy, as the reason to intervene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 21 September 1609 he appeared before Smolensk with 17,000 Poles and 10,000 Cossacks, declaring that he had come not to shed blood but to pacify Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The siege of Smolensk lasted two years, and a hasty assault in the summer of 1610 cost the besiegers some two hundred lives before the fortress was finally taken in 1611.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meanwhile Hetman Stanisław Żółkiewski destroyed Shuisky's last army at Klushino in 1610, and the boyars of Moscow accepted Sigismund's son Władysław as tsar and let a Polish garrison into the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a national rising drove the Poles out and Michael Romanov was elected, Władysław set out in 1617 'to conquer and incorporate Muscovy', but his night assault on Moscow in 1618 was beaten off with heavy loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Truce of Deulino, concluded on 1 December 1618 by the old calendar, made peace for fourteen years and left Smolensk, Starodub and Novgorod-Seversky to the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners were exchanged under its terms, and the most important of them for Moscow was the tsar's father, Filaret Romanov, who came home after nine years in Poland to rule beside his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 169–82, 190–91, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 37, 57–58, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Н. В. Эйльбарт, «Осада Смоленска Сигизмундом III в письмах ксендза Якуба Задзика (1610–1611 гг.)», <i>Вестник Забайкальского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osada-smolenska-sigizmundom-iii-v-pismah-ksendza-yakuba-zadzika-1610-1611-gg-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tomasz Bohun, “Exchange of Captives within the Framework of the Truce of Deulino,” <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/exchange-of-captives-within-the-framework-of-the-truce-of-deulino. [Open access]","Н. В. Эйльбарт, «Лжедмитрий II: происхождение и гибель. Свидетельства польских документов Государственного архива Швеции», <i>Вестник Забайкальского государственного университета</i>, no. 11 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lzhedmitriy-ii-proishozhdenie-i-gibel-svidetelstva-polskih-dokumentov-gosudarstvennogo-arhiva-shvetsii-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. О. Тюменцев and Н. Е. Тюменцева, «Шведы в событиях Смуты начала XVII столетия: из союзников в оккупанты», <i>Caurus</i> 3, no. 1 (2024): 21–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedy-v-sobytiyah-smuty-nachala-xvii-stoletiya-iz-soyuznikov-v-okkupanty. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Polish nobles fight for the pretenders before the king did?","a":"Because the king would not commit himself, while private men saw profit. Sigismund acknowledged the first pretender only in private, Jerzy Mniszech backed him as a speculation for money and lands, and later volunteers sought revenge and riches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Sigismund invade openly in 1609?","a":"Because Shuisky had allied himself with Charles IX of Sweden, Sigismund's rival for the Swedish crown. The king had already returned to plans for war by 1608, and he denounced the alliance when he laid siege to Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war end in a truce rather than a peace?","a":"Because neither side would give up its claims. The Poles still refused to recognise Michael Romanov as more than provisional tsar, and each side surrendered too much at Deulino to accept a final settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Polish–Russian War (1609–1618)"},
    "ru-235": {"questions":["In the record of his oath, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swore not to execute anyone or seize the goods of their innocent relatives without a trial held together with his boyars.","Hetman Żółkiewski presented the captive <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his family to the Polish Diet in 1611, a year after the defeat at Klushino had cost him the throne."],"abstract":"<b>Vasily IV</b>, Vasily Ivanovich Shuisky, was the boyar prince who led the conspiracy that killed False Dmitry I and reigned as tsar of Muscovy from May 1606 until he was deposed in July 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was 54 at his accession, small, plain and short-sighted, cunning rather than clever, and a firm believer in the power of wizards and magic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His record was a weathervane's, for he had testified that the real Dmitry died at Uglich, told the crowd in 1605 that the boy had escaped, and then whispered that the new tsar was an impostor after all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He came to the throne without an assembly of the land, acclaimed by his own supporters in Red Square, and some people in Moscow did not even know what was happening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the record of the oath he swore by kissing the cross, he bound himself not to put anyone to death or confiscate the property of innocent relatives without a trial held with his boyars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Historians have read this oath as the first attempt in Russia to set legal limits on the ruler, though one recent study argues that it had little real force because his own title was so weak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reign was one long civil war, from the Bolotnikov rising, which he crushed only after leading the army to Tula in person in 1607, to the camp of the second pretender at Tushino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To fight Tushino he bought Swedish help in 1609 by ceding Karelia, and the alliance gave Sigismund III his reason for open war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the defeat at Klushino in 1610 he was deposed and, in an English account, withdrew into a monastery, and Hetman Żółkiewski took him and his brothers to Poland as captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He died a prisoner of the Poles, a tsar whom, in a contemporary's phrase, a clique of boyars had taken up as children take up a plaything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 32–37, 41, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 150, 172–82, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","А. Л. Бредихин, «Крестоцеловальная (подкрестная) запись царя Василия Шуйского 1606 г.: государственно-правовой анализ», <i>Ученые записки Казанского юридического института МВД России</i> 6, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestotselovalnaya-podkrestnaya-zapis-tsarya-vasiliya-shuyskogo-1606-g-gosudarstvenno-pravovoy-analiz. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sonia E. Howe, ed., <i>The False Dmitri: A Russian Romance and Tragedy Described by British Eye-Witnesses, 1604–1612</i> (New York: Frederick A. Stokes, 1916), 181–82, https://archive.org/details/falsedmitrirussi00howeuoft. [Open access]","О. В. Русаковский, «„Нерадостный ехал он на эту войну“: Тульский поход Василия Шуйского 1607 г.», <i>Vox medii aevi</i> 1, no. 12 (2025): 60–83, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neradostnyy-ehal-on-na-etu-voynu-tulskiy-pohod-vasiliya-shuyskogo-1607-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 333–34, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the oath he swore at his accession unusual?","a":"Because no tsar had sworn such a thing before. He gave up punishment without cause, the seizure of the property of a culprit's innocent relatives and trials on bare denunciation, and the boyars objected that it had never been done in Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Russians doubt his right to rule?","a":"Because he was chosen by a crowd in Moscow, not by the land. Provincials who had sworn to Dmitry as the true son of Ivan were now told that he was an impostor, and Shuisky himself had said both things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his alliance with Sweden backfire?","a":"Because Sweden was Poland's enemy. Hiring Swedish troops against Tushino gave Sigismund III the reason to besiege Smolensk, and the war that followed brought the defeat at Klushino that cost Shuisky his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vasili IV of Russia"},
    "ru-236": {"questions":["Proclamations from the camp at Kolomenskoye in 1606 urged slaves to rise against their masters during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Soviet historians treated as a peasant war.","Unwilling to serve under a former slave, Istoma Pashkov went over to Tsar Vasily on 2 December 1606, a desertion that weakened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> just as it besieged Moscow."],"abstract":"The <b>Bolotnikov rebellion</b> was the great rising of 1606–1607 against Tsar Vasily Shuisky, led by Ivan Bolotnikov in the name of a 'Tsar Dmitry' who never appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began in the southern borderlands soon after the murder of the first pretender, when Prince Grigory Shakhovskoy in Putivl spread word that Dmitry had escaped and the towns of the Seversk land went over at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bolotnikov was usually described as a former military slave of Prince Andrei Telyatevsky who, by older accounts, had been taken by the Tatars, sold to the Turks as a galley slave and freed in Venice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Joined by the servicemen of Ryazan and by the forces of Istoma Pashkov, his army reached Moscow by the end of October 1606 and camped at Kolomenskoye to besiege it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proclamations from the camp called on slaves to rise against their masters, and historians have long disputed whether this was a peasant war of the poor against the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The nobles in the camp, disgusted with their allies and unwilling to rank below a former slave, went over to the tsar, Pashkov on 2 December, and the rebels fell back from Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bolotnikov held out in Kaluga and then in Tula, where he was joined by a Cossack pretender calling himself Tsarevich Peter, a son of Tsar Fyodor who had never existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Shuisky rode out to take command in person in May 1607 and besieged Tula, defended by some 20,000 men, from the summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was finally beaten by damming the River Upa until water flooded the fortress and the houses, and its leaders were handed over on 10 October 1607, though the sources disagree on the exact day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 'Tsarevich Peter' was hanged, Bolotnikov vanished from the record, drowned by one older account, and captured rebels went on being put 'into the water' in Moscow for two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["С. Ф. Платонов, <i>Очерки по истории Смуты в Московском государстве XVI–XVII вв.</i> (1910), 314–25, https://archive.org/details/ocherkipoistoriismutyvmoskovskomgosudars57. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Симонова, «Портрет И. И. Болотникова в российской историографии», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 394 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/portret-i-i-bolotnikova-v-rossiyskoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Русаковский, «„Нерадостный ехал он на эту войну“: Тульский поход Василия Шуйского 1607 г.», <i>Vox medii aevi</i> 1, no. 12 (2025): 60–83, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neradostnyy-ehal-on-na-etu-voynu-tulskiy-pohod-vasiliya-shuyskogo-1607-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 177, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 37, 46, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 333, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]","А. В. Абанин, «Князь А. А. Телятевский: роль личности в восстании И. И. Болотникова в Смутное время», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 4 (31) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/knyaz-a-a-telyatevskiy-rol-lichnosti-v-vosstanii-i-i-bolotnikova-v-smutnoe-vremya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the nobles desert Bolotnikov at the gates of Moscow?","a":"Because they feared their allies more than the tsar. His proclamations called on slaves to rise against their masters, and men like Istoma Pashkov would not rank below a former slave, so the Ryazan servicemen and then Pashkov went over to Shuisky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rebels fight in the name of a dead tsar?","a":"Because in those years every grievance took the form of loyalty to a 'true' tsar. Rumours that Dmitry had escaped began at once, and the rebels in Tula sent to the Mniszech family asking for someone to play him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Tula hold out for so long?","a":"Because its defenders expected no mercy. About 20,000 men with experienced leaders held a strong fortress, knowing that captured rebels were being drowned, and only the flooding of the town by a dam on the Upa broke their resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ivan Bolotnikov"},
    "ru-237": {"questions":["Marina Mniszech, widow of the first pretender, recognised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as her husband and lived with him in the fortified camp near Moscow that gave him his nickname.","Killed at Kaluga in December 1610 by the Tatar Pyotr Urusov, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been named in Polish letters as a man the king wanted captured or dead."],"abstract":"<b>False Dmitry II</b> was the second pretender to claim to be Ivan the Terrible's son Dmitry, presenting himself as the first pretender escaped from Moscow, and Russians remembered him as the 'Thief of Tushino'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rumours that the murdered tsar had survived ran for more than a year before they took shape in a man who came out of prison in the Belarusian town of Propoisk and, by August 1607, proclaimed himself Tsar Dmitry at Starodub under threat of beating and torture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His origin is unknown, for he was variously said to be a Jew, a priest's son or a native of White Russia, and the circle of the Polish king came to accept the story that he was Jewish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rebels besieged in Tula had asked the Mniszech family to send them someone to play Dmitry, and he came too late to save them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was soon joined by Polish adventurers, many of them veterans of a failed noble revolt at home, and by Cossacks eager for plunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the summer of 1608 he set up his camp at Tushino, close to Moscow, and Marina Mniszech, the first pretender's widow, recognised him as her husband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Polish commanders in the camp treated him with contempt, and when commissioners from Sigismund III arrived in December 1609 his position became dangerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the night of 6 January 1610 by the new calendar he fled with his jester in a dung sledge, dressed as a peasant, and the people of Kaluga received him as tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Shuisky's fall he marched on Moscow again as a rival to Władysław, and fear of him drove the boyars to the Polish candidate rather than be ruled 'by our own slaves'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was killed at Kaluga in December 1610 by the Tatar Pyotr Urusov, and Polish letters suggest that Sigismund, who wrote that the pretender must be captured or killed, wanted him dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Н. В. Эйльбарт, «Лжедмитрий II: происхождение и гибель. Свидетельства польских документов Государственного архива Швеции», <i>Вестник Забайкальского государственного университета</i>, no. 11 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lzhedmitriy-ii-proishozhdenie-i-gibel-svidetelstva-polskih-dokumentov-gosudarstvennogo-arhiva-shvetsii-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. Ю. Таймасова, «Лжедмитрий II: история побегов», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 2 (42) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lzhedmitriy-ii-istoriya-pobegov. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 177–81, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 37–38, 41, 46, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 333, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]","С. Ф. Платонов, <i>Очерки по истории Смуты в Московском государстве XVI–XVII вв.</i> (1910), 333–34, https://archive.org/details/ocherkipoistoriismutyvmoskovskomgosudars57. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a second pretender appear after the first was killed?","a":"Because the rumour came before the man. Talk that Dmitry had escaped began at once, the rebels in Tula asked the Mniszech family for someone to play him, and by then claiming a 'true' tsar had become the usual form of every revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his Polish allies abandon him?","a":"Because their own king offered them more. Sigismund III summoned the Poles at Tushino to join him before Smolensk, and once his commissioners reached the camp the pretender was in danger and fled to Kaluga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow's boyars prefer Władysław to him?","a":"Because they feared a social revolution. Townsmen of little means and runaway slaves flocked to the pretender, and the boyars said they would not be ruled by their own slaves, so they turned to the Polish prince instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"False Dmitry II"},
    "ru-238": {"questions":["Sigismund III's summons to the Polish soldiers to join him before Smolensk broke up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1610, and its Cossacks followed the pretender to Kaluga.","Filaret Romanov, father of the first Romanov tsar, was invited to serve as patriarch in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the rival court set up close to the walls of Moscow."],"abstract":"The <b>Tushino camp</b> was the fortified headquarters of the second false Dmitry outside Moscow, from which, between 1608 and 1610, a rival court governed much of Muscovy against Tsar Vasily Shuisky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pretender's army first reached Tushino at the beginning of June 1608, and his Polish and Lithuanian commanders made the camp almost impregnable, with a road home to the Commonwealth through Mozhaisk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From it the pretender held the heart of the realm between the Oka and the Volga, and Suzdal, Vladimir, Rostov and many other northern towns opened their gates to his men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Polish prince Roman Różyński commanded the camp as its hetman, while Jan Sapieha besieged the great Trinity monastery for sixteen months without taking it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Townsmen of little means and runaway slaves flocked to Tushino in hope of escaping their misfortunes, and wealthy citizens said they would rather serve a Polish prince than be murdered by their own slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Tushino was a capital as well as a camp, with its own court and council and at least eight government departments, run by clerks whose names survive in its scattered charters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Filaret Romanov, the future tsar's father, was invited to be patriarch there, and men who went back and forth between the two courts for double rewards were mocked as 'birds of passage'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The camp began to break up after Sigismund III invaded in 1609 and summoned the Poles at Tushino to join him before Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pretender fled to Kaluga in January 1610, a few weeks after the king's commissioners reached the camp, and Russian Tushinites sent envoys who on 4 February 1610 agreed terms at Smolensk for Sigismund's son Władysław to become tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The camp was abandoned in March 1610, most of the Poles going to Sigismund, most of the boyars to Shuisky and the Cossacks to the pretender at Kaluga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 333–35, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 37–41, 46, 62, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Н. В. Рыбалко, «Персональный состав приказной администрации Лжедмитрия II», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 11 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/personalnyy-sostav-prikaznoy-administratsii-lzhedmitriya-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 178–80, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","С. Ф. Платонов, <i>Очерки по истории Смуты в Московском государстве XVI–XVII вв.</i> (1910), 342–43, https://archive.org/details/ocherkipoistoriismutyvmoskovskomgosudars57. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. Ю. Таймасова, «Лжедмитрий II: история побегов», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 2 (42) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lzhedmitriy-ii-istoriya-pobegov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a camp rival the tsar's government?","a":"Because it was run like a state. It had a court, a council and its own departments staffed by clerks, it held the country between the Oka and the Volga, and many northern towns opened their gates to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people move back and forth between Tushino and Moscow?","a":"Because no one could be sure which tsar would win. Ambitious men took rewards from both courts in turn, and towns that could not decide which ruler was legitimate opened their gates to the Tushinites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the camp break up in 1610?","a":"Because its Polish backbone left. Sigismund III summoned the Poles to his siege of Smolensk, the pretender fled to Kaluga, and the Russians of the camp opened their own negotiations with the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tushino Camp"},
    "ru-239": {"questions":["Sweden kept Korela and gained Ivangorod, Yam, Koporye and Oreshek when the Treaty of Stolbovo ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1617, in return for handing Novgorod back.","Gustavus Adolphus laid siege to Pskov in 1615 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and talks with the English envoy John Merrick as mediator began that autumn."],"abstract":"The <b>Ingrian War</b> was the conflict of the 1610s in which Sweden, first Muscovy's paid ally in the Time of Troubles, turned on it and occupied Novgorod and the lands around the Gulf of Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the treaty of February 1609 in which Vasily Shuisky, through his nephew Mikhail Skopin-Shuisky, hired Swedish troops against the Tushino pretender and gave up the fortress of Korela and its district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The corps under Jacob De la Gardie was made up of Western European mercenaries who, like the pretender's soldiers, mutinied for pay at decisive moments, and Russian levies had freed many towns before it arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Swedish aims went beyond the alliance, for Charles IX wanted Muscovy's frontier fortresses on the Baltic and Lake Ladoga, above all Korela.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the disaster at Klushino in 1610 De la Gardie led his men back through the Novgorod land, and in the spring of 1611 Sweden invaded under the pretext of helping the Russian national militia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 16 June 1611 by the Russian calendar the Swedes broke off negotiations and seized Great Novgorod and its land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Novgorodians were then willing to accept the Swedish prince Charles Philip as their ruler, but he reached Vyborg only in July 1613, after Michael Romanov had been chosen in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gustavus Adolphus besieged Pskov in 1615 without taking it, and from the autumn of that year the two sides began to talk, with the English envoy John Merrick as mediator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Treaty of Stolbovo of 1617 returned Novgorod, Staraya Russa, Porkhov and Ladoga to Muscovy, while Sweden kept Korela and gained Ivangorod, Yam, Koporye and Oreshek with their districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow also paid 20,000 roubles, yet its rulers counted the surrender of a few fortresses, Oreshek among them, a small price for the return of Novgorod and Swedish recognition of Michael as tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["И. О. Тюменцев and Н. Е. Тюменцева, «Шведы в событиях Смуты начала XVII столетия: из союзников в оккупанты», <i>Caurus</i> 3, no. 1 (2024): 21–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedy-v-sobytiyah-smuty-nachala-xvii-stoletiya-iz-soyuznikov-v-okkupanty. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Селин, «Столбовский мир и выход из Смуты на северо-западе», <i>Альманах североевропейских и балтийских исследований</i>, no. 2 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stolbovskiy-mir-i-vyhod-iz-smuty-na-severo-zapade. [in Russian] [Open access]","Я. Н. Рабинович, «Столбовский мир: победа или поражение», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 18 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stolbovskiy-mir-pobeda-ili-porazhenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 179, 189–90, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>A Popular History of Russia, from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 1 (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, and Rivington, n.d.), 335, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf01rambuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 37, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sweden turn from ally into occupier?","a":"Because it had wanted the Baltic fortresses all along. Charles IX had planned to seize Ingria and Karelia if no treaty were made, and once Klushino and the fall of the Shuiskys removed the government he had allied with, Sweden took Novgorod for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Novgorodians ready to accept a Swedish prince?","a":"Because Muscovy seemed to have collapsed. In 1611 there was no tsar and Poles held Moscow, so a Swedish prince ruling Novgorod looked better than chaos, until Michael Romanov's election offered a Russian ruler again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow accept the loss of the coastal fortresses?","a":"Because it wanted Novgorod back and peace with one enemy at least. The tsar's government counted a few fortresses and 20,000 roubles a fair price for Novgorod, the other northern towns and Swedish recognition of Michael.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ingrian War"},
    "ru-240": {"questions":["An English horseman wrote that most of the Russians fled before a blow was struck on Midsummer Day at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that six hundred French riders went over to the Poles.","Within weeks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Vasily Shuisky had lost his throne, and the boyars of Moscow took Sigismund's son Władysław as tsar rather than the Tushino pretender."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Klushino</b> was fought near the village of Klushino on 24 June 1610 by the Russian calendar, when Hetman Stanisław Żółkiewski routed the army of Tsar Vasily Shuisky and its foreign mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar's last army, put at 46,000 men in one older history, was commanded by his brother Dmitry Shuisky, who had lost all his earlier battles, and it included the Swedish-paid corps of Jacob De la Gardie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian detachment of about 8,000 men under Grigory Valuev had fortified itself at Tsarevo-Zaymishche, where the Poles besieged it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Żółkiewski, caught between two enemies, divided his army and led the larger part against Dmitry Shuisky, so that Valuev's men did not even learn that the battle had been fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The allied commanders had feasted in their camp, and Dmitry Shuisky had held back the mercenaries' pay until after the battle, probably because a war chest had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At dawn the Polish cavalry fell on the camp after a night march, and an English soldier in the mercenary corps wrote that most of the Russians fled before a blow was struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The English horse charged three times, but their general rode off, some six hundred French horsemen went over to the enemy, and the remaining foot made terms behind their wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many mercenaries took service with the Poles while De la Gardie led the rest away through the Novgorod land, and the battle showed the superiority of the Polish winged hussars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within weeks the defeat cost Vasily Shuisky his throne, and the boyars who ruled Moscow chose Sigismund's son Władysław rather than the Tushino pretender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One study calls Klushino a turning point of the Time of Troubles, after which Żółkiewski won more with words and the pen than with the sabre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 180–81, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","И. О. Тюменцев and Н. Е. Тюменцева, «Шведы в событиях Смуты начала XVII столетия: из союзников в оккупанты», <i>Caurus</i> 3, no. 1 (2024): 21–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedy-v-sobytiyah-smuty-nachala-xvii-stoletiya-iz-soyuznikov-v-okkupanty. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. М. Коваленко, «Tworca krola & kungamakaren. Станислав Жолкевский и Якоб Делагарди в России», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (12) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tworca-krola-kungamakaren-stanislav-zholkevskiy-i-yakob-delagardi-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sonia E. Howe, ed., <i>The False Dmitri: A Russian Romance and Tragedy Described by British Eye-Witnesses, 1604–1612</i> (New York: Frederick A. Stokes, 1916), 175–81, https://archive.org/details/falsedmitrirussi00howeuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 41, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","А. В. Абанин, «Григорий Леонтьевич Валуев. Фактор личности в период распада Московского государства в Смутное время», <i>Вестник Ленинградского государственного университета им. А. С. Пушкина</i> 4, no. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grigoriy-leontievich-valuev-faktor-lichnosti-v-period-raspada-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-v-smutnoe-vremya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the allied army collapse so quickly?","a":"Because it was demoralised before the fighting began. The mercenaries had been kept waiting for their pay, their commander lost his nerve, and most of the Russians fled at the first alarm, leaving a few companies of foreigners to fight alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many mercenaries go over to the Poles?","a":"Because the Poles offered what their employers had not. The English writer says his men had received a single rouble in almost a year, and after the battle the victors promised good quarter, pay for those who served and passports for those who went home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did one battle decide Shuisky's fate?","a":"Because it destroyed his last army. With no force left to defend Moscow, the tsar was deposed within weeks, and the boyars chose to accept the Polish prince rather than open the city to the Tushino pretender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Klushino"},
    "ru-271": {"questions":["Not invited to Andrusovo in 1667, the Cossacks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> learned only afterwards that Russia and Poland had confirmed between them the division of Ukraine.","Built beside the Chortomlyk river about 1652, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was stormed by Peter's dragoons in May 1709, after its Cossacks rebelled during the war with Sweden."],"abstract":"The <b>Zaporozhian Sich</b> was the fortified camp and seat of government of the Cossacks who lived below the rapids of the lower Dnieper, and their name means 'beyond the rapids'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first chief stronghold arose in the 16th century on the island of Khortytsia, the one nearest the rapids, and in the 17th and 18th centuries it moved between some eight successive sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Behind ramparts of tree trunks the Cossacks lived in huts of brushwood covered with horse hide, kept small cannon taken from Tatar and Turkish forts, and admitted no women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each year they elected their chief, the koshevoi ataman, and his officers in the square before the church of the Sich, and at the end of his term a general assembly judged his conduct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No newcomer was asked his name, home or faith, and in summer the camp held some 3,000 men, more whenever Tatar raids or the Polish lords drove peasants to flee there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the second half of the 17th century the Sich lay between Muscovy, Poland, the Crimean Khanate and the Ottoman Empire, and both the tsar's government and the Ottoman and Crimean authorities tried to bring it under their control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its Cossacks were not invited to Andrusovo, where Russia and Poland divided Ukraine in January 1667 and declared the Cossacks' country common to both as a barrier against the Turks and Tatars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under the Russian, Crimean and Ottoman agreements of 1681 and 1682 Moscow failed to have the tsar's sovereignty over the Zaporozhians recognised, and the Cossacks were to pay dues to the Ottoman treasury for working the lower Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Eternal Peace of 1686 with Poland at last gave Russia the right to Left-Bank Ukraine, Kyiv and Zaporozhia, while the Right Bank stayed under Polish rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sich beside the Chortomlyk river had been built about 1652, and in May 1709, during the war with Sweden, Peter's dragoons stormed it after its Cossacks rebelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 110–12, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 84–85, 270–71, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","П. А. Аваков and К. А. Кочегаров, «Уникальный чертеж Запорожской Сечи и крепости Каменный Затон начала XVIII века», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (30) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/unikalnyy-chertezh-zaporozhskoy-sechi-i-kreposti-kamennyy-zaton-nachala-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Кочегаров, «Запорожская Сечь во второй половине 1680 г.: от смерти кошевого атамана Ивана Серко до Бахчисарайского мира 1681 г.», <i>Центральноевропейские исследования</i> 8 (2025): 117–60, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zaporozhskaya-sech-vo-vtoroy-polovine-1680-g-ot-smerti-koshevogo-atamana-ivana-serko-do-bahchisarayskogo-mira-1681-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Селивоненко, «Влияние России на взаимоотношения между Запорожской Сечью и Гетманщиной», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 19, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vliyanie-rossii-na-vzaimootnosheniya-mezhdu-zaporozhskoy-sechyu-i-getmanschinoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Касьянов and Т. Г. Таирова-Яковлева, «Заповедник Хортица и его место в исторической памяти», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (29) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zapovednik-hortitsa-i-ego-mesto-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 131, 155, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Sich stand below the rapids?","a":"Because the rapids kept out Polish officials and troops. Cossacks under pressure in the frontier towns fled downstream to islands they already knew, where neither commissaries nor expeditions could easily follow them."},{"q":"Why did several powers want to control the Sich?","a":"Because it was an armed, self-governing community on a contested frontier. Muscovy, Poland, the Crimean khan and the Ottoman sultan each feared what its Cossacks might do for a rival, and each tried to bind it by treaty or payment."},{"q":"Why did Peter destroy the Chortomlyk Sich in 1709?","a":"Because its Cossacks rebelled against him during the war with Sweden, when Hetman Mazepa had gone over to Charles XII. Peter called the Sich the root of the evil and sent dragoons to storm it."}],"wiki":"Zaporozhian Sich"},
    "ru-272": {"questions":["After raiding the Persian shore of the Caspian from Derbent to Baku, the Don Cossack <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came back to Astrakhan in 1669 with a pardon and great plunder.","Beaten near Simbirsk in October 1670 and anathematised by the patriarch, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was captured after the Don Cossacks turned against him and was executed in Moscow in 1671."],"abstract":"<b>Stepan Razin</b>, a Don Cossack known as Stenka, led the rising that swept the lower and middle Volga in 1670 and 1671.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He first appears in 1661 on a mission to the Kalmyks, and from 1667 he gathered Cossacks and runaway peasants to plunder shipping on the Volga, including a government treasury barge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1668 and 1669 his flotilla raided the Persian shore of the Caspian from Derbent to Baku, and in July 1669 it destroyed a Persian fleet sent against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pardoned by the tsar, he came back to Astrakhan rich and admired, and a Dutch sailor who met him there saw him throw a captive Persian princess into the Volga as a gift to the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Although the movement began among the Don Cossacks, it took on a social character once townspeople and peasants joined it against the upper classes, and its bands reached as far as the Don towns of the Voronezh region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In June 1670 his Cossacks took Astrakhan, killed the governor and many of the better-off, and set up a Cossack order in which an assembly chose the town's officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rebels carried the Cossack assembly, the krug, and the office of ataman from the Don into the towns they took, though how they governed is known only in fragments from the interrogations of captured men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Razin's letters promised to root out the boyars and officials and claimed that the dead Tsarevich Alexei and the deposed patriarch Nikon were travelling with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was beaten near Simbirsk in October 1670, anathematised by the patriarch, captured in April 1671 after the Don Cossacks turned against him, and executed in Moscow that June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising has been called the largest popular movement against the government in 17th-century Russia, and historians before the revolution, mostly hostile to it, were divided over whether he was a state criminal or a popular hero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 163–74, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Jan Struys, <i>The Voyages and Travels of John Struys</i>, trans. John Morrison (London: Abel Swalle, 1684), 186–87, https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-voyages-and-travels-_struys-jan-janszoon_1684. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 250, 276, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","М. В. Симонова, «Степан Тимофеевич Разин — государственный преступник или народный герой?», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 401 (2015): 160–64, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stepan-timofeevich-razin-gosudarstvennyy-prestupnik-ili-narodnyy-geroy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Заец, «Устройство власти в Нижнем и Среднем Поволжье в период восстания С. Т. Разина», <i>Каспийский регион: политика, экономика, культура</i>, no. 3 (76) (2023): 9–20, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ustroystvo-vlasti-v-nizhnem-i-srednem-povolzhie-v-period-vosstaniya-s-t-razina. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Волков and В. Ю. Захаров, «Восстание Степана Разина в трудах русских историков XVIII — начала XX в.», <i>Гуманитарный вестник</i>, no. 1 (111) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vosstanie-stepana-razina-v-trudah-russkih-istorikov-xviii-nachala-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Брезгунова, «Город Коротояк во время восстания Степана Разина в 1670–1671 годы», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 18, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gorod-korotoyak-vo-vremya-vosstaniya-stepana-razina-v-1670-1671-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the poor of Astrakhan welcome Razin?","a":"Because he returned from the Caspian rich, generous and seemingly unbeatable, and he stood against governors and officials whom the common people saw as living off their labour."},{"q":"Why did Razin claim the tsarevich was with him?","a":"Because the common people revered the tsar. By saying the tsarevich and Nikon were travelling with him, he could attack the boyars and officials without seeming to rebel against the throne itself."},{"q":"Why did the rebels set up assemblies in the towns?","a":"Because the krug and the elected ataman were the way the Don Cossacks governed themselves, and the rebels carried those forms with them into the Volga towns they captured."}],"wiki":"Stenka Razin","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Stepan_Razin%2C_German_portrait_(cropped).jpg/960px-Stepan_Razin%2C_German_portrait_(cropped).jpg","title":"Stepan Razin, a German print","desc":"A German portrait of Razin from the 1670s, published by Paul Fürst's widow and heirs.","credit":"German engraving, Paul Fürst's widow and heirs, 1670s; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stepan_Razin%2C_German_portrait_(cropped).jpg","alt":"An engraving of a moustached man in a tall turban and a fur-trimmed coat, holding a staff."}},
    "ru-273": {"questions":["Near the mouth of the Ulya river the party of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built a winter station with a small stockade, the first Russian settlement on the shores of the Pacific.","Coming near the mouth of the Amur in July 1640, the Cossacks under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned back almost at once, leaving later writers to argue whether they ever sighted Sakhalin."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan Moskvitin</b> was a Cossack officer from Tomsk who led the first Russian party to reach the Pacific, coming out on the shore of the Sea of Okhotsk in 1639.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He served in the force of the Tomsk ataman Dmitry Kopylov, which had been sent east to the Aldan river to bring the Tungus there under tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kopylov heard of a 'silver mountain' and of lands beside a great sea, and sent Moskvitin with 31 men to find them and bring their peoples under the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The party went up the Maya and its tributary the Yudoma, crossed the mountains to the Ulya and followed it down to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Near the mouth of the Ulya they built a winter station with a small stockade, the first Russian settlement on the shores of the Sea of Okhotsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From local people the Cossacks heard of farming tribes to the south and of a people near the Amur's mouth who traded in silk, copper and silver goods that seem to have come from China and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1639 they sailed north along the coast to the Okhota river, the start of Russian seafaring on the Pacific, and in May 1640 they put to sea southward to look for the silver in kochi, boats with planks sewn together with withies, which they had built themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They came near the mouth of the Amur at the end of July but turned back almost at once, and the flight of their guide and the gathering of native forces have both been given as reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether they also sighted Sakhalin has been argued since the 1950s, because the main record is the questioning of the men who returned, which is hard to interpret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their journey opened a decade of advance in which another party reached the Ulya and the Okhota in 1647 and Semyon Dezhnev rounded the north-eastern tip of Asia in 1648.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["E. G. Ravenstein, <i>The Russians on the Amur</i> (London: Trübner, 1861), 9–10, https://archive.org/details/russiansonamuri00rave. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 175–76, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","В. Н. Елизарьев, «Новая версия разгадки расспросных речей участников похода И. Ю. Москвитина к устью р. Амур в 1640 г.», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 4 (2022): 187–205, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novaya-versiya-razgadki-rassprosnyh-rechey-uchastnikov-pohoda-i-yu-moskvitina-k-ustyu-r-amur-v-1640-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Елизарьев, «Малоизвестные страницы покорения Сибири и Дальнего Востока XVII в. Шаман Томканей: его роль в организации походов русских землепроходцев И. Ю. Москвитина и В. Д. Пояркова», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 2 (2024): 151–76, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/maloizvestnye-stranitsy-pokoreniya-sibiri-i-dalnego-vostoka-xvii-v-shaman-tomkaney-ego-rol-v-organizatsii-pohodov-russkih. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. П. Каменецкий, «Атаман Дмитрий Копылов и его потомки на службе в Сибири в XVII в.: штрихи к портрету русского землепроходца», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 99 (2026): 42–49, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ataman-dmitriy-kopylov-i-ego-potomki-na-sluzhbe-v-sibiri-v-xvii-v-shtrihi-k-portretu-russkogo-zemleprohodtsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Филин, «Коч — судно полярных мореходов XVII в. Конструктивные особенности», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 2 (16) (2022): 209–24, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/koch-sudno-polyarnyh-morehodov-xvii-v-konstruktivnye-osobennosti. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kopylov send Moskvitin to the sea?","a":"Because local Tungus told him of a silver mountain and of lands near a great sea. Muscovy wanted silver and new tribute-paying subjects, so a detachment was sent to find both."},{"q":"Why did the Cossacks build their own boats?","a":"Because they had come overland by river and portage and had no ships on the coast. To follow the shore north and later south towards the Amur they had to build boats where they were."},{"q":"Why is it unclear whether they saw Sakhalin?","a":"Because the main evidence is the questioning of the men who came back, which retells events at second hand. Historians have read those statements in very different ways."}],"wiki":"Ivan Moskvitin"},
    "ru-274": {"questions":["Only Manchu and Latin originals of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive in the archives, for the Russian text handed over in 1689 is known only from copies and the envoy's report.","Giving up the Amur as far as the Gorbitsa, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set the limit of Russian expansion and left a wide buffer zone where neither empire was active."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Nerchinsk</b> was the first treaty between Russia and Qing China, agreed outside the Russian fort of Nerchinsk in August 1689, and it drew the first stretch of their common border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It ended years of fighting over the Amur valley, where Cossacks had settled at Albazin and the Qing had besieged the fort in 1685 and again from 1686.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The border ran along the Argun and the Gorbitsa and then along a mountain range to the sea, and Albazin was to be demolished and its people moved to Russian territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land between the range and the Ud river was left undecided, fugitives were to be returned, and travellers with passports were allowed to trade across the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only the Manchu and Latin originals survive, the Russian text being known from copies and the envoy's report, and only the Russian version ran the border up the Argun to its sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> For Russia the treaty marked the limit of its advance down the Amur, for the line of the river as far as the Gorbitsa was given up because it could not seriously be defended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tsar's envoy, Fyodor Golovin, left Moscow in January 1686 with an escort of 1,500 streltsy and reached Nerchinsk in August 1689, to find the Qing embassy there with thousands of troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Golovin first proposed the Amur itself as the border, the Qing envoys asked for Albazin, Nerchinsk and Selenginsk, and Jesuit interpreters helped to revive the faltering talks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither side wanted a full war, so each used shows of force to press the other, and the envoy made the most of his smaller army against the Qing superiority in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice no line was marked along the Amur, and the land between the two states remained a buffer zone, now reaching to the Stanovoy range, where neither side was active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. G. Ravenstein, <i>The Russians on the Amur</i> (London: Trübner, 1861), 54–64, https://archive.org/details/russiansonamuri00rave. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 240–41, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Е. А. Багрин, «Военные аспекты заключения Нерчинского договора в 1689 г.», <i>Известия Восточного института</i>, no. 3 (51) (2021): 49–60, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennye-aspekty-zaklyucheniya-nerchinskogo-dogovora-v-1689-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Р. Артемьев, «О некоторых спорных вопросах пограничного размежевания между Россией и Китаем по Нерчинскому договору 1689 г.», <i>Вестник Дальневосточного отделения Российской академии наук</i>, no. 1 (2002), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-nekotoryh-spornyh-voprosah-pogranichnogo-razmezhevaniya-mezhdu-rossiey-i-kitaem-po-nerchinskomu-dogovoru-1689-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Тураев, «Российско-китайское пограничье в XVII веке», <i>Труды Института истории, археологии и этнографии ДВО РАН</i> 27 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiysko-kitayskoe-pogranichie-v-xvii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Кириллова and В. В. Шевцов, «Нерчинск — Пекин: русские торговые караваны в 1689–1703 гг.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 435 (2018): 121–26, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nerchinsk-pekin-russkie-torgovye-karavany-v-1689-1703-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Постников, «Работы по организации охраны и картографирования территорий, разграниченных между Российской империей и цинским Китаем по условиям Нерчинского договора (1689 г.) и Кяхтинского (Буринского) трактата 1727 г.», <i>Известия Иркутского государственного университета. Серия: История</i> 17 (2016): 12–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/raboty-po-organizatsii-ohrany-i-kartografirovaniya-territoriy-razgranichennyh-mezhdu-rossiyskoy-imperiey-i-tsinskim-kitaem-po-usloviyam. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia give up Albazin?","a":"Because it could not defend the Amur against the Qing. The sieges of 1685 and 1686 had shown how slowly help reached the fort, and the envoy could not match the Qing army at Nerchinsk."},{"q":"Why did the Jesuits matter at Nerchinsk?","a":"Because they served as the Qing interpreters, working in Latin, and when the second conference broke down with a Mongol interpreter they helped bring the two embassies back to the table."},{"q":"Why did the border stay vague along the Amur?","a":"Because no demarcation was carried out there. Beyond the Argun and the Gorbitsa the land remained a buffer where neither state was active, rather than a marked frontier."}],"wiki":"Treaty of Nerchinsk"},
    "ru-275": {"questions":["Taught by Simeon Polotsky and able to read Polish and Latin, the invalid <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned only six years before dying childless in April 1682 at the age of 22.","The patriarch and a council of boyars backed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in January 1682 when he asked whether all ranks should henceforth be held without regard to precedence."],"abstract":"<b>Feodor III</b>, the eldest surviving son of Tsar Alexis, reigned from 1676 to 1682 and died at the age of 22 after a short reign full of plans for reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was an invalid all his life, but he had been taught by Simeon Polotsky, knew Polish and Latin, and helped his tutor turn the Psalms into verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His court was led at first by the old boyars of his father's day and then by new favourites such as Ivan Yazykov and the Likhachev brothers, whom the older men had brought into the tsar's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians long neglected his reign, and since the late 20th century they have argued over how far he governed for himself and how his policies relate to those of Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Abroad, Moscow extended its truce with Poland in 1678 and ended the war with the Ottomans and the Crimea by a truce of twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His best-remembered act was the abolition of mestnichestvo, the system under which a noble could refuse any post or seat lower than those his ancestors had held, though his court had already begun to rank its members by office and date of appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A commission under Prince Vasily Golitsyn, set up to reorganise the army on Western lines after the disappointing campaign of 1681, proposed abolishing it as a first step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1682 Feodor put the question to a council of boyars and churchmen, the patriarch denounced the system, and the assembly voted that all ranks should henceforth be held without precedence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The books in which the services of every noble family had been recorded were then gathered in and burned in a furnace before the tsar and his nobles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The law placed the old boyar aristocracy and the gentry on an equal footing in state service, and Feodor died childless that April without naming an heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 39–42, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 191–94, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 5, 83, 290–91, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Т. В. Бауэр, «Правление Федора Алексеевича (1676–1682): дискуссионные вопросы в новейшей отечественной историографии», <i>Ученые записки Петрозаводского государственного университета</i> 45, no. 2 (2023): 34–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravlenie-fedora-alekseevicha-1676-1682-diskussionnye-voprosy-v-noveyshey-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. В. Седов, «Ближние люди царя Федора Алексеевича», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (29) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/blizhnie-lyudi-tsarya-fedora-alekseevicha. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Новохатко, «Что общего у царя Федора Алексеевича и сэра Исаака Ньютона?», review of <i>Царь-реформатор Федор Алексеевич: старший брат Петра I</i>, by А. П. Богданов, <i>Novogardia</i>, no. 3 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chto-obschego-u-tsarya-fedora-alekseevicha-i-sera-isaaka-nyutona-retsenziya-na-monografiyu-a-p-bogdanova-tsar-reformator-fedor-alekseevich. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was mestnichestvo so harmful?","a":"Because capable men could not be put where they were needed. A noble of high birth would refuse to serve under anyone whose ancestors had ranked below his own, and campaigns had been lost to quarrels over place."},{"q":"Why did the reform meet little open resistance?","a":"Because the nobility was used to it in part. For decades the tsars had declared campaigns to be fought without precedence, so the idea of abolishing it altogether was not new, though the great families resented its authors."},{"q":"Why did Feodor's death cause a crisis?","a":"Because he died childless and named no heir. The throne lay between his sickly brother Ivan and his young half-brother Peter, whose mothers' families were rivals at court."}],"wiki":"Feodor III of Russia","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Feodor_III_of_Russia.jpg/960px-Feodor_III_of_Russia.jpg","title":"Feodor III","desc":"Portrait of Tsar Feodor III by an unknown painter, about 1676–1682.","credit":"Unknown painter, c. 1676–1682; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feodor_III_of_Russia.jpg","alt":"A young tsar in a fur-trimmed crown and patterned robe, holding a sceptre and an orb."}},
    "ru-276": {"questions":["Proclaimed tsar at the age of ten in 1682, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first saw Western Europe only in 1697, after a boyhood of war games and boats at Preobrazhenskoe.","Rejecting the Byzantine model of his ancestors, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked to the Roman Empire at its height when Russia was proclaimed an empire after the war with Sweden."],"abstract":"<b>Peter the Great</b>, Peter I, was tsar of Russia from 1682 and its first emperor from 1721 until his death in January 1725, and in his reign Russia came to be treated as a great power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Born in 1672 to Tsar Alexis and Natalia Naryshkina, he was proclaimed tsar at ten, but the streltsy revolt that year made him joint tsar with his half-brother Ivan under their sister Sophia's regency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He grew up at Preobrazhenskoe outside Moscow, where his war games with bands of boys and his boats on the Yauza and at Pereslavl were the beginnings of his army and navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1697, at the age of 25, he first saw Western Europe, travelling with the Great Embassy, which sought a place for Russia in the concert of European states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The long war with Sweden that followed, decided at Poltava in 1709, showed that Russia had the military strength of a great power, and the peace of Nystad ended it in 1721.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The name 'the Great' was formally offered to him, with 'Father of the Fatherland' and 'Emperor of All Russia', at the celebrations of that peace on 22 October 1721.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The proposal came first from the Holy Synod and was taken up with the Senate, and the chancellor Golovkin made the speech of request in the Trinity Cathedral in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since 1600 the Holy Roman Emperor had addressed the tsars with the style used for ordinary kings, and the imperial title answered a slight that the first Romanovs had failed to remove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Peter looked to the Roman Empire at its height rather than to Byzantium, and after taking Azov in 1696 he was celebrated with Caesar's motto and pictured wearing a laurel wreath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Only Prussia, the Dutch Republic and Sweden recognised the imperial title in his lifetime, and its acceptance by the other courts of Europe took several decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 195–98, 208–12, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 5–7, 13, 57, 212–13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","О. Г. Агеева, «Ништадтский мир и имперский статус России», <i>Перспективы. Электронный журнал</i>, no. 4 (27) (2021): 49–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nishtadtskiy-mir-i-imperskiy-status-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Анисимов, «Петр Великий и его империя», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 1 (15) (2022): 6–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petr-velikiy-i-ego-imperiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Россия Петра I: обретение великодержавности», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i> 14, no. 6 (2021): 30–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-petra-i-obretenie-velikoderzhavnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 33–69, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","Д. Ю. Гузевич, «Дипломатическая цель Великого посольства 1697–1698/99», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 13, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diplomaticheskaya-tsel-velikogo-posolstva-1697-1698-99. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Злобин, Р. А. Черенков and А. А. Борисова, «Принятие Петром I императорского титула в контексте эволюции представлений о характере власти московских государей», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i> 20, no. 3-1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prinyatie-petrom-i-imperatorskogo-titula-v-kontekste-evolyutsii-predstavleniy-o-haraktere-vlasti-moskovskih-gosudarey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Ф. Михайлова, «Из истории принятия Петром I титула императора», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i> (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-prinyatiya-petrom-i-titula-imperatora. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Peter called \"the Great\"?","a":"Because the Senate and the Synod offered him the name, with the titles of Father of the Fatherland and Emperor, at the celebrations of the victorious peace with Sweden in October 1721."},{"q":"Why did Peter take the title of emperor?","a":"Because he wanted a standing in Europe to match Russia's new power. He looked to imperial Rome rather than Byzantium, and only the victory over Sweden made the claim believable."},{"q":"Why was the title slow to be recognised?","a":"Because the other courts weighed it against their own interests. Prussia, the Dutch Republic and Sweden accepted it quickly, but others held back for decades and conceded only when they needed Russia."}],"wiki":"Peter the Great","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Inconnu_d%27apr%C3%A8s_J.-M._Nattier%2C_Portrait_de_Pierre_Ier_%28mus%C3%A9e_de_l%E2%80%99Ermitage%29.jpg/960px-Inconnu_d%27apr%C3%A8s_J.-M._Nattier%2C_Portrait_de_Pierre_Ier_%28mus%C3%A9e_de_l%E2%80%99Ermitage%29.jpg","title":"Peter I, after Jean-Marc Nattier","desc":"Oil portrait in the Hermitage, St Petersburg, after the likeness Jean-Marc Nattier painted in 1717, the year Peter visited France. It hung in the Romanov Gallery of the Winter Palace.","credit":"Portrait after Jean-Marc Nattier, 1717, Hermitage Museum; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inconnu_d%27apr%C3%A8s_J.-M._Nattier%2C_Portrait_de_Pierre_Ier_%28mus%C3%A9e_de_l%E2%80%99Ermitage%29.jpg","alt":"Three-quarter-length portrait of a dark-haired man with a thin moustache in gilded armour and a blue sash, his hand on a plumed helmet, a battle in the distance."}},
    "ru-277": {"questions":["A petition of the streltsy at the end of May 1682 had <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named regent for the joint tsars Ivan and Peter, an office she held until 1689.","Confined in the Novodevichy Convent after her fall in 1689, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made a nun under the name Susanna after the streltsy revolt of 1698."],"abstract":"<b>Sophia Alekseyevna</b>, a daughter of Tsar Alexis born in 1657, ruled Russia as regent for her younger brothers Ivan V and Peter I from 1682 until her fall in 1689.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She had been educated under the monk Simeon Polotsky, read Polish and could write Latin, and she broke with custom by nursing her dying brother Feodor in front of the boyars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the streltsy rising of May 1682 a council made Ivan and Peter joint tsars, and at the end of that month a fresh petition of the soldiers had Sophia named regent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her government was led by Prince Vasily Golitsyn, a cultivated admirer of the West, and it made the Eternal Peace with Poland in 1686 and sent armies against the Crimea in 1687 and 1689.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Older writers painted her as an adventuress or as a victim of circumstance, and the story that she plotted the rising which brought her to power has been challenged as a legend, like her government's own tale of a plot by the Khovanskys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> From 1682 she was styled 'Great Sovereign Lady', and from the spring of 1686, in a decree of 22 April and the treaty with Poland of 26 April, she was named autocrat beside the two tsars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Portraits showed her crowned, with sceptre and orb, in the breast of the double-headed eagle where the Russian arms placed the monarch, on a model taken from an image of the emperor Leopold I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An engraving of this kind was made in Moscow in 1689, and it may have been meant for a coronation of Sophia planned for 1 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As early as 1687 her minister Fyodor Shaklovity had tried to persuade the streltsy to petition for her coronation, but they received the idea coldly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter's flight to the Trinity monastery in August 1689 ended her rule, and she was confined in the Novodevichy Convent, made a nun as Susanna after the revolt of 1698, and died in 1704.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 195–98, 207, 213–16, 234–35, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 169–70, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 290–91, 366–68, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Б. А. Успенский, «От герба к парсуне (Портрет царевны Софьи Алексеевны „в орле“)», <i>Slověne</i> 10, no. 1 (2021): 113–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-gerba-k-parsune-portret-tsarevny-sofi-alekseevny-v-orle. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Баснословие о заговоре Милославского и Софьи во время „Хованщины“», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 21 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/basnoslovie-o-zagovore-miloslavskogo-i-sofi-vo-vremya-hovanschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 5–7, 13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Рождение Хованщины», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 23 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rozhdenie-hovanschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Sophia able to become regent?","a":"Because after the rising of 1682 she was the one member of the family to take charge. The streltsy petitioned for her, and a frightened council accepted, since Ivan was infirm and Peter only ten."},{"q":"Why was she portrayed inside the double-headed eagle?","a":"Because the centre of the Russian arms was the monarch's place. Setting her there, crowned and holding sceptre and orb, presented her as a sovereign rather than a regent, perhaps for a planned coronation."},{"q":"Why did Sophia lose power in 1689?","a":"Because Peter was grown and his supporters had gathered at the Trinity monastery. The regiments, the patriarch and the boyars went over to him one by one, and the streltsy surrendered her minister."}],"wiki":"Sophia Alekseyevna of Russia","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Sophia_Alekseyevna_of_Russia.jpg/960px-Sophia_Alekseyevna_of_Russia.jpg","title":"Regent Sophia","desc":"Portrait of Sophia Alekseyevna as regent by an unknown Western European painter, 1682–1689.","credit":"Unknown Western European painter, 1682–1689; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sophia_Alekseyevna_of_Russia.jpg","alt":"A woman in a crown and a red, jewelled mantle, holding a sceptre and orb, inside an oval with a Cyrillic inscription."}},
    "ru-278": {"questions":["A pillar on Red Square naming the officials they had killed was among the rewards the streltsy won in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that autumn they had to pull it down.","Executing Prince Khovansky and his son that September, Sophia's government blamed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on their supposed plot to seize the throne, and the rising became known as the Khovanshchina."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow uprising of 1682</b> was the revolt of the streltsy, the standing infantry of the capital, which broke out on 15 May 1682, less than three weeks after the death of Tsar Feodor III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Feodor had died on 27 April without an heir, and the patriarch and the boyars had proclaimed his ten-year-old half-brother Peter tsar over the head of the elder but sickly Ivan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The streltsy were also the police and the fire guard of Moscow, they had long grievances against commanders who exploited them, and soldiers of the elite regiments and much of the city's poor stood with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Armed and with banners flying they entered the Kremlin, where they killed Artamon Matveev before the boy tsar, their own chief Prince Yury Dolgorukov and many officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The killings went on for three days, and on the third the soldiers forced the surrender of Ivan Naryshkin, the young tsar's uncle, and tortured him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The victors won a royal charter naming them the 'Court Infantry', a pillar on Red Square proclaiming their victory over the boyars, and the joint rule of Ivan and Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of May a further petition made Sophia regent, and in July Old Believers among the streltsy took their case against the new church books to a disputation in the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 2 September an anonymous denunciation accused Prince Ivan Khovansky, the commander the soldiers trusted, and his son of plotting to seize the throne, and both were executed on 17 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court gathered troops at the Trinity monastery, the streltsy submitted and pulled down their pillar, and by the autumn the rising had been brought to a peaceful end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The government blamed the revolt on the Khovanskys, so that it became known as the Khovanshchina, and later writers blamed a plot by Sophia and her Miloslavsky kin, but both stories have been read as propaganda laid over a movement with causes of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 194–202, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 5–7, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 83, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Баснословие о заговоре Милославского и Софьи во время „Хованщины“», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 21 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/basnoslovie-o-zagovore-miloslavskogo-i-sofi-vo-vremya-hovanschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Рождение Хованщины», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 23 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rozhdenie-hovanschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 49–69, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the streltsy have so much power in Moscow?","a":"Because they were not only soldiers but the police and fire guard of the capital, so the whole of the city's order rested on them, and when they rose the court had no force to oppose them."},{"q":"Why did the government blame the Khovanskys?","a":"Because a plot by two princes made the revolt the crime of a few, which let the regime pardon the rank and file and bring the soldiers back to obedience without fighting them."},{"q":"Why did the rising matter so much to Peter?","a":"Because he saw Matveev and his own kin killed in front of him at the age of ten, and the memory stayed with him; it shaped his later hostility to the streltsy."}],"wiki":"Moscow uprising of 1682"},
    "ru-279": {"questions":["Russia's share in the Holy League's war on the Ottomans took the form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, two marches into the steppe that kept the khan's horsemen from aiding the Turks.","Sophia hailed as a triumph her favourite's return from the second of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though he had retreated from Perekop in 1689 without taking it or making peace."],"abstract":"The <b>Crimean campaigns</b> were the two expeditions that Prince Vasily Golitsyn, the leading minister of the regent Sophia, led against the Crimean Khanate in 1687 and 1689.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were Russia's part in the war of the Holy League, the alliance of the Habsburg emperor, Poland and Venice against the Ottoman Empire, which Moscow joined after making a lasting peace with Poland in 1686.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the spring of 1687 an army of about 100,000 men, with Cossack and Kalmyk allies, marched south into the steppe towards the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tatars burned the steppe ahead of it, and with no grass for the horses and little water a council of war decided on 17 June to turn back, still about 200 kilometres (125 miles) short of Perekop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hetman Ivan Samoilovich was suspected of having fired the steppe himself, and he was deposed and replaced by Ivan Mazepa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The second campaign set out in February 1689 with about 112,000 men and was slowed by snow and cold before it reached the open steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 15 to 17 May it fought the Tatars in the Green and Black valleys, where a surprise attack in a rainstorm broke into the rear of the left wing before the khan's men fell back behind Perekop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before the wall and ditch of Perekop, with his troops short of water and the arid Crimean plains ahead, Golitsyn opened talks with the khan and then retreated without a peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sophia hailed his return as a triumph in her letters to him and heaped rewards on the army, which disgusted his enemies and helped to turn opinion towards the young Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The campaigns did keep the khan's forces from joining the Ottomans against Russia's allies, and they showed that the steppe was no longer a barrier to a Russian advance south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 204–07, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 152–65, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","Н. В. Шевцов, «Предтеча петровских реформ», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 5 (44) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/predtecha-petrovskih-reform. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Торопицын, «Служба астраханской тюркской знати в Низовом полку в ходе Крымских походов 1687 и 1689 гг.», <i>Из истории и культуры народов Среднего Поволжья</i> 15, no. 3 (2025): 10–20, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sluzhbaastrahanskoytyurkskoyznativnizovompolkuvhodekrymskihpohodov-1687-i-1689-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Басхаев, «Участие калмыков в русско-турецкой войне 1686–1700 гг.», <i>Вестник Института комплексных исследований аридных территорий</i>, no. 2 (15) (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchastie-kalmykov-v-russko-turetskoy-voyne-1686-1700-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 366–68, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia attack the Crimea at all?","a":"Because it had joined the Holy League against the Ottomans, and its allies wanted the khan's horsemen kept busy. Russia also wanted to stop Tatar raids on its southern lands."},{"q":"Why did the first campaign turn back without a battle?","a":"Because the burning steppe left no grass for the horses and the small streams could not water so large an army, so a council of war decided that going on was too dangerous."},{"q":"Why did Golitsyn not storm Perekop in 1689?","a":"Because the fort was strong, his men had been without water, and even beyond it lay dry Crimean plains. He chose to negotiate and then retreat rather than risk the army."}],"wiki":"Crimean campaign"},
    "ru-280": {"questions":["Handed over by Sophia's own streltsy at Peter's demand in September 1689, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> confessed under torture to a plot against the young tsar and was beheaded.","The trial papers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that he had portraits of the regent printed, one of them setting her within the double-headed eagle of the Russian arms."],"abstract":"<b>Fyodor Shaklovity</b> was the official at the heart of Sophia's government, and his fall in September 1689 completed Peter's seizure of power from his half-sister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A man of humble origin who rose by ability from scribe to a high rank at court, he served as a secretary of the Razryad, the central military office, from 1676 to 1682.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the crisis of 1682 he dealt with the court's practical business for Sophia and Golitsyn, was raised to duma secretary that August, and later took command of the streltsy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Golitsyn's first Crimean campaign failed, Shaklovity was sent to Ukraine to build forts, raise troops and set matters right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He supplied the court monk Sylvester Medvedev with documents for an account of the events of 1682, and one modern study calls him Sophia's minister of the interior in all but name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In August 1687 he tried to persuade the streltsy to petition the tsars for Sophia's coronation, but they received the idea coldly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had portraits of Sophia engraved, one of them made in Amsterdam, and the papers of his trial show that he printed a version that set her within the double-headed eagle of the Russian arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In August 1689, when he gathered streltsy in the Kremlin and a warning came that they meant to attack him, Peter fled by night from Preobrazhenskoe to the fortified Trinity monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As regiments and boyars went over to Peter, the streltsy forced Sophia to hand Shaklovity over, and under torture he wrote a confession of a plot against the lives of Peter and his mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was beheaded with his chief accomplices that September, Sophia was shut in a convent and Golitsyn exiled, and power passed to the circle of Peter's mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 207–16, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 102, 169–87, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Рождение Хованщины», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 23 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rozhdenie-hovanschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. А. Успенский, «От герба к парсуне (Портрет царевны Софьи Алексеевны „в орле“)», <i>Slověne</i> 10, no. 1 (2021): 113–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-gerba-k-parsune-portret-tsarevny-sofi-alekseevny-v-orle. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sophia rely so much on Shaklovity?","a":"Because he was able, decisive and wholly devoted to her. He solved practical problems where others hesitated, and he was ready to act on a hint where Golitsyn would not."},{"q":"Why did Peter flee to the Trinity monastery?","a":"Because he was told that streltsy were coming to kill him. The warning seems to have been false, but the fortified monastery was a refuge no soldier would attack, and Sophia had used it herself in 1682."},{"q":"Why was the surrender of Shaklovity decisive?","a":"Because it showed that even Sophia's own streltsy would no longer protect her. Once her chief minister was given up and the boyars went over to Peter, her rule was over."}],"wiki":"Fyodor Shaklovity"},
    "ru-201": {"questions":["Hereditary votchiny and service pomestie lands were made liable alike by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Muscovy issued soon after the conquest of Kazan to tie cavalry service to land.","Drafted first for the Sovereign's Court, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left outside its rules a large layer of poor servitors who held less than 100 chetverti of land."],"abstract":"The <b>Decree on Service</b> was the Muscovite law of 1555–1556 that fixed how much cavalry service a landholder owed the tsar according to the amount of land he held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its terms survive in a chronicle text, and it crowned a run of measures begun in 1550 that limited precedence disputes in appointments, created a Chosen Thousand of servitors and fixed the staff and pay of the new streltsy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its central rule was that every 100 chetverti, a measure of land, of good arable soil owed one horseman in armour, so that a man holding 200 served in person for the first hundred and brought one armed servant for the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same obligation fell on hereditary estates, the votchiny, as on the pomestie lands granted for service, and from then on service was tied to land of every kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The men raised in this way rode in the gentry cavalry, which the military office called out by circulars and grouped into five regiments, a great regiment, a vanguard, a right hand, a left hand and a rearguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Soon after the fall of Kazan the government compiled registers that divided the service class by the size of their estates and the rate of their money salary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Extracts from registers of about 1556 docket each servitor with his estate, the number of servants he had to bring, their armament and his pay, and these rules became a regular system that lasted into the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree's norms seem to have been drawn up first for the members of the Sovereign's Court and only later extended to the mass of provincial servitors, the sons of boyars of the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large layer of poorer servitors who held less than 100 chetverti stayed outside its rules, and a new reform of 1571–1573 revised its standards again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians still disagree about what the armed men brought from the estates were, whether military slaves and retainers or conscripts called datochnye lyudi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 126–28, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","О. А. Курбатов, «„Конность, людность и оружность“ русской конницы в эпоху Ливонской войны 1558–1583 гг.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konnost-lyudnost-i-oruzhnost-russkoy-konnitsy-v-epohu-livonskoy-voyny-1558-1583-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Терехина, «Роль „Уложения о службе 1555/1556 г.“ в формировании новой категории ратных людей», <i>Гасырлар авазы — Эхо веков</i>, no. 2 (2026): 77–83, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-ulozheniya-o-sluzhbe-1555-1556-g-v-formirovanii-novoy-kategorii-ratnyh-lyudey. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 159–61, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","О. А. Курбатов, «Русское войско XV–XVII вв.: очерк истории организационных структур», <i>Исторический вестник</i> 44 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkoe-voysko-xv-xvii-vv-ocherk-istorii-organizatsionnyh-struktur. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the amount of land decide how many men a servitor brought?","a":"Because the state wanted service that matched each man's means: every 100 chetverti of good land owed one armed horseman, and a larger estate owed more armed servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did hereditary estates now owe the same service as granted ones?","a":"Because the decree laid the obligation on votchiny in the same proportion as on pomestie lands, so that all service became tied to land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians argue about the men a landholder brought?","a":"Because the sources do not name them plainly: some read them as military slaves or retainers, while another view holds that they were conscripted men called datochnye lyudi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-202": {"questions":["Ivan IV was said to have lingered at prayer while his men mounted the breach during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a delay that the official texts and Kurbsky explain in different ways.","Refusing a seventh offer of terms, the defenders answered that they would build another wall or all die, on the day before the final assault of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1552."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Kazan</b> was the Muscovite siege and storming in 1552 of Kazan, the capital of the Tatar khanate on the middle Volga, and it ended the independence of the khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It closed a series of wars in which the winter campaigns led by the young Ivan IV in 1548 and 1549 had failed through bad weather, lost artillery and quarrels over precedence among his commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Early in 1552 a plan to replace the khan with a Muscovite governor collapsed when the townspeople rose in March and took the Astrakhan prince Yediger-Mehmed as their khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivan's army set out in the summer, and the chroniclers put it at about 150,000 men, a figure that historians have long treated as an exaggeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town's ramparts were only of timber and earth, but a garrison the chroniclers reckoned at 30,000 men held them for nearly six weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The decisive weapon was the mine, for from 31 August a foreign engineer and his Russian pupils dug galleries under the walls, and on 4 September a charge destroyed the hidden passage that brought the town its water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 1 October the besiegers sent a seventh and last offer of surrender, and the defenders refused it, answering that they would raise another wall or all die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At dawn on 2 October two great explosions brought down part of the wall, and after fighting at the gates, around the mosques and before the khan's palace the town fell and Yediger-Mehmed was captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official texts and the exiled prince Andrei Kurbsky both say that Ivan lingered at prayer before he joined the assault, though they explain the delay in different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting with the peoples of the khanate went on until 1557 in what historians call the Kazan or First Cheremis War, and an English traveller in 1558 found Kazan's timber walls being rebuilt in stone and three former khans living at Ivan's court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 112–15, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 158–66, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","В. А. Волков and Р. М. Введенский, «Русско-казанская война 1547–1552 годов: осада и взятие Казани», <i>Преподаватель XXI век</i>, no. 2, pt. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-kazanskaya-voyna-1547-1552-godov-osada-i-vzyatie-kazani. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Аксанов, «Промедление Ивана IV при взятии Казани: проявление иносказательности средневековых текстов и реальной поведенческой практики», <i>Золотоордынское обозрение</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 351–58, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/promedlenie-ivana-iv-pri-vzyatii-kazani-proyavlenie-inoskazatelnosti-srednevekovyh-tekstov-i-realnoy-povedencheskoy-praktiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anthony Jenkinson, “The Voyage of M. Anthony Jenkinson, Made from the Citie of Mosco in Russia, to the Citie of Boghar in Bactria, in the Yere 1558,” in <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia</i>, ed. E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886), 48–49, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 75, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Б. Л. Хамидуллин, «„Борьба с пятью казанскими народами“: значение взятия Казани 1552 г. и Казанская (первая Черемисская) война 1552–1557 гг. в трактовке С. М. Соловьева», <i>Тюркологические исследования</i> (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-s-pyatyu-kazanskimi-narodami-znachenie-vzyatiya-kazani-1552-g-i-kazanskaya-pervaya-cheremisskaya-voyna-1552-1557-gg-v-traktovke-s-m. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Muscovy need mines to take Kazan?","a":"Because a garrison of thousands held the timber and earth ramparts for weeks, and only charges laid in galleries under the walls cut off the water and brought down part of the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why had the earlier campaigns against Kazan failed?","a":"Because they started too late and met winter, the artillery was lost on the Volga, and the commanders quarrelled over precedence instead of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Ivan's conduct during the assault still discussed?","a":"Because the official texts and Kurbsky agree that he delayed joining his troops, but they give different reasons, and the official account borrows the language of older stories of princes at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Siege of Kazan","places":["city:1159150705"]},
    "ru-203": {"questions":["In March 1552 the khan Shah Ali left Kazan with 84 hostages and withdrew to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Muscovite stronghold built the year before on the Round Hill.","The first archbishop of the conquered khanate took his title from Kazan and from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the fortress from which Moscow had controlled the right bank before the siege."],"abstract":"<b>Sviyazhsk</b> is a fortress town on the Round Hill at the mouth of the Sviyaga, where it flows into the Volga close to Kazan, built by Muscovy in 1551 as a base against the Khanate of Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fortress was cut from timber near Uglich on the upper Volga, floated down the river in pieces and assembled on the hill under the direction of the clerk Ivan Vyrodkov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work began on 25 May 1551, the day after the river army arrived, and within four weeks the new town stood, named Ivangorod Sviyazhsky in honour of the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its walls were built of 420 log cribs filled with earth, with 11 towers and six gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While the timbers went up, Muscovite troops raided the Mari and Chuvash villages around it, and the hill peoples of the right bank, the Mountain Side, were soon cut off from Kazan and drawn towards Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The purpose of the fortress was supply, since earlier campaigns had failed for want of a base in enemy country, and from Sviyazhsk the army could control the right bank and the near approaches to Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the Mountain Side lost the khanate was in effect cut in two, and in March 1552 its Muscovite-backed khan Shah Ali left Kazan and withdrew to Sviyazhsk with 84 hostages from the Kazan nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the spring of 1552 siege guns, munitions and food were shipped to Sviyazhsk from Nizhny Novgorod, and in August Ivan IV halted there on his way to the siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the conquest it remained an important centre of Muscovite power on the middle Volga with governors of its own, and in 1555 the first archbishop of the new see took the title of Kazan and Sviyazhsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicle account of its building is the main written source for the founding, and excavation has since added evidence of its mid-16th-century defences, some of which may have been only a simple palisade raised in haste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Н. В. Белов, «„Свияжская эпопея“ лета 1551 г. и начало присоединения чувашских земель к Московскому государству», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 9 (2017): 26–64, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sviyazhskaya-epopeya-leta-1551-g-i-nachalo-prisoedineniya-chuvashskih-zemel-k-moskovskomu-gosudarstvu. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Волков and Р. М. Введенский, «Русско-казанская война 1547–1552 годов: осада и взятие Казани», <i>Преподаватель XXI век</i>, no. 2, pt. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-kazanskaya-voyna-1547-1552-godov-osada-i-vzyatie-kazani. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 158–59, 163, 166–67, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","А. М. Губайдуллин and Р. Н. Хамзин, «О крепостных сооружениях Свияжска», <i>Археология евразийских степей</i>, no. 6 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-krepostnyh-sooruzheniyah-sviyazhska. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Белов, «Князь Петр Михайлович Щенятев — воевода Свияжска (1559–1560): к вопросу о служебном статусе Свияжской крепости в третьей четверти XVI века», <i>Историческое обозрение</i> 24 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/knyaz-petr-mihaylovich-schenyatev-voevoda-sviyazhska-1559-1560-k-voprosu-o-sluzhebnom-statuse-sviyazhskoy-kreposti-v-tretiey. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Медведь, «И снова о Свияжской крепости 1551 г.», <i>Археология евразийских степей</i>, no. 5 (2023): 211–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/i-snova-o-sviyazhskoy-kreposti-1551-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the fortress built in pieces far up the Volga?","a":"Because it had to rise fast in enemy country: the logs were prepared near Uglich, floated down in pieces and put up in about four weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Sviyazhsk matter so much for the siege of Kazan?","a":"Because earlier campaigns had failed for lack of supplies, and the new base let the army hold the right bank and stock guns and food close to Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the hill peoples go over to Moscow?","a":"Because raids around the new fortress cut the Mountain Side off from Kazan, and the strong Muscovite base nearby drew the Chuvash and Mari towards submission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sviyazhsk"},
    "ru-204": {"questions":["When Dervish-Ali fled by way of Azov to Mecca in 1556, the independent history of Hajji Tarkhan, the capital of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, came to an end.","A Venetian envoy of 1476 described the small, low-walled spice market at the mouth of the Volga that was the capital of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until Muscovy took it."],"abstract":"The <b>Astrakhan Khanate</b> was a Tatar state at the mouth of the Volga, one of the successors of the Golden Horde, whose capital the Tatars called Hajji Tarkhan and the Russians Astrakhan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its old town stood on the right bank above the later Russian one, and a Venetian envoy of 1476 described it as a small, low-walled place that traded in spices bound for Venice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the 1550s the khanate was weak and contested, pressed between the khan of Crimea, the Nogai Horde of the steppe and a Muscovy that had just taken Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1554 a Muscovite river army drove out Khan Yamgurchi and set up Dervish-Ali, a former khan who had taken refuge in Moscow, as a vassal bound to pay tribute and to let Muscovites sail freely between Kazan and Astrakhan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The campaign rested on an agreement with the Nogai leader Ismail, who was fighting his own rivals in the Horde and wanted Muscovy on his side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The protectorate failed as Kazan's had, because Dervish-Ali sought support from Crimea and from the sons of Ismail's murdered brother Yusuf, and in 1555 he fell out openly with Moscow's resident at his court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In March 1556 Ivan sent the musketeer captain Ivan Cheremisinov down the Volga with streltsy and Cossacks, and Dervish-Ali gave up hope of his throne and fled by way of Azov to Mecca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With that the history of independent Hajji Tarkhan ended and the history of Russian Astrakhan began, and by 1557 the khanate was formally part of Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An English traveller there in 1558 found an earth and timber castle on an island, a garrison supplied each year from Moscow, and Nogais who had come to submit dying of famine in heaps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Moscow ran the conquered khanates through a special office, the Court of Kazan, and the fall of Astrakhan drew it into the affairs of the small principalities of the Caucasus, several of which sought the tsar's protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 156–57, 168–69, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 115, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","М. В. Моисеев, «Ногайско-русские отношения при Исмаил-бие (1554–1563 гг.)», <i>Средневековые тюрко-татарские государства</i>, no. 9 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nogaysko-russkie-otnosheniya-pri-ismail-bie-1554-1563-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Пенской, «„Центурионы“ Ивана Грозного, часть II: Иван Семенов сын Черемисинов», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i> 3 (2012): 239–96, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsenturiony-ivana-groznogo-chast-ii-ivan-semenov-syn-cheremisinov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anthony Jenkinson, “The Voyage of M. Anthony Jenkinson, Made from the Citie of Mosco in Russia, to the Citie of Boghar in Bactria, in the Yere 1558,” in <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia</i>, ed. E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886), 56–58, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 257, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow first rule Astrakhan through a vassal khan?","a":"Because it tried the method it had used at Kazan, putting a khan of its choice on the throne in return for tribute and free passage on the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the vassal khan turn against Moscow?","a":"Because Dervish-Ali wanted more independence and looked for support to Crimea and to the sons of the Nogai leader Yusuf, until open conflict broke out with Moscow's resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nogai Horde matter in the conquest?","a":"Because Ismail, who took power in the Horde, had agreed with Moscow on the campaign, and the Horde's civil wars and famine left it unable to defend the khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Astrakhan Khanate"},
    "ru-205": {"questions":["Reaching Bukhara on 23 December 1558, the English merchant <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found the road to Cathay closed and turned back the way he had come.","In 1562 the Englishman <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went down the Volga again, this time bound for Persia, and crossed the Caspian with an escort of musketeers against pirates."],"abstract":"<b>Anthony Jenkinson</b> was an English merchant and agent of the Muscovy Company who in 1558 sailed down the Volga from Moscow to the Caspian Sea and crossed it towards Central Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The company had been founded in 1555, two years after an English ship reached the White Sea, and it hoped to reach the rich East through Russian territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Jenkinson, who had admired the speed and skill of Russian gunners at drill in 1557, left Moscow by water on 23 April 1558 with two company servants, a Tatar interpreter and letters from the tsar to the rulers along his road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was aiming for Cathay, but he got no farther than Bukhara, which his caravan reached on 23 December 1558 after fighting off robbers in the desert, and turned back the way he had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1562 he went down the Volga again on a second journey, this time to Persia, and sailed into the Caspian with an escort of musketeers against pirates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His account records a river only just conquered, for he wrote that Ivan had enlarged his empire to the Caspian Sea by conquering the famous river Volga and all the lands beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sailed from Nizhny Novgorod in a convoy of 500 boats carrying soldiers, supplies and goods for the new governor of Astrakhan, and found Kazan a fair town whose timber walls were being rebuilt in stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below Kazan he found deserted an island where merchants of many nations had once met to trade, and at Perevolok, near the Don, he judged the robber country safer since the conquests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He thought the trade of Astrakhan so small and beggarly that it was not worth following, yet his company's agents made seven journeys along the Volga route between 1558 and 1581, one to Central Asia and six to Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their chief import from the Caspian lands was raw silk, which brought the English large profits and drew other foreigners there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Anthony Jenkinson, “The Voyage of M. Anthony Jenkinson, Made from the Citie of Mosco in Russia, to the Citie of Boghar in Bactria, in the Yere 1558,” in <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia</i>, ed. E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886), 42–60, 125, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","E. Delmar Morgan and C. H. Coote, introduction to <i>Early Voyages and Travels to Russia and Persia</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hakluyt Society, 1886), xxi–xxii, xxvii–xxviii, https://archive.org/details/earlyvoyagestrav01morguoft. [Open access]","Э. Л. Дубман, «Английские путешественники на волжском пути (вторая половина XVI в.)», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 5/3 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/angliyskie-puteshestvenniki-na-volzhskom-puti-vtoraya-polovina-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ш. А. Магарамов, «Англичане на Каспийском море в XVI в.», <i>Вестник Кемеровского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2017): 62–66, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anglichane-na-kaspiyskom-more-v-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 128–29, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 162–63, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could an English merchant now sail the whole Volga?","a":"Because Muscovy had just conquered Kazan and Astrakhan, and Jenkinson himself wrote that the tsar had extended his empire to the Caspian by taking the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the English want a route through Russia?","a":"Because after reaching the White Sea in 1553 they hoped to trade with the rich East while bypassing their competitors, and the Volga led to the Caspian and Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Volga route still matter after his gloomy report on Astrakhan?","a":"Because Persia lay beyond the Caspian, and the company's agents made six journeys there by 1581, bringing back raw silk that earned large profits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Anthony Jenkinson","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81.%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BB.%D0%BA%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B6%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%BE_1593_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BD_%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%94%D0%B6%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%99%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5.jpg/960px-%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81.%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BB.%D0%BA%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B6%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%BE_1593_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BD_%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%94%D0%B6%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%99%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5.jpg","title":"Jenkinson's map of Muscovy","desc":"Anthony Jenkinson's map of Russia, in Gerard de Jode's 1593 edition, drawn from his journeys down the Volga to the Caspian; Tatar caravans and camels fill the south.","credit":"Map by Anthony Jenkinson, published by Gerard de Jode, 1593; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81.%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BB.%D0%BA%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B6%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%BE_1593_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BD_%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%94%D0%B6%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BD_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%99%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5.jpg","alt":"A hand-coloured old map of Russia with a Latin title cartouche, rivers, forests, animals and a camel caravan near the Caspian Sea."}},
    "ru-206": {"questions":["A naked holy fool who dared to rebuke the old tsar for his cruelty gave his name to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where a chapel dedicated to him was added in 1588.","At about 61 metres (200 feet), <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the tallest building in Moscow until the bell tower of Ivan the Great was raised under Boris Godunov."],"abstract":"<b>Saint Basil's Cathedral</b> is the popular name of the Cathedral of the Intercession of the Mother of God on the Moat, the church on Red Square in Moscow that Ivan IV built in 1555–1561 as a votive memorial to the conquest of Kazan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The idea is credited to Metropolitan Makary, who had blessed the tsar's campaign against Kazan and foretold the victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The building stands in two tiers, a high base and above it nine separate churches, each with its own dedication and its own doorway, grouped around a tall central church with a tent roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the chapels are those of Saint Gregory of Armenia and Saint Alexander of Svir, and the central church, now dedicated to the Intercession, was once called the Trinity church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At about 61 metres (200 feet) it was the tallest building in Moscow until the bell tower of Ivan the Great was raised higher under Boris Godunov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The church owes its everyday name to Basil, a holy fool who went about Moscow naked and, as an English envoy of the 1580s heard, dared to rebuke the old tsar for his cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Basil died in 1557, and the chapel in his name added to the north-east corner in 1588 was the first church in Moscow raised over the relics of a saint glorified in the new reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The English envoy reported offerings to the new saint from the people, the nobles and the tsar and tsaritsa themselves, along with a miracle exposed as a fraud and eight people killed inside by lightning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Records name two Russian masters, Barma and Postnik, as its builders, overturning an older belief that Italians designed it, and its composition has no direct parallel in Russian, European or Asian architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its onion domes are not original, since the first helmet-shaped domes burned and were replaced by bulbous ones in 1595, and frequent fires and later rebuilding have changed much of its look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Р. В. Лобзова and В. Н. Ярош, «Технологические исследования ранних изразцов архитектурного декора собора Покрова на Рву (храм Василия Блаженного, Москва)», <i>Вопросы музеологии</i>, no. 1 (7) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tehnologicheskie-issledovaniya-rannih-izraztsov-arhitekturnogo-dekora-sobora-pokrova-na-rvu-hram-vasiliya-blazhennogo-moskva. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Зайко, «Восстановление облика собора Покрова Пресвятой Богородицы на Рву в Москве по изображению на карте „Кремленаград“», <i>Архонт</i>, no. 5 (20) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vosstanovlenie-oblika-sobora-pokrova-presvyatoy-bogoroditsy-na-rvu-v-moskve-po-izobrazheniyu-na-karte-kremlenagrad. [in Russian] [Open access]","Giles Fletcher, <i>Of the Russe Common Wealth</i>, in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 117–18, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 83–84, 115, 166, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","Р. А. Суслова, «Символика и геометрия подкупольного архитектурно-декоративного мотива эпохи Ивана Грозного», <i>Культурная жизнь Юга России</i>, no. 3 (78) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/simvolika-i-geometriya-podkupolnogo-arhitekturno-dekorativnogo-motiva-epohi-ivana-groznogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Баталов, «Придел Василия Блаженного собора Покрова на Рву и особенности почитания святого в конце XVI в.», <i>Вестник ПСТГУ. Серия V: Вопросы истории и теории христианского искусства</i>, no. 1 (4) (2011): 121–32, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pridel-vasiliya-blazhennogo-sobora-pokrova-na-rvu-i-osobennosti-pochitaniya-svyatogo-v-kontse-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Беляев, review of <i>Собор Покрова Богородицы на Рву: история и иконография архитектуры</i>, by А. Л. Баталов, <i>Искусствознание</i> (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sobor-pokrova-bogoroditsy-na-rvu-istoriya-i-ikonografiya-arhitektury-m-lingva-f-2016. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexandra A. Sitnikova and Vladimir I. Zhukovsky, “Visualization of the Concept of State in the Architecture of the Moscow Cathedral of the Intercession on the Moat (1555–1561),” <i>Journal of Siberian Federal University. Humanities and Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 9 (2018): 1513–28, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/visualization-of-the-concept-of-state-in-the-architecture-of-the-moscow-cathedral-of-the-intercession-on-the-moat-1555-1561. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the cathedral built?","a":"Because Ivan IV vowed it as a memorial to the conquest of Kazan, an idea credited to Metropolitan Makary, who had blessed the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called after Saint Basil and not the Intercession?","a":"Because the holy fool Basil, who had rebuked the tsar, was buried there and made a saint, a chapel in his name was added in 1588, and his popular cult gave the church its everyday name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it look different today from the 1560s?","a":"Because fires repeatedly damaged it: its first helmet-shaped domes burned and were replaced by onion domes in 1595, and later rebuilding changed it further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saint Basil's Cathedral"},
    "ru-207": {"questions":["Stephen Báthory's trained infantry retook Polotsk in 1579 and besieged Pskov in 1581, turning the tide of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the tsar after years of Russian gains.","Because Livonia was carved into four zones in 1561–1562, some historians split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a first war against the Order and later wars with Lithuania and Sweden."],"abstract":"The <b>Livonian War</b> was the long struggle of 1558–1583 for Livonia, the lands of the Livonian Order and its bishops on the eastern Baltic, fought by Muscovy against the Order and then against Poland–Lithuania and Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ivan IV opened it in 1558 to break through to the Baltic and so to open direct relations with western Europe, whose craftsmen and experts his neighbours were trying to keep away from Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Order's state was divided and weak, and in 1558 the Russians took Narva and then Dorpat, granting Dorpat terms that kept its laws, its religion and its trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1560 the Livonian nobility was crushed at Fellin, but the Order's collapse drew in its neighbours, and by 1562 Livonia was divided into Polish–Lithuanian, Swedish, Danish and Russian zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some historians therefore treat the conflict as a chain of wars, a first Livonian war that ended with the death of the Order in 1561 followed by separate wars with Lithuania and with Sweden, though most still use the span 1558–1583.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Muscovy took Polotsk from Lithuania in 1563 and won many interior towns and castles, but it could not reach the coast, where the Poles, Swedes and Danes had forestalled it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burden was heavy, since in the ninety years from 1492 to 1582 Muscovy spent some forty at war with Lithuania, and the war's demands forced a revision of the cavalry's service norms in 1571–1573.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The turn came with Stephen Báthory, elected king of Poland in 1575, whose trained infantry retook Polotsk in 1579, took Velikiye Luki in 1580 and besieged Pskov in 1581.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pskov held out, but a ten-year truce in January 1582 gave Livonia and Polotsk to Poland–Lithuania, and a truce with Sweden left it the Ingrian towns of Yam, Ivangorod and Koporye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After some twenty years of fighting Russia was again cut off from the Baltic, though the war had destroyed the Order and set Poland and Sweden against each other for the future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 83–85, 116–17, 123–28, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 170–71, 180–87, 341–42, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 15, 75, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","А. И. Филюшкин, «Русско-литовская война 1561–1570 и датско-шведская война 1563–1570 гг.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 2, pt. 2 (2015): 219–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-litovskaya-voyna-1561-1570-i-datsko-shvedskaya-voyna-1563-1570-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Курбатов, «„Конность, людность и оружность“ русской конницы в эпоху Ливонской войны 1558–1583 гг.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konnost-lyudnost-i-oruzhnost-russkoy-konnitsy-v-epohu-livonskoy-voyny-1558-1583-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","З. А. Лурье, С. С. Смирнова and А. И. Филюшкин, «К вопросу о месте проведения переговоров о перемирии России и Речи Посполитой в 1581–1582 гг.», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-meste-provedeniya-peregovorov-o-peremirii-rossii-i-rechi-pospolitoy-v-1581-1582-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Виноградов, «Внутриполитическое и внешнеполитическое положение Речи Посполитой в статейных списках русских посольств в последние годы Ливонской войны», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnutripoliticheskoe-i-vneshnepoliticheskoe-polozhenie-rechi-pospolitoy-v-stateynyh-spiskah-russkih-posolstv-v-poslednie-gody-livonskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan IV want Livonia?","a":"Because it offered a Baltic coast and direct contact with western Europe, whose skilled men and experts Muscovy's neighbours were trying to keep from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did early Russian success turn into defeat?","a":"Because the Order's collapse brought in Poland–Lithuania and Sweden, and Stephen Báthory's trained infantry and artillery outfought the Muscovite levies from 1579.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about the war's dates?","a":"Because Livonia was divided in 1561–1562, and some treat what followed as separate wars with Lithuania and Sweden rather than one Livonian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Livonian War"},
    "ru-208": {"questions":["Modelled on the Prussian duchy of 1525, the Lutheran <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was carved out of southern Livonia for Gotthard Kettler and passed to his heirs in the male line.","When Ivan IV's invasion destroyed the Order's state, the lands south of the Dvina became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Sweden, Denmark and Russia took other parts of Livonia."],"abstract":"The <b>Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</b> was the secular duchy created in 1561–1562 in southern Livonia for Gotthard Kettler, the last master of the Livonian Order, as a vassal of the Polish–Lithuanian crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lay south of the Dvina, on lands the knightly Order had ruled since the 13th century, and the ducal title passed to Kettler's heirs in the male line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its birth belonged to the Reformation, for the change from a religious order to a lay duchy went with a change of confession from Catholic to Lutheran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The model was Prussia, whose Order became a duchy under the Polish king in 1525, but the duchy of the former master never matched the Prussian duchy in weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It depended at first on the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and later on the joint Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The duchy was the price of the Order's collapse under the Russian invasion of 1558, when Ivan IV's armies took Narva and Dorpat, and of the defeat of the Livonian nobility at Fellin in 1560.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kettler looked for help to the emperor, Denmark, Sweden and Poland, and in treaties signed at Vilnius between 31 August and 15 September 1559 a strip of southern Livonia was made over to Sigismund II Augustus in return for help against Ivan, a step prepared by an old Polish–Lithuanian right of protection over the archbishops of Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By a document of 21 November 1561 Kettler recognised the union of Livonia with Lithuania and accepted Courland with the title of duke, and on 5 March 1562 he gave up his cross, his mantle and the keys of Riga castle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of Livonia was shared out among Poland–Lithuania, Sweden, Denmark and the Russian conquests, in four zones formed by war and seizure that none of the rivals accepted as lawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For that reason the end of the Order in 1561 did not end the fighting, and a new round of Baltic wars followed in the 1560s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 84–85, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 180–87, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","И. Е. Эман, review of «От ордена осталось только имя»: судьба и смерть немецких рыцарей в Прибалтике, by Д. И. Вебер and А. И. Филюшкин, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2020-01-006-veber-d-i-filyushkin-a-i-ot-ordena-ostalos-tolko-imya-sudba-i-smert-nemetskih-rytsarey-v-pribaltike-spb-nauka-2018-247-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. И. Вебер, «Формирование конфессиональных границ Польши и Великого княжества Литовского с протестантскими герцогствами в XVI в.», <i>Религия. Церковь. Общество</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-konfessionalnyh-granits-polshi-i-velikogo-knyazhestva-litovskogo-s-protestantskimi-gertsogstvami-v-xvi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Филюшкин, «Русско-литовская война 1561–1570 и датско-шведская война 1563–1570 гг.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, special issue 2, pt. 2 (2015): 219–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-litovskaya-voyna-1561-1570-i-datsko-shvedskaya-voyna-1563-1570-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Бессуднов, «Патерналистские начала польского протектората в отношении ливонских земель», <i>Ученые записки: электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i> (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/paternalistskie-nachala-polskogo-protektorata-v-otnoshenii-livonskih-zemel. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the last master of the Order become a duke?","a":"Because the Russian invasion had shattered the Order, and by submitting to the Polish–Lithuanian ruler Kettler kept part of its lands as a hereditary duchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the duchy become Lutheran?","a":"Because turning a religious order into a lay state went with the Reformation, and Kettler himself left Catholicism for Lutheranism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Order's end not bring peace?","a":"Because Livonia was split into four zones formed by war and seizure, which none of the rival powers accepted as lawful, so new wars followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Duchy of Courland and Semigallia"},
    "ru-209": {"questions":["The Jesuit Antonio Possevino mediated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a smoky hut at Kiverova Gorka, after Ivan IV had asked Pope Gregory XIII to intervene.","Condemned by imperial-era historians as dishonourable, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was only a ten-year truce, since claims to the Russo-Lithuanian borderlands were kept out of the talks."],"abstract":"The <b>Truce of Yam-Zapolsky</b> was the ten-year truce of 15 January 1582 between Ivan IV and Stephen Báthory, king of Poland and grand duke of Lithuania, which ended Muscovy's war with Poland–Lithuania within the long Livonian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under it Ivan gave up the whole of Livonia together with Polotsk and Velizh, while Báthory gave back Velikiye Luki, Zavolochye and Nevel and left Smolensk to Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a truce rather than a peace because claims to the Russo-Lithuanian borderlands were kept out of the talks, and it closed nine decades from 1492 in which Muscovy had spent some forty years at war with Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name is a historians' label taken from the village of Yam Zapolsky on the Novgorod road, for the envoys actually met nearby at Kiverova Gorka, where the papal mediator lodged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate truce with Sweden on the river Plyussa left the Swedes the Ingrian towns of Yam, Ivangorod and Koporye and cut Muscovy off from the Gulf of Finland as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Báthory had declared war in a letter dated Vilnius 26 June 1579, and his campaigns forced the talks, and his siege of Pskov from August 1581 tied the war to a fortress that held out but could not win the war for Ivan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ivan asked Pope Gregory XIII to mediate, and the Jesuit Antonio Possevino presided over sessions held from 13 December 1581 in a smoky peasant hut with an improvised altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each side opened with extravagant demands, and a Polish success against a sortie from Pskov on 4 January 1582 persuaded Ivan to send instructions conceding all of Livonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians of the imperial era condemned the settlement as unprofitable and even dishonourable, while a more recent study argues that the Muscovite envoys did well within the limits of the military situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The loss of Russian Livonia forced Moscow to evacuate the servitors who had held estates around its conquered towns and to find them new lands at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 97–98, 124–28, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Ivan the Terrible</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (London: William Heinemann, 1904), 336–42, https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781440086342. [Open access]","З. А. Лурье, С. С. Смирнова and А. И. Филюшкин, «К вопросу о месте проведения переговоров о перемирии России и Речи Посполитой в 1581–1582 гг.», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-meste-provedeniya-peregovorov-o-peremirii-rossii-i-rechi-pospolitoy-v-1581-1582-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Селин, «„Изгнанные из Ливонии“: об устройстве помещиков „старых“ и „новых немецких городов“ после Плюсского перемирия», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (28) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izgnannye-iz-livonii-ob-ustroystve-pomeschikov-staryh-i-novyh-nemetskih-gorodov-posle-plyusskogo-peremiriya. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 15, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","А. В. Виноградов, «Внутриполитическое и внешнеполитическое положение Речи Посполитой в статейных списках русских посольств в последние годы Ливонской войны», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnutripoliticheskoe-i-vneshnepoliticheskoe-polozhenie-rechi-pospolitoy-v-stateynyh-spiskah-russkih-posolstv-v-poslednie-gody-livonskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ivan give up all of Livonia?","a":"Because Báthory's campaigns had taken Polotsk and Velikiye Luki and besieged Pskov, and a Polish success at Pskov in January 1582 showed that the besiegers would not give up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a papal envoy the mediator?","a":"Because Ivan asked Pope Gregory XIII to intervene, and Rome hoped for a union of the churches, so it sent the Jesuit Antonio Possevino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the truce named after a place where nobody met?","a":"Because the label Yam-Zapolsky stuck in the histories, although the envoys met at nearby Kiverova Gorka, where the mediator was lodged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Truce of Yam-Zapolsky"},
    "ru-210": {"questions":["Fear of Muscovy, with which they were almost always at war, drove the reluctant Lithuanian magnates to accept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the diet held in 1569.","After the Lithuanian delegates walked out, Sigismund II Augustus moved Podlasie, Volhynia and the Kyiv lands to the Polish crown, forcing through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that summer."],"abstract":"The <b>Union of Lublin</b> was the act of 1 July 1569 by which the Kingdom of Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania became a single commonwealth with one jointly elected ruler and one common Sejm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the work of a diet that met at Lublin on 10 January 1569 and of Sigismund II Augustus, the last of the Jagiellon kings, who had given up his hereditary right to Lithuania in 1564.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The act declared the two states one composite and indivisible body with one currency, and it allowed Poles and Lithuanians to settle freely in each other's lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lithuania nevertheless kept its own high offices, its own law and its own courts, so that the union created one legislature in a common Sejm but two separate executives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polish historians judge the union on many points differently from Lithuanian, Belarusian and Ukrainian ones, and some see in its border changes the root of a centuries-long conflict between Poland and Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Lithuanian magnates accepted a closer union only from fear of Muscovy, with which they were almost always at war, and Ivan IV's capture of Polotsk in 1563 deepened that fear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the ninety years from 1492 to 1582 Muscovy and Lithuania were at war for some forty, and the Livonian War had set the two against each other once more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the Lithuanian delegates walked out on 1 March, the king transferred Podlasie and Volhynia to the Polish crown that month and the Kyiv lands in June, and the Lithuanians returned to accept a compromise, while the transfer gave Poland its first common border with Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Russian reading sees the union as the end of the long contest between Muscovy and Lithuania for dominance in eastern Europe, with Lithuania all but absorbed by Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Others stress that the Grand Duchy kept its own identity, privileges and legal system inside the Commonwealth, and that its elites went on defending their equality with the Polish side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 85–88, 123, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Bogdan Rok, “Union of Lublin and Union of Brest: Creation of a State in Early Modern Europe,” <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/union-of-lublin-and-union-of-brest-creation-of-a-state-in-early-modern-europe. [in Polish] [Open access]","В. В. Пенской and А. В. Третьяков, «Две Руси: к вопросу о причинах поражения Великого княжества Литовского в борьбе с Русским государством за доминирование в Восточной Европе», <i>Ученые записки: электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (76) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dve-rusi-k-voprosu-o-prichinah-porazheniya-velikogo-knyazhestva-litovskogo-v-borbe-s-russkim-gosudarstvom-za-dominirovanie-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Петкевич, «Люблинская уния как источник многовекового польско-российского конфликта в свете польской историографии», <i>История и современность</i>, no. 2 (2014): 73–81, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lyublinskaya-uniya-kak-istochnik-mnogovekovogo-polsko-rossiyskogo-konflikta-v-svete-polskoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 2 (London: J. M. Dent and Sons, 1912), 15, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolII. [Open access]","Р. Яворский and Д. Шульц, «Польско-литовские отношения 1385–1569 гг. в польской историографии XIX–XXI вв.», trans. А. Б. Плотников, <i>Исторический вестник</i> (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polsko-litovskie-otnosheniya-1385-1569-gg-v-polskoy-istoriografii-xix-xxi-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. И. Ивонина, «Взаимодействие политических элит Великого княжества Литовского и Королевства Польского в контексте формирования Вестфальской системы международных отношений», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i> 18, no. 3 (2025): 27–50, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzaimodeystvie-politicheskih-elit-velikogo-knyazhestva-litovskogo-i-korolevstva-polskogo-v-kontekste-formirovaniya-vestfalskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Lithuanian magnates accept the union?","a":"Because they feared Muscovy, with which they were nearly always at war, and because the king moved whole provinces to the Polish crown when they walked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lithuania keep its own offices and law?","a":"Because the final text was a compromise: one Sejm and one ruler for both, but separate executive offices and a separate legal system for the Grand Duchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians judge the union so differently?","a":"Because some see it as Lithuania's absorption by Poland or as the root of a long Polish–Russian conflict, while others stress that the Grand Duchy kept its own identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Union of Lublin"},
    "ru-251": {"questions":["Ivan IV's marriage to Anastasia, daughter of Roman Zakharin, gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the blood tie to the old dynasty that later won Michael the throne in 1613.","Disgraced under Boris Godunov, who forced Nikita Romanovich's eldest son into a monastery, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gained popularity from its suffering and went on to found a dynasty in 1613."],"abstract":"The <b>House of Romanov</b> was the dynasty that ruled Russia from the election of Michael Romanov in 1613 until the revolution of March 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of an old Moscow boyar clan whose first reliable ancestor, Andrei Ivanovich Kobyla, served the grand princes Ivan I Kalita and Simeon the Proud in the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His descendants split into several branches, among them the Koshkins, the Zakharins and the Sheremetevs, and the clan kept its place in the front rank of the court even as titled princes crowded into Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The family took its name from Roman Yurievich Zakharin, whose daughter Anastasia became the first wife of Ivan IV and whose son Nikita was the first to be called Romanov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nikita Romanovich was remembered as the one boyar of his century to leave a grateful memory among the people, and an English merchant at court called him valiant and beloved of all men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When an assembly met to choose a tsar in 1613, the claim that counted most for Nikita's grandson Michael was this marriage tie, which a Moscow official of the 1660s still traced step by step from Anastasia through Nikita to Michael.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Under Boris Godunov the whole family had fallen into disgrace, and Nikita's eldest son Fyodor was forced to become a monk under the name Filaret, a persecution that later added to their popularity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assembly elected Michael at sixteen, and chroniclers of the day said he was wanted for his kinship with the old tsars rather than for any quality of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From 1619 Michael shared the title of great sovereign with his father, now Patriarch Filaret, so that for fourteen years the family stood at the head of both church and state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The male line died out in 1730 and the crown passed through the female line, but the house kept the Romanov name, first as tsars and later as emperors, until March 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 62–63, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Л. Ю. Варенцова, «Из истории землевладения боярского рода Романовых в XVI–XVII веках», <i>Приволжский научный вестник</i>, no. 10 (50) (2015): 42–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-zemlevladeniya-boyarskogo-roda-romanovyh-v-xvi-xvii-vekah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, “The Travels of Sir Jerome Horsey,” in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 226–27, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 38–39, 47–48, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 77–78, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]","Григорий Котошихин, <i>О России в царствование Алексия Михайловича</i>, 4th ed. (St Petersburg: Imperial Archaeographic Commission, 1906), ch. 1, 4–5, https://archive.org/details/orossivtsarstvov00koto. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. П. Исаев, «К вопросу о характере соправительства Михаила Федоровича и Филарета (1619–1633 годы)», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (2010): 66–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-haraktere-sopravitelstva-mihaila-fedorovicha-i-filareta-1619-1633-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","“Romanov,” in <i>Encyclopædia Britannica</i>, 11th ed., vol. 23 (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1911), 577, Wikisource, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Romanov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the assembly of 1613 choose a Romanov?","a":"Because the family was tied by marriage to the old dynasty: Michael's great-aunt Anastasia had been the first wife of Ivan IV, so people could see in him a kinsman of the old tsars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Romanovs also had a clean record and a popular name, and the assembly preferred them to grander but less trusted houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Romanovs popular before they reigned?","a":"Because of their reputation and their suffering. Nikita Romanovich was remembered kindly by the people and praised by an English merchant as valiant and beloved,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the persecution the family endured under Boris Godunov made them look like victims of a usurper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where does the name Romanov come from?","a":"From Roman Yurievich Zakharin, a boyar of Vasily III and the father of Ivan IV's first wife, Anastasia. His son Nikita was the first to be called Romanov, and the name passed to Nikita's descendants, Michael among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"House of Romanov"},
    "ru-252": {"questions":["A written nomination laid before the assembly by a Don Cossack ataman is said to have ended the deadlock that elected <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as tsar on 21 February 1613.","From 1619 until 1633 the young tsar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shared power with his own father, the patriarch Filaret, who had come home from years of captivity in Poland."],"abstract":"<b>Michael Romanov</b>, Mikhail Fyodorovich in Russian usage, was the first tsar of the Romanov dynasty, elected in 1613 and reigning until his death in 1645.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His grandfather Nikita was the brother of Ivan IV's first wife, Anastasia, and Michael was one of the few Romanovs to come through Boris Godunov's persecution of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country he took over was in ruins, with the Swedes in Novgorod, the Poles in Smolensk and much of the west, and bands of Cossacks and freebooters at large across the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first years went on clearing out the bandits and making peace, with Sweden at Stolbovo in 1617, which gave back Novgorod, and with Poland at Deulino in 1618–19, which left Smolensk in Polish hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1619 until 1633 his father Filaret, back from captivity in Poland, shared power with him as patriarch, and historians still argue whether this was a true joint rule or the father's rule in the son's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Michael was chosen at sixteen by an assembly of the land after long quarrels over grander candidates, and the decisive vote came on 21 February 1613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cossacks in Moscow pressed his candidacy, and a Don ataman's written nomination is said to have ended the deadlock in the assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Delegates found him with his mother at the Ipatiev Monastery near Kostroma, and he accepted the throne only after hours of entreaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One boyar is reported to have written that the young Michael would be 'familiar to us', and the older view drawn from such hints is that the electors wanted a convenient tsar rather than a capable one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reign later saw the failed Smolensk War of 1632–34, the first war of the new dynasty, and he died in July 1645, leaving the throne to his son Alexis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 59–63, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 38–44, 67, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 77–78, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]","Д. П. Исаев, «К вопросу о характере соправительства Михаила Федоровича и Филарета (1619–1633 годы)», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (2010): 66–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-haraktere-sopravitelstva-mihaila-fedorovicha-i-filareta-1619-1633-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Григорькин and С. В. Мещеряков, «Смоленская война 1632–1634 гг.: традиции, инновации и комплексы», <i>Огарёв-Online</i> 12, no. 8 (2024): 7–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smolenskaya-voyna-1632-1634-gg-traditsii-innovatsii-i-kompleksy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Григорий Котошихин, <i>О России в царствование Алексия Михайловича</i>, 4th ed. (St Petersburg: Imperial Archaeographic Commission, 1906), ch. 1, 4–5, https://archive.org/details/orossivtsarstvov00koto. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. Ю. Варенцова, «Из истории землевладения боярского рода Романовых в XVI–XVII веках», <i>Приволжский научный вестник</i>, no. 10 (50) (2015): 42–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-zemlevladeniya-boyarskogo-roda-romanovyh-v-xvi-xvii-vekah. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the assembly settle on a sixteen-year-old?","a":"Because his family tie to the old dynasty made him acceptable to almost everyone. He was a kinsman of the last tsars through Ivan IV's first wife,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> his family had a popular name,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the older reading of the evidence is that the boyars wanted a ruler they could live with rather than a strong one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Michael at first refuse the throne?","a":"Because the realm was in desperate straits, with Swedes, Poles, Cossacks and bandits holding or ravaging much of it. His mother protested that he was too young, and he yielded only when the delegates said they would hold him answerable to God for the ruin of Muscovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Filaret's share in the reign debated?","a":"Because the evidence fits two readings. Some historians have seen the patriarch as the real ruler behind a weak son, while others see a genuine joint rule in which both men bore the title of great sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Filaret did often settle affairs alone, though Michael's name always came first in documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Michael of Russia"},
    "ru-253": {"questions":["Ambassadors to Moscow in the 1620s presented their credentials to the tsar and to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together, the patriarch sitting in a golden chair at the tsar's right hand.","Made Metropolitan of Rostov by the first False Dmitry and named patriarch in the Tushino camp, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later spent eight years as a prisoner of the Polish king."],"abstract":"<b>Patriarch Filaret</b>, born Fyodor Nikitich Romanov about 1553, was the father of Tsar Michael and, from 1619 until his death in 1633, head of the Russian Church and his son's co-ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As a young boyar he was known for his learning, and an English merchant at court wrote that he made Fyodor a Latin grammar in Slavonic letters, in which he took great delight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under his cousin Tsar Fyodor he fought the Swedes and negotiated with imperial envoys, and when the old dynasty died out in 1598 he was spoken of as a candidate for the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About three years later Boris Godunov broke the family, forced Fyodor and his wife to take monastic vows under the names Filaret and Martha, and kept him in strict confinement in the Antoniev Monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Time of Troubles the first False Dmitry made him Metropolitan of Rostov and the second pretender's camp at Tushino named him patriarch, a past that later won his son support among the Cossacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sent in 1610 as an envoy to King Sigismund at Smolensk, he refused to accept the Polish prince as tsar and was held in Poland and Lithuania for eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He came home under the truce of Deulino in June 1619, knelt in tears with his son outside Moscow, and was enthroned as patriarch days later by the visiting Patriarch of Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like the tsar he bore the title 'great sovereign', ambassadors presented their credentials to both, the patriarch seated in a golden chair at the tsar's right hand, and he often settled affairs of state alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A German traveller of the 1630s wrote that Michael took his father's advice on matters of the greatest importance and always gave him precedence at audiences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Older accounts made Filaret the real ruler behind a weak son, while others describe a genuine diarchy rooted in the customs of a family that now stood at the head of both church and state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 47–51, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Jerome Horsey, “The Travels of Sir Jerome Horsey,” in <i>Russia at the Close of the Sixteenth Century</i>, ed. Edward A. Bond (London: Hakluyt Society, 1856), 226–27, https://archive.org/details/russiaatcloseofs20bond. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 60, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Д. П. Исаев, «К вопросу о характере соправительства Михаила Федоровича и Филарета (1619–1633 годы)», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (2010): 66–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-haraktere-sopravitelstva-mihaila-fedorovicha-i-filareta-1619-1633-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 77, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]","“Romanov,” in <i>Encyclopædia Britannica</i>, 11th ed., vol. 23 (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1911), 577, Wikisource, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Romanov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a churchman rule beside the tsar?","a":"Because he was the tsar's father and bore the same title of great sovereign, which made him a partner in the state rather than a mere adviser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ambassadors addressed both men, and Filaret often transacted affairs alone without consulting his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Filaret held captive in Poland?","a":"Because on his embassy to King Sigismund at Smolensk in 1610 he refused to acknowledge either the king or his son Władysław as tsar of Muscovy. He was detained and taken to Lithuania, where he stayed until the truce of Deulino freed him in 1619.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about his power?","a":"Because the sources can be read both ways. One school, going back to the 19th century, made him the sole real ruler beside a weak-willed tsar, while others argue for a real diarchy grounded in medieval ideas of kinship and hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Patriarch Filaret of Moscow","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Philaret_titularnik.jpg/960px-Philaret_titularnik.jpg","title":"Patriarch Filaret","desc":"Patriarch Filaret as drawn in the Tsarsky titulyarnik of 1672, the work of the Kremlin Armoury.","credit":"Tsarsky titulyarnik, Kremlin Armoury, 1672; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philaret_titularnik.jpg","alt":"A grey-bearded patriarch in a domed cap and gold vestments, holding an open book, in a frame of painted flowers."}},
    "ru-254": {"questions":["Regiments of the new foreign type, drilled by hired colonels, fought in greater numbers than ever before in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Moscow launched after the death of Sigismund III.","The peace of Polyanovka in 1634 closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, leaving the frontier near the Deulino line while Władysław IV gave up his claim to be tsar."],"abstract":"The <b>Smolensk War</b> of 1632–34 was Muscovy's attempt to win back Smolensk and the other western towns it had surrendered to Poland–Lithuania in the truce of Deulino in 1618–19.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the first war of the Romanov dynasty, and the government prepared it for years, hiring foreign officers and soldiers and buying muskets abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The death of King Sigismund III in April 1632 left Poland without a king for a time, and Moscow broke the truce without waiting for it to expire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army sent under the boyar Mikhail Shein included six infantry regiments of the new foreign type, with some 1,500 German mercenaries and nearly 13,000 Russian soldiers drilled in the Western way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At first the campaign went well, as Serpeisk, Dorogobuzh and sixteen smaller places surrendered in the autumn of 1632 before Shein laid siege to Smolensk itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Older histories gave Shein an army of 100,000 men, but the muster records point to a little over 32,000, with 158 guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smolensk held out through an eight-month siege until the new king, Władysław IV, arrived with a relief army in 1633 and in turn surrounded Shein's entrenched camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hungry, without the help Moscow had promised and pressed by his foreign officers, Shein agreed to terms in February 1634, and his army marched out leaving its guns behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Back in Moscow the boyar council condemned Shein and his second-in-command Artemy Izmailov for treason, and both were beheaded in April 1634.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The peace signed on the Polyanovka river that year left the border close to the Deulino line, while Władysław gave up his claim to the Muscovite throne and the tsar paid 200,000 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 58–61, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 224–25, 274–75, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","В. А. Григорькин and С. В. Мещеряков, «Смоленская война 1632–1634 гг.: традиции, инновации и комплексы», <i>Огарёв-Online</i> 12, no. 8 (2024): 7–15, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smolenskaya-voyna-1632-1634-gg-traditsii-innovatsii-i-kompleksy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. Н. Меньшиков, «Боевая сила армии М. Б. Шеина в Смоленском походе 1632–1634 гг.», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 4-1 (2008): 10–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boevaya-sila-armii-m-b-sheina-v-smolenskom-pohode-1632-1634-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Артёменко, «Научно-правовой комментарий приговора Боярской думы в отношении воеводы М. Б. Шеина по итогам Смоленской войны 1632–1634 гг.», <i>Вестник Хакасского государственного университета им. Н. Ф. Катанова</i>, no. 10 (2014): 135–41, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nauchno-pravovoy-kommentariy-prigovora-boyarskoy-dumy-v-otnoshenii-voevody-m-b-sheina-po-itogam-smolenskoy-voynyy-1632-1634-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 45, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow go to war in 1632?","a":"Because the death of Sigismund III opened an interregnum in Poland, and Moscow, which had spent years hiring foreign soldiers and buying arms, saw its chance to recover the towns lost at Deulino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It broke the truce without waiting for it to run out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Shein surrender?","a":"Because once Władysław IV had relieved Smolensk and surrounded the Russian camps, Shein's men began to starve, discipline broke down and the relief promised from Moscow never came. His foreign officers pressed him to come to terms, and in February 1634 he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Shein executed?","a":"Because the boyar council found him and Artemy Izmailov guilty of treason for the defeat and sentenced them to death and the loss of their estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An older view is that the two men were scapegoats for a government faced with an impossible task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Smolensk War"},
    "ru-255": {"questions":["Kyiv, pledged to Moscow for only two years by the truce of Andrusovo in 1667, was never handed back to Poland by the tsar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or by his successors.","In 1672 the tsar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened a court theatre with a play on the Book of Esther, and he was still watching its comedies only days before his death."],"abstract":"<b>Alexis Mikhailovich</b>, the second Romanov tsar, was born in 1629 and reigned from the death of his father Michael in 1645 until his own death in January 1676.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was taught the usual course of letters, the Breviary, the Psalter and church singing, yet as a small child he already had German toys and pictures in his nursery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reign was full of upheaval, with the Moscow riots of 1648, the law code of 1649, the copper money crisis and the church reforms of Patriarch Nikon, whom he first revered and then had deposed by a church council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1654 the Dnieper Cossacks swore allegiance to him at Pereiaslav, and the long war with Poland that followed ended in the truce of Andrusovo in 1667, which kept Smolensk and the left bank of the Dnieper for Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kyiv was pledged to Moscow at Andrusovo for only two years, but it was never handed back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He is remembered by the epithet 'the Most Gentle', and a clerk who fled his service wrote that as a youth he was very quiet, like his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the word was a semi-official title also given to his father and his sons, and a recently elected dynasty needed its tsar to stand for peace and quiet the more troubled his reign became.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In private he could be excitable and passionate, given to fits of rage like his son Peter, though foreigners found him gentle, humane and courteous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He kept some 200 falconers and 3,000 hawks and falcons, run through his Office of Secret Affairs, which also handled his private correspondence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1672 he opened a court theatre with a play on the Book of Esther, and only days before his death he was still watching its comedies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 158–59, 331–33, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 96–101, 107–08, 118–20, 188–89, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","И. Л. Андреев, «Алексей Михайлович: штрихи к портрету», <i>Исторический вестник</i>, no. 3 (2013): 48–69, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksey-mihaylovich-shtrihi-k-portretu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Чёрная, «Придворная культура Алексея Михайловича: от Артаксеркса до Орфея», <i>Исторический вестник</i>, no. 3 (2013): 24–47, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pridvornaya-kultura-alekseya-mihaylovicha-ot-artakserksa-do-orfeya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 78, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]","Григорий Котошихин, <i>О России в царствование Алексия Михайловича</i>, 4th ed. (St Petersburg: Imperial Archaeographic Commission, 1906), ch. 1, 4–5, https://archive.org/details/orossivtsarstvov00koto. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Г. Великоцкая (Мозгунова), «Отношение вселенских патриархов к низложению патриарха Никона», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 11 (2023): 185–92, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnoshenie-vselenskih-patriarhov-k-nizlozheniyu-patriarha-nikona. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Alexis called 'the Most Gentle'?","a":"Because it was a semi-official title of the Muscovite rulers, used for his father and his sons as well, even the far from quiet Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a dynasty elected only a generation before, a tsar who stood for peace and quiet was a political need, whatever his own temper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow keep Kyiv after Andrusovo?","a":"Because the truce of 1667, signed when Poland was exhausted by war and threatened by the Turks, pledged Kyiv to Moscow for two years, and it was never returned. The same truce gave Moscow Smolensk and the whole left bank of the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his court open a theatre?","a":"Because Alexis, while devout, was curious about Western entertainments, and plays on biblical stories suited a pious court. The first, in 1672, was on the Book of Esther, and he watched the performances from beginning to end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexis of Russia","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Alexis_I_of_Russia_(1670-1680s%2C_GIM).jpg/960px-Alexis_I_of_Russia_(1670-1680s%2C_GIM).jpg","title":"Tsar Alexis","desc":"Portrait of Tsar Alexis Mikhailovich by an unknown painter of the Armoury school, late 1670s to early 1680s; State Historical Museum, Moscow.","credit":"Unknown painter, Armoury school, c. 1670–1680; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexis_I_of_Russia_(1670-1680s%2C_GIM).jpg","alt":"A bearded tsar in a jewelled crown and gold robe, holding a sceptre and orb, with a Cyrillic inscription beside him."}},
    "ru-256": {"questions":["Hidden under birch logs and flitches of bacon, the official Nazar Chistoy was betrayed by a servant and beaten to death in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed a failed petition to Tsar Alexis.","To save his tutor Boris Morozov from the crowds of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the young Tsar Alexis pleaded for him in tears and sent him for a while to a northern monastery."],"abstract":"The <b>Salt Riot</b> is the name later given to the Moscow uprising of 1–3 June 1648, when crowds sacked the houses of the young Tsar Alexis's ministers and killed three officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name comes from a tax, for in 1646 the government raised the duty on salt by a quarter, meaning to replace two heavy direct taxes with one that everyone would pay in proportion to use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plan failed, because Volga fishermen could not afford to salt their catch and left it to rot, sales of salt fell, and the duty was withdrawn early in 1648.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anger at the government ran deeper than salt, for the favourite Boris Morozov and his allies were blamed for bribery, monopolies, unpaid salaries and new burdens on trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unrest spread that summer from Moscow to provincial towns, from Kozlov, Voronezh and Kursk in the south to Solvychegodsk and Ustyug in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The riot began when townspeople tried to petition the tsar about Leonty Pleshcheev, a judge hated for extortion, and courtiers drove them off with whips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crowd then plundered the houses of Morozov and other great men, and Nazar Chistoy, the official behind the salt duty, was found hidden under birch logs and bacon, betrayed by a servant, and beaten to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pleshcheev was clubbed to death in the square, and Peter Trakhaniotov, blamed for keeping back the pay of the gunners and craftsmen he supervised, was executed by order of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexis saved Morozov by pleading for him in tears before the crowd, sending him to the Kirillov monastery and quietly bringing him back within two months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A week after the riot, gentry and merchants petitioned for a new law code, and fear of another rising hurried the assembly that finished it in a little over six months as the Ulozhenie of 1649.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Adam Olearius, <i>The Voyages and Travells of the Ambassadors Sent by Frederick, Duke of Holstein, to the Great Duke of Muscovy and the King of Persia</i>, trans. John Davies, 2nd ed. (London, 1669), bk. 3, 82–84, https://archive.org/details/voyagestravellso00olea. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 138–39, 230–31, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 99–102, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","В. Я. Мауль, «Избиение „изменников“ и очищение от „дьявола“: восстание москвичей в 1648 году», <i>Новый исторический вестник</i>, no. 4 (54) (2017): 6–27, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izbienie-izmennikov-i-ochischenie-ot-dyavola-vosstanie-moskvichey-v-1648-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Ляпин, «Время праздника: об особенностях поведения участников народных волнений в российской провинции летом 1648 г.», <i>Tractus aevorum</i> 6, no. 1 (2019): 42–55, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vremya-prazdnika-ob-osobennostyah-povedeniya-uchastnikov-narodnyh-volneniy-v-rossiyskoy-provintsii-letom-1648-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Ляпин, «Причины „Медного бунта“ в Москве 25 июля 1662 г.», <i>Гуманитарные и юридические исследования</i>, no. 3 (2020): 51–57, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-mednogo-bunta-v-moskve-25-iyulya-1662-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Упоров, «Разработка проекта и принятие Соборного уложения 1649 г. в Московском государстве (организационно-правовой аспект)», <i>Приоритетные научные направления: от теории к практике</i>, no. 21 (2016): 23–27, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razrabotka-proekta-i-prinyatie-sobornogo-ulozheniya-1649-g-v-moskovskom-gosudarstve-organizatsionno-pravovoy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the salt tax fail?","a":"Because it priced salt beyond what many people could pay. Fishermen on the Volga could not afford to salt their catch and let it rot, far less salt was sold, and the treasury lost money, so the duty was abolished early in 1648, though the anger it caused remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the crowds attack ministers and not the tsar?","a":"Because they blamed the men around him, not Alexis himself. The rioters said they had no quarrel with the tsar but with those who abused his authority in his name,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and in the provinces too the rebels presented themselves as supporters of a just tsar against wicked boyars and officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the riot lead to a new law code?","a":"Because the government was badly frightened by the risings in Moscow and the provincial towns. It hurried the work so that the deputies could go home quickly with news of the new course and of a code promising equal justice to all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Moscow uprising of 1648"},
    "ru-257": {"questions":["Drawn up in a little over six months after the Moscow riots of 1648, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> abolished the time limit on recovering runaway peasants and so completed their bondage.","Patriarch Nikon later called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an accursed book and a law of the devil, although as an archimandrite in 1649 he had heard it read and signed it."],"abstract":"The <b>Sobornoye Ulozheniye</b>, or 'Council Code', was the law code of Muscovy, drawn up by an assembly of the land in 1648–49 and printed in 1649.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its 25 chapters and 967 articles made it the first Russian law to appear in print and the first to be arranged in chapters by subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A commission of five men under Prince Nikita Odoevsky drew up the draft from Byzantine church law, the earlier Muscovite law codes, later decrees and the registers of the government departments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elected deputies from 116 towns were summoned to Moscow for the autumn of 1648, heard the draft read and signed the finished text, together with the patriarch, the bishops and the boyars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first print run of 1,200 copies came off the Moscow press in May 1649, almost all of them were sold within weeks, and a second run of the same size was ordered in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The code was made in haste, because the government had been shaken by the Moscow riots of June 1648 and by risings in provincial towns, and the work was over by the end of January 1649.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It answered grievances the riots had raised, and its chapter on townspeople took the tax-free suburbs that churchmen and boyars owned in Moscow back into the tsar's tax-paying community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chapter on peasants met the long demand of the provincial gentry by allowing runaway peasants to be recovered with no time limit, which bound whole peasant households to the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The government presented it as a way to make justice quicker and cheaper and to give people of every rank equal justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikon, who signed it in 1649 as archimandrite of the Novospassky monastery, later damned it as an accursed book and a law of the devil, though he had kept silent when it was read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 130–46, 183–85, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","И. В. Упоров, «Разработка проекта и принятие Соборного уложения 1649 г. в Московском государстве (организационно-правовой аспект)», <i>Приоритетные научные направления: от теории к практике</i>, no. 21 (2016): 23–27, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razrabotka-proekta-i-prinyatie-sobornogo-ulozheniya-1649-g-v-moskovskom-gosudarstve-organizatsionno-pravovoy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Тимошина, «Реализация изданий тиражей Соборного Уложения на рубеже 1640-х–1650-х гг.», <i>Вестник Нижневартовского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (2009): 53–61, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/realizatsiya-izdaniy-tirazhey-sobornogo-ulozheniya-na-rubezhe-1640-h-1650-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>Соборное Уложение 1649 года (фрагменты)</i>, ch. 19, art. 1, reprinted from <i>Российское законодательство X–XX веков</i>, vol. 3, in <i>Вестник Университета имени О. Е. Кутафина</i>, no. 1 (2015): 166–87, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sobornoe-ulozhenie-1649-fragmenty. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 98, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","О. Ю. Яхшиян, «Крестьяне и помещики в системе крепостнических отношений допетровской России», <i>Вестник университета</i>, no. 8 (2020): 182–87, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyane-i-pomeschiki-v-sisteme-krepostnicheskih-otnosheniy-dopetrovskoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the code drawn up so quickly?","a":"Because the government was frightened. Work began amid rumours of risings in provincial towns after the Moscow riots of June 1648 and was finished under reports that a new upheaval was coming in the capital, so the commission worked from a few basic sources rather than studying all the records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called a council code?","a":"Because it was adopted by an assembly of the land. Deputies elected in 116 towns came to Moscow, heard the draft read and signed the finished text alongside the patriarch, the bishops and the members of the boyar council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter for the history of serfdom?","a":"Because it removed the time limit within which a landowner could sue for a runaway peasant. Peasants registered in the censuses could now be recovered at any time, and the whole household, not just the peasant, was bound to the landowner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sobornoye Ulozheniye"},
    "ru-258": {"questions":["Provincial gentry whose peasants fled to great landlords petitioned repeatedly in the 1630s and 1640s for an end to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which let a runaway stay put once the term expired.","The term <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, first used of runaway peasants in documents of the 1630s, named the years within which a landlord could sue to bring a fugitive back."],"abstract":"The <b>urochnye leta</b>, or 'fixed years', were the time limit within which a Muscovite landowner could sue to recover a peasant who had run away from his estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once the term ran out, the runaway could stay where he had settled, so the length of the term largely decided how firmly peasants were tied to the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older view dates a five-year term to a decree of 1597, but the phrase appears for runaways only in the 1630s, and the decree of 1597 may have fixed a single starting date rather than a lasting rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the early 17th century the system was unsettled, with years in which peasants were forbidden to move broken by years in which moving was allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small landowners complained that their peasants fled to 'the strong people', the great landlords and monasteries, who had the means to keep them until the term expired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The provincial gentry petitioned again and again for the fixed years to be abolished, and in 1637 the government answered a collective petition by lengthening the term to nine years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A new collective petition in 1641 won ten years for runaways and fifteen for peasants carried off by another landlord, who also had to pay a fine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government held back from going further, partly because runaways were settling the southern frontier, where many enrolled as soldiers and garrison men in towns such as Kozlov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1646 it promised that peasants entered in the new census would be bound 'without fixed years', and the law code of 1649 kept that promise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The code allowed runaways registered in the census of the 1620s or of 1646–47 to be recovered with no time limit, binding not only the peasant but his whole household to the landowner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 183–85, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Ю. А. Мизис, «Поиски беглецов на южной границе России в 30–40-х гг. XVII в. (на примере Козловского уезда)», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 10 (150) (2015): 37–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poiski-begletsov-na-yuzhnoy-granitse-rossii-v-30-40-h-gg-xvii-v-na-primere-kozlovskogo-uezda. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Аракчеев, «Проблема „урочных лет“ в Русском государстве в конце XVI – начале XVII века», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 85 (2008): 31–37, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-urochnyh-let-v-russkom-gosudarstve-v-kontse-xvi-nachale-xvii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Ю. Яхшиян, «Крестьяне и помещики в системе крепостнических отношений допетровской России», <i>Вестник университета</i>, no. 8 (2020): 182–87, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyane-i-pomeschiki-v-sisteme-krepostnicheskih-otnosheniy-dopetrovskoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 51, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the gentry want the fixed years abolished?","a":"Because a short term let their peasants slip away for good. Runaways went to great landlords and monasteries,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> who could hide them and had their own men to recover fugitives,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the term was often too short for a small landowner to trace and sue a runaway before it expired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government hesitate to abolish them?","a":"Because runaways were useful on the southern frontier. Many settled there and enrolled as soldiers or garrison men, and the government needed the south settled and its borders manned,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> so it lengthened the term in 1637 and 1641 instead of ending it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date 1597 doubtful?","a":"Because the evidence for a lasting five-year term in the 1590s is thin. The phrase is first used of runaway peasants only in the 1630s, and the decree of 1597 may simply have set November 1592 as the date from which suits could be counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-259": {"questions":["Soldiers paid in debased coins were deserting by companies when Moscow crowds rose in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1662, a year before the government restored silver money.","Some of the Muscovites arrested after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1662 were branded on the face with a letter standing for 'rebel' and exiled for life to Siberia or Astrakhan."],"abstract":"The <b>Copper Riot</b> is the usual name for the Moscow disturbance of 25 July 1662, when crowds marched to Tsar Alexis at the royal village of Kolomenskoye to demand that traitors at his court be handed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its background was a money experiment of the war with Poland, when the treasury, short of silver to pay its troops, issued copper coins at the same face value as silver ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Copper kopecks were struck from 1655 at two mints in Moscow and at mints in Novgorod and Pskov, and for a time people took them as readily as silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then counterfeiting spread, as mint workers, merchants and even the supervisors of the issue turned private copper into coins, while officials took bribes to shield them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the spring of 1663 a silver rouble cost 15 copper ones in Moscow, prices soared, and soldiers paid only in copper began to desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The rising began when a notice pasted up on the Lubyanka accused the tsar's father-in-law Ilya Miloslavsky, Fyodor Rtishchev and the merchant Vasily Shorin of treason with Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A crowd took the notice to Kolomenskoye, held the tsar by his coat and made him strike hands on a promise to investigate, before a second crowd from Moscow arrived demanding the traitors at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexis then ordered his guards and the streltsy to attack the unarmed crowd, and many were cut down or drowned in the Moskva river, while others were mutilated, branded on the face with a letter for 'rebel' and exiled to Astrakhan or Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rioters did not ask for the copper money to be withdrawn, and the name 'Copper Riot' has been called a coinage of the early 20th century that hides what the crowd actually wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The copper money itself was given up by a decree of 1663, which restored silver and ordered copper coins to be melted down or handed in to the treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 231–34, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Григорий Котошихин, <i>О России в царствование Алексия Михайловича</i>, 4th ed. (St Petersburg: Imperial Archaeographic Commission, 1906), ch. 7, 100–102, https://archive.org/details/orossivtsarstvov00koto. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Ляпин, «Причины „Медного бунта“ в Москве 25 июля 1662 г.», <i>Гуманитарные и юридические исследования</i>, no. 3 (2020): 51–57, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-mednogo-bunta-v-moskve-25-iyulya-1662-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Зверев, «Монетное дело в Новгороде в середине XVII в.», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 4 (37) (2018): 72–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/monetnoe-delo-v-novgorode-v-seredine-xvii-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 118, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Д. Е. Расков, «Денежная реформа Алексея Михайловича и проблема доверия», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Экономика</i>, no. 3 (2008): 69–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/denezhnaya-reforma-alekseya-mihaylovicha-i-problema-doveriya-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the copper coins lose their value?","a":"Because far too many were made. Copper had been issued at the face value of silver, and counterfeiters, including mint workers and the merchants meant to supervise the issue, flooded the market with coins of their own,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> so that by the spring of 1663 a silver rouble cost fifteen copper ones in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the crowd blame Ilya Miloslavsky?","a":"Because earlier in 1662 the tsar's father-in-law had been caught protecting counterfeiters, and the tsar had forgiven him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those arrested for false coining said that wealthy offenders bought their way out with large bribes to Miloslavsky and other officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some historians reject the name 'Copper Riot'?","a":"Because the investigation records show that the crowd demanded the punishment of supposed traitors and never asked for the copper money to be withdrawn. On this view the debased coinage was only the background, alongside bad harvests, defeats and plague, and the name arose in the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Copper Riot"},
    "ru-260": {"questions":["Told by the tsar's envoy to stop calling himself 'great sovereign', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid down his vestments in the Dormition Cathedral in July 1658 and withdrew to New Jerusalem.","Deposed by a council in December 1666 in the presence of two Eastern patriarchs, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent his last years as a simple monk in the monasteries of the far north."],"abstract":"<b>Patriarch Nikon</b> was head of the Russian Church from 1652, a peasant's son who became the closest friend of Tsar Alexis and then fell from power in a public quarrel with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born Nikita, son of the peasant Mina, near Nizhny Novgorod in 1605, he taught himself to read and became a married parish priest before the deaths of his three children turned him to the monastic life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took the name Nikon in a White Sea hermitage, became abbot of the Kozheozersky monastery, and in 1646 met the young Alexis, who made him archimandrite of the Novospassky monastery in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As Metropolitan of Novgorod he faced the city's rising of 1650, talking to the rebellious crowds with both flexibility and firmness, and his conduct raised him still higher in the tsar's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elected patriarch in 1652, he refused the office until the tsar and the boyars swore in the cathedral to obey him in all matters of faith and church law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For a few years he signed himself 'great sovereign' and the tsar called him his 'beloved friend', but many at court resented his power and his temper, and Nikon himself later blamed the boyars for his fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In July 1658, after the tsar stayed away from his services and told him to stop using the title, Nikon laid down his vestments in the Dormition Cathedral and withdrew to his New Jerusalem monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most historians long read the withdrawal as a demonstration meant to make the tsar beg him back, though one later study sees it as an admission that he could carry his reforms no further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A church council with the patriarchs of Alexandria and Antioch deposed him on 12 December 1666 and sent him as a simple monk to the Ferapontov monastery in the far north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Alexis died he was moved to the harsher Kirillov monastery, and when Tsar Fyodor let him return to New Jerusalem in 1681 he died on the road near Yaroslavl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 126–31, 143–51, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 145, 252, 308–09, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03kliu. [Open access]","Н. Г. Великоцкая (Мозгунова), «Отношение вселенских патриархов к низложению патриарха Никона», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 11 (2023): 185–92, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnoshenie-vselenskih-patriarhov-k-nizlozheniyu-patriarha-nikona. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. И. Мотыка, «Патриарх Никон в опале», <i>Гуманитарий: актуальные проблемы гуманитарной науки и образования</i>, no. 1 (5) (2005): 54–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/patriarh-nikon-v-opale. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Л. Андреев and А. Ю. Челнокова, «Митрополит Никон в новгородских событиях 1650 г.», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 4 (60) (2025): 6–27, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mitropolit-nikon-v-novgorodskih-sobytiyah-1650-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. В. Кричевский, «Обстоятельства отречения Никона от кафедры», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 78 (2008): 54–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obstoyatelstva-otrecheniya-nikona-ot-kafedry. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. В. Кричевский, «Бояре в процессе отстранения Никона от власти», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1 (2007): 18–26, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boyare-v-protsesse-otstraneniya-nikona-ot-vlasti. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tsar and the patriarch fall out?","a":"Because Nikon's power came to eclipse the tsar's. He signed himself great sovereign, a title the court insisted belonged to the tsar alone,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and his enemies among the boyars worked to widen the breach between the two old friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Nikon refuse the patriarchate at first?","a":"Because he wanted a free hand before he began. He held out until the tsar, the boyars and the bishops swore before the icons that they would obey him as their chief pastor in everything he taught concerning dogma and the church canons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his withdrawal of 1658 read in different ways?","a":"Because his motives were never stated plainly. The older reading is that he left in the hope that the tsar would humbly beg him to return,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while a later study argues that it was not a demonstration but an admission that he could not carry his church reforms further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Patriarch Nikon of Moscow","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Nikon_by_Ivan_Bezmin_(1680s%2C_Kremlin_museum).jpg/960px-Nikon_by_Ivan_Bezmin_(1680s%2C_Kremlin_museum).jpg","title":"Patriarch Nikon","desc":"Patriarch Nikon painted by Ivan Bezmin in the 1680s; Kremlin Armoury.","credit":"Painting by Ivan Bezmin, 1680s; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikon_by_Ivan_Bezmin_(1680s%2C_Kremlin_museum).jpg","alt":"A standing bearded churchman in a mitre and long gold vestments, raising one hand in blessing."}},
    "ru-261": {"questions":["By declaring that the fathers of the Stoglav council had erred, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned the two-fingered sign of the cross from an approved Russian custom into a condemned heresy.","Only the part printed in the service book of 1667 reached contemporaries, because the full acts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept in the patriarch's treasury, open to the patriarch alone."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Moscow Synod</b> was the church council that met in Moscow from late 1666 into 1667, with two of the Eastern patriarchs present, and made the revised rites of the Russian Church binding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its full acts were kept in the patriarch's treasury, open to the patriarch alone, so contemporaries knew only the part of them printed in the service book of 1667.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great council opened on 18 November 1666 in the presence of Tsar Alexis, who set out the charges against Patriarch Nikon himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 12 December it deposed Nikon for reviling the tsar and the Russian Church, for removing a bishop against the canons and for mistreating his dependants, and sent him away as a simple monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then did something that looked like a contradiction, confirming the reforms made under the deposed patriarch and anathematising all who refused the revised service books and the three-fingered sign of the cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The council also condemned the decisions of the Stoglav, the Russian council of 1551 that had approved the two-fingered sign and other local customs, declaring that its fathers had erred through ignorance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sixth act went further, calling the two-fingered sign an 'Armenian heresy' and so turning the old symbols of Russian Orthodoxy into errors that no compromise could accommodate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same act withdrew a ruling of 1620 that Catholics joining the Orthodox Church must be baptised again, a softening towards the Latin West at the very moment the Russian past was condemned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sentence fell hardest on the defenders of the old books, and the archpriest Avvakum, brought before the council to be tried, was condemned in July 1667 and sent into exile at Pustozersk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Old Believers' history as a body apart from the official church is usually dated from these councils, and only in 1971 did a council of the Russian Orthodox Church lift the curses they had laid on the old rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 295–96, 321–22, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 146–47, 150, 154, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Elena V. Belyakova, “K izucheniyu istorii Soborov 1666 i 1666–1667 gg.” [To the study of the history of the Councils of 1666 and 1666–1667], <i>Istoricheskii kur’er</i>, no. 2 (2019): article 5, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2019-2-5. [in Russian] [Open access]","Avvakum, <i>The Life of the Archpriest Avvakum by Himself</i>, trans. Jane Harrison and Hope Mirrlees, with a preface by D. S. Mirsky (London: Hogarth Press, 1924), 13, 31, https://archive.org/details/bwb_C0-ARS-081. [Open access]","Frederick C. Conybeare, <i>Russian Dissenters</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), 79–81, https://archive.org/details/russiandissenter00conyuoft. [Open access]","Roman V. Kapinos and Danil A. Bratishchev, “Problema otnoshenii mezhdu Russkoi Pravoslavnoi Tserkov’yu, gosudarstvom i staroobryadtsami v istoricheskom kontekste” [Relations between the Russian Orthodox Church, the state and the Old Believers in historical context], <i>Trudy Belgorodskoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 15 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-otnosheniy-mezhdu-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkovyu-gosudarstvom-i-staroobryadtsami-v-istoricheskom-kontekste. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the council depose Nikon yet confirm his reforms?","a":"Because the charges against Nikon concerned his conduct, not his books: reviling the tsar and the church, deposing a bishop against the canons and mistreating his dependants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The revised rites rested on agreement with the Greek Church, which the visiting patriarchs represented, so the council could remove the man and keep the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the council's verdict make compromise impossible?","a":"Because it did more than prefer the new forms. It called the two-fingered sign an 'Armenian heresy' and declared that the fathers of the Stoglav had erred, so the symbols the defenders of the old rites held sacred became errors in the church's own law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did contemporaries know so little of what the council decided?","a":"Because its full acts were kept in the patriarch's treasury, where only the patriarch could consult them. Contemporaries learned only the part printed in the service book of 1667, and decrees such as the call for councils twice a year were never made public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great Moscow Synod"},
    "ru-262": {"questions":["Two fingers or three in the sign of the cross, two Alleluias or three, lay at the heart of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although neither side disputed any point of doctrine.","Excommunicated by the councils of 1666 and 1667, the dissenters stopped recognising the bishops who condemned them, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has divided the Russian Church ever since."],"abstract":"The <b>Raskol</b>, the Russian name for the great schism, was the split that separated a large part of the Russian Orthodox community from the official church in the reign of Tsar Alexis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cause was a set of changes to ritual and service books introduced from 1653 by Patriarch Nikon, who wanted Russian practice to agree with that of the Greek Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The changes touched habits that Russians had followed for generations, such as crossing oneself with two fingers, spelling the name of Jesus as 'Isus' and singing the Alleluia twice rather than three times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of these were points of doctrine, and since the two sides held the same faith the church has treated the dissenters as schismatics and not as heretics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian council had confirmed several of the old customs in 1551, and the first printed service books of Moscow then spread them across the country, so to many believers they looked like the faith itself and the corrected books like a new faith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both sides argued in writing, and church writers such as Epiphanius Slavinetsky defended the reform in sermons that blamed the resistance to it on its opponents' ignorance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Nikon made the quarrel worse by the way he enforced the changes, and Paul, Bishop of Kolomna, who had opposed him at the council of 1654, was deposed, beaten and imprisoned, and died in a manner never recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decisive step came at the councils of 1666 and 1667, which declared the two-fingered sign a heresy and excommunicated those who held to the old rites, closing the way to compromise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excommunicated in turn stopped recognising the bishops who had cut them off, and the Russian Church has been divided by the schism ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Even the name is contested, and some historians call 'Raskol' an inaccurate label for what grew into a broad religious and social movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 295–96, 317–18, 322, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Frederick C. Conybeare, <i>Russian Dissenters</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), 48–49, https://archive.org/details/russiandissenter00conyuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 160, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Elena V. Belyakova, “K izucheniyu istorii Soborov 1666 i 1666–1667 gg.” [To the study of the history of the Councils of 1666 and 1666–1667], <i>Istoricheskii kur’er</i>, no. 2 (2019): article 5, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2019-2-5. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. F. Mokshin and E. N. Mokshina, “Protopop Avvakum Petrov — revnitel’ ‘istinno russkoi very’ (k 400-letiyu so dnya rozhdeniya)” [Archpriest Avvakum Petrov, zealot of the ‘true Russian faith’], <i>Istoriya i sovremennoe mirovozzrenie</i> 2, no. 2 (2020): 28–31, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protopop-avvakum-petrov-revnitel-istinno-russkoy-very-k-400-letiyu-so-dnya-rozhdeniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Avvakum, <i>The Life of the Archpriest Avvakum by Himself</i>, trans. Jane Harrison and Hope Mirrlees, with a preface by D. S. Mirsky (London: Hogarth Press, 1924), 12–13, https://archive.org/details/bwb_C0-ARS-081. [Open access]","Tamara V. Panich, “Tserkovnye pisateli vtoroi poloviny XVII veka v bor’be s raskolom” [Church writers of the second half of the 17th century in the struggle against the schism], <i>Istoricheskii kur’er</i>, no. 2 (2024): 147–55, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2024-2-10. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did small differences of ritual split the church?","a":"Because many Russians drew little line between service books and Scripture itself, so a corrected book seemed to say that God's word had been faulty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old customs had been approved by a council in 1551 and spread by the printing press, which made them look like the faith rather than habit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the dissenters called schismatics rather than heretics?","a":"Because they held the same doctrine as the official church, and the dispute concerned ritual and obedience, not belief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They separated by refusing to recognise the bishops who had excommunicated them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Nikon's methods deepen the split?","a":"Because he enforced the changes suddenly and harshly. Paul, Bishop of Kolomna, who had opposed him at the council of 1654, was deposed, beaten and imprisoned, and by persecuting such men Nikon gave his opponents the standing of martyrs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Schism of the Russian Church"},
    "ru-263": {"questions":["Over twenty thousand of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are said to have burned themselves alive in the late 17th century rather than wait for an end of the world they believed was near.","In 1683 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided into a majority that kept priests and sacraments and a radical priestless minority that held its services without them."],"abstract":"The <b>Old Believers</b> are the Russian Orthodox Christians who rejected the changes to ritual and service books made under Patriarch Nikon and kept to the rites in use before the 1650s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They call themselves the keepers of the old faith and call members of the official church 'Nikonians', while the church has treated them as schismatics who share its doctrine but refuse its authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They became a separate body after the church council of 1666 and 1667 anathematised them, and the government then persecuted them as both schismatics and state criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Persecution drove them to the edges of the state, to the far north, the Urals, the Don and the Volga, and beyond its borders into Polish-ruled Belarus, Ukraine and Moldavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1683 they divided into a majority who kept priests and the sacraments and a radical minority, the 'priestless', who held services without priests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the 17th century many Old Believers expected the end of the world, convinced, as Avvakum taught from his prison, that the rule of Antichrist had come, and over 20,000 of them burned themselves to death rather than wait for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> These burnings were often prepared with care, with buildings put up for the purpose, special death shirts and the eight-pointed cross, and were sometimes timed to fall on religious feasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Explanations have ranged from protest against persecution to a belief in the cleansing and saving power of fire, and one recent reading sees them as popular religion that put rite before doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fall of the rebel Solovetsky Monastery to the tsar's troops strengthened the priestless current, because many took it as the end of any true clergy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Toleration came only with an imperial edict of 1905, after which the priestly Old Believers made Moscow their centre, and the Russian Orthodox Church lifted its curses on the old rites only in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Ivan Korovytsky and Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Old Believers,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 3 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CO%5CL%5COldBelievers.htm. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 295, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Frederick C. Conybeare, <i>Russian Dissenters</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), 79, https://archive.org/details/russiandissenter00conyuoft. [Open access]","Irina V. Lysak, “Ritual’nye samoubiistva staroobryadtsev v Rossii kak proyavlenie narodnoi religioznosti” [Ritual suicides of Old Believers in Russia as a manifestation of popular religiosity], <i>Suitsidologiya</i> 10, no. 3 (2019): 102–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ritualnye-samoubiystva-staroobryadtsev-v-rossii-kak-proyavlenie-narodnoy-religioznosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir D. Chernykh, Galina A. Bykovskaya and Roman V. Cherenkov, “Osnovnye prichiny i khod Solovetskogo vosstaniya” [Main causes and course of the Solovetsky Monastery uprising], <i>Manuskript</i>, no. 8 (2018): 41–44, https://doi.org/10.30853/manuscript.2018-8.9. [in Russian] [Open access]","Avvakum, <i>The Life of the Archpriest Avvakum by Himself</i>, trans. Jane Harrison and Hope Mirrlees, with a preface by D. S. Mirsky (London: Hogarth Press, 1924), 13–14, https://archive.org/details/bwb_C0-ARS-081. [Open access]","Roman V. Kapinos and Danil A. Bratishchev, “Problema otnoshenii mezhdu Russkoi Pravoslavnoi Tserkov’yu, gosudarstvom i staroobryadtsami v istoricheskom kontekste” [Relations between the Russian Orthodox Church, the state and the Old Believers in historical context], <i>Trudy Belgorodskoi dukhovnoi seminarii</i>, no. 15 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-otnosheniy-mezhdu-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkovyu-gosudarstvom-i-staroobryadtsami-v-istoricheskom-kontekste. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Old Believers settle on the edges of Russia and beyond?","a":"Because church and state persecuted them as schismatics and state criminals. Distance gave safety, so communities grew in the far north, the Urals, the Don and the Volga, and across the border in Polish-ruled Belarus, Ukraine and Moldavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Old Believers burn themselves to death?","a":"Many expected the end of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Explanations have stressed persecution, a belief in the cleansing power of fire and ritual practice, and the burnings were prepared with care, down to special death shirts and the eight-pointed cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fall of the Solovetsky Monastery strengthen the priestless Old Believers?","a":"Because one of the most respected monasteries in Russia had been taken by the tsar's troops, and many took that as the end of any true clergy. Monks who escaped settled between Lake Onega and the White Sea and helped the priestless movement grow there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Old Believers"},
    "ru-264": {"questions":["Mixing the manner of a spoken sermon with everyday speech, the autobiography that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote while imprisoned at Pustozersk is counted among the masterpieces of 17th-century Russian prose.","Burned alive with three fellow prisoners in April 1682, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had urged his followers in letters smuggled from his earthen prison to choose the fire over the new rites."],"abstract":"<b>Avvakum</b> Petrov was a Russian archpriest, born around 1620 near Nizhny Novgorod, who became the best-known opponent of the church reforms of the 1650s and the first great leader of the Old Believers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The son of a village priest, he was ordained in the 1640s and made his name as a fierce preacher who denounced the sins of his parishioners and the abuses of the powerful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Moscow he belonged to a circle of zealous clergy that at first worked alongside Nikon, and he broke with him once the new patriarch began to correct the service books against Greek texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For his protest he was exiled to Siberia in 1653 and spent years with a military expedition in Dauria, beyond Lake Baikal, before he was allowed back to Moscow in 1664.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He soon resumed his attacks, was exiled again to Mezen near the White Sea, and in 1666 a church council stripped him of his holy orders and pronounced him a heretic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In July 1667 the council condemned him once more, and he was sent to Pustozersk in the far north, where he spent some fourteen years in an earthen prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There, around 1673, he wrote his <i>Life</i>, an autobiography that mixes the manner of a spoken sermon with everyday and official speech and is counted among the masterpieces of 17th-century Russian writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His letters, carried out by sympathetic guards and copied by visitors, urged his followers to stand firm and even to seek death by fire rather than submit to the new rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a violent letter to the young Tsar Fyodor he and three fellow prisoners were burned alive at Pustozersk in April 1682, the date most accounts give, though older English writers put it a year earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people of the Pustozersk district, though members of the official church, went on honouring the place of his execution from the late 17th century into the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Avvakum, <i>The Life of the Archpriest Avvakum by Himself</i>, trans. Jane Harrison and Hope Mirrlees, with a preface by D. S. Mirsky (London: Hogarth Press, 1924), 12–14, 31–32, https://archive.org/details/bwb_C0-ARS-081. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 132–34, 154–55, 159–62, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 317, 319, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","N. F. Mokshin and E. N. Mokshina, “Protopop Avvakum Petrov — revnitel’ ‘istinno russkoi very’ (k 400-letiyu so dnya rozhdeniya)” [Archpriest Avvakum Petrov, zealot of the ‘true Russian faith’], <i>Istoriya i sovremennoe mirovozzrenie</i> 2, no. 2 (2020): 28–31, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protopop-avvakum-petrov-revnitel-istinno-russkoy-very-k-400-letiyu-so-dnya-rozhdeniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir D. Chernykh and Vitalii Yu. Zakharov, “Filosofsko-religioznye vozzreniya protopopa Avvakuma v period ego deyatel’nosti v Pustozerske” [The philosophical and religious views of Archpriest Avvakum during his time in Pustozersk], <i>Gumanitarnyi vestnik</i>, no. 1 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/filosofsko-religioznye-vozzreniya-protopopa-avvakuma-v-period-ego-deyatelnosti-v-pustozerske. [in Russian] [Open access]","Tat’yana V. Kortava, “Yazyk i stil’ proizvedenii pustozerskikh uznikov” [The language and style of the works of the Pustozersk prisoners], <i>Vestnik Moskovskogo universiteta, Seriya 9: Filologiya</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yazyk-i-stil-proizvedeniy-pustozerskih-uznikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Elena G. Menshakova, “O pochitanii ‘avvakumov’ v Pustozerskoi volosti” [On the veneration of the ‘Avvakums’ in Pustozersk district], <i>Rossiiskii zhurnal istorii Tserkvi</i> 3, no. 1S (2022): 38–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-pochitanii-avvakumov-v-pustozerskoy-volosti. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Avvakum break with Nikon, his former ally?","a":"Both had belonged to the circle of zealous clergy in Moscow, but Nikon chose to correct the books against Greek texts, which Avvakum rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Nikon ordered the three-fingered sign, Avvakum wrote that it was as though winter had come upon them, so troubled were their hearts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could his writings spread from a prison in the far north?","a":"Because guards who shared the prisoners' beliefs let letters pass in and out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Copied by pilgrims and other visitors, his autobiography, treatises and epistles circulated widely among his followers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Avvakum finally executed?","a":"He sent a violent letter to the young Tsar Fyodor claiming that the late Tsar Alexis was in torment, and he was condemned to be burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most accounts now date his death at the stake to April 1682.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Avvakum"},
    "ru-265": {"questions":["Cossacks who had fled the defeat of Stenka Razin's rising took a leading part among the defenders in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ended when a deserter showed the besiegers a way in.","Spread by pilgrims along the White Sea coast and celebrated in an Old Believer epic, the story of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned rebel monks into martyrs of the old faith."],"abstract":"The <b>Solovetsky Monastery uprising</b> was the armed resistance of the monks of the Solovetsky Monastery, on an island in the White Sea, to the church reforms of the 1650s and to the troops sent to enforce them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The monastery, founded in the late 14th century, was one of the most respected in Russia, and by the 17th century it held the whole White Sea coast from the river Onega to the Kola Peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its brothers refused the new service books when they arrived, leaving them sealed in the armoury for about ten years and going on with the old rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quarrel hardened after the church councils of 1666 and 1667, as the monks turned away the abbots sent from Moscow and petitioned the tsar, one petition calling the reforms a 'new faith'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1668 the government confiscated the monastery's estates and salt works and sent several hundred musketeers to blockade it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For years the tsar wanted a blockade rather than an assault, while the defenders, armed with the monastery's cannon, fired on the besiegers and made sorties against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fugitive Cossacks from the defeated band of Stenka Razin joined the defenders and pushed them towards open war, while monks who wanted to negotiate were imprisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1674 a council inside the monastery stopped praying for the tsar in the liturgy, a direct challenge to his authority, and a new commander pressed the siege with batteries and trenches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The monastery fell on 22 January 1676, when a monk who had deserted showed the troops a way in under the White Tower, and many of the surviving defenders were executed or exiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pilgrims and escaped monks spread the story of the siege along the White Sea coast, and Old Believer histories, an illustrated account and historical songs turned its defenders into martyrs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick C. Conybeare, <i>Russian Dissenters</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), 79–85, https://archive.org/details/russiandissenter00conyuoft. [Open access]","Vladimir D. Chernykh, Galina A. Bykovskaya and Roman V. Cherenkov, “Osnovnye prichiny i khod Solovetskogo vosstaniya” [Main causes and course of the Solovetsky Monastery uprising], <i>Manuskript</i>, no. 8 (2018): 41–44, https://doi.org/10.30853/manuscript.2018-8.9. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 322–23, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Ivan Korovytsky and Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Old Believers,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 3 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CO%5CL%5COldBelievers.htm. [Open access]","Elena V. Belyakova, “K izucheniyu istorii Soborov 1666 i 1666–1667 gg.” [To the study of the history of the Councils of 1666 and 1666–1667], <i>Istoricheskii kur’er</i>, no. 2 (2019): article 5, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2019-2-5. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vasilii N. Matonin and Natal’ya N. Bedina, “Osobennosti khronotopa staroobryadcheskikh tekstov o Solovetskom vosstanii” [The chronotope of Old Believer texts about the Solovetsky uprising], <i>Vestnik slavyanskikh kul’tur</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-hronotopa-staroobryadcheskih-tekstov-o-solovetskom-vosstanii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the monastery resist the state for so long?","a":"It was rich and powerful, with lands along the whole White Sea coast and its own cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For years the tsar also preferred a blockade to an assault, and the commanders sent against it often withdrew to the mainland, against his orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stenka Razin's Cossacks matter to the defence?","a":"Because they were men trained for war who pushed the monastery towards armed resistance. Once they joined, power passed to the radicals, and monks who wanted to make peace with the tsar were imprisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the siege become a legend among Old Believers?","a":"The monastery had long been a place of pilgrimage, and pilgrims turned away from its walls carried tales of persecution along the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Escaped monks spread the story too, and an Old Believer epic written about forty years later made the defenders martyrs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Solovetsky Monastery uprising"},
    "ru-266": {"questions":["Deserted by the Crimean khan at Zboriv and again at Berestechko, the leader of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> finally turned to the tsar of Muscovy for protection against Poland.","Celebrated by Ukrainians as the work of a national hero and remembered by Jews through the chronicle of Nathan Hanover as a time of massacre, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no shared memory."],"abstract":"The <b>Khmelnytsky Uprising</b> was the rising of the Cossacks and peasants of Ukraine against the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth that began in 1648 under the hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cossack revolts had been crushed again and again in the decades before, and in 1638 the Cossacks lost their most important rights, while those struck off the register were made serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Khmelnytsky began with a small force at the Zaporozhian Sich in January 1648, won the Crimean Tatars as allies, and destroyed two Polish armies at Zhovti Vody and Korsun in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What started as a Cossack revolt became a mass rising against the Polish nobility, and by the end of 1648 his army had besieged Lviv and Zamość in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war was fought with great cruelty on both sides, and the rebels killed nobles, Catholic clergy and Jews, many of whom had sided with the Polish lords, without sparing women or children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Final victory never came, because the Tatar khan, the rising's indispensable ally, made terms with the Poles in 1649, forcing a treaty at Zboriv that Poland never carried out, and deserted the Cossacks again at Berestechko in 1651.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Out of the war came the Cossack state known as the Hetmanate, which governed most of central Ukraine from its first capital at Chyhyryn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Without a reliable ally Khmelnytsky turned to Moscow, and in 1654 he accepted the protection of the tsar, which brought Muscovy into the war against Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died in August 1657, still at war, and the rising is usually counted as ending with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its memory has never been shared, for Khmelnytsky is a national hero to Ukrainians, a traitor to many Poles and Russians, and to Jews the leader of massacres recorded by the chronicler Nathan Hanover in 1653.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Orest Subtelny, “Cossack-Polish War,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 1 (1984), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CC%5CO%5CCossack6PolishWar.htm. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 117–21, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 107–8, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Judith Kalik, review of <i>Stories of Khmelnytsky: Competing Literary Legacies of the 1648 Ukrainian Cossack Uprising</i>, ed. Amelia M. Glaser, <i>East/West: Journal of Ukrainian Studies</i> 5, no. 1 (2018): 197–200, https://doi.org/10.21226/ewjus380. [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Hetman state,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 2 (1989), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CH%5CE%5CHetmanstate.htm. [Open access]","Lubomyr Wynar, “Registered Cossacks,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 4 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CR%5CE%5CRegisteredCossacks.htm. [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Zboriv, Treaty of,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 5 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CZ%5CB%5CZborivTreatyof.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a Cossack revolt grow into a mass rising?","a":"Because peasants and townspeople shared the Cossacks' grievances against the Polish nobility. After the first victories in 1648 the population rose against the nobles, and fighting spread across Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even before, the Cossacks had become defenders of the Orthodox against the Church union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Khmelnytsky never finish the war?","a":"He depended on the Crimean Tatars, and the khan withdrew at decisive moments, at Zboriv in 1649 and again at Berestechko in 1651.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without outside help the Cossacks alone could not bring Poland to terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the uprising remembered so differently?","a":"Ukrainians honour Khmelnytsky as a national hero, while many Poles and Russians call him a traitor. Jewish memory, shaped by Nathan Hanover's chronicle of 1653, sees him as the leader of massacres in which whole communities, such as that of Tulchin, were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Khmelnytsky Uprising"},
    "ru-267": {"questions":["With the original documents lost and the terms left vague, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read in Ukraine as a temporary alliance and in Moscow as grounds for ever closer control.","Soviet celebrations in 1954 presented the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of three hundred years before as the 'reunification' of Ukraine with Russia and required that word to be used everywhere."],"abstract":"The <b>Pereiaslav Agreement</b> was the settlement of 1654 by which Bohdan Khmelnytsky and the Cossack Host of Ukraine accepted the protection of Tsar Alexis of Muscovy in the middle of their war with Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Khmelnytsky turned to Moscow after his Crimean Tatar allies betrayed him a third time at Zhvanets in 1653, and Moscow agreed because the alternative was a Cossack Ukraine under the Ottoman sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A national assembly in Moscow approved the decision in the autumn of 1653, and on 8 January 1654 the hetman and his council met the tsar's envoys at Pereiaslav, where the Cossacks swore allegiance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The envoys refused to swear in the tsar's name in return, saying that an autocrat could not take an oath to his own subjects in the way the king of Poland did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The terms were settled in Moscow that spring, confirming the rights of the Cossacks, nobles, townspeople and clergy and fixing the paid Cossack register at 60,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The original documents have not survived, and the vagueness of the copies and drafts that remain left each side free to read the agreement its own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Khmelnytsky's government treated it as a temporary military alliance, while Moscow, especially after his death, used it to justify a growing control, renegotiating the terms with each new hetman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians have called the result everything from incorporation or union to vassalage, a protectorate or a military alliance, and the argument has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet historians denied that it was a treaty at all, and in 1954 the Soviet state celebrated its 300th anniversary as the 'reunification' of Ukraine with Russia, insisting on that word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same celebrations included Khrushchev's transfer of Crimea to Soviet Ukraine, a reminder that the meaning of 1654 had long been a question of present-day politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Oleksander Ohloblyn, “Pereiaslav Treaty of 1654,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 3 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CP%5CE%5CPereiaslavTreatyof1654.htm. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 120–22, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 108, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Alexander I. Prokofiev, “Prazdnovanie 300-letiya Pereyaslavskoi rady v 1954 g.: diskursy istoricheskoi pamyati o nachale Trinadtsatiletnei voiny 1654–1667 gg.” [The celebration of the 300th anniversary of the Pereyaslav Rada in 1954], <i>Peterburgskii istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2022): 34–47, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prazdnovanie-300-letiya-pereyaslavskoy-rady-v-1954-g-diskursy-istoricheskoy-pamyati-o-nachale-trinadtsatiletney-voyny-1654-1667-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Judith Kalik, review of <i>Stories of Khmelnytsky: Competing Literary Legacies of the 1648 Ukrainian Cossack Uprising</i>, ed. Amelia M. Glaser, <i>East/West: Journal of Ukrainian Studies</i> 5, no. 1 (2018): 197–200, https://doi.org/10.21226/ewjus380. [Open access]","A. M. Ivanov and A. E. Savin, “Andrusovskii mir 1667 g. i ego rol’ v russko-pol’skikh otnosheniyakh” [The Peace of Andrusovo of 1667 and its role in Russian–Polish relations], <i>Vestnik Smolenskoi gosudarstvennoi meditsinskoi akademii</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/andrusovskiy-mir-1667-g-i-ego-rol-v-russko-polskih-otnosheniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Hetman state,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 2 (1989), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CH%5CE%5CHetmanstate.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Khmelnytsky seek the tsar's protection?","a":"His Tatar allies had betrayed him three times, most recently at Zhvanets in 1653, and he could no longer rely on Ottoman support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without help he faced defeat by Poland or submission to the sultan and the khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tsar's envoys refuse to swear an oath?","a":"They said that an absolute ruler could not swear to be faithful to his own subjects as the king of Poland did. The Cossacks had to trust their new master, which left the terms resting on Moscow's goodwill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the agreement been read in so many ways?","a":"The originals are lost and the surviving texts are vague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ukraine's government treated it as a temporary alliance, Moscow as grounds for control, and Soviet celebrations in 1954 made it a 'reunification'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pereiaslav Agreement"},
    "ru-268": {"questions":["Defeats at Konotop and Chudniv cost Muscovy most of its early gains in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Ukraine was split along the Dnieper between rival Cossack hetmans.","Paid in debased copper coin by a government close to exhaustion, Muscovite soldiers deserted in the later years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Truce of Andrusovo brought to an end."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Polish War of 1654–1667</b> was the war in which Muscovy fought the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth for Ukraine and the lands of present-day Belarus, after taking the Ukrainian Cossacks under the tsar's protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A national assembly in Moscow sanctioned it in October 1653, and in May 1654 Tsar Alexis set out for the front himself after weeks of religious ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first campaigns went Moscow's way, for Smolensk, lost since the Time of Troubles, surrendered in the autumn of 1654, and with Cossack help Muscovite armies took Vilnius in 1655.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Then Sweden overran much of Poland, and Moscow, alarmed by Swedish power, made peace with the Poles in 1656 and fought Sweden instead, ending that war in 1661 with all its conquests returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The death of Khmelnytsky in 1657 split the Cossacks, and his successor went over to Poland and destroyed a Muscovite army at Konotop in 1659.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The renewed war went badly for Moscow, which lost another army at Chudniv and with it Lithuania and Belarus, while Ukraine was divided along the Dnieper into two hostile halves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both states were close to exhaustion, and Moscow paid its soldiers in debased copper coin, which drove many to desert and provoked a rebellion in Moscow in 1662.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ukrainians remember the years of civil war that followed Khmelnytsky's death as the Ruin, when rival hetmans backed by Poland, Muscovy and the Ottomans tore the country apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Cossack hetman who had placed western Ukraine under the sultan, and a noble rebellion inside Poland, finally pushed the Commonwealth to negotiate, and the war ended with the Truce of Andrusovo in 1667.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has also been called the Thirteen Years' War, and an English historian writing in 1905 judged that it exceeded even the Thirty Years' War in brutality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 123–25, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 108–19, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","A. M. Ivanov and A. E. Savin, “Andrusovskii mir 1667 g. i ego rol’ v russko-pol’skikh otnosheniyakh” [The Peace of Andrusovo of 1667 and its role in Russian–Polish relations], <i>Vestnik Smolenskoi gosudarstvennoi meditsinskoi akademii</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/andrusovskiy-mir-1667-g-i-ego-rol-v-russko-polskih-otnosheniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Orest Subtelny, “Cossack-Polish War,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 1 (1984), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CC%5CO%5CCossack6PolishWar.htm. [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Ruin,” Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CR%5CU%5CRuin.htm. [Open access]","Alexander I. Prokofiev, “Prazdnovanie 300-letiya Pereyaslavskoi rady v 1954 g.: diskursy istoricheskoi pamyati o nachale Trinadtsatiletnei voiny 1654–1667 gg.” [The celebration of the 300th anniversary of the Pereyaslav Rada in 1954], <i>Peterburgskii istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2022): 34–47, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prazdnovanie-300-letiya-pereyaslavskoy-rady-v-1954-g-diskursy-istoricheskoy-pamyati-o-nachale-trinadtsatiletney-voyny-1654-1667-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Muscovy stop fighting Poland in 1656?","a":"Sweden had overrun much of Poland, and Moscow feared a strong Sweden more than a weakened Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It made peace with the Poles and turned on Sweden, but that war ended in 1661 with all its conquests given back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war turn against Moscow after 1657?","a":"Khmelnytsky's death split the Cossacks, and his successor went over to Poland and destroyed a Muscovite army at Konotop in 1659.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Further desertions led to the surrender at Chudniv, and Lithuania and Belarus were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did both sides finally make peace?","a":"Both were exhausted: Moscow's copper coinage provoked a rebellion in 1662, and Poland faced a noble revolt at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Cossack hetman serving the sultan then threatened them both, and the war ended at Andrusovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Polish–Russian War (1654–1667)"},
    "ru-269": {"questions":["Afanasy Ordin-Nashchokin won a boyar's rank and the direction of the tsar's foreign office for negotiating the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the commissioners of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.","Remembered in Ukraine as its first partition, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided the Cossack lands along the Dnieper between Muscovy on the east bank and Poland on the west."],"abstract":"The <b>Truce of Andrusovo</b> was the agreement signed on 30 January 1667, by the Julian calendar, in the village of Andrusovo near Smolensk, which ended the war of 1654–1667 between Muscovy and the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a truce for thirteen and a half years, not a permanent peace, and it came after negotiations that had lasted almost nine months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Muscovite delegation was led by Afanasy Ordin-Nashchokin, a provincial nobleman whose success at Andrusovo made him a boyar and the head of the tsar's foreign office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Muscovy kept Smolensk, the Seversk lands and Chernihiv, together with all of Ukraine east of the Dnieper, while Poland recovered Vitebsk, Polotsk and Livonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ukraine west of the Dnieper went back to Poland, and the Zaporozhian Cossacks were placed under both states, which promised to restrain them from raiding on the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Kyiv, on the right bank, was left in Muscovite hands for two years only, but Moscow kept it for good, and the Eternal Peace of 1686 confirmed the division, with a payment to Poland for the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Before the treaty Ordin-Nashchokin had hinted that the Cossacks might be given up for the sake of a Polish alliance, and the tsar angrily refused, saying a dog should not eat even a morsel of Orthodox bread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What forced the Poles to concede so much was the rebellion of Prince Lubomirski at home and the raids of the hetman Petro Doroshenko, who had taken western Ukraine over to the Ottoman sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Ukrainian history the truce is remembered as the first partition of Ukraine, dividing the Cossack Hetmanate along the Dnieper between two foreign powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nominally temporary, it proved one of the most lasting settlements in the region's history, for the old struggle between Moscow and Poland was never resumed in open war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["A. M. Ivanov and A. E. Savin, “Andrusovskii mir 1667 g. i ego rol’ v russko-pol’skikh otnosheniyakh” [The Peace of Andrusovo of 1667 and its role in Russian–Polish relations], <i>Vestnik Smolenskoi gosudarstvennoi meditsinskoi akademii</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/andrusovskiy-mir-1667-g-i-ego-rol-v-russko-polskih-otnosheniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","“Andrusovo, Treaty of,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 1 (1984), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CA%5CN%5CAndrusovoTreatyof.htm. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725</i> (London: A. Constable, 1905), 117–19, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 124–25, 348, 353–54, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Hetman state,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 2 (1989), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CH%5CE%5CHetmanstate.htm. [Open access]","“Eternal Peace of 1686,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 1 (1984), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CE%5CT%5CEternalPeaceof1686.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Poland give up so much?","a":"Prince Lubomirski's rebellion divided Poland at home, while Doroshenko's Cossacks, backed by the Turks and Tatars, raided deep into Polish land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Faced with that common enemy, the Commonwealth had to settle with Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Kyiv a special case in the treaty?","a":"It lies on the right bank of the Dnieper, the side returned to Poland, so Moscow was granted it for two years only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tsar Alexis would not willingly give up Orthodox lands, and Moscow kept the city, a fact confirmed by the Eternal Peace of 1686.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the truce called the first partition of Ukraine?","a":"Because it divided the Cossack Hetmanate along the Dnieper, the east under Muscovy and the west under Poland, with the Zaporozhian Cossacks placed under both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Truce of Andrusovo"},
    "ru-270": {"questions":["The Polish crown tried to tame the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Dnieper by enrolling a few hundred of them, and later thousands, in a paid register from 1572.","Self-governing hosts on the Don, the Volga, the Yaik and the Terek, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were slowly drawn into the Russian state and became a military estate of the empire."],"abstract":"The <b>Cossacks</b> were the free, armed communities of the steppe frontier of Muscovy and Poland–Lithuania, formed by men who had left settled society for the open grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their name comes from the Turkic word 'kazak', a free man, and in 16th-century Russia it was used for any homeless labourer with no fixed trade or settled home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the southern border it came to mean men who went into the steppe from the frontier towns, armed, to hunt, fish and fight the Tatar raiders still roaming it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One early mention speaks of Cossacks of Ryazan who served their town against the Tatars in 1444, and by the later 16th century the Cossacks of the Don were an organised host that included converted Tatars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the Dnieper a separate Cossack world grew up under the Polish crown, which tried to control it by enrolling a few hundred chosen men, and later several thousand, in a paid register from 1572.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> These Ukrainian Cossacks led the great risings against the Polish nobility, and from the war of 1648 they built a state of their own, the Hetmanate, which lasted until 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Cossacks of the Don, the Volga, the Yaik and the Terek lived as self-governing hosts, the Don host politically independent of Muscovy in its early years, until the Russian state slowly absorbed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Don Cossacks resisted that loss of freedom in rebellions from the Bulavin rising of the early 18th century to the Pugachev revolt, and ended as a military estate of the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether they were a people or a service estate created by governments is still argued, and some historians stress that the Don Cossacks long refused to call themselves Russians, while others see them as part of a common Russian whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Ukrainian Cossacks' part in building a state sets them apart from the Russian hosts, whose old reputation as loyal guardians of the frontier one historian has called largely a political myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 3 (London: J. M. Dent, 1913), 106–9, https://archive.org/details/AHistoryOfRussiaVolIII. [Open access]","Illia Vytanovych, “Cossacks,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 1 (1984), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CC%5CO%5CCossacks.htm. [Open access]","Lubomyr Wynar, “Registered Cossacks,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 4 (1993), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CR%5CE%5CRegisteredCossacks.htm. [Open access]","Viktor A. Brekhunenko, “Rannie kazach’i soobshchestva v ukrainskom i russkom etnicheskikh prostranstvakh” [Early Cossack communities in the Ukrainian and Russian ethnic spaces], <i>Srednevekovye tyurko-tatarskie gosudarstva</i>, no. 10 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rannie-kazachi-soobschestva-v-ukrainskom-i-russkom-etnicheskih-prostranstvah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikolai A. Mininkov, “‘Staroe pole’, ‘regulyarstvo’ i ‘blazhennyya pamyati’ zashchitnik kazachestva: spravedlivost’ i starina kak tsennosti v dvizheniyakh donskikh kazakov XVII–XVIII vekov” [‘Old field’, ‘regularity’ and a defender of the Cossacks ‘of blessed memory’: justice and antiquity in the movements of the Don Cossacks], <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta, Seriya 4</i> 29, no. 5 (2024): 85–96, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/staroe-pole-regulyarstvo-i-blazhennyya-pamyati-zaschitnik-kazachestva-spravedlivost-i-starina-kak-tsennosti-v-dvizheniyah-donskih. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Hetman state,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 2 (1989), Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CH%5CE%5CHetmanstate.htm. [Open access]","Aleksandr A. Kamentsev, “Samosoznanie kazakov XVI–XVII vv. kak faktor identichnosti: po materialam kazach’ego eposa i fol’klora” [Cossack self-awareness in the 16th–17th centuries as a factor of identity], <i>Paleorosiya</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/samosoznanie-kazakov-xvi-xvii-vv-kak-faktor-identichnosti-po-materialam-kazachiego-eposa-i-folklora. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Sopov, “Proiskhozhdenie kazachestva: vozvrashchayas’ k probleme” [The origin of the Cossacks: returning to the problem], <i>Vestnik Moskovskogo universiteta, Seriya 8: Istoriya</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/proishozhdenie-kazachestva-vozvraschayas-k-probleme. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Cossacks appear on the steppe frontier?","a":"As Tatar power weakened, men from the frontier towns went into the steppe to hunt and fish and to fight the remaining raiders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peasants fleeing harsher serfdom and religious pressure in the Commonwealth swelled their numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Polish crown create a Cossack register?","a":"The Cossacks defended the frontier against the Tatars, but their raids provoked the Turks and their numbers threatened the nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A paid register of chosen men was meant to keep the useful part and control the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it disputed whether the Cossacks were a people?","a":"Because they began as a way of life rather than a nation, yet some hosts developed a strong identity of their own. Don Cossacks long refused to call themselves Russians, though other historians see them as part of a common Russian whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cossacks"},
    "ru-281": {"questions":["Dining with Patrick Gordon in May 1690, the young Peter began visits to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so frequent that for a time he seemed almost to live among its foreigners.","Wooden houses set in gardens made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look like a prosperous village, yet its people read foreign journals and argued over the English Revolution."],"abstract":"The <b>German Quarter</b>, in Russian the Nemetskaya sloboda, was the settlement outside Moscow where the foreign officers, doctors, craftsmen and merchants in the tsars' service were made to live in the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was one of several foreign settlements outside Moscow's boundary, and despite its name its leading residents by the reign of Alexis were English and Scots, with Dutchmen foremost among its merchants and doctors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An older foreign settlement on the Yauza, peopled largely by prisoners taken in the Livonian War, had been plundered and burnt during the Time of Troubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clergy complained that foreigners were buying houses beside Orthodox churches, and a decree of October 1652 under Tsar Alexis moved them out to new ground beyond the Pokrovsky Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 150 households were taken down and rebuilt there at the treasury's cost, and plots were set out for officers, physicians, apothecaries, craftsmen and the widows of men who had died in Russian service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The quarter was meant to keep Orthodox Russians apart from western Christians, yet its people wore foreign clothes, read foreign books and journals and argued about the wars in the Low Countries and the English Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Scottish officer who arrived in 1661 called it the Slobod of the Strangers in his diary, and noted two weddings there that he attended in his first months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It lay near the village of Preobrazhenskoe, where the young Peter drilled his play regiments, and he borrowed officers and engineers from it to train and equip them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1690 his visits became so frequent that he seemed almost to live there, dining with the Scot Patrick Gordon and the Genevan Franz Lefort, who became his closest friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There he found his mistress Anna Mons, the daughter of a German goldsmith, and in 1697 he set out to see for himself the western Europe of which his friends there had so often told him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 196–202, 206–211, 334, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 9–10, 16–18, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","В. С. Дельвиг, «Отношение светской и духовной власти Московской Руси к инославным из Западной Европы во времена становления самодержавия», <i>Общество. Среда. Развитие (Terra Humana)</i>, no. 3 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnoshenie-svetskoy-i-duhovnoy-vlasti-moskovskoy-rusi-k-inoslavnym-iz-zapadnoy-evropy-vo-vremena-stanovleniya-samoderzhaviya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Patrick Gordon, <i>Passages from the Diary of General Patrick Gordon of Auchleuchries, A.D. 1635–A.D. 1699</i> (Aberdeen: Spalding Club, 1859), 48–51, https://archive.org/details/passagesfromdiar00gordrich. [Open access]","Б. Б. Вагнер and Т. С. Воронова, «География средневековых слобод Москвы и их след в московской топонимии», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Естественные науки</i>, no. 2 (34) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/geografiya-srednevekovyh-slobod-moskvy-i-ih-sled-v-moskovskoy-toponimii. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd, vol. 1 (London: William Heinemann, 1897), 16–17, https://archive.org/details/petergreat01waliuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were foreigners moved out of Moscow in 1652?","a":"Because the Church and the government wanted Orthodox Russians kept apart from western Christians. Priests had complained that foreigners were buying houses near their churches, and the decree of 1652 resettled them beyond the Pokrovsky Gate at the treasury's cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the quarter matter so much to Peter?","a":"Because it was where he met the foreign soldiers and craftsmen who trained his play regiments and became his friends. Gordon and Lefort, both living there, were for years his most trusted companions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did its people keep their own ways?","a":"Because living in a separate suburb made it easy to keep their churches, dress, languages and books, and to stay in touch with their home countries by letters and new arrivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"German Quarter"},
    "ru-282": {"questions":["Peter served as a bombardier in the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a fourteen-week siege of 1695 that failed for want of engineers, discipline and supplies.","The fortresses won in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Sea of Azov were all given up to the Ottomans after Peter's army was surrounded on the Pruth in 1711."],"abstract":"The <b>Azov campaigns</b> were Peter I's two expeditions of 1695 and 1696 against Azov, the Ottoman fortress near the mouth of the Don that barred Russia's way to the Sea of Azov and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They belonged to the war against the Ottomans and the Crimean Tatars that Russia had joined under Sophia, and while camped before Azov in July 1696 Peter agreed to a formal alliance with Austria and Venice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1695 an army of about 30,000, commanded by a council of three generals with the tsar serving as a bombardier, besieged the town for fourteen weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two assaults failed, the first costing about 1,500 men by the diary of the Scottish general Patrick Gordon, and in October the army withdrew through rain, floods and cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second campaign took the fortress in July 1696, and Russia kept it only until 1711, when Peter gave up his strongholds on the Sea of Azov after being surrounded on the Pruth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What changed between the two summers was a fleet, since in 1695 the Turks had brought help into the town by water, and in 1696 Peter's galleys ambushed and sank their relief boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Through the winter of 1696 upwards of 30,000 men built more than 1,300 barges at Voronezh, on a tributary of the Don, along with galleys modelled on one brought from Holland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians count this flotilla as the start of the Azov fleet, the first regular formation of a Russian navy, and Voronezh as the first centre of its shipbuilding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With the river closed by galleys, the besiegers piled up a great rampart of earth that was pushed forward towards the walls, a method Gordon's son-in-law credited to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a Cossack storm of the outworks the garrison surrendered and marched out with its arms, and Peter chose a harbour for his new ships at Taganrog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 240–260, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 17–18, 54, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Patrick Gordon, <i>Passages from the Diary of General Patrick Gordon of Auchleuchries, A.D. 1635–A.D. 1699</i> (Aberdeen: Spalding Club, 1859), 184–186, https://archive.org/details/passagesfromdiar00gordrich. [Open access]","Т. В. Алексеев, «Строительство Азовского флота в 1695–1712 гг. в отечественной историографии», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroitelstvo-azovskogo-flota-v-1695-1712-gg-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Шведский король Карл XII и формирование России как великой державы», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 10 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedskiy-korol-karl-xii-i-formirovanie-rossii-kak-velikoy-derzhavy. [in Russian] [Open access]","John Perry, <i>The State of Russia under the Present Czar</i> (London: Benjamin Tooke, 1716), 146–149, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-state-of-russia-und_perry-john_1716. [Open access]","Д. Ю. Гузевич, «Дипломатическая цель Великого посольства 1697–1698/99», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 13, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diplomaticheskaya-tsel-velikogo-posolstva-1697-1698-99. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first siege of Azov fail?","a":"Because the army had no supreme commander, few skilled engineers, poor discipline and broken supplies, and because the garrison could still be supplied from the sea. Gordon's warnings against hasty assaults were overruled, and two storms failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Voronezh chosen to build the flotilla?","a":"Because barges had been built there every winter for decades on a river that runs into the Don, so timber, skills and a route to Azov were all at hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia lose Azov again?","a":"Because in 1711 Peter's army was surrounded by a far larger Ottoman force on the Pruth, and the price of escape was the surrender of his strongholds on the Sea of Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Azov campaigns (1695–1696)"},
    "ru-283": {"questions":["Lefort, Golovin and Voznitsyn were named grand ambassadors of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by a decree of December 1696, at the head of a suite of about 250 people.","Over 900 Dutch seamen were hired for Russian service during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was cut short in Vienna by news of a revolt of the streltsy."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Embassy</b> was the Russian mission that travelled through western Europe in 1697 and 1698, carrying in its suite Peter I himself under the name Peter Mikhailov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No Russian ruler had been outside his own lands since the 11th century, and to keep the tsar free of ceremony it was forbidden on pain of death to reveal his presence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy was led by three grand ambassadors, Franz Lefort, Fyodor Golovin and Prokopy Voznitsyn, named by a decree of 6 December 1696, and its whole suite numbered about 250 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its declared purpose was to strengthen the Christian alliance against the Ottoman sultan and the Crimean khan, and it cost nearly 236,000 roubles, about a tenth of the state's yearly budget.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some historians think its real diplomatic aims were wider, and during the journey Russian policy turned from war with the Turks in the south towards a coalition against Sweden in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Peter himself went to learn shipbuilding, and the volunteers in the suite were told to master navigation and bring home skilled naval craftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He worked for a week in a private shipyard at Zaandam, though the cottage shown there as his may not have been his lodging, and then for more than four months at the Dutch East India Company's wharf in Amsterdam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Amsterdam, cut by canals and laid out in regular new districts, left a lasting mark, and historians of architecture trace his later plans for Saint Petersburg partly to what he saw there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In England he worked at the royal dockyard at Deptford, visited the Royal Society, the Mint and the Tower, paid a private visit to Parliament, and in all the embassy hired more than 900 Dutch seamen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> News of a revolt of the streltsy reached him in Vienna in the summer of 1698, and he gave up a visit to Venice and hurried home, receiving the boyars at Preobrazhenskoe at the end of August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 274–278, 286–292, 311–320, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 18–23, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Д. Ю. Гузевич, «Дипломатическая цель Великого посольства 1697–1698/99», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 13, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diplomaticheskaya-tsel-velikogo-posolstva-1697-1698-99. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. С. Десятсков, «О мерах секретности и предосторожности при подготовке и проведении Великого посольства 1697–1698 гг.», <i>Ученые записки Новгородского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (4) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-merah-sekretnosti-i-predostorozhnosti-pri-podgotovke-i-provedenii-velikogo-posolstva-1697-1698-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Н. Гольцева, «„Великое посольство“ 1697–1698 гг. как опыт личного восприятия Петром I западноевропейской архитектуры», <i>Architecture and Modern Information Technologies</i>, no. 3 (60) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikoe-posolstvo-1697-1698-gg-kak-opyt-lichnogo-vospriyatiya-petrom-i-zapadnoevropeyskoy-arhitektury. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd, vol. 1 (London: William Heinemann, 1897), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/petergreat01waliuoft. [Open access]","Д. С. Артамонов, «Введение брадобрития и ношения „немецкого“ платья Петром I в исторической памяти», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 21, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vvedenie-bradobritiya-i-nosheniya-nemetskogo-platya-petrom-i-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tsar travel incognito?","a":"Because as a private person he could work in shipyards and move freely without the ceremony due to a sovereign, while local authorities could help him in ways an official visit would not allow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter work as a shipwright?","a":"Because he wanted a navy and believed the art of shipbuilding could only be learned properly where it was most advanced, in the Netherlands and England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the embassy end suddenly?","a":"Because in Vienna Peter learned that four regiments of streltsy had revolted and marched on Moscow, so he abandoned the planned journey to Venice and returned home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Grand Embassy of Peter the Great"},
    "ru-284": {"questions":["Thirty gibbets raised in a square near Sophia's convent held 230 men hanged after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as an Austrian diplomat living in Moscow recorded in his diary.","News of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Peter in Vienna and made him abandon his planned visit to Venice, although the rising had been crushed before he got home."],"abstract":"The <b>Streltsy uprising of 1698</b> was the revolt of four regiments of Moscow musketeers who marched on the capital that summer while Peter I was abroad with the Grand Embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regiments had fought at Azov, lost many men in its assaults and laboured on its walls, and were then sent to the Polish frontier without leave to pass through Moscow and see their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deserters who reached the city in the spring complained of hunger and lack of pay, and returned to the camp with rumours that the tsar was dead and that boyars and foreigners now ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A petition drafted by Zorin, a leader of ten among them, cast the streltsy as faithful guardians of Orthodoxy and blamed the foreigner Lefort for their losses before Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having driven out their loyal officers and chosen new ones, they were met on 18 June near the New Jerusalem monastery north-west of Moscow by troops under Shein and Patrick Gordon, who scattered them with cannon fire in about an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Gordon's diary counts 22 rebels killed on the spot and about 40 wounded, and Shein's inquiry executed some 130 men and sent about 1,860 to prisons and monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The news reached Peter in Vienna and made him give up a planned visit to Venice, and once home he had the prisoners brought to Preobrazhenskoe and questioned under torture in fourteen chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wanted to know whether his half-sister Sophia had called the streltsy to Moscow, and although nothing reliable tied her to them she was forced to take the veil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mass executions began on 10 October, and near the convent where Sophia was held thirty gibbets were raised in a square, on which 230 men were hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An Austrian diplomat recorded three ringleaders hanged beside her window, one holding a paper folded like a petition, and some accounts say Peter beheaded rebels with his own hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 321–329, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","Patrick Gordon, <i>Passages from the Diary of General Patrick Gordon of Auchleuchries, A.D. 1635–A.D. 1699</i> (Aberdeen: Spalding Club, 1859), 187–191, https://archive.org/details/passagesfromdiar00gordrich. [Open access]","Johann Georg Korb, <i>Diary of an Austrian Secretary of Legation at the Court of Czar Peter the Great</i>, trans. Count MacDonnell, vol. 1 (London: Bradbury and Evans, 1863), 193–194, https://archive.org/details/diaryofaustrians01korbuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 23, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","В. В. Заплетин, «Об идеологической функции „Открытого письма“ десятника В. Зорина в событиях стрелецкого восстания 1698 г.», <i>Вестник Самарского университета. История, педагогика, филология</i> 23, no. 1.1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ob-ideologicheskoy-funktsii-otkrytogo-pisma-desyatnika-v-zorina-v-sobytiyah-streletskogo-vosstaniya-1698-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Johann Georg Korb, <i>Diary of an Austrian Secretary of Legation at the Court of Czar Peter the Great</i>, trans. Count MacDonnell, vol. 2 (London: Bradbury and Evans, 1863), 70–73, https://archive.org/details/diaryofaustrians02korbuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the four regiments march on Moscow?","a":"Because after hard service at Azov they were sent to the Polish frontier without being allowed home, and deserters brought back rumours that the tsar was dead and that boyars and foreigners were in control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter reopen the inquiry after the revolt was crushed?","a":"Because he suspected that his half-sister Sophia and discontented nobles stood behind it, and Shein's quick trials had not answered either question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were men hanged outside Sophia's window?","a":"Because Peter chose to believe she was guilty, and the bodies, one holding a mock petition, were a public warning to her and to anyone who looked to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Streltsy uprising"},
    "ru-285": {"questions":["A decree of 16 January 1705 introduced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a yearly levy graded by rank that let Russian men keep a custom Peter I regarded as backward.","In 1722 Peter replaced the graded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a single rate of 50 roubles and ordered every bearded man to go about in old Russian dress."],"abstract":"The <b>beard tax</b> was the yearly payment that Peter I's government demanded from Russian men who wished to keep their beards instead of shaving in the western manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To many Orthodox Russians the beard was sacred, since icons showed God the Father and Christ bearded, and Patriarch Adrian had condemned shaving as an offence against the beards of the prophets and apostles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter treated the beard as a mark of backwardness, and his decrees ordered all men except the clergy to shave or else pay for the right to keep it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An English captain in Peter's service wrote that gentlemen and merchants paid 100 roubles a year and common people a kopek at each town gate, and payers wore a bronze token about the neck as proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree that set up the levy is dated 16 January 1705, and it graded the rate by rank, from 30 roubles a year for men of low rank to 100 for the richest merchants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The campaign began in jest the day after Peter came home from western Europe in 1698, when he cut the beards of the boyars who came to greet him, sparing only the patriarch, one aged prince and the man who had been the tsaritsa's guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Days later, at a New Year feast, his jester played barber, and anyone who shrank from the razor had his ears boxed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many men paid rather than shave, and the English captain met an old carpenter at Voronezh who kept his cut beard to be buried with it, so that he could show it to St Nicholas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Resistance during the war made the government suspend the policy in 1706, but it was renewed in 1713 and 1714 and again by several decrees in the 1720s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1722 a single rate of 50 roubles replaced the graded scale and bearded men had to wear old Russian dress, and copper tokens made in 1724 were never put to use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 337–340, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","Johann Georg Korb, <i>Diary of an Austrian Secretary of Legation at the Court of Czar Peter the Great</i>, trans. Count MacDonnell, vol. 1 (London: Bradbury and Evans, 1863), 155–156, https://archive.org/details/diaryofaustrians01korbuoft. [Open access]","John Perry, <i>The State of Russia under the Present Czar</i> (London: Benjamin Tooke, 1716), 195–197, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-state-of-russia-und_perry-john_1716. [Open access]","Д. С. Артамонов, «Введение брадобрития и ношения „немецкого“ платья Петром I в исторической памяти», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 21, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vvedenie-bradobritiya-i-nosheniya-nemetskogo-platya-petrom-i-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Акельев, «Судьба проекта вестернизации внешнего облика горожан в послепетровской России (1725–1741)», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 3 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sudba-proekta-vesternizatsii-vneshnego-oblika-gorozhan-v-poslepetrovskoy-rossii-1725-1741. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 132–133, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd, vol. 2 (London: William Heinemann, 1897), 129–131, https://archive.org/details/petergreat02waliuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russians resist shaving?","a":"Because the beard had religious meaning: the clergy taught that the prophets, the apostles and Christ wore beards, and a patriarch had condemned shaving as the habit of heretics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter tax beards instead of simply banning them?","a":"Because the decree of 1705 let men buy the right to keep a beard at a high price, which pushed most to shave while bringing money to the treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the policy keep being renewed?","a":"Because it was widely evaded and resisted, so it was suspended during the war in 1706 and had to be repeated in 1713 and 1714 and again in the 1720s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Beard tax"},
    "ru-286": {"questions":["Peter called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his threefold school, since a pupil who usually mastered the art of war in seven years had needed three times as long.","Alliances signed at Preobrazhenskoe in 1699 with Saxony and Denmark drew Russia into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which it entered only after making peace with the Ottomans."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Northern War</b> of 1700 to 1721 was the long struggle in which Russia, Denmark and Saxony-Poland fought the Swedish empire, and from which Russia emerged as the strongest power on the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia signed alliances with Saxony and Denmark at Preobrazhenskoe in November 1699 but declared war on Sweden only in August 1700, after making peace with the Ottomans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By then Denmark had already left the war, forced to make peace at Travendal after the young Charles XII landed troops near Copenhagen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Russian siege of Narva ended in November 1700 with a heavy defeat by a much smaller Swedish army, one of the worst setbacks Peter suffered in the whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Charles then spent years pursuing Augustus of Saxony through Poland, and Peter used the respite to take Noteborg in 1702 and Nyenskans at the mouth of the Neva in 1703, winning back the province of Ingria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> When Charles finally marched on Russia in 1708, his relief column was destroyed at Lesnaya and his army was annihilated at Poltava on 27 June 1709.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war did not end there, for Peter refused any peace that cost him the mouth of the Neva, rebuilt the old coalition, lost his Azov fortresses on the Pruth in 1711, and was drawn into German quarrels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His new Baltic fleet beat a Swedish squadron off Gangut in 1714, and after Charles was killed in 1718 Russian forces twice invaded and ravaged the Swedish coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Treaty of Nystad, signed on 30 August 1721 by the Russian calendar, made Sweden recognise Russia's possession of Ingria, Livonia, Estonia and part of Karelia, with Riga, Reval, Dorpat, Narva and Vyborg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter later called the war his threefold school, because a pupil who normally learned the art of war in seven years had needed three times as long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 49–56, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Н. Ф. Ковалевский and Ю. В. Снегова, «Хронология Северной войны 1700–1721 гг.: к 300-летию Ништадтского мира 1721 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg-k-300-letiyu-nishtadtskogo-mira-1721-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Шведский король Карл XII и формирование России как великой державы», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 10 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedskiy-korol-karl-xii-i-formirovanie-rossii-kak-velikoy-derzhavy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Чиркин, «Военные действия под Нарвой в 1700 г. по воспоминаниям шведских воинов», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i> 21, no. 3 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennye-deystviya-pod-narvoy-v-1700-g-po-vospominaniyam-shvedskih-voinov. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Charles XII and the Collapse of the Swedish Empire, 1682–1719</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1895), 72–77, https://archive.org/details/charlesxiicollap00bainuoft. [Open access]","О. В. Лебедева, «Ништадтский мир и его роль в становлении российской государственности в период с 1721 по 1917 гг.», <i>Вестник МГИМО Университета</i> 14, no. 6 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nishtadtskiy-mir-i-ego-rol-v-stanovlenii-rossiyskoy-gosudarstvennosti-v-period-s-1721-po-1917gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1890), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 422–428, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia go to war with Sweden?","a":"Because Peter wanted an outlet on the Baltic and joined a coalition with Saxony and Denmark against Sweden, which held the coast; he struck only once peace with the Ottomans freed his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war last twelve years after Poltava?","a":"Because Russia lacked a fleet strong enough to strike at Sweden itself, and Peter's energies were split by the Pruth campaign and by quarrels among his German allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Narva so damaging at the start?","a":"Because a raw and badly supplied Russian army was routed by a much smaller Swedish force, losing its artillery and its generals in the first great battle of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great Northern War"},
    "ru-287": {"questions":["Shot through the head in the trenches before Fredriksten in Norway in 1718, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left Sweden free to make peace with all its enemies except Russia.","For years after his defeat at Poltava, the Swedish king <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived as a guest of the Ottomans at Bender, once holding his house against Turkish and Tatar troops."],"abstract":"<b>Charles XII</b> was king of Sweden from 1697 until his death in 1718, the soldier-king whose wars with Russia, Denmark and Saxony-Poland ended Sweden's age as the strongest power on the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Born in June 1682, he was declared of age in November 1697 when he was only fifteen, and absolute power passed to him almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He refused the usual coronation oath, allowed only a consecration and rode to church with the crown already on his head, and he made it a point of honour never to go back on a decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1700, at eighteen, he landed troops near Copenhagen and forced Denmark out of the war within weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then crossed to Estonia and, in a snowstorm that blew into the Russians' faces, broke Peter's army besieging Narva with a far smaller force, the greatest success of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> He turned down chances of peace after Narva and marched into Poland instead, eager to depose kings and give away crowns like a new Alexander the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His invasion of Russia in 1708 turned south into the Ukraine, and at Poltava in June 1709 his hungry, worn-out army of some 30,000 was destroyed, though he himself escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He fled across the Dnieper towards Ottoman land and spent the next years at Bender, where he once held his house against Turkish and Tatar troops until it was set on fire over his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After his return he invaded Norway, and on the night of 30 November 1718 he was shot through the head in the trenches before the fortress of Fredriksten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His death let Sweden make peace with its other enemies, leaving Peter to face it alone until the Treaty of Nystad in 1721.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Charles XII and the Collapse of the Swedish Empire, 1682–1719</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1895), 10, 37–38, 75–77, 297–299, https://archive.org/details/charlesxiicollap00bainuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 380–382, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Шведский король Карл XII и формирование России как великой державы», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 10 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedskiy-korol-karl-xii-i-formirovanie-rossii-kak-velikoy-derzhavy. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 51, 54, 56, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Н. Ф. Ковалевский and Ю. В. Снегова, «Хронология Северной войны 1700–1721 гг.: к 300-летию Ништадтского мира 1721 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg-k-300-letiyu-nishtadtskogo-mira-1721-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Чиркин, «Карл XII под Нарвой осенью 1700 года: свидетельства приближённых», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 21, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/karl-xii-karl-xii-pod-narvoy-osenyu-1700-goda-svidetelstva-priblizhyonnyh. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Ф. Куликова, «Отступление шведской армии после Полтавского сражения глазами иностранцев — участников событий», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i>, no. 4-2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otstuplenie-shvedskoy-armii-posle-poltavskogo-srazheniya-glazami-inostrantsev-uchastnikov-sobytiy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Charles refuse peace after Narva?","a":"Because he underrated his enemies, preferred war to bargaining and wanted to humble Augustus and Peter on his own terms, so he let several favourable offers pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his army fail at Poltava?","a":"Because after the long march south and a hard winter it was hungry and worn out, its relief column had been destroyed, and Peter could bring a much larger army against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his death matter to Russia?","a":"Because Sweden then made peace with Peter's allies but not with him, so Russia had to finish the war alone, which it did at Nystad in 1721.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Charles XII of Sweden","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Copy_Charles_XII_-_Nationalmuseum_-_17886.png/960px-Copy_Charles_XII_-_Nationalmuseum_-_17886.png","title":"Charles XII","desc":"Charles XII of Sweden, from the workshop of Hyacinthe Rigaud, about 1715; Nationalmuseum, Stockholm.","credit":"Workshop of Hyacinthe Rigaud, c. 1715, Nationalmuseum Stockholm; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_Charles_XII_-_Nationalmuseum_-_17886.png","alt":"A young clean-shaven man in armour with a blue cloak, his hand on a helmet, a battle in the distance."}},
    "ru-288": {"questions":["To replace the guns lost in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Peter had a quarter of the bells of Russia's churches and monasteries taken down and melted into cannon.","Fearing their own panicking soldiers more than the enemy, the Duke de Croy and other foreign officers surrendered to the Swedes during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Narova."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Narva</b> was the defeat of Peter I's army besieging the Swedish fortress of Narva in Estonia by Charles XII, the first great battle of the Great Northern War and the heaviest defeat Peter suffered in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was fought on 30 November 1700 by the Western calendar, a date that was 19 November in the Julian calendar that Russia then used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Russians had brought about 35,000 men to the town, but bad powder, untrained gunners and roads ruined by autumn rain left their bombardment almost useless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Charles landed in Estonia with a small army, and the Russian cavalry sent forward to hold the passes fell back before him without a serious fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peter left the camp for Novgorod the day before the battle, handing command to the Duke de Croy, a foreign general whom his troops did not trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Swedes attacked in the afternoon behind a snowstorm blowing into the Russians' faces, and within a quarter of an hour they held the outer entrenchments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fleeing troops crowded onto the bridge over the Narova, which broke beneath them, while Sheremetev's horsemen tried to swim the river and many drowned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only the Preobrazhensky and Semenovsky guards and the division of Weide held out, and de Croy and other foreign officers surrendered to the Swedes for fear of their own men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One modern count puts the Swedes at about 10,500 men with 37 guns against some 33,000 Russians, and the victors took about 150 cannon and nine Russian generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter replaced the lost artillery by melting down a quarter of the bells of churches and monasteries, and he rebuilt his army with new levies, so that within a few months it was larger than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 390–399, 407–408, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Charles XII and the Collapse of the Swedish Empire, 1682–1719</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1895), 72–77, https://archive.org/details/charlesxiicollap00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 50–52, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","С. А. Чиркин, «Военные действия под Нарвой в 1700 г. по воспоминаниям шведских воинов», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i> 21, no. 3 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennye-deystviya-pod-narvoy-v-1700-g-po-vospominaniyam-shvedskih-voinov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Ф. Ковалевский and Ю. В. Снегова, «Хронология Северной войны 1700–1721 гг.: к 300-летию Ништадтского мира 1721 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg-k-300-letiyu-nishtadtskogo-mira-1721-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Шведский король Карл XII и формирование России как великой державы», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 10 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shvedskiy-korol-karl-xii-i-formirovanie-rossii-kak-velikoy-derzhavy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Чиркин, «Карл XII под Нарвой осенью 1700 года: свидетельства приближённых», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 21, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/karl-xii-karl-xii-pod-narvoy-osenyu-1700-goda-svidetelstva-priblizhyonnyh. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did such a small Swedish army win?","a":"Because it attacked by surprise behind a snowstorm against thin Russian lines stretched round the town, held by raw troops who distrusted their foreign officers and broke in panic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter leave before the battle?","a":"Because he went to Novgorod to hurry up ammunition and reinforcements and to meet King Augustus, leaving the Duke de Croy in command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why melt down church bells?","a":"Because nearly all the Russian artillery had been captured, and bronze from bells was the quickest source of metal for new cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Narva (1700)"},
    "ru-289": {"questions":["The ceremony of 16 May 1703 that laid out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Neva delta is counted as the founding day of Saint Petersburg itself.","Built to guard the mouth of the Neva, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> protected nothing in war and served instead as a political prison and the burial place of the emperors."],"abstract":"The <b>Peter and Paul Fortress</b> is the six-bastioned fortress on Hare Island in the Neva delta whose foundation in May 1703 is counted as the founding of Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian troops took the Swedish fort of Nyenskans near the river's mouth on 1 May 1703, and a council of war chose to build a new stronghold nearer the sea instead of keeping it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was the first of the marshy islands of the delta, known from its Finnish name as Hare Island and liable to flood when the west wind blew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter oversaw one bastion himself and gave the other five to Menshikov, Golovin, Zotov, Trubetskoy and Naryshkin, and the bastions still carry such names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A church of Saints Peter and Paul was built inside the walls in the first year, and the stone cathedral with its tall spire that replaced it was begun in 1714 by the Italian architect Domenico Trezzini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A manuscript account of the city's beginnings says that at the ceremony of 16 May 1703 Peter himself marked where the gate should stand, bending birch tops into an arch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first walls were of earth and timber, raised by soldiers and carpenters from the Novgorod country who, lacking wheelbarrows, carried soil in mats and in their shirts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter asked for at least 2,000 convicts bound for Siberia to do the heaviest work, and though he called the new town his paradise, for its labourers it meant disease, floods and death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rebuilding in stone began in 1706 with a flank of the Menshikov bastion designed by Trezzini, and some bastions were finished only in the 1720s, enclosing remains of the first timber works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its guns never had an enemy to fight, and it served instead as a political prison and, in its cathedral, as the burial place of the emperors, remaining a military site until 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1890), 1–3, 10, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","И. Н. Баринова, «„Райские“ ворота Петропавловской крепости Санкт-Петербурга», <i>Новое искусствознание</i>, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rayskie-vorota-petropavlovskoy-kreposti-sankt-peterburga. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. О. Попов and Д. К. Бирюлева, «Зотов бастион Петропавловской крепости г. Санкт-Петербург: архитектурно-строительная часть летописи», <i>Известия Казанского государственного архитектурно-строительного университета</i>, no. 4 (46) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zotov-bastion-petropavlovskoy-kreposti-g-sankt-peterburg-arhitekturno-stroitelnaya-chast-letopisi. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 125–126, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Н. Ф. Ковалевский and Ю. В. Снегова, «Хронология Северной войны 1700–1721 гг.: к 300-летию Ништадтского мира 1721 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg-k-300-letiyu-nishtadtskogo-mira-1721-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1884), 427–428, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero01schuuoft. [Open access]","А. О. Попов, И. Ю. Матвеев and Д. К. Бирюлева, «Меншиков бастион Петропавловской крепости г. Санкт-Петербург: фрагменты изначального строения», <i>Известия Казанского государственного архитектурно-строительного университета</i>, no. 4 (42) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/menshikov-bastion-petropavlovskoy-kreposti-g-sankt-peterburg-fragmenty-iznachalnogo-stroeniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new fortress built instead of keeping Nyenskans?","a":"Because the council of war wanted a stronghold nearer the sea that could command the mouth of the Neva and protect a future port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was building it so costly in lives?","a":"Because the ground was marsh liable to floods, tools were primitive and the labour was forced, so disease and death were common among the workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it become a prison rather than a defence?","a":"Because once Kronstadt guarded the sea approach the fortress protected nothing, and its casemates proved convenient for holding political prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peter and Paul Fortress"},
    "ru-290": {"questions":["The numbered 'lines' that name the streets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Saint Petersburg are the sides of canals that Peter laid out there in the Dutch fashion.","A decree of 1719 made the nobles who owned the most peasant households build two-storey stone houses on the Neva bank of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the island Peter had chosen as his city's centre."],"abstract":"<b>Vasilyevsky Island</b> is the island at the mouth of the Neva that Peter I chose, after several changes of plan, as the centre of his new capital of Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At first the nobility and merchants had been made to build beside the fortress, and the plans then moved to Kronstadt and to higher ground upriver before Peter settled on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There a regular town was laid out with canals along every street in the Dutch fashion, of the kind Peter had seen in Amsterdam on his journey to western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The canals were later filled in, but their two sides survive in the island's streets, which are still called numbered 'lines'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under later rulers the fashionable quarter and the government offices moved to the mainland, but the island remained the centre of the city's commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 January 1716 plots began to be handed out along the river bank, the canals and the streets according to a plan of the island by Domenico Trezzini that Peter had approved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A plan of 1717 by the French architect Jean-Baptiste Le Blond gave the island a grand square centre with a palace, but it was built up in lines closer to Trezzini's simpler scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of February 1719 fixed what each noble had to build there by the number of peasant households he owned, and plot frontages of up to about 21 metres (70 feet) were wider than those of late 17th-century London or Amsterdam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trezzini built model houses on the island in 1718 and 1719 for owners to copy, and Menshikov's palace on its bank is one of the few buildings of Peter's day still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the nobles the island meant costly houses on low ground cut off by floating ice each spring and autumn, and for the labourers driven there from every province the building of the capital was ruinous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1890), 6–9, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","Е. А. Андреева, «Резиденция графа А. И. Остермана на Васильевском острове: от „образцового“ дома Д. А. Трезини к Морскому кадетскому корпусу», <i>Труды Санкт-Петербургского института истории РАН</i>, no. 5 (21) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rezidentsiya-grafa-a-i-ostermana-na-vasilievskom-ostrove-ot-obraztsovogo-doma-d-a-trezini-k-morskomu-kadetskomu-korpusu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Мазаев, «Загадка Леблона», <i>Academia. Архитектура и строительство</i>, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zagadka-leblona. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 125–126, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Т. Н. Гольцева, «„Великое посольство“ 1697–1698 гг. как опыт личного восприятия Петром I западноевропейской архитектуры», <i>Architecture and Modern Information Technologies</i>, no. 3 (60) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikoe-posolstvo-1697-1698-gg-kak-opyt-lichnogo-vospriyatiya-petrom-i-zapadnoevropeyskoy-arhitektury. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Н. Гольцева, «Регламентирование параметров жилой квартальной застройки Амстердама, Лондона, Москвы и Санкт-Петербурга второй половины XVII – первой четверти XVIII веков», <i>Architecture and Modern Information Technologies</i>, no. 2 (67) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reglamentirovanie-parametrov-zhiloy-kvartalnoy-zastroyki-amsterdama-londona-moskvy-i-sankt-peterburga-vtoroy-poloviny-xvii-pervoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the streets of the island called lines?","a":"Because they were laid out along canals, each side of a canal forming a line, and when the canals were filled in the names stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did nobles dislike building there?","a":"Because they were compelled to put up large, expensive houses sized to their estates on low, flood-prone ground that floating ice cut off from the mainland for weeks each year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Le Blond's plan not followed?","a":"Because the island was in the end built up street by street in lines much closer to Trezzini's simpler grid than to Le Blond's grand composition of squares round a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vasilyevsky Island"},
    "ru-291": {"questions":["Landowners and monasteries grouped into companies had to build and arm ships at Voronezh for the young <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which won its first great battle off Hango in 1714.","By the end of Peter's reign the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted 48 ships of the line and 800 galleys and smaller vessels, manned by some 28,000 men."],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Russian Navy</b> was the sea force that Peter I created at the end of the 17th century, first on the river Don for the war against the Ottomans and then on the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter himself traced it to an old English boat he found as a youth at Izmailovo, although his father's government had more than once tried to organise a Russian fleet before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the winter of 1696 some 26,000 labourers at Voronezh and other places in the forests of the Don built two warships, 23 galleys and many smaller craft to cut Azov off from the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Azov fell, councils of boyars on 20 October and 4 November 1696 resolved to build a fleet by gangs of workmen under foreign shipwrights, and 20 October 1696 is conventionally taken as the birthday of the Russian navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Landowners and monasteries were grouped into companies, the kumpanstva, each obliged to build and arm a ship by April 1698, and most of the vessels were in fact built by contractors from the German suburb of Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The war with Sweden moved the centre of the navy to the Baltic, where six frigates launched in 1703 formed the first Russian squadron on that sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The disastrous campaign on the Pruth in 1711 cost Russia the Sea of Azov and halted its naval progress in the south for half a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Baltic the new ships and galleys won their first great naval battle off Hango on the Finnish coast in 1714, taking a frigate, nine galleys and 116 guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter afterwards asked his companions whether any of them, twenty years earlier, had dreamed of winning a battle in the Baltic with ships built by their own hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the end of his reign Russia had 48 ships of the line, 800 galleys and smaller vessels and 28,000 men afloat, and in 1725 the fleet was budgeted at 1,500,000 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 251–54, 264–65, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034689. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 223–25, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 11, 55, 66–67, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Т. В. Алексеев, «Строительство Азовского флота в 1695–1712 гг. в отечественной историографии», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2020): 85–108, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroitelstvo-azovskogo-flota-v-1695-1712-gg-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 246, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","В. В. Остапенко, «От Азова — к Кронштадту», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 12 (2006): 24–28, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-azova-k-kronshtadtu. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Кондрескул, «История Российского военно-морского флота», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 2 (2015): 160–162, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-rossiyskogo-voenno-morskogo-flota. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the navy begin on the Don rather than at sea?","a":"Because Peter's first war was against the Ottoman fortress of Azov, and the first siege showed that he needed ships to stop the Turks supplying it from the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galleys and barges were therefore built at Voronezh, deep in the forests of the Don, and floated downstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were landowners and monasteries made to build ships?","a":"Because the state could not pay for the whole fleet at once, so the councils of 1696 laid the cost on those who held peasant households, grouped into companies that each had to deliver an armed ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most hired foreign contractors to do the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Baltic fleet matter more in the end?","a":"Because the Pruth campaign of 1711 lost Russia the Sea of Azov and its southern fleet, while the war with Sweden was won on the Baltic, where the victory off Hango in 1714 broke Swedish command of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Imperial Russian Navy"},
    "ru-292": {"questions":["Russian redoubts set a musket shot apart broke up the Swedish columns at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 27 June 1709, a date given as 8 July by the Gregorian calendar.","At dinner after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Peter raised his glass to the captured Swedish generals as his teachers in war and wrote that St Petersburg's foundation was now secure."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Poltava</b> was fought on 27 June 1709 by the Julian calendar then used in Russia, 8 July by the Gregorian, when the army of Peter I destroyed the army of Charles XII of Sweden outside the Ukrainian town of Poltava.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It decided the Great Northern War and ended the campaign in which Charles had marched into Ukraine in the hope of support from the Cossack hetman Ivan Mazepa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The winter of 1708 to 1709 was the severest Europe had known for a century, and by spring the Swedes had shrunk to about 20,000 men, many of them sick, with powder spoilt by the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In May Charles laid siege to Poltava, held by a garrison of 4,200 soldiers and 2,600 Cossacks under Colonel Kelin together with armed townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peter arrived in June and crossed the Vorskla to entrench, and a few days before the battle Charles was wounded in the foot and handed command to Field Marshal Rehnskiöld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Peter had prepared the ground with a line of six redoubts a musket shot apart and four more at right angles to it, two of them unfinished, to break up the Swedish advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Against some 50,000 men under arms in the Russian camp, only about 12,500 Swedes went into the attack, with just four guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Swedes took two unfinished redoubts but were drawn apart, and by noon their infantry, the royal guard among them, had been destroyed by Russian musketry and artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At dinner that day Peter toasted the captured Swedish generals as his teachers in the art of war, and within days the remnant of the army surrendered at Perevolochna on the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Charles escaped with Mazepa into Ottoman territory, and Peter wrote that the foundations of St Petersburg were now securely laid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 110–22, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 266–72, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 54, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","А. В. Беспалов, ««Гибель элиты». Шведская гвардия в сражении под Полтавой 27 июня (8 июля) 1709 года», <i>Norwegian Journal of Development of the International Science</i>, no. 30, pt. 2 (2019): 27–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gibel-elity-shvedskaya-gvardiya-v-srazhenii-pod-poltavoy-27-iyunya-8-iyulya-1709-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ю. Гутман and П. Д. Николаенко, «Важнейшее сражение Северной войны 1700–1721 гг. (к 300-летию Полтавской битвы)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 2 (2009): 15–22, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vazhneyshee-srazhenie-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg-k-300-letiyu-poltavskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Д. Николаенко, «Победа под Полтавой 27 июня 1709 г. — важнейший фактор укрепления российской государственности», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 2 (2019): 71–78, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobeda-pod-poltavoy-27-iyunya-1709-g-vazhneyshiy-faktor-ukrepleniya-rossiyskoy-gosudarstvennosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Рогинский, «Полтава и Фридрихсгам. 1709, 1809 годы: две эпохальные даты в истории России и Северной Европы», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 6 (2009): 65–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poltava-i-fridrihsgam-1709-1809-gody-dve-epohalnye-daty-v-istorii-rossii-i-severnoy-evropy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Беспалов, «Судьба шведского отряда генерал-майора К. Г. Рооса в сражении под Полтавой», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 7 (2009): 5–8, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sudba-shvedskogo-otryada-general-mayora-k-g-roosa-v-srazhenii-pod-poltavoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Swedish army so weak by the summer of 1709?","a":"Because it had wintered in Ukraine through the severest frost in a century, losing men to cold and sickness, and its powder had been spoilt by the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mazepa had brought far fewer Cossacks than Charles expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the redoubts matter?","a":"Because they forced the attacking Swedes either to storm them under fire or to push past them under crossfire, which broke up their formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Part of the Swedish army stayed behind attacking the redoubts and was separated from the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter call the Swedes his teachers?","a":"Because the Swedish army had beaten his own at Narva and taught him the art of war over nine years of fighting, and at Poltava the pupils turned the lesson on their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Poltava"},
    "ru-293": {"questions":["A decree of 12 November 1708 put the former hetman <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under anathema, after Menshikov's troops had stormed and burnt his capital at Baturyn.","Made hetman in 1687 with the backing of Vasily Golitsyn, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept Peter's trust for 21 years and was among the first knights of the Order of St Andrew."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan Mazepa</b> was hetman, the elected military and civil head, of the Cossack Hetmanate on the left bank of the Dnieper from 1687 until 1708, when he went over to Charles XII of Sweden in the Great Northern War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born into the Orthodox minor nobility, schooled by the Jesuits and for a time a page at the Polish royal court, he rose in Cossack service and was made hetman in 1687 with the backing of Prince Vasily Golitsyn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For 21 years he kept the tsar's trust, was among the first knights of the new Order of St Andrew and was treated in Moscow with a deference no earlier hetman had known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter's reforms meanwhile cut down the powers of the hetman's administration, and the officers of the Hetmanate resented Russian troops, taxes and interference in their affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians still disagree about his motives: one recent study finds no evidence of 'treason' before 1708, yet that summer Peter had handed over for execution the judge Vasily Kochubey, who had denounced him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In October 1708, as Charles turned south into Ukraine and Peter summoned him to the Russian army, Mazepa pleaded mortal illness and then rode to the Swedish camp with about 1,500 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His flight took the Russian government by surprise and changed the whole political situation in Ukraine, where the main Russian–Swedish struggle was then being fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Menshikov stormed and burnt the hetman's capital at Baturyn, a new hetman, Ivan Skoropadsky, was elected under Peter's eye, and a decree of 12 November 1708 put Mazepa under anathema.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peter's decrees and charters of that autumn called him a thief, a traitor and an apostate, in texts of open propaganda meant to hold the Cossacks to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Few Cossacks stayed with him, and after Poltava he fled with Charles into Ottoman territory, where he died within the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 92–93, 98–110, 120, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 266–69, 277, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","К. А. Кочегаров, «К истории побега гетмана И. С. Мазепы к шведам. Из архива походной канцелярии А. Д. Меншикова», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2024): 345–356, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-pobega-getmana-i-s-mazepy-k-shvedam-iz-arhiva-pohodnoy-kantselyarii-a-d-menshikova. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. С. Коновалов, «Таирова-Яковлева Т. Г. Иван Мазепа и Российская империя: история «предательства»», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 1 (2012): 54–60, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2012-01-010-tairova-yakovleva-t-g-ivan-mazepa-i-rossiyskaya-imperiya-istoriya-predatelstva-m-tsentrpoligraf-2011-525-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. С. Садова, «Язык именных указов и грамот Петра I о Мазепе (1708 г.)», <i>Русин</i>, no. 80 (2025): 132–146, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yazyk-imennyh-ukazov-i-gramot-petra-i-o-mazepe-1708-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Коваленко, «Обида, жажда власти, предательство?! Гетман Мазепа и отношения с Россией в конце XVII — начале XVIII вв.», <i>Современная Европа</i>, no. 3 (2008): 103–111, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obida-zhazhda-vlasti-predatelstvo-getman-mazepa-i-otnosheniya-s-rossiey-v-kontse-xvii-nachale-xviii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Д. Николаенко, ««Возвышаюсь, когда погиб»: к 310-й годовщине казни генерального судьи Малороссии Василия Леонтьевича Кочубея», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 3 (2018): 8–13, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozvyshayus-kogda-pogib-k-310-y-godovschine-kazni-generalnogo-sudi-malorossii-vasiliya-leontievicha-kochubeya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mazepa leave the tsar after so many years?","a":"Because Peter's reforms were eroding the hetman's powers and the Cossack officers resented Russian demands, while Charles's march into Ukraine forced him to choose a side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had hoped to stay neutral until the winner was clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Baturyn destroyed so quickly?","a":"Because it was the hetman's capital and held stores the Swedes needed, and Peter wanted to stop the revolt from spreading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ruin overawed most of the Cossacks into staying loyal to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about him?","a":"Because Russian decrees branded him a traitor in deliberately rhetorical texts, while some historians argue that his break came only in 1708 and grew out of the loss of the Hetmanate's autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ivan Mazepa","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Iwan_Mazepa_crop.jpg/960px-Iwan_Mazepa_crop.jpg","title":"Ivan Mazepa, 1706","desc":"Mazepa engraved by Martin Bernigeroth for the Leipzig journal Die Europäische Fama in 1706.","credit":"Engraving by Martin Bernigeroth, 1706; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iwan_Mazepa_crop.jpg","alt":"An engraved portrait of a moustached man in armour holding a mace, a tent behind him."}},
    "ru-294": {"questions":["Relying on a secret treaty with the hospodar Demetrius Cantemir, Peter led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into Moldavia in 1711, hoping the Balkan Christians would rise against the Turks.","Vice-Chancellor Shafirov was left with the Ottomans as a hostage when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with a promise to raze Taganrog and the forts on the Sea of Azov."],"abstract":"The <b>Pruth River Campaign</b> was the expedition that Peter I led into Moldavia in 1711 against the Ottoman Empire, which ended with his army surrounded on the river Pruth and saved only by a hasty peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ottomans, who were sheltering Charles XII after Poltava, went to war in late 1710 when Peter demanded to know whether the Sultan wanted peace or war, and they imprisoned the Russian envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One recent study sees the deeper cause in Russia's rise to dominance in northern and eastern Europe, whose growing strength near the Ottoman borders from Azov to the Dniester alarmed Istanbul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peter counted on the Orthodox Christians of the Balkans, and by a secret treaty the hospodar of Moldavia, Demetrius Cantemir, placed his country under Russian protection in return for military help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land had already been stripped by the Turks, the risings Peter hoped for did not come, and his army marched short of food through a hot summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His main force of 38,000 Russian soldiers and dragoons, 5,000 Moldavians and 114 guns moved down the right bank of the Pruth while the Ottomans crossed the river behind its vanguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 9 July by the Julian calendar it was surrounded in a fortified camp near Stănilești by a far larger Ottoman and Tatar army, and although the janissaries' first assault was beaten off, food and ammunition were running out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vice-Chancellor Shafirov was sent to negotiate with authority to give up almost all of Peter's Baltic conquests except St Petersburg and to bribe the grand vizier, a measure of how desperate the position was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peace signed on 12 July 1711, 23 July by the Gregorian calendar, let the army go home in return for the surrender of Azov and the razing of Taganrog and the other Russian forts on the Sea of Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shafirov stayed behind as a hostage, and the loss of Azov halted Russia's naval progress on the Black Sea for half a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 277–82, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 197–200, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","В. А. Волков, «Горький прутский урок», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 4 (2022): 5–14, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gorkiy-prutskiy-urok. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 55, 66, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","П. А. Аваков, «Новое исследование о русско-турецкой войне 1710–1713 гг.», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 2 (2022): 213–221, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novoe-issledovanie-o-russko-turetskoy-voyne-1710-1713-gg-retsenziya-na-knigu-artamonov-v-a-turetsko-russkaya-voyna-1710-1713-gg-m. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Кулинич, «Офицерский корпус русской армии в сражении при Станилештах в Прутском походе 1711 г.», <i>Новые исторические перспективы: от Балтики до Тихого океана</i>, no. 4 (2019): 79–91, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ofitserskiy-korpus-russkoy-armii-v-srazhenii-pri-stanileshtah-v-prutskom-pohode-1711-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter march so deep into Moldavia?","a":"Because he expected the Orthodox Christians of the Balkans to rise and had a secret treaty with the ruler of Moldavia, so he pushed on to unite with them despite the lack of supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Russian army trapped?","a":"Because it advanced slowly and its vanguard failed to stop the Ottomans crossing the Pruth, so a far larger Ottoman and Tatar army surrounded it in a camp short of food, forage and ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Retreat across the river was cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the peace terms seen as lucky?","a":"Because Peter had been ready to give up nearly all his Baltic conquests, yet the treaty cost him only Azov and the forts on the Sea of Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, it ended Russian naval hopes in the south for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1710–1713)"},
    "ru-295": {"questions":["Peter joked that his school of war had lasted three times the usual seven years when news of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached him on the road to Vyborg in 1721.","Russia paid two million thalers under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and undertook to guarantee the new Swedish constitution, in which the crown had ceased to be absolute."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Nystad</b> was the peace signed at the Finnish town of Nystad, now Uusikaupunki, on 30 August 1721 by the Julian calendar, 10 September by the Gregorian, which ended the Great Northern War between Russia and Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Talks opened there in May 1721, with France as mediator, between the Russian plenipotentiaries Bruce and Osterman and two Swedish envoys, after an earlier congress on the Åland Islands had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Swedes still pleaded for Vyborg, but Russian raids on the Swedish coast, burning towns and hundreds of villages while a British fleet looked on, hastened the negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sweden ceded Livonia, Estonia, Ingria and part of Karelia with Vyborg to Russia in perpetuity, while Russia handed back the rest of Finland, which its armies had occupied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia paid two million thalers over four years, let Sweden buy grain duty-free at Baltic ports, confirmed the rights and religion of the conquered provinces and promised not to meddle in Sweden's internal affairs or succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Peter received the news on the road to Vyborg and called the treaty the most profitable peace Russia had ever made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He joked that most pupils finished their schooling in seven years but that his school of war had lasted three times as long, since the war had gone on for 21 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With her Baltic provinces Sweden gave up the leadership of the north and her claim to be counted a great power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By one article of the treaty Russia even became the guarantor of Sweden's new constitutional order, in which the crown was no longer absolute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The victory celebrations reached their climax on 22 October 1721, when the Senate and the Holy Synod offered Peter the titles of Emperor, Father of the Fatherland and the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 422–25, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 301, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","О. Г. Агеева, «Ништадтский мир и имперский статус России», <i>Перспективы. Электронный журнал</i>, no. 4 (2021): 49–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nishtadtskiy-mir-i-imperskiy-status-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 56–57, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Россия Петра I: обретение великодержавности», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, 14, no. 6 (2021): 30–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-petra-i-obretenie-velikoderzhavnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Рогинский, «Полтава и Фридрихсгам. 1709, 1809 годы: две эпохальные даты в истории России и Северной Европы», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 6 (2009): 65–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poltava-i-fridrihsgam-1709-1809-gody-dve-epohalnye-daty-v-istorii-rossii-i-severnoy-evropy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Мезин, ««Основатель и отец своей империи»: Вольтер о Ништадтском мире и имперском статусе России», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 4 (2022): 43–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osnovatel-i-otets-svoey-imperii-volter-o-nishtadtskom-mire-i-imperskom-statuse-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sweden finally accept such harsh terms?","a":"Because it stood alone, its allies could not protect it, and Russian raids kept burning its coast even with a British fleet in the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each raid made the Swedish envoys more willing to yield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia give Finland back?","a":"Because Peter wanted the Baltic provinces and Vyborg, which secured St Petersburg, and did not aim to destroy Sweden; returning occupied Finland and paying money helped close the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty matter beyond the land it moved?","a":"Because Sweden lost its leading place in the north and Russia gained a say in Sweden's own constitution, while Peter took the imperial title weeks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Nystad"},
    "ru-296": {"questions":["Prussia and the Dutch Republic recognised the new title at once, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> waited until 1723 for Sweden's acceptance and until 1764 for Poland's.","Chancellor Golovkin told Peter that his labours had led Russians from non-existence to existence, in the speech that inaugurated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the peace with Sweden."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Empire</b> was the Russian state as it was styled from 1721, when Peter I took the title of Emperor of All Russia and the title of tsar fell out of official use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its official birthday is dated to 22 October 1721 by the Julian calendar, less than two months after the peace with Sweden at Nystad, a day chosen to fall on the feast of the Kazan icon of the Mother of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Foreign writers had sometimes called Russian rulers emperors before, but the new title was a claim to a place among the leading states in the political hierarchy of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition came slowly, for Prussia and the Dutch Republic accepted it at once, Sweden in 1723 and other states only over the following decades, Poland not until 1764.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians still disagree whether Peter's Russia was already a great power, one view dating that status to a treaty of 1717 and another holding that defeats such as the Pruth kept it below that rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The proposal came first from the Holy Synod, which in October resolved to beg the tsar to accept the titles of Father of the Fatherland, Peter the Great and Emperor of All Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joint sessions of the Senate and the Synod then prepared the ceremony and the speech, while 23 regiments and 125 galleys of the Baltic fleet gathered in St Petersburg and troops filled Trinity Square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the Trinity Cathedral the chancellor, Count Golovkin, spoke for both bodies, saying that Peter's labours had led his people from non-existence to existence and into the company of political nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The senators cried 'Vivat!' three times, the crowd inside and outside took up the cry, and bells, trumpets, drums and a general salute followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A banquet for about a thousand guests followed, and from that day the sovereign of Russia was officially known as Emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["О. Г. Агеева, «Ништадтский мир и имперский статус России», <i>Перспективы. Электронный журнал</i>, no. 4 (2021): 49–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nishtadtskiy-mir-i-imperskiy-status-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 368–69, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 425–27, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 212–13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Россия Петра I: обретение великодержавности», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, 14, no. 6 (2021): 30–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-petra-i-obretenie-velikoderzhavnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Артамонов, «Провозглашение Всероссийской империи — на пути к великодержавному статусу», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, 15, no. 2 (2022): 51–68, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/provozglashenie-vserossiyskoy-imperii-na-puti-k-velikoderzhavnomu-statusu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Анисимов, «Петр Великий и его империя», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 1 (2022): 6–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petr-velikiy-i-ego-imperiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Ф. Михайлова, «Из истории принятия Петром I титула императора», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i>, special issue (2006): 13–19, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-prinyatiya-petrom-i-titula-imperatora. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. С. Соколова, ««Непотребен без грому»: о репрезентативном потенциале законодательных инициатив Петра I в области моделирования политико-правовой семантики императорского титула, 1720-е гг.», <i>Электронное приложение к Российскому юридическому журналу</i>, no. 4 (2016): 101–124, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nepotreben-bez-gromu-o-reprezentativnom-potentsiale-zakonodatelnyh-initsiativ-petra-i-v-oblasti-modelirovaniya-politiko-pravovoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the new title follow the peace with Sweden?","a":"Because victory in a war of 21 years gave Russia a new weight in Europe, and the celebrations of the peace were used to proclaim it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ceremony was the climax of the peace festivities in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did other states hesitate to recognise it?","a":"Because the imperial title implied equal rank with the emperor in Vienna and the leading crowns, so recognition took decades of diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some delayed chiefly to please the German emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Church propose the title?","a":"Because the Synod's own minutes show that the idea was raised there first, by one of its vice-presidents, before it was passed to the Senate and worked out in joint sessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Empire"},
    "ru-297": {"questions":["A commoner who reached the 8th class of the civil service, that of collegiate assessor, became a hereditary noble under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issued by Peter I.","Ranks such as collegiate assessor and privy councillor, set out in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1722, survived for almost two centuries until the Bolsheviks abolished them in 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>Table of Ranks</b> was the law of 24 January 1722 by which Peter I arranged every post in the military, civil and court service of the Russian state into fourteen classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its drafters studied French, Prussian, Danish and Swedish models, and many of its titles came from German, some translated into Russian and some taken over whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As first issued it listed 262 posts, 126 military, 94 civil and 42 at court, with the military ranks divided again into army, guards, artillery and naval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Service now took precedence over birth, and no one could be granted a high grade without first passing through the lower ones, and young nobles were expected to begin as common soldiers in the guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The system lasted almost two centuries, until the Bolsheviks abolished all ranks and titles soon after taking power in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its deepest effect was on the nobility, because a rank in the state's service could now make a man noble, whatever his origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The lowest officer's rank, the 14th class, conferred hereditary nobility in the army, while in the civil service it came only with the 8th class, that of collegiate assessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lower civil grades gave only personal nobility, which passed to a wife but not to children, a status meant to slow the growth of the hereditary nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The old pedigreed families felt wronged at being made equal with ordinary servitors, and the law itself declared that noble birth gave no claim to office without service to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its purpose, in the view of one recent study, was to rationalise the hierarchy of officials and rid it of medieval survivals such as the confusion of functions and the priority of lineage over competence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 82, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 355–56, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","А. А. Белых, «Три века Табели о рангах», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 6: Экономика</i>, no. 2 (2023): 97–113, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tri-veka-tabeli-o-rangah. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Львов, «Табель о рангах 1722 года и эволюция чинопроизводства в XVIII веке», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Юридические науки</i>, no. 3 (2022): 40–51, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tabel-o-rangah-1722-goda-i-evolyutsiya-chinoproizvodstva-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. И. Пудина, «Служба дворянина в первой четверти XVIII в. согласно «Табели о рангах»», <i>Экономические и социально-гуманитарные исследования</i>, no. 1 (2022): 167–173, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sluzhba-dvoryanina-v-pervoy-chetverti-xviii-v-soglasno-tabeli-o-rangah. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Г. Киселев, «Политико-правовые аспекты становления и развития государственной службы в российском государстве, XVI–XVIII вв.», <i>Социально-гуманитарные знания</i>, no. 4 (2016): 200–210, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politiko-pravovye-aspekty-stanovleniya-i-razvitiya-gosudarstvennoy-sluzhby-v-rossiyskom-gosudarstve-xvi-xviii-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Чуб, «Из истории формирования российского законодательства в сфере государственно-служебных отношений», <i>Актуальные проблемы российского права</i>, no. 1 (2007): 30–37, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-formirovaniya-rossiyskogo-zakonodatelstva-v-sfere-gosudarstvenno-sluzhebnyh-otnosheniy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a commoner become a noble under the Table?","a":"Because rank was tied to service, so reaching a set class brought nobility with it: the first officer's rank in the army, or the 8th class in the civil service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the old families dislike it?","a":"Because it put service above pedigree and made the descendants of great houses equal in rights with ordinary servitors who had risen through the ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many titles sound German?","a":"Because the law was drafted after a study of foreign models, Prussian, Danish, Swedish and French among them, and many of its titles were translated from German or taken over unchanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Table of Ranks"},
    "ru-298": {"questions":["A list of December 1718 named nine <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Foreign Affairs, War, the Admiralty, Justice, Commerce and Mines and Manufactures.","A report from Stockholm compared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a watch in which one wheel moves another, an image that pleased Peter, though Alexander I made ministries of them in 1802."],"abstract":"The <b>Petrine collegia</b> were the boards that Peter I set up between 1717 and 1720 to replace the old prikazy as the central departments of the Russian state, each run by a council of members rather than a single official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peter took the collegiate system from Sweden, whose colleges had the highest reputation in Europe, and agents such as the Holsteiner Heinrich Fick were sent to study it and bring back its regulations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A decree of 11 December 1717 fixed a model staff for all the colleges, and a list of 12 December 1718 named nine of them, from Foreign Affairs, Revenue and Justice to War, the Admiralty, Commerce and Mines and Manufactures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five of the nine dealt with the economy and finance, and only two, Foreign Affairs and the Revision office of accounts, matched the scope of an old prikaz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A General Regulation of 28 February 1720 laid down how every college and its offices were to work and to keep their papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What set the colleges apart was that business was decided in full session by consultation, and one report compared their working to a watch in which one wheel moves another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The presidents were Russians, among them Golovkin, Menshikov and Apraksin, while most vice-presidents were foreigners and some colleges kept German names such as Kammer and Justiz-Collegium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new wheels at first turned badly, with Russian clerks unable to follow the Swedish forms and frequent quarrels between Russian and foreign members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1722 most of the foreigners were dismissed and the presidents withdrawn from the Senate, and by the end of the reign the colleges numbered ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lasted until 1802, when Alexander I turned them into ministries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 173–76, 178, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 350–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 317–18, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Клаэс Петерсон, «Коллежская реформа Петра Великого: шведские аналоги и процесс рецепции», <i>Экономическая политика</i>, no. 1 (2023): 136–175, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kollezhskaya-reforma-petra-velikogo-shvedskie-analogi-i-protsess-retseptsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. М. Емышева, «Генеральный регламент 1720 года как опыт создания организационного документа», <i>История и архивы</i>, no. 8 (2008): 248–261, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/generalnyy-reglament-1720-goda-kak-opyt-sozdaniya-organizatsionnogo-dokumenta-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. В. Киндюк and Т. В. Безрук, «Организационно-правовые основы использования шведской модели государственного управления при проведении коллежской реформы 1717–1722 годов и создания Малороссийской коллегии», <i>Вестник Воронежского государственного университета. Серия: Право</i>, no. 1 (2015): 100–108, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsionno-pravovye-osnovy-ispolzovaniya-shvedskoy-modeli-gosudarstvennogo-upravleniya-pri-provedenii-kollezhskoy-reformy-1717. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter copy Sweden?","a":"Because his inquiries found the Swedish colleges the most highly regarded in Europe, and Russia's own past offered no model for a central administration of the kind he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agents studied them and adapted their regulations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the colleges work badly at first?","a":"Because the provincial offices still worked on the old footing, Russian clerks could not follow the Swedish forms, and Russian and foreign members quarrelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were presidents taken out of the Senate?","a":"Because no member dared contradict a president who was also a senator, and his fellow senators backed him against complaints, so the two roles were separated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Collegium (ministry)"},
    "ru-299": {"questions":["In 1722 Peter set a Procurator-General over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to act as the tsar's eye in its sessions and to command the fiscals who watched for embezzlement.","Nine senators, as many as the surviving members of the Boyar Duma, made up the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Peter scolded from abroad for its waste of time."],"abstract":"The <b>Governing Senate</b> was the supreme body of state administration that Peter I created by a decree of 22 February 1711, on the eve of his campaign against the Ottomans, to govern in his absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its first members were nine senators named by the tsar, a number equal to the surviving members of the old Boyar Duma, three of whom passed into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A decree of 2 March 1711 ordered everyone to obey it as they would the tsar himself, under pain of death, and gave it charge of justice, revenue and expenditure, the enrolment of nobles as officers and trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had none of the Duma's power to make law, being at first a temporary commission for current business, but in time it became a permanent supreme institution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same legislation created the fiscals, a network of secret inspectors under a chief fiscal answerable to the Senate, who were to denounce embezzlers however eminent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Peter wrote often to 'Messieurs the Senate' from abroad, scolding the senators for wasted time and reminding them that the Senate represented the person of his Majesty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1718 the presidents of the new colleges sat in it, but they were later withdrawn because their fellow senators shielded them from complaints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To keep order in its sessions Peter first set guards officers over the senators and then, by a decree of 12 January 1722, created the post of Procurator-General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The instruction of 27 April 1722 called the Procurator-General the tsar's eye in the Senate, gave him charge of its chancellery and set the fiscals under him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first holder was Pavel Yaguzhinsky, son of a Lutheran organist in Moscow, and there was no appeal from his decisions except to the tsar himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 165–71, 183–85, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 348–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 317, 384–85, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Е. А. Чекурда, «Правительствующий сенат в России в начале XVIII века: учреждение и первоначальная компетенция», <i>Сибирское юридическое обозрение</i>, no. 17 (2011): 10–12, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravitelstvuyuschiy-senat-v-rossii-v-nachale-xviii-veka-uchrezhdenie-i-pervonachalnaya-kompetentsiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. О. Серов, «Гвардейцы и фискалы Петра I в борьбе с лихоимцами и казнокрадами», <i>Исторический вестник</i>, no. 6 (2013): 54–81, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gvardeytsy-i-fiskaly-petra-i-v-borbe-s-lihoimtsami-i-kaznokradami. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Еремин, «Сенатская прокуратура в Российской империи в XVIII веке», <i>КриминалистЪ</i>, no. 1 (2021): 32–36, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/senatskaya-prokuratura-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Соколов, «Проблемы генезиса Правительствующего Сената в России», <i>Юридическая наука и практика: Вестник Нижегородской академии МВД России</i>, no. 3 (2016): 195–199, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problemy-genezisa-pravitelstvuyuschego-senata-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Senate created in 1711?","a":"Because Peter was leaving for the war against the Ottomans and needed a body to run the state in his absence, with full authority over officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was at first meant to be temporary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter add fiscals and a Procurator-General?","a":"Because the senators neglected business and embezzlement was rife, so he set inspectors to denounce offenders and then an official to watch the Senate itself as his eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the presidents of colleges removed from it?","a":"Because members of a college dared not contradict a president who was also a senator, and complaints against him were met by his colleagues' support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Governing Senate"},
    "ru-300": {"questions":["The Spiritual Regulation of 1721 argued that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was safer than a patriarch, whom simple folk might take for a second sovereign with powers like the tsar's.","A civil Ober-Procurator appointed in May 1722 sat with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which governed the Russian Church until a council of 1917–1918 restored the patriarchate."],"abstract":"The <b>Most Holy Synod</b> was the council of bishops and clergy that governed the Russian Orthodox Church in place of a patriarch from 1721, set up by Peter I as one of the departments of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Patriarch Adrian died in 1700 no successor was chosen, and for over twenty years Stephen Yavorsky, Metropolitan of Ryazan, merely kept the patriarchal throne as its guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter wanted the Church subordinate to the state and feared a rival as dangerous as Patriarch Nikon had been in his father's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A manifesto of 25 January 1721 established a Spiritual College as the supreme authority of church government, which from its opening on 14 February took the name of the Most Holy Governing Synod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It governed the Church until the Church Council of 1917 to 1918, whose chief achievement in the official view was the restoration of the patriarchate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its charter, the Spiritual Regulation, was drafted by Feofan Prokopovich, Archbishop of Pskov, and revised article by article by Peter himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Regulation argued that rule by a council was safer than rule by one pastor, because simple folk could not tell the spiritual power from the sovereign one and might take a patriarch for a second sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It charged the Synod with rooting out ignorance and superstition and with educating the clergy, and it called on every bishop to keep a school at his house for priests' children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Synod first had ten members under Yavorsky as president, but after his death in 1722 the presidency lapsed and a civil official, the Ober-Procurator, was attached to it from 11 May 1722 without being a member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The patriarchs of Antioch and Constantinople recognised its authority in September 1723, and later critics held that the system had made the Church a state department under an official named by the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 389, 395–97, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 338–39, 392–93, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","П. И. Кизюн, «Духовный регламент Петра I и духовное образование в России XVIII века», <i>Новосибирский временник</i>, no. 1 (2022): 5–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/duhovnyy-reglament-petra-i-i-duhovnoe-obrazovanie-v-rossii-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Пинюгина, «Столетие Поместного Собора и отголоски «церковной революции» 1917 г.: векторы политики памяти руководства РПЦ и церковной интеллигенции», <i>Политическая наука</i>, no. 3 (2018): 70–90, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stoletie-pomestnogo-sobora-i-otgoloski-tserkovnoy-revolyutsii-1917-g-vektory-politiki-pamyati-rukovodstva-rpts-i-tserkovnoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Красов, «Государство и Церковь: причины упразднения патриаршества в России», <i>Ученые записки Орловского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2022): 43–50, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvo-i-tserkov-prichiny-uprazdneniya-patriarshestva-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Назаров, «Религиозная политика Петра Великого: от симфонии властей к абсолютизму», <i>Вестник РМАТ</i>, no. 4 (2022): 9–14, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/religioznaya-politika-petra-velikogo-ot-simfonii-vlastey-k-absolyutizmu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Бежанидзе and А. Г. Фирсов, ««Да не явимся неблагодарни Вышнему, аще пренебрежем исправление и чина Духовнаго…»: церковные реформы Петра Великого в контексте концепции абсолютизма», <i>Вестник Исторического общества Санкт-Петербургской Духовной Академии</i>, no. 1 (2024): 58–65, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/da-ne-yavimsya-neblagodarni-vyshnemu-asche-prenebrezhem-ispravlenie-i-china-duhovnago-tserkovnye-reformy-petra-velikogo-v-kontekste. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter not appoint a new patriarch?","a":"Because a patriarch who opposed his reforms could become a rival like Nikon, and a change during the war risked unrest, so he left the throne vacant under a guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Regulation later argued that the people might follow a patriarch against the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Synod modelled on the colleges?","a":"Because after bringing the collegiate system into the civil government Peter thought it fit for the Church too, and he had seen church government by councils in Protestant countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Synod began as a Spiritual College.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did reformers in 1917 want to end the synodal system?","a":"Because, in their view, it had made the emperor head of the Church and turned the Church into a state department under an Ober-Procurator appointed by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Most Holy Synod"},
    "ru-341": {"questions":["Copying maxims from Montesquieu and Beccaria into an Instruction that called Russia a European state, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> posed before Europe as a monarch guided by the philosophers.","A stroke in November 1796 ended the life of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose long reign had raised the empire's population from 20 million to 36 million."],"abstract":"<b>Catherine the Great</b> was the German-born empress who ruled Russia as Catherine II from the summer of 1762 until her death in November 1796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was born at Stettin on 2 May 1729 by the Western calendar as Sophia Augusta, the daughter of a minor prince of Anhalt-Zerbst who served the King of Prussia as a soldier and governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1744 she arrived with her mother at the court of Empress Elizabeth in Moscow, chosen as the bride of the heir to the throne, the future Peter III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Six months after her husband came to the throne, the Guards overthrew him in a coup led by the Orlov brothers, and she was proclaimed empress in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her first manifestos, her coronation in Moscow and the festivals that followed presented her as the saviour of Russia and of the Orthodox faith, chosen by God and likened to Minerva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> Catherine read Montesquieu and Beccaria closely and copied whole maxims from them into the Instruction she wrote in 1767 for a commission meant to draft a new law code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That Instruction opened by declaring that Russia is a European state, and her letters to Voltaire and her generous purchase of Diderot's library made her famous as a patron of the French philosophers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Her wars against the Ottoman Empire and the partitions of Poland enlarged the empire so much that, on one estimate, its population rose from 20 million at her accession to 36 million in 1796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At court she kept a long line of favourites, with Grigory Orlov and Grigory Potemkin far above the rest, and she seems to have treated the chief of them as husbands rather than passing lovers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She suffered a stroke on 5 November 1796 by the Julian calendar, struggled against death until the next evening and left the throne to her son Paul, whom she had long kept from power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 1–3, 23–26, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 93, 430–31, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Paul the First of Russia, the Son of Catherine the Great</i> (London: Heinemann, 1913), 73–77, https://archive.org/details/paulfirstofrussi00waliuoft. [Open access]","Catherine II, <i>Memoirs of the Empress Catherine II, Written by Herself</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1859), 22, https://archive.org/details/memoirsempressc01cathgoog. [Open access]","Catherine II, <i>The Grand Instructions to the Commissioners Appointed to Frame a New Code of Laws for the Russian Empire</i>, trans. Michael Tatischeff (London, 1768), https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-grand-instructions-t_russia-sovereign-1762-_1768. [Open access]","И. В. Фаизова, «Утверждение на российском престоле Екатерины II и пиар-кампания 1762–1763 гг.», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/utverzhdenie-na-rossiyskom-prestole-ekateriny-ii-i-piar-kampaniya-1762-1763-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Е. Лупанова, «Екатерина II: франкофил или франкофоб?», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 55 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ekaterina-ii-frankofil-ili-frankofob. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Е. Приказчикова, «Фаворитизм как культурно-исторический и литературный феномен России второй половины XVIII века», <i>Сибирский филологический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/favoritizm-kak-kulturno-istoricheskiy-i-literaturnyy-fenomen-rossii-vtoroy-poloviny-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, vol. 1 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1800), 291–93, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcatharinei01took. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a German princess with no Russian blood become empress?","a":"Because she had come to Russia in 1744 as the chosen bride of the heir, and in 1762 the Guards turned against her husband within months of his accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The officers who led the coup, the Orlov brothers above all, put her on the throne in his place, and her early manifestos and coronation festivals worked hard to present her as Russia's saviour, chosen by God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did she write so much about laws taken from French and Italian authors?","a":"Because she wanted Russia governed by fixed laws and saw the newest European political writing as the model for a country she declared to be European.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She copied many maxims from Montesquieu and Beccaria into her Instruction of 1767, and her friendship with Voltaire and Diderot brought her praise across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does her reign matter so much for the shape of the empire?","a":"Because her wars with the Ottoman Empire and the partitions of Poland moved the frontier far to the south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By one estimate the empire's population almost doubled during her reign, from 20 million to 36 million, mostly through the land she gained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Catherine the Great"},
    "ru-342": {"questions":["New triumphal gates on Tverskaya Street greeted the entry into Moscow that came nine days before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the ceremony that sealed the summer's coup.","A jeweller joined two openwork hemispheres with a single band for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the new crown was soon called the richest object in Europe."],"abstract":"The <b>coronation of Catherine II</b> was the ceremony in the Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin on 22 September 1762 by the Julian calendar, 3 October by the Gregorian, at which the new empress was crowned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came less than three months after the coup of June 1762, so it had to give a sacred stamp to a reign that had begun with the overthrow of a lawful emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The festivities began with her arrival near Moscow on 9 September, and on 13 September she made a solemn entry through newly built triumphal gates on Tverskaya Street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The paintings on the gates showed her as a saviour sent by God and defender of Orthodoxy, while scenes of decline recalled the reign of her deposed husband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ceremony itself followed the order used for Empress Elizabeth: Catherine placed the regalia on herself and took communion at the altar by the rite reserved for crowned rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> She took the crown from the hands of Archbishop Dmitry of Novgorod and set it on her own head, after which he handed her the sceptre and the orb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new crown, made by the court jeweller Pauzié from two openwork hemispheres joined by a band, was soon called the richest object in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rewards poured out to the makers of the coup, the Orlov brothers were made counts, and Catherine named herself colonel of each regiment of the Guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Princess Dashkova, who had helped to plan the coup, had to watch from a stand for colonels' wives, a slight she blamed on the Orlovs, who were in charge of the ceremonial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Celebrations in Moscow lasted through the winter and ended with the great public masquerade Triumphant Minerva early in 1763, which carried the same message through the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["И. В. Фаизова, «Утверждение на российском престоле Екатерины II и пиар-кампания 1762–1763 гг.», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/utverzhdenie-na-rossiyskom-prestole-ekateriny-ii-i-piar-kampaniya-1762-1763-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Аронова, «Коронационные декорации как политический текст: Екатерина I — Екатерина II», <i>Искусствознание</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/koronatsionnye-dekoratsii-kak-politicheskiy-tekst-ekaterina-i-ekaterina-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. А. Тюхменева, «Московские триумфальные ворота к коронации Екатерины II в источниках 1760-х годов. Новые материалы», <i>Человек и культура</i>, no. 6 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskie-triumfalnye-vorota-k-koronatsii-ekateriny-ii-v-istochnikah-1760-h-godov-novye-materialy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ekaterina Dashkova, <i>Memoirs of the Princess Daschkaw, Lady of Honour to Catherine II</i>, vol. 1 (London: Henry Colburn, 1840), 121–23, https://archive.org/details/memoirsprincess01wilmgoog. [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, vol. 1 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1800), 291–93, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcatharinei01took. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 26–27, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a ceremony matter so much to Catherine in 1762?","a":"Because she had come to power by overthrowing her husband, and a coronation in the old capital gave her reign the blessing of the Church that the coup could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every part of the festival, from the gates to the medals, repeated that she was the saviour of Russia chosen by God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did she put the crown on her own head?","a":"Because that was how the rite had been done for Elizabeth, and it showed that the ruler received power directly from God rather than from the clergy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archbishop handed her the crown, but she set it on her head herself and then received the sceptre and orb from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Princess Dashkova feel humiliated at the ceremony?","a":"Because court etiquette ranked people by military rank, and as the wife of a colonel she had to stand among the lowest group admitted to the cathedral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She believed the Orlov brothers, who arranged the ceremonial, did this to humble her after her part in the coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-343": {"questions":["Declaring that Russia is a European state yet one that must be ruled by a single sovereign, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1767 tried to fit Enlightenment maxims to autocracy.","Panin joked that it held maxims able to shatter walls, and the French authorities banned its translation, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was kept under lock and key in Russian offices."],"abstract":"The <b>Nakaz</b>, or Instruction, was the long treatise on law and government that Catherine II wrote between 1765 and 1767 to guide the commission she had summoned to draft a new code of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In its first form it had 20 chapters and 526 articles, and two further chapters added in 1768 on police and on state finance brought it to 655.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began from the claim that laws must fit the condition of a people, and its first chapter states simply that Russia is a European state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It argued just as firmly that so vast an empire needs a single absolute ruler, since it is better to obey the laws of one master than to please many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Catherine showed drafts to advisers who struck out large parts, and by her own account only about a quarter of what she had written survived into print.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her chief source was Montesquieu's <i>The Spirit of the Laws</i>, which supplied about 250 of the 526 articles, while the long chapter on crime and punishment came largely from Beccaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She admitted to Frederick II that, like the raven in the fable, she had dressed herself in peacock feathers and that only the arrangement and an occasional line were her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For all that, it put ideas never before published in Russia over the empress's name, condemning torture as contrary to sound reason and urging religious tolerance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The French authorities banned its translation, while in Russia the Senate ordered copies locked away, and later historians stressed how sharply its principles clashed with the deputies' own instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Diderot, writing his comments on it in 1774, concluded that its author was still a despot and that only a nation can be the true lawgiver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Catherine II, <i>The Grand Instructions to the Commissioners Appointed to Frame a New Code of Laws for the Russian Empire</i>, trans. Michael Tatischeff (London, 1768), https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-grand-instructions-t_russia-sovereign-1762-_1768. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 30–36, 42–43, 102–3, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Д. Р. Садикова, «Отражение политических идей Ш. Л. Монтескье в „Наказе“ Екатерины II (по материалам дореволюционной отечественной историографии)», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 12 (2014): 102–8, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otrazhenie-politicheskih-idey-sh-l-monteskie-v-nakaze-ekateriny-ii-po-materialam-dorevolyutsionnoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Илюхин, «Институт наказания и суд присяжных в политико-правовых взглядах Екатерины II (на примере „Наказа“ 1767 г.)», <i>Вестник Кузбасского института</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/institut-nakazaniya-i-sud-prisyazhnyh-v-politiko-pravovyh-vzglyadah-ekateriny-ii-na-primere-nakaza-1767-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 93, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","В. И. Моряков, «Французские просветители Рейналь и Дидро о самодержавии и крепостном праве в России», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/frantsuzskie-prosvetiteli-reynal-i-didro-o-samoderzhavii-i-krepostnom-prave-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Волкова, «„Комиссия для сочинения проекта нового уложения“ 1767–1768 гг. в дореволюционной отечественной историографии», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/komissiya-dlya-sochineniya-proekta-novogo-ulozheniya-1767-1768-gg-v-dorevolyutsionnoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine borrow so much from Montesquieu and Beccaria?","a":"Because she argued that laws must fit a people's condition and that Russia was a European country, so the newest European political writing seemed the right foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She openly admitted the borrowing, saying that little but the arrangement of the material was her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nakaz defend absolute monarchy while praising liberty?","a":"Because it claimed that an empire as large as Russia could only be governed quickly and safely by one ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diderot spotted the tension at once and wrote that, however humane its maxims, its author remained a despot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a text so widely praised change so little in Russian law?","a":"Because it had no legal force of its own: it was a guide for a commission that never finished a code, and its principles clashed with what the deputies wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Senate even ordered that copies be kept locked away from inquisitive clerks, so few Russians ever read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nakaz"},
    "ru-344": {"questions":["Suspended in December 1768 when war with the Ottoman Empire broke out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had met about 200 times without ever producing the code it was summoned to write.","On 12 August 1767 the deputies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered Catherine the titles Great, Most Wise, Mother of her Country in thanks for the Instruction she had given them."],"abstract":"The <b>Legislative Commission</b> was the assembly of elected deputies that Catherine II convened in Moscow in 1767 to draft a new code of laws in place of the Ulozhenie of 1649.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She summoned it by a manifesto of 14 December 1766, and deputies were chosen by the nobles of each district, the householders of each town, the state peasants of each province, Cossacks and non-Russian peoples, as well as by the chief offices of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the 564 or 565 deputies, as different counts give them, about 39 per cent came from the towns and 30 per cent from the nobility, while privately owned serfs and the parish clergy had no deputies at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each deputy brought written instructions from his electors, drew a salary and was protected from the death penalty, torture and corporal punishment while he served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tatar, Mari and other non-Christian deputies sat beside senators and bishops, so that a bishop of the Holy Synod might share a task with a mullah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The deputies took their oath in the Dormition Cathedral on 30 July 1767, and the commission opened that day in the Palace of Facets in the Kremlin, where Catherine's Instruction was read to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They chose the Kostroma noble deputy Bibikov, a serving general, as their marshal, and on 12 August they offered the empress the titles Great, Most Wise, Mother of her Country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Debate soon laid bare the clash of interests, as merchants demanded the noble right to own serfs and nobles asked for serfdom to be extended, not limited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In about 18 months it held some 200 sittings without producing a code, and after moving to St Petersburg it was suspended on 18 December 1768 because of the war with the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its committees worked on until 1774 and their drafts later fed into the provincial reform of 1775 and the Charter to the Nobility of 1785, yet historians of the 19th century already judged it a failed attempt at codification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 36–43, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Catherine II, <i>The Grand Instructions to the Commissioners Appointed to Frame a New Code of Laws for the Russian Empire</i>, trans. Michael Tatischeff (London, 1768), https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-grand-instructions-t_russia-sovereign-1762-_1768. [Open access]","Л. Ю. Варенцова, «Неудачный правовой эксперимент императрицы Екатерины II в России в 1767–1768 гг.», <i>Юридическая техника</i>, no. 19 (2025): 143–47, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neudachnyy-pravovoy-eksperiment-imperatritsy-ekateriny-ii-v-rossii-v-1767-1768-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Верхогляд, «Правовой статус депутатов „Комиссии об уложении“ (1767)», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-deputatov-komissii-ob-ulozhenii-1767. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Волкова, «„Комиссия для сочинения проекта нового уложения“ 1767–1768 гг. в дореволюционной отечественной историографии», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/komissiya-dlya-sochineniya-proekta-novogo-ulozheniya-1767-1768-gg-v-dorevolyutsionnoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. Ислаев, «Татары в екатерининской уложенной комиссии 1767–1768 гг.», <i>Гасырлар авазы — Эхо веков</i>, no. 3–4 (2003), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tatary-v-ekaterininskoy-ulozhennoy-komissii-1767-1768-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 297–98, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine invite elected deputies to help write the laws?","a":"Because she wanted to learn what she called the people's needs and wants, and because older Russian codes had been drafted with the help of assemblies of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manifesto of December 1766 set the deputies a double task: to report those needs and to draft a new code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the commission fail to produce a code?","a":"Because its members represented interests that could not be reconciled, from senators to deputies of tribes newly brought under Russian rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Merchants wanted the right to own serfs, nobles wanted serfdom extended, and the Instruction it was told to follow had no force of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war with the Ottoman Empire end its meetings?","a":"Because in December 1768 the empress ordered the general sittings closed and the deputies sent home while the war was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sub-committees carried on for some years, but the full commission was never called back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-345": {"questions":["An unknown donor whose initials betrayed the empress sent 1,000 ducats to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the best essay on whether peasants should own the land they worked.","Members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> voted 13 to 3 against publishing the prize essay by a Frenchman from Dijon, which argued for freeing the peasants and giving them land."],"abstract":"The <b>Free Economic Society</b> was a learned society founded in St Petersburg in 1765 to improve agriculture and estate management, the first scientific society of its kind in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fifteen founders joined courtiers such as Grigory Orlov and Count Vorontsov with members of the Academy of Sciences, and Catherine II approved its plan and charter in a letter of 31 October 1765.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empress granted it a coat of arms and 6,000 roubles to buy a house, while the word 'free' in its name marked its independence from any government office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its founders looked to the economic societies of Britain and Ireland, such as the London society for the encouragement of arts founded in 1754, but it remained a society of the capital, dominated by nobles close to the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its <i>Proceedings</i>, published from 1765, printed practical essays on crops and forests, and a questionnaire it sent to the provinces brought back reports of peasants made to work four or five days a week for their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 1766 an 'unknown person', whose initials showed her to be the empress, sent the society 1,000 gold ducats as a prize for an essay on whether a peasant should own land or only movable property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Foreign authors could compete in Russian, French or German, and the essays were judged anonymously under mottos, with each author's name sealed in an envelope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 120 essays arrived, and the prize went to Béardé de l'Abbaye of the Academy of Dijon, whose essay argued for freeing the peasants and giving them land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 13 votes to 3 the members opposed printing the winning essay, and Catherine's push in 1768 to have it published met a membership that wanted practical papers, not speculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The episode showed the limits of reform from above, for the society kept working on farming and forestry while its noble members made sure the question of peasant property went no further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["П. М. Лукичёв, «Императорское Вольное экономическое общество и его роль в развитии сельского хозяйства России (к 250-летию ИВЭО)», <i>Известия Санкт-Петербургского государственного аграрного университета</i>, no. 42 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imperatorskoe-volnoe-ekonomicheskoe-obschestvo-i-ego-rol-v-razvitii-selskogo-hozyaystva-rossii-k-250-letiyu-iveo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Бабич и И. В. Евдокимов, «К 240-летию основания Вольного экономического общества», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Лесной журнал</i>, no. 2 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-240-letiyu-osnovaniya-volnogo-ekonomicheskogo-obschestva. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Петриков, «Идея экономического общества в Европе и России XVIII века», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ideya-ekonomicheskogo-obschestva-v-evrope-i-rossii-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Петриков, «„Общий дух ревности“: идея соревнования в работе Вольного экономического общества», <i>Журнал Российского национального комитета по истории и философии науки и техники</i> 3, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obschiy-duh-revnosti-ideya-sorevnovaniya-v-rabote-volnogo-ekonomicheskogo-obschestva. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 79–80, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 298–99, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the society called 'free'?","a":"Because it was meant to be independent of any government office, led by a president its members elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It still depended heavily on the empress, who gave it a coat of arms and money for a house, and on courtiers such as Grigory Orlov who sat among its founders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine hide behind an 'unknown person' for the prize question?","a":"Because the question of whether peasants should own land touched serfdom, which the noble members did not want to discuss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By sending the money anonymously she could put the question without openly ordering it, though her initials gave her away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the members refuse to publish the prize essay?","a":"Because the winning essay argued for freeing the peasants and giving them land, which went against the interests of the nobles who made up most of the society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They voted 13 to 3 against printing it and insisted the society dealt only in practical matters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Free Economic Society"},
    "ru-346": {"questions":["Arseny Matseyevich of Rostov warned that Russia would become a Protestant or atheist state, and he paid with his freedom for opposing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Catherine.","More than 40 per cent of Russia's monasteries closed after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while those kept on official staffs lived on fixed payments from the treasury instead of their estates."],"abstract":"The <b>secularisation of church lands</b> was the transfer of the landed estates of Russia's monasteries and bishops to the state, decreed by Catherine II in a manifesto of 26 February 1764.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Earlier rulers had tried to limit church landholding, and in 1762 Peter III had already placed the estates under a College of Economy run by army officers instead of monks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On her accession Catherine, courting the clergy, cancelled his decree in August 1762, but in November she set up a commission of three bishops and five laymen to prepare a new reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the manifesto the church's peasants became 'economic peasants' under a restored College of Economy, paying one and a half roubles a year instead of rents and labour for the monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return the state paid fixed allowances to the bishops and to the monasteries it kept on official staffs, and more than 40 per cent of Russia's monasteries were closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The boldest protest came from Arseny Matseyevich, Archbishop of Rostov, who denounced the commission's inventories of church property in formal protests to the Synod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He warned that if such measures went on, monks and bishops would disappear and Russia would become a Protestant or even atheist state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Catherine directed the case herself, and he was stripped of his rank, sent to a monastery and later held in the fortress of Reval under the name Andrei Vral, meaning 'liar', until his death in 1772.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The treasury gained a large new revenue, and about 900,000 serfs passed out of church ownership into the freer status of state peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For many monasteries the loss of their estates meant poverty or closure, as in the province of Pskov, where 64 per cent of the monasteries were shut, though a few such as Solovetsky recovered through pilgrims' gifts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["А. В. Богданова, «Секуляризация земель Соловецкого монастыря в 1764 году», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 2 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sekulyarizatsiya-zemel-solovetskogo-monastyrya-v-1764-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Полянцева, «Секуляризация церковных вотчин в условиях развивающегося абсолютизма. Манифест 1764 года», <i>Вопросы современной юриспруденции</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sekulyarizatsiya-tserkovnyh-votchin-v-usloviyah-razvivayuschegosya-absolyutizma-manifest-1764-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Г. Кузьмина, «Церковно-государственные отношения после 1764 г.», <i>Псков. Научно-практический, историко-краеведческий журнал</i>, no. 55 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tserkovno-gosudarstvennye-otnosheniya-posle-1764-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Г. Кузьмина, «Земли экономических крестьян Псковского уезда после секуляризации 1764 г. по материалам Генерального межевания», <i>Псков. Научно-практический, историко-краеведческий журнал</i>, no. 40 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zemli-ekonomicheskih-krestyan-pskovskogo-uezda-posle-sekulyarizatsii-1764-g-po-materialam-generalnogo-mezhevaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 286–88, 291–92, 299, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","И. А. Иванов, «Деятельность обер-прокуроров Св. Синода в эпоху Екатерины II», <i>Вестник ПСТГУ. Серия 2: История. История Русской Православной Церкви</i>, no. 3 (2004): 160–76, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deyatelnost-ober-prokurorov-sv-sinoda-v-epohu-ekateriny-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 37, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine first cancel and then complete the seizure of church estates?","a":"Because she needed the clergy's support in the weeks after the coup, so she reversed Peter III's decree in August 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within months she created a commission to prepare a new reform, and the manifesto of 1764 went further than her husband had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the peasants of the church estates gain from the reform?","a":"Because they stopped owing rents and labour to monasteries and instead paid a fixed one and a half roubles a year to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 900,000 of them passed into the status of state peasants, which was freer than that of serfs belonging to a private master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Arseny Matseyevich punished so harshly?","a":"Because he attacked the reform in protests to the Synod and argued that the state had no right to interfere in the affairs of the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Catherine handled the case herself, and he died a prisoner at Reval in 1772.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-347": {"questions":["Striking the empress's knees as he talked, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent the winter of 1773 in St Petersburg, where she told him he worked on paper while she worked on human skin.","In comments written in 1774 on her Instruction, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> concluded that Catherine remained a despot and that only the nation itself could be the true lawgiver."],"abstract":"<b>Denis Diderot</b> was the French philosopher and writer who edited the great <i>Encyclopédie</i> and became the thinker of the Enlightenment most closely tied to Catherine II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Parlement of Paris condemned the <i>Encyclopédie</i> in 1759, Catherine, only nine days after her accession, offered to have its printing finished at Riga, an offer he declined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In April 1765 she bought his library of about 3,000 books for 15,000 livres, left the books with him for life and made him her librarian on 1,000 livres a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That same year she paid him 50 years of the salary in advance, and Voltaire marvelled that the 'Scythians' now rewarded in Paris the philosophy that France itself mistreated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After his death in July 1784 she had his books and manuscripts brought to St Petersburg, where they arrived in November 1785 and were shelved beside Voltaire's library.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Diderot reached St Petersburg in October 1773, as the Pugachev rebellion was spreading, and stayed until March 1774.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On most days he talked with the empress from three in the afternoon until five or six, and in his excitement he struck her knees so often that she complained of bruises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She later recalled telling him that his plans would turn her empire upside down, since he worked only on paper, which bears everything, while she worked on human skin, which is far more sensitive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Back in France he called her the soul of Brutus in the form of Cleopatra, yet in his comments on her Instruction he wrote that she was a despot and that only the nation can be the true lawgiver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He had also discussed serfdom with Princess Dashkova, who persuaded him that freeing the Russian peasants at once would be premature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["John Morley, <i>Diderot and the Encyclopaedists</i>, vol. 2 (London: Macmillan, 1886), 100–111, https://archive.org/details/diderotandencycl02morluoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 102–4, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 300–303, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, vol. 2 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1800), 141–43, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcatharinei02tookuoft. [Open access]","Н. А. Копанев, «„Энциклопедия“ и Россия: к 300-летию со дня рождения Дени Дидро», <i>Наука из первых рук</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/entsiklopediya-i-rossiya-k-300-letiyu-so-dnya-rozhdeniya-deni-didro. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Моряков, «Французские просветители Рейналь и Дидро о самодержавии и крепостном праве в России», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/frantsuzskie-prosvetiteli-reynal-i-didro-o-samoderzhavii-i-krepostnom-prave-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Е. Лупанова, «Екатерина II: франкофил или франкофоб?», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 55 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ekaterina-ii-frankofil-ili-frankofob. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine buy Diderot's library but leave it in Paris?","a":"Because she wanted to help the editor of the <i>Encyclopédie</i>, who was short of money, and to be seen in Europe as a patron of philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By paying him as her librarian she gave him an income while he kept using his books, and the gesture won her lavish praise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Diderot's advice change so little in Russia?","a":"Because Catherine enjoyed his conversation but judged his plans impossible to carry out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She told him that he worked on paper, which accepts everything, while she had to govern real people, and after that their talks turned to literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Diderot call Catherine a despot after praising her so warmly?","a":"Because when he read her Instruction closely he found that it still placed all power in one ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He argued that only a nation can be the true lawgiver and that a sovereign must be the first servant of the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Denis Diderot"},
    "ru-348": {"questions":["Sent to plague-stricken Moscow with full powers in 1771, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set up a health commission and turned monasteries and palaces into hospitals as the epidemic raged.","Learning at the peace talks at Focşani in 1772 that a rival had taken his place, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rushed back to St Petersburg, only to be stopped at its gates."],"abstract":"<b>Grigory Orlov</b> was the Guards officer who became Catherine II's lover before her accession and the leading figure of the coup that brought her to the throne in 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Born in 1734, he was one of five sons of a governor of Novgorod, and all five brothers served in the Guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was wounded three times at the battle of Zorndorf in 1758 without leaving his post, and in 1759 he was one of the officers set to guard a captured Prussian aide-de-camp in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In April 1762 Catherine bore him a son, Aleksey, later the founder of the Bobrinsky family, and that summer he and his brothers led the Guards against Peter III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At her coronation the brothers were made counts, and the makers of the coup shared rewards of 18,000 peasant souls, 180,000 roubles and life pensions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 1764 rumours in St Petersburg even named him as the next hetman of the Cossack Hetmanate in Ukraine, though the empress tried to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In September 1771, with plague killing thousands in Moscow, Catherine sent him there with full powers, and he set up a health commission, turned monasteries and palaces into hospitals and had the clothes of the dead burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Catherine herself counted the coup and the rescue of Moscow from the plague as his two great deeds, and a medal struck for the second showed him as Curtius leaping into the chasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1772 he went with Obreskov to make peace with the Ottomans at Focşani, but the talks stalled, and on hearing that Vasilchikov had replaced him in the empress's favour he hurried back without leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was stopped at the gates of St Petersburg and withdrew to his estate at Gatchina, and he finally left court in return for a large pension, an estate and the title of prince of the Holy Roman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 144–45, 430–31, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, vol. 2 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1800), 70–79, 88–90, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcatharinei02tookuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 26, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","О. П. Ведьмин, «Документы РГВИА рассказывают: учился ли Григорий Григорьевич Орлов в Сухопутном шляхетном кадетском корпусе», <i>СибСкрипт</i>, no. 2 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dokumenty-rgvia-rasskazyvayut-uchilsya-li-grigoriy-grigorievich-orlov-v-suhoputnom-shlyahetnom-kadetskom-korpuse. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Круглова, «Был ли Г. Г. Орлов реальным кандидатом в малороссийские гетманы в 1764 г.?», <i>Историческое обозрение</i>, no. 9 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byl-li-g-g-orlov-realnym-kandidatom-v-malorossiyskie-getmany-v-1764-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. И. Якушев, «„…Доколе сии главнейшие артикулы не утверждены будут, действия оружия никак не перестанут“: Фокшаны — Бухарест — Кючук-Кайнарджа», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 7 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dokole-sii-glavneyshie-artikuly-ne-utverzhdeny-budut-deystviya-oruzhiya-nikak-ne-perestanut-fokshany-buharest-kyuchuk-kaynardzha. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Е. Приказчикова, «Фаворитизм как культурно-исторический и литературный феномен России второй половины XVIII века», <i>Сибирский филологический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/favoritizm-kak-kulturno-istoricheskiy-i-literaturnyy-fenomen-rossii-vtoroy-poloviny-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Orlov brothers so important in 1762?","a":"Because the throne was won by the Guards regiments, and the five Orlov brothers were popular, fearless officers in those regiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grigory was already Catherine's lover, and the brothers led the plot that overthrew Peter III, for which they were made counts and richly rewarded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine send Orlov to Moscow during the plague?","a":"Because earlier measures had failed and the city needed a man of authority to calm the people and enforce the rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He arrived with full powers, banned gatherings and set up hospitals, and Catherine later named the rescue of Moscow as one of his two great deeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Orlov lose his place at court in 1772?","a":"Because while he was away at the peace talks his enemies at court promoted a younger rival, Vasilchikov, whom the empress came to favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Orlov abandoned the talks and raced back, but he was stopped at the city gates and eventually agreed to leave in return for rich rewards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Grigory Orlov"},
    "ru-349": {"questions":["Catherine gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the title 'of Taurida' and built him a palace in St Petersburg after he had secured the Crimea and its coast for her empire.","Laid on a carpet beside the road from Iași in October 1791, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died on the steppe while Ottoman envoys arrived to negotiate the end of another war."],"abstract":"<b>Grigory Potemkin</b> was Catherine II's favourite, partner and greatest minister, the statesman who governed Russia's new southern lands from the 1770s until his death in 1791.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in 1739 on a small estate near Smolensk to a family of modest fortune, he did much from a junior position to win support for the coup that brought Catherine to the throne in 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became her favourite in 1774, and her letters to him are so intimate that some writers state that the two were secretly married, though no proof of a wedding survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1774 he was given charge of New Russia, the Azov province and the Dnieper defensive line, and from 1775 he ruled the region as governor-general, answerable only to the empress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was also vice-president and from 1783 president of the College of War, commanding the troops of the southern frontier and later the Black Sea Fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1778 he laid out the port of Kherson near the mouth of the Dnieper, and after the annexation of the Crimea in 1783 work went ahead on a naval base at Akhtiar that became Sevastopol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He drew settlers from Russia and abroad, and the population of the Yekaterinoslav and Kherson provinces rose from 154,000 to 357,000 in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Critics mocked the haste of his schemes, such as the planned city of Yekaterinoslav with a university, factories and a cathedral larger than St Peter's, many of which were never finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For his work in the Crimea Catherine gave him the surname Tavrichesky, 'of Taurida', and built for him the Taurida Palace in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commanding the army in the war that began in 1787, he fell ill at Iași during the peace talks and died on the steppe on 5 October 1791 by the Julian calendar, laid on a carpet beside the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Memoirs of Prince Potemkin, Field-Marshal and Commander-in-Chief of the Russian Army</i>, translated from the German, 2nd ed. (London: Henry Colburn, 1813), 9–10, 15–17, 85–86, 254–55, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincep00helbuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 279–81, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","В. Г. Науменко, «„…Везде один за всех…“ Памяти Светлейшего Князя Потемкина-Таврического», <i>Горизонты гуманитарного знания</i>, no. 2 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vezde-odin-za-vseh-pamyati-svetleyshego-knyazya-potemkina-tavricheskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. И. Елисеева, «Правительственная политика по переселению в Крым и Новороссию во второй половине XVIII века», <i>Историческое обозрение</i>, no. 7 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravitelstvennaya-politika-po-pereseleniyu-v-krym-i-novorossiyu-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Белова, «Оборона морских рубежей Новороссии (1775–1787 гг.)», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, no. 2 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oborona-morskih-rubezhey-novorossii-1775-1787-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Е. Лупанова, «„Греческий проект“ Екатерины Великой», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 65 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grecheskiy-proekt-ekateriny-velikoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Е. Приказчикова, «Фаворитизм как культурно-исторический и литературный феномен России второй половины XVIII века», <i>Сибирский филологический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/favoritizm-kak-kulturno-istoricheskiy-i-literaturnyy-fenomen-rossii-vtoroy-poloviny-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Potemkin hold so much power?","a":"Because he combined the empress's personal trust with offices that gave him control of a whole region and its armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As governor-general he answered only to Catherine, and as head of the College of War he commanded the troops of the southern frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he found new towns and ports in the south?","a":"Because the lands won from the Ottomans and the Crimea had to be settled, defended and given a fleet on the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kherson and Sevastopol were to be the bases of that fleet, and settlers were brought in so fast that the population of the new provinces more than doubled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why have his projects been both praised and mocked?","a":"Because he achieved a great deal in a short time but also announced plans far beyond what could be built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city of Yekaterinoslav was to have a university and a cathedral larger than St Peter's, yet much of it stayed on paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Grigory Potemkin"},
    "ru-350": {"questions":["Victories at Larga, Kagul and Chesma in 1770 marked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Catherine fought against the Ottoman Empire while her Legislative Commission was sent home.","A Cossack raid on the border town of Balta, where Polish confederates had taken refuge, gave the Ottomans their pretext for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Catherine's Russia."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774</b> was the first of Catherine II's two wars against the Ottoman Empire, fought on the Danube, in the Crimea, in the Caucasus and in the Aegean Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of Russia's grip on Poland, where nobles of the Confederation of Bar had risen against Russian influence and looked to the Ottomans for help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The spark came when Cossack irregulars in Russian service stormed the Ottoman border town of Balta, where Polish confederates had taken refuge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sultan Mustafa III answered by imprisoning the Russian envoy Obreskov in the Seven Towers on 25 September 1768 by the Julian calendar, 6 October by the Gregorian, which by Ottoman custom meant war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war led Catherine to send home the deputies of her Legislative Commission, whose general sittings were suspended in December 1768.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In 1770 the army of Rumyantsev routed Ottoman and Tatar forces at Larga and at Kagul, near the Pruth, where his far smaller force broke the grand vizier's army with the bayonet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That summer a Baltic squadron that had sailed round Europe burned the Ottoman fleet at Chesma, and in 1771 Russian troops occupied the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peace talks at Focşani in 1772 and at Bucharest in 1772 and 1773 broke down, above all because the sultan would not accept independence for the Crimean Khanate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In June 1774 Suvorov's corps of 8,000 men scattered an Ottoman army of 40,000 at Kozludzha, and the Porte at last gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war ended with the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca, signed on 10 July 1774 by the Julian calendar, which among much else gave Russian merchant ships free passage from the Black Sea to the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Н. М. Миловский, «О недостаточно исследованных предпосылках и причинах русско-турецкой войны 1768–1774 гг. в отечественной историографии», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-nedostatochno-issledovannyh-predposylkah-i-prichinah-russko-turetskoy-voyny-1768-1774-gg-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. И. Якушев, «„…Доколе сии главнейшие артикулы не утверждены будут, действия оружия никак не перестанут“: Фокшаны — Бухарест — Кючук-Кайнарджа», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 7 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dokole-sii-glavneyshie-artikuly-ne-utverzhdeny-budut-deystviya-oruzhiya-nikak-ne-perestanut-fokshany-buharest-kyuchuk-kaynardzha. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. К. Кожолянко, «Русско-турецкая война 1768–1774 гг. и ликвидация турецкого господства в северной части Буковины», <i>Русин</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1768-1774-gg-i-likvidatsiya-turetskogo-gospodstva-v-severnoy-chasti-bukoviny. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Э. Халаева, «Политика России на Северном Кавказе в годы русско-турецкой войны 1768–1774 гг.», <i>Гуманитарные, социально-экономические и общественные науки</i>, no. 10-1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politika-rossii-na-severnom-kavkaze-v-gody-russko-turetskoy-voyny-1768-1774-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 19–20, 40, 256–57, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","William Tooke, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, vol. 2 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1800), 34–36, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcatharinei02tookuoft. [Open access]","А. С. Верхогляд, «Правовой статус депутатов „Комиссии об уложении“ (1767)», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-deputatov-komissii-ob-ulozhenii-1767. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Ottoman Empire go to war with Russia in 1768?","a":"Because Russian power in Poland alarmed the Porte, which was also urged on by French diplomats and by the Polish confederates who had fled to its lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Cossacks in Russian service stormed Balta, the sultan had the Russian envoy imprisoned, which by Ottoman custom meant war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war last six years despite Russia's early victories?","a":"Because the Ottomans would not accept independence for the Crimean Khanate, so peace talks at Focşani and Bucharest both broke down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only after Suvorov's victory at Kozludzha in 1774 did the Porte agree to terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war matter for Catherine's reforms at home?","a":"Because it ended the general sittings of the Legislative Commission in December 1768, and the full commission never met again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its committees worked on until 1774, the year the war ended, but no new code came of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)"},
    "ru-301": {"questions":["Drafted by Feofan Prokopovich and revised article by article by Peter, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> created a Spiritual College that opened in February 1721 as the Most Holy Governing Synod.","Some twenty years after Patriarch Adrian died and no successor was chosen, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the patriarchate in law, and the office stayed empty until November 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>Spiritual Regulation</b> was the church statute that Peter I confirmed by a manifesto of 25 January 1721 by the Julian calendar, setting up a Spiritual College as the supreme body governing the Russian Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was drafted on the tsar's commission by Feofan Prokopovich, a Kyiv-educated bishop of Pskov, although Peter revised every article and rewrote some of them himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The text fell into three parts, giving the reasons for governing the Church by a college, the matters the new body would judge, and its membership and working rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also charged the college with rooting out ignorance and superstition, and told every bishop to keep a school for priests' sons at his residence, with a teacher both able and honest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the college opened on 14 February 1721 it took the name of the Most Holy Governing Synod, and from 1722 a lay over-procurator watched its business on the sovereign's behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Regulation was also the legal end of the patriarchate, an office left empty since Patriarch Adrian died in October 1700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peter had chosen no successor but put Stefan Yavorsky, metropolitan of Ryazan, in charge of the Church as a stopgap, and at first there was no plan to abolish the office at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Regulation's own case was political: ordinary people, it said, could not tell spiritual from autocratic power and might take a dazzling patriarch for a second sovereign, equal to the tsar or greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a quarrel between the two they might follow the patriarch in the belief that they fought for God's cause, a peril that the Nikon affair of the previous reign had already shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yavorsky, made the Synod's first president against his own wishes, still held a patriarch to be the true form of church government, and the office was restored only in November 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 143, 395–97, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 218, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","А. А. Назаров, «Религиозная политика Петра Великого: от симфонии властей к абсолютизму», <i>Вестник РМАТ</i>, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/religioznaya-politika-petra-velikogo-ot-simfonii-vlastey-k-absolyutizmu. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. И. Кизюн, «Духовный регламент Петра I и духовное образование в России XVIII века», <i>Новосибирский временник</i>, no. 1 (5) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/duhovnyy-reglament-petra-i-i-duhovnoe-obrazovanie-v-rossii-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Бондаренко and Е. О. Тулупова, «Государство и церковь в России в период синодального управления», <i>Аграрное и земельное право</i>, no. 10 (238) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvo-i-tserkov-v-rossii-v-period-sinodalnogo-upravleniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Д. Граур, «Социально-правовой статус Русской православной церкви в период перехода от централизованного Московского государства XVII в. к Российской империи XVIII в.», <i>Право и практика</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialno-pravovoy-status-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkovi-v-period-perehoda-ot-tsentralizovannogo-moskovskogo-gosudarstva-xvii-v-k. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Regulation call a single patriarch dangerous?","a":"Because, it said, ordinary people could not separate spiritual from autocratic power and might treat a splendid patriarch as a second sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a dispute with the tsar they might follow him in the belief that they were defending God's cause, so a council of clergy seemed safer than one chief pastor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was no patriarch chosen after Adrian died in 1700?","a":"Because Peter preferred to postpone the election, and he gave the running of the Church to Stefan Yavorsky as a temporary measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Church was then placed under the Senate in 1711, and Feofan Prokopovich was later commissioned to design a governing college instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What else did the Regulation ask of the Church?","a":"It asked for education: the college was to root out ignorance and superstition, and each bishop was to keep a school for the sons of priests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its plan for such schools ran from grammar, geography and history up to two years of theology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-302": {"questions":["A contemporary complained that a soul is a thing impalpable that cannot be valued, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made each counted male the unit on which Peter's army was paid.","Ordered by a decree of November 1718, the census of every male peasant prepared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which replaced a household levy that crowded families had learned to dodge."],"abstract":"The <b>soul tax</b>, or poll tax, was the direct tax that Peter I laid on every male 'soul' of the tax-paying classes, first collected in 1724 and kept in Russia until 1 January 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It replaced the old household tax, which had yielded less and less as peasant families crowded several to a homestead to escape it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nobles and clergy were exempt, and so were the Baltic provinces, the Bashkirs and the Lapps, so the weight fell chiefly on peasants and townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Landowners' serfs paid 74 kopecks a head, state peasants 40 kopecks more in place of the dues a lord would have taken, and townspeople 1 rouble 20 kopecks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1724 the new tax already made up about 54 percent of the state's assessed revenue of 4.7 million roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> It rested on a census of every male peasant, from the oldest man to the newest-born boy, which the Senate ordered on 26 November 1718 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peter warned that any souls a landowner concealed would be handed to whoever reported them, and soldiers were used to check the returns, which took until 1724 to complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The rate was first worked out at 95 kopecks and then lowered to 74, once the count of souls, between five and six million, could be set against the cost of keeping the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because masters answered for their peasants' tax and recruits, and household slaves were entered on the lists beside serfs, the census tightened the hold of landowners over the people on their estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Contemporaries found the unit baffling: the peasant writer Pososhkov called a soul a thing impalpable that nobody could value, and villagers soon spoke of halves and quarters of a soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 138–40, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 370–71, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","Д. А. Смирнов, «Реформирование и совершенствование принципов налогообложения в эпоху Петра I», <i>Общество и право</i>, no. 5 (42) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reformirovanie-i-sovershenstvovanie-printsipov-nalogooblozheniya-v-epohu-petra-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Бегунова, «Понятие «подушная подать» в контексте изучения налоговой системы Российской империи XVIII – первой половины XIX вв.», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ponyatie-podushnaya-podat-v-kontekste-izucheniya-nalogovoy-sistemy-rossiyskoy-imperii-xviii-pervoy-poloviny-xix-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Талина, «Россия на пути к рекрутской повинности и подушному налогообложению», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, 14, no. 2 (2023): 26–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-na-puti-k-rekrutskoy-povinnosti-i-podushnomu-nalogooblozheniyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Репин, «Историческое развитие налоговых отношений в России в XVIII веке», <i>Юридическая наука и практика: Вестник Нижегородской академии МВД России</i>, no. 20 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskoe-razvitie-nalogovyh-otnosheniy-v-rossii-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter abandon the household tax?","a":"Because it no longer paid for his army: the number of taxed homesteads kept falling as several peasant families crowded into one to pay less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A new household count showed alarming losses against that of 1678, so a census of male souls was ordered instead in 1718.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did state peasants pay more than serfs?","a":"Because they had no lord to pay. On top of the basic 74 kopecks they owed 40 kopecks to the treasury in place of the dues a serf rendered to his master, which made the state their landlord in effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the census tighten serfdom?","a":"Because it entered household slaves and peasants together as the people of their masters, and made the masters answer for both the tax and the recruits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The counted soul thus became the basis of both taxation and the filling of the army, a link that lasted well into the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-303": {"questions":["A rule of 1712 had the men taken by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> branded with a cross on the left hand, much as convicts were, to stop them from deserting.","Levies that drew some 300,000 men into Peter's army in the first decade of the Swedish war grew into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted in Russia until 1874."],"abstract":"The <b>recruit levy</b> was the system by which Peter I filled his regular army and navy, ordering each community to hand over a set number of men who then served for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of forced levies of 'given men' that began in 1699, took its basic shape with Peter's decree of 20 February 1705, and was settled into the machinery of state by 1711.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state named only the number of recruits, usually one for every twenty taxpaying households, and left each community to decide which of its men to send.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Recruits were gathered at muster points, trained in batches of 500 to 1,000 and sent on wherever regiments had gaps, and a man who died or deserted was to be replaced from his own district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1711 recruits also went first to garrison regiments for basic training before joining the field army, while new recruits took their places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The burden was heavy: some 300,000 men were drawn into the army in the first ten years of the Great Northern War, from a population of about fourteen million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evasion and flight followed, and by 1712 the government counted 45,000 men who had failed to appear when called and 30,000 deserters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A War Department rule of 1712 ordered recruits to be branded like convicts, with a cross pricked into the left hand and covered with gunpowder that was set alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legend even claimed that the tsar, in contempt for the old faith, was marking his soldiers with the sign of Antichrist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1719 the counted male soul became the unit both for the new poll tax and for the levy, and recruitment on this basis lasted in Russia until 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 64–65, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (New York: D. Appleton, 1897), 184, https://archive.org/details/petergreat00walirich. [Open access]","И. А. Демина, «Основные аспекты введения рекрутской повинности в России в начале XVIII века», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 10 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osnovnye-aspekty-vvedeniya-rekrutskoy-povinnosti-v-rossii-v-nachale-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Шишов, «Военные реформы Петра Великого в ходе Северной войны 1700–1721 гг.», <i>Перспективы. Электронный журнал</i>, no. 4 (27) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennye-reformy-petra-velikogo-v-hode-severnoy-voyny-1700-1721-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Талина, «Россия на пути к рекрутской повинности и подушному налогообложению», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, 14, no. 2 (2023): 26–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-na-puti-k-rekrutskoy-povinnosti-i-podushnomu-nalogooblozheniyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Р. Славнитский, «Рекрутские наборы 1710–1711 гг. и распределение рекрутов в гарнизоны Северо-Запада России», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, 26, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rekrutskie-nabory-1710-1711-gg-i-raspredelenie-rekrutov-v-garnizony-severo-zapada-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter need a new way of raising soldiers?","a":"Because disease, desertion and long marches kept shrinking his army while the war spread to new fronts, and volunteers and 'given men' could not fill the gaps fast enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Levies by a fixed quota let the state count on a steady flow of recruits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many men try to escape the levy?","a":"Because a recruit seldom came home: service was for life, so a levy took a man from his family and village for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1712 the government counted 45,000 men who had not appeared and 30,000 deserters, and branding recruits did not stop the flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the levy linked to the census of souls?","a":"From 1719 the counted male soul became the unit both for the new poll tax and for raising recruits, so one register served the treasury and the army alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recruitment on that basis lasted until 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-304": {"questions":["Younger sons left landless by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could buy land only after seven years in the army, ten in the civil service or fifteen in trade.","Resented by the nobility and evaded wherever possible, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was repealed under Empress Anna in 1731, and estates once again went in equal shares to all sons."],"abstract":"The <b>Decree on Single Inheritance</b> was Peter I's law of 23 March 1714 by the Julian calendar, which forbade landowners to divide their estates and made all their immovable property pass to a single heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A father could leave his land to whichever son he chose, not necessarily the eldest, while his movable goods went to his other children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only when there was no will did the land go to the eldest son, and a man who was the last of his line could leave it to a kinswoman if her husband took the family's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law also merged the two old forms of noble land, the service estate and the hereditary estate, into one kind of property held under the same rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Landowners resented it and tried many ways round it, and it was repealed under Empress Anna in 1731, when estates again went in equal shares to all sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Peter gave his reasons in the decree itself: one undivided estate would pay its taxes more reliably, and its master could ease his peasants instead of ruining them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Undivided land would also keep noble houses great and glorious instead of letting them sink, share by share, into the condition of plain smallholders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above all, younger sons with no land would no longer live idly on their portion but would have to seek their bread in state service, teaching, trade or a craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To push them on, a younger son could buy land of his own only after seven years in the army, ten in the civil service or fifteen in trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law thus bound the nobles more tightly to service, and rather than widening their privileges it cut them, since owners of hereditary estates lost their old freedom to dispose of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 85–89, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 365–66, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","С. И. Пудина and И. М. Горбачева, «Отношение дворянства к Указу о единонаследии 1714 г.», <i>Экономические и социально-гуманитарные исследования</i>, no. 3 (35) (2022): 191–97, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnoshenie-dvoryanstva-k-ukazu-o-edinonasledii-1714-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Лонская, «Указ о единонаследии 1714 г.: попытка неоинституциональной интерпретации», <i>Историко-правовые проблемы: новый ракурс</i>, no. 13 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ukaz-o-edinonasledii-1714-g-popytka-neoinstitutsionalnoy-interpretatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Черникова, ««Обратная сторона» петровских реформ», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, 20, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obratnaya-storona-petrovskih-reform. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Чеснокова and К. И. Потешкин, «Возникновение и развитие наследственного права в России», <i>Наука. Общество. Государство</i>, 6, no. 4 (24) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozniknovenie-i-razvitie-nasledstvennogo-prava-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter want one heir instead of many?","a":"Because, as the decree argued, divided estates shrank with every generation until noble families sank into poverty, and many small masters squeezed their peasants harder than one large one would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One undivided estate, he claimed, would also pay its taxes more reliably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the law bring in rule by the eldest son?","a":"Not in the strict sense, because the eldest son had no automatic right: a father could leave the land to any son he chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eldest inherited only when there was no will, so the law created sole succession rather than inheritance by the firstborn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the decree repealed so soon after Peter's death?","a":"Because it ran against Russian custom and set brothers against one another, and landowners looked for every way round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Empress Anna a decree of 1731 went back to dividing property in equal shares among all sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-305": {"questions":["Bound to a works rather than to its owner, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave merchants who were not nobles a hold over bonded labour that had belonged to the nobility alone.","On many Ural works the state kept formal title to land and people, while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> living there could change hands only together with the factory itself."],"abstract":"<b>Possessional peasants</b> were serfs bought for or attached to a factory or works, who belonged to the enterprise itself rather than to its owner as a private person, a category that began under Peter I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their origin was a decree of 18 January 1721 by the Julian calendar, which let owners of factories buy villages of serfs, a right reserved until then for nobles, provided the villages served the works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such villages were to belong to the works inalienably, and an owner could neither sell nor mortgage the peasants apart from the factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name comes from possession, a legal title by which a works and its people could be held without full ownership, often with help from the treasury in land, forests, ores or labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On private works held in this way the property formally remained the state's, and the serfs could be bought and sold only together with the enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The decree was part of a wider drive in which Peter built works at the treasury's cost, handed many to merchants and loaded owners with privileges, leaving 233 factories and works at his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Labour was the great shortage, and state and private works alike leaned on 'ascribed' peasants, villagers assigned by the state to work for a factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1722 Peter even forbade the return of factory hands to their masters though they were runaway serfs, which shows how highly he ranked industry against the claims of landowners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians have called the result a new kind of serfdom, in which the peasant was parted from the land and made a labourer bound to a manufactory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Merchant factory owners thus gained a power over bonded workers that had belonged to the nobility alone, and possessional works, common in the Urals, lasted well into the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 117–18, 121, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 370, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","В. И. Афанасьева, «Нормативно-правовые акты в мануфактурном производстве Петра I (окончание)», <i>Право и государство: теория и практика</i>, no. 2 (206) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/normativno-pravovye-akty-v-manufakturnom-proizvodstve-petra-i-okonchanie. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Абрамова and Е. Д. Ведерникова, «Развитие крепостного права в XVIII веке», <i>Огарёв-Online</i>, no. 12 (197) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razvitie-krepostnogo-prava-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Б. Шайхисламов, «Заводские крестьяне Южного Урала до освобождения от крепостной зависимости», <i>Российский гуманитарный журнал</i>, 4, no. 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zavodskie-krestyane-yuzhnogo-urala-do-osvobozhdeniya-ot-krepostnoy-zavisimosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. А. Муравьева, «Российское промышленное предпринимательство в первой четверти XVIII века», <i>Финансы и кредит</i>, no. 29 (269) (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskoe-promyshlennoe-predprinimatelstvo-v-pervoy-chetverti-xviii-veka-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter let merchants own serfs?","a":"Because he treated running a factory as a service to the state, and labour was desperately short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Letting owners buy villages for their works, on condition that the villages stayed with the works, gave industry a fixed workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the peasants not be sold separately?","a":"Because they belonged to the enterprise rather than to the owner as a private person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The owner could neither sell nor mortgage them apart from the factory, so they passed with it from one owner to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the decree fit Peter's wider industrial policy?","a":"It was one privilege among many: the state built works at its own cost, lent money without interest, bought goods at high prices and trained workers in its own factories before handing them to private owners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By Peter's death some 233 factories and works were operating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-306": {"questions":["Ennobled by Peter in 1720 after long refusing honours, the Tula gunsmith <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded a family of ironmasters who were for long the real rulers of the Urals.","By 1713 the Moscow warehouses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held half a million hand grenades, part of the guns and munitions he had contracted to supply to Peter's armies."],"abstract":"<b>Nikita Demidov</b> was a Tula gunsmith who became the greatest ironmaster of Peter I's Russia and founded the Demidov dynasty of Ural industrialists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was born in 1656 into a family of smiths whose surname the documents mostly give as Antyufeev, and he began as a blacksmith making arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He rose among the wealthy gunsmiths of Tula who filled large state orders, and won the tsar's favour by supplying weapons at low prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stories of how Peter discovered the skilled smith are many and contradict one another, but that the two men knew each other is not in doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter granted him noble rank under the name Demidov on 21 September 1720, an honour he accepted only after much persuasion, and he died on 17 November 1725 by the Julian calendar, months after the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His fortune was made in the Urals, where the tsar had iron ore surveyed and a state ironworks built at Nevyansk on the Neiva river in the Verkhoturye district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By a decree of 4 March 1702 Peter handed the Nevyansk works to Demidov, who undertook to supply cannon, mortars, bombs and grenades at prices below those of foreign makers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His son Akinfy went out with a dozen of the best Tula craftsmen, and the first blast furnace was lit within days of his arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1713 Demidov's Moscow warehouses held 500,000 hand grenades, and by the end of Peter's reign five of the twelve private works in the region run from Yekaterinburg were his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The family became the true rulers of the Urals, and for long the people of their works disliked the name so much that hardly any place there was called after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 121–22, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","М. Л. Гавлин, <i>Из истории российского предпринимательства: династия Демидовых. Научно-аналитический обзор</i> (Москва: ИНИОН РАН, 1998), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-rossiyskogo-predprinimatelstva-dinastiya-demidovyh. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Запарий, «Рецензия на книгу доктора исторических наук И. Н. Юркина «Демидов Первый: опыт жизнеописания»», <i>История и современное мировоззрение</i>, 5, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/retsenziya-na-knigu-doktora-istoricheskih-nauk-i-n-yurkina-demidov-pervyy-opyt-zhizneopisaniya-m-mezhdunarodnyy-demidovskiy-fond. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Мазин, «Заводчики Демидовы – анатомия конкуренции эпохи становления капитализма в России», <i>Российские регионы: взгляд в будущее</i>, no. 1 (6) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zavodchiki-demidovy-anatomiya-konkurentsii-epohi-stanovleniya-kapitalizma-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Коршунов and О. В. Полякова, «Опыт приватизации казенных заводов на Урале в первой половине XVIII века», <i>Вестник университета</i>, no. 8 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/opyt-privatizatsii-kazennyh-zavodov-na-urale-v-pervoy-polovine-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Э. Иванова, «Имена основателей уральской промышленности на карте Урала», <i>Филологический класс</i>, no. 2 (56) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imena-osnovateley-uralskoy-promyshlennosti-na-karte-urala. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter give a state ironworks to a private smith?","a":"Because Demidov had already shown he could make good arms cheaply, and he had tested the Ural ore himself, reporting its iron no worse than Swedish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return for Nevyansk he undertook to supply guns and munitions below the prices of foreign makers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his son Akinfy so important to the works?","a":"Because it was Akinfy, Nikita's eldest son, who went to Nevyansk with the best Tula craftsmen and later became sole master of the family's Ural works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first blast furnace there was lit within days of his arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Demidov name become unpopular in the Urals?","a":"Because the family ruled the region almost as sovereigns, and the people of their works disliked them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Few places in the Demidovs' old lands bear their name, and local stories made the tower at Nevyansk a symbol of their harsh rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nikita Demidov"},
    "ru-307": {"questions":["Under the decree that created the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, hosts were fined if they greeted guests at the door, and the police announced where the next evening would be held.","When Peter ordered every Moscow lady above ten years of age to attend one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1722, only seventy came despite the threat of terrible punishment."],"abstract":"The <b>Petrine assemblies</b> were evening gatherings in private houses that Peter I imposed on the upper ranks of society by a decree issued at the end of 1718.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name came from the French word for a gathering, and Peter took the idea from the Paris salons he had seen on his journey to France in 1717.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ran from four in the afternoon until ten at night, and hosts were forbidden on pain of a fine to meet their guests at the door or to see them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nobles, officials, officers, leading merchants, master shipwrights and craftsmen came with their wives and children, while servants had to stay in the anterooms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dancing, music, chess, pipes and talk of the news filled the evening, and anyone who broke the rules could be made to drain a great beaker of strong wine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Peter's purpose was to draw Russian women into mixed company and to teach the upper classes the social habits of Western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The police ran the system: in Saint Petersburg the chief of police and in Moscow the commandant named the host of each assembly, and guests learned at one gathering where the next would be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dancing came first among the pleasures, and since his subjects did not know the steps, Peter taught them himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moscow resisted hardest, and when Peter came there in 1722 a decree ordered every lady above ten years of age to attend under threat of terrible punishment, yet only seventy came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even so, within three years the assemblies had taken root in the capital, where they broke down the old separation of the social ranks by bringing them together in one room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (New York: D. Appleton, 1897), 420–22, https://archive.org/details/petergreat00walirich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 259, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Г. Ю. Литвинцева, «Новые направления культурно-досуговой деятельности в Петровскую эпоху», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i>, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novye-napravleniya-kulturno-dosugovoy-deyatelnosti-v-petrovskuyu-epohu. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. М. Чирскова, «Власть и культура в России в первой четверти XVIII в.: у истоков культурной политики», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 2 (11) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vlast-i-kultura-v-rossii-v-pervoy-chetverti-xviii-v-u-istokov-kulturnoy-politiki. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Е. Попов, «Опыт применения светских методов культурного преобразования российского государства: на примере «реформы веселья» Петра I», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 1 (197) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/opyt-primeneniya-svetskih-metodov-kulturnogo-preobrazovaniya-rossiyskogo-gosudarstva-na-primere-reformy-veselya-petra-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. С. Коргожа, «Формирование культурно-досуговых традиций российского общества в Петровскую эпоху», <i>Вестник Кемеровского государственного университета культуры и искусств</i>, no. 17-2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-kulturno-dosugovyh-traditsiy-rossiyskogo-obschestva-v-petrovskuyu-epohu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter order people to hold parties?","a":"Because no Russian society in the Western sense existed, and he wanted women to take part in mixed company and the upper classes to learn European manners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assemblies were his tool for both, laid down, as so much else, by decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were hosts forbidden to greet their guests?","a":"Because the rules aimed at ease rather than ceremony: hosts had only to provide rooms, light, refreshments and amusements, and guests came and went as they pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Invitations were not personal but published by the police, so nobody owed the host a formal welcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the assemblies achieve what Peter wanted?","a":"In part. They took root in Saint Petersburg within about three years and mixed the social ranks as never before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> But Russian ladies refused to choose foreign partners, so Peter's hope of blending Russians with the foreigners in his service failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-308": {"questions":["A geometry printed in 1708 was the first book set in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose specimen alphabet survives with corrections in Peter's own hand.","First used among the Serbs in a primer printed at Venice in 1766, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread from Russia to the South Slavs during the 18th and 19th centuries."],"abstract":"The <b>civil script</b> was the simplified Cyrillic alphabet and typeface that Peter I introduced for secular printing in a reform carried out between 1708 and 1710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its letters were brought closer to the Latin type of the day, with less contrast between thick and thin strokes and with straightened curves, yet it remained a Cyrillic face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It set aside several letters taken from Greek that Russian speech did not need, and the first book printed in it, a geometry of 1708, also did without the old marks above the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The punches were cut by craftsmen in Amsterdam, where a Russian press had been at work since about 1700, and a compositor, a printer and a typefounder brought the new letters to Moscow in 1707.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A specimen of the alphabet survives with corrections in Peter's own hand, and he wrote in its margin that books on history and manufactures should be printed in these letters alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its purpose was to split the world of print in two, leaving the old Church Slavonic letters to religious books while everything secular went into the new type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of 1708 ordered secular books to be printed in the civil type, and the geometry was followed by a manual on writing compliments translated from German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Defenders of the old letters objected, and the learned Fyodor Polikarpov held that without the Greek letters the spirit of Orthodoxy could not be conveyed in print.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new books also looked different, smaller in format, without two-colour printing or red capitals, and set in a type more compact than the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From Russia the civil script was carried in the 18th and 19th centuries to the Serbs, Montenegrins, Bulgarians and Macedonians, first appearing among the Serbs in a primer printed at Venice in 1766.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 258, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (New York: D. Appleton, 1897), 433–34, https://archive.org/details/petergreat00walirich. [Open access]","К. В. Никулушкин, «Эстетика шрифта в реформе русской азбуки 1708 года», <i>Филологические науки. Вопросы теории и практики</i>, no. 6-1 (72) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/estetika-shrifta-v-reforme-russkoy-azbuki-1708-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Хораш, «Проблема удобочитаемости русского печатного шрифта в XVIII веке», <i>Вестник Московского государственного университета печати</i>, no. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-udobochitaemosti-russkogo-pechatnogo-shrifta-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Ермолаева, «Книжная культура России в контексте развития культуры и просвещения XVIII века», <i>Научная периодика: проблемы и решения</i>, 6, no. 3 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/knizhnaya-kultura-rossii-v-kontekste-razvitiya-kultury-i-prosvescheniya-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Гудков, «Русская азбука на славянском Юге», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, 1997 (1998), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-azbuka-na-slavyanskom-yuge. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter want a new alphabet?","a":"Because he wanted a type for secular learning apart from the Church's letters: books on history and manufactures, he wrote, should be printed in the new letters alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dividing the alphabet in two set the secular world of print apart from the religious one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the reform change in the letters?","a":"It set aside letters taken from Greek that Russian did not need, such as psi and xi, and gave up the old marks written above the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The remaining letters were redrawn with straighter lines and less contrast between thick and thin strokes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some contemporaries object to the reform?","a":"Because they tied the old letters to the faith: Fyodor Polikarpov held that without the Greek letters the spirit of Orthodoxy could not be expressed in print.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reform's clean split between church and civil printing gave such fears substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-309": {"questions":["Hermann, Delisle and Bernoulli were soon drawn to Saint Petersburg by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which its first president called neither a true university nor a French-style academy but a mixture of both.","Russia marks 8 February as the Day of Russian Science because on that Gregorian date in 1724 Peter founded what is now the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a university attached."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Academy of Sciences</b> traces its foundation to a decree announced from the Senate on 28 January 1724 by the Julian calendar, 8 February by the Gregorian, which set up an academy at Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its upkeep was charged on the customs and licence revenues of four Baltic towns, Narva, Dorpat, Pernau and Arensburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter's plan joined an academy of learned men to a university and a gymnasium, so that the academicians would also lecture as professors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Laurentius Blumentrost, Peter's physician and the first president, admitted that it was meant to be neither a true university nor a French-style academy but a mixture of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It met in public for the first time on 27 December 1725, after Peter's death, and grew to include a library, the Kunstkamera museum, an observatory, a press and workshops, for until 1803 it was an academy of sciences and arts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The idea had a long history, for Leibniz pressed the tsar to found an academy of sciences, hoping to become its president himself, and urged it again when they met at Pyrmont.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter's stay in Paris deepened his wish for an academy of his own, but he moved slowly until, in 1724, he wrote on a report the words 'Found an academy'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With few Russians ready for advanced study, the first academicians came from abroad, among them the mathematician Hermann, the astronomer Delisle and Daniel Bernoulli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As a school it achieved little at first, having almost no students able to follow such lectures, but as a learned society it served science and Russia well, not least in mapping the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia now marks 8 February, the Gregorian date of the founding decree, as the Day of Russian Science, established by a presidential decree of 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (New York: D. Appleton, 1897), 432–33, https://archive.org/details/petergreat00walirich. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 216–17, 290, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]","В. В. Окрепилов, «Заметки об истории создания в 1724 году Российской академии наук и Санкт-Петербургского университета», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Экономика</i>, 38, no. 2 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zametki-ob-istorii-sozdaniya-v-1724-godu-rossiyskoy-akademii-nauk-i-sankt-peterburgskogo-universiteta. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. А. Ростовцев, «Академия наук и Санкт-Петербургский университет – к истории общего юбилея», <i>Журнал Российского национального комитета по истории и философии науки и техники</i>, 2, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/akademiya-nauk-i-sankt-peterburgskiy-universitet-k-istorii-obschego-yubileya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Костина, ««Медицынской факультет совершенно из втораго класса… сочинен быть может»: преподавание медицины в Петербургской академии наук в 1725–1732 гг.», <i>Труды Санкт-Петербургского института истории РАН</i>, no. 5 (21) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/meditsynskoy-fakultet-sovershenno-iz-vtorago-klassa-sochinen-byt-mozhet-prepodavanie-meditsiny-v-peterburgskoy-akademii-nauk-v-1725-1732. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Келлер, ««Художества» в Петербургской академии наук в 1725–1803 годы: на стыке наук, искусств и технологий», <i>Социология науки и технологий</i>, 14, no. 4 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hudozhestva-v-peterburgskoy-akademii-nauk-v-1725-1803-gody-na-styke-nauk-iskusstv-i-tehnologiy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. В. Шелехова and Е. Е. Оконешников, «О дате образования Российской Академии наук», <i>Наука и техника в Якутии</i>, no. 1 (2) (2002), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-date-obrazovaniya-rossiyskoy-akademii-nauk. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the academy include a university and a gymnasium?","a":"Because Russia had too few people ready for advanced learning, so Peter's plan made the academicians teach as professors and attached schools to train future scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea of a university inside the academy was suggested to Peter by the German professor Christian Wolff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the first academicians foreigners?","a":"Because Russia had no scholars of that rank, so men such as Hermann, Delisle and Bernoulli were invited from abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their students could hardly follow them at first, but their research, such as Delisle's work on Russian maps, was of real value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the academy open only after Peter died?","a":"Because Peter settled the plan only a year before his death in 1725, after years of slow preparation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of Catherine I in December 1725 named Blumentrost president, and the first public session followed on 27 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Academy of Sciences"},
    "ru-310": {"questions":["Peter's purchase of the Dutch anatomist Ruysch's preparations in 1717 gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the jars of specimens for which most of its visitors still remember it.","A decree of February 1718 offered rewards for monstrous births and strange old finds sent to the capital, swelling the collections of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Peter had founded four years earlier."],"abstract":"The <b>Kunstkamera</b> was the museum that Peter I founded in Saint Petersburg in 1714, the first state museum in Russia, which later became the museum of the Academy of Sciences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began when the tsar's own collections and those of the Apothecary Chancery were brought from Moscow to the Summer Palace on the Fontanka, where a room now called the Green Cabinet stood ready for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Part of the growing collection was later shown in the Kikin Palace, but the plan was fully realised only in a purpose-built home on Vasilyevsky Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That building, with its tower, opened under Peter's grandson Peter II in 1728 and still houses the Museum of Anthropology and Ethnography named after Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fire on 5 December 1747 ended the universal museum in all but name, and in 1836 its holdings were divided among separate specialist museums of the Academy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The museum mirrored the tastes of its founder, who would visit a city's cabinet of curiosities by torchlight in the middle of the night, as he did at Dresden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On his travels Peter bought whole collections, such as that of a Dr Gottwald during a stay at Danzig, and above all the preparations of the Dutch anatomist Frederik Ruysch, purchased in 1717.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A decree of 13 February 1718 ordered monstrous births to be sent to Saint Petersburg for a reward, with a heavy fine for hiding them, and strange old objects found in the earth to be sold to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter's plan for the Academy of Sciences gave the academicians the library and the 'chamber of natural things', and the museum's specimens were put to use in research and teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The academicians rearranged Ruysch's preparations by organ in cupboards for study, setting aside the decorative display that had made them famous, yet for most visitors the jars remain what the museum is known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["А. В. Головнёв, Н. П. Копанева, Д. А. Куканов and Е. В. Перевалова, «Петровская Кунсткамера, или Башня знаний: подходы и решения», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 4 (18) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petrovskaya-kunstkamera-ili-bashnya-znaniy-podhody-i-resheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Д. Кареева, «История Зеленого кабинета в Летнем дворце Петра I как кабинета редкостей», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 4 (10) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-zelenogo-kabineta-v-letnem-dvortse-petra-i-kak-kabineta-redkostey. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ф. Хартанович, «Музеи Академии наук XIX в.: от Кунсткамеры к узкоспециальным научным центрам», <i>Вопросы музеологии</i>, no. 2 (12) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/muzei-akademii-nauk-xix-v-ot-kunstkamery-k-uzkospetsialnym-nauchnym-tsentram. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Хартанович, «Анатомическая коллекция Кунсткамеры Императорской Академии наук: от века осьмнадцатаго в век девятнадцатый», <i>Историко-биологические исследования</i>, 11, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anatomicheskaya-kollektsiya-kunstkamery-imperatorskoy-akademii-nauk-ot-veka-osmnadtsatago-v-vek-devyatnadtsatyy. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>Peter the Great</i>, trans. Lady Mary Loyd (New York: D. Appleton, 1897), 130, https://archive.org/details/petergreat00walirich. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1884), 281, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027034697. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter want monstrous births sent to the capital?","a":"Because he wanted natural rarities gathered for study, and a decree of 1718 paid a reward for such births and fined anyone who concealed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same decree asked for unusual stones, old bones, inscriptions and weapons found in the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the museum become part of the Academy of Sciences?","a":"Because Peter's plan for the Academy gave the academicians a library and a 'chamber of natural things' to work with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kunstkamera thus became the Academy's museum, and its collections served research and teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Ruysch's specimens so famous?","a":"Because his method of injecting blood vessels kept the preparations looking almost lifelike, and he decorated them with fabrics, ornaments and artificial eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peter bought the collection in 1717, and it became a symbol of the museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kunstkamera"},
    "ru-311": {"questions":["Condemned by a court of senators and officials in June 1718, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died two days later in the Peter and Paul Fortress, officially of a sudden fit like apoplexy.","Married in 1711 to Charlotte of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the father of the future Peter II and the eldest son of Peter the Great and Eudoxia Lopukhina."],"abstract":"<b>Tsarevich Alexei</b>, Alexei Petrovich in full, was the eldest son of Peter the Great and his first wife Eudoxia Lopukhina, born in February 1690 and for most of his father's reign the heir to the Russian throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first schooling was a 'mother's school' planned by his grandmother Natalia, who had begun teaching Peter at three rather than five, with picture books and verses by the court poet Karion Istomin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter shut the boy's mother in a convent and paid the son little attention, and older accounts made Alexei a devout reactionary, a picture that recent studies of his trial call a myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1711 he married Princess Charlotte of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, the emperor's sister-in-law, and their son Peter, the future Peter II, was born in October 1715, four days before Charlotte died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter now threatened to cut him off 'as though you were a gangrenous swelling', and Alexei offered to renounce the throne and then to become a monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the autumn of 1716, ordered to join his father abroad, he fled instead to Vienna, where the emperor hid him first in a Tyrolean fortress and then in the castle of St Elmo at Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter Tolstoy coaxed him home in 1717 with Peter's sworn promise of pardon, and in February 1718 he renounced the succession in Moscow in favour of his infant half-brother Peter Petrovich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inquiry that followed tortured his friends, reached his mother's convent and ended the career of Prince Vasily Dolgorukov, who had sought the heir's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After two floggings with the knout he was condemned to death by a court of senators and officials on 24 June 1718, and two days later he died in the Peter and Paul Fortress, officially of a fit like apoplexy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How he died was never settled, and his death left Peter no male heir but Alexei's small son, while the image of a tsar who killed his heir links Peter with Ivan the Terrible in Russian memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 340–67, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1890), 330, 345–47, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 266, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Е. В. Анисимов, «Документы о роли князя Василия Долгорукова в деле царевича Алексея (1718 г.)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 67, no. 2 (2022): 616–36, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dokumenty-o-roli-knyazya-vasiliya-dolgorukova-v-dele-tsarevicha-alekseya-1718-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Р. Мифтахова и Е. Ю. Федорина, «Судебный процесс первой четверти XVIII века. Суд над царевичем Алексеем Петровичем», <i>Научные записки молодых исследователей</i>, no. 6 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sudebnyy-protsess-pervoy-chetverti-xviii-veka-sud-nad-tsarevichem-alekseem-petrovichem. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «„Материнская школа“ царевича Алексея Петровича», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/materinskaya-shkola-tsarevicha-alekseya-petrovicha. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. С. Артамонов и С. В. Тихонова, «Убить сына: смерть наследников престола в механике медиазабвения», <i>Практики и интерпретации</i> 5, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ubit-syna-smert-naslednikov-prestola-v-mehanike-mediazabveniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexei flee to Vienna?","a":"Because Peter had told him to reform or become a monk, and in 1716 ordered him to join the army abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Friends such as Alexander Kikin advised him to seek the protection of his brother-in-law the emperor, who wrote that he was sheltering the tsarevich from his father's tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter's promise of pardon not save him?","a":"Because each confession was met with a demand for more, and his mistress Afrosina's account of his hopes of reigning was treated as proof of treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The senators then explained the promise away, ruling that he had returned unwillingly and so had forfeited it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does his death still raise questions?","a":"Because the official story of a fit like apoplexy was doubted at once, and rumours that Peter had beaten his son to death spread among the common people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later tales of smothering rest on letters that turned out to be forgeries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexei Petrovich, Tsarevich of Russia"},
    "ru-312": {"questions":["Issued on 5 February by the Julian calendar, Peter's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> abolished inheritance by the eldest son and was defended in a treatise on the justice of the monarch's will.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the choice of heir to the reigning sovereign, Peter's death without naming one let the Guards decide the throne until Paul I's act of 1797."],"abstract":"The <b>law of succession of 1722</b>, issued by Peter the Great on 5 February 1722 by the Julian calendar, abolished the custom that the Russian throne passed to the eldest son and gave each reigning sovereign the right to name any successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peter presented it as a curb on disobedient heirs, so that children and grandchildren would no longer be tempted 'to fall into the sin of Absalom', the son who rebelled against King David.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decree argued its case from history, citing Ivan III's choice of his grandson Dmitry as heir, and it reads less like a statute than like a pamphlet aimed at a wide public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A companion treatise, <i>The Justice of the Monarch's Will</i>, was written by Feofan Prokopovich, the leading churchman of the reign, to defend this break with centuries of custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All officials and subjects had to swear a special oath to accept whatever choice the emperor made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The law grew out of the fate of Tsarevich Alexei, since his death and the deaths of Peter's younger sons left only a small grandson, the dead tsarevich's heir and natural avenger, in the male line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter seems to have meant it to clear the way for his wife Catherine, whom he had crowned empress in 1724, yet he never named an heir and on his deathbed managed only to write 'I surrender all things unto' before the name trailed off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With no rule to follow, the throne went to whoever the Guards regiments backed, and Kluchevsky blamed this law for the era of palace revolutions that ran from 1725 to 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Catherine I's own will of 1727 tried to fix the order of succession several generations ahead, against the spirit of Peter's decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The uncertainty ended only with Paul I's act of 5 April 1797, which replaced the sovereign's free choice with a fixed set of impersonal rules and was kept in a special casket in the Dormition Cathedral of the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 265–70, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 59–60, 115, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1890), 486, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","В. Е. Степаненко, «Указ Петра I „О наследии престола“ (1722) как публицистический текст», <i>Гуманитарный вектор</i> 20, no. 3 (2025): 136–44, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ukaz-petra-i-o-nasledii-prestola-1722-kak-publitsisticheskiy-tekst. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Пронкин и К. А. Соловьев, «Механизмы „отмены“ наследника престола в России: I четверть XVIII — I четверть XIX в.», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление</i>, no. 1 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mehanizmy-otmeny-naslednika-prestola-v-rossii-i-chetvert-xviii-i-chetvert-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","З. Г. Облицова, «Эпоха дворцовых переворотов (исторический обзор)», <i>На пути к гражданскому обществу</i>, no. 2 (30) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/epoha-dvortsovyh-perevorotov-istoricheskiy-obzor. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter abandon inheritance by the eldest son?","a":"Because after Alexei's death the only male heir was Alexei's young son, whom Peter feared as the hope of the party opposed to his reforms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Letting the sovereign choose was meant to protect his work and keep that grandson from the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the law fail even for Peter himself?","a":"Because he never used it: after a year of hesitation over his choice he died unable to speak, leaving the name of his successor unwritten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it blamed for the palace revolutions?","a":"Because with no fixed rule each change of ruler was settled by intrigue or force, and the Guards, as the force on the spot, decided who reigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only Paul I's act of 1797 restored a regular order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-313": {"questions":["Gilan, Mazandaran and Astrabad, ceded by a Persian envoy in September 1723, were the prize of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but were handed back by the treaties of Rasht and Ganja.","Begun after Afghan invaders took Isfahan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Peter to Derbent, whose governor knelt to hand him the keys of the city in August 1722."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Persian War of 1722–1723</b>, often called Peter the Great's Persian campaign, was Russia's occupation of the western and southern Caspian shores while Safavid Persia was collapsing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peter's envoy Artemy Volynsky had reported from Isfahan in 1717 that the shah's realm was so badly governed that a small Russian force could take a large part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pretext came in 1721, when rebels in Shemakha plundered Russian merchants, and early in 1722 Afghan invaders took Isfahan and deposed the shah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter wanted the silk trade and the trade route along the Dagestan coast in Russian hands, and he feared that the Ottoman Empire, which was also eyeing Persian land, would take them first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Guards left Moscow in May 1722 on a river fleet of about three hundred vessels carrying some fifteen thousand regular troops, and Peter sailed from Astrakhan on 18 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Joined by Cossacks and Kalmyks the army grew to more than thirty thousand men, and on 23 August 1722 by the Julian calendar the governor of Derbent knelt to hand Peter the keys of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A storm then wrecked most of the supply ships, and with the country around exhausted Peter turned back to Astrakhan, leaving a garrison in Derbent, which an eyewitness called the key to the Persian empire on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Detachments went on to occupy Rasht in Gilan and to storm Baku, and by the Treaty of Saint Petersburg of 12 September 1723 a Persian envoy ceded Derbent, Baku and the provinces of Gilan, Mazandaran and Astrabad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shah Tahmasp II refused to accept it, Russia and the Ottomans divided the Persian borderlands by a treaty at Constantinople in 1724, and Russia's Georgian ally Vakhtang VI, left without help, went into exile in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Disease, local hostility and cost made the gains hard to hold, and Russia handed them back by the treaties of Rasht in 1732 and Ganja in 1735, which undid the results of the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["John Bell, <i>Travels from St. Petersburg in Russia to Diverse Parts of Asia</i>, vol. 2, “A Journey from Mosco to Derbent in Persia, in the Year 1722” (Glasgow: Robert and Andrew Foulis, 1763), https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.71966. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 374–76, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","М. А. Подрезов, «Взгляды в современном Иране на Персидский поход Петра I», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i> 15, no. 6 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzglyady-v-sovremennom-irane-na-persidskiy-pohod-petra-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Михайлов и С. А. Маньков, «Неудача Персидского похода Петра I и эмиграция царя Вахтанга VI в Россию», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (54) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neudacha-persidskogo-pohoda-petra-i-i-emigratsiya-tsarya-vahtanga-vi-v-rossiyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ш. А. Магарамов, «Историография Персидского похода Петра Великого 1722–1723 гг. и его последствий (к 300-летию похода)», <i>История, археология и этнография Кавказа</i> 17, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriografiya-persidskogo-pohoda-petra-velikogo-1722-1723-gg-i-ego-posledstviy-k-300-letiyu-pohoda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М.-П. Б. Абдусаламов, «Западный Прикаспий во внешней политике России в начале XVIII в. (к трёхсотлетию Персидского похода Петра I)», <i>Каспийский регион: политика, экономика, культура</i>, no. 3 (76) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zapadnyy-prikaspiy-vo-vneshney-politike-rossii-v-nachale-xviii-v-k-tryohsotletiyu-persidskogo-pohoda-petra-i. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter invade Persia in 1722?","a":"Because the Safavid state was collapsing, Russian merchants had been plundered at Shemakha, and the Afghans had taken Isfahan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also wanted the Caspian trade route and the silk provinces for Russia before the Ottoman Empire could seize them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Peter turn back after taking Derbent?","a":"Because a storm drove most of the newly arrived supply ships ashore, the country ahead was exhausted of food, and the season was too late to wait for more from Astrakhan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the conquests given up so soon?","a":"Because disease in the damp southern provinces and the hostility of the inhabitants kept Russia from taking root there, and the provinces cost more than they returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The treaties of 1732 and 1735 handed everything back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Persian War (1722–1723)"},
    "ru-314": {"questions":["Ordered after Peter's first state budget showed a deficit, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Russia's third general count of taxable households, following those of 1646 and 1678.","Recruitment, labour levies for canals and the new capital, and flight from taxes were blamed for the losses shown by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by the counts of 1716 and 1717."],"abstract":"The <b>household census of 1710</b> was the third general count of taxable households in Russia, after those of 1646 and 1678, ordered by Peter the Great to learn how many could pay for his war with Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Direct taxes were still assessed on the census of 1678, which had listed between 787,000 and 833,000 taxable households and was by then thirty years out of date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The count went with Peter's first attempt at a single state budget, drawn up in 1709 and 1710, which showed spending well above income and a deficit that had to be covered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike the count of 1678, which listed the men over sixteen with their ages, the census of 1710 recorded every member of each household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its surviving census books, such as that of the Epifan district on the upper Don, let historians study families, ages and new villages one settlement at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Peter expected to find a larger population, but by 1714 the Senate reported that the number of taxpayers had shrunk by a quarter, an estimate later research reduced to about a fifth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The losses were put down to recruitment for the army, to labour levies ten thousand at a time for wharves, canals and the new capital, and to the hundreds of thousands who fled or hid from registration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later counts in 1716 and 1717 reported still more losses, with Kazan province alone short of 33,000 households, nearly a third of those it had registered in 1710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local records complicate the picture, since in Voronezh the number of households rose between 1678 and 1710 before falling sharply by 1716 through deaths and flight, and the Epifan district shows natural growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because household counts proved so unreliable, the government decided in the next few years to register every male 'soul' instead, the basis of the later tax per soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 128–29, 141–42, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","К. А. Керимов, «Численность дворов населения Воронежа по сравнительным данным перечневых выписок подворных переписей населения 1678, 1710 и 1716 гг.», <i>История: факты и символы</i>, no. 4 (41) (2024): 144–47, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chislennost-dvorov-naseleniya-voronezha-po-sravnitelnym-dannym-perechnevyh-vypisok-podvornyh-perepisey-naseleniya-1678-1710-i-1716. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Ляпин, «Подворные переписи как источник по истории хозяйственного освоения Черноземного региона России», <i>Научный вестник Крыма</i>, no. 3 (56) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/podvornye-perepisi-kak-istochnik-po-istorii-hozyaystvennogo-osvoeniya-chernozemnogo-regiona-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Свиридова, «Социальный состав и половозрастная характеристика населения Епифанского уезда по данным переписной книги 1710 г.», <i>История: факты и символы</i>, no. 2 (43) (2025): 127–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialnyy-sostav-i-polovozrastnaya-harakteristika-naseleniya-epifanskogo-uezda-po-dannym-perepisnoy-knigi-1710-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Истомина, «Опыт централизованного бюджетного планирования при Петре I — составление бюджета 1710 г.», <i>Финансы и кредит</i>, no. 30 (510) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/opyt-tsentralizovannogo-byudzhetnogo-planirovaniya-pri-petre-i-sostavlenie-byudzheta-1710-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new census needed in 1710?","a":"Because taxes were still assessed on the household register of 1678, which thirty years of change had made unusable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter's first state budget had also shown a deficit, so the government needed to know what the country could pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the census show fewer households?","a":"Because recruitment and labour levies for Peter's wharves, canals and capital had taken men away, and many people had fled or hidden from registration to escape taxes and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should its figures be treated with caution?","a":"Because the censuses did not record the same categories in the same way, so comparisons between them are hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some districts, such as Voronezh before 1716 and Epifan, actually show growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-315": {"questions":["Named after Pushkin's poem about a statue that chases a clerk through flooded streets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Étienne Falconet shows Peter galloping up a rock above a trampled snake.","Inscribed 'To Peter the First from Catherine the Second', the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Senate Square became the city's central image of Peter, admired as a creator and feared as a tyrant."],"abstract":"The <b>Bronze Horseman</b> is the equestrian statue of Peter the Great on Senate Square in Saint Petersburg, commissioned by Catherine II from the French sculptor Étienne Falconet, who worked in Russia from 1766 to 1778.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Falconet rejected the allegories his friend Diderot proposed and showed the tsar alone, galloping up a steep rock with his hand stretched out over his country, while the horse tramples a snake standing for the forces that opposed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peter's head was modelled by Falconet's pupil Marie-Anne Collot, after Catherine had rejected the sculptor's own versions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pedestal is the Thunder Stone, a huge granite boulder found at Konnaya Lakhta near the Gulf of Finland in 1768, and only about a third of the original block was used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The monument was unveiled in 1782 with the inscription 'To Peter the First from Catherine the Second', which presented the empress as the heir and continuer of his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its popular name comes from Alexander Pushkin's poem <i>The Bronze Horseman</i>, in which the statue pursues a poor clerk through flooded streets, and the poem made it the central image in the myth of Peter and his city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That image was double from the start, because in Peter's own lifetime many of his subjects whispered that the tsar was a foreign changeling or even the Antichrist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Such talk spread among Old Believers and was fed by beard shaving, the new oaths of 1722 and the abolition of the patriarchate, which some read as the ruler usurping the place of Christ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the 19th century the Slavophiles were the first to criticise Peter's work in public, though they never called for a return to Muscovite ways, while Westernisers such as Belinsky defended him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Praised as a creator in the tradition begun by Feofan Prokopovich and dreaded as a terrible power in Pushkin's lines, the rider has remained a measure of how each generation judges Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ю. Г. Филиппова, «Момент истины Этьена Фальконе», <i>Universum: филология и искусствоведение</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moment-istiny-etiena-falkone. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. И. Конанова, «Мифологический образ Петра I в произведениях живописи и монументального искусства XVIII века», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (176) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mifologicheskiy-obraz-petra-i-v-proizvedeniyah-zhivopisi-i-monumentalnogo-iskusstva-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Г. Булах, Г. Н. Попов, С. Ю. Янсон и М. А. Иванов, «Новые данные о гранитном постаменте памятника Петру I „Медный всадник“ в Санкт-Петербурге», <i>Записки Горного института</i> 248 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novye-dannye-o-granitnom-postamente-pamyatnika-petru-i-mednyy-vsadnik-v-sankt-peterburge. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. М. Фролова, Г. В. Ясакова и С. А. Данилов, «Репрезентации образа Петра I на примере образов „Медного всадника“ Э. Фальконе и А. С. Пушкина в контексте социальной памяти», <i>Манускрипт</i> 17, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reprezentatsii-obraza-petra-i-na-primere-obrazov-mednogo-vsadnika-e-falkone-i-a-s-pushkina-v-kontekste-sotsialnoy-pamyati-ot. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 233–41, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Морин Перри, «Русская народная эсхатология и легенда о Петре I — Антихристе», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-narodnaya-eshatologiya-i-legenda-o-petre-i-antihriste. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Бадалян, «Славянофилы и их противники в спорах о реформах Петра I», <i>Русско-Византийский вестник</i>, no. 4 (15) (2023): 176–91, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/slavyanofily-i-ih-protivniki-v-sporah-o-reformah-petra-i. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine II put her own name on Peter's monument?","a":"Because the dedication 'To Peter the First from Catherine the Second' presented her as his direct successor and continuer, a claim that suited a ruler who had come to power by a coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is there a snake under the horse?","a":"Because Falconet wanted a simple monument without allegorical figures, and the snake trampled by the horse stands for the old world and the enemies that resisted Peter's reforms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some of Peter's subjects call him the Antichrist?","a":"Because his shaving of beards, his new oaths and his abolition of the patriarchate looked to Old Believers like signs of the last days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A rival legend held that the real tsar had been replaced by a foreigner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bronze Horseman"},
    "ru-316": {"questions":["Opening when the Guards installed Catherine I and closing when they backed Catherine II against Peter III, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was blamed on Peter's law letting rulers choose their heirs.","Elizabeth's night march with Preobrazhensky grenadiers to arrest the infant Ivan VI's family in 1741 was one of the coups of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 18th-century Russia."],"abstract":"The <b>era of palace revolutions</b> is the name for the years between the death of Peter the Great in 1725 and the accession of Catherine II in 1762, when the Russian throne changed hands again and again through court intrigue and the threat of force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name comes from Kluchevsky, who counted five or six palace coups in these thirty-seven years and blamed Peter's law of 1722, which let the ruler name any heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The decisive force was the Guards, the Preobrazhensky and Semyonovsky regiments with the Izmailovsky added under Anna, whose men came from the nobility and who for decades made and unmade governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six sovereigns followed Peter in that time: his widow Catherine I, his grandson Peter II, his niece Anna, the infant Ivan VI, his daughter Elizabeth and his grandson Peter III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first changes still had a legal colour: the Guards put Catherine I on the throne in 1725, Menshikov was overthrown in 1727, and in 1730 Anna threw off the limits the Supreme Privy Council had set on her power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Later changes were plain conspiracies, as when Münnich's men seized the regent Biron in his bed in November 1740 and when Elizabeth led Preobrazhensky grenadiers to the Winter Palace in 1741.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1762 Catherine II, backed by the Izmailovsky and Semyonovsky regiments, removed her husband Peter III after barely six months on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the Guards were recruited from the nobility, their part in these coups taught the nobility as a whole to see itself as the decisive force in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Older writers saw the period as a falling away from Peter's achievements, but more recent work stresses that his reforms were slowed rather than reversed after 1725.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The disputes over the throne eased only after Paul I's succession act of 1797, and even that did not save Paul himself from murder by conspirators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 265–76, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 163–76, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 53–60, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","З. Г. Облицова, «Эпоха дворцовых переворотов (исторический обзор)», <i>На пути к гражданскому обществу</i>, no. 2 (30) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/epoha-dvortsovyh-perevorotov-istoricheskiy-obzor. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Белова, «Отечественные историки рубежа XX–XXI вв. об эпохе „дворцовых переворотов“», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 6 (102) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otechestvennye-istoriki-rubezha-xx-xxi-vv-ob-epohe-dvortsovyh-perevorotov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Коршунова, «Дворцовые перевороты как инструмент утверждения абсолютной монархии в России в XVIII — начале XIX века», <i>Социум и власть</i>, no. 2 (64) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvortsovye-perevoroty-kak-instrument-utverzhdeniya-absolyutnoy-monarhii-v-rossii-v-xviii-nachale-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Была ли альтернатива курсу Петра Великого в „эпоху дворцовых переворотов“?», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 3 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byla-li-alternativa-kursu-petra-velikogo-v-epohu-dvortsovyh-perevorotov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Guards able to decide who reigned?","a":"Because, with no working law of succession, questions of power were settled by the force on the spot, and the Guards were the privileged core of the army stationed at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their officers came from the nobility and acted as its voice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Peter's law of 1722 blamed for the coups?","a":"Because it abolished both inheritance by the eldest son and election by an assembly, leaving the choice to the reigning ruler, and Peter died without making one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each later change of ruler was therefore open to dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why have historians changed their view of the period?","a":"Because studies of the governments after 1725 found that Peter's reforms went on, though more slowly, and that his course was never turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-317": {"questions":["Proclaimed autocrat empress in January 1725 after Guards officers burst into the senators' debate, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the Supreme Privy Council and left government largely to Menshikov.","Born Martha Skavronskaya and raised in a Protestant pastor's household in Livonia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first woman to rule the Russian Empire in her own right."],"abstract":"<b>Catherine I</b> was the second wife of Peter the Great and, from 1725 to 1727, the first woman to rule the Russian Empire in her own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Born Martha Skavronskaya, probably in 1683 or 1684, she was the daughter of a small Catholic farmer and was brought up as a servant in the household of Pastor Glück at Marienburg in Livonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Sheremetev's army took Marienburg during the Great Northern War she became a captive, passed into Menshikov's household and there met Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peter married her publicly and took her on his campaigns, and he credited her courage and coolness with saving his army in the disastrous Pruth campaign of 1711.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1724 he had her crowned empress in Moscow, a rite almost unknown for a tsar's wife in Russia, after a manifesto that praised her services in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When Peter died on 28 January 1725 without naming an heir, Menshikov and Tolstoy won over the Guards, whose officers crowded into the senators' debate and helped have her proclaimed autocrat empress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Her accession was presented as the Senate's reading of Peter's will, resting on her coronation, while the old aristocracy that favoured his grandson gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 1726 she set up the Supreme Privy Council, a small body of Peter's leading men placed above the Senate, and left most government to it and to Menshikov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her court was lavish, and a foreign envoy wrote that no one in her administration thought of anything but stealing, although her government did open trade at Archangel to all merchants from 1727.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> She died in May 1727 after a will, signed for her by her daughter Elizabeth, named Peter's grandson as heir, with her own daughters to follow him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 52–60, 70–115, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 266–72, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia: A Study of Historical Biography</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1890), 486–87, https://archive.org/details/petergreatempero02schu. [Open access]","А. С. Мелехов и В. В. Сухарева, «Екатерина I — первая российская императрица», <i>Наука и образование</i> 6, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ekaterina-i-pervaya-rossiyskaya-imperatritsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. П. Рассказов и А. С. Жиленко, «Обстоятельства и правовые аспекты учреждения Верховного тайного совета в 1726 году», <i>Юристъ — Правоведъ</i>, no. 2 (63) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obstoyatelstva-i-pravovye-aspekty-uchrezhdeniya-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-v-1726-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","З. Г. Облицова, «Эпоха дворцовых переворотов (исторический обзор)», <i>На пути к гражданскому обществу</i>, no. 2 (30) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/epoha-dvortsovyh-perevorotov-istoricheskiy-obzor. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Белова, «Торговая политика Верховного тайного совета (1726–1730 гг.)», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 3 (78) (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/torgovaya-politika-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-1726-1730-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a former captive succeed Peter?","a":"Because Peter had crowned her empress in 1724, which her supporters read as naming her his successor under his law of 1722.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Guards, devoted to Peter and promised their arrears of pay, backed her at the decisive moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Menshikov and Tolstoy back her against Peter's grandson?","a":"Because they had worked against the boy's father, the Tsarevich Alexei, and feared exile or worse if his son came to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did her will name Peter's grandson as heir?","a":"Because the leading institutions and the Guards' commanders preferred him to her daughters, and she gave way so that her reign might end in peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Catherine I of Russia"},
    "ru-318": {"questions":["Made co-regent for the boy Peter II by Catherine's will, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abolished by Anna in 1730 and later revived in miniature as her three-man Cabinet.","Reviewing Peter's protectionist tariff of 1724, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened trade at Archangel to all merchants and created a commission on commerce of nobles and merchants in 1727."],"abstract":"The <b>Supreme Privy Council</b> was the small body of the highest officials that governed the Russian Empire above the Senate from February 1726 to 1730.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Catherine I founded it by a decree of 8 February 1726 by the Julian calendar, after a quarrel in which Menshikov silenced a Senate debate by announcing the empress's will in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first six members were Menshikov, Tolstoy, Apraksin, Golovkin and Osterman from Peter's new men, with Prince Dmitry Golitsyn as the one voice of the old aristocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decree complained that the senators were too overloaded to decide great matters quickly, dropped the word 'governing' from the Senate's title and made it equal to the colleges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No decree was to go out without the council's consideration, the colleges of war, the navy and foreign affairs answered to it, and when its members disagreed the matter went to the sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Catherine's will made it co-regent for her young successor Peter II, and during his nominal reign it was in practice the highest organ of power in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its membership changed fast, since Tolstoy was banished and Menshikov fell in 1727, and by 1730 only two of the first six remained beside new members from the Dolgorukov family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The council also reviewed Peter's protectionist tariff of 1724, opened trade at Archangel to all merchants and in 1727 set up a commission on commerce of senators, officials, nobles and merchants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Peter II died in January 1730 the council chose his cousin Anna of Courland as empress and tried to bind her to its consent, a scheme the Guards and the lesser nobility defeated within weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anna then abolished the council and restored the Senate, though the Cabinet of three ministers she created in 1731 was in effect the old council in miniature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 280–83, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 84–86, 142, 183, 186–87, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","А. С. Жиленко, «Правовой статус Верховного Тайного Совета (1726–1730 гг.)», <i>Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-1726-1730-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. П. Рассказов и А. С. Жиленко, «Обстоятельства и правовые аспекты учреждения Верховного тайного совета в 1726 году», <i>Юристъ — Правоведъ</i>, no. 2 (63) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obstoyatelstva-i-pravovye-aspekty-uchrezhdeniya-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-v-1726-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Белова, «Торговая политика Верховного тайного совета (1726–1730 гг.)», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 3 (78) (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/torgovaya-politika-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-1726-1730-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Козлова, «Вступление на престол русской императрицы Анны Иоанновны», <i>Вестник Омского университета</i>, no. 3 (69) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vstuplenie-na-prestol-russkoy-imperatritsy-anny-ioannovny. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the council created?","a":"Because the senators were overloaded and quarrelling, and a small council of Peter's leading men offered a bargain that soothed the old aristocracy while strengthening the executive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Dmitry Golitsyn given a seat?","a":"Because Osterman suggested it, both to earn the gratitude of the old Russian party and to keep its leader under watch in the capital as a kind of hostage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Anna abolish it?","a":"Because the council had tried to limit her power, and the nobles who petitioned her to rule as autocrat also asked her to abolish it and restore the Senate of Peter's day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Supreme Privy Council"},
    "ru-319": {"questions":["Crowned in Moscow in February 1728 after his court left Saint Petersburg, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the son of the Tsarevich Alexei and the last male of the Romanov house.","Turning against Menshikov in 1727 and sending him into exile, the boy emperor <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell under the sway of the Dolgorukov princes, who took him hunting rather than to his lessons."],"abstract":"<b>Peter II</b> was emperor of Russia from 1727 to 1730, the grandson of Peter the Great and the only son of the Tsarevich Alexei and Princess Charlotte of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in October 1715, he lost his mother within days and his father in 1718, and his grandfather's law of 1722 was meant partly to keep him from the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, the old aristocracy and much of public opinion saw him as the lawful heir, the last male of the house, and Catherine I's will named him her successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne on 6 May 1727 at the age of eleven, with the Supreme Privy Council acting as regent and in practice the highest power in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Menshikov at once betrothed him to his own daughter Maria and kept the boy in his house, while Andrei Osterman took charge of his education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In September 1727 the young emperor turned against Menshikov, who was stripped of his offices and later banished to Berezov in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Dolgorukov princes took his place, and in January 1728 the court moved to Moscow for his coronation on 25 February and stayed there for the rest of the reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He gave up study for hunting with the Dolgorukovs and late in 1729 was betrothed to Princess Catherine Dolgorukova, though observers thought he disliked her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He fell ill with smallpox in January 1730 and died aged fourteen on the very day that had been fixed for his wedding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Manstein wrote that the whole nation mourned him, since Russians of the old stock liked a prince who had left Saint Petersburg, and his death opened a new crisis over the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 59, 113–57, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The First Romanovs, 1613–1725: A History of Moscovite Civilisation and the Rise of Modern Russia under Peter the Great and His Forerunners</i> (London: Archibald Constable, 1905), 350, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028446767. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 268–73, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, first ed. in English by David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 13–23, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","С. В. Семенцов, «От Екатерины Первой до Анны Иоанновны, 1724–1732 годы: метания и неопределенность в судьбе столичного города», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Искусствоведение</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-ekateriny-pervoy-do-anny-ioannovny-1724-1732-gody-metaniya-i-neopredelennost-v-sudbe-stolichnogo-goroda-ostavatsya-li-na-putyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Жиленко, «Правовой статус Верховного Тайного Совета (1726–1730 гг.)», <i>Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 5 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-1726-1730-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","З. Г. Облицова, «Эпоха дворцовых переворотов (исторический обзор)», <i>На пути к гражданскому обществу</i>, no. 2 (30) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/epoha-dvortsovyh-perevorotov-istoricheskiy-obzor. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was he passed over when Peter the Great died?","a":"Because Menshikov, Tolstoy and the other new men had worked against his father and feared that his accession would bring their ruin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Menshikov fall so quickly?","a":"Because his overbearing control of the boy, whom he kept in his own house and scolded, turned the emperor against him, while Osterman and the Dolgorukovs worked on the young ruler during Menshikov's illness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the court move to Moscow?","a":"Because the Dolgorukovs wanted the emperor away from Saint Petersburg, and the coronation, which was always held in Moscow, gave them the pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peter II of Russia"},
    "ru-320": {"questions":["Remembered for the Ice Palace of 1740 and for her favourite Biron, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named the infant Ivan VI her heir and Biron his regent shortly before her death that year.","Petitioned in February 1730 by Guards officers and lesser nobles to rule as an autocrat, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tore up the terms she had signed at Mitau and later created a Cabinet of ministers."],"abstract":"<b>Anna of Russia</b>, Anna Ioannovna, was empress from 1730 to 1740, a daughter of Tsar Ivan V, the elder brother of Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in 1693, she was married at sixteen to the Duke of Courland, who died on the road home from Saint Petersburg, and she lived on at Mitau in near poverty for almost twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In January 1730 the Supreme Privy Council chose her to succeed Peter II, expecting a needy widow far from Moscow to accept limits on her power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Moscow the Guards and the lesser nobility petitioned her to rule as autocrat, and on 25 February 1730 by the Julian calendar she tore up the document she had signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She then abolished the council, named a new Senate of twenty-one members and in 1731 created a Cabinet of three ministers through which most business passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her favourite Ernst Johann Biron, a Courlander of modest birth, became grand chamberlain and a count, and Germans such as Osterman and Münnich held leading posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later writers called her reign the 'Bironovshchina' and blamed a German faction for a reign of terror, but archival work has found no sign that Biron directed the Secret Chancellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peter's political police were revived as that chancellery, the Izmailovsky Guards were raised, Münnich rebuilt Saint Petersburg for the court's return from Moscow in 1732, and her court cost several times as much as Peter's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her government kept the Synod reporting to the Cabinet and pressed hard on the Old Believers, while abroad it fought the War of the Polish Succession and a war against the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is also remembered for costly entertainments such as the Ice Palace of 1740, and before her death that year she named the infant Ivan VI her heir and Biron his regent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 158–200, 276–314, https://archive.org/details/pupilsofpetergre00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 265, 273, 298–310, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, first ed. in English by David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 28–41, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","А. А. Козлова, «Вступление на престол русской императрицы Анны Иоанновны», <i>Вестник Омского университета</i>, no. 3 (69) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vstuplenie-na-prestol-russkoy-imperatritsy-anny-ioannovny. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Козлова, «Религиозная политика российских императриц Анны Иоанновны и Елизаветы Петровны», <i>Преподаватель XXI век</i>, no. 1–2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/religioznaya-politika-rossiyskih-imperatrits-anny-ioannovny-i-elizavety-petrovny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Черникова, «Царствование Анны Иоанновны — реалии и исторические мифы», <i>Люди и тексты. Исторический альманах</i>, no. 10 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarstvovanie-anny-ioannovny-realii-i-istoricheskie-mify. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Семенцов, «От Екатерины Первой до Анны Иоанновны, 1724–1732 годы: метания и неопределенность в судьбе столичного города», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Искусствоведение</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-ekateriny-pervoy-do-anny-ioannovny-1724-1732-gody-metaniya-i-neopredelennost-v-sudbe-stolichnogo-goroda-ostavatsya-li-na-putyah. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the council choose Anna?","a":"Because she belonged to the Romanov house but lived in poverty abroad, with no party of her own in Russia, so the councillors expected her to accept any terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She was also thought sensible and dignified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the nobles help her restore autocracy?","a":"Because they feared the rule of a few great families more than that of one sovereign, and they told her it was not their will that laws should be prescribed to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Guards officers stood with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has her reign such a dark reputation?","a":"Because her German favourites, her costly court and her secret police made her rule hated, and later writers built that into the legend of the 'Bironovshchina'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Archival study has shown parts of that legend to be myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Anna of Russia"},
    "ru-321": {"questions":["Without the consent of eight councillors Anna could not marry, name an heir, make war, raise taxes or promote anyone above colonel under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> she accepted in 1730.","Nobles and Guards officers who petitioned for the old autocracy in February 1730 asked Anna to set aside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Prince Dmitry Golitsyn had drafted."],"abstract":"The <b>konditsii</b>, the 'Conditions', were the written terms on which the Supreme Privy Council offered the Russian throne in January 1730 to Anna Ivanovna, the widowed Duchess of Courland, after Peter II died without an heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan came from Prince Dmitry Golitsyn, and Anna was chosen partly because her home at Mitau, far from Moscow, gave the councillors time to entrench it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its drafts the text was called 'Conditions' or 'Points', and Anna promised not to marry or name an heir, make war or peace, levy new taxes, grant estates or promote anyone above colonel without the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was also to leave the Guards under the council, to take no nobleman's life, property or honour without trial, and to forfeit the crown if she broke any of these promises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians still disagree about what the document was, some seeing a first step towards constitutional monarchy and others an oligarchic seizure of power by a few great families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Anna signed at Mitau, but once near Moscow she courted the Guards, proclaiming herself lieutenant-colonel of the Preobrazhensky Regiment and serving the officers vodka with her own hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many lesser nobles distrusted the councillors as much as autocracy, and within weeks they drew up a dozen or more projects of their own, most calling for an elected noble body instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 25 February 1730 a crowd of nobles and Guards officers petitioned her to take back the autocracy of her ancestors, the officers shouting that they would lay her enemies' heads at her feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Anna asked whether the Conditions had not been the wish of the whole nation and, told that they had not, had the paper signed at Mitau read out and tore it up before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A new oath to her as autocrat followed, the council itself was abolished in 1731, and the one formal limit placed on a Russian ruler of the century had lasted only a few weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 286–308, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 163–76, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028457897. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 27–35, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","С. Ю. Мазиков, «Попытка свержения самодержавия в России в 1730 г.», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/popytka-sverzheniya-samoderzhaviya-v-rossii-v-1730-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Пронкин, «Политические намерения Верховного тайного совета в 1730 г.», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление</i>, no. 3 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskie-namereniya-verhovnogo-taynogo-soveta-v-1730-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Упоров, «Принятие „самодержавства“ Анной Иоанновной и приведение к присяге на верность императрице», <i>Гуманитарный научный журнал</i>, no. 2-1 (2023): 73–78, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prinyatie-samoderzhavstva-annoy-ioannovnoy-i-privedenie-k-prisyage-na-vernost-imperatritse. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the councillors choose a widowed duchess living in Courland?","a":"Because distance served their plan. Mitau lay far from Moscow, which gave the council time to fix the new arrangement before Anna arrived, and she was a widow who could be bound not to marry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The councillors seem to have expected a poor and pliable woman who would sign whatever they sent her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the nobles help Anna destroy limits that protected them too?","a":"Because the Conditions handed power to a council of a few great families, not to the nobility as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many lesser nobles feared rule by eight magnates more than rule by one sovereign, and the Guards officers among them shouted that they wanted an autocrat as their tsars had always been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about what the Conditions were?","a":"Because the document can be read two ways. Its clauses on trial and property look like a first written limit on the crown, yet they placed that power in a small council dominated by the Dolgorukys and Golitsyns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The council's wider aims survive only in drafts and projects, which leaves room for both readings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Conditions (Russia)"},
    "ru-322": {"questions":["Archive counts showing about 820 exiles by the Secret Chancellery, not twenty thousand, have led historians to doubt the traditional picture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Anna.","Patriotic sermons after Elizabeth seized the throne attacked Ostermann and Münnich as foreign agents, helping fix the idea of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a reign of foreigners over Russians."],"abstract":"<b>Bironovshchina</b> is the name later given to the reign of Empress Anna, from 1730 to 1740, after her favourite Ernst Johann Biron, a Courlander of modest family whose grandfather had been a ducal groom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Biron held no ministry but lived beside the empress, became Grand Chamberlain and in 1737 Duke of Courland, and was widely believed to steer her decisions from behind the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anna's court was lavish, while the Secret Chancellery under Andrei Ushakov pursued denunciations and Guards officers led detachments that collected the arrears of taxation by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The great Russian families suffered heavily: Prince Dmitry Golitsyn was condemned and shut in Schlüsselburg, and in 1739 several Dolgorukys were executed at Novgorod, one broken on the wheel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Anna died in October 1740 Biron became regent for the infant Ivan VI, but within weeks Münnich had him seized in his bed by night and sent into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The classic picture comes partly from Manstein, Münnich's aide, who reported that more than 20,000 people were exiled under Anna, a figure the great Russian historians Soloviev and Kluchevsky repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its patriotic frame was set by Elizabeth's regime, whose sermons after 1741 cast Ostermann and Münnich as foreign agents who had robbed the treasury and persecuted Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A study of the Secret Chancellery's files found that 4,827 people were punished for political offences in the reign, of whom about 820 were exiled, roughly 4 per cent of Manstein's number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same files contain no case of protest against foreign domination and no sign that Biron ran the chancellery, which reported to the empress and her cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Studies of provincial records add that Anna's government could barely collect accounts or taxes from its own governors, so the name now usually appears in quotation marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 307–10, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 180–82, 292–96, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028457897. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 270–81, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","Т. В. Черникова, «Царствование Анны Иоанновны – реалии и исторические мифы», <i>Люди и тексты. Исторический альманах</i>, no. 10 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarstvovanie-anny-ioannovny-realii-i-istoricheskie-mify. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Д. Столяров, «Дворцовый переворот ноября 1741 года и иностранцы в российской правящей элите», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 8/2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvortsovyy-perevorot-noyabrya-1741-goda-i-inostrantsy-v-rossiyskoy-pravyaschey-elite. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Курукин, «„Вскоре тех ведомостей сочинить невозможно“: Административная повседневность провинции в эпоху „бироновщины“», <i>Вестник РГГУ</i>, no. 6 (2018): 35–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vskore-teh-vedomostey-sochinit-nevozmozhno-administrativnaya-povsednevnost-provintsii-v-epohu-bironovschiny. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a favourite with no ministry blamed for a whole reign?","a":"Because he was the empress's constant companion, grew immensely rich and was believed to stand behind every decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writers close to his rival Münnich, Manstein above all, portrayed him as vindictive and cruel, and those memoirs shaped how later historians told the story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Elizabeth's government encourage the idea of a German yoke?","a":"Because she had overthrown a lawful emperor and needed a reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Presenting herself as her father's heir who freed Russia from foreigners turned a palace seizure into a national rescue, and the sermons of her early reign built the image of a 'German party' that later historians inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians now doubt the traditional figures?","a":"Because the Secret Chancellery's own records give far smaller numbers: about 820 exiles among 4,827 people punished, against Manstein's 20,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The files also show the chancellery answering to the empress, not to Biron, while provincial records show a government too weak to run the efficient terror the name implies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-323": {"questions":["A Russian army of 20,000 marched across Germany to the Neckar in 1735 to help the emperor in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first Russian army seen in Central Europe.","When the death of Augustus II in 1733 opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Russia and Austria backed the Saxon elector against the French candidate, Stanisław Leszczyński."],"abstract":"The <b>War of the Polish Succession</b> was the European war of 1733 to 1735 that followed the death of Augustus II, King of Poland, in 1733, with Russia and Austria backing his son against France's candidate, Stanisław Leszczyński.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In September 1733 a great majority of the Polish electors chose Stanisław, but a Lithuanian-led minority protested, and Russian regiments under Peter Lacy crossed the border to reverse the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stanisław fled to Danzig, now Gdańsk, while the dissenting minority appealed to Empress Anna, whose ministers had already promised the Saxon court to support Augustus III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France answered by attacking Austria on the Rhine and in Italy, where it and its allies took Naples, Sicily and most of Lombardy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting stopped with an armistice in 1735, and the war was formally ended by the Treaty of Vienna in 1738.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For Russia the decisive act was the siege of Danzig, which Lacy opened on 22 February 1734 and Münnich took over in March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A night assault on the Hagelsberg failed with heavy loss, and the city fell only after a Russian fleet brought siege guns and a small French relief force on the coast surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Danzig capitulated on 30 June 1734 after 135 days, at a cost of more than 8,000 Russian dead and wounded, though Stanisław escaped in disguise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1735 a corps of 20,000 under Lacy marched through Germany to join the emperor's army on the Neckar, the first Russian army ever seen in Central Europe, arriving too late to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war left marks at home too, from Trediakovsky's solemn ode on the surrender of Gdańsk, an early Russian victory ode, to soldiers on the Rhine march who met lands without serfdom and deserted in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 310–11, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 206–34, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028457897. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 70–84, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","К. М. Белюков, «Война за „польское наследство“ 1733–1735 гг. в трудах историков Российской империи», <i>Исторические, философские, политические и юридические науки, культурология и искусствоведение. Вопросы теории и практики</i>, no. 1 (2018): 11–18, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-za-polskoe-nasledstvo-1733-1735-gg-v-trudah-istorikov-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Саприкина и К. М. Белюков, «Русские дневники и мемуары о войне за Польское наследство (1733–1735) как исторический источник», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 4 (2018): 259–78, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkie-dnevniki-i-memuary-o-voyne-za-polskoe-nasledstvo-1733-1735-kak-istoricheskiy-istochnik. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Н. Станков, «Война за польское наследство в отечественной историографии XIX – начала XXI века», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Серия История. Международные отношения</i> 25, no. 1 (2025): 4–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-za-polskoe-nasledstvo-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii-xix-nachala-xxi-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Травников и Л. А. Ольшевская, «„Лавровы вьются там венцы...“ (поэтика „Оды на взятие Хотина“ М. В. Ломоносова)», <i>Литературоведческий журнал</i>, no. 29 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lavrovy-vyutsya-tam-ventsy-poetika-ody-na-vzyatie-hotina-m-v-lomonosova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia send an army to decide a Polish election?","a":"Because a French-backed king in Warsaw threatened its influence on its western border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Russian court distrusted Stanisław as an inveterate enemy and had already promised the Saxon elector its support, so when the electors chose Stanisław it used force to install Augustus III instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the siege of Danzig take so long?","a":"Because the Russians arrived without heavy guns and the city was strong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A costly night attack on the Hagelsberg failed, and only after a Russian fleet landed the siege artillery in June did the bombardment force the French relief troops and then the city to give in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Russian scholarship call this war understudied?","a":"Because earlier historians told only pieces of it, chiefly the siege of Danzig, and passed over the Rhine march and the war in Italy that shaped the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Recent historiographical surveys conclude that no complete account of the conflict from the Russian side yet exists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War of the Polish Succession"},
    "ru-324": {"questions":["Russia regained Azov with its walls razed and agreed to keep no ships on the Black Sea at the close of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, despite storming Perekop and Ochakov.","Early in the fighting, Ottoman officials disarmed Greeks in Albania and Epirus, fearing that Balkan Christians would rise to help Russia in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the Sultan."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1735–1739</b>, known in Russian writing as Anna's or Münnich's war, was fought by Russia, with Austria as its ally, against the Ottoman Empire and its Crimean Tatar vassals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first aims were to recover Azov, lost after Peter's defeat on the Pruth, and to close the routes by which the Tatars raided Russia's southern lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It opened in October 1735 with a raid on the Crimea by General Leontiev, launched by Field Marshal Münnich, who hoped the war would wipe out the stain of his failures at Danzig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1736 Münnich stormed the Lines of Perekop and burned the khan's capital at Bakhchisaray while Lacy took Azov, but Münnich lost some 30,000 men, more than half his army, chiefly to heat, hunger and disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ochakov fell in 1737, and at a peace congress at Nemirov that year Russia demanded the Tatar lands of the coast and independence for Moldavia and Wallachia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Ottoman officials feared that Balkan Christians would rise to help Russia, and they disarmed Greeks in Albania and Epirus, though no rising came there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decisive campaign came in 1739, when Münnich marched through Polish territory to the Dniester and on 28 August, New Style, routed the Turkish army at Stavuchany for the loss of about 70 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Khotin surrendered at the first summons, Münnich entered Jassy, and the estates of Moldavia declared their submission to the empress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> But Austria, beaten elsewhere, had already made a separate peace at Belgrade, and Russia, unable to fight on alone, accepted French mediation and made its own peace there in 1739, keeping Azov only with its walls razed and no right to ships on the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One Russian historian put the losses at more than 100,000, while Lomonosov, a mining student in Freiberg, celebrated Khotin in an ode that changed the course of Russian verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 311–13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Pupils of Peter the Great: A History of the Russian Court and Empire from 1697 to 1740</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1897), 227, 235–76, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028457897. [Open access]","Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 89–240, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","Г. Л. Арш, «Русско-турецкая война 1735–1739 гг. и Западные Балканы», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1735-1739-gg-i-zapadnye-balkany. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Б. Каширин, «„Верное описание прошедшей турецкой войны“: Неизвестный стратегический меморандум генерал-аншефа графа П. И. Панина и разгаданная тайна авторства анонимных воспоминаний о войне России и Турции 1736–1739 гг.», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vernoe-opisanie-proshedshey-turetskoy-voyny-neizvestnyy-strategicheskiy-memorandum-general-anshefa-grafa-p-i-panina-i-razgadannaya. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Травников и Л. А. Ольшевская, «„Лавровы вьются там венцы...“ (поэтика „Оды на взятие Хотина“ М. В. Ломоносова)», <i>Литературоведческий журнал</i>, no. 29 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lavrovy-vyutsya-tam-ventsy-poetika-ody-na-vzyatie-hotina-m-v-lomonosova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Münnich's armies lose so many men without great battles?","a":"Because the steppe itself was the enemy. In 1736 about 30,000 men, more than half his force, died, and scarcely 2,000 of them were killed in fighting; the rest fell to fatigue, hunger and an unhealthy climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troops often marched for hours in the midday heat, and in 1737 the army came back 24,000 men smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia make peace just after its greatest victory?","a":"Because its ally had already left the war. The news of Austria's separate peace at Belgrade reached Münnich on the very day he reported Moldavia's submission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Münnich offered to fight on alone, but the ministers knew Russia could not carry the war by itself and opened talks through French mediation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians judge the peace so differently?","a":"Because they weigh it against different hopes. Kluchevsky called it the most comically shameful treaty Russia ever signed, since victories and some 100,000 lives bought only a razed Azov and no Black Sea fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bain thought it an honourable peace given that Austria had deserted, because Russia kept Azov and its surrounding district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1735–1739)"},
    "ru-325": {"questions":["After 1741 keeping a coin with the profile of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a crime, and the oath sheets once sworn to him were publicly burned on Elizabeth's orders.","Guards at Schlüsselburg obeyed orders to kill their prisoner rather than see him freed when an officer named Mirovich tried to rescue <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Catherine II."],"abstract":"<b>Ivan VI</b> was the infant emperor of Russia from October 1740 to November 1741, the son of Anna Leopoldovna, Empress Anna's niece, and Prince Anton Ulrich of Brunswick, born in Saint Petersburg on 12 August 1740 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Empress Anna adopted him as her heir and, as she lay dying in October 1740, signed an act making her favourite Biron regent until the boy reached seventeen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within three weeks Field Marshal Münnich sent his aide Manstein with twenty soldiers to seize Biron in his bed, and the boy's mother took the regency herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her government lasted just over a year, and on the night of 24–25 November 1741 Peter the Great's daughter Elizabeth led Guards grenadiers into the Winter Palace and had the whole family arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soldiers waited by the cradle until the child woke and carried him off with his nurse, ending a reign of 404 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Elizabeth's government then set out to erase him: the oath sheets sworn to him were publicly burned in 1744, and decrees bearing his title were torn from the files and sealed away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Keeping a coin with his profile or a paper with his title became a crime, and in 1747 a journeyman button-maker caught at Narva with five such coins was tortured and exiled for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The family was moved from prison to prison, the boy travelling under the name Gregory, and at Kholmogory on the White Sea he was kept apart from his parents, whom he never saw again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He grew up alone, later in the fortress of Schlüsselburg, where his guards had strict orders to kill him rather than let anyone set him free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1764, under Catherine II, an officer named Mirovich tried to free him and the guards carried out their orders, a killing that left suspicions about the empress which the evidence neither proves nor removes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 270–81, 321–22, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton; Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 22–49, 125, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 313–14, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","Е. И. Черникова и А. А. Трунов, «История Брауншвейгского семейства в России», <i>Вестник Белгородского юридического института МВД России</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-braunshveygskogo-semeystva-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Анисимов, «Историческая память как государственное преступление (по материалам политического сыска первой половины XVIII века)», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i>, no. 55 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskaya-pamyat-kak-gosudarstvennoe-prestuplenie-po-materialam-politicheskogo-syska-pervoy-poloviny-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1894), 288–89, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028401648. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Elizabeth try to wipe out the memory of a child?","a":"Because he was a crowned emperor to whom the whole country, Elizabeth included, had sworn an oath in 1740.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> While documents, coins and prayers carried his name, her own title rested on a broken oath, so the oath sheets were burned and anyone keeping his coins or decrees could be punished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a prisoner who had done nothing kept under orders to be killed?","a":"Because his very existence was a threat. He had reigned, and a coup like the one that removed him could restore him, so his guards were told to kill him rather than let him escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Among ordinary people he was one of only two rulers of the age spoken of with sympathy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Catherine II suspected of arranging his death?","a":"Because the rescue attempt removed a rival at a convenient moment. Mirovich was tried and executed without a word, no wider search for accomplices was made on her express command, and his relatives were left alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those details fed suspicion, though no proof of her complicity has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ivan VI of Russia"},
    "ru-326": {"questions":["Moscow University, the Peace of Åbo and the Russian victories over Frederick the Great all belong to the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the daughter of Peter the Great.","Courtiers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attended masquerades where men wore women's dress and women men's, and she left 15,000 gowns and an unfinished Winter Palace at her death."],"abstract":"<b>Elizabeth of Russia</b>, Elizabeth Petrovna, was empress from 1741 to 1761, the daughter of Peter the Great and his second wife Catherine, born at Kolomenskoye near Moscow on 18 December 1709 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was born before her parents' church wedding, and for some forty years people were punished for saying she was a bastard with no right to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under her cousin Anna she lived under suspicion, at one point close to being shut in a convent, and she took the throne only in November 1741 at the head of the Preobrazhensky grenadiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her government presented her as her father's heir who had freed Russia from foreigners, and the share of foreigners among Russia's generals fell faster under her than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russia gained part of southern Finland by the Peace of Åbo in 1743, and in the Seven Years' War her armies fought at Zorndorf and Kunersdorf, beat Frederick the Great and took Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At home she is remembered for the founding of Moscow University in 1755 and for a court of balls, opera and masquerades, at which she had men and women exchange their clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before seizing power she vowed never to send anyone to death, and because executions practically ceased in her reign later writers called her the 'most gentle'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In her first months she gave up her own plan of a rapprochement with France, which had backed her coup, and returned under her diplomats' pressure to the alliances of earlier reigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Contemporaries described her as kind, devout and fond of pleasure but averse to serious business, and she left 15,000 gowns and an unfinished Winter Palace when she died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She died on Christmas Day 1761 by the Julian calendar, and the throne passed to her nephew Peter III, whom one historian called the worst of the legacies she left her country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 353–57, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton; Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 66–68, 83–84, 133–42, 313–14, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","М. Ю. Анисимов, «Отказ Елизаветы Петровны от сближения с Францией в 1741–1742 гг.», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i> 19, no. 3-1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otkaz-elizavety-petrovny-ot-sblizheniya-s-frantsiey-v-1741-1742-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Анисимов, «Историческая память как государственное преступление (по материалам политического сыска первой половины XVIII века)», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i>, no. 55 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskaya-pamyat-kak-gosudarstvennoe-prestuplenie-po-materialam-politicheskogo-syska-pervoy-poloviny-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Д. Столяров, «Дворцовый переворот ноября 1741 года и иностранцы в российской правящей элите», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 8/2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvortsovyy-perevorot-noyabrya-1741-goda-i-inostrantsy-v-rossiyskoy-pravyaschey-elite. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Лунин, П. М. Зоркий и И. Е. Лубнина, «Российские университеты в XVIII веке», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 2. Химия</i> 40, no. 5 (1999), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskie-universitety-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. А. Ростовцева, «О смертной казни в „Счастливом обществе“: законодательство „Кроткой Елисавет“ и законничество А. П. Сумарокова», <i>Rhema. Рема</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-smertnoy-kazni-v-schastlivom-obschestve-zakonodatelstvo-krotkoy-elisavet-i-zakonnichestvo-a-p-sumarokova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was her birth used against her for so long?","a":"Because Peter married her mother in church only after she was born, which let enemies say she had been born outside marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Records of the political police show people punished for calling her a bastard and an unlawful sovereign over some forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did her reign stress Russians over foreigners?","a":"Because she had overthrown a lawful emperor and needed a cause. Her regime cast its predecessors' ministers as foreign agents and her as Peter's true heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In practice fewer foreigners received high army rank, though she kept able ones such as Marshal Lacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is she linked with the end of executions?","a":"Because she vowed before her coup that she would never willingly send anyone to death, and a decree of 1744 confirmed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Death sentences continued to be passed but were not carried out, which is why writers from Karamzin onwards called her the most gentle of rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Elizabeth of Russia"},
    "ru-327": {"questions":["Money from the French envoy La Chétardie and the urging of the surgeon Lestocq helped prepare the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that overthrew the Brunswick family in Saint Petersburg.","British newspapers reported the sudden change of ruler in Russia after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, often repeating the new government's own account of why the infant emperor had fallen."],"abstract":"The <b>coup of 1741</b> was the seizure of power on the night of 24–25 November 1741 by the Julian calendar, 5–6 December by the Gregorian, that made Elizabeth, Peter the Great's daughter, empress in place of the infant Ivan VI.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regent, Ivan's mother Anna Leopoldovna, had been warned again and again, by Ostermann among others, but refused to believe the reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elizabeth's surgeon Lestocq urged her on, and the French envoy La Chétardie, whose court wanted Russia busy with troubles at home, supplied money, while Sweden went to war partly in concert with her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plot rested on soldiers of the Preobrazhensky Guards, among them a former merchant named Grünstein, until about thirty grenadiers were in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was hastened by talk that the regent meant to make herself empress, a step her ministers had debated at Ostermann's house, and by orders sending Guards battalions friendly to Elizabeth to the Swedish front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At midnight she went to the grenadiers' barracks, where about 300 men swore to follow her, and led them through the snow to the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The palace guard did not resist, the regent, her husband and children were taken in their beds, and Münnich, Ostermann and other ministers were seized before dawn without bloodshed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The grenadiers who had carried her to power were promoted, ennobled and given estates, and her manifesto said she had answered the prayers of her faithful subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kluchevsky saw a burst of national feeling behind it, pointing to soldiers who broke into German houses, though foreigners such as Lestocq and Grünstein had helped prepare it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British newspapers followed the sudden change closely, but much of what they printed came from texts that suited the new government in Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Hermann von Manstein, <i>Contemporary Memoirs of Russia, from the Year 1727 to 1744</i>, ed. David Hume (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1856), 316–24, https://archive.org/details/contemporarymem00mansgoog. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton; Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 50–65, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 313–15, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","О. Д. Столяров, «Дворцовый переворот ноября 1741 года и иностранцы в российской правящей элите», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 8/2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvortsovyy-perevorot-noyabrya-1741-goda-i-inostrantsy-v-rossiyskoy-pravyaschey-elite. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. П. Артеменкова, «Британские газеты о дворцовом перевороте Елизаветы Петровны 25 ноября 1741 года», <i>Клио</i>, no. 6 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/britanskie-gazety-o-dvortsovom-perevorote-elizavety-petrovny-25-noyabrya-1741-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лысцова, «„Для суще постоянного, безопасного и благополучного... нашего Отечества пребывания“: поиск путей решения кризиса престолонаследия в России в 1741 году», <i>Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dlya-susche-postoyannogo-bezopasnogo-i-blagopoluchnogo-nashego-otechestva-prebyvaniya-poisk-putey-resheniya-krizisa-prestolonaslediya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the coup succeed so easily?","a":"Because the government ignored every warning and the Guards were on Elizabeth's side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regent laughed at reports from Ostermann and from abroad, and when grenadiers entered the palace the sentries made no resistance, so the whole family was in custody within half an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did France pay for a Russian coup?","a":"Because its court wanted Russia weakened by trouble at home so that it could not interfere in the rest of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its envoy gave Elizabeth money to win supporters and advised her surgeon on the plot, and Sweden opened a war concerted partly with her party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the overthrow presented as a rescue from foreigners?","a":"Because a lawful emperor had been deposed, which needed justifying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sermons and manifestos described the fallen ministers as foreign agents, though several foreigners had helped Elizabeth plan the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-328": {"questions":["Russian courts went on condemning people to die under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the sentences were not carried out and flogging, branding and exile took their place.","Elizabeth's vow before an icon that no one would die by her order led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for which later writers praised her as the 'most gentle' empress."],"abstract":"The <b>moratorium on the death penalty</b> introduced by Empress Elizabeth was the practice, from the start of her reign in 1741, of letting no death sentence be carried out, so that executions practically ceased in Russia for twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before seizing the throne she is said to have vowed before an icon of the Saviour that if she succeeded no one would be put to death by her order during her reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first test came within weeks, when the fallen ministers were condemned to death and Ostermann's head was already on the block before the sentence was commuted to exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Senate resisted, resolving in October 1743 to keep the death penalty and reminding her how often Peter the Great had used it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of May 1744 then required every case ending in a death sentence to be sent up to the empress, who commuted the sentences that came before her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The rule was never proclaimed as a law with fanfare: officials received half-secret instructions, partly, it was said, for fear that criminals would multiply if they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Courts went on passing death sentences, and the law gave new weight to 'political death', in which the condemned was laid on the block or led to the gallows and then flogged with the knout and exiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the 1750s those sentenced to death were flogged, had their nostrils torn and were branded on forehead and cheeks with the three letters of the Russian word for 'thief' before going to hard labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When a draft code of 1754 restored harsh penalties, Bain reports that she refused to sign it as 'written not with ink, but with blood', while Kluchevsky has her yielding in tears to the Holy Synod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of her reign a generation had grown up that had never seen a scaffold, and court records of the 1760s still cite her decrees as binding rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 357, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton; Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 63–64, 136, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","Ю. А. Ростовцева, «О смертной казни в „Счастливом обществе“: законодательство „Кроткой Елисавет“ и законничество А. П. Сумарокова», <i>Rhema. Рема</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-smertnoy-kazni-v-schastlivom-obschestve-zakonodatelstvo-krotkoy-elisavet-i-zakonnichestvo-a-p-sumarokova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Н. Марасинова, «Наказание покаянием в России в XVIII в.: церковные практики на службе у светского государства», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 12. Политические науки</i>, no. 5 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nakazanie-pokayaniem-v-rossii-v-xviii-v-tserkovnye-praktiki-na-sluzhbe-u-svetskogo-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. М. Чирскова, «Сыск и наказания „воров“ и „разбойников“ в правовой культуре периода царствования Елизаветы Петровны», <i>Вестник РГГУ</i>, no. 8 (2020): 35–51, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sysk-i-nakazaniya-vorov-i-razboynikov-v-pravovoy-kulture-perioda-tsarstvovaniya-elizavety-petrovny. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Петрова, «Практика наказаний в уголовном праве в России в 1760-е годы», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/praktika-nakazaniy-v-ugolovnom-prave-v-rossii-v-1760-e-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the moratorium kept half-secret?","a":"Because her ministers feared that if criminals knew no one would be executed, crime would increase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the decree was not promulgated with the usual public explanation; instead officials got quiet instructions to send death sentences up for the empress to decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Senate resist the empress?","a":"Because the senators thought fear of death essential to order, and they pointed to Peter the Great, who had used executions freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When she ordered soldiers convicted of murder and looting pardoned in 1743, the Senate refused and resolved to keep the death penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the moratorium not make punishment mild?","a":"Because other penalties took the place of death. The condemned could be led to the scaffold as if to die, then flogged with the knout, mutilated, branded and sent for life to hard labour or to Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Torture during investigation also continued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-329": {"questions":["Written at Freiberg in Saxony in 1739, the ode by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the capture of Khotin set Russian verse on a new metrical footing that later poets followed.","Russian writers came to compare <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Newton and Franklin, though no sure discovery is credited to him and one study calls his fame a national myth."],"abstract":"<b>Mikhail Lomonosov</b> was a Russian scientist, poet and scholar, born into a peasant family of the Kholmogory district near the White Sea and ranked in Russia among the founders of its science and literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His birth is usually dated 8 November 1711 by the Julian calendar, but the parish register was lost and the year rests on a colleague's reckoning after his death, so it may be wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Moscow he learned Latin, Greek and rhetoric at the Slavonic-Greek-Latin Academy, then went to the Academy of Sciences in Saint Petersburg and in 1736 to Germany, studying under Christian Wolff at Marburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Saint Petersburg in 1741, he founded Russia's first chemical laboratory, opened a glass factory, revived mosaic art and from 1758 ran the Academy's university and gymnasium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He also wrote odes, histories and a theory of three styles for Russian, and his ode on the capture of Khotin, composed at Freiberg in 1739, moved Russian verse to a new metre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His career depended on patrons, above all Ivan Shuvalov, Empress Elizabeth's favourite, to whom he addressed his verse 'Letter on the Usefulness of Glass'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are best remembered together for Moscow University, founded in 1755, for which Lomonosov wrote a plan of three faculties, at least twelve professors and a gymnasium to prepare students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After his death in 1765 his fame grew steadily, from Pushkin's lines on the fisherman's boy who would become a helper of tsars to the Soviet naming of Moscow University after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian writers came to set him beside Newton and Franklin, yet no sure discovery or decisive insight is universally credited to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent study treats his towering stature as a national myth built over two centuries, while others stress how hard it was for a peasant's son to make his way among academicians and courtiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Steven A. Usitalo, <i>The Invention of Mikhail Lomonosov: A Russian National Myth</i> (Boston: Academic Studies Press, 2013), https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-30897. [Open access]","И. П. Кулакова, «Михаил Ломоносов: жизненные стратегии в контексте эпохи», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mihail-lomonosov-zhiznennye-strategii-v-kontekste-epohi. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Кротов, «Загадка даты рождения Ломоносова», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 69, no. 1 (2024): 5–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zagadka-daty-rozhdeniya-lomonosova. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Травников и Л. А. Ольшевская, «„Лавровы вьются там венцы...“ (поэтика „Оды на взятие Хотина“ М. В. Ломоносова)», <i>Литературоведческий журнал</i>, no. 29 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lavrovy-vyutsya-tam-ventsy-poetika-ody-na-vzyatie-hotina-m-v-lomonosova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Гутнов, «Эволюция образа М. В. Ломоносова в русской историографии», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 10. Журналистика</i>, no. 5 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-obraza-m-v-lomonosova-v-russkoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Сурин и М. И. Панов, «И. И. Шувалов – создатель Московского университета», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление</i>, no. 1 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/i-i-shuvalov-sozdatel-moskovskogo-universiteta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Лунин, П. М. Зоркий и И. Е. Лубнина, «Российские университеты в XVIII веке», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 2. Химия</i> 40, no. 5 (1999), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskie-universitety-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a peasant's son need powerful patrons?","a":"Because learning alone did not bring rank or security in his world. Returning from Germany in 1741 he found that his training did not give him a fitting place in the Academy, and he spent years fighting for status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shuvalov's protection gave him access to the court and the means to pursue his projects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his date of birth uncertain?","a":"Because the parish register that recorded it was lost in the 18th century, and he himself never wrote down the day or year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The accepted year comes from a colleague's calculation after his death, and it may sometimes have suited him to make himself seem older than he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians call his reputation a myth?","a":"Because his stature grew far beyond what his recorded achievements show. Russian writers long ranked him with Newton and Franklin, but no clear discovery is universally attributed to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His image was shaped by jubilees, by Pushkin and by the Soviet state, which gave his name to Moscow University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mikhail Lomonosov"},
    "ru-330": {"questions":["Ivan Shuvalov became the first curator of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for which Lomonosov had drafted a plan insisting on at least twelve professors and a preparatory gymnasium.","Russia's students still keep Tatiana's Day as their holiday because Shuvalov chose it, in honour of his mother, for the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1755."],"abstract":"<b>Moscow University</b>, Russia's oldest surviving university, was founded by a decree of Empress Elizabeth signed on 12 January 1755 by the Julian calendar and opened that April on the anniversary of her coronation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The project came from the courtier Ivan Shuvalov, the empress's favourite and the university's first curator, working with Mikhail Lomonosov, who drew up its structure and staff in the summer of 1754.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was to serve nobles and commoners 'after the example of European universities, where people of every rank freely enjoy learning', with two gymnasia, one for each, and none for serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lomonosov insisted on at least twelve professors in the three faculties of law, medicine and philosophy, and on a gymnasium, without which, he wrote, a university was like ploughland without seed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Students began in the philosophy faculty and only then moved on to medicine or law, and the first teachers were Lomonosov's own pupils from the Academy of Sciences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The decision was taken in 1754 while the court was in Moscow, and the new school succeeded where the Academy's small university in Saint Petersburg, always short of students, had largely failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shuvalov chose Tatiana's Day for the founding in honour of his mother, and Tatiana's Day, now kept on 25 January, became a holiday for Russian students in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In its first years it took children of every estate except serfs, and nobles, who appeared among its students only in 1759, were few beside the commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Contemporaries gave Shuvalov the credit for the idea, and Kluchevsky marvelled that an empress who believed England could be reached by land founded Russia's first university.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later politics moved the credit about: monarchist writers exalted Shuvalov, Soviet ones made Lomonosov the sole founder, and in the Soviet period the university was named after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 355, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton; Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 158, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","В. В. Пономарева и Л. Б. Хорошилова, «Рождение университета: сподвижник М. В. Ломоносова Иван Иванович Шувалов», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 5 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rozhdenie-universiteta-spodvizhnik-m-v-lomonosova-ivan-ivanovich-shuvalov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Сурин и М. И. Панов, «И. И. Шувалов – создатель Московского университета», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление</i>, no. 1 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/i-i-shuvalov-sozdatel-moskovskogo-universiteta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Лунин, П. М. Зоркий и И. Е. Лубнина, «Российские университеты в XVIII веке», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 2. Химия</i> 40, no. 5 (1999), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskie-universitety-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Гутнов, «Эволюция образа М. В. Ломоносова в русской историографии», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 10. Журналистика</i>, no. 5 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-obraza-m-v-lomonosova-v-russkoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Steven A. Usitalo, <i>The Invention of Mikhail Lomonosov: A Russian National Myth</i> (Boston: Academic Studies Press, 2013), https://archive.org/details/oapen-20.500.12657-30897. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the university need gymnasia of its own?","a":"Because Moscow had no properly organised schools where young people could first learn the languages and basic subjects needed to follow professors' lectures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lomonosov warned that a university without a gymnasium was like ploughland without seed, so two were founded with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were nobles and commoners taught apart at first?","a":"Because the project kept the estates separate at school but meant them to study together once they became students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Learning side by side at the higher level was supposed to spur both on, while the separate gymnasia respected the ranks of the day; serfs were not admitted at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the credit for the founding shifted over time?","a":"Because each age read its own values into it. Monarchist historians praised the courtier Shuvalov, while in the Soviet period Lomonosov, a peasant's son, became the sole founder and gave the university his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Recent work credits the partnership of the two men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Moscow State University"},
    "ru-371": {"questions":["Only two battalions of infantry with four guns were sent to defend eastern Georgia under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they were withdrawn when the Ottomans declared war in 1787.","By the first article of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Georgian king renounced all dependence on Persia, a step that helped bring Agha Mohammad Khan’s army to Tbilisi in 1795."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Georgievsk</b> was the agreement signed on 24 July 1783 by the Julian calendar, 4 August by the Gregorian, at the Russian fortress of Georgievsk in the North Caucasus, by which the eastern Georgian kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti placed itself under Russian protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> King Heraclius II had long sought help against Persia and the Ottoman Empire, and his envoys Ivane Bagration-Mukhransky and Garsevan Chavchavadze signed for him, while General Pavel Potemkin negotiated for Catherine II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first article Heraclius renounced all vassalage to Persia or any other power, and in the second the empress promised to guarantee the integrity of his lands and to keep his dynasty on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kartli-Kakheti gave up an independent foreign policy, agreeing to deal with its neighbours’ envoys only with the Russian frontier commander’s consent, while Russia promised to protect Georgian interests in its own treaties with Turkey and Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ratified in January 1784, the treaty has been remembered both as a friendly protection that freed Georgia from Persian and Ottoman pressure and as the first step towards losing Georgian independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The military guarantee behind it was thin, since a separate article sent only two battalions of infantry with four guns to defend the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To bring them south, Potemkin turned the bridle path through the Darial gorge into a rough road, and the two battalions entered Tbilisi on 3 November 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Ottomans declared war on Russia in 1787, Heraclius had already made his own agreement with the pasha of Akhaltsikhe, and on 29 August the battalions were ordered out of Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian protection had angered Persia without defending Georgia, and the Persian ruler Agha Mohammad Khan took and sacked Tbilisi on 12 September 1795, before help ordered from St Petersburg could arrive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In February 1801 a manifesto read in Tbilisi’s Sioni Cathedral turned the protectorate into annexation, and Alexander I’s government confirmed the step later that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["М. А. Волхонский, «Военная политика Российской империи в Закавказье в 1783–1796 гг.», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 9 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennaya-politika-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-zakavkazie-v-1783-1796-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. К. Шаов, З. А. Жаде и Ф. В. Чирков, «Установление российского протектората над Картли-Кахетинским царством в 1783 году: международно-правовые аспекты», <i>Юридическая наука</i>, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ustanovlenie-rossiyskogo-protektorata-nad-kartli-kahetinskim-tsarstvom-v-1783-godu-mezhdunarodno-pravovye-aspekty. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Гогитидзе, «Георгиевский трактат 1783 года: к проблеме утверждения России в Грузии», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/georgievskiy-traktat-1783-goda-k-probleme-utverzhdeniya-rossii-v-gruzii. [in Russian] [Open access]","John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 19–21, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","А. К. Дудайти, «Место Грузии в \"Восточной\" политике России периода нового времени», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 28, no. 22 (165) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mesto-gruzii-v-vostochnoy-politike-rossii-perioda-novogo-vremeni. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Г. Джахиева, «Историко-правовые аспекты внешнеполитической деятельности Картли-Кахетии: от Георгиевска 1783 г. до турецко-картлийского договора 1786 г.», <i>Юридический вестник Дагестанского государственного университета</i> 21, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-pravovye-aspekty-vneshnepoliticheskoy-deyatelnosti-kartli-kahetii-ot-georgievska-1783-g-do-turetsko-kartliyskogo-dogovora. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Heraclius II seek Russian protection?","a":"Because his small Christian kingdom lay between Persia and the Ottoman Empire, and Persia still counted Georgia as a vassal state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Raiding bands from the Ottoman pashalik of Akhaltsikhe kept wasting the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russia had beaten the Turks in 1774 and was now the strongest power north of the mountains, and the treaty promised to guarantee his lands and keep his dynasty on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Russian battalions withdrawn in 1787?","a":"Because a new war with the Ottoman Empire began that summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Heraclius had also made his own agreement with the pasha of Akhaltsikhe, against the article that bound him to act with the Russian commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The order of 29 August said that the battalions’ presence had only won the king more enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the protectorate end?","a":"In annexation rather than independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Persian troops sacked Tbilisi in 1795, the last king, George XII, asked for a closer union, and in February 1801 a manifesto read in Tbilisi joined Kartli-Kakheti to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander I’s council debated the step and confirmed it later in 1801, ending the Georgian monarchy that the treaty had promised to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Georgievsk"},
    "ru-372": {"questions":["Catherine the Great gave Shelikhov and Golikov swords and gold medals in 1788 but refused them a loan and a monopoly over the trade of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which waited for Paul’s charter.","Monks from the Valaam and Konevets monasteries, chosen on Catherine’s decision, left St Petersburg at the end of 1793 to carry Orthodoxy to the peoples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by way of Irkutsk."],"abstract":"<b>Russian America</b> was the name for Russia’s possessions in north-western North America, from the Aleutian Islands and Alaska down the Pacific coast, held from the voyages of 1741 until their sale to the United States in 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It grew out of the fur trade, as merchants and hunters from Siberia pushed east from Kamchatka in pursuit of the sea otter, whose fur was among the most valuable in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hunting itself was done largely by Aleut hunters in skin boats, and the Native peoples of the islands were subdued by the newcomers, who then regulated their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Orthodox Church followed the traders, and a mission of monks from the Valaam and Konevets monasteries, chosen on Catherine II’s decision, left St Petersburg for Alaska at the end of 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1799 the colony was run by the Russian-American Company from Kodiak and later from Sitka, until a treaty signed in Washington in March 1867 sold the territory for 7.2 million dollars in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Under Catherine the Great, Russian America was still a scatter of rival trading ventures rather than a colony, and its first permanent settlement dates only from 1784.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 August that year the merchant Grigory Shelikhov anchored his two ships in a bay on Kodiak Island, naming it Three Saints after his own vessel, and began building a fortified post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His conquest of the island’s Alutiiq people was violent, for Shelikhov admitted only that some had probably been killed, while an Alutiiq eyewitness later spoke of up to 300 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Shelikhov and his partner Golikov then asked the empress for exclusive rights over the islands and coasts their men had reached, and a committee of commerce recommended a monopoly and an interest-free loan of 200,000 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Catherine’s decree of 28 September 1788 refused the loan because of foreign wars and gave the partners control only of the places they actually occupied, with swords and gold medals as their reward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Hubert Howe Bancroft, <i>History of Alaska, 1730–1885</i> (San Francisco: A. L. Bancroft, 1886), 223–24, 305–9, https://archive.org/details/historyalaska01nemogoog. [Open access]","Clarence Leroy Andrews, <i>The Story of Sitka, the Historic Outpost of the Northwest Coast</i> (Seattle: Lowman, 1922), 16–24, 60, https://archive.org/details/storyofsitkahist00andruoft. [Open access]","Л. А. Муравьева, «Экономическая жизнь Русской Америки в XVIII веке», <i>Финансы и кредит</i>, no. 17 (353) (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ekonomicheskaya-zhizn-russkoy-ameriki-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Ф. Лукин, «О Русской Аляске и её правителе А. А. Баранове», <i>Арктика и Север</i>, no. 45 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-russkoy-alyaske-i-eyo-pravitele-a-a-baranove. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Савельев и Ю. С. Егорова, «Взаимоотношения Русской православной церкви и купеческо-промысловых объединений на Аляске в конце XVIII века», <i>Вестник Московского государственного лингвистического университета. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (806) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzaimootnosheniya-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkvi-i-kupechesko-promyslovyh-obedineniy-na-alyaske-v-kontse-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лавров и Э. Е. Протасова, «Оценка деятельности морской экспедиции Г. И. Шелихова в отношении аборигенов острова Кадьяк в отечественной историографии», <i>Вестник Шадринского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 4 (52) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otsenka-deyatelnosti-morskoy-ekspeditsii-g-i-shelihova-v-otnoshenii-aborigenov-ostrova-kadyak-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Плотников, «Договор о продаже Аляски 1867 г.: история и право», <i>Американистика: актуальные подходы и современные исследования</i> 13 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dogovor-o-prodazhe-alyaski-1867-g-istoriya-i-pravo. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russians go to Alaska at all?","a":"Because of the sea otter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Siberian merchants and hunters followed its valuable fur east from Kamchatka and along the Aleutian islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian party of a hundred skin boats could take 2,000 skins on one expedition, and as the animals grew scarce in one area the hunters had to move on to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine refuse Shelikhov the loan he asked for?","a":"Because Russia was fighting foreign wars in 1788 and the treasury would not pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee had recommended both exclusive rights and a loan of 200,000 roubles, but the decree of 28 September granted control only over the places the partners actually held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rival traders stayed free to work the neighbouring coasts until Paul chartered a single company in 1799.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How violent was the conquest of Kodiak?","a":"Very violent, though the accounts disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Shelikhov wrote that his men fired mostly at rocks and huts, and admitted only that some islanders had probably been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An Alutiiq eyewitness later spoke of up to 300 dead, and after the fight the chiefs had to hand over their children as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A naval officer who inspected the colonies in 1818 accused Shelikhov of inflating his achievements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian colonization of North America"},
    "ru-373": {"questions":["Destroyed by the Tlingit in June 1802, the fort of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> near Sitka was retaken in 1804 with the help of the naval ship <i>Neva</i> and rebuilt as New Archangel.","Shelikhov’s son-in-law Mikhail Buldakov directed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from St Petersburg from 1799 to 1827, while the merchant Alexander Baranov ran its colonies until 1818."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian-American Company</b> was the chartered trading company that held a monopoly over Russia’s colonies in North America and served as their government from 1799 until the sale of Alaska in 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was formed by merging rival Siberian fur companies, among them the firm of the late Grigory Shelikhov, under an act of union signed in August 1798.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first charter, confirmed by Paul I on 8 July 1799 by the Julian calendar, granted for twenty years the exclusive right to hunt, trade and make discoveries from latitude 55 degrees north to the Bering Strait and on the Aleutian, Kuril and other islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The company could found and fortify settlements, call on the emperor’s land and sea forces for help and judge minor cases, so that a merchant enterprise became the government of a colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1800 its head office moved from Irkutsk to St Petersburg, the imperial family bought shares, and new charters in 1821 and 1844 renewed its privileges until the territory was sold to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the colonies the company’s chief manager was the merchant Alexander Baranov, who had run Shelikhov’s posts since 1790 and led the colonies until 1818.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first fort near Sitka was destroyed by the Tlingit in June 1802, and only in 1804, with help from the naval ship <i>Neva</i>, did the company retake the site and found its new capital there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The furs were taken mostly by Aleut hunters in skin boats, sometimes in parties of hundreds of boats, and the sea otter and the fur seal were the most valuable catch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1818 the chief managers were naval officers rather than merchants, a sign of the state’s growing hold over a company that has often been compared with the British East India Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its shareholders and directors sat in St Petersburg, far from the colonies, and its first director, Shelikhov’s son-in-law Mikhail Buldakov, held the post from 1799 to 1827.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Hubert Howe Bancroft, <i>History of Alaska, 1730–1885</i> (San Francisco: A. L. Bancroft, 1886), 378–82, https://archive.org/details/historyalaska01nemogoog. [Open access]","Clarence Leroy Andrews, <i>The Story of Sitka, the Historic Outpost of the Northwest Coast</i> (Seattle: Lowman, 1922), 16–24, 60, https://archive.org/details/storyofsitkahist00andruoft. [Open access]","Е. Е. Степанова, «Органы управления Российско-американской компании», <i>Научный вестник Омской академии МВД России</i>, no. 1 (72) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organy-upravleniya-rossiysko-amerikanskoy-kompanii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Е. Михалев, «Сравнительно-правовой анализ деятельности Российско-американской и Британской Ост-Индской компаний в XIX веке», <i>Образование и право</i>, no. 6 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sravnitelno-pravovoy-analiz-deyatelnosti-rossiysko-amerikanskoy-i-britanskoy-ost-indskoy-kompaniy-v-xix-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Ермолаев, «Российско-американская компания и ее роль в освоении Дальнего Востока», <i>ЭКО. Всероссийский экономический журнал</i>, no. 3 (513) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiysko-amerikanskaya-kompaniya-i-ee-rol-v-osvoenii-dalnego-vostoka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Петров и Ю. С. Егорова, «Роль Михаила Булдакова в историко-культурном наследии Русской Америки», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-mihaila-buldakova-v-istoriko-kulturnom-nasledii-russkoy-ameriki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Ф. Лукин, «О Русской Аляске и её правителе А. А. Баранове», <i>Арктика и Север</i>, no. 45 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-russkoy-alyaske-i-eyo-pravitele-a-a-baranove. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia give a private company a monopoly over its colonies?","a":"Because rival traders had been competing on the same coasts, and a single company could organise the trade and defend the claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charter of 1799 merged the existing firms, shut out anyone who refused to join and let the company found forts and call on the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state kept control by writing the company’s charters and auditing its affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the company move its centre to Sitka?","a":"Because the sea otters near the western islands were growing scarce and the richest hunting now lay in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Baranov first built a post there in 1799, lost it to a Tlingit attack in June 1802 and retook the site in 1804 with a naval ship’s help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war with the Tlingit lasted from 1802 to 1804.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Who actually did the hunting?","a":"Mostly Aleut hunters rather than Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They went out in skin boats in spring, in parties that could number up to 500 boats, and approached the sea otters closely before striking them with arrows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The company had the right to use Aleut labour for hunting sea animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian-American Company"},
    "ru-374": {"questions":["Catherine the Great called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a rebel worse than Pugachev when she read his <i>Journey from St Petersburg to Moscow</i>, noting that it ended by praising Franklin.","Sent by Catherine to study at Leipzig and later put in charge of the St Petersburg customs house, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> learned English at thirty to deal with the trade with England."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander Radishchev</b> was a Russian writer and official whose book <i>A Journey from St Petersburg to Moscow</i>, printed in 1790, attacked serfdom and autocracy and brought him a sentence of death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was one of a group of young men sent by Catherine II to study at the University of Leipzig, where he read philosophy and, for his own interest, medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Russia he served in the College of Commerce and later ran the St Petersburg customs house, where he learned English for the trade with England and came to know its literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His earlier writings already included a life of Fyodor Ushakov in 1773 and an ode, ‘Liberty’, written between 1781 and 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Journey</i> is modelled on Sterne’s <i>Sentimental Journey</i>, but it is also a political pamphlet that called openly for freeing the serfs, almost half a century before such calls became common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The book was printed on a press in Radishchev’s own house early in 1790 and handed out to friends without the censor’s approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Catherine read it she called the author a rebel worse than Pugachev who praised Franklin, and in her notes on the book she insisted that no peasant in the world was better treated than the Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was condemned to death, but the sentence was at once commuted to loss of rank, his order of St Vladimir and his nobility, with ten years of exile at Ilimsk in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In exile he kept writing, and Paul I recalled him after Catherine’s death, a reversal that historians of the reign have counted among its just acts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander I advanced him to higher honours, but in 1802, after an acquaintance warned him that his return to his youthful ideas might bring a second banishment, he took his own life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Leo Wiener, ed., <i>Anthology of Russian Literature from the Earliest Period to the Present Time</i>, pt. 1 (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1902), 326–28, 361–62, https://archive.org/details/pt1anthologyofru00wienuoft. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 52, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1894), 300–301, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","И. В. Упоров и Г. В. Кравченко, «Радищев: общественно-политические взгляды и гражданская позиция», <i>История. Культурология. Политология</i>, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/radischev-obschestvenno-politicheskie-vzglyady-i-grazhdanskaya-pozitsiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Е. Воронин, «Александр Николаевич Радищев и его замыслы политического переустройства России», <i>Власть</i> 33, no. 5 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-nikolaevich-radischev-i-ego-zamysly-politicheskogo-pereustroystva-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Н. Ермолаев, «\"Я попал в самый ябеднический край…\"», <i>Вестник Псковского государственного университета. Серия: Социально-гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ya-popal-v-samyy-yabednicheskiy-kray. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Артемьева и М. И. Микешин, «Понятие \"революция\" в философско-политическом дискурсе конца XVIII века в России», <i>Вестник Ленинградского государственного университета им. А. С. Пушкина</i>, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ponyatie-revolyutsiya-v-filosofsko-politicheskom-diskurse-kontsa-xviii-veka-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine react so harshly to the <i>Journey</i>?","a":"Because she read it as a call to revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> She called its author a rebel worse than Pugachev, whose rising had shaken her empire, and noted that he praised Franklin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Coming in 1790, when the French Revolution had already startled her, the book looked part of a wider danger, and in her notes she insisted that Russian serfs were the best-treated peasants in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What punishment did Radishchev actually receive?","a":"He was sentenced to death, but the sentence was softened at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Instead he lost his rank, his order of St Vladimir and his nobility, and was sent for ten years to the fort of Ilimsk in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There a former governor of Pskov, Ivan Pil, gave him important support and protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the book more than a work of literature?","a":"Because it was a political pamphlet as well as a sentimental journey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It judged Russian institutions by the most advanced liberalism of France and North America and called for freeing the serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its author was banished for advocating the very theories that Catherine had once set out in her own Instruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander Radishchev"},
    "ru-375": {"questions":["In 1779 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leased the Moscow University press for ten years and within three years had published more books than it had issued in the previous 24.","Founded as a charity whose proceeds kept schools for the poor, the journal <i>Morning Light</i> of 1777 was one of many ventures of the Freemason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his circle."],"abstract":"<b>Nikolay Novikov</b> was a Russian journalist, publisher and philanthropist of Catherine the Great’s reign, the most energetic spreader of books and learning in 18th-century Russia, whose work ended with his arrest in 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1769 he launched the satirical journal <i>The Drone</i>, whose sharper attacks on vice set it against the milder <i>All Kinds of Things</i>, a journal issued under the empress’s own supervision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Catherine’s part in the rival journal became known he toned down his attacks, and he went on to edit <i>The Painter</i> and <i>The Purse</i> before all the satirical journals ceased in 1774.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also published a large body of chronicles and documents on Russia’s past, which he saw as a service to the young science of Russian history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1779 he leased the Moscow University press for ten years, and within three years he had published more books than it had issued in the previous 24, selling them through bookshops across Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Novikov was a zealous Freemason, and with Johann Schwarz he led the Moscow circle of Martinists and Rosicrucians whose Friendly Society, renamed the Typographical Company in 1784, published books on a great scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His journal <i>Morning Light</i>, begun in September 1777, was founded for charity, its proceeds going to support schools for the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Catherine had never trusted the Masons, and after the French Revolution she suspected Novikov of belonging to a secret society that aimed to overturn the existing order, though Metropolitan Platon, sent to test his faith, praised him as a good Christian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In April 1792 he was arrested in Moscow on the orders of the commander-in-chief, Prince Prozorovsky, declared a state criminal and shut in the Shlisselburg fortress under a sentence of 15 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Paul I freed him in the first days of his reign in 1796, and among the writers he had encouraged was the young Nikolay Karamzin, who wrote for his magazine <i>Children’s Reading for the Heart and Mind</i> in 1787–1789.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Leo Wiener, ed., <i>Anthology of Russian Literature from the Earliest Period to the Present Time</i>, pt. 1 (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1902), 326–28, https://archive.org/details/pt1anthologyofru00wienuoft. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1894), 333–34, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 38–39, 52, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Р. З. Акавов, «Просветительская и журнально-издательская деятельность Н. И. Новикова как фактор формирования в России гражданского общества во второй половине XVIII в.», <i>Известия Дагестанского государственного педагогического университета. Общественные и гуманитарные науки</i> 12, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prosvetitelskaya-i-zhurnalno-izdatelskaya-deyatelnost-n-i-novikova-kak-faktor-formirovaniya-v-rossii-grazhdanskogo-obschestva-vo-vtoroy. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Б. Свердлов, «Н. И. Новиков как историк России древней и новой», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (20) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/n-i-novikov-kak-istorik-rossii-drevney-i-novoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Д. Кочеткова, «Н. И. Новиков в жизни и творчестве Н. М. Карамзина», <i>XVIII век</i> 31 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/h-i-novikov-v-zhizni-i-tvorchestve-n-m-karamzina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Чубасова, «Журнал \"Утренний свет\" как образец масонской журналистики», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 13 (93) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zhurnal-utrenniy-svet-kak-obrazets-masonskoy-zhurnalistiki-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine turn against Novikov?","a":"Because she distrusted the Masons and mystics among whom he was a leader, and the French Revolution made such societies look dangerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She suspected him of belonging to a secret society that aimed to overturn the existing order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian book publishing had peaked in the 1780s, and his undertakings collapsed in the 1790s, when fear of the Revolution turned her towards reaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reward was fifteen years in a fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his publishing matter so much?","a":"Because it multiplied the books within reach of Russian readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leasing the Moscow University press from 1779, he printed more in three years than it had in the previous 24 and sold through bookshops across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most Russian books of the 18th century appeared in Catherine’s reign, and his work and Schwarz’s helped make that possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His companies also issued philosophy and the works of English economists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Novikov use satire?","a":"Through his journal <i>The Drone</i>, founded in 1769, which scourged vice more sharply than the journal the empress herself supervised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The satirical journals split into a mild camp around <i>All Kinds of Things</i> and a sharper one modelled on <i>The Drone</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Catherine’s role in the rival became known he softened his attacks, and by 1774 all such journals had stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nikolay Novikov"},
    "ru-376": {"questions":["Catherine the Great mocked <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in comedies such as <i>The Siberian Shaman</i>, likening its mystical lodges to shamans who extorted money from credulous followers.","Rival English and Swedish systems competed for the loyalty of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Catherine II, while in Moscow an order of Golden Rosicrucians took shape in 1783."],"abstract":"<b>Freemasonry in Russia</b> was the network of Masonic lodges, fraternal societies devoted to moral and mystical self-improvement and imported from western Europe, that spread among the Russian nobility in the 18th century and was banned in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later Russian Masons liked to link their order with Peter the Great, but the documentary record begins in 1731, when John Phillips, an Englishman in Russian service, was appointed provincial grand master for Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russians themselves are first known to have joined around 1740, when the general James Keith became grand master, and the early lodges were largely made up of foreigners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Catherine II rival systems competed, for Ivan Yelagin received a patent from London in 1772 as provincial grand master of the English system, while supporters of the Swedish rite united in 1778 under their own chapter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Men joined for many reasons, from religious seeking to friendship and fashion, and one later confessed that he had met his first Mason at a drinking party early in 1785.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the 1780s the liveliest centre was Moscow, where an order of Golden Rosicrucians was founded in 1783 and Nikolay Novikov and Johann Schwarz turned Masonic circles into an engine of publishing and education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Catherine distrusted the movement’s mysticism and attacked it in her own comedies, likening the Masons to Siberian shamans who extorted money from the credulous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the French Revolution her suspicion hardened into repression, and the fall of Novikov in 1792 broke up the ventures of the Moscow Masons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Alexander I the lodges revived and enjoyed a short heyday, and the divided branches of the Swedish rite reunited and worked together until the ban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A decree of 1 August 1822 classed Masonic meetings with secret societies and closed the lodges, though the Swedish rite did not so much stop as go underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ю. С. Крюкова, «Социокультурные предпосылки распространения масонских идей в России в XVIII веке», <i>Ярославский педагогический вестник</i>, no. 1–2 (38) (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsiokulturnye-predposylki-rasprostraneniya-masonskih-idey-v-rossii-v-xviii-veke-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Е. Киясов, «Масонство и православная империя: начало противостояния», <i>Новый исторический вестник</i>, no. 18 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/masonstvo-i-pravoslavnaya-imperiya-nachalo-protivostoyaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Е. Кондаков, «Как становились масонами в России в XVIII веке», <i>Aliter</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kak-stanovilis-masonami-v-rossii-v-xviii-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. А. Королёв, «Феномен шведского масонства в русской и европейской истории», <i>Вестник государственного и муниципального управления</i> 10, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-shvedskogo-masonstva-v-russkoy-i-evropeyskoy-istorii. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1894), 357–58, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 38–39, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Е. Н. Соколова, «Английский след в русском масонстве», <i>Вестник Московского государственного лингвистического университета. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 10 (783) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/angliyskiy-sled-v-russkom-masonstve. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Freemasonry appeal to Russian nobles?","a":"Because it offered several things at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some men came looking for religious and moral answers, others for friendship or fashion, and one later admitted he had been drawn in at a drinking party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lodges were also a link to western Europe, where the English and Swedish systems competing in Russia had begun, and Yelagin called the English lodge ‘our mother’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine distrust the Masons?","a":"Because their mysticism ran against the rational, Voltairean outlook she wanted to spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She ridiculed them in her comedies as deceivers who preyed on the credulous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the French Revolution, when secret societies looked dangerous, she struck at the Moscow Masons through the arrest of Novikov in 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the ban of 1822 end Russian Freemasonry?","a":"Not entirely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decree of 1 August 1822 placed Masonic meetings on a level with secret societies and closed the lodges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> But the Swedish rite, which had worked in Russia since Catherine’s time, did not stop; it went underground instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"History of Freemasonry in Russia"},
    "ru-377": {"questions":["Catherine admitted pushing Austria and Prussia into war with revolutionary France to keep her elbows free, and she used that freedom for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that she agreed with Prussia.","Ringed by Russian soldiers, the diet at Grodno was forced to accept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1793, approving Prussia’s share at a session where silence was declared to be consent."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Partition of Poland</b> was the division of a large part of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth between Russia and Prussia, agreed in a treaty signed at St Petersburg on 23 January 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It followed the Commonwealth’s attempt at reform in the Constitution of 3 May 1791, which Catherine II came to see as a threat once King Stanisław August had endorsed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1792 Russian troops entered the country to support the Confederation of Targowica, a handful of magnates whose plans had been settled with the empress, and even the king went over to the confederation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Prussian troops crossed the frontier on 24 January 1793 and took Danzig, which held out until April, while Russia annexed wide, mostly East Slavic lands in what are now Belarus and Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A diet held at Grodno under Russian guns was made to accept both cessions, Prussia’s at a so-called Dumb Session where silence was declared consent, and the king blamed nearly 150,000 foreign soldiers for its surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The partition took place in the shadow of the French Revolution, which Catherine detested and preferred to call a rebellion or a disorder rather than a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> According to her own confession to her secretary, she had been racking her brains to push Austria and Prussia into the French enterprise so that she would have her elbows free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She agreed to a second partition partly, it has been argued, because Europe’s monarchs needed to stand together against the Revolution, and the treaty’s preamble justified the seizure by ‘the fatal revolution in France’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The partitioning powers claimed that ‘Jacobin’ clubs were at work in Poland, but there was almost no democratic propaganda there, and the label came from the Targowica men themselves, who called supporters of the 1791 constitution ‘Jacobins’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At home the annexation was presented as the return of lands once torn from Rus’, while the execution of Louis XVI in January 1793 had given the French émigrés at her court fresh justification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Howard Lord, <i>The Second Partition of Poland: A Study in Diplomatic History</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1915), 274–75, 384–94, 479, https://archive.org/details/cu31924005692011. [Open access]","О. В. Бабенко, «Стегний П. В. Разделы Польши и дипломатия Екатерины II: 1772, 1793, 1795», реферат, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 3 (2003), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2003-03-016-stegniyp-v-razdely-polshi-i-diplomatiya-ekateriny-ii-1772-1793-1795-m-mezhdunar-otnosheniya-2002-696-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. А. Борисёнок, «Формирование территории Российской империи на польском направлении, особенности и закономерности: дискуссионные вопросы в современной историографии», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-territorii-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-polskom-napravlenii-osobennosti-i-zakonomernosti-diskussionnye-voprosy-v-sovremennoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. П. Пискунова, «Французские эмигранты при дворе Екатерины II», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/frantsuzskie-emigranty-pri-dvore-ekateriny-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Артемьева и М. И. Микешин, «Понятие \"революция\" в философско-политическом дискурсе конца XVIII века в России», <i>Вестник Ленинградского государственного университета им. А. С. Пушкина</i>, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ponyatie-revolyutsiya-v-filosofsko-politicheskom-diskurse-kontsa-xviii-veka-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Коршунова, «Участие России в разделах Польши: политическая игра или геополитическая необходимость?», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i> 10, no. 2 (2003), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchastie-rossii-v-razdelah-polshi-politicheskaya-igra-ili-geopoliticheskaya-neobhodimost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Василюк, «Анализ политической повседневности Августа Понятовского во время гродненских сеймов 1784 г. и 1793 г.», <i>А-фактор: научные исследования и разработки. Гуманитарные науки</i> (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/analiz-politicheskoy-povsednevnosti-avgusta-ponyatovskogo-vo-vremya-grodnenskih-seymov-1784-g-i-1793-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the French Revolution help Catherine in Poland?","a":"Because it kept her neighbours busy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She admitted that she had worked to push Austria and Prussia into the war with France so that her own hands would be free, and the French declaration of war came just as she moved against Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the German powers absorbed in the west, the Russians could largely dictate the partition terms to Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the partitioning powers talk about Jacobins?","a":"Because it gave a respectable cover to an annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The treaty’s preamble spoke of the danger from the fatal revolution in France and claimed that the same spirit of insurrection was ready to break out in Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In fact there was hardly any democratic propaganda in Poland, and the word had been borrowed from Polish opponents of the 1791 constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the partition explained to Russians?","a":"As a recovery of lost lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lands Russia took were mostly East Slavic rather than Polish, and they were described as territory once torn from Rus’ and now returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One recent study argues that this idea entered public statements only in the autumn of 1793, after the treaty, rather than guiding policy from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Second Partition of Poland"},
    "ru-378": {"questions":["Round hats and French dress were banned and thousands were exiled for trifles under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was strangled by officers of his own court in March 1801.","Taking the title of grand master of the Knights of Malta, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew Russia into the war against revolutionary France and later turned to plans to strike at British India."],"abstract":"<b>Paul I</b> was emperor of Russia from November 1796 until March 1801, the only son of Peter III and Catherine II, and his short reign ended in his murder by officers of his own court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Taken from his parents at birth by Empress Elizabeth and later kept by his mother away from affairs of state, he waited for some thirty years at his estate of Gatchina, resenting her favourites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Towards the end of her reign Catherine considered passing him over for his son Alexander, a plan that failed only because Alexander refused to take the throne before his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once emperor, Paul set out to undo his mother’s work, withdrawing the charters she had granted to the nobility and the towns and ending the nobles’ freedom from corporal punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was crowned with his wife in Moscow on Easter Sunday, 5 April 1797, and that day issued new laws on the succession and the imperial family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His rule was marked by sudden and minute regulation, from bans on round hats and French-style dress to a rule that passers-by stop and kneel as the imperial carriage went by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thousands were exiled or imprisoned for trifles, and Alexander I later rehabilitated somewhere between 12,000 and 15,000 such people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not every historian sees only a tyrant, and some have argued that his measures were a deliberate attempt to strengthen autocracy and forestall a revolution like that in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He took the title of grand master of the Knights of Malta, a romance that pulled Russia into the coalition war against France and then, as he turned against Britain, into plans to strike at British India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Distrusted by his family and his guards, he was killed on the night of 11 to 12 March 1801 by the Julian calendar in his newly built fortress-palace, by conspirators acting with his son Alexander’s consent to a deposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Paul the First of Russia, the Son of Catherine the Great</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1913), 27–28, 96–97, 201, 213, 370–71, 455–57, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028412835. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 51–56, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Adam Czartoryski, <i>Memoirs of Prince Adam Czartoryski and His Correspondence with Alexander I</i>, ed. Adam Gielgud, vol. 1 (London: Remington, 1888), 234–36, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincea01czariala. [Open access]","В. Захаров, «Павел I: деспот или реформатор?», <i>Власть</i>, no. 5 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pavel-i-despot-ili-reformator. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. И. Мацейко, «Внутренняя политика Павла I как элемент укрепления самодержавия в Российской империи», <i>Вестник науки</i> 3, no. 1 (94) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnutrennyaya-politika-pavla-i-kak-element-ukrepleniya-samoderzhaviya-v-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Скоробогатов, «Коронация императора Павла I: образ монарха в церемониальной культуре эпохи Просвещения», <i>Карельский научный журнал</i>, no. 1 (10) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/koronatsiya-imperatora-pavla-i-obraz-monarha-v-tseremonialnoy-kulture-epohi-prosvescheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. А. Никишин, «Переворот 11–12 марта 1801 года: современные подходы к изучению вопроса», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 50, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/perevorot-11-12-marta-1801-goda-sovremennye-podhody-k-izucheniyu-voprosa. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Paul resent his mother’s court?","a":"Because she kept him from power for most of his adult life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elizabeth took him from his parents at birth, Catherine excluded him from state affairs and even from the army, and her favourites, Potemkin above all, treated him with contempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For thirty years he waited, and he came to suspect, rightly, that she meant to pass him over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Was Paul simply a tyrant?","a":"Historians disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His bans on French dress, the kneeling before his carriage and thousands of exiles for trifles fit the picture of a despot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others see in his centralising rule and his cult of duty and hierarchy, drawn from Prussian military ideals and the Order of Malta, a deliberate effort to strengthen autocracy and forestall revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did men of his own court kill him?","a":"Because his sudden punishments threatened everyone around him, from guards officers to members of his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plotters, led by the military governor of St Petersburg, acted only once Alexander had agreed to his father’s deposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether they meant from the start to kill him is still argued, and the official manifesto said he had died of apoplexy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Paul I of Russia"},
    "ru-379": {"questions":["Women could inherit the Russian throne under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only after every male line of the dynasty had died out, a rule that held until the monarchy fell in 1917.","Drawn up by Paul and his wife in January 1788 while Catherine still reigned, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could come into force only when he was crowned nine years later."],"abstract":"The <b>Pauline Laws</b> were the rules of succession to the Russian throne proclaimed by Paul I at his coronation on 5 April 1797, which fixed the crown to pass by primogeniture in the male line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paul read the act aloud in the Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin after he and his wife were crowned there on Easter Sunday, and it was laid on the cathedral altar for safekeeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its text was the family agreement that Paul and Maria Feodorovna had drawn up and dated in St Petersburg on 4 January 1788, nine years before he could put it into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under it the throne passed to the emperor’s eldest son and his male descendants, and only when every male line had died out could it pass to a woman, the nearest kinswoman of the last ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the same day Paul issued a statute on the imperial family, a statute of the imperial orders and a manifesto forbidding landowners to make serfs work on Sundays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The law was shaped by Paul’s own past, since in her last years Catherine had studied Peter the Great’s regulations on the succession and drafted notes on her grandsons’ rights as if Paul did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plan to pass him over had indeed been considered, and it failed only because his son Alexander refused to take the throne before his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order it laid down was followed in 1825, when Nicholas I came to the throne because his elder brother Constantine had renounced his rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taken into the Fundamental Laws of 1832 and reissued as late as 1906, it governed the Romanov succession until the monarchy fell in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The manifesto on serfs’ labour issued with it only remarked that three days a week of work for the landowner should be enough, though it was later read as a binding three-day limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Paul the First of Russia, the Son of Catherine the Great</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1913), 27–28, 96–97, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028412835. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 51–53, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Г. П. Шайрян, «Неизменный закон о престолонаследии в России и опыт его применения в начале XX века», <i>Международный научно-исследовательский журнал</i>, no. 3-2 (57) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neizmennyy-zakon-o-prestolonasledii-v-rossii-i-opyt-ego-primeneniya-v-nachale-hh-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. П. Шайрян, «О порядке российского династического престолонаследия в начале XX века», <i>Право и государство: теория и практика</i>, no. 6 (150) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-poryadke-rossiyskogo-dinasticheskogo-prestolonaslediya-v-nachale-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Л. Абрамова, «Манифест 5 апреля 1797 г. в исторической литературе: а был ли указ о трехдневной барщине?», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/manifest-5-aprelya-1797-g-v-istoricheskoy-literature-a-byl-li-ukaz-o-trehdnevnoy-barschine. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Скоробогатов, «Коронация императора Павла I: образ монарха в церемониальной культуре эпохи Просвещения», <i>Карельский научный журнал</i>, no. 1 (10) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/koronatsiya-imperatora-pavla-i-obraz-monarha-v-tseremonialnoy-kulture-epohi-prosvescheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Paul care so much about fixing the succession?","a":"Because he had nearly been excluded himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His mother kept him from affairs of state and towards the end of her reign considered making his son Alexander her heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She studied Peter the Great’s regulations on the succession and drafted notes on her grandsons’ rights as if Paul did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fixed rule of male primogeniture took that choice away from the ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Could a woman still reign after 1797?","a":"Only in a remote case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The throne passed first through the emperor’s sons and the male lines descended from them, with men always preferred to women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only when every male line had died out would it go to the nearest kinswoman of the last ruler, so female rule became all but impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did Paul really limit serfs’ labour to three days a week?","a":"Not in law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The manifesto issued with the succession act firmly banned forcing peasants to work on Sundays, but it only remarked that three days a week for the landowner should be enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There was no sanction behind the remark, and in Little Russia, where two days had been the custom, the figure was no gain for peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pauline Laws"},
    "ru-380": {"questions":["Founded on the site of Empress Elizabeth’s Summer Palace in February 1797, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its name from the Archangel Michael and was renamed the Engineering Castle in 1823.","A manifesto blamed apoplexy for the emperor’s death in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1801, although conspirators led by the city’s military governor had strangled him in his bedroom."],"abstract":"<b>Mikhailovsky Castle</b> is the fortified palace in St Petersburg that Paul I built for himself on the site of Empress Elizabeth’s Summer Palace, and in which he was murdered in March 1801, soon after moving in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundation stone was laid on 26 February 1797, a few months after his accession, and its church of the Archangel Michael was consecrated on 8 November 1800.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name tied the building to the Archangel Michael, whom Paul honoured so warmly that he also gave the name to his youngest son, born in 1798.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The work was directed by an Italian architect who had served Paul at Gatchina, Vincenzo Brenna, whose ceremonial interiors of 1798–1801 were full of heroic and triumphal motifs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moats, drawbridges and granite walls made it a fortress as much as a palace, and its building was pushed on by decree at a cost of millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Paul wanted a citadel because he no longer felt safe in any of his residences, and when he moved in he said that he had never felt happier or more at ease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conspiracy against him was led by Count Peter von der Pahlen, the military governor of St Petersburg, whose office put the garrison and the police at the plotters’ disposal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the night of 11 to 12 March 1801 a party of officers broke into the emperor’s apartments, and Paul, found in hiding, was struck down and strangled with a scarf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A manifesto announced that the emperor had died suddenly of apoplexy, and historians still argue over whether the plotters meant from the start to kill him and over Britain’s part in the plot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The imperial family moved to the Winter Palace, the castle lost its high status and much of its furnishing was sold, and from 1823 the building was called the Engineering Castle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["K. Waliszewski, <i>Paul the First of Russia, the Son of Catherine the Great</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1913), 396, 404–5, 455–57, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028412835. [Open access]","Adam Czartoryski, <i>Memoirs of Prince Adam Czartoryski and His Correspondence with Alexander I</i>, ed. Adam Gielgud, vol. 1 (London: Remington, 1888), 234–36, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincea01czariala. [Open access]","Н. И. Петров, «Неизвестное стихотворение И. И. Дмитриева и основание Михайловского замка в Санкт-Петербурге в 1797 г.», <i>Slověne</i> 13, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neizvestnoe-stihotvorenie-i-i-dmitrieva-i-osnovanie-mihaylovskogo-zamka-v-sankt-peterburge-v-1797-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Петров, «О сакральном характере наименования Михайловского замка в Санкт-Петербурге», <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i>, no. 39 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-sakralnom-haraktere-naimenovaniya-mihaylovskogo-zamka-v-sankt-peterburge. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Летин, «Исторический дискурс художественного универсума Михайловского замка: аспекты власти», <i>Мир русскоговорящих стран</i>, no. 1 (11) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskiy-diskurs-hudozhestvennogo-universuma-mihaylovskogo-zamka-aspekty-vlasti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. О. Гутенберг, «К истории разорения императорских дворцов Петербурга в первой трети XIX века: Михайловский замок», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i>, no. 2 (27) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-razoreniya-imperatorskih-dvortsov-peterburga-v-pervoy-treti-xix-veka-mihaylovskiy-zamok. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. А. Никишин, «Переворот 11–12 марта 1801 года: современные подходы к изучению вопроса», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 50, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/perevorot-11-12-marta-1801-goda-sovremennye-podhody-k-izucheniyu-voprosa. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Paul build a fortress in the middle of his capital?","a":"Because he had come to fear for his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He felt secure in none of his residences and wanted granite walls, deep moats and drawbridges, and he had the building pushed on by decree at enormous cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A courtier who knew him called it a sort of fortified castle where the emperor thought his life would be safe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the castle’s walls not save him?","a":"Because the danger came from inside his own court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plot’s leader, Pahlen, was military governor of St Petersburg and controlled the garrison and the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The plan was to arrest Paul’s loyal officials and surround the castle with guards regiments, and when soldiers from Gatchina tried to go to his rescue their officers held them back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the castle after the murder?","a":"It was abandoned as a residence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The imperial family moved to the Winter Palace, the building lost its high status, and many of its fittings and furnishings were sold off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The whole complex was handed to the engineering department in 1822, and from 1823 it was called the Engineering Castle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saint Michael's Castle"},
    "ru-381": {"questions":["Received by the deputies of Paris on 31 March 1814, the Russian emperor <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rode into the French capital between the King of Prussia and Schwarzenberg.","Rumour long held that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had not died at Taganrog in 1825 but lived on in Siberia as the hermit Fyodor Kuzmich, a story without direct evidence."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander I</b> was emperor of Russia from March 1801 until his death in 1825, the son of Paul I and a grandson of Catherine the Great, who chose his teachers herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Her choice was the Swiss Frédéric-César de La Harpe, known for his republican opinions, who directed the lessons of the grand dukes for about ten years and stayed Alexander's correspondent long after leaving Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Alexander came to the throne when a conspiracy of officers murdered his father in March 1801, and the official manifesto put Paul's death down to apoplexy, the version that held until the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His first years brought milder censorship, free travel abroad and a circle of young friends who met him after dinner to plan reforms, though he never gave up any of his autocratic power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of the reign was shaped by Napoleon, whom Alexander fought from 1805 to 1807, joined in alliance at Tilsit in 1807 and fought again from 1812 to 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> His greatest moment came on 31 March 1814, when he received the deputies of the city and made his entry between the King of Prussia and the Austrian commander Schwarzenberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crowds were said to have eyes for him alone, and he promised that the inhabitants would only be asked for provisions and that the allied troops would not be quartered on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1815 he wrote the first draft of the Holy Alliance, a pact under which monarchs were to treat their subjects as children and one another as brothers in Christ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His last years turned towards mysticism, stricter censorship and the military colonies run by Arakcheyev, and the liberal hopes of his early reign were not revived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died at Taganrog on 1 December 1825 by the Gregorian calendar, 19 November by the Julian, and the later legend that he faked his death to live on as the hermit Fyodor Kuzmich rests on no direct evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 215, 271–98, 358–59, 368–69, 374–84, 397–98, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Adam Czartoryski, <i>Memoirs of Prince Adam Czartoryski and His Correspondence with Alexander I</i>, ed. Adam Gielgud, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (London: Remington, 1888), 257–62, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincea01czariala. [Open access]","Б. А. Никишин, «Переворот 11–12 марта 1801 года: современные подходы к изучению вопроса», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 50, no. 2 (2023): 429–41, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/perevorot-11-12-marta-1801-goda-sovremennye-podhody-k-izucheniyu-voprosa. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Андреева and Л. В. Выскочков, «Утомление жизнью или уход: Александр I. 1825 год. Новые материалы», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2016): 255–67, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/utomlenie-zhiznyu-ili-uhod-aleksandr-i-1825-god-novye-materialy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Борисова, «Тильзитский мир как зеркало политики императора Александра I (по письмам Ф. С. Лагарпа)», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 24 (2022): 9–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tilzitskiy-mir-kak-zerkalo-politiki-imperatora-aleksandra-i-po-pismam-f-s-lagarpa. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Безотосный, «Россия в эпоху наполеоновских войн: выбор союзников и противников», <i>Исторические исследования. Журнал Исторического факультета МГУ имени М. В. Ломоносова</i>, no. 15 (2020): 103–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-v-epohu-napoleonovskih-voyn-vybor-soyuznikov-i-protivnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the young emperor's early reforms stay mostly on paper?","a":"Because the young friends who planned reform with him after dinner held no power of their own, and as soon as he left them he fell back under the influence of the old ministers, while his liking for compromise made him shy away from firm orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war of 1812 then interrupted the reforms already begun, and they were not resumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the alliance with Napoleon unpopular in Russia?","a":"Because the peace of Tilsit tied Russia to the blockade of Britain, its main trading partner, which cut Russian exports and the profits of Russian producers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nobles who sold their hemp and grain abroad even plotted to reopen the sea to their goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people believe he had not really died?","a":"Because he died far from the capital, in Taganrog, and the sudden death set off a crisis over the succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many ordinary people were sure that another body or even a doll was being carried across Russia while the emperor had hidden himself away, yet the writers who later built the legend of Fyodor Kuzmich admitted that they had no direct proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander I of Russia"},
    "ru-382": {"questions":["Mocked at court as the young men's party, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> discussed the schemes behind the 1802 manifesto that created Russia's first ministries in place of Peter's colleges.","Stroganov's notes, long read as formal minutes, record the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meeting regularly from 24 June 1801 to May 1802, then gathering four more times in late 1803."],"abstract":"The <b>Unofficial Committee</b> was the private circle of young advisers with whom Alexander I discussed plans of reform in the first years of his reign, called in Russian the Neglasny komitet and by one of its members the Secret Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its core was three friends of the emperor, Count Pavel Stroganov, Nikolai Novosiltsev and Prince Adam Czartoryski, joined by the older Count Viktor Kochubey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They dined with the emperor two or three times a week, then slipped through a corridor into a small dressing-room next to the private apartments, where he joined them once the other guests had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stroganov's notes, long read as its minutes, begin with a meeting on 24 June 1801 and run regularly to 12 May 1802, with four more meetings recorded in October and November 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A recent study argues that the notes are closer to a diary than to minutes, and that the circle may have gone on advising the emperor, at least on foreign policy, until about 1805.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At court the group was mocked as 'the young men's party', and its enemies called it a Jacobin gang, a jibe sharpened because Stroganov had joined the Jacobin Club in Paris in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fourteen of its sessions dealt with the Senate, and the young friends argued against turning it into a legislative body, preferring to leave it judicial power and the guarding of the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also took up the peasant question, which led to the decree of 20 February 1803 letting landowners free their serfs with land by voluntary contract, creating the class of free cultivators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its most lasting result was the ministries, for Novosiltsev drafted a scheme for them in the committee, and the manifesto of 8 September 1802 set up eight ministries over Peter the Great's colleges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Czartoryski later wrote that for a long time its talks produced nothing practical, yet that no useful reform of the reign was tried which had not begun in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Adam Czartoryski, <i>Memoirs of Prince Adam Czartoryski and His Correspondence with Alexander I</i>, ed. Adam Gielgud, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (London: Remington, 1888), 257–62, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincea01czariala. [Open access]","М. А. Приходько, «Негласный комитет и разработка административных реформ в России в начале ХIХ века», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 11 (2012): 119–29, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neglasnyy-komitet-i-razrabotka-administrativnyh-reform-v-rossii-v-nachale-hih-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Ерохина and В. Ю. Захаров, «Деятельность Негласного комитета: новые подходы к решению дискуссионных вопросов», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 1 (2023): 146–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deyatelnost-neglasnogo-komiteta-novye-podhody-k-resheniyu-diskussionnyh-voprosov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Коршунова, «Политические проекты Павла Строганова начала XIX века», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 16 (2012): 8–11, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskie-proekty-pavla-stroganova-nachala-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 271–72, 374–78, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","М. С. Дмитрян, «Промежуточные итоги министерской реформы 1802 года», <i>Юристъ — Правоведъ</i>, no. 5 (2013): 86–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/promezhutochnye-itogi-ministerskoy-reformy-1802-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the committee's plans so rarely become law at once?","a":"Because the young friends held no office at first, while the Senate and the old ministers went on governing in the usual way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As soon as Alexander left the dressing-room he came back under the influence of those ministers, and his liking for compromise made him draw back from measures that seemed too violent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the young friends called Jacobins?","a":"Because they talked of constitutions and reform at a court that still feared the French Revolution, and Stroganov really had joined the Jacobin Club in Paris in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The insult was probably coined by one of the older courtiers they had pushed aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter whether Stroganov's notes are true minutes?","a":"Because the usual dates of the committee, 1801 to 1803, rest on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> If the notes are an incomplete record closer to a diary, the long gap between May 1802 and October 1803 may reflect lost notes rather than a pause, and the circle may have advised the emperor until about 1805.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Private Committee"},
    "ru-383": {"questions":["Dismissed on 17 March 1812 and sent that night to Nizhny Novgorod, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been accused by court rumour of working for Napoleon on the eve of war.","Governor-general of Siberia from 1819, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later led the work under Nicholas I that produced the Complete Collection of Laws in 1830 and the Code of 1832."],"abstract":"<b>Mikhail Speransky</b> was the leading reformer in the government of Alexander I between about 1807 and 1812, and later the man who codified the laws of the Russian Empire under Nicholas I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The son of a poor village priest, he was educated at the seminaries of Vladimir and Saint Petersburg and taught mathematics and philosophy before entering state service as secretary to Prince Kurakin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He rose through the Ministry of the Interior under Kochubey, and after Tilsit he became the emperor's closest adviser, valued for putting Alexander's ideas into clear and graceful language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1809 he drew up a plan for a state built on the separation of powers, with elected dumas in the districts and provinces and a State Duma whose consent every new law would need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only part of it was carried out, for the State Council opened in January 1810 as an advisory body, the ministries were reorganised in 1811, and the elected dumas never met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> His fall came suddenly on 17 March 1812, when he was dismissed as state secretary and sent away from the capital that same night, first to Nizhny Novgorod and then to Perm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Officials resented his decree tying promotion to university degrees, landowners feared his views on serfdom, and rumours spread that he was a traitor working for Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Letters from the days of his fall show that Alexander himself voiced the charge of treason, though no betrayal was ever proved and the inquiry stopped only because the war began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He returned to service as governor of Penza in 1816, and in March 1819 he was named governor-general of Siberia, where he drew up the new scheme of government introduced in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Back in Saint Petersburg from 1821, he led the codification under Nicholas I that produced the Complete Collection of Laws in 1830 and the Code of Laws, finished in 1832.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 297–98, 379–82, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Л. А. Муравьева, «Конституционные взгляды и реформы М. М. Сперанского», <i>Финансы: теория и практика</i>, no. 3 (1999): 57–65, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konstitutsionnye-vzglyady-i-reformy-m-m-speranskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Корф, «Сперанский», <i>Вестник Университета имени О. Е. Кутафина</i>, no. 2 (2014): 206–13, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/speranskiy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Андреев, «Источники об отставке М. М. Сперанского в 1812 году: опыт систематического анализа», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2022): 185–200, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istochniki-ob-otstavke-m-m-speranskogo-v-1812-godu-opyt-sistematicheskogo-analiza. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. П. Белозеров, «М. М. Сперанский и его роль в формировании российского законодательства первой половины XIX в.», <i>Социология и право</i>, no. 2 (2013): 55–67, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/m-m-speranskiy-i-ego-rol-v-formirovanii-rossiyskogo-zakonodatelstva-pervoy-poloviny-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Л. Дамешек, «Сибирская ревизия и реформы М. М. Сперанского 1822 г.», <i>Известия Иркутского государственного университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 1 (2012): 66–72, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sibirskaya-reviziya-i-reformy-m-m-speranskogo-1822-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Доронин, «Сперанский и министерская реформа в России в начале XIX в.», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 2 (2020): 44–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/speranskiy-i-ministerskaya-reforma-v-rossii-v-nachale-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many people at court hate him?","a":"Because his decree of 1809 made promotion in the civil service depend on university degrees, which angered courtiers and young officials who had hoped to rise by favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Landowners also feared his plans for the serfs, and great aristocrats resented the rise of a village priest's son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was only part of his 1809 plan carried out?","a":"Because Alexander chose to move by stages: the State Council was set up in 1810, but the elected dumas that were to follow within a year never appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The council itself soon became a body that only advised on laws, and the war of 1812 cut the reforms short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Was Speransky really a traitor?","a":"No betrayal was ever proved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Letters accusing him of plotting for Napoleon passed from hand to hand in Saint Petersburg just before his fall, and Alexander was angry enough to speak of having him shot, yet the official who had passed him deciphered diplomatic letters still doubted any treason, and the inquiry stopped when the war began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mikhail Speransky"},
    "ru-384": {"questions":["With the poet Derzhavin as one of its first ministers, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left Peter's colleges standing but subordinated them to single ministers answerable for their departments.","Completed only by the General Establishment of Ministries in 1811, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had first created a Committee of Ministers alongside the new departments of state."],"abstract":"The <b>ministerial reform of 1802</b> was the change by which Alexander I set up ministries, each run by a single minister, at the head of Russia's central government in place of the colleges created by Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before it, Czartoryski recalled, the only central bodies were the Senate and the colleges of war, the navy and foreign affairs, while one official, the procurator-general, did the work of the interior, police, finance and justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Novosiltsev drafted the scheme in the emperor's private circle of advisers, where it was argued over in April 1802.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The manifesto on the establishment of ministries was signed on 8 September 1802 by the Julian calendar, creating eight ministries of foreign affairs, war, the navy, the interior, finance, justice, commerce and public education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the same day a Committee of Ministers was set up, and the first ministers included the poet Derzhavin at justice and Count Kochubey at the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The question is about how unfinished the change of 1802 was, since the colleges were not abolished but placed under the new ministers, and the old collegial way of working lingered on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ministers were to countersign the emperor's decrees, a first attempt at making them responsible, yet Alexander told one councillor that a decree must be obeyed even if a minister refused to sign it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kochubey complained that the surviving collegial routine was the chief brake on his ministry, and Speransky argued again and again for a second reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That second stage came with the General Establishment of Ministries of 25 June 1811, which gave every ministry the same structure and the same rules for handling business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Legal historians therefore divide the process into the reform proper of 1802 to 1811 and a long completion lasting to 1835, since colleges went on working into the 1830s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["М. С. Дмитрян, «Промежуточные итоги министерской реформы 1802 года», <i>Юристъ — Правоведъ</i>, no. 5 (2013): 86–89, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/promezhutochnye-itogi-ministerskoy-reformy-1802-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Adam Czartoryski, <i>Memoirs of Prince Adam Czartoryski and His Correspondence with Alexander I</i>, ed. Adam Gielgud, vol. 1, 2nd ed. (London: Remington, 1888), 294–99, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofprincea01czariala. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 377–78, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","А. М. Доронин, «Сперанский и министерская реформа в России в начале XIX в.», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 2 (2020): 44–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/speranskiy-i-ministerskaya-reforma-v-rossii-v-nachale-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Приходько, «Концептуальный подход к изучению министерской системы управления в России в 1802–1835 гг.», <i>Наука и современность</i>, no. 5-3 (2010): 331–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kontseptualnyy-podhod-k-izucheniyu-ministerskoy-sistemy-upravleniya-v-rossii-v-1802-1835-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Приходько, «Негласный комитет и разработка административных реформ в России в начале ХIХ века», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 11 (2012): 119–29, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neglasnyy-komitet-i-razrabotka-administrativnyh-reform-v-rossii-v-nachale-hih-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why replace colleges with ministers at all?","a":"Because a college decided as a board, which was slow and left no single official answerable for the result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reformers hoped that departments with clear limits, each under one responsible head, would bring order to a chaotic administration and check the abuses and frauds that plagued it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the colleges survive after 1802?","a":"Because the manifesto placed the old colleges under the ministers instead of abolishing them, and three of them carried on on their former footing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kochubey called the collegial routine the main brake on his ministry, which is why a second reform followed in 1810 and 1811.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the new ministers limit the emperor's power?","a":"Hardly, for decrees were to be countersigned by a minister, but Alexander said that a decree must be carried out even if a minister refused to sign it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ministers and the council lacked real responsibility, and autocracy gave up none of its rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ministerial reform in the Russian Empire"},
    "ru-385": {"questions":["Russian and Austrian officers quarrelled so bitterly during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Alexander I issued a personal address urging his generals to show friendship and trust towards their allies.","Kutuzov learned at Braunau on the Inn that Mack had capitulated at Ulm, an early disaster of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that left the Russian vanguard dangerously exposed."],"abstract":"The <b>War of the Third Coalition</b> was the war of 1805 in which Britain, Russia, Austria, Sweden and Naples combined against Napoleon, and it ended in a French triumph within a single autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia did much to build the alliance, sending Novosiltsev to London in 1804 with a scheme for a Europe of independent states and asking Britain for subsidies, which Pitt was not eager to grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Anglo-Russian treaty of 11 April 1805 promised to drive the French from northern Germany and free Holland and Switzerland, with a yearly British payment for every 100,000 men Russia put in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prussia hung back, and a Russian draft manifesto against Prussia, dated the same day as the Potsdam convention that drew it towards the allies, shows that Saint Petersburg was weighing pressure on Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Austrians did not wait for their allies, and Mack's army was surrounded and forced to capitulate at Ulm in October 1805, before Kutuzov's Russians could reach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The question looks at the Russian side, where Alexander joined his armies in person with Czartoryski, Novosiltsev and Stroganov, the whole Imperial Guard and the flower of the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hostility between Russian and Austrian officers ran so high that the emperor issued a personal address to his generals, written between late September and October 1805, demanding friendship and trust towards the allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kutuzov retreated down the Danube while Bagration's rearguard sacrificed itself at Schöngrabern, and the united armies were then routed at Austerlitz on 2 December 1805.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Austria made a separate peace at Pressburg on 26 December, against the coalition's rule that no ally should make peace alone, and Russia found itself almost alone on the Continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A modern verdict is that Alexander's chief error of 1805 was overrating his own army and trusting in Austrian strength, while the official French newspaper had already declared that Kutuzov stood no chance against Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 277–85, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 5–6, 23–25, 42–43, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","А. Н. Белов, «„Рекомендовать дружбу, доверие и согласие с союзниками как первейшую основу для благополучного исхода“: обращение Александра I к войскам в 1805 году», <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия „История и филология“</i> 36, no. 1 (2026): 172–77, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rekomendovat-druzhbu-doverie-i-soglasie-s-soyuznikami-kak-perveyshuyu-osnovu-dlya-blagopoluchnogo-ishoda-obraschenie-aleksandra-i-k. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Искюль, «Русско-прусский дипломатический казус за несколько месяцев до Аустерлица», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-prusskiy-diplomaticheskiy-kazus-za-neskolko-mesyatsev-do-austerlitsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Безотосный, «Россия в эпоху наполеоновских войн: выбор союзников и противников», <i>Исторические исследования. Журнал Исторического факультета МГУ имени М. В. Ломоносова</i>, no. 15 (2020): 103–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-v-epohu-napoleonovskih-voyn-vybor-soyuznikov-i-protivnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Никифорова, «Газета „Le Moniteur universel“ как источник по истории русско-французских отношений в 1801–1807 гг.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 356 (2012): 85–88, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gazeta-le-moniteur-universel-kak-istochnik-po-istorii-russko-frantsuzskih-otnosheniy-v-1801-1807-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. П. Рыбаков, «Русская мемуаристика о сражении при Аустерлице», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 4 (2012): 42–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-memuaristika-o-srazhenii-pri-austerlitse. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia ask Britain for money?","a":"Because Russia wanted British money to put effective forces in the field, and it was Russia, not Britain, that first raised the need for subsidies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty of April 1805 fixed a yearly payment for every 100,000 men Russia put under arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians blame the Austrians for the defeat?","a":"Because the Austrians had drawn up the battle plan and were fighting on ground they knew, yet failed to place the troops well or to supply forage and ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian officers even claimed the plan had been betrayed to Napoleon, but no proof of this has ever been given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the coalition fall apart so quickly?","a":"Because Austria's main army was lost at Ulm before the Russians arrived, and after Austerlitz Austria made a separate peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prussia, which had promised to join, then had good reason to stay out, so the coalition was beaten in the field and dissolved in the cabinet, leaving Russia almost alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War of the Third Coalition"},
    "ru-386": {"questions":["Russia's government presented the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> almost as a victory and blamed the Austrians, while Zhukovsky's poem on the battle even spoke of Russian triumph.","Fought on 20 November 1805 by the Russian calendar, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with allied troops drowning in frozen ponds, though French claims of 20,000 drowned were later cut to 2,000."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Austerlitz</b> was fought in Moravia on 2 December 1805, 20 November by the Russian calendar, when Napoleon defeated the combined armies of Russia and Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is called the battle of the three emperors, since Alexander I and Francis of Austria faced Napoleon in person, and it took its name from the château of Austerlitz where the two allied emperors had slept the night before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Napoleon had about 73,000 men against some 80,000 or more allies, most of them Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Encouraged by a small success at Wischau and by Napoleon's apparent timidity, the allies adopted the Austrian Weyrother's plan to turn the French right and cut it off from Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Napoleon had left that wing weak on purpose, and when the allied columns came down from the Pratzen heights into the fog, Soult's corps climbed the slopes and broke their centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Russian general Langeron later wrote that Weyrother's plan was the work either of a man unfit to draw up a battle order or of a traitor, and that Kutuzov dozed off while it was read out in the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The allied left was caught among frozen ponds, and the French at first reported 20,000 men drowned in the ice before reducing the figure to 2,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian losses alone reached some 21,000 men and 200 guns, and one estimate puts the whole allied loss at 33,000 men and 186 cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At home the defeat was presented almost as a success, with the official line blaming the Austrians, and Zhukovsky's poem on the battle spoke of a Russian victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Napoleon's official newspaper had told its readers that Kutuzov stood no chance, and a modern verdict counts overconfidence in his army and in Austria as Alexander's chief mistake of 1805.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 280–83, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Marcellin de Marbot, <i>The Memoirs of Baron de Marbot</i>, trans. Arthur John Butler, vol. 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1905), 159–62, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofbaronde01marbuoft. [Open access]","John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 36–42, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","С. П. Рыбаков, «Русская мемуаристика о сражении при Аустерлице», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 4 (2012): 42–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-memuaristika-o-srazhenii-pri-austerlitse. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. С. Парсамов, «В. А. Жуковский и Аустерлиц („Песнь барда над гробом славян-победителей“ в историческом контексте)», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i> 7, no. 2 (2007): 53–58, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-a-zhukovskiy-i-austerlits-pesn-barda-nad-grobom-slavyan-pobediteley-v-istoricheskom-kontekste. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Никифорова, «Газета „Le Moniteur universel“ как источник по истории русско-французских отношений в 1801–1807 гг.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 356 (2012): 85–88, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gazeta-le-moniteur-universel-kak-istochnik-po-istorii-russko-frantsuzskih-otnosheniy-v-1801-1807-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Безотосный, «Россия в эпоху наполеоновских войн: выбор союзников и противников», <i>Исторические исследования. Журнал Исторического факультета МГУ имени М. В. Ломоносова</i>, no. 15 (2020): 103–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-v-epohu-napoleonovskih-voyn-vybor-soyuznikov-i-protivnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the allies attack the French right?","a":"Because Weyrother's plan aimed to cut Napoleon off from the Danube and Vienna, and the allies believed he was about to retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Napoleon had left that wing weak on purpose to draw them on to marshy ground, with Davout's corps hidden behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Austerlitz such a shock in Russia?","a":"Because Russians were not used to defeat after half a century of victories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Moscow diarist wrote that the news rang through the city like the first stroke of a great bell, and the government tried to present the battle as the Austrians' defeat rather than Russia's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the drowning figures differ so much?","a":"Because the French first spoke of 20,000 men drowned in the frozen ponds and then cut the figure to 2,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Accounts agree that some men did go through the ice where French cannon fire broke it, but the scale of the disaster was inflated at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Austerlitz"},
    "ru-387": {"questions":["Bennigsen had a Te Deum sung after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although he had retreated by night towards Königsberg and the French remained masters of the snowy field.","The East Prussian town that gave its name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1807 was renamed Bagrationovsk in 1946, honouring a general who had fought there."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Eylau</b> was fought at Preussisch Eylau in East Prussia on 7 and 8 February 1807, 26 and 27 January by the Russian calendar, between Napoleon and Bennigsen's Russian army with a Prussian corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It followed a winter offensive by Bennigsen that ran into stubborn resistance, and when Cossacks seized a French despatch revealing Napoleon's counterstroke, he fell back with Bagration covering the retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town changed hands several times on 7 February, in a fight that most accounts pass over in favour of the great battle of the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 February, in driving snow, Augereau's corps lost its way and walked into a Russian battery of 72 guns, and Murat answered with a charge of some eighty squadrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian column came close to the cemetery where Napoleon stood before his Guard threw it back, and at nightfall Lestocq's Prussians checked Davout while Ney arrived too late to decide the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The losses were enormous, with the best estimate putting the Russian and Prussian loss at about 25,000 and the French at 3,000 to 5,000 more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bennigsen, short of food and ammunition, retreated in the night towards Königsberg although his generals begged him to stay, and his men had had nothing to eat but frozen snow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The French held the field and claimed the victory, but Ney, seeing the carnage, called it a massacre without result, while Bennigsen had a Te Deum sung for what had been a stubborn resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian accounts treat it as the first battle Napoleon failed to win, and Alexander greeted Bennigsen as the man who had beaten one never beaten before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The town became Bagrationovsk in 1946, when the Soviet Union renamed the towns of the newly annexed Kaliningrad region, in honour of Bagration, who had fought there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["F. Loraine Petre, <i>Napoleon’s Campaign in Poland, 1806–7</i> (London: Sampson Low, Marston, 1901), 198–201, https://archive.org/details/napoleonscampaig00petruoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 287–89, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 111–14, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","Я. Н. Рабинович, «Неизвестное сражение при Прейсиш-Эйлау 26 января 1807 года», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i> 7, no. 2 (2007): 28–33, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neizvestnoe-srazhenie-pri-preysish-eylau-26-yanvarya-1807-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Robert Wilson, <i>Brief Remarks on the Character and Composition of the Russian Army, and a Sketch of the Campaigns in Poland in the Years 1806 and 1807</i> (London: T. Egerton, 1810), https://archive.org/details/briefremarksonc00wilsgoog. [Open access]","В. Н. Маслов, «Переименование районных центров Калининградской области в 1946 году», <i>Вестник Балтийского федерального университета им. И. Канта. Серия: Гуманитарные и общественные науки</i>, no. 12 (2014): 58–69, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pereimenovanie-rayonnyh-tsentrov-kaliningradskoy-oblasti-v-1946-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bennigsen retreat if he had held his ground?","a":"Because his army had lost at least 20,000 men and had no fresh ammunition, while stores waited at Königsberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His soldiers had eaten nothing but frozen snow, and many had left the ranks during the battle to look for food in nearby villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did both sides claim the victory?","a":"Because neither destroyed the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The French kept the field, which by custom counted as winning, while the Russians had beaten off every attack before withdrawing, so Bennigsen could present a hard resistance as a victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the first day of the battle less well known?","a":"Because most accounts describe only the great fighting of 8 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 7 February Bagration's rearguard held off stronger French forces on the approaches, and the town itself changed hands several times before the French took it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Eylau"},
    "ru-388": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of June 1807, only Barclay de Tolly urged retreating into Russia and burning everything, while Alexander chose to seek an armistice with Napoleon.","Letters written by General Raevsky in June 1807 suggest that after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Russian army simply could not continue, so Tilsit was hardly a free choice."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Friedland</b> was fought on 14 June 1807, 2 June by the Russian calendar, on the River Alle in East Prussia, where Napoleon routed Bennigsen's Russian army and ended the war of 1806–1807.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bennigsen, trying to keep between Napoleon and Königsberg, crossed the Alle at Friedland to crush what he took to be Lannes's corps standing alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lannes held him with about 10,000 men from before dawn until the rest of the French army came up, and Napoleon, arriving, hailed the day as the anniversary of Marengo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Russians stood with a steep river at their backs and only the bridges of Friedland behind them, a position Napoleon called a blunder an enemy did not make every day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At five in the afternoon Ney drove into the bend of the river, Senarmont's guns swept the Russians at close range, and the bridges and the burning town became a death-trap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Survivors escaped by fording the river in the dark, and the Russians lost perhaps 15,000 to 20,000 men against French losses of about 7,000 to 8,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within days Königsberg was abandoned, Bennigsen's army was in one account no longer an army but an armed mob, and on 21 June an armistice was signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Only Barclay de Tolly proposed fighting on by retreating into the heart of Russia and burning everything on the way, and Alexander chose to negotiate instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Letters written in June 1807 by General Raevsky show that the army could not go on fighting, which is why one modern study rejects the view of Tilsit as a free choice by Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Friedland itself was renamed Pravdinsk in 1946, when the Soviet Union gave new names to the towns of the Kaliningrad region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 290–93, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 119–24, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","F. Loraine Petre, <i>Napoleon’s Campaign in Poland, 1806–7</i> (London: Sampson Low, Marston, 1901), 321–22, 334–36, https://archive.org/details/napoleonscampaig00petruoft. [Open access]","О. В. Соколов, «Военно-политическая обстановка во время подписания Тильзитского мира и реакция на договор в свете синхронных источников», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1 (2015): 35–46, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-politicheskaya-obstanovka-vo-vremya-podpisaniya-tilzitskogo-mira-i-reaktsiya-na-dogovor-v-svete-sinhronnyh-istochnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Маслов, «Переименование районных центров Калининградской области в 1946 году», <i>Вестник Балтийского федерального университета им. И. Канта. Серия: Гуманитарные и общественные науки</i>, no. 12 (2014): 58–69, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pereimenovanie-rayonnyh-tsentrov-kaliningradskoy-oblasti-v-1946-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Безотосный, «Россия в эпоху наполеоновских войн: выбор союзников и противников», <i>Исторические исследования. Журнал Исторического факультета МГУ имени М. В. Ломоносова</i>, no. 15 (2020): 103–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-v-epohu-napoleonovskih-voyn-vybor-soyuznikov-i-protivnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Bennigsen's position at Friedland so dangerous?","a":"Because his army stood in a bend of the Alle with the steep river behind it and only the bridges of Friedland as a way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once French guns reached the bridges and they caught fire, retreat meant fording the river in the dark, and hundreds drowned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Bennigsen fight there at all?","a":"Because he believed only Lannes's corps was within reach and hoped to crush it before marching on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lannes's stubborn resistance kept him engaged until Napoleon arrived with far larger forces, and a British envoy's report found it a mystery that he stayed on such ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Friedland lead straight to peace?","a":"Because Alexander no longer had a field army to speak of, and his reinforcements were only a few thousand poorly armed Kalmyks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raevsky's letters from that month show the army could not continue, so the emperor gave Bennigsen powers to treat and an armistice followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Friedland"},
    "ru-389": {"questions":["Alexander told his mother in 1808 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were only a breathing space in which Russia could quietly build up its strength in secret.","Derzhavin's verses on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displeased Alexander I and stayed unprinted until 1816, for the poet praised the peacemaker tsar but slipped in a warning."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaties of Tilsit</b> were the peace treaties signed at Tilsit on the River Niemen in July 1807, between France and Russia on 7 July and between France and Prussia on 9 July by the Gregorian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They followed a meeting of Napoleon and Alexander on a raft moored in the middle of the river on 25 June, where the two emperors talked alone for nearly two hours while the King of Prussia waited on the bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of regard for the Russian emperor, the treaty said, Napoleon gave back to Prussia Silesia and its old lands between the Elbe and the Niemen, yet Prussia still lost about half of its population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prussia's Polish lands became the Duchy of Warsaw under the King of Saxony, and Russia gained the district of Białystok while recognising Napoleon's brothers as kings and the Confederation of the Rhine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A secret treaty of alliance bound Russia to offer its mediation to Britain and to go to war with it if refused, and to press Sweden, Denmark and Portugal to close their ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the courtesies the future of Constantinople divided the emperors, and Alexander went home ill at ease and in some awe of the conqueror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian nobles met the peace with irritation, since the blockade of Britain threatened their trade, and Derzhavin's verses on it were held back by the tsar's ban and printed only in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Alexander told his mother in September 1808 that Tilsit was a breathing space in which Russia could quietly build up its means, and his old tutor La Harpe saw it as a turning point in his whole policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The alliance cooled within a year, and before the meeting at Erfurt in 1808 the emperor's mother begged him not to go, fearing it would clear Napoleon's way to an attack on Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tilsit itself became Sovetsk in 1946, when the Soviet Union renamed the towns of the newly annexed Kaliningrad region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 127–37, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 293–96, 299, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Борисова, «Тильзитский мир как зеркало политики императора Александра I (по письмам Ф. С. Лагарпа)», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 24 (2022): 9–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tilzitskiy-mir-kak-zerkalo-politiki-imperatora-aleksandra-i-po-pismam-f-s-lagarpa. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Клейн, «Державин и Тильзитский мир», <i>XVIII век</i> 31 (2024): 280–90, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/derzhavin-i-tilzitskiy-mir. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Искюль, «Франция, Россия и тернии Тильзитского „брака“», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2015): 150–72, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/frantsiya-rossiya-i-ternii-tilzitskogo-braka. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Маслов, «Переименование районных центров Калининградской области в 1946 году», <i>Вестник Балтийского федерального университета им. И. Канта. Серия: Гуманитарные и общественные науки</i>, no. 12 (2014): 58–69, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pereimenovanie-rayonnyh-tsentrov-kaliningradskoy-oblasti-v-1946-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the King of Prussia left waiting on the riverbank?","a":"Because Napoleon chose to settle the peace with Alexander rather than with Frederick William, who had rejected all his earlier offers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king waited on horseback at the water's edge, urging his horse into the river, while the fate of his kingdom was decided on the raft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Russians dislike the peace?","a":"Because a secret clause tied Russia to the blockade of Britain, its main trading partner, and without British markets Russian exporters lost much of their trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many also resented the partial rebirth of Poland on the frontier as the Duchy of Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the alliance break down so quickly?","a":"Because the two emperors wanted different things, and Constantinople already divided them at Tilsit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a year the court was openly weighing the danger of a French attack, and Alexander himself had treated the peace as a breathing space from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaties of Tilsit"},
    "ru-390": {"questions":["Russia's paper rouble fell to a quarter of its silver value by 1810 as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut off sales of hemp and grain to Britain, Russia's chief customer.","Alexander's tariff of 31 December 1810, favouring American ships and doubling duties on French wine, broke Russia away from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and helped bring on the war of 1812."],"abstract":"The <b>Continental System</b> was Napoleon's attempt to ruin Britain by shutting its goods out of the European continent, founded on the Berlin Decree of 21 November 1806.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree declared the British Isles under blockade, banned all trade with them and ordered British goods and subjects to be seized wherever French or allied troops found them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain answered with Orders in Council against neutral trade, and Napoleon summed up his aim in the phrase that the sea must be subdued by the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smuggling flourished, and the system could be held together only by more and more annexations, so that by the start of 1811 France reached along the whole North Sea coast to the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its fate depended on the great powers obliged to enforce it, and Russia proved the decisive one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Russia joined through the secret alliance signed at Tilsit in 1807, after offering a mediation between France and Britain that came to nothing, and it broke with Britain that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The blockade hit an economy that lived by selling hemp and grain to Britain, and the paper rouble, worth as much as 80 silver kopecks early in the reign, fell to 25 by 1810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia's ambassador in Paris complained that trade between the Hanse towns and Russia was strangled while smuggling thrived in France, and Britain began buying hemp and tar in America and Asia instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1809 Russia set up commissions to check that ships calling at its ports were truly neutral, since British goods kept arriving under false papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The break came with the tariff of 19 December 1810 by the Julian calendar, 31 December by the Gregorian, which favoured American ships, ordered banned goods burned and doubled duties on French wines, a step towards the war of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["John Holland Rose, <i>The Life of Napoleon I</i>, vol. 2 (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1902), 103–06, https://archive.org/details/TheLifeOfNapoleonIVolIi. [Open access]","Eli F. Heckscher, <i>The Continental System: An Economic Interpretation</i>, ed. Harald Westergaard (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), 151–54, https://archive.org/details/continentalsyste00heckuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 293–95, 320–21, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Н. А. Макаров, «Перед вступлением в континентальную блокаду: попытка России заключить всеобщий мир между Францией и Англией», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 25 (2022): 47–57, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pered-vstupleniem-v-kontinentalnuyu-blokadu-popytka-rossii-zaklyuchit-vseobschiy-mir-mezhdu-frantsiey-i-angliey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Макаров, «Состояние русской внешней торговли в конце 1808–1809 гг. (по депешам А. Б. Куракина)», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 4 (2024): 15–24, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sostoyanie-russkoy-vneshney-torgovli-v-kontse-1808-1809-gg-po-depesham-a-b-kurakina. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. В. Покидина, «Континентальная блокада и Положение о нейтральной торговле на 1811 год», <i>National Science</i> 1, no. 4 (2022): 15–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kontinentalnaya-blokada-i-polozhenie-o-neytralnoy-torgovle-na-1811-god. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Рыбаков and А. М. Шамбатуева, «Отношения Александра I и Наполеона Бонапарта до 1812 года», <i>Власть истории — История власти</i> 10, no. 4 (2024): 12–21, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otnosheniya-aleksandra-i-i-napoleona-bonaparta-do-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Napoleon think he could beat Britain this way?","a":"Because he believed Britain's wealth rested on trade and would collapse if its goods were shut out of Europe, an idea he had taken over from the Jacobins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since Britain ruled the sea and he ruled the land, he reasoned that the land must overpower the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the blockade hurt Russia so badly?","a":"Because Britain was the main buyer of Russian hemp and grain, so closing the ports cut the income of the landowning nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The paper rouble lost value fast, and Britain found other suppliers in America and Asia, so the lost market might never return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia's tariff of 1810 anger Napoleon?","a":"Because it struck at France rather than Britain: important imports such as wine had to arrive by sea to be legal, French goods could only come overland, and American ships, often British ones in disguise, were favoured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Napoleon said he would rather have received a blow on the cheek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Continental System"},
    "ru-331": {"questions":["Meant at first for Moscow beside the new university, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed in Saint Petersburg because the foreign professors refused to live so far from the court.","Sending its best graduates to study in France and Italy at its own expense, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> received a new name and statute from Catherine II in 1764."],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Academy of Arts</b> was the state school of painting, sculpture and architecture in Saint Petersburg, founded by a Senate decree of 6 November 1757 as the Academy of the Three Noblest Arts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its moving spirit was the courtier Ivan Shuvalov, curator of Moscow University, who first meant to open it in Moscow beside the university he had founded three years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan failed because the foreign professors he invited would not live so far from the court and its commissions, so the Senate placed the Academy in the capital instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had older roots, for Peter the Great's printing house had kept a drawing school and the Academy of Sciences had an art department, run from 1748 by Jacob von Stählin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Classes opened in 1758 with 16 pupils from the Moscow University gymnasium and 22 boys from soldiers' families of Saint Petersburg, and the first students graduated in 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Shuvalov took his model from France, asking the Paris Academy for advice and bringing a painter named Lorraine and the sculptor Nicolas François Gillet to teach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Academy first worked in Shuvalov's own palace and then in houses on Vasilyevsky Island, where its great Neoclassical building by Alexander Kokorinov and Jean Vallin de la Mothe rose between 1764 and 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1764 Catherine II named it the Imperial Academy of Arts and gave it a statute that made it a state body handing out official commissions and academic ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its best graduates were sent abroad at its expense to study in France and Italy, a pensioner system borrowed from the French Academy that lasted for a century and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Early students such as Losenko, Shubin and Bazhenov proved that Russia could now train its own artists, though critics later complained that the Academy long remained an imitation of a French institution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Rosa Newmarch, <i>The Russian Arts</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1916), 82–84, https://archive.org/details/russianarts0000rosa. [Open access]","Alexandre Benois, <i>The Russian School of Painting</i>, trans. Abraham Yarmolinsky (New York: A. A. Knopf, 1916), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/cu31924016810321. [Open access]","В. Г. Залесов, «Школы архитектурно-строительного образования в России во второй половине XVIII века», <i>Вестник Томского государственного архитектурно-строительного университета</i>, 2009, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shkoly-arhitekturno-stroitelnogo-obrazovaniya-v-rossii-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Василенко, «Поиск места размещения Императорской Академии художеств», <i>Academia. Архитектура и строительство</i>, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poisk-mesta-razmescheniya-imperatorskoy-akademii-hudozhestv. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Фоминых, «Итальянское пенсионерство Императорской Академии художеств (XVIII–XIX вв.)», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i>, no. 26 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/italyanskoe-pensionerstvo-imperatorskoy-akademii-hudozhestv-xviii-xix-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. С. Стецкевич, «Я. Штелин и художественное отделение Императорской Академии наук и художеств в 1730-е − 1770-е гг.», <i>Манускрипт</i>, 18, no. 3 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ya-shtelin-i-hudozhestvennoe-otdelenie-imperatorskoy-akademii-nauk-i-hudozhestv-v-1730-e-1770-e-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Russian Academy of Arts, “History,” Russian Academy of Arts, https://rah.ru/academy/history/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Academy not opened in Moscow as first planned?","a":"Because the foreign masters Shuvalov hoped to hire would not move so far from the court, where the commissions and the money were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Senate therefore ordered the Academy set up in Saint Petersburg, although for a time it stayed nominally attached to Moscow University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Academy send its graduates abroad?","a":"Because it modelled its teaching on the European academies and held that young artists had to study the great works of France and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The pensioners sent home written reports and drawings every year and were watched over by Russian envoys, and the system lasted about 150 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did later critics find fault with the Academy?","a":"Because it was built on foreign and autocratic lines, taught at first by French masters, and trained artists to fill court orders for history paintings and flattering portraits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, it gave Russia its first home-trained painters, sculptors and architects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Imperial Academy of Arts"},
    "ru-332": {"questions":["The Smolny Convent, the Stroganov Palace on Nevsky Prospect and the church of Saint Andrew in Kyiv are all buildings designed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Elizabeth's court and nobles.","Begged by Elizabeth near the end of her life to hurry her rooms in the unpaid Winter Palace, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused, much as French jewellers had refused her goods on credit."],"abstract":"<b>Bartolomeo Rastrelli</b>, in full Francesco Bartolomeo Rastrelli, was the Italian court architect whose palaces and churches gave mid-18th-century Russia its most splendid buildings and the style called Elizabethan Baroque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did not grow up in Italy, for until he was sixteen he lived in Paris with his father, a sculptor also named Rastrelli, who then went to Saint Petersburg under contract to Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the young man later trained abroad is still argued, since an old story of long study in France lacks firm proof and his only certain journeys to Italy came late, in 1762–1763 and 1769–1770.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was already the leading builder of the 1730s, when he designed Empress Anna's Winter Palace and palaces for her favourite Biron in Courland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Elizabeth his works came to include the old palace at Tsarskoe Selo, the Smolny Convent, the Stroganov Palace on Nevsky Prospect and the church of Saint Andrew in Kyiv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> His largest commission was the fourth Winter Palace, built between 1754 and 1762 as a closed square of wings around a great courtyard, which set a new scale for the whole city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Inside it he planned a throne hall 28.4 metres (93 feet) wide and 48.3 metres (158 feet) long, whose span demanded new kinds of floor and roof construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Much of the painting on his ceilings and walls was done by Venetian artists such as Giuseppe Valeriani, who worked for years with the chief architect and the building offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elizabeth left the Winter Palace unfinished and largely unpaid for, and when she begged him to hurry her own rooms he refused, much as French jewellers refused to sell to her on credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fashion turned against him after 1762, when the Louis-Seize style reached Russia under Catherine II, yet none of her architects showed his skill in bending a foreign style to Russian taste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["А. Ю. Каптиков, «Ф.-Б. Растрелли и архитектура барокко в Северной Италии», <i>Академический вестник УралНИИпроект РААСН</i>, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/f-b-rastrelli-i-arhitektura-barokko-v-severnoy-italii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Rosa Newmarch, <i>The Russian Arts</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1916), 98–100, https://archive.org/details/russianarts0000rosa. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 356–57, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","С. В. Клименко, «К характеристике стиля архитектуры Аничкова дворца в Петербурге: между аннинской и елизаветинской эпохами», <i>Architecture and Modern Information Technologies</i>, no. 4 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-harakteristike-stilya-arhitektury-anichkova-dvortsa-v-peterburge-mezhdu-anninskoy-i-elizavetinskoy-epohami. [in Russian] [Open access]","Я. Соколова, «Венецианские живописцы в России в эпоху Елизаветы Петровны: творческий и рабочий процесс», <i>Искусствознание</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/venetsianskie-zhivopistsy-v-rossii-v-epohu-elizavety-petrovny-tvorcheskiy-i-rabochiy-protsess. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Кузнецов, «Фасад Его Величеству. Строгановский дворец, дом компании «Зингер» и дворец Белосельских-Белозерских: Невский проспект как архитектурный ансамбль», <i>Искусствознание</i>, 2020, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fasad-ego-velichestvu-stroganovskiy-dvorets-dom-kompanii-zinger-i-dvorets-beloselskih-belozerskih-nevskiy-prospekt-kak. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Г. Головина, «Архитектурно-конструктивные особенности зданий Санкт-Петербурга 1740-х – начала 1760-х гг.», <i>Системные технологии</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/arhitekturno-konstruktivnye-osobennosti-zdaniy-sankt-peterburga-1740-h-nachala-1760-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Петрова, «Ансамбль Дворцовой площади в конце XIX — начале XX века. Новые черты, реставрации, проект реконструкции», <i>Новое искусствознание</i>, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ansambl-dvortsovoy-ploschadi-v-kontse-xix-nachale-xx-veka-novye-cherty-restavratsii-proekt-rekonstruktsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is his early training uncertain?","a":"Because the record of his youth is thin: he lived in Paris with his father until he was sixteen and then in Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An old story sent him back to Europe for years of study, but the only journeys to Italy that documents prove came near the end of his career, so much of what he knew of Italian Baroque may have come from books of engravings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Venetian painters so important to his buildings?","a":"Because his palaces needed vast painted ceilings and walls, and Elizabeth's court relied on a group of Venetians who worked with the chief architect for many years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Painters such as Valeriani decorated the Winter Palace, the Anichkov Palace and the country palaces at Peterhof and Tsarskoe Selo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his share in the Anichkov Palace disputed?","a":"Because the palace was begun in 1741 to a design by the architect Zemtsov and continued after his death by his pupil Dmitriev before Rastrelli took part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The large role usually given to Rastrelli has partly hidden the work of the two men who first planned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Francesco Bartolomeo Rastrelli"},
    "ru-333": {"questions":["Peter III and his court moved into the newly finished <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Holy Saturday 1762, after gangs of labourers had worked night and day to complete it.","A fire that began on the evening of 17 December 1837 raged for more than 30 hours through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, forcing the recreation of much of its decoration."],"abstract":"The <b>Winter Palace</b> is the great Baroque palace on the Neva in Saint Petersburg, built for Empress Elizabeth between 1754 and 1762 to the design of Bartolomeo Rastrelli and long the main residence of the Russian emperors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was the fourth palace of that name, replacing a residence Rastrelli had designed in the 1730s for Empress Anna, which had become too small for the growing court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> To make room, houses built in Peter the Great's time for his officials were pulled down, and the new wings were laid out as a closed square around a large cross-shaped courtyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At more than 20 metres (66 feet) high it set a new scale for the city, and nobles soon raised palaces on Nevsky Prospect that copied or answered its facades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Rastrelli planned within it a throne hall 28.4 metres (93 feet) wide and 48.3 metres (158 feet) long, whose span called for unusual floor and roof construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Elizabeth treated the building as a matter of state, saying that it rose for the glory of the whole Russian Empire, and she chose painters for its state rooms herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She never lived in it, for at her death it was still unfinished and so largely unpaid for that Rastrelli refused her request to hurry her own rooms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Labourers worked night and day through the first months of 1762, and Peter III moved in with his court on Holy Saturday, 16 April 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Less than three months later, during the coup of 28 June, the Senate and the Holy Synod met in the new palace and swore allegiance to Catherine while the guards regiments massed outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A fire that broke out on the evening of 17 December 1837 burned for more than 30 hours, and much of the interior decoration then had to be recreated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["О. В. Петрова, «Ансамбль Дворцовой площади в конце XIX — начале XX века. Новые черты, реставрации, проект реконструкции», <i>Новое искусствознание</i>, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ansambl-dvortsovoy-ploschadi-v-kontse-xix-nachale-xx-veka-novye-cherty-restavratsii-proekt-rekonstruktsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 356–57, 367, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Peter III, Emperor of Russia: The Story of a Crisis and a Crime</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1902), 109–10, 159–61, https://archive.org/details/peteriiiemperor00baingoog. [Open access]","Я. Соколова, «Венецианские живописцы в России в эпоху Елизаветы Петровны: творческий и рабочий процесс», <i>Искусствознание</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/venetsianskie-zhivopistsy-v-rossii-v-epohu-elizavety-petrovny-tvorcheskiy-i-rabochiy-protsess. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Каптиков, «Ф.-Б. Растрелли и архитектура барокко в Северной Италии», <i>Академический вестник УралНИИпроект РААСН</i>, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/f-b-rastrelli-i-arhitektura-barokko-v-severnoy-italii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Г. Головина, «Архитектурно-конструктивные особенности зданий Санкт-Петербурга 1740-х – начала 1760-х гг.», <i>Системные технологии</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/arhitekturno-konstruktivnye-osobennosti-zdaniy-sankt-peterburga-1740-h-nachala-1760-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. С. Торбик, «Реставрация мебели, спасенной от пожара Зимнего дворца 1837 года», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Искусствоведение</i>, 2014, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/restavratsiya-mebeli-spasennoy-ot-pozhara-zimnego-dvortsa-1837-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Кузнецов, «Фасад Его Величеству. Строгановский дворец, дом компании «Зингер» и дворец Белосельских-Белозерских: Невский проспект как архитектурный ансамбль», <i>Искусствознание</i>, 2020, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fasad-ego-velichestvu-stroganovskiy-dvorets-dom-kompanii-zinger-i-dvorets-beloselskih-belozerskih-nevskiy-prospekt-kak. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new Winter Palace needed in the 1750s?","a":"Because Anna's palace, designed by Rastrelli in the 1730s, could no longer hold Elizabeth's growing household and court services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empress decided to rebuild on a far larger scale, and Rastrelli's final project of 1754 amounted to a new building on the old site and the plots beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Elizabeth never live in it?","a":"Because the work dragged on until the year of her death and the treasury could not keep up with the cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When she asked Rastrelli to finish her own apartments quickly he refused, much as French jewellers refused to sell her goods on credit, and it was Peter III who moved in, in April 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the palace matter in the coup of 1762?","a":"Because it was the seat of government as well as a home: on 28 June the Senate and the Holy Synod gathered there and swore allegiance to Catherine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troops crowded round it to acclaim her, so the new palace became the stage on which her seizure of power was made official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Winter Palace"},
    "ru-334": {"questions":["Paired columns set against pilasters and cornices broken forward over them gave the late, Elizabethan phase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an architectural language all its own.","More churches in the style of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were built across Russia under Catherine II than under Elizabeth, though the capital's architects had by then moved on to Neoclassicism."],"abstract":"<b>Russian Baroque</b> is the name given to the Baroque architecture built in Russia from the late 17th century onwards, in which Western European forms and decoration were joined to Russian building traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians usually divide it into an early Moscow Baroque of the 1680s to 1700s, sometimes called Naryshkin Baroque, a mature Petrine Baroque of the 1700s to 1720s and a late Baroque of the 1730s to 1760s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Petrine phase was centred on the new capital, where the Peter and Paul Fortress was rebuilt in brick from 1706 under the supervision of the architect Trezzini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once the government moved to Saint Petersburg in 1712 a whole team of foreign architects arrived, at first all of them Germans, though what some of them actually built remains unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trezzini's Peter and Paul Cathedral is thought to have set the pattern for a new kind of church interior, hung with painted biblical scenes that provincial painters went on using through the 18th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The late phase is often called Elizabethan Baroque after the empress whose court it served, and its leading figure was Bartolomeo Rastrelli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its architects bent the classical rules freely, pairing columns, setting them against pilasters and breaking cornices forward over them until the orders formed a language of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its great palaces and cathedrals also demanded new engineering, with special foundations, vaults and roof frames to span wide halls and domes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Credit within the style has not always been fairly shared, since the Anichkov Palace, usually linked with Rastrelli, was begun in 1741 to a design by the Russian architect Zemtsov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A count of churches rather than masterpieces gives another picture, with 174 Baroque churches from Elizabeth's reign against 316 from the 1760s to the 1780s, and building in the style went on in the provinces into the 1810s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Е. Ю. Верховых, «Процесс освоения стиля барокко в русском церковном зодчестве», <i>Академический вестник УралНИИпроект РААСН</i>, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protsess-osvoeniya-stilya-barokko-v-russkom-tserkovnom-zodchestve. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Р. Возняк, «Архитектурный колонный ордер в эпоху елизаветинского барокко (1742—1762 гг.) в Санкт-Петербурге», <i>Вестник МГСУ</i>, 2013, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/arhitekturnyy-kolonnyy-order-v-epohu-elizavetinskogo-barokko-1742-1762-gg-v-sankt-peterburge-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Сергеева, «Новая система церковной декорации в эпоху петровского барокко», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novaya-sistema-tserkovnoy-dekoratsii-v-epohu-petrovskogo-barokko. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Каптиков, «Русско-немецкие архитектурные связи в эпоху барокко», <i>Академический вестник УралНИИпроект РААСН</i>, 2019, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-nemetskie-arhitekturnye-svyazi-v-epohu-barokko. [in Russian] [Open access]","Rosa Newmarch, <i>The Russian Arts</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1916), 97–100, https://archive.org/details/russianarts0000rosa. [Open access]","С. В. Клименко, «К характеристике стиля архитектуры Аничкова дворца в Петербурге: между аннинской и елизаветинской эпохами», <i>Architecture and Modern Information Technologies</i>, no. 4 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-harakteristike-stilya-arhitektury-anichkova-dvortsa-v-peterburge-mezhdu-anninskoy-i-elizavetinskoy-epohami. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Г. Головина, «Архитектурно-конструктивные особенности зданий Санкт-Петербурга 1740-х – начала 1760-х гг.», <i>Системные технологии</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/arhitekturno-konstruktivnye-osobennosti-zdaniy-sankt-peterburga-1740-h-nachala-1760-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the dates of the style depend on who is counting?","a":"Because art historians date it by its outstanding buildings, which cluster in the capital before the 1760s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A count of all Baroque churches instead shows building peaking under Catherine II and going on in the provinces until the 1810s, long after the court had turned away from the style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Baroque reach Russia largely through foreign masters?","a":"Because Peter the Great wanted a European capital quickly and hired architects and craftsmen from abroad, first Trezzini and then a team of Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their buildings, above all the Peter and Paul Cathedral, became models that Russian builders and painters went on copying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the style end at court in the 1760s?","a":"Because taste changed with the new reign: under Catherine II the Louis-Seize style was brought in by architects such as de la Motte and Rinaldi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elizabethan Baroque is therefore treated as the close of the Baroque period in Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Baroque"},
    "ru-335": {"questions":["Zorndorf, Kunersdorf and a brief entry into Berlin were all Russian feats of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet Russia ended it having gained nothing but prestige.","Frederick II opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by invading Saxony in August 1756 and was saved from ruin only when Elizabeth died and her successor made peace."],"abstract":"The <b>Seven Years' War</b> of 1756 to 1763 was a European and colonial war in which Prussia, helped by British subsidies, fought Austria, France, Russia, Sweden and many German states, while Britain and France also fought overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began at the end of August 1756, when Frederick II of Prussia invaded Saxony in what has been called a preventive war against enemies he expected to attack him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia under Empress Elizabeth was the most determined of those enemies, and it was her court that pressed a hesitant Austria to act rather than the other way round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early Russian campaigns were badly prepared, short of horses, new muskets, artillery and supplies, and the victory of Gross-Jägersdorf in 1757 was followed by a retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early in 1758 Russian troops occupied East Prussia and made its people swear allegiance to Elizabeth, who appointed governors in Königsberg and meant to keep the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1758 the Russians fought Frederick at Zorndorf, one of the bloodiest battles of the age, and in 1759 they and the Austrians destroyed his army at Kunersdorf, at a great cost in Russian dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In October 1760 Russian and Austrian troops even held Berlin for a few days and levied a heavy contribution on the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prussia was saved by Elizabeth's death in January 1762, since her nephew Peter III admired Frederick, made peace on 5 May 1762 and restored everything Russia had conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia thus gained nothing but prestige from enormous losses of men and money, while the war between Austria and Prussia ended in 1763 with neither side gaining territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war was not universally celebrated even at the time, for Sumarokov's journal of 1759 portrayed war as an apocalypse, and in 1909 the imperial government chose not to mark the 150th anniversary of Kunersdorf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 372–80, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 295–96, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","F. W. Longman, <i>Frederick the Great and the Seven Years’ War</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 148–54, 216–22, 230–31, https://archive.org/details/frederickgreata00philgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 355, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","М. Ю. Анисимов, «Восточная Пруссия в российских планах в годы Семилетней войны (1756–1763 гг.)», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i>, 2014, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vostochnaya-prussiya-v-rossiyskih-planah-v-gody-semiletney-voyny-1756-1763-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лизогуб и Е. В. Шестопалов, «Семилетняя война в пространстве памяти Российской империи начала ХХ века», <i>Ученые записки Петрозаводского государственного университета</i>, 47, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/semiletnyaya-voyna-v-prostranstve-pamyati-rossiyskoy-imperii-nachala-hh-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Растягаев и Ю. В. Сложеникина, «Рецепция Семилетней войны на страницах журнала А. П. Сумарокова «Трудолюбивая пчела» (1759): первый пацифистский пафос российской журналистики», <i>Вестник Новосибирского государственного университета. Серия: История. Филология</i>, 2023, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/retseptsiya-semiletney-voyny-na-stranitsah-zhurnala-a-p-sumarokova-trudolyubivaya-pchela-1759-pervyy-patsifistskiy-pafos-rossiyskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Ю. Ткаченко, «Провиантская служба русской армии в Семилетней (1756–1763 гг.) войне», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 7 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/proviantskaya-sluzhba-russkoy-armii-v-semiletney-1756-1763-g-g-voyne. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Russia so determined to fight Prussia?","a":"Because Elizabeth believed the King of Prussia had to be made harmless to his neighbours by cutting down his lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia's share was to be East Prussia, which its troops occupied in 1758 and whose people swore allegiance to her, and she told the Austrian envoy she would sell her diamonds rather than stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia gain nothing from its victories?","a":"Because Elizabeth died at the start of 1762 and her heir, Peter III, idolised Frederick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He made peace on 5 May and restored every conquest, and Catherine II, who overthrew him weeks later, came to power too late to change the outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the early Russian campaigns so weak?","a":"Because the army set out short of horses, modern muskets, cavalry weapons, artillery and supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its commanders were also inexperienced, and the first victory, at Gross-Jägersdorf in 1757, was won almost by accident and then followed by a retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Seven Years' War"},
    "ru-336": {"questions":["Rumyantsev's division held the Spitzberg heights against Seydlitz's cavalry at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Laudon's Austrian horsemen turned a Prussian retreat into a rout.","Saltykov wrote to Elizabeth after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that another such victory would force him to carry the news to Saint Petersburg himself, staff in hand, for want of messengers."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kunersdorf</b> was fought on 12 August 1759 by the Gregorian calendar, 1 August by the Julian, near the village of Kunersdorf east of Frankfurt on the Oder, and ended in the worst defeat of Frederick II's career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian army under Count Peter Saltykov, joined by an Austrian corps under Laudon, held a line of entrenched hills between Frankfurt and the village, covered by marshes and more than a hundred heavy guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Saltykov had been given the command only that spring without ever having led an army, yet in July he had already beaten a Prussian corps at Kay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Frederick attacked with about 48,000 men an allied force that the sources put at between 60,000 and 78,000, after a long march to strike the Russian left from the flank and rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By early evening the Prussians had stormed the Russian left wing, taking guns and some thousands of prisoners, and messengers were sent off with news of a victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Frederick refused his generals' advice to stop, and his exhausted infantry broke against the Spitzberg heights in the Russian centre, held by Rumyantsev's division with Austrian help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Laudon threw his fresh cavalry at the spent Prussians the retreat became a rout, and that night only about 3,000 of the king's 48,000 men were still with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Prussians are usually said to have lost about 19,000 men and 182 guns, and the Russians and Austrians nearly 16,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Frederick wrote that evening that he held all for lost and would not survive his country's ruin, yet the allies quarrelled and never marched on Berlin, which lay open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Russia the victory faded from memory, and in 1909 an official commission decided not to celebrate its 150th anniversary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 268–77, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","F. W. Longman, <i>Frederick the Great and the Seven Years’ War</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 150–54, https://archive.org/details/frederickgreata00philgoog. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 374–76, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Е. П. Абрамов, ««Ему нет равного». Развитие военного искусства генерал-фельдмаршалом П. А. Румянцевым-Задунайским», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, 2020, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/emu-net-ravnogo-razvitie-voennogo-iskusstva-general-feldmarshalom-p-a-rumyantsevym-zadunayskim. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лизогуб и Е. В. Шестопалов, «Семилетняя война в пространстве памяти Российской империи начала ХХ века», <i>Ученые записки Петрозаводского государственного университета</i>, 47, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/semiletnyaya-voyna-v-prostranstve-pamyati-rossiyskoy-imperii-nachala-hh-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","J. W. von Archenholz, <i>The History of the Seven Years War in Germany</i>, trans. F. A. Catty (Frankfort: C. Jugel, 1843), 250–53, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_3JoBAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Frederick attack such a strong position?","a":"Because the Russian victory at Kay had forced him to leave the Austrians and hurry to protect Brandenburg, and he always had to finish one task fast to rush to another front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He wanted the Russians not merely beaten but destroyed, so he refused to stop when his generals urged it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the victory not end the war?","a":"Because Saltykov and the Austrian commander Daun quarrelled and would not take orders from each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saltykov complained that two battles had cost Russia enough and that the Austrians should now win some victories themselves, which gave Frederick time to rebuild his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the battle forgotten in Russia?","a":"Because it was fought far from Russia, and a retired general writing before its 150th anniversary blamed that distance for its fading from memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1909 an official commission decided not to celebrate the anniversary at all, perhaps partly to save money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Kunersdorf"},
    "ru-337": {"questions":["The merchant Gotzkowsky talked Tottleben's demand for four million thalers down to one and a half million during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1760.","Promised as revenge for a humiliating failure before Kolberg, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1760 helped refill the Russian treasury but lasted only a few days before Frederick approached."],"abstract":"The <b>raid on Berlin</b> of October 1760 was the brief occupation of the Prussian capital by Russian and Austrian troops during the Seven Years' War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the Russian court it was meant as revenge for a humiliating failure before the fortress of Kolberg that summer, after which Saint Petersburg had promised its allies a blow to eclipse the setback.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 20,000 Russians under Chernyshev and 15,000 Austrians under Lacy marched on the city, and the Russian advance guard under Count Tottleben, a German who had long lived in Berlin, reached its gates on 3 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Berlin had no walls fit for defence and only about 1,900 soldiers, and although Prussian corps came to its aid, their commanders would not risk a battle and fell back to Spandau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city capitulated to Tottleben on 9 October, its magistrates choosing the Russians because they expected no mercy from the Austrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tottleben first demanded four million thalers, but the merchant Gotzkowsky, who had befriended Russian officers taken prisoner after Zorndorf, talked the sum down to one and a half million with 200,000 more for the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arsenal, the mint and the royal magazines were stripped and a powder store blew up, while Austrian troops and irregular horsemen plundered houses until Tottleben had his soldiers fire on the looters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> News that Frederick was marching to the rescue ended the occupation, and the allies left on 12 October after a stay of only a few days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saint Petersburg greeted the news with great rejoicing, and the taking of Berlin was long counted among the triumphs of Elizabeth's reign, though its real gain was money for an empty treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tottleben's mildness brought him under suspicion, and he was later arrested, while in 1909 an imperial commission judged the capitulation of Berlin to be of no historical value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["J. W. von Archenholz, <i>The History of the Seven Years War in Germany</i>, trans. F. A. Catty (Frankfort: C. Jugel, 1843), 365–71, 465, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_3JoBAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Daughter of Peter the Great: A History of Russian Diplomacy and of the Russian Court under the Empress Elizabeth Petrovna, 1741–1762</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1900), 295–96, 302, https://archive.org/details/daughterofpeterg00bainuoft. [Open access]","F. W. Longman, <i>Frederick the Great and the Seven Years’ War</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 216, https://archive.org/details/frederickgreata00philgoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 355, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","М. Ю. Анисимов, «Русские осады Семилетней войны 1756–1763 гг.», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, 2016, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkie-osady-semiletney-voyny-1756-1763-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лизогуб и Е. В. Шестопалов, «Семилетняя война в пространстве памяти Российской империи начала ХХ века», <i>Ученые записки Петрозаводского государственного университета</i>, 47, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/semiletnyaya-voyna-v-prostranstve-pamyati-rossiyskoy-imperii-nachala-hh-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Berlin choose to surrender to the Russians?","a":"Because the Russians were only auxiliaries in the war on Prussia, while the Austrians were Prussia's chief enemy and expected to show no mercy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The merchant Gotzkowsky, who had helped Russian officers held prisoner after Zorndorf, used his friendship with Tottleben to soften the terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the occupation last so short a time?","a":"Because Frederick was hurrying back from Silesia, and the allied council had decided on retreat even before the city fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the cry that the king was coming, Russians and Austrians left within days and moved off in different directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the raid bring Tottleben under suspicion?","a":"Because his treatment of the city was so mild that his loyalty was doubted, and he was later arrested and sent to Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doubt had substance, since Frederick had bribed him to pass on secret news of Russian troop movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1760 raid on Berlin"},
    "ru-338": {"questions":["An admirer who kissed Frederick II's bust in public, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made peace with Prussia in May 1762 and handed back everything Russia had won in the war.","Planning a war on Denmark to win back Schleswig for Holstein, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> angered the guards he ordered to march and was deposed before the campaign began."],"abstract":"<b>Peter III</b> was emperor of Russia for six months, from the death of his aunt Elizabeth on 25 December 1761 by the Julian calendar, 5 January 1762 by the Gregorian, until his wife Catherine overthrew him on 28 June 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was born at Kiel in February 1728 as Charles Peter Ulric, son of the Duke of Holstein and of Peter the Great's daughter Anna, and was named after both Charles XII of Sweden and his grandfather Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His childhood under a brutal tutor was miserable, and by the time Elizabeth adopted him as her heir and brought him to Saint Petersburg in 1742 he had been taught to despise Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Catherine's memoirs describe a husband who still played with puppets and toys years after his marriage, though she wrote them as the woman who had deposed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He never hid that he idolised Frederick II of Prussia, kissing the king's bust in public and putting the guards into tight Prussian-style uniforms they bitterly resented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His short reign still produced lasting measures, for he freed the nobility from compulsory service, abolished the Secret Chancellery and allowed Old Believers who had fled abroad to return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians have often traced the nobility decree to court factions such as the Vorontsovs, who gained influence at his accession, rather than to the emperor's own ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In foreign policy he reversed his aunt's course, making peace with Prussia on 5 May 1762 and returning every conquest Russia had made in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then prepared a war against Denmark to recover Schleswig for his Duchy of Holstein, with Rumyantsev chosen to command, and the guards resented being ordered to march for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the guards rose for Catherine only a few officers, such as Izmailov of the Preobrazhensky Regiment, tried to keep their men loyal, and Peter abdicated and died at Ropsha on 6 July, officially of colic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Peter III, Emperor of Russia: The Story of a Crisis and a Crime</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1902), 1–9, 39–47, 70–71, https://archive.org/details/peteriiiemperor00baingoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 359–72, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","F. W. Longman, <i>Frederick the Great and the Seven Years’ War</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 218–22, https://archive.org/details/frederickgreata00philgoog. [Open access]","Catherine II, <i>Memoirs of the Empress Catherine II</i>, with a preface by A. Herzen (New York: D. Appleton, 1859), 52, 81, https://archive.org/details/memoirsempressc01cathgoog. [Open access]","О. П. Ведьмин, «П. И. Измайлов и император Пётр III», <i>СибСкрипт</i>, no. 3-1 (63) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/p-i-izmaylov-i-imperator-pyotr-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Курукин, ««Горю желанием исполнить всевысочайшее… соизволение…». Корпус П. А. Румянцева и несостоявшаяся война с Данией в 1762 году», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, 2019, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/goryu-zhelaniem-ispolnit-vsevysochayshee-soizvolenie-korpus-p-a-rumyantseva-i-nesostoyavshayasya-voyna-s-daniey-v-1762-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Киселев, «Проблема экономических прав дворянства в Уложенной комиссии в 1761–1762 гг. и манифест о вольности дворянской», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 449 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-ekonomicheskih-prav-dvoryanstva-v-ulozhennoy-komissii-v-1761-1762-gg-i-manifest-o-volnosti-dvoryanskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Peter III give Russia's conquests back to Prussia?","a":"Because his admiration for Frederick II bordered on idolatry, and his foreign policy rested on little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Frederick sent home his Russian prisoners, Peter announced peace and the return of all conquered land, and he signed the treaty read to him by the Prussian envoy without consulting his own chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the guards turn against him?","a":"Because he favoured his Holstein troops over them, forced Prussian drill and uniforms on them and called them janissaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order to march against Denmark over a Holstein quarrel came on top of this, and many feared being reduced to ordinary line regiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are his reforms remembered so differently from his reputation?","a":"Because the same six months that made him a figure of ridicule also saw the nobility freed from service, the Secret Chancellery abolished and the Old Believers allowed home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The officer Bolotov recorded the nobles' joy at the decree, though many of these measures were pressed on Peter by the Vorontsov and Shuvalov circles to win popularity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peter III of Russia"},
    "ru-339": {"questions":["A secretary locked overnight in an empty room with a Danish dog is said to have drafted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, remembering the liberty of the nobles so often urged on the emperor.","Andrey Bolotov left the service in June 1762 to settle on his Tula estate, among the first to use the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had ended compulsory noble service."],"abstract":"The <b>Manifesto on the Freedom of the Nobility</b>, issued by Peter III on 18 February 1762 by the Julian calendar, released the Russian nobility from the compulsory state service imposed on them since Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its official title promised the grant of liberty and freedom to the whole Russian nobility, and it let nobles serve or not as they chose and travel abroad, even to enter the service of foreign rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crown kept the right to call them up in an emergency, officers could not resign during a campaign, and every noble was still ordered to have his sons educated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manifesto turned the old legal duty into a moral one, warning that a noble who neither served nor learned would be held in contempt and not received at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It said nothing about serfdom, although the nobles' right to own serfs had grown out of the very service from which they were now released.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nobles greeted it with delight, and the procurator-general proposed that the Senate raise a golden statue of the emperor in gratitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The young officer Andrey Bolotov was among the first to use it, leaving the service in June 1762 for his estate at Dvoryaninovo in Tula province, where he laid out a new kind of house and garden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Who wrote it is still unsettled, and a story told by the contemporary writer Shcherbatov has the secretary Volkov locked overnight in an empty room with a Danish dog until he produced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of its ideas had been worked out earlier in Elizabeth's law commission, and its passage has been linked to the Vorontsov faction's rise at Peter's accession, though the evidence for that link is now questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The manifesto outlived its author, and peasants who saw one class freed from its obligations expected their own release, a hope that fed the risings that ended in Pugachev's revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 338–40, 350, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","И. В. Васильева, «К вопросу об авторстве текста и значении манифеста Петра III о вольности дворянской», <i>Вестник Чувашского университета</i>, 2004, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-ob-avtorstve-teksta-i-znachenii-manifesta-petra-iii-o-volnosti-dvoryanskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Киселев, «Проблема экономических прав дворянства в Уложенной комиссии в 1761–1762 гг. и манифест о вольности дворянской», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 449 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-ekonomicheskih-prav-dvoryanstva-v-ulozhennoy-komissii-v-1761-1762-gg-i-manifest-o-volnosti-dvoryanskoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Козлов, «Русская провинциальная усадьба после Манифеста о вольности дворянства 18 февраля 1762 г. (по материалам переписки А. Т. и П. А. Болотовых)», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (32) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-provintsialnaya-usadba-posle-manifesta-o-volnosti-dvoryanstva-18-fevralya-1762-g-po-materialam-perepiski-a-t-i-p-a. [in Russian] [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Peter III, Emperor of Russia: The Story of a Crisis and a Crime</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1902), 45–47, https://archive.org/details/peteriiiemperor00baingoog. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the manifesto still insist on education?","a":"Because it turned the old legal duty into a moral one: nobles could stop serving, but every one had to have his sons taught in a Russian school, abroad or at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A noble who ignored this, it warned, would face the emperor's grave displeasure and be scorned as unworthy of the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is its authorship disputed?","a":"Because the only detailed story comes from Shcherbatov, who retold the secretary Volkov's account of being locked in a room to write a decree overnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volkov himself, listing his chief works for Peter in a letter of July 1762, did not name the manifesto, and some of its ideas went back to Elizabeth's law commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did freeing the nobles raise the peasant question?","a":"Because serfdom had been justified by the nobles' service, and once the service went, the right to own serfs remained without its reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Serf unrest grew after the manifesto, and Catherine had to begin her reign by pacifying about 150,000 serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-340": {"questions":["Unlike the guards' revolutions of 1725, 1730 and 1741, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> overthrew a lawful emperor to whom the guards themselves had already sworn allegiance.","The arrest of a guards officer named Passek on 27 June forced the plotters to launch the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at once, before he could be made to betray them."],"abstract":"The <b>coup of 1762</b> was the overthrow of Emperor Peter III by the guards regiments of Saint Petersburg on 28 June 1762 by the Julian calendar, 9 July by the Gregorian, which put his wife on the throne as Catherine II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was the last of the palace revolutions by which the guards had made rulers since 1725, but unlike those of 1725, 1730 and 1741 it removed a lawful sovereign to whom they had sworn allegiance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter had made enemies on every side, mocking the rites of the Church, humiliating the guards with Prussian drill and making a peace with Prussia that gave away all of Russia's conquests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plot drew in Count Nikita Panin, Princess Dashkova and the hetman Cyril Razumovsky, while its active core was a group of guards officers around the Orlov brothers, among them Catherine's lover Grigory Orlov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrest of one of the plotters, an officer named Passek, after a soldier's careless questions on 27 June forced the conspirators to act at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Before dawn on 28 June Alexis Orlov brought Catherine from Peterhof to the barracks of the Izmailovsky Regiment, which swore allegiance to her, and the Semyonovsky and Preobrazhensky regiments soon followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was proclaimed autocrat at the Kazan Cathedral, received the oaths of the Senate and Holy Synod at the Winter Palace and that evening rode towards Peterhof at the head of the troops in a guards uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peter, turned away from Kronstadt by its garrison, signed an abdication, was taken to Ropsha and died there on 6 July, his death announced as colic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new regime justified itself in a long manifesto of 6 July, written by Teplov, which presented the coup as the rescue of the fatherland from a ruler lacking good and humane qualities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sermons and the festivities of Catherine's coronation in Moscow that September repeated the message, since a coup that had dethroned and killed a lawful emperor could not by itself guarantee the plotters' success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 4 (London: J. M. Dent, 1926), 363–72, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia04kliuuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Peter III, Emperor of Russia: The Story of a Crisis and a Crime</i> (Westminster: Archibald Constable, 1902), 140–61, 170–71, https://archive.org/details/peteriiiemperor00baingoog. [Open access]","М. А. Киселев, «Обстоятельный манифест о восшествии Екатерины II на престол 1762 г.: авторство и идейные источники», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 5 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obstoyatelnyy-manifest-o-vosshestvii-ekateriny-ii-na-prestol-1762-g-avtorstvo-i-ideynye-istochniki. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Фаизова, «Утверждение на российском престоле Екатерины II и пиар-кампания 1762–1763 гг.», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (51) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/utverzhdenie-na-rossiyskom-prestole-ekateriny-ii-i-piar-kampaniya-1762-1763-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Д. Бугров, «Политическое богословие и легитимация екатерининского переворота 1762 года: «Слово торжественное» Константина (Борковского)», <i>Вестник Удмуртского университета. Серия «История и филология»</i>, 30, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskoe-bogoslovie-i-legitimatsiya-ekaterininskogo-perevorota-1762-goda-slovo-torzhestvennoe-konstantina-borkovskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. П. Ведьмин, «П. И. Измайлов и император Пётр III», <i>СибСкрипт</i>, no. 3-1 (63) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/p-i-izmaylov-i-imperator-pyotr-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 1–9, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the guards back Catherine against a lawful emperor?","a":"Because Peter had wounded their national pride, favoured his Holstein troops, forced Prussian drill on them and ordered them to war against Denmark over a Holstein quarrel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guards came to believe that a ruler without a legal title would understand Russia's interests better than the lawful one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the coup called bloodless when Peter died within days?","a":"Because the rising in the capital itself cost no lives and looked more like a national movement than a plot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet Peter, held at Ropsha, died on 6 July after what a note sent to Catherine called a quarrel with his guards at dinner, and the government announced his death as colic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the new regime need so much justification?","a":"Because a foreign-born empress had seized power from a lawful emperor who was then killed, and contemporaries did not all accept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her manifesto of 6 July, sermons such as the one preached in Moscow on 10 July, and the spectacle of her coronation all presented the coup as the rescue of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-351": {"questions":["Alexis Orlov was allowed to add the name of the bay to his own after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a victory Catherine marked with medals and a yearly salute of 31 guns.","British officers in Russian service, Greig, Dugdale and Mackenzie among them, led the night attack that won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and later British writers claimed much of the credit."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Chesma</b> was the destruction of the Ottoman fleet in the bay of Çeşme, on the Anatolian coast opposite the island of Chios, by a Russian squadron on 24–26 June 1770 by the Julian calendar, 5–7 July by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The squadron had sailed from Kronstadt in 1769 and gone round western Europe into the Aegean, meaning to strike at Turkey from the south and to raise the Greeks of the Morea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek rising collapsed as soon as Turkish and Albanian troops advanced, and Alexis Orlov, who had called the Greeks to freedom in May, had to take his men back on board within the month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 24 June the Russians met the Turkish fleet in the strait of Chios, and in the close fighting the Ottoman flagship and the Russian <i>Saint Eustathius</i>, locked together, caught fire and blew up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Turks then crowded into Çeşme bay under the cover of its shore batteries, where the Russian ships blockaded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the night of 26 June a division under the British-born Samuel Greig entered the bay and fired on the anchored ships, while four fireships stood in behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most of the fireships failed, but the flames spread through a fleet packed so tightly that by morning only one ship of the line and a few galleys had been brought out intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Russian count put Turkish losses at more than 10,000 killed and wounded against 534 Russians killed, while an English officer with the fleet reckoned at least 9,000 Turkish dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Who won the night is still told two ways, for a British naval writer called it practically a British affair with Russian crews, while Russian accounts honour Orlov and the fireship of Lieutenant Dmitry Ilyin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The victory did not open the Dardanelles, which Orlov never forced, but Catherine ordered a salute of 31 guns every 24 June, let him style himself Orlov-Chesmensky, and paintings of the burning fleet came to hang at Peterhof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 19–20, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","An Officer on Board the Russian Fleet, <i>An Authentic Narrative of the Russian Expedition against the Turks by Sea and Land</i> (London: S. Hooper, 1772), 76–87, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_an-authentic-narrative-o_officer-on-board-the-rus_1772. [Open access]","Fred T. Jane, <i>The Imperial Russian Navy: Its Past, Present, and Future</i> (London: W. Thacker, 1899), 82–84, https://archive.org/details/cu31924079598227. [Open access]","Albert Sorel, <i>The Eastern Question in the Eighteenth Century: The Partition of Poland and the Treaty of Kainardji</i>, trans. F. C. Bramwell (London: Methuen, 1898), 87–88, https://archive.org/details/easternquestioni00soreuoft. [Open access]","Г. А. Гребенщикова, «„Чесма заставила говорить о себе всю Европу!“ К 255-летию победы русского флота в Чесменском сражении», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 6 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chesma-zastavila-govorit-o-sebe-vsyu-evropu-k-255-letiyu-pobedy-russkogo-flota-v-chesmenskom-srazhenii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ш. Кадырбаев, «Иностранцы на российской службе в Средиземноморье в эпоху Екатерины Великой», <i>Восточный архив</i>, no. 16 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/inostrantsy-na-rossiyskoy-sluzhbe-v-sredizemnomorie-v-epohu-ekateriny-velikoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Голдовский, «О картинах и гравюрах на темы Чесменской битвы из собрания музеев Петергофа и Государственного Эрмитажа: эпизод русско-английских связей в XVIII веке», <i>Новое искусствознание</i>, no. 1–2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-kartinah-i-gravyurah-na-temy-chesmenskoy-bitvy-iz-sobraniya-muzeev-petergofa-i-gosudarstvennogo-ermitazha-epizod-russko. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Turkish fleet burn so completely?","a":"Because after the first day it had crowded into the narrow bay of Çeşme so tightly that small vessels were hauled ashore for lack of room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once burning ships drifted among the others the fire spread from ship to ship, and Russian guns kept anyone on shore from coming to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the credit for the victory disputed?","a":"Because the attack was planned and led largely by British officers serving Russia, such as Greig, Dugdale and Mackenzie, and a British naval writer called the night practically a British affair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian accounts stress Orlov’s command and the fireship of Lieutenant Ilyin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the victory not end the war at once?","a":"Because Orlov failed to pass the Dardanelles in time, so the fleet stayed in the Mediterranean instead of threatening Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It blockaded the straits from a base on Paros, while the war was decided on land by the armies of Rumyantsev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Chesma"},
    "ru-352": {"questions":["An Austrian envoy called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a model of Russian diplomatic skill, since its scattered religious clauses let Russia speak for the sultan’s Orthodox subjects.","Agreed within four hours in a general’s tent, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the Porte pay Russia 4.5 million roubles and opened the Black Sea to Russian merchant ships."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</b> was the peace that ended the Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774, signed on 10 July 1774 by the Julian calendar, 21 July by the Gregorian, at the village of Küçük Kaynarca, a few leagues from the Danube fortress of Silistria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rumyantsev had crossed the Danube and surrounded the grand vizier’s army at Shumla, and when Turkish envoys reached his tent the terms were agreed within four hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Porte recognised the Tatars of the Crimea, the Budjak and the Kuban as free nations, entirely independent of any foreign power and ruled by a khan of the line of Genghis Khan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russia kept Azov, Kerch, Yenikale and Kinburn, with the two Kabardas in the Caucasus, and won free passage for its merchant ships on the Black Sea and out into the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Porte also agreed to pay Russia an indemnity of 4.5 million roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the long run the most important articles were those on religion, scattered through the text, by which the Porte promised to protect the Christian religion and its churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia could build a church in Constantinople and make representations on behalf of its clergy, and its envoys could speak for the principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian writers of the 19th century read these clauses as a general right to protect the sultan’s Orthodox subjects, although such a right was nowhere actually written into the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Austrian envoy at Constantinople called the treaty a model of skill on the part of the Russian diplomatists and a rare example of imbecility on the part of the Turkish negotiators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Crimea’s independence proved short, for the treaty cut the khanate off from its Ottoman protector and opened the way to its annexation by Russia in 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Albert Sorel, <i>The Eastern Question in the Eighteenth Century: The Partition of Poland and the Treaty of Kainardji</i>, trans. F. C. Bramwell (London: Methuen, 1898), 247–51, https://archive.org/details/easternquestioni00soreuoft. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 256–57, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","W. Lyon Blease, <i>Suvorof</i> (London: Constable, 1920), 57, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028460792. [Open access]","W. Eton, <i>A Survey of the Turkish Empire</i>, 3rd ed. (London: T. Cadell jun. and W. Davies, 1801), 309–10, https://archive.org/details/surveyofturkishe00eton. [Open access]","Н. М. Миловский, «Кючук-Кайнарджийский мирный договор и итоги русско-турецкой войны 1768–1774 гг. в российской историографии», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i> 24, no. 2 (2025): 264–76, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8674-2025-24-2-264-276. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Стоилова, «Россия, Высокая Порта и православные подданные султана после Кючук-Кайнарджийского мира, 1774–1787 гг.», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-vysokaya-porta-i-pravoslavnye-poddannye-sultana-posle-kyuchuk-kaynardzhiyskogo-mira-1774-1787-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. П. Кудрявцева, «Строительство Черноморского флота и портов в Российской империи после заключения Кючук-Кайнарджийского мира», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroitelstvo-chernomorskogo-flota-i-portov-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-posle-zaklyucheniya-kyuchuk-kaynardzhiyskogo-mira. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia not simply annex the Crimea in 1774?","a":"Because the treaty made the Crimea nominally independent instead, which one biographer of Suvorov called a diplomatic fiction that left the peninsula to be taken whenever Russia pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cut off from the sultan, the khanate lost its old protector, and that opened the way to its annexation nine years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the religious clauses matter so much later?","a":"Because Russia later read the promises to protect Christians and to hear its representations as a general right to intervene for the sultan’s Orthodox subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Turks understood them to cover only churches, clergy and worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian church in Constantinople gave the claim a visible symbol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Turks accept such terms so quickly?","a":"Because their main army was surrounded at Shumla and deserting wholesale, so the grand vizier could only throw himself on the mercy of the victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia, strained by the Pugachev revolt, also had reason to settle fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca"},
    "ru-353": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1783, Suvorov feasted the Nogai chiefs on a hundred oxen and 800 sheep once they had sworn loyalty to Russia near Yeysk.","The Porte formally accepted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in an act of December 1783, and the following February the former Tatar state became Russia’s Taurida Province."],"abstract":"The <b>annexation of the Crimean Khanate</b> was the absorption of the Tatar state of the Crimea into the Russian Empire, proclaimed by Catherine II’s manifesto of 8 April 1783 by the Julian calendar, which took the peninsula, Taman and the Kuban side under Russian rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca had made the khanate independent in 1774, and in 1777 its nobles swore allegiance to Shahin Giray, a khan whom Russia backed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Shahin Giray tried to remodel his state on European lines, raising paid troops and striking a new coinage, but his reforms and Russian manners turned his subjects against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian troops put down Ottoman-backed revolts, and in 1778 some 75,000 Christians were made to leave for the shores of the Sea of Azov, where many died in their first winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In early 1783 Shahin Giray gave up the throne, apparently hoping for a crown in Persia, and Catherine issued her manifesto that April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The new subjects swore loyalty that summer, Potemkin taking the oath of the Crimean nobility on the rock of Ak-Kaya near Karasubazar and Suvorov that of the Nogai hordes near Yeysk on 28 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Suvorov marked the Nogai oath with a feast of a hundred oxen and 800 sheep, at which Russians and Tatars drank healths together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Porte accepted the loss in an act signed on 28 December 1783, and in February 1784 the peninsula became the Taurida Province, with Sevastopol and Feodosia declared free ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> An English observer reckoned that two-thirds of the Crimea’s people had left for Turkey during Shahin Giray’s reign, and the khan himself, pensioned off to Kaluga, later went to Constantinople and was exiled and strangled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian historian of the next century judged that a region worth not one war led Russia into two, since the second Turkish war of 1787 was fought above all to secure the hold on the Crimea won in 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["W. Eton, <i>A Survey of the Turkish Empire</i>, 3rd ed. (London: T. Cadell jun. and W. Davies, 1801), 309–17, https://archive.org/details/surveyofturkishe00eton. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 20, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Frederic Anthing, <i>History of the Campaigns of Prince Alexander Suworow Rymnikski</i>, vol. 1 (New York: William Cobbett, 1800), https://archive.org/details/historyofcampaig01anthuoft. [Open access]","В. Н. Ратушняк, «Борьба за присоединение Крымского ханства к России на рубеже 1770-х–1780-х гг.», <i>Историческая и социально-образовательная мысль</i>, no. 3 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-za-prisoedinenie-krymskogo-hanstva-k-rossii-na-rubezhe-1770-h-1780-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Жеребкин, «Присоединение Крыма к России Екатериной II как способ решения исторических задач государства во второй половине XVIII века», <i>Манускрипт</i> 13, no. 5 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prisoedinenie-kryma-k-rossii-ekaterinoy-ii-kak-sposob-resheniya-istoricheskih-zadach-gosudarstva-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Кошман, «Образование Таврической области как субъекта административно-территориального устройства Российской империи 1784–1796 г.», <i>Политематический сетевой электронный научный журнал Кубанского государственного аграрного университета</i>, no. 115 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obrazovanie-tavricheskoy-oblasti-kak-subekta-administrativno-territorialnogo-ustroystva-rossiyskoy-imperii-1784-1796-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Б. Вахидов, «Исторический портрет последнего крымского хана Шагин Гирея», <i>Вестник Майкопского государственного технологического университета</i> 17, no. 2 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoricheskiy-portret-poslednego-krymskogo-hana-shagin-gireya. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Никифоров, «О проблемах интеграции Крыма в состав Российской империи в правление Екатерины II», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-problemah-integratsii-kryma-v-sostav-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-pravlenie-ekateriny-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Shahin Giray lose the support of his own people?","a":"Because he abolished the old form of government, raised paid soldiers, took the nobles’ rents for his own use and adopted Christian and Russian manners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His spending outran his income, and Ottoman agents found a discontented country in which to stir up revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to depend on Russian troops to stay in power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia annex the khanate in 1783 rather than leave it independent?","a":"Because the independent khanate stayed unstable, with Ottoman-backed risings and repeated Russian interventions, and Catherine was tired of the alarms they caused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For Russia the Crimea had been the true goal of the first Turkish war, and keeping it secure mattered more than the fiction of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Russia make the annexation official?","a":"Through oaths of loyalty taken in the summer of 1783 from the Crimean nobles and the Nogai hordes, an act of December 1783 in which the Porte accepted the loss, and a decree of February 1784 creating the Taurida Province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1783 Russian annexation of Crimea"},
    "ru-354": {"questions":["Potemkin governed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with almost unlimited powers, filling the steppe with colonists and planning towns such as Yekaterinoslav, which was to have a cathedral larger than St Peter’s.","An English traveller of the 1820s found Germans, Greeks, Bulgarians and Nogai Tatars among the colonists of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which then comprised the governments of Kherson, Yekaterinoslav and Taurida."],"abstract":"<b>New Russia</b>, in Russian Novorossiya, was the name given in the 18th century to the steppe lands north of the Black Sea that the Russian Empire took over and set out to settle and govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name became official on 22 March 1764, when a New Russia Governorate was formed out of the military settlement of New Serbia and the lands of a Cossack regiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New Serbia had been planted in the mid-18th century with Serbian frontier soldiers invited from Austria to guard the border against the Turks and the Crimean Tatars, and their quarrels with Poles, Ukrainians and Tatars often troubled the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the frontier moved south the name came to cover the governments of Yekaterinoslav, Kherson and Taurida, which the English traveller Mary Holderness listed as New Russia in the 1820s, with Bessarabia beyond them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She found it settled by Ukrainians, Russians, Nogai Tatars, Greeks, Germans, Armenians and Bulgarians, on soil she judged three times as fertile as that of England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its great organiser was Grigory Potemkin, to whom Catherine entrusted the New Russia and Azov governorates in January 1774 and who ruled the south with almost unlimited powers until his death in 1791.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settling colonists, planning towns and raising fortresses were his principal tasks, and state peasants were moved south in large numbers to farm the land and to work the roads and river crossings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yekaterinoslav, planned from 1784, was to have a university, a conservatoire, a cathedral larger than St Peter’s in Rome and streets 61 metres (200 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of this stayed on paper, for the palace turned out to be a set of hothouses and the silk factory closed after two years, yet the region was conquered and began to fill with people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian historian of the next century counted the opening of the southern steppes to Russian labour among the real gains of Catherine’s reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Н. В. Бессарабова, «Организация управления Новороссией в 1764–1802 гг.», <i>Вестник Московского финансово-юридического университета</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsiya-upravleniya-novorossiey-v-1764-1802-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mary Holderness, <i>New Russia: Journey from Riga to the Crimea, by Way of Kiev</i> (London: Sherwood, Jones, 1823), 106–8, https://archive.org/details/newrussiajourney00holdrich. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 279–81, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","Е. В. Белова, «Гранычары Новой Сербии: южнославянская колонизация России в 1740–1760-е гг.», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/granychary-novoy-serbii-yuzhnoslavyanskaya-kolonizatsiya-rossii-v-1740-1760-e-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. И. Елисеева, «Правительственная политика по переселению в Крым и Новороссию во второй половине XVIII века», <i>Историческое обозрение</i>, no. 7 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravitelstvennaya-politika-po-pereseleniyu-v-krym-i-novorossiyu-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Л. Перечицкая, «Заселение юга Российской империи в последней четверти XVIII в.», <i>Известия Волгоградского государственного педагогического университета</i> 63, no. 9 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zaselenie-yuga-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-posledney-chetverti-xviii-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 256, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","W. Lyon Blease, <i>Suvorof</i> (London: Constable, 1920), 84, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028460792. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the empire need colonists in the south?","a":"Because the steppe it had taken was thinly peopled and undeveloped, and an empty border facing the Ottomans and the Crimean Tatars was hard to hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlers, from Serbian frontier soldiers to Russian state peasants and foreign colonists, were meant to guard, farm and supply it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Potemkin have such power there?","a":"Because Catherine’s governors-general on the frontiers were given extraordinary powers and answered only to her, and Potemkin, her most trusted servant, also commanded the troops of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Distance from the capital and the threat of war made such a ruler seem necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many of the new towns disappoint?","a":"Because they were planned on a vast scale with little thought for materials, money or settlers, and the grand designs for Yekaterinoslav shrank to hothouses and a short-lived silk factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Money assigned to the region often failed to arrive in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Novorossiya"},
    "ru-355": {"questions":["A Saxon diplomat who never made the journey spread the legend of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a Hamburg journal in 1798, claiming its houses and steeples were painted on boards.","Writing home from Tula in 1787, an Austrian prince already mocked as ridiculous the rumour behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while admitting the empress saw only what she was shown."],"abstract":"A <b>Potemkin village</b> is a showpiece built to hide a poor or unfinished reality, a phrase taken from the story that Grigory Potemkin set up painted, sham villages along Catherine II’s route to the Crimea in 1787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The journey ran from January to July 1787 and was staged as a pageant, with galleys on the Dnieper and triumphal arches in the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Catherine left St Petersburg in January with 14 carriages and 120 sledges, finding 560 fresh horses at each station and bonfires lighting the road at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> She sailed down the Dnieper from Kyiv, met the king of Poland and Joseph II on the way, and on 12 May by the Julian calendar entered Kherson, a town that had not existed eight years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The tale of houses and church towers painted on boards was written down at length by the Saxon diplomat Georg von Helbig, who had not made the journey, in a Hamburg journal in 1798.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rumours were older than Helbig, for the Prince de Ligne, writing from Tula on the way home in 1787, already called the cardboard villages a ridiculous story spread by Russians vexed at being left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet Ligne admitted that the empress, who could not go about on foot, was shown finished shops and colonnades, while some towns had streets without houses and houses without roofs, doors or windows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A French life of Catherine first published in 1797 claimed that the bare Crimean plains were disguised by villages built on purpose, and the charge became a stock part of Western writing about Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many historians now hold that Potemkin decorated real towns and settlements with arches, flowers and illuminations but never hid that they were decorations, and that the colonies, docks and fleet the visitors saw were real.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The phrase itself took longer to form, appearing as a general metaphor in French and German print in the 1840s and becoming common in Russian only around 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["К. Душенко, «„Потемкинские деревни“ и „фасадная империя“», <i>Вестник культурологии</i>, no. 2 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/potemkinskie-derevni-i-fasadnaya-imperiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Charles-Joseph, Prince de Ligne, <i>The Prince de Ligne: His Memoirs, Letters, and Miscellaneous Papers</i>, trans. Katharine Prescott Wormeley, vol. 2 (Boston: Hardy, Pratt, 1902), 38–39, https://archive.org/details/princedelignehis02lignuoft. [Open access]","Jean Henri Castéra, <i>The Life of Catharine II, Empress of Russia</i>, 4th ed., vol. 3 (London, 1800), 331, https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_vie-de-catherine-ii-en_castera-jean-henri_1800_3. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (London: Heinemann, 1894), 278–79, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00waliuoft. [Open access]","В. В. Орехов, «„Русский миф“ и „комплекс маркиза де Кюстина“. Часть I: Крым и „потемкинские деревни“», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Филологические науки</i> 8, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkiy-mif-i-kompleks-markiza-de-kyustina-chast-i-krym-i-potemkinskie-derevni. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Махотина, «Путешествия Екатерины II: идейно-художественная программа церемониала встречи императрицы», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 6 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/puteshestviya-ekateriny-ii-ideyno-hudozhestvennaya-programma-tseremoniala-vstrechi-imperatritsy. [in Russian] [Open access]","W. Lyon Blease, <i>Suvorof</i> (London: Constable, 1920), 84, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028460792. [Open access]","В. Л. Руев, «Хроника пребывания императрицы Екатерины II в Херсоне в мае 1787 г.», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i> 9, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronika-prebyvaniya-imperatritsy-ekateriny-ii-v-hersone-v-mae-1787-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did foreign visitors suspect a trick?","a":"Because they saw towns, fleets and settlements appear in what they had imagined was empty steppe, staged with arches, illuminations and crowds along the route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ligne also noticed that Catherine, who travelled by carriage and galley, saw only what was put in front of her, so even honest displays could mislead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Was the story of painted villages true?","a":"Probably not as told. Its fullest version came from Helbig, who had not been on the journey, and Ligne called the cardboard villages a ridiculous story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Potemkin did decorate real places heavily, and some new towns were far less finished than they looked, which is why the legend found believers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the phrase outlive the journey?","a":"Because Western writers kept using the story to describe a Russia of façades, from a life of Catherine in 1797 to travellers of the 1830s and 1840s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the 1840s the phrase served in French and German for any showy sham, and Russian writers took it up only in the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Potemkin village"},
    "ru-356": {"questions":["In May 1787 the fleet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, revealed to Catherine and Joseph II near Inkerman, so moved the empress that she rose at dinner to drink to her best friend.","A Russian admiral wrote that no harbour in all Europe matched the bay where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was founded, and that it could hold as many as a hundred ships of the line."],"abstract":"<b>Sevastopol</b> is a port city in the south-west of the Crimea, founded by the Russian Empire in 1783 as the base of its new Black Sea Fleet on the great bay then known as the Akhtiar harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The bay took that name from a small Tatar village on its northern shore, which had only nine clay huts when a Russian survey party mapped the anchorage in 1773.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its value was known before Russia held it, and an English traveller saw an Ottoman fleet anchored there in the winter of 1777, when the Porte sent a rival khan to the Crimea with five ships of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vice-Admiral Klokachev wrote that there was no harbour like it in all Europe and that it could hold a hundred ships of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After Catherine’s manifesto of April 1783 annexed the khanate, Russian troops and the ships of Admiral Thomas Mackenzie began building a military settlement and fortifications on the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> A decree of 10 February 1784 by the Julian calendar ordered a great fortress, an admiralty, a dockyard for ships of the first rank and a military port, and Akhtiar was renamed Sevastopol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A manifesto of February 1784 declared Sevastopol and Feodosia free ports, but the harbour was soon reserved for the navy, and merchant ships were barred from it except in distress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In May 1787 Catherine and Joseph II, approaching by way of Inkerman, saw the Sevastopol fleet revealed below them, and the empress rose at dinner to drink to the health of her best friend, the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A traveller of 1794 found a town laid out in parallel streets on the slope above the southern bay, with a palace built in 1787 for the empress, an admiralty, an arsenal and a new Greek church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Russian historian of the next century wrote that after the annexation a Black Sea fleet appeared and Sevastopol became the base of a Russian protectorate over the Christians of the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 257, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Charles-Joseph, Prince de Ligne, <i>The Prince de Ligne: His Memoirs, Letters, and Miscellaneous Papers</i>, trans. Katharine Prescott Wormeley, vol. 2 (Boston: Hardy, Pratt, 1902), 48–49, https://archive.org/details/princedelignehis02lignuoft. [Open access]","W. Eton, <i>A Survey of the Turkish Empire</i>, 3rd ed. (London: T. Cadell jun. and W. Davies, 1801), 312–14, https://archive.org/details/surveyofturkishe00eton. [Open access]","P. S. Pallas, <i>Travels through the Southern Provinces of the Russian Empire, in the Years 1793 and 1794</i>, vol. 2 (London: T. N. Longman and O. Rees, 1802), 45–47, https://archive.org/details/travelsthroughthesouthernprovincesofther81. [Open access]","Е. П. Кудрявцева, «Строительство Черноморского флота и портов в Российской империи после заключения Кючук-Кайнарджийского мира», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stroitelstvo-chernomorskogo-flota-i-portov-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-posle-zaklyucheniya-kyuchuk-kaynardzhiyskogo-mira. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Белов, «Зарождение города Севастополя: у истоков главной военно-морской крепости России на Черном море», <i>Славянский мир в третьем тысячелетии</i>, no. 11 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zarozhdenie-goroda-sevastopolya-u-istokov-glavnoy-voenno-morskoy-kreposti-rossii-na-chernom-more. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Г. Баранов, Е. П. Гармашова, Т. А. Лопатина and Д. В. Пунга, «Хозяйственная жизнь города Севастополя на рубеже XVIII–XIX веков», <i>Экономическая политика</i> 19, no. 5 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hozyaystvennaya-zhizn-goroda-sevastopolya-na-rubezhe-xviii-xix-vekov. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Никифоров, «О проблемах интеграции Крыма в состав Российской империи в правление Екатерины II», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-problemah-integratsii-kryma-v-sostav-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-pravlenie-ekateriny-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Akhtiar bay chosen for the fleet?","a":"Because it was deep, sheltered and large: officers who saw it said no harbour in Europe was like it and that it could hold a hundred ships of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A traveller of 1794 recorded depths of nine to eleven fathoms and no rocks anywhere in the harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were merchant ships kept out of the harbour?","a":"Because the government decided that the bay should be the main base of the war fleet, so trading vessels were barred except in distress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Trade was steered to other ports, although a manifesto of 1784 had first named Sevastopol and Feodosia as free ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine visit Sevastopol in 1787?","a":"Because her journey to the Crimea was meant to show off the new southern lands to her and her foreign guests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sight of the fleet, staged as the climax of the visit, delighted her, and the Prince de Ligne thought it raised hopes of a war with Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sevastopol","places":["city:1159150561"]},
    "ru-357": {"questions":["The storm of Izmail, the sea battles of Tendra and Kaliakra and the death of Potemkin during the peace talks all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fought against the Ottomans.","When the Russian envoy refused to give back the Crimea and was shut in the Seven Towers, the sultan opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Austria soon joining Russia."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1787–1792</b> was the second war of Catherine II’s reign against the Ottoman Empire, which opened it in the summer of 1787 in the hope of recovering the Crimea, annexed by Russia four years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In July 1787 the Porte demanded the return of the Crimea and the Black Sea fortresses, and when the Russian envoy Yakov Bulgakov refused, it shut him in the fortress of the Seven Towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian accounts blamed British and Prussian diplomats for egging the sultan on, and the war soon drew in Austria as Russia’s ally while Sweden threatened Russia in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suvorov beat off a Turkish landing at Kinburn in October 1787, and in 1788 Potemkin besieged Ochakov, the fortress that commanded the mouths of the Dnieper and the Bug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1789 Russian and Austrian armies routed the Turks at Focșani and on the Rymnik, and in 1790 and 1791 the Black Sea Fleet under Fyodor Ushakov won at Kerch, Tendra and Kaliakra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The bloodiest single action was the storm of Izmail on the Danube on 11 December 1790 by the Julian calendar, which Suvorov’s columns carried in a single morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its toll is still argued over, for a British biographer thought at least 25,000 of the garrison died, while a recent study of Potemkin’s draft notes puts the Turkish dead at no more than 15,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain and Prussia pressed Russia to return its conquests as Austria agreed to do, but Catherine held firm on Ochakov and the land between the Bug and the Dniester.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Preliminaries were agreed at Galați in August 1791, but Potemkin died in October, and Bezborodko signed the Treaty of Jassy on 29 December 1791 by the Julian calendar, 9 January 1792 by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty confirmed the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca and Russia’s hold on the Crimea and gave it Ochakov with the coast to the Dniester, gains that a Russian historian of the next century thought meagre for so costly a war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 20, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","W. Lyon Blease, <i>Suvorof</i> (London: Constable, 1920), https://archive.org/details/cu31924028460792. [Open access]","М. М. Якушев, «„Трактат вечного мира и дружбы“: эволюция российско-османских отношений конца XVIII века», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 12 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/traktat-vechnogo-mira-i-druzhby-evolyutsiya-rossiysko-osmanskih-otnosheniy-kontsa-xviii-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Robert Howard Lord, <i>The Second Partition of Poland: A Study in Diplomatic History</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1915), 153–54, https://archive.org/details/cu31924005692011. [Open access]","В. Д. Овчинников, «„Наши, благодаря Бога, такого перца туркам задали, что любо“: 220 лет славным победам контр-адмирала Ф. Ф. Ушакова на Чёрном море», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 7 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nashi-blagodarya-boga-takogo-pertsa-turkam-zadali-chto-lyubo-220-let-slavnym-pobedam-kontr-admirala-f-f-ushakova-na-chyornom-more. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Осиповский, «Штурм Измаила в 1790 году А. В. Суворовым: противоречия в оценках количества гарнизона и понесенных турками потерь», <i>Манускрипт</i> 19, no. 2 (2026), https://doi.org/10.30853/mns20260095. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Рассохо-Анохина, «Миротворческая миссия адмирала Ф. Ф. Ушакова», <i>Наука. Общество. Оборона</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mirotvorcheskaya-missiya-admirala-f-f-ushakova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Ottoman Empire go to war in 1787?","a":"Because it had never accepted the loss of the Crimea, and in July 1787 it demanded the peninsula and the Black Sea fortresses back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Catherine’s triumphal journey to the Crimea that year was taken as a fresh provocation, and Britain and Prussia encouraged the Porte to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine insist on keeping Ochakov?","a":"Because the fortress commanded the mouths of the Dnieper and the Bug, and in hostile hands it threatened Russia’s new possessions in the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The almost empty land beyond it as far as the Dniester also gave Russia a broader front on the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the losses at Izmail disputed?","a":"Because the usual figures go back to the victory reports of Suvorov and Potemkin, which were rarely checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A British biographer accepted that about 25,000 defenders died, while a study of Potemkin’s draft notes argues the Turkish dead did not exceed 15,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The official Russian loss of 1,815 killed was probably too low as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1787–1792)"},
    "ru-358": {"questions":["Catherine sent a jewelled field marshal’s baton to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the storm of Praga in 1794, which crushed the Polish rising and left a lasting stain on his name.","English cartoonists of the late 18th century drew <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in wildly mythologised images, but in 1799 he beat the French on the Trebbia and at Novi and was made Prince of Italy."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander Suvorov</b> was a Russian general, born in Moscow in 1730, who became the most celebrated Russian commander of his century, winning his fame in Catherine II’s wars against the Turks and the Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A short, thin and sickly boy whom his father meant for a civil post, he chose the army instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the first Turkish war he won at Kozludzha in 1774, and in 1783 he received the oath of loyalty of the Nogai hordes on the Kuban after the annexation of the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the second he defended Kinburn in 1787, shared with the Austrians the victories of Focșani and the Rymnik in 1789, for which he was made a count with the title Rymniksky, and stormed Izmail in December 1790, where estimates of the Turkish dead run from 15,000 to 25,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His biographers long claimed that a quarrel with Potemkin cost him a fair reward for Izmail, a story that a recent study finds only from the 1830s and 1840s onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1794 he marched against the Polish rising led by Kościuszko, a campaign later painted both as a punitive expedition and as a daring raid with few men, versions a recent Russian study rejects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He stormed Praga, the suburb of Warsaw across the Vistula, where about 10,000 Polish soldiers were killed and many townspeople died in the plunder, and Catherine sent him a jewelled field marshal’s baton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1799 he commanded the allied armies against the French in Italy, where he won on the Trebbia and at Novi and was made Prince of Italy, before leading his army over the St Gotthard Pass into Switzerland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His fame abroad was double-edged, for English cartoonists drew him in mythologised images that said more about British propaganda than about the man, and Praga long gave him a name as a butcher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Broken in health and out of the emperor’s favour, he returned to St Petersburg in the spring of 1800 and died there in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["W. Lyon Blease, <i>Suvorof</i> (London: Constable, 1920), https://archive.org/details/cu31924028460792. [Open access]","Frederic Anthing, <i>History of the Campaigns of Prince Alexander Suworow Rymnikski</i>, vol. 1 (New York: William Cobbett, 1800), https://archive.org/details/historyofcampaig01anthuoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Last King of Poland and His Contemporaries</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1909), 290–91, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028367971. [Open access]","С. Н. Осиповский, «Штурм Измаила в 1790 году А. В. Суворовым: противоречия в оценках количества гарнизона и понесенных турками потерь», <i>Манускрипт</i> 19, no. 2 (2026), https://doi.org/10.30853/mns20260095. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Коротун, «Карьера А. В. Суворова после штурма Измаила: развитие сюжета в биографиях полководца», <i>Культурный код</i>, no. 3 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kariera-a-v-suvorova-posle-shturma-izmaila-razvitie-syuzheta-v-biografiyah-polkovodtsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Богданов, «Польская кампания А. В. Суворова 1794 г.: правда и мифы», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2023): 188–214, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polskaya-kampaniya-a-v-suvorova-1794-g-pravda-i-mify. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Филимонова, «А. В. Суворов в английской карикатуре», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/a-v-suvorov-v-angliyskoy-karikature. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Was Suvorov really denied a reward for Izmail?","a":"Many of his biographers said so, blaming a quarrel with Potemkin and a period of disgrace that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A recent study finds that the story is missing from the earliest biographies, took shape only in the 1830s and 1840s and has been questioned since the late 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Praga damage his reputation?","a":"Because the storming of the Warsaw suburb ended in plunder and killing, and his own report said nothing of the horrors or of any rebuke to the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became the symbol of the crushing of the Polish rising, which is why he was long seen as a butcher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the storm of Izmail become so famous?","a":"Because the fortress was thought nearly impregnable and was taken by assault in a single morning, with most of its garrison killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale of the Turkish losses is still argued, from about 25,000 dead in older accounts to no more than 15,000 in a recent study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander Suvorov"},
    "ru-359": {"questions":["Calling the islet of Vido the key to Corfu, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the fortress from the French in 1799 at the head of a joint Russian and Ottoman squadron.","Buried at the Sanaksar Monastery in 1817, the admiral <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was glorified by the Russian Orthodox Church in 2001 as the righteous warrior Theodore."],"abstract":"<b>Fyodor Ushakov</b> was a Russian admiral, born in 1745, who commanded the Black Sea Fleet in the last years of Catherine II’s second Turkish war and led a Russian squadron in the Mediterranean against the French in 1798–1800.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rear-admiral of the Sevastopol fleet, he was appointed in March 1790 to command the whole Black Sea Fleet in place of Voinovich, who, Potemkin told Catherine, was quick to run away rather than fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Potemkin called Ushakov excellently informed, enterprising and keen, and the fleet won at Kerch and Tendra in 1790 and at Kaliakra in 1791, after an earlier success off Fidonisi in 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The victory at Tendra is marked in Russia today as a Day of Military Glory under a law of 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Foreign judgements were cooler, and Nelson, who met the Russian squadron in the Mediterranean, thought Ushakov inefficient and suspected that the Russians wanted Malta for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 1798, with Russia and the Ottoman Empire allied against France, a joint Russo-Turkish squadron under Ushakov passed the Dardanelles to drive the French from the Ionian Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fortress of Corfu was the hardest prize, and Ushakov judged the small island of Vido to be its key, attacking it on 18 February 1799 by the Julian calendar before Corfu surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With Count Orio and the senators of Corfu he drew up a provisional plan of government for the islands, the first step towards the Septinsular Republic, planned as a self-governing state under the protection of the Porte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He later retired to his village of Alekseevka in Tambov province near the Sanaksar Monastery, where he died in October 1817 and was buried beside the monastery cathedral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Russian Orthodox Church glorified him as a saint, the righteous warrior Theodore, in August 2001, the first naval commander to be numbered among its saints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Fred T. Jane, <i>The Imperial Russian Navy: Its Past, Present, and Future</i> (London: W. Thacker, 1899), 112–13, 710, https://archive.org/details/cu31924079598227. [Open access]","В. Д. Овчинников, «„Наши, благодаря Бога, такого перца туркам задали, что любо“: 220 лет славным победам контр-адмирала Ф. Ф. Ушакова на Чёрном море», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 7 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nashi-blagodarya-boga-takogo-pertsa-turkam-zadali-chto-lyubo-220-let-slavnym-pobedam-kontr-admirala-f-f-ushakova-na-chyornom-more. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Рассохо-Анохина, «Миротворческая миссия адмирала Ф. Ф. Ушакова», <i>Наука. Общество. Оборона</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mirotvorcheskaya-missiya-admirala-f-f-ushakova. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Рассохо-Анохина, М. В. Карнаухов and Ю. К. Чупраков, «Объективность и полнота исследования деятельности адмирала Ф. Ф. Ушакова», <i>Наука. Общество. Оборона</i>, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obektivnost-i-polnota-issledovaniya-deyatelnosti-admirala-f-f-ushakova. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Е. Петрунина and С. Г. Клисунов, «Эволюция государственного устройства Республики Семи Соединенных Островов в 1799–1803 гг.», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление: государство и общество</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-gosudarstvennogo-ustroystva-respubliki-semi-soedinennyh-ostrovov-v-1799-1803-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Звягин, «Адмирал Ушаков: медико-криминалистическое исследование», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 23. Антропология</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/admiral-ushakov-mediko-kriminalisticheskoe-issledovanie-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Potemkin replace Voinovich with Ushakov?","a":"Because Potemkin wanted a commander who would seek out and destroy the Turkish fleet, and he judged Voinovich quick to retreat rather than fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He described Ushakov as excellently informed, enterprising and keen, and named him to the command in March 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russians and Ottomans fight side by side in 1798?","a":"Because French expansion into the Mediterranean, including the Ionian Islands, threatened both empires, and the Porte’s attempts to win Bonaparte over had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A joint squadron under Ushakov then passed the Dardanelles and took the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Ushakov honoured as a saint?","a":"Because in retirement he lived near the Sanaksar Monastery and attended its services as devoutly as the monks, and in August 2001 the Russian Orthodox Church glorified him as the righteous warrior Theodore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was the first naval commander to be numbered among the saints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fyodor Ushakov"},
    "ru-360": {"questions":["Catherine claimed she had never signed a document with more satisfaction than the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which gave Russia the lands of the Dvina and the Dnieper in 1772.","A king compelled to abdicate in November 1795 saw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> complete, as the last remnant of his state was shared out after Kościuszko’s rising had failed."],"abstract":"The <b>Partitions of Poland</b> were the three seizures of territory, in 1772, 1793 and 1795, by which Russia, Prussia and Austria divided the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth among themselves until it vanished from the map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before them the Commonwealth, at about 730,000 square kilometres (282,000 square miles), was the third largest state in Europe, with more than eleven million people, of whom Poles made up perhaps half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia had long dominated its weak neighbour, and in 1764 Catherine II secured the election as king of Stanisław August Poniatowski, her former lover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russia’s demand that the Orthodox and Protestant dissidents be given equal rights with the Catholics became one of its justifications for interfering, and later for taking lands with Orthodox populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first partition was proposed from Berlin, and by treaties signed at St Petersburg on 25 July 1772 by the Julian calendar Austria took Galicia, Prussia took Polish Prussia and Russia took the lands of the Dvina and the Dnieper with about 1.6 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Catherine said she had never signed a document with so much satisfaction, while Maria Theresa wrote of the unhappy partition that had brought Austria a great advantage, if such it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poland’s attempt at reform, crowned by the Constitution of the Third of May, was answered by Russian intervention and a second partition, agreed by Russia and Prussia in January 1793 on the pretext that revolution was spreading from France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rising led by Tadeusz Kościuszko in 1794 was crushed by Suvorov, the third partition shared out what was left, and King Stanisław August was compelled to abdicate in November 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russia took the eastern lands it called Western Rus, with Lithuania and Courland, while Austria kept Galicia and Prussia pushed its frontier to the Niemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The partitions were condemned from the start, in American newspapers of the 1770s as in later Russian debate, where writers argued for a century over who was to blame for the quarrel with Poland they had created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 20–22, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Albert Sorel, <i>The Eastern Question in the Eighteenth Century: The Partition of Poland and the Treaty of Kainardji</i>, trans. F. C. Bramwell (London: Methuen, 1898), 218–20, https://archive.org/details/easternquestioni00soreuoft. [Open access]","Robert Howard Lord, <i>The Second Partition of Poland: A Study in Diplomatic History</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1915), https://archive.org/details/cu31924005692011. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>The Last King of Poland and His Contemporaries</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1909), 290–91, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028367971. [Open access]","Л. В. Ландина, «Екатерина Великая и Станислав Август Понятовский: личностный фактор в предыстории разделов Речи Посполитой», <i>Ученые записки Орловского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ekaterina-velikaya-i-stanislav-avgust-ponyatovskiy-lichnostnyy-faktor-v-predystorii-razdelov-rechi-pospolitoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. С. Лукашова and Н. В. Лукашов, «Религиозный фактор как правовое обоснование разделов Речи Посполитой», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 10 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/religioznyy-faktor-kak-pravovoe-obosnovanie-razdelov-rechi-pospolitoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Филимонова, «„Эта кровавая героиня“: Екатерина II и первый раздел Польши в восприятии американских газет 1770-х годов», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/eta-krovavaya-geroinya-ekaterina-ii-i-pervyy-razdel-polshi-v-vospriyatii-amerikanskih-gazet-1770-h-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. Ю. Гусман, «Разделы Польши, 1772–1795 гг., и общественное мнение пореформенной России», <i>Известия Лаборатории древних технологий</i> 21, no. 2 (2025): 157–65, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razdely-polshi-1772-1795-gg-i-obschestvennoe-mnenie-poreformennoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the neighbouring powers find Poland so easy to divide?","a":"Because the Commonwealth, though large, was weak and divided: Poles were perhaps only half its people, and it was split between Catholics, Orthodox and Protestants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia had long controlled its politics, from the election of its king to the question of the dissidents’ rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the second partition judged worse than the first?","a":"Because it crushed a genuine and promising national movement of reform rather than a state in disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its treaty even claimed that Poland was infected by the revolutionary spirit of France, a pretext with no real basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the partitions serve Russia’s interests?","a":"A Russian historian of the next century doubted it, arguing that Russia should have recovered the eastern lands of Western Rus without wiping out Poland, whose disappearance removed a buffer between the three powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later Russian writers kept arguing over who was to blame for the lasting quarrel with the Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Partitions of Poland"},
    "ru-361": {"questions":["Frederick II of Prussia pressed hardest for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, seeking land to balance Russian gains against the Ottomans, while Austria had already occupied the Zips district.","A Polish Sejm meeting in 1773, while foreign troops held Warsaw, was made to ratify the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Russia organised its new lands as the governorates of Pskov and Mogilev."],"abstract":"The <b>First Partition of Poland</b> was the seizure in 1772 of a large part of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth by its three neighbours, Russia, Prussia and Austria, a loss counted at between a quarter and nearly a third of its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The project was pressed first by Frederick II of Prussia, who wanted land to balance any gains Russia made in her war with the Ottoman Empire, and Austria had already occupied the Zips district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Catherine II's minister Nikita Panin at first resisted, preferring a Poland that was whole but dependent on Russia, until his rivals at court won the argument with the ease and profit of a partition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The treaty was signed at St Petersburg on 5 August 1772, and in 1773 a Polish Sejm, meeting while foreign troops held Warsaw and bribes circulated, was made to consent to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia took Polish Livonia and the palatinates of Polotsk, Vitebsk and Mstislavl as far as the Western Dvina and the Dnieper, Austria took Galicia, and Prussia took Royal Prussia apart from Danzig and Thorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The three courts justified the seizure in a joint manifesto of September 1772 that blamed the anarchy in Poland, while King Stanisław August protested that it was unjust and violent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia organised its share as two new governorates, Pskov and Mogilev, and Catherine told their governors to win the hearts of the inhabitants by mild and orderly rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The annexation also brought Russia, which had long barred Jews from settling, a large Jewish population, put by one contemporary count at over forty thousand families in Belarus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Who bore most of the blame is still argued, since Russian historians stress that Frederick began it, while Polish historians see it as the fulfilment of Catherine's own programme towards a weak neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Imperial Russian writers later presented Russia's share as East Slavic land restored, while a Soviet history of Poland held tsarism fully responsible for beginning the dismemberment of the Polish state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 391–97, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Lord Eversley, <i>The Partitions of Poland</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1915), 54–61, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028370306. [Open access]","О. В. Бабенко, «Стегний П. В. Разделы Польши и дипломатия Екатерины II: 1772, 1793, 1795. М.: Международные отношения, 2002. 696 с. [реферат]», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 3 (2003), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2003-03-016-stegniyp-v-razdely-polshi-i-diplomatiya-ekateriny-ii-1772-1793-1795-m-mezhdunar-otnosheniya-2002-696-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Филимонова, ««Эта кровавая героиня»: Екатерина II и первый раздел Польши в восприятии американских газет 1770-х годов», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (77) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/eta-krovavaya-geroinya-ekaterina-ii-i-pervyy-razdel-polshi-v-vospriyatii-amerikanskih-gazet-1770-h-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Завьялов, «Государственная и таможенная граница России во время первого раздела Польши в законодательных актах Полного собрания законов Российской империи», <i>Ученые записки Санкт-Петербургского имени В. Б. Бобкова филиала Российской таможенной академии</i>, no. 2 (42) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvennaya-i-tamozhennaya-granitsa-rossii-vo-vremya-pervogo-razdela-polshi-v-zakonodatelnyh-aktah-polnogo-sobraniya-zakonov. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1916), 306–7, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr01dubnuoft. [Open access]","Ю. А. Борисёнок, «Формирование территории Российской империи на польском направлении, особенности и закономерности: дискуссионные вопросы в современной историографии», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-territorii-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-polskom-napravlenii-osobennosti-i-zakonomernosti-diskussionnye-voprosy-v-sovremennoy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Panin hesitate before agreeing to a partition?","a":"Because he wanted Poland kept whole and dependent on Russia within his planned Northern system, and he told the Prussian envoy that openly breaking the guarantees Catherine had so often given to Poland's territory would make a very bad impression everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the three powers publish manifestos?","a":"Because they felt they had to defend an extraordinary act before the opinion of Europe, so each set out old claims to the land it took, and together they blamed the disorder of the Polish state for forcing them to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the partition raise a new question for Russia?","a":"Because the province taken from Poland held many Jews, and Russia, which had driven Jews beyond its borders before, had promised the annexed inhabitants that their religion and property would be respected, so it now had to decide how to govern them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"First Partition of Poland"},
    "ru-362": {"questions":["Defeat and capture at Maciejowice in October 1794 and the storming of Praga weeks later ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, opening the way to the final partition of Poland.","A regiment of Jewish light cavalry raised in besieged Warsaw by Berek Joselewicz fought in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose leader had become a national hero at Dubienka in 1792."],"abstract":"The <b>Kościuszko Uprising</b> was the armed rising of 1794 against Russian domination of what was left of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth after the Second Partition of 1793, led by Tadeusz Kościuszko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was set off when Catherine II ordered the Polish army cut down to a fraction of its size, and a cavalry brigade under General Madaliński refused to disband and rode out in revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kościuszko, already a national hero for holding the crossing of the Bug at Dubienka against a far larger Russian force in 1792, issued his manifestos in Kraków on 24 March 1794 and took supreme command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 4 April at Racławice his small army, about half of it peasants armed with scythes, captured the Russian guns in a charge, and later that month Warsaw and Vilnius rose and drove out their Russian garrisons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prussia then joined Russia against the rising, but its siege of Warsaw that summer failed, and the call to arms was answered by townspeople, minor nobles and peasants rather than by the great magnates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rising's breadth showed in Warsaw, where in September 1794 Kościuszko approved a regiment of Jewish light cavalry raised by Berek Joselewicz, whose appeal to his fellow Jews was printed in Polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Racławice Kościuszko put on the peasants' long coat and ennobled the peasant Bartosz, the first man to reach the Russian battery, under the name Głowacki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 10 October 1794 he was routed, wounded and captured at Maciejowice, and early in November Suvorov stormed the Warsaw suburb of Praga, where older accounts put the civilians killed at between 13,000 and 20,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kościuszko was held in the Peter and Paul Fortress in St Petersburg until Paul I freed him in 1796 on condition of an oath of allegiance, after which he sailed for America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its defeat opened the way to the Third Partition, and Soviet textbooks of 1947 to 1956 retold the rising to suit a new official view of the Polish–Russian past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Monica M. Gardner, <i>Kosciuszko: A Biography</i> (London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1920), 104–11, 155–71, https://archive.org/details/kosciuszkobiogra00garduoft. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 404–8, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Lord Eversley, <i>The Partitions of Poland</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1915), 174–78, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028370306. [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1916), 292–95, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr01dubnuoft. [Open access]","Robert Howard Lord, <i>The Second Partition of Poland: A Study in Diplomatic History</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1915), 290–91, https://archive.org/details/secondpartitiono00lord. [Open access]","А. А. Исэров, «Густав Адольф Бергуд, Тадеуш Костюшко, Франсиско де Миранда: из истории революционных движений рубежа XVIII–XIX столетий», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8: История</i>, no. 6 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gustav-adolf-bergud-tadeush-kostyushko-fransisko-de-miranda-iz-istorii-revolyutsionnyh-dvizheniy-rubezha-xviii-xix-stoletiy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Панов, «Репрезентация истории польских восстаний в советских учебниках 1947–1956 гг.», <i>Гуманитарные и юридические исследования</i>, 12, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reprezentatsiya-istorii-polskih-vosstaniy-v-sovetskih-uchebnikah-1947-1956-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rising begin in March 1794?","a":"Because Catherine ordered the Polish army cut to a fraction of its size after the Second Partition, and a cavalry brigade under General Madaliński refused to disband; Kościuszko, who would have preferred to wait for better times, would not leave his countrymen in the lurch and took command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the great nobles hold back?","a":"Because they feared losing their estates if the rising failed, and many feared that its revolutionary ideas would spread among their serfs, so the small gentry, the townspeople and the peasants carried it instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Kościuszko set free in 1796?","a":"Because Catherine's death brought her son Paul I to the throne, and he came to the prison in person to release him; the pardon required an oath of allegiance, which Kościuszko took because the release of some twelve thousand Polish prisoners depended on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kościuszko Uprising"},
    "ru-363": {"questions":["King Stanisław August signed his abdication at Grodno on 25 November 1795, soon after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had divided all the lands still left to his state among three neighbours.","By a secret article of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Russia, Austria and Prussia undertook never to include the name of a Kingdom of Poland among their own titles."],"abstract":"The <b>Third Partition of Poland</b> was the division in 1795 of everything that remained of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth among Russia, Austria and Prussia, after which the Commonwealth ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed the crushing of the Kościuszko Uprising, and Russia and Austria settled the terms first, in a treaty of 3 January 1795 whose preamble declared Poland entirely conquered by the Empress's arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prussia, which wanted Kraków and had hoped for more, held out for nine months and came close to war before it acceded to the agreement on 24 October 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia took the remaining eastern lands, among them Lithuania with Vilnius and Grodno, Austria took Kraków with the palatinates of Sandomierz and Lublin, and Prussia took Warsaw and the lands between the Bug and the Niemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> King Stanisław August, brought to Grodno under Russian supervision at the start of 1795, signed his abdication there on 25 November 1795, and the powers agreed in a secret article never to use the name of a Kingdom of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Seen from St Petersburg, the division completed the gains of 1772 and 1793, and by its end Russia had absorbed by far the largest share of the old Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian diplomat's study of the partitions argues that Catherine joined this last one chiefly to hold together the coalition against revolutionary France and to arbitrate between Austria and Prussia over Kraków and Sandomierz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The annexation of Lithuania also brought hundreds of thousands more Jews under Russian rule and completed the territory to which Russian law later confined Jewish settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Imperial Russian writers framed Russia's share as East Slavic land restored, a Soviet history stressed that Russia took no ethnically Polish land in 1795, and historians still debate whether the three partitions should be studied as one process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kościuszko, then a prisoner in St Petersburg, later angrily denied a Prussian story that he had cried 'Finis Poloniae', meaning 'the end of Poland', as he fell at Maciejowice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Lord Eversley, <i>The Partitions of Poland</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1915), 237–46, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028370306. [Open access]","R. Nisbet Bain, <i>Slavonic Europe: A Political History of Poland and Russia from 1447 to 1796</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1908), 408, https://archive.org/details/slavoniceuropepo00bainuoft. [Open access]","Н. И. Василюк, «Анализ политической деятельности Станислава Августа Понятовского в 1795–1797 гг. во время пребывания в Гродно», <i>А-фактор: научные исследования и разработки (гуманитарные науки)</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/analiz-politicheskoy-deyatelnosti-stanislava-avgusta-ponyatovskogo-v-1795-1797-gg-vo-vremya-prebyvaniya-v-grodno. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Бабенко, «Стегний П. В. Разделы Польши и дипломатия Екатерины II: 1772, 1793, 1795. М.: Международные отношения, 2002. 696 с. [реферат]», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 3 (2003), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2003-03-016-stegniyp-v-razdely-polshi-i-diplomatiya-ekateriny-ii-1772-1793-1795-m-mezhdunar-otnosheniya-2002-696-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1916), 314–17, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr01dubnuoft. [Open access]","Ю. А. Борисёнок, «Формирование территории Российской империи на польском направлении, особенности и закономерности: дискуссионные вопросы в современной историографии», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-territorii-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-polskom-napravlenii-osobennosti-i-zakonomernosti-diskussionnye-voprosy-v-sovremennoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Monica M. Gardner, <i>Kosciuszko: A Biography</i> (London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1920), 202–3, https://archive.org/details/kosciuszkobiogra00garduoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Prussia nearly go to war over the terms?","a":"Because it wanted Kraków and more land than Russia and Austria would allow; the two empires agreed the division without it in January 1795 and only later told Berlin, and Prussia gave way in October when war against both looked likely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Catherine take part, by one Russian account?","a":"Because, as a Russian diplomat's study of her policy argues, she hoped the division would help keep the coalition against revolutionary France together, and she acted as arbiter between Austria and Prussia, which could not agree over Kraków and Sandomierz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the partition matter in Jewish history?","a":"Because the Lithuanian provinces of Vilnius and Grodno, which went to Russia in 1795, held large Jewish communities, and adding them completed the territory in which Russian law allowed Jews to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Third Partition of Poland"},
    "ru-364": {"questions":["The failure of provincial officials to foresee or crush <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pushed Catherine to issue her new institutions for governing the provinces on 7 November 1775.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Yaik river and its Cossack host were renamed after the Ural, as the government tightened its control over every Cossack community."],"abstract":"<b>Pugachev's Rebellion</b> was the great rising of 1773 to 1775 in the Volga and Ural lands of the Russian Empire, led by the Don Cossack Yemelyan Pugachev, who claimed to be the dead emperor Peter III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began among the Yaik Cossacks, whose own revolt of 1772 had been put down with executions and an inquiry, and on its first day the band numbered only about 80 men, Cossacks, Bashkirs and Kalmyks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rebel forces besieged Orenburg from October 1773 and beat several government detachments, while Bashkir leaders such as Salavat Yulayev fought with their own forces and sent men, horses and fodder to the main army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 12 July 1774 Pugachev's army stormed and burned Kazan, though its kremlin held out, and after a defeat by Michelson he crossed to the right bank of the Volga, where Saransk and Saratov fell to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His own Cossacks, seeing no escape and hoping for pardon, seized him in September 1774, and he was executed in Moscow on 10 January 1775 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rising laid bare the weakness of the empire's provincial government, whose officials had failed to foresee it and for long could not crush it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That lesson fed directly into Catherine's provincial reform of 7 November 1775, which cut the empire into smaller provinces with far more officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Yaik river and the Yaik Cossack host were renamed after the Ural, and the Zaporozhian Host was abolished in the same year, as the government tightened its grip on every Cossack community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> One older biographer of Catherine judged that the rising took from her the last of her early enthusiasm for redressing social wrongs, and that her police regime grew harsher from 1775.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet historians called it a peasant war and made its leader an ideal hero of the people, a reading that later Russian scholarship has questioned without settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["А. С. Пушкин, «История Пугачева», full text at the Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/a.s.-pushkin-istoriya-pugacheva. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1900), 290–95, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00walirich. [Open access]","V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 47–48, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Н. М. Кулбахтин and А. Е. Балтинский, «Соотношение сил в Крестьянской войне 1773–1775 гг.», <i>Вестник Башкирского университета</i>, 19, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sootnoshenie-sil-v-krestyanskoy-voyne-1773-1775-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. М. Кулбахтин and С. Н. Кулбахтин, «Башкирские предводители Пугачевского восстания», <i>Проблемы востоковедения</i>, no. 4 (90) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bashkirskie-predvoditeli-pugachevskogo-vosstaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. П. Толочко and И. А. Коновалов, «Местное управление Российской империи по «Учреждениям для управления губерний» 1775 г.», <i>Вестник Омского университета. Серия «Исторические науки»</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mestnoe-upravlenie-rossiyskoy-imperii-po-uchrezhdeniyam-dlya-upravleniya-guberniy-1775-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Мильчев, «Оформление «казачьего сословия» при Екатерине II: действие первое (1762–1775)», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oformlenie-kazachiego-sosloviya-pri-ekaterine-ii-deystvie-pervoe-1762-1775. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Симонова, «Е. И. Пугачёв в отечественной историографии», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 444 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/e-i-pugachyov-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rising start among the Yaik Cossacks?","a":"Because their own revolt of 1772 had been crushed with executions and an inquiry, leaving a host that resented the government, and a man claiming to be Peter III gave them a cause; his first band mixed Cossacks with Bashkirs and Kalmyks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bashkirs matter so much?","a":"Because Bashkir leaders fought on their own and also fed the main army with recruits, horses and fodder, and Pugachev rewarded them with high ranks, making Salavat Yulayev a brigadier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rising change how Russia was governed?","a":"Because it showed that local officials could neither foresee nor quickly suppress a rising, and Catherine answered in 1775 with a reform of provincial government that multiplied the provinces and their officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pugachev's Rebellion"},
    "ru-365": {"questions":["Condemned by a sentence of January 1775, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was named in official papers by the scornful half-name Emelka, a form of naming abolished early in the 18th century.","Born in the Don stanitsa of Zimoveyskaya, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> confessed on a Moscow scaffold in January 1775 that he was a Don Cossack and asked the Orthodox people for forgiveness."],"abstract":"<b>Yemelyan Pugachev</b> was a Don Cossack from the stanitsa of Zimoveyskaya who, claiming to be the murdered emperor Peter III, led the largest rising of Catherine II's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began Cossack service at 17, fought in the Seven Years' War and rose to the junior officer rank of khorunzhy, but was later flogged for a small fault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then deserted, was arrested and escaped more than once, and wandered among Old Believer settlements near the Polish border and on the Volga and the Yaik.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was not the first pretender of the age, since more than 60 men in the 1760s and 1770s claimed royal identities, most of them Peter III's, and the supposed likeness between Pugachev and the dead emperor rests on no serious evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To convince the Yaik Cossacks who came to test him in August and September 1773, he showed them so-called tsar's signs, marks on the body that popular belief held to set a true tsar apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> His end came in September 1774, when Cossacks of his own following, hemmed in and hoping for pardon, seized him near the Uzen and took him to the Yaik town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A manifesto of 19 December 1774 told his story officially as that of a villain, rebel and impostor, and the court's sentence of 10 January 1775 condemned him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was beheaded and quartered on Bolotnaya Square in Moscow that same day, after declaring on the scaffold that he was the Don Cossack Yemelka Pugachev and asking the Orthodox people to forgive him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official papers stripped him of the ordinary forms of a name and called him by the scornful half-name Emelka or simply the villain, a usage that also recalled the earlier pretender Grishka Otrepyev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Soviet historians portrayed him as a people's hero devoted to the oppressed, while other writers before and since have seen an adventurer carried along by a movement he did not direct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["А. С. Пушкин, «История Пугачева», full text at the Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/a.s.-pushkin-istoriya-pugacheva. [in Russian] [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1900), 290–94, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00walirich. [Open access]","А. Е. Балтинский, «Емельян Пугачев: путь к самозванству», <i>Вестник Академии наук Республики Башкортостан</i>, 24, no. 3 (87) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/emelyan-pugachev-put-k-samozvanstvu. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Симонова, «Е. И. Пугачёв в отечественной историографии», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 444 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/e-i-pugachyov-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Я. Мауль and О. В. Лазарева, ««Царские знаки» и мифические путешествия в системе аргументов Пугачева/Петра III», <i>Гуманитарные исследования Центральной России</i>, no. 1 (30) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsarskie-znaki-i-mificheskie-puteshestviya-v-sisteme-argumentov-pugacheva-petra-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Мильчев, «Оформление «казачьего сословия» при Екатерине II: действие первое (1762–1775)», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oformlenie-kazachiego-sosloviya-pri-ekaterine-ii-deystvie-pervoe-1762-1775. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Руднев, «Имя человека в деловой коммуникации XVIII в. (особенности именования Пугачева в документах 1770-х гг.)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Язык и литература</i>, 18, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imya-cheloveka-v-delovoy-kommunikatsii-xviii-v-osobennosti-imenovaniya-pugacheva-v-dokumentah-1770-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a Don Cossack pass as Peter III?","a":"Because rumours that Peter III had escaped death spread soon after his murder, dozens of pretenders used his name, and many people believed that a true tsar bore special signs on his body, which Pugachev showed to the doubting Yaik Cossacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his own men hand him over?","a":"Because by September 1774 they were hemmed in by government troops, saw certain ruin ahead and hoped to be pardoned, so they seized him and carried him to the Yaik town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did officials call him Emelka?","a":"Because the authorities treated his crime as one of special gravity and denied him the usual official forms of a name, using the scornful half-name and the word for villain instead, a form that also recalled the pretender Grishka Otrepyev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Yemelyan Pugachev"},
    "ru-366": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its last kish otaman, Petro Kalnyshevsky, was exiled in 1776 to the Solovetsky Monastery and held there in harsh conditions until 1801.","Cossacks who fled to Ottoman territory after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1775 founded the Danubian Sich, while most of those who stayed were registered as state peasants."],"abstract":"The <b>destruction of the Zaporozhian Sich</b> was the occupation in June 1775 by a Russian army of the New Sich, the last fortified headquarters of the Zaporozhian Cossack Host on the lower Dnieper, and the abolition of the Host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The New Sich had been founded in 1734 by Cossacks returning to Russian-ruled Ukraine, on the Pidpilna River near present-day Pokrovske, and it governed a large territory divided into eight districts called palankas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly 100,000 people, Cossacks, their helpers and peasants, lived in the Sich, in more than 100 free villages and on some 4,000 winter farmsteads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its last chief, the kish otaman Petro Kalnyshevsky, encouraged farming and the grain trade, paid for churches and parish schools, and went more than once to St Petersburg to defend the Host's lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those lands were squeezed by the settlers of New Russia and by foreign military colonies such as New Serbia, and a long land dispute with the Don Host ended in the Don's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 16 June 1775 by the Gregorian calendar, a Russian army commanded by General Tekeli took and destroyed the New Sich on the orders of Catherine II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A manifesto published on 3 August 1775 announced the abolition of the Host, and the vestments and books of the Sich church passed to the state treasury of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kalnyshevsky was sent in 1776 to a monastery on the Solovetsky Islands in the White Sea, where he was held in very hard conditions until 1801, and he died there in 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some Zaporozhians fled to Ottoman territory and founded the Danubian Sich, while most stayed and were registered as state peasants, until Potemkin raised Cossack units again for the war of 1787, which became the Black Sea Host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Ukrainian reference work presents it as the end of a community whose independence Kalnyshevsky had defended, while a recent Russian study places it within a wider drive to bring every Cossack host under state control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“New Sich,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CN%5CE%5CNewSich.htm. [Open access]","“Kalnyshevsky, Petro,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CK%5CA%5CKalnyshevskyPetro.htm. [Open access]","“Danubian Sich,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CD%5CA%5CDanubianSich.htm. [Open access]","“Black Sea Cossacks,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CB%5CL%5CBlackSeaCossacks.htm. [Open access]","В. И. Мильчев, «Оформление «казачьего сословия» при Екатерине II: действие первое (1762–1775)», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oformlenie-kazachiego-sosloviya-pri-ekaterine-ii-deystvie-pervoe-1762-1775. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Коцур, «Вклад кошевого атамана Запорожской Сечи Петра Калнышевского в церковное строительство», <i>Теория и практика общественного развития</i>, no. 10 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vklad-koshevogo-atamana-zaporozhskoy-sechi-petra-kalnyshevskogo-v-tserkovnoe-stroitelstvo. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government move against the Sich in 1775?","a":"Because the Host's lands were contested by New Russian settlers and by the Don Host, the Senate had demanded proof of its rights, and that same year the government was tightening its hold on the Yaik and Don hosts too; Kalnyshevsky's missions had failed to stop the encroachment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the Sich's last leader?","a":"He was exiled in 1776 to a monastery on the Solovetsky Islands and kept a prisoner there in very hard conditions; released in 1801 after a quarter of a century, he gave the monastery a Gospel book, became a monk there and died on the islands in 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Cossack units reappear after 1775?","a":"Because the war with the Ottoman Empire from 1787 made Cossack troops useful again, so Potemkin organised former Zaporozhians into the Army of Loyal Cossacks, renamed the Black Sea Host in 1788 and resettled in the Kuban in 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Liquidation of the Zaporozhian Sich"},
    "ru-367": {"questions":["Catherine told Grimm that her rules of 7 November, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, filled 250 printed pages and were the best thing she had ever done.","Prompted by the failure of local officials during Pugachev's rising, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave elected noble officials and judges a large share in governing the countryside."],"abstract":"The <b>provincial reform of 1775</b> was Catherine II's reorganisation of local government, issued on 7 November 1775 as the Institutions for the Administration of the Provinces of the All-Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It replaced Peter I's twenty large provinces with about fifty smaller ones on a purely statistical basis, each with 300,000 to 400,000 people on the tax registers and divided into districts of 20,000 to 30,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The intermediate level of the provincia was abolished, and each province was run by a provincial board under a governor, with a namestnik or governor-general set over it, often over several provinces at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Administration, finance and justice were separated, with a treasury chamber for revenue and a ladder of courts in which nobles, townspeople and free peasants each had their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nobles elected many of the local officials and judges, so the reform gave the provincial nobility a large share in governing the countryside, and the law reshaped the courts as well as the administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The immediate spur was Pugachev's rising of 1773 to 1774, which provincial officials had failed to foresee and for long could not crush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Catherine chose the provinces rather than the central government, one older historian argued, because she could apply her theories there without limiting her autocratic power, and because the noble deputies of 1767 had asked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She told her correspondent Grimm that her rules of 7 November filled 250 printed pages and were the best thing she had ever done, beside which her Instruction was nonsense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new system took years to set up, and by 1781 her secretary could list 29 provinces already organised in the new form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its class courts sat uneasily with the equality before the law preached in her Instruction, and the double command of governor and governor-general was never clearly defined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 47–51, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","K. Waliszewski, <i>The Romance of an Empress: Catherine II of Russia</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1900), 214–15, 263, https://archive.org/details/romanceofempress00walirich. [Open access]","А. П. Толочко and И. А. Коновалов, «Местное управление Российской империи по «Учреждениям для управления губерний» 1775 г.», <i>Вестник Омского университета. Серия «Исторические науки»</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mestnoe-upravlenie-rossiyskoy-imperii-po-uchrezhdeniyam-dlya-upravleniya-guberniy-1775-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Киселев and А. С. Криворучко, «Проблема организации дворянского самоуправления и подготовка «Учреждений для управления губерний» в 1775 году», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 3 (26) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-organizatsii-dvoryanskogo-samoupravleniya-i-podgotovka-uchrezhdeniy-dlya-upravleniya-guberniy-v-1775-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","«Учреждения для управления губерний (1775) (фрагменты текста документа)», <i>Вестник Университета имени О. Е. Кутафина</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchrezhdeniya-dlya-upravleniya-guberniy-1775-fragmenty-teksta-dokumenta. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. В. Петров, «Правовые механизмы обеспечения эффективности работы судебных органов Российской империи в законе от 7 ноября 1775 г. «Учреждения для управления губерний»», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-mehanizmy-obespecheniya-effektivnosti-raboty-sudebnyh-organov-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-zakone-ot-7-noyabrya-1775-g-uchrezhdeniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine reform the provinces rather than the centre?","a":"Because, as one older historian argued, she could apply her ideas to provincial government without having to accept any limit on her autocratic power, the Pugachev rising had just exposed the provinces' weakness, and the noble deputies of 1767 had pressed for exactly this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the new provinces drawn by population?","a":"Because the old provinces were too large and poorly staffed to govern, so the law gave each new province 300,000 to 400,000 people and each district 20,000 to 30,000, ignoring older historical boundaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the reform strengthen the nobility?","a":"Because many of the new local posts and court seats were filled by election from the nobles of each district and province, which drew the landowning class into running the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-368": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each province's noble assembly kept a genealogical book in six parts, and only nobles of 25 with an officer's rank could hold elected office.","Catherine II confirmed nobles' ownership of the minerals beneath their land in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, issued on the same day in 1785 as her charter for the towns."],"abstract":"The <b>Charter to the Nobility</b>, formally the Charter on the Rights, Freedoms and Privileges of the Well-Born Russian Nobility, was the law of 21 April 1785 setting out the nobility's rights as an estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It confirmed the manifesto of 1762 that had freed nobles from compulsory state service, though article 20 still bound them to serve whenever the autocrat called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobles were exempted from personal taxes and corporal punishment, could be judged only by their peers, and could lose honour, estate or life only by the sentence of a court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their ownership of their estates and attached serfs was declared absolute, and it reached beyond the surface of the land to the minerals beneath, confirming a manifesto of 28 June 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was issued on the same day as a matching charter for the towns, and together the two set out the rights of nobles and townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Its lasting change was corporate, since the nobles of each province now formed a society with an assembly, building on the right to elect district marshals that the reform of 1775 had already granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only nobles aged at least 25, with an officer's rank and an income of at least 100 roubles from their villages, could be elected, and the governor chose the provincial marshal from two candidates the nobles put forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each provincial assembly kept a genealogical book in six parts, one book per province, so that nobles of the provinces had the same chance as those of the capitals to be entered and have their status proved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One older historian traced how the end of compulsory service shifted the nobles' interests from the capital to the provinces, and saw the reform of 1775 and the charter as fulfilling that long wish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lawyers today read the charter as giving one estate a full legal status in the modern sense, though it kept an inequality between nobles who had served and those who had not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 54–57, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","Н. Е. Леонтьева, «Концепция правового статуса в Жалованной грамоте дворянству 1785 года», <i>Вопросы российской юстиции</i>, no. 8 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kontseptsiya-pravovogo-statusa-v-zhalovannoy-gramote-dvoryanstvu-1785-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Васильева, «Реализация положений Жалованной грамоты дворянству 1785 года о родословных книгах в Казанской и Симбирской губерниях в конце XVIII века», <i>Вестник Чувашского университета</i>, no. 1 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/realizatsiya-polozheniy-zhalovannoy-gramoty-dvoryanstvu-1785-goda-o-rodoslovnyh-knigah-v-kazanskoy-i-simbirskoy-guberniyah-v-kontse-xviii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Лунин, «Формирование электорального законодательства в сословные органы дворянского самоуправления во второй половине XVIII – начале XIX века (на материалах Нижегородской губернии)», <i>Вестник Марийского государственного университета. Серия «Исторические науки. Юридические науки»</i>, 9, no. 4 (36) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-elektoralnogo-zakonodatelstva-v-soslovnye-organy-dvoryanskogo-samoupravleniya-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii-nachale-xix. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Гордеева, «Особенности законодательной регламентации земельной собственности в России по актам второй половины XVIII – первой четверти XIX вв.», <i>Актуальные вопросы современной науки</i>, no. 30-2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-zakonodatelnoy-reglamentatsii-zemelnoy-sobstvennosti-v-rossii-po-aktam-vtoroy-poloviny-xviii-pervoy-chetverti-xix-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Киселев and А. С. Криворучко, «Проблема организации дворянского самоуправления и подготовка «Учреждений для управления губерний» в 1775 году», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 3 (26) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-organizatsii-dvoryanskogo-samoupravleniya-i-podgotovka-uchrezhdeniy-dlya-upravleniya-guberniy-v-1775-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Зайцев, «Изучение Жалованной грамоты городам 1785 г. в дореволюционной отечественной историографии», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 11 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izuchenie-zhalovannoy-gramoty-gorodam-1785-g-v-dorevolyutsionnoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did nobles care about the genealogical books?","a":"Because entry in the province's book was the legal proof of noble status, and with one book for each province, kept in six parts, provincial nobles had the same chance as those of the capitals to be recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the charter matter for provincial life?","a":"Because nobles freed from compulsory service had moved their interests from the capital to their estates, and the charter let them meet as a provincial society, elect marshals and fill local posts, which made them the ruling class of the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the governor still involved in noble elections?","a":"Because under Catherine's rules the nobility put forward two candidates for provincial marshal and the governor appointed one of them, and at first provincial officials also organised the voting itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Charter to the Gentry"},
    "ru-369": {"questions":["Only men of 25 with enough capital could vote under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that in Irkutsk a mere 16 merchants qualified to choose the town's officers and to hold office.","Town self-government under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, issued alongside the noble charter, lasted until the reform of 1870, though governors' interference slowed it from the start."],"abstract":"The <b>Charter to the Towns</b> was the law issued by Catherine II on 21 April 1785 that set out the rights of townspeople and a new system of town self-government in the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divided the population of a town into six categories, the real town inhabitants who owned property there, guild merchants, registered craftsmen, foreign and out-of-town merchants, eminent citizens and the posad people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every inhabitant was to be entered in a town register, and a townsman's status rested on property within the town, entry in that register and a trade or craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Merchants were sorted into three guilds by their declared capital, of at least 10,000, 5,000 and 1,000 roubles, while those with less formed the meshchanstvo, the plain burghers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The poorer burghers, unlike the merchants, still paid the poll tax and remained liable to corporal punishment, so the estate of the towns was far from equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The charter's town government had two councils, a general duma of deputies from all six categories that met at intervals, and a six-member duma, one deputy per category under the town head, that sat continuously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Voting and standing for office were limited to men of at least 25 with enough capital, so that in Irkutsk only 16 merchants could vote and be elected in the assembly of the town society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Nizhny Novgorod the decree introducing the charter reached the town magistracy on 10 December 1785, and officials were told not to interfere in the first elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In practice the heavy hand of the governors held town self-government back, and it advanced far more slowly than the noble assemblies created in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system nevertheless lasted until the town reform of 1870, which kept the name duma for the main town body, and later writers debated whether the charter had really created an urban estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["V. O. Kluchevsky, <i>A History of Russia</i>, trans. C. J. Hogarth, vol. 5 (London: J. M. Dent, 1931), 54–55, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.79985. [Open access]","М. В. Зайцев, «Изучение Жалованной грамоты городам 1785 г. в дореволюционной отечественной историографии», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 11 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/izuchenie-zhalovannoy-gramoty-gorodam-1785-g-v-dorevolyutsionnoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ефремова, «Городская реформа 1785 года и ее роль в формировании института городского гражданства», <i>Историко-правовые проблемы: новый ракурс</i>, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gorodskaya-reformaya-1785-goda-i-ee-rol-v-formirovanii-instituta-gorodskogo-grazhdanstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Плотникова, «Иркутская градская дума в XVIII в.: замыслы Екатерины II и повседневная деятельность», <i>Известия Иркутского государственного университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 2-1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/irkutskaya-gradskaya-duma-v-xviii-v-zamysly-ekateriny-ii-i-povsednevnaya-deyatelnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. А. Селезнев, «Создание Нижегородской городской думы (1785–1787)», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sozdanie-nizhegorodskoy-gorodskoy-dumy-1785-1787. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1916), 308–10, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr01dubnuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were there two dumas?","a":"Because the general duma, drawn from all six categories of townspeople, met only at intervals and gave direction, while the six-member duma, one deputy for each category under the town head, stayed in session and handled current business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did town self-government lag behind the nobles'?","a":"Because the governors kept a heavy hand on the towns, while the nobles' provincial assemblies drew new life from their electoral meetings and from the class's long experience of local service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the new town rules matter for Jews in the annexed lands?","a":"Because Russian law placed Jews in the urban estates, as merchants or burghers, so the municipal rules raised the question of whether they would sit in town government or keep the self-rule of their own communities, and the law wavered between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-370": {"questions":["The Russian Provisional Government abolished the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1917, after wartime expulsions of whole Jewish communities from the front had already broken down its boundaries.","In the census of 1897 nearly 4.9 million Jews, 94 percent of all the empire's Jews, lived within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them in its towns and shtetls."],"abstract":"The <b>Pale of Settlement</b> was the region on the western edge of the Russian Empire where Jews were permitted permanent residence, and outside which most were barred from settling, from the end of the 18th century until 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stretched from the Baltic to the Black Sea across much of Lithuania, Belarus, Ukraine and Bessarabia, an area more than twice the size of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the census of 1897 it held about 4.9 million Jews, 94 percent of all the Jews in the empire, yet they were a minority in every province and lived mostly in towns and shtetls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its rules were fixed by the Statute on the Jews of 1835, eased for merchants, graduates and craftsmen under Alexander II, and tightened when the temporary rules of 1882 barred new Jewish settlement in villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Wartime expulsions after 1914 broke it down in practice, and the Provisional Government abolished it on 20 March 1917 by the Julian calendar, 2 April by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Russia had kept Jews out for most of the 18th century, but the partitions of Poland brought it the large Jewish communities of Belarus, Lithuania and right-bank Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pressure came from below: in 1790 Moscow merchants complained that Jewish traders from Belarus were undercutting them, and the government refused Jews entry to the merchant guilds of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of 23 December 1791 let Jews live as merchants and burghers only in the Belarusian provinces and in the newly won lands of Yekaterinoslav and Taurida, which the government wished to populate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of 23 June 1794 extended the area to the lands of the Second Partition and Little Russia while charging Jews double the taxes of Christian townspeople, and the Third Partition added Vilnius and Grodno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians have explained the Pale by religion, by economic rivalry or by the state's wish to tax Jews through their communities and settle the frontier, and the culture of the shtetl grew up within its borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1916), 306–18, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr01dubnuoft. [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland from the Earliest Times until the Present Day</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 2 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1918), 28, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr02dubnuoft. [Open access]","John Klier, “Pale of Settlement,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article.aspx/Pale_of_Settlement. [Open access]","Peter Potichnyj, “Pale of Settlement,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CP%5CA%5CPaleofSettlement.htm. [Open access]","В. В. Егоров, «Правовые особенности политики правительства Екатерины II в отношении еврейского населения Российской империи во второй половине XVIII века», <i>Юридическая наука и практика: Вестник Нижегородской академии МВД России</i>, no. 20 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-osobennosti-politiki-pravitelstva-ekateriny-ii-v-otnoshenii-evreyskogo-naseleniya-rossiyskoy-imperii-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xviii. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Розина and Т. И. Волкова, «Правовое положение еврейского населения в России: политико-аналитический обзор (конец XVIII – начало XX вв.)», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i>, 27, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoe-polozhenie-evreyskogo-naseleniya-v-rossii-politiko-analiticheskiy-obzor-konets-xviii-nachalo-xx-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. А. Козлитин, «Правовое положение евреев в Российской империи XVIII–XIX века: цивилистический аспект», <i>Вестник Университета мировых цивилизаций</i>, no. 8 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoe-polozhenie-evreev-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-xviii-xix-veka-tsivilisticheskiy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Catherine's government restrict where Jews could live?","a":"Because Moscow merchants, fearing competition, complained in 1790 that Jewish traders were undercutting them, and the government answered by refusing Jews entry to the merchant guilds of the interior and keeping them to the lands taken from Poland and the new south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Yekaterinoslav and Taurida included?","a":"Because these lands, newly won from the Ottoman Empire, were thinly settled and the government wanted to fill the steppes, so the decree of 1791 let Jews register there as well as in Belarus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Pale last until 1917?","a":"Because in the 19th century neither liberals nor conservatives pressed hard for its abolition, and it was the army's wartime expulsions of Jewish communities into the interior that left its rules in abeyance before the Provisional Government ended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pale of Settlement"},
    "ru-391": {"questions":["Russian columns marched across the frozen Gulf of Bothnia in March 1809, carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> onto the Swedish mainland in the weeks after a coup had deposed Gustavus IV.","Finnish soldiers who fought on at Siikajoki and Revolax while their commander kept retreating became the heroes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, remembered in later Finnish poetry as the men of 1808–9."],"abstract":"The <b>Finnish War</b> of 1808–1809 was the last war between Russia and Sweden, fought mainly in Finland, which was then the eastern part of the Swedish kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the Tilsit settlement of 1807, for Napoleon urged Alexander I to attack Sweden, the ally of Britain, and the tsar demanded that King Gustavus IV close his ports to British goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Gustavus refused, Russian troops crossed the frontier on 21 February 1808, and by April they held the whole of southern and western Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The great island fortress of Sveaborg outside Helsingfors, now Helsinki, was surrendered that spring almost without a fight, a capitulation long remembered in Finland as a disgrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Finnish regiments and armed peasants then won several battles in the north, but the army was finally broken at Oravais on 14 September 1808, 2 September by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In March 1809, after a coup in Stockholm had deposed Gustavus, Russian corps under Bagration and Barclay de Tolly marched across the frozen Gulf of Bothnia and carried the war into Sweden itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Further Russian advances in northern Sweden that summer forced the new king, Charles XIII, to open negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Peace was signed at Fredrikshamn, the Finnish town of Hamina, on 5 September 1809 by the Julian calendar and 17 September by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sweden ceded the whole of Finland as far as the river Tornio, together with the Åland Islands, and agreed to join the Continental System and close its ports to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war made Finland an autonomous grand duchy under the tsar, yet it left little lasting bitterness between the two kingdoms, which drew together against Napoleon in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 307–8, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Arthur Reade, <i>Finland and the Finns</i> (New York: Dodd, Mead, 1917), 1–4, https://archive.org/details/finlandfinns00readuoft. [Open access]","Joseph R. Fisher, <i>Finland and the Tsars, 1809–1899</i> (London: Edward Arnold, 1899), 20–22, https://archive.org/details/finlandandtsars00fishgoog. [Open access]","В. В. Рогинский, «Полтава и Фридрихсгам. 1709, 1809 годы: две эпохальные даты в истории России и Северной Европы», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 6 (2009): 65–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poltava-i-fridrihsgam-1709-1809-gody-dve-epohalnye-daty-v-istorii-rossii-i-severnoy-evropy. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Тихомиров, «Геополитические стратегии на европейском севере в эпоху наполеоновских войн: локальность в глобальном историческом процессе», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 13 (135) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/geopoliticheskie-strategii-na-evropeyskom-severe-v-epohu-napoleonovskih-voyn-lokalnost-v-globalnom-istoricheskom-protsesse-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Н. Акимова, «Русско-шведская война 1808–1809 годов в мемуарах ее участника Ф. В. Булгарина», <i>Научные труды Санкт-Петербургской академии художеств</i>, no. 43 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-shvedskaya-voyna-1808-1809-godov-v-memuarah-ee-uchastnika-f-v-bulgarina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 487, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia attack Sweden in 1808?","a":"Because the alliance Alexander I had made with Napoleon at Tilsit tied him to the blockade of Britain, and Sweden was Britain's ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Napoleon pressed for the attack partly to turn Russia away from Turkey, and when Gustavus IV refused to shut his ports to British goods the tsar had his pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Swedish defence remembered so bitterly in Finland?","a":"Because the commander sent from Stockholm kept ordering retreats while Finnish units wanted to fight, and the great fortress of Sveaborg was handed over almost without resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The battles that Finnish troops and peasants then won, largely on their own, became a lasting source of national pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did Sweden give up at Fredrikshamn?","a":"Sweden gave up all of Finland as far as the river Tornio, together with the Åland Islands, which the Swedes regarded as a key to their own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also had to join Napoleon's Continental System and close its ports to British ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Finnish War"},
    "ru-392": {"questions":["With its capital moved from Åbo to Helsingfors in 1812, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept its own Senate, laws and later currency until Finland became independent in 1917.","In 1811 Alexander I joined the old Vyborg province, conquered by Russia in the 18th century, to the newly created <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the two parts were simply called Finland."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Duchy of Finland</b> was the self-governing state that Finland formed inside the Russian Empire from 1809 to 1917, with the Russian emperor as its grand duke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made out of the provinces Sweden lost in the Finnish War, and Alexander I chose to keep their Swedish-era laws rather than turn them into ordinary Russian provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Government at home lay with a council of Finns, renamed the Senate in 1816, which was presided over by a governor-general and answered to the emperor alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The grand duchy had its own citizenship and its own civil service, and in 1811 Alexander added to it the province of Vyborg, which Russia had taken from Sweden in the 18th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its capital was moved in 1812 from Åbo, now Turku, to Helsingfors, now Helsinki, because Åbo lay dangerously close to Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The founding act was the diet that Alexander I opened in person at the small town of Borgå, now Porvoo, in March 1809, while the war with Sweden was still going on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only a modest number of members came, 70 nobles, 8 clergy, 19 burghers and 30 peasants, sitting as the four estates of the old Swedish parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Borgå cathedral the tsar's Act of Assurance was read out, promising to maintain the religion, the fundamental laws and the rights of every estate, and the members then swore their oath of loyalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether Alexander had thereby granted Finland a constitution, or only confirmed old privileges as an act of grace, later became a bitter argument between Finnish and Russian lawyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The diet was not called again until 1863, but the autonomy founded at Borgå lasted until a manifesto of February 1899 began to cut it back, and in 1917 the grand duchy became independent Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph R. Fisher, <i>Finland and the Tsars, 1809–1899</i> (London: Edward Arnold, 1899), 35–41, 52–54, https://archive.org/details/finlandandtsars00fishgoog. [Open access]","Arthur Reade, <i>Finland and the Finns</i> (New York: Dodd, Mead, 1917), 16–18, 64, https://archive.org/details/finlandfinns00readuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 308, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","В. В. Рогинский, «Полтава и Фридрихсгам. 1709, 1809 годы: две эпохальные даты в истории России и Северной Европы», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i>, no. 6 (2009): 65–79, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poltava-i-fridrihsgam-1709-1809-gody-dve-epohalnye-daty-v-istorii-rossii-i-severnoy-evropy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. М. Антюшина, «Россия – Финляндия: какой была совместная история», <i>Современная Европа</i>, no. 3 (47) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiya-finlyandiya-kakoy-byla-sovmestnaya-istoriya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Филонов, «Особенности правового статуса Великого княжества Финляндского в составе Российской империи», <i>Образование. Наука. Научные кадры</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-pravovogo-statusa-velikogo-knyazhestva-finlyandskogo-v-sostave-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Ю. Лапина и С. Ю. Каргапольцев, «Великое княжество Финляндское как инокультурный анклав в геополитической системе Российской империи», <i>Клио</i>, no. 5 (221) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikoe-knyazhestvo-finlyandskoe-kak-inokulturnyy-anklav-v-geopoliticheskoy-sisteme-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexander I keep Finland's Swedish laws?","a":"Because the stubborn resistance of the Finns in 1808 showed him that harsh terms might provoke a rising in favour of Swedish rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leaving their laws and institutions in place won the loyalty of his new subjects, which was the best guarantee against a Swedish return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Borgå he promised to maintain their religion, fundamental laws and privileges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the capital moved to Helsingfors?","a":"Because Åbo, the old capital on the south-west coast, lay dangerously close to Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move was made in April 1812, and after a great fire at Åbo in 1827 the university followed the government to Helsingfors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How independent was the grand duchy inside the empire?","a":"At home it was very largely self-governing: it had its own Senate, citizenship, civil service and courts, and the monarch could not change its laws without its diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Abroad it had no separate existence, since its foreign relations were those of the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Grand Duchy of Finland"},
    "ru-393": {"questions":["The sultan's removal of two pro-Russian princes of Moldavia and Wallachia, a breach of the Peace of Jassy, set off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the autumn of 1806.","A Serbian rising led by Karageorge won Russian help during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the eighth article of the peace promised the Serbs an amnesty and their own administration under the sultan."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1806–1812</b> was fought between Russia and the Ottoman Empire, mainly along the lower Danube, in the same years as Alexander I's wars with Napoleon, Sweden and Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its spark was the sultan's removal in 1806 of the hospodars, or princes, of Moldavia and Wallachia, two men friendly to Russia, which broke the Peace of Jassy made with Catherine II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> French diplomacy encouraged the Ottomans, and Alexander ordered his troops into the principalities without a declaration of war, after which the sultan declared war on 30 December 1806, 18 December by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A truce followed the Franco-Russian peace of Tilsit in 1807, but fighting resumed in 1809, when the Danube fortresses of Izmail and Brăila fell to the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war also reached the Caucasus, where Russia handed back its conquests on the Black Sea coast at the peace, except Sukhum, which it kept on the plea that the place had never really been Turkish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The decisive turn came in 1811, when Mikhail Kutuzov took command of the Danube army after the death of Count Nikolai Kamensky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kutuzov allowed the grand vizier to cross to the north bank of the Danube, surrounded his camp at Slobozia and forced the Turkish army there to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peace talks at Giurgiu and then Bucharest ran to 19 sessions, and the treaty was signed in Bucharest on 28 May 1812, 16 May by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia gave back Moldavia west of the Prut and Wallachia but kept Bessarabia, with the Prut and the Kilia mouth of the Danube as the new frontier, while an eighth article promised the rebel Serbs an amnesty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peace came less than a month before Napoleon crossed the Niemen, and it freed the Danube army of more than 50,000 men to march against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 310–13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 485, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","В. Я. Гросул, «Бухарестский мир 1812 г. и формирование новой юго-западной границы России», <i>Русин</i>, no. 1 (27) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/buharestskiy-mir-1812-g-i-formirovanie-novoy-yugo-zapadnoy-granitsy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Грачев, «Бухарестский мир 1812 г. и сербский вопрос», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/buharestskiy-mir-1812-g-i-serbskiy-vopros. [in Russian] [Open access]","John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 87–88, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","Sir Robert Wilson, <i>Narrative of Events during the Invasion of Russia by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the Retreat of the French Army, 1812</i> (London: John Murray, 1860), 130–31, https://archive.org/details/narrativeofevent00wils. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia settle for so little in 1812?","a":"Because war with Napoleon was about to begin and Alexander needed his Danube army in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russia had hoped to annex Moldavia and Wallachia, but it gave both back and kept only Bessarabia, which brought its frontier to the Prut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kutuzov let the Turks cross the Danube?","a":"Because an army on the north bank, far from its fortresses, could be surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once the grand vizier had crossed, Kutuzov encircled his camp at Slobozia and the trapped army had to surrender, which pushed the Ottomans towards peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Serbs matter in the peace talks?","a":"Because Russia had sent troops to support the Serbian rising against Ottoman rule led by Karageorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eighth article promised the Serbs an amnesty and their own administration under the sultan, and their fate came up again and again in the talks at Giurgiu and Bucharest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)"},
    "ru-394": {"questions":["Annexed from the Ottoman Empire by the Treaty of Bucharest in 1812, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its name from the southern part of the land between the Prut and the Dniester.","Alexander I toured his new southern lands in 1818 and issued a charter confirming the separate status of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an autonomy that a new statute of 1828 largely swept away."],"abstract":"The <b>Bessarabia Governorate</b> was the Russian province formed from the land between the rivers Prut and Dniester, which the Ottoman Empire ceded to Russia by the Treaty of Bucharest in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new territory, bounded by the Dniester, the Prut, the Black Sea and the Danube, at first had no single name, and in the second half of 1812 the name of its southern part, Bessarabia, was extended to the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took in the fortresses of Khotin and Bender and, on the lower Danube, Izmail and Kilia, and most of its parish priests were Moldavians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its capital became Kishinev, now Chișinău, after the metropolitan Gavriil Bănulescu-Bodoni pressed its central position against a rival case made for Bender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The province was first governed as an oblast with a special status and was later turned into an ordinary governorate of the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> When peasants began to leave for Moldavia across the Prut, Alexander I feared the effect on the other Orthodox peoples of the Balkans and ordered abuses against the inhabitants stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temporary rules drafted by Ioannis Kapodistrias and approved in February 1813 set up a regional government of Moldavian boyars and Russian officials, kept its business in Russian and Moldavian, and freed the inhabitants from state taxes for three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1813 the Church also gave the province its own diocese of Kishinev and Khotin under Gavriil, who opened its first seminary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the tsar visited Bessarabia in 1818, a charter dated 29 April confirmed its separate institutions, and that year an assembly of boyars approved a register of 145 noble families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> This autonomy lasted only until 1828, when a new statute brought the province much closer to the ordinary Russian pattern of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["В. Я. Гросул, «Бухарестский мир 1812 г. и формирование новой юго-западной границы России», <i>Русин</i>, no. 1 (27) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/buharestskiy-mir-1812-g-i-formirovanie-novoy-yugo-zapadnoy-granitsy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Я. Гросул, «У истоков бессарабской автономии в составе России», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/u-istokov-bessarabskoy-avtonomii-v-sostave-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Морозан, «Организация дворянского общества в Бессарабской области в 1812–1823 гг.», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 19 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsiya-dvoryanskogo-obschestva-v-bessarabskoy-oblasti-v-18121823-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Морозан, «Образование Кишиневской и Хотинской епархии (1813)», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 4 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obrazovanie-kishinevskoy-i-hotinskoy-eparhii-1813. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Калякина, «Проблема автономии Бессарабской области в составе Российской империи и развитие судебной системы Бессарабии (1813–1828 гг.)», <i>Вестник Московского университета МВД России</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-avtonomii-bessarabskoy-oblasti-v-sostave-rossiyskoy-imperii-i-razvitie-sudebnoy-sistemy-bessarabii-1813-1828-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 312–13, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 485, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","А. В. Калякина, «Формирование и развитие судебной системы Бессарабской области (губернии) в составе Российской империи в XIX в.», <i>Вестник экономической безопасности</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-i-razvitie-sudebnoy-sistemy-bessarabskoy-oblasti-gubernii-v-sostave-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia keep Bessarabia but not Moldavia and Wallachia?","a":"Because the coming war with Napoleon made a quick peace urgent, and Russia had to settle for less than it had hoped for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Prut and the Kilia mouth of the Danube became the frontier, leaving Russia the land up to the Prut with the fortresses of Khotin and Bender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the province given its own rules at first?","a":"Because Alexander I wanted his new Orthodox Moldavian subjects to be content, and feared what other Balkan peoples would think if they were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temporary rules of 1813 kept Moldavian in the courts, a regional government that included local boyars, and three years free of state taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the year 1828 matter for Bessarabia?","a":"Because the special status of the province, confirmed by the charter of 1818, ended with a new statute that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians of its law and courts therefore speak of a period of Bessarabian autonomy running from 1813 to 1828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bessarabia Governorate"},
    "ru-395": {"questions":["Kotlyarevsky's night attack on Abbas Mirza's camp at Aslanduz in October 1812, where British-made cannon were captured, decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Russia's favour.","Tsitsianov was murdered under the walls of Baku while receiving its keys in 1806, early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his head was sent to the shah in Tehran."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Persian War of 1804–1813</b> was fought between Russia and Qajar Iran, then usually called Persia, for the kingdoms and khanates of the South Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paul I had accepted the crown of the east Georgian kingdom in December 1800, and Prince Pavel Tsitsianov then pressed on against neighbouring khanates that the shah still counted as his vassals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In January 1804 Tsitsianov stormed Ganja and renamed it Elizavetpol, and that summer he beat a Persian army under the crown prince Abbas Mirza but failed to take Erivan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1806 he was murdered under the walls of Baku while receiving its keys, and his head and hands were sent to Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both France and Britain courted the shah during the war, France by a treaty of 1807 and Britain by one guaranteeing Persia against Russian attack, and British officers and guns served with the Persian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For years the fighting was a matter of sieges and raids, often fought by small Russian detachments against much larger Persian forces, while the main strength of Russia was absorbed by its wars in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The turning point came in October 1812, when General Pyotr Kotlyarevsky crossed the Aras and routed the army of Abbas Mirza in a night attack at Aslanduz, capturing British-made cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had about 1,500 regular troops and 500 Cossacks against some 10,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the turn of 1813 he stormed the Caspian fortress of Lenkoran, and his commander reported to the tsar that not one of the 4,000 defenders had escaped death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war ended in October 1813 with the Treaty of Gulistan, which confirmed Russia in the khanates it held and in Georgia and Daghestan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 60–72, 87–90, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 314, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","А. Д. Панеш, «Гюлистанский мирный договор и геополитическая ситуация на Восточном Кавказе в первой трети XIX в.», <i>История, археология и этнография Кавказа</i>, no. 4 (36) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gyulistanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-i-geopoliticheskaya-situatsiya-na-vostochnom-kavkaze-v-pervoy-treti-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Исмаилова, «Гюлистанский мирный договор 1813 года и Российская политика на Южном Кавказе в XIX веке», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 36 (327) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gyulistanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-1813-goda-i-rossiyskaya-politika-na-yuzhnom-kavkaze-v-xix-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Громов, «Реликвии забытой войны. Трофеи битвы при Асландузе в музеях Санкт-Петербурга и Севастополя», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 6 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/relikvii-zabytoy-voyny-trofei-bitvy-pri-aslanduze-v-muzeyah-sankt-peterburga-i-sevastopolya. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. В. Заяц, «Русско-персидская война 1804–1813 гг.: о некоторых этапах российской политики в Закавказье», review of <i>Гюлистанский договор 12 октября 1813 г.</i>, by В. А. Захаров and М. А. Васьков, <i>Гуманитарий Юга России</i> 11, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-persidskaya-voyna-1804-1813-gg-o-nekotoryh-etapah-rossiyskoy-politiki-v-zakavkazie-o-monografii-zaharov-v-a-vaskov-m-a. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 289–90, 732, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the war break out in 1804?","a":"Because Tsitsianov was seizing khanates that the shah still regarded as his vassal states, and Persia saw that war with Russia could not be avoided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Persia also wished to regain its authority over Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were British guns among the Persian trophies?","a":"Because Britain, worried about Russian influence in the East, courted the shah and supplied his army with officers and weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Aslanduz the Russians captured cannon of English make, and two British officers serving with the Persians took part in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Persia make peace in 1813?","a":"Because Aslanduz and the storming of Lenkoran had destroyed its field army and its strongholds on the Caspian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Talks went through the British ambassador, and the Treaty of Gulistan followed in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Persian War (1804–1813)"},
    "ru-396": {"questions":["By the third article of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the shah recognised Russian rule over Baku, Shirvan, Ganja and Karabakh and gave up all claims to Georgia and Daghestan.","Negotiated through the British ambassador after the Persian defeats at Aslanduz and Lenkoran, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted only until the next Russo-Persian war, which ended with a new treaty in 1828."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Gulistan</b> was the peace that ended the Russo-Persian War of 1804–1813, signed on 12 October 1813 by the Julian calendar, 24 October by the Gregorian, in the Russian camp on the river Zeiva at Gulistan in Karabakh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Talks had gone through the British ambassador to Persia after the Russian victories at Aslanduz and Lenkoran left the shah without an army able to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its second article fixed the frontier on the lines each side actually held, leaving the border of the Talysh khanate to be drawn later by commissioners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the third article the shah recognised the khanates of Karabakh, Ganja, Sheki, Shirvan, Derbent, Kuba and Baku, with the part of Talysh then in Russian hands, as possessions of Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also gave up all claims to Daghestan, Georgia, Imeretia, Guria, Mingrelia and Abkhazia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The fifth article allowed merchant ships of both countries on the Caspian Sea but gave Russia alone the right to keep warships there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other articles provided for the return of prisoners, the exchange of envoys and consuls and a ceiling of 5 per cent on import duties, so that trade between the two empires could grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the fourth article Russia promised to support the heir whom the shah chose, a clause that gave it a say in the succession to the Persian throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, anxious about Russian power in the East, made its own treaty with Persia, completed in 1814, which guaranteed the shah against attack from Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ratifications were exchanged at Tiflis in September 1814, but the treaty lasted only until a new war ended with another treaty in 1828, and it remains a contested landmark in the national histories of Georgia and Azerbaijan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Traité de Paix et d’Amitié perpétuelle, conclu entre l’Empire de Russie et celui de Perse, dans le Gulistan, le 12 Octobre 1813,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>Treaties, &c., Concluded between Great Britain and Persia, and between Persia and Other Foreign Powers</i> (London: Butterworths, 1891), 115–17, https://archive.org/details/treatiescconclu00britgoog. [Open access]","John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 87–90, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","А. М. Исмаилова, «Гюлистанский мирный договор 1813 года и Российская политика на Южном Кавказе в XIX веке», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 36 (327) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gyulistanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-1813-goda-i-rossiyskaya-politika-na-yuzhnom-kavkaze-v-xix-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Д. Панеш, «Гюлистанский мирный договор и геополитическая ситуация на Восточном Кавказе в первой трети XIX в.», <i>История, археология и этнография Кавказа</i>, no. 4 (36) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gyulistanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-i-geopoliticheskaya-situatsiya-na-vostochnom-kavkaze-v-pervoy-treti-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. В. Заяц, «Русско-персидская война 1804–1813 гг.: о некоторых этапах российской политики в Закавказье», review of <i>Гюлистанский договор 12 октября 1813 г.</i>, by В. А. Захаров and М. А. Васьков, <i>Гуманитарий Юга России</i> 11, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-persidskaya-voyna-1804-1813-gg-o-nekotoryh-etapah-rossiyskoy-politiki-v-zakavkazie-o-monografii-zaharov-v-a-vaskov-m-a. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 289–90, 732, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty matter so much for the Caspian Sea?","a":"Because its fifth article let merchant ships of both countries sail there but allowed only Russia to keep warships on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Persia accepted this together with a ceiling on duties that opened its markets to Russian trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a British ambassador involved?","a":"Because Britain, worried about Russian influence in the East, had its own alliance with the shah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The peace talks were carried on through its ambassador, and the British treaty guaranteed the shah against any attack from Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty not last?","a":"Because war broke out again between the two empires, and the British collection of Persia's treaties records it as cancelled by war and replaced by the treaty of February 1828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so it opened the way for Russia to consolidate its hold on the eastern Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Gulistan"},
    "ru-397": {"questions":["More than half a million of Napoleon's men were lost in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Marshal Ney, commanding the rearguard, was said to be the last of them to leave Russian soil.","The Russian people long remembered the multinational host of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the army of twenty nations, drawn from France, Italy, Poland and the German states."],"abstract":"The <b>French invasion of Russia</b> was Napoleon's campaign of 1812, which French writers call the Russian campaign and Russians the Patriotic War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from the breakdown of the alliance made at Tilsit, as Napoleon pressed Russia to enforce his blockade of British trade while Alexander I demanded that French troops leave Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 674,000 soldiers from France and its allied and subject states were gathered against Russia, about 640,000 of whom took part in the fighting, and Russians remembered them as the army of twenty nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main army began to cross the river Niemen near Kovno in the small hours of 24 June 1812, which was 12 June by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outnumbered, the Russian armies retreated and laid waste the country behind them, and the Grand Army lost more than 100,000 men to sickness, desertion and straggling before it had fought a great battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After the slaughter at Borodino on 7 September, Kutuzov gave up Moscow, and the French entered a city that most of its 250,000 inhabitants had left and that burned within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander refused to negotiate, and that autumn Napoleon began a retreat that hunger, cold, the Cossacks and the crossing of the Berezina turned into the destruction of his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marshal Ney, commanding the rearguard, was remembered as the last man of the Grand Army to leave Russia when he recrossed the Niemen at Kovno in mid-December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One careful count puts the Napoleonic losses at more than 550,000 men and the Russian losses in fighting men at nearly 150,000, besides huge numbers of civilians, though estimates still differ widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The disaster broke Napoleon's hold on central Europe and brought Prussia and then Austria into the coalition that overthrew him within little more than a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 487–88, 489–505, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 324–40, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 2 (London: H. L. Hunt and C. C. Clarke, 1827), 337, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit02sg. [Open access]","Edward Foord, <i>Napoleon’s Russian Campaign of 1812</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1914), 389–90, https://archive.org/details/cu31924024323242. [Open access]","А. В. Соловьев, «Две интерпретации войны 1812 г.: история длиной в двести лет», <i>Электронное научное издание Альманах Пространство и Время</i>, vol. 1, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dve-interpretatsii-voyny-1812-g-istoriya-dlinoy-v-dvesti-let. [in Russian] [Open access]","«Великая армия в России. Погоня за миражом (Реферат)», summary of А. И. Попов, <i>Великая армия в России. Погоня за миражом</i> (Самара: НТЦ, 2002), <i>Отечественная война 1812 года в современной историографии</i> (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikaya-armiya-v-rossii-pogonya-za-mirazhom-referat. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Шеломенцева, «Смоленск в войнах: демографическая составляющая их последствий в контексте национальной безопасности», <i>Вестник евразийской науки</i>, vol. 7, no. 4 (29) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smolensk-v-voynah-demograficheskaya-sostavlyayuschaya-ih-posledstviy-v-kontekste-natsionalnoy-bezopasnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Hilaire Belloc, <i>The Campaign of 1812 and the Retreat from Moscow</i> (London: Thomas Nelson and Sons, 1924), https://archive.org/details/campaignof1812re00bell. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Napoleon invade Russia in 1812?","a":"Because the alliance made at Tilsit had broken down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Napoleon wanted Russia to join in harsh measures against British trade, Alexander demanded that French troops leave Prussia, and each accused the other of preparing for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Grand Army shrink so fast even before Borodino?","a":"Because the Russians kept retreating and laying waste the land, so the French marched for weeks through an emptied country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sickness, desertion and straggling cost more than 100,000 men before a great battle had been fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the losses of 1812 still disputed?","a":"Because the records are incomplete and many prisoners died in captivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One count puts Napoleon's losses above 550,000, while a recent Russian study of the campaign finds that more than 430,000 men crossed the Niemen and the Bug and no more than 100,000 to 110,000 came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"French invasion of Russia"},
    "ru-398": {"questions":["Appointed commander-in-chief in August 1812 because national feeling demanded a Russian in place of Barclay de Tolly, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chose to give up Moscow in order to save the army.","A British officer at Russian headquarters described <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as so corpulent that he moved about the field in a small four-wheeled carriage, though shrewd as a Greek."],"abstract":"<b>Mikhail Kutuzov</b> was the Russian general who commanded the armies that drove Napoleon out of Russia in 1812, dying the next spring as a prince and field marshal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born into the nobility, he was wounded several times as a young officer and lost an eye, yet his face kept an engagingly intelligent expression, as a British officer who served with the army wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He commanded the Russian corps at Austerlitz in 1805, but since he had opposed the flank march that lost the battle, his reputation did not suffer from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1811 he took over the Danube army, trapped the grand vizier at Slobozia and negotiated the Peace of Bucharest of May 1812, although the tsar was displeased that he had delayed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander I had a low opinion of him, and his appointment as commander-in-chief in August 1812 was a concession to national feeling against the foreign-born Barclay de Tolly, not a mark of trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kutuzov was 67, corpulent and often moved about in a light carriage, yet his arrival lifted an army weary of retreating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Borodino on 7 September he stayed far behind the line at Gorki, and afterwards he wrote to Alexander of a bloody but victorious battle, for which the tsar made him a field marshal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the council of war at Fili he decided, against several of his generals, to abandon Moscow and save the army, and he then took the road to Ryazan, which kept him on Napoleon's flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His image as a sleepy old man who left events to providence owes much to Tolstoy's <i>War and Peace</i>, while the memoirs of his staff show him working over his maps late into the night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He would have been content to drive the invaders over the frontier, but Alexander, who joined the army at Vilna in December, resolved to carry the war into Europe, and Kutuzov died at Bunzlau in Silesia in the spring of 1813.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 485, 489, 494–96, 504–5, 516, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 312, 331–35, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Sir Robert Wilson, <i>Narrative of Events during the Invasion of Russia by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the Retreat of the French Army, 1812</i> (London: John Murray, 1860), 130–31, https://archive.org/details/narrativeofevent00wils. [Open access]","К. Б. Жучков, «М. И. Кутузов по характеристике его окружения в Отечественную войну 1812 года», <i>Вестник Псковского государственного университета. Серия: Социально-гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 3 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/m-i-kutuzov-po-harakteristike-ego-okruzheniya-v-otechestvennuyu-voynu-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Б. Жучков, «М. И. Кутузов и Александр I в декабре 1812 – феврале 1813 г.: о критериях приоритета в верховном командовании армией», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 80 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/m-i-kutuzov-i-aleksandr-i-v-dekabre-1812-fevrale-1813-g-o-kriteriyah-prioriteta-v-verhovnom-komandovanii-armiey. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Грачев, «Бухарестский мир 1812 г. и сербский вопрос», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/buharestskiy-mir-1812-g-i-serbskiy-vopros. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Собенников, ««…Это была моральная победа русских». Бородинское сражение в отечественной историографии», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 10 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/eto-byla-moralnaya-pobeda-russkih-borodinskoe-srazhenie-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexander appoint a general he disliked?","a":"Because national feeling demanded that the foreign-born Barclay de Tolly give way to a Russian, and the tsar yielded to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The appointment was a political necessity, and the word in the army that Kutuzov had come to beat the French raised its spirits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kutuzov give up Moscow without another battle?","a":"Because his army had been badly weakened at Borodino, and at the council of war at Fili he judged that saving the army mattered more than saving the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then retreated by the road to Ryazan, from which he could threaten Napoleon's flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his generalship still argued over?","a":"Because some writers have seen him as sleepy and passive, an image strengthened by Tolstoy's novel, while others credit him with wise patience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His own report called Borodino a victory, although he left the field and then Moscow, and historians still debate how much real command he kept once the tsar reached the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mikhail Kutuzov"},
    "ru-399": {"questions":["Raevsky's corps held the old walls on the Dnieper against Murat and Ney on the first day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, buying time for the two Russian armies to return.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Napoleon told his marshals that the ruined city would make an excellent base for winter quarters, yet within a week he marched on towards Moscow."],"abstract":"The <b>1812 Battle of Smolensk</b> was the struggle for the old fortress city on the upper Dnieper on 16–18 August 1812, 4–6 August by the Russian calendar, during Napoleon's invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two Russian armies of Barclay de Tolly and Bagration had united there at the start of August after weeks of retreat, and together they numbered about 113,000 men with 8,000 Cossacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smolensk was ringed by massive brick walls nearly 6.5 kilometres (4 miles) round and about 9 metres (30 feet) high, but it had almost no usable artillery and was built largely of wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Napoleon swung his army south of the Dnieper to take the city and get across the Russian road to Moscow, but Nikolai Raevsky's corps held it on 16 August while the main armies hurried back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 17 August the French and Poles took the suburbs after hours of fighting but could not breach the walls, and by nightfall the city was everywhere on fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That night Barclay ordered the city evacuated, over furious protests from generals who wanted it held, and the French entered only burning ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 7,000 Russians and perhaps 12,000 of Napoleon's men were killed or wounded, and some 20,000 men in all were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russian soldiers carried the miracle-working icon of the Mother of God of Smolensk out of the city, and before Borodino it was borne along the Russian lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the ruined city Napoleon told his marshals that Smolensk would make an excellent base for winter quarters, yet he left it on 25 August to march on Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The loss of the city deepened the anger at Barclay's retreat that soon brought Kutuzov to the command, and the province suffered heavily, losing some 86,000 people during the war by one estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 492–94, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Edward Foord, <i>Napoleon’s Russian Campaign of 1812</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1914), 117–18, 129–30, 142–44, https://archive.org/details/cu31924024323242. [Open access]","Sir Robert Wilson, <i>Narrative of Events during the Invasion of Russia by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the Retreat of the French Army, 1812</i> (London: John Murray, 1860), 87–89, https://archive.org/details/narrativeofevent00wils. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 1 (London: Treuttel and Würtz, 1825), 253–54, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit01sgur. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 329–30, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Е. К. Аксёнова и А. М. Иванов, «Оккупация Смоленской губернии в 1812 г. и разрушение православных святынь», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 18 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/okkupatsiya-smolenskoy-gubernii-v-1812-g-i-razrushenie-pravoslavnyh-svyatyn. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Шеломенцева, «Смоленск в войнах: демографическая составляющая их последствий в контексте национальной безопасности», <i>Вестник евразийской науки</i>, vol. 7, no. 4 (29) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smolensk-v-voynah-demograficheskaya-sostavlyayuschaya-ih-posledstviy-v-kontekste-natsionalnoy-bezopasnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Napoleon attack Smolensk from the south bank of the Dnieper?","a":"Because by taking the city from that side he hoped to get across the Russian line of retreat to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan had been made in advance, before the Russian moves of early August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Barclay give up the city after holding its walls?","a":"Because the road to Moscow was now secure for Bagration, and staying risked being cut off if the French crossed the river above the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many generals protested furiously, and their anger fed the demand for a new commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the city burn down?","a":"Because it was built largely of wood, and the fires spread with every shell faster than anyone could put them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The French later claimed that the Russians had burned it on purpose, which is very unlikely; the victors found only burning ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Smolensk (1812)"},
    "ru-400": {"questions":["Napoleon is said to have greeted the dawn of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by pointing to the sun of Austerlitz, though it shone straight into the eyes of his own men.","Bagration was mortally wounded defending his outworks, and the Raevsky battery fell to French cavalry and infantry in the afternoon of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet the Russian army was not destroyed."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Borodino</b> was fought on 7 September 1812 by the Western calendar, 26 August by the Russian, between Napoleon's Grand Army and the Russian army of Mikhail Kutuzov on the road to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The French called it the battle of the Moskva, after the river, which in fact flows at some distance from the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two days earlier the French had stormed the advanced redoubt at Shevardino, and each side then had roughly 120,000 to 130,000 men, the Russians with about 640 guns and the French with 587.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the eve of the battle the icon of the Mother of God of Smolensk, rescued from the burning city, was carried in solemn procession along the Russian lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> At dawn Napoleon pointed to the rising sun and called it the sun of Austerlitz, though, as one of his officers noted, it rose behind the Russians and dazzled the French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The fighting centred on three small earthworks on the Russian left, known as Bagration's outworks, and on Raevsky's great battery in the centre, both of which changed hands several times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the afternoon the French held both, Bagration had been mortally wounded, and the battle died away in the exhaustion of both armies, Napoleon having refused to send in his Guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Losses were enormous, about 40,000 on the Russian side, while a recent study based on the bodies buried in the district puts the French losses at no fewer than 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both sides claimed victory, Kutuzov writing to the tsar of a bloody but victorious battle and Napoleon telling his empress that by two in the afternoon the victory was his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kutuzov retreated the next morning and gave up Moscow a week later, and the official Russian account of the battle was shaped largely by his own staff, while even its timetable is disputed because witnesses kept Paris or Moscow time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 494–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 332–34, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 1 (London: Treuttel and Würtz, 1825), 334–35, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit01sgur. [Open access]","Д. Г. Целорунго, «О потерях русской и наполеоновской армий в Бородинском сражении 24–26 августа 1812 г.», Государственный Бородинский военно-исторический музей-заповедник, https://www.borodino.ru/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/TSelorungo.pdf. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Собенников, ««…Это была моральная победа русских». Бородинское сражение в отечественной историографии», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 10 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/eto-byla-moralnaya-pobeda-russkih-borodinskoe-srazhenie-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Бабенко, review of <i>Бородинское сражение. История русской версии событий</i>, by Л. Л. Ивченко, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5: История</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2010-02-015-ivchenko-l-l-borodinskoe-srazhenie-istoriya-russkoy-versii-sobytiy-m-kvadriga-2009-384-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Ануфриев, «Хронология Шевардинского боя», <i>Исторический формат</i>, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologiya-shevardinskogo-boya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. К. Аксёнова и А. М. Иванов, «Оккупация Смоленской губернии в 1812 г. и разрушение православных святынь», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 18 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/okkupatsiya-smolenskoy-gubernii-v-1812-g-i-razrushenie-pravoslavnyh-svyatyn. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do both sides claim Borodino as a victory?","a":"Because the French took the Russian earthworks and the great battery and the Russians retreated the next day, yet the Russian army was not destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kutuzov reported a bloody but victorious battle, while Napoleon told his empress that the victory was his by two in the afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Napoleon not send in his Guard?","a":"Because he would not hazard his last reserve and see his Guard destroyed, and he spent most of the day unwell, far in the rear near the Shevardino redoubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One later account judges that the Guard, still about 20,000 strong, might have destroyed the Russian army had it been sent in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the casualty figures so uncertain?","a":"Because the returns of both armies were incomplete, and the French counted only killed and wounded, not the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Estimates for the Russians have ranged from about 38,000 to more than 55,000, and a recent count of the dead buried in the district suggests at least 40,000 French losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Borodino"},
    "ru-401": {"questions":["Tolstoy has a six-year-old peasant girl watch the generals from the top of the stove during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Kutuzov orders the retreat on his own authority.","Aleksey Kivshenko's painting of 1880 showing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> soon entered Russian school textbooks, and the hut where it met was rebuilt as a museum in 1887."],"abstract":"The <b>Council at Fili</b> was the council of war that Mikhail Kutuzov held on 1 September 1812 by the Julian calendar, 13 September by the Gregorian, in the village of Fili just west of Moscow, a week after the Battle of Borodino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The question before the generals was whether to fight a second battle in front of the city or to give up Moscow without one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Leonty Bennigsen wanted to renew the offensive, Mikhail Barclay de Tolly argued for the opposite, and Kutuzov adopted Barclay's advice and ordered the retreat to continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army left its camp at Fili at three in the morning and marched through Moscow, followed by nearly the whole population, since only about 15,000 of the city's 250,000 inhabitants stayed behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fyodor Rostopchin, the governor of Moscow, heard of the decision only late that evening and wrote to the tsar that Kutuzov was handing the enemy the city in which the greatness of Russia lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The meeting took place in a peasant's hut, though the sources disagree over the owner, whom Tolstoy calls Andrew Savostyanov and a local history names as M. Frolov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Leo Tolstoy gave the council a chapter of <i>War and Peace</i>, in which the generals are watched from the top of the stove by Malasha, the peasant's six-year-old granddaughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the novel Kutuzov ends the argument by declaring that, by the authority entrusted to him by his sovereign and country, he orders a retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Aleksey Kivshenko's painting <i>Military Council in Fili</i> of 1880 became his best-known work and almost at once found its way into Russian school textbooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The hut itself burned down in 1868, but a reconstruction opened as a museum in August 1887, and a panorama museum of the Battle of Borodino was later built beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 496, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Р. А. Соколов, «Вступление великой армии в Москву в период наполеоновского нашествия на Россию», <i>Власть истории – История власти</i>, vol. 10, no. 4 (54) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vstuplenie-velikoy-armii-v-moskvu-v-period-napoleonovskogo-nashestviya-na-rossiyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ф. Прохоров, «Туристские ресурсы Москвы (на примере Западного административного округа)», <i>Сервис plus</i>, vol. 11, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/turistskie-resursy-moskvy-na-primere-zapadnogo-administrativnogo-okruga. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ф. Прохоров и Д. А. Троицкий, «Литературные места Подмосковного Кунцева: к проблеме туристских ресурсов», <i>Сервис в России и за рубежом</i>, no. 8 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/literaturnye-mesta-podmoskovnogo-kuntseva-k-probleme-turistskih-resursov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. П. Жилина, «Победы и поражения генерала Барклая де Толли в романе Л. Н. Толстого «Война и мир»», <i>Вестник Балтийского федерального университета им. И. Канта. Серия: Филология, педагогика, психология</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobedy-i-porazheniya-generala-barklaya-de-tolli-v-romane-l-n-tolstogo-voyna-i-mir. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Оришев, «А. Д. Кившенко: русская история на полотнах художника», <i>Бизнес и дизайн ревю</i>, no. 4 (36) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/a-d-kivshenko-russkaya-istoriya-na-polotnah-hudozhnika. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leo Tolstoy, <i>War and Peace</i>, book 11, chaps. 3–4, trans. Louise and Aylmer Maude, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2600. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kutuzov choose to give up Moscow rather than fight for it?","a":"Because the generals could not agree on a battle the army could win. Bennigsen wanted to take the offensive again, but Barclay de Tolly argued against it, and Kutuzov took Barclay's advice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Tolstoy's version Kutuzov puts the real question as whether to give up Moscow without a battle or risk losing the army as well as the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did most Muscovites leave with the army?","a":"Because the army withdrew straight through the city and nearly the whole population followed it, so that only about 15,000 of some 250,000 people remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The roads out stayed open until the evening of the next day, and many left with the retreating troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a meeting in a peasant hut so well remembered?","a":"Because writers and artists made it a scene of national memory. Tolstoy gave it a chapter of <i>War and Peace</i>,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Kivshenko's painting of 1880 went into school textbooks,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and the hut, burned in 1868, was rebuilt and opened as a museum in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Council at Fili"},
    "ru-402": {"questions":["Governor Rostopchin had the fire engines taken away before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and later called it an event he had prepared but not carried out, so his part is still argued over.","By one count more than 7,000 of about 9,000 buildings were lost in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a building commission was set up in 1813 to plan the rebuilding of the city."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow fire of 1812</b> was the great fire that broke out on 14 September 1812 by the Gregorian calendar, the day Napoleon's army entered the abandoned city, and burned until about 18 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first fires started that evening in the shops and grain stores along the outer wall of the Kremlin, and on the following night a great part of the city was in flames.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Napoleon, who had moved into the Kremlin, had to escape through the burning streets on 16 September and took refuge in the Petrovsky Palace outside the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Watching from the palace windows before he left, he is said to have cried out that the Russians were real Scythians to have done such a thing themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By one count the fire destroyed 7,632 of the city's 9,151 buildings, and another puts the loss at more than 70 per cent, including 2,041 of 2,567 stone houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Who started the fire has been argued over ever since, and the question has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Before leaving, the governor Fyodor Rostopchin had the fire engines and pumps taken out of the city, opened the prisons and ordered stores of spirits to be set alight, and he later called the fire an event he had prepared but had not carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> French courts-martial condemned about 400 supposed incendiaries, but a recent study rejects the French commission's claim that hundreds of released convicts had been ordered to burn the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An older English account held that the fire was partly accidental and partly the deliberate work of patriotic inhabitants, while Tolstoy argued that any abandoned town built of wood and full of foreign soldiers was bound to burn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Whatever its cause, it left the invaders without the quarters they had expected, loosened their discipline and has often been seen as the beginning of the Grand Army's ruin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 496–97, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 338–39, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 2 (London: H. L. Hunt and C. C. Clarke, 1827), 36–44, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit02sg. [Open access]","М. В. Борисов, «Так кто же виновен в пожаре Москвы 1812 года?», <i>История военного дела: исследования и источники</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tak-kto-zhe-vinoven-v-pozhare-moskvy-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Авдеева и С. А. Шкунов, «Пожарное вооружение в начале XIX века и его роль в московском пожаре 1812 года», <i>Пожары и чрезвычайные ситуации: предотвращение, ликвидация</i>, no. 3 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pozharnoe-vooruzhenie-v-nachale-xix-veka-i-ego-rol-v-moskovskom-pozhare-1812-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Молокова, «Восстановление Москвы после пожара 1812 г.: новый облик города», <i>Вестник МГСУ</i>, no. 6 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vosstanovlenie-moskvy-posle-pozhara-1812-g-novyy-oblik-goroda-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Соколов, «Французы в Москве: взгляд российского историка», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/frantsuzy-v-moskve-vzglyad-rossiyskogo-istorika. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leo Tolstoy, <i>War and Peace</i>, book 11, chap. 26, trans. Louise and Aylmer Maude, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2600. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do historians still disagree about who started the fire?","a":"Because the evidence points several ways. Rostopchin removed the fire engines, opened the prisons and had stores of spirits set alight, yet said only that he had prepared the event, not carried it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The French condemned hundreds as incendiaries, but a recent study rejects their commission's story of released convicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An older account blamed accident and patriotic inhabitants together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fire spread so far?","a":"Because most of Moscow was built of wood, its fire engines and pumps had been carried off in the evacuation, and a strong wind drove the flames for days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tolstoy thought an abandoned wooden town full of soldiers lighting fires was bound to burn whoever set the first one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fire matter so much for Napoleon's army?","a":"Because it took away the shelter and order the army had expected to find. A recent review describes the fire as a mortal wound that broke the army's discipline and spirit,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and an older history judged that with it all was over with the French conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fire of Moscow (1812)"},
    "ru-403": {"questions":["French general Delzons was shot dead and his brother killed beside him during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as the town on the Luzha changed hands again and again.","A band of Platov's Cossacks nearly captured Napoleon the morning after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and within two days he ordered the retreat by way of Mozhaysk."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Maloyaroslavets</b> was fought on 24 October 1812 by the Gregorian calendar, 12 October by the Julian, for a small wooden town on the river Luzha on the road from Moscow to Kaluga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Napoleon's army had begun leaving Moscow on 18 October, meaning to retreat by way of Kaluga to Smolensk, and its advance guard under Prince Eugène reached the town first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dmitry Dokhturov's Russian corps arrived in the dark and attacked at dawn, and the town changed hands again and again through the day as both sides fed in fresh troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The French general Alexis Delzons was shot dead at the head of his men, and his brother was killed beside him as he tried to shield the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By nightfall the town had burned to ashes with many of its wounded, and a British officer with the Russians thought the French had lost nearly 10,000 men and the Russians somewhat fewer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Although the French kept the ruins, Kutuzov's army now stood across the road to Kaluga, so Napoleon had either to fight a general battle or turn back to the road through Mozhaysk, stripped bare on the way in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next morning a band of Cossacks under Matvei Platov burst upon the imperial headquarters, and Napoleon came close to being captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After long hesitation he decided on 26 October to retreat by Mozhaysk, a choice an older history called nothing short of destruction for his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Letters written by French officers show that the worst stage of the retreat began after Maloyaroslavets, while they looked to Smolensk as a promised shore where their sufferings would end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Soviet historians made the battle the point at which the disastrous phase of Napoleon's retreat began, after eight desperate attacks on the burning town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 498–99, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 340, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 2 (London: H. L. Hunt and C. C. Clarke, 1827), 97–108, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit02sg. [Open access]","Robert Wilson, <i>Narrative of Events during the Invasion of Russia by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the Retreat of the French Army, 1812</i> (London: John Murray, 1860), 223–30, https://archive.org/details/narrativeofevent00wils. [Open access]","Е. В. Тарле, «Михаил Илларионович Кутузов — полководец и дипломат», <i>Народное образование</i>, no. 9 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mihail-illarionovich-kutuzov-polkovodets-i-diplomat. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Постникова, «Отступление из России: солдаты Великой армии во враждебном пространстве (по перехваченным письмам чинов армии Наполеона)», <i>Бюллетень науки и практики</i>, no. 6 (7) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otstuplenie-iz-rossii-soldaty-velikoy-armii-vo-vrazhdebnom-prostranstve-po-perehvachennym-pismam-chinov-armii-napoleona. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a battle the French won force them to retreat by the worst road?","a":"Because the ground they took mattered less than the road they lost. Kutuzov's army stood across the way to Kaluga, and Napoleon would not risk a general battle to clear it, so he turned back towards Mozhaysk and the Smolensk road, already stripped bare on the march to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the fighting in the town so destructive?","a":"Because the whole town was built of wood and both sides kept sending in fresh troops. It changed hands again and again, burned to ashes with many of its wounded,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and a later Russian account counted eight desperate attacks on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Cossack attack the next morning matter?","a":"Because it showed how exposed the emperor and his army had become. Platov's Cossacks reached the imperial headquarters and Napoleon was nearly taken;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> soon afterwards he chose the retreat by Mozhaysk that an older history called destruction for his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Maloyaroslavets"},
    "ru-404": {"questions":["Admiral Chichagov was drawn south by a feint before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, leaving only a small detachment facing the place where the French actually crossed the river.","The French saying 'c'est la Bérézina' for a total disaster recalls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the French and the Poles also remember the crossing as a feat of courage."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Berezina</b> was the fighting of 26–29 November 1812, by the Gregorian calendar, in which the remains of Napoleon's Grand Army forced a crossing of the river Berezina near the village of Studianka, north of Borisov in present-day Belarus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three Russian armies were closing in, Kutuzov's from the east, Peter Wittgenstein's from the north and Admiral Pavel Chichagov's Army of the Danube from the south, which had taken the bridge at Borisov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deceived by a feint downstream, Chichagov marched his main force south, leaving only a small detachment opposite the real crossing place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Jean-Baptiste Eblé's pontoon men, who had saved their tools and forged cramp-irons from wrecked carriages, built two trestle bridges standing up to their necks in icy water, and many died of the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 November the Russians attacked on both banks, and the corps of Oudinot, Ney and Victor held them off while the army and its stragglers crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Early on 29 November Eblé set fire to the bridges, half an hour later than ordered, and the thousands still on the eastern bank fell to the Cossacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The French lost at least half of their remaining regular troops, perhaps 20,000 to 25,000 men, besides the families and camp followers who had come with them from Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet about 40,000 men crossed with Napoleon, a third of them Poles, and an older history of Russia called the passage a victory of the desperate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wittgenstein, only three days' march away, was slow to reach the river, and an older history judged that he and Chichagov were both reluctant to come to close quarters with Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In French the phrase 'c'est la Bérézina' came to mean a total disaster, though French and Polish memory also keeps the crossing as an example of heroism and sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Eugen Stschepkin, “Russia under Alexander I, and the Invasion of 1812,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 501–503, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 343, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Philippe-Paul de Ségur, <i>History of the Expedition to Russia, Undertaken by the Emperor Napoleon, in the Year 1812</i>, vol. 2 (London: H. L. Hunt and C. C. Clarke, 1827), 266–71, https://archive.org/details/historyofexpedit02sg. [Open access]","Robert Wilson, <i>Narrative of Events during the Invasion of Russia by Napoleon Bonaparte, and the Retreat of the French Army, 1812</i> (London: John Murray, 1860), 334–36, https://archive.org/details/narrativeofevent00wils. [Open access]","П. М. Шорников, «Бухарестский мир и участие Дунайской армии в Отечественной войне 1812 г.», <i>Русин</i>, no. 4 (30) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/buharestskiy-mir-i-uchastie-dunayskoy-armii-v-otechestvennoy-voyne-1812-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Земцов, «Страсти по «французской» Березине: к истории одной дуэли», <i>Политическая лингвистика</i>, no. 2 (116) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/strasti-po-frantsuzskoy-berezine-k-istorii-odnoy-dueli. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Постникова, ««Польская» Березина: «живая» память», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 6 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polskaya-berezina-zhivaya-pamyat. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Russians fail to trap Napoleon at the river?","a":"Because their three armies did not act together. Chichagov was drawn south by a feint and left only a small force at the real crossing,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while Wittgenstein, three days' march away, did not hurry to the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An older history judged that both commanders were afraid to close with Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the bridges so costly to build?","a":"Because the river had risen and was full of drifting ice, and the pontoon men had to stand in it up to their necks to drive the piles. Many died of the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their tools and iron survived only because Eblé had kept them when the pontoon train was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Berezina mean different things in different countries?","a":"Because each nation kept a different part of the story. In French 'c'est la Bérézina' means a disaster, yet the crossing is also remembered as heroism,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> while Poles came to see it as proof of their soldiers' loyalty to the cause of freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Berezina"},
    "ru-405": {"questions":["Opened with prayers on Christmas Day 1826, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds more than 300 portraits of the generals of 1812 painted by George Dawe and two Russian assistants.","Pushkin's poem <i>The Commander</i> of 1835 is set among the portraits of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and dwells on the full-length figure of Barclay de Tolly."],"abstract":"The <b>Military Gallery of the Winter Palace</b> is a long hall in the imperial palace in Saint Petersburg hung with 332 portraits of the Russian generals who served in the war against Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the victory Alexander I wanted a pantheon of glory for the generals of 1812 in his capital, and in 1819 he invited the English portrait painter George Dawe to Saint Petersburg to paint them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dawe worked on the portraits until 1828 with two Russian assistants, Alexander Polyakov and Vasily Golike, painting many of the generals from life and others from earlier likenesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The architect Carlo Rossi designed the hall, with vaults like a line of triumphal arches and laurel wreaths, spears, shields and standards as ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was solemnly opened on 25 December 1826, at the yearly thanksgiving for the deliverance of Russia from the invader, before the imperial family and the Guards regiments that had fought in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the start it was meant as a monument of memory, and a British visitor soon after the opening called it a temple sacred to military renown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thirteen frames hold no picture, only a name on green cloth, for generals who had died or could not come to Dawe's studio and left no likeness behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Royal palaces elsewhere had their halls of military glory, such as the Waterloo Chamber at Windsor, but that room honours a single battle while the gallery in Saint Petersburg honours a whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander Pushkin's poem <i>The Commander</i> of 1835 grew out of his visits to the hall and dwells on Dawe's full-length portrait of Barclay de Tolly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The portraits were copied and lithographed for a six-volume illustrated edition published between 1845 and 1849, which carried the images far beyond the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["A. B. Granville, <i>St. Petersburgh: A Journal of Travels to and from That Capital</i>, vol. 1 (London: Henry Colburn, 1828), 536–39, https://archive.org/details/stpetersburghjou01gran. [Open access]","Н. А. Нарышкина-Прокудина-Горская, «1812 год, Петербург: памятники славы, мемориалы, произведения искусства», <i>Terra Linguistica</i>, no. 4 (160) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/1812-god-peterburg-pamyatniki-slavy-memorialy-proizvedeniya-iskusstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. А. Пашкевич, «Коллекция литографированных копий портретов Военной галереи Зимнего дворца из книжного собрания Строгановых Научной библиотеки Томского государственного университета», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. Культурология и искусствоведение</i>, no. 2 (6) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kollektsiya-litografirovannyh-kopiy-portretov-voennoy-galerei-zimnego-dvortsa-iz-knizhnogo-sobraniya-stroganovyh-nauchnoy-biblioteki. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Э. Локк, И. В. Перевозчиков, А. В. Сухова и М. Н. Тихомиров, «Антропологическое описание российских офицеров начала XIX века по материалам живописных портретов военной галереи Зимнего дворца», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 23. Антропология</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/antropologicheskoe-opisanie-rossiyskih-ofitserov-nachala-xix-veka-po-materialam-zhivopisnyh-portretov-voennoy-galerei-zimnego-dvortsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Киселева и К. А. Поташова, «Особенности поэтического экфрасиса в стихотворении А. С. Пушкина «Полководец» (1835): от черновика к беловику», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-poeticheskogo-ekfrasisa-v-stihotvorenii-a-s-pushkina-polkovodets-1835-ot-chernovika-k-beloviku. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Старк, «К истории создания стихотворения «Полководец»», <i>Временник Пушкинской комиссии</i>, no. 22 (1988), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-sozdaniya-stihotvoreniya-polkovodets. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Любин, «Военно-исторические экспозиции в резиденциях европейских монархов в XIX веке: концепции, состав и взаимовлияние», <i>Манускрипт</i>, vol. 14, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-istoricheskie-ekspozitsii-v-rezidentsiyah-evropeyskih-monarhov-v-xix-veke-kontseptsii-sostav-i-vzaimovliyanie. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tsar bring in an English painter for a Russian memorial?","a":"Because George Dawe was famous for portraits of British officers and for catching a likeness, and Alexander I invited him specially in 1819.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even so, he worked with two Russian assistants, Polyakov and Golike, whose share in the work is now seen as considerable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are some of the frames empty?","a":"Because some generals had died before the gallery was made or could not travel to Dawe's studio, and no portrait of them existed to copy. Their frames carry only their names on green cloth, so that they are honoured even without a face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the gallery matter to the memory of 1812?","a":"Because it was opened as an act of remembrance, with prayers for the soul of Alexander I and the blessing of his regiment's standards,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and its opening day became a yearly feast in memory of the expulsion of the French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pushkin's poem <i>The Commander</i> carried its portraits into Russian literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Military Gallery of the Winter Palace"},
    "ru-406": {"questions":["Kutuzov died at Bunzlau in April 1813, early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after leading the allied armies out of Russia and into Saxony.","The Treaty of Chaumont of March 1814 bound four great powers to keep fighting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until their aims were met and to stay allied for twenty years."],"abstract":"The <b>War of the Sixth Coalition</b> was the war of 1813–1814 in which Russia, Prussia, Britain, Sweden, Austria and many German states fought Napoleon from the Vistula to Paris and forced his abdication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the destruction of the Grand Army, Kutuzov and the chancellor Nikolay Rumyantsev wanted to stop at the Vistula and make peace, but Alexander I resolved to carry the war into Germany and raise its peoples against France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the Treaty of Kalisch of February 1813 Russia promised 150,000 men and Prussia 80,000, and in March Prussia declared war on France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kutuzov led the allied armies into Saxony but died at Bunzlau in April, and in May Napoleon, with a new army of young conscripts, won at Lützen and Bautzen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Austria joined the coalition in August after an armistice and a peace congress at Prague failed, and the allies formed three armies, none of them under a Russian commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Even so, Alexander I was the coalition's firmest member, insisting that there could be no peace while Napoleon remained on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decisive blow fell at Leipzig in October 1813, after which Napoleon brought only about 70,000 men back across the Rhine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1814 the Treaty of Chaumont bound the four great powers to continue the war until their aims were met and to stay allied for twenty years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The allies entered Paris at the end of March, Napoleon abdicated at Fontainebleau, and the First Treaty of Paris made peace with the restored Bourbon monarchy at the end of May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian officers who marched to Paris filled their letters and notes with the towns of Germany and France, and the campaigns were soon painted, among others by Ivan Gérin in a series of pastels of 1816–1818.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Julius von Pflugk-Harttung, “The War of Liberation, 1813–4,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 513, 540–41, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 345–58, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","C. K. Webster, <i>The Congress of Vienna, 1814–1815</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1918), 30–32, https://archive.org/details/congressofvienna00webs. [Open access]","Н. Н. Аурова, «Русские офицеры — участники Заграничных походов 1813–1814 годов», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 11 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkie-ofitsery-uchastniki-zagranichnyh-pohodov-1813-1814-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Л. Шевченко, С. А. Матвеев и В. Г. Гудымович, «Медицинское обеспечение заграничного похода русской армии в 1813 году (к 200-летию военной кампании)», <i>Вестник Национального медико-хирургического Центра им. Н. И. Пирогова</i>, vol. 8, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/meditsinskoe-obespechenie-zagranichnogo-pohoda-russkoy-armii-v-1813-godu-k-200-letiyu-voennoy-kampanii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Е. Лазарев, «Заграничные походы русской армии 1813–1814 гг. в произведениях живописи», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zagranichnye-pohody-russkoy-armii-1813-1814-gg-v-proizvedeniyah-zhivopisi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Л. Шевченко, С. А. Матвеев, В. Г. Гудымович и Е. А. Шевченко, «Завершение заграничного похода русской армии (кампания 1814 года) и развитие отечественной военной медицины (к 200-летию капитуляции Парижа)», <i>Вестник Национального медико-хирургического Центра им. Н. И. Пирогова</i>, vol. 9, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zavershenie-zagranichnogo-pohoda-russkoy-armii-kampaniya-1814-goda-i-razvitie-otechestvennoy-voennoy-meditsiny-k-200-letiyu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia fight on after driving Napoleon out?","a":"Because Alexander I believed Russia would never be safe while Napoleon could rebuild his army, and chose to raise the nations of Europe against France rather than stop at the Vistula, as Kutuzov and Rumyantsev advised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held to that line until the march on Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Who commanded the allied armies in 1813?","a":"Not a Russian: Bernadotte led the Army of the North, Blücher that of Silesia and Schwarzenberg that of Bohemia, with Russian generals serving under all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet Alexander I seemed to direct both the armies and the diplomacy of the coalition,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and in 1814 he pressed for a march on Paris to dethrone Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Treaty of Chaumont important?","a":"Because it ended the quarrels that had nearly split the alliance. The four powers bound themselves to fight on until their aims were met,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and agreed to remain allied for twenty years to guard the peace against France, the origin of the Quadruple Alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War of the Sixth Coalition"},
    "ru-407": {"questions":["Thousands of Saxon troops changed sides on the third day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a premature explosion of the bridge over the Elster trapped the French rearguard.","Alexander I overrode the Austrian commander's plan before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that the Russian troops would be placed as he chose, and stayed close to the French guns."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Leipzig</b>, also called the Battle of the Nations, was fought around the Saxon city from 16 to 19 October 1813 between Napoleon and the armies of Russia, Prussia, Austria and Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the largest battle of the Napoleonic Wars, and a recent study calls it the largest in history before the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 160,000 French and allied troops faced some 300,000 coalition soldiers with 1,200 guns, drawn up in a half-circle that left open only the way to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 16 October the French held their ground south of the city, while in the north the Prussian struggle for the village of Möckern became, in one older account, the bloodiest episode of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a day's pause the allies attacked again on 18 October, when 3,500 Saxon troops went over to their side, and that night Napoleon ordered a retreat through the narrow streets over a single bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Alexander I played a leading part, overruling the Austrian commander's plan so that the Russian troops would be placed as he chose, and staying almost under the fire of the French batteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 19 October the bridge over the Elster was blown up too early, cutting off the rearguard, and the Polish marshal Józef Poniatowski drowned with many of his men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 120,000 men were killed or wounded in the four days, and a British officer at allied headquarters reckoned that the main allied army alone had lost 50,000 since 15 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia's losses are put at about 22,000 dead, and for the centenary in 1913 the Russian memorial church of St Alexis was built on the battlefield to honour them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In Germany the victory was celebrated at once with services and patriotic sermons, and its anniversary was kept as a national festival as early as October 1814.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Julius von Pflugk-Harttung, “The War of Liberation, 1813–4,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 536–41, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 351–52, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","F. N. Maude, <i>The Leipzig Campaign, 1813</i> (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1908), 252–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924024321782. [Open access]","Robert Wilson, <i>Private Diary of Travels, Personal Services, and Public Events, during Mission and Employment with the European Armies in the Campaigns of 1812, 1813, 1814</i>, ed. Herbert Randolph, vol. 2 (London: John Murray, 1861), 181, https://archive.org/details/privatediaryoftr02wilsuoft. [Open access]","А. А. Егоров и Э. К. Пащенко, «Битва народов в освещении британской прессы», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (224) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bitva-narodov-v-osveschenii-britanskoy-pressy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Стерхов, «Битва под Лейпцигом 16–19 октября 1813 г. в отражении немецкой патриотической проповеди», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bitva-pod-leyptsigom-16-19-oktyabrya-1813-g-v-otrazhenii-nemetskoy-patrioticheskoy-propovedi. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Лизогуб, «Сохранение памяти о наполеоновских войнах: формы международного сотрудничества России и Германской империи в начале XX в.», <i>Омский научный вестник. Серия «Общество. История. Современность»</i>, vol. 9, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sohranenie-pamyati-o-napoleonovskih-voynah-formy-mezhdunarodnogo-sotrudnichestva-rossii-i-germanskoy-imperii-v-nachale-hh-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. М. Кириков, «Русский храм-памятник в Лейпциге. Рецензия», <i>Academia. Архитектура и строительство</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkiy-hram-pamyatnik-v-leyptsige-retsenziya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Napoleon trapped at Leipzig?","a":"Because three allied armies closed on the city together and outnumbered him nearly two to one, leaving him only the road west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When he finally retreated, his army had to pass through narrow streets and over a single bridge, and when it was blown up too early the rearguard was cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Saxons change sides during the battle?","a":"Because Napoleon's German allies were deserting him one after another as his power collapsed, and at Leipzig it was the Saxons' turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian general told a British officer that the Saxons' coming over with their guns saved his column far heavier losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the battle remembered so differently in Germany and in Russia?","a":"Because each country made it part of its own story. In Germany sermons and festivals from 1814 presented it as a national liberation,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> while Russia remembered its own 22,000 dead and built a memorial church on the field for the centenary in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Leipzig"},
    "ru-408": {"questions":["Barclay de Tolly was made a field marshal after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Russia awarded a silver medal for the capture of Paris to those who had fought in it.","Russia marked the fiftieth anniversary of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with celebrations in Saint Petersburg in 1864, at a time of quarrels with France over the Polish uprising."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Paris</b> was fought on 30 March 1814 for the heights of Montmartre, Belleville and Romainville on the edges of the French capital, the last great battle of the War of the Sixth Coalition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Learning Napoleon's plans from an intercepted letter, the allies left 10,000 cavalry to watch him, turned their backs on his army and marched on Paris with about 180,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marshals Marmont and Mortier defended the heights with what forces the capital had, while Barclay de Tolly's troops attacked Romainville and Blücher's army moved against Montmartre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the afternoon Marmont had been driven back and asked for terms, and the capitulation of Paris was signed that night, 18 to 19 March by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By one Russian count the battle cost the allies about 8,400 men, some 6,000 of them Russians, and the French about 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That winter Napoleon's propaganda had made the barbarous Cossack a stock figure of anti-Russian rhetoric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the morning of 31 March Alexander I received a deputation from the city and promised that the allied troops would behave with propriety, and they kept strict discipline and were not quartered on the inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He rode in at the head of the allied troops beside the King of Prussia, and the crowds along the boulevards asked which rider was the Emperor Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the Austrian emperor away, it fell largely to Alexander to decide France's future government, and he stayed in Talleyrand's house while the Senate deposed Napoleon, who abdicated on 11 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Barclay de Tolly was made a field marshal for the victory, all who took part received a silver medal for the capture of Paris, and its fiftieth anniversary was celebrated in Saint Petersburg in 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Julius von Pflugk-Harttung, “The War of Liberation, 1813–4,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 553–54, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","H. A. L. Fisher, “The First Restoration, 1814–5,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 555–56, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 358–60, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","А. А. Комзолова, «Празднование русской победы в период «польской смуты»: 50-летняя годовщина вступления в Париж русских войск (1864 г.)», <i>Большая Евразия: развитие, безопасность, сотрудничество</i>, no. 5-1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prazdnovanie-russkoy-pobedy-v-period-polskoy-smuty-50-letnyaya-godovschina-vstupleniya-v-parizh-russkih-voysk-1864-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Л. Шевченко, С. А. Матвеев, В. Г. Гудымович и Е. А. Шевченко, «Завершение заграничного похода русской армии (кампания 1814 года) и развитие отечественной военной медицины (к 200-летию капитуляции Парижа)», <i>Вестник Национального медико-хирургического Центра им. Н. И. Пирогова</i>, vol. 9, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zavershenie-zagranichnogo-pohoda-russkoy-armii-kampaniya-1814-goda-i-razvitie-otechestvennoy-voennoy-meditsiny-k-200-letiyu. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Гладышев, ««Казаки» и антирусская наполеоновская пропаганда в 1814 г.», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i>, vol. 14, no. 2 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kazaki-i-antirusskaya-napoleonovskaya-propaganda-v-1814-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. B. Granville, <i>St. Petersburgh: A Journal of Travels to and from That Capital</i>, vol. 1 (London: Henry Colburn, 1828), 536, https://archive.org/details/stpetersburghjou01gran. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the allies march on Paris instead of chasing Napoleon?","a":"Because an intercepted letter revealed his plan to strike at their rear, and royalist letters showed how weak the capital was. They left a screen of cavalry to deceive him and marched straight for Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Alexander I promise Parisians strict discipline?","a":"Because he wanted to show that the allies made war on Napoleon, not on France. He told the Senate he was the friend of the French and promised the city's deputies that his troops would behave with propriety,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> at the end of a winter in which French propaganda had pictured the Cossacks as barbarians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the fiftieth anniversary celebrated so grandly in 1864?","a":"Because Russia was then crushing the Polish uprising and quarrelling with Napoleon III's France over Poland. Remembering the entry into Paris served as a reminder of past victory and as a kind of symbolic slap at France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Paris (1814)"},
    "ru-409": {"questions":["Prussia hoped to take the whole kingdom of Saxony in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but was left with less than two-fifths of it, without the city of Leipzig.","The free city of Cracow was a compromise reached in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after the Russian emperor proposed that Thorn and Cracow become free cities under three powers."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish–Saxon crisis</b> was the quarrel at the Congress of Vienna over Alexander I's wish to keep nearly all of the Duchy of Warsaw as a kingdom of Poland under his rule and Prussia's claim to the whole of Saxony in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two claims were tied together by the alliance Russia and Prussia had made at Kalisch in 1813, which a British history likened to an incubus weighing on the congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saxony's king, Frederick Augustus, had stayed loyal to Napoleon until he was captured after Leipzig, and the Prussian chancellor Hardenberg claimed his kingdom by right of conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Austria feared a Russian army ten marches from Vienna and a Prussian border on its north, and Metternich and the British envoy Castlereagh tried to turn Prussia against the tsar's Polish plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 8 November 1814 the Russian governor of Saxony handed its administration to the Prussians, and in December the Grand Duke Constantine called on the Poles to unite in defence of their country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alarmed by Prussian hints that refusal would mean war, Castlereagh drafted a secret defensive treaty, which Britain, Austria and France signed on 3 January 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The treaty was meant to prevent war rather than make it, and within days Prussia gave way and France was admitted to the conferences of the great powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February a compromise gave Prussia less than two-fifths of Saxony but not Leipzig, along with Thorn and its old Polish lands, while Cracow, following a proposal of the tsar's, became a free city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the Final Act the rest of the duchy was united to the Russian Empire, irrevocably attached to it by its constitution, and the tsar took the title of King of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A Russian historian has called the result in effect a fourth partition of Poland, a judgement an older French history had already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["A. W. Ward, “The Congress of Vienna, I. 1814–5,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 9, <i>Napoleon</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1906), 591–99, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.80045. [Open access]","C. K. Webster, <i>The Congress of Vienna, 1814–1815</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1918), 113–15, https://archive.org/details/congressofvienna00webs. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 360–62, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Б. Л. Богоявленский, «Дипломатия Клеменса Меттерниха в решении польско-саксонского вопроса на Венском конгрессе 1814–1815 годов», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Международные отношения</i>, no. 3 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diplomatiya-klemensa-metterniha-v-reshenii-polsko-saksonskogo-voprosa-na-venskom-kongresse-1814-1815-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Троицкий, «Александр I и Венский конгресс», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i>, vol. 5, no. 1–2 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-i-i-venskiy-kongress. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Р. Соколов, «Александр I и образование Краковской республики», <i>Новый исторический вестник</i>, no. 1 (47) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-i-i-obrazovanie-krakovskoy-respubliki. [in Russian] [Open access]","“General Treaty between Great Britain, Austria, France, Portugal, Prussia, Russia, and Sweden,” Vienna, 9 June 1815, art. 1, in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 1 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 215–16, https://archive.org/details/mapofeuropebytre01hert. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Poland and Saxony argued over together?","a":"Because Russia's gains in Poland were to be balanced by compensation for Prussia, which was to give up most of its former Polish provinces, and Prussia wanted that compensation in Saxony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kalisch alliance bound Alexander to back his ally, so neither question could be settled alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain, Austria and France sign a secret treaty?","a":"Because Prussia hinted that refusing its claim to Saxony would amount to war, and Castlereagh decided the other powers had to be ready to defend themselves together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty worked as a warning: within days Prussia gave way and a compromise was negotiated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the settlement called a fourth partition of Poland?","a":"Because the Duchy of Warsaw was divided again among Russia, Prussia and Austria, with only Cracow left as a free city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The largest part became a kingdom joined to Russia by its constitution,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> which is why both an older French history and a Russian historian speak of a fourth partition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-410": {"questions":["Created by the Final Act of Vienna and joined to Russia by its constitution, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had the Russian emperor as its king and General Zajączek as its first viceroy.","After the uprising of 1830–1831 the separate administration of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abolished by an Organic Statute, though the post of viceroy lasted until 1874."],"abstract":"<b>Congress Poland</b> was the Kingdom of Poland created at the Congress of Vienna in 1815 out of most of Napoleon's Duchy of Warsaw and joined to the Russian Empire, with the Russian emperor as its king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Final Act the kingdom was irrevocably attached to Russia by its constitution while enjoying a distinct administration, and a legal study concludes that it can be treated as a state in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It covered about 127,000 square kilometres (49,000 square miles), barely a sixth of the old Polish Republic, and had about 3.2 million inhabitants in 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 21 June 1815 cannon at Warsaw announced the restoration of the kingdom, and the Polish army took the oath of allegiance on the plain of Wola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander I signed its constitution on 27 November 1815, soon after making the first state entry of a Russian monarch into Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The charter of 165 articles gave the kingdom its own two-chamber Sejm, an army of about 30,000 men and Polish as the only language of government, courts and army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was published in French and Polish in the kingdom's law gazette, and a Russian translation appeared only in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its franchise was wider than that of Restoration France, but vague clauses let the king adjourn the Sejm and rule without a voted budget, so that only four Sejms met in fifteen years instead of seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Real power lay with the tsar's brother Grand Duke Constantine as commander-in-chief and with the commissioner Nikolay Novosiltsev, and the viceroy's post went to General Józef Zajączek rather than to Adam Czartoryski as many had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After the uprising of 1830–1831 the constitution was abolished and the separate administration replaced by an Organic Statute in 1832, though the office of viceroy survived until 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. Askenazy, “Poland and the Polish Revolution,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 445–49, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodhist10actouoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 366–67, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","“General Treaty between Great Britain, Austria, France, Portugal, Prussia, Russia, and Sweden,” Vienna, 9 June 1815, art. 1, in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 1 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 215–16, https://archive.org/details/mapofeuropebytre01hert. [Open access]","О. А. Любезников, «О переводе на русский язык конституции Царства Польского в 1816 г.», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-perevode-na-russkiy-yazyk-konstitutsii-tsarstva-polskogo-v-1816-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Бучэк, “The Legal Status of the Polish Kingdom within the Russian Empire (1815–1832),” <i>Российское право: образование, практика, наука</i>, no. 5 (101) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/the-legal-status-of-the-polish-kingdom-within-the-russian-empire-1815-1832. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Д. Михайлов, «Институт наместничества в Царстве Польском (1815–1874): эволюция правового статуса», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования</i>, no. 7 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/institut-namestnichestva-v-tsarstve-polskom-1815-1874-evolyutsiya-pravovogo-statusa. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Троицкий, «Александр I и Венский конгресс», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i>, vol. 5, no. 1–2 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-i-i-venskiy-kongress. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexander I give the Poles a constitution?","a":"Because he had promised Czartoryski and the remnant of the Polish legions a restored Poland under his own sceptre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian historian sees the kingdom as a testing ground for joining a constitution to autocratic power,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and its charter was in some respects among the most liberal in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the constitution work less well than it promised?","a":"Because its vague wording left the king room to override it. He could adjourn the Sejm, no budget was ever put to it, and only four of seven Sejms met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grand Duke Constantine held real power with Novosiltsev, who became for Poles the embodiment of Russian bureaucratic oppression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called Congress Poland?","a":"Because the kingdom was the creation of the Congress of Vienna, whose Final Act united most of the Duchy of Warsaw to Russia and gave the tsar the title of King of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was smaller than many Poles had hoped, about a sixth of the old Polish state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Congress Poland"},
    "ru-411": {"questions":["Castlereagh dismissed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a piece of sublime mysticism and nonsense, and the Prince Regent of Britain sent only a letter of approval instead of signing it.","After the revolutions of 1820 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to stand for a league of monarchs against popular liberty, although its three articles had promised nothing but Christian brotherhood."],"abstract":"The <b>Holy Alliance</b> was the declaration in which the emperor of Russia, the emperor of Austria and the king of Prussia promised to govern by the precepts of the Christian religion, signed in Paris on 14 September 1815 by the Julian calendar, 26 September by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander I wrote it himself, and its three short articles bound the monarchs to treat one another as brothers and their subjects and armies as children, as members of one Christian nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It laid down no concrete obligations at all, which puzzled diplomats, and Metternich called it a loud-sounding nothing while Castlereagh called it a piece of sublime mysticism and nonsense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain's Prince Regent, pleading that he could sign nothing without a minister's countersignature, sent a letter of approval instead, and only he, the Pope and the Sultan stayed outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How far the mystic Baroness Krüdener and others in his circle shaped the text was argued even at the time, though Alexander's own turn to religion after 1812 came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> By one of the ironies of history the declaration came to stand for everything oppressive and reactionary, although nothing in its articles warranted that reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1820, shaken by the revolutions in Spain and Naples and by a mutiny in his own Guards, Alexander drew close to Metternich, and the alliance of sovereigns became a union of monarchs against nations demanding freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the congresses of Laibach and Verona he led the European reaction, and at home the curator of Kazan University proposed to organise its teaching according to the act of the Holy Alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own rhetoric fed the change, since he treated revolution as a universal evil, and out of that idea grew a doctrine of intervention in any country a revolution had struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same consistency made him side formally with the Sultan against the rebellious Greeks, a choice that cost his Greek-born minister Capodistrias his post and angered Russian opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 9–10, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","W. P. Cresson, <i>The Holy Alliance: The European Background of the Monroe Doctrine</i> (New York: Oxford University Press, 1922), 30–33, 47, https://archive.org/details/holyallianceeuro00cres. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 368–69, 384–85, 394–95, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 162–64, 209–10, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","А. Ю. Андреев, «„Политическая теология“ императора Александра I: идеи, репрезентации, практика», <i>Вестник ПСТГУ. Серия II: История. История Русской Православной Церкви</i>, no. 106 (2022): 62–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-teologiya-imperatora-aleksandra-i-idei-reprezentatsii-praktika. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Латонова, «Александр I как глава Священного союза: интерпретация британской прессы», <i>Вестник ПСТГУ. Серия II: История. История Русской Православной Церкви</i>, no. 111 (2023): 51–67, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-i-kak-glava-svyaschennogo-soyuza-interpretatsiya-britanskoy-pressy. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ф. Ильяшенко, «Священный союз в отечественной и зарубежной историографии: этапы изучения, концепции и подходы», <i>Вестник ПСТГУ. Серия II: История. История Русской Православной Церкви</i>, no. 122 (2025): 28–52, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svyaschennyy-soyuz-v-otechestvennoy-i-zarubezhnoy-istoriografii-etapy-izucheniya-kontseptsii-i-podhody. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did European diplomats distrust the declaration?","a":"Because it bound its signers to no concrete obligations and spoke only of principles, which left statesmen guessing at its real purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Metternich suspected that the Russian autocrat was hiding vast schemes of ambition under the language of the Gospel, and still called the text a loud-sounding nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain not sign it?","a":"Because the British government feared both offending its allies and exposing itself to attack from the Opposition, and found a way out in the rule that the Prince Regent could sign nothing without a minister's countersignature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Regent sent a personal letter approving its principles instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a pact of Christian brotherhood become a symbol of reaction?","a":"Because after 1820 Alexander came to see liberals and revolutionaries as one enemy and made common cause with Metternich against popular movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Treating revolution as a universal evil led to a doctrine of intervention, and the alliance was used against peoples demanding freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Holy Alliance"},
    "ru-412": {"questions":["News of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his favourite Guards regiment reached Alexander I at the congress of Troppau and helped turn him away from the liberal sympathies of his earlier years.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the regiment was disbanded and its officers posted to line regiments, where Sergey Muravyov-Apostol went on to become a leader of the conspiracy of 1825."],"abstract":"The <b>Semyonovsky Regiment mutiny</b> was a protest by soldiers of one of the oldest Guards regiments in Saint Petersburg in October 1820, set off by the cruelty of their new commander, Colonel Fyodor Schwarz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regiment had been run humanely and many of its officers were liberals, but Schwarz set out to break it with harsh punishments, even having holders of the Order of St George flogged, though the law exempted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the evening of 16 October by the Julian calendar the first grenadier company, known as the sovereign's company, complained of his treatment, and the next day it was marched off to the Peter and Paul Fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 17 to 18 October the rest of the regiment defied its officers, Grand Duke Michael and governor-general Miloradovich, but finally followed the company into arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was no political programme behind it, and the officers, Sergey Muravyov-Apostol among them, tried in vain to talk the men out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alexander was then at the congress of Troppau in Silesia, called to act against the military revolutions in Spain and Naples, and the news shocked him deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An old story says that Metternich broke the news to him with calculated exaggeration, but the tsar first learned of it from the report of his Guards commander, and the Austrian chancellor heard only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He refused to believe that soldiers had acted without secret leaders, and on 1 November by the Julian calendar, 14 November by the Gregorian, he ordered the regiment broken up and its men scattered through the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its officers were moved to line regiments, nine privates were sentenced to run the gauntlet six times, and Schwarz was simply dismissed from the service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The affair hardened Alexander against liberal ideas, and the scattered men carried their grievances through the army, where Muravyov-Apostol, now a lieutenant-colonel of the Poltava regiment, became a leader of the conspiracy of 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["И. А. Иванов, «„Нещастное и постыдное приключение“ как предвестник восстания декабристов. „Семеновская история“ 1820 г. в зеркале государственной безопасности», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (16) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neschastnoe-i-postydnoe-priklyuchenie-kak-predvestnik-vosstaniya-dekabristov-semenovskaya-istoriya-1820-g-v-zerkale-gosudarstvennoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. И. Киянская, «„Из мухи сделали слона“: С. И. Муравьев-Апостол и Семеновская история 1820 г.», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 9, part 2 (2021): 160–75, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-muhi-sdelali-slona-s-i-muraviev-apostol-i-semenovskaya-istoriya-1820-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 202–3, 209, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 384, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 438–39, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Т. В. Андреева, «Александр I и мифологема „всеевропейского заговора революционеров“», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 4, part 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-i-i-mifologema-vseevropeyskogo-zagovora-revolyutsionerov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a complaint by one company become a mutiny of the whole regiment?","a":"Because the first company was locked up in the fortress for its complaint, and the other companies would not obey until it was returned to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One contemporary wrote that the main mistake was imprisoning that company, and that the generals had made an elephant out of a fly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mutiny frighten Alexander so much?","a":"Because it came while he was meeting his allies to deal with military revolutions in Spain and Naples, and he would not believe the soldiers had acted without secret leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Guards had long been the instrument of palace coups, a threatening reminder to the son of the murdered Paul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians link it to the Decembrists?","a":"Because the scattered officers and soldiers spread discontent through the army, and the secret Union of Welfare decided to disband in the tense mood that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Officers such as Sergey Muravyov-Apostol, posted away from the Guards, became leaders of the later conspiracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-413": {"questions":["In the summer of 1819 more than 28,000 people of the Chuguev district rose against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and demanded to become ordinary state peasants again, until troops crushed the revolt.","By 1825 the separate corps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted 90 battalions in Novgorod province alone, a system Alexander I devised and Arakcheyev carried through with ruthless energy."],"abstract":"The <b>military settlements</b> were districts of the Russian Empire in which soldiers and peasants were merged into one population that farmed the land and served in the army at once, a system devised by Alexander I himself and introduced in 1810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each district was taken out of the civil administration and put under military rule, freed from taxes and dues and obliged instead to keep up a fixed body of troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first experiment, in Mogilev province, moved the local peasants away and settled a battalion of the Yelets regiment in their place, but the war of 1812 cut it short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1816 the scheme was renewed in Novgorod province, where the inhabitants were simply declared military colonists, their villages rebuilt to a plan, their beards shaved and their marriages arranged by the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By 1825 the separate corps of military settlements counted 90 battalions in Novgorod province and 36 battalions and 249 cavalry squadrons in the south, a burden carried by more than 400,000 peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Arakcheyev, as chief of all the settlements, ran them through a staff he chose himself, and supporters praised them for supplying recruits, keeping soldiers with their families and lowering the cost of the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The schools opened for the settlers' children could have become a step towards universal primary education, a scheme abandoned after Alexander's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The settlers themselves felt only a double servitude as labourers and as soldiers, a bondage worse than serfdom, and many fled even though a runaway caught a second time could be knouted and have his nostrils slit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the summer of 1819 the settlers of the Chuguev uhlan regiment rose and demanded to become state peasants again, and the revolt drew in more than 28,000 people before an army of 6,000 men gathered under Arakcheyev to crush it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The settlements outlived their founders, and only in 1857 did a new statute begin the reorganisation of the cavalry settlements, which lasted until 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 385–86, 396, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 174–79, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 415–16, 438, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","В. Л. Цубенко, «Украинское (Харьковское) военное поселение кавалерии Российской империи (1817–1857): основные тенденции развития», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 17, no. 1 (96) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ukrainskoe-harkovskoe-voennoe-poselenie-kavalerii-rossiyskoy-imperii-1817-1857-osnovnye-tendentsii-razvitiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Тороп, «Роль личностного фактора в кадровой политике самодержавия при формировании административной структуры военных поселений в первой четверти XIX века», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (12) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-lichnostnogo-faktora-v-kadrovoy-politike-samoderzhaviya-pri-formirovanii-administrativnoy-struktury-voennyh-poseleniy-v-pervoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Р. Полатханова, «Граф А. А. Аракчеев и становление системы начальных школ в военных поселениях (1815–1825 гг.)», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 23–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/graf-a-a-arakcheev-i-stanovlenie-sistemy-nachalnyh-shkol-v-voennyh-poseleniyah-1815-1825-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. И. Л. Мейер, «[Новгородские военные поселения]», publ. and comm. Г. М. Коваленко, <i>Новгородский исторический сборник</i>, no. 13 (23) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/novgorodskie-voennye-poseleniya-publikatsiya-i-kommentariy-g-m-kovalenko. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexander want soldiers to live as farmers?","a":"Because he would not reduce an army he thought must equal those of Austria and Prussia combined, yet wanted to cut its cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settling troops on the land was meant to feed them from their own fields, keep them with their families and relieve the treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the settlers hate a system that gave them land and houses?","a":"Because every part of their lives came under military command, from drill and dress to whom they could marry, and their children were taken for the service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They felt themselves in a double servitude as farm labourers and as soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Chuguev revolt of 1819 matter?","a":"Because it was a mass rising of more than 28,000 people who demanded the abolition of the settlements outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was quelled only after much bloodshed, and it was one of the events that turned Alexander towards reaction in 1819 and 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Military settlement"},
    "ru-414": {"questions":["After 1815 ministers' business reached Alexander I only through the reports of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose resolutions on the Committee of Ministers the emperor accepted almost without exception.","The murder of his mistress Nastasya Minkina at Gruzino in 1825 led <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to withdraw from affairs, and the people remembered his whole system by the name of Arakcheyevshchina."],"abstract":"<b>Alexey Arakcheyev</b> was a Russian artillery general and statesman, born in 1769 the son of a retired Guards lieutenant, who rose to be the most powerful servant of Alexander I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trained in the artillery and engineering cadet corps in Saint Petersburg, he was sent in 1792 to the private troops of the heir Paul at Gatchina, and on Paul's accession he was made a general and given the estate of Gruzino with more than 2,000 serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rough 'corporal of Gatchina' won Alexander's confidence too by his devotion to Paul's memory, his punctuality and his blunt honesty, and he served as minister of war until 1810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1815 he became a kind of prime minister without a portfolio, since ministers' business reached the emperor only through his reports on the Committee of Ministers, which Alexander accepted almost without exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also ran the military settlements as their chief, choosing their staff himself, though he insisted that the scheme was Alexander's idea and that he had opposed it at first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Contemporaries blamed him for every dark feature of the last years of the reign, and the people remembered the whole system as the Arakcheyevshchina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One early 20th-century history warned that it was convenient to blame him for what was done by Alexander's own will, and called him less a friend of the tsar than a faithful slave who never stole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recent work stresses his place in the conservative 'Russian party' of the 1820s, his help in winning Karamzin an audience and money for his history, and the schools he set up in the settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His estate was run by his mistress Nastasya Minkina, a coachman's daughter, and after her murder in September 1825 his reprisals left many serfs dead under the knout while he withdrew from affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gave up his place on the Committee of Ministers on 10 December 1825 by the Julian calendar, soon went abroad and abandoned the settlements as well, and died in 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 176–80, 186, 242, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 382–86, 398, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 416, 427, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","А. Ю. Минаков, «А. А. Аракчеев — лидер консервативной „русской партии“ в 1820-е гг.», <i>Вестник Сургутского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 4 (25) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/a-a-arakcheev-lider-konservativnoy-russkoy-partii-v-1820-e-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Плотникова and С. Н. Плотников, «Эпистолярное наследие А. А. Аракчеева как источник по истории России первой четверти XIX века (к 250-летию со дня рождения А. А. Аракчеева)», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/epistolyarnoe-nasledie-a-a-arakcheeva-kak-istochnik-po-istorii-rossii-pervoy-chetverti-xix-veka-k-250-letiyu-so-dnya-rozhdeniya-a-a-arakcheeva. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Тороп, «Роль личностного фактора в кадровой политике самодержавия при формировании административной структуры военных поселений в первой четверти XIX века», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (12) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-lichnostnogo-faktora-v-kadrovoy-politike-samoderzhaviya-pri-formirovanii-administrativnoy-struktury-voennyh-poseleniy-v-pervoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Р. Полатханова, «Граф А. А. Аракчеев и становление системы начальных школ в военных поселениях (1815–1825 гг.)», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 23–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/graf-a-a-arakcheev-i-stanovlenie-sistemy-nachalnyh-shkol-v-voennyh-poseleniyah-1815-1825-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Alexander trust him so completely?","a":"Because he was the safest of servants, punctual, obedient and incorruptible, and he admired the emperor without reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took over business that no longer interested Alexander and always tried to decide in the way that matched the emperor's mood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his reputation so dark?","a":"Because he carried out the most hated measures of the reign, above all the military settlements, with merciless severity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The killings that followed his mistress's murder confirmed the picture of a tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One early 20th-century history thought him blamed for much that was really Alexander's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his power end so quickly in 1825?","a":"Because it rested wholly on Alexander's personal trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shattered by Minkina's murder, he had already withdrawn from affairs before the emperor died, and he resigned his place on the Committee of Ministers within weeks of Alexander's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Aleksey Arakcheyev"},
    "ru-415": {"questions":["Rumours that Alexander I had not died at Taganrog in 1825 revived in the 1860s and fastened on the Siberian elder <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose grave at Tomsk became a place of pilgrimage.","Tolstoy's unfinished story of 1910 about the elder <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread through Russian literature the legend that the emperor had faked his death to live as a wanderer."],"abstract":"<b>Fyodor Kuzmich</b> was a hermit of unknown origin who lived in Siberia from 1836 until his death near Tomsk in January 1864, and whom popular legend identified with Emperor Alexander I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He first appears in the records in the autumn of 1836, when he was arrested as a vagrant near Krasnoufimsk in Perm province and sent as an exiled settler to Tomsk province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He moved several times in search of solitude, and those who met him spoke of an imposing bearing and of a knowledge of court life in Saint Petersburg astonishing in a wandering elder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his last years the Tomsk merchant Semyon Khromov looked after him, and after his death Khromov twice travelled to Saint Petersburg to hand the hermit's belongings and papers to the family of Nicholas I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His grave and cell at Tomsk became places of pilgrimage, and the heir to the throne, the future Nicholas II, visited the grave in June 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legend grew out of the death of Alexander I, who died at Taganrog on 19 November 1825 by the Julian calendar, 1 December by the Gregorian, almost 2,000 versts, about 2,100 kilometres (1,300 miles), from his capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rumours ran ahead of the funeral procession that the emperor was alive and that another man's body, or even a doll, was being carried across Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His physician Sir James Wylie rejected all talk of poison or suicide and ten doctors signed the autopsy report, but in the later 1860s the rumours revived and fastened on the hermit of Tomsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tolstoy's unfinished story <i>Posthumous Notes of the Elder Fyodor Kuzmich</i>, published in 1910, did much to spread the tale through Russian literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even the writers who built the legend admitted that there was no direct proof that the two men were one, and historians of popular belief explain its success by the hopes of provincial Russia for a returning deliverer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["А. Бараньска, «Федор Кузьмич – сибирская жизнь после смерти императора Александра I. История легенды», <i>Славянский альманах</i> 2012 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fedor-kuzmich-sibirskaya-zhizn-posle-smerti-imperatora-aleksandra-i-istoriya-legendy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Андреева and Л. В. Выскочков, «Утомление жизнью или уход: Александр I. 1825 год. Новые материалы», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (12) (2016): 255–67, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/utomlenie-zhiznyu-ili-uhod-aleksandr-i-1825-god-novye-materialy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Б. Гайдукова, «Цикл легенд о Федоре Кузьмиче на рубеже XX–XXI вв.: проблема демифологизации сюжета», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 307 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsikl-legend-o-fedore-kuzmiche-na-rubezhe-xx-xxi-vv-problema-demifologizatsii-syuzheta. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Путилин, «Сюжет о царе-старце как специфика провинциальной славянской ментальности в период модернизационного перехода», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 83 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/syuzhet-o-tsare-startse-kak-spetsifika-provintsialnoy-slavyanskoy-mentalnosti-v-period-modernizatsionnogo-perehoda. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 439, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 397–98, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did people believe the emperor had not really died?","a":"Because he died suddenly, far from his capital, and the news set off a crisis over the succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rumours spread that another body or even a doll was being carried across Russia while the emperor had hidden himself away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was this hermit in particular taken for Alexander?","a":"Because no one knew where he came from, and visitors remarked on his bearing and his knowledge of life at court, unusual in a Siberian wanderer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His patron Khromov carried his papers to the imperial family, which fed the idea that he had been someone great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians reject the legend?","a":"Because the doctors at Taganrog recorded the emperor's death and autopsy, and his own physician rejected every rumour about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The authors who built the legend themselves admitted that it rested on retellings and traditions, not on serious evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Feodor Kuzmich"},
    "ru-416": {"questions":["Founded in 1826 as an instrument of political surveillance, the Third Section of the imperial chancellery was created by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the first months of a reign opened by revolt.","Dying in the middle of the Crimean War in 1855, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had said that his successor might do as he wished but that he himself could not change."],"abstract":"<b>Nicholas I</b> was emperor of Russia from the end of 1825 until his death in 1855, the third son of Paul I, born in 1796 and so not yet five when his father was murdered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brought up under his mother's care by General Lamsdorf, who beat him freely, he was a poor pupil in most subjects but liked drawing, engineering and the exact sciences, and later showed real ability in military science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As neither elder brother had children and Constantine would not reign, Alexander warned Nicholas that he would inherit, yet gave him no training in government and left him commanding a Guards brigade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Alexander died the succession was unclear, and Nicholas swore allegiance to Constantine on 27 November 1825 by the Julian calendar, proclaiming his own accession only when Constantine's final refusal arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The day set for the troops to swear the new oath, 14 December, became the day of the Decembrist rising, which he crushed with grapeshot on the Senate Square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rising marked his whole reign: he questioned the prisoners in person, and in 1826 he made the Third Section of his own chancellery the main instrument of political surveillance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also had a digest made of the reforms the Decembrists had proposed and set Speransky to codify the laws, while secret committees discussed serfdom without abolishing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Abroad he fought Persia and Turkey, crushed the Polish rising in 1831 and sent an army to put down the Hungarian rising against Austria, and Europe came to see him as the champion of conservative order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Imposing and always buttoned into uniform, the 'crowned sergeant' governed through drill and detail, and Russian historians still call his reign the apogee of autocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Crimean War broke him, and he died on 18 February 1855 by the Julian calendar, 2 March by the Gregorian, having said near the end that his successor might do as he wished but that he himself could not change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 228–35, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 13–21, 81–82, 172–73, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 439–44, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","F. Mayne, <i>The Life of Nicholas I., Emperor of Russia: With a Short Account of Russia and the Russians</i> (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1855), 11, https://archive.org/details/lifenicholasiem00mayngoog. [Open access]","Л. В. Выскочков, «Magistra vitae императора Николая I», <i>Палеоросия. Древняя Русь во времени, в личностях, в идеях</i>, no. 7 (2017): 317–33, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/magistra-vitae-imperatora-nikolaya-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Ю. Жуков, «Николай I в отечественной историографии 1990-х гг.», <i>Наука и образование сегодня</i>, no. 9 (10) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nikolay-i-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii-1990-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Белоусов, «Государственный совет и политический кризис междуцарствия», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 67, no. 4 (2022): 1078–1100, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvennyy-sovet-i-politicheskiy-krizis-mezhdutsarstviya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was he so unprepared to rule in 1825?","a":"Because Alexander, though he warned him that he would reign, never introduced him to the State Council or the business of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His education had leaned to engineering and military matters, and his only command was a Guards brigade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Decembrist rising shape his whole reign?","a":"Because he believed that on 14 December he had saved Russia from ruin, and he spent the first months of his reign investigating the conspiracy himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of it came the Third Section and a government cut off from the thinking part of society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he never abolish serfdom though he knew its evils?","a":"Because he kept the peasant question inside secret committees and held back from any serious change to serfdom, even while paying close attention to the codification of the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The committee of 1826 on the peasants made no progress, and emancipation was left to his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nicholas I of Russia"},
    "ru-417": {"questions":["Governor-general Miloradovich was shot by Pyotr Kakhovsky during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of December 1825 while trying to persuade the mutinous Moscow regiment to give up and return to barracks.","Timed for the morning when the troops of Saint Petersburg were to swear a second oath in under three weeks, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the interregnum after Alexander I's death."],"abstract":"The <b>Decembrist revolt</b> was the rising of officers and soldiers on the Senate Square in Saint Petersburg on 14 December 1825 by the Julian calendar, 26 December by the Gregorian, the first open attempt in Russia to force a constitution on the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its occasion was the interregnum after the death of Alexander I, when the troops and officials of the capital swore allegiance to his brother Constantine, who had secretly renounced the throne years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 27 November to 13 December by the Julian calendar letters passed between Saint Petersburg and Warsaw, until Constantine's final refusal arrived and Nicholas fixed the new oath for 14 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Members of the Northern Society, meeting at the lodgings of the poet Kondraty Ryleyev, resolved to use the confusion, and on the morning of the oath they persuaded the Moscow Guards regiment to refuse it and march to the square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grenadiers of the Guards and sailors of the Guards crew joined them, perhaps 3,000 men in all, shouting for Constantine and the constitution, but their chosen dictator, Prince Sergey Trubetskoy, never appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rebels stood for hours in the cold without orders, and when the governor-general Miloradovich rode up to talk to them, Pyotr Kakhovsky shot him and wounded him mortally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the light failed Nicholas ordered his guns to fire grapeshot, and a few rounds cleared the square, killing many bystanders, while others drowned in the Neva as they fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official count of the dead cannot be trusted, but the toll was considerable, and that night most leaders were taken to the Peter and Paul Fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second rising in the south, led by Sergey Muravyov-Apostol with the Chernigov regiment, was broken by grapeshot and cavalry in the first days of 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its meaning has been argued since the government's own account, whether it grew from years of conspiracy or from chance, and some now read it as an act of republican civic virtue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 223–28, 233, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 439–42, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 13–19, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","М. С. Белоусов, «Государственный совет и политический кризис междуцарствия», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 67, no. 4 (2022): 1078–1100, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvennyy-sovet-i-politicheskiy-krizis-mezhdutsarstviya. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. В. Ильин and В. А. Шкерин, «Междуцарствие и петербургское восстание декабристов в зеркале постсоветской историографии», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 66, no. 2 (2021): 655–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mezhdutsarstvie-i-peterburgskoe-vosstanie-dekabristov-v-zerkale-postsovetskoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Д. Потапова, «„Время вдруг переломилось“: репрессивная политика Николая I и смена политических языков», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 4 (2025): 8–26, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vremya-vdrug-perelomilos-repressivnaya-politika-nikolaya-i-i-smena-politicheskih-yazykov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Цамутали and М. С. Белоусов, «190-летие восстания декабристов», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 2. История</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/190-letie-vosstaniya-dekabristov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rebels rise in the name of Constantine?","a":"Because the troops had already sworn allegiance to him, and the conspirators told them that he had not abdicated and that the second oath to Nicholas was unlawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Few soldiers understood the word constitution, and some took it for the name of Constantine's wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rising fail so quickly?","a":"Because only part of the garrison came out, the artillery they counted on stayed loyal, and their leaders showed no initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trubetskoy never took command, and the soldiers stood in the square for hours while Nicholas surrounded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the interregnum so confused?","a":"Because Alexander had kept the manifesto naming Nicholas his heir secret, sealed in envelopes in Moscow and Saint Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nicholas would not rely on an unpublished document and swore allegiance to Constantine instead, and the State Council's protocol of 27 November added to the confusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Decembrist revolt"},
    "ru-418": {"questions":["Of the 121 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sentenced in 1826, 61 belonged to the Northern Society, 37 to the Southern and 23 to the United Slavs, among them seven princes and two generals.","Exiled to the prisons of Transbaikalia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised a convict academy of lectures on history, literature and astronomy, while several of their wives followed them east."],"abstract":"The <b>Decembrists</b> were the members of the secret societies of Russian officers and nobles that grew up after the Napoleonic wars, named after the month of their failed rising in December 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most were young noblemen who had fought in the war of 1812 and the campaigns abroad of 1813 and 1814, and whose political views took shape in the years after the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikolay Turgenev wrote that liberal ideas spread in Russia from the moment the armies came home, as officers and militiamen told what they had seen in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were angered above all by serfdom, by the flogging of the soldiers who had beaten Napoleon, and by an autocrat's will standing in place of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the Union of Salvation of 1816 they passed through the more open Union of Welfare to the rival Northern and Southern Societies, one drafting a constitutional monarchy and the other a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After the rising some 500 suspects were arrested, and of the 121 men sentenced in 1826, 61 belonged to the Northern Society, 37 to the Southern and 23 to the United Slavs, among them seven princes and two generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five were hanged and most of the rest sent to hard labour or exile in Siberia, and several wives followed their husbands there, giving up their noble status and leaving their children behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the prisons of Transbaikalia they organised a 'convict academy' of lectures on history, literature, mathematics and astronomy, and once released to settlements many taught, doctored and farmed among the Siberians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rambaud called many of the 'men of December' the glory of their country in letters and political thought, and some of their demands, above all the end of serfdom, were later carried out by the very power they had defied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their meaning has been argued over since the government's first official account of the rising, through the memoirs published by Alexander Herzen, to the Soviet and present-day histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 196–201, 233–35, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 442–43, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 387–90, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 19–21, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","О. И. Киянская, «„Люди двадцатых годов“: Павел Пестель и Иван Бурцов», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 10 (2020): 94–104, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lyudi-dvadtsatyh-godov-pavel-pestel-i-ivan-burtsov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. А. Порхунов, «Декабристы в Сибири: жизнь и деятельность», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 15 (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dekabristy-v-sibiri-zhizn-i-deyatelnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Цамутали and М. С. Белоусов, «190-летие восстания декабристов», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Серия 2. История</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/190-letie-vosstaniya-dekabristov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did officers of the victorious army turn against the government?","a":"Because the campaigns abroad showed them countries with laws and rights that Russia lacked, and they came home hoping for reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead the last years of Alexander's reign brought repression, obscurantism and the hated military settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did their wives' journey to Siberia become famous?","a":"Because the women who followed their husbands to the mines lost their noble status and had to leave their children behind for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their letters carried the prisoners' first news home, and they made life in exile bearable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are they remembered though their rising failed?","a":"Because they were the first in Russia to die openly for liberty, and their demands, the end of serfdom and equality before the law, outlived them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herzen, whom their ideas inspired, published their memoirs and made them part of the revolutionary tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Decembrist revolt"},
    "ru-419": {"questions":["At yearly meetings in Kiev the leaders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> debated Pestel's plan to kill the whole imperial family, putting off a final decision from one year to the next.","Unlike the Northern Society in Saint Petersburg, which preferred a constitutional monarchy, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Ukraine wanted a centralised republic prepared by a provisional dictatorship."],"abstract":"The <b>Southern Society</b> was the secret society of Decembrist officers of the Second Army in Ukraine, formed in 1821 at Tulchin, the army's headquarters near the Moldavian frontier, and led by Colonel Pavel Pestel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It began when the Union of Welfare, alarmed by the tense mood after the Semyonovsky mutiny, decided in January 1821 to disband, and the southern members refused to accept the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They brought back the conspiratorial rules of the old Union of Salvation and made their aim a republic in Russia, to be won if necessary by Jacobin means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The society had three boards, at Tulchin under Pestel and Yushnevsky, at Kamenka under Davydov and Prince Sergey Volkonsky, and at Vasilkov under Sergey Muravyov-Apostol and Mikhail Bestuzhev-Ryumin, and the Tulchin board was the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its programme was Pestel's <i>Russian Justice</i>, which demanded the end of serfdom and a centralised republic, prepared over eight to ten years by a provisional dictatorship, with part of the land held in common to feed the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Northern Society in Saint Petersburg, revived in 1822 under Nikita Muravyov, preferred a federal constitutional monarchy, and its members distrusted Pestel as a Napoleon rather than a Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the leaders' yearly meetings in Kiev, Pestel pressed for killing not only the emperor but the whole imperial family, and the decision was put off from one meeting to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bestuzhev-Ryumin brought in the Society of United Slavs and opened talks with Polish conspirators, to whom Pestel offered an ethnically Polish state but not the frontiers of 1772.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander had already heard of the plot from an informer, and Pestel was arrested in December 1825, after which the Vasilkov board raised the Chernigov regiment and was crushed early in 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the 121 men sentenced in 1826, 37 belonged to the Southern Society, and Pestel, Muravyov-Apostol and Bestuzhev-Ryumin were among the five who were hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 197–99, 203–8, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 2, part 2 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 389–90, 396, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf0202ramb. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 442–43, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Е. Ю. Лебедева, «Тульчин времени декабристов: незамеченные источники», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 6 (14) (2020): 9–29, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tulchin-vremeni-dekabristov-nezamechennye-istochniki. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Л. Павлов, «Социально-философские идеи в программных документах Северного и Южного обществ декабристов», <i>Философский полилог</i>, no. 1 (9) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialno-filosofskie-idei-v-programmnyh-dokumentah-severnogo-i-yuzhnogo-obschestv-dekabristov. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. И. Киянская, «„Люди двадцатых годов“: Павел Пестель и Иван Бурцов», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 10 (2020): 94–104, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lyudi-dvadtsatyh-godov-pavel-pestel-i-ivan-burtsov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 14–15, 19–20, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the southern officers refuse to disband in 1821?","a":"Because Pestel and Yushnevsky, at the headquarters of the Second Army, wanted a more revolutionary organisation, not the cautious, almost open Union of Welfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Far from the capital, they restored the strict conspiratorial rules of the old Union of Salvation instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the northern conspirators distrust Pestel?","a":"Because he wanted a republic imposed by a military dictatorship, even against the will of the people, while the Northern Society preferred to submit to the people's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They feared his ambition and called him a Napoleon rather than a Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the society never act on its own plans?","a":"Because its leaders kept postponing the decision to strike, and the government learned of the plot before it was ready.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the rising came in the north, the south was unprepared, and only Muravyov-Apostol's board made a desperate attempt with the Chernigov regiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Southern Society of the Decembrists"},
    "ru-420": {"questions":["Sentenced to hard labour in 1826, the first Decembrists sent to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went down the Blagodatsky mine in fetters each morning to cut three poods of ore.","Long after the Decembrist Mikhail Lunin died at its mine of Akatui, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> received thousands of Polish insurgents transported to Transbaikalia after the rising of 1863."],"abstract":"The <b>Nerchinsk katorga</b> was the system of penal hard labour in the silver and lead mines and works of the Nerchinsk mining district in eastern Transbaikalia, near the Mongolian frontier, and it became the largest penal institution of pre-revolutionary Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek mining engineers in Russian service founded the Nerchinsk works in 1700, and from 1704 its smelter made Siberia a supplier of silver for the new Russian coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mines were worked at first by hired and forcibly settled labour, but from the early 1720s convicts sent from European Russia began to take their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its administration took shape after 1787, when the works passed to the emperor's own Cabinet and a Nerchinsk mining expedition took charge of the convicts, their labour and their prisons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Distance, a harsh climate and heavy mining work suited the aims of the punishment, to remove and to punish, while convict settlers also helped to people a frontier region facing China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Decembrists made it famous, for in 1826 Nicholas I commuted 31 death sentences to hard labour for life in Siberia, and the first state criminals were sent to the mines of the district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the Blagodatsky mine they went down the shafts in fetters at five in the morning to cut three poods, about 49 kilograms (108 pounds), of ore each before work ended at eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Guarding them led to a commandant's administration for the whole katorga, and from 1827 to 1830 some 85 Decembrists were held together in the Chita prison before moving to a new prison at Petrovsky Zavod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One of them, Mikhail Lunin, lived and died at the mine of Akatui, and after the Polish rising of 1863 the district's mines received thousands of Polish insurgents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the American traveller George Kennan visited in the 1880s, political convicts were being sent to Kara instead, but soon afterwards a new prison was built at Akatui to receive them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["George Kennan, <i>Siberia and the Exile System</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Century, 1891), 279–80, 288–89, https://archive.org/details/siberiaexilesyst02kennuoft. [Open access]","О. Б. Березина, «Нерчинская каторга в системе пенитенциарных учреждений России в конце XVIII – начале XX в.», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 4 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nerchinskaya-katorga-v-sisteme-penitentsiarnyh-uchrezhdeniy-rossii-v-kontse-xviii-nachale-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Ведерников, «Нерчинская уголовная каторга в системе серебросвинцового производства Восточного Забайкалья (XVIII–XIX вв.)», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i> 50, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nerchinskaya-ugolovnaya-katorga-v-sisteme-serebrosvintsovogo-proizvodstva-vostochnogo-zabaykalya-xviii-xix-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 443, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Г. А. Порхунов, «Декабристы в Сибири: жизнь и деятельность», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 15 (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dekabristy-v-sibiri-zhizn-i-deyatelnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. И. Потехина, «Музей декабристов в Чите», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 6 (14) (2020): 183–90, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/muzey-dekabristov-v-chite. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 233–34, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korn. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was hard labour placed in the mines of Transbaikalia?","a":"Because the district needed labour for its silver and lead works, which supplied metal for the coinage, and convicts could be made to do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its remoteness and harsh climate also suited a punishment meant to remove criminals and punish them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Decembrists gathered at Chita instead of left in the mines?","a":"Because guarding these state criminals called for a special commandant's administration, and the prisoners first scattered among the mines were brought together at Chita from 1827 and at Petrovsky Zavod from 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> There, unlike ordinary convicts, they lived together and were given only light work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mines later receive so many Poles?","a":"Because after the Polish rising of 1863 thousands of insurgents were condemned to penal servitude, and nearly all of those went to the Nerchinsk silver mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political convicts had seldom been sent there before, apart from Poles and a few Decembrists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nerchinsk katorga"},
    "ru-421": {"questions":["Abolished in August 1880, when its work passed to a new Department of State Police, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had watched sects, foreigners and political prisoners for over half a century.","A yearly report of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> warned Nicholas I in 1839 that serfdom was a powder keg under the state, a blunt judgement from the tsar's own political police."],"abstract":"The <b>Third Section</b> of His Imperial Majesty's Own Chancellery was the political police of Nicholas I, set up in July 1826 to take over the secret-police work of the Ministry of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief was General Alexander Benckendorff, named head of the new gendarmes a few days earlier, so that one man ran both the office and the uniformed force that fed it information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its duties ran from all matters of higher police and a watch on religious sects to exile, political prisons, foreigners and reports on every event without exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the statute on the gendarmes of 1827, European Russia was divided into five districts run from Saint Petersburg, Moscow, Vilna, Kharkov and Simbirsk, and by the early 1830s every province had a staff officer of gendarmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The censorship statute of 1828 also gave it the censorship of plays, in Russian and in foreign languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Nicholas founded it in the shadow of the Decembrist revolt, and Benckendorff's own project argued that the events of 14 December had shown how useless the old police had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Benckendorff presented it as a protector of the oppressed as well, and a story of its founding has the emperor handing him a white handkerchief and telling him that the more tears he wiped away with it, the better he would serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its early managers called the swelling bureaucracy a danger to the state, and gendarmes reported on the abuses and even the private lives of provincial governors and officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its yearly reports could be blunt, and the one for 1839 called serfdom a powder keg under the state, yet a history of 1907 judged it the government's most effective instrument of political espionage and oppression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was abolished on 6 August 1880, when its work passed to a new Department of State Police, and one Moscow newspaper wrote that it had prevented nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 247–48, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 443–44, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","О. В. Зайцева, «Возникновение III Отделения Собственной его императорского величества канцелярии», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки, no. S12 (2003)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozniknovenie-iii-otdeleniya-sobstvennoy-ego-imperatorskogo-velichestva-kantselyarii. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Ю. Абакумов, «III Отделение: замысел и финал охранительного проекта», <i>История и историческая память, no. 16 (2018)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iii-otdelenie-zamysel-i-final-ohranitelnogo-proekta. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Бибиков, «Надзор III отделения за частной жизнью губернских чиновников (1820–1830-е гг.)», <i>Философия. Журнал Высшей школы экономики 3, no. 2 (2019)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nadzor-iii-otdeleniya-za-chastnoy-zhiznyu-gubernskih-chinovnikov-1820-1830-e-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Л. Бредихин, «III Отделение Собственной Его Императорского Величества канцелярии в истории российского политического сыска», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета. Серия: Право 5, no. 1 (2020)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iii-otdelenie-sobstvennoy-ego-imperatorskogo-velichestva-kantselyarii-v-istorii-rossiyskogo-politicheskogo-syska. [in Russian] [Open access]","Я. В. Терещенко, «Цензура и журналистика в период правления Николая I», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология, no. 11 (2008)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsenzura-i-zhurnalistika-v-period-pravleniya-nikolaya-i-1. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nicholas I create a new political police at all?","a":"Because the Decembrist revolt had taken the government by surprise, and Benckendorff's project argued that the plot of 14 December proved the old police useless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicholas wanted the watch on opinion kept out of the ordinary ministries, so he put it in his own chancellery under a man he trusted completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the office linked to a uniformed corps of gendarmes?","a":"Because Benckendorff wanted officers in uniform rather than paid spies, men who would think reporting a duty of their office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gendarmes were spread through five districts of European Russia, with a staff officer in every province by the early 1830s, and all of them reported to the Third Section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it finally abolished in 1880?","a":"Because it had failed at its main task: even its own anniversary report admitted that it had proper surveillance only in Saint Petersburg, and terrorist attacks went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In August 1880 its work passed to a Department of State Police in the Ministry of the Interior, and the press welcomed the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Third Section of His Imperial Majesty's Own Chancellery"},
    "ru-422": {"questions":["Sergey Uvarov called the principles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the last anchor of Russia's salvation after inspecting Moscow University, and made them the declared aim of his ministry from 1833.","In 1841 the journal <i>Moskvitianin</i> praised the triad of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and called Western Europe a decaying, infectious organism from which Russia had to be guarded."],"abstract":"<b>Official Nationality</b> is the name given to the doctrine of the government of Nicholas I, summed up in the formula 'Orthodoxy, Autocracy, Nationality'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The formula was the work of Sergey Uvarov, who first used it in 1832 as deputy minister of education, and after the revolutions of 1830 it became the watchword of the reign's internal policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reporting on an inspection of Moscow University, Uvarov called the three principles the last anchor of Russia's salvation, and as minister he made schooling in their united spirit the declared aim of his ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The formula appeared twice in the first number of the ministry's journal in 1834, in the editorial preface and in his circular on taking office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the schools it meant more Russian language, literature and history at the expense of foreign languages, checks on foreign tutors and a ban on study at Western universities without special leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its third word, narodnost, is usually translated nationality, and Uvarov had grown the whole formula out of a view of history he first set down in French essays of 1813–1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1843 he told the emperor that the formula had angered liberals by its autocracy, mystics by its orthodoxy and enemies by its claim that Russia could march alongside the other nations of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its loudest supporters in print included Pogodin and Shevyrev, and in 1841 Pogodin's journal <i>Moskvitianin</i> called Western Europe a decaying, infectious organism, while the Russifying school policy of the south-western provinces had begun in 1831, as a reaction to the Polish rising, before the doctrine was put into practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The label 'official nationality' was never the government's own but was given later by the liberal scholar Alexander Pypin, and some Russian writers today dismiss his account as a caricature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Others see in the triad the source of a usable ideology for present-day Russia, so that a slogan coined to guard the schools of the 1830s is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 253, 279–81, 288–89, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","А. В. Малинов, «Понятие «народность» в историософской концепции С. С. Уварова», <i>Россия в глобальном мире, no. 8 (31) (2016)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ponyatie-narodnost-v-istoriosofskoy-kontseptsii-s-s-uvarova. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Зарубина, ««Теория официальной народности» С. С. Уварова как проекция консервативной доктрины российского государства», <i>Вестник Омского университета. Серия «Исторические науки» 11, no. 1 (41) (2024)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/teoriya-ofitsialnoy-narodnosti-s-s-uvarova-kak-proektsiya-konservativnoy-doktriny-rossiyskogo-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Ильин, «Триада С. С. Уварова «Православие — самодержавие — народность»», <i>Алтайский вестник государственной и муниципальной службы, no. 16 (2018)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/triada-s-s-uvarova-pravoslavie-samoderzhavie-narodnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Фатеев, ««Державный» историк М. П. Погодин и псевдонаучная теория «официальной народности» А. Н. Пыпина», <i>Русско-Византийский вестник, no. 2 (5) (2021)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/derzhavnyy-istorik-m-p-pogodin-i-psevdonauchnaya-teoriya-ofitsialnoy-narodnosti-a-n-pypina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 24–25, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","А. В. Ашихмин, «С. С. Уваров и политика в области просвещения в Юго-Западных губерниях в 1830–1840-е годы», <i>Труды Кольского научного центра РАН. Серия: Естественные и гуманитарные науки 2, no. 1 (2023): 126–40</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/s-s-uvarov-i-politika-v-oblasti-prosvescheniya-v-yugo-zapadnyh-guberniyah-v-1830-1840-e-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government need a doctrine in the 1830s?","a":"Because Nicholas I gave up all liberal reform after the revolutions of 1830 and wanted to guard the Russian order against the revolutionary West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Uvarov offered the formula as a set of mental dikes against destructive ideas, a way to win the minds of the young to Orthodoxy, autocracy and nationality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the word nationality hard to pin down?","a":"Because narodnost carried a theory of history, not just a reference to the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Uvarov had worked it out in French essays written during the Napoleonic wars, and in 1843 he said it had angered enemies by claiming Russia was mature enough to march alongside the other nations of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the doctrine still argued over?","a":"Because its usual name comes from a later liberal critic, Alexander Pypin, and some Russian writers today reject his picture as unfair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Others go further and treat the triad as a model for a national ideology in present-day Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Orthodoxy, Autocracy, and Nationality"},
    "ru-423": {"questions":["President of the Academy of Sciences from 1818 until 1855, the classical scholar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was nicknamed Old Woman by his friends in the literary society Arzamas.","Made a count in 1846 with his own triad as the motto on his arms, the minister <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was forced to resign in 1849 by a censorship committee's suspicions."],"abstract":"<b>Sergey Uvarov</b> was a Russian scholar and statesman, president of the Academy of Sciences from 1818 until his death in 1855 and minister of public education from 1833 to 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Born in Saint Petersburg in 1786, he was enrolled in the foreign service as a boy and sent at fifteen to study at Göttingen, and in Europe he came to know Goethe, Madame de Staël and the Humboldt brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own research was on the culture of ancient Greece, though he was more a wide-ranging amateur of learning than a professional scholar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the 1810s he belonged to Arzamas, a literary society of Karamzin's admirers, where his friends gave him the nickname Old Woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 24 he became trustee of the Saint Petersburg educational district, and in 1821 he moved to the Ministry of Finance while keeping up his work in learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Under Nicholas I the young admirer of freedom and education became, in the judgement of a historian writing in 1916, an obedient servant who agreed that the people needed only as much schooling as the state required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As deputy minister he inspected Moscow University and reported that the young must be brought to believe in Orthodoxy, Autocracy and Nationality, and the report won him the ministry in 1833.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As minister he launched the ministry's journal in 1834 and opened the University of Saint Vladimir at Kiev that July, with the openly political aim of weakening Polish influence in the south-western provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also pressed the teaching of Russian in the Baltic provinces and in 1838 closed a Riga newspaper that defended the Baltic nobles, only the second periodical he ever had banned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Made a count in 1846 with his triad as the motto on his arms, he was forced to resign in October 1849, after a censorship committee demanded he explain an article in defence of the universities that he had inspired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 279–81, 303–4, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","И. Б. Гаврилов, «Сергей Семенович Уваров. Жизнь. Труды. Мировоззрение», <i>Труды кафедры богословия Санкт-Петербургской Духовной Академии, no. 2 (4) (2019)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sergey-semenovich-uvarov-zhizn-trudy-mirovozzrenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Замостьянов and В. Т. Чумаков, «Граф С. С. Уваров — министр народного просвещения», <i>Народное образование, no. 5 (2013)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/graf-s-s-uvarov-ministr-narodnogo-prosvescheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Бадалян and Г. А. Фафурин, «Министр народного просвещения С. С. Уваров и закрытие газеты «Provinzialblatt für Kur-, Liv- und Esthland»», <i>Труды Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры 220 (2020)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ministr-narodnogo-prosvescheniya-s-s-uvarov-i-zakrytie-gazety-provinzialblatt-f-r-kur-liv-und-esthland. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Ашихмин, «С. С. Уваров и политика в области просвещения в Юго-Западных губерниях в 1830–1840-е годы», <i>Труды Кольского научного центра РАН. Серия: Естественные и гуманитарные науки 2, no. 1 (2023): 126–40</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/s-s-uvarov-i-politika-v-oblasti-prosvescheniya-v-yugo-zapadnyh-guberniyah-v-1830-1840-e-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Ильин, «Триада С. С. Уварова «Православие — самодержавие — народность»», <i>Алтайский вестник государственной и муниципальной службы, no. 16 (2018)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/triada-s-s-uvarova-pravoslavie-samoderzhavie-narodnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Малинов, «Понятие «народность» в историософской концепции С. С. Уварова», <i>Россия в глобальном мире, no. 8 (31) (2016)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ponyatie-narodnost-v-istoriosofskoy-kontseptsii-s-s-uvarova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a liberal young scholar become the voice of reaction?","a":"Because, as a historian writing in 1916 put it, the Uvarov of Nicholas I's reign was a changed man who had become an obedient servant of his master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His report on Moscow University matched the emperor's views so closely that Nicholas appointed him minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even in his early French essays, though, he already held that Russia could develop without the revolutions of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a university opened at Kiev in 1834?","a":"Because after the Polish rising of 1830–1831 the government wanted to weaken Polish influence in the provinces taken from Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The University of Saint Vladimir opened on 15 July 1834 with aims that were openly political rather than purely academic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he lose his post in 1849?","a":"Because after the revolutions of 1848 a new committee watched the press, and it treated him with suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When he inspired an article in defence of the universities, rumoured to be facing closure, the committee demanded an explanation, and he resigned in October 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sergey Uvarov"},
    "ru-424": {"questions":["Drafted by Admiral Shishkov and Prince Shirinsky-Shikhmatov, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1826 told Russian censors to strike out any passage whose second meaning might break the rules.","Replaced in April 1828 by a statute telling censors to read only the evident meaning of a text, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had governed the Russian press for less than two years."],"abstract":"The <b>cast-iron statute</b> was the nickname given to the censorship statute of Nicholas I, issued on 10 June 1826 by the Julian calendar to replace the milder statute of 1804.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work on it had begun in 1815, and its main authors were Admiral Alexander Shishkov, minister of education until 1828, and Prince Platon Shirinsky-Shikhmatov, with Mikhail Magnitsky also named among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It ran to 19 chapters and 230 paragraphs, about five times the length of its predecessor, and set up a Main Censorship Committee in Saint Petersburg with local committees in Moscow, Dorpat and Vilna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Censors were told to strike out any passage with a double meaning if one of its meanings broke the rules, any history that showed justly punished rebels as victims of the public good, and any argument hostile to monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mass of petty rules confused the censors themselves, and writers called it cast-iron for the way it crushed the printed word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It lasted less than two years, for at the emperor's own wish it was sent back for revision and a new statute was confirmed on 22 April 1828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new statute told censors to take the evident meaning of a text without reading a bad sense into it, not to judge whether an author's opinions were right and not to correct his style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reviewers of a recent study count the statute of 1828 a real step forward, but note that circulars soon added restrictions on top of it, so that editors bent less to the law than to the opinions of the monarch and his officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the revolutions of 1848 a committee under Dmitry Buturlin, formed on 2 April 1848, was empowered to watch even works that had passed the censors, and the years down to 1855 became known as a censorship terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Throughout the reign Nicholas also took a hand in censorship himself, acting from 1826 to 1835 as the personal censor of Alexander Pushkin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Я. В. Терещенко, «Цензура и журналистика в период правления Николая I», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология, no. 11 (2008)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsenzura-i-zhurnalistika-v-period-pravleniya-nikolaya-i-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. М. Горячева, «Особенности административного и уголовного законодательства в сфере борьбы с инакомыслием в годы правления Николая I», <i>Известия Тульского государственного университета. Экономические и юридические науки, no. 3-2 (2014)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-administrativnogo-i-ugolovnogo-zakonodatelstva-v-sfere-borby-s-inakomysliem-v-gody-pravleniya-nikolaya-i. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. И. Королев and Н. С. Пряников, «История развития российского цензурного законодательства XIX в. и зарождение авторского права», <i>Проблемы экономики и юридической практики, no. 2 (2011)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-razvitiya-rossiyskogo-tsenzurnogo-zakonodatelstva-xix-v-i-zarozhdenie-avtorskogo-prava. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шевцов, «Рец.: Макушин Л. М. Цензурный режим и журналистика: от «чугунного устава» 1826 г. до закона о печати 1865 г.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История, no. 3 (11) (2010)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rets-makushin-l-m-tsenzurnyy-rezhim-i-zhurnalistika-ot-chugunnogo-ustava-1826-g-do-zakona-o-pechati-1865-g-v-2-kn-ekaterinburg-izd-vo-ural-un-ta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. М. Амиров and А. П. Чудинов, «Российская журналистика под гнетом цензуры», <i>Политическая лингвистика, no. 2 (2010)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskaya-zhurnalistika-pod-gnetom-tsenzury. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 279, 303–4, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the statute called cast-iron?","a":"Because it tried to regulate every task and action of the censors in minute detail, five times more fully than the statute of 1804.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writers and journalists felt it crushed the press like a heavy weight, and it hampered every new idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it replaced so quickly?","a":"Because its cumbersome text and many petty rules confused even the censors, and the emperor himself sent it back for revision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government found it inconvenient too, and the statute of April 1828 limited the censors' freedom to read hidden meanings into a text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did censorship get harsher again after 1848?","a":"Because the revolutions in Europe frightened the government, and a committee under Buturlin was formed to watch the press even after the ordinary censors had passed a work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its methods made the position of the press unendurable, and the years to 1855 are remembered as a censorship terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-425": {"questions":["Speransky's staff found that only about 30,000 of more than 53,000 supposed laws were still valid while preparing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, printed in fifteen volumes in 1832.","A history of 1907 called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> not so much a codification as a mere digest of administrative enactments, though it framed Russian law until 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>Digest of Laws of the Russian Empire</b>, the Svod zakonov, was the arrangement by subject of the laws in force that Mikhail Speransky's staff compiled under Nicholas I, printed in 15 volumes in 1832 and in force from 1 January 1835.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Codifying Russian law had been tried again and again since the Ulozhenie of 1649, by Peter the Great, by Catherine the Great's Legislative Commission and under Alexander I, and the laws meanwhile piled up full of gaps and contradictions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 31 January 1826 Nicholas created the Second Section of his own chancellery for the work, nominally under Mikhail Balugyansky but in fact led by Speransky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Speransky's method was first to gather every law in date order and only then to sort the laws still in force by subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first stage produced the Complete Collection of the Laws of the Russian Empire, holding every law issued since 1649, and the second the Digest itself, in eight books and 15 volumes running from the fundamental laws of the state through taxes and estates to civil law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The compilers had found more than 53,000 supposedly valid enactments, but on checking only about 30,000 proved to have force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 19 January 1833 Nicholas opened the session of the State Council that approved the Digest, saying that the main task had been to make one digest of the laws that truly had force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was nothing reforming in it, a Russian historian wrote in 1916, yet its issue was an event of great importance, since the lack of a code had bred abuses in an age when people said the law was like a cart shaft that goes wherever it is turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An English history of 1907 was cooler, calling it not so much a codification as a mere digest of the most varied administrative enactments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Digest was reissued in 1842 and 1857, when it held about 90,000 articles, and its structure framed Russian law until 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 22, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 247, 253–54, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 443, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","С. В. Кодан, «М. М. Сперанский и создание Свода законов Российской империи», <i>Вестник Сибирского юридического института МВД России, no. 4 (8) (2010)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/m-m-speranskiy-i-sozdanie-svoda-zakonov-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Андреева, «Второе отделение Собственной ЕИВ канцелярии: технология законотворчества. 1826–1832 гг.», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал, no. 2 (22) (2019)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vtoroe-otdelenie-sobstvennoy-eiv-kantselyarii-tehnologiya-zakonotvorchestva-1826-1832-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Е. Владимирова, «Приемы юридической техники, использованные при разработке Свода законов Российской Империи 1832 года», <i>Юридическая наука, no. 4 (2019)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/priemy-yuridicheskoy-tehniki-ispolzovannye-pri-razrabotke-svoda-zakonov-rossiyskoy-imperii-1832-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Кодан, «Свод законов Российской империи. Место и роль в развитии юридической техники и систематизации законодательства в России в XIX — начале XX века», <i>Юридическая техника, no. 1 (2007)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svod-zakonov-rossiyskoy-imperii-mesto-i-rol-v-razvitii-yuridicheskoy-tehniki-i-sistematizatsii-zakonodatelstva-v-rossii-v-xix-nachale-xx. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had earlier attempts to codify Russian law failed?","a":"Because every ruler from Peter the Great to Alexander I began the work afresh, with German models, a great legislative commission or Speransky's ambitious early plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speransky's second attempt was more practical: he went back to the old Russian codes and collected the laws before arranging them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Speransky publish all the old laws first?","a":"Because his plan began with gathering every law in date order, which produced the Complete Collection, and only then sorting the laws in force by subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Collection was meant as the base from which the material of the Digest was chosen, so that the Digest could be read against the full texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Digest matter if it reformed nothing?","a":"Because until then judges and officials could not tell which of tens of thousands of enactments were in force: checking showed that only about 30,000 of more than 53,000 still applied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lack of a code had been one of the main sources of abuse in the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Digest of Laws of the Russian Empire"},
    "ru-426": {"questions":["Field Marshal Diebitsch lost about 15,000 men at Grochów near Warsaw in February 1831 while fighting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended only after Paskevich stormed the city.","After the Sejm declared Nicholas I dethroned as King of Poland in January 1831, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a full war that ended with the surrender of Zamość that October."],"abstract":"The <b>November Uprising</b> was the Polish rising of 1830–1831 against Russian rule in the Kingdom of Poland, which grew into a full war between the kingdom's army and the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kingdom had been bound to Russia in 1815 with the tsar as its king and with its own constitution, Sejm and army, but preliminary censorship from 1819 and the closing of the Sejm's debates to the public angered Polish patriots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It began on the evening of 29 November 1830 by the Gregorian calendar, 17 November by the Julian, when cadets of the ensigns' school led by Wysocki attacked the Russian cavalry barracks in Warsaw while others stormed the Belvedere Palace, residence of Grand Duke Constantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Constantine withdrew from the kingdom with his troops, and in January 1831 the Sejm declared Nicholas I dethroned as King of Poland and the Romanovs excluded from its throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In February Field Marshal Diebitsch crossed the frontier with about 114,000 men and 336 guns, and the battle of Grochów outside Warsaw cost the Russians some 15,000 killed and wounded without ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Poles came closest to success at the beginning of April, then lost the initiative and were crushingly defeated at Ostrołęka on 26 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cholera swept both armies and killed Diebitsch in June, and his successor Paskevich stormed Warsaw on 6 and 7 September and entered the city on 8 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last fortresses, Modlin and Zamość, surrendered in October, and many soldiers and leaders went abroad in what became known as the Great Emigration, watched there by agents of the kingdom's police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Britain and France offered sympathy but no help, and after the dethronement Palmerston made it clear that Poland would receive no aid from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising also spread into the former Polish lands of the empire, and records from Minsk province list landowners, nobles and peasants among the rebels, many of the peasants driven into the bands by their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 466–74, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 63–70, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","С. В. Морозов, «Отзвуки «Ноябрьского» восстания и польско-русской войны (1830–1831) в документах ЦГИА СПб», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История, no. 2 (2009)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otzvuki-noyabrskogo-vosstaniya-i-polsko-russkoy-voyny-1830-1831-v-dokumentah-tsgia-spb. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. И. Красник, «Английский парламент и польское восстание 1830—1831 годов», <i>Ретроспектива: Всемирная история глазами молодых исследователей, no. 7 (2012)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/angliyskiy-parlament-i-polskoe-vosstanie-1830-1831-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Карпович, «Социально-сословный состав участников восстания 1830–1831 гг. в Минской губернии», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы, no. 2 (2012)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialno-soslovnyy-sostav-uchastnikov-vosstaniya-1830-1831-gg-v-minskoy-gubernii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Акулин, «Восстание в Царстве Польском 1830–1831-х гг.», <i>Мировая наука, no. 6 (39) (2020)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vosstanie-v-tsarstve-polskom-1830-1831-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Бабоша, «Надзор тайной полиции Царства Польского за польской эмиграцией в 1831–1839 гг. (по донесениям военных губернаторов Варшавы в Третье отделение)», <i>Славянский альманах, no. 3-4 (2023): 34–52</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nadzor-taynoy-politsii-tsarstva-polskogo-za-polskoy-emigratsiey-v-1831-1839-gg-po-doneseniyam-voennyh-gubernatorov-varshavy-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Poles rise in November 1830?","a":"Because the constitution of 1815 had been whittled away by censorship and closed sessions of the Sejm, and secret societies of students, officers and nobles hoped to restore Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> News that the Polish army was to march with the Russians against revolutionary France decided the conspirators to strike at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did no foreign power help the Poles?","a":"Because Prussia actively helped the Russians with supplies and Austria played a double game, while France and Britain gave only evasive answers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palmerston told the Poles after the dethronement that they would get no help from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rising fail after its early successes?","a":"Because the Polish commander let the favourable moment of early April pass, and after the defeat at Ostrołęka quarrels and riots divided Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paskevich, helped by Prussian supplies, crossed the Vistula and attacked the capital from the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"November Uprising"},
    "ru-427": {"questions":["A history of 1907 called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a mere pretence, because its clauses promising Poland a measure of autonomy under the viceroy Paskevich were never carried out.","Drafted by a committee under Count Kochubey, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> placed the government of Warsaw in a Council of Administration acting in the emperor's name under the viceroy."],"abstract":"The <b>Organic Statute of the Kingdom of Poland</b> was the charter by which Nicholas I replaced the Polish constitution of 1815 after the defeat of the November Uprising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Dated 14 February 1832 by the Julian calendar and 26 February by the Gregorian, it was drafted in the Committee for the Affairs of the Kingdom of Poland, chaired by Count Viktor Kochubey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it the kingdom ceased to be a constitutional state and became a territory with a special administration, without a Sejm or a separate Polish army, and its officials no longer had to be Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some things survived on paper, for the kingdom kept its own civil and criminal codes, its town and village self-government and Polish as a language of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By its 22nd article the government of the kingdom was entrusted to a Council of Administration acting in the emperor's name under the viceroy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A history of 1907 called the statute a mere pretence, since its main clauses on autonomy were never carried out and remained a dead letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the viceroy Paskevich, made Prince of Warsaw, the ministries were abolished and then the kingdom's Council of State, as the government worked to fold the kingdom into the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statute said nothing at all about the viceroy's own powers, which rested for decades on a decree of 1818, and in 1847 Paskevich treated this vagueness as useful rather than as a gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Meanwhile the University of Warsaw was suppressed, Russian taxes, coinage, weights and the Julian calendar were brought in, and the old palatinates gave way to Russian-style provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Polish writer later judged that the statute had not annexed the kingdom outright but had destroyed its constitutional institutions, and a Russian historian has called the victory of 1831 military rather than political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Restoration</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1907), 474, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107364. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","Н. Г. Карнишина, «Правовой статус Царства Польского в оценках русских дореволюционных государствоведов», <i>Юридические записки, no. 2 (2013)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-tsarstva-polskogo-v-otsenkah-russkih-dorevolyutsionnyh-gosudarstvovedov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. В. Андреева, «Трансформация имперской политики в Польше: от конституционализма к бюрократической централизации», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История, no. 3 (2020)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/transformatsiya-imperskoy-politiki-v-polshe-ot-konstitutsionalizma-k-byurokraticheskoy-tsentralizatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Д. Михайлов, «Институт наместничества в Царстве Польском (1815–1874): эволюция правового статуса», <i>Genesis: исторические исследования, no. 7 (2026)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/institut-namestnichestva-v-tsarstve-polskom-1815-1874-evolyutsiya-pravovogo-statusa. [in Russian] [Open access]","Эльжбета Бучэк, “The Legal Status of the Polish Kingdom within the Russian Empire (1815–1832),” <i>Российское право: образование, практика, наука</i>, no. 5 (101) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/the-legal-status-of-the-polish-kingdom-within-the-russian-empire-1815-1832. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. Е. Эпштейн, «Деятельность комитета по делам Царства Польского при Государственном совете (1831–1832) как управленческая практика», <i>Международный научно-исследовательский журнал, no. 2-2 (33) (2015)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deyatelnost-komiteta-po-delam-tsarstva-polskogo-pri-gosudarsvtennom-sovete-1831-1832-kak-upravlencheskaya-praktika. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nicholas I replace the Polish constitution?","a":"Because the November Uprising had dethroned him as King of Poland, and after the victory he refused to restore the constitutional kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statute turned the kingdom into a territory with a special administration, ending its Sejm and separate army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the statute called a pretence?","a":"Because it kept a show of autonomy while its main clauses on self-government were never put into effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the same time Polish institutions were abolished one after another, and the government's programme aimed at ending the kingdom's political and financial autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the viceroy's powers stay undefined?","a":"Because the statute contained no rule at all on the viceroy, so his authority rested only on the decree of 1818.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Paskevich saw the uncertainty as a tool of government rather than a defect, since it left him free to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Organic Statute of the Kingdom of Poland"},
    "ru-428": {"questions":["Official records counted 96,275 Russian soldiers killed, wounded or captured in the Caucasus between 1801 and 1864, most of them in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the mountain peoples.","The Adyghe republics of the North Caucasus keep 21 May as a day of memory for the victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though attempts to have it recognised across Russia have failed."],"abstract":"The <b>Caucasian War</b> is the name usually given to the Russian Empire's long war of conquest against the mountain peoples of the North Caucasus, conventionally dated from 1817 to 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those dates were fixed in Soviet times and are still found in every school textbook, running from General Yermolov's appointment to the command to the capture of Shamil in 1859 and the end of organised resistance in the western Caucasus in 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fighting had begun earlier and went on later, for single villages in highland Dagestan held out until 1872 and a rising there in 1877–1878 cost the Caucasian army more than 2,000 killed and wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the east the struggle took a religious form known as the Murid war, after the religious spirit its leaders gave it as they called the mountaineers to holy war against Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shamil's imamate, which lasted from 1834 to 1859, governed through regulations called nizams that blended Islamic law with local custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The western war against the Circassians and their neighbours began as early as the eastern one and lasted longer, but it was fought more fitfully and never produced a single great leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia committed huge forces, and an American-edited history of 1882 put the army in the Caucasus at 200,000 men under Prince Vorontsov in the mid-1840s, adding that the British quietly favoured the mountaineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the count of the military historian Gizetti, drawn from official records, Russia lost 96,275 men in the Caucasus between 1801 and 1864, of whom 24,946 were killed, 65,322 wounded and 6,007 captured or missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even that count is incomplete, for Gizetti could not establish how many civilians died in raids on the Russian lines, because most of the local military archives had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official end of the war was marked on 21 May 1864, a date the Adyghe republics of the North Caucasus now keep as a day of memory for its victims, though attempts to have it recognised across Russia have failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), vii–viii, 229–30, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 42–43, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","В. В. Лапин, «Хронологические рамки Кавказской войны в контексте ее историографии», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История, no. 3 (2007)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/hronologicheskie-ramki-kavkazskoy-voyny-v-kontekste-ee-istoriografii-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Р. Салчинкина, «Проблемы медицинского обеспечения Отдельного Кавказского корпуса и казачества в годы Кавказской войны 1817–1864 гг.», <i>Общество: философия, история, культура, no. 10 (66) (2019)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problemy-meditsinskogo-obespecheniya-otdelnogo-kavkazskogo-korpusa-i-kazachestva-v-gody-kavkazskoy-voyny-1817-1864-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Колосовская, «О военном историке А. Л. Гизетти и его трудах», <i>Вестник Армавирского государственного педагогического университета, no. 2 (2020)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-voennom-istorike-a-l-gizetti-i-ego-trudah. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Т. Урушадзе, «Помнить — нельзя — забыть: Кавказская война в исторической памяти адыгов и российском пространстве коммеморации», <i>Политическая наука, no. 3 (2018)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pomnit-nelzya-zabyt-kavkazskaya-voyna-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati-adygov-i-rossiyskom-prostranstve-kommemoratsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Магомедов, «Особенности Имамата Дагестана и Чечни как исламской власти (1834–1859 гг.)», <i>Исторический вестник 42 (2022)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-imamata-dagestana-i-chechni-kak-islamskoy-vlasti-1834-1859-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Ч. Джабаева and А. М. Магомедова, «Проблема эмиграции кавказских горцев в середине XIX в.», <i>Вестник науки и образования, no. 6-2 (126) (2022)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-emigratsii-kavkazskih-gortsev-v-seredine-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the dates 1817 to 1864 open to question?","a":"Because they are a convention fixed in Soviet times around General Yermolov's command and the end of organised resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian troops were already fighting in the region from 1801, villages in Dagestan held out until 1872, and a rising in 1877–1878 cost the army more than 2,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the eastern war called the Murid war?","a":"Because its leaders gave it a religious spirit, calling the mountaineers to holy war in the name of a Sufi path of Islam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Shamil the imamate ruled by regulations that blended Islamic law with local custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the war still a sore subject?","a":"Because for the Adyghe it ended in the expulsion of most of their people, and they keep 21 May as a day of memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russian textbooks treat the war as one episode of imperial expansion and leave the forced emigration out, so the memory of the two sides has drifted apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Caucasian War"},
    "ru-429": {"questions":["Besieged on the rock of Akhulgo in 1839, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up his twelve-year-old son to the Russians as a hostage, then escaped when they stormed it.","After ten years of honourable confinement at Kaluga, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was allowed to make the pilgrimage to Mecca in 1870 and died at Medina the following year."],"abstract":"<b>Imam Shamil</b> was the third imam of Dagestan, the leader of the mountaineers of Dagestan and Chechnya in their war against Russia from 1834 until his capture in 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born at Gimry in Dagestan in 1797, he became a close follower of the preacher Kazi Mullah, who spread the teaching of the Murids and called for holy war against the unbelievers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the second imam, Hamzad, was killed in 1834, Shamil was chosen imam that September, on conditions laid down by the scholars and leaders who elected him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He built a state, the imamate, governed through deputies called naibs and regulations that blended Islamic law with local custom, and by 1842 it was said to hold more than three quarters of a million people, every man from sixteen to sixty liable to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1839 he was besieged on the rock of Akhulgo, where he gave up his twelve-year-old son Jamal al-Din as a hostage before slipping away as the Russians stormed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Twenty more years of war followed, until in August 1859 Prince Baryatinsky's troops cornered him in the mountain village of Gunib, where only 50 of his 400 defenders were left alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How he came to leave Gunib is still argued over, for most historians speak of a voluntary surrender while others stress a trick by Colonel Lazarev, who led him out with promises of negotiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2019 a conference backed by the head of Dagestan went further and resolved that Shamil had never been captured but had made peace with Russia, a claim with no basis in the scholarship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was taken to Russia and lived in honourable confinement at Kaluga until 1869, then in Kiev, and in captivity he told a Russian officer that power had been nothing to him but hard labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1870 he was allowed to make the pilgrimage to Mecca, and he died at Medina on 4 February 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 283–89, 337–40, 481–82, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 284–85, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","Ю. У. Дадаев, «Имам Шамиль — руководитель имамата — государства на северо-восточном Кавказе», <i>История, археология и этнография Кавказа, no. 3 (35) (2013)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imam-shamil-rukovoditel-imamata-gosudarstva-na-severo-vostochnom-kavkaze. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. У. Дадаев, «Имам Шамиль: книга в его жизни», <i>История, археология и этнография Кавказа, no. 2 (30) (2012)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imam-shamil-kniga-v-ego-zhizni. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Магомедов, «Особенности Имамата Дагестана и Чечни как исламской власти (1834–1859 гг.)», <i>Исторический вестник 42 (2022)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-imamata-dagestana-i-chechni-kak-islamskoy-vlasti-1834-1859-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. И. Тахнаева, «Пленение имама Шамиля», <i>Восточный архив, no. 2 (48) (2023)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/plenenie-imama-shamilya. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Егорова, «Шамиль в материалах Е. И. Козубского в «Русском архиве»», <i>Вестник Дагестанского государственного университета. Серия 2: Гуманитарные науки, no. 4 (2011)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/shamil-v-materialah-e-i-kozubskogo-v-russkom-arhive. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Shamil hold out for 25 years?","a":"Because he turned rival mountain communities into a single state, the imamate, with deputies in each district and laws that joined Islamic law to local custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every man from sixteen to sixty owed service, and his escapes, fasts and prayers made his followers see him as divinely protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he give up his son at Akhulgo?","a":"Because after weeks of siege the rock was covered with dead and wounded, water and food were short, and further resistance seemed hopeless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian commander demanded the boy as proof of good faith, and Shamil sent him under a white flag, though he later slipped away when the Russians stormed the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his capture at Gunib still debated?","a":"Because the sources allow different readings: most historians speak of a voluntary surrender, while others stress the role of Colonel Lazarev, who promised safety and led him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The question has also become political, and in 2019 an officially backed conference in Dagestan declared that he had not been captured at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Imam Shamil"},
    "ru-430": {"questions":["Russian officials counted about 470,000 Adyghe removed between 1858 and 1865 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a figure that historians in the Adyghe republics consider far too low.","Georgia's parliament voted 90 to none in May 2011 to call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the 1860s a genocide, a term the Russian state rejects in favour of voluntary migration."],"abstract":"The <b>Circassian expulsions</b> were the mass removal of the Circassians, or Adyghe, and their neighbours from the north-western Caucasus to the Ottoman Empire at the end of the Caucasian War, above all in 1863 and 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After 1859 the Russian command concluded that the mountaineers' submission would last only as long as they wished it, and those who refused to resettle on the plains by the Kuban were driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ottoman records show the flow rising from about 17,000 arrivals in 1858–1859 to a total exodus in 1863–1864, which one recent study calls a forced deportation by the Russian military command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Crowds waited on the Black Sea shore for ships with too little food, and hunger and disease killed tens of thousands before they had even left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dying went on in the Ottoman ports, where in the spring and summer of 1864 witnesses reported 200 to 300 deaths a day at Trabzon and 120 to 150 at Samsun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> No side kept a proper count, and the figures depend on who made them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official Russian figure, published by Adolf Berzhe in 1882, was about 470,000 Adyghe removed between 1858 and 1865, while historians in the Adyghe republics argue that the true number was more than twice as high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Estimates of all who left run from about 500,000 to 1.5 million people, and claims of up to 3.5 million also circulate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A British historian of 1908 called the wholesale expatriation of 1864 the last shameful act of a conquest marked by unjust and treacherous dealing, while an American-edited history of 1882 repeated the official view that the emigration had relieved Russia of turbulent elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether it was genocide is disputed, for the Supreme Soviet of Kabardino-Balkaria declared it so in 1992 and the parliaments of Georgia in 2011 and Ukraine in 2025 followed, while the Russian state rejects the term and speaks of voluntary migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), vii–viii, 33, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 285–86, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","Т. Ч. Джабаева and А. М. Магомедова, «Проблема эмиграции кавказских горцев в середине XIX в.», <i>Вестник науки и образования, no. 6-2 (126) (2022)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-emigratsii-kavkazskih-gortsev-v-seredine-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Б. Зубариев, «Причины и обстоятельства мухаджирства части населения Северного Кавказа в Османскую империю в 50–90-е гг. XIX века», <i>Гуманитарные и юридические исследования, no. 2 (2017)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-i-obstoyatelstva-muhadzhirstva-chasti-naseleniya-severnogo-kavkaza-v-osmanskuyu-imperiyu-v-50-90-e-gg-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. В. Чочиев, «Иммиграция, поселение и адаптация северокавказцев в Османской империи во 2-й половине XIX — начале XX в.», <i>Известия СОИГСИ, no. 46 (85) (2022)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/immigratsiya-poselenie-i-adaptatsiya-severokavkaztsev-v-osmanskoy-imperii-vo-2-y-polovine-xix-nachale-hh-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Т. Урушадзе, «Помнить — нельзя — забыть: Кавказская война в исторической памяти адыгов и российском пространстве коммеморации», <i>Политическая наука, no. 3 (2018)</i>, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pomnit-nelzya-zabyt-kavkazskaya-voyna-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati-adygov-i-rossiyskom-prostranstve-kommemoratsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Matthew Kelbaugh, “Ukraine’s and Georgia’s Recognition of the Circassian Genocide: Strategic Engagement with North Caucasian Causes,” <i>Journal of Caucasian Studies</i> 10, no. 16 (2025): 80–82, https://doi.org/10.21488/jocas.1669449. [Open access]","“Georgia Recognizes ‘Circassian Genocide’,” <i>Civil.ge</i>, 20 May 2011, https://civil.ge/archives/185901. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Circassians driven out rather than simply ruled?","a":"Because the Russian command decided after 1859 that the mountaineers' submission would never last on the old terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those who refused to move to the plains by the Kuban were deported to the Ottoman Empire, which one study calls a forced deportation by the Russian military command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the numbers so uncertain?","a":"Because neither Russia nor Turkey kept proper records of the people who left or died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official Russian figure of about 470,000 Adyghe comes from an author writing for the government, while estimates of everyone who left run from 500,000 to 1.5 million and higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the word genocide disputed?","a":"Because the events have become part of present-day politics as well as of history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Circassian organisations and the parliaments of Kabardino-Balkaria, Georgia and Ukraine have used the word, while the Russian state calls the events voluntary migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Georgia's vote of 2011 came after its war with Russia in 2008 and was part of a wider effort to win influence in the North Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Circassian genocide"},
    "ru-431": {"questions":["Exiled Decembrists reduced to the ranks fought in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one of them directed the siege works that brought down the fortress of Erivan in October 1827.","An indemnity of 20 million silver roubles and the resettlement of thousands of Armenian families followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which a treaty signed at Turkmenchay brought to an end."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Persian War of 1826–1828</b> was the last war between the Russian Empire and Qajar Persia for the South Caucasus, opened in July 1826 when a Persian army under the crown prince Abbas Mirza invaded Karabakh without any declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quarrels over the frontier left by the Treaty of Gulistan had poisoned relations, and the Russians heard that mullahs were preaching a holy war and that British officers had entered the shah’s service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scattered Russian detachments were overrun at first and Karabakh was laid waste, but the fortress of Shusha held out for six weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nicholas I sent his favourite, Ivan Paskevich, who in September 1826 routed the main Persian army near Elizavetpol, while the old commander Alexei Yermolov, blamed for his slowness, soon lost his post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sources disagree on the numbers at Elizavetpol, one giving Paskevich about 8,000 men against some 60,000 Persians and another fewer than ten thousand against 44,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 1827 Paskevich took Nakhichevan and the fortress of Abbas-Abad, and in October Erivan fell after a siege directed by Mikhail Pushchin, a Decembrist reduced to the ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A column under Prince Eristov and General Muravyov then entered Tabriz, the seat of Abbas Mirza and a city of 60,000 people, without a fight, and the shah sued for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The treaty was signed at Turkmenchay on 10 February 1828 by the Julian calendar, 22 February by the Gregorian, and it ceded the khanates of Erivan and Nakhichevan, set an indemnity of 20 million silver roubles and recognised Abbas Mirza as heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its 15th article let Persian subjects who wished to move to Russian territory do so within a year, and after Nicholas created an Armenian province in March 1828, 6,946 families, some 35,560 people, were officially settled there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The playwright Alexander Griboyedov, who had helped to draft the treaty, was killed with the Russian mission in Tehran in 1829, but its heavy terms, above all the indemnity, kept Persia from another war with Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 154–76, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","“Traité de Paix et d’Amitié entre Sa Majesté l’Empereur de toutes les Russies et Sa Majesté le Schah de Perse, signé à Tourkmantchai, 10/22 Février 1828,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>Treaties, &c., Concluded between Great Britain and Persia, and between Persia and Other Foreign Powers</i> (London: Butterworths, 1891), 116–23, https://archive.org/details/treatiescconclu00britgoog. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 36–37, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","Э. Г. Вартаньян, «Участие декабристов в войнах России в Закавказье (1826–1829 гг.)», <i>Историческая и социально-образовательная мысль</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchastie-dekabristov-v-voynah-rossii-v-zakavkazie-1826-1829-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Р. Гезалова, «Переселение армян из Каджарского Ирана на Южный Кавказ (1828–1830)», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 501 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pereselenie-armyan-iz-kadzharskogo-irana-na-yuzhnyy-kavkaz-1828-1830. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Ларин, «Туркманчайский мирный договор и формирование новой линии российской политики в Иране», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 5 (116) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/turkmanchayskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-i-formirovanie-novoy-linii-rossiyskoy-politiki-v-irane. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the war begin with Russian defeats?","a":"Because the Persian invasion of July 1826 came without a declaration of war and caught the Russian troops spread in small detachments over a vast country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many posts were overrun, and only the six-week defence of Shusha gave the Russians time to gather their forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Persia make peace soon after Tabriz fell?","a":"Because with Erivan taken and the seat of Abbas Mirza occupied, Persia lay at the mercy of the Russian army and Tehran itself looked open to attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shah signed at Turkmenchay, giving up two khanates and agreeing to pay 20 million silver roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the treaty matter in Armenian history?","a":"Because it allowed Persian subjects who wished to leave to move to Russian territory within a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thousands of Armenian families were then settled in the new province made from the khanates of Erivan and Nakhichevan, which the treaty had ceded to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Persian War (1826–1828)"},
    "ru-432": {"questions":["The capture of Erzurum on the anniversary of Poltava, with the poet Pushkin riding in behind the army, crowned the Asian campaign of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought against the Ottomans.","Disease and a bitter winter cost the Russian army some 40,000 men in the first year of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which nonetheless ended with a favourable peace signed at Adrianople in 1829."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1828–1829</b> was fought between the Russian Empire and the Ottoman Empire on two fronts, along the Danube and across the Balkan mountains in Europe and in the Armenian highlands in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the Greek War of Independence, for after British, French and Russian ships destroyed the Turco-Egyptian fleet at Navarino in 1827, Sultan Mahmud II annulled the Akkerman Convention and proclaimed a holy war aimed above all at Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia declared war in April 1828, and on 7 May its army crossed the Pruth into Moldavia, though it did not pass the Danube until 8 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The campaign of 1828 took Brăila and Varna, but disease, by some accounts the plague, and a hard winter cost the army about 40,000 men by the reckoning of a Prussian officer who studied the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> France, at odds with Britain and Austria, took a broadly friendly line towards Russia throughout the conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1829 General Diebitsch defeated the grand vizier at Kulevcha, took Silistria, crossed the Balkan range and in August entered Adrianople, the second city of the empire, without a fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same officer, Helmuth von Moltke, reckoned that once the many sick were deducted, the main body of the army at Adrianople numbered only about 4,000 or 5,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Asia Paskevich stormed Kars in June 1828, and on 27 June 1829, the anniversary of Poltava, took Erzurum, where the poet Alexander Pushkin rode in as a spectator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study based on Georgian archives puts the losses of Paskevich’s Separate Caucasus Corps, 16,883 men strong, at 4,361, about a quarter of its strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Treaty of Adrianople, signed on 14 September 1829, gave Russia the islands of the Danube delta and Anapa, Poti, Akhaltsikh and Akhalkalaki in Asia, imposed an indemnity, opened the Straits to merchant ships and confirmed the privileges of Moldavia, Wallachia and Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Helmuth von Moltke, <i>The Russians in Bulgaria and Rumelia in 1828 and 1829</i> (London: John Murray, 1854), 57–58, 271, 413–15, https://archive.org/details/russiansinbulgar00molt. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace between Russia and Turkey, signed at Adrianople, 14th September, 1829,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 2 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 812–30, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV2. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 39–41, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","John F. Baddeley, <i>The Russian Conquest of the Caucasus</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1908), 182–86, 219, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028754616. [Open access]","О. В. Жидкова, «Русско-турецкая война 1828–1829 гг. и дипломатия России и Франции», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 3 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1828-1829-gg-i-diplomatiya-rossii-i-frantsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Черкасов, С. Н. Братановский and А. Валлоу, «Потери русской армии в Закавказье в русско-турецкую войну (1828–1829 гг.): историко-статистическое исследование», <i>Русин</i>, no. 4 (50) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poteri-russkoy-armii-v-zakavkazie-v-russko-turetskuyu-voynu-1828-1829-gg-istoriko-statisticheskoe-issledovanie. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the war break out in 1828?","a":"Because the Greek revolt had drawn the powers in, and after the destruction of the Turco-Egyptian fleet at Navarino Mahmud II annulled the Akkerman Convention and declared a holy war aimed chiefly at Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia answered with a declaration of war in April 1828 and sent its army across the Pruth in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the first campaign so costly?","a":"Because the army lost a month before crossing the Danube while the Turks strengthened their fortresses, and then sickness spread through the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A severe winter followed, and by Moltke’s count the army lost about 40,000 men and nearly all its horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the peace matter for the Balkans?","a":"Because it guaranteed the immunities of Moldavia, Wallachia and Serbia and, in a separate treaty, the Porte recognised the independence of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rambaud judged that it prepared the way for the independence of the Romanians and the Serbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1828–1829)"},
    "ru-433": {"questions":["After a revolution in Wallachia drove Prince Bibescu from power in 1848, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suspended the boyars’ assemblies and put appointed councils of notables and senior clergy in their place.","Up to 35,000 Russian soldiers and as many Ottoman ones were to stay in the Danubian Principalities under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which a Russian envoy and two Ottoman ministers signed in 1849."],"abstract":"The <b>Convention of Balta Liman</b> was the agreement signed by Russia and the Ottoman Empire on 1 May 1849 to settle the government of the Danubian Principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia after the revolution of 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its preamble justified joint measures by the commotions that had shaken both provinces, Wallachia above all, and by the need to protect their privileges against revolutionary and anarchical convulsions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For this occasion the hospodars, the governing princes, were to be named by the sultan for seven years instead of being elected in the way laid down by the Organic Statute of 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The boyars’ assemblies were suspended and replaced by councils, or divans, of notables and senior clergy, while commissions at Jassy and Bucharest were to revise the statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 25,000 and 35,000 Russian and as many Ottoman troops were to stay in the provinces, falling to about 10,000 each once the frontiers were quiet, with a Russian and an Ottoman commissioner resident beside the princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The convention was the Danubian end of the part Nicholas I chose in 1848, when he set himself against revolutionary Europe and acted first in the lands nearest to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At home the same alarm produced a secret committee to watch the press, and the government treated the discussion circles of the capital as a political danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Wallachia a revolution forced Prince Bibescu to accept a constitution and then to leave the country in June 1848, and Russia protested at once and urged the sultan to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russian troops had already crossed the Pruth into Moldavia, and after Ottoman forces entered Bucharest on 13 September 1848 by the Julian calendar, Russian troops marched into Wallachia three days later, so that the province lived under two occupying armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Michael Sturdza of Moldavia abdicated rather than accept the terms, and the evacuation that the new Wallachian prince Barbu Știrbei worked for was achieved only in 1851.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Act between Russia and Turkey, relative to Moldavia and Wallachia, signed at Balta-Liman, 1st May, 1849,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 2 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 1089–93, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV2. [Open access]","E. C. Blech, “Russia and the Levant: The Levant,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 283–84, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 80–81, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","А. С. Стыкалин, «Дунайские княжества и Трансильвания глазами русских военных наблюдателей (1848–1849 гг.): по документам Российского государственного военно-исторического архива», <i>Славяне и Россия</i>, no. 1 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dunayskie-knyazhestva-i-transilvaniya-glazami-russkih-voennyh-nablyudateley-1848-1849-gg-po-dokumentam-rossiyskogo-gosudarstvennogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 301–04, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the princes appointed rather than elected?","a":"Because the convention blamed the disorders of 1848 on the old arrangements and set aside, for this occasion, the election laid down in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Naming the princes for seven years, with Russian and Ottoman commissioners beside them, let both courts keep close control of the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Ottoman troops in Bucharest before the Russians?","a":"Because Russia, which had protested against the Wallachian constitution, at first pressed the sultan to restore order himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Ottoman forces entered Bucharest in September 1848, Nicholas did not want Ottoman influence to grow at Russia’s expense and sent his own troops into Wallachia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Moldavian prince abdicate?","a":"Because Michael Sturdza, who had kept his office through the troubles, refused the terms that made the princes seven-year appointees under joint supervision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was succeeded in Moldavia by Gregory Ghica, while Barbu Știrbei became prince of Wallachia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Convention of Balta Liman"},
    "ru-434": {"questions":["Thirteen Hungarian generals were executed at Arad in October 1849 after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though Russian intercession saved the commander who had chosen to give himself up to the tsar’s army.","A Russian division carried by rail from Kraków in May 1849 opened the intervention that ended with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Count Rüdiger inspected the disarmed Hungarian ranks at dusk."],"abstract":"The <b>Surrender at Világos</b> was the capitulation of the main Hungarian revolutionary army under Artúr Görgei to a Russian corps under Count Rüdiger on 13 August 1849, which ended the Hungarian war of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Görgei, who had taken supreme power when Lajos Kossuth left Arad, offered an absolute surrender on condition that it was made before Russian troops only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told Rüdiger that he would rather see his whole corps destroyed in a desperate battle than capitulate unconditionally to the Austrians, in a letter received on 12 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the 13th the army, 23,000 men with 11 generals, 1,400 officers and 129 guns, laid down its arms near Világos, and that evening Rüdiger inspected the disarmed ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Görgei recorded the cavalry dismounted with swords hung on their saddles, the muskets piled in pyramids and the flags lying unprotected before the men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Russia had entered the war at the request of Francis Joseph, and Nicholas I’s manifesto blamed the revolt on Polish traitors of 1831 and other exiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even before the main forces moved, Paskevich sent a division of up to 15,000 men with 48 guns by rail from Kraków to Moravia in May 1849 to help cover Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then crossed the Carpathians by the Dukla pass with the main army, given by one account as 130,000 men, while General Lüders invaded Transylvania with more than 50,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paskevich wrote that Hungary lay at the tsar’s feet, and by the tsar’s order the prisoners were handed over to the Austrian commander Haynau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 6 October 1849 thirteen Hungarian generals were executed at Arad, while Görgei was spared through Russian intercession, credited by one account to the tsar and Grand Duke Constantine and by another to the heir, Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur Görgei, <i>My Life and Acts in Hungary in the Years 1848 and 1849</i> (New York: Harper, 1852), 603–08, https://archive.org/details/mylifeandactsinh00gorg. [Open access]","A. W. Ward, “The Revolution and the Reaction in Germany and Austria, II,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 212–14, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 81–84, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","А. С. Стыкалин and А. И. Колин, «Сдача революционной венгерской армии русским войскам под Вилагошем в августе 1849 г.: воспоминания очевидца», <i>Славянский альманах</i> 2013 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sdacha-revolyutsionnoy-vengerskoy-armii-russkim-voyskam-pod-vilagoshem-v-avguste-1849-g-vospominaniya-ochevidtsa. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Рошонци, «Роль железнодорожного сообщения в оказании Россией военной помощи Австрии в мае 1849 г.», <i>Славянский мир в третьем тысячелетии</i>, no. 3–4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-zheleznodorozhnogo-soobscheniya-v-okazanii-rossiey-voennoy-pomoschi-avstrii-v-mae-1849-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Черемных, «Статья Г. В. Вернадского “Венгерский поход 1849 года”», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (50) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/statya-g-v-vernadskogo-vengerskiy-pohod-1849-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Görgei surrender to the Russians rather than the Austrians?","a":"Because he wrote that he would rather see his corps destroyed than capitulate unconditionally to Austrian troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He offered an absolute surrender on condition that only Russian troops were present, and appealed to the tsar’s generosity for the former Austrian officers in his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia send an army into Hungary?","a":"Because Francis Joseph asked for help, and Nicholas announced that he would crush a revolution in which, he claimed, Polish exiles of 1831 were usurping power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An Austrian envoy had begged Paskevich in Warsaw to save Austria while its army was being beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the surrender not save the Hungarian officers?","a":"Because, by the tsar’s order, the prisoners were handed over to the Austrian commander Haynau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 490 officers who had once held Austrian commissions were tried at Arad, and on 6 October 1849 thirteen generals were shot or hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Surrender at Világos"},
    "ru-435": {"questions":["Tied to posts on Semyonovsky Square in December 1849 and reprieved at the last moment, the young Dostoevsky was one of the members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who heard a death sentence read.","Called a conspiracy of ideas even by an official who disliked it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost its host a sentence of penal servitude for life at Nerchinsk in eastern Siberia."],"abstract":"The <b>Petrashevsky Circle</b> was the group of young men, officers and writers among them, who gathered on Fridays from 1845 at the St Petersburg home of Mikhail Butashevich-Petrashevsky, a follower of the French socialist Charles Fourier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The meetings were held in his family’s house on Pokrovskaya Square in the Kolomna district, and Fyodor Dostoevsky probably first attended in November 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Guests discussed the ideas of the French utopian socialists, the backwardness of Russian institutions and the freeing of the serfs, along with juries, open courts and freedom of the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The circle was divided over the peasant question, from those who hoped for reform from above to Nikolai Speshnev, who called himself a communist and looked to a peasant revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smaller circles met at the homes of Sergei Durov and others, and a member of Durov’s group later wrote that it had no revolutionary plans and no written statutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The revolutions of 1848 alarmed the government, which set up a secret committee to watch the press and treated the circles of the capital as a political danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dostoevsky, accused among other things of reading Belinsky’s forbidden letter to Gogol aloud, was arrested with other members on 23 April 1849 and held in the Peter and Paul Fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 December 1849 the condemned were taken to Semyonovsky Square, where the death sentence was read and the first three were tied to posts before the tsar’s pardon was announced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> 21 men had been sentenced to death, and in the event Petrashevsky was sent to penal servitude at Nerchinsk for life and Speshnev for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even Baron Korf, who disliked the circle, called the affair a conspiracy of ideas, since nothing but talk and reading could be proved against its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 301–06, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Fyodor Dostoevsky, letter to his brother Michael, 22 December 1849, in <i>Letters of Fyodor Michailovitch Dostoevsky to His Family and Friends</i>, trans. Ethel Colburn Mayne (New York: Macmillan, 1914), xiii, 52–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924026648547. [Open access]","A. P. Milyukov, “Recollections of Dostoevsky,” in <i>Letters of Fyodor Michailovitch Dostoevsky to His Family and Friends</i>, trans. Ethel Colburn Mayne (New York: Macmillan, 1914), 271–73, https://archive.org/details/cu31924026648547. [Open access]","А. В. Дулов and А. А. Иванов, «Петрашевцы в Восточной Сибири», <i>Вестник Иркутского государственного технического университета</i>, no. 9 (68) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petrashevtsy-v-vostochnoy-sibiri. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Долгих, «Обсуждение “крестьянского вопроса” в кружке петрашевцев», <i>Гуманитарные исследования Центральной России</i>, no. 2 (23) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obsuzhdenie-krestyanskogo-voprosa-v-kruzhke-petrashevtsev. [in Russian] [Open access]","Б. Н. Тихомиров, «Петербург Достоевского-петрашевца: опыт построения справочника-путеводителя», <i>Неизвестный Достоевский</i>, no. 3 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/peterburg-dostoevskogo-petrashevtsa-opyt-postroeniya-spravochnika-putevoditelya. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. М. Дробышевская and Б. Н. Тихомиров, «Майор или генерал-майор? К биографии петрашевца Николая Момбелли», <i>Неизвестный Достоевский</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mayor-ili-general-mayor-k-biografii-petrashevtsa-nikolaya-mombelli. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government treat a discussion group as a conspiracy?","a":"Because the revolutions of 1848 had alarmed it, and it exaggerated the political danger of the circles in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The members were declared state criminals, and 21 of them were sentenced to death before the sentences were commuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the mock execution staged?","a":"Because the authorities wanted to frighten the condemned by making them go through the preparations for death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dostoevsky described the death sentence read, the cross to kiss, a dagger broken over their heads and the white shirts before the reprieve was read out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the members argue about most?","a":"Most often the peasant question, together with juries, open courts and the censorship, the very problems the Great Reforms took up a few years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On serfdom they divided between reform from above and Speshnev’s hope of a peasant revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Petrashevsky Circle"},
    "ru-436": {"questions":["Fought in the Baltic, the White Sea and Kamchatka as well as in the Caucasus, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was decided in the Crimea, where an Anglo-French army besieged the naval base of Sevastopol.","Remembered by later tsars as a heroic defence rather than a defeat, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exposed the failings of Nicholas I’s system and opened the way to the reforms of his son."],"abstract":"The <b>Crimean War</b> of 1853–1856 was fought by Russia against the Ottoman Empire, Britain, France and, from 1855, Piedmont-Sardinia, and it is often called the Eastern War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of a quarrel between France and Russia over the custody of the Holy Places in Palestine and out of Nicholas I’s demands on the Ottoman Empire, pressed by Prince Menshikov’s embassy in 1853.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 21 June 1853 by the Julian calendar, 3 July by the Gregorian, Russian troops entered the Danubian Principalities without a declaration of war, and the Ottoman Empire declared war in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The destruction of an Ottoman squadron at Sinop in November 1853 turned British and French opinion against Russia, and the two powers entered the war in 1854.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting ran round the whole edge of the empire, in the Caucasus, on the Danube, in the Baltic and the White Sea and at Kamchatka, but the decisive theatre was the Crimea, where the allies landed in September 1854 to destroy the naval base of Sevastopol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After their victory on the Alma the allies besieged Sevastopol for almost a year, and Nicholas I died in 1855 before it fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French took the Malakhov on 8 September 1855 and the Russians withdrew across the harbour in good order, while in the Caucasus General Muravyov forced Kars to surrender that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the Treaty of Paris of 30 March 1856 Russia returned Kars, gave up a strip of Bessarabia and accepted the neutralisation of the Black Sea, closed to all warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Losses are uncertain, for one modern estimate gives direct losses of 134,000 for Russia and 162,000 for its enemies, while an older British reckoning that counted disease put all deaths at about 600,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defeat exposed the weaknesses of the army and the bureaucracy and made reform look inevitable, yet the defence of Sevastopol later became a symbol of national heroism in Russian memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Spencer Walpole, “Great Britain and the Crimean War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 312–24, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 133–34, 196–97, 202–06, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","“General Treaty of Peace between Great Britain, Austria, France, Prussia, Russia, Sardinia, and Turkey, signed at Paris, 30th March, 1856,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 2 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 1250–65, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV2. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 308–10, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]","Е. И. Зеленев, «Война в Крыму и на Дунайском фронте: с кем и за что сражалась русская армия в 1853–1856 годах?», <i>Проблемы востоковедения</i>, no. 4 (66) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-v-krymu-i-na-dunayskom-fronte-s-kem-i-za-chto-srazhalas-russkaya-armiya-v-1853-1856-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Павленко, «Крымская война в исторической памяти Российской империи на рубеже XIX–XX вв.», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Политология. История. Международные отношения</i>, no. 18 (140) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krymskaya-voyna-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-rubezhe-xix-xx-vv-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","William Howard Russell, <i>The British Expedition to the Crimea</i> (London: Routledge, 1877), 361–62, https://archive.org/details/britishexpediti00russ. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant: Russia under Nicholas I,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 263, 266, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a dispute over churches in Palestine become a European war?","a":"Because the quarrel over the Holy Places was bound up with Nicholas I’s pressure on the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Russian troops occupied the Danubian Principalities and then destroyed an Ottoman squadron at Sinop, Britain and France joined the Ottomans against Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the allies attack the Crimea?","a":"Because they wanted to destroy the great arsenal of Sevastopol, which they saw as a standing threat to the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fall in September 1855 came only after almost a year of siege, and the garrison withdrew to the north side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a defeat become a source of pride?","a":"Because the governments of Alexander III and Nicholas II built a heroic story of a nation that had not been broken, and celebrated the war’s fiftieth anniversary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Already in 1855 the orders on the loss of Sevastopol set it beside the memory of Borodino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crimean War"},
    "ru-437": {"questions":["Nikolai Pirogov sorted the wounded by the gravity of their injuries and brought the first Russian sisters of mercy to the front during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended in September 1855.","The French capture of the Malakhov on 8 September 1855 ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as the Russian garrison crossed a floating bridge to the north side behind burning forts."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Sevastopol</b> of 1854–1855 was the allied siege of Russia’s main Black Sea naval base in the Crimea, lasting from the first bombardment on 17 October 1854 until the Russians gave up the south side of the city in September 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Alma the allies marched round to attack from the south, but instead of storming at once they waited three weeks to bring up their guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To bar the harbour the Russians scuttled ships of the Black Sea Fleet across its entrance on 11 September 1854 by the Julian calendar, and their guns and some 18,000 sailors went into the land defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The engineer Eduard Todleben used the delay to raise the earthworks of the Flagstaff, the two Redans and the Malakhov, which held the allies for nearly a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admirals Kornilov, Istomin and Nakhimov led the defence and all three died on the Malakhov, Kornilov on the first day of the bombardment and Nakhimov in July 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The surgeon Nikolai Pirogov arrived in November 1854 with the sisters of mercy of the Exaltation of the Cross, the first women to nurse a Russian army’s wounded, and introduced sorting the wounded by the gravity of their injuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 87,500 Russian soldiers were wounded or concussed during the defence, and on days of heavy bombardment the hospitals received 4,000 to 5,000 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 8 September 1855 the French stormed the Malakhov while the British were twice thrown back from the Great Redan, and that evening Prince Gorchakov ordered the south side abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the night the army crossed a floating bridge to the north side as the forts were blown up and the last ships sunk, a retreat that even the British correspondent William Russell called masterly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander II’s order on the loss promised that the names of Sevastopol and its defenders would live for ever in Russian hearts, and Tolstoy, who served there, wrote that its heroes were the Russian people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 160–63, 188–89, 194–97, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","Spencer Walpole, “Great Britain and the Crimean War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 317–22, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","William Howard Russell, <i>The British Expedition to the Crimea</i> (London: Routledge, 1877), 361–62, https://archive.org/details/britishexpediti00russ. [Open access]","Г. А. Гребенщикова, «Затопление судов Черноморского флота на рейде Севастополя в 1854–1855 гг.: вынужденная необходимость или военное преступление?», <i>Наука. Общество. Оборона</i> 14, no. 1 (46) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zatoplenie-sudov-chernomorskogo-flota-na-reyde-sevastopolya-v-1854-1855-gg-vynuzhdennaya-neobhodimost-ili-voennoe-prestuplenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Рыбак, «К истории организации медицинского обеспечения в период первой обороны Севастополя (1854–1855 гг.)», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-organizatsii-meditsinskogo-obespecheniya-v-period-pervoy-oborony-sevastopolya-1854-1855-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Павленко, «Крымская война в исторической памяти Российской империи на рубеже XIX–XX вв.», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Политология. История. Международные отношения</i>, no. 18 (140) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krymskaya-voyna-v-istoricheskoy-pamyati-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-rubezhe-xix-xx-vv-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leo Tolstoy, <i>Sebastopol</i>, trans. Frank D. Millet (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1887), 44–45, https://archive.org/details/sebastopol00tols. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the city not fall in the autumn of 1854?","a":"Because the allies, instead of attacking at once, waited three weeks to bring up guns for a bombardment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Todleben used the time to raise earthworks, and the damage done by day was repaired at night under the enemy’s fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Malakhov so important?","a":"Because it was an earthen citadel on a hill that commanded the whole Karabelnaya suburb and threatened the bridge of boats, the defenders’ only way of retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once the French held it, the Russians gave up the south side even though they had beaten off the attacks elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians sink their own ships?","a":"Because the Black Sea Fleet was heavily outnumbered by the allied fleets, and ships sunk across the entrance would stop the enemy forcing the roadstead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their guns and crews were then turned to the defence of the city on land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Siege of Sevastopol (1854–1855)"},
    "ru-438": {"questions":["Reported in London as the Massacre of Sinope, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned British opinion against Russia and helped bring Britain and France into the war against the tsar in 1854.","Osman Pasha was taken prisoner and some 180 fortress guns were wrecked at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a naval action fought on the Black Sea coast of Anatolia in late November 1853."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Sinop</b> was fought on 30 November 1853, 18 November by the Russian calendar, when a Russian squadron under Vice-Admiral Pavel Nakhimov destroyed an Ottoman squadron in the harbour of Sinop on the Black Sea coast of Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ottoman ships, seven frigates, three corvettes and two steamers by one count, had put into the open roadstead in foul weather while carrying supplies for the troops at Batumi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nakhimov brought six ships of the line and two frigates with some 312 guns against about 262 on the Ottoman side, and his 44 shell-firing guns far outnumbered the Ottomans’ five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The action began about half past one, and by five o’clock nothing remained of the Ottoman squadron except the steamer Taif, which fought its way out and escaped to Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ottomans lost every warship and three to four thousand men, Osman Pasha was taken prisoner, and two Egyptian ships sent to help the sultan were among those sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For Russia it was a legitimate blow in a war that the Ottomans had declared in October, and Nakhimov had ordered his captains to spare the consular houses flying their national flags.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Times reported it on 12 December 1853 as the Massacre of Sinope and called Nakhimov the Bear-Admiral, and its treatment of Russia, restrained until then, turned hostile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The attack was apparently Russia’s answer to the British and French decision in September to send their fleets through the Dardanelles, a blow struck before reinforcements could intervene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A later British account complained that the allied fleets could easily have come up from Besika Bay, but that the Ottoman admiral’s appeal for help was overlooked at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The news made the war party in Britain almost irresistible, and within months Britain and France had entered the war against Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 133–35, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","Spencer Walpole, “Great Britain and the Crimean War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 315, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","В. И. Шеремет, «Синоп 1853: новое прочтение», <i>Восточный архив</i>, no. 17 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sinop-1853-novoe-prochtenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Ю. Бобков, «Освещение Крымской войны в газете “The Times” (ноябрь–декабрь 1853 г.)», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 10 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osveschenie-krymskoy-voyny-v-gazete-the-times-noyabr-dekabr-1853-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. И. Зеленев, «Война в Крыму и на Дунайском фронте: с кем и за что сражалась русская армия в 1853–1856 годах?», <i>Проблемы востоковедения</i>, no. 4 (66) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-v-krymu-i-na-dunayskom-fronte-s-kem-i-za-chto-srazhalas-russkaya-armiya-v-1853-1856-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mrs Tom Kelly, <i>From the Fleet in the Fifties: A History of the Crimean War</i> (London: Hurst and Blackett, 1902), 7, https://archive.org/details/fromfleetinfift00simpgoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Russian victory so complete?","a":"Because Nakhimov’s squadron carried far more guns, and many of them fired explosive shells that set the Ottoman wooden ships and the town ablaze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a few hours all but one Ottoman ship had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain and France call it a massacre?","a":"Because the disproportion of forces and the total ruin of the enemy shocked opinion, even though Russia was within its rights in attacking an enemy convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Times used the phrase Massacre of Sinope and from then on wrote of Russia with open hostility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Ottoman squadron not escape?","a":"Because Osman Pasha, sheltering from bad weather, spent three days strengthening his position instead of returning to the Bosphorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His appeal for help from the allied fleets was overlooked at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Sinop"},
    "ru-439": {"questions":["Russia gave back Kars and recovered Sevastopol and the other occupied Crimean towns under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the peace that ended the Crimean War in the spring of 1856.","Gorchakov’s repudiation of the Black Sea clauses during the Franco-Prussian War undid the harshest part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a change confirmed by a London convention in 1871."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1856 was the peace that ended the Crimean War, signed on 30 March 1856 by Britain, Austria, France, Prussia, Russia, Sardinia and the Ottoman Empire after a congress held in Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia, represented by Count Alexei Orlov and Baron Philipp Brunnow, had accepted the allies’ terms in January 1856 after an Austrian ultimatum, and the fighting stopped under an armistice while the congress sat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The powers admitted the Ottoman Empire to the public law and concert of Europe and promised to respect its independence and territory, while the sultan’s decree on his Christian subjects gave no power a right to interfere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Danube was opened to the shipping of all nations under European commissions, and Moldavia, Wallachia and Serbia kept their privileges under the sultan with a guarantee from all the signatories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia returned Kars and the other Ottoman land it held, and in exchange the allies gave back Sevastopol, Balaklava, Eupatoria, Kerch, Kinburn and the other places they had occupied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The clauses that hurt Russia most made the Black Sea neutral, open to merchant ships of every nation but closed to warships, and forbade both Russia and the Ottomans to keep naval arsenals on its coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A separate convention limited each of the two empires to six steamers of 50 metres and 800 tons and four light vessels of up to 200 tons in that sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Russia also lost a strip of southern Bessarabia of about 10,000 square kilometres, handed to Moldavia to keep Russia from the Danube, and the transfer was completed only in March 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The French historian Alfred Rambaud judged that the treaty took from Russia both control of the Black Sea and the protectorate of the Eastern Christians, undoing the work of two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Freeing Russia from the Black Sea clauses became the first aim of its foreign minister Alexander Gorchakov, who repudiated them during France’s defeat by Prussia in 1870, a change confirmed in London on 13 March 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“General Treaty of Peace between Great Britain, Austria, France, Prussia, Russia, Sardinia, and Turkey, signed at Paris, 30th March, 1856,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 2 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 1250–65, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV2. [Open access]","Spencer Walpole, “Great Britain and the Crimean War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 322–24, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 202–06, 209–10, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","Е. И. Зеленев, «Война в Крыму и на Дунайском фронте: с кем и за что сражалась русская армия в 1853–1856 годах?», <i>Проблемы востоковедения</i>, no. 4 (66) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-v-krymu-i-na-dunayskom-fronte-s-kem-i-za-chto-srazhalas-russkaya-armiya-v-1853-1856-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Зварцев, «Борьба российского дипломатического представительства с “Крымской системой” во второй половине XIX века», <i>Вестник науки</i> 1, no. 9 (54) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-rossiyskogo-diplomaticheskogo-predstavitelstva-s-krymskoy-sistemoy-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Владислав Гросул, «Парижский конгресс 1856 г. и формирование новой юго-западной границы России», <i>Русин</i>, no. 2 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/parizhskiy-kongress-1856-g-i-formirovanie-novoy-yugo-zapadnoy-granitsy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","“Convention between Russia and Turkey, limiting their Naval Force in the Black Sea, signed at Paris, 30th March, 1856,” in Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 2 (London: Butterworths, 1875), 1269–71, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Black Sea clauses matter so much to Russia?","a":"Because they barred warships and naval arsenals from its own southern coast, leaving it only a handful of light vessels there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rambaud wrote that they destroyed the fruits of the policy pursued since Peter the Great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia have to give up part of Bessarabia?","a":"Because the allies, Austria above all, insisted that returning Kars was not enough in exchange for Sevastopol and wanted Russia kept away from the Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 10,000 square kilometres of southern Bessarabia went to Moldavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Black Sea settlement not last?","a":"Because overturning its restrictions became Russia’s chief diplomatic aim, and Gorchakov seized the moment of France’s defeat in 1870.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The neutrality of the Black Sea was kept only until then, and the related articles were abrogated in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Paris (1856)"},
    "ru-440": {"questions":["Cut so heavily by the censors that its second part appeared unsigned under another title, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> declared that the only hero of its story was the truth.","Tolstoy’s three reports from the bastions, gathered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, brought home to Russian readers a war that exposed the weakness of the army and the bureaucracy."],"abstract":"The <b>Sevastopol Sketches</b> are three short works by Leo Tolstoy drawn from his service in the besieged city, Sevastopol in December, Sevastopol in May and Sevastopol in August 1855, published in 1855 and 1856.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tolstoy, then a young artillery officer, had planned a military journal with fellow officers, and when it came to nothing he sent his work to Nikolai Nekrasov’s magazine <i>The Contemporary</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first sketch leads the reader through the town, the hospitals and the bastions and ends by saying that Russia would long keep the traces of an epoch whose heroes were the Russian people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second was so cut by the censors that it appeared under the title A Night in Spring 1855 in Sevastopol and without Tolstoy’s signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It closes by asking where the hero of the tale is, and answers that it is none of the officers, all of whom are both good and bad, but truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Contemporary readers felt the force of that truth, one review noting that Tolstoy roused admiration without a single rapturous phrase, and the American novelist William Dean Howells later wrote that Tolstoy’s books seemed the very truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tolstoy also wrote a satirical soldiers’ song on the failed Russian attack on the Chernaya in August 1855, whose ending struck at the commanders and even at the new tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war itself showed that the army, for which everything had been sacrificed, could not live up to the memory of 1812, and that the bureaucracy was riddled with sloth, incompetence and theft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russia had entered it with only some 1,020 kilometres (632 miles) of railway, from St Petersburg to Tsarskoye Selo and Moscow and from Warsaw to the frontier, and paper money stopped being convertible into coin during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> To the historian Alexander Kornilov the wounds that the Crimean campaign revealed made an epoch of reforms look inevitable, a task that fell to Alexander II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Leo Tolstoy, <i>Sebastopol</i>, trans. Frank D. Millet (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1887), 15–45, 121–22, https://archive.org/details/sebastopol00tols. [Open access]","William Dean Howells, “Leo Tolstoi,” introduction to Leo Tolstoy, <i>Sebastopol</i>, trans. Frank D. Millet (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1887), 5–7, https://archive.org/details/sebastopol00tols. [Open access]","В. Г. Андреева, «“Человек создан быть опорой другому, потому что ему самому нужна опора”: переписка Л. Н. Толстого и Н. А. Некрасова», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Российская и зарубежная филология</i> 15, no. 3 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chelovek-sozdan-byt-oporoy-drugomu-potomu-chto-emu-samomu-nuzhna-opora-perepiska-l-n-tolstogo-i-n-a-nekrasova. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Курьянова, «Роль художественного домысла в военной корреспонденции Л. Н. Толстого», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Филологические науки</i> 2 (68), no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-hudozhestvennogo-domysla-v-voennoy-korrespondentsii-l-n-tolstogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. И. Щербакова, «Севастопольские рассказы Л. Н. Толстого как документ эпохи», <i>Два века русской классики</i> 5, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sevastopolskie-rasskazy-l-n-tolstogo-kak-dokument-epohi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant: Russia under Nicholas I,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909; repr. 1921), 263, 266, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.107365. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 1 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1916), 308–10, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis01korniala. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Tolstoy send his war writing to a literary magazine?","a":"Because his plan for a journal written by officers had come to nothing, and he offered his material to Nekrasov instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>The Contemporary</i>, which had already printed his first stories, published the sketches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Tolstoy call truth his hero?","a":"Because he refused to make any officer the hero, for the brave, the vain and the timid were all both good and bad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He placed the truth of the war not in the trenches and guns but in the spirit of the defenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the war reveal about Nicholas I’s Russia?","a":"That the army could not repeat the glory of 1812 and that the bureaucracy was full of sloth, incompetence and theft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Railways were almost absent, and the wounds the campaign revealed made reform seem unavoidable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sevastopol Sketches"},
    "ru-441": {"questions":["Killed by a second bomb on the Catherine Canal in St Petersburg in March 1881, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was mourned in official speeches as both the Tsar-Liberator and the Tsar-Martyr.","Tutored as a boy by the poet Zhukovsky, the heir who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1855 nevertheless grew up loving parades and sharing his father's conservative views on the serfs."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander II</b> was emperor of Russia from March 1855 until his murder in March 1881, the son and heir of Nicholas I and the ruler whose reign is remembered above all for the abolition of serfdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His tutor, the poet Vasily Zhukovsky, hoped to make him an enlightened monarch rather than a drill-ground soldier, but the boy came to love parades and, as heir, shared his father's conservative views, defending the landowners' interests in committees on the peasant question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne in the middle of the Crimean War, whose defeats exposed how badly the system he inherited had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In March 1856 he told the nobles of Moscow that it was better to abolish serfdom from above than to wait until it began to abolish itself from below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At his coronation that year he suspended recruiting for three years and granted an amnesty that included the surviving Decembrist conspirators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His decisive act came on 19 February 1861 by the Julian calendar, 3 March by the Gregorian, when he signed the law that freed more than twenty million serfs, although the manifesto announcing it was held back for a fortnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Elected local councils followed in 1864, new law courts in the same year, elected town councils in 1870 and universal liability to military service in 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As revolutionary groups grew he turned to repression, and after several attempts on his life a second bomb thrown on the Catherine Canal in St Petersburg on 1 March 1881 by the Julian calendar, 13 March by the Gregorian, shattered his legs and he died within hours at the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official mourning added the name of Tsar-Martyr to that of Tsar-Liberator, and some clergy and believers even called for his canonisation, which never came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Biographers still disagree whether he was a weak and vacillating ruler or a firm one who pushed the emancipation through against strong resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 2–5, 10–11, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 613–15, 620, 624, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 147–48, 165, 177, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 1 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 164–67, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut01kropuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 382–86, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","О. Б. Леонтьева, «Два века императора: Александр II в зеркале исторических биографий», <i>Вестник Самарского университета. История, педагогика, филология</i> 24, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dva-veka-imperatora-aleksandr-ii-v-zerkale-istoricheskih-biografiy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. А. Сафронова, «Царь-Мученик vs Царь-Освободитель. Почему не канонизировали Александра II?», <i>Вестник Свято-Филаретовского института</i>, no. 37 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsar-muchenik-vs-tsar-osvoboditel-pochemu-ne-kanonizirovali-aleksandra-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a conservative heir become the emperor who freed the serfs?","a":"Because the defeats of the Crimean War showed how badly his father's system had failed, and he concluded that it could not go on unchanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peasants had already revolted in nine provinces during the war, and he warned the Moscow nobles that abolition from above was better than waiting for it to come from below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the emancipation manifesto kept back after it was signed?","a":"Because it was signed just before carnival week, and officials feared that the drinking in the villages during the carnival might turn the news into peasant uprisings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was finally read out on the last Sunday of the carnival, a fortnight after the signing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is he called both Tsar-Liberator and Tsar-Martyr?","a":"Because he freed the serfs in 1861 and was killed by revolutionaries in 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a speech the day after the killing one official declared that the name of Tsar-Martyr had been joined to that of Tsar-Liberator, and some churchmen began to speak of him almost as a saint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander II of Russia"},
    "ru-442": {"questions":["Provincial nobles who petitioned for local self-government and trial by jury were reprimanded for insubordination, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> granted much of what they had asked a few years later.","Leaving the autocracy itself untouched while creating elected councils, public courts and universal military service, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are read by some historians as a cautious and limited modernisation."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Reforms</b> were the series of laws with which the government of Alexander II remade the institutions of the Russian Empire in the 1860s and 1870s, beginning with the abolition of serfdom in 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The emancipation ended the landowners' police power over their peasants, the base on which provincial administration had rested, so new institutions had to be built in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In January 1864 elected district and provincial councils called zemstvos were created, and by 1876 they had been brought into force in 34 provinces of European Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In November 1864 the law courts were rebuilt on Western models, with judges who could not be removed at will, public oral trials, a professional bar and juries for serious crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Elected town councils followed under the Municipal Statute of 16 June 1870, and from 1 January 1874 every man became liable to military service whatever his estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Several of these measures were asked for before they were granted, for while the emancipation law was being drafted some provincial assemblies of nobles told the emperor that the time had come for local self-government, public courts and trial by jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government treated the request as insubordination, since all initiative was supposed to come from the throne, yet it carried out the same reforms itself a few years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reforms were cautious, for a governor could veto zemstvo decisions, the town franchise favoured the largest taxpayers and the unlimited power of the emperor was left untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even so, contemporaries saw the abolition of serfdom as the act that made all the later reforms possible, because the old class-based administration could not outlive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians still divide between a pessimistic reading, which stresses how much traditional authority the reforms preserved, and an optimistic one, which sees them as a turning point towards civil society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 620–24, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 53–54, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 164–65, 233–34, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 2, <i>The Institutions</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1894), 329–30, 349, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus02leroiala. [Open access]","П. А. Сперанский, «Великие реформы в современной российской историографии: пессимистическая и оптимистическая модели», <i>История и современное мировоззрение</i> 6, no. 2 (2024): 148–53, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikie-reformy-v-sovremennoy-rossiyskoy-istoriografii-pessimisticheskaya-i-optimisticheskaya-modeli. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Тимофеев, ««Положение о губернских и уездных земских учреждениях» от 1 января 1864 г. как правовая основа проведения земской реформы в России», <i>Вестник Российского университета кооперации</i>, no. 1 (15) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polozhenie-o-gubernskih-i-uezdnyh-zemskih-uchrezhdeniyah-ot-1-yanvarya-1864-g-kak-pravovaya-osnova-provedeniya-zemskoy-reformy-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Ю. Бурдина, «Подготовка реформы городского самоуправления в России (1862–1870 гг.)», <i>Самарский научный вестник</i>, no. 2 (7) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/podgotovka-reformy-gorodskogo-samoupravleniya-v-rossii-1862-1870-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did freeing the serfs force other reforms?","a":"Because under serfdom the landowner had acted as the state's unpaid policeman on his estate, and the emancipation abolished that authority for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Something had to take its place, and the government preferred elected local bodies open to all classes to a self-government of the nobles alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were nobles punished for asking for reforms the government later made?","a":"Because the request broke the principle that every initiative in public affairs must come from the autocratic power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some marshals of the nobility were reprimanded or deposed and two leaders were exiled, even though local self-government, public courts and juries were all introduced within a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about the reforms?","a":"Because the reforms changed a great deal and left a great deal alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They created elected councils, independent courts and a bar, yet the governor's veto, a town franchise weighted towards wealth and the unlimited power of the emperor all remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Government reforms of Alexander II of Russia"},
    "ru-443": {"questions":["Expected by many nobles merely to sort the provincial committees' projects, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead wrote a law of their own and drowned the deputies, as members boasted, in ink.","Nikolay Milyutin, whom the chairman called their Egeria, led the reforming majority of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> through about twenty months of work that ended in October 1860."],"abstract":"The <b>Editing Commissions</b> were the body that drafted the statutes of 19 February 1861 abolishing serfdom in Russia, set up in February 1859 and opened on 4 March 1859 by the Julian calendar under General Rostovtsev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their task was to examine the projects sent in by the provincial committees of nobles and to work out general and local statutes from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Formally there were two commissions, one for a general project and one for local ones, but Rostovtsev merged them and divided the work among administrative, juridical and economic departments, to which a financial department was later added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their members were officials from the ministries concerned and so-called expert members, landowners who had attracted attention by their work in the provincial committees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikolay Milyutin chaired the economic and financial departments and led the reformers, among them Samarin and Prince Vladimir Cherkassky, against opponents such as Count Shuvalov, marshal of the nobility of St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Many nobles had expected the body merely to arrange their committees' proposals, but it used them as a storehouse and wrote a law of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its instructions declared that the law should end the landowner's authority at once and help the peasants to become owners of the land they needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deputies of the provincial committees summoned to St Petersburg were refused a public assembly and told to answer printed questions in writing, and those who protested were reprimanded through the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Rostovtsev died on 6 February 1860, the conservative minister of justice Count Panin took the chair and tried to change the drafts in the landowners' favour, but Milyutin, Samarin and their allies resisted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commissions closed on 10 October 1860 after 409 sittings in about twenty months, having drafted sixteen acts that the Main Committee and the State Council then passed without fundamental change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 31–33, 38, 41–43, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 159–65, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 615, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Н. Г. Карнишина, «Реформы просвещенной бюрократии в России на примере работы Редакционных комиссий 1850-х гг.», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Поволжский регион. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 3 (39) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reformy-prosveschennoy-byurokratii-v-rossii-na-primere-raboty-redaktsionnyh-komissiy-1850-h-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Савельева, «О некоторых дискуссиях в работе Редакционных комиссий в 1859–1860 гг. по отмене крепостного права», <i>Каспийский регион: политика, экономика, культура</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-nekotoryh-diskussiyah-v-rabote-redaktsionnyh-komissiy-v-1859-1860-gg-po-otmene-krepostnogo-prava. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the nobles feel cheated by the commissions?","a":"Because the government had invited them to draw up emancipation projects in their provincial committees, and then a small body of officials and government nominees wrote the law instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their deputies were allowed only to answer printed questions, and those who petitioned the emperor in protest were formally reprimanded through the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were expert members brought in?","a":"Because Milyutin proposed adding landowners who had distinguished themselves in the provincial committees, and both the emperor and Rostovtsev approved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then helped to choose them, which brought in committed reformers such as Samarin and Cherkassky alongside firm opponents of the reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rostovtsev's death seem to threaten the reform?","a":"Because his successor, Count Panin, was a thorough conservative and an open opponent of the commissions' programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1860 he tried to leave room for later changes to the statutes in the landowners' favour, and nineteen members answered with a joint note defending their own wording.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-444": {"questions":["Signed on 19 February 1861 but held back for a fortnight in case carnival drinking turned the news into riots, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> announced that Russia's serfs were free.","Peasants who heard the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read aloud in their parish churches often suspected a forgery and waited for a second proclamation that would give them the land for nothing."],"abstract":"The <b>Emancipation Manifesto</b> was the proclamation in which Alexander II announced the abolition of serfdom, signed with the new statutes on 19 February 1861 by the Julian calendar, 3 March by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It set out the law's chief principles, that the serfs received at once the civil rights of free rural people, kept the land they held in return for dues and could buy it with the state's help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statutes it introduced were far longer, a body of seventeen acts and special rules, most of them with more than a hundred articles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The text was written by Metropolitan Filaret of Moscow after a first draft by Samarin, revised by Nikolay Milyutin, had been judged unsuitable, and Filaret took the task on reluctantly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He moved Divine Providence to the very opening of the text and ended it with a call to the Orthodox people to cross themselves and ask God's blessing on their free labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fearing that the drinking of carnival week might turn the news into riots, the government held the manifesto back until 5 March, Forgiveness Sunday and the end of the carnival, when it was read in the churches of both capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Moscow the reading began in the Dormition Cathedral of the Kremlin, and publication in the provinces followed from 7 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> St Petersburg greeted it with cheering in the streets and at the opera, but a young witness found its mixture of Russian and Church Slavonic obscure, and the serfs were to stay bound to their old duties for two more years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the villages the reading brought disappointment rather than joy, for the peasants had expected to keep their land without paying, and some asked aloud what sort of liberty this was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rumours spread that the landlords had hidden the real manifesto and that a second one would give all the land for nothing, and in a few places troops fired on peasants who refused to believe the text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 1 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 164–68, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut01kropuoft. [Open access]","Андрей Лебедев, «Святитель Филарет и манифест от 19 февраля 1861 года», <i>Филаретовский альманах</i>, no. 9 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svyatitel-filaret-i-manifest-ot-19-fevralya-1861-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. К. Гаврилов, «Манифест 1861 г. в оценках Ф. И. Тютчева», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/manifest-1861-g-v-otsenkah-f-i-tyutcheva. [in Russian] [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 165–69, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 44–45, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 1, <i>The Country and Its Inhabitants</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1893), 429–31, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus01leroiala. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 615, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the manifesto written by a churchman?","a":"Because the first draft, by Samarin and revised by Milyutin, was found unsuitable, and the emperor's request was passed to Metropolitan Filaret of Moscow at the end of January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Filaret wrote it reluctantly, since he disliked the way the reform was being carried out, and gave it a solemn religious frame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the peasants disappointed?","a":"Because they believed the land they worked was theirs, telling their masters that the serfs belonged to them but the land belonged to the serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The manifesto told them they must still pay dues, even for the communal land, so many decided that the real law was being hidden from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did readers find the text hard to follow?","a":"Because it was written in an elevated style that mixed Russian with Church Slavonic, which obscured the sense even for an educated young reader in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legal statutes behind it were harder still, running to seventeen acts and special rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Emancipation reform of 1861"},
    "ru-445": {"questions":["Abolished from 1 January 1907 by a manifesto of Nicholas II during the revolution of 1905, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the former serfs had been reduced once before, in December 1881.","Because the village commune was made answerable for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of all its members, a peasant who fell behind became a burden on every household in his village."],"abstract":"<b>Redemption payments</b> were the annual sums that Russia's former serfs paid to the state for the land they received at the emancipation of 1861, the instalments of a loan with which the treasury bought out their landlords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The price was set not by the market value of the land but by capitalising the peasants' dues at 6 per cent, which meant multiplying a year's dues by sixteen and two-thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state paid the landowner four-fifths of this sum at once in interest-bearing bonds, and the remaining fifth was owed to him by the peasants directly, though it was often remitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In return the peasants paid the treasury 6 per cent a year on the advance for 49 years, an annuity that covered both the interest and the debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The deeds went to the village commune, which was made responsible for the taxes and redemption payments of all its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Redemption was at first voluntary, and until it began a community remained temporarily obligated, still owing its former landlord labour or rent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many communes refused, hoping for a second emancipation that would cost nothing, and in about three cases in five the operation was started by the landlord, who then had to accept the state's four-fifths as full payment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By decrees of 28 December 1881 Alexander III made redemption compulsory for the peasants still under obligation and lowered the payments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arrears mounted all the same, and the accounts of the operation were kept so confusedly that the government never had an exact picture of how it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> During the revolution of 1905 a manifesto of Nicholas II dated 3 November abolished the payments from 1 January 1907, long before the 49 years were up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 161–62, 176–77, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 1, <i>The Country and Its Inhabitants</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1893), 438–44, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus01leroiala. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 41, 47, 52, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 615–16, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","С. Г. Шустов, «Завершение выкупной операции в Пермской губернии (1881–1906 годы)», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 28 (2009): 72–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zavershenie-vykupnoy-operatsii-v-permskoy-gubernii-1881-1906-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","Л. В. Каленюк, «Крестьянская реформа в России 1861 г.: кредитно-денежный аспект», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Экономика</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyanskaya-reforma-v-rossii-1861-g-kreditno-denezhnyy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did landlords welcome redemption?","a":"Because it gave them a large sum of ready money at once, in place of dues that were hard to collect from free peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state in effect turned a private debt owed by the peasants into a public debt owed by the treasury, and collected it through its own tax officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the peasants slow to begin it?","a":"Because many still expected a second emancipation that would give them the land for nothing, and few were willing to make sacrifices now for an advantage half a century away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many communes refused on any terms, so most operations were started by the landlords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the payments made compulsory in 1881?","a":"Because in the last years of Alexander II fewer and fewer peasants were entering redemption, and in 1882 over three million were still in a state of semi-servitude to their former masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Decrees of 28 December 1881 made redemption obligatory for them and reduced the payments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-446": {"questions":["Making the village commune answer as a whole for its members' taxes and redemption payments, the rule of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tied the freed serf to his community rather than his landlord.","Siberian tax inspectors described the first year after the abolition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in October 1906 as a year of exceptional pressure on the villages of western Siberia."],"abstract":"<b>Krugovaya poruka</b>, the mutual guarantee of English writers of the time, was the rule under which a Russian peasant community answered as a whole for the taxes and dues of each of its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The emancipation of 1861 kept it, because the freed serfs, like the other tributary orders, were taxed by the head rather than by income and the tax had to be paid by the group as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each group was therefore made responsible for its members and allowed to keep them within it, so a peasant could not move or change his occupation without a formal verdict of dismissal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land deeds of the emancipation were handed to the village commune, which was made responsible for all taxes and for the new redemption payments as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state thus gained an interest in keeping the commune together until the redemption debt was paid, and the peasant freed from his landlord fell into dependence on his commune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because every family was answerable for its neighbours' arrears, a drunkard or a bankrupt was the business of the whole village, and no peasant could simply walk away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pressure also drove communes to redistribute land precisely according to each household's capacity to pay, and to take holdings from those who could no longer work them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poll tax itself, the very tax that made collective responsibility necessary, was abolished by a law the State Council approved on 18 May 1886, though the guarantee for other dues lasted longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of 12 March 1903, passed on the advice of the finance minister Witte, abolished collective responsibility for taxes in European Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Siberia it survived three more years, until a decree of 5 October 1906, and tax officials there described the first year without it as one of exceptional pressure and rising arrears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 46–47, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 616, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 1, <i>The Country and Its Inhabitants</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1893), 474–75, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus01leroiala. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 1 (London: Cassell, 1905), 155, https://archive.org/details/russia0001wall. [Open access]","А. К. Кириллов, «Воплощение столыпинских реформ в Сибири: отмена круговой поруки», <i>Гуманитарные науки в Сибири</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voploschenie-stolypinskih-reform-v-sibiri-otmena-krugovoy-poruki. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. Х. Шебзухова, «Крестьянский вопрос во внутренней политике царского правительства в 80–90 гг. XIX в.: урегулирование податной системы», <i>Вестник Адыгейского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyanskiy-vopros-vo-vnutrenney-politike-tsarskogo-pravitelstva-v-80-90-gg-xix-v-uregulirovanie-podatnoy-sistemy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the emancipation keep collective responsibility?","a":"Because the freed peasants still paid a tax by the head rather than by income, and the tax was collected from the group as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deeds and the redemption debt were handed to the commune too, which gave the state a reason to keep the commune together until the debt was paid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it limit a peasant's freedom?","a":"Because a group answerable for its members' taxes needed the power to keep them, so a peasant who wished to leave had to obtain a verdict of dismissal and could not freely change his place or his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One observer concluded that the peasant had passed from dependence on his landlord to dependence on his commune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did abolishing the poll tax matter?","a":"Because the poll tax was what had made collective responsibility necessary, and collective responsibility in turn restricted the peasants' freedom of movement and choice of occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The guarantee outlived the poll tax, however, and was abolished for taxes in European Russia in 1903 and in Siberia in 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-447": {"questions":["Elected for three years by landowners, village communities and townspeople, deputies to a district <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met once a year to vote a budget and choose an executive board.","Because the governor could suspend any decision he judged contrary to the interests of the state, each provincial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked under constant watch from the central bureaucracy."],"abstract":"The <b>zemstvo</b> was the elected organ of local self-government created in rural Russia by the statute of 1 January 1864, with an assembly and an executive board in each district and each province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its duties were practical, among them roads and bridges, transport for the rural police, primary education, public health, the state of the crops and measures against famine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> District deputies were elected for three years by three curiae, the private landowners, the village communities and the townspeople, and the provincial assembly was chosen by the district assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Marshals of the nobility presided over the assemblies without election, and the State Council ruled that no curia might elect more deputies than the other two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Brought in gradually from 1865, the zemstvos were working in 34 provinces of European Russia by 1876.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A visitor to a district assembly at Novgorod in 1870 was struck that nobles and their former serfs, freed only ten years before, sat together with no trace of antagonism, the peasants forming the majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Provincial assemblies were dominated by landowners, because peasants seldom chose one of their own when deputies were unpaid and few peasants could read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The governor could suspend any zemstvo decision he judged contrary to the law or to the general interests of the state, and officials frustrated every attempt by zemstvos to combine or to act politically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within these limits they did much for schools, hospitals and asylums, and in provinces such as Kazan they built up rural medical care where no single system had existed before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their spending grew from about six million roubles at first to nearly 28 million in 1875, much of it raised from the peasants, and a counter-reform of 1890 brought them under tighter official control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 233–38, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 620–21, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","М. С. Тимофеев, ««Положение о губернских и уездных земских учреждениях» от 1 января 1864 г. как правовая основа проведения земской реформы в России», <i>Вестник Российского университета кооперации</i>, no. 1 (15) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polozhenie-o-gubernskih-i-uezdnyh-zemskih-uchrezhdeniyah-ot-1-yanvarya-1864-g-kak-pravovaya-osnova-provedeniya-zemskoy-reformy-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 97–100, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","В. Н. Кузин and Н. И. Свечников, «Политико-правовые итоги земской реформы в России во второй половине XIX в.», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Поволжский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politiko-pravovye-itogi-zemskoy-reformy-v-rossii-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Козаченко, «Земские выборы в Полтавской губернии 1865–1889 гг.», <i>Russian Journal of Economics and Law</i>, no. 3 (27) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zemskie-vybory-v-poltavskoy-gubernii-1865-1889-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Морозова and А. С. Созинов, «Организация и финансирование земского здравоохранения в Казанской губернии», <i>Казанский медицинский журнал</i> 95, no. 5 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsiya-i-finansirovanie-zemskogo-zdravoohraneniya-v-kazanskoy-gubernii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new local body needed after 1861?","a":"Because under serfdom the landowners had served as unpaid police on their estates, and the emancipation abolished their authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peasant institutions set up in its place covered only the peasantry, so a larger body in which all classes were represented was required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did few peasants sit in the provincial assemblies?","a":"Because district assemblies sent their most active members, usually landowners, and attending was costly while payment of deputies was forbidden by law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mass illiteracy also left the peasants few candidates of their own, as the reform's authors had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did officials distrust the zemstvos?","a":"Because the zemstvos were elected bodies with the right to tax property, and the bureaucracy feared they might gain political influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials frustrated every attempt by zemstvos to combine, and the governor's power to suspend their decisions gave the administration a means to restrain them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Zemstvo"},
    "ru-448": {"questions":["With principles published on 29 September 1862 and statutes signed on 20 November 1864, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> borrowed more openly from Western models than any other reform of the reign.","Military courts trying political cases after 1878 and land captains replacing elected justices in 1889 cut back the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had promised all subjects equality before the law."],"abstract":"The <b>judicial reform of Alexander II</b> was the reorganisation of Russia's courts by the Judicial Statutes of 1864, the most thoroughly planned of the Great Reforms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old courts had sunk into notorious inefficiency and corruption, with secret written procedure, judges elected by the local nobility who rarely knew the law, and bribes taken by clerks and judges alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fundamental principles were published by imperial command on 29 September 1862, and the statutes built on them were signed on 20 November 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They separated the courts from the administration, made all subjects equal before the law and replaced secret written procedure with public oral trials and juries in serious criminal cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two parallel sets of courts were created, elected justices of the peace with their district sessions for petty cases and district courts with judicial chambers for the rest, under the Senate as the final court of appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Justices of the peace were chosen by the elected district assemblies and heard civil claims of up to 500 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Judges of the regular courts could not be removed except on conviction for a crime or misdemeanour, a guarantee the drafters thought as necessary under an absolute government as under any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reform also created Russia's first real bar, since the old courts had known only ill-regarded agents who drew up and presented written petitions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Exceptions began early, and from 1878 political cases were increasingly sent to military courts, which heard 93 of the 148 political trials held between 1878 and 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A law of 12 July 1889 replaced most elected justices of the peace with land captains drawn from the nobility, yet the new courts themselves survived the terror and reaction of those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 261–75, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 622–23, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 2, <i>The Institutions</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1894), 329–33, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus02leroiala. [Open access]","В. А. Илюхина, «К вопросу о развитии идеи мирового суда в России после принятия «Основных положений преобразования судебной части в России» 29 сентября 1862 года», <i>Историко-правовые проблемы: новый ракурс</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-razvitii-idei-mirovogo-suda-v-rossii-posle-prinyatiya-osnovnyh-polozheniy-preobrazovaniya-sudebnoy-chasti-v-rossii-29. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. А. Эльдаров, «Мировая юстиция по судебному уставу от 20 ноября 1864 г.», <i>Вестник Дагестанского государственного университета. Серия 3: Общественные науки</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mirovaya-yustitsiya-po-sudebnomu-ustavu-ot-20-noyabrya-1864-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Попова, «К вопросу учреждения суда присяжных в России по реформе 1864 г. (к 150-летию судебной реформы)», <i>Известия Иркутского государственного университета. Серия: История</i> 13 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-uchrezhdeniya-suda-prisyazhnyh-v-rossii-po-reforme-1864-g-k-150-letiyu-sudebnoy-reformy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the old courts need replacing?","a":"Because procedure was secret and written, judges elected by the local nobility rarely knew the law, and everyone knew that bribes were taken by clerks and judges alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A commission found no fewer than 25 radical defects in the existing system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were judges made irremovable?","a":"Because a judge must be independent both of the central power and of party feeling, and a life term gave him security and freedom of judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice the ministry of justice could still threaten a judge with transfer to a distant and disagreeable post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were political cases taken away from the ordinary courts?","a":"Because after a jury acquitted Vera Zasulich in 1878 the government no longer trusted juries with political offences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law of 9 August 1878 placed state crimes under military courts, and most political trials of the next twelve years were held there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Judicial reform of Alexander II"},
    "ru-449": {"questions":["The acquittal of Vera Zasulich in March 1878 was the most famous verdict of a Russian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and led the government to send political cases to military courts.","Brought gradually into the provinces from 1866 but reaching western Siberia only in 1909, the Russian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> often ended in acquittal when the criminal code prescribed a punishment jurors thought too harsh."],"abstract":"A <b>jury trial</b> is a trial in which ordinary citizens decide the facts of a case, and in Russia it was introduced by the Judicial Statutes of 1864 for serious criminal cases heard in the district courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Russian jury was modelled on the French assize court, with twelve jurors and two reserves who could take the place of any who fell ill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The jurors, officially called sworn assessors, were men who met a property qualification or held local elected office, so that peasants sat on the benches beside nobles and merchants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Commissions of the district zemstvos drew up the jury lists, from which each jury was then drawn by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Courts with juries were brought in gradually from 1866, reached Tomsk and Tobolsk in western Siberia only in 1909, and were never set up in eastern Siberia at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Russian juries soon became known for acquitting defendants who had confessed, often because the criminal code left the judge no room to soften a sentence they thought too harsh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many judges and prosecutors nevertheless found peasant juries more reliable than educated ones, though peasants were lenient towards a village official who repaid public money before his trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most famous verdict came on 31 March 1878 by the Julian calendar, when a St Petersburg jury acquitted Vera Zasulich, who had shot the city's prefect, General Trepov, although the facts were not disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government answered with a law of 9 August 1878 sending political cases to military courts, and of 148 political trials between 1878 and 1890, 93 were heard there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Juries went on hearing ordinary criminal cases until 1917, and their verdicts sometimes pushed the government to revise outdated or needlessly cruel laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 268, 279–87, https://archive.org/details/russiawal02wall. [Open access]","Anatole Leroy-Beaulieu, <i>The Empire of the Tsars and the Russians</i>, trans. Zénaïde A. Ragozin, part 2, <i>The Institutions</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1894), 349–54, https://archive.org/details/empireoftsarsrus02leroiala. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 622, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1882</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: Estes and Lauriat, 1886), 382, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussia03ramb. [Open access]","В. В. Попова, «К вопросу учреждения суда присяжных в России по реформе 1864 г. (к 150-летию судебной реформы)», <i>Известия Иркутского государственного университета. Серия: История</i> 13 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-uchrezhdeniya-suda-prisyazhnyh-v-rossii-po-reforme-1864-g-k-150-letiyu-sudebnoy-reformy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Дудко, «Поэтапное введение суда присяжных в Российской империи по судебной реформе 1864 г.», <i>Актуальные проблемы российского права</i>, no. 12 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poetapnoe-vvedenie-suda-prisyazhnyh-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-po-sudebnoy-reforme-1864-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. В. Филиппов, «Формирование суда присяжных в ходе судебной реформы 1864 г.: предпосылки, история, итоги», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 2 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-suda-prisyazhnyh-v-hode-sudebnoy-reformy-1864-g-predposylki-istoriya-itogi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. С. Перова, «Модель суда присяжных по Судебным уставам 1864 года», <i>Самарский научный вестник</i> 14, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/model-suda-prisyazhnyh-po-sudebnym-ustavam-1864-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russian juries acquit people who had confessed?","a":"Because a Russian jury could not leave extenuating circumstances to the judge, who had to sentence by the letter of a harsh criminal code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Faced with a punishment out of all proportion to the crime, the jury's only remedy was to acquit, and the law even forbade defence counsel to tell jurors what the penalty would be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were peasants allowed to sit on juries?","a":"Because the law wanted the jury to be open to all classes rather than a monopoly of nobles and merchants, so local elected officers such as village elders were admitted beside those who met the property qualification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many judges and prosecutors found peasant juries more reliable than juries drawn from the educated classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Zasulich verdict change the system?","a":"Because the jury acquitted her although she had plainly shot General Trepov, after a defence that presented the act as a defence of a political prisoner's honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within months a law of 9 August 1878 moved state crimes to military courts, and most political trials of the next twelve years were held there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jury trial"},
    "ru-450": {"questions":["Replacing representation by estate with representation by property, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left lodgers such as workers and clerks without a vote in the new city councils.","Prepared over eight years after the zemstvo reform and replaced by a new law in 1892, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governed the city dumas of the Russian Empire for two decades."],"abstract":"The <b>Municipal Statute of 1870</b> was the law, approved by Alexander II on 16 June 1870, that gave the towns of the Russian Empire elected councils called city dumas and executive boards headed by a mayor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Preparing it took eight years and three drafts, as the government followed its peasant, judicial and zemstvo reforms with a reform of the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before it, official returns reported that representative institutions existed in no town except those newly set up in the two capitals and Odessa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The statute replaced representation by estate or corporation with representation of property, so that taxpayers on town property and trade voted whatever their estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voters were ranked by the taxes they paid and divided into three classes contributing equal shares of the total, so that, in one critic's words, the voter who paid more had more rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In a small town such as Tsaritsyn on the Volga only two classes were formed, with 57 voters in the first and 1,268 in the second, and the new duma began its work under a confirmed mayor only in July 1872.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The duma could levy and alter town dues and taxes, set rules for town property and charitable institutions, and give instructions to its executive board.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lodgers who owned no property in the town, among them workers, office staff and members of the educated class, were left without a vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unusually for its day, the statute even allowed women who held property to take part in the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the early 1890s provincial newspapers complained of tiny electorates, chance deputies and mounting town debts, and in 1892 the statute was replaced by a new one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 621–22, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Г. Ю. Бурдина, «Подготовка реформы городского самоуправления в России (1862–1870 гг.)», <i>Самарский научный вестник</i>, no. 2 (7) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/podgotovka-reformy-gorodskogo-samoupravleniya-v-rossii-1862-1870-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. Д. Пашенцева, «Нормотворческие полномочия органов городского общественного управления Российской империи после реформы 1870 года», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Юридические науки</i>, no. 2 (46) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/normotvorcheskie-polnomochiya-organov-gorodskogo-obschestvennogo-upravleniya-rossiyskoy-imperii-posle-reformy-1870-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Е. Камаева, «Введение Городового положения 1870 года в Царицыне», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 2 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vvedenie-gorodovogo-polozheniya-1870-goda-v-tsaritsyne. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Сорокин, «Система городских выборов в России в конце XIX века: критика в провинциальной периодической печати», <i>Научный диалог</i> 13, no. 1 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sistema-gorodskih-vyborov-v-rossii-v-kontse-xix-veka-kritika-v-provintsialnoy-periodicheskoy-pechati. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Кузин and Н. И. Свечников, «Политико-правовые итоги земской реформы в России во второй половине XIX в.», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Поволжский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politiko-pravovye-itogi-zemskoy-reformy-v-rossii-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the vote depend on taxes paid?","a":"Because the statute based town representation on property rather than on estates or corporations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Voters were ranked by their taxes and split into classes paying equal totals, so that, as one critic put it, the voter who paid more had more rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did small towns struggle with the new law?","a":"Because they had few qualified voters and little experience of self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Tsaritsyn the number of deputies first fixed did not match the electorate, the mayor's confirmation was delayed, and the provincial administration struck down several of the duma's new taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the capitals reformed first?","a":"Because earlier projects had reached only St Petersburg, which received self-government in the 1840s, and official surveys found no working representative bodies in other towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The system of 1870 was then introduced, at first tentatively, in St Petersburg, Moscow and Odessa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-451": {"questions":["Reforming <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1874, Dmitry Milyutin let a university graduate serve six months and a man with only primary schooling serve four years.","Before the reform of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a recruit served 25 years and a family could send a hired substitute or a cash payment in his place."],"abstract":"<b>Conscription in the Russian Empire</b> rested until 1874 on the recruit levy, which took soldiers only from the tax-paying estates while nobles, merchants and clergy were spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The recruiting statutes of Nicholas I, of 1827 and 1831, fixed queues of liable men and let a family replace a recruit with a hired substitute, a cash payment or a receipt for a man already given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The soldier then served 25 years, a fate that even serfs regarded as the severest and most degrading punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Crimean War, Dmitry Milyutin, war minister from 1861, laid his programme before the tsar in a report of 15 January 1862, which first raised the question of changing how the army was filled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He cut the term of service to 16 years, abolished corporal punishment, divided the empire into military districts and reformed the military courts and the training of officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His decisive change was the statute on universal military service, law from 1 January 1874 by the Julian calendar, which abolished the recruit levy and made the obligation equal for all classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Men were called at 20 to serve six years in the ranks and nine in the reserve, only sons and other breadwinners were exempted, and the rest drew lots to fill each district's quota.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schooling shortened the term, so that a university graduate served six months, a man with secondary education two years and one with primary schooling four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About a fifth of the first intake of 1874 could read, and new training rules from 1875 sent most recruits home literate, making the army a substitute for the schools Russia lacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cossacks were brought under the law but kept their own organisation, and receipts for recruits issued before 1874 were still accepted for some years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 157–62, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 295, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]","В. И. Баяндин, «Всеобщая воинская повинность как часть военных реформ императора Александра II», <i>Гуманитарные науки в Сибири</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vseobschaya-voinskaya-povinnost-kak-chast-voennyh-reform-imperatora-aleksandra-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Рудник, «Всесословная воинская повинность, русское общество и грамотность новобранцев в России», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (9) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vsesoslovnaya-voinskaya-povinnost-russkoe-obschestvo-i-gramotnost-novobrantsev-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 310, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","В. М. Шалашная, «Д. А. Малютин и военные реформы в России в 60–70-е гг. XIX в.», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i>, no. 4-4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/d-a-malyutin-i-voennye-reformy-v-rossii-v-60-70-e-gg-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the old recruit levy so hated?","a":"Because it fell only on the tax-paying estates and a recruit served 25 years, which even serfs saw as the harshest punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Families with money could buy their way out with a hired substitute, a cash payment or a receipt for a recruit already given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 1874 law reward education?","a":"Because it cut the term of service for men with schooling, down to six months for university graduates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With men of the educated classes now in the ranks and new training rules from 1875, most recruits went home able to read and write.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Milyutin carry his reform through when other reformers fell?","a":"Because he kept the full confidence of Alexander II and stayed war minister to the end of the reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His programme had been in preparation since his report of 15 January 1862, and a special commission on military service was set up in 1870.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Conscription in the Russian Empire"},
    "ru-452": {"questions":["Drafted under the minister Golovnin after the student riots of 1861, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave professors self-government but left students no legal way to organise themselves.","Count Dmitry Tolstoy never managed to overturn the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only in 1884 did a new charter strip the university councils of their autonomy."],"abstract":"The <b>University Statute of 1863</b> was the charter, confirmed by Alexander II on 18 June 1863 by the Julian calendar, that governed the Russian universities in the reform era and gave their professors a large measure of self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was prepared under the new minister of education, A. V. Golovnin, after the student riots of autumn 1861, and its draft was printed and sent for comment to experts in Russia and abroad while the press joined the debate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 12 chapters set out the rights of the universities, their faculties and their bodies of teachers and students, and it greatly increased the number of chairs and posts in every faculty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the German principle of a self-governing body of professors to the French principle of a fixed curriculum with examinations, while keeping the autocracy's habit of supervision through the ministry and the educational districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was part of a wider school reform under Golovnin, which also produced the statute on primary schools of 1864 and a secondary-school statute dividing the gymnasia into classical and modern ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The university council, made up of the professors, regained the rights the statute of 1835 had taken from it, electing the rector, the deans and the professors and deciding the university's teaching and finances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rector was chosen by the council for four years from among the professors and confirmed by the emperor, and a university court elected by the council was restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet the curator of the educational district and the minister still watched over the council, and students were given no legal way of organising their own corporate life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Count Dmitry Tolstoy replaced Golovnin in 1866 he never managed to overturn the statute, and had to make do with extra rules restricting students and professors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was finally replaced in 1884 by a statute that stripped the councils of their autonomy and forbade any corporate organisation of students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 91–95, 170, 261, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","В. Ф. Голованова, «Правовые аспекты университетского устава 1863 года», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-aspekty-universitetskogo-ustava-1863-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Новиков and Т. Б. Перфилова, «Университетский устав 1863 г.: пределы академического самоуправления», <i>Ярославский педагогический вестник</i> 1, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/universitetskiy-ustav-1863-g-predely-akademicheskogo-samoupravleniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. М. Химич, «Реформы системы образования министра народного просвещения А. В. Головнина в контексте „Великих реформ“ Александра II», <i>Право и государство: теория и практика</i>, no. 9 (237) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reformy-sistemy-obrazovaniya-ministra-narodnogo-prosvescheniya-a-v-golovnina-v-kontekste-velikih-reform-aleksandra-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 295, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new university statute needed in the early 1860s?","a":"Because the riots of autumn 1861, when students marched in St Petersburg and Moscow and hundreds were arrested, showed that the old rules had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar replaced the minister of education with Golovnin, who began work on a new statute at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Student unrest did not end with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How much freedom did the statute really give?","a":"It gave the council of professors the right to elect the rector, the deans and the professors, and to manage teaching and money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But the curator of the educational district and the minister kept watch over the council, and the elected rector still had to be confirmed by the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the statute after the reform era?","a":"It survived the reaction that followed 1866, because the minister Tolstoy could not get it changed and issued extra rules instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1884 a new statute removed the councils' autonomy and banned student organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-453": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1865, the minister of the interior could warn a periodical three times, and the third warning suspended it for up to six months.","Meant only as an experiment by a commission under Prince Obolensky, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived almost unchanged for forty years, until the revolutionary year of 1905."],"abstract":"The <b>Temporary Rules on the Press</b> were the law of 6 April 1865, by the Julian calendar, that changed how the Russian Empire policed the printed word, letting some works go to press unread and punishing them afterwards instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were drafted by a commission under Prince D. A. Obolensky in the Ministry of the Interior, which judged a full new censorship statute untimely and dangerous and offered these rules as an experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Original books of at least 10 printed sheets and translations of at least 20 were freed from preliminary censorship, as were the periodicals of St Petersburg and Moscow whose publishers asked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even that freedom depended on the minister of the interior, who also kept the sole right to license any new periodical, while the provinces and shorter works stayed under the censor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A history of the reign written before 1917 called it the most sparing and cautious of all the reforms of the 1860s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The freedom came with a weapon: the minister could issue a periodical a formal warning naming the offending articles, and a third warning suspended it for up to six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once a suspended weekly or daily reappeared, every issue had to reach the censorship committee by eleven in the evening before publication, a rule that put it back under the censor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the punitive power lay with a minister and not with the courts, another early history judged the measure hardly a reform at all, though liberal papers greeted their first uncensored issues on 1 September 1865 with gratitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The role of the courts shrank further in 1872, when a law of 7 June put the uncensored press under the Committee of Ministers, which could order books and issues destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Meant as a stopgap, the rules lasted with few real changes for forty years, until the crisis of 1905 forced a new settlement of the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 104–5, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 295, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]","В. Ф. Блохин, «Из истории цензурного реформаторства: государство и легальная печать России в политическом контексте (1865–1905 годы)», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 6 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-tsenzurnogo-reformatorstva-gosudarstvo-i-legalnaya-pechat-rossii-v-politicheskom-kontekste-1865-1905-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Биюшкина, «Ограничения и запреты в развитии цензурного законодательства Российской империи второй половины XIX века», <i>Юридическая техника</i>, no. 12 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ogranicheniya-i-zaprety-v-razvitii-tsenzurnogo-zakonodatelstva-rossiyskoy-imperii-vtoroy-poloviny-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Кумачева, «Цензура «бесцензурной» печати в пореформенной России», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsenzura-bestsenzurnoy-pechati-v-poreformennoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the rules called temporary?","a":"Because the commission that drafted them thought a complete new censorship statute untimely and dangerous, and introduced them as an experiment instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree itself spoke of making changes during the present transitional state of the courts, until experience gave further guidance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The experiment then lasted for forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the freed press still fear the government?","a":"Because the minister of the interior could issue warnings, and a third warning suspended a periodical for up to six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A suspended paper then had to submit each issue to the censors the evening before it appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The power to punish lay with a minister rather than with the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the courts' part in press cases?","a":"It was cut back in 1872, when a law of 7 June gave the Committee of Ministers control over the uncensored press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From then on that committee could authorise the destruction of books and of issues of journals, and prosecution in court for press offences almost ceased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-454": {"questions":["Britain, France and Austria sent notes to St Petersburg in April 1863 over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the foreign minister Gorchakov rejected all three.","The hanging of Romuald Traugutt in Warsaw in August 1864 ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which the Russian government swept away what remained of Polish autonomy."],"abstract":"The <b>January Uprising</b> was the armed rising of 1863–1864 against Russian rule in the Kingdom of Poland, which spread at once into the Lithuanian and Belarusian provinces of the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of patriotic demonstrations that began in 1861, which Cossacks repeatedly broke up by firing on the crowds, and out of a secret national organisation that kept growing despite the concessions of Marquis Aleksander Wielopolski.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wielopolski, head of the civil administration in Warsaw, ordered a levy of recruits in the towns only, hoping to remove the young men who were the backbone of the radicals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In reply, on 22 January 1863 the underground National Committee proclaimed itself the Provisional National Government and declared war on the tsarist regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Martial law reached the provinces of Grodno and Vilna on 4 February, two weeks after the first fighting near Brest-Litovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The insurgents were poorly armed and organised, and an early British history called the rising an act of national despair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, France and Austria tried to intervene by diplomacy, presenting their notes in St Petersburg on 17 April 1863, and on 26 April the foreign minister Gorchakov rejected them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The threat of foreign interference set off a wave of patriotic addresses to the tsar from nobles, merchants and peasants, led in the press by the editor Mikhail Katkov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rising was crushed in 1864, when its last leader, Romuald Traugutt, was hanged in Warsaw on 5 August, while other active participants were executed or exiled from Novgorod to Irkutsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1864 the government gave the peasants their holdings as freehold to divide them from the rebel gentry, abolished what was left of Polish autonomy and began a long policy of Russification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 628–29, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 85–87, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Д. А. Коско, «Александр II и польский вопрос накануне Январского восстания 1863–1864 гг.», <i>История повседневности</i>, no. 1 (17) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-ii-i-polskiy-vopros-nakanune-yanvarskogo-vosstaniya-1863-1864-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Черевык, «Вмешательство великих держав в польское восстание 1863 г. и позиция России», <i>Манускрипт</i> 12, no. 6 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vmeshatelstvo-velikih-derzhav-v-polskoe-vosstanie-1863-g-i-pozitsiya-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Арлукевич, «Войска Российской империи в Беларуси в период восстания 1863–1864 гг.», <i>Вестник Полоцкого государственного университета. Серия A. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyska-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-belarusi-v-period-vosstaniya-1863-1864-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Герасимчик, review of <i>Ромуальд Траугутт — рыцарь земли Полесской</i>, by Ю. А. Борисюк and А. К. Рейзвих, <i>Белорусский исторический обзор</i>, no. 1 (6) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/retsenziya-borisyuk-yu-a-reyzvih-a-k-romuald-traugutt-rytsar-zemli-polesskoy-nauchnyy-redaktor-a-r-benzeruk-minsk-entsiklopediks. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 336–37, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a levy of recruits start a rebellion?","a":"Because Wielopolski aimed the levy at the young townsmen who formed the core of the radicals, hoping to remove them by conscription.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The revolutionaries saw it as a threat to their plans and decided to rise at once, proclaiming a national government on 22 January 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did foreign protests strengthen the tsar's hand at home?","a":"Because the notes of Britain, France and Austria provoked a wave of patriotic addresses to the tsar in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government felt able to reject the notes outright, and the liberal and radical opposition lost much of its influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government give Polish peasants land after the rising?","a":"Because the rising had been led by the gentry while the peasants had mostly stayed out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Freeholds and an end to labour dues were meant to reward them and to keep their interests apart from those of the nobles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"January Uprising"},
    "ru-455": {"questions":["Lithuanian books printed in Cyrillic, an instrument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after 1864, were refused by readers even as gifts until the Latin alphabet returned in 1904.","A decree of 10 December 1865, a measure of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the nine western provinces, barred anyone of Polish origin from acquiring new landed estates there."],"abstract":"<b>Russification</b> was the imperial policy of spreading the Russian language, Orthodoxy and Russian officials among the non-Russian peoples of the empire, called in the officials' own word obrusenie, 'making Russian'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A programme of gradual Russification for the western borderlands was first drawn up in the 1830s, after the Polish rising of 1830–1831, to prevent further Polish revolts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It reached its height after the January Uprising of 1863 in the Kingdom of Poland and in the nine western provinces, where the landowning gentry was largely Polish and Catholic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Russian Poland the language of offices and university lectures became Russian, and a curator of education worked for the day when Polish mothers would sing their children to sleep with Russian songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1866 Count Dmitry Tolstoy attacked the education minister Golovnin's policy in the North-West as not Russifying enough, and Golovnin soon resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the North-West the governor-general Mikhail Muravyov ordered on 24 March 1864 that every sign, notice and inscription in Polish be replaced with Russian, and later decrees made Russian the only language of administration and took Polish out of the schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Catholic clergy were closely watched, since entering a monastery or leaving one now needed the governor's knowledge and processions outside the churches were forbidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A decree of 10 December 1865 forbade persons of Polish origin to acquire new landed estates in the nine provinces, and conversion to Orthodoxy did not exempt a Pole from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1864 Lithuanian books could not be printed in the Latin alphabet, but readers refused the Cyrillic editions even as gifts, Prussian Lithuania became their publishing centre, and the ban was lifted only in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Historians still disagree about Muravyov, whom some Belarusian scholars credit with sparing the region worse bloodshed while Polish historians call his system close to state terrorism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 336–37, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 112, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","С. В. Ананьев, «Политика органов государственной власти по нивелированию польского национализма в Северо-Западном крае в рамках обеспечения внутренней безопасности (60-е годы XIX века)», <i>Вестник Московского государственного лингвистического университета. Общественные науки</i>, no. 2 (855) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politika-organov-gosudarstvennoy-vlasti-po-nivelirovaniyu-polskogo-natsionalizma-v-severo-zapadnom-krae-v-ramkah-obespecheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Ананьев, «Конфессиональная политика российского правительства в Северо-Западном крае в 1863–1865 гг. и ее вклад в обеспечение общественной безопасности в регионе», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Поволжский регион. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 4 (68) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konfessionalnaya-politika-rossiyskogo-pravitelstva-v-severo-zapadnom-krae-v-1863-1865-gg-i-ee-vklad-v-obespechenie-obschestvennoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Абрамова, «Правовое регулирование статуса недвижимости лиц польской национальности в девяти западных губерниях России в XIX в.», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 11. Право</i>, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoe-regulirovanie-statusa-nedvizhimosti-lits-polskoy-natsionalnosti-v-devyati-zapadnyh-guberniyah-rossii-v-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Мусаев, «Особенности социально-политической и этноконфессиональной ситуации в Литве во второй половине XIX — начале XX в.», <i>Вестник Университета мировых цивилизаций</i> 12, no. 2 (31) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-sotsialno-politicheskoy-i-etnokonfessionalnoy-situatsii-v-litve-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-nachale-hh-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. С. Грицуто, «Правительственная политика в отношении католицизма в Литве в 1860-х — начале 1870-х гг. в восприятии католического клирика», <i>Вестник Православного Свято-Тихоновского гуманитарного университета. Серия 2: История</i>, no. 47 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravitelstvennaya-politika-v-otnoshenii-katolitsizma-v-litve-v-1860-h-nachale-1870-h-gg-v-vospriyatii-katolicheskogo-klirika. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government aim its measures at the Polish gentry?","a":"Because it held the gentry and the Catholic clergy responsible for the rising, and Muravyov chose to win over the local peasants by easing their lot and setting them against the Polish nobles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land decree of 1865 then stopped Poles from buying new estates in the nine provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Cyrillic Lithuanian books fail?","a":"Because Lithuanian readers refused to accept them, even free of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Books in the Latin alphabet came instead from Prussian Lithuania, whose importance as a publishing centre grew sharply, until the ban was lifted in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about Muravyov's rule?","a":"Because the same measures are read in opposite ways: some Belarusian historians say his energetic administration saved the region from greater loss of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polish historians describe the policy of Muravyov and his successors as bordering on state terrorism, and a Catholic priest of the time called Russification a chimera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russification"},
    "ru-456": {"questions":["Hardening the Valuev Circular of 1863, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was never examined by the State Council and left not one Ukrainian book printed in 1877.","Prepared by a commission on Ukrainophile propaganda, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost Mykhailo Drahomanov his Kiev chair and led him to denounce it in Paris in 1878."],"abstract":"The <b>Ems Ukaz</b> was a secret decree that Alexander II signed at the German spa of Ems on 18 May 1876 by the Julian calendar, 30 May by the Gregorian, to stop the printing and spread of Ukrainian books in the Russian Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It banned original works and translations in Ukrainian, the import of Ukrainian books from abroad, plays and public readings in the language, and Ukrainian words printed with music.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historical documents could still appear in their original spelling and some fiction only in Russian spelling, and every permitted manuscript had first to pass the censor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It summed up the work of a commission of 1875 on 'Ukrainophile propaganda', whose members included the ministers Tolstoy and Timashev and the Kiev official Mikhail Yuzefovich, after whom it is also named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It never went through the State Council or the Committee of Ministers and was never formally revoked, even as the government encouraged Ukrainian peasants against their Polish landlords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> It hardened the ban of 18 July 1863, Julian style, in which the interior minister Pyotr Valuev, alarmed by the Polish rising and a planned Ukrainian translation of the Gospels, halted religious books, textbooks and popular reading in Ukrainian while still allowing fiction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That measure, traditionally called the Valuev Circular, was in fact a set of secret letters to the minister of education, the head of the Third Section and the chief procurator of the Synod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its most quoted phrase, that no separate Little Russian language had existed, did exist or could exist, came from a letter of Orest Novitsky, head of the Kiev censorship committee, though it is usually credited to Valuev himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1863 and 1876 no more than 8 per cent of Ukrainian manuscripts were banned or returned, but in 1877 not one Ukrainian book was printed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kiev professors Mykhailo Drahomanov and Mykola Ziber lost their posts, and Drahomanov, who went abroad, condemned the decree before an international literary congress in Paris in 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“Ems Ukase,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, 2001–), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CE%5CM%5CEmsUkase.htm. [Open access]","“Valuev, Petr,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Toronto: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, 2001–), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CV%5CA%5CValuevPetr.htm. [Open access]","Т. Г. Таирова-Яковлева, «„Валуевский циркуляр“ и запрет использования украинского языка», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/valuevskiy-tsirkulyar-i-zapret-ispolzovaniya-ukrainskogo-yazyka. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Э. Савчинский, «Украинское книгоиздание между „Валуевским циркуляром“ и Эмским указом (1863–1876 гг.)», <i>Вестник науки и образования</i>, no. 8 (10) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ukrainskoe-knigoizdanie-mezhdu-valuevskim-tsirkulyarom-i-emskim-ukazom-1863-1876-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2, trans. Alexander S. Kaun (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 224–25, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 339, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1863 ban single out religious books and schoolbooks?","a":"Because the trigger was a planned Ukrainian translation of the Gospels, and Valuev ordered the censors to pass only fiction in Ukrainian while religious, educational and popular books were halted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He justified it by the tense political situation of the Polish rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Valuev Circular's famous phrase misattributed?","a":"Because Valuev was quoting a letter from Orest Novitsky, head of the Kiev censorship committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Novitsky put the claim that there was no separate Little Russian language into the mouths of Little Russians who opposed the movement, and the words have since been credited to the minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 1876 decree harder to undo than a law?","a":"Because it was issued outside the normal legislative channels, never examined by the State Council or the Committee of Ministers, and never formally revoked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ban on publications and stage performances fell on the whole Ukrainophile movement at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ems Ukaz"},
    "ru-457": {"questions":["Gorchakov's circular of 1864 defended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the work of a frontier that must keep moving until it met an organised state able to keep order.","The storming of Geok Tepe in 1881 and the annexation of Merv in 1884 completed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose new lands a railway soon joined to the Caspian Sea."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian conquest of Central Asia</b> was the advance of the 1860s to the 1880s that brought the khanates of Kokand, Bukhara and Khiva and then the Turkmen oases under Russian rule or protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1864 columns from Siberia and the Syr Darya took Chimkent, and the foreign minister Gorchakov sent the powers a circular arguing that a frontier facing raiding tribes must move on until it met an organised state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A memorandum of the foreign and war ministries in November 1864 warned the tsar that new conquests would weaken rather than strengthen Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Commanders on the spot preferred to advance, and in June 1865 General Mikhail Chernyaev stormed Tashkent, a city of over 100,000 people, with at most 2,000 men and twelve guns, in defiance of the tsar's orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A governor-generalship of Turkestan was created in 1867 under Konstantin von Kaufman, who took Samarkand in 1868 and imposed treaties on Bukhara that year and on Khiva after a campaign in 1873.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Kokand was abolished in 1876 and became the Russian province of Ferghana, with General Mikhail Skobelev as its first governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Further west the Turkmen fortress of Geok Tepe was stormed in January 1881, and in February 1884 Merv was annexed after its chiefs were pressed into swearing allegiance to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A railway begun for the Turkmen campaign grew within a few years into about 1,450 kilometres (900 miles) of line joining the Caspian Sea to Samarkand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian writers of the time explained the advance chiefly as defence of the frontier, and talk of a civilising mission justified it after the fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Historians have long argued over what to call it, and Soviet scholars first treated it as a seizure of colonies but later presented it as more progressive than British rule would have been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 630–32, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Turkistan: Notes of a Journey in Russian Turkistan, Khokand, Bukhara, and Kuldja</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Scribner, Armstrong, 1877), 112–15, https://archive.org/details/turkistannotesof01schu. [Open access]","George N. Curzon, <i>Russia in Central Asia in 1889 and the Anglo-Russian Question</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1889), 39–45, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027822422. [Open access]","С. Н. Брежнева, «Генерал М. Г. Черняев: личность на фоне военной кампании по завоеванию Туркестана Россией», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/general-m-g-chernyaev-lichnost-na-fone-voennoy-kampanii-po-zavoevaniyu-turkestana-rossiey. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Терентьева, «Англо-русское соперничество и движение России в Среднюю Азию в XIX в. (к историографии вопроса)», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/anglo-russkoe-sopernichestvo-i-dvizhenie-rossii-v-srednyuyu-aziyu-v-xix-v-k-istoriografii-voprosa. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. В. Цыганков, «Среднеазиатская политика Российской империи 1860–1880-х гг. в освещении журнала „Вестник Европы“», <i>Вестник Омского университета</i>, no. 3 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sredneaziatskaya-politika-rossiyskoy-imperii-1860-1880-h-gg-v-osveschenii-zhurnala-vestnik-evropy. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Дубовицкий, «Мотивы присоединения Средней Азии к России: от идеологических домыслов и эмоциональных оценок к геополитическому анализу», <i>История и современность</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/motivy-prisoedineniya-sredney-azii-k-rossii-ot-ideologicheskih-domyslov-i-emotsionalnyh-otsenok-k-geopoliticheskomu-analizu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia keep advancing when its government said it wanted no conquests?","a":"Because officers on the frontier treated perpetual advance as the only practical policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chernyaev took Tashkent almost on his own authority, against orders, although the ministries had warned in 1864 that new conquests would weaken the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the advance worry Britain?","a":"Because Britain had long feared for India, and the capture of Chimkent was enough to alarm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian successes in Central Asia increased that anxiety, and some Russian statesmen saw them as useful pressure on Britain in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a railway follow the conquest?","a":"Because a line begun with a purely strategic purpose, to pacify the Akhal oasis of the Turkmen, proved cheap and effective in the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was then carried on to Merv in 1886 and later to Samarkand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian conquest of Central Asia"},
    "ru-458": {"questions":["By a treaty of 1873 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> declared the slave trade abolished, but full abolition came only with a decree of its young ruler in 1886.","Ruled by emirs of the Uzbek Mangit dynasty until 1920, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost Samarkand and the Zarafshan valley to Russia in the peace of 1868."],"abstract":"The <b>Emirate of Bukhara</b> was the Central Asian state ruled until 1920 by emirs of the Uzbek Mangit dynasty, from the ancient city of Bukhara in what is now Uzbekistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its rulers called themselves emirs once supreme power passed to the Mangits, in 1747 by one reckoning and 1756 by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Emir Muzaffar, who ruled from 1860 to 1885, it fought Russia for the towns of the Syr Darya and was beaten at Irjar in May 1866.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1868 Konstantin von Kaufman took Samarkand and routed the emir again, but refused his offer of unconditional surrender, saying he had never meant to destroy the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the treaty of 23 June 1868, Julian style, the emir gave up the Zarafshan valley with Samarkand, which became a Russian district, and agreed to pay an indemnity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The defeat turned many of his subjects against him, and his eldest son, the Katta Tura, rose in revolt and condemned his father for making peace with Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second treaty, signed on 28 September 1873, regulated trade with Russian merchants and declared the slave trade abolished in Bukharan lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Muzaffar died in 1885 his chosen heir, Abdul Ahad, who had been sent to St Petersburg and Moscow, took the throne with a Russian general at his side, and in 1886 he decreed the total abolition of slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A British visitor of 1888 described a state of qualified independence that kept its court and native government while its emir tacitly took his orders from Tashkent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By then relations with the emir were in the hands of a Russian political agent, while inside the emirate the reformer Ahmad Donish came to call for the overthrow of the Mangit dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 631, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","George N. Curzon, <i>Russia in Central Asia in 1889 and the Anglo-Russian Question</i> (London: Longmans, Green, 1889), 156–60, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027822422. [Open access]","Eugene Schuyler, <i>Turkistan: Notes of a Journey in Russian Turkistan, Khokand, Bukhara, and Kuldja</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Scribner, Armstrong, 1877), 304–11, https://archive.org/details/turkistannotes02schu. [Open access]","Р. Ю. Почекаев, «Политико-правовые реалии Бухары в период установления российского протектората», <i>Восточный архив</i>, no. 1 (35) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politiko-pravovye-realii-buhary-v-period-ustanovleniya-rossiyskogo-protektorata. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Шевцов and Е. Н. Крупенкин, «Протекторат как форма политики Российской империи в Средней Азии», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 91 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protektorat-kak-forma-politiki-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-sredney-azii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Брежнева and О. А. Богданова, «Война Бухарского эмирата с Российской империей в воспоминаниях очевидцев», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i> 44, no. 22 (271) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-buharskogo-emirata-s-rossiyskoy-imperiey-v-vospominaniyah-ochevidtsev. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Маликов, «Стратегии по легитимации власти в Бухарском эмирате (1860–1868)», <i>The Scientific Heritage</i>, no. 104 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/strategii-po-legitimatsii-vlasti-v-buharskom-emirate-1860-1868. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia leave the emir on his throne?","a":"Because Kaufman refused the emir's offer of unconditional surrender in 1868, saying he had never intended to destroy the khanate, and his motives were never made known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia kept a semi-independent emir who took his orders from Tashkent, sparing itself the trouble and cost of annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did slavery survive the treaty of 1873?","a":"Because the treaty abolished the slave trade, but the Russians found it easier to obtain such clauses than to see them carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Total abolition came only by the decree of Emir Abdul Ahad in November 1886, which gave each freed man a written certificate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the emir's own son rebel?","a":"Because the loss of Samarkand and the humiliating peace stirred discontent against the emir, especially among the strict Muslim party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His eldest son, the Katta Tura, took up arms and condemned his father for making peace with Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Emirate of Bukhara"},
    "ru-459": {"questions":["The Panjdeh crisis of 1885, when Russian troops drove Afghans from a disputed oasis, brought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the edge of open war between Britain and Russia.","The Anglo-Russian convention signed in St Petersburg in August 1907 is usually taken to close the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, settling spheres of influence in Persia, Afghanistan and Tibet."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Game</b> is the name, common in Western writing, for the rivalry between the Russian and British empires for dominance in Central Asia in the 19th and early 20th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its limits are disputed, since one scheme runs it from 1813 to the convention of 1907 and a British survey from 1810 to 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The phrase was made famous by Rudyard Kipling's novel <i>Kim</i> of 1901, in which a spy declares that the Great Game is finished only when everyone is dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the British side the driving fear was for India, and the Russian capture of Chimkent in 1864 was enough to cause alarm in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gorchakov answered with a circular claiming that Russia sought only a secure frontier, and in February 1869 he assured Britain that Afghanistan lay entirely outside the Russian sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two governments' first agreement on Central Asia, completed in January 1873, drew a line for the northern frontier of Afghanistan that left Russia out of the oasis of Panjdeh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Russia took Merv, its troops drove an Afghan force out of Panjdeh in March 1885, and the British public clamoured for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> War was averted by the restraint of the Afghan emir Abdur Rahman and the diplomacy of the viceroy Lord Dufferin, who said a war between Britain and Russia would be a fight between a whale and an elephant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An exchange of notes in London and a joint commission fixed the frontier and the two spheres of influence in the Pamirs in 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A convention signed in St Petersburg on 18 August 1907, Julian style, settled the spheres of influence in Persia, Afghanistan and Tibet, though many historians argue the rivalry went on after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 630–32, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 343–44, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]","P. E. Roberts, “The British Empire in India,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 477–78, https://archive.org/details/cu31924070596873. [Open access]","Rudyard Kipling, <i>Kim</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), ch. 12, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2226. [Open access]","М. Габдрашитова, «Этапы „Большой игры“ в Средней Азии», <i>Вестник Волжского университета им. В. Н. Татищева</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/etapy-bolshoy-igry-v-sredney-azii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Г. Данков, «Британская историография второй половины XX в. о „Большой игре“ в Центральной Азии», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 3 (41) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/britanskaya-istoriografiya-vtoroy-poloviny-hh-v-o-bolshoy-igre-v-tsentralnoy-azii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. Х. Шебзухова, «Международно-правовые акты второй половины XIX — начала XX вв. по урегулированию англо-русских противоречий в Средней и Центральной Азии», <i>Вестник Адыгейского государственного университета. Серия: Регионоведение</i>, no. 3 (204) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mezhdunarodno-pravovye-akty-vtoroy-poloviny-xix-nachala-xx-vv-po-uregulirovaniyu-anglo-russkih-protivorechiy-v-sredney-i-tsentralnoy-azii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain care so much about Russian moves in Central Asia?","a":"Because it had long feared for India, and every Russian advance towards the khanates brought Russia nearer to Persia, Afghanistan and India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A forward school in Russia talked of India, but most Russians thought India could not be conquered and held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Panjdeh crisis not become a war?","a":"Because the Afghan emir Abdur Rahman, who was visiting the viceroy when it happened, offered to give up Panjdeh if he could keep Zulfikar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lord Dufferin worked for that settlement, and a boundary commission went on to delimit the Afghan frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about when the Great Game ended?","a":"Because one scheme ends it with the convention of 1907 and another stops at 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many British historians stress that rivalry in Central Asia went on after 1907, and some played down the importance of the agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great Game"},
    "ru-460": {"questions":["Critics mocked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Seward's Folly or Seward's Icebox until the Klondike gold strike of 1896 convinced even the harshest of them.","Agreed at a special meeting in the Russian Foreign Ministry in December 1866, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was completed when the Russian flag came down at Sitka in October 1867."],"abstract":"The <b>Alaska Purchase</b> was the sale of Russia's possessions in North America to the United States for 7.2 million dollars in gold, by a treaty signed in Washington on 30 March 1867, 18 March by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Russian settlement there had always been thin, with no more than about four hundred permanent settlers, and defeat in the Crimean War cooled St Petersburg's interest further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As early as 1857 Grand Duke Konstantin Nikolayevich, the tsar's brother, argued that the colonies brought little benefit and that losing them would cost only compensation to the Russian-American Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Russia offered the territory in 1859, hoping the United States would offset its British rival in the Pacific, but the coming of the American Civil War delayed any sale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decision was finally taken on 16 December 1866, Julian style, at a special meeting in the Foreign Ministry of Alexander II, the grand duke, Gorchakov, Reutern, Krabbe and the envoy Eduard de Stoeckl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In Washington Stoeckl agreed the terms with the secretary of state William Seward, and the Senate approved the treaty on 9 April 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For less than 2 cents an acre the United States gained about 1.5 million square kilometres (600,000 square miles), which critics mocked as Seward's Folly or Seward's Icebox until the Klondike gold strike of 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inhabitants could return to Russia within three years or stay as American citizens, except the so-called uncivilised native tribes, who were left to whatever laws the United States might adopt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 18 October 1867 the Russian flag was lowered at Sitka, the old New Archangel, without banquet or speeches, and American troops at once took over the buildings of the Russian-American Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russian scholars still argue over the motives, since the sum looked tempting to a government facing the company's collapse yet was small beside the empire's spending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “Purchase of Alaska, 1867,” <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Department of State), https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/alaska-purchase. [Open access]","“Treaty concerning the Cession of the Russian Possessions in North America by his Majesty the Emperor of all the Russias to the United States of America,” 30 March 1867, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/treatywi.asp. [Open access]","National Archives, “Check for the Purchase of Alaska (1868),” <i>Milestone Documents</i>, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/check-for-the-purchase-of-alaska. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 632–33, https://archive.org/details/cambridgemodernh11actouoft. [Open access]","Hubert Howe Bancroft, <i>History of Alaska, 1730–1885</i>, The Works of Hubert Howe Bancroft 33 (San Francisco: A. L. Bancroft, 1886), 599–600, https://archive.org/details/histofalaska00bancroft. [Open access]","И. В. Разумовский, «Уступка Россией Аляски США и закат Русской Америки», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, no. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ustupka-rossiey-alyaski-ssha-i-zakat-russkoy-ameriki. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Петров, «Уступка Аляски: дискуссионные вопросы российско-американской сделки 150-летней давности», <i>Новый исторический вестник</i>, no. 2 (52) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ustupka-alyaski-diskussionnye-voprosy-rossiysko-amerikanskoy-sdelki-150-letney-davnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Беляков, «Исследование и анализ причин продажи Аляски США в 1867 году», <i>Юридическая наука и практика: Вестник Нижегородской академии МВД России</i>, no. 19 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/issledovanie-i-analiz-prichin-prodazhi-alyaski-ssha-v-1867-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Russia willing to sell Alaska?","a":"Because it lacked the money to support large settlements or a garrison there, and defeat in the Crimean War further reduced its interest in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grand duke Konstantin argued from 1857 that the colonies brought little benefit and that selling them would save state funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia prefer to sell to the United States?","a":"Because it saw the United States as a counterweight to its great rival in the Pacific, Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia had no clear idea of the territory's value but knew well the difficulty and expense of governing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Americans call the deal Seward's Folly?","a":"Because many thought 7.2 million dollars too much for a remote northern land, and opponents kept up the name until 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great Klondike gold strike of that year convinced even harsh critics that Alaska was a valuable addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alaska Purchase"},
    "ru-461": {"questions":["Navigation of the Amur, Sungari and Ussuri was reserved for Chinese and Russian vessels alone by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also left the land beyond the Ussuri in common possession.","Muravyov earned the title Count Amursky for concluding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Russian historians long described as winning back land given up at Nerchinsk in 1689."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Aigun</b> was the agreement signed at the Manchu town of Aigun on the Amur on 16 May 1858 by the Julian calendar, 28 May by the Gregorian, between Nikolay Muravyov, governor-general of Eastern Siberia, and the Qing commander on the Amur, Yishan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its first article gave Russia the left, northern bank of the Amur from the Argun river to the sea, left the right bank down to the Ussuri to the Qing, and made the land between the Ussuri and the sea a joint possession until a frontier could be drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only Chinese and Russian vessels were to sail the Amur, the Sungari and the Ussuri, a clause that shut out the ships of every other state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Manchus already living on the left bank below the Zeya kept their homes under Manchu rule, and the peoples along the three rivers might trade with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Muravyov had prepared the ground with armed convoys floated down the Amur from 1854, and for the treaty he was rewarded with the title of Count Amursky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A British geographer writing in 1861 put the Qing readiness to sign down to the British and French campaign in China, which spared Muravyov any appeal to arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A fortnight later the Russian envoy Count Putyatin signed the Treaty of Tientsin, and the same year Muravyov founded Blagoveshchensk and Khabarovka on the newly won bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The settlement was not final, because the Qing authorities soon tried to disown the treaty or parts of it, arguing that they had agreed to give up only unsettled land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The treaty signed at Peking two years later confirmed and extended its terms, giving Russia the whole jointly held coast between the Ussuri and the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russian historians have long presented Aigun as the return of land lost at Nerchinsk in 1689, while writers abroad have argued over how far the Qing gave way to the Anglo-French war and how far to Russian pressure itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Treaty of Aigun, 1858,” in <i>Treaties, Conventions, etc., between China and Foreign States</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (Shanghai: Statistical Department of the Inspectorate General of Customs, 1917), 81–84, https://archive.org/details/treatiesconvent01chin. [Open access]","E. G. Ravenstein, <i>The Russians on the Amur: Its Discovery, Conquest, and Colonization</i> (London: Trübner, 1861), 139–43, https://archive.org/details/russiansonamuri00rave. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant: Russia under Nicholas I,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909), 272–73, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529902. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 304–5, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","В. С. Гуревич, «Айгунский договор – памятная дата Еврейской автономной области», <i>Региональные проблемы</i> 26, no. 3 (2023): 79–83, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aygunskiy-dogovor-pamyatnaya-data-evreyskoy-avtonomnoy-oblasti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Г. Темников, «Территориальный вопрос в межгосударственных отношениях России и Китая во второй половине XIX века», <i>Ученые записки Казанского университета. Серия Гуманитарные науки</i> 149, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/territorialnyy-vopros-v-mezhgosudarstvennyh-otnosheniyah-rossii-i-kitaya-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Ананьев, «Присоединение Приамурья и Приморья к России (середина XIX в.) в оценках англо- и немецкоязычных исследователей», <i>Гуманитарные науки в Сибири</i> 27, no. 1 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prisoedinenie-priamurya-i-primorya-k-rossii-seredina-xix-v-v-otsenkah-angloi-nemetskoyazychnyh-issledovateley. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Muravyov get the treaty without fighting?","a":"Because the Qing were at the same moment under pressure from the British and French campaign in China, and Muravyov found the Chinese authorities willing to sign without any appeal to arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some later British writers thought that the threat from Russia itself had mattered as much as the Anglo-French war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty keep other foreign ships off the Amur?","a":"Because its first article allowed only Chinese and Russian vessels on the Amur, the Sungari and the Ussuri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That closed the mouth of the Amur to the warships of Britain and France, which were then fighting China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the treaty not the last word on the frontier?","a":"Because it left the land between the Ussuri and the sea in joint possession until a frontier could be drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That question was settled at Peking in 1860, when the coast passed wholly to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Aigun"},
    "ru-462": {"questions":["By the sixth article of the British <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Qing emperor ceded part of Kowloon facing Hong Kong and agreed to pay eight million taels.","Months after soldiers landed in Golden Horn Bay to found Vladivostok, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew Russia's frontier up the Ussuri and past Lake Khanka to the Tumen river."],"abstract":"The <b>Convention of Peking</b> is the name given to the agreements the Qing government signed in Beijing in October and November 1860, after British and French troops had occupied the city and the emperor had fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the British convention, signed by Lord Elgin and Prince Gong, China regretted the fighting at the Taku forts, accepted a British envoy in Beijing, agreed to pay eight million taels and opened Tianjin to trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sixth article ceded to Britain the part of Kowloon facing Hong Kong, to be held as a dependency of that colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia had not fought, but its envoy Nikolay Ignatyev used the Qing collapse to obtain an Additional Treaty, signed with Prince Gong on 2 November 1860 by the Julian calendar, 14 November by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first article ran the eastern frontier along the Amur and then up the Ussuri and the Sungacha, across Lake Khanka and on to the Tumen river, so that the whole coast east of that line became Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Russians had not waited, for on 20 June 1860, Julian style, soldiers of the 4th East Siberian line battalion under Ensign Komarov landed from the transport Manchzhur in Golden Horn Bay and set up the post of Vladivostok.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within a month four guns from the corvette Gridin stood above the bay, the first battery of the later Vladivostok fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name, roughly 'ruler of the East', belonged to a vocabulary of Pacific ambition in which the bay became the Gulf of Peter the Great and a nearby strait the Eastern Bosphorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A British geographer judged in 1861 that no treaty China had made with a foreign power was more comprehensive, since it gave Russia the coast of Manchuria down to Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The post stayed a small military settlement through the 1860s, and only in 1871, when the main Pacific naval port was moved there from Nikolayevsk, did Vladivostok begin its rise into a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“Additional Treaty of Peking, 1860” and “Convention of Peking (1860),” in <i>Treaties, Conventions, etc., between China and Foreign States</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (Shanghai: Statistical Department of the Inspectorate General of Customs, 1917), 101–19, 430–35, https://archive.org/details/treatiesconvent01chin. [Open access]","E. G. Ravenstein, <i>The Russians on the Amur: Its Discovery, Conquest, and Colonization</i> (London: Trübner, 1861), 151–54, https://archive.org/details/russiansonamuri00rave. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 305, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","Е. Г. Темников, «Территориальный вопрос в межгосударственных отношениях России и Китая во второй половине XIX века», <i>Ученые записки Казанского университета. Серия Гуманитарные науки</i> 149, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/territorialnyy-vopros-v-mezhgosudarstvennyh-otnosheniyah-rossii-i-kitaya-vo-vtoroy-polovine-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Сосна, «Последствия миссии Н. П. Игнатьева в Китай в 1859–1860 гг.», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 2 (104) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/posledstviya-missii-n-p-ignatieva-v-kitay-v-1859-1860-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. С. Авилов, «Владивостокская крепость – форпост Российской империи на Дальнем Востоке (1860–1923 гг.)», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vladivostokskaya-krepost-forpost-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-dalnem-vostoke-1860-1923-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Постников, «К осмыслению названия „Владивосток“: историко-политические образы тихоокеанской России», <i>Ойкумена. Регионоведческие исследования</i>, no. 4 (15) (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-osmysleniyu-nazvaniya-vladivostok-istoriko-politicheskie-obrazy-tihookeanskoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. З. Позняк, «Города дальневосточной окраины: условия и факторы роста (вторая половина XIX – начало XX в.)», <i>Ойкумена. Регионоведческие исследования</i>, no. 3 (4) (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/goroda-dalnevostochnoy-okrainy-usloviya-i-faktory-rosta-vtoraya-polovina-xix-nachalo-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Russia gain so much without fighting?","a":"Because the Qing had just been defeated by Britain and France, whose troops held Beijing, and Ignatyev used that moment to obtain a treaty far more favourable than any before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It confirmed the Aigun and Tientsin treaties and recognised the Ussuri country as Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Vladivostok founded before the treaty was signed?","a":"Because Muravyov ordered troops to occupy Golden Horn Bay in June 1860, months before the Russian frontier there was agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The treaty of November then placed the whole coast down to Korea inside Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Vladivostok grow slowly at first?","a":"Because through the 1860s it was a small military post whose people were mostly soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It began to grow only after 1871, when the main naval port on the Pacific was moved there from Nikolayevsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Convention of Peking"},
    "ru-463": {"questions":["Romania declared its independence and sent its army across the Danube during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which Plevna held up the Russian advance for five months.","General Gurko's dash over the Balkan mountains to seize the Shipka pass from the rear was an early stroke of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ended by an armistice at Adrianople."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Turkish War of 1877–1878</b> was the last of the 19th century's wars between Russia and the Ottoman Empire, declared on 12 April 1877 by the Julian calendar, 24 April by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the risings in Bosnia, Herzegovina and Bulgaria, and Alexander II's manifesto, proclaimed at Kishinev, blamed the Porte's refusal of any guarantee for the safety of the Balkan Christians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reports of massacres in Bulgaria divided British opinion, though the British government remained the friend of the empire it had defended in the Crimean War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romania let the Russian army cross its territory under a convention of April 1877, proclaimed its independence in May and later sent its own troops into the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian forces crossed the Danube in June near Sistova, and General Gurko raced over the Balkan mountains and took the Shipka pass from the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The advance then stalled for five months before Plevna, where Osman Pasha beat off three assaults and held out until 10 December 1877, when about 39,000 men surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fall of Plevna freed more than 100,000 Russian troops, who crossed the Balkans in winter, took Sofia, surrounded the Shipka army at Sheinovo and entered Adrianople in January 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Caucasus the Russians took the fortress of Kars, and in the west Serbia and Montenegro won victories of their own against the Turks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An armistice was signed at Adrianople on 31 January 1878, while a British fleet passed the Dardanelles and the Russian army advanced to San Stefano on the Sea of Marmara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peace signed there in March was largely undone at Berlin that summer, a gap that reflected the separate policy pursued by the ambassador Nikolay Ignatyev outside the foreign ministry's official line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Miller, “The Ottoman Empire and the Balkan Peninsula,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 387–92, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 347–80, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","F. V. Greene, <i>The Russian Army and Its Campaigns in Turkey in 1877–1878</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1879), 236, https://archive.org/details/russianarmyitsca00greeuoft. [Open access]","М. М. Фролова, «Миссия полковника Э. Ариона и Сан-Стефанский мирный договор 19 февраля (3 марта) 1878 г.», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2026): 68–97, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/missiya-polkovnika-e-ariona-i-san-stefanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-19-fevralya-3-marta-1878-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Лукоянов, «Русско-турецкая война 1877–1878 гг. и изменения в механизмах внешней политики России», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1877-1878-gg-i-izmeneniya-v-mehanizmah-vneshney-politiki-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Б. Полякова, «Гренадеры своим товарищам, павшим в славном бою под Плевной 28 ноября 1877 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 11 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grenadery-svoim-tovarischam-pavshim-v-slavnom-boyu-pod-plevnoy-28-noyabrya-1877-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Манахова, «„Победа русских оплачена дорогой ценой...“: разгром турецких войск под Плевной в оценках британских военных корреспондентов», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 11 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobeda-russkih-oplachena-dorogoy-tsenoy-razgrom-turetskih-voysk-pod-plevnoy-v-otsenkah-britanskih-voennyh-korrespondentov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Romania matter so much to the Russian plan?","a":"Because the Turkish fleet controlled the Black Sea, so Russia could attack only by land, and the road ran through Romania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A convention of April 1877 let the Russian army use Romanian railways, rivers, roads and telegraph lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain send its fleet towards Constantinople?","a":"Because the British government saw a Russian occupation of the city as a threat to its Indian empire, and the fleet passed the Dardanelles as the Russians drew near.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> British opinion was divided, since the reports of massacres in Bulgaria had turned many against the Turks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fall of Plevna change the war?","a":"Because the fortress had held up the whole Russian advance for five months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its surrender freed more than 100,000 men, who crossed the Balkans in winter and reached Adrianople within weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Turkish War (1877–1878)"},
    "ru-464": {"questions":["Romanian troops planted their colours on the Grivitsa redoubt during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after Prince Charles had been given command of the allied army before the town.","A chapel near the Polytechnic Museum in Moscow honours the grenadiers who fell on the last day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Osman's breakout across the Vid failed."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Plevna</b> was the defence of the Bulgarian town now called Pleven by Osman Pasha's Ottoman army against Russian and later Romanian forces from 8 July to 28 November 1877 by the Julian calendar, 20 July to 10 December by the Gregorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Osman had marched from Vidin with about 40,000 of the best Turkish troops, arrived too late to save Nikopol and turned aside into Plevna, on the flank of the Russian advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first Russian attack on 20 July was beaten back with 74 officers and 2,771 men killed and wounded, and a second ten days later ended in a far heavier defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Osman used every pause to ring the town with earthworks and redoubts, the strongest of them on the heights of Grivitsa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the second defeat the Russians turned to Prince Charles of Romania, who was given command of the allied force before the town, and Romanian troops crossed the Danube to join the siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The third and greatest assault, in September, won one Grivitsa redoubt for the Romanians but failed elsewhere and cost the Russians about 18,000 men out of 60,000 engaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> General Totleben was then summoned to invest the town, and after the Russians took Gorni Dubnik and Telish in October the blockade was complete by early November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Skobelev's division dug its trenches right up to the Turkish lines on the Green Hills, and the allied army around the town grew to about 130,000 men with 560 guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> With his food gone, Osman tried to break out across the river Vid on 10 December, failed against the Grenadier Corps and surrendered with about 39,000 men, among them 2,128 officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> British correspondents wrote that the Russian victory had been dearly bought, and the grenadiers who fell on the last day are remembered by a chapel in the centre of Moscow, opened in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["F. V. Greene, <i>The Russian Army and Its Campaigns in Turkey in 1877–1878</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1879), 236, https://archive.org/details/russianarmyitsca00greeuoft. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 352–67, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","William Miller, “The Ottoman Empire and the Balkan Peninsula,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 388–89, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Е. Б. Полякова, «Гренадеры своим товарищам, павшим в славном бою под Плевной 28 ноября 1877 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 11 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grenadery-svoim-tovarischam-pavshim-v-slavnom-boyu-pod-plevnoy-28-noyabrya-1877-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Манахова, «„Победа русских оплачена дорогой ценой...“: разгром турецких войск под Плевной в оценках британских военных корреспондентов», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 11 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobeda-russkih-oplachena-dorogoy-tsenoy-razgrom-turetskih-voysk-pod-plevnoy-v-otsenkah-britanskih-voennyh-korrespondentov. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Фролова, «Миссия полковника Э. Ариона и Сан-Стефанский мирный договор 19 февраля (3 марта) 1878 г.», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2026): 68–97, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/missiya-polkovnika-e-ariona-i-san-stefanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-19-fevralya-3-marta-1878-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Б. Шолохов, «Генерал от инфантерии М. Д. Скобелев: „Силы не может быть вне народа“», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 9 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/general-ot-infanterii-m-d-skobelev-sily-ne-mozhet-byt-vne-naroda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Russians caught out at Plevna?","a":"Because no one followed up the reports of Osman's march, and his army reached the Russian flank without a single officer knowing of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first attack went in without proper reconnaissance and was driven back with heavy loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia ask Romania for help?","a":"Because after two failed assaults the Russian command had to wait for the Guards and the Grenadiers, and it asked Prince Charles to join the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He took command of the allied force before the town, and his troops won one of the Grivitsa redoubts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians stop storming the town and blockade it?","a":"Because the September assault cost about 18,000 men without taking the position, and Totleben, called in to direct the investment, refused to risk another storm while hunger did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Osman tried to break out in December he failed, and about 39,000 men surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Siege of Plevna"},
    "ru-465": {"questions":["Romania, which had fought beside Russia at Plevna, was kept out of the talks that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stood to lose southern Bessarabia by it.","On 30 May 1878, Salisbury and Shuvalov agreed in London to cut down the 'big Bulgaria' of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the powers met at Berlin."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of San Stefano</b> was the preliminary peace that ended the Russo-Turkish War, signed on 19 February 1878 by the Julian calendar, 3 March by the Gregorian, at the village of San Stefano on the Sea of Marmara outside Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was signed by Count Nikolay Ignatyev and Alexander Nelidov for Russia and by Safvet Pasha and Sadullah Bey for the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its centrepiece was a large autonomous Bulgarian principality, tributary to the sultan, reaching from the Danube to the Aegean and taking in the Macedonian lakes of Ohrid and Prespa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Serbia, Montenegro and Romania were to be independent, Montenegro was more than trebled in size, and the fortresses on the Danube were to be razed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia took Kars, Ardahan, Batum and Bayazet in Asia, and of the war indemnity 1,100 million roubles were to be paid in land, including the Dobruja to be exchanged for southern Bessarabia, and 310 million in money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty was above all Ignatyev's own design, a big Bulgaria with outlets to the Aegean through which Russia might dominate the Balkans, pursued outside the foreign ministry's official line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Romania, whose army had fought at Plevna, was kept out of the negotiations, and its envoy Colonel Arion could not stop the plan to take back southern Bessarabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greeks, Serbs, Albanians and Muslims all protested against the new frontiers, and Athens, Bucharest and Belgrade disliked a San Stefano Bulgaria as much as Britain and Austria-Hungary did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain held that no treaty between Russia and Turkey touching the settlements of 1856 and 1871 was valid without the other powers, and on 30 May 1878 Salisbury and Shuvalov agreed in London to revise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Congress of Berlin replaced it in July, but the big Bulgaria of San Stefano remained an ideal that Bulgarians long regretted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 4 (London: Butterworths, 1891), vii–viii, 2656–57, 2672–96, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV4. [Open access]","William Miller, “The Ottoman Empire and the Balkan Peninsula,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 391–94, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 377–79, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","М. М. Фролова, «Миссия полковника Э. Ариона и Сан-Стефанский мирный договор 19 февраля (3 марта) 1878 г.», <i>Славянский альманах</i>, no. 1–2 (2026): 68–97, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/missiya-polkovnika-e-ariona-i-san-stefanskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-19-fevralya-3-marta-1878-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Лукоянов, «Русско-турецкая война 1877–1878 гг. и изменения в механизмах внешней политики России», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1877-1878-gg-i-izmeneniya-v-mehanizmah-vneshney-politiki-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. А. Искендеров, «Берлинский конгресс 1878 г. и его политические последствия для Балкан», <i>Славянский мир в третьем тысячелетии</i>, no. 11 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/berlinskiy-kongress-1878-g-i-ego-politicheskie-posledstviya-dlya-balkan. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Кенкишвили, «К вопросу о взаимосвязи англо-турецкой конвенции и Берлинского конгресса 1878 г.», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-vzaimosvyazi-anglo-turetskoy-konventsii-i-berlinskogo-kongressa-1878-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain object so strongly to the treaty?","a":"Because it feared that the big Bulgaria would be a Russian province threatening Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also held that no treaty between Russia and Turkey affecting the settlements of 1856 and 1871 could be valid without the other powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Romania angry with its ally?","a":"Because its army had fought for Russia at Plevna, yet it was kept out of the talks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia meant to take back southern Bessarabia, which Romania had held since 1856, and give it the Dobruja instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the treaty bear one man's stamp?","a":"Because Ignatyev, who negotiated it, pursued his own plan for a big Bulgaria with outlets to the Aegean, apart from the official course of the foreign ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The large gap between its terms and the Berlin settlement shows how far that plan had run ahead of Russia's real position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of San Stefano"},
    "ru-466": {"questions":["Ivan Aksakov was banished to his estates for calling the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an impudent outrage upon Russia, though Russia kept Kars, Ardahan and Batum by it.","Austria-Hungary won the right to occupy Bosnia and Herzegovina at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which sat for exactly a month in the summer of 1878."],"abstract":"The <b>Congress of Berlin</b> was the meeting of the great powers and the Ottoman Empire that sat in the German capital from 13 June to 13 July 1878 to revise the Treaty of San Stefano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia, Britain, Austria-Hungary, France, Italy and Germany sent their leading statesmen, among them Gorchakov, Disraeli, Salisbury and Andrássy, and Bismarck, who had offered himself as an 'honest broker', presided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much had been settled in advance, above all by the Anglo-Russian agreement of 30 May on cutting down Bulgaria, so that in some respects the congress registered bargains already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The treaty signed on 13 July recognised the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania and made Bulgaria an autonomous principality tributary to the sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South of the Balkan range a separate province, Eastern Rumelia, stayed under the sultan's direct authority with a Christian governor-general, and the lands from the Rhodope to the Pindus went back to Ottoman rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Russia kept southern Bessarabia, Kars, Ardahan and Batum, which was to be a free port, but the Bulgaria it had created was split in three, a gap between San Stefano and Berlin that reflected divisions inside Russian policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Austria-Hungary received the right to occupy and administer Bosnia and Herzegovina and to keep garrisons in the Sanjak of Novi Pazar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain had secured Cyprus by a separate defensive convention with the sultan, and writers still disagree whether the island passed to Britain through that convention or through the congress itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At a meeting of the Moscow Slavonic Committee Ivan Aksakov called the congress an impudent outrage upon Russia, and he was banished to his estates for saying so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chief Ottoman delegate, Alexander Karatheodori Pasha, soon saw that the congress aimed at revising the results of the war, and the later peace between Russia and the Porte followed the Berlin treaty closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Miller, “The Ottoman Empire and the Balkan Peninsula,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 393–97, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Edward Hertslet, ed., <i>The Map of Europe by Treaty</i>, vol. 4 (London: Butterworths, 1891), vii–viii, 2769–98, https://archive.org/details/MapOfEuropeByTreatyV4. [Open access]","Alfred Rambaud, <i>History of Russia from the Earliest Times to 1880</i>, trans. L. B. Lang, ed. and enlarged by Nathan Haskell Dole, vol. 3 (Boston: H. A. Bolles, 1882), 378–80, https://archive.org/details/historyofrussiaf03ramb. [Open access]","П. А. Искендеров, «Берлинский конгресс 1878 г. и его политические последствия для Балкан», <i>Славянский мир в третьем тысячелетии</i>, no. 11 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/berlinskiy-kongress-1878-g-i-ego-politicheskie-posledstviya-dlya-balkan. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. Михнева and В. Колев, «Берлинский конгресс 1878 года глазами фанариота Александра Каратеодори-паши», <i>Вестник МГИМО-Университета</i> 14, no. 4 (2021): 7–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/berlinskiy-kongress-1878-goda-glazami-fanariota-aleksandra-karateodori-pashi. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Кенкишвили, «К вопросу о взаимосвязи англо-турецкой конвенции и Берлинского конгресса 1878 г.», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Северо-Кавказский регион. Общественные науки</i>, no. 4 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-vzaimosvyazi-anglo-turetskoy-konventsii-i-berlinskogo-kongressa-1878-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Лукоянов, «Русско-турецкая война 1877–1878 гг. и изменения в механизмах внешней политики России», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russko-turetskaya-voyna-1877-1878-gg-i-izmeneniya-v-mehanizmah-vneshney-politiki-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the powers insist on revising the treaty of San Stefano?","a":"Because Britain saw the big Bulgaria as a Russian outpost menacing Constantinople, and Austria-Hungary feared it would cut off its road to Salonica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other powers would not let Russia settle alone questions that concerned the whole of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the result resented in Russia?","a":"Because Russia had made immense sacrifices in blood and money and many felt that its gains scarcely repaid them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aksakov said so openly at the Moscow Slavonic Committee and was banished for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Cyprus linked with the congress?","a":"Because Britain occupied the island under a defensive convention with the sultan made in June, alongside the diplomacy of the congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writers have disagreed ever since whether the convention or the congress itself gave the island to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Congress of Berlin"},
    "ru-467": {"questions":["Ivan Aksakov did more than the novelist Boborykin, who later claimed the credit, to give the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its social meaning in the Russian press of the 1860s.","Feeling that their learning had been paid for by the peasants' labour, many members of the Russian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spoke of a debt they owed the people."],"abstract":"The <b>intelligentsia</b> was the name that educated Russians of the 1860s and after gave to themselves as a social group, the thinking and critically minded part of society, conscious of itself as a group with a public role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Latin-derived word had been used earlier for consciousness or understanding, and in 1836 the poet Vasily Zhukovsky wrote in his diary of the St Petersburg nobility as Russia's 'European intelligentsia'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its social meaning took shape in the 1860s, apparently under Polish influence, and the Slavophile publicist Ivan Aksakov did much to bring it into Russian journalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The novelist Pyotr Boborykin later claimed to have introduced the word, but he first used it in 1866 in a different sense and at most widened its use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the late 1870s it was used by writers of every camp, and the 1881 edition of Dahl's dictionary defined it as the reasonable, educated and mentally developed part of the inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the new word stood a real change, as young men and women of non-noble birth, the raznochintsy or 'people of various ranks', entered the universities and the press in growing numbers in the years of reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many educated Russians came to feel that they enjoyed the benefits of culture only at the expense of the people, and repaying that debt became a central theme of the radical intelligentsia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That sense of debt fed directly into populism, which some historians describe as the way the intelligentsia defined itself through the idea of serving the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Small circles for self-education sprang up in towns across Russia, and Kropotkin recalled that their endless reading and discussion turned on one question, how they could be useful to the masses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From the 1880s Russian socialist writers began to define the intelligentsia less by education than by commitment to ideas, a shift away from the older sense of an educated public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ф. А. Гайда, «„Русская интеллигенция“: рождение понятия», <i>Философия. Журнал Высшей школы экономики</i> 4, no. 2 (2020): 229–48, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-intelligentsiya-rozhdenie-ponyatiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Мотин, «О понятии „интеллигенция“ в творчестве И. С. Аксакова и П. Д. Боборыкина», <i>Известия Пензенского государственного педагогического университета им. В. Г. Белинского</i>, no. 27 (2012): 838–44, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-ponyatii-intelligentsiya-v-tvorchestve-i-s-aksakova-i-p-d-boborykina. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: A. A. Knopf, 1916), 69, 207–09, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis00korn. [Open access]","А. А. Ширинянц, «Из истории самоопределения гражданского общества в России: разночинная интеллигенция 1860–1870 гг.», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 6 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-samoopredeleniya-grazhdanskogo-obschestva-v-rossii-raznochinnaya-intelligentsiya-1860-1870-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Д. Карпачев, «Феномен народничества в жизни русской интеллигенции», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 2 (2026): 126–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-narodnichestva-v-zhizni-russkoy-intelligentsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Мокшин, «Что такое народничество? Материалы к дискуссии», <i>Вестник Сургутского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 3 (66) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chto-takoe-narodnichestvo-materialy-k-diskussii. [in Russian] [Open access]","P. Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, part 4 (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1906), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73882. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the word take on a social meaning in the 1860s?","a":"Because new people of non-noble birth were entering the universities and the press, and educated society needed a name for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The social sense of the word seems to have reached Russia through Polish usage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did educated Russians speak of a debt to the people?","a":"Because they believed that every educated person enjoyed culture only at the expense of the peasants whose labour supported it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paying back that debt became the aim of much radical activity, and fed the populist movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Boborykin's claim to have invented the word doubted?","a":"Because Aksakov had already used it in its social sense, while Boborykin first used it in 1866 in a quite different meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He spread the word rather than created it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Intelligentsia"},
    "ru-468": {"questions":["Dmitry Pisarev's journal the <i>Russian Word</i> was the chief organ of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which made the spread of natural science its weapon against prejudice.","Short-haired young women in plain dark dresses who left their families to study were among the most visible followers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Russia of the 1860s."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian nihilist movement</b> was the revolt of part of Russia's educated youth in the 1860s against the authority of the state, the church, the family and inherited custom, in the name of reason and natural science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its name came from Ivan Turgenev's novel <i>Fathers and Children</i> of 1862, in which Arkady calls his friend Bazarov a man who does not bow down before any authority or take any principle on faith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself was older, for the critic Nikolay Nadezhdin had used 'nihilists' as a term of abuse in 1829, and Mikhail Katkov turned it against the radical journal <i>The Contemporary</i> in 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The chief organ of the nihilists was the <i>Russian Word</i>, whose young critic Dmitry Pisarev made the spread of the natural sciences his weapon against every kind of authority and superstition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their programme was above all personal, a 'struggle for individuality' that sought to free a person from the chains of family, society and religion, in Kropotkin's phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It showed in daily life, where young men let their hair grow long, young women cut theirs short, and both dressed plainly and dropped the polite forms of noble society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kropotkin, who grew up among them, wrote that the nihilist declared war on the 'conventional lies' of civilised life and that girls from wealthy homes left them to study and earn their own living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of the movement came from young people of non-noble birth, for whom the good manners of the nobility were the mark of a hostile society built on serfdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fires in St Petersburg in 1862, wrongly blamed on the young radicals, turned moderate opinion against them, and the government arrested Pisarev and suspended <i>The Contemporary</i> and the <i>Russian Word</i> for eight months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kropotkin rejected the later Western habit of calling revolutionary terror nihilism, and British observers saw the movement passing at the end of the 1860s from talk into active revolutionary socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ivan Turgenev, <i>Fathers and Children</i>, ch. 5, trans. Constance Garnett, Harvard Classics Shelf of Fiction 19 (New York: P. F. Collier & Son, 1917), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30723. [Open access]","P. Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, part 4 (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1906), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73882. [Open access]","A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: A. A. Knopf, 1916), 69–70, 80–83, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis00korn. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 292–316, https://archive.org/details/russia03wallgoog. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant after the Crimean War: Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909), 629–30, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529902. [Open access]","М. А. Ицкович, «Субкультура русского нигилизма 1860-х годов и её социальная направленность», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i> 11, no. 6-1 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/subkultura-russkogo-nigilizma-1860-h-godov-i-eyo-sotsialnaya-napravlennost. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Ширинянц, «Русское общество и политика в XIX веке: революционный нигилизм», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 12. Политические науки</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkoe-obschestvo-i-politika-v-xix-veke-revolyutsionnyy-nigilizm. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the nihilists attack good manners and fine clothes?","a":"Because they saw the customs of noble society as part of a system built on the unpaid labour of serfs, and refused to live like the class they rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kropotkin described this as a war on the 'conventional lies' of civilised life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Pisarev put his faith in natural science?","a":"Because he believed that spreading science and its conclusions was the best weapon against the prejudices and superstitions that bound Russian life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That made his attacks on every authority seem more dangerous to the government than open socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it wrong to say that Turgenev invented the word?","a":"Because 'nihilist' had been used as a term of abuse since 1829 and was turned against the radical press by Katkov in 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Turgenev's novel gave the word its fame and fixed it on the young radicals of the 1860s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian nihilist movement"},
    "ru-469": {"questions":["Sentenced to fourteen years of hard labour on a spy's evidence, the radical critic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent about twelve years in exile in the Siberian town of Vilyuysk.","Taking over the economics and peasant departments of <i>The Contemporary</i> in 1858, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed its literary criticism to the young Nikolay Dobrolyubov."],"abstract":"<b>Nikolay Chernyshevsky</b> was a Russian radical critic, economist and novelist from Saratov who became the leading voice of the journal <i>The Contemporary</i> in the years before the emancipation of the serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1858 he took charge of its department of economics and the peasant question and gave the young Nikolay Dobrolyubov the post of literary critic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He at first praised Alexander II's plans to free the serfs, but by the end of 1858 he was attacking the greed of the landowners and urging that the land pass to the peasants through redemption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British account of 1909 credited him with grafting socialism onto the purely destructive doctrine preached by Mikhail Bakunin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> People who knew him well described him as a man of encyclopaedic learning, above all in political economy, and one of the ablest of his generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was arrested in 1862 and held in the Peter and Paul Fortress, where in about three and a half months he wrote the novel <i>What Is to Be Done?</i>, published in 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Senate sentenced him to fourteen years of hard labour for supposedly writing a proclamation to the landowners' peasants, a charge that rested on a spy's testimony and a disputed sample of handwriting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He spent about twelve years in exile at Vilyuysk in Siberia, where revolutionaries, among them Ippolit Myshkin disguised as an officer of gendarmes, tried more than once to free him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1883 he was moved to Astrakhan, and in 1889 he was allowed home to Saratov, where he died a few months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His novel shaped the conduct of a generation, for Kropotkin wrote that young radicals found better portraits of themselves in its pages than in Turgenev's Bazarov, and Lenin later took its title for a book of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: A. A. Knopf, 1916), 57–60, 80–82, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis00korn. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 299, https://archive.org/details/russia03wallgoog. [Open access]","P. Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, part 4 (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1906), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73882. [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant after the Crimean War: Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909), 630, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529902. [Open access]","Е. В. Бессчетнова, review of <i>Н. Г. Чернышевский. Научная биография (1859–1889)</i>, by А. А. Демченко, <i>Философические письма. Русско-европейский диалог</i> 2, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/retsenziya-na-knigu-demchenko-a-a-n-g-chernyshevskiy-nauchnaya-biografiya-1859-1889-m-politicheskaya-entsiklopediya-2019-687-s. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Лепов, А. Г. Пудов and Е. Д. Кули, «Сеятель великих истин (к 190-летию со дня рождения Н. Г. Чернышевского)», <i>Наука и техника в Якутии</i>, no. 1 (36) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/seyatel-velikih-istin-k-190-letiyu-so-dnya-rozhdeniya-n-g-chernyshevskogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Г. Юрченко, review of <i>«Срубленное древо жизни». Судьба Николая Чернышевского</i>, by В. К. Кантор, <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 7. Литературоведение</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2019-01-025-kantor-v-k-srublennoe-drevo-zhizni-sudba-nikolaya-chernyshevskogo-m-spb-tsentr-gumanitarnyh-initsiativ-2016-528-s-seriya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was he arrested?","a":"Because the government blamed the radical press for the revolutionary proclamations of 1861 and 1862 and began arresting those who had dealings with Herzen abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His visit to Herzen in London gave the pretext, and a denunciation by Vsevolod Kostomarov supplied the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his novel matter so much?","a":"Because young people tried to live by its practical ideals, forming circles and associations on its model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kropotkin wrote that they saw better portraits of themselves in it than in Turgenev's nihilist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he stay in Siberia so long?","a":"Because every attempt to rescue him failed and each failure made his conditions harsher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One rescuer, disguised as an officer of gendarmes, was caught on the road, and he was moved to Astrakhan only in 1883.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nikolay Chernyshevsky"},
    "ru-470": {"questions":["Lavrov's <i>Historical Letters</i>, with their call to repay the debt of the educated minority, became a guide for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who saw in the peasantry the key to Russia's future.","Both Plekhanov and Lenin started out among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name, taken from the Russian word for 'people', was later stretched to cover thinkers from Herzen to Tolstoy."],"abstract":"The <b>Narodniks</b>, from the Russian <i>narod</i>, 'people', were the Russian populists of the 1860s to the 1880s, educated radicals who believed that Russia could reach socialism through its peasantry without passing through Western capitalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their hopes rested on an idealised peasant and his commune, which held land in common and redistributed the fields, and which Alexander Herzen had made the basis of what he called Russian socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herzen, Nikolay Chernyshevsky and the critic Nikolay Dobrolyubov are counted among the forerunners, and Dobrolyubov was the first herald of populist ideals in Russian criticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writers of humble origin such as Nikolay Uspensky and Fyodor Reshetnikov described the misery of the freed peasants and made the intelligentsia ask what it owed the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herzen's cry 'To the people!', addressed in his <i>Bell</i> to students expelled from the universities in 1861, was taken up by populist literature and caught the minds of the young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By about 1870 two émigré leaders offered rival paths, for Pyotr Lavrov urged a long preparation of the masses by educated propagandists while Mikhail Bakunin wanted to stir them to immediate revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Lavrov's view, a practical development of the earlier nihilism, at first won far more followers than Bakunin's anarchist plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most populists rejected the authoritarian conspiracy preached by Pyotr Tkachev and the amoral methods of Sergey Nechayev, and the founders of the movement did not teach terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its boundaries have always been blurred, for already in 1884 a critic called populism vague and arbitrary, and historians still disagree whether Herzen himself should be called a Narodnik.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both Georgy Plekhanov and Lenin began as populists, and Russian Marxism grew up in argument with the movement, though historians still debate how much it owed to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["A. A. Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: A. A. Knopf, 1916), 207–11, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis00korn. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new ed., vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1905), 317–22, https://archive.org/details/russia03wallgoog. [Open access]","Ю. А. Зеленин, «Базовые компоненты идеологии классического народничества в России», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i>, no. 4-3 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bazovye-komponenty-ideologii-klassicheskogo-narodnichestva-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. Н. Мокшин, «Что такое народничество? Материалы к дискуссии», <i>Вестник Сургутского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 3 (66) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chto-takoe-narodnichestvo-materialy-k-diskussii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Д. Карпачев, «Феномен народничества в жизни русской интеллигенции», <i>Новое прошлое / The New Past</i>, no. 2 (2026): 126–45, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-narodnichestva-v-zhizni-russkoy-intelligentsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Geoffrey Drage, “Russia and the Levant after the Crimean War: Russia and the Period of Reform,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Growth of Nationalities</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1909), 629–30, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529902. [Open access]","А. А. Ширинянц, «Из истории самоопределения гражданского общества в России: разночинная интеллигенция 1860–1870 гг.», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 6 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-samoopredeleniya-grazhdanskogo-obschestva-v-rossii-raznochinnaya-intelligentsiya-1860-1870-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Narodniks put their hopes in the peasant commune?","a":"Because they saw in its common landholding a native basis for socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herzen had built his 'Russian socialism' on the commune and its redistribution of the fields, and later populists followed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lavrov and Bakunin disagree?","a":"Because Bakunin thought the masses had only to feel their oppression to rise at once, while Lavrov held that a lasting revolution needed a whole generation of propagandists to enlighten the people first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lavrov's formula was broad enough to be shared by revolutionaries and peaceful populists alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the word hard to define?","a":"Because as early as 1884 a leading critic found populism vague and arbitrary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians today still disagree about whom it should include, and whether Russian Marxism grew out of it or beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Narodniks"},
    "ru-471": {"questions":["A memorandum by Count Pahlen counted 770 people caught up in 37 provinces during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose arrests later filled the dock at the Trial of the 193 in 1877.","Educated young people who settled as artisans, teachers and clerks during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found that villagers could keep no secret, and some peasants handed them over to the police."],"abstract":"The <b>going to the people</b> was the mass movement of 1874 in which thousands of educated young Russians left the cities for the villages to spread socialist ideas among the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its watchword, as the anarchist Peter Kropotkin remembered it, was 'V narod!', to the people, and he insisted that it was a sudden awakening of conscience rather than an organised campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its teachers abroad were divided, for Pyotr Lavrov urged a long and patient propaganda to prepare the masses, while Mikhail Bakunin called for immediate revolt and held that even a small rising was the best propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the government in 1873 ordered Russian students, many of them young women, to leave Zurich by 1 January 1874, almost all came home at once and took the order as a signal to go to the villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The young propagandists dressed as peasants and found work as artisans, teachers and clerks, hoping that a people who already held land in common through the village commune would welcome socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Most of them acted clumsily and openly, and within two or three months the police had begun a sweeping investigation of what Kropotkin's circle came to call the 'mad summer'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A memorandum by the justice minister, Count Pahlen, counted 770 people charged in 37 provinces, 612 of them men and 158 women, while Kropotkin put the arrests at no fewer than 1,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later estimates put the active participants at two or three thousand, with several times as many sympathisers who sheltered or helped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The peasants mostly shunned the strangers, could keep nothing secret in villages where every newcomer was watched, and in some places handed them over to the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those arrested filled the dock at the Trial of the 193 in 1877, where prosecutors struggled to prove that one organised society lay behind the movement, and its failure pushed the survivors towards Land and Liberty and then towards terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 219–23, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 2 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 90, 97–98, 157–58, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut02kropuoft. [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 17–26, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 556–59, 562–63, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","Т. Рокки, «Народническое „Хождение в народ“ 1874 г.: первое крупное политико-эсхатологическое движение в Российской империи. Часть первая: постановка вопросов», <i>Tractus aevorum</i> 13, no. 1 (2026): 34–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/narodnicheskoe-hozhdenie-v-narod-1874-g-pervoe-krupnoe-politiko-eshatologicheskoe-dvizhenie-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-chast-pervaya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Рубцов, «Социальные сети революционного народничества 1870-х годов в материалах следственных дел», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 2 (41) (2018): 75–84, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialnye-seti-revolyutsionnogo-narodnichestva-1870-h-godov-v-materialah-sledstvennyh-del. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the peasants not respond as the propagandists hoped?","a":"Because the strangers stood out at once in villages where every newcomer was watched, and the peasants could not keep the reading of a forbidden book secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many shunned the propagandists and some handed them over to the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The revolutionaries' own later proclamation admitted that the people did not understand its friends and showed itself hostile to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many young people go at the same moment?","a":"Because the government's order of 1873 recalling Russian students from Zurich brought almost all of them home together, eager to begin propaganda at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They treated the deadline of 1 January 1874 as a signal to go to the villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kropotkin also saw a deeper cause in a generation that refused to live on its parents' wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the movement matter to what followed?","a":"Because its failure convinced many survivors that propaganda among peasants was impossible under the existing regime, so they built the firmer organisation of Land and Liberty, part of which turned to terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mass arrests also produced the Trial of the 193, whose prosecutors had to argue that an organised society stood behind the movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Going to the People"},
    "ru-472": {"questions":["The red flag first raised at the Kazan Cathedral demonstration of December 1876 was the opening act of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose young speaker that day was Georgi Plekhanov.","Meeting at Voronezh in June 1879 after a secret caucus of its terrorist wing at Lipetsk, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split into the People's Will and the smaller Black Repartition."],"abstract":"<b>Land and Liberty</b> was the secret society of Russian revolutionary populists formed in St Petersburg in 1876, when survivors of the arrests of 1874 decided that their movement needed a firmer organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its head stood Mark Natanson, his wife Olga and Alexander Mikhailov, and after Mark's arrest Olga became one of the leaders of the circle that took the name Land and Liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its programme held that only an economic revolution from below could build a just society, so its members prepared a peasant rising and sought allies among religious dissenters and city workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Besides a 'heavenly chancery' that forged passports, it had groups for students and workers, a 'disorganising' group for armed action against officials and traitors, and, largest of all, the 'rustics' who lived in the villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its slogan echoed the land and freedom the peasants had expected since the emancipation, and it was also the title of the society's newspaper, whose first issue appeared on 25 October 1878 by the Julian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its first public act was a demonstration at the Kazan Cathedral in St Petersburg on 6 December 1876, Julian style, where a red flag was raised and the young Georgi Plekhanov spoke before the police scattered the few hundred present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The rustics grew more cautious after 1874 but concluded that the peasants were not ready for their ideas, while the disorganising group grew until it became the core of a terrorist party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south Valerian Osinsky's circle called itself the society's executive committee and accepted the usefulness of a constitution, and the programme of April 1878 admitted that some link with liberals was necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The quarrel between the rustics and the politicals came to a head at a congress in Voronezh on 18–21 June 1879, after the terrorist wing led by Zhelyabov had met separately at Lipetsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The society then split, the larger People's Will making systematic terror its chief weapon, while Plekhanov's smaller Black Repartition kept to social propaganda among the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 220–23, 237–38, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 558, 562–63, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","Н. Р. Славнитский, «Ольга Александровна Шлейснер-Натансон: жизнь и революционная деятельность», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия История. Международные отношения</i> 20, no. 3 (2020): 298–303, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/olga-aleksandrovna-shleysner-natanson-zhizn-i-revolyutsionnaya-deyatelnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Спицина, «„Еретики“ и „политики“: революционный конституционализм в России на рубеже 70-х и 80-х годов XIX века», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 2 (41) (2018): 56–64, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/eretiki-i-politiki-revolyutsionnyy-konstitutsionalizm-v-rossii-na-rubezhe-70-h-i-80-h-godov-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Милевский, «Яков Стефанович в 1878–1879 гг.: невыученные уроки „Чигиринского дела“», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 96 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yakov-stefanovich-v-1878-1879-gg-nevyuchennye-uroki-chigirinskogo-dela. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Рубцов, «От беспорядков к демонстрациям: интерпретация массовых политических выступлений 1870-х гг. российскими властями», <i>Антропологический форум</i>, no. 44 (2020): 114–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-besporyadkov-k-demonstratsiyam-interpretatsiya-massovyh-politicheskih-vystupleniy-1870-h-gg-rossiyskimi-vlastyami. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the society keep a 'disorganising' group?","a":"Because its founders wanted a means of armed action against the government and against traitors in their own ranks, alongside the propaganda of the rustics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As persecution made work in the villages harder, that group grew into the executive committee that pursued terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south Osinsky's circle had already begun to act in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rustics lose ground inside the society?","a":"Because, though more careful than in 1874, they still found the peasants unprepared to accept their ideas, and faced a choice between peaceful cultural work for life and leaving the village to win political freedom first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Growing persecution pushed many towards the second course, which led to terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the society split in 1879?","a":"Because its members could no longer agree whether political struggle and terror should replace propaganda among the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The terrorist wing met first at Lipetsk, and the majority at Voronezh accepted its resolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A minority led by Plekhanov left to form Black Repartition, while the larger group became the People's Will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Land and Liberty (Russia)"},
    "ru-473": {"questions":["Ten days after killing Alexander II, the Executive Committee of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote to his son offering to end its campaign in return for an amnesty and a freely elected assembly.","Betrayed from within by the police informer Degaev, the remnants of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were rounded up after the coronation of 1883, and its leading spokesman Tikhomirov later recanted."],"abstract":"<b>Narodnaya Volya</b>, or the People's Will, was the Russian revolutionary party formed in 1879, when Land and Liberty split after its congresses at Lipetsk and Voronezh, and it set out to destroy the autocracy by systematic terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its name expressed its programme, for the will of the nation was to be the only source of law, to be forced on the government by a popular rising and, meanwhile, by assassination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A small Executive Committee directed the party, centralised its circles and imposed strict discipline and secrecy, and critics in other circles complained that it demanded unquestioning obedience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Women stood among its leaders, Sophia Perovskaya and Vera Figner among them, and the trials of the 16, the 6 and the 20 sent more than forty of its members to the dock between 1880 and 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kropotkin wrote that this handful declared war on absolutism backed by growing discontent among the educated, and its members believed that killing the tsar would set off a general rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A train was blown up near Moscow that turned out not to be the imperial one, and in February 1880 the carpenter Khalturin set off dynamite beneath the Winter Palace dining hall, killing ten soldiers of the guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 March 1881 by the Julian calendar a group led by Perovskaya killed Alexander II with hand bombs on the Catherine Canal in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten days later the Executive Committee wrote to Alexander III offering to give up violence in return for an amnesty and a national assembly elected with freedom of the press, speech and meeting, and promising to submit to its decisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead five of the regicides were executed, the police destroyed most of the organisation, the informer Degaev betrayed what was left in 1883, and Tikhomirov, who led the party from abroad, came to call terror a mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian historians still argue over whether its members were destroyers or fighters against tyranny, and judgements of its terrorism remain sharply polarised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 237–48, 260, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 568–73, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 313–19, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]","Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 2 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 239–45, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut02kropuoft. [Open access]","О. А. Милевский, «„Бомбисты“ — разрушители или „тираноборцы“: „Народная воля“ в оценках постсоветской историографии», <i>Вестник Сургутского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 3-1 (54) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bombisty-razrushiteli-ili-tiranobortsy-narodnaya-volya-v-otsenkah-postsovetskoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Сотников, А. А. Сотников and Г. П. Камнева, «Феномен женского терроризма в народовольческом движении (70–80-е гг. XIX века)», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i> 25, no. 4 (2019): 32–36, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-zhenskogo-terrorizma-v-narodovolcheskom-dvizhenii-70-80-e-gg-xix-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Милевский, «Яков Стефанович в 1878–1879 гг.: невыученные уроки „Чигиринского дела“», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 96 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/yakov-stefanovich-v-1878-1879-gg-nevyuchennye-uroki-chigirinskogo-dela. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the party aim at the tsar himself?","a":"Because its members believed that killing the emperor would be the signal for a general rising of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wallace adds that killing mere officials had only brought hundreds of new arrests, so the survivors decided to strike at the source of power itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the assassination not bring the party closer to its goal?","a":"Because it had made no preparations to take power and had no support in the army, the bureaucracy or among the masses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Liberals who had tolerated attacks on police officials were horrified by the killing of the tsar, and the police, newly zealous, broke up the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the party judged so differently today?","a":"Because its use of terror and its turn to the fight for political freedom are the most disputed questions in post-Soviet writing, which is sharply polarised between those who see destroyers and those who see fighters against tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its own letter of March 1881 asked only for an amnesty and a freely elected assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Narodnaya Volya"},
    "ru-474": {"questions":["Five months after Vera Zasulich's acquittal, the killing of the gendarme chief Mezentsov in a St Petersburg street in August 1878 turned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a systematic campaign against the state.","Both Pyotr Lavrov and Mikhail Bakunin opposed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a method, but after the failure of propaganda among the peasants it became the chosen weapon of Land and Liberty's successors."],"abstract":"<b>Russian revolutionary terrorism</b> was the campaign of political murder that Russian revolutionaries waged against officials of the autocracy and the emperor himself, beginning with Dmitry Karakozov's shot at Alexander II in April 1866.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Karakozov came from the Moscow circle of the Ishutintsy, and in 1869 Sergei Nechaev wrote the <i>Catechism of a Revolutionary</i> for his own society, the People's Reprisal, which demanded iron discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea that a revolution must destroy its enemies appeared in the 1862 proclamation <i>Young Russia</i>, and Pyotr Tkachev first gave revolutionary terror a theoretical justification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet Pyotr Lavrov and Mikhail Bakunin, the teachers most admired by the radicals of the 1870s, firmly opposed political murder, and most of their followers first chose propaganda among the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When that propaganda failed and the arrests of 1874 filled the prisons, the first victims were police spies, and the revolutionary writer Stepniak described a logic climbing from them step by step towards the heads of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The turning point came in 1878, when a jury acquitted Vera Zasulich of shooting the St Petersburg prefect Trepov, a verdict hailed across Europe, and five months later Stepniak killed General Mezentsov, chief of the gendarmes, in a street of the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the south Valerian Osinsky's circle issued proclamations in 1878 under the seal of a non-existent Executive Committee, attacked the Kiev prosecutor Kotlyarevsky and killed the gendarme officer Baron Heyking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within a year the governor of Kharkov, Prince Kropotkin, had been shot dead, and as arrests multiplied the militants decided to strike at the tsar himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The People's Will killed the tsar on 1 March 1881, but Wallace judged that terror stimulated the police more than it frightened the government, and Tikhomirov, the party's leader abroad, later called the terrorist idea a mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians still weigh its results differently, for many stress that it never overthrew the autocracy, while a recent study argues that it won some tactical and ideological aims at the price of provoking repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["А. В. Маньков, «Терроризм в России: исторические аспекты (вторая половина XIX — начало XX века)», <i>Вестник Чувашского университета</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/terrorizm-v-rossii-istoricheskie-aspekty-vtoraya-polovina-xix-nachalo-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","Я. Ю. Шашкова, «Революция и террор: к вопросу об отношении революционных народников 60–70-х гг. XIX в. к террору как методу деятельности революционной партии», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (1998), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/revolyutsiya-i-terror-k-voprosu-ob-otnoshenii-revolyutsionnyh-narodnikov-60-70-h-gg-xix-v-k-terroru-kak-metodu-deyatelnosti-revolyutsionnoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 30–39, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 567–68, 573, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","О. А. Милевский, «Буревестники политического террора: Валериан Осинский и Исполнительный комитет Русской социально-революционной партии», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i> 78, no. 1 (2023): 63–88, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/burevestniki-politicheskogo-terrora-valerian-osinskiy-i-ispolnitelnyy-komitet-russkoy-sotsialno-revolyutsionnoy-partii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. К. Рокки, «Победы и поражения политического терроризма в Российской империи», <i>Вестник МГПУ. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (54) (2024): 18–30, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobedy-i-porazheniya-politicheskogo-terrorizma-v-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 2 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 223–25, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut02kropuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did revolutionaries first turn to killing spies and officials?","a":"Because the mass arrests that ended the propaganda among the peasants filled the prisons and left the movement looking for another way to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stepniak describes the first killings as revenge on police spies, which the logic of the struggle then extended to the officers and officials above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Theorists had long disagreed, with Lavrov and Bakunin opposing such killings and Tkachev justifying them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Zasulich's acquittal matter so much to the terrorists?","a":"Because it seemed to give terror the approval of public opinion, and Stepniak wrote that it surrounded the method with the halo of sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Newspapers across Europe praised her, and almanacs printed her portrait beside that of the Russian chancellor at the Congress of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the campaign achieve what its authors wanted?","a":"Not its main aim, for the autocracy survived, and Wallace saw terror stimulate the police rather than frighten the government, while Tikhomirov came to think it a mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One recent study argues that the terrorists still won some tactical and ideological goals, though they pushed the government into repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-475": {"questions":["With Anatoly Koni presiding and Pyotr Alexandrov defending, a St Petersburg jury acquitted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1878, and crowds at the court gates saved her from rearrest.","In 1883 in Geneva, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> joined Plekhanov, Axelrod and Deutsch in founding the Marxist Emancipation of Labour group, after Marx had answered her letter about the fate of the peasant commune."],"abstract":"<b>Vera Zasulich</b> was a Russian revolutionary who lived from 1849 to 1919 and became famous across Europe in 1878, when she shot the prefect of St Petersburg and a jury set her free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She had taken part in the revolutionary movement for some years when she heard that General Trepov had ordered the political prisoner Bogolyubov to be flogged for not taking off his hat to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She did not know the victim, but on 24 January 1878 by the Julian calendar she came to Trepov's office as a petitioner and fired a revolver at him, wounding him seriously but not fatally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The government, sure that the trial would discredit the revolutionaries, let it go before an ordinary jury, although under the law she faced 15 to 20 years in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the trial on 31 March 1878, with Anatoly Koni presiding, her counsel Pyotr Alexandrov turned the case into an indictment of Trepov, and she said she had fired only when every other way of making the outrage known had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The jury acquitted her, crowds of young people at the court gates kept the police from rearresting her, and she escaped abroad to Switzerland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The verdict was hailed in the European press, but Wallace, who watched the trial, predicted rightly that no political case would again be tried publicly before an ordinary Russian jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stepniak, who killed the chief of the gendarmes five months later, wrote that she was an angel of vengeance rather than a terrorist, yet her act gave terror the sanction of public opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Abroad she revised her populist views, and after Marx replied in 1881 to her letter about the future of the Russian peasant commune she adopted Marxism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1883 she joined Plekhanov, Axelrod, Deutsch and Ignatov in founding the Emancipation of Labour group in Geneva, and she went on writing as a Marxist until her death in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 2 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 223–25, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut02kropuoft. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 536–37, 564, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 235–36, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","К. А. Чуйченко, «Дело Веры Засулич: к вопросу о балансе правовых ценностей в истории отечественного судопроизводства», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Право</i> 16, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/delo-very-zasulich-k-voprosu-o-balanse-pravovyh-tsennostey-v-istorii-otechestvennogo-sudoproizvodstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Степанов, «Социально-эстетические воззрения В. И. Засулич», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 16 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialno-esteticheskie-vozzreniya-v-i-zasulich. [in Russian] [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 35–37, 106–12, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the jury acquit a woman who admitted the shooting?","a":"Wallace, who was in court, believed the jurors wanted to make a small political demonstration and suspected that the prison officials had flogged the prisoner without the formalities the law required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her counsel made Trepov rather than Zasulich the real defendant, and she explained that she had fired only after every other way of publicising the flogging had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government let the case go to a jury?","a":"Because it expected the trial to damage the revolutionaries in the eyes of the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trepov also had many enemies in St Petersburg, who helped bring the affair before an ordinary jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the acquittal political cases were sent to special courts instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did she later become a Marxist?","a":"Because in emigration she read Plekhanov's translation of the <i>Communist Manifesto</i> and received Marx's reply to her question about the peasant commune, and she revised her social and political ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1883 she helped to found the Emancipation of Labour group in Geneva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vera Zasulich"},
    "ru-476": {"questions":["On the morning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the emperor had approved Loris-Melikov's plan to bring elected representatives into lawmaking, a plan his son soon abandoned.","Sophia Perovskaya waved a handkerchief to signal the bomb throwers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the imperial carriage turned onto the Catherine Canal on 1 March 1881."],"abstract":"The <b>assassination of Alexander II</b> took place in St Petersburg on 1 March 1881 by the Julian calendar, 13 March by the Gregorian, when members of the People's Will killed the emperor with hand bombs on the embankment of the Catherine Canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The party had hunted him with mines under railways and streets, and at the end it switched to bombs because it could never know the exact moment the imperial carriage would pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bombs were made by the chemist Kibalchich, and when Zhelyabov was arrested at the end of February, Sophia Perovskaya decided to hurry the plan rather than drop it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That Sunday morning the emperor approved Loris-Melikov's project to bring elected representatives into the preparation of laws and then, against the minister's warnings, rode out to a military parade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On his way back, as the carriage turned onto the canal, Perovskaya waved a handkerchief, Rysakov's bomb wounded several Cossacks of the escort, and the emperor got out to see them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A second bomb thrown by Grinevitsky exploded at his feet and shattered his legs, and he was carried to the Winter Palace, where he died about an hour and a half later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official telegram sent to the provinces that day reported him dangerously wounded at a quarter to two, and oaths of allegiance to Alexander III were taken everywhere within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ten days later the Executive Committee wrote to the new emperor offering to give up violence in return for an amnesty and a freely elected assembly, but five of the regicides, Perovskaya among them, were publicly executed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The killing set off no popular rising and horrified many liberals, and the new emperor, who had already opposed constitutional plans, buried them in the manifesto of 29 April 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Witte wrote that the murder left an indelible scar on Alexander III, and preachers presented the dead ruler as a martyr, yet Russian memory kept him as the Tsar-Liberator and he was never canonised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 246–52, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Peter Kropotkin, <i>Memoirs of a Revolutionist</i>, vol. 2 (London: Smith, Elder, 1899), 241–45, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofrevolut02kropuoft. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 570–72, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 313–19, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]","Е. Б. Долгов, «„Государственный строй не может быть потрясен даже такими несчастиями, как святотатственное цареубийство“: 1 марта 1881 г. на страницах архивных документов», <i>Гасырлар авазы — Эхо веков</i>, no. 3-4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvennyy-stroy-ne-mozhet-byt-potryasen-dazhe-takimi-neschastiyami-kak-svyatotatstvennoe-tsareubiystvo-1-marta-1881-g-na. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. А. Сафронова, «Царь-мученик vs царь-освободитель: почему не канонизировали Александра II?», <i>Вестник Свято-Филаретовского института</i>, no. 37 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsar-muchenik-vs-tsar-osvoboditel-pochemu-ne-kanonizirovali-aleksandra-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 38, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the conspirators switch from mines to bombs?","a":"Because after many attempts they concluded that mines rarely worked, since it was hard to know the exact moment the emperor would pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hand bombs let them follow the carriage, and Perovskaya's signal sent the throwers into action as it turned onto the canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the timing of the attack often called tragic?","a":"Because on the very morning of his death the emperor had approved Loris-Melikov's plan to bring elected representatives into lawmaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kropotkin calls it a belated and half-hearted concession that had not yet been announced when he was killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His son, who had already opposed constitutional projects, then shelved it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the killing not help the revolutionaries?","a":"Because they had made no plans to take power and had no support in the army or among the masses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Liberals were horrified that even the tsar's person was not spared, and the police, newly zealous, broke up the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Assassination of Alexander II of Russia"},
    "ru-477": {"questions":["In the manifesto of 29 April 1881, drafted in secret by Pobedonostsev, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> declared his faith in the power and truth of the autocracy, and three liberal ministers resigned.","Witte praised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a thrifty ruler who kept Russia out of war, though the title 'the Peacemaker' first appeared only in a decree issued by his son after his death."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander III</b> was emperor of Russia from the assassination of his father, Alexander II, in March 1881 until his own death at Livadia in the Crimea in 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second son, he was raised for a military career until his elder brother Nicholas died in 1865, after which professors including the historian Solovyov and the jurist Pobedonostsev were brought in to widen his education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He married his late brother's Danish fiancée, Princess Dagmar, lived a quiet family life and founded the Imperial Russian Historical Society, and a legend that he was a liberal grew up because so little was known of his views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In fact he had argued against constitutional plans at a conference in 1880, and Witte, who served him, called him a man of limited education but of broad and sympathetic understanding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Older histories drew him as narrow-minded, an image Soviet historians inherited, while some recent writers treat his policies as a correction of his father's course rather than a reversal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Ten days after his father's death the Executive Committee of the People's Will wrote to him demanding an amnesty and a freely elected assembly, but he sided instead with Pobedonostsev against Loris-Melikov's project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manifesto of 29 April 1881, drafted in secret by Pobedonostsev, declared faith in the power and truth of the autocracy, and Loris-Melikov, Abaza and Milyutin resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1882, under the interior minister Dmitry Tolstoy, his government restricted universities, the press and local self-government, and the temporary rules of May 1882 barred Jews from settling anew outside the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Witte praised him as a thrifty ruler who kept the peace without shedding Russian blood, and noted that the name 'the Peacemaker' first appeared in a decree issued by his son after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He survived the wreck of the imperial train at Borki in 1888, in which 21 people were killed, but his health failed in the following years and doctors found nephritis before he died in November 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 240, 248–65, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 38–46, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Charles Lowe, <i>Alexander III of Russia</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1895), 257–58, 275–76, 292–99, https://archive.org/details/cu31924068919814. [Open access]","Stepniak, <i>Underground Russia: Revolutionary Profiles and Sketches from Life</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1883), 313–19, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028106221. [Open access]","И. Е. Барыкина, «Внутренняя политика и личность императора Александра III: источники и историография», <i>Вестник Русской христианской гуманитарной академии</i> 14, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnutrennyaya-politika-i-lichnost-imperatora-aleksandra-iii-istochniki-i-istoriografiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Биюшкина, «К вопросу об особенностях внутриполитического курса Александра III», <i>Власть</i>, no. 6 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-ob-osobennostyah-vnutripoliticheskogo-kursa-aleksandra-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Simon Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 2 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1918), 309–15, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr02dubnuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the new emperor reject Loris-Melikov's project?","a":"Because at the council of March 1881 he sided with the reactionaries and was impressed by Pobedonostsev's passionate speech predicting disaster and revolution if it were accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also cited advice from Emperor Wilhelm I and from Danish ministers that constitutional institutions were dangerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he called 'the Peacemaker'?","a":"Because Russia fought no war in his reign, and Witte credited him with raising the country's prestige without shedding a drop of Russian blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Witte noted that the title first appeared in a decree issued by his son after his death, and that Nicholas II himself thought it did not fit his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians judge him so differently?","a":"Because older accounts built the image of a narrow-minded ruler, which Soviet historiography inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some recent writers instead see the measures of 1881–1895 as a correction of his father's liberal course rather than a return to the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Liberal historians such as Kornilov stressed the persecution of Jews, Poles and religious dissenters under his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander III of Russia"},
    "ru-478": {"questions":["The nobleman Pazukhin, who wanted the reign to restore the estate order destroyed by the reforms of the 1860s, helped draft the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the interior minister Dmitry Tolstoy.","Narrowing the jury's competence, tightening censorship and handing peasant literacy schools to the church in 1884 were all parts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a label some historians now reject."],"abstract":"The <b>counter-reforms</b> were the measures by which the government of Alexander III revised the Great Reforms of the 1860s in local government, the courts, schools and the press, to strengthen the autocracy and the nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The turn came when Dmitry Tolstoy became minister of the interior in 1882 after the fall of Ignatyev, and the university statute of 1884 then stripped university councils of their autonomy and banned student organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law of 13 June 1884 handed the peasants' own reading and writing schools to the church, though most zemstvos refused to give up the schools they ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temporary rules of 1882 let four ministers close any publication and bar its editor for life, and by the decade's end few liberal periodicals survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jury trial survived, but laws of 1882, 1884 and 1889 moved some offences to other courts and changed how jurors were chosen, while political cases had left ordinary juries after the Zasulich trial of 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The programme took shape when the Simbirsk nobleman Pazukhin, later head of Tolstoy's chancery, argued in 1885 that the reign must restore the estate order the reforms had destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statute of 12 July 1889 by the Julian calendar created the land captains, officials drawn from local hereditary nobles who held both administrative and judicial power over the peasants and replaced the elected justices of the peace in the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The zemstvo statute of 12 June 1890 gave the nobles a dominant share of seats, let the governor choose the peasant members from candidates named by the volosts, and placed the zemstvo boards under the governor's control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A new municipal statute of 11 June 1892 replaced the tax qualification for town voters with a property qualification and made the decisions of town councils subject to provincial approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name itself is disputed, for liberal historians saw a deliberate restoration of the past, while some recent writers argue that the measures corrected the reforms to suit the crisis of the late 1870s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 259–65, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Н. А. Бузанова, «Основные требования, предъявляемые к должности земского участкового начальника по контрреформе Александра III», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i> 22, no. 1 (165) (2017): 86–92, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osnovnye-trebovaniya-predyavlyaemye-k-dolzhnosti-zemskogo-uchastkovogo-nachalnika-po-kontrreforme-aleksandra-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Ю. Агафонов, «Эволюция российского суда присяжных в 1870–1880-е годы как модели суда с народным представительством», <i>Юридическая наука и практика: Вестник Нижегородской академии МВД России</i>, no. 2 (26) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-rossiyskogo-suda-prisyazhnyh-v-1870-1880-e-gody-kak-modeli-suda-s-narodnym-predstavitelstvom. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Биюшкина, «К вопросу об особенностях внутриполитического курса Александра III», <i>Власть</i>, no. 6 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-ob-osobennostyah-vnutripoliticheskogo-kursa-aleksandra-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Воронин, «Сущность политики „контрреформ“ Александра III», <i>Вестник Московского университета МВД России</i>, no. 7 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/suschnost-politiki-kontrreform-aleksandra-iii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enlarged ed. (New York: Henry Holt, 1905), 536–37, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028389801. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the land captains so resented?","a":"Because they were chosen from local hereditary nobles and held both administrative and judicial power over the peasants and their self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 1889 law also swept away the elected justices of the peace in the countryside, breaking the reformers' principle that administration and justice should be kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government favour the nobility in particular?","a":"Because the autocracy regarded the hereditary nobles as the mainstay of the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reactionary nobles such as Pazukhin blamed the reforms of the 1860s for destroying estate distinctions and called for their restoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 1890 zemstvo statute duly widened the nobility's role in local government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some historians avoid the word 'counter-reforms'?","a":"Because they argue that the measures of 1881–1895 corrected the liberal course of Alexander II, adapting it to the crisis of the late 1870s, rather than turning the country back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Liberal historians of the early 20th century, by contrast, described them as a deliberate restoration of the old estate order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-479": {"questions":["Calling parliamentary government 'the great falsehood of our time', the ober-procurator <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that the sovereignty of the people was among the falsest of political principles.","For a quarter of a century as head of the Holy Synod's lay administration, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> blocked concessions to Old Believers and had the Stundists declared an especially harmful sect."],"abstract":"<b>Konstantin Pobedonostsev</b> was a Russian jurist and statesman who served from 1880 until October 1905 as ober-procurator of the Holy Synod, the lay official through whom the state governed the Orthodox Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before that he had been one of the most brilliant Russian lawyers, had helped prepare the judicial reform of the 1860s and had taught the heirs to the throne, first Nicholas and then Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Loris-Melikov himself recommended him for the Synod post in 1880, and his former pupil, once he became Alexander III, placed great confidence in him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his <i>Reflections of a Russian Statesman</i> he called parliamentary government 'the great falsehood of our time' and attacked the sovereignty of the people as one of the falsest of political principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held that Orthodoxy and Russian nationality were bound together, and he feared that a peasant who became a Protestant or a sectarian would cease to be Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His decisive moment came at the council of 8 March 1881, Julian style, where he opened a passionate speech against Loris-Melikov's project with the words <i>Finis Russiae</i>, 'the end of Russia', and predicted revolution if it passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then drafted in secret the manifesto of 29 April 1881 on the strength of the autocracy, which drove the liberal ministers from office, and a year later helped defeat Ignatyev's plan for an assembly of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As head of the Synod he pressed for church control of primary schooling and won the transfer of the peasants' literacy schools to the church in 1884, though most zemstvos refused to support the church's parish schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Witte, who worked beside him, called him a 25-year obstacle to any liberal settlement for the sectarians, yet also recorded that he spoke out against Tolstoy's university statute of 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He refused to soften his course as revolution approached and resisted toleration for Old Believers and Stundists, but a decree on religious toleration came in April 1905 and he left office after the October Manifesto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 249–52, 259–61, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","Konstantin Pobedonostsev, <i>Reflections of a Russian Statesman</i>, trans. Robert Crozier Long (London: Grant Richards, 1898), 32–34, https://archive.org/details/reflectionsofrus00pobeuoft. [Open access]","А. Ю. Полунов, «Национальное и религиозное в системе имперского управления: к вопросу о деятельности и политических взглядах К. П. Победоносцева», <i>Государственное управление. Электронный вестник</i>, no. 34 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/natsionalnoe-i-religioznoe-v-sisteme-imperskogo-upravleniya-k-voprosu-o-deyatelnosti-i-politicheskih-vzglyadah-k-p-pobedonostseva. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Куратов, «К. П. Победоносцев и его взгляды на взаимоотношения церкви, общества и государства», <i>Вестник Омского университета. Серия «Исторические науки»</i>, no. 3 (7) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-p-pobedonostsev-i-ego-vzglyady-na-vzaimootnosheniya-tserkvi-obschestva-i-gosudarstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 42, 223, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Н. Г. Карнишина, «Обер-прокурор Синода К. П. Победоносцев: штрихи к портрету», <i>Известия высших учебных заведений. Поволжский регион. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 3 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ober-prokuror-sinoda-k-p-pobedonostsev-shtrihi-k-portretu. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he oppose parliamentary government?","a":"Because he held that the principle of popular sovereignty was false, and that a parliament could work only if deputies and ministers became impersonal executors of the people's will, which in practice they never were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He used the same conviction in March 1881 to persuade Alexander III that a constitution would bring disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he so hostile to Old Believers and sectarians?","a":"Because he saw Orthodoxy and Russian identity as inseparable and believed that a peasant who left the church stopped being Russian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He fought every softening of policy towards them, and Witte called him an obstacle to toleration for 25 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his school policy meet resistance?","a":"Because the nobles who ran the zemstvos would not give up their control of popular education, and the state lacked the money to take the schools over by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only the peasants' own literacy schools passed to the church in 1884, and many zemstvos declined to support church parish schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Konstantin Pobedonostsev"},
    "ru-480": {"questions":["Citing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as proof of popular hostility, the interior minister Ignatyev won the temporary rules of May 1882 that barred Jews from settling anew in the countryside.","Rumours that an imperial decree had ordered attacks at Orthodox Easter preceded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose main wave ended after Dmitry Tolstoy warned officials they would answer for any disorders."],"abstract":"The <b>pogroms of 1881–1884</b> were the wave of anti-Jewish riots that broke out in the south-western provinces of the Russian Empire a month after the assassination of Alexander II and recurred, more sporadically, until 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first began at Yelisavetgrad in Kherson province on 15 April 1881 by the Julian calendar, during Orthodox Easter, when a crowd attacked Jewish shops and houses while troops without clear orders stood by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Riots spread through Kherson, Yekaterinoslav and Kiev provinces until July 1881, struck Warsaw in December and returned in 1882, most terribly at Balta in Podolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mobs chiefly looted and wrecked Jewish homes, shops and taverns, although people were also killed, and rumours ran ahead of them that an imperial decree had ordered attacks on the Jews at Easter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later outbreaks struck Yekaterinoslav in 1883 and, in 1884, Nizhny Novgorod, far outside the Pale of Settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Who was responsible has been argued ever since: the historian Simon Dubnow held that the riots were prepared from above, pointing to their near-simultaneous outbreak and the passivity of the authorities, while other writers find no evidence of direct government involvement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The authorities at first feared that revolutionaries were behind the disorders, and some populists did try to exploit them, though a workers' union in Kiev urged workers not to give way to national hatred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The government's answer, prepared by the interior minister Ignatyev, treated the riots as a popular protest against Jewish 'exploitation' and produced the temporary rules of 3 May 1882, which forbade Jews to settle anew outside towns or to buy property in the villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Balta, Ignatyev was dismissed, and his successor Dmitry Tolstoy warned that officials would be prosecuted for any disorder, after which the wave subsided into scattered local outbreaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commissions on the Jewish question met in the provinces, a high commission to review all laws on the Jews was set up in February 1883, and violence and restrictions together set off a mass emigration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Simon Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 2 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1918), 247–51, 297–318, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr02dubnuoft. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 265, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhis02korniala. [Open access]","В. А. Малахов, «Региональная локализация еврейских погромов 1881–1882 гг. в Российской империи», <i>Вестник Брянского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (1) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/regionalnaya-lokalizatsiya-evreyskih-pogromov-1881-1882-gg-v-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Егоров, «К вопросу о погромах еврейского населения Российской империи в конце XIX — начале XX вв.», <i>Вестник Забайкальского государственного университета</i> 22, no. 5 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-pogromah-evreyskogo-naseleniya-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-kontse-xix-nachala-xx-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Кудряшев, «„Черный передел“, „Народная воля“ и еврейский вопрос: реакция русского народничества на еврейские погромы 1881 г.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 349 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chernyy-peredel-narodnaya-volya-i-evreyskiy-vopros-reaktsiya-russkogo-narodnichestva-na-evreyskie-pogromy-1881-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Прохоров, «Проблема еврейских погромов в Российской империи и общественное мнение (по материалам газеты „Таврида“)», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i> 3 (69), no. 2 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-evreyskih-pogromov-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-i-obschestvennoe-mnenie-po-materialam-gazety-tavrida. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the riots spread so fast from one town to the next?","a":"Because news of the easy 'victory' at Yelisavetgrad stirred crowds in nearby villages and towns, and rumours claimed that an imperial decree had ordered the attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dubnow also blamed troops and police who stood by without orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the riots fell in Kherson, Yekaterinoslav and Kiev provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government restrict the Jews rather than protect them?","a":"Because officials led by Ignatyev argued that the pogroms were a popular protest against Jewish exploitation and that Russia should return to treating Jews as aliens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temporary rules of May 1882 duly forbade Jews to settle anew outside the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the main wave of violence end in 1882?","a":"Because the new minister Dmitry Tolstoy, an enemy of mob rule, announced that officials who failed to prevent disorders would be prosecuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dubnow took this as proof that pogroms could not happen when the authorities were firmly against them, though later outbreaks still struck Yekaterinoslav and Nizhny Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-481": {"questions":["Signed by the emperor in June 1895, the statute behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led to a count eighteen months later whose general summary appeared only in 1905.","Officials reading the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the urban share of the population at just 14 per cent, with only 19 of 1,321 towns above 100,000 people."],"abstract":"The <b>1897 Russian census</b> was the first general census of the Russian Empire, taken on 28 January 1897 by the Julian calendar, 9 February by the Gregorian, under a statute the emperor had signed on 5 June 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A main census commission in St Petersburg ran the work and met 98 times between November 1895 and May 1897, while provincial and district commissions organised the count on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counting and the reports of the commissions went on until 28 July 1897, and the final tables gave the empire a population of 125,640,021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Finland was left out altogether, and in Khiva and Bukhara only the subjects of the empire living there were counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Money was short because the poll tax had been abolished in 1885, and the census cost 4.2 kopecks a head against 13.5 kopecks for the American census of 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its results gave the first full picture of the peoples of the empire, because every person was asked for a native language and the tables grouped the population by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Speakers of Great Russian numbered about 55.7 million, well under half of the total, and only with Little Russian and Belarusian added did the Russian group reach about two thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The count also showed how rural the empire remained, for officials put the urban share at 14 per cent and only 19 of its 1,321 towns had more than 100,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> St Petersburg itself had grown from about 540,000 inhabitants in 1864 to 1,330,000 in 1897.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Processing dragged on for eight years, a two-volume general summary appeared only in 1905, and the second census planned for 1910 and then 1915 was never held, which left 1897 the only general census of the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 397, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, new and enlarged ed., vol. 1 (London: Cassell, 1905), 226–27, https://archive.org/details/russia00wall. [Open access]","Ю. В. Семенов, «Организация Первой всеобщей переписи населения 1897 года в Вооруженных силах Российской империи», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 95 (2025): 156–61, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsiya-pervoy-vseobschey-perepisi-naseleniya-1897-goda-v-vooruzhennyh-silah-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. С. Воропаева, «Формирование и состав переписных комиссий Тобольской губернии при подготовке Первой всеобщей переписи населения Российской империи 1897 года», <i>Magistra Vitae: электронный журнал по историческим наукам и археологии</i>, no. 6 (297) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-i-sostav-perepisnyh-komissiy-tobolskoy-gubernii-pri-podgotovke-pervoy-vseobschey-perepisi-naseleniya-rossiyskoy-imperii-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Д. Борщик, «История публикации сведений о населении Таврической губернии (по материалам всеобщей переписи 1897 г.)», <i>Материалы по археологии, истории и этнографии Таврии</i>, no. 28 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriya-publikatsii-svedeniy-o-naselenii-tavricheskoy-gubernii-po-materialam-vseobschey-perepisi-1897-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","«Первая всеобщая перепись населения Российской Империи 1897 г. Распределение населения по родному языку, губерниям и областям», <i>Демоскоп Weekly</i>, https://www.demoscope.ru/weekly/ssp/rus_lan_97.php. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it the only general census of the Russian Empire?","a":"Because the second census planned for 1910, and then for 1915, never took place: the chief organiser of the first died in 1913 and the First World War followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Processing the returns of 1897 alone took about eight years, ending in 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the census so poorly funded?","a":"Because the poll tax had been abolished in 1885, the Ministry of Finance cut the money for the work, and the census cost 4.2 kopecks a head against 13.5 kopecks in the American census of 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money assigned for processing ran short, and a government commission had to be set up in December 1901 to rescue the project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the census show about towns?","a":"That the empire was still overwhelmingly rural: officials put the urban share at only 14 per cent, against 72 per cent in Great Britain, and about three fifths of the 1,321 towns had fewer than 5,000 inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, St Petersburg had more than doubled since 1864, to 1,330,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1897 Russian census"},
    "ru-482": {"questions":["Raising duties on 291 kinds of goods, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lifted the average duty on imports to 37 per cent of their value in the second half of that year.","German discrimination against Russian grain answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Witte replied with a double tariff aimed almost wholly at German manufactures."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian tariff of 1891</b> was the general customs tariff that came into force on 1 July 1891 and gave Russia the highest duties of any developed country of the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was prepared by a commission under the finance minister Ivan Vyshnegradsky, who presented the draft in March 1891 and linked the growth of Russian industry to the rising duties of the 1880s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chemist Dmitri Mendeleev, asked in September 1889 to look at duties on chemical products, widened the task into a revision of the whole tariff and defended it in a treatise aimed at the British case for free trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tariff protected every stage of production in every branch of industry, and only fourteen items, which were hardly imported at all, were left free of duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Duties rose on 291 kinds of goods and fell on 42, and the level of duties against the value of imports reached its peak of 37 per cent in the second half of 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Iron shows the method, for its duty was raised in steps from 1887 and stood in 1891 at 30 kopecks in gold per pood at the seaboard and 35 kopecks on the western land frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Contemporaries disagreed over how far the tariff caused the boom of the 1890s, and one leading economist rejected protection as a general cause while admitting its effect on iron and coal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its defenders pointed out that foreign investment in Russia rose from 186 million roubles in 1890 to 762 million in 1900, much of it into shielded branches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tariff also became a weapon in trade bargaining, for Germany charged Russian grain more than that of other countries, and Witte answered with a maximum scale about 20 per cent higher aimed almost wholly at German goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tariff war that followed ended in a commercial treaty with Germany on 29 January 1894, after which some rates of 1891 were eased while protection remained the basis of Russian policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["А. Ю. Пиджаков and А. В. Татаренко, «Д. И. Менделеев и таможенный тариф 1891 года», <i>Ученые записки Санкт-Петербургского имени В. Б. Бобкова филиала Российской таможенной академии</i>, no. 3 (32) (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/d-i-mendeleev-i-tamozhennyy-tarif-1891-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Сорокин, «Протекционизм и промышленное развитие России в XIX веке», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Экономика</i>, no. 4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protektsionizm-i-promyshlennoe-razvitie-rossii-v-xix-veke. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Ю. Барсукова, «Условия эффективного протекционизма. Размышления С. Ю. Витте и Д. И. Менделеева», <i>ЭКО. Всероссийский экономический журнал</i>, no. 7 (445) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/usloviya-effektivnogo-protektsionizma-razmyshleniya-s-yu-vitte-i-d-i-mendeleeva-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 364, 380–81, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 62–67, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","К. В. Гаврилов, «Таможенный протекционизм С. Ю. Витте», <i>Вестник Ленинградского государственного университета им. А. С. Пушкина</i>, no. 4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tamozhennyy-protektsionizm-s-yu-vitte. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mendeleev, a chemist, work on a customs tariff?","a":"Because in September 1889 the finance minister asked him to look at the duties on chemical products, and he soon saw that the groups of goods in a tariff were too closely linked to stop there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became a convinced protectionist, believing that a country living by farming alone would stay poor, and he argued the case in a long treatise of 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tariff lead to a quarrel with Germany?","a":"Because Germany had a double tariff and charged its higher rates on countries without a commercial treaty, which in practice meant Russia and its grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Witte replied with a double tariff of his own, adding about 20 per cent to the existing rates for German manufactures, and the tariff war ended with a treaty on 29 January 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the tariff really cause Russian industrial growth?","a":"Opinions differed then and since: one economist of the period thought protection was not a general cause of growth, though he admitted it helped iron and coal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Supporters noted that foreign investment rose fourfold in the 1890s, and a modern study calls the tariff a preparation for the industrial leap that followed the currency reform of 1895–1897.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-483": {"questions":["Moved on 15 August 1903 from the finance ministry to the Committee of Ministers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nonetheless led the Russian delegation at the peace talks with Japan in 1905.","Answering German discrimination against Russian grain, the finance minister <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised a double tariff and ended the tariff war with a treaty in January 1894."],"abstract":"<b>Sergei Witte</b> was the Russian minister of finance from 1892 to 1903, the official most closely identified with the industrial growth of the empire in the 1890s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born in Tiflis in 1849 to a father of Baltic origin, whose ancestors were Dutch settlers in the Baltic provinces, and to a mother of Russian family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made his name in the railways, writing a book on railway tariffs and rising to manage the South-Western Railways before he entered government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As minister he defended the protective tariff of 1891, fought a tariff war with Germany and closed it with a commercial treaty on 29 January 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He also turned the sale of vodka into a state monopoly, and he backed a factory law of 1897 that limited the working day to eleven and a half hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> His proudest reform was the gold standard, which an enlarged Finance Committee under Nicholas II approved on 2 January 1897 after Witte had withdrawn a bill the State Council would not pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of 3 January marked the gold imperial at 15 roubles, new 5 and 10 rouble coins carried a third less gold than the old ones, and from November the State Bank exchanged notes for gold without limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He pushed the Trans-Siberian Railway forward, and his enemies on the right accused him of favouring the Peasant Bank and of creating an industrial working class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 15 August 1903 he was moved from the finance ministry to the chairmanship of the Committee of Ministers, a post with little power, and the change set off a wave of rumours in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even so he led the Russian delegation that made peace with Japan at Portsmouth in 1905, and the memoirs he wrote in retirement present him as a statesman without equal, an image historians treat with care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 3–4, 48–62, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 670–71, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","М. В. Мельников, «Этапы проведения денежной реформы С. Ю. Витте», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 15 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/etapy-provedeniya-denezhnoy-reformy-s-yu-vitte. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Н. Веревкина, ««Неуправляемая империя» vs «соломенное чучело на огороде» (конструирование образа С. Ю. Витте на страницах «Воспоминаний»)», <i>Ученые записки Казанского университета. Серия Гуманитарные науки</i> 163, no. 6 (2021): 61–70, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/neupravlyaemaya-imperiya-vs-solomennoe-chuchelo-na-ogorode-konstruirovanie-obraza-s-yu-vitte-na-stranitsah-vospominaniy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Андреев, «Правительственная «перемена» 15 августа 1903 года в зеркале руморологии», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 2-1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravitelstvennaya-peremena-15-avgusta-1903-goda-v-zerkale-rumorologii. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. В. Гаврилов, «Таможенный протекционизм С. Ю. Витте», <i>Вестник Ленинградского государственного университета им. А. С. Пушкина</i>, no. 4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tamozhennyy-protektsionizm-s-yu-vitte. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 288, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Witte go around the State Council over the gold standard?","a":"Because the bill he laid before the council in April 1896 met strong opposition and would not have passed, so he withdrew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then had the emperor chair an enlarged Finance Committee on 2 January 1897, which approved the reform, and he presented the first decree as no more than a change in the figures stamped on gold coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he moved out of the finance ministry in 1903?","a":"Because he lost the emperor's support at a critical moment, while conservative landowners attacked him over the Peasant Bank and the growth of an industrial working class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His transfer to the Committee of Ministers on 15 August 1903 was seen as a fall and set off rumours about its causes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians read his memoirs with caution?","a":"Because he wrote them out of office while still fighting for public opinion, and in them he built the image of a statesman with no equal in talent or stature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They remain a unique source, and in them he claims the gold standard as the first of his reforms and credits it with carrying Russia through the war with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sergei Witte"},
    "ru-484": {"questions":["Until rails were laid round Lake Baikal, travellers on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the lake on the ice or aboard an ice-breaker shipped from England in pieces.","To shorten the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by 514 versts, Witte sent it straight across northern Manchuria to Vladivostok instead of along the difficult Amur River."],"abstract":"The <b>Trans-Siberian Railway</b> is the line that joins European Russia to the Pacific at Vladivostok, decided upon by Alexander III in 1891 and built by the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An imperial rescript of 17 March 1891 ordered a continuous line across Siberia, and on 19 May the heir, the future Nicholas II, filled a wheelbarrow with earth at Vladivostok and laid the first stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work went on from both ends at once, from Chelyabinsk on the eastern slope of the Urals and from Vladivostok, with peasants, Cossacks, soldiers, exiles and convicts among the workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Foreign firms offered help but were turned down, and the line was built by Russian engineers and workers from Russian materials, with about 86 kilometres (53 miles) of bridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Siberian line proper measured 6,484 kilometres (4,029 miles) from Chelyabinsk to Vladivostok, and the whole journey from St Petersburg was 9,431 kilometres (5,860 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Costs ran far over the estimates, from about 30 million roubles a year planned to 85.5 million in 1896, and with the Chinese Eastern Railway the total passed a billion roubles by the end of 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> That Manchurian branch was Witte's idea, since a line across Chinese territory saved 514 versts and avoided the hard Amur section, though he insisted the railway was meant for trade and defence and not for conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lake Baikal broke the line for years, and trains were carried across it by the ice-breaker <i>Baikal</i>, built by Armstrong in England, shipped in pieces and assembled on the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The section round the southern shore of the lake was finished only during the war with Japan, in 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Few single lines have changed world politics so much, for the railway drew Russian ambitions towards Manchuria and Korea and helped to bring on the war with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 226–27, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","<i>Guide to the Great Siberian Railway</i> (St Petersburg: Ministry of Ways of Communication, 1900), 63–64, https://archive.org/details/guidetogreatsibe00russuoft. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 86–87, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Michael Myers Shoemaker, <i>The Great Siberian Railway from St. Petersburg to Pekin</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1903), 100–101, https://archive.org/details/greatsiberianrai00shoeuoft. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 760–62, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 293–94, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Н. В. Савченко, «Из истории строительства Транссибирской магистрали», <i>Проблемы социально-экономического развития Сибири</i>, no. 2 (12) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-stroitelstva-transsibirskoy-magistrali. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Шильникова, «Финансовые аспекты строительства Транссибирской магистрали: структура бюджетных расходов», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/finansovye-aspekty-stroitelstva-transsibirskoy-magistrali-struktura-byudzhetnyh-rashodov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government refuse foreign help with the railway?","a":"Because it wanted the vast state order kept at home: the industrialists of southern Russia and the Urals did not wish to share it, and all foreign offers were rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The line was built by Russian specialists and workers from Russian materials, under the direction of the state as the rescript of 1891 required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did part of the railway cross Chinese territory?","a":"Because a line wholly on Russian soil had to make an enormous bend to the north along the Amur, while a line across northern Manchuria to Vladivostok was much shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Witte reckoned it saved 514 versts, avoided a technically difficult section and ran through better soil and a milder climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the railway matter for the war with Japan?","a":"Because it drew Russian policy towards Manchuria and Korea, and the commercial ambitions tied to it helped to bring on the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1895 Russia could not stop Japan's gains in China because the railway was unfinished and no large army could be moved east, and even during the war the loop round Lake Baikal was still being built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Trans-Siberian Railway"},
    "ru-485": {"questions":["Tank steamers named Zoroaster, Spinoza and Darwin carried kerosene for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the Caspian and up the Volga, driving American kerosene out of the Russian market.","Although its head office stood in St Petersburg, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drilled and refined in Baku and was by 1914 largely financed by English and French capital."],"abstract":"<b>Branobel</b>, the Nobel Brothers Petroleum Production Company, was the oil firm founded by the Swedish Nobel family in 1879, for decades the leading enterprise of the Baku oilfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of a visit to Baku in 1874 by Robert Nobel, who had come to buy walnut wood for an arms factory and instead leased oil land and in 1875 bought a small kerosene refinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The company was registered with a capital of 3 million roubles, of which Ludwig Nobel held 1.61 million, and its founders were Ludwig, Robert and Alfred Nobel and their friend Colonel Peter Bilderling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its head office and laboratories were in St Petersburg, although the work itself was done in the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1879 and 1883 its share of Russian oil output rose from 1 to 26 per cent and its share of kerosene from 5 to 49 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The chief advantage of the Nobels was transport, for their pipeline from the Balakhany wells to the refineries of the Black Town of Baku replaced thousands of carts and paid for itself in its first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ludwig Nobel designed a steamer that carried oil in its hull, and the <i>Zoroaster</i> and its sisters, built in Sweden and named after thinkers such as Spinoza and Darwin, took kerosene across the Caspian and up the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cheap Baku kerosene drove American imports out of Russia, which fell from 2,716,000 poods in 1873 to 69,000 in 1885, a pood being about 16 kilograms (36 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other foreign money followed, above all that of the Paris Rothschilds, who by 1890 controlled 42 per cent of Baku oil exports, and 29 joint-stock companies with foreign capital were founded there between 1874 and 1899.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By 1914 the Nobel works were largely financed by English and French capital, and after the Bolsheviks nationalised the oil industry in 1918 the owners sold the assets of the firm to Standard Oil of New Jersey in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["М. Н. Новиков, «Из истории нефтяной корпорации братьев Нобель», <i>История и педагогика естествознания</i>, no. 2 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-neftyanoy-korporatsii-bratiev-nobel. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Н. Земляной, «Братья Нобели и российская нефть: от инноваций к экономическому успеху», <i>Экономический журнал</i> 30, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bratya-nobeli-i-rossiyskaya-neft-ot-innovatsiy-k-ekonomicheskomu-uspehu. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Мелуа, «Динамика хозяйственно-экономической деятельности фирмы Бранобель в конце XIX – начале XX в.», <i>Известия Санкт-Петербургского государственного экономического университета</i>, no. 1 (85) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dinamika-hozyaystvenno-ekonomicheskoy-deyatelnosti-firmy-branobel-v-kontse-xix-nachale-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 380, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","Charles Marvin, <i>The Region of the Eternal Fire: An Account of a Journey to the Petroleum Region of the Caspian in 1883</i>, new ed. (London: W. H. Allen, 1891), 198–201, 283–84, https://archive.org/details/regionofeternalf00marv. [Open access]","В. Ш. Уразгалиев, «Становление и развитие нефтяного дела в Российской империи», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. Экономика</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stanovlenie-i-razvitie-neftyanogo-dela-v-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a pipeline give the Nobels such an advantage?","a":"Because crude oil had been carried from the wells to the refineries in skins and barrels on carts, at great cost, and the other Baku firms refused to combine to build a pipe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nobels laid one themselves, recovered the cost in the first year, and cut the cost of moving a pood of oil from 10 kopecks to half a kopeck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did American kerosene disappear from Russian markets?","a":"Because the Nobels moved kerosene in bulk by tank steamer and railway tank wagon, which lowered its price from 1.5 roubles a pood in 1878 to 33 kopecks in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of the century they had depots in 115 towns of the empire, and Russian kerosene was being exported as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did other foreign investors come to Baku?","a":"Because the end of the old system of leasing out the oilfields in 1873 set off rapid growth, and 29 joint-stock companies, some with foreign capital, were founded in the region between 1874 and 1899.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Rothschilds arrived with the Baku–Batum railway, opened in 1883, and in 1912 they sold their Russian oil assets to Royal Dutch Shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Branobel"},
    "ru-486": {"questions":["Promised a shorter working day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, factory workers saw a law of June 1897 cap the adult working day at eleven and a half hours.","Demands drawn up by a hundred strikers during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were printed and circulated by the Union of Struggle, a Marxist circle in the Russian capital."],"abstract":"The <b>St Petersburg textile strike of 1896</b> was the stoppage of the cotton spinners and weavers of the capital in May and June 1896, the first simultaneous mass movement of the Russian working class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began at the end of May at the Russian, or Kalinkin, cotton mill on the Obvodny Canal, after the workers were refused pay for holiday days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Witte, as minister of finance, told the factory inspectors to announce that no concessions would be made, so that workers would learn that a strike could win them nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The refusal spread the stoppage, and on 28 May work stopped at the Russian mill, the König factory, the Ekaterinhof spinning mill and others, with unrest among metalworkers too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A hundred strikers met, drew up their demands and passed them to the Marxist Union of Struggle for the Emancipation of the Working Class, which printed and circulated them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By early June the stoppage had become a general strike of the textile workers of St Petersburg, but the police and the refusal of the owners to give way brought it to an end on the old terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its lesson was organisation, and after that summer the interest of the workers of the capital in unions and strike funds was greater than ever before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government, alarmed by the scale and discipline of the movement, promised a shorter day, and a law of 2 June 1897 limited the adult working day to eleven and a half hours, and to ten on Saturdays and the eves of holidays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law reached only about a fifth of the ten million wage workers in Russia, since it left out craftsmen and others outside factory inspection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Witte meant the law to win workers back to a paternal government, but a circular of August 1897 banned meetings of workers, and the Social Democrats founded their party in 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 420–21, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","В. Б. Морозов, «Рабочее движение как необходимый фактор зарождения профсоюзов в России», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 108 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rabochee-dvizhenie-kak-neobhodimyy-faktor-zarozhdeniya-profsoyuzov-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Бондарюк, «Фабричное законодательство о рабочем времени», <i>Вестник Томского государственного педагогического университета</i>, no. 8 (149) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fabrichnoe-zakonodatelstvo-o-rabochem-vremeni. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. Х. Шебзухова, «Российское законодательство: проблема продолжительности рабочего дня (конец XIX – начало XX вв.)», <i>Вестник Адыгейского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskoe-zakonodatelstvo-problema-prodolzhitelnosti-rabochego-dnya-konets-xix-nachalo-hh-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 288, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the strike collapse?","a":"Because the police intervened and the factory owners refused to give way, so the workers went back on the old terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Witte had ordered the factory inspectors to announce that no concessions would be made, to teach workers that a strike could win them nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the strike matter even though it failed?","a":"Because it gave an object lesson in organisation: a union with a strike fund now interested the workers of St Petersburg, and the Union of Struggle began printing model rules for unions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its size and discipline also forced the government to promise a shorter working day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the law of 1897 fall short?","a":"Because it covered only about a fifth of the ten million people working for wages, leaving out craftsmen and others not under factory inspection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It still allowed up to 120 hours of overtime a year by agreement, and it was followed by a circular banning workers' meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-487": {"questions":["From 1895 a new charter let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> buy noble estates on its own account and resell them in plots to peasant buyers.","Conservative landowners attacked Witte for protecting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose loans were moving the estates of the nobility into peasant hands faster than they liked."],"abstract":"The <b>Peasants' Land Bank</b> was a state bank founded under a statute of 18 May 1882 to lend peasants money on long terms for buying land from private owners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It answered the land hunger that followed emancipation, for the male peasant population of 50 provinces of European Russia rose from 23.1 million in 1860 to 31.5 million in 1880 while allotments did not grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first nine branches opened on 10 April 1883, and it worked in European Russia without Poland and the Baltic provinces, adding Poland in 1890 and the Baltic and the South Caucasus in 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Loans went to single peasants, village communes and partnerships bound by mutual guarantee, covered up to 80 or 90 per cent of the value of the land and carried interest of 7.5 to 8.5 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until 1895 the bank only financed purchases, standing between noble seller and peasant buyer, but a charter of that year let it buy estates itself and resell them to peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> For the peasants of 1900 it was one of the few routes to more land, and by 1905 about 9 million desyatinas, some 9.8 million hectares (24 million acres), had been bought with its help, 62.4 per cent of the growth in private peasant landholding since 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same operations served the nobility, for the bank bought estates from landowners hit by the agricultural crisis, which kept land prices high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1 July 1905 the mortgages it held came to 405.1 million roubles, against 716 million held by the Nobility Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Conservatives came to fear that it was moving noble estates into peasant hands too fast, and they used its success against Witte, who protected it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the Stolypin reform after 1906 it became a chief instrument of land policy, yet a buyer still paid 4.5 per cent a year on the price, a burden that could drive a family into debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Н. А. Кистенева, «Деятельность Крестьянского поземельного банка на территории Пензенской губернии в 1884–1895 гг.», <i>Экономическая история</i> 16, no. 4 (2020): 407–17, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deyatelnost-krestyanskogo-pozemelnogo-banka-na-territorii-penzenskoy-gubernii-v-1884-1895-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Проскурякова, «Крестьянский поземельный банк и столыпинская реформа (1906–1916 годы)», <i>Имущественные отношения в Российской Федерации</i>, no. 5 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/krestyanskiy-pozemelnyy-bank-i-stolypinskaya-reforma-1906-1916-gody-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Кухаренко, «Роль Крестьянского поземельного банка в решении аграрного вопроса на территории Беларуси», <i>International Journal of Innovative Technologies in Economy</i>, no. 2 (4) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-krestyanskogo-pozemelnogo-banka-v-reshenii-agrarnogo-voprosa-na-territorii-belarusi. [in Russian] [Open access]","James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 289, 343–44, 355, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 670–71, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the bank founded?","a":"Because emancipation had left many peasants with too little land, and their numbers grew fast while their allotments did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After lowering the redemption payments in 1881 the government almost at once created a bank to give peasants cheap long-term credit for buying land from private owners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the nobility also gain from the bank?","a":"Because from 1895 the bank could buy estates itself, which helped landowners hit by the agricultural crisis to sell without loss instead of to speculators at low prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Critics, then and later, said this pushed land prices up and served large landowners more than peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could buying land through the bank ruin a family?","a":"Because the interest came on top of taxes and fines: in one district studied around 1908, land cost about 125 roubles a desyatina, and the bank's 4.5 per cent interest plus zemstvo taxes added about 60 roubles a year to a household budget that was already in deficit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A family that could not find the money sank into insolvency and lost the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peasants' Land Bank"},
    "ru-488": {"questions":["Cholera followed hunger in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and estimates of the excess deaths range from about half a million to about 650,000 people.","When Witte took over the finance ministry in 1892, the treasury had not recovered from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had cut the harvest to its lowest level in decades."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian famine of 1891–1892</b> was the hunger that followed the failure of the 1891 harvest in the central black-earth provinces, the Volga and the Urals, the worst famine of the late imperial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Warnings came in 1890, when a future minister of agriculture told the finance minister that a terrible spectre of famine was approaching, but the government was slow to admit that famine existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A summer drought in 1891 struck several Volga provinces, among them Samara, Kazan and Nizhny Novgorod, after a run of poor years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Misery and starvation spread across some twenty provinces, most of them in the black-earth region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The harvest fell to the lowest level known in the second half of the century, and when Witte took over the finance ministry in 1892 the treasury was nearly empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Relief came through grain bought by the zemstvos, whose agents raised prices by buying in distant provinces, and through public works that employed more than 88,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Special Committee under the heir to the throne led the charity, and Leo Tolstoy, Vladimir Korolenko and Vladimir Vernadsky went into the villages to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British correspondent found fifteen provinces on public relief and visited the free eating-houses Tolstoy ran in Ryazan, where a poor widow cooked for her neighbours in return for her own meals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cholera followed the hunger, and estimates of excess deaths in 1891 and 1892 range from about 500,000 to about 650,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet historians blamed exports of grain and the weight of taxes and redemption payments on peasant farms, and the famine left large numbers of peasants starving in a decade otherwise remembered for industrial growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["А. С. Рогожина, «Голод и продовольственная политика Российской империи в конце XIX – начале XX веков», <i>Научный диалог</i>, no. 4 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/golod-i-prodovolstvennaya-politika-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-kontse-xix-nachale-hh-vekov. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Пьянков and Н. А. Михалев, «Голод 1891–1892 гг. в России в советской и современной отечественной историографии», <i>Вестник Вятского государственного университета</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/golod-1891-1892-gg-v-rossii-v-sovetskoy-i-sovremennoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Н. Фоломеев, «Власть и общество в условиях кризиса: социальные последствия голода 1891–1892 годов в Самарской губернии», <i>Историческая психология и социология истории</i> 8, no. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vlast-i-obschestvo-v-usloviyah-krizisa-sotsialnye-posledstviya-goloda-1891-1892-godov-v-samarskoy-gubernii. [in Russian] [Open access]","E. A. Brayley Hodgetts, <i>In the Track of the Russian Famine: The Personal Narrative of a Journey through the Famine Districts of Russia</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1892), 52, 67–71, https://archive.org/details/intrackofrussian00hodgrich. [Open access]","James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 142, 174, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 48, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 269, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did relief arrive so slowly?","a":"Because the government long refused to admit that there was a famine, and only in mid-1891 did governors and zemstvos begin to prepare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grain bought far away piled up at railway stations for lack of storage and could not be moved to the villages over muddy roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many people die?","a":"Because cholera, the usual companion of famine at the time, followed the hunger, and death rates rose sharply in 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Estimates of the excess deaths range from about 500,000 to about 650,000, and young children made up a large share of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did writers and students go to the villages?","a":"Because the famine set off an unprecedented movement of public help, led by a committee under the heir to the throne, in which Tolstoy, Korolenko and Vernadsky took part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moscow students gave the takings of a concert, 5,669 roubles, and Tolstoy's eating-houses fed peasants for about three shillings a head a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian famine of 1891–1892"},
    "ru-489": {"questions":["When a French squadron visited Kronstadt in July 1891, the tsar stood bareheaded for the Marseillaise, a first public step towards the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> completed at the turn of 1894.","Paris bankers placed the Russian conversion loans from 1888 onwards, a financial rapprochement that prepared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Germany, Austria and Italy."],"abstract":"The <b>Franco-Russian Alliance</b> was the defensive alliance of the French Republic and the Russian Empire, built from a political agreement of August 1891 and a military convention of August 1892 and completed by an exchange of notes at the turn of 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its background was the isolation of Russia in Europe, the end of its treaty with Germany and quarrels with Austria in the Balkans, which Bismarck was suspected of feeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> French money prepared the way, for the Russian conversion loans of 1888 to 1891 were placed by the Paris Rothschilds and their partners, and earlier loans had already been floated in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July 1891 a French squadron under Admiral Gervais anchored at Kronstadt, and the tsar listened bareheaded while a Russian band played the <i>Marseillaise</i>, the anthem of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A diplomatic agreement followed in August 1891, but the French wanted firmer terms, and the tsar refused to be hurried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The military convention signed by Obruchev and Boisdeffre bound Russia to attack Germany with all available forces if Germany, or Italy backed by Germany, attacked France, and France to do the same if Russia was attacked by Germany or by Austria backed by Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both would mobilise at once if any Triple Alliance power mobilised, France putting 1,300,000 men and Russia 700,000 or 800,000 into the field, so that Germany would have to fight in the east and the west together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither would make a separate peace, the convention was to last as long as the Triple Alliance, and every clause was to be kept absolutely secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only after a Russian squadron visited Toulon in October 1893 did the foreign minister Giers formally accept the convention in a letter of 27 December 1893, and a French reply of 4 January 1894 made the alliance a reality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians have argued ever since over whether money, strategy or the public enthusiasm of the naval visits drew the autocracy and the republic together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["William Leonard Langer, <i>The Franco-Russian Alliance, 1890–1894</i>, Harvard Historical Studies 30 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), 184–85, 354–55, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183777. [Open access]","“The Franco-Russian Alliance Military Convention, August 18, 1892,” Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/frrumil.asp. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 50–51, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 270–71, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Ю. М. Галкина, «Франко-русский союз в зеркале историографии: к вопросу о механизмах сближения», <i>Historia provinciae – журнал региональной истории</i> 7, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/franko-russkiy-soyuz-v-zerkale-istoriografii-k-voprosu-o-mehanizmah-sblizheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Баев, «Европейские рынки капиталов и трансформация внешнего долга Российской империи в конце 80-х – начале 90-х гг. XIX в.», <i>СибСкрипт</i>, no. 2 (54) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evropeyskie-rynki-kapitalov-i-transformatsiya-vneshnego-dolga-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-kontse-80-h-nachale-90-h-gg-xix-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Сильянов, «Российско-французское сближение конца XIX века в зеркале общественного мнения: историографические оценки», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i> 23, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiysko-frantsuzskoe-sblizhenie-kontsa-xix-veka-v-zerkale-obschestvennogo-mneniya-istoriograficheskie-otsenki. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the alliance kept secret?","a":"Because the military convention itself required that all its clauses be kept absolutely secret, and it was to last only as long as the Triple Alliance it answered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement was purely defensive, and the tsar did not want trouble with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did French money matter to the alliance?","a":"Because from the late 1880s Russia borrowed heavily on European markets, and the conversion loans of 1888 to 1891 were placed through Paris banks and the Rothschilds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first large Russian loan floated in France came after the rapprochement that began under Alexander III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tsar take so long to ratify the convention?","a":"Because Alexander III followed his own programme and would not act until he was sure the time was right, whatever the impatience of the French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The visit of the Russian squadron to Toulon in October 1893, with its outpouring of public sympathy, came just before he finally accepted the convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Franco-Russian Alliance"},
    "ru-490": {"questions":["War minister Kuropatkin welcomed the Boxer Rising as an excuse for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling Witte he would turn the conquered province into a second Bukhara.","Imposed on the Mukden governor in October 1900, a provisional agreement placed his administration under Russian control after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had cleared the railway zone."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian invasion of Manchuria</b> was the occupation of north-east China by Russian troops in 1900 during the Boxer Rising, officially called the military campaign in China of 1900–1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia already had large interests there, for the Chinese Eastern Railway ran for more than 1,300 kilometres (800 miles) across the region and Harbin was growing into a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When news of the rising reached St Petersburg, the war minister Kuropatkin told Witte that he was glad, because it gave an excuse for seizing Manchuria, which he meant to turn into a second Bukhara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boxer bands and Chinese troops attacked the railway that summer, and on 1 July 1900 by the Russian calendar Chinese guns opened fire across the Amur on Blagoveshchensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mobilisation followed across Siberia, where regiments of reservists and Cossacks were formed, and the Siberian railway carried troops east while ordinary traffic stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In Blagoveshchensk panic turned on the Chinese of the town, and on the orders of the military governor between 3,000 and 3,500 of them were driven into the Amur to cross it, and many drowned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Killings spread through the countryside, and police found 444 bodies in eight districts, a figure the criminal case itself called too low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russian columns entered Manchuria without a declaration of war, cleared the railway zone by 20 August, and on 27 October 1900 imposed an agreement that placed the Mukden governor under Russian control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Witte complained that the army behaved as if in a conquered country, and that the military party wanted not only to punish the Boxers but to annex Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia promised to withdraw yet still held Manchuria in 1904, and its conduct drove Britain and Japan into their treaty of 1902 and led on to war with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i> (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 107–11, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","James Mavor, <i>An Economic History of Russia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Industry and Revolution</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1914), 235–38, https://archive.org/details/cu31924077066565. [Open access]","О. Е. Сорокопудова and Д. Р. Драгунов, «Планы российского руководства в восстании «боксеров» 1900–1901 гг. и их реализация», <i>Каспийский регион: политика, экономика, культура</i>, no. 4 (81) (2024): 66–74, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/plany-rossiyskogo-rukovodstva-v-vosstanii-bokserov-1900-1901-gg-i-ih-realizatsiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Я. С. Гузей, «Боксерское восстание и синдром «желтой опасности»: антикитайские настроения на российском Дальнем Востоке (1898–1902 гг.)», <i>Известия Алтайского государственного университета</i>, no. 4-2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bokserskoe-vosstanie-i-sindrom-zheltoy-opasnosti-antikitayskie-nastroeniya-na-rossiyskom-dalnem-vostoke-1898-1902-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Дубинина, «Н. И. Гродеков о военных событиях в Маньчжурии в 1900–1901 гг. и их последствиях для освоения Приамурья», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 2 (104) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/n-i-grodekov-o-voennyh-sobytiyah-v-manchzhurii-v-1900-1901-gg-i-ih-posledstviyah-dlya-osvoeniya-priamurya. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Г. Дацышен, «Сибирский округ во время военной кампании в Китае в 1900–1901 гг.», <i>Science for Education Today</i>, no. 6 (22) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sibirskiy-okrug-vo-vremya-voennoy-kampanii-v-kitae-v-1900-1901-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Witte oppose sending troops into China?","a":"Because he thought the rising was a result of the Russian seizure of the Kwantung peninsula, and he argued that Russia must not irritate the Chinese if it wished to protect its position in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and the foreign minister also objected that troops were raising Russian flags over Chinese towns while the emperor declared that Russia sought no territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the massacre at Blagoveshchensk happen?","a":"Because the shelling from the Chinese bank of the Amur threw the town into panic, and its people suddenly felt how far away Russia was and how close and large China seemed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In that fear the governor ordered the Chinese of the town across the river, and the order became a massacre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the occupation lead to war with Japan?","a":"Because Russia kept its troops in Manchuria despite repeated promises to leave, and its diplomacy isolated it and drew Britain and Japan into alliance in 1902.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan concluded that it could strengthen its own position only by confrontation, since it did not believe Russian assurances that the occupation was temporary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian invasion of Manchuria"},
    "ru-491": {"questions":["By its contract China could buy back the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after 36 years or receive it free after eighty, while the company ran its strip of land with its own police.","After the southern branch below Changchun passed to Japan at Portsmouth, the northern line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed in Russian and then Soviet hands until its sale in 1935."],"abstract":"The <b>Chinese Eastern Railway</b> was a line across Manchuria that joined the Trans-Siberian at Chita to Vladivostok and, by a southern branch, to Port Arthur, about 2,527 kilometres (1,570 miles) of track built in less than six years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The idea was that of Sergei Witte, Russia's finance minister, who reckoned that a straight line through Chinese territory would save 514 versts, about 550 kilometres (340 miles), on the route along the Amur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Li Hongzhang, sent to Russia in 1896 as China's ambassador extraordinary, agreed only if the road belonged to a nominally private company, and the bargain was tied to a secret defensive alliance against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The contract of 8 September 1896, 27 August by the Russian calendar, gave the work to the Russo-Chinese Bank and its Chinese Eastern Railway Company, on the Russian gauge of 1,524 millimetres (5 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China was to receive the line free after eighty years and could buy it back after 36, while the company gained the absolute and exclusive right to administer its lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> With its own police and full authority inside the strip of land along the track, the company made that zone a place where Russian power, not Chinese, ruled in practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1898 the lease of Port Arthur extended the concession to a branch running south to the sea, an extension of about 970 kilometres (600 miles) from Harbin to Port Arthur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its building angered the local population and helped to provoke the Boxer rising of 1900, which broke out soon after construction began in 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Built to the lowest standard commerce would bear, the single track had to carry the whole supply of the army in the war with Japan, and the Treaty of Portsmouth handed the branch south of Changchun to Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern line stayed Russian and then Soviet until its sale to Manchukuo in 1935, and the population of north-east China rose from under seven million in 1898 to over 38 million in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["John V. A. MacMurray, ed., <i>Treaties and Agreements with and concerning China, 1894–1919</i>, vol. 1, <i>Manchu Period (1894–1911)</i> (New York: Oxford University Press, 1921), 74–77, 119–21, 523, https://archive.org/details/treatiesagreemen01macm. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 86–92, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 578, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Ю. В. Пикалов, «Влияние строительства Китайской Восточной железной дороги на развитие отношений России и Китая в конце XIX — первой четверти XX вв.», <i>Вестник Приамурского государственного университета им. Шолом-Алейхема</i>, no. 3 (36) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vliyanie-stroitelstva-kitayskoy-vostochnoy-zheleznoy-dorogi-na-razvitie-otnosheniy-rossii-i-kitaya-v-kontse-hih-pervoy-chetverti-hh-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Н. Дудин, «Договорный механизм обеспечения российского стратегического присутствия на Дальнем Востоке (на примере КВЖД)», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dogovornyy-mehanizm-obespecheniya-rossiyskogo-strategicheskogo-prisutstviya-na-dalnem-vostoke-na-primere-kvzhd. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Е. Королев, «Московский договор 1896 г.: тайные страницы русско-китайских отношений», <i>Метаморфозы истории</i>, no. 2 (2002), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-dogovor-1896-g-taynye-stranitsy-russko-kitayskih-otnosheniy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Witte want the line to cross Chinese territory?","a":"Because a straight line from Chita to Vladivostok through northern Manchuria was shorter by 514 versts and avoided the difficult country along the Amur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also argued to Li Hongzhang that Russia could only defend China against Japan if its troops could reach the Far East by rail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the railway owned by a private company?","a":"Because Li Hongzhang refused to let it be built or owned by the Russian treasury, so a company was formed under the Russo-Chinese Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In practice, Witte admitted, the company was completely in the hands of the Russian government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the line matter so much in 1904?","a":"Because it was the only link between European Russia and the army in Manchuria, and it had been built to the lowest standard that commerce required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its stations, sidings and rolling stock were not made for the strain of supplying a great army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chinese Eastern Railway"},
    "ru-492": {"questions":["Warned by Witte that seizing a Chinese port would be treachery to an ally, Nicholas II nonetheless sent warships to occupy <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Talienwan in December 1897.","Built up in the 1880s by Li Hongzhang as a base for China's northern fleet, the harbour of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed by lease to Russia in 1898 and to Japan in 1905."],"abstract":"<b>Port Arthur</b> was the naval base at the tip of the Liaodong peninsula in Manchuria that Russia held on lease from China from 1898 to 1905, the town the Chinese call Lüshun, now the Lüshunkou district of the city of Dalian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It began in the 1880s as a fishing village where the Chinese statesman Li Hongzhang built a harbour and forts, and by 1889 it was a base of China's northern fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan took it in the war of 1894–1895 and was granted the peninsula at Shimonoseki, but Russia, France and Germany pressed it to hand the territory back to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Germany seized Kiaochow in 1897, the foreign minister Muravyov urged the seizure of a Chinese port, and Russian warships entered Port Arthur in December against the protests of Sergei Witte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A convention signed in Peking on 27 March 1898, 15 March by the Russian calendar, leased it for 25 years as a naval port closed to every fleet but those of Russia and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Russia now had the ice-free port on the Pacific it had long sought, and Japanese statesmen, who had won the place by force, read its arrival as a threat to Korea and to Japan itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The leased Kwantung territory had about 250,000 inhabitants, and while Russian officers governed it, local people trusted only the Chinese magistrates of their own nearby town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Russian civilian population of the territory grew tenfold in 1901 to 10,326 people, and Orthodox churches went up for soldiers and settlers alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Work on the sea front began in April 1898, but the new fortress was planned for completion only in 1909, and by the outbreak of war less than a third of the money it needed had been spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan besieged and took it in 1904–1905, the Treaty of Portsmouth transferred the lease to Japan, and historians in Russia still argue whether Kwantung was a colony or simply a leased territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Р. С. Авилов, «Порт-Артур: страницы истории русской крепости (1898–1905 гг.)», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/port-artur-stranitsy-istorii-russkoy-kreposti-1898-1905-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","John V. A. MacMurray, ed., <i>Treaties and Agreements with and concerning China, 1894–1919</i>, vol. 1, <i>Manchu Period (1894–1911)</i> (New York: Oxford University Press, 1921), 119–21, 523, https://archive.org/details/treatiesagreemen01macm. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 99–102, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Я. Л. Салогуб and В. П. Казанцев, «Городское управление Квантунской области в 1898–1905 гг.», <i>Общество и государство в Китае</i>, 43, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gorodskoe-upravlenie-kvantunskoy-oblasti-v-1898-1905-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Кан Шисинь, «Православные храмы в Люйшуне (Порт-Артуре) в конце XIX – начале XX века», <i>Клио</i>, no. 9 (213) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravoslavnye-hramy-v-lyuyshune-port-arture-v-kontse-xix-nachale-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Друзяка, «Квантунская область и полоса отчуждения КВЖД: «русские колонии» или «арендованные территории»?», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 3 (2021): 94–105, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kvantunskaya-oblast-i-polosa-otchuzhdeniya-kvzhd-russkie-kolonii-ili-arendovannye-territorii. [in Russian] [Open access]","F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 576–77, 595, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia want Port Arthur?","a":"Because it was an ice-free port on the Pacific, a goal Russia had long sought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The lease of 1898 described it as an entirely secure base for the Russian navy on the coast of northern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Witte oppose the occupation?","a":"Because Russia had just forced Japan out of the same peninsula in the name of China's territorial integrity and had signed a secret defensive alliance with China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seizing a Chinese port, he argued, would be treachery and would turn the country against the railway Russia was building there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the fortress unfinished in 1904?","a":"Because, under pressure from the Ministry of Finance and the cost of building the commercial port of Dalny, the works were split into three stages aimed at completion only in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war only about 4.4 million roubles had been granted, less than a third of the cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lüshunkou District","places":["pl:q623329"]},
    "ru-493": {"questions":["General Dragomirov's quip that Russia was attacking the Japanese with icons while they used bullets went round the country during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one defeat followed another.","Fought largely on Chinese soil for control of Manchuria and Korea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost each side over 200,000 men killed, wounded or dead of disease, by one contemporary estimate."],"abstract":"The <b>Russo-Japanese War</b> of 1904–1905 was fought between the Russian Empire and Japan for control of Manchuria and Korea, mostly on Chinese soil and in the seas around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia had leased Port Arthur in 1898 and occupied Manchuria during the Boxer rising, then delayed the evacuation it promised in 1902, while a timber concession on the Yalu brought Russian troops towards Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan broke off relations in early February 1904, and in the night of 8–9 February its torpedo boats struck the Russian squadron at Port Arthur before any declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia's strength in the Far East hung on a single railway still broken at Lake Baikal, so Japan could land armies in Korea and Manchuria faster than Russia could reinforce its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On land the Russian armies fell back from the Yalu to Liaoyang and Mukden, Port Arthur surrendered at the start of 1905, and in May the fleet sent from the Baltic was destroyed at Tsushima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The war was unpopular in Russia from the start, and its commander Kuropatkin later wrote that soldiers reached Manchuria after a month on the train without knowing whom they were to fight or why.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicholas II blessed departing regiments with icons, and General Dragomirov joked that Russia was attacking the Japanese with icons while they used bullets against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each defeat deepened discontent at home, and in July 1904 the interior minister Plehve, who relied on a system of suspicion, was blown up by a revolutionary in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Provincial newspapers blamed the defeat on commanders without initiative and on a war that ordinary soldiers and the people neither understood nor supported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peace came through American mediation at Portsmouth on 5 September 1905, after a war in which each side mobilised about a million men and lost some 220,000 to 230,000 killed, wounded or dead of disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 576–601, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 344–45, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","A. N. Kuropatkin, <i>The Russian Army and the Japanese War</i>, trans. A. B. Lindsay, ed. E. D. Swinton, vol. 2 (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1909), 79, https://archive.org/details/russianarmyjapa02kuro. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 130–31, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","John V. A. MacMurray, ed., <i>Treaties and Agreements with and concerning China, 1894–1919</i>, vol. 1, <i>Manchu Period (1894–1911)</i> (New York: Oxford University Press, 1921), 522–27, https://archive.org/details/treatiesagreemen01macm. [Open access]","И. А. Хвостова, «Причины поражения России в войне с Японией 1904–1905 гг. По материалам «Нижегородской земской газеты»», <i>Достижения вузовской науки</i>, no. 18 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-porazheniya-rossii-v-voyne-s-yaponiey-1904-1905-gg-po-materialam-nizhegorodskoy-zemskoy-gazety. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Чаплыгин, «Цусимское сражение: ход боя», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (19) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsusimskoe-srazhenie-hod-boya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 346, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Japan risk war with a great European power?","a":"Because what mattered was not the size of Russia's whole army but the number of troops it could deliver to the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1904 Russia had only about 80,000 field troops east of Lake Baikal, and everything else had to come along a single railway broken by the lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the war so unpopular in Russia?","a":"Because, as Kuropatkin admitted, Russians had neither wanted nor expected it and were not prepared for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A provincial zemstvo newspaper wrote that the people had not sympathised with the war from the day it began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia agree to make peace in 1905?","a":"Because after Tsushima the command of the Pacific was settled, and the country was torn by internal dissension and open rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The war had come to look like a disastrous colonial enterprise to be ended at any reasonable cost, and Japan's own money and men were nearly exhausted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russo-Japanese War"},
    "ru-494": {"questions":["General Kondratenko's death in December 1904 removed the one Russian commander with a single purpose during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when Stessel sent a flag of truce to Nogi.","Sappers of both sides tunnelled beneath the eastern forts during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a struggle of 1904 that cost Japan some 57,780 killed and wounded against Russia's 28,200."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Port Arthur</b> was the Japanese investment and capture of Russia's leased naval fortress on the Liaodong peninsula, fought from the end of July 1904 until the fortress surrendered at the start of January 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After General Oku broke the Russian line at Nanshan, the narrow neck of the peninsula, in May, General Nogi's Third Army closed in on a fortress whose land garrison numbered about 43,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The defence was divided from the outset, for General Stessel kept command in defiance of Kuropatkin's order to hand it to the commandant, General Smirnov, and leave the fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nogi's first general assault in August cost him more than 15,000 killed and wounded for two small redoubts, a second in September won only outworks, and the siege settled into sap, mine and countermine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japanese sappers dug trenches up to the eastern forts and galleries beneath them, and the Russians answered underground, so that the fighting went on below the earth as well as on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Japan also brought up 18 heavy howitzers of 280 millimetres (11 inches), whose shells did much to wreck the forts and the Russian ships in the harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decisive prize was 203 Metre Hill, taken on 5 December after the Japanese had lost about 9,000 men around it, because observers on its top could direct fire onto the harbour and town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Kondratenko, the only senior commander with a single purpose, was killed on 15 December, the eastern forts fell one after another, and on 1 January Stessel sent a flag of truce to Nogi without his council's knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 24,000 Russians marched out as prisoners with three months' food still in store, after a siege that cost Russia some 28,200 killed and wounded and Japan 57,780.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1907 Stessel and other senior officers were indicted, and the charges against him included keeping command against orders and failing to build up the fortress's supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 586–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","E. K. Nojine, <i>The Truth about Port Arthur</i>, trans. and abridged A. B. Lindsay, ed. E. D. Swinton (London: John Murray, 1908), 207, 336–38, https://archive.org/details/truthaboutportar00nozh. [Open access]","М. В. Виниченко, «Оборона Порт-Артура (1904–1905 гг.): хроника подземной войны», <i>Пространство и Время</i>, no. 1–2 (19–20) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oborona-port-artura-1904-1905-gg-hronika-podzemnoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Кислицын, «Артиллерия в сражении за Порт-Артур: устаревшее вооружение или нереализованный потенциал?», <i>Клио</i>, no. 1 (229) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/artilleriya-v-srazhenii-za-port-artur-ustarevshee-vooruzhenie-ili-nerealizovannyy-potentsial. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. С. Авилов, «Порт-Артур: страницы истории русской крепости (1898–1905 гг.)», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/port-artur-stranitsy-istorii-russkoy-kreposti-1898-1905-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 131, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Кан Шисинь, «Православные храмы в Люйшуне (Порт-Артуре) в конце XIX – начале XX века», <i>Клио</i>, no. 9 (213) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravoslavnye-hramy-v-lyuyshune-port-arture-v-kontse-xix-nachale-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was 203 Metre Hill worth so many lives?","a":"Because it was not part of the main line of defence but looked down on the harbour and the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With observers on it the Japanese could direct their artillery accurately and make sure the warships still in the harbour never again put to sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Russian command so divided?","a":"Because Stessel ignored Kuropatkin's order to hand over to the fortress commandant, Smirnov, and leave Port Arthur, and kept the command himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official indictment of 1907 accused him of interfering with the commandant's duties and so weakening the defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the surrender anger Russians?","a":"Because, in a British officer's judgement, the means of defence had not been exhausted when Stessel gave in, and large stocks of food and ammunition were found in the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The garrison's courage was not in doubt, only the conduct of its senior commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Siege of Port Arthur","places":["pl:q623329"]},
    "ru-495": {"questions":["Fought over 17 days in February and March 1905 by some 600,000 men, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with the Russian armies retreating north past Tieling towards Harbin.","Admitting he had ordered the retreat a day too late, Kuropatkin took the chief blame for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his report to the tsar and was replaced by Linevich."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Mukden</b> was the last great land battle of the Russo-Japanese War, fought around the Manchurian city now called Shenyang from 19 February to 10 March 1905, 6 to 25 February by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 600,000 men fought on a front of some 155 kilometres (96 miles) for 17 days, which made it one of the largest land battles of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kuropatkin had about 330,000 men in three armies under Linevich, Kaulbars and Bilderling, against about 270,000 under Marshal Oyama, who now had General Nogi's army, freed by the fall of Port Arthur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oyama struck first, sending General Kawamura through the mountains against the Russian left, so that Kuropatkin hurried his reserves there while Nogi marched secretly round the Russian right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By early March the Russian Second Army was almost surrounded, and Kuropatkin's counter-attack against Nogi on 6 March was beaten off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the rear a divisional doctor wrote that when guns were heard behind the lines, everyone understood at once that the Japanese were outflanking them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kuropatkin ordered a general retreat only on 10 March, a day too late by his own admission, and the armies fell back past Tieling towards Harbin in a withdrawal that came close to a rout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Japan lost about 70,000 killed and wounded, and Russia, by different counts, between 85,000 and 89,000 men including prisoners, but Oyama's exhausted army could not turn the retreat into destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his report to the tsar Kuropatkin took the main blame for the defeat, and he was replaced as commander-in-chief by Linevich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The abandonment of Mukden demoralised Russian society, and together with the loss of the fleet at Tsushima two months later it decided the outcome of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 596–98, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","A. N. Kuropatkin, <i>The Russian Army and the Japanese War</i>, trans. A. B. Lindsay, ed. E. D. Swinton, vol. 2 (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1909), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/russianarmyjapa02kuro. [Open access]","В. В. Иванов, «Мукденское сражение. К 100-летию русско-японской войны 1904–1905 гг.», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 3 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mukdenskoe-srazhenie-k-100-letiyu-russko-yaponskoy-voyny-1904-1905-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Хвостова, «Мукденская операция в ходе русско-японской войны 1904–1905 гг.: причины, содержание, итоги», <i>Новый взгляд. Международный научный вестник</i>, no. 5 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mukdenskaya-operatsiya-v-hode-russko-yaponskoy-voyny-1904-1905-gg-prichiny-soderzhanie-itogi. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Российский, «Мукденское сражение в дневнике В. П. Кравкова», <i>Известия Лаборатории древних технологий</i>, no. 3 (16) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mukdenskoe-srazhenie-v-dnevnike-v-p-kravkova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 131, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kuropatkin move his reserves to the wrong flank?","a":"Because the Japanese let it be known that a division from Port Arthur was with Kawamura's army, so the Russians believed Nogi was about to turn their left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kuropatkin took the attack on the First Army's front for the main blow and sent his key forces there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the retreat nearly become a rout?","a":"Because the order came late and the withdrawal past Mukden was disorderly, with the baggage trains shelled by Japanese artillery and panic spreading through the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kuropatkin himself wrote that retiring a day sooner would have saved the army's order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese not destroy the Russian armies?","a":"Because their own troops had suffered heavy losses and were worn out by three weeks of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They occupied Mukden but could never turn the retreat of the Russian columns into a rout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Mukden"},
    "ru-496": {"questions":["Badly wounded early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Admiral Rozhestvensky handed command to Nebogatov, who surrendered the surviving Russian battleships to Togo the following morning.","Losses of 5,045 Russian sailors killed or drowned and 7,282 taken prisoner in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the blow that finally inclined Nicholas II towards peace with Japan."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tsushima</b> was the naval battle of 27–28 May 1905, 14–15 May by the Russian calendar, in which Admiral Togo's fleet destroyed the Russian squadron sent from the Baltic, in the strait between Korea and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Second Pacific Squadron under Admiral Zinovy Rozhestvensky left Libava in October 1904 and on the way fired on British fishing boats on the Dogger Bank, believing that torpedo boats were hidden among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Port Arthur fell while it was still at sea, leaving it no harbour but distant Vladivostok, and Sergei Witte thought the fleet doomed before it ever arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It brought eight battleships, four of them slow and old, against a Japanese fleet that was rested, modern and of one type, with dozens of destroyers and torpedo boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Too short of coal to sail round Japan, Rozhestvensky took the direct route through the strait in columns with his transports in the middle, which let Togo concentrate his fire on the leading flagships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Within about seventy minutes the battleship <i>Oslyabya</i> had sunk and the flagship <i>Knyaz Suvorov</i> was a wreck, and Rozhestvensky, badly wounded, handed command to Admiral Nebogatov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese torpedo boats attacked through the night, and on 28 May Nebogatov surrendered his remaining battleships, an act a Russian naval critic called a disgrace never heard of before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Russians lost 5,045 men killed or drowned and 7,282 taken prisoner, and only two cruisers and two destroyers reached Vladivostok, while Togo reported 116 Japanese killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The censorship could not hide the news, the zemstvo liberals met in Moscow to ask the tsar for an elected assembly, and newspapers wrote that Mukden and Tsushima had ended all hope of victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russian writers have blamed the defeat on Japanese ships and shells, on Rozhestvensky's own mistakes or on the training of officers and crews, and the weight of each is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 594–95, 598–600, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","В. А. Чаплыгин, «Цусимское сражение: ход боя», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (19) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsusimskoe-srazhenie-hod-boya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Лихарев, «Российские мемуаристы и историки о материальных факторах Цусимского сражения», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 1 (79) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskie-memuaristy-i-istoriki-o-materialnyh-faktorah-tsusimskogo-srazheniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nicolas Klado and others, <i>The Battle of the Sea of Japan</i>, trans. J. H. Dickinson and F. P. Marchant (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1906), 93–95, https://archive.org/details/battleofseaofjap00klad. [Open access]","И. А. Хвостова, «Причины поражения России в войне с Японией 1904–1905 гг. По материалам «Нижегородской земской газеты»», <i>Достижения вузовской науки</i>, no. 18 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-porazheniya-rossii-v-voyne-s-yaponiey-1904-1905-gg-po-materialam-nizhegorodskoy-zemskoy-gazety. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 354, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 132, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the squadron take the direct route through the strait?","a":"Because it could not carry enough coal both to fight a battle and to make the long detour round the islands of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Togo had set up his base on the Korean side of the strait and a net of scouts over every approach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Nebogatov's surrender so bitterly condemned?","a":"Because the ships that surrendered had lost very few men, which suggested to a Russian naval critic that the means of resistance had not been exhausted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tsar refused to let Nebogatov and his officers be released on parole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Tsushima lead to peace talks?","a":"Because it was the death blow to Russia's ambitions in the Far East, and after it, Witte wrote, the emperor became inclined towards peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> With the command of the sea settled, both sides agreed in June to name representatives to discuss terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Tsushima"},
    "ru-497": {"questions":["Sergei Witte was made a count for negotiating the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by which Russia handed Japan the lease of Port Arthur and the railway south of Changchun.","Crowds rioted in Japan against the moderate terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, under which Russia recognised Japan's paramount interests in Korea and agreed to leave Manchuria."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Portsmouth</b> was the peace that ended the Russo-Japanese War, signed on 5 September 1905, 23 August by the Russian calendar, at Portsmouth in New Hampshire after talks arranged by President Theodore Roosevelt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Russia sent Sergei Witte and its ambassador in Washington, Roman Rosen, and Japan its foreign minister Komura Jutaro and its minister in Washington, Takahira Kogoro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicholas II told Witte he would not pay a kopeck of indemnity or cede an inch of Russian territory, while Japan, deep in debt to Britain and America, wanted money above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The talks nearly broke down over the indemnity and Sakhalin, until the American ambassador persuaded the tsar to give up the southern half of the island and Japan dropped its demand for payment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the treaty Russia recognised Japan's paramount political, military and economic interests in Korea, and both powers agreed to evacuate Manchuria except the leased territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Russia transferred to Japan, with China's consent, the lease of Port Arthur and Dalny and the railway from Changchun south to Port Arthur, together with its coal mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ceded Sakhalin south of the 50th parallel in full sovereignty and promised Japanese subjects fishing rights along its coasts in the Sea of Japan, the Sea of Okhotsk and the Bering Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Japan crowds that had expected far more rioted against what they saw as moderate terms, while abroad the peace was hailed, Witte wrote, as Russia's first victory after more than a year of defeats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nicholas made Witte a count despite his dislike of him, but at home many papers called the terms bearable but not honourable, and provincial editors set about explaining why the war had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The war it ended had cost Russia, by one count of the time, about 400,000 men and nearly five and a half billion roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John V. A. MacMurray, ed., <i>Treaties and Agreements with and concerning China, 1894–1919</i>, vol. 1, <i>Manchu Period (1894–1911)</i> (New York: Oxford University Press, 1921), 522–27, https://archive.org/details/treatiesagreemen01macm. [Open access]","F. B. Maurice, “The Russo-Japanese War,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 600–601, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 135, 161, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Д. С. Кабанов, «Роман Романович Розен и заключение Портсмутского мирного договора 1905 года», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 2 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/roman-romanovich-rozen-i-zaklyuchenie-portsmutskogo-mirnogo-dogovora-1905-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Сорокин and Ю. В. Соколов, «Портсмутский мирный договор 1905 года в оценках российского общественного мнения», <i>Научный диалог</i>, 14, no. 3 (2025): 512–30, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/portsmutskiy-mirnyy-dogovor-1905-goda-v-otsenkah-rossiyskogo-obschestvennogo-mneni. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 296, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","И. А. Хвостова, «Причины поражения России в войне с Японией 1904–1905 гг. По материалам «Нижегородской земской газеты»», <i>Достижения вузовской науки</i>, no. 18 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prichiny-porazheniya-rossii-v-voyne-s-yaponiey-1904-1905-gg-po-materialam-nizhegorodskoy-zemskoy-gazety. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan give up its demand for an indemnity?","a":"Because its own men and money were nearly exhausted, and an indemnity could have been forced from the tsar only by more fighting that would cost as much as it was worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roosevelt, who at first backed Japan's claim, pressed for a compromise as American opinion turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Witte call the peace a kind of victory?","a":"Because the emperor had forbidden him to pay a kopeck or cede an inch, and he came away paying no indemnity and giving up only half of Sakhalin, which Japan already occupied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The world, he wrote, proclaimed it the first Russian victory after more than a year of defeats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Russian newspapers unhappy with the treaty?","a":"Because, after so many defeats, no peace could feel honourable, and several papers wrote that the terms were bearable but not honourable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Provincial papers used the moment to list the causes of defeat, from the army's poor command to a war the people never supported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Portsmouth"},
    "ru-498": {"questions":["Having cut the first sod of the Trans-Siberian Railway at its Pacific end while heir, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> overruled Witte in 1897 and sent warships to seize Port Arthur.","Abdicating in March 1917 in favour of his brother Michael, the last emperor <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was shot with his family in the Ipatiev house at Yekaterinburg in July 1918."],"abstract":"<b>Nicholas II</b> was the last emperor of Russia, who reigned from the death of his father Alexander III in 1894 until his abdication in March 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As heir he had travelled in the Far East and presided over the Trans-Siberian Railway, cutting its first sod at the Pacific end, and those interests set the tone of his early policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An English historian who knew Russia well judged him a man of amiable disposition, weak will and only moderate ability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1897 he overruled Sergei Witte and ordered warships to seize Port Arthur, a step Witte put down to the young emperor's thirst for military glory and conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war with Japan that followed brought defeat abroad and revolution at home, and in June 1905 he assured a zemstvo deputation that his will to summon national representatives was unchangeable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> His reign is remembered above all for a short speech of 17 January 1895, 29 January by the Western calendar, to deputations of the nobility, the zemstvos and the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some zemstvos had asked to share in the work of government, and he told them such hopes were senseless dreams and that he would uphold autocracy as firmly as his late father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rumour said he had misread a text that spoke of baseless dreams, but the draft prepared by Pobedonostsev called them insane dreams, and the emperor himself chose the milder word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Liberal opinion heard the speech as a notice that nothing would change, though some historians now read it as a rejection of parliamentary rule rather than of every kind of reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He abdicated on 2 March 1917 by the Russian calendar in favour of his brother Michael, and on the night of 16–17 July 1918 he and his family were shot in the Ipatiev house at Yekaterinburg on the orders of the Ural regional soviet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 322, 344, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 278, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 99–101, 130–31, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","С. В. Куликов, «От цесаревича к царю, или Метаморфозы вхождения во власть Николая Второго», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, 68, no. 3 (2023): 807–22, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-tsesarevicha-k-tsaryu-ili-metamorfozy-vhozhdeniya-vo-vlast-nikolaya-vtorogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. А. Андреев, «Как мечтания из «безумных» стали «бессмысленными»: к истории речи императора Николая II 17 января 1895 года», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kak-mechtaniya-iz-bezumnyh-stali-bessmyslennymi-k-istorii-rechi-imperatora-nikolaya-ii-17-yanvarya-1895-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Сафонов and А. И. Рупасов, «Вокруг отречения Николая II», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 2 (26) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vokrug-otrecheniya-nikolaya-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. О. Серов and А. В. Федоров, «Расстрел бывшего императора Николая II и его семьи (1918 г.): историко-правовые аспекты», <i>Журнал российского права</i>, no. 10 (262) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rasstrel-byvshego-imperatora-nikolaya-ii-i-ego-semi-1918-g-istoriko-pravovye-aspekty. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 354, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the speech of 1895 matter so much?","a":"Because it was the new emperor's first public statement of policy, and it told the country that the principle of autocracy would be kept as firmly as under his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It drove many moderate liberals into open opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Witte blame the emperor for the seizure of Port Arthur?","a":"Because Nicholas had first refused the plan and then, after one report that British ships were near, ordered the occupation without telling his finance minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Witte thought it natural that the young emperor followed the advice that suited his own thirst for glory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the authorship of the speech disputed?","a":"Because several versions of the text survive, and the printed one may not match what was actually said in the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A study of the drafts suggests he worked from a text by Pobedonostsev and changed its wording himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nicholas II of Russia"},
    "ru-499": {"questions":["Nominally temporary but renewed again and again, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept the provinces of St Petersburg, Moscow and Kiev under reinforced protection without a break until 1905.","In 1901 the reinforced protection allowed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was spread across almost the whole empire, letting governors ban meetings and close factories by decree."],"abstract":"The <b>Statute of 14 August 1881</b>, in full the Statute on Measures for the Protection of State Order and Public Tranquillity, was the emergency law under which much of the Russian Empire was governed from 1881 to 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Approved by Alexander III on the advice of the Committee of Ministers after the assassination of his father, it gathered the scattered emergency rules of earlier years into a single code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A contemporary English historian credited the drafting to Vyacheslav Plehve and called such ordinances nominally temporary but far more permanent than the laws they modified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It let the interior minister or a governor-general declare an area to be in a state of reinforced protection, and the Committee of Ministers a state of extraordinary protection, renewable every year and every six months respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under reinforced protection a governor could issue binding decrees, imprison people for up to three months or fine them up to 500 roubles, ban meetings, close shops and factories and expel people from the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cases could be moved from the ordinary courts to military courts, or heard behind closed doors if publicity might excite minds and disturb order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provinces of St Petersburg, Moscow, Kharkov, Kiev, Volhynia and Podolia stayed under reinforced protection without a break for 24 years, and in 1901 it spread to almost the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sergei Witte recalled that this reinforced rule, proclaimed as temporary in the early 1880s, kept expanding in area and scope, and that even a committee of ministers called it intolerable in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The zemstvo congress of November 1904 put the abolition of the exceptional laws among its demands, but the regime outlived the revolution of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where Western European states used such powers as a temporary answer to a crisis, in Russia the emergency became a permanent way of governing that lasted until the fall of the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Н. Г. Шурухнов, «Исключительное положение определённых территорий Российской империи: усиленная и чрезвычайная охрана по закону от 14 августа 1881 года», <i>Научный вестник Орловского юридического института МВД России имени В. В. Лукьянова</i>, no. 4 (105) (2025): 83–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/isklyuchitelnoe-polozhenie-opredelyonnyh-territoriy-rossiyskoy-imperii-usilennaya-i-chrezvychaynaya-ohrana-po-zakonu-ot-14-avgusta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Ермаков, «Формирование режима исключительного управления в Российской империи», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 21. Управление (государство и общество)</i>, no. 4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-rezhima-isklyuchitelnogo-upravleniya-v-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 316, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 225, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 347, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a temporary law still in force decades later?","a":"Because it could be renewed by the Committee of Ministers each year for reinforced protection and every six months for extraordinary protection, and it was renewed again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Witte noted that it kept growing in area and scope instead of lapsing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the statute worry liberals so much?","a":"Because it let officials punish people without trial, by administrative arrest, fines and expulsion, and move cases out of the ordinary courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The zemstvo congress of 1904 asked for the exceptional laws to be abolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called a regime and not just a law?","a":"Because applied year after year from 1881 to 1917 it became a whole way of running the empire on emergency legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Western Europe, by contrast, such powers were brought in only for a passing crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-500": {"questions":["Grown from a secret political desk in the St Petersburg police after Karakozov's shot at Alexander II in 1866, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had 27 city sections by the early 20th century.","Paid by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while helping to plan the murder of the interior minister Plehve, Azef sat on the council of the Socialist Revolutionaries as their organiser of terror."],"abstract":"The <b>Okhrana</b> is the usual name for the political police of late imperial Russia, taken from the sections for the protection of order and public security that hunted revolutionaries for the Department of Police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first grew out of a secret political desk in the office of the St Petersburg police chief, which General Fyodor Trepov tried to enlarge after Dmitry Karakozov's attempt on Alexander II in April 1866.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Department of Police, created on 6 August 1880, took charge of political investigation, and a security section was set up in Moscow in 1881.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1902 such sections opened in provincial cities too, and by the early 20th century there were 27 security sections and 12 district sections beside 75 provincial gendarme offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their success was to be measured by the crimes they prevented rather than by arrests, and their chiefs answered to the Department of Police, to the annoyance of gendarme officers who often outranked them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its boldest experiment was police socialism, begun in Moscow by Sergei Zubatov, who from 1901 set up workers' mutual-aid societies, lectures and meetings under the guidance of the secret police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Witte, then finance minister, opposed it, and critics complained that it promoted strikes and disorder, as in Odessa, rather than stopping revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Zubatov was dismissed in 1903, but one of his followers, the priest Georgy Gapon, went on to lead the crowd of petitioners to the Winter Palace in January 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The service also kept agents inside the revolutionary parties, the most notorious being Azef, a leader of the Socialist Revolutionaries' terror who helped plan the murder of the interior minister Plehve while in the government's pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians outside Russia long studied it mainly through the records held at the Hoover Institution at Stanford University in California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["А. Ю. Кеклис, «Из истории создания Санкт-Петербургского охранного отделения как органа политической полиции Российской Империи», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 3 (47) (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-istorii-sozdaniya-sankt-peterburgskogo-ohrannogo-otdeleniya-kak-organa-politicheskoy-politsii-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Ю. Плужников, «Система местных органов политического сыска Российской империи в начале XX в.», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 5 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sistema-mestnyh-organov-politicheskogo-syska-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-nachale-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. Р. Хаснетдинова and А. Д. Камзина, «Зубатовщина (полицейский социализм и рабочее движение)», <i>Проблемы науки</i>, no. 5 (29) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zubatovschina-politseyskiy-sotsializm-i-rabochee-dvizhenie. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 250–51, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","E. J. Dillon, <i>The Eclipse of Russia</i> (New York: George H. Doran, 1918), 133, 158–59, https://archive.org/details/eclipseofrussi00dill. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>Russia and Reform</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1907), 84, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028378366. [Open access]","Я. А. Крутова and Л. П. Рассказов, «Органы политического розыска Российской империи глазами зарубежных исследователей», <i>Общество и право</i>, no. 1 (28) (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organy-politicheskogo-rozyska-rossiyskoy-imperii-glazami-zarubezhnyh-issledovateley. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the secret police organise workers?","a":"Because Zubatov argued that the revolution could be fought with its own weapons, winning workers away from the socialists by imitating their language and methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His societies offered lectures and mutual aid so that workers would look to the government for support against their employers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did police socialism fail?","a":"Because it never really captured the workers, and the strikes it encouraged, notably in Odessa, led to disorder that the police then had to put down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After Zubatov's dismissal in 1903 his organisations lingered but lost their importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Azef affair so damaging?","a":"Because the government's own agent had helped to plan the killing of a minister, yet was kept on and paid even after more murders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> To contemporaries it showed how far the state and its enemies had come to use the same methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Okhrana"},
    "ru-501": {"questions":["Its programme of 1884 called for a Russian workers' socialist party yet still accepted terror against the autocracy, a compromise the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> group's own leader later admitted.","Two mandates at the party congress of 1903 still belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> group, the small émigré circle that had answered the populists with Marxism since the 1880s."],"abstract":"The <b>Emancipation of Labour</b> group was the first Russian Marxist organisation, founded in Geneva in 1883 by Georgi Plekhanov with Pavel Axelrod, Vera Zasulich, Lev Deutsch and Vasily Ignatov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founders came from Black Repartition, the wing of the old Land and Liberty society that had rejected terror, and they regrouped abroad after the People's Will had been broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name echoed the German socialists' Gotha programme of 1875, and the group was a literary circle meant to popularise the ideas of Marx rather than a party of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its programme of 1884 set the aim of spreading socialist ideas in Russia and working out the elements of a Russian workers' socialist party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet the same text accepted terrorist struggle against the absolute government and differed from the People's Will only on the seizure of power, a compromise Plekhanov later admitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The group's real work was the argument with the populists, who hoped that Russia could reach socialism through the peasant commune and saw the peasants as born socialists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It declared Marxism the only scientific theory and its creed, and began to apply to Russia the Marxist idea that the working class had a world-historic mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In <i>Socialism and the Political Struggle</i> of 1883 and <i>Our Differences</i> of 1885, Plekhanov argued that capitalism was already growing in Russia and that industrial centres must come first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the present, he wrote, the only sensible aim was a democratic constitution with universal suffrage, which would not frighten liberals with a red spectre that was still far off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When economist leaders in St Petersburg later urged strikes over politics, the exiled group answered them, and in 1903 it still held two mandates at the party's second congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Г. П. Журавлева, «Выдающийся русский мыслитель - революционер георгий Валентинович Плеханов», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 5 (401) (2017): 167–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vydayuschiysya-russkiy-myslitel-revolyutsioner-georgiy-valentinovich-plehanov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 292–93, 325–28, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","Paul Milyoukov, <i>Russia and Its Crisis</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1905), 424–27, https://archive.org/details/russiaanditscri03miligoog. [Open access]","Georgi Plekhanov, “Programme of the Social-Democratic Emancipation of Labour Group” (1884), in <i>Selected Philosophical Works</i>, vol. 1 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1974), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/plekhanov/1883/xx/sdelg1.htm. [Open access]","Н. М. Багновская, «Динамика взглядов Г. В. Плеханова на роль интеллигенции в рабочем движении России на рубеже XIX-XX вв», <i>Ярославский педагогический вестник</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dinamika-vzglyadov-g-v-plehanova-na-rol-intelligentsii-v-rabochem-dvizhenii-rossii-na-rubezhe-xix-xx-vv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>Russia and Reform</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1907), 338–39, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028378366. [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), First Session, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/ch01.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the group founded abroad rather than in Russia?","a":"Because its founders were revolutionaries who had already fled the police, and by the early 1880s the People's Will had been broken by arrests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plekhanov had sought refuge in western Europe in 1880, and in Geneva the circle could print and translate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the programme of 1884 still accept terror?","a":"Because it was a compromise with the traditions of the People's Will, which most young revolutionaries still admired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The text says the group differed from that party only over the seizure of power and work among the workers, and Plekhanov later agreed that it conceded too much to the populists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Plekhanov want an alliance with liberals at first?","a":"Because he thought a socialist revolution in Russia was far off, so the first goal had to be political freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A demand for a democratic constitution, he argued, could win the sympathy of liberals as well as socialists and make the revolutionaries look politically mature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Emancipation of Labour"},
    "ru-502": {"questions":["Topping the poll for the <i>Iskra</i> editorial board at the party congress of 1903, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Martov and the dropped editors back that November, and Lenin resigned from the paper.","Speaking at the Kazan Cathedral demonstration of December 1876 as a mining student, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was driven underground and later founded the first Russian Marxist group in Geneva."],"abstract":"<b>Georgi Plekhanov</b> was the founder of Russian Marxism, an émigré thinker who led the Emancipation of Labour group from its creation in Geneva in 1883.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The son of a noble family, he gave up a military career, entered the Mining Institute in St Petersburg and joined the populist revolutionaries while still a student.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Kazan Cathedral demonstration of 6 December 1876, the first open demonstration of the populists, he made a speech against the autocracy and the police ordered his arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Land and Liberty split in 1879 he led Black Repartition, the faction that rejected terror, and from 1880 he lived abroad for thirty-seven years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He taught himself German to translate the <i>Communist Manifesto</i> in 1882, and his pamphlets of the 1880s argued that Russia was already on the capitalist road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By the 1890s he was a socialist of European standing who wrote for the German party press and corresponded with Engels, and he answered Struve's revisionist challenge in 1901 and 1902.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the party congress of 1903 he stood with Lenin, telling the delegates that the good of the revolution was the highest law, and he topped the poll for the <i>Iskra</i> board with 23 votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within months he changed course, bringing Martov and the three editors the congress had dropped back onto the board, and from its 53rd issue in November 1903 <i>Iskra</i> was a Menshevik paper without Lenin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He became a leader of the Mensheviks while keeping a lonely position of his own, and in 1914 he called for the war with Germany to be fought to a victorious end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Petrograd in 1917 he attacked Lenin's April Theses and rejected the October seizure of power, warning that it would bring the dictatorship of one party and one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 325–28, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","Paul Milyoukov, <i>Russia and Its Crisis</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1905), 424–27, https://archive.org/details/russiaanditscri03miligoog. [Open access]","Г. П. Журавлева, «Выдающийся русский мыслитель - революционер георгий Валентинович Плеханов», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 5 (401) (2017): 167–71, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vydayuschiysya-russkiy-myslitel-revolyutsioner-georgiy-valentinovich-plehanov. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Thirty-first Session, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/ch31.htm. [Open access]","Г. Г. Касаров, «Политическая борьба меньшевиков за центральные органы партии (1903-1904 гг.)», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-menshevikov-za-tsentralnye-organy-partii-1903-1904-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), “The Second Congress, July–August 1903,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol06.htm. [Open access]","Я. Б. Головин, «П. Б. Струве и Г. В. Плеханов: к истории одной полемики», <i>Известия Московского государственного технического университета МАМИ</i> 2, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/p-b-struve-i-g-v-plehanov-k-istorii-odnoy-polemiki. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Plekhanov break with the populists?","a":"Because he came to believe that capitalism was already developing in Russia and that the industrial workers, not the peasant commune, would carry the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had also opposed the terror of the People's Will, arguing that killing a tsar would only bring another tsar and the arrest of the best revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he turn against Lenin after 1903?","a":"Because he feared an open split more than he valued Lenin's victory, and in the autumn of 1903 he agreed to co-opt the former editors whom the congress had not re-elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin resigned from the board in protest, and the paper passed to the Mensheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he oppose the October Revolution?","a":"Because he held that Russia had to pass through a long capitalist stage before socialism was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A seizure of power by one party in an unready country would, he warned, end not in a dictatorship of the workers but in the rule of one party and one person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Georgi Plekhanov"},
    "ru-503": {"questions":["The programme of 1903 committed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to overthrowing the tsarist autocracy and to an eight-hour day, and named the dictatorship of the proletariat as the condition of social revolution.","Bolsheviks and Mensheviks alike still claimed the name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for years after the split of 1903, until the Bolshevik wing called itself communist in 1918."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Social Democratic Labour Party</b> was the Marxist party of the Russian Empire, founded at a congress of only nine delegates held in Minsk in March 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That congress adopted no more than the party's name and a manifesto announcing its formation, and it approved no rules, so the manifesto had to stand in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under police repression its local committees and émigré groups then worked largely on their own, while the so-called economists, who put wages and reforms first, fought the Marxists for its direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Bund, the union of Jewish workers founded in 1897, joined the new party at its first congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second congress opened in Brussels on 30 July 1903, moved to London when the Belgian police took an interest, and sat there until 23 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It gave the party its first programme, whose most immediate political task was the overthrow of the tsarist autocracy and its replacement by a democratic republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The programme also demanded an eight-hour working day and named the dictatorship of the proletariat, the conquest of power by the workers, as the condition of the social revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its rules made the congress the supreme organ, set a Party Council over the Central Committee and the editorial board of the central organ, and defined who counted as a member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The five delegates of the Bund walked out when their demand for a federal place in the party failed, and the congress ended divided into a majority and a minority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those factions became the Bolsheviks and the Mensheviks, weakened by internal division on the eve of the war, and they shared the one name until the Bolshevik wing became the Communist Party in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Tom Kemp, foreword to <i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/foreword.htm. [Open access]","С. С. Войтиков, «„Святая святых“: из истории становления и эволюции Устава (Манифеста, организационного Устава) РСДП/РСДРП — РСДРП(б) — РКП(б) — ВКП(б) (1898 — 1920-е годы)», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 3 (20) (2017): 63–80, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svyataya-svyatyh-iz-istorii-stanovleniya-i-evolyutsii-ustava-manifesta-organizatsionnogo-ustava-rsdp-rsdrp-rsdrp-b-rkp-b-vkp-b-1898-1920-e. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), “The Second Congress, July–August 1903,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol06.htm. [Open access]","Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 296–97, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), “Programme of the Social-Democratic Workers’ Party”, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/program.htm. [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), “Organisational Rules of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party”, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/rules.htm. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Albert Rhys Williams, <i>Lenin: The Man and His Work</i> (New York: Scott and Seltzer, 1919), 34, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027005606. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the party need a second founding congress in 1903?","a":"Because the congress of 1898 had produced only a name and a manifesto, with no programme and no rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the years that followed, local groups acted independently under police pressure, so the congress of 1903 had virtually to found the party anew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bund leave the party in 1903?","a":"Because it demanded to represent the whole Jewish working class and to be only federally attached to the party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the congress rejected this, its five delegates were alone in voting for their proposals, and the Bund withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the programme put a democratic republic first?","a":"Because it held that the tsarist autocracy was the mightiest bulwark of everything backward in Russia and the worst enemy of the workers' strivings for freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Socialism was the final aim, but the immediate task was to replace autocracy with a republic ruled by an elected assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Social Democratic Labour Party"},
    "ru-504": {"questions":["Led by Yuli Martov, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> favoured a broad party and cooperation with the liberal Kadets, but won only 3.2 per cent in the Constituent Assembly elections of 1917.","Refusing his seat on the three-man <i>Iskra</i> board in August 1903, Martov led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a boycott of the party's central bodies from a bureau in Geneva."],"abstract":"The <b>Mensheviks</b> were the minority faction of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party, named from the Russian word for 'minority' after the party's second congress in 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yuli Martov led them, and Pavel Axelrod, Alexander Potresov and Fyodor Dan sat with the young Trotsky on the faction bureau they formed that autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They held that the workers must first be educated in socialist principles before any decisive struggle, and they feared that rigid centralism would weaken the party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1905 they favoured work in the Duma and cooperation with the liberal Kadets, which the Bolsheviks rejected, and ten of their 'minority men' were first elected to the First Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the autumn of 1917 they claimed some 200,000 members, but in the elections to the Constituent Assembly that November they won only 3.2 per cent of the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The split began at the congress of 1903 over the first paragraph of the party rules, which had to say who counted as a member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin wanted every member to take personal part in one of the party organisations, while Martov asked only for regular personal assistance under the direction of one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Martov's wording was adopted by 28 votes to 22 with one abstention, but once the delegates of the Bund had walked out, Lenin's supporters held the majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The congress then cut the <i>Iskra</i> board to three, electing Plekhanov with 23 votes, Martov with 22 and Lenin with 20, and Martov refused to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lenin called the quarrel over the first paragraph a slight crack in the pot, but by September 1903 the minority had its own bureau and was boycotting the party's central bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 296–98, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","Г. Г. Касаров, «Политическая борьба меньшевиков за центральные органы партии (1903-1904 гг.)», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-menshevikov-za-tsentralnye-organy-partii-1903-1904-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 365, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Tom Kemp, foreword to <i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/foreword.htm. [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Twenty-third Session, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/ch23.htm. [Open access]","<i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Thirty-first Session, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/ch31.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, <i>One Step Forward, Two Steps Back</i> (1904), section I, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 7 (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1961), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1904/onestep/i.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a small difference of wording split the party?","a":"Because behind it lay two ideas of a party, a tight body of active members or a broader movement of supporters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin admitted the difference over the first paragraph was slight, but he argued that it started the minority's swing towards views he called opportunist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Mensheviks the 'minority' when Martov won the vote on membership?","a":"Because the names came from the later votes on the central bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the Bund left the congress, Lenin's supporters had the majority, and the election of a three-man <i>Iskra</i> board left Martov's side in the minority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Mensheviks support cooperation with liberals?","a":"Because they believed the coming revolution would first bring political freedom, so the workers should be educated and organised rather than rushed into a final struggle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That made them favour the Duma and joint action with the Kadets, while the Bolsheviks boycotted the Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mensheviks"},
    "ru-505": {"questions":["Going abroad after his Siberian exile to found <i>Iskra</i> with Plekhanov and Martov, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the majority faction that emerged from the party congress of 1903 as the Bolsheviks.","Calling in 1914 for socialists to wish defeat on their own governments, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that the imperialist war should be transformed into a civil war."],"abstract":"<b>Vladimir Lenin</b> was the founder and leader of the Bolshevik Party and of the Soviet state, the pen name of Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov, who was born at Simbirsk on the Volga in 1870.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His father, a schools inspector descended from serfs, had risen into the hereditary nobility through state service, so hostile writers could call Lenin a nobleman's son and friendly ones a peasant's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In May 1887 his elder brother Alexander was hanged for plotting to kill the emperor, and the same year Vladimir was expelled from Kazan University for taking part in student disturbances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He passed the law examinations of St Petersburg University as an external student in November 1891, worked as an assistant barrister in Samara, and moved to the capital in the autumn of 1893.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There he joined the Marxist circles, was arrested and was exiled to the village of Shushenskoye in Siberia, where he wrote steadily under several pen names, Lenin among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In the last year of his exile he conceived a plan for an all-Russian newspaper printed abroad, and once free he left Russia to found <i>Iskra</i> with Plekhanov, Martov, Axelrod and Zasulich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He set out his ideas on organisation in <i>What Is to Be Done?</i>, which Dietz published in Stuttgart early in March 1902.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the party congress of 1903 his followers emerged as the majority, the Bolsheviks, against a minority led by Martov, and the two factions soon disagreed on tactics as well as organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Within months Plekhanov brought back the old editors and Lenin left the <i>Iskra</i> board, and he spent most of the next years in exile in western European cities, watched by the Russian police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When war came in 1914 he predicted the defeat of the tsarist government and called on all socialists to work for the defeat of their own governments and to turn the war into civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Benno Ennker, “Lenin, Vladimir Il’ich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10194. [Open access]","Albert Rhys Williams, <i>Lenin: The Man and His Work</i> (New York: Scott and Seltzer, 1919), 23–35, https://archive.org/details/cu31924027005606. [Open access]","В. П. Зиновьев, «В. И. Ленин политик», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 4 (12) (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-i-lenin-politik. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Б. Ерофеев, «В. И. Ленин и адвокатура», <i>Евразийская адвокатура</i>, no. 3 (10) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-i-lenin-i-advokatura. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), “St. Petersburg, 1893–1898,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol01.htm. [Open access]","Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), “In Exile, 1898–1901,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol02.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, <i>What Is to Be Done? Burning Questions of Our Movement</i> (1902), in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 5 (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1961), editorial note, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1901/witbd/index.htm. [Open access]","Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 296–97, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","Г. Г. Касаров, «Политическая борьба меньшевиков за центральные органы партии (1903-1904 гг.)», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 2 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskaya-borba-menshevikov-za-tsentralnye-organy-partii-1903-1904-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did his brother's execution matter to him?","a":"Because it struck a close and devoted family, and the brother had been a brilliant student who went to the scaffold refusing to ask for mercy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same year Lenin himself was expelled from Kazan University, and while the brother was in prison almost no one would even be seen travelling with their mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he want a newspaper published abroad?","a":"Because the scattered local groups needed a centre that the police could not close, and a paper could direct their work and draw them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He worked out the plan in Siberian exile and later developed it in <i>Iskra</i> and in <i>What Is to Be Done?</i><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin welcome the defeat of Russia in 1914?","a":"Because he saw the war as an imperialist war fought for the ruling classes, not for the workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defeat would weaken the tsarist government, and he hoped socialists everywhere would turn the war into a civil war against their own rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vladimir Lenin"},
    "ru-506": {"questions":["Borrowing the title of Chernyshevsky's novel of 1863, Lenin's polemic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called for a stable organisation of professional revolutionaries to lead the Russian workers' movement.","'Give us an organisation of revolutionaries, and we will overturn Russia,' Lenin wrote in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, his attack of 1902 on the economists who put wages before politics."],"abstract":"<b><i>What Is to Be Done?</i></b> is the pamphlet in which Vladimir Lenin set out his plan for a party of revolutionaries, conceived in 1901 and written in Munich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was published in Stuttgart by the firm of Dietz in 1902, and its title repeated that of Nikolay Chernyshevsky's novel of 1863, which had become the programme of the young radicals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lenin wrote it after an attempt to unite the Russian social democratic organisations abroad had failed in June 1901, developing ideas first sketched in the leading articles of <i>Iskra</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It asked three questions, about the content of political agitation, the organisational tasks of Russian social democracy and the plan for an all-Russian organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its target was the so-called economists, who wanted the movement to concentrate on wages and social reforms, and those who wanted a broad party on the West European model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its most famous claim was that the working class, by its own effort alone, could develop only trade union consciousness, the conviction that it must combine in unions and fight the employers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Social democratic consciousness, Lenin wrote, would have to be brought to the workers from without, by people who had mastered socialist theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the movement needed was a stable organisation of leaders maintaining continuity, made up of professional revolutionaries whether they came from among students or working men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Recalling the shame he had felt as an amateur in a study circle, he wrote, 'Give us an organisation of revolutionaries, and we will overturn Russia'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The pamphlet framed the debate at the party congress of 1903, where its call for a strong, centralised and disciplined party set the terms of the quarrel over who could be a member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), “Munich, 1901–1902,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol03.htm. [Open access]","Леонид Терещенков, «В. И. Ленин (Н. Ленин) что делать? Наболевшие вопросы нашего движения. Stuttgart: Verlag von J. H. W. Dietz, 1902», <i>Laboratorium: Журнал социальных исследований</i> 4, no. 3 (2012): 154–56, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-i-lenin-n-lenin-chto-delat-nabolevshie-voprosy-nashego-dvizheniya-stuttgart-verlag-von-j-h-w-dietz-1902. [in Russian] [Open access]","Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 11–12, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","Tom Kemp, foreword to <i>1903: Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party</i>, trans. Brian Pearce (London: New Park Publications, 1978), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/history/international/social-democracy/rsdlp/1903/foreword.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, <i>What Is to Be Done? Burning Questions of Our Movement</i> (1902), chap. 2, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 5 (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1961), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1901/witbd/ii.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, <i>What Is to Be Done? Burning Questions of Our Movement</i> (1902), chap. 4, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 5 (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1961), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1901/witbd/iv.htm. [Open access]","А. Н. Смертин, «Некоторые подходы к организационно-правовым основам партийного строительства радикального крыла политического спектра на II съезде РСДРП», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 4 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nekotorye-podhody-k-organizatsionno-pravovym-osnovam-partiynogo-stroitelstva-radikalnogo-kryla-politicheskogo-spektra-na-ii-sezde-rsdrp. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Lenin think workers could not reach socialism by themselves?","a":"Because he held that strikes and unions taught workers to bargain with employers and press the government for laws, which he called trade union consciousness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Socialist ideas, in his view, came from theory worked out by educated people and had to be carried into the movement from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he want professional revolutionaries?","a":"Because amateur circles were easily broken by the police and could not keep a movement going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A stable core of people who gave all their time to the work would maintain continuity, and the broader the mass movement grew, the more it would need such an organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the pamphlet matter for the split of 1903?","a":"Because it had already made clear what kind of party Lenin wanted, so delegates came to the congress knowing what the argument would be about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its idea of a centralised party of professionals lay behind his wording of the first paragraph of the rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"What Is to Be Done?"},
    "ru-507": {"questions":["A police spy named Azef sat for years on the Central Committee of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while its Combat Organisation killed two interior ministers and a grand duke.","Drawn together in 1901 by Gershuni from scattered groups of veteran populists, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent its propagandists to live among the peasants rather than the factory workers."],"abstract":"The <b>Socialist Revolutionary Party</b> was the agrarian socialist party of late imperial Russia, formed by veterans of the People's Will at almost the same time as the Social Democrats and devoted above all to the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scattered groups, whose beginnings went back to about 1892, were drawn together in 1901 by Grigory Gershuni, who toured the provinces gathering their mandates before going abroad that December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its propagandists lived among the peasants, learned that their grievances were simple and economic, and proved far more enterprising than the Marxists in reaching them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gershuni wrote that the party saw itself as the spiritual heir of the People's Will, and it was the only Russian party to put terror officially into its programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the autumn of 1917 it claimed a million members, and in the elections to the Constituent Assembly that November it won 39.5 per cent of the vote, against 22.5 per cent for the Bolsheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its Combat Organisation, which Gershuni created and led from 1902, acted almost on its own, answered to no other party body and drew large sums from party funds for terror alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 2 April 1902 the student Balmashev, whom Gershuni had enrolled, killed the interior minister Sipyagin at the building of the Committee of Ministers in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation went on to kill Sipyagin's successor Plehve in 1904 and the Grand Duke Sergei Alexandrovich on 4 February 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The party insisted that its terror was purely political, aimed at the institutions of state power, and it treated killing as one means among several of rousing the masses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1909 it emerged that Azef, a member of its Central Committee, had for years been a police spy who knew of the murders of Sipyagin, Plehve and the Grand Duke and did not prevent them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “Reaction and Revolution in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 332, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Thomas Garrigue Masaryk, <i>The Spirit of Russia: Studies in History, Literature and Philosophy</i>, trans. Eden and Cedar Paul, vol. 2 (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 295–96, https://archive.org/details/spiritofrussiast02masauoft. [Open access]","М. И. Леонов, «„Тигр революции“ – Г. А. Гершуни», <i>Вестник Самарского университета. История, педагогика, филология</i> 26, no. 1 (2020): 17–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tigr-revolyutsii-g-a-gershuni. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. И. Аликина, «Социалисты-революционеры и террор», <i>Вестник Красноярского государственного педагогического университета им. В. П. Астафьева</i>, no. 2 (24) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotsialisty-revolyutsionery-i-terror. [in Russian] [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Р. С. Закиров, «Об одной провокации Департамента полиции (из материалов следствия над членом Боевой организации партии социалистов-революционеров И. П. Каляевым в 1905 г.)», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 2 (7) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ob-odnoy-provokatsii-departamenta-politsii-iz-materialov-sledstviya-nad-chlenom-boevoy-organizatsii-partii-sotsialistov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 377, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the party turn to the peasants rather than the workers?","a":"Because it grew out of the populist tradition, which looked to the village rather than the factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Marxist programme offered the peasants little hope, while the party's propagandists lived among them and spoke to their practical grievances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it use terror?","a":"Because it saw itself as heir to the People's Will, whose killings of officials it admired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Terror was meant to frighten and weaken the authorities, force concessions, protect the revolutionaries from repression and win new supporters through the publicity of each attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Azef affair so damaging?","a":"Because the man at the top of the party's own secret work turned out to be serving the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He had sat on the Central Committee while knowing of its greatest assassinations, so the revelation of 1909 threw doubt on the party's whole terrorist campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Socialist Revolutionary Party"},
    "ru-508": {"questions":["Led by the historian Pavel Miliukov, the liberal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> formed the largest group in the First Duma and drafted the Vyborg appeal after its dissolution in 1906.","Also called the Party of People's Freedom, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were nicknamed the professors' party and were banned by the Soviet government in December 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>Kadets</b>, formally the Constitutional Democratic Party, were the leading liberal party of late imperial Russia, and their nickname came from the Russian initials of that title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The party grew out of two semi-legal bodies founded in 1903, the Union of Zemstvo Constitutionalists and the Union of Liberation, and so joined liberal zemstvo landowners with professional men in the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding congress met in Moscow from 12 to 18 October 1905, during the general strike, and only a fraction of the delegates were able to reach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its second congress declared that Russia must be a constitutional and parliamentary monarchy, and the party kept that position until March 1917, when it came out for a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its leader was the historian Pavel Miliukov, a founder of the party who chaired its Central Committee from 1907 to 1918 and directed its newspaper <i>Rech</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the elections to the First Duma in 1906 the Kadets alone ran an organised campaign, and with somewhere between 179 and 184 deputies by different counts they formed the largest group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Miliukov himself had been excluded on formal grounds, yet he was always in the lobbies, and every decision of the house was referred to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the government dissolved the Duma after seventy-two days, Kadet deputies took the lead in drafting the Vyborg appeal for passive resistance, issued in Finland beyond the reach of the Russian police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 120 Kadets who signed it were prosecuted and deprived of the franchise, and after the electoral law of 1907 the party shrank to a group of fifty or sixty deputies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1917 it entered the Provisional Government but won only 4.5 per cent in the Constituent Assembly elections, and the Soviet government banned it on 11 December 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Harold Williams, <i>Russia of the Russians</i> (London: Sir Isaac Pitman, 1914), 71, 86–89, https://archive.org/details/russiaofrussians00willuoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>Russia and Reform</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1907), 519–20, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028378366. [Open access]","С. А. Степанов, «Кадеты (конституционно-демократическая партия)», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: Политология</i>, no. 8 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kadety-konstitutsionno-demokraticheskaya-partiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Miliukov, Pavel Nikolaevich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10191. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 359, 365, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 305, 326, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Kadets called the professors' party?","a":"Because their rank and file were unusually well educated and their leadership included many distinguished names from the universities and the professions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The party had been formed by joining zemstvo liberals with professional men in the towns, and its leader Miliukov was a historian who had lectured at Moscow University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Vyborg appeal backfire?","a":"Because its call for passive resistance had no real means of pressing the government and stayed a verbal threat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its main result was the prosecution of the deputies who signed it, many of the party's ablest men, who were jailed and lost the right to stand again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Kadets lose ground after 1907?","a":"Because the new electoral law of 1907 took away the voting power that had given them their majorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government also refused to legalise the party, broke up its meetings and kept it from holding congresses, so it came to the later Dumas as a small opposition group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Constitutional Democratic Party"},
    "ru-509": {"questions":["Congratulated by Dubrovin on dissolving the Second Duma in June 1907, Nicholas II replied that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be a trusted bulwark of his throne.","Purishkevich's Union of Michael the Archangel broke away from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest Black Hundred body, which leaned heavily on government subsidies after 1908."],"abstract":"The <b>Union of the Russian People</b> was the largest monarchist organisation of late imperial Russia, founded in St Petersburg in October and November 1905 and led by the doctor Alexander Dubrovin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the main body of what were called the Black Hundreds, who styled themselves 'True Russians' and openly demanded the restoration of unlimited autocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its local branches sprang up across the country after the manifesto of 17 October 1905, its newspaper <i>Russkoe Znamya</i> first appeared on 27 November, and at its height in 1908 it claimed some 400,000 members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its critics saw its chief organisers as semi-official adventurers, and the movement came to control much of the machinery of the Church and at times terrorised local officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Government money became its main source of funds, above all from 1908 to 1909, when members' dues of 50 kopecks a year had come to mean almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Nicholas II treated the union as an ally, and when Dubrovin congratulated him on the dissolution of the Second Duma on 3 June 1907, he replied that it would be a trusted bulwark of the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1906 the union hounded a mob against the Jews of Belostok, where murder and robbery went on for three days while the officers and civil authorities looked on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its local branches petitioned the emperor to pardon men convicted for such riots and the pardons were published, while Dubrovin was suspected of a part in the murder of the Kadet deputy Mikhail Herzenstein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The union then broke apart, as Vladimir Purishkevich and other expelled members founded a Russian People's Union named after Michael the Archangel and Dubrovin's followers later formed a union of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stolypin's government, which did not want so strong a union, backed first one leader and then another and so played its part in the break-up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["О. А. Шимбирёва, «Образ союза русского народа и его лидеров в восприятии современников: по материалам архивного фонда союза русского народа», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obraz-soyuza-russkogo-naroda-i-ego-liderov-v-vospriyatii-sovremennikov-po-materialam-arhivnogo-fonda-soyuza-russkogo-naroda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Иванов, «Доктор Дубровин: новые факты биографии и оценки современников», <i>Новейшая история России</i> 14, no. 4 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/doktor-dubrovin-novye-fakty-biografii-i-otsenki-sovremennikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 191–93, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 3 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1920), 149–51, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr03dubn. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 366, 370, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","А. И. Курицын, «\"Союз русского народа\" в показаниях Н. Е. Маркова», <i>Вестник Псковского государственного университета. Серия: Социально-гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/soyuz-russkogo-naroda-v-pokazaniyah-n-e-markova. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Станкова, «Раскол в черносотенно-монархическом движении и союзнические организации Западной Сибири», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 17 (2001), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/raskol-v-chernosotenno-monarhicheskom-dvizhenii-i-soyuznicheskie-organizatsii-zapadnoy-sibiri. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the emperor favour such an organisation?","a":"Because he and the empress had come to see these monarchists of the right as their salvation against revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The union preached loyalty to unlimited autocracy, and after June 1907 he publicly hailed it as a bulwark of the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the union linked to pogroms?","a":"Because its agitation was aimed at Jews and revolutionaries alike, and its supporters took part in anti-Jewish violence, as at Belostok in 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its branches also petitioned for the pardon of convicted rioters, and the pardons were granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the union fall apart after 1908?","a":"Because its leaders quarrelled over tactics and over the Duma, and Purishkevich and other expelled members left to found a rival union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The government, wary of a movement grown too strong, supported different leaders in turn, which deepened the split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Union of the Russian People"},
    "ru-510": {"questions":["Officials enforcing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1882 renamed townlets as villages so that Jews who had settled there could be expelled, and from 1887 banned moves between villages.","Proposed by Count Ignatyev after the pogroms of 1881, the supposedly temporary <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed in force for thirty-five years, until the Provisional Government abolished them in 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>May Laws</b> were the temporary rules on the Jews of Russia proposed by the interior minister Count Nicholas Ignatyev and sanctioned by Alexander III on 3 May 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They forbade Jews to settle anew outside the towns and townlets, suspended deeds and leases of property in their names in the countryside, and banned them from trading on Sundays and Christian holidays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They applied within the Pale of Settlement, the provinces to which Jewish residence was confined, though not in the ten provinces of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were issued as a temporary measure until a general revision of the laws on the Jews, and they bypassed the State Council, the ordinary path of legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official reason was to protect the Jews from the hostility shown in the riots and to lessen the peasants' economic dependence on them, which turned the victims of the pogroms into the accused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Their effect was to crowd millions of people into the towns and townlets of the western region, shutting them out, by one estimate, from nine-tenths of the territory open to them before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials stretched them further, renaming townlets as villages so as to expel the Jews who had settled there, and a decree of 29 December 1887 stopped village Jews from moving to another village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More restrictions followed, with a quota for Jewish pupils from 1887 and the expulsion from Moscow in 1891 of some 17,000 Jewish artisans who had held the right to live there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ignatyev's refusal to soften the rules helped bring his dismissal on 30 May 1882, and Pobedonostsev was credited with predicting that a third of Russia's Jews would convert, a third emigrate and the rest starve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The rules were still in force in 1916, and they fell only with the Provisional Government's decree of 20 March / 2 April 1917 abolishing religious and national restrictions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herman Rosenthal, “May Laws,” in <i>The Jewish Encyclopedia</i> (New York: Funk and Wagnalls, 1901–1906), https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/10508-may-laws. [Open access]","S. M. Dubnow, <i>History of the Jews in Russia and Poland</i>, trans. I. Friedlaender, vol. 2 (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society of America, 1918), 309–14, https://archive.org/details/historyofjewsinr02dubnuoft. [Open access]","Г. А. Жолобова, «Смена курса в правовом регулировании торговой правоспособности лиц иудейского вероисповедания в условиях перехода к политике «Контрреформ» Александра III», <i>Lex Russica</i>, no. 9 (130) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smena-kursa-v-pravovom-regulirovanii-torgovoy-pravosposobnosti-lits-iudeyskogo-veroispovedaniya-v-usloviyah-perehoda-k-politike. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 265, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Илья Печенин, «Лакеи и поэты: изгнание евреев из Москвы в 1891–1892 гг. и читательская аудитория журнала «Восход»», <i>Тирош. Труды по иудаике, славистике, ориенталистике</i>, no. 15 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lakei-i-poety-izgnanie-evreev-iz-moskvy-v-1891-1892-gg-i-chitatelskaya-auditoriya-zhurnala-voshod. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Вах and А. Ю. Полунов, «К истории деятельности Н. П. Игнатьева на посту министра внутренних дел (1881-1882): по материалам дневника митрополита Исидора (Никольского)», <i>Государственное управление. Электронный вестник</i>, no. 83 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-istorii-deyatelnosti-n-p-ignatieva-na-postu-ministra-vnutrennih-del-1881-1882-po-materialam-dnevnika-mitropolita-isidora. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 284–85, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the rules called temporary?","a":"Because they were issued as a stopgap until a general revision of the laws on the Jews, which a commission later prepared but which was never acted on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Calling them temporary also let the government avoid the State Council and enact them quickly by the emperor's sanction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government punish the victims of the pogroms?","a":"Because it blamed the riots on the Jews themselves, claiming they exploited the poorest classes around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rules were presented as a way to calm relations between Jews and peasants, so the people who had been attacked were treated as the cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rules weigh so heavily on village Jews?","a":"Because a Jew living in a village when they were issued was in effect tied to it, losing the right to move to another village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local officials used the vague wording to harass and extort, and appeals to the Senate cost time and money even when they succeeded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"May Laws"},
    "ru-511": {"questions":["Lenin counted 2,800,000 strikers during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, more than twice the number of factory workers in the whole country, in the course of a single year.","Because most of the army stayed loyal during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the monarchy survived the strikes, peasant risings and mutinies, though it had to concede limits on its power."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Revolution of 1905</b> was a wave of strikes, peasant risings, mutinies and liberal protest that swept the Russian Empire between January 1905 and June 1907 and forced the autocracy to accept an elected legislature for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rapid industrialisation had created an urban working class with no legal outlet, peasants resented paying for land they thought already theirs, and educated society had almost no freedom of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defeat by Japan brought these grievances into the open, and by late 1904 zemstvo congresses and banquets were demanding civil liberties and an elected national assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shooting of peaceful workers' processions in St Petersburg on 9 January 1905, by the Julian calendar then used in Russia, or 22 January by the Western one, turned that pressure into revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the spring and summer strikes spread from city to city, peasants attacked manor houses, Poland, the Baltic provinces and the Caucasus rose, and sailors of the Black Sea Fleet mutinied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The climax came in October, when a railway strike grew into a general strike that stopped trains, factories, banks and newspapers across the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Witte told Nicholas II that the choice lay between a military dictatorship and a constitution, and on 17 October the tsar signed a manifesto promising civil freedoms and a legislative State Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The concession split the opposition, since moderates accepted it while workers' councils on the St Petersburg model fought on until the council's arrest and the crushing of an armed rising in Moscow in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because most of the army stayed loyal, punitive expeditions restored order, and the revolution is usually said to end on 3 June 1907, when the Second Duma was dissolved and the electoral law rewritten for landowners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Lenin counted 2,800,000 strikers in 1905, more than twice the number of factory workers, and historians still disagree over whether the year fatally weakened the old order or whether only the First World War could have brought it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Revolution of 1905 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10135. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 346–63, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Lecture on the 1905 Revolution,” January 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 23 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 236–53, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/jan/09.htm. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 237–48, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","В. Ю. Кузнецов и В. Л. Маркин, «Московский университет и Всероссийская политическая стачка в октябре 1905 года», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-universitet-i-vserossiyskaya-politicheskaya-stachka-v-oktyabre-1905-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","B. E. Shatsky, “The Role Played by the State Duma in the Formation of the New Russia,” <i>American Journal of International Law</i> 11, no. 3 (1917): 635–38, https://archive.org/details/jstor-2188030. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>1905</i>, ch. 8, “The Creation of the Soviet of Workers’ Deputies,” trans. Anya Bostock, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1907/1905/ch08.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the war with Japan matter so much?","a":"Because defeat after defeat discredited the government and gave the liberal opposition its opening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zemstvo congresses and banquets in late 1904 used the moment to demand civil liberties and a national assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the monarchy survive 1905 when it fell in 1917?","a":"Because most of the army remained loyal to the tsar, unlike in the wartime conditions of 1917, so the regime could crush the Moscow rising and send punitive expeditions into the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the October Manifesto split the opposition?","a":"Because moderate liberals were frightened by the violence and chose to pursue their aims through the promised Duma, while the radicals and the workers' councils wanted a constituent assembly and fought on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Revolution of 1905"},
    "ru-512": {"questions":["Official figures gave 96 dead and 333 wounded for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in St Petersburg, while a reporters' list of 4,600 victims cited by Lenin has never been traced.","A petition asking Nicholas II for an eight-hour day and a constituent assembly was being carried to the Winter Palace on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when soldiers opened fire on the marchers."],"abstract":"<b>Bloody Sunday</b> was the massacre of 9 January 1905, by the Julian calendar then used in Russia, or 22 January by the Western one, when troops fired on processions of unarmed workers marching to the Winter Palace in St Petersburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The marchers belonged to the Assembly of Russian Factory and Mill Workers, a legal society led by the priest Georgy Gapon that had grown out of a police scheme to keep workers away from the revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Early in January the dismissal of members at the Putilov works set off a strike that spread from factory to factory, and Gapon's committee resolved to carry a petition straight to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The petition asked for an eight-hour day, fair wages and the right to discuss grievances, and for a constituent assembly elected by universal suffrage, since Russia was too vast to be governed by officials alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the Sunday morning columns of men, women and children set out from the suburbs with icons, church banners and portraits of the tsar, who had already left the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the Narva Gate, Gapon wrote, Cossacks rode through his column with drawn swords and the infantry then fired volley after volley, killing the men who carried the icons and the tsar's portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other processions were stopped in the suburbs or on the Nevsky Prospekt, and in the Alexander Garden boys who had climbed trees to watch were shot down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official figures gave 96 killed and 333 wounded, a commission of lawyers counted more than 1,200 dead, and Lenin later spoke of over a thousand killed, so the true toll has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figure of 4,600 killed and wounded that Lenin published within days came from a foreign newspaper's report of a reporters' list that has never been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Witte, who watched the shooting from his balcony, wrote that the workmen were completely alienated from the tsar, and strikes soon spread to Moscow, Riga, Vilna and other cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["George Gapon, <i>The Story of My Life</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1906), 180–85, 257–59, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_n-Y2AAAAMAAJ. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 349–50, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>Russia and Reform</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1907), 491–93, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028378366. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 251–53, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Lecture on the 1905 Revolution,” January 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 23 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 236–53, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/jan/09.htm. [Open access]","А. Н. Зашихин, «О числе жертв Кровавого воскресенья (по поводу цифры 4 600)», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 3 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-chisle-zhertv-krovavogo-voskresenya-po-povodu-tsifry-4-600. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. М. Пушкарева, «Был ли Георгий Гапон „организатором“ революции 1905 г.?», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 4 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byl-li-georgiy-gapon-organizatorom-revolyutsii-1905-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Revolution of 1905 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10135. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the workers carry a petition to the tsar himself?","a":"Because negotiations with the employers at the Putilov works had failed, and the strike committee turned to the characteristically Russian step of a mass appeal to the sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The petition asked him to break down the wall between himself and his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of dead still disputed?","a":"Because the official figure of 96 killed was rejected at once as too low, rumours abroad ran to thousands, and the figures of later commissions and historians differ widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lenin's figure of 4,600 rested on a list that has never been traced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the massacre matter so much?","a":"Because it destroyed the workers' trust in the tsar: Witte wrote that they were completely alienated from him, and Lenin regarded the day as the beginning of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strikes spread to other cities within weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bloody Sunday (1905)"},
    "ru-513": {"questions":["Witte refused to meet the priest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the October Manifesto and gave an intermediary 500 rubles to have him taken out of Russia instead.","Telling his followers to come unarmed because they were not robbers but citizens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew up the petition that the strikers of St Petersburg carried towards the Winter Palace."],"abstract":"<b>Georgy Gapon</b> was a Russian Orthodox priest who built the largest legal workers' organisation in St Petersburg and led the procession that ended in the massacre of Bloody Sunday in January 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was drawn into the police-sponsored labour movement begun by Sergei Zubatov, and an English observer reported that by Gapon's own account he had received money from Zubatov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Assembly of Russian Factory and Mill Workers that he organised was allowed by the city governor to hold meetings even before the minister had approved its rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It grew fast, and Gapon later wrote that it had nine branches with 5,000 paying members in October 1904 and more than 20,000 members by the time of the January strike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1904 its inner circle of worker activists drew up a broad democratic programme, which later went into the petition carried to the tsar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> When the Putilov strike spread in January 1905, Gapon drew up the petition and told his followers to come without arms, as they were not robbers but citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He marched at the head of his column to the Narva Gate, survived the volleys lying among the dead, and was smuggled abroad, where revolutionaries received him with a mixture of sympathy and suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Returning after the October Manifesto, he again had dealings with the government, and Witte, who refused to see him, paid 500 rubles to have him taken out of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In March 1906 the police were bargaining with him over the price of betraying the revolutionaries' fighting organisation, and that year he was killed by revolutionaries who judged him a traitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He has been cast as hero, police agent and provocateur, but one Russian study argues that his organisation drifted steadily away from the police's purposes and that he cannot be called the organiser of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["George Gapon, <i>The Story of My Life</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1906), 130–31, 180–85, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_n-Y2AAAAMAAJ. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>Russia and Reform</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1907), 491–93, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028378366. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 251–54, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","И. М. Карусева, «Собрание русских фабрично-заводских рабочих Санкт-Петербурга. К истории создания крупнейшей легальной рабочей организации под руководством Георгия Гапона», <i>Известия Российского государственного педагогического университета им. А. И. Герцена</i>, no. 85 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sobranie-russkih-fabrichno-zavodskih-rabochih-sankt-peterburga-k-istorii-sozdaniya-krupneyshey-legalnoy-rabochey-organizatsii-pod. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. М. Пушкарева, «Был ли Георгий Гапон „организатором“ революции 1905 г.?», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 4 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byl-li-georgiy-gapon-organizatorom-revolyutsii-1905-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 349–50, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Revolution of 1905 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10135. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the police tolerate Gapon's organisation?","a":"Because it began as part of a police scheme to keep workers under the influence of the authorities and away from the revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The interior ministry wanted to patronise it, though its real activity moved further and further from that aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the petition include political demands?","a":"Because its worker activists had already drawn up a broad democratic programme in March 1904, and Gapon added the political claims of the zemstvos and professions to the workers' economic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did revolutionaries kill him?","a":"Because after his return he again had dealings with the government, and in March 1906 the police were bargaining with him over the price of betraying the revolutionaries' fighting organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Revolutionaries judged him a traitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Georgy Gapon"},
    "ru-514": {"questions":["Lenin named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the best known of the mutinies of 1905, and a film of 1925 and an official anniversary in 1955 made it a legend of Soviet revolutionary memory.","Ending with the crew's surrender at Constanța after eleven days at sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw the battleship renamed and its captured sailors shot, hanged or sentenced to hard labour."],"abstract":"The <b>Potemkin mutiny</b> was the rising of the crew of the Black Sea Fleet battleship Prince Potemkin-Tavrichesky in June 1905, the most famous of the naval mutinies of the Russian Revolution of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It broke out on 14 June by the Julian calendar, 27 June by the Western one, while the ship lay off the Tendra Spit near Odessa for exercises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The crew had been served meat full of maggots, and when most refused the borscht made from it, an officer called the guard and ordered a group of sailors covered with a tarpaulin to be shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sailors seized rifles, the senior officer shot one of their leaders, Grigory Vakulenchuk, and in the fighting that followed most of the officers, the captain among them, were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Led by the sailor Matyushenko and a ship's committee, the battleship sailed for Odessa, where strikes were already under way, and laid Vakulenchuk's body on shore with an appeal to the townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Witte later blamed the spirit of revolt in the fleet on misrule by the naval authorities and on the recruitment of sailors from the more educated part of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The admiral commanding the rest of the squadron did not dare to engage the mutineers, and when the crew of another battleship gave in, panic swept the Potemkin and its men cried 'To Romania!'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After eleven days at sea, with a call at Feodosia between two visits to Constanța, the crew handed the ship to the Romanian authorities on 25 June, or 8 July by the Western calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The battleship was renamed the Panteleimon, and of the mutineers who fell into the authorities' hands four were shot, two hanged and dozens sent to hard labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin named it the best known of the year's mutinies, and Eisenstein's film of 1925 and an official fiftieth anniversary in 1955 made it a central legend of Soviet revolutionary memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Constantine Feldmann, <i>The Revolt of the “Potemkin”</i>, trans. Constance Garnett (London: William Heinemann, 1908), 33–46, 138–42, https://archive.org/details/revoltofpotemkin00feld. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 355, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","А. Д. Попов, «Празднование 50-летия восстания на броненосце „Потемкин“ в 1955 году: мероприятия официальной коммеморации и их противоречивость», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 6 (38) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prazdnovanie-50-letiya-vosstaniya-na-bronenostse-potemkin-v-1955-godu-meropriyatiya-ofitsialnoy-kommemoratsii-i-ih-protivorechivost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>1905</i>, ch. 18, “The Red Fleet,” trans. Anya Bostock, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1907/1905/ch18.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Lecture on the 1905 Revolution,” January 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 23 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 236–53, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/jan/09.htm. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 273, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a quarrel over food become a mutiny?","a":"Because the officers answered a silent refusal to eat with the threat of shooting the men who refused, and the sailors seized rifles rather than watch their comrades killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mutiny not spread through the fleet?","a":"Because the rest of the squadron sailed away without either fighting or joining, and when another battleship's crew gave in, panic broke the Potemkin's own resolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the mutiny so well remembered?","a":"Because Lenin singled it out among the mutinies of 1905, and the Soviet state made it a founding legend through Eisenstein's film of 1925 and the anniversary celebrations of 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-515": {"questions":["With St Petersburg left without light, trams or trains during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Witte advised Nicholas II that only a military dictatorship or a constitution could end the crisis.","Lenin counted more than half a million strikers in a single month during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which spread from the Moscow railways to factories, banks, schools and law courts."],"abstract":"The <b>October general strike</b> of 1905 was the nationwide stoppage of railways, factories, offices and public services that paralysed the Russian Empire and forced Nicholas II to issue the October Manifesto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of a strike of Moscow printers that began in September, partly under the influence of mass meetings in Moscow University, and turned into a citywide strike against the autocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decisive step came on 7 October by the Julian calendar, 20 October by the Western one, when the Moscow–Kazan railway stopped work and the railwaymen's union called a strike of the whole network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within days nearly every railway in the empire had followed, and Lenin later counted more than half a million strikers in October alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Factories, banks, law courts, schools and chemists closed, newspapers stopped appearing, and in St Petersburg the electric light failed and people feared for the water supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Witte recorded that the capital was left without light, trams, telephones or trains, while the war minister admitted that he had too few troops to reopen even the line to Peterhof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strikers demanded civil freedoms, an amnesty and a constituent assembly elected by universal suffrage, and in St Petersburg a council of workers' delegates first met on 13 October to coordinate the stoppage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Faced with the choice that Witte put to him between a military dictatorship and a constitution, the tsar signed the manifesto on 17 October, promising civil liberties and a legislative Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The strike began to ebb within days, and on 19 October the St Petersburg council resolved to call it off two days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American study of 1908 saw it as the culmination of the protest against the narrow consultative Duma promised in August, a protest that obliged the government to concede far more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>1905</i>, ch. 7, “The Strike in October,” trans. Anya Bostock, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1907/1905/ch07.htm. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 358–60, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 240–42, 270–72, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","В. Ю. Кузнецов и В. Л. Маркин, «Московский университет и Всероссийская политическая стачка в октябре 1905 года», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-universitet-i-vserossiyskaya-politicheskaya-stachka-v-oktyabre-1905-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Revolution of 1905 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10135. [Open access]","Samuel N. Harper, <i>The New Electoral Law for the Russian Duma</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1908), 1–7, https://archive.org/details/cu31924030532091. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Lecture on the 1905 Revolution,” January 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 23 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 236–53, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/jan/09.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the railways decisive?","a":"Because a railway strike cut every city off from every other and from the government, and once the Moscow lines stopped, nearly every railway in the empire followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the government not simply break the strike by force?","a":"Because the war minister admitted that, although there were enough troops in the capital to put down a rising, there were not enough to restore even the railway between St Petersburg and Peterhof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the strike end so soon after the manifesto?","a":"Because the manifesto met the demand for civil freedoms and a legislative Duma, the movement in Moscow and elsewhere began to wane, and railway traffic was soon restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-516": {"questions":["Witte had the chairman of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrested in November 1905 and then waited for a full session in December to round up 190 of its delegates at once.","Nicknamed the working men's government, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> decreed an eight-hour day and called a second general strike against martial law in Poland before its leaders were tried in 1906."],"abstract":"The <b>Saint Petersburg Soviet</b> of Workers' Deputies was the council of delegates elected by the factories of the capital during the October general strike of 1905, which became the model for workers' councils in Moscow, Odessa and other cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first meeting, of no more than thirty to forty delegates, was held on the evening of 13 October, by the Julian calendar, in the Technological Institute, and it at once called on the workers to strike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Factories elected one delegate for every 500 workers, and the council counted more than two hundred members by 17 October and between 500 and 600 later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chairman was Georgy Nosar, an assistant attorney who had worked in a factory under the name Khrustalev, and its paper, <i>Izvestiya</i>, was printed by armed print-workers who seized the presses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Witte, who became premier on 17 October, found that it seemed at first a considerable power, because the workers obeyed it and the printers' loyalty gave it a hold over the newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It went on to decree the eight-hour day by direct action and called a second general strike in November against martial law in Poland and the punishment of the Kronstadt mutineers, but both campaigns failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People nicknamed it the working men's government and asked whether Witte would arrest Khrustalev or Khrustalev would arrest Witte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government struck once its authority had begun to decline, arresting Nosar on 26 November and then rounding up 190 members at a session on 3 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Leon Trotsky, one of its members, later claimed to have drafted its key resolutions and edited its newspaper, though recent Russian research argues that Nosar was more than the nominal chairman he was later made out to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the trial, which opened on 19 September 1906, the defence won the political argument in open court, but 15 of the accused were sentenced to loss of all rights and exile to Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>1905</i>, chs. 8, 20 and 30, trans. Anya Bostock, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1907/1905/index.htm. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 270–76, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 358–62, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","И. Н. Стрекалов, «Г. С. Хрусталёв-Носарь в 1905 г. К предыстории создания Петербургского Совета рабочих депутатов», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/g-s-hrustalyov-nosar-v-1905-g-k-predystorii-sozdaniya-peterburgskogo-soveta-rabochih-deputatov. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Н. Стрекалов, «Был ли Л. Д. Троцкий лидером в Петербургском совете рабочих депутатов 1905 г.?», <i>Новейшая история России</i> 11, no. 2 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byl-li-l-d-trotskiy-liderom-v-peterburgskom-sovete-rabochih-deputatov-1905-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Варфоломеев, «Противостояние защиты и обвинения на политических судебных процессах в позднеимперской России (по материалам Уголовного дела С.-Петербургского Совета рабочих депутатов)», <i>Базис</i>, no. 1 (3) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/protivostoyanie-zaschity-i-obvineniya-na-politicheskih-sudebnyh-protsessah-v-pozdneimperskoy-rossii-po-materialam-ugolovnogo-dela-s. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were delegates elected by factories?","a":"Because the workplace was the only link among workers who had no organisation of their own, so one delegate was chosen for every 500 workers, on the precedent of the Shidlovsky commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the printers matter so much?","a":"Because whether newspapers appeared depended on the printers' willingness to work, and their loyalty to the council gave it a hold over the whole press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Witte wait before arresting the council?","a":"Because he was advised to wait until the workers themselves would welcome the arrest, once Nosar and the council had lost their prestige, and so avoid a bloody clash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Saint Petersburg Soviet"},
    "ru-517": {"questions":["The moderate liberals who accepted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a sufficient reform took the name of their new party from its date, calling themselves the Union of 17 October.","Drafted from a report by Witte, who had told Nicholas II to choose between dictatorship and a constitution, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was published on the same day as that report."],"abstract":"The <b>October Manifesto</b> was the decree that Nicholas II signed at Peterhof on 17 October 1905 by the Julian calendar, 30 October by the Western one, promising civil liberties and a legislative State Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the first time the autocracy had accepted a limit on its power, since no law was henceforth to take effect without the Duma's consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was issued at the height of the October general strike, when the railways, factories and public services of the empire had stopped and force had failed to end the stoppage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Sergei Witte had advised the tsar that he must choose between a military dictatorship and a constitution, and Prince Obolensky drafted the text on the basis of Witte's report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tsar also consulted other advisers behind Witte's back, and the Grand Duke Nikolai Nikolaevich, who refused to become dictator, is said to have threatened to shoot himself unless the tsar signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The manifesto promised freedom of conscience, speech, assembly and association, a wider franchise for the coming Duma, and the unalterable rule that no law could take effect without the Duma's consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was published on the same day as Witte's report, and Witte's post of President of the Committee of Ministers became that of a premier at the head of a cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moderate liberals accepted it and organised as the Union of 17 October, the Octobrists, whose name came from its date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The newly founded Constitutional Democrats resolved to use the new order only as one means towards a fuller democracy, while the St Petersburg Soviet called a strike to show its dissatisfaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joyful demonstrations greeted it across the country but were met by bands of the Black Hundreds whose counter-demonstrations ended in pogroms, mostly against Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 237–48, 270–74, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Samuel N. Harper, <i>The New Electoral Law for the Russian Duma</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1908), 1–7, https://archive.org/details/cu31924030532091. [Open access]","Э. И. Аглиуллин, «Манифест 17 октября 1905 года и учреждение российского парламента», <i>Инновационная наука</i>, no. 5-3 (17) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/manifest-17-oktyabrya-1905-goda-i-uchrezhdenie-rossiyskogo-parlamenta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Ю. Кузнецов и В. Л. Маркин, «Московский университет и Всероссийская политическая стачка в октябре 1905 года», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/moskovskiy-universitet-i-vserossiyskaya-politicheskaya-stachka-v-oktyabre-1905-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Revolution of 1905 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10135. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nicholas II sign it?","a":"Because the general strike had paralysed the empire, there were not enough troops to restore even the railways around the capital, and the grand duke whom he might have made dictator refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Witte at first oppose issuing a manifesto at all?","a":"Because he thought it unwise to go into details in a document proclaimed in every church, and preferred that the tsar simply approve his report, which would leave Witte answerable for the measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the left reject it?","a":"Because it fell short of a constituent assembly and universal suffrage, so the Constitutional Democrats treated it as only a step and the St Petersburg Soviet called a strike against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"October Manifesto"},
    "ru-518": {"questions":["Promulgated days before the first Duma met, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still called the emperor's power autocratic but dropped the word unlimited, a change Nicholas II accepted only reluctantly.","Witte, on the point of leaving office, insisted that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be published before the first Duma convened, fearing that the new assembly would otherwise turn itself into a constituent assembly."],"abstract":"The <b>Fundamental Laws of 1906</b> were the revised basic laws of the Russian Empire that Nicholas II promulgated on 23 April 1906 by the Julian calendar, 6 May by the Western one, days before the first State Duma met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are often described as Russia's first constitution, marking the passage from unlimited autocracy towards a constitutional monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Article 4 still declared that supreme autocratic power belonged to the emperor, but the word 'unlimited' was dropped, and Nicholas agreed to the change only reluctantly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tsar kept a veto over legislation, command of the army and navy, control of foreign policy, the appointment of ministers and the power to dissolve the Duma, provided he named a date for new elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old State Council became an upper house with the same legislative rights as the Duma, half of its members appointed by the emperor and half elected by bodies such as the nobility, the zemstvos and the universities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Article 87 let the government issue emergency measures while the Duma was not sitting, though they could not alter the Fundamental Laws or the electoral rules and lapsed unless approved within two months of its return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The laws themselves could be revised only on the emperor's initiative, so the Duma had no power to change the framework under which it worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Witte, who left office just before they were published, wrote that he had insisted they be issued before the Duma met, fearing that otherwise it would turn itself into a constituent assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also claimed credit for keeping foreign policy and supreme command in the emperor's hands, against a draft he thought would have left the tsar weaker than the President of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Critics noted that the liberties of October were repeated with so many exceptions that they meant little, and in June 1907 the government changed the electoral law by decree in breach of its own rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 345–50, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 364, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","А. С. Смыкалин, «Свод основных государственных законов от 23 апреля 1906 г. — начало развития отечественного конституционализма», <i>Вестник Саратовской государственной юридической академии</i>, no. 2 (109) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/svod-osnovnyh-gosudarstvennyh-zakonov-ot-23-aprelya-1906-g-nachalo-razvitiya-otechestvennogo-konstitutsionalizma. [in Russian] [Open access]","Samuel N. Harper, <i>The New Electoral Law for the Russian Duma</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1908), 1–7, https://archive.org/details/cu31924030532091. [Open access]","B. E. Shatsky, “The Role Played by the State Duma in the Formation of the New Russia,” <i>American Journal of International Law</i> 11, no. 3 (1917): 635–38, https://archive.org/details/jstor-2188030. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were they issued before the Duma met?","a":"Because Witte and the Council of Ministers feared that a Duma left to settle its own powers would become a constituent assembly and be drawn into futile disputes about the extent of its rights, provoking the use of force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the word 'autocratic' survive?","a":"Because Nicholas II accepted the loss of the word 'unlimited' only reluctantly, and Article 4 still gave the emperor supreme autocratic power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Article 87 matter?","a":"Because it let ministers legislate while the Duma was in recess, subject to the legislature's approval within two months of its return, so the government had a way around the new parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-519": {"questions":["Deputies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be arrested during a session without the house's consent, yet many of them balked at the oath of loyalty to the emperor and autocrat.","Only the third <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, elected under the decree of 3 June 1907 that favoured landowners, sat its full five-year term, while the first two were dissolved within months."],"abstract":"The <b>State Duma</b> was the elected lower house of the Russian Empire's legislature, which sat from 1906 until the fall of the monarchy in 1917 and was the country's first national parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A merely consultative Duma was announced in August 1905, but the October Manifesto made it legislative, so that no law could take effect without its consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its powers were bounded by the Fundamental Laws of 1906, which made the State Council an upper house with equal rights and left the tsar a veto and the power of dissolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Deputies were chosen indirectly, through separate electoral assemblies for landowners, townspeople and peasants, and they swore an oath of loyalty to the emperor and autocrat that troubled many in the first two Dumas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They could not be arrested during a session without the Duma's consent unless caught in the act, and they were guaranteed full freedom of speech on the questions before the house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The first Duma met in the Tauride Palace in St Petersburg on 27 April 1906, by the Julian calendar, after the deputies had been received by the tsar in the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An English visitor admired the dignity of its president, the jurist Muromtsev, and the natural tone of its debates, and found the house far more moderate than he had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first two Dumas, dominated by the opposition, were dissolved after a few months each, and on 3 June 1907 a new electoral law issued by decree gave landowners far more weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third Duma, elected under that law, was the only one to sit its full five-year term, from 1907 to 1912, and like the fourth it was dominated by moderates and the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the First World War the Duma met only briefly, its Progressive Bloc turned against the government, and in February 1917 its leaders formed the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 356, 366, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","B. E. Shatsky, “The Role Played by the State Duma in the Formation of the New Russia,” <i>American Journal of International Law</i> 11, no. 3 (1917): 635–38, https://archive.org/details/jstor-2188030. [Open access]","Samuel N. Harper, <i>The New Electoral Law for the Russian Duma</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1908), 1–7, https://archive.org/details/cu31924030532091. [Open access]","Maurice Baring, <i>A Year in Russia</i> (London: Methuen, 1907), 191–94, https://archive.org/details/yearinrussia00bari. [Open access]","С. И. Ушаков, «Правовой статус депутатов Государственной Думы Российской империи 1906–1917 гг.», <i>Евразийский Союз Ученых</i>, no. 4-11 (13) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-deputatov-gosudarstvennoy-dumy-rossiyskoy-imperii-1906-1917-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. И. Аглиуллин, «Манифест 17 октября 1905 года и учреждение российского парламента», <i>Инновационная наука</i>, no. 5-3 (17) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/manifest-17-oktyabrya-1905-goda-i-uchrezhdenie-rossiyskogo-parlamenta. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the August 1905 Duma not enough?","a":"Because it was expressly consultative and excluded the factory workers altogether, and the protest against it culminated in the October general strike, which forced the government to make the Duma legislative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the oath trouble deputies?","a":"Because it bound them to loyalty to the emperor as autocrat, which many deputies of the first two Dumas, who wanted a constitutional order, found a moral and political problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the third Duma last when the first two did not?","a":"Because the electoral law of 3 June 1907 gave the landowners far more weight, producing a house dominated by the moderate Octobrists, who were ready to work with the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"State Duma (Russian Empire)"},
    "ru-520": {"questions":["Signing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost 167 former deputies three months in prison and their political rights, which kept many leaders of the first Duma out of the second.","Witte judged the 1907 trial of those who signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> both unlawful and unfair, a political move against the Constitutional Democrats rather than an act of justice."],"abstract":"The <b>Vyborg Manifesto</b> was the appeal issued in July 1906 by some two hundred deputies of the dissolved first State Duma, calling on the people to refuse taxes and recruits until the Duma was restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The government had dissolved the Duma after less than three months, and soldiers guarded the doors of the Tauride Palace when the decree was posted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its leaders, mostly Constitutional Democrats and members of the Labour group, crossed into autonomous Finland and met in the Belvedere hotel at Vyborg, since a protest in the capital risked bloodshed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The text was first drafted by Cadet leaders in St Petersburg on 9 July 1906, by the Julian calendar, and was completed and signed at Vyborg the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It urged passive resistance only, and since the Cadets had no organisation for conspiracy the country did not respond and the signatories simply went home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The appeal sealed the fate of the first Duma's leaders, for 169 people were tried in December 1907 and 167 former deputies were sentenced to three months in prison and the loss of their political rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Witte, no friend of the Cadets, thought the trial unlawful and unfair, a political move against the Cadets designed to stop the leaders of the first Duma from being elected again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those who had signed were struck off the electoral rolls, so the second Duma, which met in 1907, lacked many of the most prominent men of the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second Duma, elected under the same law, was even more polarised than the first, and it was dissolved in June 1907 after months of bitter conflict over land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An English writer in St Petersburg heard even moderate men say at the first dissolution that the government had tried from the start to discredit the Duma and provoke it to rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 365–72, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Sergei Witte, <i>The Memoirs of Count Witte</i>, trans. and ed. Avrahm Yarmolinsky (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page, 1921), 372–73, https://archive.org/details/memoirsofcountwi00wittuoft. [Open access]","Н. В. Митрофанов, «К вопросу об участии Ф. Ф. Кокошкина в Выборгском воззвании 10 июля 1906 г.», <i>Актуальные проблемы государства и права</i> 8, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-ob-uchastii-f-f-kokoshkina-v-vyborgskom-vozzvanii-10-iyulya-1906-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. М. Усманова и С. В. Шебалков, «Девяносто дней одиночного заключения: тюремная жизнь перводумцев, осуждённых за подписание Выборгского воззвания», <i>Ученые записки Казанского университета. Серия Гуманитарные науки</i> 159, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/devyanosto-dney-odinochnogo-zaklyucheniya-tyuremnaya-zhizn-pervodumtsev-osuzhdyonnyh-za-podpisanie-vyborgskogo-vozzvaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Maurice Baring, <i>A Year in Russia</i> (London: Methuen, 1907), 281–86, https://archive.org/details/yearinrussia00bari. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the deputies go to Finland?","a":"Because Finland was an autonomous grand duchy where they could meet, and a protest in St Petersburg itself risked bloodshed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the appeal fail?","a":"Because the Cadets had no organisation for conspiracy, the country was exhausted by the disorders of the previous winter, and nobody acted on the call to refuse taxes and recruits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the trial matter for the second Duma?","a":"Because the conviction cost the signatories their political rights, so many of the first Duma's most prominent members could not stand for the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vyborg Manifesto"},
    "ru-521": {"questions":["Poland's seats in the Duma fell from 37 to 14 after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose manifesto declared that the assembly must be Russian in spirit.","Lenin dated the so-called June Third monarchy from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which followed Stolypin's demand that the Duma hand its Social Democratic members to the government."],"abstract":"The <b>coup of June 1907</b> was the dissolution of Russia's Second Duma on 3 June 1907, 16 June by the Western calendar, together with a new electoral law that the emperor issued without the consent of the Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Second Duma had met in March 1907 with more socialists and peasant radicals than the first, and the government of Pyotr Stolypin found no reliable majority in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 June by the Russian calendar Stolypin demanded in a secret session that the Duma surrender all its Social Democratic members, whom the police accused of plotting to win over the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Duma referred the demand to a committee, and without waiting for its report the government published a manifesto of dissolution early on the morning of 3 June, while the members were still in bed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The manifesto argued that only the historic power of the tsar, which had granted the first electoral law, had the right to replace it, since the emperor answered for the Russian state before God alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The new law cut the Duma from 524 seats to 442, most of the loss falling on the borderlands, and it took away the representation of Central Asia altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poland's members fell from 37 to 14, because the manifesto declared that the Duma must be Russian in spirit and that other peoples must never sit in numbers that made them arbiters of purely Russian questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Electors were redistributed so that in 34 of 53 provinces the landowners held an absolute majority over all other classes together, and the Third Duma that met in November was far more conservative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its critics called the act a coup d'état, and Lenin wrote that it had restored the tsar's autocratic rule in alliance with a Duma of landlords and commercial magnates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stolypin's defenders answered that he had saved the Duma itself, since several ministers wanted to abolish it and he insisted on one more attempt under a modified electoral law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 372–74, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 327–28, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 736–37, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Р. В. Королева, «Польский вопрос и Третьеиюньский государственный переворот», <i>Вестник Балтийского федерального университета им. И. Канта. Серия: Гуманитарные и общественные науки</i>, no. 12 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polskiy-vopros-i-tretieiyunskiy-gosudarstvennyy-perevorot. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “The Revolutionary Upswing,” <i>Sotsial-Demokrat</i>, no. 27, 17 (4) June 1912, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 18, trans. Stepan Apresyan (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 102–9, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1912/jun/17.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Stolypin and the Revolution,” <i>Sotsial-Demokrat</i>, no. 24, 18 (31) October 1911, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 17, trans. Dora Cox (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1974), 247–56, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1911/oct/18.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stolypin demand that the Social Democrats be handed over?","a":"Because the police accused them of conspiring to win over the army, and the government used the charge to bring the Duma to a crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One English historian found the act of accusation loosely drawn and supported by evidence against only a third of the accused, while another observer noted that their leader Tsereteli declared it true from beginning to end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the electoral law changed by decree?","a":"Because no Duma elected under the old law would have voted for its own reshaping, so the manifesto claimed that the power which had granted the first electoral law could also take it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It presented the change as the act of an emperor responsible only to God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the borderlands lose most?","a":"Because the manifesto declared that the Duma must be Russian in spirit, and the deputies of Poland and the Caucasus had joined the opposition on the questions that divided the Duma from the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Caucasus lost 19 of its 29 seats and Central Asia lost all of its seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Coup of June 1907"},
    "ru-522": {"questions":["Lenin predicted that Stolypin would balance between two majorities in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one joining the Octobrists to the right and the other to the Cadets.","Alexander Guchkov led the Octobrists, the largest party in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and used its army debates to invite grand dukes to give up their military posts."],"abstract":"The <b>Third Duma</b> was the lower house of the Russian parliament elected under the restricted franchise of June 1907, and it sat from 1 November 1907 to 9 June 1912 by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike the first two Dumas, each dissolved within months, it served its full five years in five sessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its 442 members came above all from the landed gentry, with a sprinkling of merchants, and an English visitor noticed that most were better dressed than before and that no peasant member wore peasant costume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest party was the Octobrists, 153 strong by one count, who accepted the October Manifesto and wished to work with the government but had no absolute majority of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their leader Alexander Guchkov chaired the commission on state defence and, from 1910 to 1911, presided over the Duma itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The government of Pyotr Stolypin depended on this house, and Lenin predicted in October 1907 that it would steer between two majorities, the Octobrists with the right and the Octobrists with the Cadets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Soviet collection of documents puts the right-Octobrist bloc at about 300 votes and the Octobrist-Cadet combination at about 250, each supporting the government on different questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the first time the budget was discussed in detail, and Guchkov used the debate on the army to name grand duke after grand duke and invite them to resign their military posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In five years the Duma examined 2,432 bills besides the annual budgets, most of them small local matters that critics mocked as legislative vermicelli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Cadet leader Pavel Miliukov concluded in 1912 that to win the single right to exist, the Duma had had to become one of the wheels of the bureaucratic machine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 375–76, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 737–40, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 324–28, 333, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “The Third Duma,” <i>Proletary</i>, no. 18, 29 October 1907, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 13 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 123–32, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1907/oct/29.htm. [Open access]","«Третья государственная Дума», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i> 1, no. 1 (1992), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tretya-gosudarstvennaya-duma. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Дёмин, «Парламентская деятельность А. И. Гучкова», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 4 (48) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/parlamentskaya-deyatelnost-a-i-guchkova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was this Duma so much more conservative than the first two?","a":"Because it was elected under the law of June 1907, which handed the choice of electors to the large landowners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The result was a house of country gentlemen who had served in the army or the administration, with few peasants and almost no revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Octobrists matter so much?","a":"Because they were the largest party but had no absolute majority, so every vote depended on whether they joined the right or the Cadets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lenin saw that the government could lean on either combination as it chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Miliukov judge its record so harshly?","a":"Because the government kept the exceptional laws and governed around the Duma when it wished, so the house survived only by fitting in with the bureaucracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of its bills were minor local measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"October 1907 Russian legislative election"},
    "ru-523": {"questions":["Telling revolutionary deputies that they desired great commotions while the government desired a great Russia, the premier <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governed with the Octobrists of the Third Duma.","Called the Russian Bismarck by his admirers and the arch-hangman by Lenin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was shot in the Kiev city theatre in September 1911."],"abstract":"<b>Pyotr Stolypin</b> was the Russian statesman who served as minister of the interior from April 1906 and as chairman of the Council of Ministers from July 1906 until his death in September 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A landowner and marshal of the nobility, he governed the province of Saratov from 1903 to 1906, through the peasant risings of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There he made heavy use of the emergency statute of 1881 to keep order, and an English historian wrote that he had kept his head amid the general panic of the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As premier he defined his policy as having two fronts, to fight revolution relentlessly while separating revolutionaries from the peaceful population and pressing reforms of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He met terrorism with field courts-martial, and in 1906 his villa in St Petersburg was blown up by bombs while he sat at work, leaving one of his daughters badly injured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His chief reform was a land law of November 1906 that allowed peasants to leave the village commune and hold their land as private property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In June 1907 he dissolved the Second Duma and changed the electoral law, and he then governed with the Octobrists of the Third Duma, telling revolutionary deputies that they desired great commotions while the government desired a great Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1911 he pushed through a law on zemstvos for six western provinces by suspending both chambers for three days, a step that showed how little he respected the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 1 September 1911 by the Russian calendar he was shot in the Kiev city theatre in the presence of the imperial family, and he died four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His admirers called him the Russian Bismarck, while Lenin, writing weeks after the shooting, called him the arch-hangman and a mere agent of the landed nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 366, 369–70, 376–77, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 740–42, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 319–20, 324–30, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Stolypin and the Revolution,” <i>Sotsial-Demokrat</i>, no. 24, 18 (31) October 1911, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 17, trans. Dora Cox (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1974), 247–56, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1911/oct/18.htm. [Open access]","В. Ю. Соловьев and А. А. Трегуб, «Саратовский период в биографии П. А. Столыпина», <i>Промышленность: экономика, управление, технологии</i>, no. 17 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/saratovskiy-period-v-biografii-p-a-stolypina. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Ермаков, «Роль Положения от 14 августа 1881 г. в административной практике саратовского губернатора П. А. Столыпина», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 1 (57) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-polozheniya-ot-14-avgusta-1881-g-v-administrativnoy-praktike-saratovskogo-gubernatora-p-a-stolypina. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Ванюков, «Смерть П. А. Столыпина в воспоминаниях современников: С. Ю. Витте, В. Н. Коковцов и другие», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия Филология. Журналистика</i> 22, no. 2 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/smert-p-a-stolypina-v-vospominaniyah-sovremennikov-s-yu-vitte-v-n-kokovtsov-i-drugie. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did both the right and the left distrust him?","a":"Because to the reactionaries he was too liberal, while to the revolutionary left he was a pillar of autocracy and an advocate of police repression and drum-head courts-martial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lenin saw him as the clerk of the landed nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he dissolve the Second Duma?","a":"Because it would not become an obedient partner of the government, and his hand was forced by reactionaries at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then changed the electoral law rather than abolish the Duma outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the reactionaries try to remove him in 1909?","a":"Because they accused him and the Duma of encroaching on the emperor's prerogatives over the army and navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a sharp crisis he was kept in power but ordered to redraft the law in question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pyotr Stolypin"},
    "ru-524": {"questions":["Peasants who left the commune under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could demand one consolidated plot, either a separate farmstead called a khutor or a single field called an otrub.","Lenin called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the last valve that could be opened without expropriating the landed estates, and predicted that it would leave the smallholders paupers."],"abstract":"The <b>Stolypin agrarian reform</b> was the series of measures, begun by the government of Pyotr Stolypin in 1906, that allowed Russian peasants to leave the village commune and hold their allotment land as private property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its basis was a decree of 9 November 1906 by the Russian calendar, applied at once while no Duma was sitting, under which any head of a household could claim his share of communal land as his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The claimant could also demand that his scattered strips be gathered into one plot, either a separate farmstead called a khutor or a consolidated field called an otrub.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A peasant could now leave the commune altogether once he had paid what he owed, and an English observer wrote that the new law struck at the root of the communal system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stolypin hoped to create a class of small landholders loyal to the government, since the revolution had shown that the commune made whole villages act as one man in agrarian disturbances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Third Duma confirmed the decree as the law of 14 June 1910, and a law on land settlement of 29 May 1911 widened the work of the commissions that redrew village fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supporters saw it as a revolution against state socialism that would reward initiative, though an English historian of the time noted that peasants were slow to use it and that it left room for injustice to those who stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lenin called it the last valve that could be opened without expropriating the landed estates, and he predicted that the smallholders would remain paupers in bondage beside the great estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By one count, by 1 January 1916 some 2,478,000 households, about 22 per cent of commune members, had left the commune and secured their land, on about 14 per cent of communal land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Village assemblies resisted unevenly, turning down 57 per cent of applications to leave in Vyatka province by 1914, and whether the separate farms raised harvests is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 369–70, 377, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 325, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 124–25, 151, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “The Last Valve,” <i>Nevskaya Zvezda</i>, no. 20, 5 August 1912, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 18 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 248–53, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1912/aug/05.htm. [Open access]","Н. Ю. Белокопытова, «Законодательные основы аграрной реформы П. А. Столыпина», <i>Северо-Кавказский юридический вестник</i>, no. 4 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zakonodatelnye-osnovy-agrarnoy-reformy-p-a-stolypina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. В. Кузнецов, «Проблема изучения социальных итогов столыпинской аграрной реформы в отечественной историографии», <i>Современные исследования социальных проблем</i>, no. 1 (25) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-izucheniya-sotsialnyh-itogov-stolypinskoy-agrarnoy-reformy-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Нефедов, «Региональные эффекты столыпинской реформы», <i>Новейшая история России</i> 12, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/regionalnye-effekty-stolypinskoy-reformy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government want to break up the commune?","a":"Because the risings of 1905 and 1906 had shown that a commune could act as one body in seizing and burning estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Separate owners were expected to be loyal, and the communal system was blamed for poor farming and a weak respect for property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the khutor and the otrub important?","a":"Because owning scattered strips did little for farming, so the law let a peasant demand his land in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land settlement commissions were set up to divide village fields into consolidated plots and farms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the reform's success still argued over?","a":"Because only about a fifth of commune members had left by 1916, while many applications were refused by village assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Studies that try to link the new farms to higher yields reach different results from province to province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-525": {"questions":["Field courts of four line officers, with no appeal and execution within a day, lie behind the grim phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Russian name for the hangman's noose.","Remembered beside the barred Stolypin wagon for prisoners, the phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became popular shorthand for the gallows of the courts-martial created in August 1906."],"abstract":"<b>Stolypin's necktie</b> was a name for the hangman's noose, a phrase that tied the executions after 1905 to Pyotr Stolypin, whom the revolutionary left saw as an advocate of police repression and drum-head courts-martial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It grew out of the field courts-martial that Stolypin's government created by a regulation of 19 August 1906 by the Russian calendar, to deal with terrorism and armed robbery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each court consisted of a president and four line officers, with no military lawyers among them, and it sat behind closed doors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trial had to end within two days, the sentence had to be carried out within 24 hours, and there was no appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By one count the courts passed 1,102 death sentences in eight months and 683 people were executed, while a legal journal of 1908 put the number sentenced to death at 1,144.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The regulation lapsed on 20 April 1907 because the government never laid it before the Duma, but death sentences by ordinary courts-martial went on rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1907, in the first days of the Third Duma, the Cadet deputy Fyodor Rodichev answered Stolypin's statement of policy by attacking a government that executed men it dared not try in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What Purishkevich called the Muravyov collar, he said, his descendants would perhaps call Stolypin's necktie, and the right of the chamber rose in uproar until the sitting was suspended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rodichev apologised to the Duma and to the premier the same day, and he later wrote that the image came to him only because Purishkevich had twice praised the Muravyov collar as a means of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The phrase outlived both men, and in popular memory it came to mean the gallows of the field courts-martial, just as the Stolypin wagon came to mean the railway carriage for prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernard Pares, “The Reform Movement in Russia,” in <i>The Cambridge Modern History</i>, vol. 12, <i>The Latest Age</i>, ed. A. W. Ward, G. W. Prothero and Stanley Leathes (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1910), 369, 374, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.529903. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 325, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 740, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","Н. Н. Веретенников, «Военно-полевые суды и первая русская революция», <i>Власть</i>, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-polevye-sudy-i-pervaya-russkaya-revolyutsiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Лоос, «Организационно-правовые основы деятельности военно-полевых судов в Российской империи в период революции 1905-1907 гг.», <i>Алтайский юридический вестник</i>, no. 2 (14) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/organizatsionno-pravovye-osnovy-deyatelnosti-voenno-polevyh-sudov-v-rossiyskoy-imperii-v-period-revolyutsii-1905-1907-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. А. Гаврилова, «Российский Мирабо. Ф. И. Родичев депутат четырех государственных Дум», <i>Ленинградский юридический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskiy-mirabo-f-i-rodichev-deputat-chetyreh-gosudarstvennyh-dum. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Ч. Реут and Т. П. Тетеревлева, ««Вагон в галстуке» или «Великий государственник»: репрезентации исторической личности в политическом дискурсе современной России», <i>Труды Карельского научного центра Российской академии наук</i>, no. 4 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vagon-v-galstuke-ili-velikiy-gosudarstvennik-reprezentatsii-istoricheskoy-lichnosti-v-politicheskom-diskurse-sovremennoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were field courts-martial created?","a":"Because terrorism was revived in 1906 and the government wanted swift punishment, so it gave line officers the power to try and execute within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The death penalty was extended even to ordinary robberies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the courts disappear in 1907?","a":"Because an emergency regulation lapsed if the government did not lay it before the Duma, and the government chose not to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stopped on 20 April 1907, though ordinary courts-martial kept passing death sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rodichev's words cause such a storm?","a":"Because they linked the premier's own name with the noose in front of him and a hostile majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The right and the Octobrists raised such a noise that the president left the chair, and Rodichev withdrew the words, saying he had not meant to insult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pyotr Stolypin"},
    "ru-526": {"questions":["Hanged on the night of 12 September 1911 after a closed military trial, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had fired two pistol shots at Stolypin during an interval at the Kiev theatre.","Given the choice by suspicious comrades of a party trial or a sensational act of terror, the young lawyer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chose terror and killed Pyotr Stolypin."],"abstract":"<b>Dmitry Bogrov</b> was the young Kiev lawyer who shot Pyotr Stolypin, chairman of the Council of Ministers, on 1 September 1911 by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came from a wealthy and largely assimilated Jewish family, and in 1906 he joined the anarcho-communists of Kiev while remaining, in his own favourite phrase, a party unto himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1907 he offered his services to the Kiev secret police as a paid informer, and until the end of 1910 his reports helped to break up several groups of Socialist Revolutionaries and Maximalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the summer of 1911 revolutionaries came to suspect him, and on 16 August they offered him the choice of a party trial and death or a sensational act of terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ten days later he told his police handlers that an attempt was being prepared on Stolypin during the celebrations in Kiev for the fiftieth anniversary of the emancipation of the serfs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> With that story he was admitted to the city theatre, where in the second interval he walked down the aisle and fired two shots from a Browning pistol at a range of two or three paces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stolypin was struck in the presence of the imperial family and died on the evening of 5 September, and four days later Bogrov was tried behind closed doors by the Kiev military district court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sentenced to death by hanging, he was executed on the night of 12 September 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That a police agent could kill the head of the government raised suspicion at once, and studies still weigh four versions, from a plot in high places to Bogrov acting entirely on his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An English writer who had known Stolypin described his killer as at once a terrorist and an agent of the secret police, while Lenin called the shot itself quite insignificant beside the period it closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 329–30, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","Donald Mackenzie Wallace, <i>Russia</i>, rev. and enl. ed. (London: Cassell, 1912), 742, https://archive.org/details/russiamackenzie00wall. [Open access]","И. В. Усанов, «Гибель П.А. Столыпина (криминалистический анализ)», <i>Вестник Саратовской государственной юридической академии</i>, no. 6 (149) (2022): 257–62, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gibel-p-a-stolypina-kriminalisticheskiy-analiz. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Н. Лозовская, «Уголовно-политический процесс в поздний период Российской империи (на примере дела Богрова 9 сентября 1911 г. )», <i>Общество и право</i>, no. 2 (12) (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ugolovno-politicheskiy-protsess-v-pozdniy-period-rossiyskoy-imperii-na-primere-dela-bogrova-9-sentyabrya-1911-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Г. Красанов, «Эволюция изучения двойных агентов (Е.Ф. Азеф, Д.Г. Богров, Р.В. Малиновский) в отечественной историографии», <i>Гуманитарный акцент</i>, no. 4 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-izucheniya-dvoynyh-agentov-e-f-azef-d-g-bogrov-r-v-malinovskiy-v-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Stolypin and the Revolution,” <i>Sotsial-Demokrat</i>, no. 24, 18 (31) October 1911, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 17, trans. Dora Cox (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1974), 247–56, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1911/oct/18.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the secret police let him into the theatre?","a":"Because he had been a trusted informer for years, and he told his handlers that terrorists were coming to Kiev whom he alone could recognise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His protectors later explained his presence near the tsar and the premier in exactly those terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some suspect a wider plot?","a":"Because an agent of the police killed the head of the government, which looked to many like more than negligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One Soviet-era study blamed the deliberate connivance of four high officials, while a recent forensic analysis concludes that Bogrov acted on his own initiative and that his handlers were incompetent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he tried so quickly?","a":"Because he confessed to the killing, and the military prosecutor therefore found it possible to shorten the hearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Kiev military district court tried him behind closed doors on 9 September and sentenced him to hang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dmitrii Bogrov"},
    "ru-527": {"questions":["Interior minister Makarov told the Duma 'So it was, and so it will be' when he defended the troops responsible for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Siberia.","Lenin hailed the strikes that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the start of a revolutionary upswing, reckoning that some 300,000 workers had joined them."],"abstract":"The <b>Lena massacre</b> was the shooting of striking gold miners by soldiers at the Lena goldfields in eastern Siberia on 4 April 1912 by the Russian calendar, 17 April by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mines belonged to the Lena Gold Mining Partnership, known as Lenzoto, which by 1910 controlled the food supply, transport and electricity of a vast territory and even paid supplements to the local police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reaching the fields from Irkutsk meant more than 1,700 kilometres (1,050 miles) by road and river, the last stretch up the Vitim to Bodaibo, the company's centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strike began at the Andreevsky mine on 29 February 1912 over poor-quality food from the company store, and on 3 March a meeting of seven mines resolved to stop work until every demand was met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strike stayed peaceful and disciplined, but on the night before 4 April ten members of the strike committee were arrested, and about 3,000 workers marched to the Nadezhdinsky mine to protest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Troops under the gendarme captain Treshchenkov met them and kept firing even as the mining engineer Tulchinsky waved his cap and begged them to stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government's final figure was 270 dead and 250 wounded, and survivors pointed out that 117 had been hit lying down and 69 in the back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 11 April the interior minister Alexander Makarov told the Duma that when a crowd threw itself on the troops they could only shoot, adding, 'So it was, and so it will be in future.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strikes of protest spread across the country, involving about 300,000 workers by Lenin's estimate, and he hailed them as the beginning of a revolutionary upswing against the whole June Third regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A senator was sent to investigate and a commission led by the lawyer Alexander Kerensky visited the fields, while one recent study compares the national outrage with the reaction to Bloody Sunday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["О. Г. Назаров, «К вопросу о роли К. Н. Тульчинского в Ленских событиях 1912 г», <i>Сервис в России и за рубежом</i>, no. 5 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-roli-k-n-tulchinskogo-v-lenskih-sobytiyah-1912-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Ф. Сычев, «Разъяснения министра внутренних дел А.А. Макарова по запросам о Ленских событиях в общественном мнении (апрель 1912 г.)», <i>Клио</i>, no. 3 (207) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razyasneniya-ministra-vnutrennih-del-a-a-makarova-po-zaprosam-o-lenskih-sobytiyah-v-obschestvennom-mnenii-aprel-1912-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Кудряшов, «Ленские события 1912 г. на страницах журнала «Cибирские вопросы»», <i>Проблемы социально-экономического развития Сибири</i>, no. 3 (45) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lenskie-sobytiya-1912-g-na-stranitsah-zhurnala-cibirskie-voprosy. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Большакова, «2008. 03. 015-016. Ленские события и их роль в кризисе старого режима. (сводный реферат)», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5. История</i>, no. 3 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2008-03-015-016-lenskie-sobytiya-i-ih-rol-v-krizise-starogo-rezhima-svodnyy-referat. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “The Revolutionary Upswing,” <i>Sotsial-Demokrat</i>, no. 27, 17 (4) June 1912, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 18, trans. Stepan Apresyan (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 102–9, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1912/jun/17.htm. [Open access]","А. П. Толочко, «Стачечные выступления рабочих в Сибири в начале нового революционного подъема (июнь 1910 – апрель 1912 г.)», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета. История</i>, no. 3 (19) (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stachechnye-vystupleniya-rabochih-v-sibiri-v-nachale-novogo-revolyutsionnogo-podema-iyun-1910-aprel-1912-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the arrest of the strike committee cause the march?","a":"Because the workers regarded their elected delegates as untouchable, since the administration itself had asked for them to be chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their arrest on the night before 4 April brought thousands out to demand their release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the shootings anger moderate opinion too?","a":"Because Makarov defended the troops in the Duma with words that seemed to promise more of the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the Octobrists complained that the government had not explained what had happened, and professional bodies across Russia joined the protests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the company have so much power over the miners?","a":"Because the fields were remote and Lenzoto controlled food, transport and even payments to officials, while senior figures in St Petersburg held its shares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Soviet-era study goes further and claims that the shooting was decided in the highest government circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lena massacre"},
    "ru-528": {"questions":["Officials of the justice ministry pressed a ritual-murder version in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after Kiev detectives had already set it aside on the evidence of the autopsy.","The conservative editor of <i>Kievlyanin</i> attacked the prosecution in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose jury found that the boy had died at the brickworks but acquitted the accused."],"abstract":"The <b>Beilis affair</b> was the case of Menahem Mendel Beilis, a Jewish clerk in Kiev who was arrested in 1911 and tried in 1913 on a charge of murdering a Christian boy for ritual purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In late March 1911 the body of Andrei Yushchinsky, a 13-year-old pupil of a church school, was found in Kiev covered in stab wounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Detectives first suspected his family and then Vera Cheberyak, a woman with links to the criminal world whose son was the boy's friend, while a Black Hundred student began his own inquiry to prove that Jews had killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An official sent from the ministry of justice pressed the ritual version on the detectives, who had already set it aside because the autopsy suggested an unplanned attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beilis, a clerk at the Zaitsev brick works near the place where the body was found, was arrested by the Kiev secret police on 22 July 1911 and spent more than two years in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The trial ran in Kiev from 23 September to 28 October 1913 by the Russian calendar, with the jurors under secret police watch and a presiding judge who plainly favoured the prosecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even Vasily Shulgin, the nationalist editor of <i>Kievlyanin</i>, wrote that the charge was babble any half-capable defender could demolish, and he was later sentenced to three months in prison for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The jury agreed that the murder had been committed at the Zaitsev brick works but answered that Beilis was not guilty, so that the extreme right claimed the verdict as its own victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The writer Vasily Rozanov argued in <i>Novoye Vremya</i> that Jews could commit such a murder, a stand that left him isolated, while Petrograd barristers who protested against the charge were tried and imprisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the fall of the monarchy, a commission of the Provisional Government investigated officials of the justice and interior ministries for their conduct of the case, including the surveillance of the jurors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mendel Beilis, <i>The Story of My Sufferings</i> (New York: Mendel Beilis Publishing, 1926), 23–27, 187–88, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89442. [Open access]","Alexander S. Kaun, “Russia under Nicolas II, 1894–1916,” in Alexander Kornilov, <i>Modern Russian History</i>, trans. Alexander S. Kaun, vol. 2 (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1917), 329, 337, https://archive.org/details/modernrussianhi02korngoog. [Open access]","М. М. Минц, «2016. 02. 016. Левин Э. Кровь христианского ребенка: заговор и убийство в царской России. Кровавый навет на Бейлиса», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5. История</i>, no. 2 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2016-02-016-levin-e-krov-hristianskogo-rebenka-zagovor-i-ubiystvo-v-tsarskoy-rossii-krovavyy-navet-na-beylisa-levin-e-a-child-of-christian-blood. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. И. Бабков, «Дело Менделя Бейлиса и позиция Василия Шульгина», <i>Вестник Брянского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (52) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/delo-mendelya-beylisa-i-pozitsiya-vasiliya-shulgina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Варфоломеев, «Дело М. Бейлиса в материалах Чрезвычайной следственной комиссии Временного правительства», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия Экономика. Управление. Право</i> 12, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/delo-m-beylisa-v-materialah-chrezvychaynoy-sledstvennoy-komissii-vremennogo-pravitelstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Ю. Казакова, «Публицистическая позиция В. В. Розанова в \"деле Бейлиса\"», <i>Вестник РГГУ. Серия: Литературоведение. Языкознание. Культурология</i>, no. 11 (44) (2018): 111–16, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/publitsisticheskaya-pozitsiya-v-v-rozanova-v-dele-beylisa. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government pursue so weak a case?","a":"Because the minister of justice Shcheglovitov seems to have hoped to please an emperor who believed such charges, even though officials knew the evidence was weak and an acquittal likely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The authorities also feared pogroms and unrest throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the jury's verdict satisfy neither side completely?","a":"Because the jurors answered yes to the first question, that the boy had been murdered at the brick works, and no to the question of Beilis's guilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The far right read the first answer as proof of a ritual killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a conservative editor turn against the prosecution?","a":"Because Shulgin thought the case shamed Russian justice before the whole world and that courts must serve neither left nor right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For his articles he was sentenced on 20 January 1914 to three months in prison, which he never served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Beilis affair"},
    "ru-529": {"questions":["Purishkevich told the Duma in November 1916 that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must not guide Russia's inner life, and a month later he helped to kill him.","When the heir lay near death at Spała in 1912, a telegram from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> assured the empress that the little one would not die."],"abstract":"<b>Grigori Rasputin</b> was a Siberian peasant and self-styled man of God whose closeness to Nicholas II and Alexandra made him the most notorious figure of the last years of the Russian monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> He was born on 9 January 1869 by the Russian calendar in the village of Pokrovskoye in Tobolsk province, the fifth child of a peasant family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He married in 1887, and from 1892 he wandered on pilgrimage to holy places after a conversation with a priest he had driven to the monastery of Verkhoturye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was neither priest nor monk but a strannik, a wandering pilgrim, and after he reached St Petersburg in 1904 church dignitaries helped to make his name in high society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He met the imperial family in the autumn of 1905, and the empress came to believe that his prayers could save her son Alexei, who suffered from haemophilia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> When the boy lay close to death at Spała in 1912, the empress had a telegram sent asking for his prayers, and he answered that the little one would not die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1910 newspapers attacked his conduct, and after a quarrel with Bishop Germogen at the end of 1911 a press campaign began and his name was first spoken from the tribune of the Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nicholas II ordered the interior minister Alexander Makarov to stop such publications, but Makarov could not, and the campaign died down only in the spring of 1912.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In November 1916 the monarchist Purishkevich told the Duma that Rasputin must not guide Russia's inner life, and on 17 December he killed him together with Grand Duke Dmitry Pavlovich and Prince Felix Yusupov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Trotsky later called the whole phenomenon Rasputinism, a crowned hooliganism at the very top of society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Pierre Gilliard, <i>Thirteen Years at the Russian Court</i>, trans. F. Appleby Holt (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 52–60, https://archive.org/details/thirteenyearsatr00gilluoft. [Open access]","Anna Vyrubova, <i>Memories of the Russian Court</i> (London: Macmillan, 1923), 94, https://archive.org/details/memoriesofrussia00vyruuoft. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 4, “The Tzar and the Tzarina,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch04.htm. [Open access]","А. С. Мелехов and А. А. Дрокова, «Григорий Распутин и царская семья», <i>Наука и образование</i> 7, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grigoriy-rasputin-i-tsarskaya-semya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Елдашев, «Григорий Распутин: казанский период», <i>Гасырлар авазы — Эхо веков</i>, no. 1-2 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grigoriy-rasputin-kazanskiy-period. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. К. Каледа, «Формирование образа Распутина в контексте его взаимоотношений с церковными деятелями в 1910-1916 гг.», <i>Клио</i>, no. 5 (209) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-obraza-rasputina-v-kontekste-ego-vzaimootnosheniy-s-tserkovnymi-deyatelyami-v-1910-1916-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. Ф. Сычев, «Министр внутренних дел А.А. Макаров и газетная кампания против Григория Распутина в общественном мнении (январь-март 1912 г.)», <i>Исторический журнал: научные исследования</i>, no. 4 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ministr-vnutrennih-del-a-a-makarov-i-gazetnaya-kampaniya-protiv-grigoriya-rasputina-v-obschestvennom-mnenii-yanvar-mart-1912-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Черникова, «Григорий Распутин как символ разложения самодержавия», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 5 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grigoriy-rasputin-kak-simvol-razlozheniya-samoderzhaviya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the empress trust him so completely?","a":"Because she had lost hope that doctors or prayers could cure her son, and he told her that if she believed in his prayers the boy would live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chance improvements in the boy's health convinced her that his life lay in Rasputin's hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did churchmen turn against him?","a":"Because his conduct cast doubt on his holiness, and sincere believers feared he was damaging the Church's reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His former supporter Bishop Germogen quarrelled with him and was dismissed from the Synod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did monarchists decide to kill him?","a":"Because they believed his influence was destroying the monarchy and hoped that removing him would restore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His death did not save the situation, as one of the killers later admitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Grigori Rasputin"},
    "ru-530": {"questions":["Two sailors of the imperial yacht guarded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> day and night, since a fall or a simple cut might kill a boy whose blood would not clot.","Genetic study of Romanov remains in 2009 showed that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, heir of Nicholas II, had suffered from haemophilia B, a defect of clotting factor IX."],"abstract":"<b>Alexei Nikolaevich</b> was the only son of Nicholas II and Alexandra Feodorovna and heir to the Russian throne, born at Peterhof on 30 July 1904 by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because only males could inherit the throne, his birth after four daughters was greeted with joy, which a lady of the court recalled turning almost at once to dread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 40 days old he began to bleed from the navel, the bleeding did not stop for two weeks, and the surgeon Sergei Fedorov concluded that the boy had haemophilia, the disease of kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A doctor at court explained to the boy's tutor that the disease passed through the women of certain families to their sons, and that a fall or a simple cut might kill him because his blood did not clot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His parents treated the illness as a state secret, and two sailors of the imperial yacht were appointed to watch over him in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The worst crisis came at Spała in Russian Poland in the autumn of 1912, when a fall left him with a large internal haemorrhage in the groin and he came close to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From October 1912 the surgeon Vladimir Derevenko served in effect as his personal doctor, and his young son became one of the boy's closest friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empress came to believe that the prayers of Grigori Rasputin eased her son's suffering, and at Spała a telegram from Rasputin assured her that the little one would not die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was shot with his parents and sisters in the Ipatiev house at Yekaterinburg on the night of 16–17 July 1918, aged thirteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 2009 genetic analysis of Romanov remains identified a mutation in the gene for clotting factor IX, showing that the royal disease was the severe form known as haemophilia B, although some medical accounts still describe haemophilia A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Pierre Gilliard, <i>Thirteen Years at the Russian Court</i>, trans. F. Appleby Holt (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 30–32, 37–38, https://archive.org/details/thirteenyearsatr00gilluoft. [Open access]","Anna Vyrubova, <i>Memories of the Russian Court</i> (London: Macmillan, 1923), 10–11, 94, https://archive.org/details/memoriesofrussia00vyruuoft. [Open access]","М. И. Давидов, «Гемофилия - неизлечимое заболевание цесаревича Алексея Николаевича Романова», <i>Вестник хирургии имени И. И. Грекова</i> 173, no. 3 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gemofiliya-neizlechimoe-zabolevanie-tsesarevicha-alekseya-nikolaevicha-romanova. [in Russian] [Open access]","З. И. Шевцова and В. В. Гапонов, «Страницы жизни лейб-медика семьи Николая II Владимира Николаевича Деревенко», <i>Гастроентерологія</i>, no. 4 (50) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stranitsy-zhizni-leyb-medika-semi-nikolaya-ii-vladimira-nikolaevicha-derevenko. [in Russian] [Open access]","Evgeny I. Rogaev, Anastasia P. Grigorenko, Gulnaz Faskhutdinova, Ellen L. W. Kittler and others, “Genotype Analysis Identifies the Cause of the ‘Royal Disease’,” <i>Science</i> 326, no. 5954 (2009): 817, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1180660. [Paywalled]","А. В. Черникова, «Григорий Распутин как символ разложения самодержавия», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 5 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grigoriy-rasputin-kak-simvol-razlozheniya-samoderzhaviya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. О. Серов and А. В. Федоров, «Расстрел бывшего императора Николая II и его семьи (1918 г.): историко-правовые аспекты», <i>Журнал российского права</i>, no. 10 (262) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rasstrel-byvshego-imperatora-nikolaya-ii-i-ego-semi-1918-g-istoriko-pravovye-aspekty. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was his illness kept secret?","a":"Because his parents did not want the world to know the nature of the heir's disease and regarded it as a matter of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They appeared smiling before guests at Spała while he lay in agony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his illness matter politically?","a":"Because the empress's despair over her son made her trust Rasputin, whose prayers she believed could relieve him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The influence of that healer became a scandal that damaged the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could haemophilia not be treated then?","a":"Because the clotting factors had not yet been discovered or classified, and doctors did not know that the disease had several forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All that could be done was to watch him closely and try to prevent accidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexei Nikolaevich, Tsarevich of Russia"},
    "ru-531": {"questions":["A regulation of 1913 said that war is always preceded by a prolonged spell of diplomatic tension and laid down how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should be declared by ministers and the emperor.","Historians who stress Russia’s responsibility for 1914 point to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, decided on 25 July, as the first Russian step down a slippery slope that ended in general mobilisation."],"abstract":"The <b>Period Preparatory to War</b> was a procedure of the Russian Empire, set out in a regulation of 1913, for the weeks of diplomatic tension that were expected to come before the outbreak of a war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its preamble said that the start of military action is always preceded by a more or less prolonged period of diplomatic tension, and for the first time in Russian history it laid down how such a period should be declared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ministers of war, the navy and foreign affairs first had to agree that war was inevitable, an extraordinary meeting of the cabinet then examined their decision, and the emperor gave the final word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> While the period lasted, the state was to take the measures needed to prepare a mobilisation and make it succeed, without yet calling up the reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sources disagree on the day it was approved, giving 17 February or 2 March 1913 by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 24 July 1914, the day after Austria-Hungary delivered its ultimatum to Serbia, the foreign minister Sergei Sazonov told the Council of Ministers that further concessions to the Central Powers would only be read as weakness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The council resolved to ask Vienna to extend its time limit, to advise Serbia to be conciliatory, and to ask the emperor for permission to begin preparations for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The next morning a council under Nicholas II approved those decisions and, in the words of the foreign ministry’s diary, chose not to order mobilisation yet but to take every preparatory measure for speeding it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The step was decided on 25 July and came into force early on 26 July, dates which the Russian calendar of the day counted as 12 and 13 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russian ministers insisted that it did not make war unavoidable, but historians who stress Russia’s share of the blame point to it, and even a cautious account calls it the first Russian move down a slippery slope to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Annika Mombauer, “July Crisis 1914,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11027/1.1. [Open access]","Evgenii Iur’evich Sergeev, “Pre-war Military Planning (Russian Empire),” trans. Trevor Goronwy, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10451. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Sazonov, Sergeĭ Dmitrievich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10477. [Open access]","<i>How the War Began in 1914: Being the Diary of the Russian Foreign Office from the 3rd to the 20th (Old Style) of July, 1914</i>, trans. W. Cyprian Bridge, intro. M. F. Schilling (London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1925), 16–17, 34–35, 49–66, https://archive.org/details/howwarbeganbeing00russ. [Open access]","В. В. Пахомов, «Историко-правовые аспекты становления мобилизации и мобилизационной подготовки в России», <i>Теория и практика социогуманитарных наук</i>, no. 4 (4) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-pravovye-aspekty-stanovleniya-mobilizatsii-i-mobilizatsionnoy-podgotovki-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Nicholas II, Emperor of Russia,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10334. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regulation need ministers, the cabinet and the emperor to agree?","a":"Because declaring the period was itself a serious step towards war, so the regulation set out a fixed procedure for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war, naval and foreign ministers had to agree that war was inevitable, an extraordinary cabinet meeting examined their decision, and the emperor endorsed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russia not simply mobilise on 25 July?","a":"Because the council chose to wait, deciding not to order mobilisation yet but to take every preparatory measure that would speed it up if it became necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia also wanted to support British proposals for mediation while it prepared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about this decision?","a":"Because Russian ministers stressed that it did not make war unavoidable, while historians who blame Russia point to how early it came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sazonov’s own memoirs presented his policy as defensive and peaceful, a reading some later scholars have challenged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-532": {"questions":["Telling the chief of staff that he could now smash his telephone, Sazonov passed on the emperor’s order for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the palace at Peterhof on 30 July 1914.","Generals warned in July 1914 that starting with a partial call-up against Austria-Hungary would wreck the plans for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the emperor finally ordered on 30 July."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian general mobilisation</b> of 1914 was the call-up of the whole Russian army against both Germany and Austria-Hungary, ordered by Nicholas II on the afternoon of 30 July, which the Russian calendar of the day counted as 17 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For several days the government had planned only a partial mobilisation against Austria-Hungary, until the chief of the general staff, General Yanushkevich, warned that it would be impossible to pass from a partial to a general mobilisation without upsetting all the plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 29 July the emperor signed an order for general mobilisation and then suspended it that same night after a telegram from his cousin, the German emperor Wilhelm II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next morning Yanushkevich and the war minister Sukhomlinov tried by telephone to change his mind, and the emperor refused and ended the conversation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sazonov was granted an audience at Peterhof that afternoon instead, and spent nearly an hour arguing that Germany had decided on a collision and that it was better to meet war fully armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The French ambassador recorded that the emperor, deadly pale, answered that it meant sending thousands and thousands of men to their death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At about four o’clock the emperor gave way, and Sazonov telephoned the order to Yanushkevich, adding, by the foreign ministry’s diary, “Now you can smash your telephone.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany answered with an ultimatum and on 1 August declared war on Russia, so the general mobilisation became the step by which Russia entered the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within a month an imperial decree renamed Saint Petersburg as Petrograd, removing a German-sounding name from the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether Sazonov pushed his sovereign towards war or did what any minister had to do is still disputed, and the harshest reading of him has been faulted for overstating his freedom of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>How the War Began in 1914: Being the Diary of the Russian Foreign Office from the 3rd to the 20th (Old Style) of July, 1914</i>, trans. W. Cyprian Bridge, intro. M. F. Schilling (London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1925), 16–17, 34–35, 49–66, https://archive.org/details/howwarbeganbeing00russ. [Open access]","Maurice Paléologue, <i>An Ambassador’s Memoirs</i>, vol. 1, trans. F. A. Holt (New York: George H. Doran, 1925), 40–46, 106–8, https://archive.org/details/ambassadorsmemoi01pal. [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 1 (London: Cassell, 1923), 199–203, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi01buchuoft. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Sazonov, Sergeĭ Dmitrievich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10477. [Open access]","Annika Mombauer, “July Crisis 1914,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11027/1.1. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Nicholas II, Emperor of Russia,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10334. [Open access]","О. В. Ерохина, «Положение петербургских (петроградских) немцев на начальном этапе Первой мировой войны», <i>Новый исторический вестник</i>, no. 1 (83) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polozhenie-peterburgskih-petrogradskih-nemtsev-na-nachalnom-etape-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the emperor suspend the order on 29 July?","a":"Because a telegram from Wilhelm II arrived that evening, and the emperor hoped it opened the way to a direct understanding with Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The foreign ministry’s diary records a pencilled note that the Kaiser’s reply had caused him to cancel the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals refuse a partial mobilisation?","a":"Because, as Yanushkevich explained, the plans prepared in the war ministry were for a general mobilisation, and starting with four districts would leave Russia in a perilous position if Germany then joined in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French ambassador was told the same: for technical reasons a partial mobilisation would throw everything into confusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Saint Petersburg renamed?","a":"Because in the first weeks of the war a German-sounding name for the capital seemed intolerable, and an imperial decree turned it into Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The French ambassador thought the step emphatic as a protest of Slav nationalism but a mistake from a historical point of view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-533": {"questions":["Named after a village whose name recalled the Teutonic Knights’ defeat of 1410, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1914 became a national myth in Germany between the wars.","Sazonov told the French ambassador that Russia owed France the sacrifice of the army destroyed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fought in East Prussia while German armies marched on Paris."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tannenberg</b> was fought in East Prussia from 26 to 30 August 1914, when the German Eighth Army surrounded and destroyed the Russian Second Army of General Aleksandr Samsonov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belonged to the East Prussian operation of August and September 1914, which Russia launched in haste to help France and which long remained a symbol of Russian weakness in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Two Russian armies were to advance around the Masurian Lakes from the north and the south, but they were some 90 kilometres (56 miles) apart and kept in touch only by courier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the First Army under General Rennenkampf halted, the Germans left a thin cavalry screen in front of it and moved the bulk of their forces south against Samsonov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their commanders, Paul von Hindenburg and Erich Ludendorff, were also helped by Russian radio messages sent without encryption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Within days the Germans turned both of Samsonov’s flanks and three Russian corps vanished in the encirclement, while Samsonov himself took his own life, despite rumours long afterwards that he had escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russian losses are usually put at about 120,000 to 125,000 men, some 95,000 of them prisoners, against German casualties of 10,000 to 15,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The victory took its name from the nearby village of Tannenberg, a name Hindenburg called a Slav cry of triumph from more than five hundred years before, so that it read as revenge for the Teutonic Knights’ defeat of 1410.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the French ambassador asked about the battle on 30 August, Sazonov replied that Samsonov’s army had been destroyed and that Russia owed this sacrifice to France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russian newspapers began to report the disaster only from 1 September, and the defeat was later blamed on a supposed treachery by Rennenkampf that no evidence supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Oxana Sergeevna Nagornaja, “Tannenberg, Battle of,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10310/1.1. [Open access]","Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]","John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","Paul von Hindenburg, <i>Out of My Life</i>, trans. F. A. Holt (London: Cassell, 1920), 86–99, https://archive.org/details/outofmylife00hinduoft. [Open access]","Maurice Paléologue, <i>An Ambassador’s Memoirs</i>, vol. 1, trans. F. A. Holt (New York: George H. Doran, 1925), 105–6, https://archive.org/details/ambassadorsmemoi01pal. [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 78–91, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy101knoxuoft. [Open access]","О. В. Пименов, «Освещение Восточно-Прусской операции 1914 года в российских газетах», <i>Самарский научный вестник</i>, 8, no. 1 (30) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osveschenie-vostochno-prusskoy-operatsii-1914-goda-v-rossiyskih-gazetah. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Пахалюк, «Восточно-Прусская катастрофа 1914 года», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vostochno-prusskaya-katastrofa-1914-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the two Russian armies fail to support each other?","a":"Because the front commander, General Zhilinsky, failed to coordinate his two armies and did not tell Samsonov where the First Army was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The armies were some 90 kilometres apart and kept in touch only by courier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Rennenkampf blamed for the defeat?","a":"Because a story of his treachery fitted a wider belief in a German conspiracy, and it took shape in Russian society even before 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No evidence supports the tale of a personal feud with Samsonov; Rennenkampf had halted because he had outrun his supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the battle matter more as a symbol than as strategy?","a":"Because it did not decide the war in the east or even end the Russian occupation of East Prussia, yet it became a German national myth and a heavy blow to Russian morale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hindenburg’s report to the Kaiser spoke of three corps destroyed and more than 60,000 prisoners taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Tannenberg"},
    "ru-534": {"questions":["Fought only days after Tannenberg, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw the Russian First Army escape encirclement by retreating almost 30 kilometres (19 miles) a day back to Russian soil.","The front commander Zhilinsky accused Rennenkampf of slowness and even cowardice after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of September 1914 and asked for his removal, though most charges were groundless."],"abstract":"The <b>First Battle of the Masurian Lakes</b> was fought in East Prussia in September 1914, when the German Eighth Army turned on the Russian First Army of General Paul von Rennenkampf and drove it out of German territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed directly on Tannenberg, and Hindenburg wrote that the sound of battle there had hardly died down before preparations began for the attack on Rennenkampf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two army corps and a cavalry division were sent from the Western Front, raising German strength in East Prussia to about 175,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rennenkampf stood still on an extended line from Wehlau to Angerburg, with his left flank hanging in the air by the Masurian Lakes, where a new Tenth Army was still being formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hindenburg himself asked why the Russian general, famous since the war in Manchuria, had given him time to rest and regroup instead of attacking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pinning attacks held the Russian centre on 7 September, and two German corps struck the exposed left flank on 9 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rennenkampf grasped the danger only on 8 September and moved a corps from his right to his left, and a sudden counter-attack by battered units of his left wing made the Germans believe they faced superior forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pause let the First Army escape encirclement, and marching almost 30 kilometres (19 miles) a day it was back on Russian soil by 13 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German accounts put the Russian loss at 60,000 men and 150 guns, while one modern estimate gives some 100,000 Russian casualties against nearly 70,000 German ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The front commander Zhilinsky then blamed Rennenkampf for slowness and even cowardice and asked for his removal, charges that were mostly groundless, and Russian troops entered East Prussia again briefly that winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]","John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","Paul von Hindenburg, <i>Out of My Life</i>, trans. F. A. Holt (London: Cassell, 1920), 99–109, https://archive.org/details/outofmylife00hinduoft. [Open access]","К. А. Пахалюк, ««Мог быть Мюратом, если бы жил сотней лет раньше». Генерал П. К. фон Ренненкампф в годы Первой мировой войны», <i>Локус: люди, общество, культуры, смыслы</i>, no. 4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mog-byt-myuratom-esli-by-zhil-sotney-let-ranshe-general-p-k-fon-rennenkampf-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 90–91, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy101knoxuoft. [Open access]","Oxana Sergeevna Nagornaja, “Tannenberg, Battle of,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10310/1.1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rennenkampf not attack while the Germans were busy at Tannenberg?","a":"Hindenburg himself could not explain it: Rennenkampf had more than twenty divisions, yet he stood still while the German army came up and deployed against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Russian armies in East Prussia had also outrun their supply lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans fail to surround the First Army?","a":"Because a counter-attack by battered units of the Russian left wing took them by surprise, and by the time they saw their mistake their turning wing had changed direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rennenkampf then retreated fast enough to reach Russian soil by 13 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the casualty figures so different?","a":"Because they come from different sides: German accounts reported by a British observer gave 60,000 Russians killed, wounded and captured, while a modern estimate puts Russian casualties at some 100,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"First Battle of the Masurian Lakes"},
    "ru-535": {"questions":["Costing the Habsburg army over 300,000 men and much of its officer corps, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drove the Austrians back to the foot of the Carpathian Mountains in September 1914.","Planned and directed in practice by the chief of staff Mikhail Alekseyev, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1914 brought Galicia east of the Carpathians under Russian occupation until the next summer."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Galicia</b> was a series of engagements between the Russian Southwestern Front and the Austro-Hungarian army in the Austrian crown land of Galicia in August and September 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russian historians date the operation from 18 August to 21 September 1914, or from 5 August to 8 September by the Russian calendar of the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russia committed four armies instead of the two first planned, under General Nikolai Ivanov, whose chief of staff Mikhail Alekseyev in practice directed the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Austrian commander, Conrad von Hötzendorf, had split his forces between Serbia and Galicia, and each side, expecting the other elsewhere, found itself pushing at an open door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Austrians struck first and won early victories at Kraśnik on 23 August and at Komarów two days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the east, General Aleksei Brusilov’s Eighth Army and General Nikolai Ruzsky’s Third Army routed the Austrians on the Gnila Lipa between 26 and 30 August, and Brusilov’s forces took Lemberg, the modern Lviv, on 3 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russian cavalry then found a gap in the Austrian line, and after a defeat at Rava-Ruska the Austro-Hungarian armies fell back some 160 kilometres (100 miles) to the foot of the Carpathians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Austro-Hungarian army lost over 300,000 men, nearly a third of its effective strength, and a large share of its officers, losses from which it never truly recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the Austro-Hungarian soldiers who ended up as prisoners in Russia were taken in Galicia in 1914, and the Russians went on to besiege the fortress of Przemyśl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The victory cost Ivanov’s armies nearly a fifth of their men and was overshadowed by the disaster in East Prussia, yet it kept eastern Galicia under Russian occupation until the summer of 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]","John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","Sofya Anisimova, “Alekseyev, Mikhail Vasilʹevich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11557. [Open access]","Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Brusilov, Alekseĭ Alekseevich,” trans. Christopher Gilley, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11246. [Open access]","П. В. Пантелеев, «Взаимодействие войск Юго-Западного фронта русской императорской армии в ходе подготовки Галицийской операции в кампании маневренного периода Первой мировой войны», <i>Via in tempore. История. Политология</i>, 52, no. 3 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzaimodeystvie-voysk-yugo-zapadnogo-fronta-russkoy-imperatorskoy-armii-v-hode-podgotovki-galitsiyskoy-operatsii-v-kampanii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Reinhard Nachtigal and Lena Radauer, “Prisoners of War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10386. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russia do better against Austria-Hungary than against Germany?","a":"Because the Austro-Hungarian army was arguably the weakest of the Great Powers’ armies and had split its forces between Galicia and Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Russian flanking armies also struck where the Austrians did not expect them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Alekseyev given credit for the victory?","a":"Because, though he was formally only chief of staff of the Southwestern Front, contemporaries noticed that he ran it in practice and often told the commander, Ivanov, of decisions already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Russians not drive on into Hungary?","a":"Because Ivanov’s armies had suffered heavy losses too, and the northern front was in disarray after the defeats in East Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supply and transport problems also slowed every Russian operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Galicia"},
    "ru-536": {"questions":["Jews and German colonists were deported and villages burned under a scorched-earth policy during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which drove millions of refugees into the Russian interior.","Short of heavy guns and shells, the Russian army lost more than a million men in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but kept its armies intact, falling back some 480 kilometres (300 miles)."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Retreat of 1915</b> was the withdrawal of the Russian army from Galicia, Russian Poland and much of the western borderlands between May and September 1915, after the Central Powers broke through its line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began on 2 May 1915, when a new German army under August von Mackensen opened the Gorlice–Tarnów offensive after a four-hour bombardment and tore a gap of some 65 kilometres (40 miles) in the Russian front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russians, short of heavy guns and in the midst of a critical shortage of shells, could not answer the German artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Przemyśl was lost again on 3 June and Lemberg on 22 June, Warsaw was evacuated on 5 August, and by the end of the summer the whole Polish salient was in German and Austrian hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In about four months the army fell back more than 480 kilometres (300 miles) and lost more than a million men killed, wounded and captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Prisoners made up a large part of that loss, and estimates of the Russians captured in the summer and autumn of 1915 run from about 750,000 to 976,000, depending on the source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As it withdrew, the army deported groups it considered disloyal, above all Jews and German colonists, and followed a scorched-earth policy that destroyed villages and drove civilians east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> These deportations have usually been read as deliberate persecution, though one recent study argues that commanders acted less from chauvinism than from a belief that unreliable groups had no place in the war zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some 6 million refugees reached the Russian interior by 1917, against the 4.9 million that officials recorded, in conditions so chaotic that the number who died on the way cannot be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The retreat ended in September when the German drive ran out of momentum, but it led Nicholas II to dismiss the Grand Duke Nikolai and take supreme command himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “War Losses (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11284. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Refugees (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10235. [Open access]","А. Б. Асташов, «Переселенческая политика военных властей России во время «великого отступления» 1915 г.», <i>Россия и современный мир</i>, no. 1 (98) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pereselencheskaya-politika-voennyh-vlastey-rossii-vo-vremya-velikogo-otstupleniya-1915-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Олейников, «Военнопленные на русском фронте Первой мировой войны», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 9 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennoplennye-na-russkom-fronte-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy101knoxuoft. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Russians not stop the German advance?","a":"Because they lacked heavy artillery and were short of shells, so a short German bombardment could wreck a front line that had no second line behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A British officer with the army had already noted in the winter that the shortage of shell was general, not local.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army deport its own civilians?","a":"Because commanders treated whole groups, such as Jews and German colonists, as of doubtful loyalty and removed them from the war zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One study argues that this came from a wish to clear unreliable elements from the front rather than from chauvinism, though the suffering was the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the retreat not knock Russia out of the war?","a":"Because the Russian commanders avoided the encirclements the Germans tried to create and kept their armies intact, and Russia’s vast distances wore the advance down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-537": {"questions":["Court records show that Nicholas II spent 263 of 427 days in 1916 and early 1917 at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the provincial city that housed the Stavka of the Russian army.","After abdicating at Pskov in March 1917, Nicholas II returned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and left the headquarters town five days later in the custody of commissars sent from Petrograd."],"abstract":"<b>Mogilev</b> is a city in modern Belarus that became the seat of a governorate of the Russian Empire after the first partition of Poland in 1772.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> By the start of the 20th century it had about 43,000 inhabitants and stood among the larger towns of the Belarusian lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In August 1915, with German armies advancing into Russian territory, the headquarters of the Russian army, the Stavka, moved there from Baranovichi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 5 September 1915 Nicholas II dismissed the Grand Duke Nikolai and took supreme command himself, announcing that he would defend the country to the last and not dishonour the Russian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ministers had objected, and the British ambassador warned the empress that by joining the duties of commander with those of an autocrat the emperor was taking on a task beyond any one man’s strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At Mogilev the Stavka worked in the local administration building, while the emperor lived in the governor’s house and brought a more formal court protocol with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He left military decisions largely to his new chief of staff, General Mikhail Alekseyev, who became the army’s commander in all but name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Court records show that in the 14 months from January 1916 he spent 263 of 427 days at Mogilev, and taking command tied him personally to every loss the army suffered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A general who served there later wrote that the army had given little credence to rumours of the change, and that dissatisfaction with the conduct of the war was its main cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After abdicating at Pskov in March 1917, Nicholas returned to Mogilev on 3 March by the Russian calendar and left it five days later in the custody of commissars sent from Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Sofya Anisimova, “General Headquarters (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11561. [Open access]","Sofya Anisimova, “Alekseyev, Mikhail Vasilʹevich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11557. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Nicholas II, Emperor of Russia,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10334. [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 330–31, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy101knoxuoft. [Open access]","Basil Gourko, <i>War and Revolution in Russia, 1914–1917</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1919), https://archive.org/details/warandrevolution009671mbp. [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 1 (London: Cassell, 1923), 238–39, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi01buchuoft. [Open access]","М. В. Шиловский, «Император Николай II на посту верховного главнокомандующего по информации камер-фурьерского журнала за январь 1916 – февраль 1917 гг.», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 415 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/imperator-nikolay-ii-na-postu-verhovnogo-glavnokomanduyuschego-po-informatsii-kamer-furierskogo-zhurnala-za-yanvar-1916-fevral-1917-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. Е. Пашкевич, «Основные тенденции развития белорусских городов XIX – начала XX века», <i>Вестник Полоцкого государственного университета. Серия А. Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 3 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osnovnye-tendentsii-razvitiya-belorusskih-gorodov-xix-nachala-xx-veka. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nicholas take command himself?","a":"Because, beyond his sense of duty, he distrusted the Grand Duke’s links with the Duma and public bodies, and the Grand Duke was blamed in some quarters for incompetence and for chaos behind the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The change came after the losses of the summer retreat had pushed the Stavka back to Mogilev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did ministers and the British ambassador fear the decision?","a":"Because the emperor would bear the whole responsibility for any new disaster, and combining supreme command with autocratic rule seemed beyond any one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The empress, by contrast, protested that he ought to have taken command from the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Who really ran the army from Mogilev?","a":"In practice General Alekseyev did: the emperor delegated most military decisions to him, and observers at the Stavka noted that the tsar gave more of his attention to internal policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mogilev"},
    "ru-538": {"questions":["Named after the general who commanded the Southwestern Front, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recovered much of Galicia in 1916 but stalled when the fronts to its north would not attack.","Launched in part to relieve Italy and France, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Lutsk and Czernowitz in 1916 and helped bring Romania into the war on the Allied side."],"abstract":"The <b>Brusilov offensive</b> was the attack of the Russian Southwestern Front on the Austro-Hungarian armies in the summer of 1916, the largest Russian success of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began on 4 June 1916, which was 22 May by the Russian calendar, and Russian historians usually close it on 22 August, or 9 August by the old style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its commander, General Aleksei Brusilov, offered to attack along his whole front when other commanders declined, although the main Russian blow of the year was planned further north, towards Vilna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lacking the guns for one massive breakthrough, he prepared attacks at many points at once, digging saps close to the enemy lines, rehearsing assaults on copies of the Austrian trenches and keeping the bombardment short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The offensive was also meant to relieve allies under pressure, the French at Verdun and the Italians in the Trentino.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The Austro-Hungarian line broke in several places, the Eighth Army took Lutsk within days, and in the south the Ninth Army overran Czernowitz and pushed the Austrians back to the Carpathians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By one Russian count of 18 September the front had taken more than 8,900 officers and 408,000 men prisoner, together with 581 guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The offensive stalled because the fronts to the north would not attack, German reserves were rushed south, and Brusilov’s armies lacked the supplies and reinforcements to exploit their gains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It cost Russia about a million men by one estimate and 1,454,140 by October by another, while the Austro-Hungarian army lost some 790,000 by a count from Austrian records and never recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian press hailed it as a victory and Romania entered the war on the Allied side, but critics argue that several commanders, not Brusilov alone, deserved the credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]","Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Brusilov, Alekseĭ Alekseevich,” trans. Christopher Gilley, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11246. [Open access]","Г. М. Ипполитов and С. Ф. Кужилин, «Славная страница российской военной истории. К 100-летию Брусиловского прорыва», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i>, 18, no. 3-1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/slavnaya-stranitsa-rossiyskoy-voennoy-istorii-k-100-letiyu-brusilovskogo-proryva. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. Ю. Стародубова, «Освещение событий Брусиловского прорыва в периодической печати 1916 года», <i>История и историческая память</i>, no. 10 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osveschenie-sobytiy-brusilovskogo-proryva-v-periodicheskoy-pechati-1916-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Мелехов and М. А. Воронин, «Брусиловский прорыв», <i>Наука и образование</i>, 5, no. 4 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/brusilovskiy-proryv. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 2 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 404–47, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy02alfr. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Brusilov’s tactics surprise the Austrians?","a":"Because he attacked on many sectors at once after careful preparation, with saps dug close to the enemy line and short bombardments, so the enemy could not tell where the real blow would fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the offensive run out of steam?","a":"Because the army lacked the munitions to exploit its first success, the Stavka sent no reinforcements, and the generals to the north refused to launch diversionary attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That freed the Germans to move troops south and shore up the Austrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the credit for it disputed?","a":"Because critics say several commanders, not Brusilov alone, prepared and carried out the breakthrough at Lutsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet historians later fixed his name to it, and his own reputation suffered for a time because he joined the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Brusilov offensive"},
    "ru-539": {"questions":["Bolsheviks urged a boycott of the workers’ groups attached to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Mensheviks argued that workers should cooperate with employers for the defence of the country.","Orders placed through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to only about 3 per cent of Russian military orders by May 1916, though their political influence was far greater."],"abstract":"The <b>War Industries Committees</b> were public bodies set up by Russian businessmen in 1915 to bring private industry into the production of munitions for the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They grew out of the shortages of shells and rifles that crippled the army in the first year of the war, when a British officer found the shortage of shell not local but general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Ninth Congress of representatives of industry and trade, meeting in late May 1915, resolved to organise all the unused power of Russian industry for defence after a speech by the Moscow industrialist Pavel Ryabushinsky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The committees first met as an organised body on 25 July 1915 and chose the Octobrist leader Aleksandr Guchkov as chairman, with Aleksandr Konovalov as his deputy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of 1915 there were some 32 regional and more than 220 local committees, financed by government advances on orders and by a levy of up to 1 per cent on the orders they placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their share of production stayed small, for the orders placed through them came to 261.4 million roubles by 1 May 1916, just 3.29 per cent of all military orders, and big firms kept most contracts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their political weight was far greater, since they gave liberal businessmen a platform to attack official mismanagement, and Guchkov was later among a group that planned a palace coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The committees also set up workers’ groups, whose elections split the socialists, as Bolsheviks called for a boycott while Mensheviks and socialist revolutionaries urged workers to take part for the sake of national defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most regional industrialists refused the concessions on wages and conditions that working-class leaders demanded, and these sections remained something of a side-show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alongside the committees the war minister Aleksei Polivanov brought industrial leaders and Duma members into special councils that helped his ministry procure arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Peter Gatrell, “Organization of War Economies (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10513. [Open access]","Ю. Н. Кряжев, «Военно-промышленные комитеты и экономическое положение Российской империи (1915–1917 гг.)», <i>Омский научный вестник</i>, no. 1 (51) (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voenno-promyshlennye-komitety-i-ekonomicheskoe-polozhenie-rossiyskoy-imperii-1915-1917-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Лоскутов, «Борьба политических партий в рабочих группах военно-промышленных комитетов в годы Первой мировой войны», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, 1, no. 2 (1993), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-politicheskih-partiy-v-rabochih-gruppah-voenno-promyshlennyh-komitetov-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” trans. Trevor Goronwy, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 1 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy101knoxuoft. [Open access]","Timothy C. Dowling, “Eastern Front,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10316. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did businessmen create the committees?","a":"Because the army was short of munitions, and many smaller firms overlooked by the official procurement agencies wanted war orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ryabushinsky told their congress that the task of the day was to organise industry to equip and arm the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government distrust them?","a":"Because the committees mixed patriotic appeals with charges of official mismanagement and corruption, and their leaders were among the government’s political opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the socialists quarrel over the workers’ groups?","a":"Because taking part meant cooperating with employers in a war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bolsheviks wanted a boycott, while Mensheviks and socialist revolutionaries called for sending workers’ representatives for the sake of national defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Industry Committee"},
    "ru-540": {"questions":["Overwhelming German plans at Tannenberg in 1914 and swelling again during the retreat of 1915, the numbers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remain the hardest part of Russia’s losses to count.","Held mostly in Germany and Austria-Hungary on poorer rations than other Allied captives, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> received less help from home than French or British prisoners."],"abstract":"<b>Russian prisoners of war</b> in the First World War were the soldiers of the Russian army captured by Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire and Bulgaria between 1914 and 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They are the hardest part of Russia’s war losses to count, because the figures for its dead, wounded, missing and captured were never settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army itself counted more than 5.3 million losses of all kinds by 1916, including about 439,000 missing, perhaps a third of whom had in fact become prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of military deaths have ranged from about 511,000 to 908,000 in older counts to some 1.89 million deaths in battle in a calculation made after the fall of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners were the largest category, for the Stavka counted 2,044,000 by June 1917, an émigré general reckoned 2,417,000, and one Russian estimate gives more than 3.4 million, three quarters of all combat losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Modern estimates of the total range from 2.4 to 4.1 million men, depending on the sources used and on whether the exchanged, the escaped and the dead are counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first great haul came at Tannenberg in August 1914, where the Germans were overwhelmed with Russian prisoners they had made no plans for, and another came in the retreat of 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most were held in Germany and Austria-Hungary, and Russian prisoners lived in harder conditions than those of the other Allies, above all in their food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How many died is disputed, from up to 40,000 in one count to about 190,000, or 7.3 per cent, in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Newspapers at home followed their fate, but distance, poverty and official suspicion that they had surrendered too readily left them with less help than French or British captives, and many came home only after the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexandre Sumpf, “War Losses (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11284. [Open access]","Е. С. Сенявская, «Положение русских военнопленных в годы Первой мировой войны: очерк повседневной реальности», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: История России</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polozhenie-russkih-voennoplennyh-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny-ocherk-povsednevnoy-realnosti. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. Б. Белова, «Возвращение русских военнопленных Первой мировой войны в Советскую Россию: историографический аспект», <i>Вестник Вятского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozvraschenie-russkih-voennoplennyh-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny-v-sovetskuyu-rossiyu-istoriograficheskiy-aspekt. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Олейников, «Военнопленные на русском фронте Первой мировой войны», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 9 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voennoplennye-na-russkom-fronte-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. П. Назарова, «Периодическая печать о судьбе русских военнопленных в годы Первой мировой войны», <i>Вестник Волгоградского государственного университета. Серия 4: История. Регионоведение. Международные отношения</i>, no. 5 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/periodicheskaya-pechat-o-sudbe-russkih-voennoplennyh-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are Russia’s First World War losses so hard to count?","a":"Because military, civilian, national and local bodies kept lists for different purposes and by different methods, soldiers had no identity tags, and press lists often disagreed with the statisticians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Russian prisoners get less help than French or British ones?","a":"Because of distance, poor information and lack of money, and because the government suspected them of having given themselves up, a gap the Red Cross and the zemstvos could not fill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their food was also the poorest of any Allied prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do estimates of deaths in captivity differ so much?","a":"Because they rest on different sources: one military-historical count gives up to 40,000 deaths, while a figure accepted by other Russian researchers is about 190,000, or 7.3 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-541": {"questions":["The premier Goremykin told his ministers in September 1915 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been formed to seize power and that its aim of limiting the Tsar was poorly concealed.","Signed for the Kadets by Miliukov, the programme of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked for a political amnesty, autonomy for Poland and steps towards ending the restrictions placed on Jews."],"sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 134–38, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 154–66, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>The Fall of the Russian Monarchy: A Study of the Evidence</i> (1939; repr., New York: Vintage Books), 259–62, 277, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89642. [Open access]","А. Н. Мичурин, «„Общественный кабинет“ министров летом 1915 года и политическая оппозиция», <i>Terra Linguistica</i> 1, no. 118 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/obschestvennyy-kabinet-ministrov-letom-1915-goda-i-politicheskaya-oppozitsiya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. В. Сметанин, «Межфракционные парламентские блоки как признак организационного кризиса: наблюдения из начала XX в.», <i>Власть</i>, no. 10 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mezhfraktsionnye-parlamentskie-bloki-kak-priznak-organizatsionnogo-krizisa-nablyudeniya-iz-nachala-xx-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bloc form in the summer of 1915?","a":"Because the fall of Warsaw had deepened the political crisis, and most Duma factions and three groups of the State Council saw an advantage in an alliance across both chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its signatories declared that only a strong authority resting on popular confidence could lead the country to victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Goremykin refuse to bargain with it?","a":"Because he thought talking with its leaders would get the government nowhere, regarded the bloc itself as unacceptable and its real aim as limiting the Tsar's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also expected it to break up through quarrels among its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about the bloc?","a":"Because its record supports more than one reading: émigré and Western historians saw it as the last chance to save the monarchy, Soviet historians as too weak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One account doubts that the liberals ever genuinely sought compromise in August 1915, and another questions the usual view that the Kadets were its organisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Progressive Bloc</b> was the alliance of moderate and liberal groups in the State Duma and the State Council that took shape in August 1915, after the fall of Warsaw had deepened Russia's political crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined six Duma factions, from Shulgin's Progressive Nationalists and the Octobrists through the Progressists to Miliukov's Kadets, together with three groups of the upper house, while the far right and the socialists stayed outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its programme, signed on 25 August 1915 by Russia's Julian calendar, 7 September by the Western one, demanded a united government of men who enjoyed the confidence of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also asked for an amnesty for political and religious offenders, autonomy for Poland, steps towards ending the restrictions on Jews, the return of the Ukrainian press and the freedom of trade unions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its slogan of a 'ministry of confidence', new to Russian politics that summer, stopped short of a ministry answerable to the Duma, which the bloc's own debates treated as a matter for the future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Several ministers wanted to bargain, and the foreign minister Sazonov urged the government to back the bloc for its moderation, since whatever replaced it would be far more radical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The premier Goremykin answered that the bloc had been formed to seize power, that its aim of limiting the Tsar was poorly concealed, and that its members would soon fall out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Duma was suspended on 16 September 1915, and Kerensky later summed up the result by saying that the Empress had challenged the whole nation on the question and won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bloc lived on to open the session of November 1916, when Miliukov asked of one failure after another whether it was stupidity or treason, in a speech remembered as the storm signal of revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its meaning is still argued: émigré and Western writers saw it as the last chance to save the monarchy, Soviet historians as too timid, and one recent study credits the Progressists, not the Kadets, with drawing it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","wiki":"Progressive Bloc (Russia)"},
    "ru-542": {"questions":["Head of the zemstvo union and of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Prince Lvov told a forbidden congress at the end of 1916 that there were no authorities left to work with.","Paid largely from the state treasury yet never properly legalised, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the joint committee through which the unions of zemstvos and towns helped from 1915 to supply Russia's army."],"sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Union of Zemstvos and Towns,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10153. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 130–31, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","В. М. Шевырин, «Сотрудничество и борьба: власть и общественные организации в годы Первой мировой войны», <i>Россия и современный мир</i>, no. 1 (86) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sotrudnichestvo-i-borba-vlast-i-obschestvennye-organizatsii-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. В. Егорышева и В. В. Чалова, «Медико-санитарная деятельность российских общественных организаций в годы Первой мировой войны (1914—1916)», <i>Бюллетень Национального научно-исследовательского института общественного здоровья имени Н. А. Семашко</i>, no. 4 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mediko-sanitarnaya-deyatelnost-rossiyskih-obschestvennyh-organizatsiy-v-gody-pervoy-mirovoy-voyny-1914-1916. [in Russian] [Open access]","Iaroslav Golubinov, “Food and Nutrition (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11292. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government distrust the unions?","a":"Because it was ambivalent about their independent initiative and worried about the politics of their professional employees, and in 1916 it curtailed their meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The unions' own people felt the old authorities tolerated them only because they could not do without them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the unions add to the food crisis?","a":"Because their purchasing and supply work added to the organisational chaos underlying the crisis in food supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state's own grain-buying agents had already made scarcity worse, and new councils were piled on top of them in 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lvov head the government formed in 1917?","a":"Because the unions' leaders had come to demand a government of public confidence, as the Progressive Bloc in the Duma proposed, and Lvov had led the largest of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Chelnokov he headed Zemgor itself from July 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Zemgor</b> was the joint committee of the All-Russian Union of Zemstvos and the All-Russian Union of Towns, set up in July 1915 to help supply the Russian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two unions had been founded early in the war, the zemstvo union under Prince Georgy Lvov and the town union under the mayor of Moscow, Mikhail Chelnokov, to give medical aid to the wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The joint committee was made up of the president and four members of each union, and its task was to take government credits and war orders, share them among zemstvos and towns, and build the factories needed to fill them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The unions were never properly legalised, yet the money they spent came overwhelmingly from the state treasury, so they were at once voluntary bodies and part of the state's war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1916 they employed thousands of people on monthly budgets of tens of millions of roubles, and had added refugees, orphans, vaccination and food supply to their hospitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The zemstvo union alone ran 75 hospital trains, which carried some 2.5 million sick and wounded to the rear, and was the first body to care for refugees, who numbered 2.7 million by the summer of 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Overall the unions set up more than 3,000 hospitals and supplied much of the army's meat, but they did less for munitions and added to the confusion behind the food crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As food grew scarcer at the end of 1916, the interior minister Protopopov tried to take control of food prices in the provinces, and the conflict between the zemstvos and the government reached its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In December 1916 the government banned the congresses of the zemstvos and towns, but the zemstvo delegates met anyway and resolved that the government was leading Russia to ruin and shaking the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At one such meeting Lvov declared that there were no authorities left to work with, and after the February Revolution he became prime minister of the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","wiki":"Zemgor"},
    "ru-543": {"questions":["Married to a niece of Nicholas II, the Oxford-educated prince <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sent to his estate in Kursk province after the killing that shocked Petrograd in December 1916.","After Purishkevich's speech in the Duma against the dark forces around the throne, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called on him and said there were men in Russia ready to get rid of Rasputin."],"sources":["Prince Youssoupoff, <i>Rasputin: His Malignant Influence and His Assassination</i>, trans. Oswald Rayner (London: Jonathan Cape, 1927), 62–63, 73–74, 134–35, 144–47, 228–29, 238, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.459487. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 175–77, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Rasputin, Grigorii Efimovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10325. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>The Fall of the Russian Monarchy: A Study of the Evidence</i> (1939; repr., New York: Vintage Books), 399, 409, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89642. [Open access]","Н. Ф. Неделько, «Конец „Святого старца“ (судебно-медицинские аспекты смерти Г. Распутина)», <i>Байкальский медицинский журнал</i> 66, no. 8 (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konets-svyatogo-startsa-sudebno-meditsinskie-aspekty-smerti-g-rasputina. [in Russian] [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1923), 36–38, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi02buch. [Open access]","К. Г. Райх, «Мемуары князя Феликса Юсупова как опыт автобиографического повествования», <i>Общество: философия, история, культура</i>, no. 10 (78) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/memuary-knyazya-feliksa-yusupova-kak-opyt-avtobiograficheskogo-povestvovaniya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yusupov want Rasputin dead?","a":"Because he believed that Rasputin's power over the Empress, who in his words really governed the State, would only grow whatever the Duma said.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his own account he also set out to find evidence of what he believed was Rasputin's treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the killers never tried?","a":"Because their status precluded a formal investigation, and the silence fed fantastic speculation about the crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead the Grand Duke was sent to the forces in Persia and Yusupov to his country estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the murder not save the monarchy?","a":"Because the belief that Rasputin ruled the country had already undermined Nicholas's authority, and popular revolt swept the monarchy away a few months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yusupov himself wrote that the bright hopes behind the murder were extinguished one by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"Prince <b>Felix Yusupov</b> was the heir to one of the richest families in Russia, and in December 1916 he organised the murder of Grigori Rasputin, the peasant holy man trusted by the imperial family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Educated at Oxford, he had married Irina, a niece of Nicholas II, just before the war, and in 1916 he was training in the Corps of Pages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By his own account he became convinced that Rasputin was ruining the country, and he first chose as accomplices Grand Duke Dmitri Pavlovich and a wounded officer, Captain Sukhotin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Duma deputy Vladimir Purishkevich attacked the dark forces around the throne late in 1916, Yusupov called on him and said there were men ready to get rid of Rasputin, one of them before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Purishkevich brought in a fifth man, Dr Lazovert, and the plan was to lure Rasputin to the Yusupov house on the Moika and poison him with cyanide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the night of 16–17 December 1916 by the Julian calendar, 29–30 December by the Western one, Yusupov fetched Rasputin to a cellar room there and gave him cakes and wine laced with poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the poison seemed to have no effect, Yusupov shot him with the Grand Duke's revolver, but Rasputin rose, struggled out into the courtyard and was shot again, according to the British ambassador by Purishkevich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The body was pushed through a hole in the ice from a bridge over the Malaya Nevka, and the autopsy found three bullet wounds, while the version of its report later published records no trace of poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The killers' rank ruled out a formal investigation, so Yusupov was banished to his estate at Rakitnoe in Kursk province and the Grand Duke to the army in Persia, and the mystery fed rumours that further damaged the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yusupov told his story in a book published in English in 1927 and again in memoirs written in 1953, in which the murder remains the central event of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","wiki":"Felix Yusupov"},
    "ru-544": {"questions":["By the Western calendar it happened in March 1917, but Russians still name the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the Julian month in which Petrograd's workers and garrison rose against the Tsar.","Rodzianko telegraphed the Tsar that the capital was in a state of anarchy during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, days before a Duma committee and a workers' soviet emerged as rival centres of power."],"sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, trans. Max Eastman, vol. 1, <i>The Overthrow of Tzarism</i> (1932), ch. 7, “Five Days,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch07.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 263, 278–79, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1923), 58–60, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi02buch. [Open access]","С. А. Поляков, «Начало Февральской революции», <i>Актуальные вопросы общественных наук: социология, политология, философия, история</i>, no. 32 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalo-fevralskoy-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Michael S. Melancon, “Social Conflict and Control, Protest and Repression (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10308. [Open access]","Gleb J. Albert, “Labour Movements, Trade Unions and Strikes (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10458. [Open access]","Alexander F. Kerensky, <i>The Catastrophe: Kerensky's Own Story of the Russian Revolution</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1927), 99–100, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.180014. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the protests begin on Women's Day?","a":"Because the women textile workers of the Vyborg district came out of their own accord, many of them soldiers' wives worn down by the long bread lines, though no organisation had called a strike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their protest against food shortages then widened into a political general strike across the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the government not put the rising down?","a":"Because the garrison it relied on would not hold: a company of the Pavlovsky Regiment mutinied after its own training squad fired on the crowds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 February the soldiers went over to the crowds, the authorities hesitated, and they lost control of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called the February Revolution if it happened in March?","a":"Because Russia still used the Julian calendar, which ran behind the Western one, so the garrison's revolt of 27 February fell on 12 March abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Western witnesses such as the British ambassador dated the first bread riots to 8 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>February Revolution</b> was the rising in Petrograd that overthrew the Russian monarchy at the end of February 1917 by the Julian calendar Russia still used, in March by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It began on 23 February, or 8 March, International Women's Day, when women textile workers in the Vyborg district walked out in protest at the bread queues, and about 90,000 workers were on strike by evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By 25 February it had become almost a general strike, with 240,000 out by the government's own count, and the crowds' banners asked for bread but also for an end to the autocracy and the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That evening Nicholas II, at army headquarters, ordered the military commander in Petrograd, General Khabalov, to put an end to the disorders the very next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 26 February troops fired on the crowds, but that evening a company of the Pavlovsky Regiment of the Guards mutinied in protest at the shooting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 27 February, or 12 March, the soldiers of the garrison went over to the crowds one unit after another, and the authorities lost control of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Duma's president Rodzianko had already telegraphed the Tsar that the capital was in a state of anarchy and that someone enjoying the confidence of the country must form a new government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mutinous soldiers marched to the Tauride Palace, seat of the Duma, and within a day a committee of Duma members and a soviet of workers' deputies had both emerged as centres of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nicholas abdicated on 2 March, or 15 March, and a Provisional Government drawn from the Progressive Bloc took office, with the Petrograd Soviet beside it as a rival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early accounts stressed a spontaneous rising of a hungry city and later ones the generals who pressed the Tsar to go, while one Russian study blames the queues on a shortage of bakers rather than of grain, against contemporary reports that the towns' stores were exhausted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","wiki":"February Revolution"},
    "ru-545": {"questions":["Led by a senior NCO, Timofei Kirpichnikov, the reserve soldiers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shot their commander on the morning of 27 February 1917 and called out the neighbouring guards regiments.","Reckoned before the war the most disciplined regiment in the Russian army, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Imperial Guard became the first unit of the Petrograd garrison to rise in 1917."],"sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, trans. Max Eastman, vol. 1, <i>The Overthrow of Tzarism</i> (1932), ch. 7, “Five Days,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch07.htm. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>The Fall of the Russian Monarchy: A Study of the Evidence</i> (1939; repr., New York: Vintage Books), 444–46, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89642. [Open access]","В. Ю. Савинов, «Участие лейб-гвардии Преображенского полка в Февральской революции: карательная экспедиция полковника А. П. Кутепова 27 февраля 1917 года», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Исторические науки</i> 12, no. 1 (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/uchastie-leyb-gvardii-preobrazhenskogo-polka-v-fevralskoy-revolyutsii-karatelnaya-ekspeditsiya-polkovnika-a-p-kutepova-27-fevralya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Смирнов, «Индивидуальные черты частей русской гвардейской пехоты в начале ХХ в.», <i>Пространство и Время</i>, no. 2–4 (28–30) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/individualnye-cherty-chastey-russkoy-gvardeyskoy-pehoty-v-nachale-hh-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Поляков, «Начало Февральской революции», <i>Актуальные вопросы общественных наук: социология, политология, философия, история</i>, no. 32 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalo-fevralskoy-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Michael S. Melancon, “Social Conflict and Control, Protest and Repression (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10308. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the guard's own reserve turn against the government?","a":"Because the garrison was made up almost entirely of reserve battalions of men who had barely been trained, not the pre-war guardsmen, who were at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Volynsky soldiers had been made to fire on a crowd the day before, and they resolved not to fire on their own people again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mutiny spread so quickly?","a":"Because once their commander was dead the mutineers had burned their bridges, and their only safety lay in widening the revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They called out the Lithuanian and Preobrazhensky regiments next door, and by evening most of the troops had gone over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 27 February taken as the end of tsarist rule?","a":"Because that day the authorities lost control of the city, and the Duma formed its Provisional Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> General Khabalov was left with about 2,000 loyal soldiers, far too few to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Volynsky Regiment</b> of the Life Guards was the imperial guard unit whose reserve soldiers in Petrograd began the mutiny of the garrison on 27 February 1917 by the Julian calendar, 12 March by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Formed in 1817 from a battalion of the Finland Regiment, it was known before the war as the most disciplined and best-drilled regiment in the whole Russian army, and German troops called it the iron regiment in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Like the rest of the Guards, its fighting battalions were at the front, and the Petrograd garrison of some 150,000 to 160,000 men consisted almost entirely of reserve battalions of barely trained recruits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The authorities' plan for crushing a rising really counted on only about 10,000 of those soldiers, and the training squads of the reserve battalions were among the units most relied on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 26 February the training squad of the Volynsky reserve battalion broke up a demonstration on Znamenskaya Square with volleys of rifle fire, killing at least 11 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Before dawn the next day its soldiers agreed that they would not fire on their own people again, and when the squad was paraded that morning it refused to march out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its commander was shot, and a senior NCO, Timofei Kirpichnikov, emerged as a leader of the revolt, later admitting that he had not known whether within the hour he would be a national hero or hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mutineers ran to the neighbouring barracks of the Lithuanian and Preobrazhensky regiments and called their soldiers out, as strikers call out a factory, then seized the Arsenal on the Liteiny Prospekt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By evening most of the garrison, the Guards included, had gone over, and General Khabalov had only about 2,000 reliable men left, against at least 250,000 people in the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The day of the mutiny is the date usually given for the overthrow of the tsarist regime, since after it the government no longer had any force it could use in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},
    "ru-546": {"questions":["The Duma's envoys Guchkov and Shulgin reached Pskov at ten at night to ask for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only to hear that the Tsar had already decided to give up the throne.","General Alekseev polled the commanders of every front by telegraph before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their replies, urging him to go, were laid before the Tsar on 2 March 1917."],"sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 275–77, 294, 297–98, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Bernard Pares, <i>The Fall of the Russian Monarchy: A Study of the Evidence</i> (1939; repr., New York: Vintage Books), 463–66, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89642. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Nicholas II, Emperor of Russia,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10334. [Open access]","М. М. Сафонов и А. И. Рупасов, «Вокруг отречения Николая II», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 2 (26) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vokrug-otrecheniya-nikolaya-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Я. Дегтярев, «Генерал М. В. Алексеев и отречение императора Николая II», <i>Власть</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/general-m-v-alekseev-i-otrechenie-imperatora-nikolaya-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Николаева и А. А. Богданова, «Отречение Николая II в воспоминаниях современников», <i>Наука, образование и культура</i>, no. 3 (18) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otrechenie-nikolaya-ii-v-vospominaniyah-sovremennikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Поляков, «Начало Февральской революции», <i>Актуальные вопросы общественных наук: социология, политология, философия, история</i>, no. 32 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nachalo-fevralskoy-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nicholas abdicate for his brother rather than his son?","a":"Because his doctor, Fedorov, told him that Alexei's illness was incurable, and Nicholas told the envoys he could not part with his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Duma committee's plan had depended on Alexei, since a regency was the obvious road to a constitutional regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals' telegrams matter so much?","a":"Because one reading holds that the immediate cause of the abdication was not the streets but the front commanders' demand that he go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They put it to him that, facing a foreign enemy, this was the only course that could unite the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the time on the manifesto misleading?","a":"Because it was dated three in the afternoon, the hour the Tsar first decided, while his talks with Guchkov and Shulgin only began that night and the signing was reported after midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shulgin recalled receiving the text at twenty minutes to midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>abdication of Nicholas II</b> was the act by which the last Russian emperor gave up the throne for himself and his son in the imperial train at Pskov on 2 March 1917 by the Julian calendar, 15 March by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leaving army headquarters for Tsarskoe Selo as Petrograd rose, his train was turned aside by a false report that the line was cut and went to Pskov, headquarters of the Northern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There he at last offered a ministry with the confidence of the country, but the Duma's president Rodzianko replied that such concessions came too late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chief of staff, General Alekseev, had asked the commanders of the fronts by telegraph whether the Tsar should go, and their replies, all urging abdication, were laid before him that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nicholas first agreed to give the throne to his son Alexei, with his brother Michael as regent, which had been the plan of the Duma leaders and the Progressive Bloc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After his doctor confirmed that the boy's haemophilia was incurable he changed his mind, and he told the Duma's envoys Guchkov and Shulgin, who arrived at ten that night, that he would abdicate in favour of Michael.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manifesto he handed them spoke of uniting the people for victory in the war and enjoined Michael to govern in harmony with the people's representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was dated three in the afternoon, though it was signed close to midnight, and its text first reached the public through the commander of the Baltic Fleet, who printed it in Reval as a manifesto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The change of heir was of doubtful legality, since the order of succession fixed by Paul I allowed no departure without a formal renunciation, and some writers in Russia have even claimed the act was forged, against the Tsar's own diary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians still argue whether the Duma or the generals forced the decision, and one Russian study insists that Alekseev stayed loyal to his oath throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","wiki":"Abdication of Nicholas II"},
    "ru-547": {"questions":["Commander of the Savage Division of Caucasian horsemen in the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had married Natalia Brasova morganatically in 1912, to the dismay of the imperial family.","At a flat on Millionnaya Street, Miliukov argued for an hour that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should take the throne, but Rodzianko and Prince Lvov urged him to refuse it."],"sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 294, 297–99, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Nicholas II, Emperor of Russia,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10334. [Open access]","Л. В. Перескоков, «„Он такой легковерный…“: штрихи к портрету великого князя Михаила Александровича», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i> 20, no. 1 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/on-takoy-legkovernyy-shtrihi-k-portretu-velikogo-knyazya-mihaila-aleksandrovicha. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. В. Софьина и Д. М. Софьин, «Великий князь Михаил Александрович: краткий обзор исследований», <i>Русско-Византийский вестник</i>, no. 3 (14) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/velikiy-knyaz-mihail-aleksandrovich-kratkiy-obzor-issledovaniy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander F. Kerensky, <i>The Catastrophe: Kerensky's Own Story of the Russian Revolution</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1927), 66–70, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.180014. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Michael refuse the crown?","a":"Because, faced with no support and no real means of taking up power, he left the question of government to a Constituent Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The revolutionary democracy of Petrograd would by then have nothing more to do with the Romanovs, on the throne or anywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Miliukov want him to accept?","a":"Because Miliukov held that Russia needed a constitutional monarchy, and that quarrelling over the form of government at once would lead to civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He argued the minority's case before the Grand Duke for more than an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Michael's own position in the dynasty awkward?","a":"Because his morganatic marriage in 1912 had been made in breach of the law of the imperial house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many saw his wartime command of a newly raised division of Caucasian mountaineers as a sign that he had not yet been forgiven for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Grand Duke Michael Alexandrovich</b> was the younger brother of Nicholas II, named by him as emperor on 2 March 1917, whose refusal of the throne the next day ended three centuries of Romanov rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The youngest son of Alexander III, born in 1878, he became the real heir to the throne when his brother George died in 1899, and Tsarevich Alexei's illness kept him close to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1912 he married Natalia Wulfert, who was given the name Brasova, in a morganatic marriage that broke the law of the imperial house and shocked the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the war he commanded a new cavalry division raised from Muslim mountaineers of the North Caucasus, nicknamed the Savage Division, and in November 1916 he warned his brother in writing of the alarming change in the mood of loyal people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Duma leaders had planned to make him regent for Alexei, but Nicholas, unwilling to part with his son, passed the crown directly to Michael.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the morning of 3 March, or 16 March by the Western calendar, ministers of the new Provisional Government met him in a private flat at 12 Millionnaya Street in Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rodzianko and Prince Lvov, for the majority, urged him to refuse, while Miliukov argued for more than an hour that he should accept, and Guchkov backed Miliukov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After talking with Lvov and Rodzianko alone, he declared that he would accept supreme power only if the people wished it, as expressed by a Constituent Assembly elected by universal, direct, equal and secret ballot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until then he asked all citizens to obey the Provisional Government, and his refusal took aback many of the elite revolutionaries, who had prepared nothing for it, so that the monarchy collapsed by default.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exiled by the Bolsheviks to Perm in March 1918, he was killed there on the night of 12–13 June 1918, and the Russian Orthodox Church Outside Russia counted him among its saints in 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","wiki":"Grand Duke Michael Alexandrovich of Russia"},
    "ru-548": {"questions":["Four chairmen of the Council of Ministers in barely a year and four war ministers in the war gave Russia's critics the jibe of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the monarchy staggered towards collapse.","Contemporaries blamed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1915–1917 on Rasputin and the Empress, though one later view traces it to the small pool of men loyal to autocracy and acceptable to the Duma."],"sources":["Bernard Pares, <i>The Fall of the Russian Monarchy: A Study of the Evidence</i> (1939; repr., New York: Vintage Books), 375–76, 396, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89642. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Duma,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10435. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Е. В. Бей и А. В. Евдокимов, «Министерская чехарда. Последние военные министры Российской империи», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ministerskaya-cheharda-poslednie-voennye-ministry-rossiyskoy-imperii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 108–09, 225–26, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Rasputin, Grigorii Efimovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10325. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1923), v, 35, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi02buch. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did contemporaries blame the Empress and Rasputin?","a":"Because the Tsar was away at headquarters, and voices across the empire condemned the dark forces behind the throne that made and unmade ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Empress's own letters show her pressing for ministers' dismissal, as with Polivanov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some historians doubt Rasputin's hand in it?","a":"Because there is no firm evidence that he had a significant political role outside church affairs, and his influence was magnified by gossip and his own boasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Tsar sometimes overruled him, replacing Stürmer with Trepov in November 1916 despite objections from Rasputin and the Empress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the turnover matter so much in wartime?","a":"Because it came on top of defeats, supply crises and soaring prices, and it fed the mounting discontent that turned Duma members against the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each new appointment of a doubtful minister, such as Protopopov, was read as a provocation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Ministerial leapfrog</b> was the name given in wartime Russia to the rapid turnover of ministers in 1915–1917, which contemporaries and many later writers have counted among the reasons the monarchy fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The phrase was coined by Vladimir Purishkevich, a right-wing monarchist deputy in the Duma, to describe how fast one cabinet appointment followed another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In little more than a year the chairmanship of the Council of Ministers passed from Goremykin to Stürmer in February 1916, to Trepov in November and to Prince Golitsyn early in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The war ministry had four ministers during the war, and the second of them, Polivanov, was dismissed in March 1916 soon after the Empress wrote that she wished her husband could get rid of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the autumn of 1916 the interior ministry went to Alexander Protopopov, whose surprise appointment Purishkevich mocked in verse and the Progressive Bloc treated as a provocation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Across the empire voices condemned the dark forces behind the throne, meaning above all the Empress and Rasputin, which were said to make and unmake ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Purishkevich held the leapfrog and the dark forces behind it up to scorn in the Duma in November 1916, and the Duma's president Rodzianko recalled the public talk of irresponsible influence and the leapfrog game.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chief of staff, General Alekseev, complained privately that the honest ministers left and the worthless remained, and that the Tsar was not a free man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not every historian accepts that Rasputin chose the ministers, and one view holds that the turnover reflected the small pool of men who were both champions of autocracy and able to work with the Duma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Either way the government became steadily more inept and subject to the Tsar's capricious interventions, and the turnover deepened the discontent that turned the Duma against the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},
    "ru-549": {"questions":["Its first programme promised an amnesty, free speech and a Constituent Assembly, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deferred land reform and kept Russia fighting the war against Germany.","Chosen by the Duma's Temporary Committee after talks with the Petrograd Soviet, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to rule only until a Constituent Assembly could decide Russia's form of government."],"sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 308–10, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Provisional Government,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10242. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Ф. А. Гайда, «Проблема верховной власти в России в феврале 1917 – январе 1918 г.: юридическая и политическая реальность», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (47) (2022), https://doi.org/10.25688/20-76-9105.2022.47.3.01. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Коваленко, «К вопросу о порядке формирования центральной власти в 1917 году», <i>Научный вестник Московского государственного технического университета гражданской авиации</i>, no. 142 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-poryadke-formirovaniya-tsentralnoy-vlasti-v-1917-godu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander F. Kerensky, <i>The Catastrophe: Kerensky's Own Story of the Russian Revolution</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1927), 61–62, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.180014. [Open access]","George Buchanan, <i>My Mission to Russia and Other Diplomatic Memories</i>, vol. 2 (London: Cassell, 1923), 91–92, 206–08, https://archive.org/details/mymissiontorussi02buch. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, trans. Max Eastman, vol. 1, <i>The Overthrow of Tzarism</i> (1932), ch. 10, “The New Power,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch10.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was it called provisional?","a":"Because it was set up to run the country only until authority could be handed to a popularly elected Constituent Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its programme promised to prepare that assembly at once, to decide the form of government and draw up a constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did its authority crumble?","a":"Because it would not end the war or pass a land law before the Constituent Assembly, so peasants and deserting soldiers seized land themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under dual power the Soviet, not the government, held the only real authority over workers and soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did socialists join it in May 1917?","a":"Because violent protests over Miliukov's secret note to the Allies, committing Russia to the old war aims, threatened the government, and the Soviet agreed to shore it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From then on cabinets were formed by agreement between party centres and the leadership of the soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Provisional Government</b> was the cabinet that ruled Russia between the fall of the monarchy in February 1917 and the Bolshevik seizure of power in October, pending an elected Constituent Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was drawn up on 2 March, or 15 March by the Western calendar, by the Duma's Temporary Committee after negotiations with the Executive Committee of the Petrograd Soviet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prince Georgy Lvov, head of the Union of Zemstvos, became prime minister and minister of the interior, the Kadet Miliukov took foreign affairs the Octobrist Guchkov war and the navy, and the sugar magnate Tereshchenko finance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Soviet's Executive Committee voted against joining the cabinet, but its vice-president, Kerensky, accepted the ministry of justice all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first programme promised an amnesty for political and religious offences, freedom of speech, the press, unions and strikes, an end to social, religious and national restrictions, and a Constituent Assembly elected by universal and secret vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within weeks it brought in universal adult suffrage, made the police answerable to local government and abolished capital punishment, and the Allies recognised it, the United States first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Yet no election stood behind it, since it had been chosen by Duma leaders and filled later vacancies by co-option, and it at once kept the Duma itself away from real power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From May its cabinets and programmes were settled by bargaining with the soviets, as socialist leaders joined a coalition after protests over Miliukov's note to the Allies on war aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It deferred land reform to the Constituent Assembly and kept Russia in the war, while the Petrograd Soviet held the only real authority over the workers and the soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kerensky replaced Lvov as prime minister in July, and his last coalition was overthrown on 25 October, or 7 November, 1917, when Soviet forces entered the Winter Palace and arrested the ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","wiki":"Russian Provisional Government"},
    "ru-550": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1917 the Provisional Government held formal authority, while the Petrograd Soviet commanded the troops, the railways and the telegraph.","Kerensky dismissed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a legend spread by the government's enemies, though both rival authorities of 1917 had first taken shape in two wings of the Tauride Palace."],"sources":["V. I. Lenin, “The Dual Power,” <i>Pravda</i>, no. 28, 9 April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 38–41, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/09.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, trans. Max Eastman, vol. 1, <i>The Overthrow of Tzarism</i> (1932), ch. 11, “Dual Power,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch11.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Dual Power,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10075. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 309–10, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Н. А. Коваленко, «„Двоевластие“ в России в 1917 году: новые подходы и взгляды», <i>Научный вестник Московского государственного технического университета гражданской авиации</i>, no. 113 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dvoevlastie-v-rossii-v-1917-godu-novye-podhody-i-vzglyady. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Яковлев, «Феномен двоевластия в России в 1917 г. в англоязычной историографии», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/fenomen-dvoevlastiya-v-rossii-v-1917-g-v-angloyazychnoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet not simply take power itself?","a":"Because its Menshevik leaders held that a bourgeois revolution and a long period of capitalism had to come before socialism, and Soviet leaders feared counter-revolution and civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One reading puts it that the Soviet could have built a full central government but would not, while the government wanted to but could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin compare the soviets to the Paris Commune?","a":"Because their power rested not on a law passed by a parliament but on the direct initiative of the people from below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like the Commune, he argued, they replaced the police and the army with the armed people themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kerensky deny that dual power existed?","a":"Because he sincerely believed that the Provisional Government held supreme power and that the Soviet's Executive Committee had no right to interfere in its work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He served at once as vice-chairman of the Soviet and minister of justice, so the two powers were never neatly divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Dual power</b>, in Russian dvoevlastie, is the name given to the division of authority between the Provisional Government and the Petrograd Soviet of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies after the fall of the monarchy in February 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Both bodies took shape in the Tauride Palace during the February rising, the Duma's Temporary Committee in its right wing and the Soviet in its left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government held formal power over the empire, while the Soviet, which commanded the loyalty of the troops, the railways and the telegraph, held the closest thing to real authority in the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet's Executive Committee refused to join the cabinet and instead set conditions for its support, which the government had little choice but to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Socialist leaders promised to stand behind the government only in so far as it carried out its announced programme, a conditional formula that summed up the arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The term itself was Lenin's: writing in Pravda on 9 April 1917, he declared that the most remarkable feature of the revolution was that it had brought about a dual power, which nobody had foreseen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside the government of the bourgeoisie, he argued, another government had arisen in the soviets, still weak but real, a power of the same type as the Paris Commune of 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trotsky later made it a general feature of revolutions, holding that a double government could arise only out of irreconcilable class conflict and so only in a revolutionary epoch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kerensky, by contrast, called dual power a legend invented by the government's enemies, and historians still date its end variously, to the spring, to July or to October 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Studies of the provinces have questioned the whole picture, finding soviets and officials working closely together in some towns and, elsewhere, so many local committees that the period looks less like dual power than a multitude of powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","wiki":"Dual power"},
    "ru-551": {"questions":["Abolishing compulsory saluting off duty and titles such as 'Your Excellency', the Soviet's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread as a leaflet from the Petrograd garrison along the whole front.","The British attaché Alfred Knox called the Soviet's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a deathblow to the Russian army, while Tsereteli defended it as a way to organise an unorganised mob."],"abstract":"<b>Order No. 1</b> was the order of the Petrograd Soviet of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies, dated 1 March 1917 by the Russian calendar and 14 March by the Western one, that laid down new relations between the soldiers of the Petrograd garrison and their officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It told every company, battalion, battery and ship to elect a committee from the lower ranks and to send deputies to the Soviet, which the garrison was to obey in all its political actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Orders of the Duma's military commission were to be carried out only where they did not conflict with the Soviet, and all weapons were to stay with the committees and never be handed to officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On duty the strictest discipline still applied, but off duty soldiers kept every right of a citizen, standing to attention and compulsory saluting were abolished, and officers lost titles such as 'Your Excellency'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rudeness to soldiers was forbidden, above all addressing them with the familiar 'thou', and the order was to be read out to every unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was written not by the Soviet's leaders, who were then negotiating with the Duma committee, but by a commission of a noisy soldiers' meeting chaired by the lawyer Nikolai Sokolov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Although it was addressed only to the Petrograd district, it was printed in vast numbers and spread rapidly along the whole front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Duma deputy Vasily Shulgin remembered Rodzianko shouting that it had been written on purpose to help the Germans, and the British attaché Alfred Knox told Sokolov it was a deathblow to the Russian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order said nothing about electing officers, yet soldiers widely read it that way, and a second order soon explained that the new committees were not to choose their commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet leaders later defended it as a necessity, and at a meeting with the generals in May Irakli Tsereteli said that they had faced an unorganised mob and had to organise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 274, 386–89, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 2 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 565–69, 572–73, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy02alfr. [Open access]","Anton Denikin, <i>The Russian Turmoil: Memoirs, Military, Social, and Political</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1922), 62–63, https://archive.org/details/russianturmoilme00deniuoft. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 14, “The Ruling Group and the War,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch14.htm. [Open access]","Э. В. Костяев, «Российские социал-демократы и приказ № 1 Петроградского Совета от 1 марта 1917 г.», <i>Власть</i>, no. 4 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rossiyskie-sotsial-demokraty-i-prikaz-1-petrogradskogo-soveta-ot-1-marta-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Л. Кожевин, «Воинский ритуал отдания чести глазами русских солдат и матросов в условиях «медового месяца» революции 1917 г.», <i>Вестник Омского университета. Серия «Исторические науки»</i>, no. 3 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voinskiy-ritual-otdaniya-chesti-glazami-russkih-soldat-i-matrosov-v-usloviyah-medovogo-mesyatsa-revolyutsii-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. А. Тарасов, «Дискуссия о «Декларации прав солдата» в Петроградском гарнизоне в 1917 г.», <i>Петербургский исторический журнал</i>, no. 4 (8) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussiya-o-deklaratsii-prav-soldata-v-petrogradskom-garnizone-v-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the order give the committees control of the weapons?","a":"Because the garrison feared that officers who had not joined the revolution would disarm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rumours that disarming had begun hung over the soldiers' meeting, and Sokolov later told Knox the rule was needed because nobody yet knew which officers could be trusted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did officers see the order as the end of the army?","a":"Because it put the soldiers under the Soviet in politics and took their weapons out of the officers' hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shulgin felt on reading it that this was the end of the army, and Knox judged that, printed in millions, it became a charter of licence throughout the armies at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did saluting matter so much to the soldiers?","a":"Because the most resented form of greeting, standing to attention for a passing officer, was a drawn-out drill of fixed steps and movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The order abolished it off duty, and the debates of May over a declaration of soldiers' rights showed how much further many garrison soldiers wanted such rights to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Petrograd Soviet Order No. 1"},
    "ru-552": {"questions":["With its newspaper <i>Izvestiia</i> handed out free from motor cars, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Nikolai Chkheidze shared authority with the Provisional Government in what Lenin called dual power.","In September 1917 a Bolshevik resolution passed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by 279 votes to 115, and Chkheidze's moderate presidium resigned rather than carry it out."],"abstract":"The <b>Petrograd Soviet</b> of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies was the council of elected delegates that met in the Tauride Palace from 27 February 1917, 12 March by the Western calendar, and became the voice of the socialist left in the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formed in the same building as the Duma's own committee, and its provisional executive asked factories to elect one deputy for every thousand workers and each army company to send one more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that rate it soon had about 3,000 members, more than 2,000 of them soldiers, so many that there was no room for them in the palace and little business could be done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its working leadership was an Executive Committee that had constituted itself before the Soviet was elected, drawn mainly from radical intellectuals of the socialist parties, with Nikolai Chkheidze as the Soviet's chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its newspaper <i>Izvestiia</i> printed 485,000 copies of its first issue, handed out free from motor cars on the streets of the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> From the first days workers and soldiers obeyed the Soviet before the Provisional Government, and Lenin wrote in April that alongside that government a second one had arisen, an arrangement he called dual power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Yet the Soviet backed the government conditionally rather than replacing it, and some historians argue that it saw itself as a pressure group rather than a rival government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Executive Committee approved the text of Order No. 1 for the garrison on 1 March, 14 March by the Western calendar, an early sign of where the soldiers' loyalty lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 14 March by the Russian calendar the Soviet unanimously adopted an appeal to the peoples of the whole world, its first public statement on the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In September a Bolshevik resolution passed the Soviet by 279 votes to 115, and Chkheidze and his moderate presidium resigned rather than take responsibility for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 285–91, 578–79, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 12, “The Executive Committee,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch12.htm. [Open access]","М. В. Федоров, «Распространение «Известий Петроградского Совета рабочих и солдатских депутатов» 1917 г.», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rasprostranenie-izvestiy-petrogradskogo-soveta-rabochih-i-soldatskih-deputatov-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Рачковский, «Война и Петроградский Совет рабочих и солдатских депутатов в феврале–марте 1917 г.», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 1 (12) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/voyna-i-petrogradskiy-sovet-rabochih-i-soldatskih-deputatov-v-fevrale-marte-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Provisional Government,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10242. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “The Dual Power,” <i>Pravda</i>, 9 April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 38–41, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/09.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many of its deputies soldiers?","a":"Because the founding call asked each company of the garrison to send a deputy but each factory only one for every thousand workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that rate soldiers soon made up more than 2,000 of about 3,000 deputies, and the body quickly changed its name to the Soviet of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet not take power itself in March?","a":"Because its leaders recognised the Provisional Government as the government and offered it conditional support, while voting against joining it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some historians therefore argue that the Soviet saw itself as a pressure group rather than a political alternative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it matter that the Soviet had its own newspaper?","a":"Because <i>Izvestiia</i> carried the Soviet's decisions straight to the streets, with 485,000 copies of the first issue given away from motor cars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its orders to the garrison, Order No. 1 among them, were printed there for the soldiers to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Petrograd Soviet"},
    "ru-553": {"questions":["Wearing an English-style tunic without badges of rank, the war minister <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> toured the front in June 1917 to rouse soldiers for the offensive that bore his name.","Born in Simbirsk, where his father ran the gymnasium the young Lenin attended, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was elected to the Duma in 1912 for the peasant-based Trudovik group."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander Kerensky</b> was the lawyer and politician who served the Provisional Government of 1917 as minister of justice, then minister of war, and from July as prime minister until the Bolsheviks overthrew it in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born in Simbirsk in 1881, where his father ran the classical gymnasium whose pupils included the future Lenin, and he studied law at St Petersburg University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made his name defending rebellious peasants and members of national minorities, and in 1912 he was elected to the Duma for the peasant-based Trudovik group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1917 he became vice-chairman of the Petrograd Soviet's Executive Committee and, alone among the Soviet's leaders, accepted a ministry, as minister of justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His eloquence helped persuade the Soviet's Executive Committee to join a coalition in May, in which he became minister of war, and in July he succeeded Prince Lvov as prime minister and moved into the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kerensky was the first leader of the revolution to become the object of a cult, built by admirers and by himself, which hailed him as the people's tribune, the leader of the revolutionary army and even a Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the platform his soft voice turned hoarse and his speeches ended in broken shouts, and he sometimes spoke three or four times a day to crowds of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As minister of war he wore a short English-style tunic and cap without badges of rank, a half-military look meant to show that the minister was a civilian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He toured the front to rouse the troops for the June offensive that came to bear his name, and its collapse, followed by the Kornilov affair, left him distrusted by both right and left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died in New York in 1970, and in an early book of exile he recalled telling friends at the height of his fame that the crowd which bowed to him would one day come and smite him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Kerenskii, Aleksandr Fedorovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10419. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Provisional Government,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10242. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Б. И. Колоницкий, «Антимонархическая революция 1917 года и начало формирования культа вождя народа», <i>Исторические исследования. Журнал Исторического факультета МГУ имени М. В. Ломоносова</i>, no. 6 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/antimonarhicheskaya-revolyutsiya-1917-goda-i-nachalo-formirovaniya-kulta-vozhdya-naroda. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. П. Федюк, «Александр Керенский: феномен политического лидерства», <i>Вестник Костромского государственного университета</i>, 21, no. 2 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/aleksandr-kerenskiy-fenomen-politicheskogo-liderstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. М. Ляндрес and А. Б. Николаев, «О культе Александра Керенского и историографической традиции», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i> 65, no. 2 (2020): 668–78, https://doi.org/10.21638/11701/spbu02.2020.220. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexander Kerensky, <i>The Prelude to Bolshevism: The Kornilov Rising</i> (New York: Dodd, Mead, 1919), v–vi, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028351496. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 348–50, 426–27, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did crowds treat him as a hero in the spring of 1917?","a":"Because he seemed to stand above party politics, greeted demonstrators, arrested tsarist ministers and spoke with great force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admirers and the press built him into a people's tribune, and he worked hard on his own speeches and public image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he lose support so quickly?","a":"Because the offensive he had championed in June collapsed into retreat, and the Kornilov affair then cost him the trust of both camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Officers on the right hated him for turning on Kornilov, while the Soviet gave him no credit because he had first worked with the general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he dress in a half-military style?","a":"Because as minister of war he wanted to look the part without pretending to be an officer, so his short tunic and cap carried no badges of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Critics mocked the Napoleonic pose of his hand tucked into the tunic, though he explained it by the thousands of hands he had to shake at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander Kerensky"},
    "ru-554": {"questions":["Nothing was literally locked, but because no one could board it during the journey, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a taunt that Lenin's opponents threw at him after his return to Petrograd.","Germany let Lenin through in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hoping he would weaken Russia's war effort, though later research found that he refused the go-between Parvus and received little German money."],"abstract":"The <b>sealed train</b> is the name given to the railway carriage in which Lenin and other Russian émigrés crossed Germany in April 1917 on their way home from Switzerland to revolutionary Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain and France would not give passage to émigrés who opposed the war, and Martov first proposed buying passage through Germany in exchange for German and Austrian internees held in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Swiss socialist Fritz Platten agreed terms with the German ambassador in Switzerland, under which the carriage enjoyed extraterritoriality and no one could enter it or check passports or luggage without his permission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thirty-two émigrés travelled, 19 of them Bolsheviks, and they reached Petrograd on the night of 3 April by the Russian calendar, 16 April by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nothing was literally sealed, and the name grew out of the rule that no one would board the carriage during the journey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The German government let Lenin pass because it hoped he would undermine Russia's ability to go on fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the Finland Station the Bolsheviks staged a triumphal welcome with several thousand workers and soldiers and a column of armoured cars, and Lenin was greeted by the Soviet's chairman Chkheidze in the former Tsar's Room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His opponents made much of the journey, and after the July Days a right-wing paper printed claims that Lenin and his associates were German spies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later research found that the émigrés had first tried to return through the Allied countries and that Lenin refused the go-between Parvus, although other Bolsheviks did business with him in Stockholm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> One recent assessment puts the German money Lenin received at less than 40,000 dollars, too little to have decided either the revolution or the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["V. I. Lenin, “How We Arrived,” report to the Executive Committee of the Petrograd Soviet, April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 27–29, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/04b.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 15, “The Bolsheviks and Lenin,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch15.htm. [Open access]","Benno Ennker, “Lenin, Vladimir Il’ich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10194. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “July Crisis 1917 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10173. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “The Tasks of the Proletariat in the Present Revolution,” <i>Pravda</i>, 7 April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 19–26, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/04.htm. [Open access]","Г. А. Папилин, «Политическое сдерживание следствия по делу о «немецких деньгах» большевиков: роль ВЦИК и Временного правительства», <i>Тульский научный вестник. Серия История. Языкознание</i>, no. 3 (27) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskoe-sderzhivanie-sledstviya-po-delu-o-nemetskih-dengah-bolshevikov-rol-vtsik-i-vremennogo-pravitelstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. А. Ермолаев, «Соболев Г. Л. Тайный союзник. Русская революция и Германия, 1914–1918. СПб.: Изд-во С.-Петербургского ун-та, 2009. 476 с. [review]», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 3. Философия</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2012-01-023-sobolev-g-l-taynyy-soyuznik-russkaya-revolyutsiya-i-germaniya-1914-1918-spb-izd-vo-s-peterburgskogo-un-ta-2009-476-s. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Lenin travel through an enemy country?","a":"Because the Allies would not let anti-war émigrés pass, and the telegrams the émigrés sent home were held up, apparently by the Provisional Government or its supporters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trotsky put the choice simply: either stay in Switzerland or travel through Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it called sealed when it was not?","a":"Because the agreement gave the carriage extraterritoriality, so that no one could enter it or inspect passports and luggage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trotsky wrote that this rule was the origin of the legend of the 'sealed' train.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany agree to the journey?","a":"Because it hoped Lenin's agitation would weaken Russia's ability to keep fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky later put it that Ludendorff meant to use Lenin and then crush him, while Lenin meant to ride in Ludendorff's carriage and repay the favour in his own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sealed train"},
    "ru-555": {"questions":["Published in <i>Pravda</i> as 'The Tasks of the Proletariat in the Present Revolution', the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called unacceptable by the paper's own editors the next day.","Called the raving of a lunatic by a Menshevik heckler, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nevertheless won over Lenin's party within weeks and kept it out of every coalition of 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>April Theses</b> were the ten short theses in which Lenin set out his programme on returning to Russia, read on 4 April 1917 by the Russian calendar and published in <i>Pravda</i> on 7 April as 'The Tasks of the Proletariat in the Present Revolution'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He read them twice, first to a meeting of Bolsheviks and then to a joint meeting of Bolsheviks and Mensheviks, the day after he reached Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first thesis refused any concession to 'revolutionary defencism', since under the new government of Lvov the war remained a predatory imperialist war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The theses demanded no support for the Provisional Government and a republic of soviets rather than a parliamentary republic, with the police, the army and the bureaucracy abolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also called for the confiscation of landed estates and the nationalisation of all land, a single national bank under the soviets' control, a new party name and a new International.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Lenin argued that Russia was passing from a first stage of revolution, which had given power to the bourgeoisie, to a second that must give it to the workers and the poorest peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even his own party was taken aback, and on 8 April the editors of <i>Pravda</i> called his general scheme unacceptable because it assumed that the bourgeois-democratic revolution was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Menshevik Bogdanov shouted that it was the raving of a lunatic, and the former Bolshevik Goldenberg said Lenin had planted the banner of civil war amid the revolutionary democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Lenin answered in his <i>Letters on Tactics</i> that anyone still repeating the old Bolshevik formula belonged in an archive of 'old Bolsheviks', since theory is grey but the tree of life is green.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His victory inside the party was complete within weeks, and the most lasting provision of the theses, no support for the Provisional Government, kept the Bolsheviks out of every coalition of 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["V. I. Lenin, “The Tasks of the Proletariat in the Present Revolution,” <i>Pravda</i>, 7 April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 19–26, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/04.htm. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “Letters on Tactics,” April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 42–54, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/x01.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 15, “The Bolsheviks and Lenin,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch15.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 16, “Re-arming the Party,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch16.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Benno Ennker, “Lenin, Vladimir Il’ich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10194. [Open access]","А. В. Горлов, «Об Апрельских тезисах В. И. Ленина», <i>Вестник экспертного совета</i>, no. 3 (38) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ob-aprelskih-tezisah-v-i-lenina. [in Russian] [Open access]","Р. А. Шумяков, «“Большевики” как “партия гражданской войны”: радикальные социалисты в пропаганде их противников (март — апрель 1917 г.)», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/bolsheviki-kak-partiya-grazhdanskoy-voyny-radikalnye-sotsialisty-v-propagande-ih-protivnikov-mart-aprel-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the theses shock Lenin's own party?","a":"Because the Bolsheviks had long held that Russia must first complete a bourgeois-democratic revolution, and Lenin now said that stage was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The editors of <i>Pravda</i> called his scheme unacceptable for exactly that reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin refuse to support the Provisional Government?","a":"Because he held that a government of capitalists would go on fighting a predatory imperialist war whatever it promised about annexations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Peace, he argued, could come only by overthrowing that government and replacing it with the power of the soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin call for patient explanation rather than a rising?","a":"Because the Bolsheviks were still a small minority in most soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The theses asked the party to explain the errors of the soviet majority until the workers learned from experience, which is why Lenin rejected the charge that he had raised the banner of civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"April Theses"},
    "ru-556": {"questions":["Soldiers of the Finland Regiment marched on the Mariinsky Palace demanding Milyukov's removal on 20 April 1917, at the height of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that brought socialists into the cabinet.","General Kornilov offered to crush the street demonstrations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by force, but Prince Lvov and Kerensky refused, and the Soviet declared the incident settled."],"abstract":"The <b>April Crisis</b> was the first political crisis of the Provisional Government, set off in April 1917 by a note on war aims from the foreign minister Pavel Milyukov and ended by his fall and the entry of socialists into the cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 18 April by the Russian calendar, 1 May by the Western one, Milyukov sent the Allies a note to accompany the government's declaration on war aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It promised that the nation wanted to carry the war to a decisive victory and that the government would observe its obligations to the Allies, which included the transfer of the Black Sea Straits to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To workers and soldiers who believed the government had renounced annexations, the note read as a return to the predatory war aims of the old regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 20 and 21 April, 3 and 4 May by the Western calendar, Petrograd was in turmoil, with crowds, meetings and processions in the streets day and night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The first to march was the Finland Regiment, brought out to the Mariinsky Palace, seat of the government, by a non-party mathematician named Linde, under banners demanding Milyukov's removal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> General Kornilov, commanding the Petrograd district, offered to put the demonstrations down by force, but according to Admiral Kolchak's later testimony Prince Lvov and Kerensky spoke against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clashes between supporters and opponents of the note left dead and wounded, and the Soviet's Executive Committee told soldiers to come out armed only on its own written order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government then explained that a decisive victory meant only the aims of its earlier declaration, and the Soviet declared itself satisfied and the incident settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crisis showed that the cabinet could not govern without the Soviet's leaders, and within two weeks Guchkov and Milyukov had resigned and a coalition with the socialists was formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 333–36, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Miliukov, Pavel Nikolaevich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10191. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 17, “The “April Days”,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch17.htm. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “Lessons of the Crisis,” <i>Pravda</i>, 23 April 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 24 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1964), 213–16, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/apr/22b.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Provisional Government,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10242. [Open access]","А. А. Смирнова, «Петроградский Совет и проблема вхождения социалистов во Временное правительство в апреле–мае 1917 г.», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petrogradskiy-sovet-i-problema-vhozhdeniya-sotsialistov-vo-vremennoe-pravitelstvo-v-aprele-mae-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a diplomatic note bring crowds into the streets?","a":"Because many workers and soldiers believed the government had given up annexations, and the note seemed to confirm the old war aims instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It promised to observe every obligation to the Allies, which included Russia's claim to the Black Sea Straits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why would Milyukov not give up the claim to the Straits?","a":"Because winning the Straits for Russia was, as he said himself in May, the main thread of his policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He boasted that while in office he had done nothing that gave the Allies the right to say Russia had renounced them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the crisis lead to a coalition government?","a":"Because only the Soviet's leaders could persuade the soldiers to leave the square and return to their barracks, which showed where real authority lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet's Executive Committee therefore agreed to let its socialist leaders join the government in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"April Crisis"},
    "ru-557": {"questions":["Kerensky's eloquence turned a vote of 23 to 22 against into one of 41 to 18 for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which he became minister of war in May 1917.","Just back from America, Trotsky condemned the entry of socialists into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of May 1917, in which Chernov took agriculture and Tsereteli posts and telegraphs."],"abstract":"The <b>first coalition government</b> was the cabinet of the Provisional Government formed on 5 May 1917 by the Russian calendar, 18 May by the Western one, in which socialist leaders of the Petrograd Soviet sat for the first time beside the liberals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prince Lvov stayed on as prime minister, and the socialists took six of the fifteen portfolios, a minority they had chosen for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kerensky moved from justice to the ministry of war, the Socialist Revolutionary leader Viktor Chernov became minister of agriculture, and the Mensheviks Irakli Tsereteli and Matvei Skobelev took posts and telegraphs and labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Milyukov left the foreign ministry, which passed to the non-party industrialist Mikhail Tereshchenko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coalition adopted the Soviet's policy of revolutionary defencism, seeking a general peace without annexations or indemnities while defending the country and the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It grew out of the April Crisis, after which Prince Lvov wrote to the Soviet's chairman Chkheidze asking its parties to share in the responsible work of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet's Executive Committee first rejected the idea on 29 April by 23 votes to 22, but the war minister Alexander Guchkov announced his resignation the next day and changed the situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 1 May, after a speech by Kerensky that deeply impressed the committee, it voted 41 to 18 for coalition, with only the Bolsheviks and the internationalists against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 5 May the Petrograd Soviet confirmed the socialist ministers against about 100 Bolshevik votes, while Trotsky, back from America the day before, condemned their entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By joining the cabinet the socialist parties took responsibility for its policy, and the coalition broke up in July, when the Kadet ministers resigned over Ukraine and Prince Lvov over agrarian policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 347–58, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 18, “The First Coalition,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch18.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Provisional Government,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10242. [Open access]","А. А. Смирнова, «Петроградский Совет и проблема вхождения социалистов во Временное правительство в апреле–мае 1917 г.», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/petrogradskiy-sovet-i-problema-vhozhdeniya-sotsialistov-vo-vremennoe-pravitelstvo-v-aprele-mae-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. А. Хвалин, «Отставка А. И. Гучкова в сатире и карикатурах в мае 1917 года», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 4 (55) (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/otstavka-a-i-guchkova-v-satire-i-karikaturah-v-mae-1917-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Волобуев, «1917 год в России: хроника революционного процесса», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/1917-god-v-rossii-hronika-revolyutsionnogo-protsessa. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet's leaders agree to join the government?","a":"Because the April Crisis showed that the cabinet could not govern alone, and Kerensky warned the Executive Committee that the government was in an impossibly difficult situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guchkov's public resignation on 30 April then left the war ministry empty and tipped the balance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the socialists take only a minority of the posts?","a":"Because they wanted to share responsibility without taking charge, and so accepted six portfolios out of fifteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tsereteli even warned the liberals that if they proved stubborn the socialists would leave the cabinet with a loud bang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks vote against the coalition?","a":"Because they wanted the soviets to take all power rather than share it with the liberals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the decisive vote of the Executive Committee they and the internationalists were the only groups against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-558": {"questions":["When Tsereteli told the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in June 1917 that no party in Russia was ready to take power alone, Lenin interrupted to say that such a party existed.","Besides forbidding all demonstrations for three days to stop a Bolshevik march planned for 10 June, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> created a permanent Central Executive Committee with Chkheidze as chairman."],"abstract":"The <b>First All-Russian Congress of Soviets</b> of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies met in Petrograd from 3 to 24 June 1917 by the Russian calendar, 16 June to 7 July by the Western one, as the first national gathering of the soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Petrograd Soviet called it to answer the charge that a city council could not speak for the whole country, and soviets and army organisations sent delegates in proportion to their size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of its 1,090 delegates, 882 with full votes, 285 were Socialist Revolutionaries and 248 Mensheviks, against only 105 Bolsheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The moderate socialists' resolution in support of the coalition government passed by 543 votes to 126.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congress created a permanent All-Russian Central Executive Committee of the soviets to act in its name between congresses, with Nikolai Chkheidze as its chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its most remembered moment came on 4 June, when the minister Irakli Tsereteli said that no party in Russia would ask to be handed power and Lenin interrupted to say that one did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his own speech Lenin argued that the soviets were a new type of government, found elsewhere only at moments of great revolutionary upsurge such as France in 1792 and 1871 or Russia in 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fearing an armed Bolshevik demonstration planned for 10 June, the congress forbade all demonstrations for three days, and the Bolsheviks called theirs off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its own demonstration of 18 June, meant as a show of unity behind the government, was dominated by Bolshevik slogans calling for all power to the soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The congress also left it to the military authorities to decide whether to attack, and the offensive at the front opened on the day of that demonstration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 359–63, 426, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “First All-Russia Congress of Soviets of Workers’ and Soldiers’ Deputies,” speeches of June 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 25 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1977), 15–42, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/jul/07.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 22, “The Soviet Congress and the June Demonstration,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch22.htm. [Open access]","О. В. Волобуев, «1917 год в России: хроника революционного процесса», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/1917-god-v-rossii-hronika-revolyutsionnogo-protsessa. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. А. Папилин, «Борис Осипович Богданов – «архитектор» модели советской власти», <i>Вестник Пермского университета. Серия: История</i>, no. 4 (67) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boris-osipovich-bogdanov-arhitektor-modeli-sovetskoy-vlasti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “July Crisis 1917 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10173. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Petrograd Soviet call a national congress?","a":"Because supporters of the Provisional Government said that Petrograd was not Russia and that the city's soviet had no right to speak for anyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A congress of delegates from soviets and army organisations all over the country answered that charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin's interruption become famous?","a":"Because Tsereteli had just told the congress that no party in Russia would say 'hand the power over to us', and Lenin called out that one did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his speech he insisted that there could be either a bourgeois government or a government of the soviets, and nothing in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the congress's own demonstration go wrong for its leaders?","a":"Because the march of 18 June was meant as a show of unity behind the government, yet many in the crowd carried Bolshevik slogans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Banners called for all power to the soviets and the removal of the ten capitalist ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-559": {"questions":["Kornilov's 8th Army took Halicz and Kalusz in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of July 1917, but an enemy counter-attack near Zloczow turned the advance into a rout that lost Tarnopol.","Shock units of volunteers led the attack in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1917, whose collapse brought back the death penalty at the front and raised General Kornilov to supreme command."],"abstract":"The <b>Kerensky offensive</b> was the last great Russian attack of the First World War, launched in Galicia on 18 June 1917 by the Russian calendar, 1 July by the Western one, and named after the war minister who had urged the troops forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had been planned at Allied conferences since late 1916, and the coalition hoped that success would strengthen Russia's hand with the Allies and halt the breakdown of order in the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kerensky's order to the army and fleet declared that the country was in danger, and he toured the front in person, though he failed to win over some units such as the 2nd Guard Infantry Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The main blow fell on a front of about 45 kilometres (28 miles) held by the 11th and 7th Armies, aiming to cross the Zlota Lipa and then wheel north-west on Lemberg, the city now called Lviv.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Officers formed volunteer shock units to lead the attack and set an example to the rest of the infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The first days went well, and General Kornilov's 8th Army broke the Austrian front west of Stanislau and went on to take Halicz and Kalusz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then the enemy counter-attacked, and on 19 July by the Western calendar broke through the 11th Army east of Zloczow after a regiment withdrew without orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The retreat spread to the 7th and 8th Armies along a front of about 240 kilometres (150 miles), Tarnopol was lost, and fleeing soldiers pillaged towns and killed civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A chronicle of 1917 counts 6,905 Russian dead, 36,240 wounded, 5,653 missing and 3,860 deserters between 18 June and 6 July by the Russian calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The failure brought back the death penalty at the front on 12 July and opened the way to Kornilov's rise, and it showed that the old army could no longer fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Knox, <i>With the Russian Army, 1914–1917</i>, vol. 2 (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 627, 639–40, 665–66, https://archive.org/details/withrussianarmy02alfr. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 425–27, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Kerenskii, Aleksandr Fedorovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10419. [Open access]","John W. Steinberg, “Warfare 1914-1918 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10752. [Open access]","О. В. Волобуев, «1917 год в России: хроника революционного процесса», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/1917-god-v-rossii-hronika-revolyutsionnogo-protsessa. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Лаптев, «Офицеры ударных частей русской армии во время подготовки и проведения летнего наступления 1917 г.», <i>История: факты и символы</i>, no. 2 (23) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ofitsery-udarnyh-chastey-russkoy-armii-vo-vremya-podgotovki-i-provedeniya-letnego-nastupleniya-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Восстановление в России смертной казни на фронте летом 1917 г.», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 1 (12) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vosstanovlenie-v-rossii-smertnoy-kazni-na-fronte-letom-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a revolutionary government order an attack?","a":"Because it believed success would strengthen Russia's diplomatic hand, secure Allied credits and restore order in the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allies, for their part, met every Russian plea to revise the war aims with the demand that Russia fight first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the advance turn into a rout so quickly?","a":"Because many units debated whether to obey orders, and the official communiqué blamed total insubordination and Bolshevik propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reliable troops had been spent in the first attacks, the weather worsened and German reinforcements arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the offensive matter for politics in Petrograd?","a":"Because its failure discredited the government and helped provoke the July Days on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the right it made Kornilov's name, and the death penalty for soldiers at the front was restored on 12 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kerensky offensive"},
    "ru-560": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1917 collapsed, <i>Pravda</i> was closed, Lenin fled to Finland and Trotsky gave himself up, while Kerensky became prime minister.","Demonstrators briefly seized the minister Viktor Chernov during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Petrograd, before claims that Lenin was a German agent helped turn opinion against the Bolsheviks."],"abstract":"The <b>July Days</b> were the armed demonstrations of soldiers, sailors and workers in Petrograd on 3 and 4 July 1917 by the Russian calendar, 16 and 17 July by the Western one, demanding that the soviets take power from the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They began in the First Machine Gun Regiment, which had been ordered to send men and guns to the front after the offensive and resolved on 3 July to take to the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By evening tens of thousands of workers, above all from the Vyborg district and the Putilov works, were marching with soldiers to the Tauride Palace, and the Kronstadt sailors joined them the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the balcony of the Kshesinsky palace Lenin and Sverdlov cheered on the passing columns, though the Bolshevik Lunacharsky admitted that the party knew no way forward once the demand was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the Tauride Palace the crowd briefly seized the Socialist Revolutionary minister Viktor Chernov, and shooting in the streets left perhaps 400 dead or wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet's leaders refused to take power, and as the demonstrators dispersed, regiments that had stayed neutral came out to defend the Soviet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the causes of the collapse was the release of material claiming that Lenin worked for the German General Staff, printed by a right-wing paper on 5 July under a headline calling Lenin and Ganetsky spies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Historians still disagree whether the demonstrations were a peaceful protest or a failed rising, and Lunacharsky himself wrote that his party went down to temporary defeat with its eyes open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bolshevik papers were banned at the front, <i>Pravda</i> was closed, Lenin fled to Finland and Trotsky gave himself up to the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 7 July Kerensky replaced Prince Lvov as prime minister, and for two months the left was in retreat while the right gathered strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “July Crisis 1917 (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10173. [Open access]","Frank Alfred Golder, ed., <i>Documents of Russian History, 1914–1917</i>, trans. Emanuel Aronsberg (New York: Century, 1927), 450–52, https://archive.org/details/documentsofrussi027937mbp. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 2, ch. 25, “The July Days: Culmination and Rout,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch25.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","А. А. Антонов-Овсеенко, «Реабилитация военной цензуры и другие итоги июльского выступления 1917 г. в Петрограде», <i>Вестник Российского университета дружбы народов. Серия: Литературоведение, журналистика</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/reabilitatsiya-voennoy-tsenzury-i-drugie-itogi-iyulskogo-vystupleniya-1917-g-v-petrograde. [in Russian] [Open access]","Г. А. Папилин, «Политическое сдерживание следствия по делу о «немецких деньгах» большевиков: роль ВЦИК и Временного правительства», <i>Тульский научный вестник. Серия История. Языкознание</i>, no. 3 (27) (2026), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskoe-sderzhivanie-sledstviya-po-delu-o-nemetskih-dengah-bolshevikov-rol-vtsik-i-vremennogo-pravitelstva. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Волобуев, «1917 год в России: хроника революционного процесса», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/1917-god-v-rossii-hronika-revolyutsionnogo-protsessa. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the machine-gunners take to the streets?","a":"Because they were ordered to send men and guns to the front, in breach of agreements that the revolutionary garrison would stay in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anarchist-Communists in the regiment also pressed openly for the government's overthrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks not seize power in July?","a":"Because Lenin and the Central Committee doubted their support and had already called off an armed demonstration in June as premature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lunacharsky later wrote that an attempt at power was extremely risky, since the provinces, the front and above all Moscow were not yet ripe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the July Days end with a crackdown on the press?","a":"Because the government blamed the Bolsheviks and moved against their newspapers, banning them at the front on 8 July and then closing <i>Pravda</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measures aimed first at the Bolshevik press soon became a wider retreat from the freedom of the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"July Days"},
    "ru-561": {"questions":["Planned in seven chapters, Lenin's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> never got its seventh, on 1905 and 1917, because, as his postscript put it, the eve of the October revolution interrupted him.","Lenin crossed into Finland disguised as a locomotive fireman in 1917 partly so that he could write <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Helsingfors, building it on a blue notebook of Marx and Engels quotations."],"abstract":"<i>The <b>State and Revolution</b></i> is the pamphlet on the Marxist theory of the state that Vladimir Lenin wrote in August and September 1917, while he was hiding from the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a blue notebook of quotations from Marx and Engels, headed 'Marxism on the State', that he had compiled in Zurich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its central claim, drawn from Marx on 1848 and the Paris Commune of 1871, is that a workers' revolution must destroy the old state machine rather than take it over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In its place Lenin set the model of the Commune, a state run by the armed workers themselves that would gradually wither away once society as a whole had taken over its tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He planned seven chapters but never wrote the seventh, on the Russian revolutions of 1905 and 1917, and the book was first printed in 1918, under his own name rather than a pseudonym meant to save it from confiscation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Lenin went into hiding after the July Days of 1917, when the Bolsheviks were accused of acting on German instructions and their newspapers were shut down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With Grigory Zinoviev he first lay up at Razliv near Sestroretsk, helped by the Bolshevik factory worker Yemelyanov, in a hut by a lake, sheltering from the rain in a haystack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As autumn came the hut became impossible, and Krupskaya recalled that he crossed into Finland in a wig, with a false passport, as the fireman of a locomotive, because he wanted to write the book there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From Helsingfors, the city now called Helsinki, he poured out letters urging his party to seize power, and he emerged to attend the Central Committee meeting of 10 October that put an armed rising on the agenda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a postscript dated 30 November 1917 he wrote that it was more pleasant and useful to go through the experience of revolution than to write about it, while the hut at Razliv later became a museum and a carefully tended Soviet legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, <i>The State and Revolution</i>, with the editors’ notes, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 25 (Moscow: Progress Publishers), 381–492, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/staterev/index.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Postscript to the First Edition,” dated Petrograd, 30 November 1917, in <i>The State and Revolution</i>, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/staterev/postscpt.htm. [Open access]","Nadezhda Krupskaya, <i>Reminiscences of Lenin</i>, “Underground Again,” trans. Bernard Isaacs (New York: International Publishers, 1970), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/krupskaya/works/rol/rol23.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 2, ch. 27, “The Month of the Great Slander,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch27.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Д. А. Шелест, ««ГОСУДАРСТВО И РЕВОЛЮЦИЯ» В. И. ЛЕНИНА КАК ДОКТРИНАЛЬНОЕ ОБОСНОВАНИЕ РЕВОЛЮЦИОННОГО ПЕРЕУСТРОЙСТВА ОБЩЕСТВА», <i>Медицина. Социология. Философия. Прикладные исследования</i>, no. 5 (2021): 89–93, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/gosudarstvo-i-revolyutsiya-v-i-lenina-kak-doktrinalnoe-obosnovanie-revolyutsionnogo-pereustroystva-obschestva. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. С. Клименко, «ИСТОРИКО-КУЛЬТУРНЫЙ МУЗЕЙНЫЙ КОМПЛЕКС В РАЗЛИВЕ: ОТ МИФОЛОГИЗАЦИИ К МУЗЕЕФИКАЦИИ», <i>Кунсткамера</i>, no. 2 (8) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-kulturnyy-muzeynyy-kompleks-v-razlive-ot-mifologizatsii-k-muzeefikatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Lenin plan to publish the book under a false name?","a":"Because he was a wanted man in the autumn of 1917, and the editors of his works explain that the Provisional Government would have confiscated the book for his name alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the time it was printed in 1918 the Bolsheviks were in power and the pseudonym was no longer needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin leave the hut at Razliv for Finland?","a":"Because the shanty could not be lived in once autumn came, and in Finland it was also easier to follow the newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Krupskaya adds that he wanted to write the book there, using the mass of notes he had collected and thought out in every detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the hut at Razliv treated with caution today?","a":"Because Soviet museums made it the setting of a legend about a leader who lived simply among Finnish haymakers, and early reconstructions of the hut did not match the original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Even the haystack beside it came and went in the displays until a model was built in 1955 with advice from Yemelyanov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The State and Revolution"},
    "ru-562": {"questions":["A teleprinter exchange in which Kerensky asked the commander-in-chief to confirm a message brought by Lvov set off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which Kerensky denounced the general as a traitor.","The chief winners of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the Bolsheviks, whose part in rallying the capital against the general's march in August 1917 left Kerensky's government discredited and them stronger than ever."],"abstract":"The <b>Kornilov affair</b> was the failed attempt by General Lavr Kornilov, commander-in-chief of the Russian army, to send troops into Petrograd at the end of August 1917 and impose military order on the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officers, industrialists and Kadet politicians alarmed by the collapse of discipline at the front and by the July Days had come to see him as a saviour, and many of them wanted an outright dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early in August he moved the Savage Division and the Third Cavalry Corps towards Petrograd under the guise of reserves, and he put General Krymov at the head of the whole operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 25 August, 7 September by the Western calendar, with the Germans in Riga, he ordered the divisions on, and a go-between named Lvov carried to Kerensky his demand that all military and civil authority pass to the commander-in-chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kerensky checked the message with Kornilov over the Hughes teleprinter on 26 August, then denounced him as a traitor and ordered him to resign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The general refused, and the Petrograd Soviet answered by mobilising its own forces, as railwaymen held up the troop trains and workers in the capital formed armed detachments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Agitators met the advancing troops and talked them out of fighting, so that by 31 August Krymov had shot himself and Kornilov and his associates were under arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kornilov and his fellow generals were held at Bykhov in a girls' school turned into a prison, guarded inside by Tekke horsemen loyal to him, until they slipped away to the Don after the October Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians still dispute whether the affair was a planned coup, a trap Kerensky laid for a simple-minded general, or a botched attempt by the two men to strengthen the government together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its outcome is not in doubt, since it left Kerensky hated by the officers and distrusted on the left, and raised the Bolsheviks, who had led the resistance, to an authority they had never had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Kornilov, Lavr Georgievich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/kornilov-lavr-georgievich/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “Kornilov Affair,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/kornilov-affair/. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 2, ch. 32, “Kornilov’s Insurrection,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch32.htm. [Open access]","Alexander Kerensky, <i>The Prelude to Bolshevism: The Kornilov Rising</i> (New York: Dodd, Mead, 1919), https://archive.org/details/preludetobolshev008537mbp. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","А. Н. Якушев, «Корниловский мятеж 1917 г.: причины и политико-правовые последствия», <i>Закон и право</i>, no. 6 (2026): 34–44, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kornilovskiy-myatezh-1917-g-prichiny-i-politiko-pravovye-posledstviya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Шевцов, «\"Быховское сидение\" генерала Корнилова», <i>Вестник МГИМО Университета</i>, no. 6 (57) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byhovskoe-sidenie-generala-kornilova. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many on the right put their hopes in Kornilov?","a":"Because by August 1917 discipline at the front had collapsed, prices were soaring and peasants were seizing land, and many liberals and conservatives concluded that only an authoritarian ruler could restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kornilov's escape from captivity and his harsh measures against fleeing soldiers had given him the reputation of a strongman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the troops never reach Petrograd?","a":"Because the Petrograd Soviet mobilised railwaymen to stop the trains and sent agitators who easily persuaded the soldiers to give up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bolshevik riot they had been sent to suppress never happened, so they had nothing to march against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Kerensky's part in the affair still argued over?","a":"Because he had wanted to bring the Soviet to heel and turned on Kornilov only after the teleprinter exchange, and much of the evidence comes from men trying to clear themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kerensky's own book prints the teleprinter conversation as part of his defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kornilov affair"},
    "ru-563": {"questions":["Calling himself the son of a peasant Cossack, the supreme commander <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered cavalry towards Petrograd in August 1917 and demanded that all authority pass into his hands.","Leaving Bykhov for the Don after the October Revolution, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped found the Volunteer Army and was killed outside Ekaterinodar in April 1918 after the Ice March."],"abstract":"<b>Lavr Kornilov</b> was the Russian general whose failed march on Petrograd in August 1917 became known as the Kornilov affair, and who then led the first White army of the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Siberian Cossack by birth, he entered the regular army rather than the Cossack forces, and he presented himself to the country as the son of a peasant Cossack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the spring of 1915 most of his 48th Division was destroyed in the fighting around Przemyśl and he was taken prisoner, and his escape in 1916 made him a hero to a press hungry for good news.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the February Revolution he commanded the Petrograd Military District, and on 8 March 1917 he personally arrested the former empress and the emperor's children at Tsarskoye Selo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His Eighth Army made the most progress in the June offensive, and on 18 July he was appointed supreme commander, insisting that he answered only to his conscience and the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He demanded the death penalty in the rear as well as at the front and martial law on the railways and in defence factories, and in late August he ordered troops towards the capital and called for all authority to be placed in his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agitators talked his troops out of fighting, and Kornilov was arrested and held with his fellow generals at Bykhov, guarded inside by the Tekke horsemen who were devoted to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After the October Revolution he left Bykhov for the Don, where with General Alekseev he built the Volunteer Army, the first White force in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His volunteers revered him, but he was a harsh commander who told recruits to take no prisoners, and the Ice March he led into the Kuban saw atrocities against peasants and reprisals for Red violence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was killed on 13 April 1918 outside Ekaterinodar, the city now called Krasnodar, before the decisive assault on it, and Anton Denikin succeeded him in command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “Kornilov, Lavr Georgievich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/kornilov-lavr-georgievich/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “Kornilov Affair,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/kornilov-affair/. [Open access]","Anton Denikin, <i>The Russian Turmoil: Memoirs, Military, Social, and Political</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1922), https://archive.org/details/russianturmoilme00deniuoft. [Open access]","Г. Н. Чарыгина, «Политические фигуры 1917 года: генерал лавр корнилов», <i>Социально-политические науки</i>, no. 2 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskie-figury-1917-goda-general-lavr-kornilov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Пученков, «Генералы М. В. Алексеев, Л. Г. Корнилов, А. М. Каледин и начало белого движения на Юге России», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/generaly-m-v-alekseev-l-g-kornilov-a-m-kaledin-i-nachalo-belogo-dvizheniya-na-yuge-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. В. Шевцов, «\"Быховское сидение\" генерала Корнилова», <i>Вестник МГИМО Университета</i>, no. 6 (57) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/byhovskoe-sidenie-generala-kornilova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kornilov rise so fast in 1917?","a":"Because his escape from captivity had made him a hero, and his modest background and his arrest of the empress gave him a democratic pedigree after February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Advocates of order, from the former terrorist Savinkov to Kadets and industrialists, also liked his hard line on fleeing soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was General Alekseev uneasy about Kornilov?","a":"Because Alekseev held against him the rout and capture of the 48th Division in 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the Don the two quarrelled constantly over who controlled what, and Kornilov never forgave Alekseev for his part in his arrest at Mogilev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his volunteers revere him?","a":"Because his war record, his escape from captivity, his stand in August 1917 and his departure from Bykhov had given him a legendary reputation as a soldier and patriot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Volunteer Army, and its infantry above all, idolised him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lavr Kornilov"},
    "ru-564": {"questions":["Lenin's letter claiming that the Bolsheviks could and must take power rested on their new majorities in the Petrograd Soviet and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the councils of both capitals.","John Reed found Red Guards at the headquarters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the former governor-general's palace after a week of fighting for the Kremlin in late October 1917."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Soviet</b> was the council of deputies of Russia's second city, formed in the February Revolution of 1917, and in September of that year it became the second of the great soviets to pass into Bolshevik hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Through the spring and summer it followed the moderate socialists who backed the coalition with the liberals, and its presidium was headed by Khinchuk, one of the men the Bolsheviks called the Compromisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kornilov affair changed the mood, and on 5 September 1917, 18 September by the Western calendar, the soviet voted by 355 to 254 for a Bolshevik resolution calling the Provisional Government a weapon of counter-revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The presidium resigned, and the Bolshevik Nogin, a professional revolutionary of working-class origin who had worked in the soviet since February, was later elected its chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The vote came days after the Petrograd Soviet had taken the same turn on 31 August, so that the soviets of both capitals now had Bolshevik majorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From hiding Lenin seized on those majorities, writing to the Central Committee in mid-September that the Bolsheviks, having won the soviets of both capitals, could and must take state power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the rising came in Petrograd on 25 October Moscow followed, but there the fighting between the soviet's forces and the officer cadets lasted almost a week, from 27 October to 2 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kremlin changed hands, its garrison surrendering to the cadets on the morning of 28 October before the soviet's side won the city in the street fighting that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American journalist John Reed found the soviet's headquarters in the former governor-general's palace on Skobelev Square, with Red Guards at the door and women inside sewing banners for the funeral of the revolutionary dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its opponents remembered those days as Moscow's bloody week, and victory there gave the new Soviet government both of Russia's capitals within a week of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 2, ch. 35, “The Rising Tide,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch35.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “The Bolsheviks Must Assume Power,” letter of 12–14 September 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 19–21, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/sep/14.htm. [Open access]","Е. В. Панин, «Дискуссии о создании первого советского правительства на II Всероссийском съезде Советов», <i>Известия Московского государственного технического университета МАМИ</i> 5, no. 3 (21) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussii-o-sozdanii-pervogo-sovetskogo-pravitelstva-na-ii-vserossiyskom-sezde-sovetov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Э. М. Щагин, «Вновь о малоизвестных фактах из истории большевистского вооруженного восстания в Петрограде и Москве осенью 1917 г», <i>Преподаватель ХХI век</i> 2, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnov-o-maloizvestnyh-faktah-iz-istorii-bolshevistskogo-vooruzhennogo-vosstaniya-v-petrograde-i-moskve-osenyu-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Moscow Soviet turn to the Bolsheviks in September 1917?","a":"Because the Kornilov affair frightened the moderate parties' own supporters, and voters switched rapidly from the Mensheviks and Socialist Revolutionaries to the Bolsheviks in August and September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vote of 5 September condemned both the Provisional Government and the coalition policy of the soviets' leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fighting in Moscow last so much longer than in Petrograd?","a":"Because the cadets and their allies drove the soviet's men from the telephone and telegraph, cut off the Kremlin garrison and forced it to surrender, and the struggle went on for almost a week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reed reported the cadets holding the centre of the city while the soviet's troops mustered all around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin attach such weight to the two capitals?","a":"Because he argued that the active majority of revolutionary elements in the two chief cities was large enough to carry the people with it and overcome the opponent's resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He took the new majorities in their soviets as proof that the majority of the people were on the Bolsheviks' side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mossoviet"},
    "ru-565": {"questions":["A statute adopted by the Vyborg district soviet on 28 April 1917 organised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in tens and hundreds under elected leaders, and was later copied as far away as Chelyabinsk.","About 6,000 strong by the end of June 1917, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was driven underground after the July Days but revived when workers armed themselves against Kornilov."],"abstract":"The <b>Petrograd Red Guard</b> was the armed militia of the capital's factory workers in 1917, the volunteer force that the Petrograd Soviet used alongside the garrison to take power in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armed workers' bands had first appeared in the revolution of 1905 and sprang up again in March 1917, when a force was needed to keep order in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 28 April the soviet of the Vyborg district adopted a statute that defined the Red Guard as the armed force of the revolution and opened it to any worker belonging to a socialist party or a trade union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its men were organised in tens and hundreds under elected leaders, ten hundreds making a battalion, and by the end of June the city had about 6,000 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No law of the Provisional Government ever recognised it and the government tried to disarm it, so that after the July Days it was driven underground with only a few thousand rifles left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Kornilov affair revived it, as workers formed armed detachments to defend the capital, and the Vyborg statute became a model for Red Guards as far away as Chelyabinsk in the Urals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In October the Petrograd Soviet set up a special department of the Red Guard, and the regiments of the garrison, once suspicious of the armed workers, now demanded that they be armed and stood beside them under the Military Revolutionary Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 24 October Red Guards and soldiers under the committee's command began to occupy the key points of the city, and the next night Red Guards stormed the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> John Reed, who found them untrained and undisciplined but full of revolutionary zeal, watched them march along the Nevsky after the rising, very young and without music.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such units proved quite inadequate against real armies, and in January 1918 the Soviet government decreed a Workers' and Peasants' Red Army of volunteers to defend the new regime instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]","М. Ю. Гутман, «Правовые основы организации и деятельности милиции Временного правительства, рабочей милиции и Красной гвардии Петрограда (март-октябрь 1917 г. )», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета МВД России</i>, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-osnovy-organizatsii-i-deyatelnosti-militsii-vremennogo-pravitelstva-rabochey-militsii-i-krasnoy-gvardii-petrograda-mart. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 41, “The Military-Revolutionary Committee,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch41.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Kornilov, Lavr Georgievich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/kornilov-lavr-georgievich/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “State-Building: the Red Army,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/red-guard-into-army/. [Open access]","А. П. Абрамовский and Е. Г. Морозов, «Особенности формирования Красной гвардии в 1917-1918 годах», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i> 1, no. 2 (1992), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-formirovaniya-krasnoy-gvardii-v-1917-1918-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the factory workers arm themselves in March 1917?","a":"Because a force was needed to keep order in the city in the days of the February Revolution, and workers' detachments formed in the factory districts to do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reed notes that the Provisional Government's efforts to disarm them were more or less unsuccessful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Red Guard and the garrison end up side by side?","a":"Because by October the regiments' suspicion of the armed workers had gone, and almost all their resolutions demanded that the workers be armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both came under the Military Revolutionary Committee, which directed them in the rising of 24 and 25 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a Red Army created in 1918?","a":"Because the rag-tag Red Guard units and the soldiers who had gone over to the Bolsheviks were quite inadequate to defend the new government against external foes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sovnarkom therefore decreed the formation of a Worker-Peasant Red Army of volunteers in January 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-566": {"questions":["Lenin's proclamation of ten in the morning on 25 October 1917 announced that state power had passed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Petrograd Soviet, which headed the proletariat and the garrison.","Dissolved on 5 December 1917, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had seen a commission against counter-revolution proposed at one of its November sessions, a forerunner of the Cheka."],"abstract":"The <b>Military Revolutionary Committee</b> was the body set up by the Petrograd Soviet in October 1917 that directed the armed seizure of power in the capital and for some weeks afterwards acted as the new regime's executive arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a proposal of 9 October for a committee of revolutionary defence, as the government tried to send the capital's revolutionary regiments to the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The soviet's executive committee formally created it at a closed session on 12 October, and a full meeting of the soviet confirmed it on 16 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To show that it was a soviet and not a party organ, a young Left Socialist Revolutionary named Lazimir was put at its head, though its leading workers were Bolsheviks under Trotsky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first step was to place commissars in the units of the garrison and in arsenals and stores, so that the soviet gained organised control of troops it had already won over politically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Kerensky tried to close the Bolshevik press on 24 October, the committee ordered its soldiers and Red Guards to take over the city's strong points, usually by persuasion rather than force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At ten in the morning of 25 October, 7 November by the Western calendar, it issued Lenin's proclamation that the Provisional Government had been deposed and that state power had passed to the Military Revolutionary Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its forces took the Winter Palace that night and arrested the ministers, and in the weeks that followed its office in the Smolny Institute handed out passes and permits as though it were itself the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Trotsky called it the general staff of the insurrection, but it was also a seedbed of the new state's security apparatus, since a commission against counter-revolution was first proposed at one of its November sessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was dissolved on 5 December 1917, 18 December by the Western calendar, and its struggle against counter-revolution and sabotage passed to the Cheka, created that same month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 41, “The Military-Revolutionary Committee,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch41.htm. [Open access]","И. А. Концевой, «РОЛЬ ЛЕВЫХ ЭСЕРОВ В ОРГАНИЗАЦИИ И ДЕЯТЕЛЬНОСТИ ПЕТРОГРАДСКОГО ВРК», <i>ХХ век и Россия: общество, реформы, революции</i>, no. 8 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-levyh-eserov-v-organizatsii-i-deyatelnosti-petrogradskogo-vrk. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “To the Citizens of Russia!,” 25 October 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 236, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25.htm. [Open access]","А. С. Соколов, «От Петроградского военно-революционного комитетак всероссийской чрезвычайной комиссии», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-petrogradskogo-voenno-revolyutsionnogo-komitetak-vserossiyskoy-chrezvychaynoy-komissii. [in Russian] [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a Left Socialist Revolutionary put at its head?","a":"Because the Bolsheviks wanted the committee to look like an institution of the soviet rather than of their party, partly as camouflage for its real purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lazimir had helped draft its plan and was the Left Socialist Revolutionary closest to the Bolsheviks, and the election tied him firmly to their side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the committee take the city with so little fighting?","a":"Because it had already won the garrison politically and placed its own commissars in the units, so most strong points were taken from government troops by persuasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government's attempt to shut the Bolshevik printing press on 24 October gave it the signal to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee not last long after October?","a":"Because once a Soviet government existed its tasks passed to new bodies, and the committee ceased to exist on 5 December 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its fight against sabotage and counter-revolution was taken over by a special commission proposed by Dzerzhinsky, the Cheka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Military Revolutionary Committee"},
    "ru-567": {"questions":["Telling the delegates who walked out of the Second Congress of Soviets to go to the rubbish heap of history, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the first Soviet commissar for foreign affairs.","Chairman of the Saint Petersburg soviet in 1905 and detained at Halifax on his way home in 1917, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the Red Army as commissar for war from 1918 to 1925."],"abstract":"<b>Leon Trotsky</b>, born Lev Bronstein, was the Marxist revolutionary who led the Petrograd Soviet in the autumn of 1917 and directed the rising that brought the Bolsheviks to power, before founding the Red Army and losing the struggle to succeed Lenin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Born in 1879, he had already been chairman of the soviet of workers' deputies in Saint Petersburg during the revolution of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The war found him in exile, writing against it in the émigré press, and after his expulsion from France he passed through Spain to New York, where the February Revolution called him home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was detained at Halifax in Canada on the way, and he reached Petrograd only on 4 May 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Long a critic of Lenin's methods, he now found him a ready ally, since both demanded that all power pass to the soviets and neither was held back by old factional loyalties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After the July Days he gave himself up to the authorities, and he sat in the Kresty prison during the Kornilov affair until the government, needing the left, began to release the Bolsheviks it had arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By October he was president of the Petrograd Soviet, now with a Bolshevik majority, and he steered its Military Revolutionary Committee, which he later called the general staff of the insurrection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Menshevik and Socialist Revolutionary delegates walked out of the Second Congress of Soviets in protest at the rising, he told them to join the rubbish heap of history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The congress made him people's commissar for foreign affairs in the first Soviet government, and from March 1918 to 1925 he was commissar for the army and navy, building the Red Army from volunteers, conscripts and former tsarist officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After Lenin's death he was crushed politically by Stalin and driven into exile, and in 1940 one of Stalin's agents murdered him in his study in Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ian D. Thatcher, “Trotsky, Leon,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/trotsky-leon/. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>My Life: An Attempt at an Autobiography</i>, ch. 26, “From July to October” (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1930), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/mylife/ch26.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 41, “The Military-Revolutionary Committee,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch41.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","В. А. Рачковский and Р. А. Шумяков, «Возвращение Л. Д. Троцкого в Россию в зеркале социалистической печати 1917 года», <i>Russian Colonial Studies</i>, no. 4 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozvraschenie-l-d-trotskogo-v-rossiyu-v-zerkale-sotsialisticheskoy-pechati-1917-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “State-Building: the Red Army,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/red-guard-into-army/. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Decision to Form the Workers’ and Peasants’ Government,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October 1917, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26e.htm. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Trotsky give himself up after the July Days?","a":"Because he argued that the authorities would not be strong enough to hold him for long, and the Kornilov affair proved him right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Needing the left to defend the capital, the government began releasing the Bolsheviks it had imprisoned, and he went straight from the Kresty to the committee for the defence of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Trotsky and Lenin work together so easily in 1917?","a":"Because both demanded that all power pass to the soviets and wanted to carry the revolution forward, and neither was held back by old factional loyalties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their partnership proved particularly effective as Russian political, economic and social life disintegrated through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Trotsky's arrival in Petrograd delayed?","a":"Because he and the other émigrés sailing with him from New York were detained at the Canadian port of Halifax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The socialist press of Petrograd, the Bolshevik papers above all, protested and presented him as a fighter for freedom and the former chairman of the soviet of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Leon Trotsky"},
    "ru-568": {"questions":["Celebrated for seventy years as a sacred founding act, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called by its enemies a conspiratorial coup that cost Russia its chance of democracy.","Swift in Petrograd but bloody in Moscow, where fighting lasted almost a week, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when Kerensky tried to shut down the Bolshevik printing presses."],"abstract":"The <b>October Revolution</b> was the seizure of power in Petrograd by the Bolsheviks in the name of the soviets, traditionally dated to 25 October 1917, which was 7 November by the Western calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia still kept the Julian calendar, thirteen days behind the Gregorian, until the Soviet government itself changed it, so that the morning after 31 January 1918 was 14 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It followed eight months after the fall of the monarchy, during which a popular movement of peasants, workers and soldiers grew steadily more radical while the Provisional Government became almost powerless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lenin, writing from hiding, pressed his party to act before the Second Congress of Soviets could form an all-socialist coalition, and on 10 October the Central Committee voted ten to two to make an armed rising the order of the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet governments celebrated it for over seventy years as the act that founded a new political order, while their enemies and many Western historians called it a conspiratorial coup that deprived Russia of a democratic future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The rising was set off by Kerensky's attempt to close the Bolshevik printing presses on 24 October, after which Red Guards and soldiers of the Military Revolutionary Committee took over the key points of the city, mostly by persuasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At ten the next morning a proclamation written by Lenin declared the Provisional Government deposed and state power in the hands of the soviet's Military Revolutionary Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Winter Palace fell in the night and its ministers were arrested, while the Second Congress of Soviets, meeting at the Smolny Institute, took power in the name of the soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trotsky later put the speed and the small number of victims down to a party conspiracy, a workers' insurrection and a peasant garrison fighting for its own preservation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Moscow was another matter, for there the soviet's forces needed almost a week of street fighting, from 27 October to 2 November, to defeat the officer cadets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The New Calendar and Alphabet Reform,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/new-letters-and-dates/. [Open access]","Benno Ennker, “Lenin, Vladimir Il’ich,” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/lenin-vladimir-ilich/. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “To the Citizens of Russia!,” 25 October 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 236, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25.htm. [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 46, “The October Insurrection,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch46.htm. [Open access]","Э. М. Щагин, «Вновь о малоизвестных фактах из истории большевистского вооруженного восстания в Петрограде и Москве осенью 1917 г», <i>Преподаватель ХХI век</i> 2, no. 1 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vnov-o-maloizvestnyh-faktah-iz-istorii-bolshevistskogo-vooruzhennogo-vosstaniya-v-petrograde-i-moskve-osenyu-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it called the October Revolution if it happened in November?","a":"Because Russia used the Julian calendar until February 1918, thirteen days behind the Gregorian, so 25 October in Petrograd was 7 November elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The February Revolution likewise fell in early March, and the April Days in early May, by the Western reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kerensky's move against the Bolshevik press matter so much?","a":"Because closing the printing press raised the spectre of counter-revolution and gave the Bolsheviks the impetus for their rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Military Revolutionary Committee answered by ordering its forces to take over the strong points held by government troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do people disagree about whether it was a revolution or a coup?","a":"Because the Soviet state celebrated it as the act that founded a new political order, while its enemies and many Western historians saw a conspiracy that deprived Russia of a democratic chance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A well-known remark held that the Bolsheviks found power lying in the streets and picked it up, a phrase whose origin is unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"October Revolution"},
    "ru-569": {"questions":["Having escaped from Tsushima to internment at Manila in 1905, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay in Petrograd for repairs in 1917 and was moored for good as a museum ship in 1948.","Sailors of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked Trotsky in the Kresty prison whether to defend or storm the Winter Palace, and a plaque of 1927 credited their gun with the historic shot."],"abstract":"The <b>cruiser Aurora</b> is the Russian warship whose blank shot from the Neva on the night of 25 October 1917 became the best-known symbol of the October Revolution and of the taking of the Winter Palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She had fought at Tsushima in May 1905 and escaped with two other cruisers to internment at Manila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> She came to Petrograd for repairs in December 1916, and her crew, like the other Baltic sailors, was swept into the revolution of 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> During the Kornilov affair she entered the Neva to help defend the capital, and her sailors asked Trotsky, then in the Kresty prison, whether they should defend the Winter Palace or storm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In October the Military Revolutionary Committee made her one of the headquarters of the operation against the palace, anchored in midstream with her six-inch guns looking in on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The plan agreed for the night of 25 October was that a signal from the Peter and Paul Fortress would be followed by a blank volley from the Aurora to frighten the defenders, with live fire only if the palace held out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her ship's committee wrote to the newspapers on 27 October that only one blank round had been fired from a six-inch gun, as a signal to the ships in the river, and historians now ascribe the live shells to the fortress's guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace was stormed that night, and the captured ministers were taken across the river to the Trubetskoy Bastion of the fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet memory nonetheless turned the blank into a thunderous volley, since a plaque of 1927 on the gun called it the historic shot fired as the palace was taken, and films, paintings and children's books repeated the story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moored permanently in 1948, the Aurora became the first ship in the Soviet Union to be turned into a museum, one of the city's places of memory of 1917 alongside the Winter Palace and the fortress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 45, “The Capture of the Winter Palace,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch45.htm. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]","В. В. Головин, ««Залп Авроры»: проекции мифа в детской литературе», <i>Детские чтения</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 192–218, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zalp-avrory-proektsii-mifa-v-detskoy-literature. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. М. Щербакова, О. Ю. Отрокова and О. Ю. Гольцева, «Матросы-балтийцы в революции 1917 года: очерк современной историографии», <i>Историческая и социально-образовательная мысль</i> 9, no. 5-1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/matrosy-baltiytsy-v-revolyutsii-1917-goda-ocherk-sovremennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Минц, «2018. 05. 002. 1917: вокруг Зимнего / ред. -сост. Кантор Ю. - М. : политическая энциклопедия, 2017. - 247 с. : ил», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5. История</i>, no. S1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2018-05-002-1917-vokrug-zimnego-red-sost-kantor-yu-m-politicheskaya-entsiklopediya-2017-247-s-il. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. А. Чаплыгин, «Цусимское сражение: ход боя», <i>Ученые записки. Электронный научный журнал Курского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (19) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsusimskoe-srazhenie-hod-boya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ю. В. Степанчук, «Методы и этапы музеефикации объектов морского культурного наследия», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского государственного института культуры</i>, no. 4 (57) (2023): 80–86, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/metody-i-etapy-muzeefikatsii-obektov-morskogo-kulturnogo-naslediya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>My Life: An Attempt at an Autobiography</i>, ch. 26, “From July to October” (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1930), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/mylife/ch26.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Aurora fire only a blank?","a":"Because the plan was graduated: a blank volley from the cruiser was meant to frighten the palace, with live fire from the fortress and then from the ship's six-inch guns only if the defenders held out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ship's committee said its single blank round was a signal to the ships on the Neva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the blank shot grow into a volley in Soviet memory?","a":"Because a founding myth needs a decisive opening act, and a shot with a blank charge could hardly begin anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1927 a plaque on the gun named it the historic shot of 25 October, and films, paintings and books later made it the signal for the storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Aurora in Petrograd in 1917?","a":"Because she had come to the capital for repairs in December 1916, and the work was finished by the end of October 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> During the Kornilov affair she entered the Neva when the moderate socialists called on the Kronstadt sailors to help defend the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian cruiser Aurora"},
    "ru-570": {"questions":["Kamenev chaired the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which heard in the small hours of 26 October that the Winter Palace had fallen and the whole Provisional Government but Kerensky was under arrest.","Besides the decrees on peace and on land, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> created a Council of People's Commissars, all Bolsheviks after the Left Socialist Revolutionaries declined to join."],"abstract":"The <b>Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets</b> was the national assembly of soviet delegates that opened at the Smolny Institute in Petrograd on the evening of 25 October 1917, 7 November by the Western calendar, and endorsed the Bolshevik seizure of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kerensky's government had tried to stop it meeting, and while most Bolsheviks expected it to create a government of all the socialist parties, Lenin insisted on an insurrection first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> When it opened, the factions' own count found 649 delegates present, of whom 390 were Bolsheviks, 160 Socialist Revolutionaries of all tendencies and 72 Mensheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky noticed how the delegates had changed since the first congress in June, the officers' chevrons and the intellectuals' spectacles giving way to the grey of soldiers' and workers' clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Menshevik Dan opened the session in the name of the old executive committee, and Kamenev took the chair at the head of a new presidium of Bolsheviks and Left Socialist Revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Mensheviks, the right Socialist Revolutionaries and the Bund denounced the rising as a mad and criminal step and walked out, and Trotsky told them they were bankrupts bound for the rubbish heap of history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shortly after two in the morning of 26 October Kamenev read out the news that the Winter Palace had fallen and that the Provisional Government, all but Kerensky, was under arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The congress then adopted Lenin's appeal declaring that it took power into its own hands and that all power in the localities passed to the soviets of workers', soldiers' and peasants' deputies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 26 October it passed the decrees on peace and on land, the second taking over the Socialist Revolutionaries' peasant mandate, and set up a Council of People's Commissars under Lenin to govern until the Constituent Assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Left Socialist Revolutionaries, who had stayed in the hall, turned down the Bolsheviks' offer of seats, so the government took office as a one-party cabinet amid protests from the other parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “To Workers, Soldiers and Peasants!,” appeal of the Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 25 October 1917, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/25b.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Decision to Form the Workers’ and Peasants’ Government,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October 1917, Lenin Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26e.htm. [Open access]","Е. В. Панин, «Партийный состав II Всероссийского съезда Советов», <i>Известия Московского государственного технического университета МАМИ</i> 5, no. 2 (20) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/partiynyy-sostav-ii-vserossiyskogo-sezda-sovetov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Панин, «Дискуссии о создании первого советского правительства на II Всероссийском съезде Советов», <i>Известия Московского государственного технического университета МАМИ</i> 5, no. 3 (21) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/diskussii-o-sozdanii-pervogo-sovetskogo-pravitelstva-na-ii-vserossiyskom-sezde-sovetov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, ch. 47, “The Congress of the Soviet Dictatorship,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch47.htm. [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The October Revolution,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1917/bolsheviks-seize-power/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kerensky's government try to stop the congress?","a":"Because Kerensky chose to try to hold the nation together against the soviets rather than side with them, at a moment when the country was polarising fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His attempt to prevent the congress meeting as scheduled, together with the move against the Bolshevik press, proved self-destructive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many delegates walk out?","a":"Because the Mensheviks, the right Socialist Revolutionaries and the Bund condemned the seizure of power as a mad and criminal step taken in the congress's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky answered that their role was played out and that no compromise with them was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the first Soviet government made up only of Bolsheviks?","a":"Because the Left Socialist Revolutionaries turned down the offer of seats, fearing that joining would destroy the chance of a government of the whole democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The one-party cabinet provoked protests from the parties that had stayed at the congress and from those that had left it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies"},
    "ru-571": {"questions":["Its promise to publish the tsar's secret treaties and to abolish secret diplomacy made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a direct challenge to the governments of every warring power in 1917.","The officer Baron Budberg wrote that Lenin's telegram had destroyed the last chance of saving the front, so swiftly did news of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread among the soldiers."],"abstract":"The <b>Decree on Peace</b> was the first decree of Soviet power, drafted by Lenin and adopted by the Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets on the evening of 26 October 1917 by the Russian calendar, 8 November by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It called on all the warring peoples and their governments to open immediate negotiations for a just, democratic peace, which it defined as a peace without annexations and without indemnities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An annexation, the decree said, was any incorporation of a small or weak nation into a large state without its freely expressed consent, whenever that had happened and wherever in the world it lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new government also abolished secret diplomacy, promised to publish the secret treaties of the tsar and the Provisional Government, and proposed an armistice of not less than three months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its appeal went beyond governments to the workers of Britain, France and Germany, which made the carefully worded text, in effect, a call for revolution across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The American journalist John Reed watched the vote at 10:35 that night, when one delegate raised his card against and quickly lowered it, so that the decree passed unanimously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The delegates then sang the Internationale, and a young workman near Reed kept repeating that the war was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the front the effect was immediate, and an officer, Baron Budberg, wrote that the promise of an armistice had removed the last chance of saving the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The decree was printed in small booklets and spread across Russia, and in the weeks that followed great numbers of soldiers left the trenches to go home to the promised peace and land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allies decided not to reply, but the Central Powers agreed an armistice in December, and the talks that followed ended in the harsh separate peace of Brest-Litovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Report on Peace” and “Decree on Peace,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October (8 November) 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26b.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Decree on Peace,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10524. [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), chap. 5, “Plunging Ahead,” Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, chap. 47, “The Congress of the Soviet Dictatorship,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch47.htm. [Open access]","А. А. Шмаков, «Первые декреты советской власти с точки зрения пропаганды», <i>Вестник Марийского государственного университета. Серия «Исторические науки. Юридические науки»</i> 9, no. 1 (33) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervye-dekrety-sovetskoy-vlasti-s-tochki-zreniya-propagandy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Susanne Schattenberg, “Brest-Litovsk, Treaty of,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10500. [Open access]","John W. Wheeler-Bennett, <i>Brest-Litovsk: The Forgotten Peace, March 1918</i> (London: Macmillan, 1938), 269, 304–5, 362, https://archive.org/details/brestlitovskthef018745mbp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the decree refuse to issue an ultimatum?","a":"Because Lenin wanted to deny the enemy governments any excuse to say that talks were pointless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He told the congress that the government would consider any peace terms, that considering them did not mean accepting them, and that the final word would rest with the Constituent Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the decree address peoples as well as governments?","a":"Because the Bolsheviks expected the workers of the West to push their own governments towards peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lenin argued that governments and peoples were everywhere at odds, so the new state had to help peoples intervene in questions of war and peace, and the appeal to British, French and German workers made the decree a call for European revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the decree not bring a general peace?","a":"Because no European rising followed and the Allies chose not to reply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Russia was left to negotiate alone with the Central Powers, and the separate peace signed at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 cost it about a third of its population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Decree on Peace"},
    "ru-572": {"questions":["Lenin later admitted that the Bolsheviks had won by taking over the agrarian programme of the Socialist Revolutionaries, which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1917 turned into law overnight.","Read out by Lenin from a barely legible draft, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed landed, crown and church estates to local land committees without any compensation to their owners."],"abstract":"The <b>Decree on Land</b> was the second decree of Soviet power, adopted by the Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets at about two in the morning of 27 October 1917 by the Russian calendar, 9 November by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first clause abolished the landed property of the nobility at once and without compensation, and placed estates and crown, monastery and church lands under local land committees and peasant soviets until the Constituent Assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To this short decree was attached a Peasant Mandate on the Land, compiled from 242 local peasant instructions and first printed in August 1917 by the newspaper of the peasant soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mandate abolished private ownership of land for ever, banned hired labour and gave the use of land to all who would work it, to be shared out by a labour or a subsistence standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole decree ran to scarcely thirty lines, and Lenin read it from a rough draft so badly written that he stumbled and finally handed it to someone else to finish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its programme was not the Bolsheviks' own but that of their rivals the Socialist Revolutionaries, the party most peasants trusted, and Lenin later admitted that the Bolsheviks had won by adopting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It largely confirmed what was already happening, for since the spring of 1917 peasants had been encroaching on estates and by the summer were exercising a veto over how landowners ran them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Lenin defended the borrowing at the congress, arguing that a democratic government could not ignore the decision of the masses even when it disagreed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vote, Reed noted, was carried with only one delegate against, and leaflets printing its first two clauses in large type soon carried the news to the villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Equal sharing did not last, for after the break with the Left Socialist Revolutionaries in 1918 official acts spoke of nationalisation rather than socialisation of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Report on Land” and “Decree on Land,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October (8 November) 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26d.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, chap. 47, “The Congress of the Soviet Dictatorship,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch47.htm. [Open access]","John Reed, <i>Ten Days That Shook the World</i> (New York: Boni and Liveright, 1919), chap. 5, “Plunging Ahead,” Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3076. [Open access]","Л. Г. Берлявский, «Первые декреты советской власти и формирование государственно-правовых основ советского аграрного строя», <i>Труды Института государства и права Российской академии наук</i> 18, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervye-dekrety-sovetskoy-vlasti-i-formirovanie-gosudarstvenno-pravovyh-osnov-sovetskogo-agrarnogo-stroya. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Шмаков, «Первые декреты советской власти с точки зрения пропаганды», <i>Вестник Марийского государственного университета. Серия «Исторические науки. Юридические науки»</i> 9, no. 1 (33) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervye-dekrety-sovetskoy-vlasti-s-tochki-zreniya-propagandy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","Oliver Henry Radkey, <i>The Election to the Russian Constituent Assembly of 1917</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), 1–2, 14–21, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.504284. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks adopt the Socialist Revolutionaries' land programme?","a":"Because it was what the peasants wanted and were already doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Socialisation of the land matched the expectations of the countryside, where peasants were seizing and dividing estates without waiting for any law, and by taking over the slogans the Bolsheviks won the support of most of the rural population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the decree only a provisional law?","a":"Because the final settlement of the land question was reserved for the Constituent Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mandate was proclaimed a provisional law, to be carried out as far as possible at once and in some parts gradually, until the Assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the decree not say the land was nationalised?","a":"Because Lenin did not want to tie the new government's hands in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The basic decree committed itself to no form of property, while the attached mandate spoke of land becoming the property of the whole people, a phrase later Soviet law treated as nationalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Decree on Land"},
    "ru-573": {"questions":["Proclaimed on 26 October 1917 to govern only until the Constituent Assembly met, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chaired by Lenin, with Trotsky for foreign affairs and Stalin for the nationalities.","The Left Socialist Revolutionaries quit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1918 in protest at the Brest-Litovsk peace, after only a few months of sharing in the Soviet government."],"abstract":"<b>Sovnarkom</b>, the Council of People's Commissars, was the government proclaimed by the Second All-Russian Congress of Soviets on 26 October 1917 by the Russian calendar, 8 November by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was set up as a provisional workers' and peasants' government to rule until the Constituent Assembly met, with each branch of administration run by a commission whose chairman sat on the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Bolshevik leaders chose the name because the word 'minister' smelled of the old bureaucracy, while 'people's commissar' at least had a fresher sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lenin was its chairman, Trotsky took foreign affairs and Stalin the affairs of the nationalities, and the Congress and its Central Executive Committee kept the right to replace any commissar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first council was made up of Bolsheviks only, after the Bolshevik Central Committee adopted Lenin's motion that no other government was thinkable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That first membership lasted only ten days, and every change to the council was confirmed by the Central Executive Committee until the Constituent Assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By December 1917 Left Socialist Revolutionaries had joined it, and when their commissar of justice freed people arrested by the Cheka, the council ruled that the Cheka answered to the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the Assembly was dispersed in January 1918, the Third Congress of Soviets confirmed the government, which no longer called itself provisional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its decrees reached into daily life, among them the decree of January 1918 introducing the Western European calendar and a decree of October 1918 on the new spelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Left Socialist Revolutionaries left the council in March 1918 in protest at the Brest-Litovsk peace, and Sovnarkom became the government of a one-party state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Decision to Form the Workers’ and Peasants’ Government,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October (8 November) 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26e.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, chap. 47, “The Congress of the Soviet Dictatorship,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch47.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Decree on Peace,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10524. [Open access]","Ф. А. Гайда, «Проблема верховной власти в России в феврале 1917 – январе 1918 г.: юридическая и политическая реальность», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (47) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-verhovnoy-vlasti-v-rossii-v-fevrale-1917-yanvare-1918-g-yuridicheskaya-i-politicheskaya-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. В. Карпычева, «Создание и оформление управленческой документации Совета народных комиссаров в 1917–1919 гг.», <i>Научный вестник Крыма</i>, no. 3 (21) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sozdanie-i-oformlenie-upravlencheskoy-dokumentatsii-soveta-narodnyh-komissarov-v-1917-1919-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Эволюция государственных представлений большевиков в послеоктябрьский период на примере ВЧК», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 15 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-gosudarstvennyh-predstavleniy-bolshevikov-v-posleoktyabrskiy-period-na-primere-vchk. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Ильюхов, «„Конфликт в благородном семействе“: Брестский мир и распад коалиции большевиков и левых эсеров в 1918 г.», <i>Вестник университета</i>, no. 20 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konflikt-v-blagorodnom-semeystve-brestskiy-mir-i-raspad-koalitsii-bolshevikov-i-levyh-eserov-v-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the new government at first called provisional?","a":"Because it was meant to rule only until the Constituent Assembly was convened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once the Assembly had been dispersed, the Third Congress of Soviets confirmed the government as a permanent one, no longer provisional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Left Socialist Revolutionaries leave the government?","a":"Because they rejected the separate peace with Germany signed at Brest-Litovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A meeting of the party's deputies demanded the recall of its people's commissars, and the party announced its withdrawal from the government on 18 March 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Cheka answer to Sovnarkom rather than to the justice commissariat?","a":"Because the Bolshevik majority wanted the security police free of Left Socialist Revolutionary control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the justice commissar released people the Cheka had arrested, the council adopted a resolution by Lenin and Stalin confirming that the Cheka was subordinate to the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Council of People's Commissars"},
    "ru-574": {"questions":["Its own official figure for 1918 was 6,300 people shot, but how many the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed during the Red Terror and the Civil War is still disputed.","Abolished in February 1922 and replaced by the State Political Directorate, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been led for most of its existence by the Polish revolutionary Felix Dzerzhinsky."],"abstract":"The <b>Cheka</b>, named from the Russian initials of the All-Russian Extraordinary Commission for Combating Counter-Revolution and Sabotage, was the political police founded by Sovnarkom on 7 December 1917 by the Russian calendar, 20 December by the Western one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of a strike by government employees against the new regime, and its first chairman was Felix Dzerzhinsky, who had reported to the council that day on how to fight the saboteurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At first it was tiny, with 23 people including drivers and couriers, and from 10 December it worked from the former office of the city governor on Gorokhovaya Street in Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its powers widened with each crisis, and the decree 'The Socialist Fatherland Is in Danger', issued as German troops advanced, gave it the right to shoot offenders on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Local commissions followed from 1918 in provinces, districts, armies and on the railways, and in February 1922 it was replaced by the State Political Directorate, which Dzerzhinsky went on to lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> After the attempt on Lenin's life in August 1918, Sovnarkom's resolution 'On the Red Terror' of 5 September let the Cheka shoot anyone connected with White organisations, plots and risings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How many it killed is disputed, for its own official figure for 1918 was 6,300 shot, while an émigré historian put the toll at 50,000 for the second half of that year alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official figures also left out people shot while risings were crushed, as in the Tambov revolt, where Cheka and Red Army units fought side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin defended it on the first anniversary of October at the Cheka's own club in Moscow, dismissing the attacks on it as worthless gossip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> During the Civil War it arrested, by one estimate, some 500,000 of the peasant rebels known as Greens, and the regime showed no compunction about using terror to keep power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Note to F. E. Dzerzhinsky, with a Draft of a Decree on Fighting Counter-Revolutionaries and Saboteurs,” 7 (20) December 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 374–76, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/dec/07.htm. [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Эволюция государственных представлений большевиков в послеоктябрьский период на примере ВЧК», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 15 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-gosudarstvennyh-predstavleniy-bolshevikov-v-posleoktyabrskiy-period-na-primere-vchk. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Каргапольцева, «К вопросу о правовых основаниях организации и деятельности Всероссийской чрезвычайной комиссии в 1917–1922 гг.», <i>Вестник Уральского юридического института МВД России</i>, no. 2 (42) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-pravovyh-osnovaniyah-organizatsii-i-deyatelnosti-vserossiyskoy-chrezvychaynoy-komissii-v-1917-1922-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Соколов, «Роль и место органов ВЧК в политике красного террора», <i>Власть</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-i-mesto-organov-vchk-v-politike-krasnogo-terrora. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. В. Годунов, «Празднование первой годовщины Октября и красный террор: легитимация революционного насилия», <i>Новейшая история России</i> 10, no. 4 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prazdnovanie-pervoy-godovschiny-oktyabrya-i-krasnyy-terror-legitimatsiya-revolyutsionnogo-nasiliya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10171. [Open access]","О. М. Санковская, «Ф. Э. Дзержинский: „Фанатик“ или „Рыцарь революции“?», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/f-e-dzerzhinskiy-fanatik-ili-rytsar-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anthony Heywood, “Post-war Economies (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10352. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the commission called extraordinary?","a":"Because it was conceived as a special body with emergency powers against counter-revolution and sabotage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dzerzhinsky argued that such an organ was needed, and its first target was the strike of officials that was paralysing government offices in December 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the Cheka's death tolls so uncertain?","a":"Because its own statistics were incomplete and partisan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official count left out people shot in operations against risings, some death sentences were never carried out, and émigré and Soviet sources give figures that differ many times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin defend the Cheka in public in November 1918?","a":"Because its methods were drawing criticism even among Bolsheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speaking at the Cheka club on the first anniversary of October, he said that what mattered was not who served in it but the decisiveness, speed and loyalty of its work, and he called the attacks worthless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cheka"},
    "ru-575": {"questions":["Born into a Polish family of minor nobility near Vilna in 1877, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a devout Catholic as a boy before turning to Marxism in an illegal school circle.","Head of the political police and, from 1924, of the Supreme Council of the National Economy, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed after a speech to a party plenum and died in July 1926."],"abstract":"<b>Felix Dzerzhinsky</b> was a Polish revolutionary from a family of minor nobility who became the first chairman of the Cheka, the Soviet political police, and remained at the head of it and its successors until his death in 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was born on 11 September 1877 on his family's small estate of Dzerzhinovo, and until about seventeen he was a devout Catholic who even thought of becoming a priest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At school in Vilna he joined an illegal circle that read Marx and Engels, and in 1896, after his mother's death, he left the gymnasium in its final year to devote himself to the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arrested six times, he spent about ten years in prison and some months in exile, and he caught tuberculosis in a tsarist jail in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The February Revolution freed him from Moscow's Butyrka prison on 1 March 1917 by the Russian calendar, 14 March by the Western one, and in October he spoke at the Soviet congress for the social democrats of Poland and Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 December 1917 he reported to Sovnarkom on how to fight a strike of officials, and the council created the Cheka that day with him as its chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eleven days later he co-signed the order for the arrest of leaders of the Union for the Defence of the Constituent Assembly, among them the Socialist Revolutionary Viktor Chernov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under him the Cheka grew from 23 staff into a nationwide apparatus of repression, and in February 1922 it was replaced by the State Political Directorate, which he went on to lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin also made him commissar of railways in April 1921, and in February 1924 he became chairman of the Supreme Council of the National Economy, which ran Soviet industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He collapsed after a speech to a party plenum on 20 July 1926 and died that afternoon, aged 48, of disease of the arteries of the heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Note to F. E. Dzerzhinsky, with a Draft of a Decree on Fighting Counter-Revolutionaries and Saboteurs,” 7 (20) December 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 374–76, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/dec/07.htm. [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Эволюция государственных представлений большевиков в послеоктябрьский период на примере ВЧК», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 15 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-gosudarstvennyh-predstavleniy-bolshevikov-v-posleoktyabrskiy-period-na-primere-vchk. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Здоровье „Железного Феликса“», <i>Политематический сетевой электронный научный журнал Кубанского государственного аграрного университета</i>, no. 98 (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/zdorovie-zheleznogo-feliksa. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. М. Санковская, «Ф. Э. Дзержинский: „Фанатик“ или „Рыцарь революции“?», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/f-e-dzerzhinskiy-fanatik-ili-rytsar-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Рогачевская, «В поиске „правильной линии в управлении страной и хозяйством“: Ф. Э. Дзержинский», <i>Историко-экономические исследования</i> 14, no. 1–2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-poiske-pravilnoy-linii-v-upravlenii-stranoy-i-hozyaystvom-f-e-dzerzhinskiy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 3, chap. 47, “The Congress of the Soviet Dictatorship,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch47.htm. [Open access]","Н. И. Каргапольцева, «К вопросу о правовых основаниях организации и деятельности Всероссийской чрезвычайной комиссии в 1917–1922 гг.», <i>Вестник Уральского юридического института МВД России</i>, no. 2 (42) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-pravovyh-osnovaniyah-organizatsii-i-deyatelnosti-vserossiyskoy-chrezvychaynoy-komissii-v-1917-1922-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Dzerzhinsky chosen to lead the Cheka?","a":"Because he had made the case for it himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He argued that a new special body with extraordinary powers was needed against counter-revolution and sabotage, and on 7 December 1917 he set out its tasks to Sovnarkom, which then created the commission under his chairmanship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a police chief also run the railways and industry?","a":"Because the party used him as a troubleshooter for its most urgent crises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In April 1921 he became commissar of railways while keeping the Cheka, and in 1924 he took over the Supreme Council of the National Economy, which had been left without an effective head at the end of 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his health fail so early?","a":"Because years in tsarist prisons had worn him down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He caught tuberculosis in prison in 1901, and the autopsy after his death in 1926 found severe hardening of the arteries of the heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Felix Dzerzhinsky"},
    "ru-576": {"questions":["Lenin dismissed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as out of date, because the Socialist Revolutionaries had submitted joint party lists in October and split into two parties only after polling.","About two-thirds of the voters turned out, women often more readily than men, for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that was meant to choose the body deciding Russia's constitution."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Constituent Assembly election</b> of 1917 was the vote for the body that was meant to decide Russia's form of government and its constitution after the fall of the monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Provisional Government had been committed from its founding to holding it, and it was held on the basis of universal, equal, direct and secret suffrage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the first attempt anywhere to hold such an election on a nationwide scale, using proportional representation with separate party lists in each electoral district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polling was set for 12 to 14 November 1917 by the Russian calendar, 25 to 27 November by the Western one, less than three weeks after the Bolsheviks had taken power, and some districts voted later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Turnout was high, about two-thirds of the electorate in the districts where it can be measured, and women often voted in larger numbers than men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> More than 41 million votes were cast, and the Socialist Revolutionaries, the party most peasants trusted to give them the land, won the largest share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Bolsheviks took roughly a quarter of the vote, while the liberal Kadets were routed with only about two million votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 707 deputies whose party is known, about 370 were Socialist Revolutionaries, 40 were Left Socialist Revolutionaries and 175 were Bolsheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin argued that the result was already out of date, because the Socialist Revolutionaries had drawn up joint lists in mid-October and had split only after the voting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The party totals still differ between sources, which give the Socialist Revolutionaries from about 38 to almost 40 per cent of the vote, because the returns from some districts were lost or never complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Oliver Henry Radkey, <i>The Election to the Russian Constituent Assembly of 1917</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), 1–2, 14–21, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.504284. [Open access]","Fedor Aleksandrovich Gaida, “Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Russian Empire),” trans. Trevor Goronwy, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10189. [Open access]","Л. Г. Протасов, «Проблема абсентеизма на выборах Всероссийского Учредительного собрания (1917 г.)», <i>Pro Nunc. Современные политические процессы</i>, no. 1 (4) (2006), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-absenteizma-na-vyborah-vserossiyskogo-uchreditelnogo-sobraniya-1917-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10171. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Theses on the Constituent Assembly,” December 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 379–83, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/dec/11a.htm. [Open access]","Ф. А. Гайда, «Проблема верховной власти в России в феврале 1917 – январе 1918 г.: юридическая и политическая реальность», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (47) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-verhovnoy-vlasti-v-rossii-v-fevrale-1917-yanvare-1918-g-yuridicheskaya-i-politicheskaya-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Socialist Revolutionaries win the most votes?","a":"Because the peasant majority voted for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peasants associated the party with the immediate expropriation of the land without compensation to its owners, and rural voters turned out in larger numbers than townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the exact results still uncertain?","a":"Because the returns from some districts were incomplete, voting was postponed in places, and the figures were never fully collected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different tallies therefore give the main parties shares that differ by a point or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin call the result unrepresentative?","a":"Because the party lists had been drawn up in mid-October 1917, before the October Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Voters could not choose between Right and Left Socialist Revolutionaries, who split only after the election, so in his view the vote could not express the will of the working people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1917 Russian Constituent Assembly election"},
    "ru-577": {"questions":["The sailor Zheleznyakov told the deputies that the guard was tired, ending the only session of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the Bolsheviks and Left Socialist Revolutionaries had walked out.","Petrograd demonstrators marching in support of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the day it opened were fired upon, leaving between seven and twenty-one dead by differing estimates."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Constituent Assembly</b> was the elected body that met in the Tauride Palace in Petrograd on 5 January 1918 by the Russian calendar, 18 January by the Western one, to decide the country's future form of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its calling had been one of the conditions the Petrograd Soviet set in March 1917 for supporting the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Of 820 planned seats 767 had been filled, but only between 407 and 410 deputies took part in its single session, which opened once half the legal number had reached the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Socialist Revolutionaries held a clear majority, and their leader Viktor Chernov was elected chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soldiers, sailors and Red Guards packed the galleries and the doorways, shouting, whistling, stamping and aiming rifles at the speakers they disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The majority refused to discuss a declaration drafted by Lenin that would have recognised Soviet power and its decrees, and the Bolsheviks walked out, followed later by the Left Socialist Revolutionaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early on 6 January the guard closed the session, the sailor Zheleznyakov telling the deputies that the guard was tired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That night the Central Executive Committee decreed the Assembly dissolved, declaring that what remained of it could only serve as a screen for counter-revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large peaceful demonstration in its support was fired on in Petrograd on the day it opened, and estimates of the dead range from 7 to 21 in Petrograd and from 6 to 15 in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Socialist Revolutionary deputies later formed a government on the Volga in its name, but after the walkout fewer than a third of the legal number of deputies had stayed, which made its decisions easy to challenge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Oliver Henry Radkey, <i>The Election to the Russian Constituent Assembly of 1917</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), 1–2, 14–21, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.504284. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Draft Decree on the Dissolution of the Constituent Assembly,” 6 (19) January 1918, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/jan/06a.htm. [Open access]","А. И. Юрьев, «Подготовка большевиков к созыву Учредительного собрания и его разгон 5–6 января 1918 года», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 1 (33) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/podgotovka-bolshevikov-k-sozyvu-uchreditelnogo-sobraniya-i-ego-razgon-5-6-yanvarya-1918-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Эволюция государственных представлений большевиков в послеоктябрьский период на примере ВЧК», <i>Труды Исторического факультета Санкт-Петербургского университета</i>, no. 15 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-gosudarstvennyh-predstavleniy-bolshevikov-v-posleoktyabrskiy-period-na-primere-vchk. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. А. Гайда, «Проблема верховной власти в России в феврале 1917 – январе 1918 г.: юридическая и политическая реальность», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 3 (47) (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-verhovnoy-vlasti-v-rossii-v-fevrale-1917-yanvare-1918-g-yuridicheskaya-i-politicheskaya-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10171. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Dual Power,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10075. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks walk out of the Assembly?","a":"Because the majority would not discuss their declaration recognising Soviet power and the decrees of Sovnarkom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Accepting it would have meant the Assembly sanctioning its own end, since the declaration said its tasks were limited to laying the foundations of a socialist society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few people fight to save the Assembly?","a":"Because by January 1918 much of the population no longer saw it as necessary to get what it wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cheka had also arrested many of its organised supporters beforehand, so the demonstration in its defence was no serious threat and was quickly dispersed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Lenin's government justify dissolving the Assembly?","a":"By claiming that it reflected an outdated balance of forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its decree argued that the deputies had been elected from lists drawn up before October, that only class institutions such as the soviets could build socialism, and that the rump of the Assembly would shelter counter-revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Constituent Assembly"},
    "ru-578": {"questions":["By 1 June 1918 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted 513 nationalised or sequestered enterprises, before Sovnarkom's decree of 28 June took over the largest firms in whole branches of industry.","Greeting the first congress of economic councils in May 1918, Lenin said that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> faced a difficult but most rewarding task in organising a socialist economy."],"abstract":"The <b>Supreme Council of the National Economy</b>, known by its Russian initials as VSNKh, was the body set up by Soviet decree in December 1917 to organise the economy and state finances, and it carried out the nationalisation of Russian industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its founding decree, usually dated 2 December 1917 by the Russian calendar, 15 December by the Western one, gave it the power to confiscate, requisition and sequester firms and to force whole industries into syndicates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exact date is still disputed and is also given as 1 or 5 December, because the text passed the Central Executive Committee before it was signed and published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the end of 1917 it set up provincial and district economic councils to carry out its policy across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin told the first congress of these councils in May 1918 that the new economy would have to be built by experiment as they went along, with the help of bourgeois experts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Nationalisation often began from below, as local soviets and the factory committees born in the workers' movement of 1917 took over firms faster than the centre could regulate them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1 June 1918 the council counted 513 nationalised or sequestered enterprises, and by September only 604 of 2,279 had been taken over by acts of Sovnarkom and the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive step was Sovnarkom's decree of 28 June 1918 nationalising the largest firms in mining, metals, textiles, electrical goods, timber and other industries, after which most of the work passed to the council's own apparatus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the system later called War Communism all industry and trade were taken over without compensation, and by 1920 industrial output had fallen to at most 25 to 30 per cent of its 1913 level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1923 it became an all-Union body of the new Soviet Union, from 1924 it was chaired by Felix Dzerzhinsky, and the system of economic councils it headed lasted until 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["А. А. Борисов, «Правовые акты о национализации промышленности и правотворчество Высшего совета народного хозяйства в 1917–1921 годы», <i>Историко-правовые проблемы: новый ракурс</i>, no. 1 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-akty-o-natsionalizatsii-promyshlennosti-i-pravotvorchestvo-vysshego-soveta-narodnogo-hozyaystva-v-1917-1921-gody. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Борисов and Е. В. Сафронова, «Периодизация огосударствления объектов промышленности в Советской России (1917–1922 гг.)», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Юридические науки</i> 10, no. 3 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/periodizatsiya-ogosudarstvleniya-obektov-promyshlennosti-v-sovetskoy-rossii-1917-1922-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Anthony Heywood, “Post-war Economies (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10352. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Speech at the First Congress of Economic Councils, May 26, 1918,” in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 27 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/may/26b.htm. [Open access]","Н. К. Борисюк and Ж. А. Ермакова, «От совнархозов до кластеров. Часть 1. Совнархозы в 1917–1932 гг.», <i>Вестник Самарского государственного университета</i>, no. 8 (119) (2014): 161–67, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-sovnarhozov-do-klasterov-chast-1-sovnarhozy-v-1917-1932-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]","О. М. Санковская, «Ф. Э. Дзержинский: „Фанатик“ или „Рыцарь революции“?», <i>Вестник Северного (Арктического) федерального университета. Серия: Гуманитарные и социальные науки</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/f-e-dzerzhinskiy-fanatik-ili-rytsar-revolyutsii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did nationalisation run ahead of the law?","a":"Because local soviets and factory committees acted on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Telegrams poured into the council asking for firms to be sequestered or confiscated, and by September 1918 most nationalised enterprises had been taken over by provincial, district, parish or village authorities rather than by the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date of its founding decree disputed?","a":"Because the text went through several stages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Central Executive Committee approved the draft on 1 December, but it was not yet signed by the leaders of the government, and it was published only on 5 December, so editors have given all three dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Soviet industry collapse under War Communism?","a":"Because nationalisation, requisitioning and the collapse of the currency came together in a civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Output fell despite, and many would say largely because of, the Bolsheviks' policies, and by 1920 it was perhaps a quarter of the 1913 level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Supreme Soviet of the National Economy"},
    "ru-579": {"questions":["The church council in Moscow denounced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as open persecution disguised as a law on freedom of conscience, only days after Lenin's government had approved it.","Drafted with the help of the legal scholar Mikhail Reisner, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed religious rites from every act of the state and abolished the religious oath."],"abstract":"The <b>Decree on Separation of Church and State</b> was the Soviet law of January 1918 that separated the church from the state and the school from the church, and it set the terms of the state's dealings with religion for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A commission of the justice commissariat drafted it from December 1917, among them the legal scholar Mikhail Reisner, the commissar of education Lunacharsky and a former Petrograd priest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sovnarkom approved it on 20 January 1918 by the Russian calendar, 2 February by the Western one, after Lenin had sharpened its first article to read simply that the church is separated from the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It forbade any law that restricted freedom of conscience, removed religious rites and ceremonies from all acts of the state and abolished religious oaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its last articles denied churches and religious societies the right to own property and the rights of a legal person, though buildings and objects for worship could be lent back to believers free of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The decree has two titles and several dates, for it was drafted as a decree on freedom of conscience and on church and religious societies, and published on 23 January under the name by which it is now known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The church council then sitting in Moscow called it an open act of persecution under the guise of a law on freedom of conscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without legal personality the church lost its registers of births and marriages, its seminaries were closed, and parishes had to be registered through groups of twenty lay founders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In May 1918 Sovnarkom created a special department of the justice commissariat to enforce the decree, which became known as the liquidation department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It built on the Decree on Land of October 1917, which had already handed monastery and church estates to local land committees without compensation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["П. С. Исаков, «Роль М. А. Рейснера в подготовке Декрета об отделении церкви от государства и школы от церкви», <i>Вестник Православного Свято-Тихоновского гуманитарного университета. Серия 2: История. История Русской Православной Церкви</i>, no. 98 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-m-a-reysnera-v-podgotovke-dekreta-ob-otdelenii-tserkvi-ot-gosudarstva-i-shkoly-ot-tserkvi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ф. Н. Козлов, «Декрет „О свободе совести...“ или „Об отделении церкви...“ (1918 г.)», <i>Гасырлар авазы – Эхо веков</i>, no. 3–4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dekret-o-svobode-sovesti-ili-ob-otdelenii-tserkvi-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Кашеваров, «Акты советской власти по проведению в жизнь декрета об отделении церкви от государства в восприятии высшего церковного управления», <i>Клио</i>, no. 2 (206) (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/akty-sovetskoy-vlasti-po-provedeniyu-v-zhizn-dekreta-ob-otdelenii-tserkvi-ot-gosudarstva-v-vospriyatii-vysshego-tserkovnogo. [in Russian] [Open access]","К. Г. Аристова, «Декрет об отделении Церкви от государства от 20 января 1918 г. и обновленчество в Пензенской епархии: первые уроки советской власти», <i>Известия Пензенского государственного педагогического университета им. В. Г. Белинского</i>, no. 23 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dekret-ob-otdelenii-tserkvi-ot-gosudarstva-ot-20-yanvarya-1918-g-i-obnovlenchestvo-v-penzenskoy-eparhii-pervye-uroki-sovetskoy-vlasti-1. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Г. Коробова, «Правовой статус Русской Православной Церкви в первые годы советской власти», <i>Общество и право</i>, no. 2 (20) (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoy-status-russkoy-pravoslavnoy-tserkvi-v-pervye-gody-sovetskoy-vlasti. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Report on Land” and “Decree on Land,” Second All-Russia Congress of Soviets, 26 October (8 November) 1917, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 26 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1917/oct/25-26/26d.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the decree have two names?","a":"Because the draft and its first published and circulated versions called it a decree on freedom of conscience and on church and religious societies, while the official collection of laws printed it as the decree separating the church from the state and the school from the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians have dated it to 20, 21 or 23 January 1918 as a result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the loss of legal personality so damaging?","a":"Because a church that could not own property or act as a legal person could not hold its own buildings or keep its diocesan administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Responsibility passed to groups of twenty lay founders, which broke up church government at the level of the diocese and the parish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin change the first article?","a":"Because the draft's opening, that religion is the private affair of every citizen, was milder than the party's programme required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lenin replaced it with the flat statement that the church is separated from the state, dropping the mention of freedom of conscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Decree on Separation of Church and State"},
    "ru-580": {"questions":["Trotsky's declaration of 'neither war nor peace' broke off the talks that led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the German offensive that followed forced Lenin to accept every German term.","The Fourth Congress of Soviets ratified the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by 784 votes to 261, after which the Left Socialist Revolutionaries left the Soviet government in protest."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> was the separate peace signed on 3 March 1918 between Soviet Russia and the Central Powers, Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire and Bulgaria, which took Russia out of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Talks opened at the German eastern headquarters in the fortress of Brest-Litovsk on 9 December 1917 by the Russian calendar, 22 December by the Western one, a week after an armistice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet delegation, which included a sailor, a soldier, a peasant and a worker, faced aristocratic diplomats, and each side invoked national self-determination for the occupied western borderlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trotsky, sent to drag out the talks until revolution broke out in Europe, declared in February 1918 that Russia would neither make war nor sign peace, and Germany answered with a new offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin then sent a delegation that accepted the German terms in full, and Russia gave up Poland, the Baltic lands and Finland, recognised Ukraine's separate peace and agreed to demobilise its army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> By one count the treaty cost Russia 34 per cent of its population, 32 per cent of its farmland, 54 per cent of its industrial undertakings and 89 per cent of its coal mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lenin wrote that the army could not fight, called the treaty an unfortunate and infinitely humiliating peace, and compared it with Napoleon's crushing of Prussia, from which Prussia had recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Fourth Congress of Soviets ratified it in March by 784 votes to 261, and the Left Socialist Revolutionaries left the government in protest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Further secret agreements signed in Berlin on 27 August 1918 imposed on Soviet Russia an indemnity of six billion gold marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany's defeat ended the treaty, for the Soviet government annulled it on 13 November 1918, and the Treaty of Versailles cancelled it again the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Susanne Schattenberg, “Brest-Litovsk, Treaty of,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10500. [Open access]","John W. Wheeler-Bennett, <i>Brest-Litovsk: The Forgotten Peace, March 1918</i> (London: Macmillan, 1938), 269, 304–5, 362, https://archive.org/details/brestlitovskthef018745mbp. [Open access]","“Peace Treaty of Brest Litovsk,” 3 March 1918, translation telegraphed by the Consul General at Moscow to the Secretary of State, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/bl34.asp. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Decree on Peace,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10524. [Open access]","Б. Л. Хавкин, «Проблема Брестского мира в российско-германских отношениях (1918)», <i>Философические письма. Русско-европейский диалог</i> 1, no. 2 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/problema-brestskogo-mira-v-rossiysko-germanskih-otnosheniyah-1918. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Ильюхов, «„Конфликт в благородном семействе“: Брестский мир и распад коалиции большевиков и левых эсеров в 1918 г.», <i>Вестник университета</i>, no. 20 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konflikt-v-blagorodnom-semeystve-brestskiy-mir-i-raspad-koalitsii-bolshevikov-i-levyh-eserov-v-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “An Unfortunate Peace,” <i>Pravda</i>, 24 February 1918, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 27 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 51–52, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/feb/24a.htm. [Open access]","Anthony Heywood, “Post-war Economies (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10352. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Note on the Necessity of Signing the Peace Treaty,” 24 February 1918, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 27 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 57, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/feb/24c.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Lenin insist on signing such harsh terms?","a":"Because he held that Russia could not fight at that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> He wrote that the army was against the war and unable to fight, and that refusing peace meant either empty phrase-making or playing into the hands of those who wanted the Germans to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> His delegate at Brest-Litovsk then accepted the German terms in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Left Socialist Revolutionaries leave the government?","a":"Because they opposed the separate peace with imperial Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their central committee at first meant to keep its people's commissars in Sovnarkom, but a meeting of the party's deputies demanded their recall, and the party announced its withdrawal on 18 March 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty last less than a year?","a":"Because Germany lost the war in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two days after the armistice the Soviet government annulled the treaty and its supplementary agreements, and the Treaty of Versailles later cancelled every agreement Germany had made with the Soviet government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of Brest-Litovsk"},
    "ru-581": {"questions":["Partners in Lenin's government until the Brest peace, the Left Socialist-Revolutionaries lost every share of power after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke out during the Fifth Congress of Soviets.","Rebels who had seized the Moscow telegraph office wired the whole country news of a revolt against German imperialism during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 6 and 7 July 1918."],"abstract":"The <b>Left SR uprising</b> was the armed revolt of the Left Socialist-Revolutionaries in Moscow on 6 and 7 July 1918, which began with the assassination of the German ambassador, Count Mirbach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Left SRs were the radical wing of the old peasant socialist party, and from December 1917 they shared power with the Bolsheviks, so that for its first months the Soviet government was in fact a coalition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They left the Council of People's Commissars when the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk was ratified in March 1918, but kept their seats in the soviets, the Central Executive Committee and many commissariats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They also fought the Bolsheviks' grain requisitioning, calling the new committees of the village poor committees of village loafers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Fifth Congress of Soviets, which opened in the Bolshoi Theatre on 4 July, they held about a third of some 800 seats and attacked the peace, the poor committees and the death penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 24 June their Central Committee had sanctioned terrorist acts against the leading representatives of German imperialism, to end the breathing-space that the peace had bought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the afternoon of 6 July two party members serving in the Cheka, Yakov Blumkin and Nikolai Andreev, entered the German embassy with forged papers and killed Mirbach with pistol shots and a bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dzerzhinsky, who went to demand the assassins, was himself held by the party's Cheka detachment, while some forty rebels seized the central telegraph office and wired the country news of a revolt against imperialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lenin told Stalin that night that Russia stood a hair's breadth from war, and in Berlin the Soviet envoy Adolf Ioffe worked to keep the crisis from reopening hostilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the afternoon of 7 July Latvian riflemen had shelled the rebels out of their headquarters, about 400 were captured and 13 shot, and from then on the Communists ruled the soviets alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 52–57, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","R. H. Bruce Lockhart, <i>Memoirs of a British Agent</i> (London: Putnam, 1932), 292–97, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.237389. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Telegram to J. V. Stalin,” 7 July 1918, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 27, trans. Clemens Dutt (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 523, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/jul/07.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Interview Granted to an <i>Izvestia</i> Correspondent in Connection with the Left Socialist-Revolutionary Revolt,” 8 July 1918, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 27, trans. Clemens Dutt (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 534–35, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/jul/08.htm. [Open access]","А. В. Медведев, «Мятеж левых эсеров 6 июля 1918 г. и его оценки в исторической литературе», <i>Вестник Нижегородского университета им. Н. И. Лобачевского</i>, no. 1-1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/myatezh-levyh-eserov-6-iyulya-1918-g-i-ego-otsenki-v-istoricheskoy-literature. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Токарев, «Недолгий союз: к истории коалиционного правительства большевиков и левых эсеров», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i> 1, no. 2 (1992), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/nedolgiy-soyuz-k-istorii-koalitsionnogo-pravitelstva-bolshevikov-i-levyh-eserov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ю. Ватлин, «Полпредство Адольфа Иоффе: кризисный менеджмент между Москвой и Берлином в июле 1918 г.», <i>Вестник Брянского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (57) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/polpredstvo-adolfa-ioffe-krizisnyy-menedzhment-mezhdu-moskvoy-i-berlinom-v-iyule-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Left SRs kill the German ambassador?","a":"Because their Central Committee had decided on 24 June 1918 that terrorist acts against the leading representatives of German imperialism were needed to end the breathing-space created by the Brest-Litovsk peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They hoped the murder would tear up the treaty and bring back a war fought by peasant bands and individual terrorists rather than by regular armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin react so fiercely?","a":"Because the murder could bring a German attack at a moment when the Soviet state was very weak, and that night he wrote to Stalin that Russia was a hair's breadth from war and that the rebels must be crushed mercilessly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a statement to <i>Izvestia</i> the next day he said the masses would never forgive a party that had tried to drag them into war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the Left SRs really mean to seize power?","a":"Nobody agrees. The Fifth Congress resolved on 10 July that the murder was part of a plot to take power, while the party leader Maria Spiridonova insisted it was an act of protest and that the rest was self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian study of the assessments concludes that the revolt was also an attempt to overthrow the government, and calls a later claim that the Bolsheviks staged it themselves conjecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Left SR uprising"},
    "ru-582": {"questions":["A British visitor called it the old Courts of Justice when the Communist International was founded inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1919, under red banners in many languages.","A Civil War sanitary inspection found dust and cobwebs even in Lenin's study inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which housed both the Soviet cabinet and the Central Executive Committee."],"abstract":"The <b>Kremlin Senate</b> is the triangular classical building in the north-east of the Moscow Kremlin, built in 1776–1787 to designs by Matvei Kazakov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was conceived as an image of orderly government, and its plan around three inner courtyards culminates in a domed rotunda built as the hall for assemblies of the nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That dome, on the model of the Pantheon, rises behind the Kremlin wall at the midpoint of Red Square, an axis the Soviet era strengthened by building Lenin's Mausoleum in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the revolution it housed the judicial institutions, and a British visitor in 1919 still called it the old Courts of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Soviet rule it was known as the building of the Workers' and Peasants' Government, and the cabinets of the Russian republic and then of the Soviet Union met there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> It became the seat of government because of the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, for after the peace was signed on 3 March 1918 the Soviet leaders prepared to leave Petrograd, which Germany or the Allies might seize at one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lenin left the Smolny Institute at 9.30 in the evening of 10 March and reached Moscow the next night, and the Fourth Congress of Soviets confirmed the move and a 'temporary' transfer of the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Central Executive Committee and the Council of People's Commissars took over the Senate, while most of the leaders lived in the nearby Cavalry Building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin's study was in the building, and a sanitary inspection during the Civil War found even that room dusty, with cobwebs in the corners of the ceiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In March 1919 the founding congress of the Communist International met in a red-draped room of the same building, with Lenin presiding at a long table under banners in many languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["А. Н. Яковлев, «Ротонда Сената в Московском Кремле. Архитектурная графика, иконография проекта», <i>Актуальные проблемы теории и истории искусства</i>, no. 8 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rotonda-senata-v-moskovskom-kremle-arhitekturnaya-grafika-ikonografiya-proekta. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. Н. Горлов, «Матвей Казаков: архитектор и власть», <i>Вестник Московского государственного лингвистического университета. Общественные науки</i>, no. 3 (860) (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/matvey-kazakov-arhitektor-i-vlast. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>My Life</i>, ch. 30, “In Moscow” (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1930), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/mylife/ch30.htm. [Open access]","Т. В. Ковалёва, «Из „Разжалованной столицы…“ (по материалам оппозиционной прессы 1918 г.)», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 2 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/iz-razzhalovannoy-stolitsy-po-materialam-oppozitsionnoy-pressy-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. В. Девятов and О. К. Кайкова, «Кремлевские жители», <i>Свободная мысль</i>, no. 2 (1698) (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kremlevskie-zhiteli. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. К. Кайкова, «Деятельность управления Санитарного надзора Кремля в годы гражданской войны», <i>Вестник Московского университета. Серия 8. История</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deyatelnost-upravleniya-sanitarnogo-nadzora-kremlya-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arthur Ransome, <i>Six Weeks in Russia in 1919</i> (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/sixweeksinrussia00ransuoft. [Open access]","Ю. П. Бочаров and С. Б. Ткаченко, «Формирование столичных функций Москвы в планировочной структуре города с 1918 по 2018 год», <i>Academia. Архитектура и строительство</i>, no. 3 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-stolichnyh-funktsiy-moskvy-v-planirovochnoy-strukture-goroda-s-1918-po-2018-god. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government leave Petrograd in 1918?","a":"Because after the Brest-Litovsk peace Lenin and Trotsky feared that Germany or the Allies could take the revolutionary capital, and the government with it, in one swift blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky insisted that the move protected Petrograd itself as well, and he stayed behind for a week or two to soften the impression that the October capital was being demoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Senate's dome matter to Red Square?","a":"Because the dome rises behind the Kremlin wall at the midpoint of the square and marks its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet regime strengthened that axis when Lenin's Mausoleum was built in front of the Senate Tower, directly before the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it called the Courts of Justice in 1919?","a":"Because before 1917 it housed the judicial institutions and was known as their building, the name a British visitor still used when he attended the founding of the Communist International there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kremlin Senate"},
    "ru-583": {"questions":["Nicholas II, his wife, their five children and four of their household were shot in the basement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the night of 16 to 17 July 1918.","Demolished in September 1977, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Yekaterinburg left an empty site on which the Church on the Blood was consecrated in 2003 to the last imperial family."],"abstract":"The <b>Ipatiev House</b> was a two-storey mansion in Yekaterinburg in which the deposed Nicholas II, his wife, their five children and four of their household were shot on the night of 16 to 17 July 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Built in the 1880s on a steep slope that gave it an unusually large basement, it was bought in 1908 by the engineer N. N. Ipatiev and requisitioned by the Bolsheviks in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its guards called it the House of Special Purpose, and it was hemmed in by a wall of boards so high that only the tops of the windows could be seen from the street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nicholas, Alexandra and their daughter Maria were brought there from Tobolsk on 30 April 1918, 17 April by the Russian calendar, and the other children followed once the sick heir Alexei could travel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guards, mostly Bolshevik workers from the Ural factories, watched every move of the prisoners, and the food was far worse than in Tobolsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In July, with Czechoslovak and anti-Bolshevik forces closing on the town, the Ural regional soviet decided on the killing after consulting Moscow, and the commandant of the house, Yurovsky, carried it out in the basement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow announced only that Nicholas had been shot and that his wife and son had been sent to a safe place, which was untrue, and the presidium of the Central Executive Committee approved the Ural decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether the leaders in Moscow ordered the deaths or only approved them afterwards is still debated, since the official Soviet version held that the Ural soviet acted without consulting the party's Central Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Whites took Yekaterinburg, the tutor Pierre Gilliard found the basement walls and floor scarred by bullets and bayonets, and the investigator Sokolov reconstructed the killing step by step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The remains hidden in 1918 became widely known only when a grave was opened in 1991, and the house itself, demolished in September 1977, gave way to the Church on the Blood, consecrated in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 84–95, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","Pierre Gilliard, <i>Thirteen Years at the Russian Court</i>, trans. F. Appleby Holt (London: Hutchinson, 1921), 271–76, https://archive.org/details/thirteenyearsatr00gilluoft. [Open access]","Robert Wilton, <i>The Last Days of the Romanovs</i> (London: Thornton Butterworth, 1920), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/lastdaysofromano00wilt. [Open access]","Д. О. Серов and А. В. Федоров, «Расстрел бывшего императора Николая II и его семьи (1918 г.): историко-правовые аспекты», <i>Журнал российского права</i>, no. 10 (262) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rasstrel-byvshego-imperatora-nikolaya-ii-i-ego-semi-1918-g-istoriko-pravovye-aspekty. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. М. Горинов, «Е. А. Преображенский и расстрел царской семьи», <i>Известия Самарского научного центра Российской академии наук</i>, no. 3-1 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/e-a-preobrazhenskiy-i-rasstrel-tsarskoy-semi. [in Russian] [Open access]","О. В. Розина, «Историко-культурологическая реконструкция мест ссылки Николая II», <i>Верхневолжский филологический вестник</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/istoriko-kulturologicheskaya-rekonstruktsiya-mest-ssylki-nikolaya-ii. [in Russian] [Open access]","М. А. Князев, «„Конечно, самое-то ответственное было, чтобы укрыть, чтобы следов не осталось“: воспоминания чекиста И. И. Родзинского о сокрытии останков Царской семьи под Екатеринбургом в 1918 г.», <i>Вестник Екатеринбургской духовной семинарии</i>, no. 34 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/konechno-samoe-to-otvetstvennoe-bylo-chtoby-ukryt-chtoby-sledov-ne-ostalos-vospominaniya-chekista-i-i-rodzinskogo-o-sokrytii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the family moved to Yekaterinburg?","a":"Because the Soviet authorities thought the Ural industrial town a safer place of confinement than remote Tobolsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A commissar sent from Moscow in April 1918 took the train east towards Omsk instead, and the Ural soviet, suspecting him of double-dealing, had it stopped and turned back to Yekaterinburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the children killed as well?","a":"Because a public trial of Nicholas had first been planned, but no court could have sentenced young children, whereas a secret killing could dispose of the whole family as the front closed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The announcement that the wife and son had been sent to safety was meant to hide their deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the bodies so hard to find?","a":"Because the Ural Chekists' first concern, as one of them recalled, was to hide the remains so that no trace would be left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The grave became widely known only when it was opened in 1991, and a further burial was found in 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ipatiev House"},
    "ru-584": {"questions":["Twenty-one conditions of admission adopted in 1920 bound every member party of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to iron discipline and to an illegal organisation beside its legal one.","Saying that the workers of every country could no longer be led from one centre, Stalin dissolved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in May 1943 during the war with Germany."],"abstract":"The <b>Communist International</b>, or Comintern, was the world organisation of communist parties founded in Moscow in March 1919, which lasted until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its first congress met from 2 to 7 March 1919 in a smallish red-draped room in the Kremlin, with Lenin presiding behind a long table under banners hailing the Third International in many languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Communication with the outside world was so difficult that only nineteen parties and groups were admitted with full votes, and apart from the Russian Communist Party no large organised body was represented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin told a Moscow meeting that the congress showed the victory of the world communist revolution to be assured, and promised that his listeners would see a World Federative Republic of Soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the start its Executive Committee was firmly controlled by the Russian party, and the foreign delegations had only a formal role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The International was the instrument of the Bolsheviks' hope that the world war would end in a world revolution, and Germany, with its great industry, was the pivot on which that hope turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its Second Congress in the summer of 1920 adopted twenty-one conditions of admission, shaped largely by Lenin, which demanded democratic centralism and iron discipline and barred named reformist socialists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conditions also obliged every member party to build an illegal organisation beside its legal one and to agitate among the troops, so that some seemed designed to make legal communist parties impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same congress turned to Asia and the colonies as new fields of revolution, following Lenin's view that imperialism was the final stage of capitalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin dissolved the International in May 1943, in the middle of the war with Germany, saying that the workers' movement of every country could not be led from one international centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Vladimir Lenin, “Founding of the Communist International,” speech of 6 March 1919, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 28, trans. Jim Riordan (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 480–84, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1919/mar/06.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Terms of Admission into Communist International,” July 1920, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 31, trans. Julius Katzer (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1965), 206–11, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1920/jul/x01.htm. [Open access]","Arthur Ransome, <i>Six Weeks in Russia in 1919</i> (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1919), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/sixweeksinrussia00ransuoft. [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 381–83, 390–91, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","И. А. Суздальцев, «Роль РКП(б) в формировании организационной структуры Коминтерна», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-rkp-b-v-formirovanii-organizatsionnoy-struktury-kominterna. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. А. Суздальцев, «Когда же состоялось подлинное основание Коминтерна?», <i>Вестник Государственного университета просвещения. Серия: История и политические науки</i>, no. 3 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kogda-zhe-sostoyalos-podlinnoe-osnovanie-kominterna. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. И. Прищепа, «Эволюция роли Коминтерна во внешнеполитической доктрине СССР (1939–1945 гг.)», <i>Северный регион: наука, образование, культура</i>, no. 1 (31) (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-roli-kominterna-vo-vneshnepoliticheskoy-doktrine-sssr-1939-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks found an international?","a":"Because they believed that the unrest in Europe after the war could turn into revolution, and that a Soviet Germany joined to Soviet Russia would change everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lenin greeted the first congress by declaring that the victory of the world communist revolution was assured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the conditions of admission so strict?","a":"Because by 1920 many parties of the old Second International were applying to join without becoming truly communist, and Lenin feared the new body would be diluted into a copy of the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conditions imposed the centralised discipline of the Russian party on an international scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin dissolve it in 1943?","a":"Because, as he told the Politburo on 21 May 1943, experience had shown that the workers' movement of all countries could not be led from one international centre, and the announcement suited the wartime alliance with the Western powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A Russian study warns that it would be naive to read the step as the Bolsheviks giving up the idea of world revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Communist International"},
    "ru-585": {"questions":["Giant factories of over 1,000 workers, employing a larger share of the workforce in Russia than in America by 1914, were evidence for the theory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Trotsky's account of 1917.","Now used as a general model in international relations, the theory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew from an argument that workers could take power sooner in a backward country than in an advanced one."],"abstract":"<b>Uneven and combined development</b> is the explanation of the Russian Revolution set out by Leon Trotsky, who argued that a backward country catching up with advanced ones does not repeat their history stage by stage but combines archaic and modern forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Unevenness, Trotsky wrote, is the most general law of the historic process, and from it he derived what he called, for lack of a better name, the law of combined development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in his writing after the revolution of 1905, when he argued that the workers could come to power in an economically backward country sooner than in an advanced one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Working on the idea abroad, he finally formulated it as a law in his <i>History of the Russian Revolution</i>, whose English translation appeared in 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The theory has outlived its author and is now offered as a general explanatory model in the study of international relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Applied to 1917, it explained why the most backward great power of Europe had the first workers' revolution, a puzzle for Marxists who expected socialism to come first where capitalism was most advanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky's evidence was that Russian industry, built late, was more concentrated than any other, for in 1914 giant enterprises employed 41.4 per cent of Russian industrial workers against 17.8 per cent in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bourgeoisie too weak and too afraid of the workers to lead left the peasants to follow the young working class, so that within months the revolution put the proletariat in power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lenin likewise said that Russia had been first not because its workers were better prepared but because they were worse prepared, and he asked critics who said Russia lacked the economic premises for socialism why power could not be won first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other explanations put the world war at the centre, holding that it did not create the revolutionary situation but unleashed it, by breaking a state that had held back half a century of social change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, <i>The History of the Russian Revolution</i>, vol. 1, ch. 1, “Peculiarities of Russia’s Development,” trans. Max Eastman (1932), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/hrr/ch01.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>Results and Prospects</i> (St Petersburg, 1906), ch. 4, “Revolution and the Proletariat,” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1931/tpr/rp04.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Our Revolution (Apropos of N. Sukhanov’s Notes),” January 1923, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 33 (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1965), 476–80, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1923/jan/16.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “Founding of the Communist International,” speech of 6 March 1919, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 28, trans. Jim Riordan (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 480–84, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1919/mar/06.htm. [Open access]","Б. С. Илизаров, «О синхронизации истории России и Европы в ХХ веке: Ленин, Троцкий, Милюков», <i>История и современное мировоззрение</i> 6, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/o-sinhronizatsii-istorii-rossii-i-evropy-v-hh-veke-lenin-trotskiy-milyukov. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Е. Курапов, «Место теории неравномерного и комбинированного развития в современной науке о международных отношениях», <i>Общество: политика, экономика, право</i>, no. 7 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mesto-teorii-neravnomernogo-i-kombinirovannogo-razvitiya-v-sovremennoy-nauke-o-mezhdunarodnyh-otnosheniyah. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Russian industry look so modern?","a":"Because, Trotsky argued, a backward country adopts whatever is ready-made abroad and skips intermediate stages, so its late industry was more concentrated than that of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also noted that foreigners owned about 40 per cent of Russia's stock capital, and more in the leading branches of industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Marxists argue that 1917 was premature?","a":"Because many held, as Lenin summed it up, that the development of Russia's productive forces had not reached the level that makes socialism possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lenin replied that a country on the border between the West and the East was bound to show peculiarities without breaking the general line of world history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is this the only explanation of 1917?","a":"No. Another stresses the war: revolution had been incubating for half a century, but the war unleashed it by weakening the state and splitting the parties of the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A Russian study sets Trotsky's view beside the different accounts of Russia's path given by Lenin and by the liberal historian and politician Miliukov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Uneven and combined development"},
    "ru-586": {"questions":["Lenin told the Bolshevik party in July 1919 that the Soviet Republic must become a single military camp, in deed and not in word, to win the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Kolchak and Denikin.","Remembered in Soviet times as a heroic epoch bound up with foreign intervention, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a martial birthmark on the Soviet state that lasted until its end."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Civil War</b> was the armed struggle for power across the former empire that followed the October Revolution, in which the Bolsheviks' Reds defended the new Soviet state against the Whites, peasant rebels and national movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even its dates are disputed, for the long-standing scheme runs from the spring of 1918 to the Red occupation of Crimea in November 1920, while wider ones stretch it from February 1917 to 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russian demographers put the human losses of the whole period from 1914 to 1922, the world war included, at 11 to 18 million, and the population fell by about 11 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 2 and 3 million peasants took part in risings against grain requisitioning, many of them deserters from the Red Army in 1919 and 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By October 1920 the Red Army was 5.5 million strong on paper, although only about 700,000 of its men were active fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Seen from Moscow, it was a war on fronts all round the Soviet heartland, which Lenin told the party in July 1919 must become a single military camp, not in word but in deed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decisive crisis came in mid-October 1919, when Denikin's Volunteers took Orel, less than 400 kilometres (250 miles) from Moscow, and Yudenich's Northwestern Army fought its way into the suburbs of Petrograd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <i>Pravda</i> wrote the day after Orel fell that even the blind could now see the decisive days of the revolution had come, but within weeks Red counter-attacks were driving the Whites back towards the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet state wrote the war as a heroic epoch and a history of the victors, closely bound up with the foreign intervention, and the names of its Red heroes passed into national memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also left a martial birthmark on the Soviet system, visible to its end in its authoritarianism, its heavy-industry economy and its vast military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,”, trans. Jocelyne Serveau in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10171. [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 249–50, 266–67, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “All Out for the Fight Against Denikin!,” letter of the Central Committee, July 1919, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 29, trans. George Hanna (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 436–55, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1919/jul/03.htm. [Open access]","А. В. Черноножкин, «К изучению вариантов периодизации Гражданской войны в современной отечественной историографии», <i>Ученые записки Петрозаводского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-izucheniyu-variantov-periodizatsii-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-sovremennoy-otechestvennoy-istoriografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Соловьева, «Основные тенденции демографического развития России в пертурбационный период (1914–1922 гг.)», <i>Мир Большого Алтая</i>, no. 2 (2.1) (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osnovnye-tendentsii-demograficheskogo-razvitiya-rossii-v-perturbatsionnyy-period-1914-1922-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. И. Голдин, «Взаимоотношения власти и историков в контексте формирования исторической памяти о Гражданской войне в России», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 5 (37) (2024): 125–38, https://doi.org/10.31518/2618-9100-2024-5-10. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Gilley, “Red Army,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10294. [Open access]","Christopher Read, “Revolutions (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the war so hard to date?","a":"Because historians disagree about what the war was. One long-standing scheme runs from the spring of 1918 to November and December 1920, when the Reds occupied Crimea, while others begin with the February Revolution and end in 1922, when the fighting died down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was October 1919 the decisive moment?","a":"Because Denikin's forces stood at Orel on the direct road to Moscow while Yudenich was outside Petrograd, and the course of Russian history would have changed profoundly had either city fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet the White front was thinly held, and its last gains had been made by shifting units from other sectors because no reserves were left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the war shape the Soviet system?","a":"It militarised the ruling party: military service became a central marker of political loyalty, and even the language of politics took on a military tone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its mark remained in Soviet authoritarianism, in an economy geared to heavy industry and in a vast military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Russian Civil War"},
    "ru-587": {"questions":["Celebrated every 23 February after the rallies of 1918 that answered Lenin's call to defend the socialist fatherland, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was founded by a decree of the Soviet government.","Official Soviet figures counted 2,846,000 deserters from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1919 and 1920, most of them peasant conscripts who ran home at the first chance."],"abstract":"The <b>Red Army</b> was the army of Soviet Russia, created by the Bolsheviks in 1918 to defend their new state and to fight the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A decree of the Council of People's Commissars of 15 January 1918 by the Russian calendar, 28 January by the Western, called for a new army of volunteers aged 18 and over who accepted the ideals of the October Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only about 20,000 volunteers came forward instead of the 300,000 hoped for, and the new force was beaten at Narva when the Germans resumed their advance in February 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet tradition dates the army to 23 February 1918, when rallies in Petrograd, Moscow and other cities answered Lenin's call to defend the socialist fatherland, and the day was kept from 1922 as Red Army Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the end of the Civil War three quarters of its soldiers were peasants, and at its height in October 1920 it was 5.5 million strong on paper, though only about 700,000 were active fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under Trotsky, war commissar from the spring of 1918, the army was rebuilt as a regular force, with the election of officers abolished, military tribunals restored and service made compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the clash with the Czechoslovak Legion the government broke with the volunteer system, and the army grew from 331,000 men on 1 August 1918 to 800,000 by the end of the year and 3 million by January 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Official Soviet figures counted 2,846,000 deserters in 1919 and 1920, and about a million of them were caught in raids on towns and railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin wrote in July 1919 that the guerrilla spirit had caused far more defeats and losses than all the betrayals of former officers, and that where discipline and political work were firmest there were the most victories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trotsky held its fronts together from a special train, organised on the night of 7 August 1918, which carried a printing press and a radio station and ran over 105,000 kilometres (65,000 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Gilley, “Red Army,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10294. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, “The Path of the Red Army,” introduction to <i>How the Revolution Armed</i>, vol. 1, <i>1918</i>, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1918/military/ch02.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “All Out for the Fight Against Denikin!,” letter of the Central Committee, July 1919, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 29, trans. George Hanna (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 436–55, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1919/jul/03.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>My Life</i>, ch. 34, “The Train” (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1930), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/mylife/ch34.htm. [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 25–41, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","С. В. Чибисов, «Правовые основы становления Советской армии (100-летию со времени создания посвящается)», <i>Историко-правовые проблемы: новый ракурс</i>, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovye-osnovy-stanovleniya-sovetskoy-armii-100-letiyu-so-vremeni-sozdaniya-posvyaschaetsya. [in Russian] [Open access]","С. Ю. Чимаров and К. В. Шеянов, «„День защитника Отечества“ на страницах истории его учреждения: в контексте патриотического воспитания личного состава органов внутренних дел», <i>Международный журнал гуманитарных и естественных наук</i>, no. 2-2 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/den-zaschitnika-otechestva-na-stranitsah-istorii-ego-uchrezhdeniya-v-kontekste-patrioticheskogo-vospitaniya-lichnogo-sostava. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. А. Шувалов, «Боеспособность Рабоче-крестьянской Красной армии в период гражданской войны в России», <i>Вестник Брянского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boesposobnost-raboche-krestyanskoy-krasnoy-armii-v-period-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the volunteer army fail?","a":"Because the country was sick of war, so few men came forward, and units that elected their commanders and lacked trained officers would not obey orders and could not fight well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The defeat at Narva in February 1918 showed how weak the first volunteer force was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 23 February celebrated as its birthday?","a":"Because on that day in 1918 rallies in Petrograd, Moscow and other cities answered Lenin's appeal to defend the socialist fatherland, which began the mass mobilisation of revolutionary forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The day was kept as Red Army Day from 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many soldiers desert?","a":"Because most conscripts were peasants who went only under threat and often ran home at the first chance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A lack of supplies made it worse, since shortages caused desertion and desertion in turn forced more conscription, so the government added amnesties and welfare for soldiers' families to the punishments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Red Army"},
    "ru-588": {"questions":["Trotsky compared driving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of the Red Army because some had turned traitor to dismissing every engineer from the railways for the sake of a few saboteurs.","Nearly 50,000 former tsarist officers served the Reds as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1918 and 1920, a policy that Voroshilov's men at Tsaritsyn bitterly resented."],"abstract":"<b>Military specialists</b>, in Russian voenspetsy, were the former officers of the tsarist army whom the Bolsheviks took into the Red Army as commanders and staff officers during the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The policy belonged above all to Leon Trotsky, war commissar from the spring of 1918, who argued from the start for the general use of veteran officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 12 June 1918 and 15 August 1920, 48,409 former officers were taken into the Red Army, more than the 39,914 new Red commanders who graduated from Soviet military schools in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each was paired with a political commissar under regulations published in April 1918, and a commander's order had legal force only once the commissar had signed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trotsky also used harsher means, announcing in July 1918 that officers who refused to serve would go to concentration camps and ordering lists of the families of officers who deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The policy was bitterly contested inside the party, where many front-line Bolsheviks saw the specialists as enemies and the central military apparatus as their protector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the turn of 1919 Trotsky answered the critics in print, writing that the army had thousands of former regular officers in its service and that rejecting them because some had turned traitor would be like driving the engineers off the railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The resistance centred on Voroshilov at Tsaritsyn, where, Trotsky later wrote, specialists, staff graduates and Moscow were mentioned with hatred, and where Stalin backed the local men against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lenin sided with Trotsky in July 1919, writing that hundreds of specialists were betraying the Republic and would be shot, but that without the tens of thousands who served loyally the Red Army could not have been formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In fact the specialists deserted no more often than other Red commanders, though the damage could be great when a trained staff officer went over to the Whites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Leon Trotsky, “The Military Specialists and the Red Army,” in <i>How the Revolution Armed</i>, vol. 1, <i>1918</i>, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1918/military/ch24.htm. [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>My Life</i>, ch. 36, “The Military Opposition” (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1930), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1930/mylife/ch36.htm. [Open access]","Vladimir Lenin, “All Out for the Fight Against Denikin!,” letter of the Central Committee, July 1919, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 29, trans. George Hanna (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1972), 436–55, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1919/jul/03.htm. [Open access]","Christopher Gilley, “Red Army,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10294. [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 30–32, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","С. С. Войтиков, «„Разногласие есть и вынесено в печать“: дискуссия по военному вопросу в Советской России во второй половине 1918 – начале 1919 г.», <i>Новейшая история России</i>, no. 2 (10) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/raznoglasie-est-i-vyneseno-v-pechat-diskussiya-po-voennomu-voprosu-v-sovetskoy-rossii-vo-vtoroy-polovine-1918-nachale-1919-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Н. Моисеев, «Институт военных комиссаров в частях, учреждениях Красной армии и органах военного управления в годы Гражданской войны в России (1918–1920 гг.)», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/institut-voennyh-komissarov-v-chastyah-uchrezhdeniyah-krasnoy-armii-i-organah-voennogo-upravleniya-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks need tsarist officers?","a":"Because volunteer units with elected commanders could not stand against trained enemies, and the new army had no other source of competent leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lenin wrote that without the tens of thousands of specialists working loyally for it the Red Army could not have been formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was each specialist paired with a commissar?","a":"Because the Bolsheviks did not trust officers of the old regime, so the commissar was there to secure the commander's political loyalty and to strengthen his authority over the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A commander's orders became valid only with the commissar's signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the policy split the party?","a":"Because many Bolsheviks still wanted a class-based militia, and at the Eighth Party Congress in March 1919 the Military Opposition attacked the regular-army line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trotsky later saw Stalin and Voroshilov at Tsaritsyn as the centre of that resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ru-589": {"questions":["Holding to the slogan of a great, united, undivided Russia, the generals of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> quarrelled with Kuban Cossack politicians who wanted autonomy for their own land.","Kolchak in Siberia, Denikin in the south and Yudenich before Petrograd led the armies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, none of whose leaders openly promised to restore the Romanovs."],"abstract":"The <b>White movement</b> was the loose alliance of anti-Bolshevik armies and governments that fought the Reds in the Russian Civil War from late 1917 to 1920, led mostly by former generals and admirals of the imperial forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from the Alekseev officers' organisation founded at Novocherkassk on the Don in November 1917, which became the Volunteer Army when Kornilov arrived in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its first fighters were overwhelmingly officers, cadets and students, and in its early months it had few ordinary soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its other great centre was Siberia, where Admiral Kolchak was proclaimed Supreme Ruler, while Yudenich threatened Petrograd from the north-west and the Cossacks of the Don and the Kuban supplied much of the strength in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians remain divided between a critical view that stresses its reactionary aims and an apologetic one that sees it as Russia's healthy forces resisting a Bolshevik coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> None of its leaders openly made the restoration of the Romanovs his aim, held back by the memory of Rasputin, the lack of a striking Romanov prince and fear of losing opinion in France, Britain and America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their one fixed principle was a 'great, united, undivided Russia', and Denikin held to the slogan all the more firmly after the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk had carved new states out of the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That principle set them against the Kuban Cossack politicians, who cared above all for driving the Bolsheviks from their own territory and resented the generals' interference in its autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> White thinkers did consider autonomy and even federal arrangements for some peoples, but always within the territorial integrity of a restored Russian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The movement was also divided from within, between monarchist career officers and the often more left-leaning officers of wartime, and the narrow, conservative views of part of the generals repelled many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Volunteer Army,”, trans. Gary Goldberg in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10249. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,”, trans. Jocelyne Serveau in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10171. [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 94–95, 140–41, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","George Stewart, <i>The White Armies of Russia: A Chronicle of Counter-Revolution and Allied Intervention</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1933), 45–46, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89241. [Open access]","В. П. Изергина, «Взаимосвязь идеологии и социального состава Белого движения в годы Гражданской войны в России», <i>Инженерные технологии и системы</i>, no. 3-4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vzaimosvyaz-ideologii-i-sotsialnogo-sostava-belogo-dvizheniya-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. И. Наумова, «Автономизм и федерализм в идеологии и политике белого движения (1918–1920 гг.)», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 281 (2004), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/avtonomizm-i-federalizm-v-ideologii-i-politike-belogo-dvizheniya-1918-1920-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. М. Ипатов, «К вопросу о зарождении и становлении Белого движения на юге России в 1917–1918 гг.», <i>Культурный код</i>, no. 4 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/k-voprosu-o-zarozhdenii-i-stanovlenii-belogo-dvizheniya-na-yuge-rossii-v-1917-1918-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Whites not promise to restore the monarchy?","a":"Because the scandal of Rasputin, the absence of a striking personality among the Romanov princes and the fear of alienating opinion in France, Britain and America all argued against it, so no anti-Bolshevik leader avowed restoration as his aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Whites clash with the non-Russian peoples?","a":"Because they insisted on a Russia one and indivisible at the very moment when the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk had let Finland, the Baltic lands, Poland, Ukraine and others break away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some White thinkers offered autonomy, but only inside a restored Russian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Who fought in the first White army?","a":"Mostly officers and cadets: in its first weeks more than 30 per cent of the volunteers were officers, about half were cadets and about a tenth were students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later the armies grew by mobilising civilians and enlisting captured Red soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"White movement"},
    "ru-590": {"questions":["Arrested with Kornilov's supporters at Berdichev in 1917, the general <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> escaped to the Don and took command of the Volunteer Army when Kornilov was killed in 1918.","After the Novorossiysk catastrophe of March 1920 a military council at Feodosia replaced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Wrangel, and the general went into exile, dying at Ann Arbor in 1947."],"abstract":"<b>Anton Denikin</b> was a Russian general who led the Volunteer Army and then the Armed Forces of South Russia, the main White force in the south, from 1918 to 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in 1872 in Russian Poland to a father who had risen from the ranks to major, he graduated from the Academy of the General Staff and fought in the war with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the First World War he commanded the 4th Rifle Brigade, later a division, was honoured for twice taking Lutsk, and in 1917 rose to command the Western and then the South-Western Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He opposed the democratisation of the army and supported Kornilov's rising, and was arrested and held with other generals at Berdichev and then Bykhov, as his memoir <i>The Russian Turmoil</i> describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the October Revolution he escaped to the Don, took command of the Volunteer Army when Kornilov was killed outside Yekaterinodar in 1918, and held throughout to the slogan of a Russia one and indivisible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> His great moment came in the summer of 1919, when after the capture of Tsaritsyn he signed on 3 July the 'Moscow directive', aiming his armies at the capital by the shortest road through Kharkiv, Orel and Tula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wrangel later called the directive militarily illiterate and a death sentence for the armies of the south, because it scattered them across an enormous front from Kyiv through Voronezh to Tsaritsyn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 13 October 1919 his Volunteers took Orel, less than 400 kilometres (250 miles) from Moscow, but the line was thinly held and no reserves were left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Driven back to the sea, his armies broke up in what contemporaries called the Novorossiysk catastrophe of March 1920, and a military council at Feodosia chose Wrangel in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lived in exile in Europe and the United States, refused to work with the Germans during the occupation of France, and died at Ann Arbor in 1947, and in 2005 his remains were reburied at the Donskoy Monastery in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Denikin, Anton Ivanovich,”, trans. Christopher Gilley in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11245. [Open access]","Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Volunteer Army,”, trans. Gary Goldberg in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10249. [Open access]","Anton Denikin, <i>The Russian Turmoil: Memoirs, Military, Social, and Political</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1922), 329–33, https://archive.org/details/russianturmoilme00deniuoft. [Open access]","Р. Г. Гагкуев, «Гражданская война на Юге России: „Поход на Москву“ летом–осенью 1919 г.», <i>iPolytech Journal</i>, no. 10 (57) (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/grazhdanskaya-voyna-na-yuge-rossii-pohod-na-moskvu-letom-osenyu-1919-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Кулаков, «Лидер белого Юга России генерал А. И. Деникин», <i>Вестник Адыгейского государственного университета</i>, no. 3 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lider-belogo-yuga-rossii-general-a-i-denikin. [in Russian] [Open access]","William Henry Chamberlin, <i>The Russian Revolution, 1917–1921</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Macmillan, 1935), 249–50, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.178167. [Open access]","George Stewart, <i>The White Armies of Russia: A Chronicle of Counter-Revolution and Allied Intervention</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1933), 45–46, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.89241. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Denikin aim straight at Moscow?","a":"Because he took the shortest line, through Kharkiv, Orel and Tula, as the main direction of the offensive, rejecting advice to strike towards Tsaritsyn to link up with Kolchak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The directive also forced wavering Cossacks to decide whether to fight beyond their own lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his advance fail after Orel?","a":"Because his last gains had been made by moving units from other sectors, so no reserves were left, while Makhno's partisans took town after town in his rear and threatened his headquarters at Taganrog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is he judged so differently?","a":"Because his defenders see a patriot who fought an illegal Bolshevik regime, while his critics stress the reactionary politics of his government in the south, the atrocities and anti-Jewish pogroms of his army and his strategic mistakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Anton Denikin"},
    "ru-591": {"questions":["A polar explorer on Baron Toll's expedition and later commander of the Black Sea Fleet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resigned in June 1917 rather than accept the authority of the sailors' committees.","Handed over at Irkutsk by the Czechoslovak troops guarding his train and Russia's gold reserve, the former Supreme Ruler <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was shot there in February 1920."],"abstract":"<b>Alexander Kolchak</b> was a Russian admiral and polar explorer who, as Supreme Ruler from November 1918, led the White movement in Siberia and was recognised by the other White leaders as its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in 1874 into a noble family, he graduated from the Naval College in 1894, sailed on Baron Toll's polar expedition of 1900–1902 and commanded a battery at Port Arthur in the war with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the First World War he laid the minefields that kept the German fleet out of the Gulf of Riga in 1915, and in June 1916 he was made vice admiral and given the Black Sea Fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He resigned in June 1917 in protest at the sailors' committees, went to Britain and the United States on a naval mission, and in 1918 was sent with British backing to unite the anti-Bolshevik forces in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That attempt failed, partly because the Cossack atamans Semenov and Kalmykov, armed and paid by Japan, would not accept his authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In October 1918 he arrived in Omsk and became war minister of the Directory, the coalition government formed at Ufa, and on 18 November a coup by officers and Cossacks handed him dictatorial power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A British officer in Omsk reported that the Council of Ministers had pressed him to accept supreme power, which he said he had taken to end anarchy, not to restore the old regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His armies took Perm and Ufa in an offensive in the spring of 1919 but were thrown back beyond the Urals by midsummer, and Omsk fell in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Retreating east with the gold reserve, he was handed over at Irkutsk in January 1920 by the Czechoslovak troops guarding his train, and he was shot there on the night of 7 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet writers treated him as a reactionary who ruled by repression, a charge the Czechoslovak leaders had made in November 1919, while post-Soviet Russia raised a monument to him in Irkutsk in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Kolchak, Aleksandr Vasil'evich,” trans. Gary Goldberg, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/kolchak-aleksandr-vasilevich/. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","John Ward, <i>With the “Die-Hards” in Siberia</i> (New York: George H. Doran, 1920), 155–58, https://archive.org/details/withdiehardsinsi00ward. [Open access]","Н. П. Бучко and Ю. Н. Ципкин, «Политические взгляды и деятельность А. В. Колчака в 1917 - 1920 гг», <i>Армия и общество</i>, no. 6 (43) (2014), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/politicheskie-vzglyady-i-deyatelnost-a-v-kolchaka-v-1917-1920-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Czechoslovak Legions (Russian Empire),” trans. Gary Goldberg, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/czechoslovak-legions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Paul Dunscomb, “Siberian Intervention 1918-1922,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/siberian-intervention-1918-1922/. [Open access]","С. Н. Савченко, «Дальневосточный казачий сепаратизм в годы гражданской войны (1918-1920)», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dalnevostochnyy-kazachiy-separatizm-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny-1918-1920. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the ministers at Omsk turn to a military dictator?","a":"Because the Directory created at Ufa had broken down: a British officer was told that a Socialist Revolutionary proclamation was undermining army discipline and that the ministers saw no choice but to place supreme power in one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian study adds that Kadet politicians and officers, backed by a British battalion, had given up on the moderate socialists and staked everything on a military dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Kolchak not command the atamans of the Far East?","a":"Because Japan armed and financed Semenov and Kalmykov and backed their efforts to undermine the government at Omsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Semenov refused to recognise the Supreme Ruler at all, and the Far Eastern atamans never sent a single Cossack to the front against the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Czechoslovaks give him up at Irkutsk?","a":"Because they wanted to leave Russia, not to fight for him: in November 1919 their leaders publicly accused his regime of mass terror and declared that they took no part in Russian affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Irkutsk their command handed him, his prime minister and the gold reserve to the local authorities in exchange for free passage east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alexander Kolchak"},
    "ru-592": {"questions":["The land orders of 25 May 1920, which let Crimean peasants acquire part of the great estates as property, were issued by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as commander-in-chief of the White south.","After the Red Army broke through at Perekop in November 1920, 126 ships carried the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and tens of thousands of civilians from the Crimea to Constantinople."],"abstract":"<b>Pyotr Wrangel</b> was a Russian baron and cavalry general, the last commander-in-chief of the White forces in southern Russia, which he held in the Crimea from April to November 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in 1878 into a noble family of German origin, he trained as a mining engineer before turning to the army, and in August 1914 he won the Order of St George for seizing an enemy battery in East Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Bolshevik seizure of power he joined the Volunteer Army, and his Caucasus Army took Tsaritsyn, the city now called Volgograd, in June 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He quarrelled with Anton Denikin over the march on Moscow, preferring a drive along the Volga to join Kolchak's armies, and he was removed in December 1919 and left for Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Denikin's beaten forces had been shipped from Novorossiysk to the Crimea, a military council invited Wrangel to take command, and he arrived at Sevastopol on a British battleship on 4 April 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> He renamed the remnant the Russian Army and set out to make the Crimea a White state whose laws might win over the population, rather than to march on Moscow again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 May 1920 he approved a set of land orders that let peasants acquire part of the great estates as property, a programme a former Duma deputy likened to Stolypin's agrarian policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British refused further military aid, and after the Red Army, joined for a time by Nestor Makhno's insurgents, broke through at Perekop in November 1920, he ordered the evacuation of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 31 October to 3 November by the Russian calendar, 13 to 16 November by the Western one, 126 ships carried soldiers and civilians to Constantinople, though estimates of their number range from 56,000 to about 146,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In exile he founded the Russian All-Military Union in 1924 to hold the émigré officers together, and he died in Brussels on 25 April 1928, his memoirs appearing in print a few months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Wrangel, Petr Nikolaevich, Baron,” trans. Gary Goldberg, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/wrangel-petr-nikolaevich-baron/. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Pyotr Wrangel, <i>The Memoirs of General Wrangel, the Last Commander-in-Chief of the Russian National Army</i>, trans. Sophie Goulston (London: Williams & Norgate, 1929), https://archive.org/details/memoirsgeneralwrangel. [Open access]","С. Г. Кащенко, П. Е. Таран and В. И. Шевченко, «Правовое регулирование земельных отношений вКрыму при Врангеле (март - ноябрь 1920 г. )», <i>Ученые записки Крымского федерального университета имени В. И. Вернадского. Юридические науки</i>, 25 (64), no. 2 (2012): 43–52, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pravovoe-regulirovanie-zemelnyh-otnosheniy-vkrymu-pri-vrangele-mart-noyabr-1920-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. П. Челышев, «Исход на юг», <i>Пространство и Время</i>, no. 1 (11) (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ishod-na-yug. [in Russian] [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Christopher Gilley, “Makhno, Nestor Ivanovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/makhno-nestor-ivanovich/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Wrangel and Denikin fall out in 1919?","a":"Because Wrangel opposed the march on Moscow and wanted to push along the Volga from Tsaritsyn to join Kolchak's armies in the east, which he thought would strengthen the Whites as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Denikin insisted on taking Moscow quickly and believed Wrangel's objections came from personal ambition rather than strategy, and the quarrel ended with Wrangel's removal in December 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he issue land orders in the Crimea?","a":"Because he needed the peasants on his side in a war he was losing, and he said that the army must carry land to the peasants on its bayonets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His orders of 25 May 1920 aimed to create a numerous class of small peasant proprietors by limiting the great estates, an aim close to Stolypin's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many leave with him in November 1920?","a":"Because the Crimea was the last White territory, and his order of the day warned that there was no Russian land left to move to while leaving everyone free to choose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ships carried soldiers and civilians, thousands of women and children among them, to Constantinople, where with Allied help the refugees were settled on the Princes' Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pyotr Wrangel"},
    "ru-593": {"questions":["Trotsky's order of 25 May 1918 that every armed man of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found on the railway be shot was answered by a revolt from the Volga to Siberia.","The American aide-mémoire of 17 July 1918 justified sending American troops to Vladivostok as a way of helping the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> consolidate its forces, which it called an immediate necessity."],"abstract":"The <b>Czechoslovak Legion</b> was a volunteer army of Czechs and Slovaks formed inside the Russian army to fight Austria-Hungary, whose revolt along the Trans-Siberian Railway in May 1918 turned scattered resistance to the Bolsheviks into full civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 100,000 Czechs and Slovaks lived in Russia in 1914, and a volunteer battalion formed that autumn was later swelled by Austro-Hungarian prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rifle brigade fought in the Brusilov breakthrough of 1916 and in the Galician offensive of June 1917, and by September 1917 it had grown into an independent corps of two divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Bolsheviks made peace with Germany, the Czechoslovak National Council under Tomáš Masaryk arranged for France to treat the corps as French troops and bring it to the Western Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The route chosen ran east along the Trans-Siberian Railway to Vladivostok and on by sea, and by May 1918 its trains were strung out from Penza on the Volga to the Pacific coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 14 May 1918 a legionary was badly wounded at Chelyabinsk station by an iron object thrown from a train of Hungarian prisoners, and the quarrel with the local soviet that followed turned the corps against the Bolsheviks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 May Leon Trotsky ordered every Czechoslovak found armed on the railway to be shot on the spot, and that same day the legionaries began to fight, seizing town after town along the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the revolt was planned in advance by Czech commanders or grew out of a chance incident is still argued, and recent Russian studies have reached opposite conclusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under its protection Socialist Revolutionaries set up a government at Samara in June 1918, and the American landing at Vladivostok that summer was justified as help for the Czechoslovaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Weary of a war no longer theirs once Czechoslovakia was proclaimed in October 1918, the legionaries handed Kolchak over at Irkutsk in January 1920, and over 56,000 soldiers had left Vladivostok by 2 September 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Evgenii Vladimirovich Volkov, “Czechoslovak Legions (Russian Empire),” trans. Gary Goldberg, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/czechoslovak-legions-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Д. В. Салдугеев, «Чехословацкий легион в России», <i>Вестник Челябинского государственного университета</i>, no. 2 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/chehoslovatskiy-legion-v-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Миронов and П. Э. Сальников, «ЧЕШСКО-СЛОВАЦКИЙ КОРПУС И БОЛЬШЕВИКИ ВЕСНОЙ 1918 Г», <i>Вестник Тамбовского университета. Серия: Гуманитарные науки</i>, no. 6 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/cheshsko-slovatskiy-korpus-i-bolsheviki-vesnoy-1918-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","“The Secretary of State to the Allied Ambassadors: Aide-Mémoire,” 17 July 1918, in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Russia</i>, vol. 2 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office), 287–90, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Russiav02/d345. [Open access]","Paul Dunscomb, “Siberian Intervention 1918-1922,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/siberian-intervention-1918-1922/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the corps travel east to reach France?","a":"Because France had agreed to treat it as part of the French army, and the route agreed for the journey ran by the Trans-Siberian Railway to Vladivostok, then by ship to the Americas and on to Western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet authorities let the trains go on condition that the regiments hand over part of their weapons, keeping their rifles and a few machine guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the revolt matter so much?","a":"Because the legion was one of the few forces in Russia that had kept its military effectiveness, and its rising let local anti-Communist groups take the central Volga and the railway backbone of Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One Russian study calls the mutiny of 25 May the beginning of full-scale civil war in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the legionaries stop fighting for the Whites?","a":"Because the proclamation of Czechoslovakia in October 1918 and the end of the World War left them wanting only to go home, and some regiments refused orders to take up positions at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1919 their leaders publicly accused Kolchak's regime of mass terror and declared that they took no part in Russian affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Czechoslovak Legion"},
    "ru-594": {"questions":["An American aide-mémoire of 17 July 1918 warned that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be a way of making use of Russia rather than of serving her.","Japanese troops, the last foreign force of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to leave, did not withdraw from Vladivostok and the Maritime Province until 25 October 1922."],"abstract":"The <b>Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War</b> was the landing of British, French, American, Japanese and other troops on the edges of the former Russian Empire between 1918 and 1922, begun to keep an Eastern Front against Germany and continued as support for the Whites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roughly 2,000 British soldiers arrived at Murmansk in March 1918 to guard the northern ports and their war stores, and Allied troops later held Arkhangelsk too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the Czechoslovak revolt, President Woodrow Wilson proposed in July 1918 a joint landing of 7,000 American and 7,000 Japanese troops at Vladivostok, and an expedition of many nations followed in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American aide-mémoire of 17 July warned that intervention would be a way of making use of Russia, not of serving her, and limited American troops to guarding stores and helping the Czechoslovaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan sent about 72,000 men and backed the Cossack atamans in the Far East, while about 5,500 Americans fought through the winter on the Dvina front south of Arkhangelsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The armistice of November 1918 removed the original reason for the landings, yet Britain and France now gave direct help to the Whites, and the French landed at Odessa in December 1918 and left in April 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commander of the American force in Siberia, William Graves, kept to orders not to interfere in Russian affairs and accused Japan of arming and protecting atamans whose troops murdered civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet historians, following a formula of Stalin's, presented the war as three campaigns of the Entente against the revolution, a view later scholarship has abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The intervening forces were small, divided in their aims and quick to leave, and one historian argues that the occupation of Ukraine and the Baltic by the Central Powers in 1918 was the more significant foreign intervention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Americans left Arkhangelsk in June 1919 and Vladivostok on 1 April 1920, but Japanese troops stayed in the Maritime Province until 25 October 1922, the last foreign force to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Paul Dunscomb, “Siberian Intervention 1918-1922,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/siberian-intervention-1918-1922/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","“The Secretary of State to the Allied Ambassadors: Aide-Mémoire,” 17 July 1918, in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Russia</i>, vol. 2 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office), 287–90, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Russiav02/d345. [Open access]","William S. Graves, <i>America's Siberian Adventure, 1918–1920</i> (New York: Jonathan Cape & Harrison Smith, 1931), https://archive.org/details/americas-siberian-adventure-1918-20. [Open access]","Joel R. Moore, Harry H. Mead and Lewis E. Jahns, <i>The History of the American Expedition Fighting the Bolsheviki: Campaigning in North Russia, 1918–1919</i> (Detroit: Polar Bear Publishing, 1920), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/22523. [Open access]","В. В. Рябов and А. Ю. Нефедов, «ИНТЕРВЕНЦИЯ В РОССИИ: БЕСКОРЫСТНАЯ ПОМОЩЬ ИЛИ СОБСТВЕННЫЕ ИНТЕРЕСЫ?», <i>Вестник Московского городского педагогического университета. Серия: Исторические науки</i>, no. 4 (36) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/interventsiya-v-rossii-beskorystnaya-pomosch-ili-sobstvennye-interesy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies land troops in Russia in 1918?","a":"Because Russia's separate peace with Germany threatened their war: they wanted to keep the war stores in the northern and Pacific ports out of enemy hands and even to rebuild an Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The revolt of the Czechoslovak Legion along the Trans-Siberian Railway gave them a further reason, since the legion could be presented as an ally in need of rescue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the intervention continue after the armistice?","a":"Because victory let the British and French give direct help to the Russians who had stayed loyal to them, and their fleets could now enter the Baltic and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet the same victory removed the strategic reason for the landings, and war-weariness and demobilisation left few troops to send.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan stay longer than the others?","a":"Because expansionists in the Japanese army wanted to take control of the region from Russia, and in March 1920 the government announced that its troops would remain until order was restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The massacre of about 700 Japanese soldiers and civilians at Nikolaevsk that spring strengthened the decision, and the last troops left Vladivostok only on 25 October 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War"},
    "ru-595": {"questions":["Branded bandits by the Bolsheviks and swollen by deserters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew between 2 and 3 million rebels in the regions hardest hit by grain requisitions.","Peasant risings such as the war of the chapany on the Volga in March 1919 are often grouped under the name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though few rebels used it."],"abstract":"The <b>Green armies</b> were the peasant insurgents of the Russian Civil War, bands and armies that fought the Reds, and often the Whites too, to defend their villages against requisitioning, conscription and outside rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Contemporaries called them Greens probably after the forests in which they hid, though few groups used the name for themselves, and the Bolsheviks branded them bandits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many were deserters from the Red Army, and historians estimate that between 2 and 3 million people took part in the rebellions, of whom about 500,000 were arrested by the Cheka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their aims were local and seldom tied to a party, but the larger risings called for genuine soviets free of party dictatorship and claimed the legacy of the revolution for the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Classifying them is still a problem, since Green bands shaded into nationalist forces, anarchist armies such as Nestor Makhno's and plain criminal gangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war of the chapany, named after the peasants' kaftan-like coats, spread across the Volga districts of Syzran and Sengilei in March 1919 after a clash with a food brigade and was crushed within two weeks by 13,000 troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Risings in the Soviet rear in 1919 helped the White advances, yet when the Whites neared Moscow many peasants turned back to the Reds, fearing that the generals would take away the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The largest risings came after the Whites were beaten, when Aleksandr Antonov's partisan army in Tambov province had a mounted force of 20,000 to 30,000 and took the Bolsheviks more than a year to defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most deserters in Tambov province gave themselves up under an amnesty in August 1920, which suggests that the Antonov rising was a peasant rebellion rather than a movement of Greens in the strict sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Together with the Kronstadt rising, these revolts pushed Lenin to abandon forced grain requisitioning for the New Economic Policy in 1921, a reprieve for the villages that lasted until collectivisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Gilley, “Peasant Uprisings/Tambovshchina,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/peasant-uprisingstambovshchina/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “War Communism,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/war-communism/. [Open access]","И. Д. Лошкарёв, ««Третьи силы» Гражданской войны в России: некоторые концептуальные аспекты», <i>Актуальные проблемы гуманитарных и естественных наук</i>, no. 8 (2011), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/treti-sily-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-rossii-nekotorye-kontseptualnye-aspekty. [in Russian] [Open access]","Д. П. Иванов, «Тамбовское восстание 1920-1921 гг. С точки зрения терминологии», <i>Манускрипт</i>, no. 6-2 (80) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tambovskoe-vosstanie-1920-1921-gg-s-tochki-zreniya-terminologii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did peasants take up arms against the Bolsheviks?","a":"Because requisitioning parties took their grain and mobilisation orders took their sons, and armed resistance often began as a village's answer to a food brigade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Bolshevik food army and the committees of poor peasants used force on a scale that recalled an army occupying enemy territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Greens sometimes help the Reds against the Whites?","a":"Because peasants feared the White generals more, expecting them to take back the land seized in the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Denikin threatened Moscow in October 1919 the number of risings fell and 250,000 deserters returned to the Red Army of their own accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the name Green hard to define?","a":"Because it covered very different things, from village self-defence militias to the warlord bands of Makhno or Grigoriev, and few groups chose it for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russian historians also sort peasant resistance into banditry, desertion, risings and small war, and some keep the word Green for the most desperate and politicised deserters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Green armies"},
    "ru-596": {"questions":["Proclaiming free soviets and land without compensation, the insurgent leader <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made peace with the Bolsheviks in October 1920 to fight Wrangel and was hunted by them weeks later.","Crossing into Romania with his last detachment in August 1921, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent his exile in Poland and Paris writing defences of his movement until his death in 1934."],"abstract":"<b>Nestor Makhno</b> was a Ukrainian anarchist and peasant commander whose insurgent army controlled much of the steppe around Huliaipole, in the south-east of present-day Ukraine, between 1918 and 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The youngest son of a poor peasant family, he joined an anarcho-communist group in 1905, was arrested for its robberies and escaped the death penalty in 1910 only because of his age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Freed by the February Revolution, he returned home, chaired the local peasant union that became the Huliaipole soviet and oversaw the seizure of landowners' estates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the German and Austro-Hungarian occupation of 1918 he formed a partisan band, defeated an Austro-Hungarian force near Dibrivka in October and built from the captured arms an army that at its height may have numbered 80,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving on peasant carts, it was in one historian's judgement the only combat-ready regular army that a peasant movement of the war ever created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Makhno fought beside the Red Army against Denikin and Wrangel, and three times the Bolsheviks turned on him, for he defended a programme of free soviets, land for the peasants without compensation and free exchange between town and village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His anarchist advisers, among them Pyotr Arshinov, denounced the Bolshevik party dictatorship, yet historians still debate whether the movement was anarchist, peasant or simply a band held together by violence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Like most armies in Ukraine his followers committed pogroms against Jews and Mennonites, although anarchists, and one recent Russian study, insist that he punished antisemitism and armed Jewish villagers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In October 1920 he made peace with the Bolsheviks to fight Wrangel, whose envoy he had publicly executed, but once the Crimea fell the Red Army hunted him for half a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In August 1921 he crossed into Romania with his last detachment, and he died of tuberculosis, in poverty, in Paris in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher Gilley, “Makhno, Nestor Ivanovich,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/makhno-nestor-ivanovich/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","П. А. Аршинов, <i>История махновского движения (1918–1921 гг.)</i> (Берлин, 1923), https://archive.org/details/arshinovpaistorijamahnovskogodvizhenij27. [in Russian] [Open access]","В. В. Кондрашин, «МАХНОВЩИНА», <i>Исторический курьер</i>, no. 4 (36) (2024): 138–50, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mahnovschina. [in Russian] [Open access]","П. Ф. Алешкин, «Махновщина: реалии и небылицы анархизма», <i>Власть</i>, no. 1 (2008), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mahnovschina-realii-i-nebylitsy-anarhizma. [in Russian] [Open access]","Christopher Gilley, “Peasant Uprisings/Tambovshchina,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/peasant-uprisingstambovshchina/. [Open access]","Pyotr Wrangel, <i>The Memoirs of General Wrangel, the Last Commander-in-Chief of the Russian National Army</i>, trans. Sophie Goulston (London: Williams & Norgate, 1929), https://archive.org/details/memoirsgeneralwrangel. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Makhno and the Bolsheviks keep falling out?","a":"Because he kept his military and political independence even while his troops were formally part of the Red Army, and his meetings attacked the party dictatorship, the Cheka and Moscow's agricultural policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian study puts the conflict down to his refusal to apply war communism and the Soviet emergency organs in the territory his army held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did peasants follow an anarchist?","a":"Because his anarchist slogans largely matched the peasants' old wish for land and local power, and he joined a peasant movement that already existed rather than starting one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To many villagers free soviets meant an end to taxes and requisitions for a town that gave them nothing in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his record on pogroms disputed?","a":"Because his troops, like most armies in Ukraine, committed pogroms, and there were cases where he seems to have shielded commanders who had led them from Bolshevik reprisals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His defenders point out that many anarchists close to him were Jewish, that his revolutionary council publicly condemned pogroms and that one battery of his army was made up entirely of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nestor Makhno"},
    "ru-597": {"questions":["In April 1921 Lenin described <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a makeshift forced on the regime by war and ruin, under which the state took the peasants' surpluses on loan for paper money.","The Kronstadt rising and the peasant war in Tambov persuaded the Bolsheviks in March 1921 to abandon <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a New Economic Policy that permitted free trade."],"abstract":"<b>War communism</b> is the name given to the economic system of Soviet Russia from mid-1918 to early 1921, when the Bolsheviks ran the whole economy as an arm of the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its measures included forced requisitioning of food, the nationalisation of almost all industry, a state monopoly of trade, strict labour discipline and the partial replacement of money by payment in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decree of 13 May 1918 that opened the food dictatorship ordered every holder of grain to surrender all he had beyond seed and his family's needs, and declared those who held back surpluses enemies of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Armed food detachments and committees of poor peasants enforced it, and in January 1919 planned requisitioning by quota replaced their raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of this continued wartime controls, for the tsarist and Provisional governments had already fixed grain prices and tried to buy grain through state agencies, with little success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Historians still argue whether war communism was forced on the Bolsheviks by emergency or was an attempt to leap straight into a moneyless communist society, and both sides can quote Lenin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In April 1921 Lenin called it a makeshift that war and ruin had forced on the regime, under which the state took from the peasant all his surpluses and sometimes part of his necessities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six months later he admitted that the party had assumed it could proceed straight to socialism through state production and distribution, and he called that earlier policy a mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1921 industrial output had collapsed, the hungry towns had emptied, the illegal market supplied an estimated 65 to 70 percent of the food people lived on, and peasants in Tambov and elsewhere were in open revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kronstadt rising of March 1921 completed the crisis, and the New Economic Policy replaced requisitioning with a tax in kind and allowed private trade again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, “War Communism,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/war-communism/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “The Tax in Kind,” 21 April 1921, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 32, trans. Yuri Sdobnikov (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1965), 329–65, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1921/apr/21.htm. [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “Seventh Moscow Gubernia Conference of the Russian Communist Party,” 29 October 1921, in <i>Collected Works</i>, vol. 33, trans. David Skvirsky and George Hanna (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1965), 81–108, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1921/oct/29.htm. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","«Декрет ВЦИК и СНК о чрезвычайных полномочиях народного комиссара по продовольствию», 13 мая 1918 г., in <i>Декреты Советской власти</i>, vol. 2 (Москва, 1959), 264–66, Электронная библиотека исторического факультета МГУ, http://www.hist.msu.ru/ER/Etext/DEKRET/18-05-13.htm. [in Russian] [Open access]","Iaroslav Golubinov, “Food and Nutrition (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/food-and-nutrition-russian-empire/. [Open access]","П. Ф. Алешкин, «Трагичный финал политики военного коммунизма в советской России», <i>Власть</i>, no. 8 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tragichnyy-final-politiki-voennogo-kommunizma-v-sovetskoy-rossii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks requisition grain instead of buying it?","a":"Because money had lost its value and there were almost no manufactured goods to offer the villages, so peasants had little reason to sell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime also treated requisitioning as class war, setting up committees of poor peasants on the mistaken assumption that they would help seize the surpluses of the richer ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ideology ruled out simply paying the peasants more for their grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about its causes?","a":"Because the same measures can be read as answers to an emergency or as socialist doctrine put into practice, and Lenin and other Bolsheviks can be quoted on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some historians also trace it back to the wartime controls of 1914–1918 in Russia and Germany, which the Bolsheviks inherited and pushed much further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin replace it in 1921?","a":"Because the crop failure of 1920 had deepened the ruin, large-scale industry could not be restored quickly, and he concluded that the first task was to improve the condition of the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tax in kind went with exchange between town and country, which Lenin admitted was freedom of trade and therefore capitalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War communism"},
    "ru-598": {"questions":["In March 1921 Lenin asked the Tenth Party Congress to replace <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a tax in kind, the step that opened the New Economic Policy.","The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was also given to a grain levy proposed by the tsarist agriculture minister Aleksandr Rittikh on the eve of the February Revolution."],"abstract":"<b>Prodrazvyorstka</b>, short for the Russian words for 'food apportionment', was the system under which the Soviet state set compulsory quotas of grain and other food for each province to deliver at fixed prices during the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method was older than the Bolsheviks, for in the last weeks of the monarchy the agriculture minister Aleksandr Rittikh had proposed a grain levy of the same name, without success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1918 the Bolsheviks tried to seize grain directly, sending armed food detachments into the villages with help from committees of poor peasants, but by one estimate only 10 percent of the targets were met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of the Council of People's Commissars and the Central Executive Committee of 11 January 1919 replaced these raids with planned apportionment, a quota fixed in Moscow and handed down to the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each province divided its quota among its districts, each district among its rural districts and villages, and each village shared it out household by household, the whole commune answering for every member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The amounts often exceeded what could be had: Siberia was told to deliver 110 million poods of grain, about 1.8 million tonnes, while the regional economic council thought 60 million poods the most that could be shipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Payment came in a currency that was losing its value and in manufactured goods that were scarce, so peasants met the quotas with hidden stores, smaller sowings and, in the end, armed revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Local soviets that dragged their feet were treated as saboteurs, and in Vyatka province chairmen of village soviets were arrested and held in prison until their villages delivered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The risings of 1920 and 1921, from Tambov province to Western Siberia, showed the party how dangerous the system had become.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the Tenth Party Congress on 15 March 1921 Lenin proposed replacing prodrazvyorstka with a tax in kind, calling it above all a question of the working class's relations with the peasantry, and the change opened the New Economic Policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Iaroslav Golubinov, “Food and Nutrition (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/food-and-nutrition-russian-empire/. [Open access]","Д. Н. Китаев, «Формирование и функционирование системы реализации продовольственной разверстки в Вятской губернии в 1919-1920 гг», <i>Вестник Вятского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-i-funktsionirovanie-sistemy-realizatsii-prodovolstvennoy-razverstki-v-vyatskoy-gubernii-v-1919-1920-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Е. Н. Бурдина, «Власть, продразвёрстка и сибирское крестьянство накануне восстания 1921 г», <i>Вестник Томского государственного университета</i>, no. 426 (2018): 64–73, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vlast-prodrazvyorstka-i-sibirskoe-krestyanstvo-nakanune-vosstaniya-1921-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. I. Lenin, “Report on the Substitution of a Tax in Kind for the Surplus Grain Appropriation System,” 15 March 1921, in “Tenth Congress of the R.C.P.(B.),” Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1921/10thcong/ch03.htm. [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “War Communism,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/war-communism/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the quota fall on the village rather than on each farmer?","a":"Because the state handed the quota down through province, district and rural district, and the village commune was made collectively responsible for delivering it, each member answering for the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Members of the village soviets, by contrast, were held personally responsible for their own share and could be arrested if their village fell short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the system fail to feed the towns?","a":"Because peasants received little of value in return, as the currency collapsed and manufactured goods were short, and they resisted by hiding grain and sowing less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the campaign of 1918 only about a tenth of the targets were met, and planned apportionment from 1919 improved matters only a little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lenin call replacing it a political question?","a":"Because it concerned the attitude of the working class to the peasantry, the two classes whose struggle or agreement he said would decide the fate of the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1921 peasants from Tambov to Siberia were in revolt, and a tax in kind with local free exchange was his answer to that crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Prodrazverstka"},
    "ru-599": {"questions":["A Soviet decree of 5 September 1918 on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered class enemies isolated in concentration camps and everyone linked to White Guard plots shot.","Trotsky called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a weapon used against a class doomed to destruction, without which the Russian bourgeoisie would have throttled the revolution before Europe rose."],"abstract":"The <b>Red Terror</b> was the campaign of mass arrests, hostage-taking and executions that the Bolsheviks proclaimed against their class and political enemies in the autumn of 1918, carried out chiefly by the Cheka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Executions by the Cheka had already been rising through the summer, from about 200 in the first half of 1918 to more than 800 in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The trigger came on 30 August 1918, when Moisei Uritsky, head of the Petrograd Cheka, was assassinated and Lenin was badly wounded in an attempt blamed on the Socialist Revolutionary Fanny Kaplan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of the Council of People's Commissars on 5 September ordered class enemies to be isolated in concentration camps and everyone connected with White Guard organisations, plots and risings to be shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hostages from the old elite, officers, officials, bankers and their relatives, were among the first victims, and lists of them were printed in the Cheka's own weekly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In September alone the Cheka shot at least 5,000 people and in October and November another 2,000, by the count of one Russian historian of the death penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cheka's own published figure for the whole of 1918 was 6,300 executions, while the émigré historian Sergei Melgunov counted 50,000 for the second half of the year alone, a figure some Russian scholars treat as a minimum and others as a ceiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figure of 1.7 million victims quoted widely in the late 1980s came from a commission set up by Denikin and is not trusted, and many historians think a full count of either Red or White terror impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trotsky defended the terror as a weapon against a class doomed to destruction, arguing that without it the Russian bourgeoisie would have throttled the revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The mass terror was scaled back after an amnesty in November 1918, but executions, hostage-taking and camps remained tools of the Cheka through the rest of the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Evan Mawdsley, “International Responses to the Russian Civil War (Russian Empire),” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/international-responses-to-the-russian-civil-war-russian-empire/. [Open access]","М. Б. Колотков, «Значение Декрета «о красном терроре» от 5 сентября 1918 г. В формировании системы политических репрессий в советской России в 1918-1922 гг», <i>Актуальные проблемы российского права</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/znachenie-dekreta-o-krasnom-terrore-ot-5-sentyabrya-1918-g-v-formirovanii-sistemy-politicheskih-repressiy-v-sovetskoy-rossii-v-1918-1922-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","И. С. Ратьковский, «Эволюция института смертной казни в первый год Советской власти», <i>Вестник Санкт-Петербургского университета. История</i>, no. 2 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evolyutsiya-instituta-smertnoy-kazni-v-pervyy-god-sovetskoy-vlasti. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. С. Соколов, «Роль и место органов ВЧК в политике красного террора», <i>Власть</i>, no. 2 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-i-mesto-organov-vchk-v-politike-krasnogo-terrora. [in Russian] [Open access]","Н. А. Почешхов, Ж. О. Абрегова and Р. М. Шхачемуков, «ПОТЕРИ НАСЕЛЕНИЯ В ГОДЫ ГРАЖДАНСКОЙ ВОЙНЫ В РОССИИ (1917-1922 ГГ.): ДИСКУССИОННОЕ ПРОСТРАНСТВО ИСТОРИЧЕСКОЙ ДЕМОГРАФИИ», <i>Современная научная мысль</i>, no. 6 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/poteri-naseleniya-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-rossii-1917-1922-gg-diskussionnoe-prostranstvo-istoricheskoy-demografii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Leon Trotsky, <i>Dictatorship vs. Democracy (Terrorism and Communism): A Reply to Karl Kautsky</i> (New York: Workers Party of America, 1922), Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/38982. [Open access]","О. В. Бабенко, «2018. 03. 003. Ратьковский И. С. Хроника красного террора ВЧК. Карающий меч революции. - М. : Яуза: Эксмо, 2017. - 320 с», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5. История</i>, no. 3 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2018-03-003-ratkovskiy-i-s-hronika-krasnogo-terrora-vchk-karayuschiy-mech-revolyutsii-m-yauza-eksmo-2017-320-s. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the terror begin when it did?","a":"Because on 30 August 1918 the head of the Petrograd Cheka was killed and Lenin was wounded, and the regime answered with mass reprisals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The death penalty had already returned in practice that summer, and once the Left Socialist Revolutionaries were driven out of the Cheka in July nothing stood in the way of mass repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the numbers of victims so uncertain?","a":"Because the Cheka's published statistics left out people shot while putting down revolts, and some death sentences were never carried out or were suspended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of what is known about the killings was reconstructed later from eyewitness accounts rather than from official returns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bolsheviks defend terror openly?","a":"Because they saw it as a weapon of class war: Trotsky argued that it hastened the destruction of a bourgeoisie that would not give up power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lenin had written that a revolutionary who halts before the law in a moment of sharp struggle is a poor one, since laws in a time of transition are only temporary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Red Terror"},
    "ru-600": {"questions":["The American commander in Siberia held Japan responsible for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the murderous rule of Semenov and Kalmykov, since Japan armed and protected both atamans.","Refusing to obey Admiral Kolchak and sending no Cossacks to his front, the Far Eastern <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned peasants and even Kolchak's own troops against the White cause."],"abstract":"<b>Atamanshchina</b> is the Russian name for the rule of independent or half-independent warlords during the Civil War, men who raised their own armed bands and governed the territory they held by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The word comes from ataman, the elected leader of a Cossack host or settlement, and it was applied to self-made chiefs on every side, Red, White and Green alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its most notorious examples were the White Cossack atamans of Siberia and the Far East, where, in the words of one history of the Cossacks, brutal reigns of atamans were established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Grigory Semenov held the Trans-Baikal and Ivan Kalmykov, ataman of the Ussuri Cossacks, ruled at Khabarovsk, both nominally Whites but armed and paid by Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan gave Semenov's detachment more than 3 million roubles by June 1918, and the atamans refused to recognise Admiral Kolchak or to send a single Cossack to his front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The American commander William Graves wrote that Semenov's and Kalmykov's soldiers roamed the country killing and robbing under Japanese protection, and that Kalmykov killed with his own hands where Semenov ordered others to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American officers estimated that several hundred people were executed by Kalmykov's troops near Khabarovsk alone, and Graves wrote of the 'killing stations' that Semenov kept in his territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese troops helped them repress the partisans, and the violence fed both anti-Japanese resistance and criticism of the intervention in the Japanese press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian historian of the Far Eastern Cossacks concludes that the atamans' terror against civilians turned workers, peasants, Kolchak's own troops and most of the local Cossacks against the White cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Whites collapsed, Kalmykov fled to China and was killed by the Chinese, while Semenov escaped to Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William S. Graves, <i>America's Siberian Adventure, 1918–1920</i> (New York: Jonathan Cape & Harrison Smith, 1931), https://archive.org/details/americas-siberian-adventure-1918-20. [Open access]","Paul Dunscomb, “Siberian Intervention 1918-1922,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/siberian-intervention-1918-1922/. [Open access]","С. Н. Савченко, «Дальневосточный казачий сепаратизм в годы гражданской войны (1918-1920)», <i>Россия и АТР</i>, no. 4 (2007), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dalnevostochnyy-kazachiy-separatizm-v-gody-grazhdanskoy-voyny-1918-1920. [in Russian] [Open access]","Siobhan Peeling, “Cossacks,” in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/cossacks/. [Open access]","Alexandre Sumpf, “Russian Civil War,” trans. Jocelyne Serveau, in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/russian-civil-war/. [Open access]","Д. П. Иванов, «Тамбовское восстание 1920-1921 гг. С точки зрения терминологии», <i>Манускрипт</i>, no. 6-2 (80) (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tambovskoe-vosstanie-1920-1921-gg-s-tochki-zreniya-terminologii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Т. Б. Уварова, «2017. 03. 023. «Атаманщина» и «Партизанщина» в Гражданской войне: идеология, военное участие, кадры: сб. Статей и материалов / Под ред. Пасадского А. В. - М. : АИРО-XXI, 2015. - 856 с», <i>Социальные и гуманитарные науки. Отечественная и зарубежная литература. Серия 5. История</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2017-03-023-atamanschina-i-partizanschina-v-grazhdanskoy-voyne-ideologiya-voennoe-uchastie-kadry-sb-statey-i-materialov-pod-red-pasadskogo-a. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan support the atamans?","a":"Because Japanese expansionists, especially on the army general staff, wanted to wrest the region from Russia, and pliant atamans served that aim better than a strong government at Omsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japan paid Semenov's detachment more than 3 million roubles by June 1918 and, unlike the British and French, never asked how the money was spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans clash with Kalmykov?","a":"Because his troops arrested people of some means, tortured them for their money and shot some as Bolsheviks, until his own men mutinied and sought American protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Graves proposed warning Kalmykov in writing that if he or his men murdered another Russian he would be arrested and handed to a Russian civil court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the term not only a White one?","a":"Because it describes a pattern rather than a side: any independent or half-independent leader who built a militarised local regime could be called an ataman, and the word is used of the first stage of the Tambov peasant rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A Russian collection on the subject covers Red commanders and peasant leaders such as Makhno and Antonov as well as White Cossack atamans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atamanshchina"}
  };
  (window.CARD_EXTRA_IN = window.CARD_EXTRA_IN || []).push({ CARD_EXTRA: CARD_EXTRA });
})();
